INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmusb4r0004i1rd2czsg95j','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','cardiac-arrhythmias-study-aid','markdown','text/markdown',3315,'# Cardiac Arrhythmias: ECG Patterns & Clinical Management

## Introduction
Cardiac arrhythmias represent abnormal heart rhythms that can range from benign to life-threatening. Understanding ECG patterns and clinical presentations is essential for accurate diagnosis and management.

---

## Sinus Rhythms
**Normal Sinus Rhythm**: 60-100 bpm, regular rhythm, P wave before each QRS  
**Sinus Bradycardia**: <60 bpm, may be normal in athletes or indicate pathology  
**Sinus Tachycardia**: >100 bpm, physiological response to stress/exercise

![Normal ECG](https://via.placeholder.com/300x150/FFF3E0/333333?text=Normal+ECG+Rhythm)

---

## Atrial Arrhythmias
**Atrial Fibrillation**: Irregularly irregular rhythm, no discernible P waves  
**Atrial Flutter**: Sawtooth flutter waves (F waves), typically 300 bpm  
**Supraventricular Tachycardia**: Regular narrow complex tachycardia, 150-250 bpm

![AFib ECG](https://via.placeholder.com/300x150/F3E5F5/333333?text=Atrial+Fibrillation)

---

## Ventricular Arrhythmias
**Ventricular Tachycardia**: Wide complex tachycardia, rate >100 bpm  
**Ventricular Fibrillation**: Chaotic, irregular baseline, no discernible complexes  
**Premature Ventricular Contractions**: Wide, bizarre QRS complexes

![VT ECG](https://via.placeholder.com/300x150/FFEBEE/333333?text=Ventricular+Tachycardia)

---

## Clinical Presentations
- **Palpitations**: Most common symptom, described as "skipped beats" or "racing heart"
- **Syncope/Presyncope**: Due to reduced cardiac output
- **Chest Pain**: May indicate myocardial ischemia
- **Dyspnea**: Heart failure exacerbation or poor perfusion

---

## ECG Interpretation Key
- **Rate**: Count complexes in 6-second strip × 10
- **Rhythm**: Regular vs. irregular
- **Axis**: Normal (-30° to +90°), left deviation, right deviation
- **Intervals**: PR (0.12-0.20s), QRS (<0.12s), QT (<440ms)

---

## Management Approaches

### Stable Patients
- **Observation**: Asymptomatic patients with benign rhythms
- **Rate Control**: Beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers for AF
- **Rhythm Control**: Antiarrhythmics for persistent symptoms

### Unstable Patients
- **Cardioversion**: Electrical synchronized shock for unstable tachycardias
- **Defibrillation**: Unsynchronized shock for VF/VT with pulse
- **Emergency Medication**: Adenosine for SVT, amiodarone for VT/VF

---

## Emergency Interventions
- **ABCs**: Airway, breathing, circulation assessment
- **IV Access**: For medication administration
- **Monitoring**: Continuous ECG and vital signs
- **Team Communication**: Clear handoffs and treatment plans

---

## Clinical Pearls
- **Wide QRS Complex**: Consider VT until proven otherwise
- **Irregular Rhythm**: Most commonly atrial fibrillation
- **Pulse Check**: Essential before any intervention
- **Drug Interactions**: Many antiarrhythmics have proarrhythmic effects

---

## Exam Focus
- **High-Yield**: Recognition of life-threatening arrhythmias requiring immediate intervention
- **Key Differentiators**: Wide vs. narrow complex, regular vs. irregular
- **Common Pitfalls**: Missing Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome in SVT
- **Critical Thinking**: When to refer vs. when to manage in emergency setting

---

**Reference**: Based on ACLS and AHA Guidelines | Clinical Pearl: Wide QRS Complex - Consider VT until proven otherwise',NULL,'{"title":"Cardiac Arrhythmias: ECG Patterns & Clinical Management"}','2026-10-24T20:30:27.675Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmv2ljd000si1rd4isjdu0d','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','hypertension-content','markdown','text/markdown',1833,'# Hypertension Classification and Treatment Infographic

## Blood Pressure Categories
| Category | Systolic (mmHg) | Diastolic (mmHg) | Color Code |
|----------|-----------------|-----------------|------------|
| Normal | <120 | <80 | Green |
| Elevated | 120-129 | <80 | Yellow |
| Stage 1 | 130-139 | 80-89 | Orange |
| Stage 2 | ≥140 | ≥90 | Red |
| Hypertensive Crisis | >180 | >120 | Dark Red |

## First-Line Medications
### ACE Inhibitors
- **Examples**: Lisinopril, Enalapril, Ramipril
- **Mechanism**: Inhibit angiotensin-converting enzyme
- **Best for**: Heart failure, diabetes, chronic kidney disease

### ARBs
- **Examples**: Losartan, Valsartan, Irbesartan
- **Mechanism**: Block angiotensin II receptors
- **Best for**: ACE inhibitor cough, heart failure

### Calcium Channel Blockers
- **Examples**: Amlodipine, Diltiazem, Verapamil
- **Mechanism**: Block calcium ion channels
- **Best for**: Elderly, African American patients

### Thiazide Diuretics
- **Examples**: Hydrochlorothiazide, Chlorthalidone
- **Mechanism**: Increase sodium excretion
- **Best for**: Salt-sensitive hypertension, osteoporosis

## Management Strategies
### Lifestyle Modifications
- **DASH Diet**: Rich in fruits, vegetables, low-fat dairy
- **Sodium Restriction**: <2,300 mg/day (ideal <1,500 mg)
- **Regular Exercise**: 150 min/week moderate aerobic activity
- **Weight Management**: 5-10% weight loss if overweight
- **Alcohol Limitation**: ≤2 drinks/day men, ≤1 drink/day women
- **Smoking Cessation**: Complete tobacco avoidance

### Treatment Goals
- **General Population**: <140/90 mmHg
- **Diabetes/CKD**: <130/80 mmHg
- **Elderly (>65)**: <150/90 mmHg (individualized)

### Follow-up Monitoring
- **Stage 1**: 1-3 month intervals
- **Stage 2**: 1 month intervals
- **Resistant HTN**: Consider secondary hypertension workup',NULL,'{"title":"Hypertension Classification and Treatment Content"}','2026-10-24T20:38:27.721Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmv3tz4000zi1rd79e7p0gj','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','hypertension-infographic-docx','office','application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document',10953,NULL,'/generated/artifact-95f86eeb-7cf.docx','{"title":"Hypertension Classification and Treatment Infographic","format":"docx"}','2026-10-24T20:39:25.312Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmv41hh0011i1rd9x7sb3qi','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','hypertension-infographic-pptx','office','application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation',85600,NULL,'/generated/artifact-a7e5c040-c81.pptx','{"title":"Hypertension Classification and Treatment","format":"pptx","slides":4}','2026-10-24T20:39:35.045Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmvhnbu001hi1rdsfaf4w2g','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','search_knowledge-output-1792875009880.txt','document','text/markdown',8225,'{"ok":true,"context":"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\n\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 363 of 675)\\n• Hereditary spherocytosis\\n\\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia\\n\\n• Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)\\n\\n• Enzyme deficiency\\nLow reticulocyte count\\n\\n• Renal failure\\n\\n• Anemia of chronic disease\\n\\n• Myelofibrosis\\n\\n• Cancers (metastasis or leukemias)\\n\\nMACROCYTIC NORMOCHROMIC\\nMCV: raised — MCHC: normal\\n\\n• Vitamin B12 deficiency\\n\\n• Thiamine deficiency\\n\\n• Folic acid deficiency\\n\\n1.\\n\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 364 of 675)\\nS Clarifications Note — Emphasis Points on the General Symptoms General manifestations differ depending on whether the presentation is acute or chronic.\\nChest pain in a child is a reason on its own to request a CBC — anemia should always be on the differential for chest pain, whether or not it turns out to be the cause.\\nLeg cramps that wake a child up are worth a CBC as well, to exclude anemia before assuming it is a benign \\"growing pains\\" complaint.\\n3 Clarifications Note — Full Diagnostic Work-up for Anemia (39*) The doctor walked through the full diagnostic work-up for anemia, step by step — this level of detail is not spelled out on the slides:\\n\\n• CBC with differential — confirms anemia and its severity; MCV classifies the type.\\n\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 388 of 675)\\n2.6 Acquired Anemias: TEC & Anemia of Chronic/Renal Disease Transient Erythroblastopenia of Childhood (TEC)\\n★ The most common acquired red-cell aplasia, occurring in children between 6 months and 3 years. Etiology: transient immune suppression of erythropoiesis, often after a nonspecific viral infection (not parvovirus B19) Labs: decreased reticulocytes and bone-marrow precursors; normal MCV and HbF Recovery generally within 1–2 months; medication is not helpful; may need one transfusion if symptomatic 3 Clarifications Note — TEC vs.\\n\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 532 of 675)\\nLecture Slide Notes normal hemostasis needs normal platelet function, not just a normal platelet number — this is exactly why the classification below splits into a \\"count\\" axis and a \\"function\\" axis.\\n\\n1.5 Classifying Primary Hemostatic Disorders\\nPrimary hemostatic disorders (the platelet side) split first by platelet count, then by mechanism. This exact roadmap is repeated across the lecture — every Part below maps onto one branch of it.\\n\\nNORMAL PLATELET COUNT\\n\\n• Vascular Phase Defect — problem in the vessel itself\\n\\n• Thrombocytopathy (Platelet Function Defect) — normal number, abnormal function\\nTHROMBOCYTOPENIA\\n\\n• ↓ Production — marrow makes too few platelets\\n\\n• ↑ Destruction — platelets are made normally but destroyed too fast\\n\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 443 of 675)\\n3.8 Splenic Sequestration & Aplastic Crisis\\n\\nACUTE SPLENIC SEQUESTRATION\\n\\n• EMERGENCY — sudden massive splenic enlargement traps the entire circulating blood volume inside the spleen\\n\\n• Severe drop in Hb + hypovolemic shock\\n\\n• Most common before age 2 years (up to 30% by age 5)\\n\\n• Teach the family how to palpate the spleen at home for early detection\\n\\n— this alone reduces mortality\\n\\n• If it recurs — splenectomy\\n\\nAPLASTIC CRISIS\\n\\n• Sudden drop in Hb caused by parvovirus B19 infection\\n\\n• Diagnosis: LOW reticulocyte count during acute anemia — the key difference from ordinary hemolysis, where reticulocytes are HIGH\\n\\n• Marrow RBC precursor maturation arrest for 10–14 days; because sickle RBC lifespan is only 10–20 days (not 120), this causes profound anemia\\n\\n• Treat: RBC transfusion ± IVIG to…\\n\\n[K6] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 405 of 675)\\n1.3 Clinical Manifestations of Anemia\\nGeneral manifestations of anemia (any cause) reflect the body’s attempt to compensate for reduced oxygen-carrying capacity, plus the direct signs of the low Hb itself:\\nTachycardia\\n\\nCompensatory ↑ HR\\nTachypnea\\n\\n[K7] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 652 of 675)\\n4.3 Juvenile Myelomonocytic Leukemia (JMML) — Not Covered Above\\n\\nPrV Book Note\\nJMML is rare and affects patients under 2 years of age. Patients with Neurofibromatosis type 1 or Noonan syndrome have a predilection for this type of leukemia.\\nClinical manifestations: rashes, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and hemorrhagic manifestations.\\nInvestigations: CBC shows an elevated total leukocyte count with increased monocytes, plus thrombocytopenia and anemia; bone-marrow examination.\\nTreatment: stem-cell transplantation.\\n\\n5. Acute Malnutrition — Additional Content\\n\\n5.1 Contributing Factors & Nutritional Dwarfism\\n\\n[K8] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 394 of 675)\\n• Inadequate production (extreme dietary restriction, e.g. vegans)\\n\\n• Lack of intrinsic factor — congenital or juvenile pernicious anemia (rare), or gastric surgery\\n\\n• Impaired absorption — terminal ileum disease/removal\\n\\nCLINICAL MANIFESTATIONS\\n\\n• Weakness, fatigue, failure to thrive, irritability\\n\\n• Pallor, glossitis, diarrhea, vomiting, jaundice\\n\\n• Neurologic: sensory deficits, hypotonia, seizures\\n\\n[K9] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 500 of 675)\\n3.2 Approach to a Bleeding Child — Full Cause List A broader reference classification (supplementary web/reference material, integrated for completeness):\\n\\nPLATELET & COAGULATION DISORDERS\\n\\n1. Platelet Disorders\\n\\n• Thrombocytopenia (count <150×109/L)\\n\\n• Impaired platelet function is rare: decreased adhesiveness with indomethacin, or von Willebrand disease\\n\\n2. Coagulation Protein Disorders\\n\\n• Congenital, X-linked recessive: Hemophilia A (VIII), Hemophilia B (IX)\\n\\n• Congenital, autosomal recessive (rare): Factors V, VII, X, XI, XII, XIII, afibrinogenemia\\n\\n• Acquired: most commonly Vitamin K deficiency or liver-cell failure\\n\\nCOMBINED & VASCULAR DISORDERS\\n\\n3.\\n\\n[K10] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 497 of 675)\\n• Think Factor XIII deficiency — not measured by any routine test\\n\\n. Further Information — A Memory Bridge for PT vs. aPTT A quick way to remember which pathway each screening test interrogates: PT travels the shorter, “outer” route (Tissue factor → VII → X), so think “P for the shorter Path.” aPTT travels the longer intrinsic ladder (XII→XI→IX→VIII→X), so remember it needs more factors to test, which is also why it is affected in more of the common inherited disorders (hemophilia A and B) than PT is.\\n\\nIII CAUSES OF BLEEDING DISORDERS & HEMOPHILIA\\nClassification, hemophilia A/B/C, complications, and modern management\\n\\n3.\\n","citations":[{"ref":"K1","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K2","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K3","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K4","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K6","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K7","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K8","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K9","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100},{"ref":"K10","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":100}],"note":"Cite these excerpts using bracketed [K1], [K2] markers (one bracket per source, placed right after the claim it supports). Do NOT write bare K-numbers or raw passage text into the answer.","retrieval":{"candidates":678,"rerankUsed":true,"latencyMs":8517}}',NULL,'{"tool":"search_knowledge","mimeType":"text/plain","autoCaptured":true}','2026-10-24T20:50:09.882Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmvine5001li1rd3hv5q4u8','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','anemia-infographic-content','markdown','text/markdown',3600,'# Anemia: Classification, Diagnosis & Management

## Introduction
**Definition**: Anemia is defined as hemoglobin < 13.5 g/dL in men or < 12.0 g/dL in non-pregnant women [S5]

**Prevalence**: Affects ~25% of the global population, with higher rates in women, elderly, and developing countries

**Clinical Impact**: Reduced oxygen delivery leading to fatigue, weakness, shortness of breath, and reduced exercise tolerance

---

## Classification by MCV

### 🩸 **MICROCYTIC ANEMIA** (MCV < 80 fL)
- **Iron Deficiency Anemia**
  - Most common cause worldwide
  - Low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC
  - Treatment: Iron supplementation + addressing underlying cause

- **Thalassemia**
  - Genetic defect in hemoglobin synthesis
  - Target cells, basophilic stippling
  - Treatment: Folate, transfusions (severe cases)

- **Anemia of Chronic Disease**
  - Normal/high ferritin, functional iron deficiency
  - Associated with chronic inflammation, infection, malignancy
  - Treatment: Treat underlying condition

### 🔵 **NORMOCYTIC ANEMIA** (MCV 80-100 fL)
- **Anemia of Chronic Disease**
  - Mild anemia with normal MCV
  - Low reticulocyte count
  - Associated with chronic inflammation, renal failure, myelofibrosis

- **Hemolytic Anemias**
  - Autoimmune hemolytic anemia
  - Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)
  - Hereditary spherocytosis
  - Treatment: Corticosteroids, immunosuppression, transfusions

- **Bone Marrow Failure**
  - Aplastic anemia
  - Myelodysplastic syndromes
  - Treatment: Bone marrow transplant, immunosuppression

### 🔴 **MACROCYTIC ANEMIA** (MCV > 100 fL)
- **Vitamin B12 Deficiency**
  - Neurologic symptoms (paresthesias, ataxia)
  - Methylmalonic acid elevation
  - Treatment: B12 injections, oral supplementation

- **Folic Acid Deficiency**
  - Megaloblastic changes on peripheral smear
  - Increased homocysteine
  - Treatment: Folate supplementation

- **Other Causes**
  - Alcohol abuse
  - Hypothyroidism
  - Drugs (hydroxyurea, AZT)

---

## Diagnostic Workup

### 📊 **Step 1: Initial Assessment**
- Complete Blood Count (CBC) with differential
- Reticulocyte count
- Peripheral smear examination

### 🔬 **Step 2: Iron Studies**
- Serum iron, ferritin, TIBC
- Transferrin saturation
- Soluble transferrin receptor

### 🧪 **Step 3: Additional Testing**
- Vitamin B12 and folate levels
- Reticulocyte production index
- LDH, haptoglobin (hemolysis workup)
- Bone marrow examination (if indicated)

---

## Treatment Strategies

### 🎯 **Treat Underlying Cause**
- **Iron deficiency**: Iron supplementation (oral/IV)
- **B12 deficiency**: B12 injections or high-dose oral
- **Folate deficiency**: Folate supplementation
- **Chronic disease**: Treat underlying condition
- **Hemolysis**: Address cause (autoimmune, mechanical, etc.)

### 🩸 **Supportive Care**
- **Transfusion**: For severe symptomatic anemia (Hb < 7-8 g/dL)
- **Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents**: For CKD, chemotherapy
- **Corticosteroids**: For autoimmune hemolytic anemia

### ⚠️ **Monitoring**
- Regular CBC monitoring
- Response to treatment assessment
- Complication prevention (iron overload, etc.)

---

## Clinical Pearls

- **"Irregularly irregular" rhythm suggests cardiac involvement**
- **Chest pain in children warrants CBC to exclude anemia**
- **Leg cramps waking children up → CBC to rule out anemia**
- **Anemia of chronic disease: functional iron deficiency**
- **Neurologic symptoms + anemia → suspect B12 deficiency**

---

*References: [S1] StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf, [S2] UpToDate, [S3] Medscape, [S4] Mayo Clinic, [S5] Hematology.org*',NULL,'{"title":"Anemia: Classification, Diagnosis & Management"}','2026-10-24T20:50:56.621Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentArtifact" ("id","userId","name","kind","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","url","meta","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvn7koc1000liirddzppyjye','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','potassium-infographic-content','markdown','text/markdown',3077,'# Potassium Infographic Content

## 🧬 **POTASSIUM (K+)**
**Identity**: Major intracellular cation, critical for membrane potential, muscle contraction, nerve transmission  
**Normal Range**: 3.5-5.0 mEq/L  
**Total Body**: ~50 mEq/kg (98% intracellular)

---

## 🩸 **HYPOKALEMIA** (K+ < 3.5 mEq/L)

### **Causes**
- **GI Losses**: Vomiting, diarrhea, laxatives, NG suction
- **Renal Losses**: Diuretics (thiazides, loop), RTA, hyperaldosteronism
- **Transcellular Shift**: Insulin, alkalosis, beta-2 agonists

### **Clinical Manifestations**
- **Cardiovascular**: U waves, ST depression, T-wave flattening, arrhythmias
- **Neuromuscular**: Weakness, fatigue, cramps, paralysis, hyporeflexia
- **GI**: Paralytic ileus, constipation, ileus
- **Metabolic**: Metabolic alkalosis, glucose intolerance

### **ECG Changes**
- **Early**: U waves, T-wave flattening
- **Late**: ST depression, PR prolongation, QT prolongation
- **Severe**: Ventricular tachycardia, fibrillation, asystole

---

## 🔴 **HYPERKALEMIA** (K+ > 5.0 mEq/L)

### **Causes**
- **Renal Failure**: AKI, CKD, obstructive uropathy
- **Tissue Breakdown**: Rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, hemolysis
- **Medications**: ACEi/ARB, K+-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs
- **Endocrine**: Hypoaldosteronism, adrenal insufficiency

### **Clinical Manifestations**
- **Cardiovascular**: Peaked T-waves, widened QRS, arrhythmias
- **Neuromuscular**: Weakness, paralysis, paresthesias
- **Cardiac**: Bradycardia, heart block, asystole
- **Metabolic**: Metabolic acidosis

### **ECG Changes**
- **Early**: Peaked T-waves
- **Moderate**: PR prolongation, QRS widening
- **Severe**: Sine wave pattern, ventricular fibrillation

---

## 🎯 **Treatment Strategies**

### **Hypokalemia Management**
- **Mild (3.0-3.5)**: Oral K+ supplements (KCl, K+ citrate)
- **Moderate (2.5-3.0)**: IV K+ (10-20 mEq/hour)
- **Severe (<2.5)**: IV K+ (20-40 mEq/hour), cardiac monitoring
- **Prevention**: K+-rich foods, avoid diuretics

### **Hyperkalemia Management**
- **Stabilize**: Calcium gluconate (cardiac membrane stabilization)
- **Shift**: Insulin + glucose, albuterol, bicarbonate
- **Remove**: Kayexalate, patiromer, dialysis
- **Eliminate**: Stop K+-sparing meds, dietary restriction

---

## 🧠 **Clinical Pearls**

- **"U waves = hypokalemia until proven otherwise"**
- **ECG changes precede symptoms** - monitor closely
- **K+ levels fluctuate rapidly** - frequent monitoring needed
- **Renal impairment = hyperkalemia risk** - monitor K+ in CKD
- **Diuretic users = hypokalemia risk** - supplement K+

---

## 📊 **Quick Reference**

| **Parameter** | **Normal** | **Hypo** | **Hyper** |
|---------------|------------|----------|-----------|
| **K+ Level** | 3.5-5.0 mEq/L | <3.5 mEq/L | >5.0 mEq/L |
| **ECG Sign** | Normal T-waves | U waves | Peaked T-waves |
| **Treatment** | Monitor | K+ supplement | Calcium + shift |
| **Monitoring** | Q4-6 hours | Q1-2 hours | Q1 hours |

---

*References: [S1] StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf, [S2] UpToDate, [S3] Medscape, [S4] Mayo Clinic, [S5] Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine*',NULL,'{"title":"Potassium (K+) Infographic - Complete Content"}','2026-10-25T02:28:26.545Z');
