All questions are now pending review in your question bank and can be accessed through your MedQ dashboard. The questions cover different causes of short stature including constitutional, genetic, and skeletal dysplasia conditions.','[{"id":"call_-7222937554989872705","name":"run_question_generation_agent","args":"{\\"topic\\":\\"short stature\\",\\"system\\":\\"Endocrine\\",\\"types\\":[\\"mcq\\"],\\"difficulties\\":[\\"Medium\\"],\\"count\\":3,\\"focusHint\\":\\"Include clinical scenarios and require images for visual understanding\\",\\"useKnowledgeBase\\":true}"},{"id":"call_-7222915255519667583","name":"task","args":"{\\"description\\":\\"Generate exactly 3 MCQ questions about short stature in the endocrine system. Each question should be Medium difficulty, include clinical scenarios, and require images for visual understanding. Return the questions as a complete JSON array with all required fields including explanations, distractor analysis, clinical pearls, mnemonics, tags, and media requirements.\\",\\"subagent_type\\":\\"question-author\\"}"},{"id":"gen-tool-1","name":"task","args":{}},{"id":"call_-7222910032839434882","name":"search_medical_references","args":"{\\"query\\":\\"short stature endocrine causes diagnosis guidelines\\",\\"maxResults\\":3}"},{"id":"gen-tool-2","name":"search_medical_references","args":{}},{"id":"call_-7222895258151939339","name":"generate_medical_illustration","args":"{\\"prompt\\":\\"Anatomical diagram showing the pituitary gland and its relationship to growth hormone production, including the hypothalamus-pituitary axis and growth hormone pathway to target tissues like bones and muscles\\"}"},{"id":"gen-tool-3","name":"generate_medical_illustration","args":{}},{"id":"call_-7222895326871418744","name":"save_question","args":"{\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"text\\":\\"A 5-year-old boy presents with height below the 3rd percentile for age. His growth velocity is normal, and his bone age is appropriate for his chronological age. On examination, he has no dysmorphic features and his parents are both of short stature. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\\",\\"options\\":[\\"Constitutional growth delay\\", \\"Growth hormone deficiency\\", \\"Turner syndrome\\", \\"Achondroplasia\\", \\"Hypothyroidism\\"],\\"answer\\":\\"Constitutional growth delay\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"Constitutional growth delay is the most likely diagnosis given the family history of short stature, normal growth velocity, appropriate bone age, and absence of dysmorphic features. This is a common cause of short stature in children and represents a normal variant of growth. Growth hormone deficiency would typically show decreased growth velocity, Turner syndrome would have characteristic features like webbed neck and short fourth metacarpals, achondroplasia would have disproportionate short stature with rhizomelic shortening, and hypothyroidism would show delayed bone age.\\",\\"mnemonic\\":\\"\\\\\\"Family short, growth normal, bone age on track - constitutional growth delay\\\\\\"\\",\\"clinicalPearl\\":\\"Constitutional growth delay is a diagnosis of exclusion and typically resolves with normal adult height.\\",\\"distractorAnalysis\\":\\"Growth hormone deficiency: Would show decreased growth velocity and delayed bone age. Turner syndrome: Would have characteristic dysmorphic features and short stature in females. Achondroplasia: Would show disproportionate shortening with rhizomelia. Hypothyroidism: Would show delayed bone age and other systemic symptoms.\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"system\\":\\"Endocrine\\",\\"topic\\":\\"short stature\\",\\"tags\\":[\\"growth\\", \\"pediatrics\\", \\"endocrine\\"],\\"imageUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"audioUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"illustrationDescription\\":\\"Growth chart showing child with normal growth velocity but short stature, appropriate bone age compared to chronological age\\"}"},{"id":"call_-7222885087669381127","name":"save_question","args":"{\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"text\\":\\"An 8-year-old girl presents with short stature, webbed neck, and widely spaced nipples. Her height is below the 5th percentile for age. On physical examination, she has a high-arched palate and shield chest. Which of the following findings would be most characteristic on her hand X-ray?\\",\\"options\\":[\\"Shortened fourth metacarpals\\", \\"Delayed bone age\\", \\"Increased bone density\\", \\"Epiphyseal dysplasia\\", \\"Osteopenia\\"],\\"answer\\":\\"Shortened fourth metacarpals\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"This presentation is classic for Turner syndrome (45,X), which is characterized by short stature, webbed neck, shield chest, and other dysmorphic features. A key radiographic finding in Turner syndrome is shortened fourth metacarpals, which occurs in approximately 50% of cases. Delayed bone age can occur but is not specific, increased bone density is not typical, epiphyseal dysplasia is not a feature, and osteopenia is not characteristic.\\",\\"mnemonic\\":\\"\\\\\\"Turner''s triad: webbed neck, shield chest, short 4th metacarpal\\\\\\"\\",\\"clinicalPearl\\":\\"Turner syndrome should be considered in any girl with unexplained short stature, especially with dysmorphic features.\\",\\"distractorAnalysis\\":\\"Delayed bone age: Can occur but is not specific to Turner syndrome. Increased bone density: Not a feature of Turner syndrome. Epiphyseal dysplasia: Not associated with Turner syndrome. Osteopenia: Not a characteristic finding.\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"system\\":\\"Endocrine\\",\\"topic\\":\\"short stature\\",\\"tags\\":[\\"turner syndrome\\", \\"genetic\\", \\"dysmorphic\\"],\\"imageUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"audioUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"illustrationDescription\\":\\"Hand X-ray showing shortened fourth metacarpal characteristic of Turner syndrome\\"}"},{"id":"call_-7222940235049467693","name":"save_question","args":"{\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"text\\":\\"A 3-year-old child presents with disproportionate short stature, macrocephaly, and midfacial hypoplasia. On examination, the child has a prominent forehead, depressed nasal bridge, and trident hand configuration. Which of the following genetic mutations is most likely responsible for this condition?\\",\\"options\\":[\\"FGFR3 gene mutation\\", \\"SHOX gene mutation\\", \\"GHRH receptor mutation\\", \\"IGF-1 receptor mutation\\", \\"GH gene mutation\\"],\\"answer\\":\\"FGFR3 gene mutation\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"This presentation is classic for achondroplasia, the most common form of dwarfism. Achondroplasia is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the FGFR3 gene, which leads to impaired endochondral bone formation. The characteristic features include disproportionate short stature (rhizomelic shortening), macrocephaly, midfacial hypoplasia, prominent forehead, depressed nasal bridge, and trident hand configuration. SHOX gene mutations cause Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis, GHRH receptor mutations cause isolated growth hormone deficiency, IGF-1 receptor mutations cause severe growth retardation, and GH gene mutations also cause growth hormone deficiency.\\",\\"mnemonic\\":\\"\\\\\\"FGFR3 mutation = achondroplasia: rhizomelic shortening, macrocephaly, trident hand\\\\\\"\\",\\"clinicalPearl\\":\\"Achondroplasia accounts for approximately 70% of all dwarfism cases and is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern.\\",\\"distractorAnalysis\\":\\"SHOX gene mutation: Causes Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis with mesomelic shortening. GHRH receptor mutation: Causes isolated growth hormone deficiency with proportionate short stature. IGF-1 receptor mutation: Causes severe growth retardation but not the characteristic facial features. GH gene mutation: Causes growth hormone deficiency with proportionate short stature.\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"system\\":\\"Endocrine\\",\\"topic\\":\\"short stature\\",\\"tags\\":[\\"achondroplasia\\", \\"genetic\\", \\"skeletal dysplasia\\"],\\"imageUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"audioUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"illustrationDescription\\":\\"Clinical photograph showing achondroplasia with rhizomelic shortening, macrocephaly, and midfacial hypoplasia\\"}"}]','[{"toolName":"run_question_generation_agent","content":"","isError":false},{"toolName":"save_question","content":"","isError":false},{"toolName":"save_question","content":"","isError":false},{"toolName":"save_question","content":"","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-07T19:57:22.482Z');
