INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmuyl4wa0000rsomamlqtf2oz','75a65a65-bf3b-40d5-b2e2-40b12990f516','assistant','
# Diuretic Classes Overview

## Comparison of Main Diuretic Classes

| Class | Mechanism | Site of Action | Potency | Common Examples |
|-------|-----------|----------------|---------|----------------|
| Thiazide | Inhibit Na+/Cl- cotransporter | Distal convoluted tubule | Moderate | Hydrochlorothiazide, Chlorthalidone |
| Loop | Inhibit Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter | Ascending limb of Henle | High | Furosemide, Bumetanide, Torsemide |
| Potassium-sparing | Block ENaC channels | Collecting duct | Mild | Spironolactone, Eplerenone, Amiloride |

## Key Side Effects

- **Thiazide diuretics:**
  - Hypokalemia (low potassium)
  - Hyponatremia (low sodium)
  - Hypercalcemia (elevated calcium)
  - Hyperuricemia (elevated uric acid, gout risk)
  - Hyperglycemia (worsening diabetes control)
  - Hyperlipidemia

- **Loop diuretics:**
  - Severe hypokalemia
  - Ototoxicity (hearing loss, especially with rapid IV administration)
  - Dehydration and electrolyte imbalances
  - Hypotension
  - Metabolic alkalosis
  - Nephrotoxicity with prolonged use

- **Potassium-sparing diuretics:**
  - Hyperkalemia (high potassium, especially in renal impairment or with ACE inhibitors)
  - Gynecomastia (with spironolactone)
  - Menstrual irregularities
  - Anti-androgen effects
  - Renal impairment in susceptible individuals',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-07T20:53:50.568Z');
