ufficiency\\"],\\"answer\\":\\"Pituitary adenoma secreting growth hormone\\",\\"explanation\\":\\"The patient''s presentation with progressive enlargement of hands, feet, and facial features (prognathism, prominent brow ridge, thickened lips) is classic for acromegaly, which is caused by excessive growth hormone secretion, most commonly from a pituitary adenoma. The other options are incorrect because: thyroid hormone excess causes different symptoms like weight loss and tachycardia; cortisol excess leads to Cushing''s syndrome with central obesity and moon face; prolactinoma typically causes galactorrhea and amenorrhea in women; adrenal insufficiency presents with fatigue and hypotension.\\",\\"mnemonic\\":\\"\\\\\\"FACE\\\\\\" - Facial features, Acral enlargement, Coarse features, Enlarged extremities\\",\\"clinicalPearl\\":\\"Acromegaly develops gradually over years and is often missed initially. Early diagnosis is crucial as untreated acromegaly increases mortality from cardiovascular disease, diabetes, and cancer.\\",\\"distractorAnalysis\\":\\"Thyroid hormone excess: Causes weight loss, heat intolerance, and tachycardia, not progressive enlargement of facial features and extremities. Cortisol excess: Results in central obesity, moon face, and striae, not the specific acromegalic features. Prolactinoma: Primarily affects reproductive function rather than causing skeletal overgrowth. Adrenal insufficiency: Presents with fatigue, hypotension, and hyperpigmentation, not progressive enlargement.\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Easy\\",\\"system\\":\\"Endocrine\\",\\"topic\\":\\"acromegaly\\",\\"tags\\":[\\"pituitary\\", \\"growth hormone\\", \\"endocrine\\", \\"acromegaly\\"],\\"imageUrl\\":\\"/generated/agent-45a3af19-fb5.jpg\\",\\"audioUrl\\":\\"\\",\\"illustrationDescription\\":\\"Comparison of normal facial features versus characteristic acromegaly features including enlarged jaw, prominent brow ridge, and thickened lips\\"}"}]','[{"toolName":"run_question_generation_ag