    "type": "mcq",
    "text": "A 45-year-old male presents to his primary care physician complaining of progressive enlargement of his hands and feet over the past 2 years. He also reports that his wedding ring no longer fits and he has had to buy larger shoes multiple times. On examination, he has coarse facial features with a prominent brow ridge, enlarged nose, and thickened lips. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "options": [
      "Cushing''s syndrome",
      "Acromegaly",
      "Marfan syndrome",
      "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1",
      "Pituitary adenoma"
    ],
    "answer": "Acromegaly",
    "explanation": "Acromegaly is caused by excessive growth hormone secretion, typically from a pituitary adenoma. The clinical presentation includes progressive enlargement of hands and feet (acral enlargement), coarse facial features (enlarged nose, thickened lips, prominent brow ridge), and soft tissue swelling. The history of wedding rings and shoes no longer fitting is classic for this condition. Cushing''s syndrome presents with central obesity, moon face, and other features. Marfan syndrome involves tall stature, long limbs, and joint hypermobility but not progressive acral enlargement. Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 can include pituitary tumors but typically presents with other endocrine abnormalities. While pituitary adenoma is the underlying cause, acromegaly is the specific syndrome resulting from excessive GH secretion.",
    "mnemonic": "ACRO = Acral enlargement, Coarse features, Ring/size changes, Overgrowth",
    "clinicalPearl": "The progressive enlargement of hands and feet is a hallmark feature of acromegaly and should prompt evaluation for growth hormone excess.",
    "distractorAnalysis": "Cushing''s syndrome is a common distractor as it also involves pituitary pathology but presents with different features like central obesity and moon face. Marfan syndrome involves tall stature but not progressive acral enlargement. Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 can include pituitary tumors but typically has other endocrine manifestations. Pituitary adenoma is the underlying cause but not the specific syndrome diagnosis.",
    "difficulty": "easy",
