INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvigh52e0002vru84imgai4q','0016750d-a8a1-4067-913c-621ad079cdfc','assistant','I''ll search for heart images using medical reference sources to find existing educational images and resources.

I found several excellent resources for heart images:

**Educational Heart Anatomy Resources:**
- [S1] Texas Heart Institute provides comprehensive heart anatomy information with educational content about the heart''s location and structure
- [S2] Cleveland Clinic offers detailed heart anatomy and function information with available online images
- [S3] Adobe Stock has over 1,000 royalty-free human heart anatomy images and videos available for educational use
- [S4] University of Michigan''s BlueLink project provides labeled heart images that can be downloaded for educational purposes
- [S5] Facebook has educational videos showing detailed frontal views of heart interior anatomy with chamber animations

These resources should provide you with a variety of heart images ranging from basic anatomical diagrams to detailed interior views and educational animations. The BlueLink images [S4] are particularly useful for educational purposes as they come with labels, and the Cleveland Clinic resource [S2] offers medically accurate heart imagery.

Would you like me to help you find more specific types of heart images, such as cross-sectional views, pathological conditions, or create custom heart illustrations for your educational needs?','[{"id":"call_-7145184422000584830","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"heart anatomy images medical education","maxResults":5}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145184422000584830","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Heart Anatomy\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.texasheart.org/heart-health/heart-information-center/topics/heart-anatomy\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Heart Anatomy: Your heart is located between your lungs in the middle of your chest , behind and slightly to the left of your breastbone.\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.texasheart.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Heart: Anatomy & Function\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/21704-heart\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"The heart and the major arteries and veins going into and out of it . Image content: This image is available to view online. ... Your heart is a muscular organ ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"my.clevelandclinic.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"\\\\\\"Human Heart Anatomy\\\\\\" Images – Browse 1068\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://stock.adobe.com/search?k=%22human+heart+anatomy%22\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Search from thousands of royalty-free \\\\\\"Human Heart Anatomy\\\\\\" stock images and video for your next project. Download royalty-free stock photos, vectors, ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"stock.adobe.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Labeled Heart Images\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://sites.google.com/a/umich.edu/bluelink/resources/bluelink/heart\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Labeled Heart Images . To download a BlueLink image, click on the photo and select the download option on the following page. Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"sites.google.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Frontal View of Heart Interior Anatomy Diagram Explained\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.facebook.com/abdallah89othman/videos/check-out-this-detailed-frontal-view-of-the-heart-interior-a-great-visual-for-un/1395890264963827\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"This is a short, educational video providing a detailed frontal view of the heart''s interior . The animation shows the heart''s chambers, ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.facebook.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] Heart Anatomy\\\\n    https://www.texasheart.org/heart-health/heart-information-center/topics/heart-anatomy (www.texasheart.org)\\\\n    Heart Anatomy: Your heart is located between your lungs in the middle of your chest , behind and slightly to the left of your breastbone.\\\\n[S2] Heart: Anatomy & Function\\\\n    https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/21704-heart (my.clevelandclinic.org)\\\\n    The heart and the major arteries and veins going into and out of it . Image content: This image is available to view online. ... Your heart is a muscular organ ... Read more\\\\n[S3] \\\\\\"Human Heart Anatomy\\\\\\" Images – Browse 1068\\\\n    https://stock.adobe.com/search?k=%22human+heart+anatomy%22 (stock.adobe.com)\\\\n    Search from thousands of royalty-free \\\\\\"Human Heart Anatomy\\\\\\" stock images and video for your next project. Download royalty-free stock photos, vectors, ...\\\\n[S4] Labeled Heart Images\\\\n    https://sites.google.com/a/umich.edu/bluelink/resources/bluelink/heart (sites.google.com)\\\\n    Labeled Heart Images . To download a BlueLink image, click on the photo and select the download option on the following page. Read more\\\\n[S5] Frontal View of Heart Interior Anatomy Diagram Explained\\\\n    https://www.facebook.com/abdallah89othman/videos/check-out-this-detailed-frontal-view-of-the-heart-interior-a-great-visual-for-un/1395890264963827 (www.facebook.com)\\\\n    This is a short, educational video providing a detailed frontal view of the heart''s interior . The animation shows the heart''s chambers, ...\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T18:38:47.270Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvigjbhn0003vru877y9q3ds','0016750d-a8a1-4067-913c-621ad079cdfc','user','from my notes: why is ReSoMal used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition?',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-21T18:40:28.907Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm2w0bg0000tnrds9xco1qo','f48a8f63-9ca8-48a1-993b-037fec4d4627','user','Reply with exactly the word PONG and nothing else',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-24T07:29:31.008Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmf3a030007yjrdmcjbjfst','7e053ffc-11d9-4ffe-ac42-f8e97cb7fd5d','user','Make me a 4-week USMLE Step 1 study plan based on my real quiz performance',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-24T13:11:05.569Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvigjmwm0004vru8pf0ttxyj','0016750d-a8a1-4067-913c-621ad079cdfc','assistant','I''ll search through your knowledge base to find information about why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition.

Based on your notes, here''s why ReSoMal (Rehydration Solution for Malnutrition) is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition:

**Key Reasons:**

1. **High Sodium Risk**: Standard ORS has too much sodium content, which can push an already-atrophied heart into heart failure [K1, K2]

2. **Cardiac Vulnerability**: Severely malnourished children have atrophied heart muscle that cannot handle rapid volume loads [K1, K3]

3. **Electrolyte Balance**: ReSoMal has less sodium and more potassium compared to standard ORS, making it more appropriate for malnourished children [K1, K2]

**Additional Important Points:**

- **Slow Rehydration**: Rehydration must be done slowly over 12 hours because the heart cannot handle rapid volume changes [K1]
- **Monitoring**: Don''t rely on skin turgor or sunken eyes to judge rehydration status in malnourished children, as these signs are unreliable due to loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle [K1, K3]
- **Preferred Route**: Oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration and precipitating congestive heart failure [K4]

The critical difference is that standard ORS''s high sodium load poses a significant risk to the compromised cardiovascular system of severely malnourished children, while ReSoMal provides a safer electrolyte profile for this vulnerable population.','[{"id":"call_-7145197203823262623","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"ReSoMal severe malnutrition ORS standard oral rehydration solution","top_k":10}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145197203823262623","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 129 of 675)\\\\nS Clarifications Note — Why ReSoMal, Not Standard ORS — and How to Monitor Progress We deliberately avoid Standard ORS here — its Sodium content is too high and can push an already-atrophied heart into Heart failure.\\\\nInstead use ReSoMal (Rehydration Solution for Malnutrition), which has less Sodium and more Potassium.\\\\nRehydration must be done *Slowly, over 12 hours, because the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load.\\\\nSchedule: first 2 hours — 5 mL/kg every half hour; remaining 10 hours — 5 mL/kg every hour.\\\\nDo NOT rely on Skin turgor or Sunken eyes to judge rehydration status — these signs are unreliable in a malnourished child (loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle independently changes skin appearance).\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 144 of 675)\\\\nD) Simple underweight\\\\nExplanation\\\\nWeight-for-age 60–80% WITH edema = Kwashiorkor (Wellcome classification). The weaning history, moon face (edema masking real fat loss), and flag-sign hair are all classic for Kwashiorkor rather than Marasmus.\\\\nQ3. Which of the following is the correct rehydration fluid of choice for a severely malnourished, dehydrated child, and why?\\\\n\\\\nA) Standard ORS — highest sodium content available 3 B) ReSoMal — lower sodium, higher potassium than standard ORS\\\\n\\\\nC) Normal saline bolus — fastest correction\\\\n\\\\nD) 5% Dextrose alone — avoids all electrolyte risk Explanation\\\\nStandard ORS’s high sodium load risks precipitating heart failure in a child with an atrophied heart.\\\\n\\\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 145 of 675)\\\\nH Exam Highlights\\\\nH Severe malnutrition admission criteria: Weight-for-Height < −3 SD OR MUAC < 115 mm, PLUS Infection, Dehydration, or Edema. H Golden rule: Marasmus = NEVER has edema. Any edema in a wasted child = Marasmic-Kwashiorkor. H Never press on the eye when Keratomalacia/Xerophthalmia is suspected — risk of rupture and permanent blindness. H Use ReSoMal, not standard ORS, for rehydration in severe malnutrition — monitor via urine output, not skin turgor/sunken eyes. H F-75 (stabilization, 75 kcal/100 mL) → F-100 (rehabilitation, 100 kcal/100 mL) once edema resolves and appetite returns. H Refeeding syndrome: hypophosphatemia is the most dangerous electrolyte derangement — prevent with slow, gradual refeeding.\\\\n\\\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 264 of 675)\\\\nKey Rehydration Rules\\\\nIn malnourished children, oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure — slow rehydration is preferred in this group.\\\\nThe WHO recommends low-osmolarity ORS at all ages.\\\\nAntidiarrheal compounds should never be used in children at any age.\\\\n\\\\n⚠ Red Flag — Never Give Antidiarrheals to Children Antidiarrheal (anti-motility) agents are contraindicated in children of any age — they slow clearance of the pathogen/toxin and can precipitate toxic megacolon or worsen invasive disease. Rehydration (oral or IV) and zinc are the evidence-based interventions; antimotility drugs are not.\\\\n\\\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 657 of 675)\\\\nStep Numeric Detail Not Given Above\\\\nHypoglycemia Blood glucose <55 mg/dL. Severe: lethargy, rigidity, irritability, convulsion, coma. Treat with 2 mL/kg of 10% Dextrose IV or via NG tube; recheck after 30 min, then every 2 hours. Prevent with frequent feeds. Hypothermia Feed every 2 hours, day and night. Keep the room at 30–33°C even overnight. Use a heater near the child, skin-to-skin contact with the mother, and a warm blanket; avoid bathing/exposure. Often occurs together with hypoglycemia, and both can be a sign of sepsis. Dehydration Rehydrate orally or via NG tube — IV is avoided (risk of volume overload/heart failure) unless in shock or oral rehydrat","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T18:40:43.702Z');
