These resources include stock images, educational atlases, interactive diagrams, and comprehensive heart anatomy references that should provide you with a variety of visual materials for studying heart anatomy.','[{"id":"call_-7145163118962802071","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"heart anatomy images diagrams resources educational","maxResults":5}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145163118962802071","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Human Heart Diagram Images – Browse 133747\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://stock.adobe.com/search?k=human+heart+diagram\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Search from thousands of royalty-free Human Heart Diagram stock images and video for your next project. Download royalty-free stock photos, vectors, ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"stock.adobe.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Heart anatomy: Structure, valves, coronary vessels\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.kenhub.com/en/library/anatomy/heart\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"This page discusses the heart anatomy . Click here to learn everything about the anatomy of the heart, heart valves and vessels at Kenhub!\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.kenhub.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Atlas of Human Cardiac Anatomy - University of Minnesota\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.vhlab.umn.edu/atlas\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"The atlas of human cardiac anatomy has live videos and still images of reanimated human hearts . The videos and images. Anatomy are free to download and use for ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.vhlab.umn.edu\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Heart Anatomy\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.texasheart.org/heart-health/heart-information-center/topics/heart-anatomy\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Heart Anatomy: Your heart is located between your lungs in the middle of your chest , behind and slightly to the left of your breastbone.\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.texasheart.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Heart: illustrated anatomy | e-Anatomy\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.imaios.com/en/e-anatomy/thorax/heart\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"This interactive atlas of human heart anatomy is based on medical illustrations and cadaver photography. The user can show or hide the anatomical labels\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.imaios.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] Human Heart Diagram Images – Browse 133747\\\\n    https://stock.adobe.com/search?k=human+heart+diagram (stock.adobe.com)\\\\n    Search from thousands of royalty-free Human Heart Diagram stock images and video for your next project. Download royalty-free stock photos, vectors, ...\\\\n[S2] Heart anatomy: Structure, valves, coronary vessels\\\\n    https://www.kenhub.com/en/library/anatomy/heart (www.kenhub.com)\\\\n    This page discusses the heart anatomy . Click here to learn everything about the anatomy of the heart, heart valves and vessels at Kenhub!\\\\n[S3] Atlas of Human Cardiac Anatomy - University of Minnesota\\\\n    https://www.vhlab.umn.edu/atlas (www.vhlab.umn.edu)\\\\n    The atlas of human cardiac anatomy has live videos and still images of reanimated human hearts . The videos and images. Anatomy are free to download and use for ...\\\\n[S4] Heart Anatomy\\\\n    https://www.texasheart.org/heart-health/heart-information-center/topics/heart-anatomy (www.texasheart.org)\\\\n    Heart Anatomy: Your heart is located between your lungs in the middle of your chest , behind and slightly to the left of your breastbone.\\\\n[S5] Heart: illustrated anatomy | e-Anatomy\\\\n    https://www.imaios.com/en/e-anatomy/thorax/heart (www.imaios.com)\\\\n    This interactive atlas of human heart anatomy is based on medical illustrations and cadaver photography. The user can show or hide the anatomical labels\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T18:46:43.765Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvigt5th0003fwu8gw7kud5y','ee596281-bd27-447a-8d82-983b6871536c','user','from my notes: why is ReSoMal used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition?',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-21T18:48:08.117Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvigthiq0004fwu80jpss70d','ee596281-bd27-447a-8d82-983b6871536c','assistant','I''ll search through your knowledge base to find information about why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition.

Based on your notes, here''s why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition:

**Key Reasons:**

1. **High Sodium Content Risk**: Standard ORS has too much sodium, which can push an already-atrophied heart into heart failure [K1].

2. **Cardiac Atrophy**: In severe malnutrition, the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load [K1].

3. **Electrolyte Balance**: ReSoMal has less sodium and more potassium compared to standard ORS, making it safer for malnourished children [K2].

**Additional Important Considerations:**

- **Slow Rehydration**: Rehydration must be done slowly over 12 hours because the atrophied heart cannot handle rapid volume changes [K1].
- **Monitoring Schedule**: First 2 hours at 5 mL/kg every half hour, then remaining 10 hours at 5 mL/kg every hour [K1].
- **Unreliable Clinical Signs**: Skin turgor and sunken eyes are unreliable indicators of dehydration status in malnourished children due to loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle [K1].

The core principle is that standard ORS''s high sodium content poses a significant risk to the compromised cardiovascular system of severely malnourished children, while ReSoMal provides a safer electrolyte profile that won''t overwhelm the weakened heart.','[{"id":"call_-7145163084603056638","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"ReSoMal severe malnutrition ORS standard oral rehydration solution","top_k":10}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145163084603056638","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 129 of 675)\\\\nS Clarifications Note — Why ReSoMal, Not Standard ORS — and How to Monitor Progress We deliberately avoid Standard ORS here — its Sodium content is too high and can push an already-atrophied heart into Heart failure.\\\\nInstead use ReSoMal (Rehydration Solution for Malnutrition), which has less Sodium and more Potassium.\\\\nRehydration must be done *Slowly, over 12 hours, because the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load.\\\\nSchedule: first 2 hours — 5 mL/kg every half hour; remaining 10 hours — 5 mL/kg every hour.\\\\nDo NOT rely on Skin turgor or Sunken eyes to judge rehydration status — these signs are unreliable in a malnourished child (loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle independently changes skin appearance).\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 144 of 675)\\\\nD) Simple underweight\\\\nExplanation\\\\nWeight-for-age 60–80% WITH edema = Kwashiorkor (Wellcome classification). The weaning history, moon face (edema masking real fat loss), and flag-sign hair are all classic for Kwashiorkor rather than Marasmus.\\\\nQ3. Which of the following is the correct rehydration fluid of choice for a severely malnourished, dehydrated child, and why?\\\\n\\\\nA) Standard ORS — highest sodium content available 3 B) ReSoMal — lower sodium, higher potassium than standard ORS\\\\n\\\\nC) Normal saline bolus — fastest correction\\\\n\\\\nD) 5% Dextrose alone — avoids all electrolyte risk Explanation\\\\nStandard ORS’s high sodium load risks precipitating heart failure in a child with an atrophied heart.\\\\n\\\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Age-dependent causes of upper GI bleeding: infants (gastritis, vitamin K deficiency), 2-5 years (esophagitis, ulcers).)\\\\n3. Causes of Upper GIT Bleeding by Age\\\\nThe differential for UGIB is strongly age-dependent — a newborn, a toddler, and an older child each have a different most-likely cause list.\\\\nINFANTS (incl. NICU)\\\\nStomach\\\\n\\\\n• Gastritis from stress\\\\nVariable location\\\\n\\\\n• Vitamin K deficiency\\\\n\\\\n• Sepsis\\\\n\\\\n• Trauma (NG tubes)\\\\n\\\\n• CMPA\\\\n\\\\n2–5 YEARS\\\\nEsophagus\\\\n\\\\n• Esophagitis\\\\n\\\\n• Esophageal varices\\\\n\\\\n• Mallory-Weiss syndrome\\\\nStomach\\\\n\\\\n• Gastritis\\\\n\\\\n• Gastric ulcer\\\\n\\\\n• Gastric varices\\\\nDuodenum\\\\n\\\\n• Duodenitis\\\\n\\\\n• Duodenal ulcer\\\\nVariable location\\\\n\\\\n• Caustic ingestions\\\\n\\\\n• Foreign bodies\\\\n\\\\n• NSAIDs use\\\\n\\\\nOLDER CHILDREN\\\\nEsophagus\\\\n\\\\n• Esophagitis\\\\n\\\\n• Mallory-Weiss syndrome\\\\n\\\\n• Esophageal varices\\\\n\\\\n• Dieulafoy lesion\\\\nStomach\\\\n\\\\n• PHG\\\\n\\\\n• Hemobilia\\\\nVariable location\\\\n\\\\n• Polyps\\\\n\\\\n• Crohn''s disease\\\\n\\\\n• Telangiectasia\\\\n\\\\n• Aortoenteric fistula\\\\n\\\\n•…\\\\n\\\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Newborn-specific causes of UGIB including septicemia, stress ulcers, cow''s milk allergy, and vitamin K deficiency.)\\\\nS Clarifications Note\\\\nNewborns / NICU babies are more likely to bleed for reasons unique to that age: Septicemia — sepsis-related DIC can start with the baby vomiting blood. Stress ulcer — a gastric ulcer that develops from the physiologic stress of critical illness. Cow’s milk allergy — sensitization to the proteins found in cow milk; the mother eating dairy products and then breastfeeding can transfer that sensitization into her milk, and the baby then bleeds because of it. Vitamin K deficiency — hemorrhagic disease of the newborn — babies who did not receive vitamin K at birth, or who had a difficult delivery, can develop bleeding because vitamin K stores at birth are already very low.\\\\n\\\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Importance of asking about cow''s milk exposure as a common cause of GI bleeding in children.)\\\\nAlways specifically ask about cow’s milk exposure as a possible cause, since it is a common one: has the child had any milk products, and did the bleeding start around the first time milk was introduced or not?\\\\n\\\\n[K6] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 657 of 675)\\\\nStep Numeric Detail Not Given ","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T18:48:23.282Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvihaiaf00016hu825xmo7q5','4cc75352-9eec-4f98-a4c3-130e505672b9','user','from my notes: why is ReSoMal used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition?',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-21T19:01:37.417Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvihat7r00026hu8lfp0o1w0','4cc75352-9eec-4f98-a4c3-130e505672b9','assistant','I''ll search your knowledge base to find information about why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition.

Based on your notes, here''s why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition:

**Key Reasons:**

1. **High sodium content in standard ORS** - Standard ORS has too much sodium which can push an already-atrophied heart into heart failure [K1, K2]

2. **Cardiac vulnerability** - In severe malnutrition, the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load [K1]

3. **Electrolyte balance** - ReSoMal has less sodium and more potassium compared to standard ORS, which is better suited for the electrolyte needs of malnourished children [K1, K2]

**Additional Important Points:**

- **Slow rehydration** - Rehydration must be done slowly over 12 hours because the heart cannot handle rapid volume changes [K1]
- **Monitoring** - Don''t rely on skin turgor or sunken eyes to judge rehydration status in malnourished children, as these signs are unreliable due to loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle [K1]
- **Preferred route** - Oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure [K4]

The golden rule is: **Never use standard ORS in severe malnutrition due to the risk of heart failure from the high sodium load** [K2, K3].','[{"id":"call_-7145222389511484314","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"ReSoMal severe malnutrition ORS standard oral rehydration solution","top_k":10}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145222389511484314","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 129 of 675)\\\\nS Clarifications Note — Why ReSoMal, Not Standard ORS — and How to Monitor Progress We deliberately avoid Standard ORS here — its Sodium content is too high and can push an already-atrophied heart into Heart failure.\\\\nInstead use ReSoMal (Rehydration Solution for Malnutrition), which has less Sodium and more Potassium.\\\\nRehydration must be done *Slowly, over 12 hours, because the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load.\\\\nSchedule: first 2 hours — 5 mL/kg every half hour; remaining 10 hours — 5 mL/kg every hour.\\\\nDo NOT rely on Skin turgor or Sunken eyes to judge rehydration status — these signs are unreliable in a malnourished child (loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle independently changes skin appearance).\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 144 of 675)\\\\nD) Simple underweight\\\\nExplanation\\\\nWeight-for-age 60–80% WITH edema = Kwashiorkor (Wellcome classification). The weaning history, moon face (edema masking real fat loss), and flag-sign hair are all classic for Kwashiorkor rather than Marasmus.\\\\nQ3. Which of the following is the correct rehydration fluid of choice for a severely malnourished, dehydrated child, and why?\\\\n\\\\nA) Standard ORS — highest sodium content available 3 B) ReSoMal — lower sodium, higher potassium than standard ORS\\\\n\\\\nC) Normal saline bolus — fastest correction\\\\n\\\\nD) 5% Dextrose alone — avoids all electrolyte risk Explanation\\\\nStandard ORS’s high sodium load risks precipitating heart failure in a child with an atrophied heart.\\\\n\\\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 145 of 675)\\\\nH Exam Highlights\\\\nH Severe malnutrition admission criteria: Weight-for-Height < −3 SD OR MUAC < 115 mm, PLUS Infection, Dehydration, or Edema. H Golden rule: Marasmus = NEVER has edema. Any edema in a wasted child = Marasmic-Kwashiorkor. H Never press on the eye when Keratomalacia/Xerophthalmia is suspected — risk of rupture and permanent blindness. H Use ReSoMal, not standard ORS, for rehydration in severe malnutrition — monitor via urine output, not skin turgor/sunken eyes. H F-75 (stabilization, 75 kcal/100 mL) → F-100 (rehabilitation, 100 kcal/100 mL) once edema resolves and appetite returns. H Refeeding syndrome: hypophosphatemia is the most dangerous electrolyte derangement — prevent with slow, gradual refeeding.\\\\n\\\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 264 of 675)\\\\nKey Rehydration Rules\\\\nIn malnourished children, oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure — slow rehydration is preferred in this group.\\\\nThe WHO recommends low-osmolarity ORS at all ages.\\\\nAntidiarrheal compounds should never be used in children at any age.\\\\n\\\\n⚠ Red Flag — Never Give Antidiarrheals to Children Antidiarrheal (anti-motility) agents are contraindicated in children of any age — they slow clearance of the pathogen/toxin and can precipitate toxic megacolon or worsen invasive disease. Rehydration (oral or IV) and zinc are the evidence-based interventions; antimotility drugs are not.\\\\n\\\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Describes growth assessment using MUAC and WHO growth charts for nutritional status.)\\\\n4. Growth Assessment\\\\n\\\\na) Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC)\\\\nA quick, field-friendly assessment of nutritional status taken at the mid-point of the upper arm.\\\\n> 12.5 cm\\\\nNormal\\\\n< 12.5 cm\\\\nMalnutrition\\\\n(general)\\\\n< 11.5 cm\\\\nSevere malnutrition b) Growth Charts\\\\nThis project (and the NHS) uses the WHO Growth Charts — the Universal charts used worldwide. The three charts needed are Weight-for-Age, Weight-for-Height, and Height-for-Age — plotting weight and height against each other shows how well they track together.\\\\n\\\\nc) Standard Deviation (Z-scores)\\\\nEvery WHO growth chart has a Normal curve in the middle (Z = 0). M","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T19:01:51.591Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvihpb040001vnu8ayvuyx8g','6e907c0d-c6ff-435b-a414-3674cbd7c161','user','from my notes: why is ReSoMal used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition?',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-21T19:13:07.820Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvir768c000fgpu81df9vbq8','53fa03f9-70b2-4405-b8e9-3f633da607b5','assistant','I found the latest heart failure guidelines from major cardiology societies. Here''s a summary of the current guideline updates:

## Latest Heart Failure Guidelines Summary

### 2025 ACC/AHA/HRS/ISACHD/SCAI Guideline [S1]
- Released December 18, 2025
- Features new recommendations on guideline-directed medical therapy for heart failure
- Includes updates on the management of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy

### 2026 ESC Guidelines for Heart Failure [S2]
- Focuses on refining classification of heart failure according to left ventricular ejection fraction
- Aims to facilitate practical application in clinical settings
- Provides updated management strategies based on the latest evidence

### Key Focus Areas in Current Guidelines:

1. **HFpEF (Heart Failure with Preserved Ejection Fraction)** [S3]
   - Increasing incidence and prevalence with aging population
   - Growing recognition of this important heart failure subtype

2. **Guideline-Directed Medical Therapy**
   - Updated pharmacological approaches
   - Evidence-based treatment algorithms

3. **Classification Systems**
   - Improved categorization based on ejection fraction
   - Better stratification for treatment decisions

These guidelines represent the most current evidence-based approaches to heart failure management, with particular attention to the evolving understanding of HFpEF and the optimization of guideline-directed medical therapy.','[{"id":"call_-7145208542536922983","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"heart failure guideline summary 2024 2025","maxResults":3}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145208542536922983","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"2025 ACC/AHA/HRS/ISACHD/SCAI Guideline for the\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001402\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Dec 18, 2025 — New recommendations on guideline -directed medical therapy for heart failure ... 2024 Guideline for the Management of Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy . Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.ahajournals.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"Dec 18, 2025\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"2026 ESC Guidelines for the management of heart failure\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.escardio.org/guidelines/clinical-practice-guidelines/all-esc-practice-guidelines/heart-failure\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"These guidelines refine the classification of heart failure according to left ventricular ejection fraction, with the aim of facilitating its application in ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.escardio.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Focus on Heart Failure | HFpEF: Where We Stand in 2025\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/articles/2025/06/01/01/focus-on-heart-failure-hfpef\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Jun 1, 2025 — The incidence and prevalence of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) is increasing, along with the aging population and ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.acc.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"Jun 1, 2025\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] 2025 ACC/AHA/HRS/ISACHD/SCAI Guideline for the\\\\n    https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001402 (www.ahajournals.org) — Dec 18, 2025\\\\n    Dec 18, 2025 — New recommendations on guideline -directed medical therapy for heart failure ... 2024 Guideline for the Management of Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy . Read more\\\\n[S2] 2026 ESC Guidelines for the management of heart failure\\\\n    https://www.escardio.org/guidelines/clinical-practice-guidelines/all-esc-practice-guidelines/heart-failure (www.escardio.org)\\\\n    These guidelines refine the classification of heart failure according to left ventricular ejection fraction, with the aim of facilitating its application in ... Read more\\\\n[S3] Focus on Heart Failure | HFpEF: Where We Stand in 2025\\\\n    https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/articles/2025/06/01/01/focus-on-heart-failure-hfpef (www.acc.org) — Jun 1, 2025\\\\n    Jun 1, 2025 — The incidence and prevalence of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) is increasing, along with the aging population and ... Read more\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T23:38:57.996Z');
