INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvihpmw80002vnu84mpzxs1l','6e907c0d-c6ff-435b-a414-3674cbd7c161','assistant','I''ll search your knowledge base to find information about why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition.

Based on your notes, here''s why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition:

**Key Reasons:**

1. **High Sodium Risk**: Standard ORS has too high sodium content, which can push an already-atrophied heart into heart failure [K1, K2].

2. **Cardiac Atrophy**: In severe malnutrition, the heart muscle itself atrophies, resulting in permanently low cardiac output. This means these children cannot tolerate the same volume load as well-nourished children [K8].

3. **Electrolyte Balance**: ReSoMal has lower sodium and higher potassium compared to standard ORS, making it more appropriate for the electrolyte needs of malnourished children [K2].

**Additional Important Points:**

- **Slow Rehydration**: Rehydration must be done slowly over 12 hours because the atrophied heart cannot handle rapid volume loads [K1]
- **Monitoring**: Don''t rely on skin turgor or sunken eyes to judge rehydration status, as these signs are unreliable in malnourished children due to loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle [K1]
- **Preferred Route**: Oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure [K4]

The fundamental issue is that severely malnourished children have organ atrophy (especially cardiac atrophy) that makes them extremely vulnerable to fluid and electrolyte imbalances, requiring a more cautious approach to rehydration than standard ORS can provide.','[{"id":"call_-7145221324359596139","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"ReSoMal standard ORS severe malnutrition","top_k":10}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145221324359596139","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 129 of 675)\\\\nS Clarifications Note — Why ReSoMal, Not Standard ORS — and How to Monitor Progress We deliberately avoid Standard ORS here — its Sodium content is too high and can push an already-atrophied heart into Heart failure.\\\\nInstead use ReSoMal (Rehydration Solution for Malnutrition), which has less Sodium and more Potassium.\\\\nRehydration must be done *Slowly, over 12 hours, because the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load.\\\\nSchedule: first 2 hours — 5 mL/kg every half hour; remaining 10 hours — 5 mL/kg every hour.\\\\nDo NOT rely on Skin turgor or Sunken eyes to judge rehydration status — these signs are unreliable in a malnourished child (loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle independently changes skin appearance).\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 144 of 675)\\\\nD) Simple underweight\\\\nExplanation\\\\nWeight-for-age 60–80% WITH edema = Kwashiorkor (Wellcome classification). The weaning history, moon face (edema masking real fat loss), and flag-sign hair are all classic for Kwashiorkor rather than Marasmus.\\\\nQ3. Which of the following is the correct rehydration fluid of choice for a severely malnourished, dehydrated child, and why?\\\\n\\\\nA) Standard ORS — highest sodium content available 3 B) ReSoMal — lower sodium, higher potassium than standard ORS\\\\n\\\\nC) Normal saline bolus — fastest correction\\\\n\\\\nD) 5% Dextrose alone — avoids all electrolyte risk Explanation\\\\nStandard ORS’s high sodium load risks precipitating heart failure in a child with an atrophied heart.\\\\n\\\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 145 of 675)\\\\nH Exam Highlights\\\\nH Severe malnutrition admission criteria: Weight-for-Height < −3 SD OR MUAC < 115 mm, PLUS Infection, Dehydration, or Edema. H Golden rule: Marasmus = NEVER has edema. Any edema in a wasted child = Marasmic-Kwashiorkor. H Never press on the eye when Keratomalacia/Xerophthalmia is suspected — risk of rupture and permanent blindness. H Use ReSoMal, not standard ORS, for rehydration in severe malnutrition — monitor via urine output, not skin turgor/sunken eyes. H F-75 (stabilization, 75 kcal/100 mL) → F-100 (rehabilitation, 100 kcal/100 mL) once edema resolves and appetite returns. H Refeeding syndrome: hypophosphatemia is the most dangerous electrolyte derangement — prevent with slow, gradual refeeding.\\\\n\\\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 264 of 675)\\\\nKey Rehydration Rules\\\\nIn malnourished children, oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure — slow rehydration is preferred in this group.\\\\nThe WHO recommends low-osmolarity ORS at all ages.\\\\nAntidiarrheal compounds should never be used in children at any age.\\\\n\\\\n⚠ Red Flag — Never Give Antidiarrheals to Children Antidiarrheal (anti-motility) agents are contraindicated in children of any age — they slow clearance of the pathogen/toxin and can precipitate toxic megacolon or worsen invasive disease. Rehydration (oral or IV) and zinc are the evidence-based interventions; antimotility drugs are not.\\\\n\\\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Defines severe malnutrition admission criteria based on weight-for-height and MUAC with complications.)\\\\n5. Severe Malnutrition: Definition & Hospital Admission Criteria\\\\n\\\\n⚠ Severe Malnutrition Admission Criteria\\\\nA child is admitted for Severe malnutrition if EITHER of the following is met:\\\\n\\\\n1) Weight-for-Height on the growth chart is under −3 Standard Deviations, OR\\\\n\\\\n2) MUAC is under 115 mm (11.5 cm).\\\\nAND any of these three Complications are present: Infection, Dehydration, or Edema.\\\\n\\\\n[K6] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Lists complications in severe malnutrition including infection, dehydration, and anemia.)\\\\nComplications That Can Occur in Severe Malnutrition INFECTION\\\\n\\\\n• Can progress to Sepsis\\\\nDEHY","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T19:13:23.241Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvii1b3i0001g0u8ioq1ck7v','4ee7a4ae-5662-4d64-bb42-cc768cd65450','user','from my notes: why is ReSoMal used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition?',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-21T19:22:27.812Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvii1mqh0002g0u87pjkp0gl','4ee7a4ae-5662-4d64-bb42-cc768cd65450','assistant','I''ll search your knowledge base to find information about why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition.

Based on your notes, here''s why ReSoMal is used instead of standard ORS in severe malnutrition:

**Primary Reason: Risk of Heart Failure**
Standard ORS has too much sodium content, which can push an already-atrophied heart into heart failure in severely malnourished children [K1, K2].

**Key Differences Between ReSoMal and Standard ORS:**
- **ReSoMal**: Lower sodium, higher potassium content
- **Standard ORS**: Higher sodium content, insufficient potassium [K1, K2, K7]

**Additional Important Considerations:**

1. **Slow Rehydration Required**: Rehydration must be done slowly over 12 hours because the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle rapid volume load [K1].

2. **Monitoring Method**: Do NOT rely on skin turgor or sunken eyes to judge rehydration status in malnourished children, as these signs are unreliable due to loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle [K1, K3].

3. **Rehydration Schedule**: 
   - First 2 hours: 5 mL/kg every half hour
   - Remaining 10 hours: 5 mL/kg every hour [K1]

4. **Alternative Approach**: Oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure [K4, K5].

The use of ReSoMal instead of standard ORS is a critical safety measure in managing severe malnutrition, as it addresses the unique physiological vulnerabilities of these patients, particularly the weakened cardiac function that cannot tolerate the sodium load in standard rehydration solutions.','[{"id":"call_-7145222423871225473","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"ReSoMal severe malnutrition ORS why used instead","top_k":10}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145222423871225473","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 129 of 675)\\\\nS Clarifications Note — Why ReSoMal, Not Standard ORS — and How to Monitor Progress We deliberately avoid Standard ORS here — its Sodium content is too high and can push an already-atrophied heart into Heart failure.\\\\nInstead use ReSoMal (Rehydration Solution for Malnutrition), which has less Sodium and more Potassium.\\\\nRehydration must be done *Slowly, over 12 hours, because the heart muscle has atrophied and cannot handle a rapid volume load.\\\\nSchedule: first 2 hours — 5 mL/kg every half hour; remaining 10 hours — 5 mL/kg every hour.\\\\nDo NOT rely on Skin turgor or Sunken eyes to judge rehydration status — these signs are unreliable in a malnourished child (loss of subcutaneous fat/muscle independently changes skin appearance).\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 144 of 675)\\\\nD) Simple underweight\\\\nExplanation\\\\nWeight-for-age 60–80% WITH edema = Kwashiorkor (Wellcome classification). The weaning history, moon face (edema masking real fat loss), and flag-sign hair are all classic for Kwashiorkor rather than Marasmus.\\\\nQ3. Which of the following is the correct rehydration fluid of choice for a severely malnourished, dehydrated child, and why?\\\\n\\\\nA) Standard ORS — highest sodium content available 3 B) ReSoMal — lower sodium, higher potassium than standard ORS\\\\n\\\\nC) Normal saline bolus — fastest correction\\\\n\\\\nD) 5% Dextrose alone — avoids all electrolyte risk Explanation\\\\nStandard ORS’s high sodium load risks precipitating heart failure in a child with an atrophied heart.\\\\n\\\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 145 of 675)\\\\nH Exam Highlights\\\\nH Severe malnutrition admission criteria: Weight-for-Height < −3 SD OR MUAC < 115 mm, PLUS Infection, Dehydration, or Edema. H Golden rule: Marasmus = NEVER has edema. Any edema in a wasted child = Marasmic-Kwashiorkor. H Never press on the eye when Keratomalacia/Xerophthalmia is suspected — risk of rupture and permanent blindness. H Use ReSoMal, not standard ORS, for rehydration in severe malnutrition — monitor via urine output, not skin turgor/sunken eyes. H F-75 (stabilization, 75 kcal/100 mL) → F-100 (rehabilitation, 100 kcal/100 mL) once edema resolves and appetite returns. H Refeeding syndrome: hypophosphatemia is the most dangerous electrolyte derangement — prevent with slow, gradual refeeding.\\\\n\\\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 264 of 675)\\\\nKey Rehydration Rules\\\\nIn malnourished children, oral hydration is preferred over rapid IV rehydration to avoid overhydration or precipitating congestive heart failure — slow rehydration is preferred in this group.\\\\nThe WHO recommends low-osmolarity ORS at all ages.\\\\nAntidiarrheal compounds should never be used in children at any age.\\\\n\\\\n⚠ Red Flag — Never Give Antidiarrheals to Children Antidiarrheal (anti-motility) agents are contraindicated in children of any age — they slow clearance of the pathogen/toxin and can precipitate toxic megacolon or worsen invasive disease. Rehydration (oral or IV) and zinc are the evidence-based interventions; antimotility drugs are not.\\\\n\\\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 657 of 675)\\\\nStep Numeric Detail Not Given Above\\\\nHypoglycemia Blood glucose <55 mg/dL. Severe: lethargy, rigidity, irritability, convulsion, coma. Treat with 2 mL/kg of 10% Dextrose IV or via NG tube; recheck after 30 min, then every 2 hours. Prevent with frequent feeds. Hypothermia Feed every 2 hours, day and night. Keep the room at 30–33°C even overnight. Use a heater near the child, skin-to-skin contact with the mother, and a warm blanket; avoid bathing/exposure. Often occurs together with hypoglycemia, and both can be a sign of sepsis. Dehydration Rehydrate orally or via NG tube — IV is avoided (risk of volume overload/heart failure) unless in shock or oral rehydrat","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T19:22:42.905Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvii4k600003g0u8ajz6vh9n','4ee7a4ae-5662-4d64-bb42-cc768cd65450','user','search online for heart anatomy images, give me links',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-21T19:24:59.544Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvii4pds0004g0u8ill41sn0','4ee7a4ae-5662-4d64-bb42-cc768cd65450','assistant','I''ll search for heart anatomy images online for you.

Here are some excellent heart anatomy image resources I found online:

[S1] **Labeled Heart Images** - BlueLink from University of Michigan provides downloadable labeled heart images with cardiovascular illustrations

[S2] **Human Heart Anatomy Stock Photos** - Over 40,200 human heart anatomy stock photos and images available for browsing

[S3] **47 Best Human Heart Diagram Ideas** - Pinterest collection featuring various labeled heart diagrams and educational resources

[S4] **Interactive Biology Heart Diagrams** - Multiple human heart diagrams including myocardium and coronary vessels, with download options

[S5] **Human Heart Diagram with Labels** - Pinterest resource for learning to draw simple heart diagrams with accurate labels

These sources should provide you with comprehensive heart anatomy images for educational purposes. The BlueLink resource [S1] and Interactive Biology site [S4] appear to be particularly good for educational use with clear labels and downloadable content.','[{"id":"call_-7145202185985319643","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"heart anatomy images diagrams educational","maxResults":5}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7145202185985319643","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Labeled Heart Images\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://sites.google.com/a/umich.edu/bluelink/resources/bluelink/heart\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Cardiovascular Illustrations Labeled Heart Images To download a BlueLink image, click on the photo and select the download option on the following page.\\",\\"host\\":\\"sites.google.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Human Heart Anatomy Stock Photos\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.istockphoto.com/photos/human-heart-anatomy\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Browse 40,200+ human heart anatomy stock photos and images available, or search for human heart anatomy vector to find more great stock photos and pictures. Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.istockphoto.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"47 Best Human heart diagram ideas\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.pinterest.com/msjdlittles70/human-heart-diagram\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Feb 24, 2021 - Explore Juno D. Littles''s board \\\\\\"Human heart diagram \\\\\\" on Pinterest. See more ideas about labeled diagram of the heart , human heart diagram , ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.pinterest.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Show me a diagram of the human heart? Here are a bunch!\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://interactivebiology.com/75/show-me-a-diagram-of-the-human-heart-here-are-a-bunch\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Below is a list of all the human heart diagrams and pictures included in this post: Heart diagram: The myocardium and coronary vessels; Feel free to download ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"interactivebiology.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Human Heart Diagram with Labels\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://ar.pinterest.com/pin/805722189623750665\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Learn to draw a simple heart diagram with accurate labels . This diagram is a great tool for studying and understanding the human heart.\\",\\"host\\":\\"ar.pinterest.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] Labeled Heart Images\\\\n    https://sites.google.com/a/umich.edu/bluelink/resources/bluelink/heart (sites.google.com)\\\\n    Cardiovascular Illustrations Labeled Heart Images To download a BlueLink image, click on the photo and select the download option on the following page.\\\\n[S2] Human Heart Anatomy Stock Photos\\\\n    https://www.istockphoto.com/photos/human-heart-anatomy (www.istockphoto.com)\\\\n    Browse 40,200+ human heart anatomy stock photos and images available, or search for human heart anatomy vector to find more great stock photos and pictures. Read more\\\\n[S3] 47 Best Human heart diagram ideas\\\\n    https://www.pinterest.com/msjdlittles70/human-heart-diagram (www.pinterest.com)\\\\n    Feb 24, 2021 - Explore Juno D. Littles''s board \\\\\\"Human heart diagram \\\\\\" on Pinterest. See more ideas about labeled diagram of the heart , human heart diagram , ...\\\\n[S4] Show me a diagram of the human heart? Here are a bunch!\\\\n    https://interactivebiology.com/75/show-me-a-diagram-of-the-human-heart-here-are-a-bunch (interactivebiology.com)\\\\n    Below is a list of all the human heart diagrams and pictures included in this post: Heart diagram: The myocardium and coronary vessels; Feel free to download ...\\\\n[S5] Human Heart Diagram with Labels\\\\n    https://ar.pinterest.com/pin/805722189623750665 (ar.pinterest.com)\\\\n    Learn to draw a simple heart diagram with accurate labels . This diagram is a great tool for studying and understanding the human heart.\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-21T19:25:06.304Z');
