 most recent guidance when sources conflict — flag older sources as superseded where relevant.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T05:12:56.026Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvlywi3x0001v6rd8a278uyl','228c86f9-7798-44b5-b9cf-a72f67403045','user','Explain the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems in detail including neurotransmitters, receptors, and clinical drug targets',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-24T05:37:55.473Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvlyx2210006v6rd33yp6wtu','228c86f9-7798-44b5-b9cf-a72f67403045','assistant','I''ll provide a comprehensive explanation of the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems, covering their neurotransmitters, receptors, and clinical drug targets.

## Sympathetic and Parasympathetic Nervous Systems: Comprehensive Overview

The autonomic nervous system (ANS) is divided into two main divisions: the sympathetic nervous system (SNS) and parasympathetic nervous system (PNS). These systems work in opposition to maintain homeostasis, with the SNS responsible for "fight-or-flight" responses and the PNS managing "rest and digest" functions [S2].

### **Sympathetic Nervous System (SNS)**

**Physiology and Function:**
- **Primary Role**: "Fight-or-flight" response
- **Origin**: Thoracolumbar region (T1-L2 spinal cord segments)
- **Ganglia**: Located close to the spinal cord (paravertebral chain)
- **Pathway**: Short preganglionic fibers, long postganglionic fibers
- **Effects**: Increases heart rate, blood pressure, bronchodilation, glycogenolysis, and blood flow to skeletal muscles

**Neurotransmitters and Receptors:**
- **Preganglionic neurons**: Release **acetylcholine** (ACh) which binds to **nicotinic receptors** (N1) on postganglionic neurons
- **Postganglionic neurons**: Release **norepinephrine** (NE) which binds to **adrenergic receptors**:
  - **α1 receptors**: Found on smooth muscle (vasoconstriction, mydriasis, gastrointestinal motility inhibition)
  - **α2 receptors**: Presynaptic inhibition of NE release
  - **β1 receptors**: Cardiac muscle (increased heart rate and contractility)
  - **β2 receptors**: Smooth muscle (bronchodilation, vasodilation in skeletal muscle)
  - **β3 receptors**: Adipose tissue (lipolysis)

**Clinical Drug Targets:**
- **α1 antagonists**: Prazosin (hypertension), Tamsulosin (BPH)
- **α2 agonists**: Clonidine (hypertension), Dexmedetomidine (sedation)
- **β1 antagonists**: Metoprolol, Atenolol (hypertension, angina)
- **β2 agonists**: Albuterol, Salbutamol (asthma)
- **β2 antagonists**: Not clinically used due to side effects
- **Mixed α/β blockers**: Labetalol, Carvedilol

### **Parasympathetic Nervous System (PNS)**

**Physiology and Function:**
- **Primary Role**: "Rest and digest" response
- **Origin**: Craniosacral region (brainstem nuclei S2-S4)
- **Ganglia**: Located near or within target organs
- **Pathway**: Long preganglionic fibers, short postganglionic fibers
- **Effects**: Decreases heart rate, promotes digestion, constricts pupils, stimulates salivation and lacrimation

**Neurotransmitters and Receptors:**
- **Preganglionic neurons**: Release **acetylcholine** (ACh) which binds to **nicotinic receptors** (N1) on postganglionic neurons
- **Postganglionic neurons**: Release **acetylcholine** (ACh) which binds to **muscarinic receptors**:
  - **M1 receptors**: Gastric secretion, CNS effects
  - **M2 receptors**: Heart (negative chronotropy, negative dromotropy)
  - **M3 receptors**: Smooth muscle (contraction of bronchi, GI tract, bladder)
  - **M4 receptors**: CNS modulation
  - **M5 receptors**: CNS modulation

**Clinical Drug Targets:**
- **Muscarinic antagonists**:
  - M1: Pirenzepine (peptic ulcer)
  - M2: Not selectively targeted
  - M3: Ipratropium (COPD), Tolterodine (overactive bladder)
  - Non-selective: Atropine, Scopolamine, Glycopyrrolate
- **Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors**: Donepezil (Alzheimer''s), Neostigmine (myasthenia gravis)
- **Nicotinic agonists**: Nicotine (smoking cessation)
- **Nicotinic antagonists**: Tubocurarine, Vecuronium (muscle relaxation)

### **Neurotransmitter Synthesis and Metabolism**

**Sympathetic Pathway:**
1. **Tyrosine** → **DOPA** (tyrosine hydroxylase)
2. **DOPA** → **Dopamine** (DOPA decarboxylase)
3. **Dopamine** → **Norepinephrine** (dopamine β-hydroxylase)
4. **Norepinephrine** → **Epinephrine** (phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase) in adrenal medulla

**Parasympathetic Pathway:**
- **Choline** + **Acetyl-CoA** → **Acetylcholine** (choline acetyltransferase)
- **Acetylcholine** breakdown by **acetylcholinesterase** → Choline + Acetate

### **Clinical Applications and Drug Classes**

**Sympathetic Drugs:**
- **Direct-acting agonists**: Epinephrine, Norepinephrine, Phenylephrine
- **Indirect-acting agonists**: Amphetamine, Cocaine (block NE reuptake)
- **Mixed agonists-antagonists**: Dobutamine (β1 selective)

**Parasympathetic Drugs:**
- **Direct-acting agonists**: Pilocarpine (glaucoma), Bethanechol (urinary retention)
- **Indirect-acting agonists**: Donepezil (AChE inhibitor)
- **Receptor-specific agents**: Various muscarinic and nicotinic agents

### **Physiological Interactions**

The SNS and PNS typically work in opposition:
- **Heart**: SNS increases rate/force, PNS decreases rate
- **Blood vessels**: SNS causes vasoconstriction (α1), PNS causes vasodilation (endothelial M3)
- **GI tract**: SNS inhibits motility, PNS stimulates motility
- **Pupils**: SNS causes mydriasis, PNS causes miosis

Understanding these systems is crucial for pharmacological interventions, as many drugs target specific receptors to achieve therapeutic effects while minimizing side effects [S1][S4].','[{"id":"call_-7142205054726962470","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"sympathetic parasympathetic nervous system neurotransmitters receptors drug targets","top_k":10}},{"id":"call_-7142203542898467755","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"sympathetic parasympathetic nervous system neurotransmitters receptors pharmacology","maxResults":5}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7142205054726962470","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (e2e-diabetes.md — This chunk introduces Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes definitions, diagnostic criteria including fasting glucose >=126 mg/dL, and first-line treatments like Metformin.)\\\\n# Diabetes Mellitus Core Notes\\\\n\\\\nType 1 diabetes is autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells → absolute insulin\\\\ndeficiency, young onset, ketoacidosis risk. Type 2 is insulin resistance with progressive\\\\nbeta-cell decline. Diagnostic criteria: fasting glucose >= 126 mg/dL, HbA1c >= 6.5%,\\\\nrandom glucose >= 200 with symptoms, or OGTT 2h >= 200. Metformin is first-line for T2DM\\\\n(decreases hepatic gluconeogenesis, GI side effects, lactic acidosis risk in renal failure).\\\\nSGLT2 inhibitors cause glycosuria, weight loss, and cardiovascular/renal benefit.\\\\nGLP-1 agonists slow gastric emptying and promote satiety. DKA: hyperglycemia, ketosis,\\\\nmetabolic acidosis — treat with IV fluids, insulin, potassium replacement.\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (e2e-lecture.md — Describes adrenal cortex zones, cortisol rhythm, Cushing syndrome features, and dexamethasone suppression test.)\\\\n# Adrenal Cortex Physiology\\\\n\\\\nThe adrenal cortex has three zones: zona glomerulosa (aldosterone), zona fasciculata (cortisol),\\\\nand zona reticularis (androgens). Cortisol follows a diurnal rhythm, peaks at 8 AM, and is\\\\nreleased under ACTH control from the anterior pituitary. Cushing syndrome results from chronic\\\\nglucocorticoid excess: central obesity, moon facies, buffalo hump, purple striae, hypertension,\\\\nand hyperglycemia. The dexamethasone suppression test distinguishes ACTH-dependent causes.\\\\nConn syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism) causes resistant hypertension with hypokalemia and\\\\nmetabolic alkalosis. Addison disease is primary adrenal insufficiency: fatigue, hyperpigmentation,\\\\nhyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and low cortisol with HIGH ACTH.\\\\n\\",\\"citations\\":[{\\"ref\\":\\"K1\\",\\"title\\":\\"e2e-diabetes.md\\",\\"sourceId\\":\\"cmuyzuuww0002ewma8ndzgi1c\\",\\"relevance\\":0},{\\"ref\\":\\"K2\\",\\"title\\":\\"e2e-lecture.md\\",\\"sourceId\\":\\"cmuyygery0003ydma3bobkh6a\\",\\"relevance\\":0}],\\"note\\":\\"Cite these excerpts using bracketed [K1], [K2] markers (one bracket per source, placed right after the claim it supports). Do NOT write bare K-numbers or raw passage text into the answer.\\",\\"retrieval\\":{\\"candidates\\":2,\\"rerankUsed\\":true,\\"latencyMs\\":2184}}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142203542898467755","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System - PMC - NIH\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1959222\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by LK McCorry · 2007 · Cited by 1842 — The neurotransmitters of the ANS and the circulating catecholamines bind to specific receptors on the cell membranes of the effector tissue. All adrenergic ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"4.2 Autonomic Nervous System Basics\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://wtcs.pressbooks.pub/pharmacology/chapter/4-2-ans-basics\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"The sympathetic system is associated with the \\\\\\"fight-or-flight\\\\\\" response, and parasympathetic activity is often referred to as “rest and digest.”\\",\\"host\\":\\"wtcs.pressbooks.pub\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"18. Agents and Actions of the Autonomic Nervous System\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://open.lib.umn.edu/pharmacology/chapter/unit-4-1-agents-and-actions-of-the-autonomic-nervous-system\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Most organs receive dual parasympathetic [PNS] and sympathetic innervation [SNS]. These two divisions are complementary to one another and often result in the ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"open.lib.umn.edu\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Autonomic Nervous System Overview\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.vitalscan.com/dtr_ans_overview.html\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves release neurotransmitters , primarily norepinephrine and epinephrine for the sympathetic nervous system,\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.vitalscan.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Neuroanatomy, Sympathetic Nervous System - StatPearls\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542195\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by MN Alshak · 2023 · Cited by 95 — Similar to the PANS, the neurotransmitter used at this junction is acetylcholine . This acetylcholine activates nicotinic receptors. These neurotransmitters act ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System - PMC - NIH\\\\n    https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1959222 (pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov)\\\\n    by LK McCorry · 2007 · Cited by 1842 — The neurotransmitters of the ANS and the circulating catecholamines bind to specific receptors on the cell membranes of the effector tissue. All adrenergic ... Read more\\\\n[S2] 4.2 Autonomic Nervous System Basics\\\\n    https://wtcs.pressbooks.pub/pharmacology/chapter/4-2-ans-basics (wtcs.pressbooks.pub)\\\\n    The sympathetic system is associated with the \\\\\\"fight-or-flight\\\\\\" response, and parasympathetic activity is often referred to as “rest and digest.”\\\\n[S3] 18. Agents and Actions of the Autonomic Nervous System\\\\n    https://open.lib.umn.edu/pharmacology/chapter/unit-4-1-agents-and-actions-of-the-autonomic-nervous-system (open.lib.umn.edu)\\\\n    Most organs receive dual parasympathetic [PNS] and sympathetic innervation [SNS]. These two divisions are complementary to one another and often result in the ... Read more\\\\n[S4] Autonomic Nervous System Overview\\\\n    https://www.vitalscan.com/dtr_ans_overview.html (www.vitalscan.com)\\\\n    Both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves release neurotransmitters , primarily norepinephrine and epinephrine for the sympathetic nervous system,\\\\n[S5] Neuroanatomy, Sympathetic Nervous System - StatPearls\\\\n    https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542195 (www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov)\\\\n    by MN Alshak · 2023 · Cited by 95 — Similar to the PANS, the neurotransmitter used at this junction is acetylcholine . This acetylcholine activates nicotinic receptors. These neurotransmitters act ...\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically. Results are re-ranked for recency (freshest first): when a source carries a date, mention the year the moment you cite it (e.g. \\\\\\"per the 2024 ESC guideline [S1]\\\\\\"), and prefer the most recent guidance when sources conflict — flag older sources as superseded where relevant.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T05:38:21.481Z');
