INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm0izum0013wnrdaifm3jin','7da42c7a-961a-4ebd-94d4-ca7c297eb8ca','assistant','I''ll generate a micro-drill on your weakest topic, which is **constipation** (0% accuracy from your quiz performance).

Perfect! I''ve generated a micro-drill on your weakest topic: **constipation**. Here''s what was created:

**Micro-Drill Summary:**
- **Count:** 3 questions
- **Topic:** Constipation (pathophysiology, diagnosis, and management)
- **Difficulty:** Medium
- **System:** Gastrointestinal

The questions are now saved to your question bank and pending review. They cover:
1. Constipation pathophysiology
2. Constipation diagnosis (including red flags)
3. Constipation management (treatment approaches)

These questions are specifically targeted at your weakest area to help improve your understanding and performance. You can access them through your question bank for review and practice.

Would you like me to help you with anything else, such as creating flashcards for constipation concepts or setting up a schedule for reviewing these questions?','[{"id":"call_-7142178975685538266","name":"run_question_generation_agent","args":{"topic":"constipation","system":"Gastrointestinal","types":["mcq"],"difficulties":["Medium"],"count":3,"focusHint":"Focus on constipation pathophysiology, diagnosis, and management","useKnowledgeBase":true,"weakTopics":["Constipation"]}},{"id":"call_-7142197942261119576","name":"task","args":{"description":"Generate exactly 3 medium-difficulty MCQ questions on constipation focusing on pathophysiology, diagnosis, and management. The questions should target learner weak areas in constipation and be grounded in the provided source material. Each question must have 4-5 answer options with one correct answer, plus detailed explanations, clinical pearls, distractor analysis, and appropriate citations from the grounding material.","subagent_type":"question-author"}},{"id":"search_medical_references-1-1792822907643","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation pathophysiology diagnosis management guidelines","maxResults":5}},{"id":"search_medical_references-2-1792822910673","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation pathophysiology colonic motility defecation disorders","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-3-1792822920478","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation diagnostic criteria Rome criteria evaluation","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-4-1792822923278","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation management laxatives treatment guidelines","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-5-1792822926061","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation red flags alarm symptoms diagnostic evaluation","maxResults":3}},{"id":"call_-7142203371099781084","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by straining during defecation, lumpy or hard stools, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation for the past 6 months. She reports no rectal bleeding, weight loss, or family history of colorectal cancer. Which of the following pathophysiological mechanisms is most likely responsible for her constipation?","options":["Colonic obstruction from a proximal colon mass","Decreased colonic motility due to chronic opioid use","Functional constipation with normal colonic transit time","Pelvic floor dyssynergia with impaired defecation","Hypothyroidism-induced decreased gastrointestinal motility"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The patient''s symptoms (straining during defecation, lumpy/hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation) for 6 months without red flags (bleeding, weight loss, family history) are classic for functional constipation. According to Rome IV criteria [S1], functional constipation requires at least two of: straining during ≥25% of defecations, lumpy/hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation, or need for manual maneuvers. Functional constipation typically has normal colonic transit time, distinguishing it from slow transit constipation [S3]. The absence of opioid use, pelvic floor dysfunction symptoms, or systemic symptoms makes other options less likely.","mnemonic":"ROME criteria: Straining, Lumpy stools, Incomplete evacuation, Manual maneuvers needed","clinicalPearl":"Functional constipation is the most common type of chronic constipation and typically responds well to lifestyle modifications and osmotic laxatives rather than invasive testing.","distractorAnalysis":"A is incorrect as colonic obstruction would present with more acute/severe symptoms and likely abdominal distension. B is incorrect as there''s no mention of opioid use. D is incorrect as pelvic floor dyssynergia typically involves paradoxical anal contraction during defecation, not just straining. E is incorrect as hypothyroidism would typically present with other systemic symptoms like fatigue, weight gain, or cold intolerance.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"gastrointestinal","topic":"constipation pathophysiology","tags":["functional constipation","Rome criteria","colonic motility"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004xi8rml3uq311z"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0059i8rmv0gfj0f7"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004zi8rmd11271yd"}]}},{"id":"call_-7142203474178997596","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 68-year-old man with chronic constipation presents to the clinic with new-onset severe abdominal pain, distension, and inability to pass gas or stool for 48 hours. On examination, he has a distended abdomen with high-pitched bowel sounds and tenderness in the left lower quadrant. Which of the following diagnostic findings would be most concerning for a serious underlying pathology requiring immediate intervention?","options":["Elevated C-reactive protein (CRP) of 15 mg/L","Mild leukocytosis of 11,000/mm³","Air-fluid levels on abdominal X-ray","Thickened colonic wall on CT scan","Elevated serum amylase of 120 U/L"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The patient''s presentation with severe abdominal pain, distension, inability to pass gas/stool, and high-pitched bowel sounds suggests possible mechanical obstruction or fecal impaction. Air-fluid levels on abdominal X-ray are highly concerning for mechanical bowel obstruction [S3], which is a surgical emergency. The other findings (elevated CRP, mild leukocytosis, thickened colonic wall) could be seen in various conditions including constipation but are not as specific for obstruction. Elevated amylase is not typically associated with constipation complications.","mnemonic":"SBO signs: Severe pain, Bloating, Obstipation (no stool/gas), Air-fluid levels","clinicalPearl":"In patients with constipation presenting with severe abdominal pain and distension, always consider mechanical obstruction as a life-threatening complication that requires immediate imaging and intervention.","distractorAnalysis":"A is incorrect as elevated CRP is non-specific and can be seen in many inflammatory conditions. B is incorrect as mild leukocytosis is common in constipation and not specific for obstruction. D is incorrect as thickened colonic wall can be seen in inflammatory conditions but is not diagnostic of obstruction. E is incorrect as elevated amylase suggests pancreatic involvement, not related to constipation complications.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"gastrointestinal","topic":"constipation diagnosis","tags":["constipation complications","bowel obstruction","red flags"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0052i8rm9esfk2uc"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005bi8rmu8gdpz1a"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005ki8rmgwfl542w"}]}},{"id":"call_-7142180212636116246","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 72-year-old nursing home resident with chronic constipation fails to respond adequately to increased fiber intake and adequate fluid consumption. She has normal thyroid function and no evidence of mechanical obstruction. Which of the following treatment approaches would be most appropriate as the next step in her management?","options":["Immediate referral for surgical consultation","Trial of polyethylene glycol (PEG) osmotic laxative","Addition of docusate sodium stool softener","Discontinuation of all current medications","Initiation of monthly bisacodyl suppositories"],"answer":"B","explanation":"For patients with chronic constipation who fail lifestyle modifications (fiber, fluids), the next step is typically an osmotic laxative such as polyethylene glycol [S2]. The 2023 ACG/AGA guidelines specifically recommend osmotic laxatives as first-line pharmacological treatment [S1]. PEG is preferred over stool softeners (docusate) which have limited efficacy, and stimulant laxatives (bisacodyl) are generally reserved for later use due to potential tolerance and electrolyte disturbances. Surgical consultation is premature without evidence of structural disease, and medication discontinuation should be guided by specific medication review rather than blanket discontinuation.","mnemonic":"PEG progression: Polyethylene glycol after fiber fails","clinicalPearl":"Osmotic laxatives like PEG are the cornerstone of pharmacological treatment for chronic constipation and can be used long-term safely in most patients.","distractorAnalysis":"A is incorrect as surgery is reserved for specific structural causes or severe complications not present here. C is incorrect as docusate has limited efficacy as a single agent and is generally not recommended as monotherapy. D is incorrect as medication review should be targeted rather than blanket discontinuation. E is incorrect as bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative typically used after osmotic agents and carries risks of electrolyte disturbances with long-term use.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"gastrointestinal","topic":"constipation management","tags":["laxatives","polyethylene glycol","treatment guidelines"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005bi8rmu8gdpz1a"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005ki8rmgwfl542w"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0053i8rm4h1pm4nj"}]}}]','[{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-1-1792822907643","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Constipation Treatment & Management\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/184704-treatment\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Dec 5, 2025 — Manual disimpaction and transrectal enemas may be used after any critical illness associated with constipation has been ruled out. Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Dec 5, 2025\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Evidence-Based Clinical Guidelines for Chronic\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://karger.com/dig/article/106/1/62/912065/Evidence-Based-Clinical-Guidelines-for-Chronic\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Aug 19, 2024 — Based on the latest evidence, these guidelines describe the definition","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-2-1792822910673","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"The pathophysiology of chronic constipation - PMC - NIH\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3206564\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Of note, colonic motility is strongly attenuated during sleep to avoid incontinence; thus nocturnal bowel movements or anal leakage is significantly abnormal.\\",\\"host\\":\\"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Constipation: Background, Pathophysiology, Etiology\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/184704-overview\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Colon obstruction (neoplasm, volvulus, stricture). Slow colonic motility, particularly in patients with a history o","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-3-1792822920478","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Rome IV Criteria\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://theromefoundation.org/rome-iv/rome-iv-criteria\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"FUNCTIONAL CONSTIPATION Diagnostic criteria * Must include two or more of the following:** Straining during more than 1⁄4 (25%) of defecations Lumpy or hard ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"theromefoundation.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Rome IV Diagnostic Criteria for Functional Constipation\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.mdcalc.com/calc/10003/rome-iv-diagnostic-criteria-functional-constipation\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"The Rome IV Criteria for Constipation provides criteria for diagnosis of constipation . Signs or symptoms of g","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-4-1792822923278","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"ACG and AGA guideline on chronic constipation\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://gastro.org/press-releases/acg-and-aga-guideline-on-chronic-constipation-management-is-first-to-recommend-supplements-magnesium-oxide-and-senna-as-evidence-based-treatments\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"May 19, 2023 — ACG and AGA guideline on chronic constipation management is first to recommend supplements Magnesium Oxide and Senna as evidence-based ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"gastro.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"May 19, 2023\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Constipation Treatment & Management\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/184704-treatment\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"De","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-5-1792822926061","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Constipation danger signs: What to watch for\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://oshihealth.com\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Sep 17, 2025 — Constipation danger signs include abdominal pain, bleeding, inability to pass gas, and more . These issues warrant prompt medical evaluation. Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"oshihealth.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Sep 17, 2025\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"The approach to diagnosis and treatment of chronic constipation\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by P Paré · 2011 · Cited by 71 — Patient assessment is clinically based on the presence or absence of red flags . Commonly used therapies (eg, bulk-form","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142197942261119576","toolName":"task","content":"Now I''ll create 3 medium-difficulty MCQ questions on constipation focusing on pathophysiology, diagnosis, and management, based on the evidence I''ve gathered:\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\"type\\": \\"mcq\\",\\n    \\"text\\": \\"A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by straining during defecation, lumpy or hard stools, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation for the past 6 months. She reports no rectal bleeding, weight loss, or family history of colorectal cancer. Which of the following pathophysiological mechanisms is most likely responsible for her constipation?\\",\\n    \\"options\\": [\\n  ","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142203371099781084","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvm0i50z000ywnrdf85syx40\\",\\"index\\":1,\\"message\\":\\"Question 1 saved (id cmvm0i50z000ywnrdf85syx40). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142203474178997596","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvm0ijmg0010wnrdhmbhytdu\\",\\"index\\":2,\\"message\\":\\"Question 2 saved (id cmvm0ijmg0010wnrdhmbhytdu). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142180212636116246","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvm0iwle0012wnrdq7iwy2ru\\",\\"index\\":3,\\"message\\":\\"Question 3 saved (id cmvm0iwle0012wnrdq7iwy2ru). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142178975685538266","toolName":"run_question_generation_agent","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"savedCount\\":3,\\"requestedCount\\":3,\\"questions\\":[{\\"index\\":1,\\"id\\":\\"cmvm0i50z000ywnrdf85syx40\\",\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"system\\":\\"gastrointestinal\\",\\"topic\\":\\"constipation pathophysiology\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"preview\\":\\"A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by straining during defecation, lumpy or hard stools, and a sensation o\\"},{\\"index\\":2,\\"id\\":\\"cmvm0ijmg0010wnrdhmbhytdu\\",\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"system\\":\\"gastrointestinal\\",\\"topic\\":\