,{"id":"call_-7142179113124487609","name":"task","args":{"description":"Generate exactly 5 medium-difficulty MCQ questions on constipation, focusing on pathophysiology, diagnosis, and treatment. The questions should target the user''s weak areas (0% accuracy on constipation). Use the provided grounding source material which covers pediatric constipation definitions, causes, diagnostic criteria, and treatment approaches. Include citations from the source material for each question. The questions should be clinically accurate and cover key concepts like functional vs organic constipation, diagnostic red flags, treatment approaches, and pathophysiology mechanisms.","subagent_type":"question-author"}},{"id":"search_medical_references-1-1792821342790","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation pathophysiology diagnosis treatment functional organic","maxResults":5}},{"id":"search_medical_references-2-1792821345702","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation diagnostic criteria red flags pediatric Rome IV","maxResults":5}},{"id":"call_e20786aec79f44c2b2fd7781","name":"task","args":{"description":"Generate exactly 5 MCQ questions on constipation focusing on pathophysiology, diagnosis, and treatment. The questions should target the user''s weakest areas where they have 0% accuracy. Use the provided grounding source material from the user''s knowledge library which covers pediatric constipation definitions, causes, diagnostic criteria, and treatment approaches. The questions should be Medium difficulty and cover the key concepts from the source material including: functional vs organic constipation, diagnostic criteria, red flags for organic causes, common triggers, and treatment approaches.","subagent_type":"question-author"}},{"id":"search_medical_references-1-1792821361378","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation pathophysiology diagnosis treatment functional organic pediatric","maxResults":5}},{"id":"search_medical_references-2-1792821365000","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"Rome IV criteria pediatric functional constipation diagnosis","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-3-1792821367684","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation red flags organic causes Hirschsprung disease spinal cord tumor","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-4-1792821371403","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation treatment polyethylene glycol lactulose enemas pediatric first line","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-5-1792821374837","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"constipation pathophysiology colonic motility rectal retention fecal impaction","maxResults":3}},{"id":"call_d3dba8ba0e5d47949ec4686f","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 6-year-old child presents with constipation that began at age 3 years. The child has hard stools, straining during defecation, and stools less than 3 times per week for the past 2 months. Physical examination is normal. What is the most appropriate diagnosis and what key diagnostic criteria must be met?","options":["Organic constipation - requires evidence of structural abnormality on imaging","Functional constipation - must meet Rome IV criteria of 2+ symptoms occurring at least once weekly for 1 month","Functional constipation - requires abdominal distension and palpable fecal mass","Organic constipation - requires family history of Hirschsprung disease"],"answer":"Functional constipation - must meet Rome IV criteria of 2+ symptoms occurring at least once weekly for 1 month","explanation":"Functional constipation is a clinical diagnosis based on history and physical examination, with no evidence of organic disease. The Rome IV criteria require at least 2 of the following occurring at least once weekly for 1 month: 2 or fewer defecations per week, retentive posturing, painful or hard bowel movements, large fecal mass in rectum, or fecal incontinence [S1]. This child meets criteria with hard stools, straining, and decreased frequency.","mnemonic":"ROME criteria: 2+ symptoms, weekly, monthly duration","clinicalPearl":"Functional constipation is diagnosed clinically using Rome IV criteria; organic causes are rare (<5% of cases) and require red flag evaluation [S5].","distractorAnalysis":"Option A is incorrect because organic constipation requires specific structural/organic findings, not just symptoms. Option C is incorrect because abdominal distension and fecal mass are supportive but not diagnostic criteria. Option D is incorrect because family history is a red flag but not diagnostic criteria.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Gastrointestinal","topic":"Constipation diagnosis","tags":["functional constipation","Rome criteria","diagnosis"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004xi8rml3uq311z"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0059i8rmv0gfj0f7"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005ki8rmgwfl542w"}]}},{"id":"call_7d46912836d94deb90d164fb","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 2-month-old infant presents with severe constipation, failure to pass meconium for 72 hours after birth, and abdominal distension. What is the most concerning red flag that suggests an organic cause requiring urgent evaluation?","options":["Constipation onset after 6 months of age","Passage of meconium >48 hours after delivery","Family history of constipation","Normal growth and development"],"answer":"Passage of meconium >48 hours after delivery","explanation":"Delayed passage of meconium (>48 hours after birth) is a classic red flag for Hirschsprung disease, a common organic cause of constipation in infants [S2]. Hirschsprung disease results from absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus, causing functional obstruction. Other red flags include onset in the first month, family history of Hirschsprung disease, and ribbon-like stools [S2].","mnemonic":"Meconium delay = Hirschprung suspicion","clinicalPearl":"In infants, delayed meconium passage (>48h) is highly specific for Hirschsprung disease and requires urgent evaluation with rectal biopsy [S2].","distractorAnalysis":"Option A is incorrect because early onset (<1 month) is more concerning for organic causes. Option C is a red flag but less specific than delayed meconium passage. Option D is incorrect because organic constipation often presents with failure to thrive.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Gastrointestinal","topic":"Constipation red flags","tags":["organic constipation","Hirschsprung disease","red flags"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004zi8rmd11271yd"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0052i8rm9esfk2uc"}]}},{"id":"call_5babea8ec0ba4d779a456397","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 7-year-old with functional constipation has failed dietary modifications and behavioral interventions. What is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?","options":["Polyethylene glycol (PEG) 1 g/kg/day","Lactulose 1-3 mL/kg/day divided into 2 doses","Mineral oil 15-30 mL twice daily","Bisacodyl suppositories as needed"],"answer":"Polyethylene glycol (PEG) 1 g/kg/day","explanation":"Polyethylene glycol (PEG) without electrolytes is the first-line treatment for pediatric functional constipation [S1, S2]. PEG works by drawing water into the colon, softening stool and promoting bowel movements. It has excellent efficacy and safety profile in children. Lactulose is second-line, mineral oil has safety concerns, and bisacodyl should be reserved for short-term use due to potential dependency [S1, S3].","mnemonic":"PEG = Pediatric constipation first-line","clinicalPearl":"PEG is osmotic laxative of first choice in children with functional constipation; start with 1 g/kg/day and titrate to response [S2].","distractorAnalysis":"Option B is incorrect because lactulose is second-line. Option C is incorrect due to aspiration risk and poor palatability. Option D is incorrect because bisacodyl can cause dependency and should not be used as first-line.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Gastrointestinal","topic":"Constipation treatment","tags":["polyethylene glycol","first-line treatment","pharmacotherapy"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004xi8rml3uq311z"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0059i8rmv0gfj0f7"}]}},{"id":"call_e979bd9c166a4e5596ee459c","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism underlying functional constipation in children?","options":["Structural obstruction of the colon","Colonic dysmotility and rectal retention","Metabolic electrolyte abnormalities","Neurological spinal cord abnormalities"],"answer":"Colonic dysmotility and rectal retention","explanation":"Functional constipation primarily involves colonic dysmotility and rectal retention [S1]. The pathophysiology includes delayed colonic transit time, impaired rectal sensation, and learned stool withholding behavior. When stool enters the rectum, it causes distension and conscious perception of the urge to defecate, but in functional constipation, children often withhold stool due to fear or pain, leading to further ...[TRUNCATED]','[{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-1-1792821342790","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Functional Constipation: What Is It? Treatment &\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://primehealthdenver.com/blog/functional-constipation\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"May 16, 2023 — To diagnose functional constipation , your