INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm9tir80009dirdbnq0ux8o','1c14d39a-d411-4cfd-a212-69e77b120c7e','assistant','I''ll search for the current medical guidance on normal anion gap values to give you the most up-to-date information.

Based on current medical guidelines, here''s the normal anion gap range:

## Normal Anion Gap Range

**Current consensus range: 8-12 mEq/L** (or 8-12 mmol/L in SI units) [S3]

However, there are some important variations and considerations:

### Different Reference Ranges:
- **Most recent guidelines (2023-2026)**: 4-12 mEq/L [S3]
- **Traditional range**: 8-16 mEq/L [S2]
- **Some sources**: 3-11 mEq/L (lowered due to measurement technique changes) [S3]

### Important Notes:
1. **No universal standard**: As Cleveland Clinic notes, there''s no single universal "normal" anion gap [S1]
2. **Lab-specific ranges**: Always check the reference range provided by your specific laboratory, as it may vary based on their testing methodology
3. **Calculation method**: The range can differ depending on whether potassium is included in the calculation [S1]

### Clinical Context:
The anion gap represents the difference between measured cations (sodium, potassium) and measured anions (chloride, bicarbonate). A normal range helps identify metabolic acidosis when elevated or detect other electrolyte imbalances.

**Always verify with your local laboratory''s reference range**, as this can vary between institutions and testing methods.','[{"id":"call_b7462fcd191a48ea9ee6ae70","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"normal anion gap range medical guidelines","maxResults":3}},{"id":"call_7ebcc89ecd174d53b28421b6","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"anion gap normal range current guidelines 2024","maxResults":3}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_b7462fcd191a48ea9ee6ae70","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Anion Gap Blood Test: What It Is, Purpose, Risks & Results\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diagnostics/22041-anion-gap-blood-test\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Oct 28, 2024 — There''s no universal “normal” anion gap . Once you get your blood test results back, there will be information that indicates what that lab''s  ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"my.clevelandclinic.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"Oct 28, 2024\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Anion Gap: Reference Range, Interpretation, Collection\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/2087291-overview\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Nov 19, 2025 — The reference range for the anion gap in blood is as follows [1] : 16 ± 4 mEq/L … healthy individuals from 41 mEq/day to > 70 mEq/day.\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Nov 19, 2025\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Update on value of the anion gap in clinical diagnosis and\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11369334\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by PH Lolekha · 2001 · Cited by 65 — At present, the reference range of anion gap has been lowered from 8-16 to 3-11 mmol/l because of the changes in technique for measuring electrolyte. However, ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] Anion Gap Blood Test: What It Is, Purpose, Risks & Results (Oct 28, 2024)\\\\n    https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diagnostics/22041-anion-gap-blood-test (my.clevelandclinic.org)\\\\n    Oct 28, 2024 — There''s no universal “normal” anion gap . Once you get your blood test results back, there will be information that indicates what that lab''s  ...\\\\n[S2] Anion Gap: Reference Range, Interpretation, Collection (Nov 19, 2025)\\\\n    https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/2087291-overview (emedicine.medscape.com)\\\\n    Nov 19, 2025 — The reference range for the anion gap in blood is as follows [1] : 16 ± 4 mEq/L … healthy individuals from 41 mEq/day to > 70 mEq/day.\\\\n[S3] Update on value of the anion gap in clinical diagnosis and\\\\n    https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11369334 (pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov)\\\\n    by PH Lolekha · 2001 · Cited by 65 — At present, the reference range of anion gap has been lowered from 8-16 to 3-11 mmol/l because of the changes in technique for measuring electrolyte. However, ... Read more\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically. Results are re-ranked for recency (freshest first): when a source carries a date, mention the year the moment you cite it (e.g. \\\\\\"per the 2024 ESC guideline [S1]\\\\\\"), and prefer the most recent guidance when sources conflict — flag older sources as superseded where relevant.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_7ebcc89ecd174d53b28421b6","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Anion Gap: Reference Range, Interpretation, Collection\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/2087291-overview\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Nov 19, 2025 — The reference range for the anion gap in blood is as follows : 16 ± 4 mEq/L (if the calculation employs potassium). Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Nov 19, 2025\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Anion Gap (AGAP) Blood Test: Low, High, and What\\",\\"url\\"