INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm9yjt5000pdird3vi8zo7b','1c14d39a-d411-4cfd-a212-69e77b120c7e','assistant','Based on the medical reference lookup from the Test Reference Server, here''s the current information on the normal anion gap:

## Normal Anion Gap Range

**Current normal range: 3-11 mEq/L**

**Historical range: 8-12 mEq/L** (older references)

### Key Points:
- The reference server indicates that the modern understanding has lowered the normal range to 3-11 mEq/L
- Older references typically cited 8-12 mEq/L as the normal range
- An elevated anion gap suggests metabolic acidosis caused by unmeasured anions (MUDPILES mnemonic)

### Clinical Significance:
The anion gap calculation helps identify the presence of unmeasured anions in the blood, which is crucial for diagnosing various types of metabolic acidosis. The shift to the lower range (3-11 mEq/L) reflects improvements in laboratory measurement techniques and updated clinical understanding.

Always remember to verify with your specific laboratory''s reference range, as individual institutions may have slightly different normal values based on their testing methodologies.','[{"id":"call_4bdec1c278534548965eaae5","name":"mcp__test-reference-server__medical-reference-lookup","args":{"topic":"anion gap"}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_4bdec1c278534548965eaae5","toolName":"mcp__test-reference-server__medical-reference-lookup","content":"Normal anion gap is 3-11 mEq/L (8-12 by older references). Elevated gap suggests metabolic acidosis from unmeasured anions (MUDPILES). [test-mcp-server]","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T10:47:26.921Z');
