These questions are now available in your MedQ question bank and will appear in your review queue. You can access them through the Generate tab or when reviewing your saved questions.','[{"id":"call_2e1b924bc64f4bbd851de544","name":"run_question_generation_agent","args":{"topic":"renal physiology","system":"Renal","types":["mcq"],"difficulties":["Easy"],"count":2,"focusHint":"basic renal physiology concepts","useKnowledgeBase":true}},{"id":"call_2878e4c980524a0990e6d6a9","name":"task","args":{"description":"Generate exactly 2 easy MCQ questions on basic renal physiology concepts. The questions should cover fundamental renal physiology topics like glomerular filtration, tubular reabsorption, urine concentration, or basic renal function. Each question should have 4-5 answer options with one correct answer, include a clear explanation, and be suitable for medical students learning basic renal concepts. Return the questions in a JSON array format with all required fields for the save_question function.","subagent_type":"question-author"}},{"id":"search_medical_references-1-1792839177560","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"glomerular filtration rate basic renal physiology medical student","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-2-1792839181214","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"tubular reabsorption kidney function basic physiology","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-3-1792839184050","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"urine concentration loop of Henle medullary osmotic gradient","maxResults":3}},{"id":"call_a2b5a07336c14c598715d6e0","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"What is the primary function of the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in kidney physiology?","options":["To measure the rate of urine production","To represent the flow of plasma from the glomerulus into Bowman space","To regulate blood pressure through renin secretion","To reabsorb filtered glucose and amino acids","To concentrate urine through countercurrent multiplication"],"answer":"To represent the flow of plasma from the glomerulus into Bowman space","explanation":"The glomerular filtration rate (GFR) represents the flow of plasma from the glomerulus into Bowman space during a specified period [S2]. It is a key measure of kidney function that indicates how well the kidneys are filtering blood. The other options describe different renal functions: urine production is the result of filtration and reabsorption processes, renin secretion regulates blood pressure, tubular reabsorption handles glucose and amino acids, and countercurrent concentration occurs in the loop of Henle.","mnemonic":"GFR = Glomerular Filtration Rate - think ''Filtration'' not ''Function''","clinicalPearl":"Normal GFR is approximately 120-130 mL/min/1.73m² in young adults; a decline in GFR is an early indicator of kidney dysfunction.","distractorAnalysis":"Option A confuses GFR with urine output; Option C describes juxtaglomerular cell function; Option D refers to proximal tubular reabsorption; Option E describes loop of Henle function.","difficulty":"easy","system":"renal","topic":"glomerular filtration","tags":["GFR","glomerulus","Bowman''s capsule","kidney function"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoj0070bjrmk07ekt96"}]}},{"id":"call_9716767c13b94fe3b713cfd1","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"Which structure is primarily responsible for generating the osmotic gradient that enables urine concentration?","options":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Distal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Collecting duct","Glomerulus"],"answer":"Loop of Henle","explanation":"The loop of Henle generates the osmotic gradient within the renal medulla through countercurrent multiplication [S1]. This gradient enables the kidney to concentrate urine and conserve water. The descending limb is permeable to water but not solutes, while the ascending limb actively transports solutes out, creating the medullary osmotic gradient necessary for urine concentration.","mnemonic":"Loop of Henle = ''Loop'' for creating the osmotic ''Loop'' needed for concentration","clinicalPearl":"Damage to the loop of Henle (e.g., by loop diuretics like furosemide) impairs the kidney''s ability to concentrate urine, leading to dilute urine production.","distractorAnalysis":"Option A reabsors ~65% of filtered sodium and nearly all glucose; Option B fine-tunes electrolyte balance; Option D uses the gradient but doesn''t create it; Option E performs filtration.","difficulty":"easy","system":"renal","topic":"urine concentration","tags":["loop of Henle","countercurrent multiplication","osmotic gradient","urine concentration"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoj0070bjrmk07ekt96"}]}}]','[{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-1-1792839177560","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR) and GFR Tests\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/estimated-glomerular-filtration-rate-egfr-and-gfr-tests\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Sep 30, 2026 — An eGFR between 15 and 60 means that you may have kidney disease ; An eGFR below 15 means that you may have kidney failure. If your eGFR is lower ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"medlineplus.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"Sep 30, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Physiology, Glomerular Filtration Rate - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500032\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by DP Kaufman · 2023 · Cited by 138 — Glomerul","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-2-1792839181214","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Understanding Renal Tubular Function: Key Mechanisms\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12285995\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by M Alamilla-Sanchez · 2025 · Cited by 38 — the tubular system carries out active mechanisms of secretion and reabsorption of solutes and proteins using specific transporters in the epithelial cells.\\",\\"host\\":\\"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Tubular reabsorption and secretion: Function: Video & Qbank\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.osmosis.org/learn/Tubular_reabsorption_and_secretion\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Tubular reabsorption and secretion are important processe","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-3-1792839184050","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Loop of Henle - Counter Current Multiplication\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://teachmephysiology.com/urinary-system/nephron/loop-henle\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Aug 24, 2026 — Loop of Henle generates the osmotic gradient within the renal medulla . This gradient enables the kidney to concentrate urine and conserve water.\\",\\"host\\":\\"teachmephysiology.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Aug 24, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Urine-Concentrating Mechanism in the Inner Medulla - PMC\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4186519\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by WH Dantzler · 2013 · Cited by 147 — This urine -concentrating process involves generation of","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_2878e4c980524a0990e6d6a9","toolName":"task","content":"```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\"type\\": \\"mcq\\",\\n    \\"text\\": \\"What is the primary function of the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in kidney physiology?\\",\\n    \\"options\\": [\\n      \\"To measure the rate of urine production\\",\\n      \\"To represent the flow of plasma from the glomerulus into Bowman space\\",\\n      \\"To regulate blood pressure through renin secretion\\",\\n      \\"To reabsorb filtered glucose and amino acids\\",\\n      \\"To concentrate urine through countercurrent multiplication\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\"answer\\": \\"To represent the flow of plasma from the glomerulus into Bowman space\\",\\n    \\"explanation\\": \\"The glomerular filtra","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_a2b5a07336c14c598715d6e0","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvma6cox0015dirdfm0a6hhj\\",\\"index\\":1,\\"message\\":\\"Question 1 saved (id cmvma6cox0015dirdfm0a6hhj). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_9716767c13b94fe3b713cfd1","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvma6p2f0017dirdp3xwg9rp\\",\\"index\\":2,\\"message\\":\\"Question 2 saved (id cmvma6p2f0017dirdp3xwg9rp). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_2e1b924bc64f4bbd851de544","toolName":"run_question_generation_agent","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"savedCount\\":2,\\"requestedCount\\":2,\\"questions\\":[{\\"index\\":1,\\"id\\":\\"cmvma6cox0015dirdfm0a6hhj\\",\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"system\\":\\"renal\\",\\"topic\\":\\"glomerular filtration\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"preview\\":\\"What is the primary function of the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in kidney physiology?\\"},{\\"index\\":2,\\"id\\":\\"cmvma6p2f0017dirdp3xwg9rp\\",\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"system\\":\\"renal\\",\\"topic\\":\\"urine concentration\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"preview\\":\\"Which structure is primarily responsible for generating the osmotic gradient that enables urine concentration?\\"}],\\"note\\":\\"Summarize what was saved (count + topics + difficulty) and remind the user the new questions are pending review in the question bank.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T10:53:49.832Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmca85e000272rdd3fh8x8n','c2a960a3-2870-44b9-b7b9-f6597d369fc8','user','A patient weighs 80 kg. Amiodarone loading dose is 5 mg/kg IV, given in 100 mL of D5W over 30 minutes. Compute the total dose in mg and the infusion rate in mL/hr exactly using your code interpreter.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-10-24T11:52:30.909Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmdkpis001ke3rdjrl3pyv0','7b60b22c-efc1-4cf3-8dc1-35772dcf44b1','assistant','I''ll generate 2 multiple-choice questions on pediatric asthma treatment and save them to your question bank.

Perfect! I''ve successfully generated 2 multiple-choice questions on pediatric asthma treatment and saved them to your question bank.

## Summary:
- **Count**: 2 questions generated
- **Topics**: Pediatric asthma treatment guidelines
- **Difficulty**: Medium
- **Status**: Questions are now pending review in your question bank

The questions cover key aspects of pediatric asthma management, including appropriate treatment escalation and emergency department management. You can find them in your question bank for review and practice.','[{"id":"call_-7142161624017659819","name":"run_question_generation_agent","args":{"topic":"pediatric asthma treatment","system":"General","types":["mcq"],"difficulties":["Medium"],"count":2,"focusHint":"pediatric asthma treatment guidelines","useKnowledgeBase":true}},{"id":"call_-7142177223338877639","name":"task","args":{"description":"Generate exactly 2 medium-difficulty MCQ questions on pediatric asthma treatment. The questions should be based on pediatric asthma treatment guidelines and be suitable for a general medical system. Each question must have 4-5 answer options with one correct answer, and include explanations, clinical pearls, distractor analysis, and appropriate tags. Focus on evidence-based treatment approaches, medication choices, and management strategies for pediatric asthma.","subagent_type":"question-author"}},{"id":"search_medical_references-1-1792844856155","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"pediatric asthma treatment guidelines GINA 2024","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-2-1792844859037","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"pediatric asthma controller medications step therapy guidelines","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-3-1792844862248","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"pediatric asthma rescue medication guidelines SABA vs ICS-formoterol","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-4-1792844865251","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"pediatric asthma exacerbation management emergency department treatment","maxResults":3}},{"id":"call_-7142158325482779221","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 7-year-old child with persistent asthma has been using a short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) as needed for symptom control. Despite this, the child experiences nighttime awakenings 2-3 times per month and uses SABA more than twice per week. According to current pediatric asthma treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate next step in management?","options":["Add montelukast as a controller medication","Switch to low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) plus SABA as needed","Initiate low-dose ICS-formoterol combination used daily and as needed","Increase frequency of SABA use to prevent symptoms","Add a long-acting beta-agonist (LABA) to current SABA regimen"],"answer":"Initiate low-dose ICS-formoterol combination used daily and as needed","explanation":"According to current guidelines (GINA 2024), for children with persistent asthma who are using SABA as needed but still experiencing poor control (nighttime awakenings and frequent SABA use), the preferred approach is to initiate low-dose ICS-formoterol combination used both daily and as needed [S1]. This approach has been shown to be more effective than traditional step-up therapy with ICS alone, as it provides both controller and reliever therapy in a single medication [S2].","mnemonic":"ICS-formoterol = Controller + Reliever in one","clinicalPearl":"ICS-formoterol combination therapy provides both controller and reliever medication in a single inhaler, improving adherence and reducing exacerbations compared to traditional step-up approaches.","distractorAnalysis":"Option A (montelukast) is not first-line controller therapy for persistent asthma. Option B (ICS + SABA as needed) is traditional step-up therapy but less preferred than ICS-formoterol combination. Option D (increasing SABA) is incorrect as it doesn''t address underlying inflammation. Option E (LABA added to SABA) is incorrect as LABA should never be used without ICS due to safety concerns.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"General","topic":"pediatric asthma treatment","tags":["asthma","pediatrics","controller medications","GINA guidelines"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwom00hfbjrmg3a7pn7k"},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoj006vbjrms3c6iffq"}]}},{"id":"call_-7142161486578705418","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 10-year-old child presents to the emergency department with a severe asthma exacerbation. The child is in respiratory distress with a respiratory rate of 35 breaths/min, accessory muscle use, and wheezing. Initial treatment includes oxygen, albuterol nebulizations, and ipratropium bromide. After 3 treatments, the child''s symptoms have improved but are not resolved. What is the most appropriate next intervention?","options":["Continue with additional albuterol nebulizations only","Add oral prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day","Administer intravenous magnesium sulfate","Initiate continuous albuterol nebulization","Add inhaled fluticasone to current regimen"],"answer":"Add oral prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day","explanation":"For children with moderate to severe asthma exacerbations not responding to initial bronchodilator therapy, systemic corticosteroids are the standard of care [S1]. Oral prednisone at 1-2 mg/kg/day (maximum 40-60 mg/day) for 5-7 days is recommended and has been shown to reduce hospitalization rates and improve outcomes [S3]. While magnesium sulfate may be considered for severe exacerbations, it''s typically reserved for cases not responding to initial bronchodilator therapy, and continuous albuterol is generally reserved for more severe cases.","mnemonic":"Severe exacerbation not improving? Add steroids!","clinicalPearl":"Systemic corticosteroids should be administered early in moderate to severe asthma exacerbations, not as a last resort, to prevent progression and reduce hospitalization risk.","distractorAnalysis":"Option A (continuing albuterol only) is insufficient as it doesn''t address the underlying inflammation. Option C (magnesium sulfate) may be considered in severe cases but is not first-line after initial bronchodilator therapy. Option D (continuous albuterol) is typically reserved for more severe exacerbations. Option E (inhaled fluticasone) is incorrect as systemic corticosteroids are preferred for acute exacerbations.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"General","topic":"pediatric asthma treatment","tags":["asthma exacerbation","emergency department","corticosteroids","pediatric emergency medicine"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoj006vbjrms3c6iffq"},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwom00hfbjrmg3a7pn7k"}]}}]','[{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-1-1792844856155","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"GINA 2024 Stategy Report\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://ginasthma.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/GINA-2024-Strategy-Report-24_05_22_WMS.pdf\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"May 22, 2024 — Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2024 . Adapting and implementing asthma clinical practice guidelines. There are two ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"ginasthma.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"May 22, 2024\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"GINA-2025-Update-25_11_08-WMS.pdf\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://ginasthma.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/GINA-2025-Update-25_11_08-WMS.pdf\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Nov 15, 2025 — asthma management guidelines .140. There are two approaches: subcutaneous i","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-2-1792844859037","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Pediatric Asthma Treatment & Management\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/1000997-treatment\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"May 5, 2026 — Step -down therapy is essential to identify the minimum medication necessary to maintain control . When asthma is well controlled, it is ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"May 5, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Consensus Guidelines for Inpatient Management of Asthma\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://medconnection.ucsfbenioffchildrens.org/asthma-guidelines\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Feb 21, 2023 — Treatment · Inhaled corticosteroids are “ controller ” medications meant to","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-3-1792844862248","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Pediatric Asthma Treatment & Management\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/1000997-treatment\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"May 5, 2026 — Step 3 (persistent) - Low-dose ICS - formoterol daily and as needed (preferred); alternatively, (1) medium-dose ICS daily, with SABA as needed, or ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"May 5, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"The Role of ICS-containing Rescue Therapy versus SABA\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10999356\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by JG Krings · 2024 · Cited by 36 — ICS-albuterol and ICS-formoterol, with evidence that both app","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-4-1792844865251","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Acute asthma exacerbations in children younger than 12\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.uptodate.com/contents/acute-asthma-exacerbations-in-children-younger-than-12-years-emergency-department-management\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Aug 4, 2026 — Thus, most children with moderate or severe asthma exacerbations should be managed in an emergency department ( Pediatric respiratory ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.uptodate.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Aug 4, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Consensus Guidelines for Inpatient Management of Asthma\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://medconnection.ucsfbenioffchildrens.org/asthma-guidelines\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Feb 21, 2023 — Treatment","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142177223338877639","toolName":"task","content":"```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\"type\\": \\"mcq\\",\\n    \\"text\\": \\"A 7-year-old child with persistent asthma has been using a short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) as needed for symptom control. Despite this, the child experiences nighttime awakenings 2-3 times per month and uses SABA more than twice per week. According to current pediatric asthma treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate next step in management?\\",\\n    \\"options\\": [\\n      \\"Add montelukast as a controller medication\\",\\n      \\"Switch to low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) plus SABA as needed\\",\\n      \\"Initiate low-dose ICS-formoterol combination ","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142158325482779221","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvmdk923001he3rdgq1nl8v3\\",\\"index\\":1,\\"message\\":\\"Question 1 saved (id cmvmdk923001he3rdgq1nl8v3). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142161486578705418","toolName":"save_question","content":"{\\"saved\\":true,\\"id\\":\\"cmvmdkne4001je3rd4hksvtcw\\",\\"index\\":2,\\"message\\":\\"Question 2 saved (id cmvmdkne4001je3rd4hksvtcw). Continue with the next question or summarize when done.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142161624017659819","toolName":"run_question_generation_agent","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"savedCount\\":2,\\"requestedCount\\":2,\\"questions\\":[{\\"index\\":1,\\"id\\":\\"cmvmdk923001he3rdgq1nl8v3\\",\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"system\\":\\"General\\",\\"topic\\":\\"pediatric asthma treatment\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"preview\\":\\"A 7-year-old child with persistent asthma has been using a short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) as needed for symptom control. Despite this, the\\"},{\\"index\\":2,\\"id\\":\\"cmvmdkne4001je3rd4hksvtcw\\",\\"type\\":\\"mcq\\",\\"system\\":\\"General\\",\\"topic\\":\\"pediatric asthma treatment\\",\\"difficulty\\":\\"Medium\\",\\"preview\\":\\"A 10-year-old child presents to the emergency department with a severe asthma exacerbation. The child is in respiratory distress with a resp\\"}],\\"note\\":\\"Summarize what was saved (count + topics + difficulty) and remind the user the new questions are pending review in the question bank.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T12:28:39.604Z');
