```

The skill is ready to use and can generate professional medical infographics in the same style as your uploaded example. You can now create study aids, reference posters, and educational materials for any medical topic following the workflow outlined in the skill file.','[{"id":"call_-7142147673963887264","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"# Medical Infographic Generator Skill\\n\\n## Description\\nCreates professional medical education infographics with clean, clinical design based on high-yield medical content. Generates visually appealing study aids, reference posters, and educational materials with anatomical accuracy and clinical relevance.\\n\\n## When to Use\\n- Creating medical study aids and reference materials\\n- Generating educational infographics for medical topics\\n- Designing clinical comparison charts and study guides\\n- Creating high-yield content visualization for exam preparation\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Step 1: Define Topic and Content\\n1. Identify the medical topic and key learning objectives\\n2. Research essential clinical information using search_knowledge or search_medical_references\\n3. Organize content into logical sections with clear hierarchy\\n\\n### Step 2: Design Layout Structure\\n1. Create a grid-based layout with 2-4 main sections\\n2. Plan color scheme using medical-appropriate pastels (blues, greens, soft purples)\\n3. Include anatomical illustrations where relevant\\n4. Add visual elements like icons, arrows, and comparison tables\\n\\n### Step 3: Generate Content Sections\\n1. **Introduction**: Brief overview of the topic with clinical relevance\\n2. **Core Content**: High-yield facts organized by system/function\\n3. **Clinical Correlations**: Real-world applications and exam connections\\n4. **Summary**: Key takeaways and memory aids\\n\\n### Step 4: Create Visual Elements\\n1. Generate anatomical illustrations using generate_medical_illustration\\n2. Create comparison tables and charts\\n3. Add icons and visual indicators\\n4. Ensure consistent typography and spacing\\n\\n### Step 5: Final Assembly\\n1. Combine all elements into a cohesive infographic\\n2. Add footer with references and clinical pearls\\n3. Export as appropriate format (PDF, PNG, etc.)\\n\\n## Output Format\\n- Professional medical infographic with clean design\\n- Color-coded sections for easy navigation\\n- High-yield clinical content with educational value\\n- Anatomically accurate illustrations where applicable\\n- Clinical correlations and exam-focused information\\n\\n## Examples\\n- Essential electrolytes infographic with hypo/hyper comparisons\\n- Cardiac arrhythmias study aid with ECG patterns\\n- Diabetes mellitus reference poster with pathophysiology\\n- Pharmacology drug comparison charts\\n\\n## Best Practices\\n- Maintain medical accuracy in all content\\n- Use evidence-based information with proper citations\\n- Keep text concise and focused on high-yield points\\n- Ensure visual elements enhance learning, not distract\\n- Use consistent color scheme and typography throughout","file_path":"/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/SKILL.md"}},{"id":"call_-7142148601676823596","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"#!/bin/bash\\n\\n# Test script for medical-infographic-generator skill\\n# This script demonstrates how to use the skill to create medical infographics\\n\\necho \\"Testing Medical Infographic Generator Skill\\"\\necho \\"==========================================\\"\\n\\n# Test Case 1: Essential Electrolytes\\necho \\"Test 1: Essential Electrolytes Infographic\\"\\ncat > /tmp/electrolytes_content.md << ''EOF''\\n# Essential Electrolytes: Clinical Reference\\n\\n## Sodium (Na+)\\n- **Normal Range**: 135-145 mEq/L\\n- **Function**: Osmotic balance, nerve conduction, muscle function\\n- **Hyponatremia**: <135 mEq/L - confusion, seizures, cerebral edema\\n- **Hypernatremia**: >145 mEq/L - thirst, agitation, neurological symptoms\\n\\n## Potassium (K+)\\n- **Normal Range**: 3.5-5.0 mEq/L\\n- **Function**: Cardiac muscle contraction, nerve impulses, cellular metabolism\\n- **Hypokalemia**: <3.5 mEq/L - muscle weakness, arrhythmias, ECG changes\\n- **Hyperkalemia**: >5.0 mEq/L - peaked T-waves, bradycardia, cardiac arrest\\n\\n## Calcium (Ca2+)\\n- **Normal Range**: 8.5-10.5 mg/dL\\n- **Function**: Bone health, muscle contraction, coagulation, nerve function\\n- **Hypocalcemia**: <8.5 mg/dL - tetany, Chvostek''s sign, prolonged QT\\n- **Hypercalcemia**: >10.5 mg/dL - confusion, constipation, renal stones\\n\\n## Magnesium (Mg2+)\\n- **Normal Range**: 1.5-2.5 mEq/L\\n- **Function**: Enzyme cofactor, neuromuscular function, cardiac stability\\n- **Hypomagnesemia**: <1.5 mEq/L - arrhythmias, seizures, hypokalemia\\n- **Hypermagnesemia**: >2.5 mEq/L - weakness, hypotension, cardiac arrest\\n\\n## Clinical Correlations\\n- **ECG Changes**: Electrolyte imbalances directly affect cardiac conduction\\n- **Renal Impact**: Kidneys maintain electrolyte balance through filtration and reabsorption\\n- **Emergency Management**: IV replacement protocols based on severity and symptoms\\n\\n## Exam Bytes\\n- \\"Na+ affects water balance, K+ affects heart rate, Ca2+ affects muscles\\"\\n- \\"Think of electrolytes as the body''s electrical system\\"\\n- \\"Renal compensation is key in chronic electrolyte disorders\\"\\nEOF\\n\\n# Test Case 2: Cardiac Arrhythmias\\necho \\"Test 2: Cardiac Arrhythmias Study Aid\\"\\ncat > /tmp/arrhythmias_content.md << ''EOF''\\n# Common Cardiac Arrhythmias: Quick Reference\\n\\n## Sinus Rhythm\\n- **Rate**: 60-100 bpm\\n- **Rhythm**: Regular P waves, PR interval 0.12-0.20s\\n- **Clinical**: Normal sinus rhythm, baseline for comparison\\n\\n## Atrial Fibrillation (AFib)\\n- **Rate**: Usually 100-180 bpm\\n- **Rhythm**: Irregularly irregular, no P waves, fibrillatory waves\\n- **ECG**: Chaotic baseline, variable R-R intervals\\n- **Treatment**: Rate control, anticoagulation, cardioversion\\n\\n## Atrial Flutter\\n- **Rate**: Usually 150-300 bpm (typically 150:1 AV block)\\n- **Rhythm**: Regular sawtooth flutter waves\\n- **ECG**: \\"Sawtooth\\" pattern in inferior leads\\n- **Treatment**: Rate control, cardioversion, ablation\\n\\n## Ventricular Tachycardia (VT)\\n- **Rate**: >100 bpm\\n- **Rhythm**: Regular wide QRS complexes\\n- **ECG**: Wide QRS (>120ms), AV dissociation\\n- **Treatment**: Immediate cardioversion if unstable, amiodarone\\n\\n## Ventricular Fibrillation (VFib)\\n- **Rate**: Chaotic, 300-500 bpm\\n- **Rhythm**: Irregular, chaotic baseline\\n- **ECG**: No identifiable complexes, coarse/fine VF\\n- **Treatment**: Immediate CPR and defibrillation\\n\\n## Bradyarrhythmias\\n- **Sinus Bradycardia**: Rate <60 bpm, regular rhythm\\n- **Heart Block**: PR prolongation, dropped beats, complete AV block\\n- **Treatment**: Atropine, pacing, pacemaker for severe cases\\n\\n## Clinical Pearls\\n- \\"Wide complex tachycardia = VT until proven otherwise\\"\\n- \\"Irregular rhythm with no P waves = likely AFib\\"\\n- \\"Pulse check: VFib has no pulse, VT may or may not have pulse\\"\\nEOF\\n\\n# Test Case 3: Diabetes Mellitus\\necho \\"Test 3: Diabetes Mellitus Reference\\"\\ncat > /tmp/diabetes_content.md << ''EOF''\\n# Diabetes Mellitus: Clinical Overview\\n\\n## Type 1 Diabetes\\n- **Pathophysiology**: Autoimmune destruction of beta cells\\n- **Onset**: Usually acute, often in childhood/adolescence\\n- **Treatment**: Insulin therapy required\\n- **Ketosis**: Prone to DKA due to absolute insulin deficiency\\n\\n## Type 2 Diabetes\\n- **Pathophysiology**: Insulin resistance + relative insulin deficiency\\n- **Onset**: Gradual, usually adult onset\\n- **Treatment**: Lifestyle, oral agents, may require insulin\\n- **Ketosis**: Rare, but can occur in severe stress\\n\\n## Gestational Diabetes\\n- **Timing**: First recognized during pregnancy\\n- **Risk**: Increased maternal/fetal complications\\n- **Treatment**: Diet, exercise, may require insulin\\n- **Follow-up**: High risk of developing type 2 diabetes later\\n\\n## Diagnostic Criteria\\n- **Fasting Plasma Glucose**: ≥126 mg/dL\\n- **2-hour OGTT**: ≥200 mg/dL\\n- **HbA1c**: ≥6.5%\\n- **Random**: ≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms\\n\\n## Complications\\n- **Microvascular**: Retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy\\n- **Macrovascular**: CAD, stroke, PVD\\n- **Metabolic**: DKA, HHS, hypoglycemia\\n\\n## Management Goals\\n- **Glycemic Control**: HbA1c <7% (individualized)\\n- **Blood Pressure**: <130/80 mmHg\\n- **Lipids**: LDL <100 mg/dL\\n- **Lifestyle**: Diet, exercise, weight management\\n\\n## Emergency Conditions\\n- **DKA**: Hyperglycemia, ketonemia, metabolic acidosis\\n- **HHS**: Extreme hyperglycemia, severe dehydration, minimal ketosis\\nEOF\\n\\necho \\"Test content files created successfully!\\"\\necho \\"Files created:\\"\\necho \\"- /tmp/electrolytes_content.md\\"\\necho \\"- /tmp/arrhythmias_content.md\\"\\necho \\"- /tmp/diabetes_content.md\\"\\n\\necho \\"\\"\\necho \\"To use the medical-infographic-generator skill:\\"\\necho \\"1. Read the skill instructions: cat /workspace/medical-infographic-generator/SKILL.md\\"\\necho \\"2. Generate content for your topic following the workflow\\"\\necho \\"3. Use generate_medical_illustration for visual elements\\"\\necho \\"4. Combine content and visuals into final infographic\\"","file_path":"/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/test_script.sh"}},{"id":"call_-7142150903779294379","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"# Medical Infographic Generator\\n\\n## Overview\\nCreates professional medical education infographics with clean, clinical design based on high-yield medical content.\\n\\n## Usage\\n```bash\\n# Run the test script\\ncd /workspace/medical-infographic-generator\\nchmod +x test_script.sh\\n./test_script.sh\\n\\n# Create your own medical infographic\\n# 1. Define your topic and content\\n# 2. Follow the workflow in SKILL.md\\n# 3. Generate visual elements\\n# 4. Combine into final infographic\\n```\\n\\n## Example Topics\\n- Essential electrolytes with hypo/hyper comparisons\\n- Cardiac arrhythmias with ECG patterns\\n- Diabetes mellitus reference poster\\n- Pharmacology drug comparisons\\n- Anatomy study guides\\n- Pathophysiology flowcharts\\n\\n## Files Created\\n- `/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/SKILL.md` - Skill instructions\\n- `/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/test_script.sh` - Test script with examples\\n- `/tmp/electrolytes_content.md` - Electrolytes infographic content\\n- `/tmp/arrhythmias_content.md` - Cardiac arrhythmias content\\n- `/tmp/diabetes_content.md` - Diabet...[TRUNCATED]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7142147673963887264","toolName":"write_file","content":"Successfully wrote to ''/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/SKILL.md''","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142148601676823596","toolName":"write_file","content":"Successfully wrote to ''/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/test_script.sh''","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142150903779294379","toolName":"write_file","content":"Successfully wrote to ''/workspace/medical-infographic-generator/README.md''","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:24:48.252Z');
