The anemia infographic is now ready for use as a study aid, reference material, or educational resource!','[{"id":"call_-7142143138478421128","name":"read_file","args":{"file_path":"/skills/image/SKILL.md","limit":1000}},{"id":"call_-7142143207197899523","name":"search_knowledge","args":{"query":"anemia types causes diagnosis treatment management medical","top_k":10}},{"id":"call_-7142143138478420400","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"anemia classification types diagnosis treatment guidelines","maxResults":5}},{"id":"call_-7142148739115771464","name":"generate_medical_illustration","args":{"prompt":"Professional medical infographic layout for anemia classification and management. Grid-based design with 4 main sections: 1) Introduction to anemia with definition and prevalence, 2) Classification by MCV (microcytic, normocytic, macrocytic) with color-coded boxes, 3) Key diagnostic tests and workup approach, 4) Treatment strategies by type. Use soft medical color scheme (blues, greens, soft purples) with clear typography and anatomical elements like red blood cells. Include icons for different anemia types and flow arrows for diagnostic approach."}},{"id":"call_-7142147983201531373","name":"create_artifact","args":{"content":"# Anemia: Classification, Diagnosis & Management\\n\\n## Introduction\\n**Definition**: Anemia is defined as hemoglobin < 13.5 g/dL in men or < 12.0 g/dL in non-pregnant women [S5]\\n\\n**Prevalence**: Affects ~25% of the global population, with higher rates in women, elderly, and developing countries\\n\\n**Clinical Impact**: Reduced oxygen delivery leading to fatigue, weakness, shortness of breath, and reduced exercise tolerance\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Classification by MCV\\n\\n### 🩸 **MICROCYTIC ANEMIA** (MCV < 80 fL)\\n- **Iron Deficiency Anemia**\\n  - Most common cause worldwide\\n  - Low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC\\n  - Treatment: Iron supplementation + addressing underlying cause\\n\\n- **Thalassemia**\\n  - Genetic defect in hemoglobin synthesis\\n  - Target cells, basophilic stippling\\n  - Treatment: Folate, transfusions (severe cases)\\n\\n- **Anemia of Chronic Disease**\\n  - Normal/high ferritin, functional iron deficiency\\n  - Associated with chronic inflammation, infection, malignancy\\n  - Treatment: Treat underlying condition\\n\\n### 🔵 **NORMOCYTIC ANEMIA** (MCV 80-100 fL)\\n- **Anemia of Chronic Disease**\\n  - Mild anemia with normal MCV\\n  - Low reticulocyte count\\n  - Associated with chronic inflammation, renal failure, myelofibrosis\\n\\n- **Hemolytic Anemias**\\n  - Autoimmune hemolytic anemia\\n  - Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)\\n  - Hereditary spherocytosis\\n  - Treatment: Corticosteroids, immunosuppression, transfusions\\n\\n- **Bone Marrow Failure**\\n  - Aplastic anemia\\n  - Myelodysplastic syndromes\\n  - Treatment: Bone marrow transplant, immunosuppression\\n\\n### 🔴 **MACROCYTIC ANEMIA** (MCV > 100 fL)\\n- **Vitamin B12 Deficiency**\\n  - Neurologic symptoms (paresthesias, ataxia)\\n  - Methylmalonic acid elevation\\n  - Treatment: B12 injections, oral supplementation\\n\\n- **Folic Acid Deficiency**\\n  - Megaloblastic changes on peripheral smear\\n  - Increased homocysteine\\n  - Treatment: Folate supplementation\\n\\n- **Other Causes**\\n  - Alcohol abuse\\n  - Hypothyroidism\\n  - Drugs (hydroxyurea, AZT)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Diagnostic Workup\\n\\n### 📊 **Step 1: Initial Assessment**\\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC) with differential\\n- Reticulocyte count\\n- Peripheral smear examination\\n\\n### 🔬 **Step 2: Iron Studies**\\n- Serum iron, ferritin, TIBC\\n- Transferrin saturation\\n- Soluble transferrin receptor\\n\\n### 🧪 **Step 3: Additional Testing**\\n- Vitamin B12 and folate levels\\n- Reticulocyte production index\\n- LDH, haptoglobin (hemolysis workup)\\n- Bone marrow examination (if indicated)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Treatment Strategies\\n\\n### 🎯 **Treat Underlying Cause**\\n- **Iron deficiency**: Iron supplementation (oral/IV)\\n- **B12 deficiency**: B12 injections or high-dose oral\\n- **Folate deficiency**: Folate supplementation\\n- **Chronic disease**: Treat underlying condition\\n- **Hemolysis**: Address cause (autoimmune, mechanical, etc.)\\n\\n### 🩸 **Supportive Care**\\n- **Transfusion**: For severe symptomatic anemia (Hb < 7-8 g/dL)\\n- **Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents**: For CKD, chemotherapy\\n- **Corticosteroids**: For autoimmune hemolytic anemia\\n\\n### ⚠️ **Monitoring**\\n- Regular CBC monitoring\\n- Response to treatment assessment\\n- Complication prevention (iron overload, etc.)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clinical Pearls\\n\\n- **\\"Irregularly irregular\\" rhythm suggests cardiac involvement**\\n- **Chest pain in children warrants CBC to exclude anemia**\\n- **Leg cramps waking children up → CBC to rule out anemia**\\n- **Anemia of chronic disease: functional iron deficiency**\\n- **Neurologic symptoms + anemia → suspect B12 deficiency**\\n\\n---\\n\\n*References: [S1] StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf, [S2] UpToDate, [S3] Medscape, [S4] Mayo Clinic, [S5] Hematology.org*","kind":"markdown","name":"anemia-infographic-content","title":"Anemia: Classification, Diagnosis & Management"}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7142143138478421128","toolName":"read_file","content":"","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142143207197899523","toolName":"search_knowledge","content":"{\\"ok\\":true,\\"context\\":\\"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\\\n\\\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 363 of 675)\\\\n• Hereditary spherocytosis\\\\n\\\\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia\\\\n\\\\n• Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)\\\\n\\\\n• Enzyme deficiency\\\\nLow reticulocyte count\\\\n\\\\n• Renal failure\\\\n\\\\n• Anemia of chronic disease\\\\n\\\\n• Myelofibrosis\\\\n\\\\n• Cancers (metastasis or leukemias)\\\\n\\\\nMACROCYTIC NORMOCHROMIC\\\\nMCV: raised — MCHC: normal\\\\n\\\\n• Vitamin B12 deficiency\\\\n\\\\n• Thiamine deficiency\\\\n\\\\n• Folic acid deficiency\\\\n\\\\n1.\\\\n\\\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 364 of 675)\\\\nS Clarifications Note — Emphasis Points on the General Symptoms General manifestations differ depending on whether the presentation is acute or chronic.\\\\nChest pain in a child is a reason on its own to request a CBC — anemia should always be on the differential for chest pain, whether or not it turns out to be the cause.\\\\nLeg cramps that wake a child up are worth a CBC as well, to exclude anemia before assuming it is a benign \\\\\\"growing pains\\\\\\" complaint.\\\\n3 Clarifications Note — Full Diagnostic Work-up for Anemia (39*) The doctor walked through the full diagnostic work-up for anemia, step by step — this level of detail is not spelled out on the slides:\\\\n\\\\n• CBC with differential — confirms anemia and its severity; MCV classifies the type.\\\\n\\\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 388 of 675)\\\\n2.6 Acquired Anemias: TEC & Anemia of Chronic/Renal Disease Transient Erythroblastopenia of Childhood (TEC)\\\\n★ The most common acquired red-cell aplasia, occurring in children between 6 months and 3 years. Etiology: transient immune suppression of erythropoiesis, often after a nonspecific viral infection (not parvovirus B19) Labs: decreased reticulocytes and bone-marrow precursors; normal MCV and HbF Recovery generally within 1–2 months; medication is not helpful; may need one transfusion if symptomatic 3 Clarifications Note — TEC vs.\\\\n\\\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\\\\\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\\\\\", part 532 of 675)\\\\nLecture Slide Notes normal hemostasis needs normal platelet function, not just a normal platelet number — this is exactly why the classification below splits into a \\\\\\"count\\\\\\" axis and a \\\\\\"function\\\\\\" axis.\\\\n\\\\n1.5 Classifying Primary Hemostatic Disorders\\\\nPrimary hemostatic disorders (the platelet side) split first by platelet count, then by mechanism. This exact roadmap is repeated across the lecture — eve\\n\\n[Full output (8,043 chars) saved as artifact \\"search_knowledge-output-1792875009880.txt\\" — download: /api/medq-agent/artifacts/cmvmvhnbu001hi1rdsfaf4w2g. Ask the user to open it from the Files tab for the complete content.]","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142143138478420400","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Anemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499994\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Jul 5, 2026 — Management of anemia is directed at the underlying cause. The following section provides an overview of general management principles and key ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\",\\"date\\":\\"Jul 5, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Diagnostic approach to anemia in adults\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.uptodate.com/contents/diagnostic-approach-to-anemia-in-adults\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Sep 23, 2026 — THE DIAGNOSIS Categories to. Acute blood loss Congenital anemia or a family history of anemia Premenopausal females Older adults Malabsorption/ ...\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.uptodate.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Sep 23, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Anemia Workup: Approach Considerations, Investigation\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/198475-workup\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Jun 2, 2025 — The first step in the diagnosis of anemia is detection with reliable, accurate tests so that important clues to underlying disease are not ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Jun 2, 2025\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Anemia - Diagnosis and treatment\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/anemia/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20351366\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"May 4, 2026 — Treatment for folic acid and vitamin B-12 deficiency. Anemia of chronic disease. Aplastic anemia . Hemolytic anemias . Sickle cell anemia .\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.mayoclinic.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"May 4, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"Anemia - Hematology.org\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.hematology.org/education/patients/anemia\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Anemia is diagnosed when a blood test shows a hemoglobin value of less than 13.5 g/dL for men or less than 12.0 g/dL for non-pregnant women. Normal values for ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.hematology.org\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"}],\\"formatted\\":\\"[S1] Anemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH (Jul 5, 2026)\\\\n    https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499994 (www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov)\\\\n    Jul 5, 2026 — Management of anemia is directed at the underlying cause. The following section provides an overview of general management principles and key ... Read more\\\\n[S2] Diagnostic approach to anemia in adults (Sep 23, 2026)\\\\n    https://www.uptodate.com/contents/diagnostic-approach-to-anemia-in-adults (www.uptodate.com)\\\\n    Sep 23, 2026 — THE DIAGNOSIS Categories to. Acute blood loss Congenital anemia or a family history of anemia Premenopausal females Older adults Malabsorption/ ...\\\\n[S3] Anemia Workup: Approach Considerations, Investigation (Jun 2, 2025)\\\\n    https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/198475-workup (emedicine.medscape.com)\\\\n    Jun 2, 2025 — The first step in the diagnosis of anemia is detection with reliable, accurate tests so that important clues to underlying disease are not ... Read more\\\\n[S4] Anemia - Diagnosis and treatment (May 4, 2026)\\\\n    https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/anemia/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20351366 (www.mayoclinic.org)\\\\n    May 4, 2026 — Treatment for folic acid and vitamin B-12 deficiency. Anemia of chronic disease. Aplastic anemia . Hemolytic anemias . Sickle cell anemia .\\\\n[S5] Anemia - Hematology.org\\\\n    https://www.hematology.org/education/patients/anemia (www.hematology.org)\\\\n    Anemia is diagnosed when a blood test shows a hemoglobin value of less than 13.5 g/dL for men or less than 12.0 g/dL for non-pregnant women. Normal values for ... Read more\\",\\"note\\":\\"Cite these inline as [S1], [S2]… markers placed right after the claim each source supports. NEVER paste raw URLs into the answer text — the interface renders [S#] markers as clickable source cards and lists every URL in a Web sources panel automatically. Results are re-ranked for recency (freshest first): when a source carries a date, mention the year the moment you cite it (e.g. \\\\\\"per the 2024 ESC guideline [S1]\\\\\\"), and prefer the most recent guidance when sources conflict — flag older sources as superseded where relevant.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142148739115771464","toolName":"generate_medical_illustration","content":"{\\"provider\\":\\"zai\\",\\"url\\":\\"/generated/agent-f93815a5-afd.jpg\\",\\"width\\":1344,\\"height\\":768,\\"note\\":\\"Provider \\\\\\"seedream\\\\\\" has no API key configured — used the built-in ZAI image provider instead.\\"}","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142147983201531373","toolName":"create_artifact","content":"{\\"created\\":true,\\"artifact\\":{\\"id\\":\\"cmvmvine5001li1rd3hv5q4u8\\",\\"name\\":\\"anemia-infographic-content\\",\\"kind\\":\\"markdown\\",\\"sizeBytes\\":3600,\\"downloadUrl\\":\\"/api/medq-agent/artifacts/cmvmvine5001li1rd3hv5q4u8\\"},\\"note\\":\\"Artifact saved. Mention it in your final answer with the download link.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:51:03.850Z');
