Let me ask a few questions to refine the skill requirements:
','[{"id":"call_-7142134720342515735","name":"analyze_visual_material","args":{"imageUrl":"attachment://file-1792893804861-4vjr","question":"Analyze this medical infographic image to understand its style, layout, color scheme, typography, content organization, and visual elements. Describe the key characteristics that make it effective for medical education."}},{"id":"call_-7142129016625951520","name":"read_file","args":{"file_path":"/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md","limit":1000}},{"id":"call_-7142129154064904470","name":"write_todos","args":{"todos":[{"content":"Capture intent for medical infographic generator skill","status":"in_progress"},{"content":"Interview user about skill requirements and edge cases","status":"pending"},{"content":"Write SKILL.md for medical infographic generator","status":"pending"},{"content":"Create test cases for skill evaluation","status":"pending"},{"content":"Run test cases and gather feedback","status":"pending"},{"content":"Iterate on skill based on feedback","status":"pending"},{"content":"Package final skill for user","status":"pending"}]}},{"id":"call_-7142128398150661735","name":"ask_user","args":{"question":"To create the perfect medical infographic generator skill, I need to understand your specific requirements. What types of medical topics would you most want this skill to handle? (e.g., electrolytes, medications, diseases, lab values, etc.)","inputs":[{"id":"topic_types","type":"multiselect","label":"Medical topic categories","options":[{"label":"Electrolytes & Lab Values","value":"electrolytes"},{"label":"Medications & Drug Classes","value":"medications"},{"label":"Diseases & Conditions","value":"diseases"},{"label":"Anatomy & Physiology","value":"anatomy"},{"label":"Procedures & Techniques","value":"procedures"},{"label":"Diagnostic Criteria","value":"diagnostic"},{"label":"Other (please specify)","value":"other"}],"required":true}]}}]','[{"toolCallId":"call_-7142134720342515735","toolName":"analyze_visual_material","content":"This medical infographic is a **high-yield study guide** designed for rapid clinical review, likely targeting medical students and healthcare professionals preparing for board exams (USMLE/PLAB). Here is a comprehensive analysis of its design characteristics:\\n\\n## 1. **Layout & Structural Organization**\\n- **Modular Grid System**: The content is organized into four distinct horizontal rows (one per electrolyte: K⁺, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺), creating a scannable \\"cheat sheet\\" format.\\n- **Tripartite Column Structure**: Each row follows a consistent pattern:\\n  - **Left**: Electrolyte identity with normal range and anatomical icon\\n  - **Center**: Hypo- (low) clinical manifestations\\n  - **Right**: Hyper- (high) clinical manifestations\\n- **Information Hierarchy**: Primary headers use bold sans-serif; secondary information uses bullet points for quick parsing.\\n\\n## 2. **Color Scheme & Visual Coding**\\n- **Color-Coded Categories**:\\n  - **Potassium (K⁺)**: Lavender/purple background\\n  - **Sodium (Na⁺)**: Light blue/cyan background\\n  - **Magnesium (Mg²⁺)**: Mint green background\\n  - **Calcium (Ca²⁺)**: Soft purple/lavender background\\n- **Functional Color Use**: \\n  - Pastel backgrounds reduce eye strain during long study sessions\\n  - High-contrast black text ensures readability\\n  - Color differentiation allows users to locate specific electrolytes instantly via peripheral vision\\n\\n## 3. **Typography**\\n- **Font Style**: Bold, rounded sans-serif (likely Montserrat or similar) for headers—conveying authority while remaining approachable.\\n- **Visual Weight**: \\n  - Electrolyte symbols (P⁺, So⁻, Mg, Ca) are oversized (approximately 48-60pt) to serve as visual anchors\\n  - Normal ranges in smaller but legible text (14-16pt)\\n  - Bullet points in compact size (12-14pt) for dense information packing\\n- **Abbreviation Strategy**: Uses \\"HYPO\\" and \\"HYPER\\" as column headers to save space while maintaining clarity.\\n\\n## 4. **Visual Elements & Iconography**\\n- **Anatomical Illustrations**: Each electrolyte features a relevant medical illustration:\\n  - **Potassium**: Heart with ECG tracing (emphasizing cardiac effects)\\n  - **Sodium**: Blood vessel with water droplets (osmotic/fluid balance)\\n  - **Magnesium**: Skeletal muscle with neuromuscular junction (neuromuscular excitability)\\n  - **Calcium**: Bone with nerve ending (structural/neural function)\\n- **Mnemonic Aids**: \\n  - Creative spelling (\\"P⁺\\" for Potassium, \\"So⁻\\" for Sodium) creates visual distinctiveness\\n  - Hand-drawn style annotations (thumbs up, circles, arrows) add personality and memory hooks\\n\\n## 5. **Content Organization & Educational Strategy**\\n- **High-Yield Filtering**: Includes only board-exam-critical symptoms (e.g., \\"Torsades risk\\" for hypomagnesemia, \\"Peaked T waves\\" for hyperkalemia).\\n- **Comparative Format**: Placing Hypo vs. Hyper side-by-side facilitates **contrastive learning**—essential for understanding inverse relationships (e.g., hypoCa = tetany vs. hyperCa = stones).\\n- **Clinical Correlations**: Links electrolytes to specific diagnostic findings:\\n  - ECG changes (U waves, peaked T, QT prolongation)\\n  - Physical exam signs (Chvostek, Trousseau)\\n  - Lab associations (Mg²⁺ low often with K⁺ low)\\n\\n## 6. **Supplementary Educational Features**\\n- **\\"Exam Byte\\" Box** (Top Right): High-testability facts (K⁺ = ECG favorite, Mg²⁺ low causes torsades).\\n- **\\"MCQ Trap\\" Box** (Bottom Right): Common pitfalls and distractors (hyponatremia causing brain swelling vs. hyperkalemia''s peaked T waves).\\n- **Call-to-Action**: Telegram link for PDF download suggests this is part of a digital study community.\\n\\n## **Key Characteristics Making It Effective for Medical Education:**\\n\\n1. **Cognitive Load Management**: The consistent row-by-row layout reduces cognitive processing time—users know exactly where to look for normal values vs. pathology.\\n\\n2. **Dual Coding Theory Application**: Combines verbal information (text) with visual representations (icons, color coding), enhancing retention through multiple neural pathways","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142129016625951520","toolName":"read_file","content":"","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142129154064904470","toolName":"write_todos","content":"Updated todo list to [{\\"content\\":\\"Capture intent for medical infographic generator skill\\",\\"status\\":\\"in_progress\\"},{\\"content\\":\\"Interview user about skill requirements and edge cases\\",\\"status\\":\\"pending\\"},{\\"content\\":\\"Write SKILL.md for medical infographic generator\\",\\"status\\":\\"pending\\"},{\\"content\\":\\"Create test cases for skill evaluation\\",\\"status\\":\\"pending\\"},{\\"content\\":\\"Run test cases and gather feedback\\",\\"status\\":\\"pending\\"},{\\"content\\":\\"Iterate on skill based on feedback\\",\\"status\\":\\"pending\\"},{\\"content\\":\\"Package final skill for user\\",\\"status\\":\\"pending\\"}]","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_-7142128398150661735","toolName":"ask_user","content":"{\\"displayed\\":true,\\"question\\":\\"To create the perfect medical infographic generator skill, I need to understand your specific requirements. What types of medical topics would you most want this skill to handle? (e.g., electrolytes, medications, diseases, lab values, etc.)\\",\\"fields\\":1,\\"guidance\\":\\"The interactive question form is now shown to the learner. END YOUR TURN NOW with no further tool calls — do not answer for them, do not re-ask in text. Their answers will arrive as the next user message; continue from those.\\"}","isError":false}]',NULL,'2026-10-25T02:04:23.994Z');
