INSERT INTO "AgentThreadMessage" ("id","threadId","role","content","toolCalls","toolResults","metadata","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvo413120013guwuvexkijkx','4af6691a-0a34-4de9-8b1f-5ba39560a598','assistant','I''ll create a clinical vignette example for you. Let me generate a realistic medical case with a clinical scenario, questions, and explanations.

## ✅ **Clinical Vignette Created Successfully!**

I''ve generated a clinical vignette on **Myocardial Infarction** and saved it to your question bank. The question is currently pending review.

Here''s a preview of the vignette:
- **Topic**: Myocardial Infarction (Cardiovascular system)
- **Difficulty**: Medium
- **Patient**: 62-year-old male with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and smoking history
- **Presentation**: Sudden onset symptoms in the emergency department

The complete clinical vignette has been added to your question bank and will be available for review and practice. You can access it through the "Questions" tab in your MedQ dashboard.

Would you like me to:
- Generate more clinical vignettes on different topics?
- Show you the complete question details?
- Create vignettes with specific characteristics (e.g., different difficulty levels, organ systems)?','[{"id":"call_f965988c9eb6468880ac6db3","name":"run_question_generation_agent","args":{"topic":"Myocardial Infarction","system":"Cardiovascular","types":["mcq"],"difficulties":["Medium"],"count":1,"focusHint":"Create a comprehensive clinical vignette with a realistic patient presentation, diagnostic findings, and management questions","useKnowledgeBase":false}},{"id":"call_db3ba76c5eb14b90b3110618","name":"task","args":{"description":"Create exactly 1 medium-difficulty MCQ question on Myocardial Infarction for the Cardiovascular system. The question must include a comprehensive clinical vignette with realistic patient presentation, diagnostic findings, and management questions. Format the output as a strict JSON array with one question object containing all required fields: type, text, options, answer, explanation, mnemonic, clinicalPearl, distractorAnalysis, difficulty, system, topic, tags, imageUrl, audioUrl, illustrationDescription, attachSourceMediaId, and citations.","subagent_type":"question-author"}},{"id":"search_medical_references-1-1792949760847","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"myocardial infarction clinical presentation diagnosis management guidelines","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-2-1792949764497","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"STEMI NSTEMI ECG changes cardiac troponin management protocols","maxResults":3}},{"id":"search_medical_references-3-1792949766954","name":"search_medical_references","args":{"query":"myocardial infarction ECG findings ST elevation T inversion pathophysiology","maxResults":3}},{"id":"call_3bd09be987cc499ca4ca7fc3","name":"save_question","args":{"type":"mcq","text":"A 62-year-old male with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and smoking presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of severe crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm and jaw. The pain began 2 hours ago while he was shoveling snow and is associated with diaphoresis, nausea, and shortness of breath. On examination, he appears anxious, his blood pressure is 155/95 mmHg, heart rate is 112 bpm, respiratory rate is 24 breaths/min, and oxygen saturation is 92% on room air. His ECG shows ST elevation of 4 mm in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Initial cardiac troponin I is 0.08 ng/mL (normal <0.04 ng/mL). Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management strategy for this patient?","options":["Start aspirin 325 mg orally, nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual every 5 minutes as needed, and arrange for urgent coronary angiography within 90 minutes","Administer morphine 4 mg IV for pain control, start heparin infusion, and schedule elective cardiac catheterization within 24 hours","Begin beta-blocker therapy, start statin, and arrange for stress testing the following day","Initiate fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase and transfer to a community hospital with monitoring capabilities","Provide oxygen therapy, administer sublingual nitroglycerin, and arrange for non-emergency cardiology consultation"],"answer":"Start aspirin 325 mg orally, nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual every 5 minutes as needed, and arrange for urgent coronary angiography within 90 minutes","explanation":"This patient presents with classic symptoms of an acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) including severe crushing chest pain, radiation, associated symptoms, and ECG findings of ST elevation in the anterior leads (V1-V4) with reciprocal changes. The elevated troponin I confirms myocardial injury. According to current guidelines [S1], the initial management of STEMI includes immediate aspirin administration, nitroglycerin for symptom relief, and urgent coronary angiography within 90 minutes (primary percutaneous coronary intervention, PCI) when available. This approach is superior to fibrinolytic therapy when timely PCI can be achieved and is preferred over delayed interventions or conservative management in STEMI [S2].","mnemonic":"STEMI Protocol: A.N.G.I.O. - Aspirin, Nitrates, Get to cath lab Immediately, Oxygen","clinicalPearl":"In STEMI, time is muscle - the ''door-to-balloon'' time should be <90 minutes for primary PCI. Reciprocal ST depression is a specific indicator of STEMI and helps localize the infarct to the anterior wall when seen in leads II, III, and aVF.","distractorAnalysis":"Option B is incorrect because morphine may be used but is not first-line, and elective catheterization is inappropriate for STEMI. Option C is wrong as beta-blockers are contraindicated in acute STEMI with heart failure or hypotension. Option D is incorrect because fibrinolytics are second-line when timely PCI is available. Option E is wrong because STEMI requires urgent intervention, not non-emergency consultation.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"cardiovascular","topic":"myocardial infarction","tags":["STEMI","acute coronary syndrome","ECG interpretation","emergency medicine","cardiology"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"ECG showing ST elevation in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF, representing anterior STEMI","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/155919-clinical","chunkId":"S1"},{"sourceId":"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001309","chunkId":"S2"},{"sourceId":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459269","chunkId":"S3"}]}}]','[{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-1-1792949760847","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Myocardial Infarction Clinical Presentation\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/155919-clinical\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Aug 10, 2026 — A high index of suspicion for MI should be maintained, especially when evaluating women, patients with diabetes, older patients, patients with ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"emedicine.medscape.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Aug 10, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001309\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by SV Rao · 2025 · Cited by 2621 — Contemporary diagnosis and management of patients with my","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-2-1792949764497","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"Acute coronary syndrome - Knowledge @ AMBOSS\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://www.amboss.com/us/knowledge/acute-coronary-syndrome\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Jun 15, 2026 — Unstable angina is a clinical diagnosis associated with normal troponin levels. Suggestive ECG changes can support the diagnosis but are not ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"www.amboss.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"Jun 15, 2026\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"NSTEMI vs STEMI; Diagnosis, and Treatment\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://baledoneen.com/blog/nstemi-vs-stemi\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"Oct 23, 2025 — Troponin elevation differentiates NSTEMI from unstable angina , where troponin levels remain normal. Wha","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"search_medical_references-3-1792949766954","toolName":"search_medical_references","content":"{\\"results\\":[{\\"title\\":\\"The ST Segment - ECG Library Basics\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://litfl.com/st-segment-ecg-library\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"There is ST elevation with deep Q waves and inverted T waves in V1-3. This pattern suggests the presence of a left ventricular aneurysm due to a prior ... Read more\\",\\"host\\":\\"litfl.com\\",\\"date\\":\\"\\"},{\\"title\\":\\"The association between T wave inversion in leads with ST\\",\\"url\\":\\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7802195\\",\\"snippet\\":\\"by A Ranjbar · 2021 · Cited by 12 — concomitant inverted T wave in leads with ST elevation could be a proper marker of spontaneous reperfusion of in","isError":false},{"toolCallId":"call_db3ba76c5eb14b90b3110618","toolName":"