INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm0hye5000xwnrd4uya3czc','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo',NULL,'save_question','completed',8604,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by straining during defecation, lumpy or hard stools, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation for the past 6 months. She reports no rectal bleeding, weight loss, or family history of colorectal cancer. Which of the following pathophysiological mechanisms is most likely responsible for her constipation?","options":["Colonic obstruction from a proximal colon mass","Decreased colonic motility due to chronic opioid use","Functional constipation with normal colonic transit time","Pelvic floor dyssynergia with impaired defecation","Hypothyroidism-induced decreased gastrointestinal motility"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The patient''s symptoms (straining during defecation, lumpy/hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation) for 6 months without red flags (bleeding, weight loss, family history) are classic for functional constipation. According to Rome IV criteria [S1], functional constipation requires at least two of: straining during ≥25% of defecations, lumpy/hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation, or need for manual maneuvers. Functional constipation typically has normal colonic transit time, distinguishing it from slow transit constipation [S3]. The absence of opioid use, pelvic floor dysfunction symptoms, or systemic symptoms makes other options less likely.","mnemonic":"ROME criteria: Straining, Lumpy stools, Incomplete evacuation, Manual maneuvers needed","clinicalPearl":"Functional constipation is the most common type of chronic constipation and typically responds well to lifestyle modifications and osmotic laxatives rather than invasive testing.","distractorAnalysis":"A is incorrect as colonic obstruction would present with more acute/severe symptoms and likely abdominal distension. B is incorrect as there''s no mention of opioid use. D is incorrect as pelvic floor dyssynergia typically involves paradoxical anal contraction during defecation, not just straining. E is incorrect as hypothyroidism would typically present with other systemic symptoms like fatigue, weight gain, or cold intolerance.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"gastrointestinal","topic":"constipation pathophysiology","tags":["functional constipation","Rome criteria","colonic motility"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004xi8rml3uq311z"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0059i8rmv0gfj0f7"},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b004zi8rmd11271yd"}]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvm0i50z000ywnrdf85syx40","index":1,"message":"Question 1 saved (id cmvm0i50z000ywnrdf85syx40). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T06:22:36.125Z');
