sourceUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/1df6e1f2bac0.jpg","cached":true},{"index":5,"imageUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/d3d7969dfe57.jpg","caption":"Medical illustration of heart defects labeled \\"Patent ductus arteriosus\\" and \\"Atrial septal defect,\\" with \\"Normal\\" comparison; contains \\"© AMBOSS\\" watermark.","source":"AMBOSS","dimensions":"1006pxx1000px","cached":false}],"note":"Show the most relevant images inline with their sources. Each imageUrl is a LOCAL copy that always loads — embed it directly as ![caption](imageUrl) and name the original source site next to it. sourceUrl (when present) is the original remote image for attribution; only embed sourceUrl if imageUrl somehow fails."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T08:14:47.682Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm4mb8g000fhqrdtd11q7ga','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596','e72adbd5-6bed-4187-86e5-6926ab87dd70','save_question','completed',46719,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of severe tearing chest pain radiating to the back. His blood pressure is 210/110 mmHg in the right arm and 160/90 mmHg in the left arm. ECG shows no ST-segment elevation. Chest X-ray reveals a widened mediastinum. Which of the following statements regarding the initial diagnostic approach is most accurate?","options":["CT angiography should be performed first as it has the highest sensitivity and specificity for aortic dissection","Transesophageal echocardiography should be performed first as it can be done at the bedside and has high sensitivity","MRI should be performed first as it provides the most comprehensive evaluation without radiation","D-dimer testing should be performed first as a negative result effectively rules out aortic dissection","Transthoracic echocardiography should be performed first as it is widely available and non-invasive"],"answer":"CT angiography should be performed first as it has the highest sensitivity and specificity for aortic dissection","explanation":"CT angiography is the gold standard initial diagnostic test for suspected aortic dissection, with sensitivity of 93-100% and specificity of 87-100%. While transesophageal echocardiography has high sensitivity (98-99%) and can be performed at the bedside, CT angiography provides superior spatial resolution and is more widely available. MRI has excellent diagnostic accuracy but is less accessible and takes longer to perform. D-dimer testing has poor sensitivity in acute aortic dissection (only 31-68%) and cannot be used to rule out the diagnosis. Transthoracic echocardiography has lower sensitivity (59-85%) compared to other modalities. The blood pressure discrepancy between arms is a classic finding supporting aortic dissection, and CT angiography remains the preferred initial diagnostic test.","mnemonic":"CTA: Gold standard for dissection (high sensitivity/specificity)","clinicalPearl":"Blood pressure discrepancy between arms is a classic sign of aortic dissection and should prompt urgent imaging.","distractorAnalysis":"TEE: High sensitivity but less accessible than CTA; MRI: Excellent but less available; D-dimer: Poor sensitivity in acute dissection; TTE: Lower sensitivity than other modalities","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"aortic dissection diagnosis","tags":["diagnostic imaging","emergency medicine","cardiovascular"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"CT angiography image showing aortic dissection"}','{"approved":false,"reason":"The request was cancelled.","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T08:17:57.856Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm4nfpk000khqrd3tssyhd6','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596','e72adbd5-6bed-4187-86e5-6926ab87dd70','save_question','completed',5,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 72-year-old female with a history of hypertension presents with sudden onset of severe \\"tearing\\" interscapular pain and diaphoresis. Physical examination reveals blood pressure of 190/100 mmHg in the right arm and 150/95 mmHg in the left arm. Her chest X-ray shows a widened mediastinum. Which of the following findings would most strongly suggest Stanford type A aortic dissection rather than type B?","options":["Blood pressure difference between arms","Presence of pericardial effusion on echocardiography","Elevation of serum D-diter levels","Pulse deficit in the femoral arteries","Involvement of the ascending aorta on imaging"],"answer":"Involvement of the ascending aorta on imaging","explanation":"The key distinction between Stanford type A and type B aortic dissection is whether the ascending aorta is involved. Type A dissection involves the ascending aorta (and may extend to the aortic arch or descending aorta), while type B dissection is confined to the descending aorta distal to the left subclavian artery. Blood pressure differences, pulse deficits, and pericardial effusions can occur in both types. D-diter levels are elevated in both types and are not specific for distinguishing between them. The involvement of the ascending aorta is the definitive diagnostic criterion for type A dissection, which requires urgent surgical intervention, whereas type B dissection is typically managed medically unless complications arise.","mnemonic":"Type A = Ascending aorta involved (surgical emergency)","clinicalPearl":"Stanford type A dissection requires immediate surgical consultation, while type B is typically managed medically initially.","distractorAnalysis":"BP difference: Occurs in both types; Pericardial effusion: Can occur in both types; D-diter: Elevated in both types; Pulse deficit: Can occur in both types","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"aortic dissection diagnosis","tags":["Stanford classification","aortic dissection","emergency medicine"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"Diagram showing Stanford type A and type B aortic dissection"}','{"approved":false,"reason":"The request was cancelled.","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T08:18:50.312Z');
