INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm6o0vr001cfqrdxpw2suz1','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596','0393944e-1fd0-4325-bffc-420f132f5106','save_question','completed',300004,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 45-year-old male presents with fatigue, pallor, and shortness of breath on exertion. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 8.2 g/dL, MCV 72 fL, MCH 24 pg, RDW 18%. Peripheral smear shows microcytic hypochromic red blood cells. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient''s anemia?","options":["Iron deficiency anemia","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease","Hemolytic anemia"],"answer":"Iron deficiency anemia","explanation":"This patient presents with microcytic hypochromic anemia (low MCV, low MCH, high RDW), which is classic for iron deficiency anemia. The microcytic size (MCV < 80 fL) and hypochromic appearance (MCH < 27 pg) indicate impaired hemoglobin synthesis, most commonly due to iron deficiency. The high RDW reflects anisocytosis with both small and normal-sized red blood cells, typical in early iron deficiency. Vitamin B12 and folate deficiencies typically cause macrocytic anemia (high MCV), while anemia of chronic disease is usually normocytic or mildly microcytic with low RDW. Hemolytic anemias are typically normocytic.","mnemonic":"\\"Microcytic hypochromic = think iron deficiency first\\"","clinicalPearl":"Iron deficiency is the most common cause of anemia worldwide and should be suspected in any patient with microcytic hypochromic red blood cells.","distractorAnalysis":"Vitamin B12/folate: cause macrocytic anemia; Anemia of chronic disease: normocytic/mildly microcytic with low RDW; Hemolytic: normocytic with reticulocytosis","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Hematology","topic":"anemia","tags":["iron deficiency","microcytic","anemia"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"Peripheral smear showing microcytic hypochromic red blood cells"}','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T09:15:16.983Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmf5c8v000gyjrdr1kc1civ','cmusenqrl0000oir8zrgpm0p7','7e053ffc-11d9-4ffe-ac42-f8e97cb7fd5d','analyze_my_weaknesses','completed',13,'{"limit":10}','{"analyzed":true,"hasHistory":true,"overall":{"totalAttempts":40,"last30AttemptsAccuracy":0},"weakestTopics":[{"topic":"ACE inhibitors","attempts":4,"accuracy":0},{"topic":"Constipation","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"topic":"Hirschsprung Disease","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"topic":"Platelet Disorders","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"topic":"Rickets","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"topic":"Tracheo-esophageal Fistula","attempts":2,"accuracy":0}],"weakestSystems":[{"system":"General","attempts":4,"accuracy":0},{"system":"Gastroenterology","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"system":"Gastrointestinal","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"system":"Endocrinology","attempts":2,"accuracy":0},{"system":"Endocrine","attempts":2,"accuracy":0}],"note":"Grounded in real attempt data. Recommended follow-ups: (1) offer a micro-drill — run_question_generation_agent on the top 2-3 weakest topics; (2) offer a recurring weekly schedule_task on weak topics; (3) when building study plans, weight these areas heavier. Present the numbers to the learner transparently.","formatted":"Weakest topics:\\n- ACE inhibitors: 0/4 correct (0%)\\n- Constipation: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Hirschsprung Disease: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Platelet Disorders: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Rickets: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Tracheo-esophageal Fistula: 0/2 correct (0%)\\nWeakest systems:\\n- General: 0/4 correct (0%)\\n- Gastroenterology: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Gastrointestinal: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Endocrinology: 0/2 correct (0%)\\n- Endocrine: 0/2 correct (0%)"}',NULL,'2026-10-24T13:12:41.791Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmp6n4k000nm2rdd7wlvmta','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','c8601477-a3bd-448b-b00e-5dffad64fbc8','search_knowledge','completed',8393,'{"query":"ai-pdf-builder tool usage PDF generation","top_k":5}','{"ok":true,"context":"# SOURCE CONTEXT (retrieved from the user''s library)\\n\\n[K1] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — Management of caustic ingestion with early endoscopy and avoiding NSAIDs which can cause peptic ulceration.)\\nWhat to do when a caustic ingestion presents with severe inflammation: antiviral treatment for a presumed herpes diagnosis is not the answer. The correct approach is early endoscopy to see and document the ulcerations, because a badly burned esophagus that later strictures may eventually need a colonic transplant — using a segment of colon to replace the damaged part of the esophagus. NSAIDs: prolonged use can cause peptic ulceration and bleeding. Mallory-Weiss syndrome: a tear in the lower esophagus resulting from forceful, repeated vomiting.\\n\\n[K2] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 568 of 675)\\nS Clarifications Note\\nCure rates have risen sharply in recent years — most children today respond excellently to modern chemotherapy protocols, which is the single biggest change in this field over the last generation.\\nAML: arises from myeloid cells and behaves faster and more aggressively than ALL.\\nCML and JMML: both have much lower incidence in children than in adults; JMML in particular is a rare disease of very young children.\\n\\n1.4 Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) — Overview\\nALL was the first disseminated cancer ever shown to be curable.\\nPeak incidence: 2–3 years of age; more common in boys than girls.\\nEtiology is unknown in most cases, though several factors are predisposing.\\n\\n[K3] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 612 of 675)\\n. Further Information — Why the Lecture Flags \\"N-myc Oncogene\\" A neuroblastoma’s MYCN (N-myc) gene amplification status is one of the single most important prognostic markers in the whole disease. Amplified MYCN marks aggressive, poor-prognosis disease and pushes a tumor into a higher risk/treatment-intensity group REGARDLESS of stage — it is checked routinely at diagnosis specifically to guide how intensively the child needs to be treated.\\n\\n⚠ Spinal Cord Compression Emergency\\nNeuroblastoma can present as a true oncological emergency when a huge mass directly compresses the spinal cord, causing paralysis or nerve compression — this needs urgent chemotherapy, urgent radiation, or emergency decompressive surgery.\\n\\n[K4] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 343 of 675)\\nSafe Formula Preparation Checklist\\nWash hands thoroughly; sterilize bottles, nipples, and mixing equipment.\\nUse safe, clean water — boil tap water and let it cool; follow the manufacturer’s mixing temperature instructions.\\nAccurate measurement: use the scoop that comes with that specific tin — never a household teaspoon — to avoid dilution or over-concentration.\\nStorage: prepare fresh formula per feed where possible; discard any leftover formula after a feed.\\nHeating: never microwave (creates dangerous hot spots) — warm in a bowl of warm water.\\nFeeding technique: hold the infant close, support the head, keep the bottle tilted to minimize air intake.\\n\\n[K5] (Peditric-Med (1).pdf — From \\"Peditric-Med (1).pdf\\", part 578 of 675)\\n3 Clarifications Note\\nIf even a small fraction of blast cells appears on a peripheral smear (as low as 5%), a bone marrow aspirate must be done immediately\\n\\n— there is no \\"wait and repeat the CBC\\" step once blasts are visible.\\nWhy cytogenetics matters so much: it is the main tool for risk stratification. Favorable translocations / hyperdiploidy → Very Low Risk, meaning a very high cure rate with less-intensive chemo and usually no need for radiation or transplant. Unfavorable Aberrations / high-risk cytogenetics → the child will need urgent bone marrow transplantation as soon as complete remission is reached.\\n","citations":[{"ref":"K1","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","relevance":0},{"ref":"K2","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","',NULL,'2026-10-24T17:53:38.708Z');
