Reproductive System","weeks":0,"reason":"7% of the exam"},{"area":"Endocrine System","weeks":0,"reason":"8% of the exam"},{"area":"Multisystem Processes & Disorders","weeks":0,"reason":"8% of the exam"}],"note":"Approximate published content distribution (midpoints of official ranges). ALWAYS tell the learner to verify against the current official test plan (USMLE.org / NCSBN) before committing weeks. When you also have weakness data (analyze_my_weaknesses), shift 1-2 weeks from their strongest high-weight areas toward weak high-weight areas. Sequence suggestion: foundational/high-weight areas early, weakest areas getting two passes (learn + revisit), final 1-2 weeks for review + practice questions."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T13:12:44.848Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm6us22001wfqrd86rzqm4g','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596','0393944e-1fd0-4325-bffc-420f132f5106','save_question','completed',300006,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 72-year-old male with chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min) and hypertension develops hyperkalemia (K+ 6.2 mEq/L) 1 week after starting lisinopril. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","options":["Discontinue lisinopril and switch to losartan","Continue lisinopril and add sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Reduce lisinopril dose and monitor potassium","Discontinue lisinopril and switch to amlodipine","Continue lisinopril and increase fluid intake"],"answer":"Discontinue lisinopril and switch to amlodipine","explanation":"ACE inhibitors can cause hyperkalemia, particularly in patients with chronic kidney disease due to reduced aldosterone production. In CKD patients with hyperkalemia, ACE inhibitors should be discontinued. ARBs like losartan can also cause hyperkalemia and are not appropriate alternatives. Calcium channel blockers like amlodipine do not typically cause hyperkalemia and are safe alternatives for hypertension in CKD. Simply reducing the dose or continuing the medication is not appropriate given the significant hyperkalemia. Sodium polystyrene sulfonate is used for acute potassium management but doesn''t address the underlying cause.","mnemonic":"ACE + CKD = hyperK+ (ACEi contraindicated in CKD with hyperkalemia)","clinicalPearl":"ACE inhibitors should be avoided or used with extreme caution in CKD patients with hyperkalemia, eGFR <30, or on potassium-sparing diuretics.","distractorAnalysis":"Losartan: ARB also causes hyperkalemia; Reduce dose: not appropriate with significant hyperK+; Continue: unsafe with CKD and hyperK+; Increase fluids: doesn''t address mechanism","difficulty":"Hard","system":"General","topic":"ACE inhibitors","tags":["ACE inhibitors","CKD","hyperkalemia","nephrology","pharmacology"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"ACE inhibitor effects on potassium handling in chronic kidney disease"}','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T09:20:32.138Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvm70uot0027fqrd7hot23lv','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596','0393944e-1fd0-4325-bffc-420f132f5106','save_question','completed',300005,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 45-year-old female presents with fatigue, pallor, and shortness of breath on exertion. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 8.2 g/dL, MCV 78 fL, MCH 26 pg, MCHC 30 g/dL, RDW 18%. Peripheral smear shows microcytic hypochromic red blood cells. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of her anemia?","options":["Iron deficiency anemia","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease","Hemolytic anemia"],"answer":"Iron deficiency anemia","explanation":"The patient presents with microcytic hypochromic anemia (low MCV, low MCH, low MCHC) with increased RDW, which is classic for iron deficiency anemia. Iron deficiency is the most common cause of anemia worldwide and results from inadequate iron intake, poor absorption, or chronic blood loss. The increased RDW reflects the variation in red cell size as the bone marrow produces smaller cells due to iron limitation. Vitamin B12 and folate deficiencies typically cause macrocytic anemia (high MCV), while anemia of chronic disease is usually normocytic. Hemolytic anemia typically shows reticulocytosis and other findings not present here.","mnemonic":"\\"Microcytic hypochromic with high RDW = Iron deficiency\\"","clinicalPearl":"Iron deficiency anemia is the most common nutritional deficiency worldwide and should be investigated in all patients with microcytic hypochromic anemia.","distractorAnalysis":"Vitamin B12/folate deficiencies cause macrocytic anemia, not microcytic. Anemia of chronic disease is typically normocytic. Hemolytic anemia would show reticulocytosis and other findings not present.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Hematology","topic":"anemia","tags":["anemia","iron deficiency","microcytic","diagnosis"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"Blood smear showing microcytic hypochromic red blood cells in iron deficiency anemia"}','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T09:25:15.485Z');
