INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmtw7x70015udrddp3xqlsy','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','4f7d579f-a7a0-4509-a83a-64c4b351d7f1','analyze_visual_material','completed',14639,'{"imageUrl":"attachment://file-1792872298327-68r7","question":"Analyze this image to understand its style, content, and structure so I can create a skill to generate similar images"}','# Analysis of Medical Infographic: "Essential Electrolytes"

## **Overall Style & Aesthetic**
- **Type**: Educational medical infographic (high-yield study aid)
- **Design Language**: Clean, clinical, "Instagram/Pinterest medical" aesthetic
- **Color Palette**: Soft pastels with high contrast text
  - Potassium: Lavender/purple (#E6D5F5)
  - Sodium: Light blue (#D5E8F5)
  - Magnesium: Mint green (#D5F5E0)
  - Calcium: Pale violet (#E8D5F5)
  - Accents: Cream/beige for notes, white backgrounds
- **Typography**: Bold sans-serif headers (likely Montserrat or similar), clean body text
- **Visual Elements**: Anatomical illustrations, ECG waveforms, icons

---

## **Structural Layout**

### **Header Section**
```
[Logo: Pathology Maviator]     ESSENTIAL ELECTROLYTES
                               High-Yield Hypo vs Hyper Clues
```
- Title: Large, bold, black, centered
- Subtitle: Smaller, gray/dark gray
- Top-right corner: Circular logo with "PATHOLOGY MAVIATOR" branding

### **Main Content Grid (4 Rows)**
Each electrolyte follows this **consistent template**:

| Column 1 (25%) | Column 2 (37.5%) | Column 3 (37.5%) |
|---|---|---|
| **Symbol + Name**<br>Normal range<br>**Anatomical Icon** | **HYPO** (colored header)<br>• Bullet points<br>• 3-4 symptoms | **HYPER** (colored header)<br>• Bullet points<br>• 3-4 symptoms |

**Row Details:**
1. **Potassium (P+)**: Heart icon with ECG tracing, purple theme
2. **Sodium (So-)**: Blood vessel/capillary icon, blue theme  
3. **Magnesium (Mg)**: Muscle fiber with needle, green theme
4. **Calcium (Ca)**: Bone + nerve ending, lavender theme

### **Footer/Call-to-Action**
- Centered rounded rectangle button: "Notes in Telegram / Comment PDF to get the link"
- Side boxes: "EXAM BYTE" and "MCQ TRAP" (yellow/cream background with bullet points)

### **Decorative Elements**
- Subtle grid/graph paper background pattern
- Social media icons (thumbs up, comment, share) on left margin
- Rounded corners on all content boxes (border-radius: ~15-20px)
- Drop shadows or borders for depth

---

## **Content Specifications**

### **Data Fields per Electrolyte:**
1. **Chemical symbol** (large, colored, bold)
2. **Element name** (bold, black)
3. **Reference range** (smaller, gray)
4. **Visual metaphor**:
   - K+: Cardiac conduction system
   - Na+: Vascular/osmotic
   - Mg2+: Neuromuscular junction
   - Ca2+: Musculoskeletal/nervous tissue

5. **Hypoglycemia/Hypokalemia etc. clues** (left column under HYPO)
6. **Hyperglycemia/Hyperkalemia etc. clues** (right column under HYPER)

### **Text Hierarchy:**
- **Headers**: 24-32pt, bold, colored backgrounds
- **Element names**: 20-24pt, bold
- **Body text**: 14-16pt, regular weight
- **Ranges/Notes**: 12-14pt, lighter color

---

## **Technical Generation Parameters**

If creating via AI image generation (Midjourney/DALL-E/Stable Diffusion), use:

**Prompt Structure:**
```
Medical education infographic, titled "ESSENTIAL ELECTROLYTES", 
clean flat design, pastel color-coded sections (purple, blue, green, lavender),
anatomical icons (heart, blood vessel, muscle, bone), 
comparison table layout showing HYPO vs HYPER symptoms,
rounded rectangles, sans-serif typography, grid background,
educational poster style, high contrast, professional medical illustration,
4 rows of data, each with chemical symbol, normal range, and two columns of bullet points
```

**Key Visual Rules:**
- Maintain consistent column widths across all 4 rows
- Use medical accuracy in iconography (ECG for K+, neuron for Ca2+)
- Ensure text is legible (dark on light backgrounds)
- Include "high-yield" clinical correlations (Torsades, QT prolongation, etc.)
- Add meta-learning boxes (Exam Bytes, MCQ Traps)

Would you like me to provide specific CSS/HTML code to recreate this layout, or detailed prompts for specific AI image generators?',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:05:30.523Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmu09v20017udrdko25gvcm','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','4f7d579f-a7a0-4509-a83a-64c4b351d7f1','create_word_document','completed',91,'{"title":"Common Cardiac Arrhythmias Study Aid","content":"# Common Cardiac Arrhythmias: Study Aid Infographic\\n\\n## Title: Common Cardiac Arrhythmias: ECG Patterns, Clinical Presentations & Treatment\\n\\n### Section 1: Normal Sinus Rhythm (NSR)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Regular rhythm (60-100 bpm)\\n- P wave before each QRS complex\\n- PR interval 0.12-0.20 seconds\\n- QRS duration <0.12 seconds\\n- T wave in same direction as QRS\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Asymptomatic in most cases\\n- Normal cardiac output\\n- No treatment needed unless symptomatic\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- Observation\\n- Address underlying causes if present\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 2: Atrial Fibrillation (AFib)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Irregularly irregular rhythm\\n- No discernible P waves\\n- Fibrillatory waves (f-waves)\\n- Variable ventricular rate\\n- QRS complex usually normal\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Palpitations\\n- Fatigue\\n- Dyspnea\\n- Increased stroke risk\\n- May be asymptomatic\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- Rate control (beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers)\\n- Rhythm control (antiarrhythmics)\\n- Anticoagulation (warfarin, DOACs)\\n- Ablation therapy for refractory cases\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 3: Atrial Flutter\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Regular atrial rate ~300 bpm\\n- \\"Sawtooth\\" flutter waves (F waves)\\n- Typically 2:1, 3:1, or 4:1 AV block\\n- Ventricular rate depends on AV conduction\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Palpitations\\n- Fatigue\\n- Similar to AFib but more organized\\n- Less stroke risk than AFib\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- Rate control\\n- Anticoagulation (especially if AFib history)\\n- Catheter ablation (highly effective)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 4: Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Regular narrow complex tachycardia (150-250 bpm)\\n- P waves may be buried or absent\\n- QRS duration <0.12 seconds\\n- Sudden onset and termination\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Palpitations\\n- Dizziness\\n- Chest discomfort\\n- Anxiety\\n- Syncope in severe cases\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- Vagal maneuvers (Valsalva)\\n- Adenosine (first-line acute)\\n- Beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers\\n- Radiofrequency ablation for recurrent cases\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 5: Ventricular Tachycardia (VT)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Regular wide complex tachycardia (>100 bpm)\\n- QRS duration >0.12 seconds\\n- AV dissociation may be present\\n- Capture and fusion beats possible\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Palpitations\\n- Chest pain\\n- Hypotension\\n- Syncope or presyncope\\n- Sudden cardiac arrest risk\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- **Unstable**: Immediate synchronized cardioversion\\n- **Stable**: Antiarrhythmics (amiodarone, lidocaine)\\n- Beta-blockers\\n- ICD implantation for secondary prevention\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 6: Ventricular Fibrillation (VFib)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Chaotic, irregular waveform\\n- No identifiable QRS complexes\\n- No discernible P waves\\n- Coarse or fine fibrillation\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Sudden cardiac arrest\\n- Unconsciousness\\n- No pulse\\n- No breathing\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- **Immediate CPR**\\n- **Defibrillation** (as soon as possible)\\n- Advanced cardiac life support (ACLS)\\n- Post-resuscitation care\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 7: First-Degree AV Block\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Regular rhythm\\n- P waves present\\n- PR interval >0.20 seconds\\n- All P waves conducted to ventricles\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Usually asymptomatic\\n- May be found on routine ECG\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- Usually no treatment needed\\n- Monitor for progression\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 8: Second-Degree AV Block (Type I/Wenckebach)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Progressive PR interval prolongation\\n- Eventually dropped QRS complex\\n- Cycle repeats\\n- Regular pattern of dropped beats\\n\\n**Clinical Presentation:**\\n- Usually asymptomatic\\n- May have palpitations\\n\\n**Treatment:**\\n- Usually no treatment needed\\n- Monitor for progression\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 9: Second-Degree AV Block (Type II/Mobitz II)\\n**ECG Pattern:**\\n- Intermittent non-conducted P waves\\n- Constant PR interval when conducted\\n- No progressive PR prol','{"created":true,"artifact":{"id":"cmvmu09xk0018udrdado42ys2","name":"cardiac-arrhythmias-infographic","filename":"cardiac-arrhythmias-infographic.docx","downloadUrl":"/api/medq-agent/artifacts/cmvmu09xk0018udrdado42ys2","previewUrl":"/generated/artifact-e1bbb724-341.docx","pdfPreviewUrl":"/api/medq-agent/artifacts/cmvmu09xk0018udrdado42ys2/pdf","mimeType":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","kind":"office"},"note":"File saved. Reference it in your final answer with the download link."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:08:39.663Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmu225q001gudrdfplbz6ra','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','4f7d579f-a7a0-4509-a83a-64c4b351d7f1','ask_user','completed',4,'{"question":"What type of images would you like me to create a skill for? Please describe the style, purpose, and any specific characteristics you want the images to have.","inputs":[{"id":"image_type","type":"select","label":"Image Type","required":true,"options":[{"value":"medical_illustrations","label":"Medical illustrations (anatomy, procedures, diseases)"},{"value":"infographics","label":"Educational infographics (data visualization, study aids)"},{"value":"diagrams","label":"Technical diagrams (workflows, processes, systems)"},{"value":"diagnostic_images","label":"Diagnostic medical images (X-rays, scans, pathology)"},{"value":"patient_education","label":"Patient education materials (explanatory visuals)"},{"value":"research_visualizations","label":"Research data visualizations (charts, graphs, scientific)"},{"value":"other","label":"Other (please specify)"}]},{"id":"style_description","type":"textarea","label":"Style Description","placeholder":"Describe the visual style, colors, layout, and any specific characteristics you want","required":true},{"id":"primary_use","type":"select","label":"Primary Use","required":true,"options":[{"value":"study_materials","label":"Study materials and revision aids"},{"value":"presentations","label":"Medical presentations and teaching"},{"value":"patient_education","label":"Patient education and communication"},{"value":"research","label":"Research documentation and papers"},{"value":"clinical_reference","label":"Clinical reference materials"},{"value":"other","label":"Other (please specify)"}]}]}','{"displayed":true,"question":"What type of images would you like me to create a skill for? Please describe the style, purpose, and any specific characteristics you want the images to have.","fields":3,"guidance":"The interactive question form is now shown to the learner. END YOUR TURN NOW with no further tool calls — do not answer for them, do not re-ask in text. Their answers will arrive as the next user message; continue from those."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:10:02.990Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmu2vpo001hudrdpf5tor0o','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','4f7d579f-a7a0-4509-a83a-64c4b351d7f1','generate_medical_illustration','completed',300005,'{"prompt":"Anatomical cross-section of the human heart showing all four chambers, valves, and major blood vessels. Use medical color coding: blue for deoxygenated blood, red for oxygenated blood. Include proper anatomical labels for chambers (right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle), valves (tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral, aortic), and major vessels (vena cava, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, aorta). Show the heart in proper anatomical orientation with apex pointing down. Professional medical illustration style with clean lines and educational clarity."}','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:10:41.292Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmuecca002judrdx3ta64yl','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','4f7d579f-a7a0-4509-a83a-64c4b351d7f1','create_artifact','completed',10,'{"name":"atherosclerosis-visual-guide","kind":"markdown","content":"# Atherosclerosis Progression Visual Guide\\n\\n## Stage 1: Normal Artery\\n**Cross-sectional view:**\\n- Patent lumen with smooth endothelial lining\\n- Three distinct layers: intima (thin), media (thick with smooth muscle), adventitia (outer)\\n- No lipid accumulation or plaque formation\\n- Blood flows freely through open channel\\n\\n**Key structures labeled:**\\n- Endothelium (single cell layer)\\n- Internal elastic lamina\\n- Media (smooth muscle cells)\\n- External elastic lamina\\n- Adventitia (connective tissue)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Stage 2: Fatty Streak\\n**Cross-sectional view:**\\n- Mild intimal thickening\\n- Yellowish discoloration in intima\\n- Foam cells visible under microscope\\n- Lumen slightly reduced (<20%)\\n- No fibrous cap formation\\n\\n**Key pathological changes:**\\n- Foam cells: Lipid-laden macrophages\\n- Early lipid accumulation\\n- Monocyte infiltration\\n- Endothelial activation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Stage 3: Fibrous Plaque\\n**Cross-sectional view:**\\n- Significant intimal thickening\\n- Fibrous cap covering lipid core\\n- Yellow/white plaque protruding into lumen\\n- Lumen reduced 20-50%\\n- Smooth surface\\n\\n**Key structures:**\\n- Fibrous cap (collagen-rich)\\n- Lipid core (cholesterol crystals)\\n- Inflammatory cells beneath cap\\n- Vasa vasorum proliferation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Stage 4: Complicated Plaque\\n**Cross-sectional view:**\\n- Irregular plaque surface\\n- Areas of ulceration and erosion\\n- Thrombus formation over ruptured areas\\n- Intraplaque hemorrhage visible\\n- Lumen reduced 50-90%\\n\\n**Pathological features:**\\n- Plaque rupture with cap disruption\\n- Surface ulceration\\n- Thrombus (red fibrin mesh)\\n- Hemorrhage from vasa vasorum\\n- Intense inflammation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Stage 5: Complete Occlusion\\n**Cross-sectional view:**\\n- Total luminal blockage\\n- Organized thrombus filling lumen\\n- Collateral vessels may be visible\\n- Extensive calcification\\n- No patent channel for blood flow\\n\\n**Final pathological state:**\\n- 100% occlusion\\n- Chronic inflammatory infiltrate\\n- Fibrosis and organization\\n- Collateral circulation attempts\\n- Downstream tissue ischemia\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clinical Significance Timeline\\n\\n**Stage 1-2 (Asymptomatic):**\\n- Endothelial dysfunction begins\\n- Risk factor accumulation\\n- No clinical symptoms\\n\\n**Stage 3 (Symptomatic):**\\n- Exercise-induced symptoms\\n- Reduced coronary reserve\\n- Angina pectoris (coronary)\\n\\n**Stage 4 (Acute Events):**\\n- Plaque rupture\\n- Thrombosis\\n- Acute coronary syndromes\\n- Myocardial infarction risk\\n\\n**Stage 5 (Chronic Disease):**\\n- Complete ischemia\\n- Organ damage\\n- Heart failure\\n- Sudden cardiac death risk\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Medical Terminology Reference\\n\\n**Anatomical Terms:**\\n- Intima: Inner arterial layer\\n- Media: Middle muscular layer\\n- Adventitia: Outer connective layer\\n- Lumen: Hollow channel for blood\\n- Endothelium: Innermost cell layer\\n\\n**Pathological Terms:**\\n- Atherosclerosis: Hardening of arteries\\n- Plaque: Atheromatous deposit\\n- Stenosis: Narrowing of vessel\\n- Thrombus: Blood clot\\n- Occlusion: Complete blockage\\n\\n**Clinical Terms:**\\n- Ischemia: Reduced blood flow\\n- Infarction: Tissue death\\n- Angina: Chest pain\\n- Collateral: Alternative blood supply\\n- Aneurysm: Vessel dilation\\n\\n---\\n\\n*This visual guide provides a comprehensive reference for understanding atherosclerosis progression through its five key stages.*"}','{"created":true,"artifact":{"id":"cmvmuecch002kudrdgjen8c0i","name":"atherosclerosis-visual-guide","kind":"markdown","sizeBytes":3210,"downloadUrl":"/api/medq-agent/artifacts/cmvmuecch002kudrdgjen8c0i"},"note":"Artifact saved. Mention it in your final answer with the download link."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T20:19:36.058Z');
