ich of the "},{"index":5,"id":"cmvmwzvnu001gg7rdgm52g1ei","type":"mcq","system":"pharmacology","topic":"Rickets","difficulty":"Medium","preview":"A 2-year-old child presents with rickets characterized by bowed legs, delayed fontanelle closure, and hypocalcemia. Laboratory findings show"}],"note":"Summarize what was saved (count + topics + difficulty) and remind the user the new questions are pending review in the question bank."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:30:15.563Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvn6pr8t0038g7rdfvqa33m7','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','c877942c-d28e-4009-994b-d420e9296952','ask_user','completed',5,'{"question":"To create the perfect medical infographic generator skill, I need to understand your specific requirements. What types of medical topics would you most want this skill to handle? (e.g., electrolytes, medications, diseases, lab values, etc.)","inputs":[{"id":"topic_types","type":"multiselect","label":"Medical topic categories","required":true,"options":[{"value":"electrolytes","label":"Electrolytes & Lab Values"},{"value":"medications","label":"Medications & Drug Classes"},{"value":"diseases","label":"Diseases & Conditions"},{"value":"anatomy","label":"Anatomy & Physiology"},{"value":"procedures","label":"Procedures & Techniques"},{"value":"diagnostic","label":"Diagnostic Criteria"},{"value":"other","label":"Other (please specify)"}]}]}','{"displayed":true,"question":"To create the perfect medical infographic generator skill, I need to understand your specific requirements. What types of medical topics would you most want this skill to handle? (e.g., electrolytes, medications, diseases, lab values, etc.)","fields":1,"guidance":"The interactive question form is now shown to the learner. END YOUR TURN NOW with no further tool calls — do not answer for them, do not re-ask in text. Their answers will arrive as the next user message; continue from those."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T02:04:23.981Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3i65001ug7rdemise14y','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',2,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 58-year-old patient with heart failure (EF 25%) and hypertension develops a dry, persistent cough 3 weeks after starting lisinopril. Which of the following would be the MOST appropriate next step in management?","options":["Discontinue lisinopril and start furosemide","Switch to losartan (an ARB)","Add dextromethorphan for cough suppression","Continue lisinopril and add an inhaled corticosteroid","Switch to amlodipine (a calcium channel blocker)"],"answer":"Switch to losartan (an ARB)","explanation":"ACE inhibitors commonly cause a dry cough in 5-35% of patients due to bradykinin accumulation and prostaglandin release. ARBs like losartan do not inhibit ACE and therefore do not cause this side effect, making them an excellent alternative when ACE inhibitor-induced cough develops. The cough typically resolves within 1-2 weeks after switching to an ARB.","mnemonic":"ACE cough? Remember ARBs - ACE causes cough, ARBs don''t (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers)","clinicalPearl":"ACE inhibitor cough is non-productive, dry, and persistent. Switch to ARB rather than adding symptomatic treatment - the cough will resolve with the switch.","distractorAnalysis":"A) Discontinue lisinopril and start furosemide - Incorrect, as ACE inhibitors are beneficial in heart failure and should not be stopped without replacement. C) Add dextromethorphan - Symptomatic treatment doesn''t address the underlying cause and cough may persist. D) Continue lisinopril and add inhaled corticosteroid - Inhaled steroids don''t help ACE inhibitor cough. E) Switch to amlodipine - While CCBs are alternatives, ARBs are preferred for patients who can''t tolerate ACE inhibitors due to cough.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"ACE Inhibitors & ARBs","tags":["ACE inhibitor","cough","heart failure","ARB"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx3i66001vg7rdhlfic0fw","index":2,"message":"Question 2 saved (id cmvmx3i66001vg7rdhlfic0fw). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:09.245Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3mx0001wg7rddnsr9iuo','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',4,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 72-year-old patient with stable angina is prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin as needed for chest pain. Which of the following statements regarding nitrate tolerance is MOST accurate?","options":["Tolerance develops within hours of continuous nitrate therapy","Tolerance is specific to nitrates and does not affect other vasodilators","Nitrate tolerance can be prevented by taking daily vitamin C supplements","Tolerance is primarily due to depletion of nitric oxide stores","A 10-12 hour nitrate-free interval each day prevents tolerance development"],"answer":"A 10-12 hour nitrate-free interval each day prevents tolerance development","explanation":"Nitrate tolerance develops rapidly with continuous nitrate exposure and is characterized by diminished vasodilatory effects. The most effective strategy to prevent tolerance is to provide a nitrate-free interval of 10-12 hours each day, typically achieved by using short-acting nitrates during the day and avoiding them at night. This allows restoration of vascular responsiveness and sulfhydryl groups needed for nitrate metabolism.","mnemonic":"Nitrate-free interval: ''Give your vessels a break - 10-12 hours off nitrates daily''","clinicalPearl":"For chronic nitrate therapy, use isosorbide mononitrate twice daily with a 10-12 hour gap, or short-acting nitrates during the day only to prevent tolerance.","distractorAnalysis":"A) Tolerance develops within hours - True but incomplete answer - the key is how to prevent it. B) Tolerance is specific to nitrates - False, cross-tolerance with other vasodilators occurs. C) Vitamin C prevents tolerance - Not supported by evidence. D) Depletion of nitric oxide stores - Incorrect, tolerance involves complex mechanisms including oxidative stress and impaired bioactivation.","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Nitrates","tags":["nitrate tolerance","angina","pharmacology"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx3mx2001xg7rdx09jwjhq","index":3,"message":"Question 3 saved (id cmvmx3mx2001xg7rdx09jwjhq). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:15.396Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3s7o001yg7rddbbp5t1t','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',4,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 68-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation and mechanical mitral valve replacement is started on warfarin. Two weeks later, the INR is 1.2 (target 2.0-3.0). The patient recently started taking phenytoin for a seizure disorder. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["The warfarin dose needs to be increased due to inadequate anticoagulation","Phenytoin inhibits warfarin metabolism, leading to decreased INR","Phenytoin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes, accelerating warfarin clearance","The patient has developed warfarin resistance","Phenytoin competes with warfarin for protein binding sites"],"answer":"Phenytoin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes, accelerating warfarin clearance","explanation":"Phenytoin is a potent inducer of cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, which are responsible for warfarin metabolism. This induction accelerates warfarin clearance, leading to decreased INR levels. When starting or stopping phenytoin in patients on warfarin, close INR monitoring and frequent dose adjustments are required over 6-8 weeks as the enzyme induction develops or resolves.","mnemonic":"Phenytoin = P450 inducer = Warfarin metabolism accelerator = Lower INR","clinicalPearl":"CYP inducers (phenytoin, rifampin, carbamazepine) decrease INR; CYP inhibitors (fluconazole, amiodarone) increase INR. Monitor INR closely when starting/stopping these medications.","distractorAnalysis":"A) Warfarin dose needs to be increased - True, but doesn''t explain the mechanism. B) Phenytoin inhibits warfarin metabolism - False, phenytoin induces, not inhibits. D) Warfarin resistance - Incorrect term; this is a drug interaction. E) Protein binding competition - Not the primary mechanism with phenytoin.","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Anticoagulants","tags":["warfarin","drug interactions","INR","phenytoin"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx3s7p001zg7rdrdjp03iu","index":4,"message":"Question 4 saved (id cmvmx3s7p001zg7rdrdjp03iu). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:22.260Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3wkh0020g7rda644i4iz','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',3,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 75-year-old patient with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone. Which of the following adverse effects should be MOST closely monitored for during long-term therapy?","options":["Hypertension","Pulmonary fibrosis","Hyperthyroidism","Bradycardia","Peripheral edema"],"answer":"Pulmonary fibrosis","explanation":"Amiodarone has numerous serious adverse effects, but pulmonary toxicity (including pulmonary fibrosis) is among the most dangerous and potentially fatal complications. It can present as progressive dyspnea, cough, and hypoxia, and may occur even after discontinuation of the drug. Other significant toxicities include thyroid dysfunction (both hypo- and hyperthyroidism), photosensitivity, and corneal deposits, but pulmonary toxicity has the highest mortality rate when it occurs.","mnemonic":"Amiodarone lungs: ''Remember the pulmonary price - fibrosis can be fatal''","clinicalPearl":"Baseline chest CT, pulmonary function tests, and regular monitoring (every 6-12 months) are recommended for patients on long-term amiodarone to detect pulmonary toxicity early.","distractorAnalysis":"A) Hypertension - Amiodarone typically causes hypotension, not hypertension. C) Hyperthyroidism - A concern, but less immediately life-threatening than pulmonary fibrosis. D) Bradycardia - Common but usually manageable with dose adjustment. E) Peripheral edema - Common side effect but not as serious as pulmonary toxicity.","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Antiarrhythmics","tags":["amiodarone","pulmonary toxicity","adverse effects"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx3wkj0021g7rdztdxkx9h","index":5,"message":"Question 5 saved (id cmvmx3wkj0021g7rdztdxkx9h). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:27.905Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx41l20022g7rdq1duaep0','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',3,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 62-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes has an ejection fraction of 35% and symptoms of heart failure. Which of the following calcium channel blockers would be MOST appropriate for this patient?","options":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":"Amlodipine","explanation":"In patients with heart failure reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (verapamil and diltiazem) are contraindicated because they have negative inotropic effects that can worsen heart failure. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers like amlodipine, nifedipine, and felodipine are generally safer in heart failure as they primarily cause vasodilation without significant negative inotropic effects. Amlodipine is often preferred due to its once-daily dosing and extensive safety data in heart failure patients.","mnemonic":"HFrEF + CCB: ''Dihydropyridines are safe, non-dihydropyridines contraindicated''","clinicalPearl":"In HFrEF, avoid verapamil and diltiazem (negative inotropes). Amlodipine is the CCB of choice if needed for hypertension or angina in heart failure patients.","distractorAnalysis":"B) Verapamil - Contraindicated in HFrEF due to negative inotropic effects. C) Diltiazem - Contraindicated in HFrEF due to negative inotropic effects. D) Nifedipine - Dihydropyridine but may cause reflex tachycardia; amlodipine preferred. E) Felodipine - Dihydropyridine but less commonly used than amlodipine.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Calcium Channel Blockers","tags":["calcium channel blocker","heart failure","HFrEF"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx41l30023g7rd3tgf0jwo","index":6,"message":"Question 6 saved (id cmvmx41l30023g7rd3tgf0jwo). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:34.406Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx46df0024g7rdjfdwl654','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',4,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 70-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min) is being evaluated for anticoagulation. Which of the following direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) would be MOST appropriate for this patient?","options":["Rivaroxaban","Apixaban","Dabigatran","Edoxaban","All of the above require dose adjustment"],"answer":"Apixaban","explanation":"Apixaban is the preferred DOAC for patients with moderate to severe chronic kidney disease (eGFR 15-50 mL/min) due to its favorable pharmacokinetic profile and reduced accumulation compared to other DOACs. Rivaroxaban and edoxaban require significant dose reduction in CKD and are not recommended for eGFR <15 mL/min. Dabigatran is primarily renally excreted and requires substantial dose reduction in CKD, with accumulation risk at lower eGFR levels. Apixaban has the most favorable renal clearance profile among the DOACs.","mnemonic":"CKD + DOAC: ''Apixaban first, others need careful consideration''","clinicalPearl":"In moderate CKD (eGFR 15-50), apixaban 5mg BID is preferred. Avoid DOACs in severe CKD (eGFR <15) - use warfarin instead.","distractorAnalysis":"A) Rivaroxaban - Requires significant dose reduction in CKD and not recommended for eGFR <15. C) Dabigatran - High renal excretion, requires substantial dose reduction, accumulation risk. D) Edoxaban - Requires dose reduction in CKD, not recommended for eGFR <15. E) All require adjustment - False, apixaban has the most favorable profile.","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Anticoagulants","tags":["DOAC","chronic kidney disease","anticoagulation"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx46dg0025g7rddly3u30e","index":7,"message":"Question 7 saved (id cmvmx46dg0025g7rddly3u30e). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:40.611Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvmx4b8q0026g7rdl5qcakhh','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'save_question','completed',4,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 78-year-old patient with heart failure and atrial fibrillation is being treated with digoxin. The patient presents with nausea, confusion, and visual disturbances. Serum digoxin level is 2.8 ng/mL. Which of the following medications is MOST likely to have contributed to this toxicity?","options":["Metoprolol","Furosemide","Amiodarone","Lisinopril","Atorvastatin"],"answer":"Amiodarone","explanation":"Amiodarone significantly increases digoxin levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein, which is responsible for digoxin renal excretion. This interaction can increase digoxin concentrations by 50-100%, leading to toxicity even at previously therapeutic doses. The classic symptoms of digoxin toxicity include gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting), neurological symptoms (confusion, fatigue), and visual disturbances (yellow-green halos, blurred vision). The therapeutic range for digoxin is typically 0.5-2.0 ng/mL, and levels >2.4 ng/mL are considered toxic.","mnemonic":"Amiodarone + Digoxin = ''Double trouble - P-glycoprotein inhibition raises levels''","clinicalPearl":"When starting amiodarone in a patient on digoxin, reduce digoxin dose by 50% and monitor levels closely. Watch for classic toxicity symptoms: GI upset, confusion, visual changes.","distractorAnalysis":"A) Metoprolol - Beta blocker, no significant interaction with digoxin. B) Furosemide - Loop diuretic, can cause hypokalemia which potentiates digoxin toxicity, but not a direct interaction. D) Lisinopril - ACE inhibitor, no significant interaction with digoxin. E) Atorvastatin - Statin, no significant interaction with digoxin.","difficulty":"Hard","system":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Antiarrhythmics","tags":["digoxin toxicity","amiodarone","drug interactions"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvmx4b8r0027g7rdbvxuc572","index":8,"message":"Question 8 saved (id cmvmx4b8r0027g7rdbvxuc572). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-24T21:35:46.922Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvn61ba9002og7rdfl33d53m','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','c877942c-d28e-4009-994b-d420e9296952','create_flashcards','completed',300010,'{"cards":[{"front":"What are the initial laboratory tests for anemia diagnosis?","back":"Complete Blood Count (CBC) with differential, Reticulocyte count, Peripheral smear examination","system":"Hematology","topic":"Anemia Diagnosis"},{"front":"What iron studies are ordered in anemia workup?","back":"Serum iron, Ferritin, Total Iron Binding Capacity (TIBC), Transferrin saturation, Soluble transferrin receptor","system":"Hematology","topic":"Iron Studies"},{"front":"When to order additional testing beyond basic iron studies?","back":"When initial tests are inconclusive: Vitamin B12 and folate levels, Reticulocyte production index, LDH and haptoglobin (hemolysis workup), Bone marrow examination (if indicated)","system":"Hematology","topic":"Advanced Testing"},{"front":"What does reticulocyte count tell us in anemia evaluation?","back":"Helps determine if bone marrow is responding appropriately. Low reticulocyte count suggests inadequate production (bone marrow failure, chronic disease). High reticulocyte count suggests increased destruction (hemolysis) or response to treatment.","system":"Hematology","topic":"Reticulocyte Count"},{"front":"What is the diagnostic significance of peripheral smear in anemia?","back":"Shows morphological changes: microcytic cells (iron deficiency), macrocytic cells (B12/folate deficiency), schistocytes (hemolysis), target cells (thalassemia), or abnormal white blood cells suggesting bone marrow disorders.","system":"Hematology","topic":"Peripheral Smear"},{"front":"How does transferrin saturation help differentiate iron deficiency anemia from anemia of chronic disease?","back":"Iron deficiency: Transferrin saturation < 15%. Anemia of chronic disease: Transferrin saturation normal or slightly decreased (> 15-20%) despite low serum iron (functional iron deficiency).","system":"Hematology","topic":"Transferrin Saturation"},{"front":"When is bone marrow examination indicated in anemia evaluation?","back":"When peripheral blood tests are inconclusive, suspected bone marrow failure (aplastic anemia, myelodysplastic syndromes), unexplained cytopenias, or to evaluate for malignancy infiltration.","system":"Hematology","topic":"Bone Marrow Exam"},{"front":"What laboratory findings suggest hemolytic anemia?","back":"Elevated LDH, decreased haptoglobin, increased indirect bilirubin, increased reticulocyte count, peripheral smear showing schistocytes, spherocytes, or other evidence of red cell destruction.","system":"Hematology","topic":"Hemolysis Workup"},{"front":"How does vitamin B12 level differ from methylmalonic acid in deficiency?","back":"Vitamin B12 level can be normal or borderline in deficiency. Methylmalonic acid is more specific and elevated in B12 deficiency. Homocysteine also increases in both B12 and folate deficiency.","system":"Hematology","topic":"Vitamin B12 Testing"},{"front":"What is the reticulocyte production index and why is it important?","back":"Corrects for anemia severity: RPI = (reticulocyte % × patient Hct/normal Hct) / maturation time. RPI < 2 suggests inadequate production; RPI > 3 suggests appropriate response or hemolysis.","system":"Hematology","topic":"Reticulocyte Production Index"}],"deckLabel":"Anemia Diagnostic Steps"}','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T01:45:23.553Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvn6smwp003wg7rdsfzprz6b','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','c877942c-d28e-4009-994b-d420e9296952','manage_skills','completed',300007,'{"action":"create","name":"medical-infographic-generator","description":"Generate high-yield medical education infographics similar to professional medical study materials. Creates modular grid-based infographics with color-coded sections, anatomical illustrations, and clinical correlations for board exam preparation.","body":"# Medical Infographic Generator Skill\\n\\n## Overview\\nA comprehensive skill for generating high-yield medical education infographics in the style of professional medical study materials. Creates modular grid-based layouts with color-coded sections, anatomical illustrations, and clinical correlations optimized for board exam preparation.\\n\\n## When to Use\\n- Creating study aids for medical board exams (USMLE, COMLEX, NCLEX)\\n- Generating quick reference materials for clinical rotations\\n- Producing educational content for medical lectures or presentations\\n- Creating visual summaries of complex medical topics\\n- Developing review materials for medical students and residents\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Step 1: Topic Analysis\\n1. **Identify medical topic category**:\\n   - Electrolytes (Na+, K+, Ca2+, Mg2+, Cl-, HCO3-)\\n   - Diseases (organ-specific, systemic, infectious)\\n   - Medications (drug classes, mechanisms, side effects)\\n   - Anatomy (regional, systemic, cross-sectional)\\n   - Procedures (techniques, steps, complications)\\n   - Diagnostic (lab values, imaging findings, criteria)\\n   - Other (physiology, pathophysiology, biochemistry)\\n\\n2. **Determine infographic type**:\\n   - **System-Based Disease Overview**: Pathophysiology → Presentation → Diagnosis → Treatment\\n   - **Comparison Grid**: Side-by-side comparison of entities\\n   - **Algorithm/Flowchart**: Step-by-step diagnostic or therapeutic approach\\n   - **Reference Table**: Quick lookup of values, criteria, or guidelines\\n   - **Anatomical Overview**: Structure → Function → Clinical correlations\\n\\n### Step 2: Content Organization\\n1. **Extract high-yield information**:\\n   - Key definitions and diagnostic criteria\\n   - Essential clinical manifestations\\n   - Critical diagnostic approaches\\n   - First-line treatment strategies\\n   - Common pitfalls and red flags\\n   - Memory aids and mnemonics\\n\\n2. **Structure content by priority**:\\n   - **Tier 1**: Must-know information for exams\\n   - **Tier 2**: Important clinical correlations\\n   - **Tier 3**: Reference details for advanced understanding\\n\\n3. **Organize in logical flow**:\\n   - Pathophysiology → Clinical presentation → Diagnosis → Treatment\\n   - Definition → Epidemiology → Etiology → Clinical features → Management\\n   - Normal → Abnormal → Clinical significance → Management\\n\\n### Step 3: Visual Design Planning\\n1. **Select appropriate color scheme**:\\n   - **Electrolytes**: Blue (Na+), Green (K+), Orange (Ca2+), Purple (Mg2+)\\n   - **Cardiovascular**: Red/pink theme\\n   - **Neurological**: Blue/purple theme\\n   - **Renal**: Blue/green theme\\n   - **Hepatic**: Green/brown theme\\n   - **Pulmonary**: Blue/white theme\\n\\n2. **Plan layout structure**:\\n   - **Grid-based organization**: Clear sections with defined boundaries\\n   - **Hierarchical typography**: Bold headers, bullet points, emphasis\\n   - **Visual flow**: Logical left-to-right, top-to-bottom progression\\n   - **White space**: Adequate spacing for readability\\n\\n3. **Determine visual elements**:\\n   - **Anatomical illustrations**: Relevant structures and organs\\n   - **Diagnostic images**: ECGs, X-rays, lab results (when appropriate)\\n   - **Algorithms**: Flowcharts for decision-making processes\\n   - **Icons**: Medical symbols and visual cues\\n   - **Color coding**: Consistent use of colors for categories/severities\\n\\n### Step 4: Content Creation\\n1. **Write concise, high-yield content**:\\n   - Use bullet points for easy scanning\\n   - Include key statistics and prevalence data\\n   - Add clinical correlations and real-world applications\\n   - Incorporate memory aids and mnemonics\\n   - Include common pitfalls and exam tips\\n\\n2.','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T02:06:38.329Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvn6pvd4003dg7rdhkp4gixx','cmusenr950006oir80jr4ludo','c877942c-d28e-4009-994b-d420e9296952','ask_user','completed',2,'{"question":"What level of detail should the infographics contain? Should they focus on high-yield exam content like your example, or should they be more comprehensive with additional details and references?","inputs":[{"id":"detail_level","type":"radio","label":"Content detail level","required":true,"options":[{"value":"high_yield","label":"High-yield exam focus (concise, board-relevant content only)"},{"value":"balanced","label":"Balanced approach (high-yield + some additional context)"},{"value":"comprehensive","label":"Comprehensive reference (detailed with citations and additional info)"}]}]}','{"displayed":true,"question":"What level of detail should the infographics contain? Should they focus on high-yield exam content like your example, or should they be more comprehensive with additional details and references?","fields":1,"guidance":"The interactive question form is now shown to the learner. END YOUR TURN NOW with no further tool calls — do not answer for them, do not re-ask in text. Their answers will arrive as the next user message; continue from those."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T02:04:29.320Z');
