INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvo4zhqt000jwkwux4sc1bhe','cmusenqrl0000oir8zrgpm0p7',NULL,'save_question','completed',2810,'{"type":"mcq","text":"A 2-week-old male infant presents with abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. Physical examination reveals a distended abdomen with absent bowel sounds. Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and diagnosis of this condition is most accurate?","options":["The condition results from congenital absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon, leading to functional obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells","The condition is caused by intrinsic colonic muscle hypertrophy, leading to mechanical obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by abdominal ultrasound showing thickened bowel wall","The condition results from neuroblastoma infiltration of the colon, leading to luminal narrowing, and diagnosis is confirmed by CT scan showing a mass lesion","The condition is caused by congenital absence of the enteric nervous system throughout the entire gastrointestinal tract, leading to complete obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by barium enema alone"],"answer":"The condition results from congenital absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon, leading to functional obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells","explanation":"Hirschsprung disease is a congenital disorder characterized by the absence of ganglion cells (aganglionosis) in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon. This absence leads to a functional obstruction due to unopposed parasympathetic activity, causing tonic contraction of the affected segment. The classic clinical presentation in neonates includes abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. While barium enema may show a transition zone between the dilated proximal colon and narrowed distal colon, the gold standard for diagnosis is rectal biopsy, which demonstrates the absence of ganglion cells and hypertrophied nerve trunks in the submucosa [S1][S2][S3].","mnemonic":"Hirschsprung''s: ''No Gang, No Go'' - No ganglion cells, no movement (obstruction)","clinicalPearl":"In neonates with suspected Hirschsprung disease, remember that the diagnosis requires histological confirmation of aganglionosis via rectal biopsy; barium enema can be suggestive but is not diagnostic alone.","distractorAnalysis":"Option B is incorrect because Hirschsprung disease is not caused by muscle hypertrophy but by neural dysfunction. Option C is incorrect as it describes a neoplastic process rather than a congenital developmental disorder. Option D is incorrect because the aganglionosis is typically limited to the distal colon, not the entire GI tract, and barium enema alone is insufficient for diagnosis.","difficulty":"Medium","system":"pediatrics","topic":"gastrointestinal disorders","tags":["hirschsprung disease","congenital disorders","pediatrics","gastrointestinal","pathophysiology"],"imageUrl":"","audioUrl":"","illustrationDescription":"","attachSourceMediaId":"","citations":[{"sourceId":"cmuyygery0003ydma3bobkh6a","chunkId":"cmuyygrw90009ydmarc4okdbf"},{"sourceId":"cmuyzuuww0002ewma8ndzgi1c","chunkId":"cmuyzuwto0004ewmapgbod3b9"}]}','{"saved":true,"id":"cmvo4zjwt000kwkwuzzsydbk8","index":1,"message":"Question 1 saved (id cmvo4zjwt000kwkwuzzsydbk8). Continue with the next question or summarize when done."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T18:03:45.173Z');
