Stuff","dimensions":"1500pxx1500px","sourceUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/34c1e02ac879.jpg","cached":true},{"index":5,"imageUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/599909fa4431.png","caption":"Vintage anatomical heart poster ad on aged paper, labeled Latin terms, \\"1643\\", promotional text: \\"Premium museum-quality paper\\", \\"Anatomically correct\\".","source":"Animus Medicus","dimensions":"1080pxx1080px","cached":false}],"note":"Show the most relevant images inline with their sources. Each imageUrl is a LOCAL copy that always loads — embed it directly as ![caption](imageUrl) and name the original source site next to it. sourceUrl (when present) is the original remote image for attribution; only embed sourceUrl if imageUrl somehow fails."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T23:34:54.738Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvqprgua000qe9oica90l31m','cmuser2vb0000scr8wuwwi596',NULL,'search_knowledge_base','completed',14294,'{"query":"ACE inhibitors mechanism of action adverse effects"}','{"passages":[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoi003qbjrmuex45q0d","source":"Peditric-Med.pdf","excerpt":"Pediatrics | Acute Malnutrition & Vitamin A Toxicity S Amber boxes = Clarifications Note | H = High-yield / exam-priority point | Green = Further Information\\n\\n1. Introduction & Definition of Malnutrition\\nPediatric malnutrition (undernutrition) is a state of nutrition in which a deficiency of energy, protein, and/or other nutrients causes measurable adverse effects on tissue/body function and a clinical outcome of growth deviation. The ASPEN definition: an imbalance between nutrient requirement and intake, resulting in cumulative deficits of energy, protein, or micronutrients that may negatively affect growth, development, and other relevant outcomes."},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwol00ghbjrmymxw16dh","source":"Peditric-Med.pdf","excerpt":"Drug Mechanism Dose Key Notes\\nCorticosteroids\\n(Prednisone)\\n↓ Antibody production + ↓ phagocytosis of coated platelets\\n2 mg/kg/day (taper), or 4 mg/kg/day × 4 days (high-dose, fewer steroid side-effects)\\n~2/3 respond; relapse is common\\nIV methylprednisolone\\n(Solu-Medrol)\\nSame as above Up to 30 mg/kg/dose More rapid response than oral steroids IVIG Blocks macrophage Fc receptors\\n→ protects platelets from destruction\\n400 mg/kg × 5 days, or 800 mg/kg × 3 days, or 1 g/kg × 1–2 days\\n\\nRapid rise (>20,000/mm3 in 95% within 2 days); great for urgent bleeding/surgery\\n\\nIntravenous Anti-D\\n(Anti-Rh)"},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoi0044bjrm6hb9ff8t","source":"Peditric-Med.pdf","excerpt":"3 Clarifications Note — The Chain of Organ Atrophy Lactose intolerance: Lactase is a critical enzyme normally sitting on the intestinal Brush border, which itself atrophies. Once the child’s gut atrophies, giving milk (lactose) triggers Diarrhea because there’s no working Lactase enzyme left to break the lactose down.\\nPancreatic Atrophy: the Pancreas atrophies too, decreasing digestive enzyme output and worsening Malabsorption further."},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwol00f2bjrmalhqcf2y","source":"Peditric-Med.pdf","excerpt":"• Joint deformities may develop secondary to hemarthrosis.\\n\\n• Development of antibodies (inhibitors) to Factor VIII after repeated replacement therapy — leads to poor clinical response.\\n\\n• Hepatitis and AIDS from repeated transfusion of blood or blood products.\\n\\n⚠ Mandatory Inhibitor Check Before Any Surgery\\nBefore ANY surgery in a hemophilia patient, confirm the patient does NOT have antibodies (inhibitors) against the factor — if inhibitors are present and unrecognized, and bleeding occurs during the operation, it will NOT respond to factor replacement therapy at all.\\n\\n3.8 Management\\n\\n1. First Aid: RICE rule (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation).\\n\\n2."},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwoi005gbjrmyple04y1","source":"Peditric-Med.pdf","excerpt":"S Clarifications Note — VDDR Named as Type 1 and Type 2 The doctor specifically named the two Vitamin D-dependent hereditary types: Type 1 and Type 2 Hereditary Vitamin D-Dependent Rickets (VDDR) — both present with classic rachitic signs, but the underlying enzyme/receptor defect differs (see Further Information below for the mechanism distinction)."}]}',NULL,'2026-10-27T13:20:55.042Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvoh68km0022spwuj9nohdq1','cmusenqrl0000oir8zrgpm0p7','cb3d48a8-c2aa-4bb9-9f96-45bdd224be0a','manage_skills','completed',8,'{"action":"create","name":"capability-demo-skill","description":"A simple skill that instructs to always cite sources in medical answers","body":"# Capability Demo Skill\\n\\n## Purpose\\nThis skill ensures that all medical answers include proper citations to sources.\\n\\n## When to Use\\nAlways use this skill when providing medical information or answering medical questions.\\n\\n## Instructions\\n- Always cite sources in medical answers using [S#] format for web sources and [K#] format for knowledge base sources\\n- Never provide medical information without proper citations\\n- Use search_medical_references for current guidelines and evidence\\n- Use search_knowledge for personal knowledge library content\\n- Include at least one relevant citation for every medical claim made\\n\\n## Citation Format\\n- Web sources: [S1], [S2], etc.\\n- Knowledge base sources: [K1], [K2], etc.\\n- Place citations immediately after the claims they support"}','{"ok":true,"created":true,"skillId":"cmvoh68kt0023spwuhhmgi0pv","name":"capability-demo-skill"}',NULL,'2026-10-25T23:44:55.270Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvoh6f5p002aspwuko2cf85m','cmusenqrl0000oir8zrgpm0p7','cb3d48a8-c2aa-4bb9-9f96-45bdd224be0a','generate_medical_illustration','completed',300005,'{"prompt":"Anatomically accurate educational illustration of the cardiac cycle showing the four phases: diastole (ventricular filling), isovolumetric contraction, ventricular ejection, and isovolumetric relaxation. Include the heart chambers (atria and ventricles), major vessels (aorta, pulmonary artery, vena cava, pulmonary veins), and valves (tricuspid, mitral, pulmonary, aortic). Use clear color coding: red for oxygenated blood, blue for deoxygenated blood. Show sequential phases with arrows indicating blood flow direction. Include labels for all major structures and phases."}','{"approved":false,"reason":"Timed out waiting for approval (5 minutes).","note":"Do not retry this tool call in this turn. Acknowledge the decision to the user and continue without performing the action."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T23:45:03.805Z');
INSERT INTO "AgentToolExecution" ("id","userId","threadId","toolName","status","latencyMs","inputJson","outputJson","error","createdAt") VALUES ('cmvohcvzm002dspwuofi45upj','cmusenqrl0000oir8zrgpm0p7','cb3d48a8-c2aa-4bb9-9f96-45bdd224be0a','search_medical_images','completed',15348,'{"query":"cardiac cycle phases medical illustration","count":5}','{"found":true,"query":"cardiac cycle phases medical illustration","images":[{"index":1,"imageUrl":"/generated/imgcache/c819c4060aabccb1.jpg","caption":"Labeled anatomical diagram of human heart chambers and vessels, with inset showing myocytes and intercalated disks; contains Britannica copyright.","source":"Britannica","dimensions":"2000pxx1323px","sourceUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/bf2dcf1927e0.jpg","cached":true},{"index":2,"imageUrl":"/generated/imgcache/39a478b687dcd749.jpg","caption":"Scientific figure showing human heart proteomics workflow, with large amounts of text and data charts; not suitable for background image.","source":"Nature","dimensions":"2029pxx1628px","sourceUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/eca924d5f9b3.jpg","cached":true},{"index":3,"imageUrl":"/generated/imgcache/9454ec978746f56d.jpg","caption":"A labeled anatomical diagram of the human heart''s major vessels and structures with extensive text, copyrighted by Encyclopædia Britannica.","source":"Britannica","dimensions":"2000pxx1380px","sourceUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/810b7c968826.jpg","cached":true},{"index":4,"imageUrl":"/generated/imgcache/b1e5570f2c3ec75d.png","caption":"Scientific diagram comparing HFpEF pathology and Glabridin pharmacological therapy showing cardiomyocyte cell cycle, CCNA2, ROS, SASP, fibrosis.","source":"Nature","dimensions":"950pxx868px","sourceUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/96ee5201f46c.png","cached":true},{"index":5,"imageUrl":"https://z-cdn.chatglm.cn/image-search-mcp/images-ppt/9c1c478d2aa8.jpg","caption":"Hand-drawn anatomical heart diagram with labels (SVC, IVC, valves, chambers); hand holding marker; contains multiple text labels.","source":"Physiopedia","dimensions":"1280pxx720px","cached":false}],"note":"Show the most relevant images inline with their sources. Each imageUrl is a LOCAL copy that always loads — embed it directly as ![caption](imageUrl) and name the original source site next to it. sourceUrl (when present) is the original remote image for attribution; only embed sourceUrl if imageUrl somehow fails."}',NULL,'2026-10-25T23:50:05.554Z');
