DELETE FROM "GroupLectureChunk";
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentbp000lq0r8qqz67o8a','cmusentbi000kq0r8qv1aqjlw','1. Definitions & Classification','Short Intro','5-minute high-yield overview','text','','Heart failure is a clinical syndrome characterized by the heart''s inability to pump sufficient blood to meet metabolic demands, OR doing so only at elevated filling pressures.

Two key classifications:
• HFrEF (reduced EF ≤ 40%): systolic dysfunction
• HFpEF (preserved EF ≥ 50%): diastolic dysfunction
• HFmEF (mid-range 41-49%): grey zone

NYHA Functional Classification (symptoms):
• Class I — no limitation
• Class II — slight limitation, ordinary activity causes symptoms
• Class III — marked limitation, less-than-ordinary activity causes symptoms
• Class IV — symptoms at rest

ACC/AHA Stages (structural progression):
• A — at risk, no structural change, no symptoms
• B — structural change, no symptoms
• C — structural change + current/past symptoms
• D — advanced disease, refractory to GDMT',0,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.837Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.837Z','Heart failure definitions, NYHA classes, and ACC/AHA stages — the foundation for understanding disease progression.','["Define heart failure with reduced vs preserved EF","Distinguish NYHA class from ACC/AHA stage","Identify the four ACC/AHA stages"]','["high-yield","definitions"]',15);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentbu000mq0r84o30dmov','cmusentbi000kq0r8qv1aqjlw','1. Definitions & Classification','Deep Dive','Detailed 20-min lecture','text','','The NYHA and ACC/AHA systems are complementary — NYHA tracks CURRENT symptomatic burden (changes day-to-day with treatment), while ACC/AHA tracks DISEASE PROGRESSION (one-way: once you are Stage C, you never go back).

Key pearls:
1. A patient can be Stage C with NYHA class II today and NYHA class III tomorrow after a salty meal
2. The "dangling modifier" trap: HFrEF and HFpEF describe EF category; Stage A-D describes disease stage; NYHA I-IV describes functional status — NEVER mix these
3. Mortality at 5 years is ~50% after hospitalization for HF — worse than many cancers',0,1,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.842Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.842Z','Heart failure definitions, NYHA classes, and ACC/AHA stages — the foundation for understanding disease progression.','["Define heart failure with reduced vs preserved EF","Distinguish NYHA class from ACC/AHA stage","Identify the four ACC/AHA stages"]','["high-yield","definitions"]',15);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentc0000nq0r8hq1tgfci','cmusentbi000kq0r8qv1aqjlw','2. Neurohormonal Activation','Core Concept','RAAS + SNS pathophysiology','text','','Decreased cardiac output → decreased renal perfusion → juxtaglomerular apparatus releases renin → angiotensinogen → angiotensin I → angiotensin II (via ACE in lungs).

Angiotensin II effects (all maladaptive long-term in HF):
• Potent vasoconstriction (↑ afterload)
• Aldosterone release (Na+ retention, K+ wasting, ↑ preload)
• ADH release (water retention)
• Cardiac remodeling (hypertrophy, fibrosis)
• Sympathetic activation

Sympathetic Nervous System (SNS):
• Initially compensatory — ↑ HR, ↑ contractility
• Chronic activation → myocyte apoptosis, β-receptor downregulation, arrhythmias

THE FOUR PILLARS of GDMT for HFrEF (each targets a different neurohormonal pathway):
1. ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) or ACEi/ARB — block angiotensin II
2. β-blocker (carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol) — block SNS
3. MRA (spironolactone, eplerenone) — block aldosterone
4. SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) — glycemic control independent of EF benefit

All four reduce mortality — start ALL four (low dose, titrate up).',0,2,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.848Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.848Z','The RAAS and sympathetic nervous system in heart failure — both adaptive short-term, maladaptive long-term.','["Explain why RAAS activation initially helps but ultimately worsens HF","Trace the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone cascade","Identify the four pillars of GDMT by mechanism"]','["high-yield","mechanism","pharmacology"]',20);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentc4000oq0r8pmle3ild','cmusentbi000kq0r8qv1aqjlw','3. Acute Decompensation','Clinical Algorithm','Stevenson profile + treatment','text','','Acute HF classification (Stevenson profiles):

              | Congested (wet)        | Not congested (dry)
───────────┼────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────
Perfused   | Profile B (warm & wet) | Profile A (warm & dry)
(warm)     | Most common             | Goal state
───────────┼────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────
Hypoperfused| Profile C (cold & wet) | Profile L (cold & dry)
(cold)     | Cardiogenic shock risk  | Over-diuresed

Treatment by profile:
• Warm & wet (B): IV loop diuretic ± vasodilator (NTG) if BP allows
• Cold & wet (C): IV inotrope (dobutamine, milrinone) + diuretic — this is the dangerous one
• Warm & dry (A): oral GDMT, watch for over-diuresis
• Cold & dry (L): cautious fluids, reduce diuretics

Red flags → ICU + consider mechanical support (IABP, Impella, ECMO):
• Lactate > 2 mmol/L
• SBP < 90 mmHg
• End-organ dysfunction (creatinine ↑, LFTs ↑, altered mental status)
• Cold extremities with poor urine output',0,3,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.852Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.852Z','Recognition and management of acute decompensated heart failure — a common board and clinical scenario.','["Identify the four clinical profiles (wet/dry, warm/cold)","Choose correct initial therapy by profile","Recognize indications for mechanical circulatory support"]','["emergency","high-yield"]',25);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentcd000qq0r819jth33r','cmusentc8000pq0r8rk1p91gg','1. ECG Recognition','ECG Pearls','High-yield ECG for ACS','text','','STEMI diagnostic criteria (in adults):
• ST elevation ≥ 1 mm in 2 contiguous limb leads (I, II, III, aVF, aVL, aVR)
• ST elevation ≥ 2 mm in 2 contiguous chest leads (V1-V6) for men, ≥ 1.5 mm for women
• New LBBB with appropriate clinical picture = STEMI equivalent

Territory → culprit artery:
• V1-V4 → LAD (anterior)
• II, III, aVF → RCA (inferior)
• I, aVL, V5-V6 → LCx/OM (lateral)
• V7-V9 (posterior leads) → LCx or RCA (posterior)
• V1-V2 ST depression with tall R waves → posterior STEMI (mirror image)

STEMI equivalents (don''t miss these):
1. New LBBB with chest pain
2. Posterior MI: V1-V2 ST depression + tall R waves (use V7-V9 to confirm)
3. Hyperacute T waves (early, before ST elevation)
4. De Winter T waves (upsloping ST depression in precordial leads)
5. Wellens syndrome (deep biphasic T waves in V2-V3, critical LAD)',0,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.862Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.862Z','STEMI equivalents, territory localization, and pitfalls.','["Identify ST elevation with proper MI criteria","Localize occluded artery by ECG territory","Recognize STEMI equivalents (LBBB, posterior MI, hyperacute T)"]','["ECG","emergency","high-yield"]',20);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentcf000rq0r8w7nzfqk4','cmusentc8000pq0r8rk1p91gg','2. Reperfusion Strategy','Reperfusion Ladder','PCI vs lysis decision tree','text','','STEMI (ST-elevation MI): immediate reperfusion is the priority.
• First 12 hours: primary PCI preferred if available within 120 min of first medical contact
• Door-to-balloon time goal: ≤ 90 min
• If PCI not available within 120 min: fibrinolysis (tenecteplase, alteplase) within 30 min of arrival
• Fibrinolysis contraindications: prior ICH, known cerebral vascular lesion, malignant intracranial neoplasm, ischemic stroke < 3 months, suspected aortic dissection, active bleeding, significant closed-head/facial trauma < 3 months

NSTEMI (non-ST elevation MI): no immediate reperfusion — risk-stratify instead.
• High-risk features (refractory angina, hemodynamic instability, sustained VT, dynamic ST changes) → urgent angiography (< 2 hours)
• Intermediate-risk (TIMI ≥ 3, GRACE 109-140, renal dysfunction) → early invasive (< 24 hours)
• Low-risk → selective angiography after non-invasive testing

Pharmacotherapy for ALL ACS (MONA-B + dual antiplatelet + anticoagulation):
M — Morphine (pain, anxiety, preload reduction) — but may delay antiplatelet absorption
O — Oxygen (only if SpO2 < 90%)
N — Nitrates (SL NTG, IV NTG for ongoing pain)
A — Aspirin 162-325 mg chewed
B — Beta blocker (carvedilol, metoprolol) — UNLESS acute HF, hypotension, bradycardia, severe COPD
+ P2Y12 inhibitor (ticagrelor preferred, clopidogrel if high bleed risk)
+ Anticoagulation (UFH, enoxaparin, bivalirudin)
+ High-intensity statin (atorvastatin 80 mg)',0,1,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.863Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.863Z','Primary PCI vs fibrinolysis — when and how.','["Identify door-to-balloon time targets","List absolute contraindications to fibrinolysis","Differentiate STEMI from NSTEMI management"]','["emergency","guidelines"]',18);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentcm000tq0r8864i9qna','cmusentcj000sq0r8woz7x6o5','1. First-Line Agents','Drug Class Cheat Sheet','First-line agents with compelling indications','text','','First-line antihypertensive classes (per JNC8 / ACC-AHA 2017):

1. ACEi (Lisinopril) / ARB (Losartan)
   • Compelling indications: diabetes, CKD, heart failure, post-MI
   • Side effects: dry cough (ACEi), hyperkalemia, ↑ Cr (initial 30% bump is OK), angioedema (ACEi > ARB)
   • CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy (teratogenic — fetal renal agenesis)
   • NEVER combine ACEi + ARB (no added benefit, ↑ harm)

2. Calcium Channel Blockers
   • Dihydropyridines (amlodipine, nifedipine): vasodilators → peripheral edema, headache, reflex tachycardia
   • Non-dihydropyridines (diltiazem, verapamil): HR & contractility ↓ → constipation (verapamil), avoid in HFrEF
   • Compelling indication: older adults, African-American patients, angina

3. Thiazide diuretics (chlorthalidone > HCTZ)
   • Compelling indication: older adults, African-American patients, osteoporosis (thiazides retain Ca)
   • Side effects: hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperuricemia (precipitates gout), hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia
   • Avoid: gout, severe hyponatremia

Second-line (only if ≥ 3 first-line agents at max dose fail):
• Spironolactone (resistant HTN — block aldosterone escape)
• Beta-blockers (NOT first-line for HTN alone unless compelling indication: post-MI, HF, AFib)
• Alpha-blockers (BPH + HTN)
• Direct vasodilators (hydralazine, minoxidil) — last resort; cause reflex tachycardia + fluid retention

Race caveat (population-level, not absolute): African-American patients often respond better to CCB + thiazide than to ACEi/ARB monotherapy. Guidelines recommend CCB or thiazide as first-line.',0,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.870Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.870Z','ACEi/ARB, CCB, thiazide — when to choose each.','["List the three first-line antihypertensive classes","Match compelling indications to specific drug classes","Identify contraindications for each class"]','["pharmacology","guidelines","high-yield"]',22);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentcx000xq0r8fy8gsa09','cmusentcu000wq0r8ifevepdc','1. Classification by Hemodynamics','Hemodynamic Profiles','The 4 shock types compared','text','','Four types of shock, distinguished by cardiac output (CO) and systemic vascular resistance (SVR):

Type                    | CO      | SVR     | PCWP    | Typical cause
────────────────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────┼──────────────────
Hypovolemic             | ↓↓      | ↑↑      | ↓↓      | Hemorrhage, dehydration
Cardiogenic             | ↓↓      | ↑↑      | ↑↑      | MI, cardiomyopathy
Obstructive             | ↓       | ↑       | ↓ or ↑  | PE, tamponade, tension PTX
Distributive (septic)   | ↑↑      | ↓↓      | ↓ or NL | Sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic

Mixed venous saturation (SvO2) helps identify septic shock early:
• Normal SvO2: 65-75%
• Septic shock: ↑ SvO2 (> 75%) — tissues can''t extract O2 (mitochondrial dysfunction)
• Cardiogenic shock: ↓ SvO2 (< 65%) — tissues extracting maximally to compensate

Key clinical clues:
• Warm extremities + hypotension = distributive (vasodilated)
• Cold extremities + hypotension = cardiogenic / hypovolemic (vasoconstricted)
• JVD + hypotension + muffled heart sounds (Beck triad) = tamponade
• JVD + hypotension + unilateral decreased breath sounds = tension pneumothorax
• Sudden hypotension + hypoxia + recent surgery or immobility = pulmonary embolism

Early goal-directed therapy (EGDT) — surviving sepsis 1-hour bundle:
1. Lactate > 2 mmol/L → measure again at 2-4 hours
2. Blood cultures × 2 BEFORE antibiotics
3. Broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour
4. Crystalloid 30 mL/kg bolus
5. Norepinephrine first-line if MAP < 65 after fluids
6. Reassess lactate clearance and MAP',0,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.881Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.881Z','The four shock types distinguished by cardiac output and systemic vascular resistance.','["Classify shock by SVR/CO profile","Identify the most likely type from clinical vignette","Predict mixed venous saturation patterns"]','["emergency","high-yield"]',18);
INSERT INTO "GroupLectureChunk" ("id","lectureId","title","sourceTitle","sourceDescription","type","url","textContent","duration","order","createdAt","updatedAt","sectionDescription","sectionObjectives","sectionTags","estimatedMinutes") VALUES ('cmusentdm000zq0r8oe6k8y0z','cmusentd1000yq0r86i313at3','1. Acute Stroke Evaluation','tPA Decision Tree','Alteplase criteria + thrombectomy','text','','Acute stroke pathway:

1. Recognize — sudden focal neurologic deficit (FAST: Face, Arm, Speech, Time). Use NIHSS to quantify severity (0 = normal, 21+ = severe).

2. Last known well (LKW) — critical timestamp. Treatment windows are from LKW, not from arrival.

3. Non-contrast CT head FIRST — rule out hemorrhage (blood is bright on CT). Do NOT give tPA before CT.

4. Ischemic stroke treatment options:

   A. IV Alteplase (tPA) — 0.9 mg/kg (max 90 mg), 10% bolus over 1 min, rest over 60 min
      • Window: within 4.5 hours of LKW
      • Absolute contraindications:
        - Prior ICH
        - Known cerebral vascular lesion (AVM, aneurysm)
        - Intracranial neoplasm
        - Active internal bleeding
        - Recent intracranial/intraspinal surgery (< 3 mo)
        - Ischemic stroke within 3 months
        - Suspected aortic dissection
        - Platelets < 100k, INR > 1.7, anticoagulant use with elevated levels
      • Relative contraindications (weigh risks):
        - Severe neurologic deficit (NIHSS > 25 — low benefit, high bleed risk)
        - Seizure at onset (was it really a stroke?)
        - Pregnancy
        - Age > 80 (still treat, but acknowledge higher bleed risk)

   B. Mechanical thrombectomy (MT)
      • Window: within 24 hours of LKW for large vessel occlusion (LVO) in anterior circulation
      • Selection: NIHSS ≥ 6 + LVO on CTA (ICA, M1, M2) + ASPECTS ≥ 6 on CT
      • Even after tPA, MT candidates proceed to thrombectomy (tPA doesn''t dissolve large clots well)
      • Mismatch between CT perfusion (core vs penumbra) guides extended window 6-24h (DAWN/DEFUSE-3 criteria)

5. Hemorrhagic stroke management:
   • Reverse anticoagulation (PCC for warfarin, andexanet for DOACs, protamine for heparin)
   • BP control: SBP 140-160 mmHg (don''t drop too fast)
   • Neurosurgery consult — ICH > 30 mL or cerebellar hemorrhage > 3 cm may need evacuation
   • Monitor ICP — head of bed 30°, sedation, osmotic therapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline)',0,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:58.906Z','2026-10-03T13:05:58.906Z','The first 60 minutes — recognition, imaging, and treatment decisions.','["List tPA inclusion and exclusion criteria","Identify thrombectomy-eligible patients","Differentiate stroke mimics"]','["emergency","neurology","high-yield"]',25);

