INSERT INTO "KnowledgeDocument" ("id","sourceId","title","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","chunkCount","createdAt") VALUES ('cmuyygrw00008ydma56an9hhe','cmuyygery0003ydma3bobkh6a','e2e-lecture.md','text/markdown',753,'# Adrenal Cortex Physiology

The adrenal cortex has three zones: zona glomerulosa (aldosterone), zona fasciculata (cortisol),
and zona reticularis (androgens). Cortisol follows a diurnal rhythm, peaks at 8 AM, and is
released under ACTH control from the anterior pituitary. Cushing syndrome results from chronic
glucocorticoid excess: central obesity, moon facies, buffalo hump, purple striae, hypertension,
and hyperglycemia. The dexamethasone suppression test distinguishes ACTH-dependent causes.
Conn syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism) causes resistant hypertension with hypokalemia and
metabolic alkalosis. Addison disease is primary adrenal insufficiency: fatigue, hyperpigmentation,
hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and low cortisol with HIGH ACTH.
',1,'2026-10-08T03:06:59.760Z');
INSERT INTO "KnowledgeDocument" ("id","sourceId","title","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","chunkCount","createdAt") VALUES ('cmuyzuwt70003ewma7fnk41m8','cmuyzuuww0002ewma8ndzgi1c','e2e-diabetes.md','text/markdown',723,'# Diabetes Mellitus Core Notes

Type 1 diabetes is autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells → absolute insulin
deficiency, young onset, ketoacidosis risk. Type 2 is insulin resistance with progressive
beta-cell decline. Diagnostic criteria: fasting glucose >= 126 mg/dL, HbA1c >= 6.5%,
random glucose >= 200 with symptoms, or OGTT 2h >= 200. Metformin is first-line for T2DM
(decreases hepatic gluconeogenesis, GI side effects, lactic acidosis risk in renal failure).
SGLT2 inhibitors cause glycosuria, weight loss, and cardiovascular/renal benefit.
GLP-1 agonists slow gastric emptying and promote satiety. DKA: hyperglycemia, ketosis,
metabolic acidosis — treat with IV fluids, insulin, potassium replacement.
',1,'2026-10-08T03:45:58.939Z');
INSERT INTO "KnowledgeDocument" ("id","sourceId","title","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","chunkCount","createdAt") VALUES ('cmv6wr5ae0001j6rm95jznclm','cmv6wqust0000j6rmojonyoak','prevention-of-transmission.pdf','application/pdf',24135,'Training workshop on screening, diagnosis and treatment of

hepatitis B and C
Viral hepatitis transmission and prevention

Session 5
Learning objectives
At the end of this sessions, participants will understand the following:

• Modes of transmission of the various hepatitis viruses (hepatitis A to hepatitis E)

• Strategies for prevention of transmission of these viruses At the end of this sessions, participants should be able to understand the following:

• Modes of transmission of various hepatitis viruses (hepatitis A to hepatitis E)

• Strategies for prevention of transmission of these viruses

HAV HBV HCV HDV HEV

Virus family Picornaviridae Hepadnaviridae Flaviviridae Deltaviridae Hepeviridae Chronicity rate No 5% of adults
90% of infants

~75% Co-infection of
HBV; modifies course
Very rare
Hepatocellular carcinoma

No Yes Yes Yes No

Route of transmission
Person-to person
Foodborne
Waterborne
Perinatal
Bloodborne
Sexual
Bloodborne
(perinatal)
(sexual)
Bloodborne
Sexual
(perinatal)
Waterborne
Foodborne
Person-to-person

Vaccine Yes Yes No HBV vaccine Not generally
(licensed in
China)
Treatment options
None Available -lifelong treatment

Available -CURE
No treatment, if
HBV–HDV coinfected,

treat as for HBV
None
Main features of the hepatitis viruses
This slide summarizes the several clinical features of the hepatitis viruses from HAV to HEV in general.
There are some differences among these viruses regarding chronicity rate, complication of hepatocellular carcinoma and routes of transmission.
We shall see that only hepatitis B is a DNA virus; the rest are all RNA viruses.
Hence, hepatitis B virus, akin to other DNA viruses such as CMV, HSV, etc. if it enters the human body, its DNA gets integrated with human DNA and remains inside the host body for the rest of his/her life.
Hepatitis viruses: Transmission

Hepatitis A
B C D*

Hepatitis E
Oral-faecal (enteric) transmission
Oral-faecal (enteric) transmission
Blood-borne (parenteral), perinatal, sexual transmission

* Hepatitis D: can only exist in

the presence of Hepatitis B
For ease of understanding, hepatitis A and E viruses can be grouped together because both of them are transmitted through the faecal–oral route by consumption of contaminated food and water; cause acute viral hepatitis and acute liver failure, and do not cause chronic viral hepatitis, liver cirrhosis or hepatocellular carcinoma. In rare cases, HEV can cause chronic hepatitis.
Similarly, hepatitis B, C and D can be grouped together because all of them are transmitted through the parenteral route; further, all these viruses cause chronic hepatitis, liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.
Parenteral transmission of hepatitis viruses

• Bloodborne

– Transfusion: blood or blood products (and organ transplant)

– Other nosocomial routes

• Dialysis

• Unsafe injections, needle-stick injury

• Surgery, dental procedures, etc.

• Organ transplantation

– Sharing of needles among persons who inject drugs (PWID)

• Sexual

• Perinatal (mother to child)

• Possible horizontal transmission (children, household contacts)
Parenteral transmission of viruses occurs following exposure through transfusion of contaminated blood or blood products, unprotected sex, in utero transmission from a pregnant woman to her baby, and possible horizontal transmission.
It is important to know that hepatitis viruses can be transmitted through transfusion, dialysis, unsafe injections, needle-stick injury, surgery, dental procedures, organ transplantation or sharing of needles among persons who inject drugs because these are also interventional points.

• Sporadic
Occurrence of scattered cases at irregular intervals

• Epidemic (=outbreak*)
Occurrence of a larger-than-usual number of cases of a disease in a community over a short period of time
*Sometimes, use of the term “epidemic” is limited to only larger outbreaks.
Enterically transmitted hepatitis
Enterically transmitted viral hepatitis could present as either sporadic hepatitis, which means the occurrence of scattered cases at irregular intervals, or in the form of an epidemic, which means the occurrence of a large number of cases of a disease in a small geographical region over a short period of time.
Enterically transmitted hepatitis

• Endemic
An infection is said to be endemic in a population when it is constantly maintained at a baseline level in a geographical area without external input

• Degree of endemicity of an infection may vary

– Very highly endemic

– Highly endemic

– Intermediate endemicity

– Low endemicity
A health condition, specially those of infective etiology, in a geographical region is said to be endemic if it occurs commonly round the year without external input.
Depending upon the disease frequency in a region, the region may be categorized as low endemic, intermediate endemic, highly endemic and very highly endemic. The cut-offs to define these endemicity categories vary from disease to disease and are not universally constant.

Hepatitis A
Then, please let me explain some features of each virus. At first, about hepatitis A virus.
Hepatitis A virus

• Excreted in faeces of infected persons

• Enters a new host through the intestinal tract
(enteric or faecal–oral transmission)
Close personal contact
Contamination of food
(e.g. at farm level)
Contamination of food
(e.g. food handlers)
Contaminated drinking water
An infected person excretes hepatitis A virus in the faeces. If the water contaminated with the virus containing faeces comes in contact with food and water the virus can be transmitted to a non-infected person.
Faeces-contaminated water could come in contact with food at several points, such as during irrigation of crops, washing of raw food, contamination of drinking water because of a breach in safe water supply.
Further, hepatitis A can also be transmitted from person to person by close personal contacts.
Age and hepatitis A virus infection
Source: Aggarwal R and Goel A. Hepatitis A: epidemiology in resource-poor countries. Curr Opin Infect Dis 2015, 28:488–496

• The outcome of HAV infection is determined primarily by the age at the time of infection

• In young children, the infection is usually asymptomatic

• Older children and adults: more likely to have symptomatic infection
All those infected with HAV do not develop clinical illness. The majority of HAV-infected persons remain asymptomatic or develop a non-specific minor illness that usually goes unnoticed.
The clinical illness is marked by the development of jaundice coupled with a marked elevation of serum levels of liver enzymes, namely, ALT and AST.
The risk of developing clinical illness is primarily determined by the age of the person at the time of infection.
As the age of the host increases, the risk of clinical disease and severe illness increases.
Generally, in young children, the infection is usually asymptomatic, but older children and adults have symptomatic infection.
Age and hepatitis A virus
Source: Aggarwal R and Goel A. Hepatitis A: epidemiology in resource-poor countries. Curr Opin Infect Dis 2015, 28:488–496 Anti-HAV antibody persists for life once it develops and hence the seroprevalence of anti-HAV antibodies keeps on increasing with age. The most likely reason for the lifelong presence of anti-HAV is repeated exposure to the virus, in particular, in those who are living in HAV-endemic countries where drinking water quality is compromised.
Based on the anti-HAV seroprevalence, countries in the world are divided into those with low, intermediate, high or very high endemicity. In highly endemic regions, almost 100% of children have been exposed to HAV by the age of 10 years. As we know, the majority of infections in children are asymptomatic. Hence, in highly endemic countries, the majority of infections occur in children and are not severe.
In countries with low endemicity, where exposure occurs mainly in adults, clinical disease usually occurs in adults and is severe.
Other routes of transmission

• Sexual activity (e.g. oral–anal sex)
HAV can be transmitted by sexual activity. This mode of transmission is most common among adults in developed countries where endemicity of HAV infection is low. The reason for this we have just discussed in the previous slide.

Prevention of hepatitis A

• Personal hygiene

– Handwashing

• Food hygiene

– Prevent faecal contamination

– Food handlers need attention

• Prevention of water contamination

• Hepatitis A vaccine

– Pre-exposure/universal childhood

– Post-exposure
To prevent faecal–oral transmission, personal hygiene, food hygiene and prevention of water contamination is needed.
Vaccination is recommended for individuals without anti-hepatitis A antibody who plan to go to a highly endemic area.
Hepatitis E virus
Next is hepatitis E virus, which is also transmitted through consumption of contaminated food and water.
Hepatitis E virus
Recently, two detailed documents on hepatitis E virus infection have been published by WHO.
The first one contains a comprehensive description of the seroprevalence of HEV in different countries across the world.
The second one is about HEV outbreak investigation and control. HEV outbreaks are very frequent in developing countries, in particular, in south Asian countries.

Hepatitis E virus: Epidemiology

• Two epidemiological patterns

• Hyperendemic pattern (a public health problem)

– Many parts of Asia and Africa

– Developing countries

– Occurs as large outbreaks and frequent sporadic cases

– Primarily through faecal contamination of water

• Low-endemicity pattern (needs ongoing surveillance in case of outbreaks)

– Europe, North America, Japan, etc.

– Developed countries

– Occasional sporadic cases – mostly in elderly persons, those with other diseases, including immunosuppressed persons There are two epidemiological patterns of hepatitis E virus.
In Asian and African countries, where HEV is hyperendemic, large outbreaks are frequent. In these regions, ...[TRUNCATED]',36,'2026-10-13T16:41:13.862Z');
INSERT INTO "KnowledgeDocument" ("id","sourceId","title","mimeType","sizeBytes","content","chunkCount","createdAt") VALUES ('cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b','cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz','Peditric-Med.pdf','application/pdf',446068,'Pediatrics | Approach to Upper & Lower GIT Bleeding in Children S Amber boxes = Clarifications Note | H = High-yield / exam-priority point | Green = Further Information

1. Introduction & Why Upper vs. Lower Matters
Acute GI bleeding is a potentially life-threatening abdominal emergency and remains a common cause of pediatric hospitalization. Upper GI bleeding (UGIB) occurs in 1 to 2 per 10,000 children per year, while lower GI bleeding (LGIB) accounts for approximately one-fifth of all cases of acute GI bleeding. Knowing which category a bleed falls into is not academic — each has its own likely causes, work-up, and emergency plan, so being systematic and skilled at recognizing which is which changes how quickly a child gets the right treatment.
(Romano C, et al. World J Gastroenterol 2017.)

S Clarifications Note
A related, related bleed can look "similar" in front of you but be completely different in origin — the whole point of separating Upper vs.
Lower is that a skilled, systematic approach at the point of first contact changes the outcome, because behind every bleed there is either one straightforward cause or two things happening at once that must both be caught.

Key Definitions
Upper GI Bleeding (UGIB) = bleeding derived from a source proximal to the ligament of Treitz (esophagus, stomach, or duodenum).
Lower GI Bleeding (LGIB) = bleeding distal to the ligament of Treitz (located at the duodenojejunal junction) — includes the small bowel, colon, rectum, and anus.

2. Signs & Symptoms of GIT Bleeding

DIRECT EVIDENCE OF BLEEDING

• Hematemesis

• Melena

• Hematochezia

• Jaundice

ASSOCIATED / HEMODYNAMIC SIGNS

• Syncope

• Presyncope

• Dyspepsia

• Epigastric pain

• Heartburn

• Diffuse abdominal pain

• Dysphagia

• Weight loss

Clarifications Note
Melena — a black, tarry, "digested" stool. It signals a slow bleed, most often from small varices in the upper tract that leak slowly rather than gush.
Syncope — fainting from a sudden, large-volume loss; think massive bleeding when this is present.
Vague abdominal pain, presence of not vomited blood — the child has abdominal pain and cannot tell where as the blood has not come up yet (vomited) and still inside.
Hematemesis — always assume this is frank (obvious, unmistakable) blood in the vomitus.
Fresh bleeding per rectum (hematochezia) — if the bleeding is upper GI but severe and very rapid, fresh (undigested) blood can still reach and pass through the rectum, because transit is too fast for the blood to be digested into melena.
Jaundice — whenever bleeding is seen together with jaundice, this is an easy exam question: the underlying cause is usually chronic liver disease. Portal hypertension leads to esophageal varices, and jaundice found alongside those varices means liver cirrhosis.

3. Causes of Upper GIT Bleeding by Age
The differential for UGIB is strongly age-dependent — a newborn, a toddler, and an older child each have a different most-likely cause list.
INFANTS (incl. NICU)
Stomach

• Gastritis from stress
Variable location

• Vitamin K deficiency

• Sepsis

• Trauma (NG tubes)

• CMPA

2–5 YEARS
Esophagus

• Esophagitis

• Esophageal varices

• Mallory-Weiss syndrome
Stomach

• Gastritis

• Gastric ulcer

• Gastric varices
Duodenum

• Duodenitis

• Duodenal ulcer
Variable location

• Caustic ingestions

• Foreign bodies

• NSAIDs use

OLDER CHILDREN
Esophagus

• Esophagitis

• Mallory-Weiss syndrome

• Esophageal varices

• Dieulafoy lesion
Stomach

• PHG

• Hemobilia
Variable location

• Polyps

• Crohn''s disease

• Telangiectasia

• Aortoenteric fistula

• Coagulation disorders

• Caustic ingestions

• Foreign bodies

• NSAIDs use

S Clarifications Note
Newborns / NICU babies are more likely to bleed for reasons unique to that age: Septicemia — sepsis-related DIC can start with the baby vomiting blood. Stress ulcer — a gastric ulcer that develops from the physiologic stress of critical illness. Cow’s milk allergy — sensitization to the proteins found in cow milk; the mother eating dairy products and then breastfeeding can transfer that sensitization into her milk, and the baby then bleeds because of it. Vitamin K deficiency — hemorrhagic disease of the newborn — babies who did not receive vitamin K at birth, or who had a difficult delivery, can develop bleeding because vitamin K stores at birth are already very low.

Further Information — Working Backward From a UVC History to Gastric Varices A useful "detective" case pattern for gastric varices in a 2-year-old presenting with a first episode of hematemesis: always check the birth history for whether the child had a prolonged NICU stay with an umbilical vein catheter (UVC). The key question is whether that line was ever properly heparinized — if it was left in place too long without correct heparinization, it can cause portal vein thrombosis. That clot raises pressure in the portal vein (portal hypertension), and the clinical result is gastric varices — which is exactly what can explain a sudden bleed at age two with no other obvious cause. In short: the story behind the catheter IS the clinical explanation for how a small child’s stomach and bowels ended up bleeding.

Clarifications Note
Caustic ingestion vs. herpetic stomatitis — a case to tell apart: a 4-year-old with mouth ulcers, refusing feeds and refusing anything except water, was first treated as herpes with anti-virals, but this was wrong. The real clue was on the mother’s side: she said she had been making soap at home and stored it in a repurposed cooking-oil bottle, kept for washing potatoes. The child drank from it by mistake.
Two features gave away that this was caustic ingestion rather than herpes: the soap was odorless and colorless, so nothing about it warned the child, and the child developed corneal opacity — the eyes were affected too, and the whole esophagus was severely irritated by the time it was caught.
What to do when a caustic ingestion presents with severe inflammation: antiviral treatment for a presumed herpes diagnosis is not the answer. The correct approach is early endoscopy to see and document the ulcerations, because a badly burned esophagus that later strictures may eventually need a colonic transplant — using a segment of colon to replace the damaged part of the esophagus.
NSAIDs: prolonged use can cause peptic ulceration and bleeding.
Mallory-Weiss syndrome: a tear in the lower esophagus resulting from forceful, repeated vomiting.

4. Apt-Downey Test — Neonatal vs. Maternal Blood
In newborns with suspected UGIB, an alkali denaturation (Apt-Downey) test can differentiate neonatal blood from swallowed maternal blood.

S Clarifications Note
How to tell whose blood it is when a newborn brings up blood in the nursery: the blood could be the baby’s own, or maternal blood swallowed during delivery (or from a cracked nipple/fissure during breastfeeding). A blood sample is taken and mixed with an alkali denaturation solution. Fetal hemoglobin (HbF), the baby’s hemoglobin, is resistant to denaturation and stays pink/red. Adult hemoglobin (the mother’s) denatures and turns brown or greenish in color. So a pink result = the baby’s own blood; a brown/green result = swallowed maternal blood.

5. History Taking
From the lecture file — age- and etiology-specific questions Ask about acuteness or chronicity of bleeding, color and quantity of blood in stools or emesis, antecedent symptoms, history of straining, abdominal pain, and trauma.
Melena, rather than bright red blood per rectum, is usually a sign of bleeding from a source proximal to the ligament of Treitz. However, massive upper GI bleeding can still produce bright red blood per rectum if GI transit time is rapid.
Intestinal malrotation is suspected with the sudden onset of melena combined with bilious emesis in a previously healthy, nondistended baby.
A history of vomiting, diarrhea, fever, ill contacts, or travel suggests an infectious etiology.
Bloody diarrhea with signs of obstruction suggests volvulus, intussusception, or necrotizing enterocolitis, particularly in premature infants.
Milk or soy enterocolitis (allergic colitis) causes vomiting with blood staining after these products are introduced into the diet.
Recurrent or forceful vomiting is associated with Mallory-Weiss tears.
A family history or NSAID use may suggest ulcer disease.
Recent jaundice, easy bruising, and changes in stool color may signal liver disease.
Other evidence of coagulation abnormalities may also point to disorders of the kidney or reticuloendothelial system.

Clarifications Note
Essential opening questions for ANY GI bleed, regardless of source: what technology/device was used to see the blood (did you actually see it, or is this reported)? What was the exact amount in mL — not "a cup" or "a diaper," a real volume? Did the family bring a photo or a soiled item with them, or is this a verbal report only? This determines your triage. Also ask specifically about how the blood/stool looked, whether this has happened before, and whether the child has known varices or a known indwelling line (e.g. a UVC), since that changes the differential immediately.
If bleeding is coming from more than one orifice at once (what the doctor called "bleeding from other orifices"), always think coagulation deficiency first — it is unlikely that a child would develop several completely separate, unrelated bleeding sources all at the same time.
Always specifically ask about cow’s milk exposure as a possible cause, since it is a common one: has the child had any milk products, and did the bleeding start around the first time milk was introduced or not?

6. Physical Examination
Signs of shock.
Nasal polyps, and oropharyngeal erosions from caustics and other ingestions.
Abdominal tenderness, with or without a mass, raises suspicion of intussusception or ischemia.
Hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, jaundice, or caput medusa suggests liver disease and subsequent portal hypertension.
Inspection of the perianal a...[TRUNCATED]',675,'2026-10-13T16:42:59.073Z');
