INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('demo-q-cardiology-1','mcq','A 65-year-old man presents with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to the left arm, diaphoresis, and nausea. ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Unstable angina","Anterior wall myocardial infarction","Pericarditis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism"]','Anterior wall myocardial infarction','ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4 corresponds to the anterior wall of the left ventricle, supplied by the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The clinical presentation of crushing substernal chest pain with radiation to the left arm, diaphoresis, and nausea is classic for acute myocardial infarction. This is an anterior wall STEMI requiring immediate reperfusion therapy.','V1-V4 = Anterior = LAD = ''LADdies first''','ST elevation in V1-V4 = anterior STEMI = LAD artery occlusion. Time is muscle – immediate reperfusion is critical!','{"Unstable angina":"No ST elevation; pain at rest but no biomarker elevation","Pericarditis":"Diffuse ST elevation and PR depression; positional pain","Aortic dissection":"Tearing pain radiating to back; pulse deficits","Pulmonary embolism":"Pleuritic pain, dyspnea, tachycardia; no ST elevation in V1-V4"}','Medium','Cardiovascular','Myocardial Infarction','["cardiology","ECG","STEMI","emergency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:56.162Z','2026-10-03T13:05:56.162Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('demo-q-pharmacology-1','mcq','A 45-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation is started on warfarin. She presents 2 weeks later with a significantly elevated INR of 8.5. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?','["Discontinue warfarin and observe","Discontinue warfarin and administer vitamin K orally","Discontinue warfarin and administer fresh frozen plasma","Discontinue warfarin and administer prothrombin complex concentrate","Continue warfarin at a reduced dose"]','Discontinue warfarin and administer vitamin K orally','For an INR > 10 without serious bleeding, the recommended management is to discontinue warfarin and administer oral vitamin K (phytonadione) 2.5-5 mg. Vitamin K reverses the anticoagulant effect of warfarin by enabling the hepatic synthesis of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X). For life-threatening bleeding, FFP or PCC would be indicated instead.','Warfarin affects factors 2, 7, 9, 10 (1972 = year of their discovery). Vitamin K reverses it.','INR > 10 without bleeding: hold warfarin + oral vitamin K. Life-threatening bleed: PCC/FFP + IV vitamin K.','{"Observe only":"Too passive for INR 8.5; active reversal needed","FFP":"Reserved for serious bleeding; vitamin K sufficient here","PCC":"Reserved for life-threatening bleeding; overkill for elevated INR without bleed","Continue at reduced dose":"Dangerous with INR 8.5; must hold the drug"}','Hard','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants','["pharmacology","warfarin","anticoagulation","toxicology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:56.167Z','2026-10-03T13:05:56.167Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('demo-q-neurology-1','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with sudden-onset severe headache, neck stiffness, and photophobia. Non-contrast CT of the head shows hyperdensity in the subarachnoid space. What is the most common cause of spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage?','["Arteriovenous malformation","Ruptured berry aneurysm","Hypertensive hemorrhage","Trauma","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy"]','Ruptured berry aneurysm','The most common cause of spontaneous (non-traumatic) subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) is rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm, accounting for approximately 80% of cases. Berry aneurysms most commonly occur at bifurcations of the Circle of Willis, particularly the anterior communicating artery. The classic presentation is a ''thunderclap'' headache described as ''the worst headache of my life.''','SAH = Sudden Awful Headache = Berry aneurysm at bifurcations of Circle of Willis','SAH = worst headache of life + berry aneurysm (80%). Most common site: ACom artery. Hunt-Hess grading guides prognosis.','{"AVM":"Only 2% of SAH; more common in young patients with seizures","Hypertensive hemorrhage":"Typically causes intraparenchymal bleed (basal ganglia, pons, cerebellum)","Trauma":"Most common cause overall, but question specifies ''spontaneous''","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy":"Causes lobar hemorrhages in elderly, not typically SAH"}','Easy','Nervous','Subarachnoid Hemorrhage','["neurology","SAH","aneurysm","emergency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:56.168Z','2026-10-03T13:05:56.168Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('demo-q-endocrine-1','mcq','A 30-year-old woman presents with weight gain, moon facies, central obesity, purple striae, and easy bruising. Laboratory findings show elevated 24-hour urinary cortisol and loss of normal diurnal cortisol rhythm. A high-dose dexamethasone suppression test shows partial suppression of cortisol. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Adrenal adenoma","Ectopic ACTH secretion","Cushing disease (pituitary adenoma)","Pseudo-Cushing syndrome","Nelson syndrome"]','Cushing disease (pituitary adenoma)','The clinical presentation is classic for Cushing syndrome. The key differentiator is the high-dose dexamethasone suppression test: partial suppression of cortisol suggests a pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease), as these tumors retain some responsiveness to negative feedback. Adrenal adenomas and ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors would NOT suppress with high-dose dexamethasone. Cushing disease accounts for approximately 70% of endogenous Cushing syndrome cases.','Cushing Disease = Pituitary ADENOMA (suppresses with high-dose dex) vs. Cushing Syndrome = any cause of excess cortisol','High-dose dex suppression: Cushing disease suppresses, ectopic/adrenal does not. Always check ACTH first to differentiate!','{"Adrenal adenoma":"No suppression with high-dose dex; ACTH would be LOW","Ectopic ACTH":"No suppression with high-dose dex; ACTH very high; often bronchial carcinoid or SCLC","Pseudo-Cushing":"Caused by alcohol/depression; normal with dex-CRH test","Nelson syndrome":"Occurs after bilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing; enlarging pituitary + hyperpigmentation"}','Hard','Endocrine','Cushing Syndrome','["endocrine","cushing","pituitary","dexamethasone"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:56.171Z','2026-10-03T13:05:56.171Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-endo-1','mcq','A 19-year-old with T1DM presents with vomiting, Kussmaul respirations, glucose 580 mg/dL, pH 7.20, HCO3 8 mEq/L. What is the most important initial therapy?','["IV insulin bolus only","IV normal saline + IV insulin","IV bicarbonate","Subcutaneous insulin","IV dextrose + insulin"]','IV normal saline + IV insulin','DKA management: 1) IV fluids (NS) — restore volume, 2) IV insulin (no bolus needed if infusion used), 3) K+ replacement when <5.2. Bicarbonate only if pH <6.9.','Volume first, then insulin. Always replace K+ BEFORE insulin if K+ <3.3.','Treat DKA: "FIG-PICK" — Fluids, Insulin, Glucose (when <200), Potassium, Inspect (monitor), CK (electrolytes).','Bicarb only if pH<6.9. SQ insulin ineffective in DKA. Dextrose added only when glucose <200.','Hard','Endocrine','DKA','["dka","emergency","diabetes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.674Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.674Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqy000zder8je1s458k','mcq','At what age the infant doubles his birth weight?','["2 years old","4 years old","7 years old","12 years old"]','2 years old','','','','','Basic','Growth & Development','Growth Milestones','["growth","weight","doubling","infant"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.290Z','2026-10-03T15:41:29.290Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('demo-q-respiratory-1','mcq','A 70-year-old man with a 40-pack-year smoking history presents with chronic productive cough for 3 months per year for the past 2 years, progressive dyspnea, and wheezing. Spirometry shows FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55 and FEV1 45% of predicted. Which of the following findings is most consistent with this patient''s condition?','["Increased lung compliance and decreased recoil","Decreased lung compliance and increased recoil","Normal lung compliance with increased airway resistance","Decreased total lung capacity","Increased diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO)"]','Increased lung compliance and decreased recoil','This patient has COPD (chronic bronchitis phenotype based on chronic productive cough, plus emphysema given the smoking history and spirometry findings). In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls leads to loss of elastic recoil and increased lung compliance. The FEV1/FVC ratio < 0.7 confirms obstructive lung disease. DLCO is typically decreased (not increased) in emphysema due to loss of alveolar-capillary surface area.','Emphysema = ELASTASE destroys elastin = Loss of Elastic recoil = Enlarged airspaces','COPD = decreased recoil + increased compliance + decreased DLCO. ''Pink puffer'' (emphysema) vs ''blue bloater'' (chronic bronchitis).','{"Decreased compliance + increased recoil":"This describes restrictive lung disease (e.g., pulmonary fibrosis)","Normal compliance + increased resistance":"Oversimplification; compliance is increased in emphysema","Decreased TLC":"TLC is increased in COPD due to air trapping; decreased TLC = restrictive disease","Increased DLCO":"DLCO is decreased in emphysema; increased DLCO suggests pulmonary hemorrhage or V/Q mismatch"}','Medium','Respiratory','COPD','["respiratory","COPD","spirometry","pulmonology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:05:56.179Z','2026-10-03T13:05:56.179Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-cardio-1','mcq','A 55-year-old man has repeated BP readings of 152/96 mmHg over 3 separate office visits. He is asymptomatic, no prior CV events. What is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic therapy per JNC 8?','["Lisinopril","Hydralazine","Spironolactone","Sodium nitroprusside","Clonidine"]','Lisinopril','First-line therapy for stage 2 HTN without compelling contraindication is a thiazide, ACE inhibitor/ARB, or CCB. Lisinopril (ACE inhibitor) is appropriate.','ABCD — ACEi, Beta-blocker (post-MI), CCB, Diuretic (thiazide).','JNC 8: target <140/90 (<150/90 if ≥60). First-line = thiazide / ACEi / ARB / CCB.','Hydralazine/clonidine are 3rd-line. Spironolactone is 4th-line/resistant HTN. Nitroprusside is for hypertensive emergency.','Easy','Cardiovascular','Hypertension','["hypertension","pharmacology","guidelines"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.645Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.645Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-cardio-2','mcq','A 68-year-old woman presents with dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, and lower extremity edema. Echocardiogram shows EF 35%. Which medication has been shown to reduce mortality in this patient?','["Furosemide","Carvedilol","Digoxin","Nifedipine","Hydralazine alone"]','Carvedilol','Beta-blockers (carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol) reduce mortality in HFrEF by ~35%.','MRA + Beta + ACEi + SGLT2i = Mortality reduction.','HFrEF mortality reducers: beta-blocker + ACEi/ARB/ARNI + MRA + SGLT2i. ("Fantastic Four")','Furosemide is symptom-only (no mortality benefit). Digoxin reduces hospitalizations, not mortality. Nifedipine can worsen HF.','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure','["heart-failure","pharmacology","mortality"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.652Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.652Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-cardio-3','mcq','A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation, HTN, T2DM, and prior stroke has a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 5. What is the recommended anticoagulation?','["Aspirin 81 mg daily","Clopidogrel","Apixaban","Warfarin with INR 1.5-2.0","No anticoagulation"]','Apixaban','CHA2DS2-VASc ≥2 in men (≥3 in women) → oral anticoagulation. DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban) preferred over warfarin unless mitral stenosis or mechanical valve.','CHA2DS2-VASc: CHF, HTN, Age≥75(2), DM, Stroke(2), Vascular, Age 65-74, Sex category.','DOACs preferred over warfarin for non-valvular AFib. Avoid in mechanical valves / severe MS.','Aspirin alone is insufficient. Warfarin target INR is 2-3, not 1.5-2.0.','Moderate','Cardiovascular','AFib','["afib","anticoagulation","stroke-prevention"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.655Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.655Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-resp-1','mcq','A 25-year-old woman with asthma presents with wheezing and shortness of breath. Peak flow is 70% predicted. What is the most appropriate acute treatment?','["IV antibiotics","Albuterol nebulizer + oral corticosteroid","Intubation","Nebulized hypertonic saline","Montelukast alone"]','Albuterol nebulizer + oral corticosteroid','Acute asthma exacerbation: SABA (albuterol) ± ipratropium + systemic corticosteroid. Peak flow 50-80% = moderate exacerbation.','Asthma attack = "SAM" — Steroids, Albuterol, Magnesium (if severe).','If peak flow <33% or silent chest → ICU. Steroids take 4-6 hours to work.','Antibiotics only if bacterial infection. Montelukast is for chronic management, not acute.','Easy','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","acute","emergency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.658Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.658Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-resp-2','mcq','A 65-year-old smoker with COPD has FEV1 45% predicted and ≥2 exacerbations/year. What is the most appropriate maintenance therapy?','["Albuterol PRN only","Salmeterol monotherapy","Tiotropium + formoterol","Oral corticosteroids daily","Azithromycin daily"]','Tiotropium + formoterol','GOLD group D (high symptoms, high exacerbation risk) → LAMA + LABA is preferred. ICS added only if eos ≥300.','GOLD A/B/C/D — symptoms + exacerbations drive the choice.','COPD: LAMA+LABA > LABA+ICS for exacerbation prevention. ICS risk: pneumonia.','Daily oral steroids cause osteoporosis. Azithromycin is for frequent exacerbators as adjunct, not first-line.','Moderate','Respiratory','COPD','["copd","guidelines","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.663Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.663Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-gi-1','mcq','A 45-year-old man has heartburn 3x/week for 6 months, no alarm features. What is the most appropriate initial management?','["Endoscopy immediately","PPI trial for 8 weeks","Barium swallow","Antireflux surgery","H2 blocker PRN"]','PPI trial for 8 weeks','Typical GERD without alarm features → empiric PPI trial 8 weeks. Endoscopy reserved for alarm symptoms or refractory symptoms.','GERD without alarm = PPI. With alarm = scope.','Alarm features: dysphagia, weight loss, anemia, vomiting, GI bleed, age >50 with new onset.','Endoscopy is overkill without alarm features. H2 blockers are inferior to PPIs.','Easy','Gastrointestinal','GERD','["gerd","guidelines","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.666Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.666Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-gi-2','mcq','A 58-year-old with alcoholic cirrhosis has new-onset ascites. Diagnostic paracentesis shows SAAG ≥1.1, PMN 10 cells/mm³. What is the most appropriate next step?','["IV ceftriaxone","Spironolactone + furosemide","TIPS procedure","Large-volume paracentesis daily","Oral metronidazole"]','Spironolactone + furosemide','SAAG ≥1.1 = portal hypertension-related ascites. PMN <250 = no SBP. First-line = spironolactone + furosemide (100:40 ratio).','Ascites meds ratio: "100/40" spironolactone/furosemide. Maximizes natriuresis, minimizes K+ imbalance.','SBP = PMN ≥250. Treat with ceftriaxone. Add albumin if Cr>1, bili>4, or creatinine rising.','No SBP → no antibiotics. TIPS is for refractory ascites.','Hard','Gastrointestinal','Cirrhosis','["cirrhosis","ascites","sbp"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.670Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.670Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-endo-2','mcq','A 35-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain, TSH 12 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0), free T4 low. Anti-TPO antibodies positive. Most likely diagnosis?','["Subacute thyroiditis","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Graves disease","Toxic multinodular goiter","Iodine deficiency"]','Hashimoto thyroiditis','Primary hypothyroidism (high TSH, low T4) + anti-TPO+ = Hashimoto. Most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient regions.','Hashimoto = High TSH, Anti-TPO Positive.','Hashimoto can have transient hyperthyroid phase ("hashitoxicosis") early in disease.','Subacute thyroiditis has ESR elevation and jaw/neck pain. Graves = hyperthyroidism.','Moderate','Endocrine','Thyroid','["hypothyroidism","autoimmune","hashimoto"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.680Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.680Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-neuro-1','mcq','A 70-year-old man has sudden right-sided weakness and aphasia, last known well 90 minutes ago. CT head shows no hemorrhage. NIHSS 12. What is the most appropriate next step?','["IV tPA (alteplase)","Aspirin only","Wait for MRI","Carotid endarterectomy","Heparin infusion"]','IV tPA (alteplase)','Ischemic stroke within 4.5 hours of LKW, no hemorrhage on CT, no contraindications → IV tPA. NIHSS doesn''t determine eligibility, only time window.','Stroke time windows: tPA 4.5h, thrombectomy 24h (with imaging criteria).','tPA absolute contraindications: prior ICH, recent surgery, active bleed, BP >185/110 (must lower first).','Aspirin is given AFTER tPA (24h later). Heparin not used in acute ischemic stroke.','Moderate','Nervous','Stroke','["stroke","tPA","emergency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.682Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.682Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-neuro-2','mcq','A 28-year-old has 2 unprovoked generalized tonic-clonic seizures 3 months apart. MRI and EEG are normal. Most appropriate treatment?','["No treatment","Levetiracetam","Phenytoin","Vagus nerve stimulation","Ketogenic diet"]','Levetiracetam','Two unprovoked seizures = epilepsy. Start antiepileptic drug. Levetiracetam is first-line for generalized seizures due to favorable side effect profile.','Levetiracetam: broad spectrum, no hepatic metabolism, no drug interactions.','EEG normal in 50% of epilepsy patients — doesn''t rule out. Repeat EEG after sleep deprivation if needed.','Phenytoin has narrow therapeutic index, gingival hyperplasia, ataxia. VNS/diet are refractory options.','Moderate','Nervous','Seizures','["seizure","epilepsy","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.684Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.684Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-renal-1','mcq','A 70-year-old with CHF on furosemide develops AKI. BUN/Cr ratio 25, FENa 0.4%, urine Na 15 mEq/L. Most likely cause?','["Acute tubular necrosis","Prerenal azotemia","Postrenal obstruction","Acute interstitial nephritis","Glomerulonephritis"]','Prerenal azotemia','Prerenal: BUN/Cr >20, FENa <1%, urine Na <20, bland sediment. ATN: FENa >2%, muddy brown casts. This patient is over-diuresed + CHF.','Prerenal: BUN/Cr >20, FENa <1%, UNa <20. ATN: opposite.','Prerenal is the most common cause of AKI in hospitalized patients. Treat underlying cause.','ATN has muddy casts. AIN has eosinophils and rash/fever. Obstruction has post-void residual.','Hard','Renal','AKI','["aki","prerenal","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.707Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.707Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-heme-1','mcq','A 30-year-old vegan has fatigue. Hgb 9.5 g/dL, MCV 75 fL, ferritin 8 ng/mL. What is the most likely cause and best initial treatment?','["Anemia of chronic disease → treat underlying","Iron deficiency → oral ferrous sulfate","Thalassemia → no treatment","B12 deficiency → IM B12","Sideroblastic anemia → pyridoxine"]','Iron deficiency → oral ferrous sulfate','Microcytic anemia + ferritin <15 = iron deficiency. Vegan diet = inadequate intake. Treat with oral iron 325mg daily, recheck reticulocytes in 7-10 days.','Microcytic causes: TAILS — Thalassemia, Anemia of chronic disease, Iron deficiency, Lead poisoning, Sideroblastic.','Ferritin is an acute phase reactant — normal/high ferritin doesn''t rule out IDI if inflammation present.','B12 deficiency is macrocytic. Thalassemia has normal/high ferritin.','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","iron-deficiency","hematology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.712Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.712Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-id-1','mcq','A 65-year-old with fever, HR 120, BP 85/50, lactate 4.5 mmol/L, no clear source. What is the most appropriate initial management within the first hour?','["Wait for cultures then treat","30 mL/kg IV crystalloid + broad-spectrum antibiotics + blood cultures","Norepinephrine only","IV corticosteroids","CT scan first"]','30 mL/kg IV crystalloid + broad-spectrum antibiotics + blood cultures','SEPSIS BUNDLE (first hour): lactate, blood cultures ×2, broad-spectrum antibiotics, 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate ≥4, vasopressors if MAP <65 after fluids.','Sepsis 1-h bundle: "LAB-MASS" — Lactate, Antibiotics, Blood cultures, MAP target, Antibiotics, Saline, Stabilize.','Every hour delay in antibiotics increases mortality 7.6%. Don''t wait for cultures.','Steroids only if fluid/vasopressor refractory. CT delays antibiotics.','Hard','Infectious','Sepsis','["sepsis","bundle","emergency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.714Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.714Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-id-2','mcq','A 50-year-old IV drug user has fever, new murmur, and 3/3 blood cultures positive for Staph aureus. Echocardiogram shows vegetation on tricuspid valve. What is the most likely diagnosis and empiric therapy?','["Native valve endocarditis → nafcillin/oxacillin + gentamicin","Prosthetic valve endocarditis → vancomycin + gentamicin + rifampin","Culture-negative endocarditis → ceftriaxone","Liberman-Sacks → steroids","Marantic endocarditis → heparin"]','Native valve endocarditis → nafcillin/oxacillin + gentamicin','Native-valve S. aureus endocarditis in IVDU typically affects the tricuspid valve. Empiric therapy is nafcillin/oxacillin (MSSA) or vancomycin (MRSA) ± gentamicin for first 3-5 days.','Right-sided endocarditis = IVDU + S. aureus. Often less severe than left-sided.','Modified Duke criteria: 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor = definite.','Prosthetic valve requires vancomycin + rifampin + gentamicin. Culture-negative = Coxiella, Bartonella, HACEK.','Moderate','Infectious','Endocarditis','["endocarditis","staph-aureus","ivdu"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.716Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.716Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-rheum-1','mcq','A 45-year-old woman has symmetric morning stiffness in PIP and MCP joints >1 hour for 6 weeks. RF positive, anti-CCP positive. What is the most appropriate initial disease-modifying therapy?','["NSAIDs only","Methotrexate","Prednisone 60 mg daily","Infliximab monotherapy","Hydroxychloroquine alone"]','Methotrexate','RA with high disease activity → start DMARD within 3 months of diagnosis. Methotrexate is first-line anchor DMARD. Biologics reserved for inadequate MTX response.','RA treatment: "M" first (Methotrexate), then biologics if no response.','Anti-CCP is more specific than RF for RA. Treat-to-target strategy aims for remission/low disease activity.','NSAIDs treat symptoms, not disease. Prednisone is bridge therapy, not long-term. Biologic monotherapy is inferior to MTX combination.','Moderate','Musculoskeletal','Rheumatoid Arthritis','["ra","dmard","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.718Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.718Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hau002lqjlre52r4je5','mcq','Upper GI bleeding means   ?','["Bleeding from stomach & esophagus","Bleeding above ligament of treitz","Bleeding from small intestine","Bleeding below ligament of treitz"]','Bleeding above ligament of treitz','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["gi bleeding","upper gi","anatomy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.734Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.734Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-psych-1','mcq','A 40-year-old woman reports 4 weeks of depressed mood, anhedonia, insomnia, fatigue, poor concentration, and occasional thoughts of death without plan. What is first-line treatment?','["Cognitive behavioral therapy + SSRI","Electroconvulsive therapy","MAO inhibitor","Benzodiazepine","Antipsychotic"]','Cognitive behavioral therapy + SSRI','Moderate-to-severe MDD: first-line is SSRI (sertraline, escitalopram) ± CBT. Mild depression: psychotherapy alone may suffice.','Depression meds: "SECRET" — Sertraline, Citalopram/Escitalopram, (fluoxetine), (paroxetine).','SSRIs take 4-6 weeks for full effect. Start low, go slow. Watch for activation in young adults.','ECT is for refractory/psychotic depression. MAOIs are 3rd-line due to diet restrictions.','Easy','Psychiatry','Depression','["depression","ssri","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.722Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.722Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-peds-1','mcq','A 3-month-old infant has fever 38.5°C (101.3°F) without source, fussy but consolable. What is the appropriate workup?','["Home antipyretics only","CBC, urinalysis, blood culture ± LP","Chest X-ray only","Antibiotics and discharge","Trial of acetaminophen and return in 24h"]','CBC, urinalysis, blood culture ± LP','Febrile infant <90 days: full sepsis workup (CBC, UA, blood culture, LP if indicated). 3-12 months: depends on appearance and labs (Philadelphia/Boston criteria).','Febrile infant <90d = always full workup. 90d-24mo = risk-stratify.','Urinary tract infection is the most common bacterial infection in febrile infants <2 months.','CXR only if respiratory signs. Antibiotics without workup misses UTI/bacteremia/meningitis.','Moderate','Pediatrics','Fever','["pediatrics","fever","sepsis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.729Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.729Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-cardio-4','mcq','A 60-year-old smoker has 30 min of crushing chest pain. ECG shows ST-depressions in V1-V4. Troponin I elevated. What is the diagnosis and immediate management?','["NSTEMI → dual antiplatelet + anticoagulation + angiography within 24h","Stable angina → outpatient stress test","STEMI → immediate PCI","Pericarditis → NSAIDs","Costochondritis → acetaminophen"]','NSTEMI → dual antiplatelet + anticoagulation + angiography within 24h','NSTEMI = ischemic symptoms + troponin elevation + no ST-elevation. Treat with DAPT (aspirin + P2Y12), anticoag (heparin), and early invasive strategy (angiography ≤24h) if high-risk.','TIMI/GRACE score risk-stratifies NSTEMI for early invasive vs conservative.','STEMI = ST-elevation → immediate reperfusion (PCI ≤90 min or tPA). NSTEMI = early invasive within 24-72h.','STEMI goes to cath lab immediately. Stable angina has normal troponin.','Hard','Cardiovascular','ACS','["nstemi","acs","cardiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.733Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.733Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-cardio-5','mcq','A 75-year-old man has exertional chest pain, syncope, and heart failure. Exam: systolic ejection murmur radiating to carotids, delayed carotid pulse. Echo shows AVA 0.7 cm². What is the most appropriate management?','["AVR (aortic valve replacement)","Medical therapy with ACEi","Diuretics only","Stress test","Anticoagulation"]','AVR (aortic valve replacement)','Symptomatic severe AS (AVA <1.0 cm²) → AVR (surgical or TAVR). Medical therapy does NOT improve survival once symptomatic.','SAD = Syncope (3y), Angina (5y), Dyspnea (2y) — AS symptoms = AVR.','Once AS becomes symptomatic (SAD: Syncope, Angina, Dyspnea), median survival without AVR is 2-3 years.','ACEi can cause hypotension in AS. Vasodilators contraindicated.','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["as","valvular","tavr"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.734Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.734Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-endo-3','mcq','A 40-year-old woman has central obesity, moon facies, purple striae, hypertension, and easy bruising. 24h urine cortisol elevated, ACTH elevated, high-dose dexamethasone suppresses cortisol. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)","Adrenal adenoma","Ectopic ACTH (small cell lung cancer)","Exogenous steroid use","Pseudo-Cushing (alcoholism)"]','Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)','High ACTH + suppressible with high-dose dex = pituitary source (Cushing disease, ~70% of endogenous Cushing). Adrenal tumor = low ACTH. Ectopic ACTH = no suppression.','Cushing triad of tests: late-night salivary, 24h urine, dex suppression.','Confirm with late-night salivary cortisol, 24h urine cortisol, or 1-mg dex suppression test. Then localize with MRI.','Adrenal adenoma suppresses ACTH. Ectopic ACTH doesn''t suppress with high-dose dex.','Hard','Endocrine','Cushing Syndrome','["cushing","endocrine","pituitary"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.739Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.739Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9f000co7lrhn0f85hu','mcq','Causes of Congestive heart failure','["Anemia","Septicemia","Pneumonia","Myocardial infarction"]','Pneumonia','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure','["chf","pediatrics","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.059Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.059Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-derm-1','mcq','A 50-year-old diabetic has a rapidly spreading, warm, erythematous, edematous plaque on the lower leg with no ulceration. He is afebrile. What is the most appropriate treatment?','["Topical mupirocin","Oral cephalexin","IV vancomycin","Surgical debridement","Observation"]','Oral cephalexin','Non-purulent cellulitis in stable patient = oral cephalexin (covers strep and MSSA). IV therapy if systemic signs, rapidly progressive, immunocompromised.','Cellulitis: cephalexin (oral), cefazolin (IV). Add MRSA coverage if purulent.','Mark the border with pen to track progression. Reassess in 24-48h.','Topical doesn''t penetrate. Vancomycin is for MRSA/severe. Debridement is for necrotizing fasciitis.','Easy','Integumentary','Cellulitis','["cellulitis","skin","antibiotics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.731Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.488Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-gi-3','mcq','A 25-year-old has chronic bloody diarrhea, abdominal pain, and tenesmus. Colonoscopy shows continuous mucosal inflammation from rectum to splenic flexure. Biopsy shows crypt abscesses. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn disease","Ischemic colitis","C. difficile colitis","Microscopic colitis"]','Ulcerative colitis','Continuous inflammation from rectum (always involved) proximally, limited to mucosa/submucosa, crypt abscesses = UC. Crohn has skip lesions, transmural, can affect any part of GI.','UC: "Continuous from rectum, mucosal, bloody." Crohn: "Skip, transmural, fistulas."','PANCA+ ASCA- → UC. ASCA+ pANCA- → Crohn. UC has higher colon cancer risk with long duration.','Crohn has skip lesions and transmural inflammation. Ischemic is acute and segmental.','Hard','Gastrointestinal','IBD','["uc","ibd","colon"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.749Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.749Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-heme-2','mcq','A 60-year-old post-op patient has sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, HR 110, SpO2 92%. CT angiogram shows segmental PE. Hemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate treatment?','["Apixaban (DOAC)","IV tPA (systemic thrombolysis)","Embolectomy","IVC filter","Warfarin only"]','Apixaban (DOAC)','Submassive PE (stable BP, normal RV function) → anticoagulation. DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban) first-line. Thrombolysis reserved for massive PE (hypotension) or submassive with worsening.','PE: stable = anticoagulate. Unstable = thrombolyze. Can''t anticoagulate = IVC filter.','Wells score + d-dimer stratify risk. PE protocol: low risk → d-dimer; high risk → CTA.','Thrombolysis for massive PE only. IVC filter if anticoagulation contraindicated.','Moderate','Hematology','DVT/PE','["pe","doac","anticoagulation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.754Z','2026-10-03T13:08:31.754Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-id-3','mcq','A 28-year-old man has CD4 count 180 cells/µL and is started on ART (tenofovir + emtricitabine + dolutegravir). What opportunistic infection prophylaxis is indicated?','["TMP-SMX (Bactrim)","Azithromycin","Fluconazole","No prophylaxis needed","Acyclovir"]','TMP-SMX (Bactrim)','PCP prophylaxis (TMP-SMX) when CD4 <200. Toxoplasmosis prophylaxis added when CD4 <100 (also covered by TMP-SMX DS). MAC prophylaxis (azithromycin) when CD4 <50.','PCP <200, Toxo <100, MAC <50. Bactrim covers first two.','Start ART in all HIV+ regardless of CD4. Within 2 weeks if opportunistic infection (except TB: wait 2 weeks).','Azithromycin for MAC at CD4<50. Fluconazole for recurrent candidiasis or cryptococcus.','Moderate','Infectious','HIV','["hiv","pcp","prophylaxis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.756Z','2026-10-08T05:03:09.934Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-renal-2','mcq','A 35-year-old man has sudden onset severe flank pain radiating to groin with hematuria. Non-contrast CT shows 4 mm stone in distal ureter. What is the most appropriate management?','["Hydration + NSAIDs + tamsulosin (medical expulsive therapy)","Immediate ureteroscopy","ESWL (lithotripsy)","Percutaneous nephrostomy","IV antibiotics"]','Hydration + NSAIDs + tamsulosin (medical expulsive therapy)','Ureteral stones <5 mm pass spontaneously ~80% of time. Medical expulsive therapy (NSAIDs, hydration, alpha-blocker) for 4-6 weeks. Ureteroscopy/ESWL if >10mm, refractory pain, infection, or renal failure.','Stone size matters: <5mm pass, 5-10mm maybe, >10mm intervention.','Indications for immediate intervention: obstruction with infection, AKI, intractable pain/vomiting.','ESWL best for renal stones 1-2 cm. PCN only for sepsis with obstruction.','Easy','Renal','Stones','["nephrolithiasis","ureter","urology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.750Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.484Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqv000xder8emyrzxah','mcq','Children should be measured lying down until which age?','["9 months","12 months","18 months","24 months"]','24 months','','','','','Basic','Growth & Development','Measurement','["measurement","anthropometry","position"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.287Z','2026-10-03T15:41:29.287Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqw000yder8cizzpfna','mcq','If a child''s weight is on the 25th centile it means that:','["25% children are lighter","25% children are heavier","There has been poor weight gain since birth","The child is undernourished"]','25% children are lighter','','','','','Basic','Growth & Development','Growth Charts','["growth chart","centile","weight"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.288Z','2026-10-03T15:41:29.288Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqk000oder862gxgj9b','mcq','Child stand freely, Walks well, Run well. The child''s age is:','["2 years"]','2 years','<p><strong>Why 2 years is correct:</strong> By 2 years of age, children typically achieve several major motor milestones including standing freely without support, walking well with a steady gait, and running well with coordination. This represents the culmination of gross motor development that begins with pulling to stand at around 9-12 months, walking independently at 12-15 months, and progresses to running with good balance by 18-24 months. The ability to run well specifically indicates sufficient neuromuscular maturation and balance control that is characteristic of the 2-year-old developmental stage.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. 2 years:</strong> This is correct, so no explanation needed.</li>
<li><strong>B. 1 year:</strong> At 1 year, most children are just beginning to walk independently but cannot run well or stand freely with the stability of a 2-year-old.</li>
<li><strong>C. 3 years:</strong> While 3-year-olds can certainly run well, they typically have already mastered more advanced skills like jumping and climbing stairs alternating feet, which aren''t mentioned in the stem.</li>
<li><strong>D. 6 months:</strong> At 6 months, infants typically sit with support and may begin to sit independently, but they cannot stand freely or walk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The sequence of motor development follows a predictable cephalocaudal and proximodistal pattern.</li>
<li>Running well typically emerges between 18-24 months, marking a significant advancement in balance and coordination.</li>
<li>The ability to run requires integration of multiple systems including vestibular function, proprioception, and muscle strength.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Running well with good balance is a hallmark of the 2-year-old developmental stage, occurring after independent walking but before more advanced skills like jumping.</p>','<p>SWSR: Sit (6 months), Walk (1 year), Stand freely (18 months), Run well (2 years)</p>','<p>When assessing development, always consider the sequence of milestones rather than isolated achievements, as this provides more accurate age assessment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>1 year:</strong> At 1 year, children are just beginning to walk independently but cannot run well.</li>
<li><strong>3 years:</strong> 3-year-olds have typically mastered more advanced skills like jumping and stair climbing.</li>
<li><strong>6 months:</strong> At 6 months, infants sit with support but cannot stand or walk.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Growth & Development','Motor Development','["motor","development","walking","running"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.276Z','2026-10-18T19:27:28.173Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqs000vder827lkdbxj','mcq','Which of the following is a sign of abnormal gross motor development?','["Running at 2 years of age","Pull to stand at 9-12 months.","Crawling at 18 months.","Pedaling a tricycle at 3 years old."]','Crawling at 18 months.','<p><strong>Why Crawling at 18 months is correct:</strong> Crawling at 18 months is abnormal as most children achieve this milestone between 7-10 months of age. Delayed crawling beyond 12 months may indicate neuromuscular disorders, developmental delays, or environmental factors limiting movement opportunities. This delay suggests possible issues with muscle strength, coordination, or neurological development that warrant further evaluation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Running at 2 years:</strong> Running is typically achieved between 15-18 months, so running at 2 years is within normal variation. - <strong>Pull to stand at 9-12 months:</strong> This is a normal developmental milestone achieved within the expected timeframe. - <strong>Pedaling a tricycle at 3 years old:</strong> This represents normal gross motor development for this age group. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Normal gross motor development follows a predictable sequence: rolling, sitting, crawling, standing, walking, running. - Developmental milestones have a range of normal, but significant delays (more than 25% of the expected timeframe) warrant evaluation. - Abnormal motor patterns (e.g., toe walking, scissoring) are more concerning than isolated delays in achieving specific milestones. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Crawling should be achieved by 12 months; persistence beyond this age indicates possible abnormal gross motor development.</p>','<p>Crawl by one, walk by two, run by three</p>','<p>When evaluating motor delays, always consider the child''s overall development and environmental factors, as some delays may be due to limited opportunities for movement rather than neurological issues.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Running at 2 years:</strong> Running is typically achieved by 15-18 months, making 2 years within normal variation. - <strong>Pull to stand at 9-12 months:</strong> This is a normal developmental milestone achieved within the expected timeframe. - <strong>Pedaling a tricycle at 3 years old:</strong> This represents normal gross motor development for this age group.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Motor Development','["motor","development","abnormal","milestone"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.284Z','2026-10-18T19:27:41.521Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gxh0003qjlrpaikw4yi','mcq','The correct order for the emergence of temporary teeth:','["Central incisors > Lateral incisors > 1st molar > Canines"]','Central incisors > Lateral incisors > 1st molar > Canines','','','','','Basic','General','Growth & Development','["teeth","dentition","development"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.253Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.253Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr60015der8use76nd6','mcq','Which one of the following is a cause of delayed teeth eruption','["hyperthyroidism","diabetes","cerebral palsy","Rickets"]','Rickets','<p><strong>Why Rickets is correct:</strong> Rickets, caused by vitamin D deficiency or impaired vitamin D metabolism, leads to defective mineralization of growing bones and teeth. The lack of properly mineralized bone matrix delays the eruption of both primary and permanent teeth because the alveolar bone surrounding the developing teeth fails to reach the necessary density and strength to allow teeth to emerge through the gums. This mineralization defect directly impacts the timing of tooth eruption, making rickets a classic cause of delayed dentition.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hyperthyroidism:</strong> This condition typically accelerates bone turnover and may lead to premature tooth eruption rather than delay it.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes:</strong> While diabetes can affect oral health through increased risk of infections and periodontal disease, it is not typically associated with delayed tooth eruption.</li>
<li><strong>Cerebral palsy:</strong> While children with cerebral palsy may have various dental issues due to feeding difficulties and oral motor dysfunction, cerebral palsy itself is not a direct cause of delayed tooth eruption.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Rickets causes delayed tooth eruption due to impaired mineralization of alveolar bone.</li>
<li>The classic dental findings in rickets include delayed eruption, enamel hypoplasia, and increased susceptibility to dental caries.</li>
<li>Vitamin D deficiency is the most common cause of rickets worldwide, though genetic disorders of vitamin D metabolism can also cause it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rickets causes delayed tooth eruption by impairing the mineralization of alveolar bone, preventing proper tooth eruption through the gums.</p>','<p>Rickets delays teeth: R for Rickets, D for Delayed, T for Teeth</p>','<p>Delayed tooth eruption can be an early sign of nutritional deficiencies, particularly vitamin D deficiency, and warrants further investigation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hyperthyroidism:</strong> Typically accelerates bone turnover and may cause premature tooth eruption.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes:</strong> Affects oral health but is not directly associated with delayed tooth eruption.</li>
<li><strong>Cerebral palsy:</strong> May cause dental issues but is not a direct cause of delayed eruption.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Dental Development','["teeth","eruption","delayed","rickets"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.298Z','2026-10-18T19:28:24.282Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7y001dqjlrn02hhf1v','mcq','9-month-old infant fed with small chunks of raw carrots as snacks between feeds. What is the hazard','["Chocking"]','Chocking','<p><strong>Why Chocking is correct:</strong> Raw carrots are hard, firm foods that pose a significant choking hazard for infants under 12 months due to their immature swallowing coordination and inability to properly chew. The small, hard pieces of carrot can easily become lodged in the infant''s airway, blocking oxygen flow and leading to asphyxiation. This is particularly dangerous because infants have a natural tendency to place objects in their mouths but lack the oral motor skills to manage such foods safely. The American Academy of Pediatrics specifically recommends avoiding raw, hard vegetables like carrots for infants until they develop appropriate chewing abilities, typically around 12 months of age.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Nutritional deficiency:</strong> While carrots are nutritious, the primary concern here is not nutritional but rather the physical hazard of choking. - <strong>Allergic reaction:</strong> Carrot allergies are rare in infants and not the immediate danger posed by this feeding practice. - <strong>Gastrointestinal obstruction:</strong> While theoretically possible, the more immediate and common risk with hard foods like carrots is choking rather than intestinal obstruction.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Infants under 12 months should never be given hard, raw, or firm foods that could obstruct their airway. - The AAP recommends cutting foods into very small pieces and cooking them until soft for infants. - Choking is a leading cause of injury and death in infants, with foods being a common culprit.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hard, raw vegetables like carrots are choking hazards for infants under 12 months due to their immature swallowing and chewing abilities.</p>','<p>HARD foods for HARD times: Halt All Raw foods for Development</p>','<p>Always test foods by gently pressing with a fork - if it doesn''t easily mash or break apart, it''s too hard for an infant.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Nutritional deficiency:</strong> The primary concern is choking, not nutrition. - <strong>Allergic reaction:</strong> Carrot allergies are rare and not the immediate danger. - <strong>Gastrointestinal obstruction:</strong> Choking is a more immediate risk than intestinal obstruction with hard foods.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Infant feeding safety','["choking hazard","complementary feeding","raw vegetables"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.630Z','2026-10-18T19:30:56.194Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h6f0013qjlrekh34lc9','mcq','Mother complain that the milk looks thin and watery at the start of feeding. Which of the following is true','["Abnormal milk","It should be discarded","It is Foremilk which is higher in Lactose for hydration of the baby","It lacks all nutrients"]','It is Foremilk which is higher in Lactose for hydration of the baby','<p><strong>Why It is Foremilk which is higher in Lactose for hydration of the baby is correct:</strong> Foremilk is the thin, watery milk that is released at the beginning of a feeding session. It is characterized by lower fat content but higher concentrations of lactose, water-soluble vitamins, and immunoglobulins. This composition serves the important physiological purpose of hydrating the infant and providing readily available energy through lactose. The watery appearance is completely normal and represents the first phase of milk expression during breastfeeding, which gradually transitions to hindmilk (richer in fat) as the feeding progresses.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Abnormal milk:</strong> The thin, watery appearance of foremilk is a normal characteristic of breast milk, not an abnormality.</li>
<li><strong>It should be discarded:</strong> Discarding foremilk would deprive the infant of important hydration factors and nutrients, and is not recommended.</li>
<li><strong>It lacks all nutrients:</strong> Foremilk actually contains important nutrients including lactose, water-soluble vitamins, and immunological factors, though it is lower in fat than hindmilk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foremilk and hindmilk are not different types of milk but rather different phases of the same milk during a feeding.</li>
<li>The fat content increases throughout a feeding as the milk is expressed, making hindmilk richer in calories.</li>
<li>The balance of foremilk and hindmilk helps ensure the infant receives both hydration and adequate nutrition.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The thin, watery milk at the start of feeding is normal foremilk, rich in lactose for hydration, and transitions to fattier hindmilk as the feeding progresses.</p>','<p>Foremilk = First, Fluid, for Hydration</p>','<p>Foremilk-hindmilk imbalance is a misnomer; the issue is typically related to feeding management rather than an actual imbalance in milk composition.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abnormal milk:</strong> The thin appearance is normal, not abnormal.</li>
<li><strong>It should be discarded:</strong> Discarding foremilk would deprive the infant of important nutrients and hydration factors.</li>
<li><strong>It lacks all nutrients:</strong> Foremilk contains important nutrients including lactose and immunological factors.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast milk composition','["foremilk","breastfeeding","lactose"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.575Z','2026-10-18T22:35:41.866Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gze0005qjlrm1co7zyh','mcq','Most common form of PEM','["Nutritional dwarfism","[Not provided in source]","[Not provided in source]","[Not provided in source]"]','Nutritional dwarfism','<p><strong>Why Nutritional dwarfism is correct:</strong> Nutritional dwarfism, also known as stunting, is the most common form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) worldwide. It results from chronic inadequate nutrition during critical periods of growth, particularly in the first 1000 days of life, leading to impaired linear growth and short stature. This condition is characterized by a height-for-age Z-score below -2 standard deviations and represents a failure to achieve genetic growth potential due to prolonged nutritional deficiency.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>[Not provided in source]:</strong> Since the other options were not provided in the source, we cannot analyze them specifically, but they would represent less common forms of PEM such as kwashiorkor (protein deficiency with edema) or marasmus (generalized energy deficiency without edema).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Nutritional dwarfism is characterized by impaired linear growth (height) rather than weight.</li>
<li>It results from chronic nutritional deficiency during critical growth periods.</li>
<li>The condition is largely irreversible if nutritional intervention occurs after the critical growth window has passed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Nutritional dwarfism is the most common form of PEM, characterized by impaired linear growth due to chronic nutritional deficiency during critical developmental periods.</p>','','<p>Early identification and intervention in nutritional dwarfism is crucial as the growth impairment becomes largely permanent once the critical growth window has closed.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>[Not provided in source]:</strong> Other forms of PEM like kwashiorkor or marasmus are less common globally compared to nutritional dwarfism.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["PEM","common","nutritional dwarfism"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.322Z','2026-10-18T19:28:55.049Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzz000cqjlry45vg3vq','mcq','Regarding anti-infective properties of human breast milk, which is correct?','["Contains numerous antibodies important for local GIT immunity.","Helps rapid involution of the uterus.","Increases the incidence of pregnancy during lactation.","Increases the maternal bone density."]','Contains numerous antibodies important for local GIT immunity.','<p><strong>Why Contains numerous antibodies important for local GIT immunity is correct:</strong> Human breast milk contains numerous antibodies, particularly secretory IgA, which provides crucial local immunity to the infant''s gastrointestinal tract. These antibodies coat the mucosal surfaces, preventing pathogen attachment and invasion, and neutralizing viruses and bacteria before they can cause infection. This passive immunity is especially important in the first months of life when the infant''s own immune system is immature. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Helps rapid involution of the uterus:</strong> This is a benefit of breastfeeding for the mother, not related to the anti-infective properties of breast milk. - <strong>Increases the incidence of pregnancy during lactation:</strong> Breastfeeding typically suppresses ovulation through hormonal mechanisms, decreasing (not increasing) the risk of pregnancy during lactation. - <strong>Increases the maternal bone density:</strong> While breastfeeding does cause temporary bone loss, it is associated with long-term benefits for maternal bone density, not an increase during lactation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Breast milk contains secretory IgA, lactoferrin, lysozyme, and oligosaccharides that provide anti-infective protection to the infant. - The anti-infective properties of breast milk are particularly important in the first 6 months of life when the infant''s immune system is developing. - Breast milk changes composition over time (foremilk vs. hindmilk) and throughout the lactation period to meet the infant''s evolving needs. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The anti-infective properties of breast milk primarily come from its numerous antibodies, especially secretory IgA, which protect the infant''s gastrointestinal tract.</p>','<p>Breast milk antibodies = Baby''s Bodyguards (protecting the gut)</p>','<p>The anti-infective components of breast milk are most concentrated in colostrum, making early breastfeeding initiation critical for protecting newborns.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Helps rapid involution of the uterus:</strong> This is a maternal benefit, not related to anti-infective properties. - <strong>Increases the incidence of pregnancy during lactation:</strong> Breastfeeding typically decreases fertility. - <strong>Increases the maternal bone density:</strong> Breastfeeding causes temporary bone loss, not increase.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["antibodies","immunity","GIT"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.344Z','2026-10-18T19:29:11.272Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr20012der8s8rhqx4z','mcq','The children who were born prematurely, the assessment of development by corrected age is used till what chronological age?','["6 months","1 year","3 years","2 years"]','2 years','<p><strong>Why 2 years is correct:</strong> Corrected age should be used for assessing development in premature children until 2 years chronological age, as this accounts for the time spent in utero and allows for catch-up growth during this critical period. The brain undergoes rapid development during the first two years, and prematurity can disrupt this trajectory, making chronological age assessment misleading. By 2 years, most premature children have caught up in their development, and further assessment using chronological age becomes appropriate. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>6 months:</strong> Using corrected age only until 6 months is too early, as significant catch-up development continues beyond this point. - <strong>1 year:</strong> While some catch-up occurs by 1 year, many premature children still benefit from corrected age assessment beyond this milestone. - <strong>3 years:</strong> Extending corrected age assessment to 3 years is unnecessary, as most premature children have caught up developmentally by 2 years. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Corrected age is calculated by subtracting the number of weeks premature from the chronological age. - Premature infants often show catch-up growth in the first 2 years, particularly in head circumference. - Developmental assessments at corrected ages help identify true delays rather than those attributable to prematurity. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Premature children should have their development assessed using corrected age until 2 years chronological age to account for early growth disruption.</p>','<p>PC-2: Premature Correction until age 2.</p>','<p>Premature infants require specialized developmental monitoring, particularly for neurodevelopmental outcomes, which may lag beyond 2 years in some cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>6 months:</strong> Too early; significant catch-up development continues beyond this point. - <strong>1 year:</strong> Many premature children still benefit from corrected age assessment beyond this milestone. - <strong>3 years:</strong> Unnecessary; most have caught up developmentally by 2 years.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Prematurity','["prematurity","corrected age","development"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.294Z','2026-10-18T22:35:09.926Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0i000fqjlr2fm48th3','mcq','Breast milk is','["Dynamically stable.","Dynamically adaptive to the baby''s needs.","Poor in growth factors","Poor in all micronutrients"]','Dynamically adaptive to the baby''s needs.','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Breast milk is dynamically adaptive to the baby''s needs, meaning its composition changes over time and even within a single feeding to meet the infant''s specific requirements. For example, the foremilk (first milk of a feeding) is typically more watery and higher in lactose, while the hindmilk is richer in fat and calories. Additionally, breast milk composition changes throughout the lactation period, with colostrum (early milk) being particularly rich in immunoglobulins and growth factors, while mature milk provides balanced nutrition. The mother''s body also responds to pathogens in the environment by producing specific antibodies that are passed to the infant through breast milk, providing targeted immune protection. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A:</strong> While breast milk has consistent nutritional value, it is not dynamically stable as its composition actively changes in response to the infant''s needs and environmental factors. - <strong>C:</strong> Breast milk is actually rich in growth factors, including epidermal growth factor, insulin-like growth factors, and others that support organ development and maturation. - <strong>D:</strong> Breast milk is not poor in micronutrients; it contains a balanced profile of vitamins and minerals that are highly bioavailable, though some may require supplementation in certain circumstances (like vitamin D). <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Breast milk changes composition from the beginning to the end of a feeding, with foremilk being more watery and hindmilk richer in fat. - The composition of breast milk varies throughout lactation, with colostrum containing higher concentrations of immune factors than mature milk. - Breast milk provides targeted immune protection by producing antibodies specific to pathogens the mother has been exposed to. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Breast milk is not static but dynamically adapts to the infant''s changing nutritional and immunological needs throughout feeding and lactation.</p>','<p>DAD = Dynamic Adaptive to baby''s needs</p>','<p>Breast milk contains over 200 beneficial components, including antibodies, enzymes, and growth factors that cannot be replicated in infant formula.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A:</strong> Breast milk composition actively changes rather than remaining stable. - <strong>C:</strong> Breast milk is rich in growth factors, not poor in them. - <strong>D:</strong> Breast milk contains a balanced profile of micronutrients with high bioavailability.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["composition","adaptive","dynamic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.362Z','2026-10-18T19:29:16.372Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0r000iqjlr7n48dsek','mcq','2-day full-term newborn frequently crying, he is very well with intact reflexes. What is your feeding advice for the mother?','["Feeding every 4 h","Formula after every breast-fed","Breastfed on demand (8-12 times/day)","Postpone breastfeeding until crying stops"]','Breastfed on demand (8-12 times/day)','<p><strong>Why Breastfed on demand (8-12 times/day) is correct:</strong> A 2-day-old full-term newborn with intact reflexes who is frequently crying is likely exhibiting normal newborn behavior indicating hunger, requiring breastfeeding on demand (8-12 times per day) to establish adequate milk supply and meet nutritional needs. Newborns have small stomach capacity (approximately 5-7 mL at birth, growing to 60-90 mL by 1 week) and need frequent feedings to maintain blood sugar levels and promote weight gain. On-demand feeding stimulates prolactin and oxytocin release, establishing and maintaining milk production through the principle of supply and demand.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Feeding every 4 h:</strong> This infrequent schedule doesn''t meet the nutritional needs of a 2-day-old newborn and can lead to hypoglycemia and inadequate weight gain.</li>
<li><strong>Formula after every breast-fed:</strong> This practice can interfere with establishing breastfeeding by reducing the baby''s suckling at the breast, which is necessary to stimulate milk production.</li>
<li><strong>Postpone breastfeeding until crying stops:</strong> Waiting until the baby is crying to feed may result in a frantic, inefficient feeding session and doesn''t address the early hunger cues that precede crying.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Newborns should feed 8-12 times in 24 hours, with each feeding lasting 10-20 minutes per breast.</li>
<li>Early hunger cues include rooting, sucking on hands, and lip smacking; crying is a late hunger cue.</li>
<li>Frequent feeding in the first 1-2 weeks helps establish milk supply and prevents jaundice.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A 2-day-old newborn who is frequently crying should be breastfed on demand (8-12 times daily) to establish adequate milk supply and meet nutritional needs.</p>','<p>Demand feeding: 8-12 times daily, Never wait for baby to cry, Early cues are key.</p>','<p>The first two weeks postpartum are critical for establishing milk supply; frequent breastfeeding during this period helps prevent future supply issues.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Feeding every 4 h:</strong> This infrequent schedule doesn''t meet the high nutritional needs of a newborn.</li>
<li><strong>Formula after every breast-fed:</strong> This can interfere with establishing breastfeeding by reducing breast stimulation.</li>
<li><strong>Postpone breastfeeding until crying stops:</strong> Crying is a late hunger cue; feeding should occur at earlier cues.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["newborn","feeding","demand"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.371Z','2026-10-18T19:29:28.928Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56har002iqjlrco7mazwy','mcq','An 18-month-old boy presents with sudden onset of vomiting, crampy abdominal pain, draws up his knees, cries out, and exhibits pallor in a colicky pattern every 15–20 minutes. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute appendicitis","Gastroenteritis","Pancreatitis","Intussusception"]','Intussusception','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["intussusception","pediatrics","abdominal pain"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.731Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.731Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56has002jqjlr79n7gyj9','mcq','most common cause of lower git bleeding in infancy is;','["intussusception","intestinal obstruction","gastric ulcer"]','intussusception','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["gi bleeding","lower gi","infancy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.732Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.732Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h24000mqjlrl39phxfm','mcq','Mother with active untreated TB who gives birth. The safe feeding plan is','["Wearing a mask during breastfeeding","Express milk and start treatment","Exclusive formula for 2 years","Mixed Feeding"]','Express milk and start treatment','<p><strong>Why Express milk and start treatment is correct:</strong> The correct approach involves the mother expressing her milk and starting TB treatment immediately because the mother''s milk can be safely consumed by the infant once the mother has been on effective treatment for at least 2 weeks, at which point she is considered non-infectious. This approach maintains the benefits of breastfeeding while ensuring the infant is not exposed to active TB bacteria. The mother should be started on a standard 4-drug anti-TB regimen (isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol), and the infant should receive isoniazid prophylaxis until the mother is confirmed to be non-infectious.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wearing a mask during breastfeeding:</strong> While mask-wearing reduces transmission risk, it doesn''t eliminate the risk of transmission through close contact, and it doesn''t address the need for the mother to receive treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Exclusive formula for 2 years:</strong> This approach unnecessarily deprives the infant of the immunological and nutritional benefits of breast milk, which is particularly important in resource-limited settings where TB is prevalent.</li>
<li><strong>Mixed Feeding:</strong> Mixed feeding increases the risk of other infections in the infant and doesn''t address the mother''s active TB infection, which requires treatment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Breastfeeding can continue after the mother has been on effective TB treatment for 2 weeks and is considered non-infectious.</li>
<li>Infants of mothers with active TB should receive isoniazid prophylaxis regardless of feeding method.</li>
<li>The benefits of breastfeeding generally outweigh the risks of TB transmission once the mother is on treatment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For a mother with active TB, express milk after starting treatment to maintain breastfeeding benefits while ensuring the infant isn''t exposed to infectious bacteria.</p>','<p>Treat TB, Express milk, Protect baby (TEP)</p>','<p>TB transmission through breast milk is rare, and the benefits of breastfeeding generally outweigh the risks once the mother is on effective treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Wearing a mask during breastfeeding:</strong> Mask-wearing reduces but doesn''t eliminate transmission risk and doesn''t address the need for treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Exclusive formula for 2 years:</strong> This unnecessarily deprives the infant of breast milk benefits and is overly restrictive.</li>
<li><strong>Mixed Feeding:</strong> This increases infection risk and doesn''t treat the mother''s active TB infection.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["TB","treatment","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.420Z','2026-10-18T19:29:45.147Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h2f000oqjlr8gyhcrl3','mcq','A 7-month-old baby ready for complementary food','["Start with Cow''s milk","Start with iron fortified cereals and pureed meats","Raw carrots sticks"]','Start with iron fortified cereals and pureed meats','<p><strong>Why Start with iron fortified cereals and pureed meats is correct:</strong> At 7 months, infants need complementary foods to meet their increasing nutritional requirements, particularly iron, as iron stores from birth begin to deplete. Iron-fortified cereals and pureed meats are excellent first complementary foods because they provide highly bioavailable heme iron from animal sources and non-heme iron from fortified cereals, which is crucial for preventing iron deficiency anemia. These foods are also appropriate texture for a 7-month-old who is developing oral motor skills and can handle smooth purees. Additionally, meats provide zinc, protein, and other essential nutrients that support rapid growth and development during this stage.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start with Cow''s milk:</strong> Cow''s milk is not appropriate as a primary beverage before 12 months due to its low iron content, high renal solute load, and potential to cause microscopic blood loss in the intestines, all of which can contribute to iron deficiency anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Raw carrots sticks:</strong> Raw vegetables pose a choking hazard for infants and are difficult to digest, as they require more developed chewing abilities that a 7-month-old typically hasn''t yet acquired.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Iron-fortified cereals and pureed meats are ideal first complementary foods due to their high iron content and appropriate texture.</li>
<li>Iron deficiency is common in infants 6-12 months and can have long-term developmental consequences.</li>
<li>Complementary foods should be introduced one at a time, with several days between new foods to monitor for allergic reactions.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> First complementary foods at 6-8 months should be iron-rich, easily digestible purees like fortified cereals and meats to prevent iron deficiency anemia.</p>','<p>First foods: Iron-rich, pureed, not raw (FIR)</p>','<p>Iron deficiency anemia peaks between 6-24 months, making iron-rich complementary foods critical at this developmental stage.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Start with Cow''s milk:</strong> Cow''s milk is low in iron and can cause microscopic blood loss, increasing anemia risk before 12 months.</li>
<li><strong>Raw carrots sticks:</strong> Raw vegetables pose choking hazards and are difficult for infants to digest.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["complementary food","iron","cereals"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.431Z','2026-10-18T19:29:45.150Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h2p000pqjlrx65j3lo1','mcq','A mother returning to work and want continue breastfeeding. The best support for mother is','["Express and store breast milk","Switch to formula during working hours","Weaning","Breast milk restricted to at night only"]','Express and store breast milk','<p><strong>Why Express and store breast milk is correct:</strong> The best support for a mother returning to work who wishes to continue breastfeeding is expressing and storing breast milk because this maintains milk supply through regular stimulation while allowing the baby to receive breast milk in the mother''s absence. Breast milk expression follows the principle of supply and demand—regular removal of milk signals the body to continue production, preventing engorgement and maintaining lactation. This approach preserves the immunological benefits of breast milk for the infant and supports the mother''s bonding experience even when physically separated. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Switch to formula during working hours:</strong> Formula lacks the unique immunological and nutritional benefits of breast milk and can lead to decreased milk supply due to reduced stimulation. - <strong>Weaning:</strong> This would abruptly end breastfeeding, which contradicts the mother''s stated desire to continue breastfeeding. - <strong>Breast milk restricted to at night only:</strong> This approach significantly reduces milk stimulation frequency, risking supply decline and does not provide adequate nutrition during daytime hours. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Breast milk should be stored in clean, BPA-free containers and can be refrigerated for up to 4 days or frozen for 6-12 months. - Regular pumping every 2-3 hours during work hours mimics a baby''s feeding pattern and maintains supply. - Proper storage techniques include labeling milk with date and time, using the oldest milk first, and avoiding refrozen thawed milk. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> To continue breastfeeding after returning to work, mothers should express and store breast milk regularly to maintain supply while providing breast milk during separation.</p>','<p>PUMP: Pump regularly, Use proper storage, Maintain schedule, Protect milk supply</p>','<p>Breast milk maintains its nutritional and immunological properties even after storage, making expressed milk an excellent option for working mothers.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Switch to formula during working hours:</strong> Formula lacks breast milk benefits and reduces milk supply due to less frequent stimulation. - <strong>Weaning:</strong> Abruptly ends breastfeeding against the mother''s wishes. - <strong>Breast milk restricted to at night only:</strong> Insufficient stimulation leads to decreased milk production.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["returning to work","express milk","storage"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.441Z','2026-10-18T22:35:35.102Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h2t000rqjlr3zisyjhc','mcq','Mother breastfeeding with mastitis, presenting by pain and fever. The best management is','["Antibiotics and continue breastfeeding","Express milk till recovered","Stop breastfeeding till fever resolves","Cold packs"]','Antibiotics and continue breastfeeding','<p><strong>Why Antibiotics and continue breastfeeding is correct:</strong> The best management for a mother with mastitis presenting with pain and fever is antibiotics and continued breastfeeding because breastfeeding helps clear the milk ducts of obstruction and infection while antibiotics target the bacterial cause, typically Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus species. Continued breastfeeding ensures proper milk drainage, prevents engorgement, and helps resolve the infection more quickly than stopping nursing. Antibiotics such as cephalexin, dicloxacillin, or flucloxacillin are typically used and are compatible with breastfeeding, posing minimal risk to the infant. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Express milk till recovered:</strong> While expressing milk is important, it alone without antibiotics is insufficient for treating the bacterial infection causing mastitis. - <strong>Stop breastfeeding till fever resolves:</strong> Stopping breastfeeding leads to milk stasis, worsening the obstruction and infection, and delays recovery. - <strong>Cold packs:</strong> Cold packs may provide temporary pain relief but do not address the underlying bacterial infection or help clear the duct obstruction. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Mastitis is most commonly caused by bacterial infection entering through cracked nipples or milk duct obstruction. - Effective antibiotics should cover common mastitis pathogens and be compatible with breastfeeding, typically from the penicillin or cephalosporin class. - Signs of improvement should be seen within 24-48 hours of starting treatment; lack of improvement warrants reevaluation for abscess or resistant organisms. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Mastitis requires antibiotics to treat the infection while continuing breastfeeding to ensure proper milk drainage and prevent complications.</p>','<p>Mastitis Management: Antibiotics + Continue = MAC</p>','<p>Mastitis is not a contraindication to breastfeeding; in fact, continued breastfeeding helps resolve the condition by ensuring proper milk drainage.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Express milk till recovered:</strong> Insufficient alone without antibiotics for bacterial infection. - <strong>Stop breastfeeding till fever resolves:</strong> Leads to milk stasis and worsens the infection. - <strong>Cold packs:</strong> Provides only temporary relief without treating the underlying infection.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["mastitis","antibiotics","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.445Z','2026-10-18T22:35:35.105Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriudk00fle9oirnrrdul1','mcq','3 week old infant with poor feeding, lethargy and jaundice, examination show hepatosplenomegaly. The most likely diagnosis is','["Biliary atresia","ABO incompatibility","Breast-milk jaundice","Cephalhematoma"]','Biliary atresia','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Biliary Atresia','["biliary","hepatosplenomegaly","jaundice"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.912Z','2026-10-27T16:43:13.447Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7t001aqjlrixsfztm7','mcq','Infant with Lactose intolerance:','["Lactose free formula"]','Lactose free formula','<p><strong>Why Lactose free formula is correct:</strong> Lactose intolerance in infants is typically due to lactase deficiency, which results in the inability to digest lactose, the primary carbohydrate in breast milk and standard cow''s milk formula. This deficiency leads to symptoms such as diarrhea, bloating, and failure to thrive when lactose-containing products are consumed. A lactose-free formula provides alternative carbohydrates (like corn syrup solids or maltodextrin) that can be properly digested, allowing the infant to receive adequate nutrition without experiencing gastrointestinal distress. This is particularly important in infants as proper nutrition is critical for growth and development during this vulnerable period.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Standard cow''s milk formula:</strong> This contains lactose, which would continue to cause symptoms in a lactose-intolerant infant. - <strong>Breast milk:</strong> While breast milk is ideal for most infants, it contains lactose and would not be appropriate for a diagnosed lactose intolerance case. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> While soy formula is lactose-free, it''s not the first-line treatment for lactose intolerance as it may introduce other potential issues like allergies or mineral absorption problems.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Lactose intolerance in infants is different from cow''s milk protein allergy; the former involves carbohydrate digestion while the latter involves immune response to proteins. - The prevalence of primary lactose intolerance is very low in infants and is more commonly seen in premature infants or those with gastrointestinal damage. - Lactose-free formulas are nutritionally complete and provide all necessary vitamins and minerals for infant growth.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For lactose-intolerant infants, a lactose-free formula provides digestible carbohydrates while avoiding the symptoms caused by undigested lactose.</p>','','<p>True lactose intolerance is rare in infants and should be distinguished from cow''s milk protein allergy, which presents with different symptoms including blood in stool.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Standard cow''s milk formula:</strong> Contains lactose which would worsen symptoms in lactose-intolerant infants. - <strong>Breast milk:</strong> Contains lactose and would not be appropriate for diagnosed lactose intolerance. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Not first-line for lactose intolerance due to potential allergy concerns.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Lactose intolerance','["lactose intolerance","formula","milk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.625Z','2026-10-18T19:30:52.762Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7v001bqjlro21i9ntt','mcq','Preterm infant with GERD with low mother milk supply. The appropriate formula is','["AR formula","Lactose free formula","Standard cow''s milk formula","Soy-based formula"]','AR formula','<p><strong>Why AR formula is correct:</strong> AR (Anti-Reflux) formula is thickened with rice starch or other thickeners, which increases the viscosity of the gastric contents and reduces the frequency of gastroesophageal reflux (GER) episodes. In preterm infants, who have underdeveloped lower esophageal sphincter tone and more horizontal positioning, GER is particularly problematic. The thickened formula creates a physical barrier that prevents easy reflux of gastric contents into the esophagus, while still providing adequate nutrition. Additionally, AR formulas are typically whey-dominant (like breast milk) rather than casein-dominant, making them easier to digest for preterm infants with immature gastrointestinal systems.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Lactose free formula:</strong> While lactose-free formulas may be needed for specific conditions, they don''t address the reflux mechanism and may not be appropriate unless lactose intolerance is confirmed. - <strong>Standard cow''s milk formula:</strong> Standard formulas are not thickened and would not help control reflux symptoms in a preterm infant. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Soy formulas are not typically recommended for preterm infants due to potential mineral absorption issues and lack of certain nutrients found in whey-based formulas.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Preterm infants have higher rates of GER due to immature anatomy and function of the gastrointestinal tract. - Thickened formulas (AR formulas) reduce reflux episodes by increasing gastric viscosity without significantly affecting gastric emptying time. - Positioning changes and proper feeding techniques should be combined with appropriate formula selection for optimal GER management.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For preterm infants with GERD and limited maternal milk supply, an anti-reflux (AR) formula provides both appropriate nutrition and physical thickening to reduce reflux episodes.</p>','<p>AR = Anti-Reflux (helps prevent vomiting in preemies)</p>','<p>Thickened formulas should be used with caution in infants with significant reflux as they may increase gastric pressure and potentially worsen reflux in some cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lactose free formula:</strong> Does not address the reflux mechanism and is only appropriate if lactose intolerance is confirmed. - <strong>Standard cow''s milk formula:</strong> Not thickened and would not help control reflux symptoms. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Not ideal for preterm infants due to potential mineral absorption issues.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Preterm formula','["preterm","gerd","ar formula"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.627Z','2026-10-18T19:30:52.764Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h94001zqjlrs5phqsm7','mcq','3- Functional constipation is presented in children about;','["60-70 %","50-60 %","90-95 %"]','90-95 %','','','','','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["functional constipation","pediatrics","prevalence"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.672Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.672Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7n0018qjlru9i6lznt','mcq','A mother in BFHI hospital asked the nurse to give pacifier to her 1-day-old baby','["Explain step 9: no artificial teats or pacifiers for breastfeeding infant","Give pacifier after each feed","Pacifier only at night","Replace breastfeeding with pacifier use"]','Explain step 9: no artificial teats or pacifiers for breastfeeding infant','<p><strong>Why Explain step 9: no artificial teats or pacifiers for breastfeeding infant is correct:</strong> The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) Step 9 specifically advises against giving artificial teats or pacifiers to breastfeeding infants in the first few days of life to ensure proper establishment of breastfeeding. Pacifiers can interfere with the infant''s ability to latch effectively, as they create a different sucking pattern than breastfeeding. Early pacifier use may lead to nipple confusion, decreased milk supply due to reduced stimulation, and shorter breastfeeding duration. The BFHI emphasizes establishing proper breastfeeding technique before introducing any artificial nipples.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give pacifier after each feed:</strong> This contradicts BFHI guidelines and risks nipple confusion and reduced breastfeeding effectiveness.</li>
<li><strong>Pacifier only at night:</strong> Even limited pacifier use can interfere with breastfeeding establishment and is discouraged in the early days.</li>
<li><strong>Replace breastfeeding with pacifier use:</strong> This directly contradicts the purpose of BFHI and would be harmful to the breastfeeding relationship and infant nutrition.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>BFHI Step 9 specifically prohibits artificial teats and pacifiers for breastfeeding infants in the early postpartum period.</li>
<li>Pacifiers can cause nipple confusion and interfere with proper latch and milk transfer.</li>
<li>Breastfeeding should be well-established before considering pacifier use, typically after 3-4 weeks.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Early pacifier use can interfere with breastfeeding establishment, so BFHI guidelines recommend avoiding artificial teats until breastfeeding is well-established.</p>','<p>BFHI Step 9: ''No teats, no pacifiers, proper breastfeeding creates''</p>','<p>Nipple confusion occurs when infants struggle to switch between different sucking patterns of breastfeeding and artificial nipples.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Give pacifier after each feed:</strong> This contradicts BFHI guidelines and risks nipple confusion.</li>
<li><strong>Pacifier only at night:</strong> Even limited pacifier use can interfere with breastfeeding establishment.</li>
<li><strong>Replace breastfeeding with pacifier use:</strong> This directly contradicts BFHI principles and would harm breastfeeding.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','BFHI guidelines','["bfhi","pacifier","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.619Z','2026-10-18T22:35:57.402Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8g001mqjlrxygiqqyf','mcq','The "let-down reflex," which causes the contraction of myoepithelial cells to force milk into larger ducts, is mediated by:','["Prolactin","Estrogen","Oxytocin","Progesterone"]','Oxytocin','','','','','Basic','General','Lactation physiology','["breastfeeding","oxytocin","let-down reflex"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.648Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.648Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8i001nqjlr3bpthtij','mcq','The "First 1000 Days" of a child''s life is a critical window defined as the period from:','["Conception to the first birthday.","Birth to the second birthday.","Conception to the second birthday.","Birth to the third birthday."]','Conception to the second birthday.','','','','','Basic','General','Child development','["first 1000 days","child development","nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.650Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.650Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8p001oqjlrisq42ilp','mcq','Nipple pain and injury during breastfeeding can occur due to:','["Good position and latch","Poor position and latch","Neonatal hyperbilirubinemia","Neonatal convulsions"]','Poor position and latch','','','','','Basic','General','Breastfeeding difficulties','["breastfeeding","nipple pain","latch"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.657Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.657Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8q001pqjlrqe5blkdg','mcq','61- Lactogenesis 1 begins:','["After delivery","During pregnancy","2 weeks postpartum","After weaning"]','After delivery','','','','','Basic','Pediatrics','Lactation','["lactogenesis","breastfeeding","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.658Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.658Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8r001qqjlr8h9ug355','tf','62- Prolactin from anterior pituitary = Milk production or secretion','[]','True','','','','','Basic','Pediatrics','Lactation','["prolactin","milk production","endocrine"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.659Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.659Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8s001rqjlrm9ve2pc3','mcq','63- Preterm infant was breast-fed but switched to formula due to maternal illness contraindicating breastfeeding. The component that the infant will lose and can''t be replaced by formula:','["Protein","Carbohydrates","Live immune cells"]','Live immune cells','','','','','Moderate','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["preterm","immune cells","breast milk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.660Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.660Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h89001jqjlr73ppo4za','mcq','10-month-old infant eating small pieces of bread, fruit, yogurt but the parents give unpasteurized cheese and raw apple slices with snacks. What is the primary concern','["Low Calcium","Risk of Chocking and food born illness","Low fibers","Vitamin B excess"]','Risk of Chocking and food born illness','<p><strong>Why Risk of Chocking and food born illness is correct:</strong> At 10 months of age, infants are developing oral motor skills but still have limited chewing ability and a narrow airway. Unpasteurized cheese poses a risk of Listeria monocytogenes infection, which can cause severe sepsis and meningitis in infants. Raw apple slices are hard and slippery, creating a significant choking hazard as they can easily obstruct the airway. The combination of these risks makes this the primary concern in this scenario.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low Calcium:</strong> While calcium intake is important for bone development, unpasteurized cheese actually provides calcium, making this concern unfounded.</li>
<li><strong>Low fibers:</strong> Bread and fruit in the diet provide adequate fiber for this age group, and this is not the primary concern with the described feeding practices.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin B excess:</strong> There is no risk of vitamin B excess from the described foods, and this is not a typical concern in infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Infants under 12 months should never be given unpasteurized dairy products due to risk of bacterial infections like listeriosis.</li>
<li>Hard, raw fruits and vegetables should be cooked or cut into very small pieces to prevent choking in young children.</li>
<li>Choking is a leading cause of injury and death in infants and young children, with common offenders including whole grapes, nuts, and hard raw vegetables.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For infants, unpasteurized dairy and hard raw foods pose dual threats of serious infection and life-threatening choking.</p>','<p>C.H.O.K.E. - Cheese (unpasteurized), Hard foods, Objects (small), Kids under 4, Eating unsupervised</p>','<p>Infants have a higher risk of foodborne illness due to immature immune systems and should never consume unpasteurized dairy products.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Low Calcium:</strong> Unpasteurized cheese actually provides calcium, making this concern unfounded.</li>
<li><strong>Low fibers:</strong> Bread and fruit in the diet provide adequate fiber for this age group.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin B excess:</strong> There is no risk of vitamin B excess from the described foods.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Infant feeding safety','["choking hazard","food safety","pasteurization"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.641Z','2026-10-18T19:31:12.574Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8u001sqjlrteugd03p','mcq','64- Preterm infant in NICU, fed with mother''s expressed milk. NICU staff noticed it contains high protein and immune factors than mature term milk.','["Contaminated milk","Mother''s diet high protein","Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors","Milk was frozen"]','Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors','<p><strong>Why Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors is correct:</strong> Human breast milk dynamically changes composition based on the infant''s gestational age and postnatal needs. Preterm milk contains higher concentrations of protein (particularly whey proteins), immunoglobulins (like IgA), growth factors, and fatty acids compared to term milk to support the accelerated growth and immature immune system of preterm infants. This biological adaptation provides enhanced nutrition and protection against infections that preterm infants are more susceptible to. The increased protein content supports rapid brain development and catch-up growth, while elevated immune factors help compensate for underdeveloped passive immunity transfer.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Contaminated milk:</strong> Preterm milk''s higher protein and immune factors are a normal physiological adaptation, not contamination. Contamination would imply bacterial or foreign substance presence, which isn''t the case here.</li>
<li><strong>Mother''s diet high protein:</strong> While maternal diet can influence some milk components, it doesn''t account for the specific, consistent increase in protein and immune factors seen exclusively in preterm milk.</li>
<li><strong>Milk was frozen:</strong> Freezing may slightly alter some immunological factors but doesn''t selectively increase protein content or immune factors in a way that differentiates preterm from term milk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Preterm milk has 1.5-2x more protein than term milk to support rapid growth of underdeveloped systems.</li>
<li>Preterm milk contains higher levels of sodium, chloride, and immunoglobulins to compensate for immature renal and immune systems.</li>
<li>The composition of breast milk changes throughout lactation (colostrum → transitional → mature) and is also influenced by the infant''s gestational age.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Preterm milk is biologically ''supercharged'' with higher protein and immune factors to meet the unique needs of premature infants, making it perfectly adapted rather than contaminated or abnormal.</p>','<p>Preterm milk = ''Power milk'' (P for Preterm, Power for Protein and Protection)</p>','<p>Preterm milk should be prioritized for preterm infants whenever possible, as its unique composition provides significant nutritional and immunological advantages over formula or term milk.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Contaminated milk:</strong> Preterm milk''s composition is a normal physiological adaptation, not contamination.</li>
<li><strong>Mother''s diet high protein:</strong> Maternal diet doesn''t account for the specific, consistent increases seen in preterm milk.</li>
<li><strong>Milk was frozen:</strong> Freezing doesn''t selectively increase protein and immune factors in preterm milk.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["preterm milk","NICU","protein"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.662Z','2026-10-18T19:31:44.681Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hat002kqjlrh0jcj4ws','mcq','Confirmation of a Meckel''s diverticulum can be difficult. Of the following, what is, the MOST sensitive study?','["plain abdominal radiographs","barium studies","radionuclide technetium-99m scans","abdominal ultrasound"]','radionuclide technetium-99m scans','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Meckel''s Diverticulum','["meckel","diagnosis","imaging"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.733Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.733Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h84001gqjlrrpq2wljc','mcq','2-week-old infant exclusively breast-fed with persistent crying after feeding. He is poor weight gain and the mother complain of nipple pain. On examination the infant cheeks dimple while suckling. What is the most likely cause and best first response','["Tongue tie, immediately frenolotomy","Poor latch, correction of position latch"]','Poor latch, correction of position latch','<p><strong>Why Poor latch, correction of position latch is correct:</strong> Poor latch is the most likely cause in this scenario as evidenced by the infant''s cheeks dimpling during suckling, which indicates inadequate mouth formation around the breast. This mechanical issue prevents effective milk transfer, leading to poor weight gain and maternal nipple pain. Correcting the latch involves proper positioning (such as the cross-cradle or football hold) and ensuring the infant opens wide with flanged lips to create a proper seal, which resolves the transfer issues without invasive procedures.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tongue tie, immediately frenolotomy:</strong> While tongue tie can cause similar symptoms, the dimpled cheeks are a more specific indicator of latch problems rather than tongue tie. Frenulotomy is not the first-line intervention without proper evaluation by a lactation consultant.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Dimpling cheeks during breastfeeding is a classic sign of poor latch rather than tongue tie.</li>
<li>Correct positioning (infant''s nose opposite the nipple, wide mouth opening) is crucial for effective latch.</li>
<li>Nipple pain is often positional and resolves with proper latch correction rather than indicating infection or other pathology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Dimpling cheeks during breastfeeding indicates poor latch, which is corrected by proper positioning and technique before considering anatomical issues like tongue tie.</p>','<p>Dimples mean poor latches, not ties</p>','<p>Nipple pain that persists beyond the first week of breastfeeding is almost always due to poor positioning and latch rather than infection.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tongue tie, immediately frenolotomy:</strong> Dimpling cheeks is more indicative of latch problems than tongue tie, and frenulotomy should not be the first intervention.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breastfeeding difficulties','["breastfeeding","latch","nipple pain"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.636Z','2026-10-18T22:36:02.473Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h93001yqjlrkgyjdjqc','mcq','70- In BFIH hospital, the mother was discharged without post-charge feeding support. Which step is violated?','["Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups","On-demand feeding","Exclusively breastfeeding","Avoid teats and pacifiers"]','Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups','','','','','Basic','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["BFIH","breastfeeding support","hospital policy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.671Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.671Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h9c0020qjlr7n5k28el','mcq','4- Which is true about Functional Constipation','["Common In preschool children","Associated with breastfeeding","Associated with organic cause","Passing of hard stool less than 2 times per day"]','Common In preschool children','','','','','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["functional constipation","preschool","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.680Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.680Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8y001wqjlrki3huh51','mcq','68- 2-week-old infant exclusively breast-fed with persistent crying after feeding. He has poor weight gain and the mother complains of nipple pain. On examination, the infant''s cheeks dimple while suckling. What is the most likely cause and best first response?','["Tongue tie, immediately frenulotomy","Poor latch, correction of position latch"]','Poor latch, correction of position latch','<p><strong>Why Poor latch, correction of position latch is correct:</strong> This infant''s presentation with persistent crying after feeding, poor weight gain, and dimpling of the cheeks during suckling strongly suggests ineffective breastfeeding due to poor latch. Dimpling or clicking sounds indicate that the infant is not properly latching onto the areola, resulting in inadequate milk transfer and maternal nipple pain. The best initial response is to correct the latch and positioning, which can be achieved with proper guidance from a lactation consultant. This approach addresses the root cause without unnecessary interventions, as many latch issues can be resolved with proper technique.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tongue tie, immediately frenulotomy:</strong> While tongue tie (ankyloglossia) can cause breastfeeding difficulties, it should not be assumed as the primary cause without proper evaluation. Immediate frenulotomy is not indicated as the first step; latch correction should be attempted first, and frenulotomy is reserved for cases where latch correction fails and tongue tie is confirmed as the primary issue.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Proper latch requires the infant to take a large mouthful of breast tissue, with the nipple positioned toward the soft palate, not the front of the mouth.</li>
<li>Signs of effective latch include rhythmic sucking and swallowing, audible swallowing sounds, and visible jaw movement.</li>
<li>Nipple pain that persists beyond the first week of breastfeeding is often a sign of poor latch rather than ''normal'' discomfort.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For an exclusively breastfed infant with poor weight gain and nipple pain, always address latch and positioning before considering anatomical issues like tongue tie.</p>','<p>Dimple Click = Poor Lick (latch)</p>','<p>Nipple pain that persists beyond the first week of breastfeeding is abnormal and typically indicates a latch problem rather than ''normal'' discomfort.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tongue tie, immediately frenulotomy:</strong> Tongue tie should be evaluated after addressing latch issues, and frenulotomy is not first-line without confirmation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["latch","infant feeding","nipple pain"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.666Z','2026-10-18T19:31:46.990Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56had0028qjlr9sao7bn3','mcq','12- Which of the following statements characterizes functional constipation?','["Common with breast fed children.","Common in children above 5 years of age.","Can have organic cause.","The child passes stool less than 2 times/week."]','The child passes stool less than 2 times/week.','<p><strong>Why The child passes stool less than 2 times/week is correct:</strong> Functional constipation is defined by Rome IV criteria as having at least two of the following for at least one month: stool frequency less than three times per week, at least one episode of fecal incontinence per week, history of excessive stool retention, painful or hard bowel movements, presence of a large fecal mass in the rectum, or history of excessive stool evacuation. The specific criterion of passing stool less than 2 times per week is a key diagnostic feature that distinguishes functional constipation from normal variations.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Common with breast fed children:</strong> Breastfed infants typically have more frequent bowel movements than formula-fed infants, making this incorrect.</li>
<li><strong>Common in children above 5 years of age:</strong> Functional constipation is actually more common in toddlers and preschool children (ages 2-5) than in older children.</li>
<li><strong>Can have organic cause:</strong> By definition, functional constipation has no identifiable organic cause; constipation with an organic cause would be classified as organic constipation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Functional constipation is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring the absence of organic pathology.</li>
<li>Rome IV criteria provide standardized diagnostic criteria for functional constipation in children.</li>
<li>Behavioral factors and stool withholding are common contributors to functional constipation in children.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation is defined by specific Rome IV criteria including stool frequency less than 2 times per week, with no identifiable organic cause.</p>','<p>Functional constipation criteria: ''2-2-2'' - less than 2 stools/week, at least 2 symptoms for 2 months.</p>','<p>Functional constipation is often behavioral in origin and responds well to a combination of education, stool softeners, and regular toileting routines.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Common with breast fed children:</strong> Breastfed infants typically have more frequent bowel movements.</li>
<li><strong>Common in children above 5 years of age:</strong> More common in toddlers and preschool children.</li>
<li><strong>Can have organic cause:</strong> By definition, functional constipation has no organic cause.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["functional constipation","diagnostic criteria"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.717Z','2026-10-18T19:32:20.312Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56ham002gqjlrixr3ch2g','mcq','Which of the following is a common cause of lower gastrointestinal bleeding in infancy?','["Toilet training","Intussusception","Anal fissure","Oesophageal varices"]','Intussusception','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["gi bleeding","lower gi","infancy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.726Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.726Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hao002hqjlr2b079c71','mcq','Current jelly stool is a characteristic feature of ;','["Hirshsprung disease","intussusception","gastric ulcer"]','intussusception','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["stool","intussusception","symptom"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.728Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.728Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbn002zqjlryyu5edsp','mcq','Which of the following should be avoided in the child with acute diarrhea?','["Wheat.","Yogurt.","Rice.","Fruit juice (Hyperosmolar foods)."]','Fruit juice (Hyperosmolar foods).','<p><strong>Why D is correct:</strong> Fruit juice should be avoided in children with acute diarrhea because it is hyperosmolar, meaning it has a high concentration of solutes that draw water into the intestinal lumen, potentially worsening diarrhea and increasing the risk of osmotic diarrhea. Hyperosmolar foods can overwhelm the immature or damaged intestinal mucosa''s ability to absorb nutrients and water, leading to increased stool output and potentially worsening dehydration. The high fructose content in many fruit juices is particularly problematic as it requires specific transporters (GLUT5) for absorption, and when these are saturated, the unabsorbed fructose acts as an osmotic agent in the colon. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A. Wheat:</strong> Wheat contains complex carbohydrates that are generally well-tolerated in children with diarrhea and are often recommended as part of a gradual reintroduction of solid foods. - <strong>B. Yogurt:</strong> Yogurt is actually beneficial in acute diarrhea as it contains probiotics that can help restore gut flora and may reduce diarrhea duration. - <strong>C. Rice:</strong> Rice is considered a staple in the management of acute diarrhea, particularly in the BRAT diet (Bananas, Rice, Applesauce, Toast), as it is easily digestible and helps form stools. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hyperosmolar foods like fruit juice can worsen diarrhea by drawing water into the intestinal lumen. - The BRAT diet (Bananas, Rice, Applesauce, Toast) is recommended for acute diarrhea as these foods are binding and easily digestible. - Probiotics found in yogurt can help reduce the duration and severity of acute diarrhea. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Avoid hyperosmolar foods like fruit juice in children with acute diarrhea as they can worsen symptoms and increase dehydration risk.</p>','<p>Fruit Juice Fuels Further Fluid Loss - remember to avoid hyperosmolar fruit juice in diarrhea.</p>','<p>Hydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS) is more important than dietary restrictions in acute diarrhea, but avoiding hyperosmolar foods like fruit juice can help reduce stool output.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. Wheat:</strong> Wheat is generally well-tolerated and recommended in diarrhea management. - <strong>B. Yogurt:</strong> Yogurt contains beneficial probiotics that help reduce diarrhea duration. - <strong>C. Rice:</strong> Rice is part of the recommended BRAT diet for acute diarrhea.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diet in Diarrhea','["diet","avoid","acute"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.763Z','2026-10-19T03:35:31.886Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56heh0043qjlr6qzxiuqy','mcq','A 6 months old child on goat milk, what you suspect in this case?','["The child will develop B12 deficiency anemia.","The child will develop folic acid deficiency anemia.","The child will be in good nutritional state.","The child will develop autoimmune anemia."]','The child will develop B12 deficiency anemia.','','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Nutritional Deficiencies','["goat milk","b12 deficiency","infant"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.865Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.865Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hb8002pqjlrarkicfpm','mcq','Meckel''s diverticulum has been conveniently referred to by the ''rule of 2s'' which explains the classic presentation of this congenital anomaly. Which one of the following is FALSE in this rule?','["found in approximately 2% of the general population","usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve","approximately 2 inches in length","found twice as commonly in females"]','found twice as commonly in females','<p><strong>Why found twice as commonly in females is correct:</strong> The ''rule of 2s'' for Meckel''s diverticulum is a clinical mnemonic that describes its epidemiology, anatomical location, size, and complications. The correct components are: 2% prevalence in the general population, located approximately 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve, typically 2 inches in length, and 2% of diverticula contain ectopic gastric mucosa. However, Meckel''s diverticulum is actually found twice as commonly in males, not females, making option D the false statement in this rule. The male predominance is thought to be related to the persistence of the vitelline duct during embryonic development, which occurs more frequently in males.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>found in approximately 2% of the general population:</strong> This is correct according to the rule of 2s, as Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract.</li>
<li><strong>usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve:</strong> This is anatomically correct, as Meckel''s diverticulum is a remnant of the omphalomesenteric duct and typically appears in the distal ileum.</li>
<li><strong>approximately 2 inches in length:</strong> This is correct, as most Meckel''s diverticula measure 2-5 cm in length, with 2 inches being the classic teaching point.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Meckel''s diverticulum is a true diverticulum containing all layers of the intestinal wall, unlike acquired diverticula which lack muscularis propria.</li>
<li>The most common complication is painless rectal bleeding due to ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa, which secretes acid and erodes adjacent ileal mucosa.</li>
<li>Diagnosis is best made with a technetium-99m pertechnetate scan (Meckel scan), which detects ectopic gastric mucosa.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember the rule of 2s for Meckel''s diverticulum but note the exception that it''s twice as common in males, not females.</p>','<p>2-2-2-2: 2% prevalence, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2% have ectopic gastric mucosa (but remember it''s 2:1 male predominance).</p>','<p>Meckel''s diverticulum should be considered in any child with painless lower GI bleeding, as it''s the most common cause of significant lower GI bleeding in children under 2 years.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>found in approximately 2% of the general population:</strong> This is correct according to the rule of 2s.</li>
<li><strong>usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve:</strong> This is anatomically correct.</li>
<li><strong>approximately 2 inches in length:</strong> This is correct as most measure 2-5 cm.</li>
<li><strong>found twice as commonly in females:</strong> This is false as it''s actually twice as common in males.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Meckel''s Diverticulum','["meckel","rule of 2s","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.748Z','2026-10-27T14:12:40.137Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hak002eqjlrjawtwwyc','mcq','Which of the following isn''t a common cause of gastrointestinal bleeding in infancy?','["Bacterial enteritis","Intussusception","Meckel''s diverticulum","Lymphonodular hyperplasia"]','Lymphonodular hyperplasia','<p><strong>Why Lymphonodular hyperplasia is correct:</strong> Lymphonodular hyperplasia is not a common cause of gastrointestinal bleeding in infancy, making it the correct answer to this question. This condition is a benign proliferation of lymphoid tissue in the colon that is typically asymptomatic and discovered incidentally during endoscopy. While it may be associated with symptoms like abdominal pain or changes in bowel habits, significant bleeding is rare and not a typical presentation. The other options listed are well-established causes of GI bleeding in infancy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bacterial enteritis:</strong> Common pathogens like Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter frequently cause colitis with bloody diarrhea in infants.</li>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> A leading cause of acute abdominal emergency and GI bleeding in infants, typically presenting with currant jelly stools.</li>
<li><strong>Meckel''s diverticulum:</strong> The most common cause of painless lower GI bleeding in children under 2 years due to ectopic gastric mucosa causing ulceration.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The most common causes of GI bleeding in infancy include infectious enteritis, intussusception, and Meckel''s diverticulum.</li>
<li>Lymphonodular hyperplasia is a benign condition that is usually asymptomatic and not associated with significant bleeding.</li>
<li>When evaluating an infant with GI bleeding, age-specific considerations are crucial as the differential diagnosis varies significantly with age.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Among the options provided, lymphonodular hyperplasia is the least likely to cause significant gastrointestinal bleeding in infancy compared to the other conditions.</p>','<p>BIM causes bleeding in infancy: Bacterial enteritis, Intussusception, Meckel''s diverticulum.</p>','<p>In infants with GI bleeding, always consider age-specific causes as the differential diagnosis changes significantly with development.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Bacterial enteritis:</strong> Common pathogens frequently cause colitis with bloody diarrhea in infants.</li>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> A leading cause of acute abdominal emergency and GI bleeding in infants.</li>
<li><strong>Meckel''s diverticulum:</strong> The most common cause of painless lower GI bleeding in children under 2 years.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["gi bleeding","infancy","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.724Z','2026-10-18T19:32:39.616Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56haw002mqjlr61fwneej','mcq','Cause of upper GI bleeding in neonates include :','["Gastritis from stress","Vit K deficiency","Mallory Weiss syndrome"]','Vit K deficiency','<p><strong>Why Vit K deficiency is correct:</strong> Vitamin K deficiency is a leading cause of upper GI bleeding in neonates due to the immaturity of their hepatic coagulation system. Neonates have low levels of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X) at birth, and this deficiency is exacerbated by the absence of gut flora, which normally produces vitamin K. Without adequate vitamin K, these factors cannot undergo gamma-carboxylation, rendering them inactive and causing a bleeding diathesis that can manifest as upper GI bleeding, melena, or hematemesis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gastritis from stress:</strong> While stress gastritis can occur in critically ill neonates, it is not a common cause of upper GI bleeding in otherwise healthy neonates and is more typically associated with severe illness or prolonged ICU stays.</li>
<li><strong>Mallory Weiss syndrome:</strong> This syndrome involves mucosal laceration at the gastroesophageal junction due to forceful vomiting or retching and is extremely rare in neonates whose vomiting patterns are different from older children and adults.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Vitamin K deficiency bleeding typically presents within the first week of life, with bleeding from the umbilicus, circumcision site, or GI tract.</li>
<li>Prophylactic vitamin K administration at birth is standard practice to prevent deficiency bleeding.</li>
<li>The classic triad of findings in vitamin K deficiency includes prolonged PT, PTT, and normal platelet count.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Neonatal upper GI bleeding is most commonly due to vitamin K deficiency, which impairs the production of functional clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X.</p>','<p>Vitamin K deficiency causes bleeding in ''newborns'' (Factors II, VII, IX, X)</p>','<p>Vitamin K deficiency bleeding can be prevented by routine intramuscular vitamin K administration at birth.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastritis from stress:</strong> Stress gastritis is uncommon in healthy neonates and more associated with critical illness.</li>
<li><strong>Mallory Weiss syndrome:</strong> This is extremely rare in neonates due to their different vomiting patterns.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["gi bleeding","neonate","vitamin k"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.736Z','2026-10-18T19:33:06.909Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hay002oqjlr8a3p6mbk','mcq','Orthostatic changes as indicator of blood loss : upon moving from supine to sitting position ?','["Increase in HR by 20BPM & decrease in BP by 10mmHg","Increase in HR by 10BPM & increase BP by 20mmHg","Increase in HR by 30BPM & increase BP by 20mmHg","Decrease in HR by 20BPM & increase BP by 10mmHg"]','Increase in HR by 20BPM & decrease in BP by 10mmHg','<p><strong>Why Increase in HR by 20BPM &amp; decrease in BP by 10mmHg is correct:</strong> Orthostatic changes indicating blood loss reflect the body''s compensatory response to decreased venous return when moving from supine to sitting position. In a healthy individual, this change typically causes a mild increase in heart rate (10-20 BPM) and minimal change in blood pressure. However, with significant blood loss, the compensatory mechanisms are maximized, resulting in a more pronounced tachycardia (20 BPM increase) as the heart attempts to maintain cardiac output, along with a drop in blood pressure (10 mmHg) as the reduced blood volume cannot adequately maintain perfusion pressure against gravity.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Increase in HR by 10BPM &amp; increase BP by 20mmHg:</strong> An increase in blood pressure with orthostatic changes is not physiologic and would suggest an inappropriate response, possibly due to autonomic dysfunction rather than blood loss.</li>
<li><strong>Increase in HR by 30BPM &amp; increase BP by 20mmHg:</strong> This combination is not a typical compensatory response to blood loss, as significant tachycardia without a corresponding drop in blood pressure would suggest other pathologies like anxiety or pheochromocytoma.</li>
<li><strong>Decrease in HR by 20BPM &amp; increase BP by 10mmHg:</strong> A decrease in heart rate with orthostatic changes suggests a vasovagal response or other forms of dysautonomia rather than the expected sympathetic response to hypovolemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Orthostatic vital signs should be measured after 3-5 minutes of standing to allow for full compensatory response.</li>
<li>A significant orthostatic drop in blood pressure (&gt;20 mmHg) with tachycardia indicates hypovolemia.</li>
<li>The degree of orthostatic changes correlates with the severity of blood loss, with more pronounced changes indicating greater volume depletion.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Orthostatic vital signs showing a 20 BPM increase in heart rate with a 10 mmHg drop in blood pressure indicate significant compensatory response to blood loss.</p>','<p>20/10 rule: 20 BPM HR increase and 10 mmHg BP drop indicate blood loss</p>','<p>Orthostatic vital signs are more sensitive indicators of volume depletion than supine vital signs alone.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Increase in HR by 10BPM &amp; increase BP by 20mmHg:</strong> Blood pressure increase with orthostatic changes is not physiologic.</li>
<li><strong>Increase in HR by 30BPM &amp; increase BP by 20mmHg:</strong> This combination suggests pathology like anxiety, not blood loss.</li>
<li><strong>Decrease in HR by 20BPM &amp; increase BP by 10mmHg:</strong> Bradycardia with orthostatic changes suggests vasovagal response, not hypovolemia.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Blood Loss','["blood loss","vital signs","orthostatic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.738Z','2026-10-18T19:33:06.911Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbj002wqjlrhzdk9qt3','mcq','A female child 4 years old came to emergency department with history of vomiting and diarrhea, she was irritable and, drinks eagerly, with normal skin elasticity, and eye. What is the most likely diagnosis of this child?','["Gastroenteritis with some dehydration.","Gastroenteritis with no dehydration.","Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration.","Gastroenteritis with disturbed conscious level."]','Gastroenteritis with no dehydration.','<p><strong>Why Gastroenteritis with no dehydration is correct:</strong> This child presents with vomiting and diarrhea but maintains normal skin elasticity and eye turgor, with the ability to drink eagerly, which are classic signs of no dehydration according to WHO guidelines. The absence of clinical indicators of fluid loss (such as prolonged capillary refill, dry mucous membranes, or decreased urine output) confirms adequate hydration status. The irritability is likely due to the acute illness itself rather than dehydration, as children with no dehydration typically remain alert and interactive.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with some dehydration:</strong> This would typically show at least two signs of dehydration such as restlessness, sunken eyes, or skin that goes back slowly when pinched.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration:</strong> This would present with multiple severe signs including lethargy, sunken eyes with no tears, very dry mouth and tongue, and skin that goes back very slowly when pinched.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with disturbed conscious level:</strong> This indicates severe dehydration or other serious complications like sepsis or electrolyte abnormalities, which would show additional concerning signs beyond irritability.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>WHO dehydration assessment focuses on general appearance, eyes, mouth/tongue, and skin turgor</li>
<li>Children with no dehydration maintain normal hydration status and can drink normally</li>
<li>Irritability alone is not sufficient to diagnose dehydration; specific physical findings must be present</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The absence of classic dehydration signs (normal skin elasticity, eye turgor, and ability to drink) confirms no dehydration in this child with gastroenteritis.</p>','<p>NO DEHYDRATION: Normal skin elasticity, No sunken eyes, Drinks eagerly</p>','<p>Dehydration assessment in children with gastroenteritis relies on specific clinical signs rather than subjective symptoms like irritability alone.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with some dehydration:</strong> Would show at least two signs of dehydration like restlessness or slow skin recoil.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration:</strong> Would present with lethargy, very dry mucous membranes, and extremely slow skin recoil.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with disturbed conscious level:</strong> Indicates severe complications beyond simple dehydration.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Dehydration','["diagnosis","dehydration","gastroenteritis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.759Z','2026-10-19T03:35:17.642Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbm002yqjlr7vl88m2w','mcq','Which is correct regarding antibiotic prescription for a child with acute diarrhea?','["The duration should be 7-10 days.","Should be reserved for cases with dysentery and cholera.","Amoxicillin is the first line of choice.","Should never be given at all."]','Should be reserved for cases with dysentery and cholera.','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Antibiotics should be reserved for specific cases of acute diarrhea in children, particularly when dysentery (bloody diarrhea) or cholera is present, as these conditions often have bacterial etiologies that benefit from targeted antimicrobial therapy. Most cases of acute diarrhea in children are viral or caused by non-invasive bacteria like ETEC, which do not require antibiotics and may actually prolong symptoms or increase the risk of complications like antibiotic-associated diarrhea. The indiscriminate use of antibiotics contributes to antimicrobial resistance and disrupts the normal gut flora, which is particularly important in children whose microbiome is still developing. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A. The duration should be 7-10 days:</strong> This is incorrect because antibiotic duration should be tailored to the specific pathogen identified, not a fixed 7-10 day course, and many cases don''t require antibiotics at all. - <strong>C. Amoxicillin is the first line of choice:</strong> This is incorrect because amoxicillin is not first-line for most causes of acute diarrhea; specific antibiotics like azithromycin for cholera or cefixime for Shigella are preferred when indicated. - <strong>D. Should never be given at all:</strong> This is incorrect because there are specific indications for antibiotics in acute diarrhea, including dysentery (Shigella), cholera, and severe invasive bacterial infections. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Antibiotics are indicated only for bloody diarrhea (dysentery), cholera, or severe invasive bacterial infections in children with acute diarrhea. - Viral causes (rotavirus, norovirus) account for most cases and do not require antibiotic treatment. - Antibiotic use in non-bacterial diarrhea increases the risk of antibiotic resistance and complications like C. difficile infection. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Antibiotics for acute diarrhea in children should be reserved for specific bacterial causes like dysentery and cholera, not used indiscriminately.</p>','<p>Dysentery and Cholera need Drugs (DCD) - remember these are the main indications for antibiotics in pediatric diarrhea.</p>','<p>In children with acute diarrhea, antibiotics should only be used when there''s evidence of bacterial infection, as most cases are viral and self-limiting.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. The duration should be 7-10 days:</strong> Antibiotic duration should be pathogen-specific, not fixed. - <strong>C. Amoxicillin is the first line of choice:</strong> Amoxicillin is not first-line for most acute diarrheal pathogens. - <strong>D. Should never be given at all:</strong> Antibiotics are indicated for specific bacterial causes like dysentery and cholera.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Antibiotics in Diarrhea','["antibiotics","treatment","acute"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.762Z','2026-10-19T03:35:31.883Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbp0031qjlrltrt6i6t','mcq','The commonest cause of non-infectious diarrhea is:','["Heavy metals","Oral antibiotics","Overfeeding","None of the above"]','Oral antibiotics','<p><strong>Why Oral antibiotics is correct:</strong> Oral antibiotics are the most common cause of non-infectious diarrhea due to their disruption of the normal gut flora, leading to an imbalance that allows pathogenic bacteria to proliferate. This condition, known as antibiotic-associated diarrhea, occurs when antibiotics kill off beneficial bacteria that normally compete with pathogens, creating an environment where organisms like Clostridium difficile can overproduce toxins. The mechanism involves direct mucosal irritation, bile salt deconjugation, and fermentation of undigested carbohydrates, all contributing to increased water secretion and decreased absorption in the colon.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heavy metals:</strong> Heavy metals can cause diarrhea through direct mucosal irritation and enzyme inhibition, but they are far less common than antibiotics as a cause of non-infectious diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Overfeeding:</strong> Overfeeding typically causes vomiting rather than diarrhea, as the infant''s immature gastrointestinal system cannot handle excessive volume.</li>
<li><strong>None of the above:</strong> This is incorrect because oral antibiotics are indeed the most common cause of non-infectious diarrhea.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Antibiotic-associated diarrhea affects up to 30% of patients receiving antibiotics, with higher rates in elderly and hospitalized patients.</li>
<li>The most common pathogens implicated are Clostridium difficile, Klebsiella, and Candida species.</li>
<li>Prevention includes using narrow-spectrum antibiotics when possible and probiotic supplementation in high-risk patients.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Oral antibiotics cause non-infectious diarrhea by disrupting gut flora balance, making them the most common etiology.</p>','<p>BAD: Bacteria After Disruption (antibiotics)</p>','<p>Antibiotic-associated diarrhea can range from mild self-limiting illness to life-threatening pseudomembranous colitis, requiring prompt recognition and treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Heavy metals:</strong> Cause diarrhea through direct mucosal irritation but are much less common than antibiotics.</li>
<li><strong>Overfeeding:</strong> Typically causes vomiting rather than diarrhea in infants.</li>
<li><strong>None of the above:</strong> Incorrect because antibiotics are indeed the most common cause.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Non-infectious Diarrhea','["non-infectious","antibiotics","cause"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.765Z','2026-10-18T19:41:01.506Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbu0032qjlrxvgmd22s','mcq','What is the most common organism causing diarrhea in children who consume eggs and milk?','["E. coli","Salmonella","Campylobacter","Shigella"]','Salmonella','<p><strong>Why Salmonella is correct:</strong> Salmonella is the most common organism causing diarrhea in children who consume eggs and milk because these foods are frequently contaminated with Salmonella enteritidis, which can penetrate the intestinal epithelium and cause inflammation. The organism produces endotoxins that stimulate fluid secretion and neutrophil infiltration, leading to bloody diarrhea in some cases. Children are particularly vulnerable due to their immature immune systems and higher likelihood of consuming undercooked eggs or unpasteurized milk.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>E. coli:</strong> While certain pathogenic strains of E. coli cause diarrhea, they are less commonly associated with eggs and milk compared to Salmonella.</li>
<li><strong>Campylobacter:</strong> Campylobacter is typically associated with undercooked poultry rather than eggs and milk.</li>
<li><strong>Shigella:</strong> Shigella causes bacillary dysentery but is more commonly transmitted through person-to-person contact or contaminated water rather than eggs and milk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Salmonella infection typically presents with fever, abdominal cramps, and diarrhea 12-72 hours after ingestion.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by stool culture or PCR testing for Salmonella antigens.</li>
<li>Treatment is primarily supportive with hydration, as antibiotics may prolong shedding and increase resistance risk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Salmonella is the most common diarrheal pathogen in children consuming eggs and milk due to frequent contamination of these food sources.</p>','<p>SEMS: Salmonella in Eggs, Milk, and Sick children</p>','<p>Salmonella infection can lead to bacteremia in immunocompromised individuals, making prompt recognition crucial even when symptoms appear mild.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>E. coli:</strong> Less commonly associated with eggs and milk compared to Salmonella.</li>
<li><strong>Campylobacter:</strong> Typically linked to undercooked poultry, not eggs and milk.</li>
<li><strong>Shigella:</strong> More commonly transmitted person-to-person or through contaminated water.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Foodborne Diarrhea','["salmonella","eggs","milk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.770Z','2026-10-18T19:41:01.507Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hc50038qjlrlk4wkqp2','mcq','For management of an infant with acute watery diarrhea and no dehydration we should use:','["Ampicillin","Ceftriaxone","ORS","Ondansetron"]','ORS','<p><strong>Why ORS is correct:</strong> Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS) is the cornerstone of management for acute watery diarrhea without dehydration because it replaces fluid and electrolytes lost in stool through the sodium-glucose cotransport system in the small intestine. The World Health Organization (WHO) and UNICEF recommend a specific formulation (glucose-based with sodium, potassium, chloride, and citrate) that promotes water absorption even in the presence of ongoing diarrhea. ORS is effective, safe, inexpensive, and can be administered at home, reducing the need for hospitalization and intravenous fluids in uncomplicated cases.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ampicillin:</strong> An antibiotic that is not indicated for acute watery diarrhea without dehydration, as most cases are viral and antibiotics can prolong diarrhea and increase resistance.</li>
<li><strong>Ceftriaxone:</strong> A third-generation cephalosporin antibiotic reserved for specific bacterial causes of diarrhea (like Shigella) and not for routine management of watery diarrhea without dehydration.</li>
<li><strong>Ondansetron:</strong> An antiemetic that may reduce vomiting but does not address the underlying fluid and electrolyte losses in diarrhea and is not first-line for uncomplicated cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>ORS should be given in small, frequent amounts (5-10 mL every 5-10 minutes) to improve tolerance and reduce vomiting.</li>
<li>Breastfeeding should be continued during diarrhea episodes, as breast milk provides essential nutrients and immune factors.</li>
<li>Antibiotics are only indicated for specific bacterial causes (e.g., Shigella, Campylobacter) and not for viral gastroenteritis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For acute watery diarrhea without dehydration, ORS is the treatment of choice to replace lost fluids and electrolytes.</p>','<p>ORS = Only Rehydration Solution (for uncomplicated diarrhea)</p>','<p>ORS is more effective than other fluids (like rice water or fruit juices) for rehydration in diarrhea because its specific electrolyte composition maximizes water absorption.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Ampicillin:</strong> Not indicated for routine watery diarrhea as most cases are viral and antibiotics can worsen outcomes.</li>
<li><strong>Ceftriaxone:</strong> Reserved for specific bacterial causes like Shigella, not first-line for uncomplicated diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Ondansetron:</strong> An antiemetic that doesn''t address fluid/electrolyte replacement in diarrhea.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Management','["management","ors","dehydration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.781Z','2026-10-18T19:41:03.758Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdt003jqjlraq64yud4','mcq','Which is incorrect about duodenal atresia','["Bilous vomiting","History of polyhydraminos","Double bubble","With abdominal distension"]','Bilous vomiting','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Duodenal Atresia','["duodenal atresia","diagnosis","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.841Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.841Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdu003kqjlru53llrq3','mcq','The hallmark of duodenal atresia is bilous vomiting and','["History of polyhydraminos","Jaundice","No abdominal distension","Visible prestalisis","Bile stained amniotic fluid"]','History of polyhydraminos','','','','','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Duodenal Atresia','["duodenal atresia","hallmark","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.842Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.842Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdz003oqjlrm9qy8guz','mcq','Gastroesophageal reflux is usually not associated with:','["Regurgitation following feeding","Self limited course in some cases","Bile stained vomitus","Possible wheezy chest"]','Bile stained vomitus','<p><strong>Why Bile stained vomitus is correct:</strong> Bile-stained vomitus is not associated with gastroesophageal reflux (GER) because bile is produced in the liver and stored in the gallbladder, then released into the duodenum. For bile to appear in vomitus, the reflux must extend beyond the stomach into the duodenum, which is characteristic of bilious vomiting rather than simple GER. GER involves reflux of gastric contents (acid and partially digested food) into the esophagus without bile contamination, making bile-stained vomitus an exclusion criterion for GER.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Regurgitation following feeding:</strong> This is a classic symptom of GER, occurring when gastric contents flow back into the esophagus after feeding.</li>
<li><strong>Self-limited course in some cases:</strong> GER often resolves spontaneously as the lower esophageal sphincter matures, making this a characteristic feature rather than an exclusion criterion.</li>
<li><strong>Possible wheezy chest:</strong> GER can cause respiratory symptoms including wheezing due to microaspiration of gastric contents into the lungs, making this associated with GER.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Bile-stained vomiting suggests pathology distal to the pylorus (e.g., malrotation, duodenal atresia) rather than GER.</li>
<li>GER symptoms typically include regurgitation, heartburn, and respiratory symptoms, but never bile-stained vomitus.</li>
<li>The presence of bile in vomitus should prompt immediate evaluation for surgical causes of intestinal obstruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Bile-stained vomitus excludes GER because bile originates in the duodenum, beyond the reach of typical gastroesophageal reflux.</p>','<p>GER = No Bile (GERB - Gastroesophageal Reflux without Bile)</p>','<p>Bile-stained vomiting in an infant is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise, as it may indicate malrotation with volvulus.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Regurgitation following feeding:</strong> This is a classic symptom of GER.</li>
<li><strong>Self-limited course in some cases:</strong> GER often resolves spontaneously as the infant matures.</li>
<li><strong>Possible wheezy chest:</strong> GER can cause respiratory symptoms including wheezing.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','GERD','["gerd","symptoms","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.847Z','2026-10-19T03:36:24.466Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdw003lqjlrul8lh2en','mcq','What is the most common type of tracheo-esophageal fistula?','["Atresia with distal fistula","Atresia with double fistula","Atresia with proximal fistula","Atresia with H-shaped fistula"]','Atresia with distal fistula','','','','','Basic','Respiratory','Tracheo-esophageal Fistula','["tracheo-esophageal fistula","diagnosis","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.844Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.482Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hef0041qjlrz1q75ygg','mcq','Mean definition of anemia is reduction of Hb concentration, what is correct about HB molecule?','["Its type is determined according to the heme group.","Contains iron in the ferrous form.","Hypoxia decrease it.","Contains four similar poly-peptide chains."]','Contains iron in the ferrous form.','','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Hemoglobin','["hemoglobin","iron","molecule"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.863Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.863Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56heg0042qjlrqwhocqxu','mcq','A 6 years old child complains of iron deficiency anemia, his physician prescribed him oral iron therapy, what is the first lab sign that ensures his response to therapy?','["Increased HB level.","Reticulocytosis.","Increased serum ferritin.","Increased MCV."]','Reticulocytosis.','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Iron Deficiency Anemia','["iron deficiency","iron therapy","reticulocytosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.864Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.864Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he2003qqjlrn05m9dmr','mcq','An infant aged 4 weeks presents with progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting after feeds. The most likely diagnosis is:','["Gastroesophageal reflux","Pyloric stenosis","Intussusception","Gastroenteritis"]','Pyloric stenosis','<p><strong>Why Pyloric stenosis is correct:</strong> Pyloric stenosis is characterized by hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle, causing gastric outlet obstruction in infants typically between 2-8 weeks of age. The progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting is classic due to the mechanical obstruction preventing gastric emptying. The diagnosis is supported by palpation of an "olive-sized" mass in the epigastrium and confirmed by ultrasound showing a thickened pyloric muscle (&gt;3mm) and elongated channel (&gt;15mm). This condition is more common in firstborn males and has a genetic component.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux:</strong> Typically presents with frequent spitting up, irritability, or failure to thrive rather than projectile vomiting, and vomiting may be bilious if complicated.</li>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> Presents with acute onset of bilious vomiting, currant jelly stools, and a palpable abdominal mass, usually in infants 3-36 months.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> Usually presents with bilious vomiting, diarrhea, fever, and systemic symptoms rather than the classic projectile vomiting pattern of pyloric stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Pyloric stenosis occurs most commonly in firstborn male infants between 2-8 weeks of age.</li>
<li>The classic "olive" mass may be palpable in the epigastrium in 60-90% of cases.</li>
<li>Ultrasound findings of a pyloric muscle thickness &gt;3mm and channel length &gt;15mm are diagnostic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pyloric stenosis presents in young infants with progressive non-bilious projectile vomiting and a palpable olive-sized mass in the epigastrium.</p>','<p>Pyloric: Progressive, Projectile, Pyloric olive, Positive ultrasound</p>','<p>Pyloric stenosis is a diagnosis of exclusion in infants with vomiting; always rule out malrotation with volvulus which is a surgical emergency.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux:</strong> Typically presents with frequent spitting up rather than projectile vomiting.</li>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> Presents with acute onset, bilious vomiting, currant jelly stools, and a palpable mass.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> Usually presents with bilious vomiting, diarrhea, and fever rather than classic projectile vomiting.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Pyloric Stenosis','["pyloric stenosis","vomiting","infant"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.850Z','2026-10-27T14:13:17.677Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he5003tqjlr640emv7h','mcq','Child with lead poisoning and lead level is 50 microgram. What you should do','["Evaluate source and education","IV EDTA","oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid"]','Evaluate source and education','<p><strong>Why Evaluate source and education is correct:</strong> For a lead level of 50 μg/dL, the initial management focuses on identifying and eliminating the source of exposure while providing education to prevent further exposure. This approach addresses the root cause of toxicity and is sufficient for moderate lead poisoning without immediate chelation therapy. The CDC guidelines recommend environmental investigation and family education for levels between 45-69 μg/dL, reserving chelation for higher levels or when symptoms are present. This stepwise approach ensures that the most critical intervention—removing the source—occurs before considering pharmacological treatment.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>IV EDTA:</strong> IV EDTA is reserved for severe lead poisoning (typically &gt;70 μg/dL) or when encephalopathy is present, making it inappropriate for this moderate level.</li>
<li><strong>oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> While DMSA is an option for lead levels &gt;45 μg/dL, it''s typically reserved for levels &gt;70 μg/dL or when environmental interventions fail, not as first-line for moderate poisoning.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lead management follows a stepwise approach: source elimination → education → chelation (if needed).</li>
<li>The threshold for chelation therapy is generally &gt;70 μg/dL or presence of symptoms.</li>
<li>Environmental investigation is critical in all cases of lead poisoning to prevent re-exposure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For moderate lead poisoning (50 μg/dL), the priority is identifying and eliminating the source of exposure before considering chelation therapy.</p>','<p>SEE: Source elimination, Education, then chelation if needed</p>','<p>Always investigate the source of lead exposure thoroughly</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>IV EDTA:</strong> Reserved for severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL) or encephalopathy, not moderate levels.</li>
<li><strong>oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> Typically used for higher lead levels (&gt;70 μg/dL) or when environmental interventions fail.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Lead Poisoning','["lead poisoning","treatment","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.853Z','2026-10-27T15:39:35.422Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56heq004aqjlrextd43e7','mcq','What is the most common cause of physiological anemia in infants?','["Decreased erythropoietin production","Increased hemolysis of fetal red blood cells","Dilution of blood volume due to growth","Iron deficiency due to inadequate intake"]','Dilution of blood volume due to growth','<p><strong>Why Dilution of blood volume due to growth is correct:</strong> Physiologic anemia in infants occurs between 2-6 months of age due to a disproportionate increase in plasma volume compared to red blood cell mass. This hemodilution happens because the infant''s blood volume expands approximately 50% in the first 3 months of life while red blood cell production decreases after the initial fetal-to-neonatal transition. The bone marrow''s response to this relative anemia is appropriate, with normal erythropoietin levels and reticulocyte response, distinguishing it from pathologic anemias.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Decreased erythropoietin production:</strong> This is incorrect because erythropoietin levels actually increase appropriately in response to the relative anemia of physiologic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Increased hemolysis of fetal red blood cells:</strong> While fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has a shorter lifespan than adult hemoglobin, this alone doesn''t explain the timing and magnitude of physiologic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency due to inadequate intake:</strong> Iron deficiency would cause a pathologic anemia with microcytic hypochromic cells and inappropriately low reticulocyte count, not the normocytic anemia seen in physiologic anemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Physiologic anemia occurs at 2-6 months of age and is a normal developmental phenomenon.</li>
<li>Hemoglobin nadir is typically 7-9 g/dL in full-term infants and 6-8 g/dL in preterm infants.</li>
<li>No treatment is required for physiologic anemia as it represents a normal adaptation to extrauterine life.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Physiologic anemia in infants is caused by hemodilution from disproportionate plasma volume expansion, not by decreased red cell production or increased destruction.</p>','<p>PHD: Plasma Hemodilution causes physiologic anemia</p>','<p>Physiologic anemia is a normal developmental phenomenon that should not be treated with iron supplements, which can actually interfere with normal erythropoiesis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Decreased erythropoietin production:</strong> Erythropoietin levels actually increase appropriately in physiologic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Increased hemolysis of fetal red blood cells:</strong> While fetal hemoglobin has a shorter lifespan, this doesn''t explain the timing and magnitude of physiologic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency due to inadequate intake:</strong> Iron deficiency causes microcytic hypochromic cells, not the normocytic anemia seen in physiologic anemia.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Physiological Anemia','["anemia","infancy","physiological"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.874Z','2026-10-19T03:38:05.011Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hep0049qjlrtnkka3z9','mcq','What is the MOST prevalent anemia in infancy?','["Pyruvate kinase deficiency.","Thalassemia.","Diamond Blackfan anemia.","Iron deficiency anemia."]','Iron deficiency anemia.','<p><strong>Why Iron deficiency anemia is correct:</strong> Iron deficiency anemia is the most common anemia in infancy worldwide, affecting approximately 25-50% of infants globally, particularly in those who are exclusively breastfed beyond 6 months without iron supplementation or those consuming cow''s milk before 12 months. This prevalence is due to the rapid growth and expansion of blood volume during infancy, which requires increased iron intake, coupled with limited iron stores at birth (depleted by 4-6 months) and insufficient dietary iron in many infants. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends iron supplementation for exclusively breastfed infants starting at 4 months of age to prevent deficiency.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pyruvate kinase deficiency:</strong> This is a rare hereditary hemolytic anemia that typically presents in childhood or adolescence, not infancy, and is much less common than iron deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> While thalassemia is common in certain populations, it is not the most prevalent anemia overall in infancy and is typically diagnosed later in childhood.</li>
<li><strong>Diamond Blackfan anemia:</strong> This is a rare congenital bone marrow failure syndrome that presents in infancy but affects only about 5-7 cases per million live births, making it extremely uncommon compared to iron deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Iron deficiency anemia peaks between 6-24 months of age due to rapid growth and depletion of fetal iron stores.</li>
<li>Risk factors include exclusive breastfeeding beyond 6 months without supplementation, early introduction of cow''s milk, and low birth weight.</li>
<li>Prevention includes iron-fortified formula, iron supplementation for breastfed infants starting at 4 months, and delaying cow''s milk until after 12 months.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Iron deficiency anemia is the most common anemia in infancy due to rapid growth depleting limited fetal iron stores and insufficient dietary iron intake.</p>','<p>IDA = Infancy''s Dominant Anemia</p>','<p>Iron deficiency in infancy can cause long-term neurodevelopmental deficits if not treated promptly, emphasizing the importance of early screening and prevention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pyruvate kinase deficiency:</strong> This is a rare hereditary hemolytic anemia that typically presents later in childhood.</li>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> While common in certain populations, it''s not the most prevalent anemia overall in infancy.</li>
<li><strong>Diamond Blackfan anemia:</strong> This is an extremely rare congenital bone marrow failure syndrome.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Anemia in Infancy','["anemia","infancy","prevalence"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.873Z','2026-10-19T03:38:10.906Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfs004jqjlrw6ntpnao','mcq','Female patient 9 years old diagnosed with iron deficiency anemia due to chronic malabsorption, which is correct about iron absorption?','["Iron is absorbed in the ferrous state","Iron is absorbed in the jejuna mucosa","Iron is absorbed 10 times more from human than from cow''s milk","Only 1% of dietary iron is absorbed/day"]','Iron is absorbed in the ferrous state','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Iron Absorption','["iron","absorption","malabsorption"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.912Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.912Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hft004kqjlrbo2zo9sa','mcq','A 2 year''s old child is suspected to have megaloblastic anemia, which of the following support your diagnosis?','["Decreased MCV","Hypo cellular bone marrow with megaloblastic changes","Hyper segmented neutrophils","Marked decrease in lactate dehydrogenase"]','Hyper segmented neutrophils','','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Megaloblastic Anemia','["anemia","megaloblastic","neutrophils"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.913Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.913Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfu004lqjlrd5m82aev','mcq','Basophilic stippling of RBCs is characteristic of','["Vitamin B12 deficiency","sideroblastic anemia","lead poisoning","Diamond- Blackfan"]','lead poisoning','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Anemias','["rbc","stippling","lead"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.914Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.914Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfw004mqjlrw572rvh6','mcq','which of the following types of anemia is normocytic normochromic?','["sickle cell anemia","sideroblastic anemia","lead poisoning","thalassemia"]','sickle cell anemia','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Anemias','["normocytic","normochromic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.916Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.916Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfx004nqjlrmt8pxaya','mcq','An infant 9 months old on cow''s milk and no weaning till now presented with pallor, his physician had ordered complete blood picture that showed microcytic hypo chromic anemia with reticulocytopenia, the next test to order for him is?','["Bone marrow aspiration","Iron study","HB electrophoresis","TSH, T4"]','Iron study','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Iron Deficiency Anemia','["infant","iron","microcytic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.917Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.917Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfz004oqjlrllcjsj6f','mcq','A five years old child comes to ER because he had been discovered to have increased serum lead level. Which of the following is characteristic of lead poisoning?','["Diarrhea","Constipation","Macrocytic hypo chromic anemia","Decreased erythrocyte protoporphyrin"]','Constipation','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Lead Poisoning','["lead","constipation","anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.919Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.919Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnw000btzlr13fhz6cc','mcq','True about Marasmus','["thrombocytosis","Leukopenia","Leukocytosis","Anemia"]','Anemia','','','','','Moderate','Nutrition','Marasmus','["marasmus","anemia","blood findings"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.116Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.523Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hew004fqjlre3pv3pgs','mcq','What is the BEST curative option for a chronic immune thrombocytopenia in a 10-year-old male child, with recurrent epistaxis and gum bleeding that impairs his daily active life style?','["Observation","Splenectomy","IVIG","Rituximab","Thrombopoietin receptor agonist"]','Splenectomy','<p><strong>Why Splenectomy is correct:</strong> Splenectomy is the most effective curative option for chronic immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) in children who fail first-line therapies and have significant bleeding symptoms, as it removes the primary site of platelet destruction and autoantibody production. The spleen is responsible for clearing antibody-coated platelets, and its removal can lead to sustained remission in approximately 70% of pediatric cases with chronic ITP. This approach is particularly effective in children who have failed corticosteroids and IVIG, as it addresses the underlying pathophysiology rather than just providing temporary platelet increments. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Observation:</strong> While observation may be appropriate for asymptomatic ITP, this child has significant bleeding (epistaxis, gum bleeding) impairing his lifestyle, requiring intervention. - <strong>IVIG:</strong> Provides temporary platelet increments but is not curative, requiring repeated administration. - <strong>Rituximab:</strong> While effective in some cases, it typically induces remission rather than cure, and responses may not be sustained long-term. - <strong>Thrombopoietin receptor agonist:</strong> These agents stimulate platelet production but do not address the autoimmune process and require continuous treatment. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Splenectomy is considered the most effective curative option for chronic pediatric ITP that fails first-line therapy. - The procedure typically results in sustained remission in approximately 70% of cases. - Post-splenectomy vaccination against encapsulated organisms is mandatory due to increased infection risk. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For chronic immune thrombocytopenia in children with significant bleeding that impairs lifestyle, splenectomy is the most effective curative option after first-line therapies have failed.</p>','<p>Splenectomy = Success for chronic ITP</p>','<p>Splenectomy for ITP in children has a higher response rate than in adults and can provide long-term remission, but requires careful consideration of infection risks.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Observation:</strong> Inadequate for symptomatic ITP impairing daily activities. - <strong>IVIG:</strong> Provides temporary benefit but is not curative. - <strong>Rituximab:</strong> Typically induces remission rather than cure and may not be sustained. - <strong>Thrombopoietin receptor agonist:</strong> Requires continuous treatment and doesn''t address autoimmune process.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Immune Thrombocytopenia','["itp","chronic","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.880Z','2026-10-18T19:43:10.148Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg8004vqjlrbjwzn9wg','mcq','What does congenital deficiency or absence of red cell precursors in otherwise normally cellular bone marrow results in?','["sideroblastic anemia","Blackfan-Diamond","Fanconi anemia","Aplastic anemia"]','Blackfan-Diamond','','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia Types','["blackfan","diamond","anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.928Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.928Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hga004wqjlr59pmqeaq','mcq','What is the most common cause of inherited pancytopenia?','["Fanconi anemia","Blackfan-Diamond","Leukemia","megaloblastic anemia"]','Fanconi anemia','','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Pancytopenia','["fanconi","pancytopenia","inherited"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.930Z','2026-10-04T01:28:19.930Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg0004pqjlrbe7f7g7x','mcq','A one year old child presented with malnutrition and severe pallor, he had history of resection of the terminal ileum at the neonatal period. The child is suspected to have','["Iron deficiency anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Thalassemia","Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood"]','Iron deficiency anemia','<p><strong>Why Iron deficiency anemia is correct:</strong> Iron deficiency anemia is the most likely diagnosis in this case due to the history of terminal ileum resection, which impairs iron absorption. The terminal ileum is the primary site for iron absorption, and its resection leads to decreased iron uptake despite adequate dietary intake. Iron is essential for hemoglobin synthesis, and its deficiency results in microcytic, hypochromic anemia with severe pallor. The child''s malnutrition further exacerbates the iron deficiency by reducing dietary iron intake and increasing iron demands for growth.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> This is caused by deficiencies in vitamin B12 or folate, not iron deficiency. The terminal ileum resection could theoretically affect B12 absorption (as it''s absorbed there), but the presentation with severe pallor and malnutrition is more characteristic of iron deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> This is a genetic disorder causing hemoglobin synthesis defects, not related to surgical resection or malnutrition. It typically presents earlier in life with microcytic anemia but without the surgical history.</li>
<li><strong>Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood:</strong> This is a self-limited condition causing temporary anemia in young children, typically without associated malnutrition or surgical history.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Terminal ileum resection is a classic cause of iron deficiency due to impaired absorption.</li>
<li>Iron deficiency anemia presents with microcytic, hypochromic red blood cells and severe pallor.</li>
<li>In children, rapid growth demands increase iron requirements, making them particularly susceptible to deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Terminal ileum resection causes iron deficiency anemia by impairing iron absorption, leading to microcytic hypochromic anemia in a growing child.</p>','<p>TILE = Terminal Ileum resection Leads to Iron deficiency</p>','<p>In children with surgical resection of the terminal ileum, lifelong iron supplementation monitoring is essential due to permanent absorption impairment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Caused by B12/folate deficiency, not iron deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Genetic disorder unrelated to surgical resection.</li>
<li><strong>Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood:</strong> Self-limited condition without surgical history.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Iron Deficiency Anemia','["iron","malnutrition","ileum"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.920Z','2026-10-19T03:39:16.622Z');
