INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krai000yo7lraemxore3','mcq','In nutritional rickets, what is the primary cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism?','["Direct activation of the parathyroid gland by high phosphate.","Hypocalcemia resulting from reduced intestinal calcium absorption.","Excess accumulation of unmineralized osteoid.","Hypervitaminosis D."]','Hypocalcemia resulting from reduced intestinal calcium absorption.','','','','','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","parathyroid","calcium"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.098Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.098Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kraj000zo7lrdkwjq16i','mcq','What physical sign is described as a horizontal groove at the insertion of the diaphragm?','["Rachitic rosary","Harrison''s sulcus","Craniotabes","Frontal bossing"]','Harrison''s sulcus','','','','','Moderate','Musculoskeletal','Rickets','["rickets","physical-sign","diaphragm"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.099Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.099Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kra1000qo7lr65gw5sgb','mcq','Cause of death in acute severe malnutrition include one of the following :','["Hyperglycemia","Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance","Hyperthermia","Brain atrophy"]','Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance','<p><strong>Why Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance is correct:</strong> Dehydration and electrolyte imbalances are the most common causes of death in acute severe malnutrition due to several factors: these patients have reduced body water reserves, impaired renal concentrating ability, and often have associated diarrhea that exacerbates fluid losses. Additionally, the refeeding process itself can trigger dangerous shifts in electrolytes, particularly hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia, which can lead to cardiac arrhythmias, respiratory failure, and sudden death. The combination of pre-existing dehydration and the metabolic stress of refeeding creates a lethal scenario if not carefully managed.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hyperglycemia:</strong> While refeeding can cause metabolic derangements, hyperglycemia is not a primary cause of death in acute malnutrition; hypoglycemia is actually more common and dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Hyperthermia:</strong> Elevated temperature may occur in infections but is not a direct cause of death in malnutrition; hypothermia is actually more common and dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Brain atrophy:</strong> While malnutrition can lead to developmental delays and brain changes, it is not an acute cause of death in these patients.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypoglycemia (blood glucose &lt;3.0 mmol/L) is a medical emergency in severe malnutrition requiring immediate treatment.</li>
<li>Refeeding syndrome typically develops within the first few days of treatment and is characterized by electrolyte shifts (especially phosphate, potassium, and magnesium).</li>
<li>Cardiac failure is a common complication of refeeding syndrome and requires careful monitoring and slow advancement of feeds.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Dehydration and electrolyte imbalances are the leading causes of death in acute severe malnutrition, requiring careful fluid and electrolyte management during treatment.</p>','<p>Death in SAM: Dehydration first, then Electrolytes</p>','<p>The ''refeeding syndrome'' is a critical risk in malnutrition treatment, with potentially fatal cardiac and neurological consequences from electrolyte shifts.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hyperglycemia:</strong> Not a primary cause; hypoglycemia is more common and dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Hyperthermia:</strong> Not a direct cause; hypothermia is more common and dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Brain atrophy:</strong> A chronic effect, not an acute cause of death.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Complications','["death","malnutrition","dehydration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.081Z','2026-10-19T03:40:23.087Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbz002eo7lrnk0ctm3b','mcq','4- How to differentiate between Blackfan Diamond syndrome and Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood','["HbF","Bone marrow","Normal skeletal survey"]','Normal skeletal survey','<p><strong>Why Normal skeletal survey is correct:</strong> Blackfan Diamond syndrome (DBA) is characterized by congenital pure red cell aplasia and distinctive skeletal abnormalities including short stature, thumb anomalies, and rib cage defects, which are visible on skeletal survey. Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood (TEC) is a self-limited condition with no associated congenital anomalies or skeletal abnormalities. The skeletal survey is the key differentiating factor because DBA shows characteristic physical anomalies while TEC does not. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>HbF:</strong> Both conditions can have elevated hemoglobin F levels, so this is not a reliable differentiator. - <strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Both conditions initially show a normocellular bone marrow with erythroid hypoplasia, making this finding non-specific for differentiation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - DBA is a congenital disorder with physical anomalies while TEC is an acquired condition that resolves spontaneously. - DBA is associated with mutations in ribosomal proteins (RPS19, RPS24, etc.) while TEC has no known genetic basis. - DBA often requires chronic corticosteroid therapy while TEC typically resolves without treatment. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Blackfan Diamond syndrome shows characteristic skeletal abnormalities on imaging while Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood does not.</p>','<p>DBA = Defects, Bone, Anemia (remember the physical anomalies in Diamond-Blackfan anemia)</p>','<p>In a newborn with anemia, always consider Blackfan Diamond syndrome if there are associated physical anomalies, as early recognition is crucial for appropriate management.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>HbF:</strong> Both conditions can have elevated hemoglobin F levels, so this is not a reliable differentiator. - <strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Both conditions initially show a normocellular bone marrow with erythroid hypoplasia, making this finding non-specific for differentiation.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Hematology','Anemias','["blackfan","diamond","erythroblastopenia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.151Z','2026-10-19T03:43:39.676Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krae000vo7lrhxanwgqo','mcq','Which one from the following investigations are incorrect in case of rickets?','["We may do genetic testing","Decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase level","Decrease in serum phosphorous level","Bone biopsy may be done"]','Decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase level','<p><strong>Why Decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase level is correct:</strong> In rickets, serum alkaline phosphatase levels are typically elevated, not decreased. Alkaline phosphatase is an enzyme produced by osteoblasts, and its levels increase as osteoblasts attempt to mineralize the bone matrix in response to vitamin D deficiency. This elevation is one of the key laboratory findings in rickets. A decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase would be inconsistent with the active bone remodeling and mineralization defect seen in rickets.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>We may do genetic testing:</strong> Genetic testing can be indicated in some cases of rickets, particularly when there''s a suspicion of hereditary forms like vitamin D-dependent rickets types 1 and 2.</li>
<li><strong>Decrease in serum phosphorous level:</strong> Hypophosphatemia is a common finding in rickets due to impaired renal reabsorption of phosphate and/or dietary deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Bone biopsy may be done:</strong> Bone biopsy can be performed in atypical cases of rickets to confirm the diagnosis and assess the degree of mineralization defect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Elevated serum alkaline phosphatase is a hallmark laboratory finding in rickets due to increased osteoblast activity.</li>
<li>Hypophosphatemia and hypocalcemia are common in rickets, though hypocalcemia may be less prominent in early stages.</li>
<li>Genetic testing is indicated in suspected hereditary forms of rickets, while bone biopsy may be used in atypical cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rickets is characterized by elevated alkaline phosphatase (not decreased), along with hypophosphatemia, and may require genetic testing or bone biopsy in certain cases.</p>','<p>Remember ''ALP goes UP in rickets'' - Alkaline Phosphatase is elevated, not decreased.</p>','<p>In rickets, the combination of elevated alkaline phosphatase with hypophosphatemia (and sometimes hypocalcemia) is more diagnostic than any single laboratory finding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>We may do genetic testing:</strong> Indicated in suspected hereditary forms of rickets.</li>
<li><strong>Decrease in serum phosphorous level:</strong> Common finding in rickets due to impaired phosphate handling.</li>
<li><strong>Bone biopsy may be done:</strong> Useful in atypical cases to confirm diagnosis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","laboratory","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.094Z','2026-10-27T14:15:48.327Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kram0013o7lrjayat676','mcq','Sign of Hypo-phosphatic rickets','["Delayed teething","Short stature","Deformities","Large head"]','Short stature','<p><strong>Why Short stature is correct:</strong> Hypophosphatemic rickets is caused by defects in renal phosphate rehandling or vitamin D metabolism, leading to hypophosphatemia. The impaired mineralization of growth plates results in inadequate bone formation, which manifests as short stature due to reduced longitudinal bone growth. Unlike vitamin D deficiency rickets, which typically presents with growth plate widening and rachitic rosary, hypophosphatemic rickets primarily affects growth velocity rather than causing classic bone deformities. The phosphate deficiency directly impacts the mineralization process, stunting overall skeletal growth.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Delayed teething:</strong> While nutritional deficiencies can affect dental development, delayed teething is not a specific or primary feature of hypophosphatemic rickets.</li>
<li><strong>Deformities:</strong> Bone deformities like bowing of legs or frontal bossing are more characteristic of vitamin D deficiency rickets rather than hypophosphatemic rickets.</li>
<li><strong>Large head:</strong> Macrocephaly is not associated with hypophosphatemic rickets; it may occur in conditions like hydrocephalus or certain genetic syndromes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypophosphatemic rickets is distinguished from vitamin D deficiency rickets by its resistance to vitamin D therapy and need for phosphate supplementation.</li>
<li>The condition is often X-linked dominant, affecting males more severely than females.</li>
<li>Laboratory findings show low serum phosphate with normal or high calcium and alkaline phosphatase levels.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypophosphatemic rickets primarily causes short stature due to impaired mineralization of growth plates, unlike vitamin D deficiency rickets which causes classic bone deformities.</p>','<p>PHOSPHATE: Poor Height, Osteopenia, Short Stature, Phosphate deficiency, Hypophosphatemia, Alkaline phosphatase normal/elevated, Treatment with phosphate</p>','<p>Hypophosphatemic rickets requires both phosphate and active vitamin D supplementation, as it''s resistant to standard vitamin D therapy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Delayed teething:</strong> Not a specific feature of hypophosphatemic rickets.</li>
<li><strong>Deformities:</strong> More characteristic of vitamin D deficiency rickets.</li>
<li><strong>Large head:</strong> Not associated with this condition.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","phosphorus","short-stature"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.102Z','2026-10-19T03:41:01.177Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krar0018o7lru7ec0ed6','mcq','Preterm infant ready for discharge but can''t latch effectively. The mother has insufficient milk despite good lactational support. What is the feeding plan?','["Exclusively Cow''s milk formula","Soy-based formula","AR formula","Express breast milk with preterm formula supplementation"]','Express breast milk with preterm formula supplementation','<p><strong>Why Express breast milk with preterm formula supplementation is correct:</strong> Preterm infants have unique nutritional requirements that exceed those of term infants, including higher protein, energy, and specific micronutrient needs. When a preterm infant cannot latch effectively and the mother has insufficient milk despite lactational support, expressing breast milk and supplementing with preterm formula provides the optimal nutrition. This approach combines the immunological benefits of breast milk with the specialized nutritional content of preterm formula, ensuring the infant receives adequate calories, protein, and nutrients for catch-up growth while still receiving the protective factors in breast milk. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Exclusively Cow''s milk formula:</strong> Standard cow''s milk formula does not meet the increased nutritional needs of preterm infants and lacks the specific immunological benefits of breast milk. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Soy formula is not appropriate for preterm infants as it has lower protein quality and bioavailability than either breast milk or cow''s milk-based preterm formulas. - <strong>AR (Anti-Reflux) formula:</strong> There is no indication of reflux in this case, and AR formula would not address the core issues of inadequate milk supply and inability to latch. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Preterm infants require higher protein and energy intake than term infants for appropriate catch-up growth. - The combination of expressed breast milk and preterm formula provides both immunological protection and specialized nutrition. - Fortification of breast milk may be necessary for very preterm or growth-restricted infants. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For preterm infants who cannot latch and have insufficient milk, the optimal feeding plan is expressed breast milk supplemented with preterm formula to meet their unique nutritional needs while still receiving breast milk''s immunological benefits.</p>','<p>P.R.E.M.: Preterm requires specialized nutrition, Retain breast milk benefits, Express milk regularly, Mix with preterm formula.</p>','<p>Preterm infants have such high nutritional requirements that even when mothers are expressing breast milk, supplementation with preterm formula is often necessary to achieve adequate growth.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Exclusively Cow''s milk formula:</strong> Standard cow''s milk formula does not meet the increased nutritional needs of preterm infants. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Soy formula has lower protein quality and bioavailability than either breast milk or cow''s milk-based preterm formulas. - <strong>AR formula:</strong> There is no indication of reflux in this case, and AR formula would not address inadequate milk supply.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Breastfeeding','["preterm","breastfeeding","milk supply"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.107Z','2026-10-19T03:41:13.777Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kray001do7lryh2zpu1g','mcq','An 18-month-old child under evaluation for abdominal distension, failure to thrive, and constipation starting in infancy with a poor response to medical management. Rectal examination shows an empty rectum with explosive passage of stool. Of the following, what is the MOST appropriate diagnostic tool?','["Abdominal ultrasound","Plain radiograph of abdomen","Anorectal manometry","Barium enema"]','Barium enema','<p><strong>Why Barium enema is correct:</strong> A barium enema is the most appropriate diagnostic tool for this clinical presentation because it can demonstrate the classic transition zone between the dilated proximal colon and the narrowed distal aganglionic segment, which is pathognomonic for Hirschsprung''s disease. This test is particularly useful in older infants and children where the diagnosis is being reconsidered after initial medical management failure, as it can show the characteristic funnel-shaped rectum and the absence of normal haustra in the distal colon. The barium enema also helps differentiate Hirschsprung''s disease from other causes of constipation such as functional constipation or hypothyroidism. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> While ultrasound can show dilated bowel loops, it lacks the specificity to diagnose Hirschsprung''s disease and cannot demonstrate the transition zone. - <strong>Plain radiograph of abdomen:</strong> This may show dilated bowel loops but is nonspecific and cannot confirm the diagnosis of Hirschsprung''s disease. - <strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> This test can be supportive but is less definitive than barium enema for diagnosing Hirschsprung''s disease, especially in older children where the diagnosis is being reconsidered. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The explosive passage of stool after rectal examination is a classic finding in Hirschsprung''s disease due to the functional obstruction. - Barium enema is the imaging modality of choice to demonstrate the transition zone in suspected Hirschsprung''s disease. - Definitive diagnosis requires rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Barium enema is the most appropriate diagnostic tool for suspected Hirschsprung''s disease as it demonstrates the characteristic transition zone.</p>','<p>Barium enema for Hirschsprung''s: ''See the Transition Zone'' - the classic radiographic finding.</p>','<p>In older children with constipation and an empty rectum on exam, always consider Hirschsprung''s disease even if initial medical management was attempted.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> Lacks specificity for diagnosing Hirschsprung''s disease. - <strong>Plain radiograph of abdomen:</strong> Nonspecific and cannot confirm the diagnosis. - <strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> Less definitive than barium enema for diagnosing Hirschsprung''s disease.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["diagnostic","abdominal","constipation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.114Z','2026-10-19T03:41:42.992Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb3001io7lre7db33py','mcq','Which of the following statements characterizes functional constipation?','["Common with breast fed children.","Common in children above 5 years of age.","Can have organic cause.","The child passes stool less than 2 times/week."]','Common with breast fed children.','<p><strong>Why Common with breast fed children is correct:</strong> Functional constipation is indeed more common in breastfed children due to the unique composition of breast milk, which promotes softer stools and more frequent bowel movements. The high lactose content in breast milk creates an acidic environment in the colon that stimulates peristalsis, while the presence of human milk oligosaccharides (HMOs) promotes a healthy gut microbiome that facilitates regular bowel movements. Additionally, breastfed infants typically have more efficient digestion and absorption of nutrients compared to formula-fed infants, resulting in less residual waste to form stool. This physiological advantage explains why functional constipation is less common in breastfed children compared to formula-fed ones.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Common in children above 5 years of age:</strong> Functional constipation is actually more common in toddlers and preschool children (1-5 years) during toilet training rather than being specifically associated with children above 5 years.</li>
<li><strong>Can have organic cause:</strong> Functional constipation is defined by the absence of organic causes; if an organic cause is present, it would be classified as organic constipation, not functional.</li>
<li><strong>The child passes stool less than 2 times/week:</strong> While decreased stool frequency can be a feature of constipation, the diagnostic criteria for functional constipation in children typically include stool frequency less than 3 times per week for at least 1 month, along with other symptoms like hard stools or painful defecation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Functional constipation is more common in formula-fed children due to different stool composition and digestion patterns.</li>
<li>The Rome IV criteria are used for diagnosing functional constipation in children, requiring symptoms for at least 1 month.</li>
<li>Behavioral factors like toilet training resistance and withholding behaviors are major contributors to functional constipation in toddlers.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation is paradoxically more common in formula-fed children, not breastfed children, due to differences in stool composition and digestion.</p>','<p>BFS: Breastfeeding Frequency Stools (breastfed babies have more frequent stools)</p>','<p>When evaluating constipation in infants, always consider the feeding method as breastfed infants typically have more frequent bowel movements than formula-fed infants.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Common in children above 5 years of age:</strong> Functional constipation is more common in toddlers during toilet training.</li>
<li><strong>Can have organic cause:</strong> Functional constipation specifically excludes organic causes by definition.</li>
<li><strong>The child passes stool less than 2 times/week:</strong> The diagnostic threshold is typically less than 3 times per week.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["functional","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.119Z','2026-10-19T03:42:03.603Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcc002so7lr055z1ktq','mcq','18- What is the MOST prevalent anemia in infancy?','["Pyruvate kinase deficiency.","Thalassemia.","Diamond Blackfan anemia.","Iron deficiency anemia."]','Iron deficiency anemia.','','','','','Basic','Hematology','Anemia Prevalence','["anemia","infancy","prevalent"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.164Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.164Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugny000etzlr7h7oaoog','mcq','What is the first clinical sign in active rickets:','["Delayed teething","Knockknee","rossary beads","Crainotabes"]','Crainotabes','<p><strong>Why Crainotabes is correct:</strong> Craniotabes is the earliest clinical sign of active rickets, characterized by softening of the skull bones that feels like a ping-pong ball ball when pressed. This occurs because vitamin D deficiency impairs calcium absorption, leading to inadequate mineralization of the osteoid matrix in growing bones. The parietal and occipital bones are most affected due to their rapid growth during infancy, and this sign typically appears in infants under 6 months of age when the skull is still soft and pliable. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Delayed teething:</strong> While teething may be delayed in rickets, it is not the earliest sign and typically appears later in infancy. - <strong>Knockknee:</strong> This is a late manifestation of rickets that occurs when weight-bearing bones are affected, usually after the child has started walking. - <strong>Rosary beads:</strong> Beading of the ribs (rachitic rosary) is a later sign that appears as costochondral junctions become enlarged due to unmineralized osteoid. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Craniotabes is pathognomonic for early rickets but can also be seen in normal newborns, so it must be interpreted in the context of other signs and vitamin D levels. - Rickets follows a predictable progression: early signs (craniotabes, frontal bossing) → skeletal deformities (bowing, knockknee) → complications (hypocalcemia, tetany). - The skull is affected first in rickets because it has the most rapid growth rate in infancy. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Craniotabes is the earliest sign of rickets because the skull bones have the highest growth rate and are the first to show inadequate mineralization in vitamin D deficiency.</p>','<p>CRANIOTABES: Cranium first affected in rickets (Cranium Rapidly Affected, Not Initially Other Tissues)</p>','<p>Craniotabes can be a normal finding in newborns and typically resolves by 3-6 months of age; persistence beyond this age strongly suggests rickets.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Delayed teething:</strong> Teething delays occur later in infancy, not as the first sign. - <strong>Knockknee:</strong> This is a late weight-bearing deformity, not an early finding. - <strong>Rosary beads:</strong> This represents costochondral beading, a later manifestation of rickets.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","clinical sign","crainotabes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.118Z','2026-10-27T14:40:18.419Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb8001oo7lrkjzb158y','mcq','Which 2 symptoms in the Past 2 months are sufficient to diagnose constipation in children ?','["Having 2 or fewer defecations per week & large fecal mass in rectum","Painful hard bowel movements & abdominal tenderness","Large diameter stool obstructing the toilet & defecation every other day","At least 2 fecal incontinence per week in toilet trained child & abdominal pain"]','Having 2 or fewer defecations per week & large fecal mass in rectum','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["diagnosis","symptoms","criteria"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.124Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.124Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb9001po7lr7qt03bg7','mcq','Which of the following metabolic disorders is a metabolic cause of constipation?','["Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hyperthyroidism","hypernatremia"]','Hypokalemia','','','','','Basic','Endocrine','Metabolic Constipation','["metabolic","constipation","electrolyte"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.125Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.125Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbd001qo7lr0e3jho3b','mcq','Which of the following metabolic disorders doesn''t cause constipation','["Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypothyroidism","Diabetes insipidus"]','Hyperkalemia','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["metabolic","constipation","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.129Z','2026-10-04T02:35:25.129Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugoc000rtzlrwxwlb65y','mcq','What are the causes of tetany?','["Alkalosis","Acidosis","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia"]','Alkalosis','','','','','Moderate','Endocrine','Tetany','["tetany","alkalosis","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.132Z','2026-10-04T03:38:57.132Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbj001xo7lrhe99syxq','mcq','A mother of a 10-month-old infant, who recently underwent hydrostatic reduction of intussusception, asks you if there is a risk of recurrence. Within which period will you inform them that the recurrence rate is approximately 10%?','["7 days of reduction","3 days of reduction","14 days of reduction","21 days of reduction"]','7 days of reduction','<p><strong>Why 7 days of reduction is correct:</strong> The recurrence rate of intussusception after hydrostatic reduction is highest within the first 7 days, approximately 10%, due to incomplete reduction or persistent lead points that may not be immediately apparent. The pathophysiology involves the telescoping of one segment of bowel into another, and if the reduction is incomplete or if a pathologic lead point (such as Meckel''s diverticulum or lymphoma) is present, the intussusception is likely to recur within this timeframe. The hydrostatic reduction itself does not address underlying pathologic lead points, which may cause recurrence. Additionally, the edema and inflammation in the bowel wall after reduction may predispose to re-intussusception during the immediate post-reduction period.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3 days of reduction:</strong> While recurrence can occur within 3 days, the peak recurrence rate is specifically documented at approximately 10% within the first 7 days, making this timeframe too narrow.</li>
<li><strong>14 days of reduction:</strong> By 14 days, the recurrence rate drops significantly below 10% as the bowel wall heals and any transient edema resolves.</li>
<li><strong>21 days of reduction:</strong> After 21 days, the recurrence rate is very low (less than 5%) as the risk period for immediate recurrence has passed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Recurrence of intussusception is most common within the first 7 days after reduction, with a rate of approximately 10%.</li>
<li>Recurrence is more likely if the initial reduction was incomplete or if a pathologic lead point is present.</li>
<li>Parents should be counseled to return immediately if symptoms recur, as prompt re-reduction is necessary.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Intussusception recurrence peaks at 10% within 7 days of reduction due to incomplete reduction or persistent lead points.</p>','<p>7-day rule: Recurrence risk is highest within 7 days of intussusception reduction.</p>','<p>Recurrence of intussusception is more common in children with pathologic lead points, such as Meckel''s diverticulum or lymphoma, and may require surgical intervention rather than repeated hydrostatic reduction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>3 days of reduction:</strong> The recurrence rate peaks at 10% within 7 days, not 3 days.</li>
<li><strong>14 days of reduction:</strong> By 14 days, the recurrence rate drops below 10%.</li>
<li><strong>21 days of reduction:</strong> After 21 days, the recurrence rate is very low.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["intussusception","recurrence","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.135Z','2026-10-19T03:42:47.213Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbk001yo7lrbe3dfkov','mcq','Confirmation of a Meckel''s diverticulum can be difficult. Of the following, what is the investigation of choice in this case?','["Barium studies","Plain abdominal radiographs","Radionuclide technetium-99m scans","Abdominal ultrasound"]','Radionuclide technetium-99m scans','<p><strong>Why Radionuclide technetium-99m scans is correct:</strong> The investigation of choice for confirming Meckel''s diverticulum is a radionuclide technetium-99m scan because it detects ectopic gastric mucosa, which is present in approximately 50% of Meckel''s diverticula and secretes technetium-99m pertechnetate, allowing for visualization. The scan works by exploiting the property of gastric mucosa to actively transport the pertechnetate ion, which is then concentrated in the ectopic gastric tissue within the diverticulum. This makes it highly specific for Meckel''s diverticulum when positive, with a sensitivity of about 85-90% in symptomatic children.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Barium studies:</strong> Barium studies are not sensitive for detecting Meckel''s diverticulum as they may miss small diverticula and cannot reliably identify ectopic gastric mucosa.</li>
<li><strong>Plain abdominal radiographs:</strong> Plain abdominal radiographs are nonspecific and cannot visualize Meckel''s diverticulum or its complications effectively.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> While ultrasound can detect complications like intussusception or bowel obstruction, it is not reliable for diagnosing Meckel''s diverticulum itself due to its small size and variable location.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract and is caused by incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct.</li>
<li>The classic presentation is painless rectal bleeding in a young child, often with a ''currant jelly'' stool.</li>
<li>The radionuclide scan is most accurate when performed during active bleeding, as the pertechnetate will be concentrated in the ectopic gastric mucosa.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Technetium-99m scan detects ectopic gastric mucosa in Meckel''s diverticulum by concentrating pertechnetate, making it the diagnostic gold standard.</p>','<p>Meckel''s scan: ''Tc-99m for ectopic gastric mucosa'' - think ''Technetium for Tissue''.</p>','<p>Meckel''s diverticulum is often called the ''great imitator'' because its symptoms can mimic appendicitis, inflammatory bowel disease, or intussusception.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Barium studies:</strong> Cannot reliably detect ectopic gastric mucosa in Meckel''s diverticulum.</li>
<li><strong>Plain abdominal radiographs:</strong> Too nonspecific to visualize Meckel''s diverticulum.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> Not reliable for diagnosing Meckel''s diverticulum itself.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Meckel''s diverticulum','["meckel","diagnosis","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.136Z','2026-10-19T03:42:47.215Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugod000stzlrgaablu5r','mcq','Not seen in Rickets is:','["Craniotabes","Bow legs","Increased alkaline phosphatase","Increased acid phosphatase"]','Increased acid phosphatase','','','','','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","acid phosphatase","findings"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.133Z','2026-10-04T03:38:57.133Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbe001ro7lrs8n63z9b','mcq','Hirschsprung disease hasn''t been seen in association with which of the following?','["Microcephaly","mental retardation","autism","cleft lip"]','cleft lip','<p><strong>Why cleft lip is correct:</strong> Hirschsprung disease is a congenital disorder characterized by the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon, leading to functional obstruction. It has well-established associations with genetic syndromes such as Down syndrome, Waardenburg syndrome, and Mowat-Wilson syndrome, which often include microcephaly and mental retardation. Additionally, Hirschsprung disease has been linked to autism spectrum disorders due to shared genetic pathways affecting enteric nervous system development. However, cleft lip is not part of the typical constellation of associated anomalies with Hirschsprung disease, as they arise from distinct embryological processes—neural crest cell migration for Hirschsprung versus fusion of the maxillary and medial nasal processes for cleft lip.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Microcephaly:</strong> Microcephaly is commonly associated with Hirschsprung disease, particularly in syndromic forms like Mowat-Wilson syndrome, which involves mutations in the ZEB2 gene affecting both neural crest development.</li>
<li><strong>Mental retardation:</strong> Cognitive impairment is frequently seen in Hirschsprung disease, especially in syndromic presentations where the same genetic defects impact central nervous system development.</li>
<li><strong>Autism:</strong> There is a well-documented association between Hirschsprung disease and autism spectrum disorders, with shared genetic factors affecting enteric and central nervous system development.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hirschsprung disease is caused by failure of neural crest cell migration to the colon, resulting in aganglionosis.</li>
<li>The most common presentation is failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life, followed by constipation and abdominal distension.</li>
<li>Associated anomalies are more common in long-segment disease and include Down syndrome, Waardenburg syndrome, and Mowat-Wilson syndrome.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hirschsprung disease is associated with neurodevelopmental disorders (microcephaly, mental retardation, autism) but not with cleft lip, as they arise from distinct embryological processes.</p>','<p>Hirschsprung''s ''MAD'' associations: Microcephaly, Autism, and mental retardation (but not cleft lip)</p>','<p>Hirschsprung disease should be suspected in any newborn with delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours, particularly if there is a family history or associated anomalies.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Microcephaly:</strong> Commonly associated with Hirschsprung disease in syndromic forms.</li>
<li><strong>Mental retardation:</strong> Frequently seen in Hirschsprung disease, especially in syndromic presentations.</li>
<li><strong>Autism:</strong> Well-documented association due to shared genetic factors affecting neural development.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Hirschsprung disease','["hirschsprung","association","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.130Z','2026-10-27T16:04:54.186Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbf001so7lrdynjuvkn','mcq','Which Rome IV criterion supports the diagnosis of functional constipation in a toilet-trained child?','["Bloody diarrhea","Fewer than two defecations per week","Fever with abdominal pain","Persistent vomiting"]','Fewer than two defecations per week','<p><strong>Why Fewer than two defecations per week is correct:</strong> The Rome IV criterion of ''fewer than two defecations per week'' is a specific diagnostic threshold for functional constipation in toilet-trained children, reflecting the normal frequency of bowel movements in this population. This criterion is based on extensive epidemiological data showing that healthy children typically have at least three bowel movements per week, with fewer than two indicating significant constipation. The Rome IV criteria were developed through international consensus to standardize the diagnosis of functional gastrointestinal disorders, and this specific frequency criterion helps distinguish functional constipation from normal variations in bowel habits.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bloody diarrhea:</strong> Bloody diarrhea suggests an inflammatory or infectious process rather than functional constipation and would prompt evaluation for conditions like inflammatory bowel disease or infectious colitis.</li>
<li><strong>Fever with abdominal pain:</strong> Fever with abdominal pain indicates an infectious or inflammatory process that requires urgent evaluation, not consistent with the chronic, pain-predominant nature of functional constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Persistent vomiting:</strong> Persistent vomiting suggests a mechanical obstruction, metabolic disorder, or other serious condition that would not be classified as functional constipation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The Rome IV criteria for functional constipation in children include at least two of: fewer than three defecations per week, painful defecation, large stool mass in rectum, or history of excessive stool withholding.</li>
<li>Functional constipation is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring evaluation for red flags like failure to thrive, bloody stools, or onset before 4 years of age.</li>
<li>Treatment involves a combination of behavioral interventions, dietary modifications, and laxatives, with maintenance therapy often needed for 6-12 months.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The Rome IV criterion of ''fewer than two defecations per week'' is a key diagnostic threshold for functional constipation in toilet-trained children, based on epidemiological data defining normal bowel frequency.</p>','<p>Rome IV constipation: ''2 or less'' means ''yes'' to functional constipation diagnosis</p>','<p>Functional constipation in children often presents with ''retentive posturing'' (crossing legs, squeezing buttocks) and may lead to fecal incontinence due to overflow from impaction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Bloody diarrhea:</strong> Suggests inflammatory or infectious process, not functional constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Fever with abdominal pain:</strong> Indicates infectious or inflammatory process requiring urgent evaluation.</li>
<li><strong>Persistent vomiting:</strong> Suggests mechanical obstruction or serious condition, not functional constipation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Functional constipation','["rome iv","functional constipation","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.131Z','2026-10-27T16:04:54.190Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbt0026o7lrabbzua3i','mcq','8- Gastroesophageal reflux is usually not associated with:','["Regurgitation following feeding","Self limited course in some cases","Bile stained vomitus","Possible wheezy chest"]','Bile stained vomitus','<p><strong>Why bile stained vomitus is correct:</strong> Bile stained vomitus is not typically associated with gastroesophageal reflux (GER) because GER involves the reflux of gastric contents (acid, partially digested food) into the esophagus without entering the duodenum. Bile is produced in the liver, stored in the gallbladder, and released into the duodenum to aid in fat digestion. For bile to appear in vomitus, the reflux must extend beyond the pylorus into the duodenum, which is characteristic of conditions like intestinal obstruction or malrotation, not typical GER.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Regurgitation following feeding:</strong> This is a classic symptom of GER, as the increased intra-abdominal pressure during feeding can push gastric contents back into the esophagus.</li>
<li><strong>Self limited course in some cases:</strong> Many cases of GER in infants resolve spontaneously as the lower esophageal sphincter matures, typically by 12-18 months of age.</li>
<li><strong>Possible wheezy chest:</strong> GER can cause respiratory symptoms including wheezing due to microaspiration of gastric contents into the lungs, triggering bronchospasm.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>GER is common in infants and typically presents with regurgitation, vomiting, and irritability, often improving with age.</li>
<li>The "rule of twos" describes GER: resolves in most by 2 years of age, occurs 2 hours after feeding, lasts 2 minutes, and occurs twice daily.</li>
<li>Differentiating GER from GERD is important; GERD requires intervention when complications like failure to thrive, esophagitis, or respiratory symptoms occur.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Bile-stained vomitus indicates duodenogastric reflux, which is not seen in typical gastroesophageal reflux.</p>','','<p>In infants, bile-stained vomiting should always raise concern for intestinal obstruction or malrotation, not just GER.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Regurgitation following feeding:</strong> Classic GER symptom due to incompetent lower esophageal sphincter.</li>
<li><strong>Self limited course in some cases:</strong> GER often resolves spontaneously as the infant matures.</li>
<li><strong>Possible wheezy chest:</strong> GER can cause respiratory symptoms from microaspiration.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Gastroesophageal Reflux','["ger","reflux","vomiting"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.145Z','2026-10-19T03:43:20.311Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbv0029o7lri3afttin','mcq','11- An 18-month-old boy presents with sudden onset of vomiting, crampy abdominal pain, draws up his knees, cries out, and exhibits pallor in a colicky pattern every 15-20 minutes. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute appendicitis","Gastroenteritis","Pancreatitis","Intussusception"]','Intussusception','<p><strong>Why Intussusception is correct:</strong> Intussusception is the most likely diagnosis in this 18-month-old boy presenting with the classic triad of sudden onset vomiting, colicky abdominal pain (drawing up knees), and pallor occurring in a periodic pattern. Intussusception occurs when a segment of intestine telescopes into an adjacent segment, causing obstruction and ischemia. The intermittent nature of symptoms corresponds to the episodes of peristalsis attempting to push the intussuscepted segment along before it becomes completely obstructed. This condition is most common in infants 6-36 months old and often follows a viral illness that causes hypertrophy of Peyer''s patches in the ileum.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Acute appendicitis:</strong> While appendicitis can cause vomiting and abdominal pain, it typically presents with constant, worsening pain rather than the intermittent colicky pattern described, and would not have the periodic resolution of symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> Gastroenteritis usually presents with more frequent vomiting and diarrhea, and the pain is typically more diffuse rather than the intermittent, severe colicky pain characteristic of intussusception.</li>
<li><strong>Pancreatitis:</strong> Pancreatitis is uncommon in this age group and would present with more constant, severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, often with elevated serum amylase and lipase levels.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The classic triad for intussusception is intermittent colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stools (though this is only present in about 50% of cases).</li>
<li>Intussusception is a surgical emergency requiring prompt reduction, typically by air or barium enema.</li>
<li>The peak incidence is between 6-36 months of age, with a male predominance of 3:1.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Intussusception should be suspected in any infant or toddler with intermittent colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and drawing up of the knees.</p>','<p>Pain-Vomit-Pallor (PVP) pattern suggests Intussusception</p>','<p>Intussusception is a surgical emergency; delay in diagnosis can lead to bowel necrosis, perforation, and sepsis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Acute appendicitis:</strong> Presents with constant, worsening pain rather than intermittent colicky pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> Typically has more frequent vomiting and diarrhea without the periodic severe pain.</li>
<li><strong>Pancreatitis:</strong> Uncommon in this age group and presents with constant epigastric pain radiating to back.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["abdominal pain","vomiting","intussusception"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.147Z','2026-10-19T03:43:20.313Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krby002do7lribr931ed','mcq','3- Child with lead poisoning and lead level is more than 70 microgram. What you should do','["Evaluate source and education","IV EDTA and dimercaprol","oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid"]','IV EDTA and dimercaprol','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> For lead levels &gt;70 μg/dL, chelation therapy with IV EDTA and dimercaprol is indicated due to the high risk of severe toxicity and irreversible organ damage. EDTA works by binding lead in the extracellular space, while dimercaprol (BAL) crosses cell membranes to bind intracellular lead. This combination provides comprehensive chelation and is the standard of care for severe lead poisoning according to CDC guidelines.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. Evaluate source and education:</strong> While source evaluation remains important, at levels &gt;70 μg/dL, chelation therapy is mandatory as environmental intervention alone cannot rapidly reduce dangerously high lead levels.</li>
<li><strong>C. oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> While DMSA is an effective chelator, it is typically used for moderate lead poisoning or as follow-up after IV chelation for severe cases, not as initial monotherapy for levels &gt;70 μg/dL.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL) requires urgent chelation therapy with IV EDTA and dimercaprol.</li>
<li>Dimercaprol is given first to redistribute lead from the brain before EDTA administration to prevent worsening neurological symptoms.</li>
<li>Chelation therapy should be administered in a hospital setting with close monitoring for potential side effects including renal impairment and electrolyte disturbances.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL), immediate chelation with IV EDTA and dimercaprol is required, not just source evaluation.</p>','<p>BAL First: Before EDTA, BAL (dimercaprol) must be given first in severe lead poisoning.</p>','<p>Dimercaprol must be administered before EDTA in severe lead poisoning to prevent lead redistribution to the brain, which could worsen neurological symptoms.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. Evaluate source and education:</strong> Inadequate for severe lead poisoning; chelation is mandatory at these levels.</li>
<li><strong>C. oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> Not first-line for severe lead poisoning; typically used for moderate cases or as follow-up.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Lead Poisoning','["lead","poisoning","chelation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.150Z','2026-10-19T03:43:38.899Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbz002fo7lrjpnwtvko','mcq','5- A male patient receives chronic blood transfusion and you noticed he has triphalangeal thumbs. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Blackfan-Diamond anemia","Fanconi anemia","Dyskeratosis congenital","Thalassemia"]','Blackfan-Diamond anemia','<p><strong>Why D is correct:</strong> Blackfan-Diamond anemia (DBA) is characterized by the classic triad of anemia, reticulocytopenia, and congenital malformations, with triphalangeal thumbs being one of the most common skeletal abnormalities. DBA is a rare inherited bone marrow failure disorder caused by mutations in ribosomal protein genes, leading to impaired erythroid progenitor differentiation. The association between DBA and triphalangeal thumbs is well-established, occurring in approximately 15-25% of cases.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. Blackfan-Diamond anemia:</strong> This is actually the correct answer, not a distractor. DBA is characterized by the triphalangeal thumbs association.</li>
<li><strong>B. Fanconi anemia:</strong> Fanconi anemia typically presents with radial ray defects, thumb abnormalities, and skin pigmentation, but triphalangeal thumbs are not a characteristic feature.</li>
<li><strong>C. Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> This condition presents with the classic triad of abnormal skin pigmentation, nail dystrophy, and oral leukoplakia, along with bone marrow failure, but not typically with triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Blackfan-Diamond anemia is the only condition among the options strongly associated with triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
<li>DBA is a ribosomopathy caused by mutations in ribosomal protein genes, leading to selective failure of erythroid progenitor differentiation.</li>
<li>The diagnosis of DBA is confirmed by the presence of macrocytic anemia with reticulocytopenia and elevated fetal hemoglobin in the absence of other causes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Triphalangeal thumbs are a pathognomonic skeletal abnormality in Blackfan-Diamond anemia, a congenital pure red cell aplasia.</p>','<p>DBA: Diamond-Blackfan Anemia - remember the ''Diamond'' in the name for the triphalangeal thumbs association.</p>','<p>Blackfan-Diamond anemia is one of the few congenital anemias where the defect is specific to erythroid precursors, with other cell lines typically unaffected.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B. Fanconi anemia:</strong> Typically presents with radial ray defects but not specifically triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
<li><strong>C. Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> Characterized by skin pigmentation, nail dystrophy, and oral leukoplakia, not triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Hematology','Anemias','["anemia","transfusion","thumbs"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.151Z','2026-10-19T03:43:38.900Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krc0002go7lrc45zmhjs','mcq','6- Which of the following is correct about fanconia anemia','["It autosomal dominant","Risk of cancers","Normal skeleton"]','Risk of cancers','<p><strong>Why Risk of cancers is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is a genetic disorder characterized by genomic instability and defective DNA repair, leading to a markedly increased risk of developing malignancies, particularly acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and solid tumors such as head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, gastrointestinal cancers, and gynecologic cancers. This cancer predisposition is a hallmark feature of the disease and occurs at a much younger age than in the general population. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>It autosomal dominant:</strong> Fanconi anemia is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, not autosomal dominant. - <strong>Normal skeleton:</strong> Fanconi anemia is associated with congenital skeletal abnormalities including radial ray defects, thumb abnormalities, hip dysplasia, and scoliosis in approximately 60% of patients. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Fanconi anemia is caused by mutations in genes involved in the Fanconi anemia DNA repair pathway (FANC genes). - The diagnosis is confirmed by chromosomal breakage testing using diepoxybutane or mitomycin C, which reveals characteristic chromosomal breaks. - Patients with Fanconi anemia are extremely sensitive to DNA-damaging agents including chemotherapy and radiation. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is defined by bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies, and dramatically increased cancer risk due to defective DNA repair.</p>','<p>Fanconi = Failure (bone marrow), Anomalies (congenital), Cancer risk</p>','<p>Before performing a bone marrow biopsy or administering chemotherapy to a child with suspected bone marrow failure, always rule out Fanconi anemia due to extreme sensitivity to DNA-damaging agents.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It autosomal dominant:</strong> Fanconi anemia is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, not autosomal dominant. - <strong>Normal skeleton:</strong> Fanconi anemia is associated with congenital skeletal abnormalities including radial ray defects, thumb abnormalities, hip dysplasia, and scoliosis in approximately 60% of patients.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Fanconi Anemia','["fanconi","anemia","cancer"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.152Z','2026-10-19T03:43:39.678Z');
