INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq30028tzlrhqrn0olc','mcq','Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is an inherited form of bleeding tendency. What are the characteristics of this syndrome?','["Thrombocytopenia and hypermobile joints.","Thrombocytopathy and hypermobile joints.","Thrombocytopenia, infantile eczema, and immunodeficiency.","Thrombocytopenia, skeletal anomalies, and immunodeficiency."]','Thrombocytopenia, infantile eczema, and immunodeficiency.','<p><strong>Why Thrombocytopenia, infantile eczema, and immunodeficiency is correct:</strong> Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the WAS gene, which encodes the Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASP). This protein is crucial for actin cytoskeleton reorganization in hematopoietic cells, leading to defective platelet formation, abnormal T-cell and B-cell function, and impaired immune responses. The classic triad includes thrombocytopenia (due to decreased platelet production and increased destruction), infantile eczema (resulting from immune dysregulation), and recurrent infections (due to combined immunodeficiency with defective antibody responses). <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Thrombocytopenia and hypermobile joints:</strong> Hypermobile joints are characteristic of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, not Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. - <strong>Thrombocytopathy and hypermobile joints:</strong> Thrombocytopathy refers to dysfunctional platelets, not low platelet count, and again, hypermobile joints are not part of this syndrome. - <strong>Thrombocytopenia, skeletal anomalies, and immunodeficiency:</strong> Skeletal anomalies are not a feature of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome; they are more characteristic of other disorders like myelodysplastic syndromes or congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is X-linked, affecting males predominantly with female carriers. - The small platelets (microthrombocytopenia) are a distinctive feature on peripheral smear. - Patients have increased risk of autoimmune disorders and malignancies (especially lymphoma) due to immune dysregulation. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome presents with the classic triad of thrombocytopenia with small platelets, eczema, and recurrent infections due to WAS gene mutation.</p>','<p>Wiskott-Aldrich: TIE (Thrombocytopenia, Infantile eczema, Immunodeficiency)</p>','<p>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome patients have small platelets on peripheral smear, which is a key diagnostic clue distinguishing it from other causes of thrombocytopenia.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia and hypermobile joints:</strong> Incorrect because hypermobile joints are not part of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. - <strong>Thrombocytopathy and hypermobile joints:</strong> Incorrect because thrombocytopathy refers to platelet dysfunction, not low count, and hypermobile joints are unrelated. - <strong>Thrombocytopenia, skeletal anomalies, and immunodeficiency:</strong> Incorrect because skeletal anomalies are not a feature of this syndrome.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome','["wiskott-aldrich","thrombocytopenia","eczema","immunodeficiency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.195Z','2026-10-27T16:07:06.850Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq5002atzlri4boxo3n','mcq','Thrombocytpathy is a form of bleeding diathesis. Which of the following represent an acquired form of thrombocytopathy?','["Infective endocarditis.","Henoch-schonlein purpura.","Chronic renal failure.","Scurvy."]','Scurvy.','<p><strong>Why Scurvy is correct:</strong> Scurvy is an acquired form of thrombocytopathy caused by vitamin C deficiency, which is essential for collagen synthesis and maintenance of endothelial integrity. Vitamin C is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases, enzymes that cross-link collagen and stabilize capillary walls. In scurvy, defective collagen synthesis leads to fragile capillaries, causing perivascular hemorrhages and impaired platelet adhesion to damaged endothelium. This results in a qualitative platelet dysfunction despite normal platelet counts. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Infective endocarditis:</strong> This causes thrombocytopenia (consumption) rather than thrombocytopathy, as platelets are consumed in the formation of vegetations. - <strong>Henoch-Schönlein purpura:</strong> This is a small-vessel vasculitis with immune complex deposition, causing purpura but not primarily a platelet dysfunction disorder. - <strong>Chronic renal failure:</strong> This typically causes uremic bleeding due to multiple factors including platelet dysfunction, but it''s not classified as thrombocytopathy in the same way as scurvy. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Scurvy presents with classic findings like perifollicular hemorrhages, bleeding gums, and poor wound healing. - Vitamin C deficiency can be confirmed by low serum ascorbic acid levels. - Treatment is high-dose vitamin C replacement, which rapidly improves symptoms within days. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Scurvy is an acquired thrombocytopathy caused by vitamin C deficiency leading to defective collagen synthesis and impaired platelet function.</p>','<p>Scurvy Stops Collagen (SSC) - leading to fragile vessels and platelet dysfunction</p>','<p>Scurvy is often overlooked in developed countries but can occur in malnourished individuals, alcoholics, and those with malabsorption syndromes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Infective endocarditis:</strong> Incorrect because it causes thrombocytopenia, not platelet dysfunction. - <strong>Henoch-Schönlein purpura:</strong> Incorrect because it''s a vasculitis, not primarily a platelet dysfunction disorder. - <strong>Chronic renal failure:</strong> Incorrect because while it can cause platelet dysfunction, it''s not classified as thrombocytopathy in the same way as scurvy.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Thrombocytopathy','["thrombocytopathy","acquired","scurvy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.197Z','2026-10-27T16:07:06.853Z');
