INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmutc1rs10008xnlrhf71eybc','mcq','An 18-month-old child under evaluation for abdominal distension, failure to thrive, and constipation starting in infancy with a poor response to medical management. Rectal examination shows an empty rectum with explosive passage of stool. Of the following, what is the MOST appropriate diagnostic tool ?','["Abdominal ultrasound","Plain radiograph of abdomen","Anorectal manometry","Barium enema"]','[Not provided in source]','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Constipation evaluation','["diagnostic","hirshsprung","evaluation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T04:40:37.345Z','2026-10-04T05:29:12.767Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqt002xtzlrmes2rzr9','mcq','A 14-year-old girl presents with short stature and bitemporal visual field defects. CT scan shows calcification at the sella turcica. Which diagnosis is most likely?','["Craniopharyngioma"]','Craniopharyngioma','<p><strong>Why Craniopharyngioma is correct:</strong> Craniopharyngioma is the most likely diagnosis in this 14-year-old girl presenting with short stature and bitemporal visual field defects with calcification at the sella turcica on CT scan. Craniopharyngiomas are benign epithelial tumors arising from Rathke''s pouch remnants, commonly located in the suprasellar region near the pituitary stalk. They often present with growth hormone deficiency leading to short stature, visual field defects (typically bitemporal hemianopia due to compression of the optic chiasm), and calcification on imaging. The sellar/suprasellar location with calcification is highly characteristic of craniopharyngioma, which accounts for 5-10% of all intracranial tumors in children.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Pituitary adenoma:</strong> While pituitary adenomas can cause visual field defects, they rarely calcify and typically present with hormonal excess rather than deficiency in adolescents. - <strong>Hypothalamic hamartoma:</strong> This would present with precocious puberty or gelastic seizures, not short stature and bitemporal visual field defects with calcification. - <strong>Pituitary apoplexy:</strong> This is an acute hemorrhagic event in a pre-existing pituitary adenoma, presenting with sudden headache, visual loss, and meningismus, not the chronic presentation described.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Craniopharyngiomas are the most common suprasellar tumors in children, with a bimodal peak incidence (5-14 years and 50-74 years). - The classic triad of symptoms includes visual disturbances (bitemporal hemianopia), endocrine dysfunction (growth hormone deficiency leading to short stature), and increased intracranial pressure. - Calcification is present in 70-90% of cases on CT scan, making it a key diagnostic feature.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Craniopharyngioma should be suspected in any child with short stature, visual field defects, and calcification at the sella turcica on imaging.</p>','','<p>Craniopharyngiomas, though histologically benign, can cause significant morbidity due to their location near critical structures like the optic chiasm and pituitary stalk, requiring careful surgical planning.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pituitary adenoma:</strong> Rarely calcifies and typically presents with hormonal excess rather than deficiency. - <strong>Hypothalamic hamartoma:</strong> Presents with precocious puberty or seizures, not short stature and visual field defects. - <strong>Pituitary apoplexy:</strong> An acute hemorrhagic event with sudden headache and visual loss, not a chronic presentation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrinology','Pituitary Tumors','["craniopharyngioma","visual field","sella turcica"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.221Z','2026-10-27T16:07:57.457Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyn84j1001ksomavswp9gva','mcq','An 8-year-old girl is evaluated for short stature. Her height is 110 cm (<3rd percentile), weight is 20 kg (10th percentile), and her bone age is 6 years (chronological age 8 years). She has normal growth velocity of 5 cm/year. Her physical examination shows no dysmorphic features. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Growth hormone deficiency","Turner syndrome","Constitutional growth delay","Hypothyroidism","Celiac disease"]','Constitutional growth delay','The key findings are: 1) Significant short stature (<3rd percentile), 2) Normal growth velocity (5 cm/year), 3) Delayed bone age (6 years vs chronological age 8 years), and 4) No dysmorphic features. Constitutional growth delay is characterized by normal growth velocity but delayed skeletal maturation, leading to a "catch-up" growth pattern during adolescence. Growth hormone deficiency typically shows decreased growth velocity. Turner syndrome usually has specific dysmorphic features and other characteristic findings. Hypothyroidism typically presents with decreased growth velocity. Celiac disease may present with short stature but often has gastrointestinal symptoms and other nutritional deficiencies.','"Delayed bone age, normal velocity = constitutional growth delay"','Constitutional growth delay is the most common cause of short stature, affecting approximately 1% of children, and is more common in boys.','Growth hormone deficiency: Typically shows decreased growth velocity, not normal velocity. Turner syndrome: Usually has dysmorphic features, webbed neck, and other characteristic findings. Hypothyroidism: Presents with decreased growth velocity and other symptoms like fatigue, cold intolerance. Celiac disease: May cause short stature but often has GI symptoms and nutritional deficiencies.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature diagnosis','["short stature diagnosis","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth","pediatrics","constitutional"]',true,'X-ray of child''s hand showing delayed bone age compared to chronological age','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-aba0939e-117.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:52:20.461Z','2026-10-07T21:52:20.461Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyw8kbz0021soma40y4645e','mcq','A 10-year-old boy with short stature undergoes evaluation for possible growth hormone deficiency. His height is 3rd percentile, growth velocity is 4 cm/year, and bone age is 8 years. Initial screening shows normal thyroid function and IGF-1 level of 150 ng/mL (normal for age 250-800 ng/mL). Which of the following stimulation tests would be most appropriate as the next step in diagnostic evaluation?','["Oral glucose tolerance test","Clonidine stimulation test","Insulin tolerance test","Arginine stimulation test","Levothyroxine stimulation test"]','Clonidine stimulation test','This patient has clinical features suggestive of growth hormone deficiency (short stature, decreased growth velocity, delayed bone age, low IGF-1). Among the stimulation tests, clonidine is often preferred as a first-line test in children because it has fewer side effects compared to insulin tolerance test (which can cause hypoglycemia) and is more readily available than arginine. Clonidine works by stimulating growth hormone release through central alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. The insulin tolerance test is considered the gold standard but carries risks of severe hypoglycemia and requires close monitoring. Arginine is also commonly used but may be less available.','GHD testing: Clonidine first (Child-friendly), Insulin gold standard, Arginine alternative','Growth hormone stimulation testing should be performed when clinical suspicion exists despite normal screening tests. Two different stimulation tests are typically required for diagnosis, and peak growth hormone levels <10 ng/mL are diagnostic of deficiency.','Oral glucose tolerance test is incorrect as it suppresses rather than stimulates growth hormone release. Insulin tolerance test is the gold standard but carries higher risks and is not typically first-line. Levothyroxine stimulation test is not a standard test for growth hormone deficiency. Arginine stimulation test is appropriate but clonidine is often preferred first in children due to better safety profile.','Medium','Endocrine','growth hormone deficiency diagnosis','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","growth hormone deficiency diagnosis","growth hormone stimulation test","clonidine","short stature evaluation"]',true,'Growth hormone stimulation test protocol showing clonidine administration with growth hormone response curve, including normal and abnormal response patterns for diagnosing growth hormone deficiency','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-c02d6bdd-81e.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-08T02:04:37.487Z','2026-10-08T02:04:37.487Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyhmox60002koma6nygxu2x','mcq','A 28-year-old patient with type 1 diabetes presents with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Initial laboratory findings show: pH 7.15, bicarbonate 8 mEq/L, glucose 450 mg/dL, and anion gap 22. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management step?','["Administer 0.45% saline at 500 mL/hour","Start insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour","Administer sodium bicarbonate to correct metabolic acidosis","Initiate potassium replacement immediately","Provide dextrose 10% in water to prevent hypoglycemia"]','Start insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour','The initial management of DKA focuses on three key components: fluid resuscitation, insulin therapy, and potassium replacement. Insulin infusion should be started immediately at 0.1 units/kg/hour to halt ketone production and correct hyperglycemia. While fluid resuscitation is important, insulin takes priority as it addresses the underlying metabolic derangement. Sodium bicarbonate is generally not recommended unless pH is <6.9 or severe hyperkalemia is present. Potassium replacement should be initiated only after serum potassium levels are confirmed, as insulin therapy can cause hypokalemia. Dextrose is not needed initially as glucose levels are already elevated.','FLUID - First give fluids, then insulin, then monitor electrolytes','Insulin therapy should begin immediately in DKA, even before potassium levels are known, as the risk of hyperkalemia is lower than the risk of uncontrolled ketosis.','{"Administer 0.45% saline at 500 mL/hour": "While fluid resuscitation is important, insulin therapy should be started simultaneously as it addresses the core metabolic problem.", "Administer sodium bicarbonate to correct metabolic acidosis": "Sodium bicarbonate is generally not recommended in DKA unless pH is <6.9 or severe hyperkalemia is present, as it can cause paradoxical CNS acidosis and hypokalemia.", "Initiate potassium replacement immediately": "Potassium replacement should be guided by serum potassium levels; insulin therapy can cause rapid shifts in potassium levels.", "Provide dextrose 10% in water to prevent hypoglycemia": "Dextrose is not needed initially as glucose levels are already elevated; it may be added later if glucose levels fall below 200 mg/dL."}','Medium','Endocrine','Diabetic Ketoacidosis Management','["diabetic ketoacidosis management","Endocrine","deep-agent","Diabetic Ketoacidosis Management","DKA","type 1 diabetes","insulin therapy","fluid resuscitation"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-07T19:15:42.378Z','2026-10-07T19:17:01.557Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-obgyn-1','mcq','A 32-year-old at 36 weeks gestation has BP 160/105, proteinuria 3+, headache, and visual disturbances. What is the most appropriate management?','["Outpatient BP monitoring","Hospital admission, magnesium sulfate, deliver","Oral labetalol and follow-up in 1 week","Bedrest at home","Induction at 39 weeks"]','Hospital admission, magnesium sulfate, deliver','Severe pre-eclampsia with features ≥34 weeks → deliver. Magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis. Antihypertensives (labetalol, hydralazine, nifedipine) for BP control.','Pre-eclampsia: BP ≥140/90 + proteinuria after 20w. Severe features: ≥160/110, symptoms, labs.','Magnesium sulfate: monitor for toxicity — loss of reflexes, respiratory depression. Antidote = calcium gluconate.','Outpatient management is inappropriate with severe features. Delaying delivery risks eclampsia, stroke, HELLP.','Moderate','Reproductive','Pre-eclampsia','["preeclampsia","ob","emergency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.727Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.489Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6d004ae9oiy8w1awdw','mcq','A 5-month-old infant presented with persistent cyanosis for 1 month duration, he had ejection systolic murmur, what is most likely diagnosis?','["VSD","Fallot tetralogy","Isolated PS","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome"]','Fallot tetralogy','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","infant","murmur","tetralogy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.653Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.653Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyigev20008komapthz6o8t','mcq','Aspirin''s primary mechanism of action as a cyclooxygenase (COX) inhibitor involves which of the following molecular modifications?','["Reversible inhibition of COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes through competitive binding at the active site","Irreversible acetylation of a specific serine residue (Ser530 in COX-1, Ser516 in COX-2) in the cyclooxygenase active site","Non-competitive inhibition of COX enzymes by binding to the allosteric site","Selective inhibition of COX-2 over COX-1 through preferential binding affinity","Inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis at the level of phospholipase A2"]','Irreversible acetylation of a specific serine residue (Ser530 in COX-1, Ser516 in COX-2) in the cyclooxygenase active site','Aspirin''s unique mechanism involves irreversible acetylation of a serine residue (Ser530 in COX-1, Ser516 in COX-2) within the cyclooxygenase active site. This covalent modification permanently inactivates the enzyme, preventing it from converting arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2 (PGH2). Unlike other NSAIDs that reversibly inhibit COX enzymes, aspirin''s effect lasts for the entire lifespan of the platelet (7-10 days) due to this irreversible binding. This mechanism explains aspirin''s prolonged antiplatelet effects and its utility in cardiovascular disease prevention.','ASPIRIN = Acetylation Serine Permanent Inhibition Reversible Irreversible Never','Aspirin''s irreversible COX inhibition explains why its antiplatelet effects last the entire lifespan of platelets (7-10 days), while its anti-inflammatory effects are shorter-lived due to new enzyme synthesis in other cell types.','Option 1 is incorrect because aspirin causes irreversible, not reversible, inhibition. Option 3 is incorrect because aspirin binds to the active site, not an allosteric site. Option 4 is incorrect because aspirin inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 non-selectively, unlike COX-2 selective inhibitors. Option 5 is incorrect because aspirin acts downstream of phospholipase A2 at the COX enzyme level.','Medium','Pharmacology','Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)','["aspirin mechanism of action","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)","COX inhibition","aspirin","pharmacology","irreversible inhibition"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-07T19:38:49.022Z','2026-10-08T05:03:09.914Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h27000nqjlrtmwvhf74','mcq','A mother has 4-month-old child who is exclusively breastfeeding and he seems hungry. The mother wanted to introduce complementary foods. Your advice is','["Start complementary foods immediately","Start by fresh juices","Continue breastfeeding till 6 months","Add rice water with breastfeeding"]','Continue breastfeeding till 6 months','<p><strong>Why Continue breastfeeding till 6 months is correct:</strong> The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life, as breast milk provides all the necessary nutrients and hydration for optimal growth and development during this period. At 4 months, the infant''s digestive system and kidneys are still developing, and introducing complementary foods too early can increase the risk of allergies, infections, and obesity. The mother''s perception of the infant being ''hungry'' at 4 months is often normal behavior rather than a true need for complementary foods, as infants go through growth spurts that may increase feeding frequency temporarily.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start complementary foods immediately:</strong> Introducing solids before 6 months can lead to nutritional deficiencies, increased risk of infections, and potential allergic reactions, as the infant''s system is not yet ready.</li>
<li><strong>Start by fresh juices:</strong> Juice is not appropriate for infants under 6 months as it lacks necessary nutrients, can cause diarrhea, and may lead to early preference for sweet tastes, potentially causing later feeding difficulties.</li>
<li><strong>Add rice water with breastfeeding:</strong> Rice water is nutritionally empty and would displace more nutrient-rich breast milk, potentially leading to malnutrition in the infant.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months provides optimal nutrition and protection against infections.</li>
<li>Complementary foods should only be introduced after 6 months when the infant shows developmental readiness.</li>
<li>Early introduction of solids is associated with increased risk of allergies and obesity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Exclusive breastfeeding should continue until 6 months of age, as breast milk provides complete nutrition and protection during this critical developmental period.</p>','<p>Six months before solids, breast milk is complete (6B)</p>','<p>A 4-month-old infant''s hunger cues during growth spurts don''t indicate readiness for solids; exclusive breastfeeding should continue until 6 months.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Start complementary foods immediately:</strong> Early introduction can cause nutritional deficiencies, infections, and allergies.</li>
<li><strong>Start by fresh juices:</strong> Juice is inappropriate for infants under 6 months as it lacks nutrients and can cause diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Add rice water with breastfeeding:</strong> Rice water is nutritionally empty and displaces more nutrient-rich breast milk.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["complementary foods","weaning","6 months"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.423Z','2026-10-18T19:29:45.149Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyil53v000bkoma8edmvgid','mcq','Which of the following best describes the primary characteristic of rapid-acting insulin?','["Onset of action within 30-60 minutes, peak at 2-4 hours, duration of 12-16 hours","Onset of action within 10-30 minutes, peak at 1-3 hours, duration of 3-5 hours","Onset of action within 1-2 hours, peak at 4-6 hours, duration of 12-18 hours","Onset of action within 2-4 hours, peak at 6-8 hours, duration of 18-24 hours"]','Onset of action within 10-30 minutes, peak at 1-3 hours, duration of 3-5 hours','Rapid-acting insulin (e.g., lispro, aspart, glulisine) has the fastest onset of action (10-30 minutes), peaks within 1-3 hours, and has a relatively short duration of action (3-5 hours). This makes it ideal for mealtime coverage and allows for more flexible dosing compared to other insulin types.','RAPID: Rapid onset, Action peaks quickly, Post-meal coverage, Intermediate duration, Dinner time coverage','Rapid-acting insulin can be given immediately before meals or even after starting to eat, which provides greater flexibility for patients with unpredictable meal timing compared to regular insulin.','Option 1 describes intermediate-acting insulin (NPH). Option 3 describes long-acting insulin (glargine, detemir). Option 4 describes ultra-long-acting insulin (degludec). Each distractor represents a different insulin classification with distinct pharmacokinetic profiles.','Easy','Endocrine','Insulin Classification and Characteristics','["insulin classification","Endocrine","deep-agent","Insulin Classification and Characteristics","diabetes","insulin","pharmacology","endocrine"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T19:42:29.659Z','2026-10-07T19:42:29.659Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyj40ux000mkomagefrj70c','mcq','A 5-year-old boy presents with height below the 3rd percentile for age. His growth velocity is normal, and his bone age is appropriate for his chronological age. On examination, he has no dysmorphic features and his parents are both of short stature. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Constitutional growth delay","Growth hormone deficiency","Turner syndrome","Achondroplasia","Hypothyroidism"]','Constitutional growth delay','Constitutional growth delay is the most likely diagnosis given the family history of short stature, normal growth velocity, appropriate bone age, and absence of dysmorphic features. This is a common cause of short stature in children and represents a normal variant of growth. Growth hormone deficiency would typically show decreased growth velocity, Turner syndrome would have characteristic features like webbed neck and short fourth metacarpals, achondroplasia would have disproportionate short stature with rhizomelic shortening, and hypothyroidism would show delayed bone age.','"Family short, growth normal, bone age on track - constitutional growth delay"','Constitutional growth delay is a diagnosis of exclusion and typically resolves with normal adult height.','Growth hormone deficiency: Would show decreased growth velocity and delayed bone age. Turner syndrome: Would have characteristic dysmorphic features and short stature in females. Achondroplasia: Would show disproportionate shortening with rhizomelia. Hypothyroidism: Would show delayed bone age and other systemic symptoms.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature','["short stature","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth","pediatrics","endocrine"]',true,'Growth chart showing child with normal growth velocity but short stature, appropriate bone age compared to chronological age','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T19:57:10.617Z','2026-10-07T19:57:10.617Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyj44f4000nkoma3j4kslvv','mcq','An 8-year-old girl presents with short stature, webbed neck, and widely spaced nipples. Her height is below the 5th percentile for age. On physical examination, she has a high-arched palate and shield chest. Which of the following findings would be most characteristic on her hand X-ray?','["Shortened fourth metacarpals","Delayed bone age","Increased bone density","Epiphyseal dysplasia","Osteopenia"]','Shortened fourth metacarpals','This presentation is classic for Turner syndrome (45,X), which is characterized by short stature, webbed neck, shield chest, and other dysmorphic features. A key radiographic finding in Turner syndrome is shortened fourth metacarpals, which occurs in approximately 50% of cases. Delayed bone age can occur but is not specific, increased bone density is not typical, epiphyseal dysplasia is not a feature, and osteopenia is not characteristic.','"Turner''s triad: webbed neck, shield chest, short 4th metacarpal"','Turner syndrome should be considered in any girl with unexplained short stature, especially with dysmorphic features.','Delayed bone age: Can occur but is not specific to Turner syndrome. Increased bone density: Not a feature of Turner syndrome. Epiphyseal dysplasia: Not associated with Turner syndrome. Osteopenia: Not a characteristic finding.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature','["short stature","Endocrine","chat-agent","turner syndrome","genetic","dysmorphic"]',true,'Hand X-ray showing shortened fourth metacarpal characteristic of Turner syndrome','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T19:57:15.232Z','2026-10-07T19:57:15.232Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuymv7ed0018soma688ntx8f','mcq','A 9-year-old girl presents with a height of 120 cm (3rd percentile) and a growth velocity of 4 cm/year over the past year. Her bone age is delayed by 2 years. She has no dysmorphic features and her physical examination is normal. Her thyroid function tests are normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her evaluation?','["Immediate growth hormone therapy initiation","IGF-1 level measurement and stimulation testing","Karyotype analysis for Turner syndrome","Nutritional assessment and dietary counseling","Observation with repeat height measurement in 6 months"]','IGF-1 level measurement and stimulation testing','This patient has short stature with delayed bone age and decreased growth velocity. The absence of dysmorphic features makes Turner syndrome less likely. The appropriate next step is to evaluate for growth hormone deficiency by measuring IGF-1 levels and performing stimulation testing. Growth hormone therapy should not be initiated without proper diagnostic evaluation. Nutritional assessment is important but should not delay the endocrine evaluation.','','Short stature with delayed bone age and decreased growth velocity warrants evaluation for growth hormone deficiency, even in the absence of dysmorphic features.','Option 1 (immediate GH therapy) is incorrect as GH therapy requires proper diagnosis. Option 3 (karyotype) is less likely without dysmorphic features. Option 4 (nutritional) is important but secondary to endocrine evaluation. Option 5 (observation) is inappropriate given the decreased growth velocity.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature evaluation','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","short stature evaluation","growth hormone deficiency","short stature","pediatric endocrinology"]',true,'Pediatric growth chart showing a 9-year-old girl''s height at 3rd percentile with delayed bone age assessment, illustrating the evaluation process for short stature with growth velocity measurement','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-94b0dd79-338.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:42:17.654Z','2026-10-07T21:42:17.654Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuynazsy001nsoman7bqr8gk','mcq','A 45-year-old male presents to his primary care physician complaining of progressive enlargement of his hands and feet over the past 2 years. He also reports that his wedding ring no longer fits and he has had to buy larger shoes multiple times. On examination, he has coarse facial features with a prominent brow ridge, enlarged nose, and thickened lips. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Cushing''s syndrome","Acromegaly","Marfan syndrome","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","Pituitary adenoma"]','Acromegaly','Acromegaly is caused by excessive growth hormone secretion, typically from a pituitary adenoma. The clinical presentation includes progressive enlargement of hands and feet (acral enlargement), coarse facial features (enlarged nose, thickened lips, prominent brow ridge), and soft tissue swelling. The history of wedding rings and shoes no longer fitting is classic for this condition. Cushing''s syndrome presents with central obesity, moon face, and other features. Marfan syndrome involves tall stature, long limbs, and joint hypermobility but not progressive acral enlargement. Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 can include pituitary tumors but typically presents with other endocrine abnormalities. While pituitary adenoma is the underlying cause, acromegaly is the specific syndrome resulting from excessive GH secretion.','ACRO = Acral enlargement, Coarse features, Ring/size changes, Overgrowth','The progressive enlargement of hands and feet is a hallmark feature of acromegaly and should prompt evaluation for growth hormone excess.','Cushing''s syndrome is a common distractor as it also involves pituitary pathology but presents with different features like central obesity and moon face. Marfan syndrome involves tall stature but not progressive acral enlargement. Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 can include pituitary tumors but typically has other endocrine manifestations. Pituitary adenoma is the underlying cause but not the specific syndrome diagnosis.','Easy','Endocrine','acromegaly','["acromegaly","Endocrine","deep-agent","acromegaly","growth hormone","pituitary","acral enlargement","facial features"]',true,'Clinical illustration showing a patient with acromegaly demonstrating characteristic facial features including enlarged hands and feet, prominent brow ridge, enlarged nose and ears, and coarse facial features','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-05b6a4f0-819.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:54:34.306Z','2026-10-07T21:54:34.306Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyj489t000okomal5oh5xoz','mcq','A 3-year-old child presents with disproportionate short stature, macrocephaly, and midfacial hypoplasia. On examination, the child has a prominent forehead, depressed nasal bridge, and trident hand configuration. Which of the following genetic mutations is most likely responsible for this condition?','["FGFR3 gene mutation","SHOX gene mutation","GHRH receptor mutation","IGF-1 receptor mutation","GH gene mutation"]','FGFR3 gene mutation','This presentation is classic for achondroplasia, the most common form of dwarfism. Achondroplasia is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the FGFR3 gene, which leads to impaired endochondral bone formation. The characteristic features include disproportionate short stature (rhizomelic shortening), macrocephaly, midfacial hypoplasia, prominent forehead, depressed nasal bridge, and trident hand configuration. SHOX gene mutations cause Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis, GHRH receptor mutations cause isolated growth hormone deficiency, IGF-1 receptor mutations cause severe growth retardation, and GH gene mutations also cause growth hormone deficiency.','"FGFR3 mutation = achondroplasia: rhizomelic shortening, macrocephaly, trident hand"','Achondroplasia accounts for approximately 70% of all dwarfism cases and is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern.','SHOX gene mutation: Causes Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis with mesomelic shortening. GHRH receptor mutation: Causes isolated growth hormone deficiency with proportionate short stature. IGF-1 receptor mutation: Causes severe growth retardation but not the characteristic facial features. GH gene mutation: Causes growth hormone deficiency with proportionate short stature.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature','["short stature","Endocrine","chat-agent","achondroplasia","genetic","skeletal dysplasia"]',true,'Clinical photograph showing achondroplasia with rhizomelic shortening, macrocephaly, and midfacial hypoplasia','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T19:57:20.225Z','2026-10-07T19:57:20.225Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuylvlef000ysomalpn2w5sn','mcq','A 35-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight gain. Her thyroid function tests show: TSH 2.5 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.5 mIU/L), Free T4 1.2 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8 ng/dL), and Free T3 3.2 pg/mL (normal 2.3-4.2 pg/mL). What is the most likely interpretation of these results?','["Primary hypothyroidism","Subclinical hypothyroidism","Euthyroid state","Primary hyperthyroidism","Central hypothyroidism"]','C','The patient''s TSH, Free T4, and Free T3 levels are all within normal ranges, indicating a euthyroid state. In primary hypothyroidism, TSH would be elevated with low Free T4. In subclinical hypothyroidism, TSH would be elevated with normal Free T4. In primary hyperthyroidism, TSH would be suppressed with elevated Free T4. In central hypothyroidism, TSH would be inappropriately normal or low with low Free T4.','Normal TSH + Normal T4 = Euthyroid','When all thyroid function tests are within normal ranges, the patient is euthyroid. Always interpret TSH in the context of Free T4 levels.','A) Primary hypothyroidism would show elevated TSH with low Free T4. B) Subclinical hypothyroidism would show elevated TSH with normal Free T4. D) Primary hyperthyroidism would show suppressed TSH with elevated Free T4. E) Central hypothyroidism would show inappropriately normal/low TSH with low Free T4.','Medium','Endocrine','Thyroid Function Tests','["thyroid function tests","Endocrine","deep-agent","Thyroid Function Tests","thyroid","TSH","Free T4","euthyroid"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:14:36.183Z','2026-10-07T21:14:36.183Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hc20036qjlrqnlyxh0s','mcq','Cow''s milk protein intolerance is characterized by:','["abdominal tenderness","streaks of bloody mucus in stools& abdominal distention","vomiting","peripheral eosinophilia"]','streaks of bloody mucus in stools& abdominal distention','<p><strong>Why Streaks of bloody mucus in stools &amp; abdominal distention is correct:</strong> Cow''s milk protein intolerance (CMPI) is an immune-mediated reaction to cow''s milk proteins, primarily affecting infants. The classic presentation includes gastrointestinal symptoms such as blood-streaked mucus in stools (due to colonic mucosal inflammation and damage), abdominal distention (from gas production and inflammation), and sometimes vomiting. Unlike lactose intolerance (which is due to lactase deficiency), CMPI involves an allergic response that can damage the intestinal mucosa, leading to microscopic or macroscopic bleeding. The abdominal distention results from inflammation, increased gas production from bacterial fermentation of undigested nutrients, and possible protein-losing enteropathy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Abdominal tenderness:</strong> While abdominal discomfort may occur in CMPI, tenderness is not a specific or characteristic finding and can be present in many other gastrointestinal conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Vomiting:</strong> Vomiting can occur in CMPI but is not specific to this condition and is seen in many other infant disorders like gastroenteritis or pyloric stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Peripheral eosinophilia:</strong> While eosinophilia may be present in some allergic conditions, it is not consistently found in CMPI and is more characteristic of parasitic infections or eosinophilic gastroenteritis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>CMPI presents with blood-streaked mucus stools and abdominal distension due to mucosal inflammation and damage.</li>
<li>Unlike lactose intolerance, CMPI involves an immune response to milk proteins, not enzyme deficiency.</li>
<li>Diagnosis is often made by elimination of cow''s milk protein from diet and observing symptom resolution.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cow''s milk protein intolerance is characterized by blood-streaked mucus in stools and abdominal distension due to immune-mediated damage to the intestinal mucosa.</p>','<p>CMPI: Colonic Mucosal damage with Protein intolerance causing Inflammation</p>','<p>Differentiate cow''s milk protein intolerance from lactose intolerance by the presence of blood in stools and the immune-mediated nature of the reaction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abdominal tenderness:</strong> Not specific to CMPI and occurs in many other gastrointestinal conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Vomiting:</strong> Can occur in CMPI but is not specific and is seen in many other infant disorders.</li>
<li><strong>Peripheral eosinophilia:</strong> Not consistently found in CMPI and more characteristic of parasitic infections or eosinophilic gastroenteritis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Cow''s Milk Protein Intolerance','["milk","intolerance","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.778Z','2026-10-18T19:40:39.112Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuylzqwx0013soma5bxisjyb','mcq','A 12-year-old boy presents to the pediatric clinic with his parents concerned about his rapid growth over the past 2 years. On examination, he is 185 cm tall (above the 99th percentile for age), has disproportionately long limbs, large hands and feet (shoe size 12 US), and a prominent forehead. His growth velocity has increased from 6 cm/year to 12 cm/year over the past year. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this clinical presentation?','["Growth hormone deficiency","Thyroid hormone excess","Pituitary adenoma secreting growth hormone","Adrenal cortical hyperplasia"]','Pituitary adenoma secreting growth hormone','This clinical presentation is classic for gigantism, which is caused by excessive growth hormone (GH) secretion before epiphyseal closure. The key findings include rapid growth velocity, disproportionate limb elongation, large hands and feet (acromegalic features), and increased height above the 99th percentile. Gigantism is most commonly caused by a pituitary adenoma that secretes GH. Growth hormone deficiency would cause short stature, not excessive growth. Thyroid hormone excess can cause accelerated growth but typically doesn''t cause the disproportionate limb enlargement seen here. Adrenal cortical hyperplasia usually causes precocious puberty with growth acceleration but not the characteristic gigantism features.','RAPID - Rapid growth, Accelerated velocity, Proportional limb elongation, Increased height, Disproportionate features','Gigantism must be distinguished from familial tall stature by the rapid growth velocity and disproportionate limb enlargement. The presence of acromegalic features (large hands/feet) is a key differentiator.','Option 1 (Growth hormone deficiency) is incorrect because it causes short stature, not excessive growth. Option 2 (Thyroid hormone excess) can cause accelerated growth but typically doesn''t cause the disproportionate limb enlargement characteristic of gigantism. Option 4 (Adrenal cortical hyperplasia) usually causes precocious puberty with growth acceleration but not the classic gigantism features seen here.','Medium','endocrine','gigantism','["gigantism","Endocrine","deep-agent","gigantism","growth hormone","pituitary adenoma","pediatrics","endocrine disorders"]',true,'A clinical illustration showing a young adolescent patient with gigantism, demonstrating disproportionate growth with elongated limbs and enlarged hands/feet, alongside a comparison of normal growth chart versus gigantism growth pattern, and anatomical highlighting of pituitary gland with a small pituitary adenoma','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-c4eaea3a-10a.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:17:49.953Z','2026-10-07T21:17:49.953Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuylvoe4000zsomag4n3o08h','mcq','A 35-year-old woman presents with fatigue, weight gain, and cold intolerance. Laboratory results show elevated TSH and low free T4 levels. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Primary hypothyroidism","Secondary hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Euthyroid sick syndrome","Thyroiditis"]','Primary hypothyroidism','The patient''s symptoms (fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance) along with elevated TSH and low free T4 levels are classic for primary hypothyroidism. In primary hypothyroidism, the thyroid gland itself is underactive, leading to decreased T4 production. The pituitary gland responds by increasing TSH secretion in an attempt to stimulate the thyroid. Secondary hypothyroidism would show low TSH and low T4, while hyperthyroidism would show low TSH and high T4.','High TSH + Low T4 = Primary hypothyroidism','TSH is the most sensitive screening test for thyroid dysfunction; elevated TSH with low T4 confirms primary hypothyroidism','"Secondary hypothyroidism" is incorrect because it would show low TSH, not elevated. "Hyperthyroidism" is incorrect because it would show low TSH and high T4. "Euthyroid sick syndrome" typically shows abnormal TSH but normal T4. "Thyroiditis" can cause transient thyroid dysfunction but the pattern here is more consistent with chronic primary hypothyroidism.','Easy','Endocrine','thyroid function tests','["thyroid function tests","Endocrine","chat-agent","TSH","T4","hypothyroidism","diagnosis"]',true,'Thyroid feedback loop diagram showing TSH stimulation of thyroid gland and T4 production','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-07T21:14:40.060Z','2026-10-07T21:25:35.693Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('seed-q-neuro-3','mcq','A 65-year-old man has resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, and postural instability. What is the most appropriate initial symptomatic therapy?','["Levodopa/carbidopa","Benztropine","Haloperidol","Deep brain stimulation","Amantadine"]','Levodopa/carbidopa','Parkinson disease: cardinal features (TRAP — Tremor, Rigidity, Akinesia, Postural instability). Levodopa/carbidopa is the most effective symptomatic therapy.','TRAP = Tremor (resting), Rigidity (cogwheel), Akinesia/bradykinesia, Postural instability.','Levodopa side effects: nausea, orthostatic hypotension, hallucinations, dyskinesias with long-term use.','Benztropine for tremor in young patients (anticholinergic side effects limit use in elderly). DBS for refractory motor fluctuations.','Moderate','Nervous','PD','["parkinson","movement","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T13:08:31.747Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.485Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuymvbna0019somazcx260nd','mcq','A 12-year-old boy with a confirmed diagnosis of growth hormone deficiency has been on growth hormone therapy for 6 months. His height velocity has increased from 3 cm/year to 6 cm/year. However, his IGF-1 levels remain persistently low despite adequate dosing. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this finding?','["Growth hormone resistance","Poor adherence to therapy","Underdosing of growth hormone","Concurrent hypothyroidism","Growth hormone antibody formation"]','Growth hormone resistance','Growth hormone resistance is the most likely explanation for persistent low IGF-1 levels despite clinical response to GH therapy. This can occur due to defects in the GH receptor or downstream signaling pathways. Poor adherence and underdosing are less likely given the clinical response. Hypothyroidism would typically cause decreased growth velocity, not just low IGF-1. GH antibody formation is rare with recombinant GH therapy.','','Clinical response to GH therapy (improved growth velocity) can occur despite persistently low IGF-1 levels, suggesting growth hormone resistance rather than treatment failure.','Option 2 (poor adherence) is unlikely given the clinical response. Option 3 (underdosing) would typically affect both growth velocity and IGF-1. Option 4 (hypothyroidism) would cause decreased growth velocity. Option 5 (antibodies) is rare with modern recombinant GH.','Medium','Endocrine','growth hormone therapy monitoring','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","growth hormone therapy monitoring","growth hormone deficiency","IGF-1 monitoring","growth hormone resistance"]',true,'Growth hormone therapy monitoring graph showing IGF-1 levels and height velocity response over time in a pediatric patient with growth hormone deficiency','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-eb0b16e4-6e6.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:42:23.158Z','2026-10-07T21:42:23.158Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuymvg43001asomaj4tjs923','mcq','A 14-year-old girl with Turner syndrome (45,X) has a height of 135 cm (below 3rd percentile). She has been on growth hormone therapy for 2 years with a good response. Her current height velocity is 6 cm/year. Which of the following additional therapies should be considered at this time?','["Androgen therapy to accelerate puberty","Estrogen therapy to initiate puberty","Oxandrolone to enhance growth response","Insulin-like growth factor-1 therapy","No additional therapy needed at this time"]','Estrogen therapy to initiate puberty','In Turner syndrome patients on growth hormone therapy, estrogen therapy should be initiated around age 12-14 to induce puberty. This is important for both psychological development and to achieve optimal adult height. Estrogen therapy should be started at low doses and gradually increased to allow for adequate growth during early puberty. Androgen therapy is not typically used in Turner syndrome. Oxandrolone may be used in some cases but is not first-line. IGF-1 therapy is not standard for Turner syndrome.','','In Turner syndrome, estrogen therapy should be initiated around age 12-14 in patients on GH therapy to balance the benefits of continued growth with the need for normal pubertal development.','Option 1 (androgen) is incorrect as androgens are not typically used in Turner syndrome. Option 3 (oxandrolone) may be used but is not first-line. Option 4 (IGF-1) is not standard for Turner syndrome. Option 5 (no therapy) is incorrect as estrogen therapy is needed for pubertal development.','Medium','Endocrine','Turner syndrome management','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","Turner syndrome management","Turner syndrome","growth hormone therapy","pubertal development"]',true,'Turner syndrome management timeline showing growth hormone therapy and estrogen therapy initiation for pubertal development in a 14-year-old girl with Turner syndrome','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-5b9f0c5e-546.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:42:28.947Z','2026-10-07T21:42:28.947Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuymwsqb001bsoma47fh3vh3','mcq','A 6-year-old boy presents for evaluation of short stature. His height has been consistently below the 3rd percentile on growth charts since early childhood. His growth velocity is normal, and he has no dysmorphic features. His bone age is appropriate for his chronological age. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Constitutional growth delay","Growth hormone deficiency","Turner syndrome","Hypothyroidism","Malnutrition"]','Constitutional growth delay','Constitutional growth delay is the most likely diagnosis in this case. The key findings are: 1) Consistent short stature below the 3rd percentile since early childhood, 2) Normal growth velocity (ruling out growth hormone deficiency), 3) No dysmorphic features (ruling out Turner syndrome), and 4) Appropriate bone age for chronological age (ruling out hypothyroidism and other endocrine disorders). Constitutional growth delay is a common, benign condition where children have a delayed but otherwise normal growth pattern, often with a family history of short stature.','"CANH" - Constitutional, Appropriate bone age, Normal growth velocity, No dysmorphic features','Constitutional growth delay is a diagnosis of exclusion and the most common cause of short stature in children.','Growth hormone deficiency would typically show decreased growth velocity; Turner syndrome would have dysmorphic features and delayed bone age; hypothyroidism would have delayed bone age and other systemic symptoms; malnutrition would typically show weight issues as well.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature','["short stature","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth","pediatrics","endocrine"]',true,'Growth chart showing height percentiles with a child''s growth curve below the 3rd percentile','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-e1955ec9-25b.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:43:31.955Z','2026-10-07T21:43:31.955Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuymxz2u001csoma007h6g7k','mcq','A 9-year-old girl presents with short stature. On examination, she has a webbed neck, low posterior hairline, and widely spaced nipples. Her bone age is delayed by 2 years compared to her chronological age. Which of the following laboratory findings would be most characteristic of this condition?','["Elevated FSH with normal LH","Decreased estradiol","Elevated prolactin","Decreased TSH","Elevated cortisol"]','Elevated FSH with normal LH','This presentation is classic for Turner syndrome (45,X karyotype). The characteristic laboratory finding is elevated follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) with normal or low luteinizing hormone (LH), due to ovarian dysgenesis and lack of negative feedback on the pituitary. The other findings are incorrect: estradiol would be decreased (not elevated), prolactin is typically normal, TSH is normal, and cortisol is normal. Turner syndrome is the most common chromosomal abnormality causing short stature in females.','"WEB" - Webbed neck, Elevated FSH, Bone age delay','Turner syndrome should be suspected in any girl with unexplained short stature and dysmorphic features.','Decreased estradiol is true but not the most characteristic finding; elevated prolactin, decreased TSH, and elevated cortisol are not typical of Turner syndrome.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature','["short stature","Endocrine","chat-agent","Turner syndrome","genetics","pediatrics"]',true,'Child with Turner syndrome showing webbed neck and other characteristic features','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-50df0b08-79a.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:44:26.839Z','2026-10-07T21:44:26.839Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcc002ro7lricnt4rnu','mcq','17- Vitamin B12 is important for RBCs production. What is correct about it?','["Humans have low stores of it.","Its deficiency is presented in early months of life.","Only animal sources are adequate for it in humans.","All causes B12 deficiency anemia are acquired."]','Humans have low stores of it.','','','','','Basic','Hematology','B12 Deficiency','["b12","stores","production"]',false,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b00awi8rmgure6kyz","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Treatment: daily oral folic acid; transfuse only if severe and symptomatic. 3.3 Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency\\n★ Sources are exclusively animal-derived (produced by microorganisms; humans cannot synthesize it). Stores last 3–5 years in older children/adults, but an infant born to a B12-deficient mother can show signs in the first 4–5 months.","chunkContent":"• Inadequate intake — pregnancy, goat milk feeding, rapid growth in infancy, chronic hemolysis\\n\\n• Decreased absorption\\n\\n• Congenital defects of folate metabolism\\n★ Clinical: peaks at 4–7 months of age — irritability, failure to thrive, chronic diarrhea and glossitis. Labs: low serum folate; hypersegmented neutrophils (>5 lobes) on peripheral smear. Treatment: daily oral folic acid; transfuse only if severe and symptomatic.\\n\\n3.3 Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency\\n★ Sources are exclusively animal-derived (produced by microorganisms; humans cannot synthesize it). Stores last 3–5 years in older children/adults, but an infant born to a B12-deficient mother can show signs in the first 4–5 months.\\n\\nDIETARY SOURCES\\n\\n• Meat, fish, eggs\\n\\n• Dairy products — milk, yoghurt, cheese\\n\\n• Seaweed, nori, spirulina\\n\\n• Chlorella tablets, sardines\\nCAUSES","answerCoverage":0.67,"judgeScore":8},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b00b0i8rmcofptoka","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Infants of deficient mothers: signs at 4–6 months\\nCauses Goat milk feeding; chronic hemolysis; decreased absorption; congenital defects of folate metabolism Vegans; congenital/juvenile pernicious anemia; gastric surgery; terminal ileum disease\\nFindings Low serum folate, normal-to-increased iron and B12 Normal serum folate, decreased vitamin B12 Neurologic symptoms Absent Present — and can be irreversible if untreated Treatment Daily oral folic acid Parenteral (injectable) vitamin B12","chunkContent":"Feature Folic Acid Deficiency Vitamin B12 Deficiency Food sources Green vegetables, fruits, animals, whole grains Animal sources only, made by microorganisms Presentation Peaks at 4–7 months of age Older children/adults: stores last 3–5 yrs. Infants of deficient mothers: signs at 4–6 months\\nCauses Goat milk feeding; chronic hemolysis; decreased absorption; congenital defects of folate metabolism Vegans; congenital/juvenile pernicious anemia; gastric surgery; terminal ileum disease\\nFindings Low serum folate, normal-to-increased iron and B12 Normal serum folate, decreased vitamin B12 Neurologic symptoms Absent Present — and can be irreversible if untreated Treatment Daily oral folic acid Parenteral (injectable) vitamin B12","answerCoverage":0.33,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.164Z','2026-10-14T17:54:04.638Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuymz8ca001dsomagokkgvkk','mcq','A 10-year-old boy presents with progressive short stature. His growth velocity has decreased from the 25th percentile to below the 5th percentile over the past year. He has delayed bone age (chronological age 10 years, bone age 7 years) and appears younger than his peers. Which of the following diagnostic tests would be most appropriate to confirm the suspected diagnosis?','["Insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) level","Growth hormone stimulation test","Karyotype analysis","Thyroid function tests","Calcium metabolism panel"]','Growth hormone stimulation test','This presentation is highly suggestive of growth hormone deficiency (GHD). The key findings are: 1) Progressive decrease in growth velocity, 2) Delayed bone age, and 3) Younger appearance. While IGF-1 level is often low in GHD, it can be affected by nutritional status and other factors, making it less specific. The growth hormone stimulation test is the gold standard for diagnosing GHD, as it assesses the pituitary''s ability to secrete GH in response to provocative stimuli. Karyotype analysis would be appropriate for Turner syndrome (which this boy doesn''t have), and thyroid tests would be appropriate for hypothyroidism.','"GHST" - Growth Hormone Stimulation Test for Growth Hormone deficiency','Growth hormone deficiency should be considered when growth velocity decreases and bone age is delayed.','IGF-1 is supportive but not diagnostic; karyotype is for genetic disorders like Turner syndrome; thyroid tests are for hypothyroidism; calcium metabolism is not relevant to this presentation.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature','["short stature","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth hormone","diagnosis","endocrine"]',true,'Growth hormone stimulation test showing diagnostic response patterns','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-91491a25-e14.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:45:25.498Z','2026-10-07T21:45:25.498Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuywq1010028soma6mp9l4ih','mcq','A 12-year-old girl presents with short stature (height <5th percentile), primary amenorrhea, and webbed neck. On physical examination, she has a shield chest, widely spaced nipples, and cubitus valgus. Her bone age is consistent with chronological age. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial diagnostic test to confirm the suspected diagnosis?','["Growth hormone stimulation test","Karyotype analysis","Thyroid function tests","Pelvic ultrasound","Antigliadin antibodies"]','Karyotype analysis','The clinical presentation is classic for Turner syndrome (45,X karyotype), characterized by short stature, webbed neck, shield chest, widely spaced nipples, cubitus valgus, and primary amenorrhea. Karyotype analysis is the definitive diagnostic test for Turner syndrome. Growth hormone stimulation test is not indicated as the short stature in Turner syndrome is not due to growth hormone deficiency. Thyroid function tests may be abnormal in some cases but are not diagnostic. Pelvic ultrasound may show streak ovaries but is not diagnostic. Antigliadin antibodies are for celiac disease evaluation.','Turner - Test with Karyotype','Turner syndrome should be suspected in any girl with short stature and characteristic physical features; karyotype analysis is diagnostic.','A) Growth hormone stimulation - not indicated as Turner syndrome short stature is not due to GHD; C) Thyroid function - may be abnormal but not diagnostic; D) Pelvic ultrasound - may show streak ovaries but not diagnostic; E) Antigliadin antibodies - for celiac disease evaluation','Medium','endocrine','Turner syndrome','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","Turner syndrome","short stature","Turner syndrome","karyotype","amenorrhea"]',true,'Anatomically precise educational illustration showing characteristic Turner syndrome physical features including webbed neck, shield chest, widely spaced nipples, cubitus valgus, and a comparison of normal female karyotype (46,XX) vs Turner syndrome karyotype (45,X)','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0.5,-1,2,'2026-10-08T02:18:12.241Z','2026-10-13T16:37:41.861Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyn65t3001hsomaiy7748vu','mcq','A 45-year-old male presents with progressive enlargement of his hands, feet, and facial features over the past 5 years. On examination, he has a prominent brow ridge, enlarged jaw (prognathism), thickened lips, and coarse facial features. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient''s condition?','["Pituitary adenoma secreting growth hormone","Thyroid hormone excess","Cortisol excess","Prolactinoma","Adrenal insufficiency"]','Pituitary adenoma secreting growth hormone','The patient''s presentation with progressive enlargement of hands, feet, and facial features (prognathism, prominent brow ridge, thickened lips) is classic for acromegaly, which is caused by excessive growth hormone secretion, most commonly from a pituitary adenoma. The other options are incorrect because: thyroid hormone excess causes different symptoms like weight loss and tachycardia; cortisol excess leads to Cushing''s syndrome with central obesity and moon face; prolactinoma typically causes galactorrhea and amenorrhea in women; adrenal insufficiency presents with fatigue and hypotension.','"FACE" - Facial features, Acral enlargement, Coarse features, Enlarged extremities','Acromegaly develops gradually over years and is often missed initially. Early diagnosis is crucial as untreated acromegaly increases mortality from cardiovascular disease, diabetes, and cancer.','Thyroid hormone excess: Causes weight loss, heat intolerance, and tachycardia, not progressive enlargement of facial features and extremities. Cortisol excess: Results in central obesity, moon face, and striae, not the specific acromegalic features. Prolactinoma: Primarily affects reproductive function rather than causing skeletal overgrowth. Adrenal insufficiency: Presents with fatigue, hypotension, and hyperpigmentation, not progressive enlargement.','Easy','Endocrine','acromegaly','["acromegaly","Endocrine","chat-agent","pituitary","growth hormone","endocrine","acromegaly"]',true,'Comparison of normal facial features versus characteristic acromegaly features including enlarged jaw, prominent brow ridge, and thickened lips','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-45a3af19-fb5.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:50:48.807Z','2026-10-07T21:50:48.807Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyn80jy001jsomamejv4ha5','mcq','A 6-year-old boy presents for evaluation of short stature. His height is 105 cm (3rd percentile), weight is 18 kg (25th percentile), and his growth velocity has been 4 cm/year over the past 2 years. Physical examination is normal. The growth chart shows his height percentile dropping from the 25th percentile at age 4 to the 3rd percentile at age 6. What is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?','["Order thyroid function tests","Perform bone age X-ray","Measure insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) levels","Refer for growth hormone stimulation testing","Wait and re-evaluate in 6 months"]','Perform bone age X-ray','The child has normal growth velocity but a significant drop in height percentile, which suggests possible constitutional growth delay or pathological causes. A bone age X-ray is the most appropriate next step to assess skeletal maturation. If bone age is delayed, it supports constitutional growth delay. If bone age is appropriate, further evaluation for endocrine disorders, genetic syndromes, or chronic diseases would be warranted. Thyroid function tests and IGF-1 levels may be indicated later, but bone age assessment provides crucial information about growth potential first.','"Bone age first - helps decide if growth is just delayed"','Constitutional growth delay is the most common cause of short stature, typically presenting with delayed bone age and normal growth velocity.','Thyroid function tests: Important but should follow bone age assessment; hypothyroidism typically shows decreased growth velocity. IGF-1 levels: Useful for growth hormone evaluation but bone age provides more immediate information about growth potential. Growth hormone testing: Indicated only after other causes are ruled out. Waiting 6 months: Inappropriate given the significant drop in percentile.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature evaluation','["short stature evaluation","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth","pediatrics","endocrine"]',true,'Growth chart showing height percentile curve dropping from 25th to 3rd percentile','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-a9fe39c0-0d2.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:52:15.310Z','2026-10-07T21:52:15.310Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyn88sn001lsoma7q9sl2fp','mcq','A 10-year-old boy presents with short stature (height 120 cm, <3rd percentile), delayed bone age (8 years), and normal growth velocity. His IGF-1 level is low, and growth hormone stimulation testing shows a peak GH level of 5 ng/mL. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology of his condition?','["Primary pituitary dysfunction leading to inadequate GH secretion","Peripheral tissue resistance to GH action","Defect in GH receptor signaling","Abnormal IGF-1 production","Delayed hypothalamic-pituitary maturation"]','Delayed hypothalamic-pituitary maturation','This case represents constitutional growth delay, which is characterized by delayed hypothalamic-pituitary maturation rather than true growth hormone deficiency. The low IGF-1 and suboptimal GH response to stimulation testing are due to immaturity of the growth hormone axis, not permanent dysfunction. In constitutional growth delay, the hypothalamic-pituitary system eventually matures, leading to catch-up growth during adolescence. True growth hormone deficiency would show persistently abnormal GH secretion, while peripheral resistance would present with normal/high GH but low IGF-1. The key distinguishing feature is the eventual normalization of growth in constitutional growth delay.','"Temporary immaturity, not permanent deficiency - catch-up growth comes later"','Constitutional growth delay is a normal variant of growth where the growth tempo is simply slower, not abnormal.','Primary pituitary dysfunction: Would be permanent and not resolve with time. Peripheral tissue resistance: Would show normal/high GH levels with low IGF-1. Defect in GH receptor signaling: Would present with normal GH but poor response. Abnormal IGF-1 production: Would show normal GH but low IGF-1 without receptor issues.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature pathophysiology','["short stature pathophysiology","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth","pathophysiology","endocrine"]',true,'Comparison of normal growth hormone pathway versus growth hormone deficiency','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-cb545052-ac0.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-07T21:52:25.991Z','2026-10-07T21:52:25.991Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyw892j001ysoma9h1e84op','mcq','A 9-year-old boy presents with short stature (height <3rd percentile) and delayed bone age of 6 years. His growth velocity is normal for his chronological age but low for his bone age. His father was also a ''late bloomer'' and reached his final adult height at 17 years. Initial laboratory workup shows normal thyroid function, normal IGF-1, and normal complete blood count. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Growth hormone deficiency","Constitutional growth delay","Turner syndrome","Hypothyroidism","Small for gestational age without catch-up growth"]','Constitutional growth delay','This patient presents with classic features of constitutional growth delay (CGD): short stature with delayed bone age, normal growth velocity for chronological age but low for bone age, positive family history of delayed puberty, and normal initial laboratory workup. CGD is a common cause of short stature characterized by normal growth velocity but delayed skeletal maturation. The bone age delay typically exceeds 2 years, and patients often have a family history of delayed puberty. Growth hormone deficiency would typically show abnormal growth velocity and low IGF-1 levels. Turner syndrome is specific to females and would require karyotype confirmation. Hypothyroidism would typically show elevated TSH and low free T4.','CGD = Constitutional Growth Delay: Chronological age delayed, Growth velocity normal, Delayed bone age','Constitutional growth delay is a diagnosis of exclusion that requires normal growth velocity, delayed bone age (>2 years), and positive family history. No treatment is typically needed as these patients will eventually achieve their genetic potential height.','Growth hormone deficiency is incorrect because the patient has normal growth velocity and IGF-1 levels. Turner syndrome is incorrect as it only affects females and would require karyotype confirmation. Hypothyroidism is incorrect as thyroid function tests are normal. SGA without catch-up growth would show persistent growth failure without the delayed bone age pattern.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature evaluation','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","short stature evaluation","constitutional growth delay","bone age","growth velocity","diagnosis of exclusion"]',true,'Pediatric growth chart showing constitutional growth delay with delayed bone age compared to chronological age, including a height percentile curve that follows the lower percentiles but shows normal growth velocity, with bone age markers showing significant delay','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-88272726-10d.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-08T02:04:22.891Z','2026-10-08T02:04:22.891Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6d004be9oi28rzdsi4','mcq','What is mandatory sign in chest X ray with TOF?','["Lung plethora","Exaggerated hilar shadow","RV dilatation","Pleural effusion"]','RV dilatation','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Tetralogy of Fallot','["tof","chest-xray","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.653Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.653Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyw8es60020somal0kamcnq','mcq','A 12-year-old girl presents with short stature (height <3rd percentile) and delayed puberty. She has a webbed neck, low posterior hairline, and widely spaced nipples. Her growth velocity has been consistently below the 5th percentile for the past 2 years. Laboratory evaluation shows elevated FSH and LH levels with low estradiol. Which diagnostic test is most appropriate to confirm the suspected diagnosis?','["Growth hormone stimulation test","Karyotype analysis","Thyroid function tests","IGF-1 level","Antigliadin antibodies"]','Karyotype analysis','This patient presents with classic features of Turner syndrome: short stature, delayed puberty, and characteristic physical findings including webbed neck, low posterior hairline, and widely spaced nipples. The elevated gonadotropins (FSH/LH) with low estradiol indicate primary ovarian failure. Karyotype analysis is the definitive diagnostic test for Turner syndrome and can identify the characteristic 45,X karyotype or other variants. Growth hormone stimulation test would be appropriate if growth hormone deficiency was suspected, but the physical findings strongly suggest Turner syndrome. Thyroid function tests and IGF-1 are normal in Turner syndrome unless there are additional endocrine issues.','Turner = Tall Uterus, No Ovaries, Recessive X - features: short stature, webbed neck, delayed puberty','Turner syndrome should be suspected in any female with short stature and delayed puberty, especially with characteristic physical features. Early diagnosis is important as growth hormone therapy can improve final adult height.','Growth hormone stimulation test is incorrect as the physical findings strongly suggest Turner syndrome rather than isolated growth hormone deficiency. Thyroid function tests and IGF-1 are normal in Turner syndrome and not diagnostic. Antigliadin antibodies are relevant for celiac disease, which can cause short stature but wouldn''t explain the physical findings.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature diagnosis','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","short stature diagnosis","Turner syndrome","karyotype","short stature","delayed puberty"]',true,'Turner syndrome physical findings in a female child showing webbed neck, low posterior hairline, widely spaced nipples, and short stature with characteristic facial features','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-6ae275ba-8bc.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-08T02:04:30.294Z','2026-10-08T02:04:30.294Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuywm8vc0024somav169pmrj','mcq','A 4-year-old boy presents with short stature. His height is below the 3rd percentile for age, but his growth velocity has been normal over the past year. On examination, he has no dysmorphic features and his bone age is appropriate for his chronological age. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his short stature?','["Growth hormone deficiency","Constitutional growth delay","Turner syndrome","Celiac disease","Hypothyroidism"]','Constitutional growth delay','Constitutional growth delay is the most likely diagnosis in this case. The child has short stature but normal growth velocity, no dysmorphic features, and appropriate bone age for chronological age. This is a common, benign condition where children have a delayed but ultimately normal growth pattern. Growth hormone deficiency would typically show decreased growth velocity. Turner syndrome would present with dysmorphic features and other characteristic findings. Celiac disease and hypothyroidism would usually have additional systemic symptoms and abnormal laboratory findings.','"Normal velocity, normal bone age = constitutional delay"','Constitutional growth delay is a diagnosis of exclusion and typically presents as familial short stature with delayed but normal pubertal development.','Growth hormone deficiency: Would show decreased growth velocity over time. Turner syndrome: Would have dysmorphic features and other characteristic findings. Celiac disease: Would have gastrointestinal symptoms and positive serology. Hypothyroidism: Would have other symptoms of hypothyroidism and elevated TSH.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature causes','["short stature causes","Endocrine","chat-agent","growth","pediatrics","endocrine"]',true,'Growth chart showing constitutional growth delay pattern with normal growth velocity','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/example1.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-08T02:15:15.816Z','2026-10-08T02:15:15.816Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuywpuli0027somahb83v9v0','mcq','A 7-year-old girl presents with short stature (height <3rd percentile for age), delayed bone age (chronological age 7 years, bone age 5 years), and normal thyroid function tests. Her growth velocity has been consistently below the 25th percentile for the past 2 years. On physical examination, she has a high-pitched voice, chubby cheeks, and a prominent forehead. Which of the following diagnostic tests is most appropriate to confirm the suspected diagnosis?','["Karyotype analysis","Insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) level","Growth hormone stimulation test","Antigliadin antibodies","Thyroid ultrasound"]','Growth hormone stimulation test','The clinical presentation suggests growth hormone deficiency (GHD), characterized by short stature, delayed bone age, and normal thyroid function. The growth hormone stimulation test is the gold standard for diagnosing GHD. While IGF-1 levels can be supportive, they are not diagnostic as they can be normal in some cases of GHD and are influenced by nutrition and other factors. Karyotype analysis would be appropriate for Turner syndrome, but this patient lacks the characteristic features (webbed neck, shield chest, etc.). Antigliadin antibodies would suggest celiac disease, which can cause short stature but typically presents with gastrointestinal symptoms. Thyroid ultrasound is not indicated with normal thyroid function tests.','GHST - Growth Hormone Stimulation Test for suspected GHD','Growth hormone stimulation testing is required for definitive diagnosis of GHD; IGF-1 alone is insufficient for diagnosis.','A) Karyotype - appropriate for Turner syndrome but not suggested by clinical findings; B) IGF-1 - supportive but not diagnostic for GHD; D) Antigliadin antibodies - for celiac disease, which typically has GI symptoms; E) Thyroid ultrasound - unnecessary with normal thyroid function','Medium','endocrine','growth hormone deficiency','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","growth hormone deficiency","short stature","growth hormone","pediatric endocrinology","diagnostic testing"]',true,'Anatomically precise educational illustration showing the pituitary gland anatomy, hypothalamic-pituitary axis, and growth hormone secretion pathway with labeled structures including hypothalamus, pituitary gland, growth hormone, and target tissues','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-81d7d271-208.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-08T02:18:03.942Z','2026-10-08T02:18:03.942Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuywmeec0025somaqfaeknvp','mcq','A 12-year-old girl presents with short stature. Her height is below the 3rd percentile, and she has not yet started puberty. On examination, she has webbed neck, low posterior hairline, and widely spaced nipples. Which of the following diagnostic tests would be most appropriate to confirm the suspected diagnosis?','["Karyotype analysis","Growth hormone stimulation test","Thyroid function tests","Celiac serology","Bone age assessment"]','Karyotype analysis','The clinical presentation is highly suggestive of Turner syndrome (45,X), which is characterized by short stature, delayed puberty, and specific dysmorphic features including webbed neck, low posterior hairline, and widely spaced nipples. Karyotype analysis is the definitive diagnostic test to confirm Turner syndrome. Growth hormone stimulation test would be appropriate for growth hormone deficiency, but the dysmorphic features point to Turner syndrome. Thyroid function tests and celiac serology are less likely given the characteristic physical findings. Bone age assessment would show delayed bone age but is not diagnostic.','"Webbed neck + short stature + no puberty = think Turner"','Turner syndrome affects approximately 1 in 2500 live female births and is the most common chromosomal abnormality causing short stature in females.','Growth hormone stimulation test: Would be appropriate for isolated growth hormone deficiency but not for Turner syndrome with characteristic dysmorphic features. Thyroid function tests: Would be normal in Turner syndrome unless there''s associated thyroid dysfunction. Celiac serology: Would not explain the dysmorphic features. Bone age assessment: Would show delay but is not diagnostic.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature Turner syndrome','["short stature Turner syndrome","Endocrine","chat-agent","genetics","pediatrics","endocrine"]',true,'Diagram showing characteristic features of Turner syndrome including webbed neck and other dysmorphic features','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/example2.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-08T02:15:22.980Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.478Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmutc1rs4000bxnlrwuqxjged','mcq','An 18-month-old boy presents with sudden onset of vomiting, crampy abdominal pain, draws up his knees, cries out, and exhibits pallor in a colicky pattern every 15- 20 minutes. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute appendicitis","Gastroenteritis","Pancreatitis"]','Acute appendicitis','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Appendicitis','["abdominal pain","vomiting","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T04:40:37.348Z','2026-10-08T05:03:09.887Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyhobei0004komagx6rmz49','mcq','A 72-year-old patient with heart failure, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 35 mL/min) is prescribed an ACE inhibitor. Which of the following laboratory parameters should be most closely monitored during the first 2 weeks of therapy?','["Serum potassium levels","Liver function tests (AST, ALT)","Complete blood count (CBC)","Serum creatinine and blood urea nitrogen (BUN)","Fasting blood glucose"]','Serum creatinine and blood urea nitrogen (BUN)','ACE inhibitors can cause acute kidney injury, particularly in patients with pre-existing renal impairment, heart failure, or volume depletion. The mechanism involves reduced glomerular filtration pressure due to efferent arteriolar dilation without compensatory afferent arteriolar dilation. This can lead to increased serum creatinine and BUN levels. Monitoring renal function within 1-2 weeks of initiating therapy and with dose adjustments is crucial, with a rise in creatinine of up to 30% often considered acceptable.','ACE Inhibitors → Check Creatinine First','A significant increase (>30%) in serum creatinine after starting an ACE inhibitor may indicate renal artery stenosis or volume depletion. In such cases, the drug should be discontinued or the dose reduced, and volume status should be assessed before restarting.','Option 1 is important but potassium monitoring is more relevant for chronic therapy and patients at risk of hyperkalemia. Option 2 is not a primary concern with ACE inhibitors. Option 3 is not routinely monitored. Option 5 is not specifically related to ACE inhibitor therapy.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","pharmacology","renal monitoring","heart failure"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,2,'2026-10-07T19:16:58.170Z','2026-10-08T05:03:09.888Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuywmjdx0026soma6qt60eby','mcq','A 3-year-old child presents with short stature. On examination, the child has a pot-belly, thin extremities, and delayed bone age. Laboratory findings show elevated alkaline phosphatase and low serum zinc levels. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Growth hormone deficiency","Celiac disease","Malnutrition","Hypothyroidism","Chronic kidney disease"]','Malnutrition','The clinical presentation of short stature with pot-belly, thin extremities, delayed bone age, elevated alkaline phosphatase (reflecting bone turnover), and low serum zinc levels is classic for malnutrition. Malnutrition is a common cause of short stature worldwide and affects multiple organ systems including bone growth. Growth hormone deficiency would typically show decreased growth velocity over time. Celiac disease would have gastrointestinal symptoms and positive serology. Hypothyroidism would have other symptoms of hypothyroidism and elevated TSH. Chronic kidney disease would typically have other renal manifestations.','"Pot-belly + thin limbs + delayed bone age = malnutrition"','Malnutrition is a leading cause of short stature globally and can be prevented with adequate nutrition and healthcare access.','Growth hormone deficiency: Would show decreased growth velocity over time without the characteristic physical findings. Celiac disease: Would have gastrointestinal symptoms and positive serology. Hypothyroidism: Would have other symptoms of hypothyroidism and elevated TSH. Chronic kidney disease: Would typically have other renal manifestations.','Medium','Endocrine','short stature malnutrition','["short stature malnutrition","Endocrine","chat-agent","nutrition","pediatrics","endocrine"]',true,'Clinical photograph showing signs of malnutrition including pot-belly and thin extremities in a child','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/example3.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-08T02:15:29.445Z','2026-10-08T05:03:09.915Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusgbtqn0003gyr88asqejjo','mcq','<div class=''q-callout q-callout-blue''>🖼️ <strong>Examine:</strong> study the image above before answering.</div>A 58-year-old male with a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of facial swelling, lip swelling, and difficulty breathing 3 hours after taking his newly prescribed lisinopril. He has no previous history of allergies or similar episodes. Examine the image above and identify the most likely mechanism for this adverse reaction.','["Activation of the kallikrein-kinin system","Direct histamine release from mast cells","Complement activation via the alternative pathway","IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity reaction","Bradykinin degradation by angiotensin-converting enzyme"]','Activation of the kallikrein-kinin system','ACE inhibitors like lisinopril cause angioedema primarily through activation of the kallikrein-kinin system. ACE normally degrades bradykinin, a potent vasodilator that increases vascular permeability. By inhibiting ACE, these drugs lead to bradykinin accumulation, resulting in vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and subsequent angioedema [1]. This mechanism is distinct from histamine release or IgE-mediated reactions, which would typically present with urticaria or pruritus. The kallikrein-kinin system involves factor XII activation, which converts prekallikrein to kallikrein, which then cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to produce bradykinin. ACE inhibition prevents bradykinin breakdown, leading to its accumulation and angioedema symptoms. <table class=''q-table''><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect of ACE Inhibition</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Bradykinin</td><td>↑ Accumulation</td><td>Vasodilation, increased permeability</td></tr><tr><td>Angiotensin II</td><td>↓ Production</td><td>Reduced blood pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Substance P</td><td>↑ Accumulation</td><td>Further inflammation</td></tr></table> [2]','<div class=''q-mnemo''><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>K</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>A</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>L</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>L</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>I</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>K</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>R</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>E</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>I</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>N</div></div></div><div class=''q-callout q-callout-red''>Kallikrein-kinin system activation leads to bradykinin accumulation, causing ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema.</div>','<div class=''q-callout q-callout-amber''>💎 ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema is more common in Black patients and those with a history of hereditary angioedema. Discontinuation of the ACE inhibitor is required, and alternative antihypertensives should be considered.</div>','<div class=''q-grid q-grid-2''><div class=''q-card q-card-correct''>✅ Activation of the kallikrein-kinin system: Correct. ACE inhibition prevents bradykinin degradation, leading to accumulation and angioedema.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ Direct histamine release from mast cells: Incorrect. ACE inhibitors don''t directly cause histamine release; histamine-mediated reactions typically present with urticaria.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ Complement activation via the alternative pathway: Incorrect. This mechanism is associated with hereditary angioedema due to C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity reaction: Incorrect. This would typically present with urticaria, pruritus, and potentially anaphylaxis, not isolated angioedema.</div></div>','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","agent","Mechanism of angioedema as a side effect of ACE inhibitors"]',true,'A diagram showing the kallikrein-kinin pathway with ACE inhibition, highlighting bradykinin accumulation and its effects on vascular permeability leading to angioedema.','','','','','','[{"type":"paper","title":"Angioedema induced by angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors: a review of the literature"},{"type":"guideline","title":"2017 ACC/AHA/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/AGS/APhA/ASH/ASPC/NMA/PCNA guideline for the prevention, detection, evaluation, and management of high blood pressure in adults"}]','/generated/agent/images/img_1791035558732_qe1em8xn.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','Cardiovascular','Mechanism of Disease','[]','[]','approved','{"verdict":"approved","issues":[],"confidence":1}',0,-1,2,'2026-10-03T13:52:38.735Z','2026-10-08T05:03:09.916Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusg9ia80001gyr8aglhj25j','mcq','<div class=''q-callout q-callout-blue''>🖼️ <strong>Examine:</strong> study the image above before answering.</div>🔊 Listen to the audio presentation. A 58-year-old male with a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of swelling of his lips, tongue, and difficulty breathing. He recently started lisinopril for his blood pressure control. Examine the image above, which shows facial angioedema and the mechanism of ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which ACE inhibitors cause this adverse effect?','["Increased production of angiotensin II leading to vasoconstriction","Inhibition of aldosterone secretion causing electrolyte imbalances","Accumulation of bradykinin due to decreased degradation","Direct mast cell degranulation and histamine release","Increased prostaglandin synthesis causing vasodilation"]','Accumulation of bradykinin due to decreased degradation','ACE inhibitors (ACEIs) cause angioedema primarily through <span class=''q-hl-green''>bradykinin accumulation</span> due to inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), which normally degrades bradykinin [1]. ACE is identical to kininase II, the enzyme responsible for breaking down bradykinin. When ACE is inhibited by drugs like lisinopril, bradykinin levels increase significantly. Bradykinin is a potent vasodilator that increases vascular permeability, leading to fluid extravasation and tissue edema. This mechanism explains the characteristic swelling of lips, tongue, and airway involvement seen in ACEI-induced angioedema. The table below compares ACEI effects on vasoactive substances:

<div class=''q-table''><table><tr><th>Substance</th><th>Effect of ACE Inhibition</th><th>Clinical Consequence</th></tr><tr><td>Angiotensin II</td><td>Decreased</td><td>Reduced vasoconstriction</td></tr><tr><td>Bradykinin</td><td>Increased</td><td>Vasodilation, increased permeability, edema</td></tr><tr><td>Aldosterone</td><td>Decreased</td><td>Mild diuresis, hyperkalemia</td></tr></table></div>

Unlike the other options, bradykinin accumulation directly explains the angioedema mechanism [2].','<div class=''q-mnemo''><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>B</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>R</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>A</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>D</div></div><div class=''q-letter''><div class=''q-letter-text''>Y</div></div></div>
<div class=''q-callout q-callout-red''>B - Bradykinin accumulation, R - Reduced degradation, A - ACE inhibition, D - Direct mechanism, Y - You must remember this!</div>','<div class=''q-callout q-callout-amber''>💎 ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema can occur at any time during treatment but is most common within the first week of therapy. The risk is higher in Black patients and those with a history of angioedema. Alternative antihypertensives should be used in patients with this adverse reaction.</div>','<div class=''q-grid q-grid-2''><div class=''q-card q-card-correct''>✅ Accumulation of bradykinin due to decreased degradation - Correct. ACE (kininase II) normally degrades bradykinin; inhibition leads to increased bradykinin levels causing vasodilation and increased vascular permeability.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ Increased production of angiotensin II leading to vasoconstriction - Incorrect. ACE inhibitors decrease angiotensin II production, causing vasodilation, not vasoconstriction.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ Inhibition of aldosterone secretion causing electrolyte imbalances - Incorrect. While ACE inhibitors do reduce aldosterone, this causes hyperkalemia and mild diuresis, not angioedema.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ Direct mast cell degranulation and histamine release - Incorrect. This mechanism is associated with other drugs (like opioids), not ACE inhibitors.</div><div class=''q-card q-card-red''>❌ Increased prostaglandin synthesis causing vasodilation - Incorrect. ACE inhibitors don''t directly affect prostaglandin synthesis; this is more relevant to NSAID adverse effects.</div></div>','Moderate','General','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","agent","Mechanism of angioedema as a side effect of ACE inhibitors"]',true,'A diagram showing the enzymatic conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II and the degradation of bradykinin by ACE, with ACE inhibition leading to bradykinin accumulation and subsequent angioedema.','','','','','','[{"type":"review","title":"ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema: advances in understanding and management"},{"type":"guideline","title":"ACC/AHA/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/AGS/APhA/ASH/ASPC/NMA/PCNA guideline for the prevention, detection, evaluation, and management of high blood pressure in adults"}]','/generated/agent/images/img_1791035440376_yuihi82n.png','/generated/agent/audio/tts_1791035450571_3u5umen6.wav','','','','[]','','[]','[]','Cardiovascular','Pharmacologic Management','[]','[]','approved','{"verdict":"approved","issues":[],"confidence":1}',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T13:50:50.576Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.481Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuyho6gy0003koma68x332no','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus develops a persistent dry cough 3 weeks after starting lisinopril. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this adverse effect?','["Accumulation of bradykinin due to inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme","Direct irritation of the bronchial mucosa by the drug","Hypersensitivity reaction mediated by IgE antibodies","Increased prostaglandin E2 production in the lungs","Inhibition of aldosterone secretion leading to electrolyte imbalances"]','Accumulation of bradykinin due to inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme','The persistent dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is primarily due to the accumulation of bradykinin and other inflammatory mediators. ACE normally breaks down bradykinin, so when ACE is inhibited, bradykinin levels increase, leading to inflammation and irritation of the bronchial mucosa. This adverse effect occurs in 5-35% of patients and is the most common reason for discontinuing ACE inhibitor therapy.','ACE Inhibitors → Bradykinin Build-up → Cough','ACE inhibitor-induced cough is non-productive, dry, and persistent. It typically resolves within 1-4 weeks after discontinuing the ACE inhibitor. Switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) is usually effective as ARBs do not affect bradykinin metabolism.','Option 2 is incorrect because ACE inhibitors don''t directly irritate bronchial mucosa. Option 3 is incorrect as this is not a true hypersensitivity reaction. Option 4 is incorrect because ACE inhibitors don''t increase prostaglandin E2. Option 5 is incorrect as aldosterone inhibition doesn''t cause cough.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","pharmacology","adverse effects","hypertension"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-07T19:16:51.778Z','2026-10-08T08:27:31.493Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmuywq6nv0029somat498rk3m','mcq','A 9-year-old boy presents with short stature (height <3rd percentile) and a 2-year history of diarrhea, abdominal pain, and weight loss. His growth velocity has decreased from the 50th percentile to the 10th percentile over the past year. On physical examination, he has pallor, abdominal distension, and muscle wasting. Laboratory studies show microcytic anemia, elevated transaminases, and low albumin. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient''s short stature?','["Growth hormone therapy","Gluten-free diet","Thyroid hormone replacement","Testosterone replacement","Nutritional supplementation"]','Gluten-free diet','The clinical presentation is consistent with celiac disease, which can cause short stature due to malabsorption and chronic inflammation. The key findings include gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, abdominal pain), weight loss, microcytic anemia, elevated transaminases, and low albumin. The most appropriate initial treatment is a gluten-free diet, which addresses the underlying cause of malabsorption and should lead to improvement in growth parameters. Growth hormone therapy is not indicated as the short stature is due to malabsorption, not growth hormone deficiency. Thyroid hormone replacement would be appropriate for hypothyroidism, which is not suggested by the presentation. Testosterone replacement is for delayed puberty, not indicated here. Nutritional supplementation may be adjunctive but does not address the underlying cause.','Celiac - Cure with Gluten-free diet','Celiac disease should be considered in any child with short stature and gastrointestinal symptoms; gluten-free diet is the cornerstone of treatment.','A) Growth hormone - not indicated as short stature is due to malabsorption; C) Thyroid hormone - not suggested by presentation; D) Testosterone - for delayed puberty, not indicated; E) Nutritional supplementation - adjunctive but doesn''t address underlying cause','Medium','endocrine','celiac disease','["short stature","Endocrine","deep-agent","celiac disease","short stature","celiac disease","malabsorption","gastrointestinal"]',true,'Anatomically precise educational illustration showing normal intestinal villi vs. celiac disease villous atrophy, including cross-sectional view of small intestine with damaged villi, inflammatory infiltrate, and comparison of normal vs. flattened mucosal architecture','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-665fe23e-969.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,2,'2026-10-08T02:18:19.579Z','2026-10-13T16:37:24.357Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wn71g000mbnrm39spchr7','mcq','A 2-week-old male infant presents with abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium in the first 48 hours of life. Rectal biopsy is performed for suspected Hirschsprung disease. Which of the following histopathological findings would be most characteristic of this condition?','["Complete absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal plexus with hypertrophied nerve trunks","Increased ganglion cell density in the myenteric plexus with normal nerve trunks","Normal ganglion cell distribution with acetylcholinesterase staining","Partial circumferential aganglionosis affecting less than 1/8 of the circumference","Myenteric hypoganglionosis without nerve trunk hypertrophy"]','Complete absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal plexus with hypertrophied nerve trunks','Hirschsprung disease is characterized by the congenital absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon, resulting in a functional obstruction. The diagnostic gold standard is rectal biopsy, which typically shows complete absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal plexus along with hypertrophied nerve trunks. Acetylcholinesterase staining demonstrates increased enzyme activity in the hypertrophied nerve fibers of the aganglionic segment. The biopsy should be taken from the posterior rectal wall about 2 cm above the dentate line to avoid the normal ganglionated zone near the anus.','AGH - Absence of Ganglion cells + Hypertrophied nerve trunks','In newborns with failure to pass meconium by 48 hours, Hirschsprung disease must be considered. The rectal biopsy should be taken from the posterior rectal wall to avoid the normal ganglionated zone near the dentate line.','Option 2 describes the opposite finding (increased ganglion cells) which would suggest a different diagnosis. Option 3 is incorrect because acetylcholinesterase staining shows increased activity, not normal distribution. Option 4 describes partial aganglionosis which is not diagnostic for classic Hirschsprung disease. Option 5 describes myenteric hypoganglionosis without the characteristic nerve trunk hypertrophy seen in Hirschsprung disease.','Medium','gastrointestinal','Hirschsprung disease','["Hirschsprung Disease","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Hirschsprung disease","pathophysiology","clinical presentation","diagnosis","pediatrics"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-13T16:38:09.508Z','2026-10-13T16:38:09.508Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wsyew0019j6rmf42of3uo','mcq','A 65-year-old male with hypertension, heart failure, and chronic kidney disease is started on lisinopril. Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism of action of ACE inhibitors and their most common side effect?','["A) Block angiotensin II receptors directly; most common side effect is hyperkalemia","B) Inhibit conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II; most common side effect is dry cough due to bradykinin accumulation","C) Inhibit renin release; most common side effect is hypotension","D) Block aldosterone receptors; most common side effect is hyperglycemia"]','B) Inhibit conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II; most common side effect is dry cough due to bradykinin accumulation','ACE inhibitors work primarily by inhibiting the angiotensin-converting enzyme, which prevents the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. This leads to decreased angiotensin II levels, resulting in vasodilation, reduced aldosterone secretion, and decreased blood pressure. The most common side effect is a dry cough, affecting approximately 10-20% of patients, which is thought to be due to bradykinin accumulation and subsequent inflammation in the airways. Other important side effects include hyperkalemia, hypotension, and acute kidney injury in susceptible patients.','ACE = Angiotensin Converting Enzyme inhibition → Less angiotensin II → More bradykinin → Cough','ACE inhibitors are first-line for hypertension, heart failure, and diabetic nephropathy, but the characteristic dry cough (due to bradykinin accumulation) often necessitates switching to ARBs in patients who cannot tolerate this side effect.','Option A is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not directly block angiotensin II receptors (that''s ARBs); hyperkalemia is a side effect but not the most common. Option C is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not inhibit renin release (that''s renin inhibitors); hypotension is a side effect but not the most common. Option D is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not block aldosterone receptors directly; hyperglycemia is not a typical side effect.','Medium','cardiovascular','pharmacology','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","pharmacology","ACE inhibitors","mechanism of action","side effects","bradykinin"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-13T16:42:38.264Z','2026-10-13T16:42:38.264Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hcz0039qjlrgmgbeser','mcq','Chronic diarrhea in children is generally defined as diarrhea lasting for:','["More than 3 days","More than 7 days","More than 14 days","More than 30 days"]','More than 14 days','<p><strong>Why More than 14 days is correct:</strong> Chronic diarrhea in children is defined as diarrhea lasting more than 14 days because this duration distinguishes it from acute infectious diarrhea and allows for the identification of persistent conditions requiring different diagnostic approaches. The 14-day threshold is based on clinical observation that most infectious diarrheal episodes resolve within this timeframe, and persistence beyond this suggests either ongoing infection, malabsorption syndromes, inflammatory bowel disease, or other chronic gastrointestinal disorders. This definition helps clinicians differentiate between self-limiting illnesses and conditions that require more extensive evaluation including stool studies, endoscopy, or specialized testing.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>More than 3 days:</strong> This duration is too short, as most acute infectious diarrheas resolve within 3-7 days, and defining chronic diarrhea at 3 days would incorrectly classify many acute cases.</li>
<li><strong>More than 7 days:</strong> While some acute infections may persist up to 10-14 days, this threshold is still too short to distinguish between acute and chronic conditions requiring different management strategies.</li>
<li><strong>More than 30 days:</strong> This duration is too long and would miss important chronic conditions that require intervention between 14-30 days, such as persistent infections or early manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The 14-day threshold helps guide diagnostic evaluation, with chronic diarrhea prompting investigation for causes like celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or chronic infections.</li>
<li>Acute diarrhea (&lt;14 days) is typically managed with rehydration, while chronic diarrhea requires nutritional assessment and specific diagnostic testing.</li>
<li>In resource-limited settings, the 14-day definition helps prioritize patients who need more extensive evaluation beyond simple rehydration.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Chronic diarrhea in children is defined as lasting more than 14 days, distinguishing it from acute infections and guiding appropriate diagnostic evaluation.</p>','<p>14 days = chronic diarrhea (remember: 2 weeks)</p>','<p>The duration of diarrhea is a critical factor in determining the likelihood of specific pathogens and the need for specialized testing.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>More than 3 days:</strong> Too short, as most acute diarrheas resolve within this timeframe.</li>
<li><strong>More than 7 days:</strong> Still within the range of many acute infections, not sufficient to define chronicity.</li>
<li><strong>More than 30 days:</strong> Too long, missing important chronic conditions that require intervention earlier.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Chronic Diarrhea','["chronic","definition","duration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.811Z','2026-10-18T19:41:03.759Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wu3n800ivj6rmchsuimgd','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by infrequent bowel movements (less than 3 times per week), straining during defecation, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation for the past 6 months. She reports no red flag symptoms. Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology, diagnosis, and treatment of her condition is most accurate?','["The Rome IV criteria require at least 3 months of symptoms with at least 6 months of onset for diagnosis, and polyethylene glycol (PEG) is first-line therapy as it works by stimulating colonic peristalsis","Constipation can be categorized into slow transit constipation (colonic dysmotility), outlet obstruction (pelvic floor dysfunction), and normal transit constipation, with PEG being first-line due to its osmotic effect that retains water in the stool","Pelvic floor dysfunction is the most common cause of chronic constipation and is best diagnosed with anorectal manometry alone, while stimulant laxatives are preferred over osmotic agents for initial treatment","All patients with chronic constipation should undergo extensive colonic imaging first, and magnesium hydroxide is superior to PEG for long-term management due to fewer side effects"]','The Rome IV criteria require at least 3 months of symptoms with at least 6 months of onset for diagnosis, and polyethylene glycol (PEG) is first-line therapy as it works by stimulating colonic peristalsis','The correct answer addresses key aspects of constipation pathophysiology, diagnosis, and treatment. Rome IV criteria require symptoms for at least 3 months with onset at least 6 months prior for diagnosis of chronic constipation. Constipation pathophysiology includes three main categories: slow transit constipation (colonic dysmotility), outlet obstruction (pelvic floor dysfunction), and normal transit constipation. Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is indeed first-line therapy as an osmotic laxative that works by retaining water in the stool, not by stimulating peristalsis. PEG has been shown to be effective with complete improvement in 77% of patients versus 20% with placebo. The other options contain inaccuracies: Rome IV criteria require 3 months of symptoms with 6-month onset, not 6 months of symptoms; pelvic floor dysfunction is not the most common cause; extensive imaging is not routinely indicated; and magnesium hydroxide has more side effects than PEG.','ROME-PEG: Rome criteria (3 months symptoms, 6 months onset), PEG (osmotic, first-line, 77% efficacy)','When evaluating chronic constipation, always consider the three pathophysiological categories (slow transit, outlet obstruction, normal transit) as this guides diagnostic testing and treatment selection. PEG is first-line due to its osmotic mechanism and proven efficacy.','Option 2 is partially correct but misses the Rome IV timeline requirement. Option 3 incorrectly states pelvic floor dysfunction is most common and misrepresents diagnostic approach. Option 4 incorrectly advocates for extensive imaging and overstates magnesium hydroxide advantages. All distractors test common misconceptions about constipation management.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["Constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Constipation","constipation","pathophysiology","diagnosis","treatment"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-13T16:43:31.700Z','2026-10-13T16:43:31.700Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wxpkj00jdj6rmn8x909i9','mcq','A 62-year-old male with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 45 mL/min) presents with a persistent dry cough that has developed over the past 3 weeks. He was recently started on lisinopril 10 mg daily for his hypertension. Physical examination reveals blood pressure of 135/85 mmHg, no pulmonary rales, and no peripheral edema. Laboratory studies show serum potassium of 5.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL (baseline 1.6 mg/dL). Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this patient''s cough and the most appropriate next step in management?','["The cough is due to direct bronchial irritation from lisinopril; switch to an ARB like losartan","The cough is due to angiotensin II receptor blockade; continue lisinopril and add a cough suppressant","The cough is due to bradykinin accumulation from ACE inhibition; switch to an ARB like losartan","The cough is due to hyperkalemia from ACE inhibition; discontinue lisinopril and start a calcium channel blocker","The cough is due to renal dysfunction exacerbated by ACE inhibition; reduce the dose of lisinopril to 5 mg daily"]','The cough is due to bradykinin accumulation from ACE inhibition; switch to an ARB like losartan','ACE inhibitors cause cough through inhibition of bradykinin degradation, leading to bradykinin accumulation in the lungs. Bradykinin stimulates sensory nerve endings and prostaglandin release, causing the characteristic dry cough. This side effect occurs in 5-35% of patients and is a common reason for discontinuing ACE inhibitors. The patient''s symptoms (persistent dry cough, elevated potassium, and mild creatinine increase) are consistent with ACE inhibitor effects. The most appropriate management is to switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB), which provides similar cardiovascular and renal benefits without affecting bradykinin metabolism, thus avoiding the cough side effect.','ACE = Accumulate Bradykinin, Cough Effect','ACE inhibitor cough is mediated by bradykinin accumulation and is not dose-dependent; switching to an ARB is the preferred management strategy when this side effect occurs.','Option A is incorrect because ARBs do not cause cough through direct bronchial irritation. Option B is incorrect because ACE inhibitors, not ARBs, cause bradykinin-mediated cough. Option D is incorrect because while hyperkalemia can occur with ACE inhibitors, it''s not the cause of the cough. Option E is incorrect because reducing the dose won''t eliminate the bradykinin-mediated cough effect.','Medium','cardiovascular','pharmacology','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","pharmacology","ACE inhibitors","mechanism of action","side effects","clinical applications"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-13T16:46:20.083Z','2026-10-13T16:46:33.629Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wvhbm00j4j6rmuchych0q','mcq','A 2-week-old male infant presents with failure to pass meconium in the first 48 hours of life, progressive abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting. Physical examination reveals a distended abdomen with absent bowel sounds. A contrast enema shows a transition zone in the sigmoid colon. Which of the following statements regarding this condition is most accurate?','["The primary pathophysiological defect involves absence of ganglion cells in the affected bowel segment, leading to functional obstruction","The diagnosis is confirmed by barium enema alone, as it shows the characteristic ''transition zone'' with proximal dilatation","Initial management includes immediate surgical resection without any preoperative optimization","The Soave procedure is contraindicated due to its high rate of anastomotic complications"]','The primary pathophysiological defect involves absence of ganglion cells in the affected bowel segment, leading to functional obstruction','Hirschsprung disease is characterized by the absence of ganglion cells (aganglionosis) in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon, resulting in a functional obstruction due to unopposed sympathetic tone and lack of peristalsis. While contrast enema showing a transition zone is suggestive, the gold standard diagnosis requires rectal biopsy demonstrating the absence of ganglion cells. Initial management involves decompression with rectal tube, correction of fluid/electrolyte imbalances, and antibiotics if enterocolitis is present. The Soave procedure (endorectal pull-through) is a common surgical approach with acceptable complication rates when performed appropriately.','AGANGLIONOSIS = Absence of Ganglion cells leading to functional Obstruction','Hirschsprung disease can present at any age - not just newborns. Older children may present with chronic constipation, failure to thrive, or enterocolitis.','Option 2 is incorrect because barium enema is suggestive but not diagnostic - biopsy is required. Option 3 is incorrect because preoperative optimization is crucial to prevent complications. Option 4 is incorrect because the Soave procedure is a standard surgical approach with appropriate patient selection.','Medium','gastrointestinal','Hirschsprung Disease','["Hirschsprung Disease","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Hirschsprung Disease","pathophysiology","clinical presentation","diagnosis","management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-13T16:44:36.082Z','2026-10-13T16:46:52.633Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wyc5e00jmj6rm0km6pkry','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of constipation characterized by straining during defecation, lumpy stools, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation. She reports having fewer than 3 bowel movements per week. She has no red flag symptoms and a normal physical examination. According to current guidelines, which of the following statements regarding her condition is most accurate?','["Her symptoms meet Rome IV criteria for functional constipation and should be treated first with stimulant laxatives","Her symptoms meet Rome IV criteria for functional constipation and should be treated first with osmotic laxatives like polyethylene glycol","Her symptoms do not meet Rome IV criteria for functional constipation because she has fewer than 3 bowel movements per week","Her symptoms suggest slow transit constipation and require colonic transit testing before initiating treatment"]','Her symptoms meet Rome IV criteria for functional constipation and should be treated first with stimulant laxatives','The patient''s symptoms (straining during defecation, lumpy stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation, and fewer than 3 bowel movements per week for at least 6 months) meet the Rome IV criteria for functional constipation, which requires at least two of the following symptoms for at least 3 months in the last 6 months: straining during defecation, lumpy or hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation, sensation of anorectal obstruction/blockage, manual maneuvers to facilitate defecation, fewer than 3 bowel movements per week. Current guidelines recommend osmotic laxatives like polyethylene glycol as first-line treatment for functional constipation, not stimulant laxatives. The frequency of bowel movements (<3/week) is actually one of the diagnostic criteria, not a reason to exclude the diagnosis. While slow transit constipation is a consideration, colonic transit testing is not routinely recommended before initiating treatment in typical cases of functional constipation.','ROME criteria: 2+ symptoms for 3+ months in 6+ months; PEG first for functional constipation','Functional constipation diagnosis relies on Rome IV criteria requiring at least 2 symptoms for 3+ months in 6+ months. Osmotic laxatives (PEG) are first-line treatment, not stimulant laxatives.','Option 2 is incorrect because osmotic laxatives like polyethylene glycol are first-line, not stimulant laxatives. Option 3 is incorrect because fewer than 3 bowel movements per week is actually one of the Rome IV diagnostic criteria. Option 4 is incorrect because colonic transit testing is not routinely required before initiating treatment in typical functional constipation cases.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','constipation','["Constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation","rome criteria","functional constipation","laxatives","diagnosis"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0.25,1,4,'2026-10-13T16:46:49.346Z','2026-10-13T16:58:45.213Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wm1vc0007bnrmy2rtpx6r','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes is started on lisinopril. Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism of action of ACE inhibitors and explains their most common side effect?','["Competitive antagonism of angiotensin II receptors, leading to increased bradykinin levels and dry cough","Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme, leading to decreased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin levels","Direct vasodilation through calcium channel blockade, causing hypotension and reflex tachycardia","Inhibition of aldosterone synthesis, resulting in potassium retention and metabolic acidosis"]','Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme, leading to decreased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin levels','ACE inhibitors work by inhibiting the angiotensin-converting enzyme, which converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II and also breaks down bradykinin. This results in decreased angiotensin II levels (reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone release) and increased bradykinin levels (causing vasodilation). The increased bradykinin is responsible for the most common side effect - a dry cough affecting 10-20% of patients. This mechanism also explains why ACE inhibitors are beneficial in heart failure (reducing afterload) and in diabetic patients (protective renal effects).','ACE = Angiotensin Converting Enzyme → Less Ang II, More Bradykinin → Cough','ACE inhibitors are first-line for hypertension with diabetes or heart failure due to their renal protective effects, but the characteristic dry cough may lead to switching to ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) which don''t affect bradykinin.','Option 1 incorrectly describes ACE inhibitors as angiotensin II receptor antagonists (that''s ARBs). Option 3 describes calcium channel blockers, not ACE inhibitors. Option 4 incorrectly describes aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone), not ACE inhibitors.','Medium','Pharmacology','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","mechanism of action","side effects","clinical applications","cardiovascular"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-13T16:37:16.152Z','2026-10-14T13:33:30.093Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6wmnme000gbnrmgrsn02se','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by infrequent bowel movements (every 5-7 days), straining during defecation, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation. She reports no red flag symptoms. Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology and treatment approach for her condition is most accurate?','["Her condition is most likely due to slow transit constipation caused by abnormalities in enteric nerve function, and first-line treatment should include stimulant laxatives like bisacodyl","Her symptoms are consistent with outlet obstruction defecation disorder, and initial treatment should focus on osmotic laxatives like polyethylene glycol","Her constipation is primarily due to decreased colonic motility, and treatment should prioritize bulk-forming laxatives with adequate fluid intake","Her condition represents a motility disorder requiring prokinetic agents as first-line therapy, as stimulant laxatives carry significant long-term risks"]','B','The patient''s symptoms (infrequent bowel movements, straining, sensation of incomplete evacuation) are most consistent with outlet obstruction defecation disorder (OBD), which involves impaired coordination of defecation muscles leading to impaired relaxation or paradoxical contraction of the anus (Vlismas et al., 2024). OBD is distinct from slow transit constipation, which involves prolonged colonic transit time due to reduced motility of the large intestine caused by enteric nerve abnormalities (Better Health Victoria). 

Osmotic laxatives like polyethylene glycol are appropriate first-line treatment for OBD as they soften stool and facilitate easier passage without directly stimulating bowel motility. Stimulant laxatives (option A) are generally avoided as first-line due to concerns about potential dependency and abdominal cramping with long-term use (Portalatin et al., 2012). Bulk-forming laxatives (option C) can worsen symptoms in OBD by increasing stool bulk without addressing the underlying coordination issue. Prokinetic agents (option D) are not standard treatment for constipation and are typically used for gastroparesis.','OBD = Outlet obstruction defecation disorder → Osmotic laxatives first','Outlet obstruction defecation disorder presents with straining and incomplete evacuation sensation, distinct from slow transit constipation. Osmotic laxatives are preferred over stimulants as first-line treatment to avoid potential dependency.','{"A": "Incorrect because stimulant laxatives are not first-line for outlet obstruction and carry risks of dependency and cramping with chronic use", "C": "Incorrect because bulk-forming laxatives can worsen symptoms in outlet obstruction by increasing stool bulk without addressing coordination issues", "D": "Incorrect because prokinetic agents are not standard treatment for constipation and are typically reserved for gastroparesis"}','Medium','Gastrointestinal','constipation pathophysiology and treatment','["Constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation pathophysiology and treatment","constipation","pathophysiology","treatment","outlet obstruction"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-13T16:37:44.342Z','2026-10-14T13:33:48.366Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6xkr1v00hgxurmmeadi8z0','mcq','A 3-year-old child presents with microcytic anemia (MCV 70 fL), fatigue, and irritability. Laboratory findings show low serum ferritin (8 ng/mL), low transferrin saturation (4%), and elevated total iron-binding capacity (450 μg/dL). The child lives in an older home built in the 1950s. Which of the following statements regarding the relationship between iron deficiency and lead poisoning is most accurate?','["Iron deficiency protects against lead absorption by reducing intestinal uptake","Iron deficiency increases lead absorption from the gut due to enhanced uptake mechanisms","Lead poisoning and iron deficiency are mutually exclusive conditions in children","Treatment of iron deficiency should be delayed until lead levels are normalized","Lead poisoning causes microcytic anemia through direct inhibition of heme synthesis"]','Iron deficiency increases lead absorption from the gut due to enhanced uptake mechanisms','The child''s presentation is classic for iron deficiency anemia (low ferritin, low transferrin saturation, elevated TIBC, microcytic indices). The living situation suggests potential lead exposure. Research shows that children with iron deficiency absorb lead from the gut more ''efficiently'' than those with adequate iron stores due to upregulated iron absorption mechanisms that also transport lead. This creates a dangerous synergy where iron deficiency increases susceptibility to lead poisoning while lead poisoning can exacerbate iron deficiency.','IDA → ↑ Lead absorption (IDA = Iron Deficiency Anemia)','In children with iron deficiency living in older housing, always screen for lead exposure as iron deficiency increases lead absorption risk by up to 2-3 fold.','Option A is incorrect because iron deficiency actually increases lead absorption. Option C is incorrect as both conditions can coexist. Option D is incorrect as treating iron deficiency should not be delayed. Option E is incorrect as lead poisoning typically causes normocytic anemia, not microcytic.','Medium','General','anemia','["anemia","General","deep-agent","anemia","iron deficiency","lead poisoning","pediatrics","microcytic anemia"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,2,'2026-10-13T17:04:15.092Z','2026-10-14T17:31:16.566Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sq4000fder8tx2qxkg7','mcq','Neural pattern is rapid during:','["First year of life"]','First year of life','<p><strong>Why First year of life is correct:</strong> The first year of life represents a period of explosive neural development, with synaptogenesis peaking around 2-6 months of age. During this time, the brain creates approximately 1 million new neural connections per second, driven by genetic programming and environmental stimulation. This rapid neural patterning is essential for acquiring fundamental skills like motor control, language acquisition, and social attachment, forming the foundation for all subsequent cognitive development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Other years of childhood:</strong> While neural development continues throughout childhood, the rate of synaptogenesis and neural plasticity is significantly lower after the first year, with pruning becoming more dominant. - <strong>Adolescence:</strong> The adolescent brain undergoes significant remodeling, particularly in the prefrontal cortex, but this involves more synaptic pruning and myelination rather than the rapid pattern formation seen in infancy. - <strong>Adulthood:</strong> Neural development in adulthood is characterized by more limited plasticity, primarily focused on learning specific skills rather than the widespread pattern formation of infancy.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The first year represents the period of greatest neural plasticity, with synaptogenesis exceeding synaptic pruning. - Critical periods for sensory and language development coincide with this rapid neural patterning phase. - Disruptions during this time (malnutrition, toxins, neglect) can have disproportionate and lasting effects on brain development.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The first year of life is the period of most rapid neural patterning due to peak synaptogenesis, establishing the foundation for all future development.</p>','<p>RAPID: Rapid neural development occurs in the first year of life, forming the foundation for all future learning.</p>','<p>Early environmental enrichment during the first year can significantly enhance neural development, while deprivation can cause irreversible deficits due to this critical period of heightened plasticity.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Other years of childhood:</strong> Neural development continues at a slower pace after infancy with more pruning than formation. - <strong>Adolescence:</strong> Involves significant brain remodeling but focuses more on pruning and myelination than rapid pattern formation. - <strong>Adulthood:</strong> Characterized by limited plasticity and learning specific skills rather than widespread neural patterning.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Growth & Development','Neural Development','["neural","development","rapid","first year"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.260Z','2026-10-18T14:41:47.516Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sq6000gder8vvj4166v','mcq','Salem is 4-month old infant, His mother asked about his growth','["Doubles birth weight at 4th month and doubles birth length at 4th year"]','Doubles birth weight at 4th month and doubles birth length at 4th year','<p><strong>Why Doubles birth weight at 4th month and doubles birth length at 4th year is correct:</strong> This statement accurately reflects normal infant growth patterns. During the first 4-6 months of life, infants typically double their birth weight due to rapid cell division and increased nutritional demands. The weight doubling occurs because of the high metabolic rate needed for brain development, organ growth, and overall tissue accretion during this period. For length, infants generally double their birth length by approximately 4 years of age, which represents a more gradual linear growth pattern compared to the rapid weight gain in infancy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Not provided:</strong> Since this is a true/false question format with only one correct option, there are no other specific options to analyze.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Infants typically double their birth weight by 4-6 months and triple it by 1 year of age. - Length doubling occurs more gradually, typically by 4 years of age. - Growth velocity is fastest in the first 6 months for weight and the first 2 years for length.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember that infants double their birth weight by 4-6 months and double their birth length by 4 years as fundamental milestones of normal growth.</p>','<p>Weight doubles by 5 months, length doubles by 4 years (W5L4)</p>','<p>Monitoring growth velocity is often more important than single measurements, as deviations from established growth percentiles can indicate underlying medical issues.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Doubles birth weight at 4th year and doubles birth length at 4th month:</strong> Reverses the correct timing for weight and length doubling milestones.</li>
<li><strong>Triples birth weight at 4th month and triples birth length at 4th year:</strong> Overestimates growth velocity for both weight and length milestones.</li>
<li><strong>Quadruples birth weight at 4th month and quadruples birth length at 4th year:</strong> Significantly overestimates growth velocity and incorrectly applies both milestones to the same timeframe.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Growth Milestones','["growth","infant","weight","length"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.262Z','2026-10-18T14:48:13.008Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sq9000hder86xelpphz','mcq','Female full term, Head circumference at birth is:','["35 cm"]','35 cm','<p><strong>Why 35 cm is correct:</strong> The average head circumference at birth for a full-term female infant is approximately 35 cm, which falls within the normal range of 33-36 cm. This measurement is taken at the largest occipitofrontal circumference, typically just above the eyebrows and around the most prominent part of the occiput. The head circumference is crucial for assessing normal brain development and detecting potential abnormalities such as microcephaly or macrocephaly. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided in the question, so this section is not applicable.</strong> <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Head circumference should be measured at every well-child visit during the first year of life, with rapid growth occurring in the first few months. - Normal head circumference at birth is approximately 33-36 cm for both sexes, with males typically having slightly larger heads than females. - Abnormal head circumference measurements may indicate neurological disorders, genetic syndromes, or metabolic conditions. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The average head circumference at birth for a full-term female infant is 35 cm, a critical measurement for assessing normal brain development.</p>','','<p>Head circumference measurements should always be plotted on growth charts, as deviations from the expected percentile can be early indicators of neurological or developmental issues.</p>','','Easy','Growth & Development','Anthropometry','["head circumference","birth","term infant"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.265Z','2026-10-18T14:53:18.641Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmve7xztt000mdbucqex610cb','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation for the past 6 months. She reports infrequent bowel movements (every 3-4 days), straining during defecation, and a sense of incomplete evacuation. Physical examination is normal. Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology and management of her condition is most accurate?','["Slow transit constipation is characterized by impaired coordination of pelvic floor muscles during defecation and is best managed with stimulant laxatives like bisacodyl","Outlet obstruction constipation involves reduced colonic motility due to enteric nerve abnormalities and should be treated first with polyethylene glycol","Chronic idiopathic constipation can be categorized as either slow transit (reduced colonic motility) or outlet obstruction (impaired evacuation), with polyethylene glycol being first-line therapy for both subtypes","Digital rectal examination is not necessary in the evaluation of chronic constipation as it does not differentiate between slow transit and outlet obstruction subtypes"]','Chronic idiopathic constipation can be categorized as either slow transit (reduced colonic motility) or outlet obstruction (impaired evacuation), with polyethylene glycol being first-line therapy for both subtypes','Chronic idiopathic constipation is classified into two main pathophysiologic subtypes: slow transit constipation (reduced colonic motility due to enteric nerve abnormalities) and outlet obstruction/dyssynergic defecation (impaired coordination of pelvic floor muscles during defecation). Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is considered first-line therapy for both subtypes as it increases stool water content and promotes regular bowel movements through osmotic action. Stimulant laxatives like bisacodyl are generally second-line agents. Digital rectal examination is crucial in evaluation as it can help identify outlet obstruction by assessing pelvic floor function and anorectal anatomy.','SLOW-PEG: Slow transit colonic motility + Outlet obstruction = PEG first-line','Always perform digital rectal examination in constipation evaluation - it can identify outlet obstruction and guide management decisions.','Option 1 incorrectly suggests stimulant laxatives are first-line for outlet obstruction (PEG is preferred). Option 2 reverses the pathophysiology (slow transit involves reduced motility, outlet obstruction involves impaired evacuation). Option 3 incorrectly states DRE is unnecessary when it''s actually crucial for subtype differentiation.','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation','["Constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation","pathophysiology","evaluation","management","chronic idiopathic constipation"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-18T19:28:52.385Z','2026-10-18T19:28:52.385Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqe000lder8z4nfu9lw','mcq','Normally the newborn loss 5-10% of weight during 1st 3-4 days and regained by','["10th day"]','10th day','<p><strong>Why 10th day is correct:</strong> Newborns typically lose 5-10% of their birth weight during the first 3-4 days due to fluid loss, meconium passage, and initial limited intake. By the 10th day, they have regained this lost weight as their feeding patterns establish, fluid balance normalizes, and they begin gaining weight at a rate of approximately 20-30 grams per day. This physiological weight loss followed by regain is a normal adaptation to extrauterine life and reflects adequate hydration and feeding establishment.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Other days (not specified):</strong> The question specifically asks for the day when weight is regained, which is typically the 10th day, not earlier or later days.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Normal newborn weight loss occurs in the first 3-4 days, with maximum loss by day 3-4.</li>
<li>Weight should be regained by day 10, after which newborns should gain approximately 20-30 grams daily.</li>
<li>Failure to regain birth weight by day 10 may indicate inadequate feeding or other medical issues.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Newborns lose 5-10% of birth weight in the first 3-4 days and typically regain it by the 10th day as they adapt to extrauterine life.</p>','','<p>Monitoring newborn weight patterns is crucial for identifying feeding issues and ensuring adequate nutrition in the early days of life.</p>','','Easy','Growth & Development','Newborn Weight','["newborn","weight loss","regain","physiological"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.270Z','2026-10-18T19:27:12.317Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdf003gqjlr59iomdiq','mcq','Which of the following is not diagnostic for malabsorption ?','["Low serum albumin","Reducing substance in stool","Stool ova","Stool culture"]','Stool culture','<p><strong>Why Stool culture is correct:</strong> Stool culture is not diagnostic for malabsorption because it primarily identifies infectious pathogens (bacteria, parasites) causing diarrhea rather than assessing the digestive and absorptive functions of the gastrointestinal tract. Malabsorption involves impaired breakdown or absorption of nutrients due to conditions like celiac disease, pancreatic insufficiency, or small bowel resection, which would be evaluated through tests like fecal fat analysis, D-xylose absorption test, or breath tests. Stool culture is specifically designed to detect infectious etiologies of diarrhea, not to evaluate the absorptive capacity of the gut.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low serum albumin:</strong> This is a classic indicator of malabsorption as albumin is a protein that requires proper digestion and absorption in the small intestine.</li>
<li><strong>Reducing substance in stool:</strong> This indicates carbohydrate malabsorption, commonly seen in conditions like lactase deficiency or disaccharidase deficiencies.</li>
<li><strong>Stool ova:</strong> The presence of ova and parasites in stool can indicate parasitic infections (like giardiasis) that directly impair absorption in the small intestine.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Malabsorption testing focuses on evaluating digestive and absorptive functions, not infectious causes.</li>
<li>Fecal fat analysis is the gold standard for diagnosing fat malabsorption.</li>
<li>Celiac disease, a common cause of malabsorption, is diagnosed through serology and biopsy, not stool culture.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Stool culture identifies infectious causes of diarrhea, not malabsorption, which requires specific tests to evaluate nutrient absorption.</p>','<p>MALAB: Malabsorption tests assess Lipid, Amylase, B12, and fat Absorption (not Culture).</p>','<p>Malabsorption should be suspected in patients with chronic diarrhea, weight loss, and nutritional deficiencies despite adequate oral intake.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Low serum albumin:</strong> Indicates protein malabsorption due to impaired intestinal function.</li>
<li><strong>Reducing substance in stool:</strong> Suggests carbohydrate malabsorption from disaccharidase deficiencies.</li>
<li><strong>Stool ova:</strong> Indicates parasitic infections that directly damage the intestinal mucosa and impair absorption.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Malabsorption','["malabsorption","diagnostic","stool"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.827Z','2026-10-18T19:41:34.859Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqf000mder8bnxzer8q','mcq','Normally the newborn loss 5-10% of weight during 1st 3-4 days and regain how much grams every month','["750 gm in 1st 4th months"]','750 gm in 1st 4th months','<p><strong>Why 750 gm in 1st 4th months is correct:</strong> During the first 4 months of life, infants typically gain weight at a rate of approximately 150-200 grams per week, which accumulates to about 750 grams per month. This rapid growth rate reflects the high metabolic demands and developmental spurts occurring during infancy, particularly in the first few months after birth. The weight gain follows a predictable pattern that healthcare providers use to monitor normal development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Other amounts or timeframes:</strong> The question specifically asks for the amount gained per month during the first 4 months, which is approximately 750 grams, not other amounts or different timeframes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Infants typically gain 150-200 grams per week during the first 4 months, accumulating to about 750 grams monthly.</li>
<li>This rapid growth rate gradually slows after 4 months as the infant becomes more mobile.</li>
<li>Weight gain patterns are important indicators of adequate nutrition and overall health in infancy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Infants gain approximately 750 grams per month during the first 4 months of life, reflecting their rapid growth phase.</p>','','<p>Consistent weight gain is a critical indicator of infant health; deviations from expected patterns warrant further evaluation.</p>','','Basic','Growth & Development','Newborn Weight Gain','["newborn","weight gain","monthly"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.271Z','2026-10-18T19:27:12.319Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqh000nder89hxuvl2z','mcq','Skull and neural growth are at highest growth at','["1st year","3-5 years","4-5 years","6 years"]','1st year','<p><strong>Why 1st year is correct:</strong> The skull and neural system experience their most rapid growth during the first year of life, with approximately 50% of brain growth occurring by 6 months and 80% by the first year. This period corresponds to critical brain development milestones, including synaptogenesis and myelination, which are essential for neurological function. The skull must expand rapidly to accommodate this brain growth, with the head circumference increasing by approximately 12 cm during the first year.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3-5 years:</strong> While significant growth continues during this period, it is not as rapid as during the first year.</li>
<li><strong>4-5 years:</strong> Growth during this period is slower compared to the first year of life.</li>
<li><strong>6 years:</strong> By this age, the majority of skull and neural growth has already occurred, with growth rates significantly decreased.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The first year of life is the period of most rapid skull and neural growth, with 80% of brain growth occurring by 12 months.</li>
<li>Head circumference measurements are crucial during infancy to monitor for normal brain development.</li>
<li>Failure of head circumference to follow the expected percentile curve may indicate neurological issues.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The skull and neural system grow most rapidly during the first year of life, with 80% of brain growth occurring by 12 months.</p>','<p>First year brain growth is fastest: 50% by 6 months, 80% by 12 months.</p>','<p>Monitoring head circumference during the first year is critical for detecting potential developmental issues early.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>3-5 years:</strong> Growth during this period is significant but not as rapid as during the first year.</li>
<li><strong>4-5 years:</strong> This timeframe represents a slower growth period compared to infancy.</li>
<li><strong>6 years:</strong> By this age, the majority of skull and neural growth has already occurred.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Neural Development','["skull","neural","growth","peak","infancy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.273Z','2026-10-18T19:27:12.320Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr80017der8m3f44obl','mcq','Which one of the following is a cause of delayed sitting','["myopathy","thyrotoxicosis","anemia","blindness"]','myopathy','<p><strong>Why myopathy is correct:</strong> Myopathy refers to a primary disorder of muscle tissue that causes weakness and hypotonia, directly impairing the infant''s ability to develop the muscle strength required for sitting independently. Sitting requires adequate muscle tone, particularly in the trunk and hip muscles, to maintain an upright position against gravity. Myopathies cause progressive muscle weakness due to intrinsic muscle fiber dysfunction, which would delay the achievement of motor milestones like sitting, typically expected around 6-9 months of age.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Thyrotoxicosis:</strong> This condition causes hypermetabolism and increased muscle activity, potentially leading to irritability rather than motor delay; it would not cause delayed sitting.</li>
<li><strong>Anemia:</strong> While significant anemia can cause fatigue and poor feeding, it does not directly impair the muscle strength required for sitting.</li>
<li><strong>Blindness:</strong> Visual impairment primarily affects sensory development and may delay some milestones, but it does not directly cause the muscle weakness required to delay sitting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Myopathies typically present with proximal muscle weakness and hypotonia, affecting motor milestones like sitting, crawling, and walking.</li>
<li>Sitting is typically achieved between 6-9 months of age; persistent inability to sit by 12 months warrants evaluation for neuromuscular disorders.</li>
<li>Hypotonia is a common finding in myopathies and can be distinguished from central hypotonia by the pattern of weakness and reflexes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Myopathy causes delayed sitting because it directly impairs muscle strength and tone required for maintaining an upright position.</p>','<p>Myopathy Makes Muscles Move poorly, delaying Motor milestones like sitting.</p>','<p>Persistent hypotonia with delayed sitting should raise suspicion for neuromuscular disorders like myopathy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Thyrotoxicosis:</strong> This condition causes increased muscle activity, not weakness.</li>
<li><strong>Anemia:</strong> Significant anemia may cause fatigue but doesn''t directly impair muscle strength for sitting.</li>
<li><strong>Blindness:</strong> Visual impairment affects sensory development but not the muscle strength needed for sitting.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Motor Development','["sitting","delayed","motor","myopathy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.300Z','2026-10-18T19:28:39.408Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmv6xkyql00hhxurmbe63ro78','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue and pallor. Her complete blood count shows: hemoglobin 9.5 g/dL, hematocrit 28%, MCV 85 fL, MCH 27 pg, MCHC 32 g/dL, reticulocyte count 1.5%, and platelet count 250,000/μL. Which of the following statements regarding the classification and diagnostic approach to this patient''s anemia is most accurate?','["This is a normocytic anemia requiring evaluation of bone marrow function","The MCV indicates this is a macrocytic anemia requiring vitamin B12 and folate testing","The reticulocyte count suggests this is a hyporegenerative anemia requiring iron studies","The MCHC indicates this is a hypochromic anemia requiring immediate transfusion","The platelet count rules out any underlying hematologic malignancy"]','The reticulocyte count suggests this is a hyporegenerative anemia requiring iron studies','The patient has normocytic anemia (MCV 85 fL is within normal range). The reticulocyte count of 1.5% is inappropriately low for the degree of anemia, indicating a hyporegenerative state. This suggests decreased production rather than increased destruction or loss. Given the normocytic indices and low reticulocyte count, the diagnostic approach should focus on evaluating for iron deficiency (which can present as normocytic early on), anemia of chronic disease, or bone marrow disorders. Iron studies (ferritin, transferrin saturation) would be appropriate first-line tests.','Low reticulocyte → Hyporegenerative → Evaluate production','In normocytic anemia, always check the reticulocyte count first - a low reticulocyte count (<2-3%) indicates decreased production and directs evaluation toward iron studies, chronic disease, or bone marrow disorders.','Option A is incorrect because the MCV is normal, not normocytic in the sense of requiring bone marrow evaluation. Option B is incorrect as MCV is normal, not macrocytic. Option D is incorrect as MCHC is normal, not hypochromic. Option E is incorrect as normal platelets don''t rule out malignancy.','Medium','General','anemia','["anemia","General","deep-agent","anemia","anemia classification","normocytic anemia","reticulocyte count","diagnostic approach"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,3,'2026-10-13T17:04:25.053Z','2026-10-18T19:27:23.529Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzw000aqjlrmoy2a4id','mcq','Which of the following is considered an absolute maternal contraindication to breast feeding?','["Maternal ttt with cytotoxic drugs.","Chronic maternal illness.","Acute maternal illness until controlled","Pregnancy after 20th week."]','Maternal ttt with cytotoxic drugs.','<p><strong>Why Maternal ttt with cytotoxic drugs is correct:</strong> Cytotoxic drugs, such as chemotherapy agents, are excreted in breast milk and can cause significant harm to the infant, including bone marrow suppression, immunosuppression, and potential long-term developmental issues. These drugs are designed to rapidly dividing cells, which includes not only cancer cells but also the infant''s developing tissues. The American Academy of Pediatrics considers maternal chemotherapy an absolute contraindication to breastfeeding due to the significant risk of toxicity to the nursing infant. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Chronic maternal illness:</strong> Many chronic illnesses (like well-controlled diabetes or hypertension) do not contraindicate breastfeeding; the key is whether the condition or its treatments pose a risk to the infant. - <strong>Acute maternal illness until controlled:</strong> Most acute illnesses (like infections) are not contraindications once the mother is stable and not contagious; breastfeeding often provides protective antibodies to the infant. - <strong>Pregnancy after 20th week:</strong> Pregnancy itself is not a contraindication to breastfeeding; many women continue to nurse throughout a subsequent pregnancy. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Cytotoxic drugs, radioactive compounds, and certain antimetabolites are absolute contraindications to breastfeeding due to excretion in milk and infant toxicity. - Most medications are compatible with breastfeeding, with only a small percentage requiring temporary discontinuation. - The risk-benefit analysis must consider the benefits of breastfeeding against the potential harm from maternal medications or illnesses. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Only medications that are toxic to infants (like cytotoxic drugs) are absolute contraindications to breastfeeding, while most maternal illnesses and medications are compatible with proper management.</p>','<p>Cytotoxic drugs = Contraindicated (remember ''C-C'' for Cytotoxic-Contraindicated)</p>','<p>When counseling mothers on breastfeeding safety, always check the most current LactMed database for medication compatibility rather than relying on outdated contraindications.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Chronic maternal illness:</strong> Most chronic illnesses do not contraindicate breastfeeding if well-controlled. - <strong>Acute maternal illness until controlled:</strong> Once stable, most acute illnesses do not prevent breastfeeding. - <strong>Pregnancy after 20th week:</strong> Pregnancy itself is not a contraindication to continued breastfeeding.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["contraindication","cytotoxic","maternal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.340Z','2026-10-18T19:29:11.269Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sql000pder8yt8muun6','mcq','Child use tricycle, 6 cubes. His age is:','["36 Months"]','36 Months','<p><strong>Why 36 Months is correct:</strong> By 36 months (3 years of age), children have developed sufficient fine motor coordination, balance, and cognitive understanding to successfully use a tricycle and stack 6 cubes. Tricycle riding requires coordinated pedaling, steering, and balancing skills that emerge around this age. The ability to stack 6 cubes indicates advanced fine motor control and demonstrates the pincer grasp development that progresses from stacking 2 cubes at 12 months, 4 cubes at 24 months, to 6-8 cubes by 36 months.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. 36 Months:</strong> This is correct, so no explanation needed.</li>
<li><strong>B. 24 Months:</strong> At 24 months, children can typically stack 4 cubes but lack the coordination and balance needed for tricycle riding.</li>
<li><strong>C. 18 Months:</strong> At 18 months, children can stack 3-4 cubes but cannot pedal a tricycle effectively.</li>
<li><strong>D. 12 Months:</strong> At 12 months, children can stack 2 cubes and may begin to walk but lack the motor skills for tricycle use.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fine motor development follows a predictable progression in stacking ability: 2 cubes at 12 months, 4 cubes at 24 months, 6-8 cubes at 36 months.</li>
<li>Tricycle riding requires integration of multiple skills including balance, coordination, and understanding of cause-and-effect.</li>
<li>The 36-month mark represents significant advancement in both gross and fine motor skills that prepare children for more complex activities.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The combination of tricycle use and stacking 6 cubes is a classic developmental marker for the 36-month-old child.</p>','<p>3-6-3: 3 years old, 6 cubes stacked, 3-wheeled tricycle</p>','<p>When assessing fine motor skills, the number of blocks a child can stack provides a quick and reliable developmental indicator.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>24 Months:</strong> At 24 months, children can stack 4 cubes but lack coordination for tricycle riding.</li>
<li><strong>18 Months:</strong> At 18 months, children can stack 3-4 cubes but cannot pedal effectively.</li>
<li><strong>12 Months:</strong> At 12 months, children can stack 2 cubes but cannot use a tricycle.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Motor Development','["motor","development","tricycle","cubes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.277Z','2026-10-18T19:27:28.175Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqn000qder8rcb4wbh2','mcq','blinks to flashing lights, fixate light in line of vision:','["At birth"]','At birth','<p><strong>Why At birth is correct:</strong> The ability to blink in response to flashing lights and to fixate on a light in the line of vision are present at birth, representing primitive visual reflexes that indicate basic neurological function. The blink reflex to bright light is a brainstem-mediated protective response that is fully developed at birth. Visual fixation, while initially brief and poorly coordinated, is present from birth and improves significantly in the first months of life as the visual pathways mature.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. At birth:</strong> This is correct, so no explanation needed.</li>
<li><strong>B. 1 month:</strong> While visual fixation improves at 1 month, the basic ability to blink to light and fixate is already present at birth.</li>
<li><strong>C. 3 months:</strong> By 3 months, visual fixation is more sustained and following begins, but these basic reflexes were present at birth.</li>
<li><strong>D. 6 months:</strong> By 6 months, vision is much more developed with depth perception and hand-eye coordination, but these primitive reflexes were present from birth.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Visual reflexes (blinking to light, fixation) are among the earliest neurological functions to develop and are present at birth.</li>
<li>The blink reflex is mediated by the brainstem and represents a protective mechanism.</li>
<li>While present at birth, visual fixation improves dramatically in the first 6 months of life as the visual cortex and connections mature.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The blink reflex to light and visual fixation are primitive neurological responses that are fully present at birth, serving as early indicators of basic visual function.</p>','<p>BFF: Birth Flash Fixation - these visual reflexes are present at birth</p>','<p>Absence of the blink reflex to light in a newborn can indicate significant neurological impairment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>1 month:</strong> Visual fixation improves at 1 month, but these reflexes are present at birth.</li>
<li><strong>3 months:</strong> By 3 months, vision is more developed, but these basic reflexes were present at birth.</li>
<li><strong>6 months:</strong> By 6 months, vision is much more developed, but these reflexes were present from birth.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Growth & Development','Visual Development','["vision","reflex","newborn"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.279Z','2026-10-18T19:27:28.176Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmve7w88h0008dbucynj81u60','mcq','A 58-year-old male with hypertension and type 2 diabetes is started on lisinopril. Two weeks later, he develops a persistent dry cough. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this side effect?','["Direct irritation of the respiratory tract by the drug","Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition","Increased angiotensin II levels causing bronchoconstriction","Hypersensitivity reaction to the sulfhydryl group in the drug","Renin-angiotensin system activation compensatory mechanism"]','Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition','The persistent dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is primarily due to the accumulation of bradykinin. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) normally degrades bradykinin, a potent inflammatory mediator. When ACE is inhibited, bradykinin accumulates and stimulates sensory nerves in the respiratory tract, leading to cough. This is the most common side effect of ACE inhibitors, occurring in 5-35% of patients. The cough is typically dry, persistent, and non-productive, and often requires discontinuation of the medication. Option A is incorrect because ACE inhibitors don''t directly irritate the respiratory tract. Option C is incorrect because ACE inhibitors decrease angiotensin II levels. Option D is incorrect because lisinopril doesn''t contain a sulfhydryl group (this is more characteristic of ACE inhibitors like captopril). Option E is incorrect because ACE inhibition suppresses, not activates, the renin-angiotensin system.','ACE Inhibitors → Bradykinin Buildup → B for Best explanation of cough','ACE inhibitor-induced cough is bradykinin-mediated, not due to sulfhydryl groups (more common with captopril), and is the most common reason for discontinuing these medications.','Option A (direct irritation) is a common misconception but incorrect. Option C (increased angiotensin II) is the opposite of what happens with ACE inhibition. Option D (sulfhydryl group) is specific to certain ACE inhibitors like captopril, not lisinopril. Option E (RAS activation) is incorrect as ACE inhibition suppresses the system.','Medium','cardiovascular','pharmacology','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","pharmacology","ACE inhibitors","side effects","mechanism","bradykinin"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-18T19:27:29.969Z','2026-10-18T19:27:29.969Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqr000uder85zpw99i6','mcq','At what age the child doubles his birth length?','["1 year","2 years","3 years","4 years"]','1 year','<p><strong>Why 1 year is correct:</strong> By 12 months of age, a child typically doubles their birth length due to rapid linear growth during infancy. This milestone reflects the significant influence of growth hormone and adequate nutrition during the first year of life. The average birth length of 50 cm reaches approximately 100 cm by 12 months, representing a fundamental growth parameter that pediatricians closely monitor. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>2 years:</strong> By 24 months, children typically triple their birth weight, not double their birth length. - <strong>3 years:</strong> At 3 years, growth slows considerably, and children are typically about 4 times their birth weight, not double their birth length. - <strong>4 years:</strong> By age 4, children have usually reached about 50% of their adult height, not doubled their birth length. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Birth length typically doubles by 12 months and triples by 24 months. - Growth velocity decreases after the first year, with growth spurts occurring during adolescence. - Growth charts are essential tools for monitoring normal growth patterns and identifying potential growth disorders. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Children double their birth length by 1 year and triple their birth weight by 2 years.</p>','<p>Double length by 1, triple weight by 2</p>','<p>Growth parameters should be plotted on standardized growth curves at every well-child visit to detect deviations from normal patterns early.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>2 years:</strong> By 2 years, children triple their birth weight, not double their birth length. - <strong>3 years:</strong> Growth slows significantly by 3 years, and children are typically 4 times their birth weight. - <strong>4 years:</strong> By age 4, children have reached about 50% of their adult height, not doubled birth length.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Growth Milestones','["growth","length","doubling","milestone"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.283Z','2026-10-18T19:27:41.520Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7squ000wder8nl8bi08m','mcq','Which one of the following is not a sign of social development?','["Social smile at 2 months age.","No stranger anxiety with unfamiliar adults at 12 months.","Puts subjects in mouth at 6 months.","Waves bye-bye at age of 10 months."]','No stranger anxiety with unfamiliar adults at 12 months.','<p><strong>Why No stranger anxiety with unfamiliar adults at 12 months is correct:</strong> Stranger anxiety typically emerges between 8-10 months of age, peaks around 12-15 months, and then gradually decreases. The absence of stranger anxiety at 12 months is abnormal as it indicates a lack of normal social-emotional development or possible attachment issues. This absence may suggest developmental disorders, autism spectrum disorder, or atypical caregiver-child interactions. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Social smile at 2 months age:</strong> This is a normal social developmental milestone appearing between 6-8 weeks. - <strong>Puts subjects in mouth at 6 months:</strong> This represents normal oral exploration and sensorimotor development expected at this age. - <strong>Waves bye-bye at age of 10 months:</strong> This is a normal social gesture that typically emerges between 9-12 months. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Stranger anxiety is a normal developmental stage indicating object permanence and attachment formation. - Social development follows a predictable sequence: social smile, stranger anxiety, separation anxiety, and then decreasing anxiety with familiar caregivers. - Abnormal social development may be an early sign of autism spectrum disorder or other neurodevelopmental conditions. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Stranger anxiety should be present by 8-10 months and peak at 12-15 months; its absence at 12 months is abnormal.</p>','<p>Smile at 2 months, fear strangers at 8 months, wave bye at 10 months</p>','<p>Social and emotional milestones are just as important as cognitive and motor milestones in assessing child development; delays in social interaction may be early indicators of neurodevelopmental disorders.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Social smile at 2 months age:</strong> This is a normal social developmental milestone appearing between 6-8 weeks. - <strong>Puts subjects in mouth at 6 months:</strong> This represents normal oral exploration and sensorimotor development expected at this age. - <strong>Waves bye-bye at age of 10 months:</strong> This is a normal social gesture that typically emerges between 9-12 months.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Social Development','["social","development","milestone","anxiety"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.286Z','2026-10-18T19:27:41.522Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmve7wuai000fdbucizatfabh','mcq','A 3-year-old child presents with bowing of the legs, delayed fontanelle closure, and growth retardation. Laboratory findings show low serum calcium (7.5 mg/dL), low serum phosphate (3.0 mg/dL), and elevated alkaline phosphatase (450 U/L). X-rays reveal widened epiphyseal plates and fraying of the metaphyses. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology and appropriate treatment for this condition?','["Impaired osteoblast function due to parathyroid hormone excess; treatment involves calcium supplementation alone","Defective mineralization of osteoid due to vitamin D deficiency; treatment requires high-dose vitamin D and calcium supplementation","Increased bone resorption from hypercalcemia; treatment involves bisphosphonate therapy","Abnormal collagen synthesis due to genetic mutation; treatment requires surgical correction of deformities"]','Defective mineralization of osteoid due to vitamin D deficiency; treatment requires high-dose vitamin D and calcium supplementation','This case describes classic rickets, characterized by defective mineralization of osteoid due to vitamin D deficiency. Vitamin D is essential for calcium and phosphate absorption in the gut and for bone mineralization. Deficiency leads to inadequate mineralization of the osteoid matrix produced by osteoblasts, resulting in the characteristic clinical manifestations (bowing of legs, growth retardation) and radiographic findings (widened epiphyseal plates, fraying metaphyses). Treatment requires addressing both vitamin D deficiency and calcium insufficiency, as both are necessary for proper bone mineralization. High-dose vitamin D (typically 1000-6000 IU/day or single large doses) along with calcium supplementation (1000-2000 mg/day) is the standard approach to restore mineralization and heal bone deformities.','Vitamin D = Vital for mineralization; Deficiency = Deformed bones; Treatment = D + Ca','Remember that rickets treatment requires addressing both vitamin D deficiency AND calcium insufficiency, as isolated vitamin D supplementation without adequate calcium may be ineffective due to the mineralization process requiring both nutrients.','Option 1 is incorrect because parathyroid hormone excess would typically cause hypercalcemia, not hypocalcemia, and the primary issue is mineralization defect, not osteoblast dysfunction. Option 3 is incorrect because hypercalcemia would suppress bone resorption, not increase it, and bisphosphonates are not used for rickets. Option 4 is incorrect because genetic mutations causing collagen synthesis defects (like osteogenesis imperfecta) present with different clinical and radiographic features and require different management approaches.','Medium','endocrine','rickets','["Rickets","Endocrine","deep-agent","rickets","vitamin D deficiency","bone mineralization","calcium metabolism","pediatric endocrine"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-18T19:27:58.554Z','2026-10-18T19:27:58.554Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr30013der82wy77h6q','mcq','which one of these tools is used for screening of the developmental delay?','["Growth charts","Standard deviation curves","Mosteller formula","Denver scale"]','Denver scale','<p><strong>Why Denver scale is correct:</strong> The Denver Developmental Screening Test (DDST) is specifically designed to screen for developmental delays in children by assessing four key areas: personal-social, fine motor-adaptive, language, and gross motor skills. It uses age-appropriate developmental milestones to identify children who may need further evaluation for developmental delays, making it the only screening tool among the options that directly addresses developmental assessment rather than physical growth or calculation. The Denver scale is validated for use from birth to 6 years and is widely implemented in pediatric primary care settings for early detection of developmental issues.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Growth charts:</strong> These are used to monitor a child''s physical growth (height, weight, head circumference) over time but do not assess developmental milestones or delays.</li>
<li><strong>Standard deviation curves:</strong> These statistical tools help interpret growth data by showing how a child''s measurements compare to a population mean, but they don''t evaluate developmental progress.</li>
<li><strong>Mosteller formula:</strong> This is a mathematical formula used to calculate body surface area for medication dosing, completely unrelated to developmental screening.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The Denver scale assesses four developmental domains: personal-social, fine motor, language, and gross motor.</li>
<li>Screening tools like the Denver scale are meant to identify children who need further evaluation, not to diagnose developmental disorders.</li>
<li>Early identification of developmental delays through screening allows for timely intervention, which can significantly improve outcomes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The Denver scale is the only tool among the options specifically designed to screen for developmental delays by assessing age-appropriate milestones across multiple developmental domains.</p>','<p>Denver DDST: D for Developmental, D for Delay, S for Screening, T for Test</p>','<p>Developmental screening should be performed at all well-child visits from 9 months to 30 years of age, with specific tools validated for different age groups.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Growth charts:</strong> Used for monitoring physical growth parameters, not developmental assessment.</li>
<li><strong>Standard deviation curves:</strong> Statistical tools for interpreting growth data, not developmental screening.</li>
<li><strong>Mosteller formula:</strong> Mathematical calculation for body surface area, unrelated to development.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Developmental Screening','["screening","development","delay","tool"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.295Z','2026-10-18T19:28:24.279Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr50014der8ptflfefm','mcq','If a child''s weight is on the second centile it means that','["2% children are lighter","2% children are heavier","There has been poor weight gain since birth","The child is undernourished"]','2% children are lighter','<p><strong>Why 2% children are lighter is correct:</strong> When a child''s weight is on the second centile, it means that 2% of children in the reference population weigh less than this child, while 98% weigh more. Centiles represent the percentage of children in a standardized population who fall below a particular measurement value, so the second centile specifically indicates that only 2% of children have a lower weight than this child.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>2% children are heavier:</strong> This would be true if the child''s weight was on the 98th centile, not the 2nd centile.</li>
<li><strong>There has been poor weight gain since birth:</strong> A single measurement on the centile chart doesn''t indicate the rate of weight gain; this would require plotting weight over time.</li>
<li><strong>The child is undernourished:</strong> While being on a low centile might raise concern, it doesn''t automatically indicate undernutrition without considering the child''s growth trajectory and other clinical factors.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Centile charts show the distribution of measurements in a population, with the 50th centile representing the median.</li>
<li>A child consistently tracking along a particular centile line (even if low) is generally normal, while crossing centile lines warrants investigation.</li>
<li>The 2nd and 98th centiles are commonly used as the outer limits of "normal" range in growth charts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The second centile means exactly 2% of children in the reference population weigh less than this child, with 98% weighing more.</p>','<p>Centile clarity: 2nd centile = 2% below, 50th centile = 50% below (median), 98th centile = 98% below</p>','<p>Growth centiles should always be interpreted in the context of the child''s previous measurements and genetic potential, not as isolated values.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>2% children are heavier:</strong> This would be true for the 98th centile, not the 2nd centile.</li>
<li><strong>There has been poor weight gain since birth:</strong> A single measurement doesn''t indicate the rate of weight gain.</li>
<li><strong>The child is undernourished:</strong> A low centile alone doesn''t indicate undernutrition without considering growth trajectory.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Growth Charts','["growth chart","centile","weight"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.297Z','2026-10-18T19:28:24.281Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr70016der86javsx26','mcq','Which one of the following isn''t a cause of delayed smiling','["lack of parental stimulation","blindness","ADHD","autism"]','ADHD','<p><strong>Why ADHD is correct:</strong> ADHD is a neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity, but it does not directly impact the development of social smiling, which is a milestone typically achieved by 2-3 months of age. Social smiling is a complex developmental milestone that requires intact visual processing, social interaction, and neurological maturation of facial expression muscles and pathways. ADHD primarily affects executive function and attention rather than the fundamental neurological pathways responsible for social engagement and smiling.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lack of parental stimulation:</strong> Inadequate social interaction and stimulation can delay the development of social smiling as the infant needs responsive social interactions to learn and practice this skill.</li>
<li><strong>Blindness:</strong> Visual impairment can delay smiling because the infant cannot see facial expressions or social cues that typically elicit and reinforce smiling behavior.</li>
<li><strong>Autism:</strong> Autism spectrum disorder can affect social communication and may manifest as delayed or atypical social smiling, as it impacts the infant''s ability to engage in reciprocal social interactions.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Social smiling typically emerges between 6-8 weeks of age and is considered an important social milestone.</li>
<li>Delayed smiling beyond 3-4 months warrants evaluation for visual impairment, hearing loss, or neurological disorders.</li>
<li>The absence of social smiling by 6 months is considered a red flag for potential developmental disorders including autism spectrum disorder.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> ADHD is not associated with delayed smiling because it doesn''t affect the fundamental neurological pathways required for social engagement and smiling.</p>','<p>BLA - Blindness, Lack of stimulation, and Autism cause delayed smiling; ADHD does not.</p>','<p>Social smiling is a critical early social milestone; its absence beyond 3-4 months warrants developmental evaluation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lack of parental stimulation:</strong> Inadequate social interaction can delay social smiling development.</li>
<li><strong>Blindness:</strong> Visual impairment prevents the infant from seeing social cues that elicit smiling.</li>
<li><strong>Autism:</strong> ASD can affect social communication and may cause atypical or delayed social smiling.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Social Development','["smiling","delayed","social","milestone"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.299Z','2026-10-18T19:28:39.407Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr90018der80qr79xhd','mcq','Anterior fontanelle normally closes at age of','["3 mons","18 mons","3 years","9 mons"]','18 mons','<p><strong>Why 18 mons is correct:</strong> The anterior fontanelle normally closes between 12-18 months of age, with 18 months being the upper limit of normal closure. This closure occurs as the skull bones grow and fuse, following the process of intramembranous ossification. The fontanelle allows for rapid brain growth during infancy and gradually closes as the brain growth slows and the skull bones meet and interlock.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3 mons:</strong> At 3 months, the anterior fontanelle is still wide open (typically 2-4 cm) and shows no signs of closure.</li>
<li><strong>3 years:</strong> By 3 years, the anterior fontanelle should have long since closed; persistence beyond 18 months is considered delayed closure.</li>
<li><strong>9 mons:</strong> While some fontanelles may begin to close around this time, 9 months is too early for complete closure in most infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The anterior fontanelle is diamond-shaped and located at the junction of the frontal and parietal bones.</li>
<li>Closure typically begins between 6-12 months and is complete by 18 months in most infants.</li>
<li>A bulging fontanelle in an infant can indicate increased intracranial pressure, while a sunken fontanelle may suggest dehydration.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The anterior fontanelle normally closes by 18 months as skull bones fuse following brain growth.</p>','<p>Fontanelle closes by 18 months: ''One and a half years'' is the upper limit of normal.</p>','<p>The anterior fontanelle serves as a clinical window to assess intracranial pressure and hydration status in infants.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>3 mons:</strong> The fontanelle is still wide open at 3 months and shows no closure.</li>
<li><strong>3 years:</strong> By 3 years, the fontanelle should have long since closed.</li>
<li><strong>9 mons:</strong> While closure may begin around this time, it''s too early for complete closure in most infants.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Skull Development','["fontanelle","closure","skull"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.301Z','2026-10-18T19:28:39.409Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h91001xqjlrbozil1ed','mcq','69- Preterm infant ready for discharge but can''t latch effectively. The mother has insufficient milk despite good lactational support. What is the feeding plan?','["Exclusively Cow''s milk formula","Soy-based formula","AR formula","Express breast milk with preterm formula supplementation"]','Express breast milk with preterm formula supplementation','<p><strong>Why Express breast milk with preterm formula supplementation is correct:</strong> For preterm infants who cannot latch effectively and have insufficient maternal milk despite lactational support, the optimal feeding plan is to combine expressed breast milk with preterm formula supplementation. Preterm formula is specifically designed to meet the higher nutritional needs of premature infants, with increased protein, calories, and specific nutrients like docosahexaenoic acid (DHA) and arachidonic acid (ARA). Combining expressed breast milk with preterm formula provides both the immunological benefits of breast milk and the enhanced nutritional content required for catch-up growth in preterm infants. This approach maximizes nutrition while maintaining some of the protective factors of breast milk.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Exclusively Cow''s milk formula:</strong> This is incorrect because standard cow''s milk formula does not meet the increased nutritional requirements of preterm infants, who need higher protein, calories, and specific nutrients for optimal growth and development.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> This is incorrect for preterm infants as soy formulas do not contain the optimal nutrient profile for premature infants and may contain phytoestrogens that could potentially interfere with development.</li>
<li><strong>AR formula (Antireflux formula):</strong> This is incorrect as there is no indication of gastroesophageal reflux in this case. AR formulas are thickened and are only appropriate for infants with documented reflux issues, not for general preterm feeding.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Preterm infants require 20-30% more calories per kg than term infants and need higher protein intake for adequate growth.</li>
<li>Human milk fortifiers should be added to expressed breast milk for preterm infants to meet their increased nutritional needs.</li>
<li>The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends human milk as the primary nutrition source for preterm infants when available, often supplemented with fortifiers or preterm formula.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For preterm infants who cannot latch and have insufficient milk, combine expressed breast milk with preterm formula supplementation to meet their unique nutritional requirements.</p>','<p>PEP: Preterm Express + Preterm formula</p>','<p>Preterm infants require 20-30% more calories per kg than term infants and need higher protein intake for adequate growth and development.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Exclusively Cow''s milk formula:</strong> Does not meet the increased nutritional requirements of preterm infants.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Lacks optimal nutrient profile for preterm infants and may contain phytoestrogens.</li>
<li><strong>AR formula:</strong> Only indicated for reflux, not general preterm feeding needs.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Pediatrics','Preterm Nutrition','["preterm feeding","breast milk","supplementation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.670Z','2026-10-18T19:31:46.991Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gxj0004qjlr8461qcj2','mcq','Normally, the newborn loses 5-10% of weight during the first 3-4 days and regains it by:','["10th day"]','10th day','<p><strong>Why 10th day is correct:</strong> Newborns typically lose 5-10% of their birth weight during the first 3-4 days due to fluid loss, meconium passage, and initial limited intake. By the 10th day, most healthy infants regain this lost weight as their feeding patterns establish, gastrointestinal function matures, and they adapt to extrauterine life. This physiological weight loss followed by regain is a normal part of neonatal adaptation and reflects adequate nutritional intake and proper lactation establishment.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Other days (not specified):</strong> The question specifically asks for the day when weight is regained, and the 10th day is the established timeframe. Other days would either be too early (before adequate intake is established) or too late (beyond the normal physiological timeframe).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Normal newborn weight loss occurs in the first 3-4 days post-birth.</li>
<li>Weight should be regained by 10-14 days in exclusively breastfed infants.</li>
<li>Failure to regain birth weight by 2 weeks warrants further evaluation for feeding issues or medical problems.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Newborns lose 5-10% of birth weight in the first 3-4 days and typically regain it by the 10th day as feeding is established.</p>','','<p>Monitoring weight patterns in newborns is crucial for identifying early signs of feeding difficulties or medical issues that may not be apparent through clinical examination alone.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Other days (not specified):</strong> The timeframe for weight regain is specifically established as the 10th day in normal neonatal physiology.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Growth & Development','["newborn","weight loss","growth"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.255Z','2026-10-18T19:28:55.046Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzg0006qjlribs58t3g','mcq','What is the name of the disease caused by Vitamin B1 deficiency?','["Scurvy","Beri-Beri","Angular stomatitis","Rickets"]','Beri-Beri','<p><strong>Why Beri-Beri is correct:</strong> Beri-Beri is the specific disease caused by thiamine (Vitamin B1) deficiency. Thiamine is essential for carbohydrate metabolism as a cofactor for enzymes like pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase in the Krebs cycle. Deficiency leads to impaired energy production, particularly in tissues with high energy demands like the nervous system and heart, resulting in the characteristic symptoms of beri-beri including neuropathy, cardiomyopathy, and potentially Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome in severe cases.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scurvy:</strong> Caused by vitamin C deficiency, characterized by impaired collagen synthesis leading to bleeding gums, poor wound healing, and perifollicular hemorrhages.</li>
<li><strong>Angular stomatitis:</strong> A condition characterized by inflammation at the corners of the mouth, most commonly caused by riboflavin (B2) deficiency, though it can also result from B12, iron, or zinc deficiencies.</li>
<li><strong>Rickets:</strong> Caused by vitamin D deficiency, leading to defective bone mineralization, growth plate abnormalities, and skeletal deformities in children.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Beri-Beri has two forms: wet (cardiovascular) and dry (neurological).</li>
<li>Thiamine deficiency is commonly seen in populations with high carbohydrate intake and low thiamine intake, such as polished rice diets.</li>
<li>Alcoholism is a major risk factor for thiamine deficiency due to poor nutrition and impaired absorption.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Beri-Beri is the classic disease of thiamine (B1) deficiency, affecting both the nervous system and cardiovascular system due to impaired energy metabolism.</p>','<p>Beri-Beri: B for B1 deficiency</p>','<p>Thiamine deficiency should be suspected in patients with heart failure, neuropathy, or encephalopathy who have risk factors such as alcoholism, bariatric surgery, or prolonged IV nutrition without thiamine supplementation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Scurvy:</strong> Caused by vitamin C deficiency, not B1.</li>
<li><strong>Angular stomatitis:</strong> Primarily associated with riboflavin (B2) deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Rickets:</strong> Results from vitamin D deficiency, not B1.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Nutrition','Vitamin Deficiencies','["vitamin b1","beri-beri","deficiency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.324Z','2026-10-18T19:28:55.050Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0a000dqjlrmopf4owr','mcq','The first 1000 days of the infant life is:','["The period from conception to a child''s second birthday.","The period from delivery to the child''s 2nd birthday.","The period from conception to a child''s 1st birthday.","The period from conception to the child''s 3rd birthday."]','The period from conception to a child''s second birthday.','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> The first 1000 days of infant life is defined as the period from conception (approximately 280 days of gestation) through the child''s second birthday (approximately 730 days postnatal), totaling approximately 1000 days. This critical window encompasses the fetal period, infancy, and early childhood, representing a period of rapid development when nutritional and environmental factors can have profound and lasting effects on health outcomes. The concept emphasizes that interventions during this period can influence long-term health trajectories, including metabolic programming, immune system development, and cognitive potential. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>B:</strong> This option incorrectly starts from delivery rather than conception, missing the critical prenatal period when organogenesis and foundational development occur. - <strong>C:</strong> This option ends at the first birthday, which is only 730 days post-conception, missing the second year of life when brain development continues rapidly. - <strong>D:</strong> This option extends beyond the second birthday to the third birthday, which would exceed the 1000-day window and is not the standard definition. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The first 1000 days represent a critical window of opportunity for interventions that can impact lifelong health. - This period includes the fetal stage, infancy, and early childhood, with the most rapid brain development occurring prenatally and in the first two years. - Nutritional deficiencies during this period can lead to irreversible developmental deficits. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The first 1000 days of life span from conception to the child''s second birthday, representing a critical developmental window with lifelong health implications.</p>','<p>1000 days = 9 months (gestation) + 12 months (first year) + 3 months (second year) = conception to 2nd birthday</p>','<p>Early interventions during the first 1000 days can significantly impact long-term health outcomes, making this period crucial for pediatric preventive care.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B:</strong> Incorrectly starts from delivery rather than conception, missing the critical prenatal period. - <strong>C:</strong> Ends at the first birthday, missing the second year of life. - <strong>D:</strong> Extends beyond the second birthday to the third, exceeding the 1000-day window.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["1000 days","development","nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.354Z','2026-10-18T19:29:16.370Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0w000jqjlrd0zbkwqx','mcq','3-week-old baby who is exclusively breast-fed. Mother complain that her baby is poorly weight gain. The first step done is','["Immediately start formula","Assess latch and position of lactation","PKU analysis","Glucose 5% orally along with breastfeeding"]','Assess latch and position of lactation','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> The first step in evaluating a breastfed infant with poor weight gain is to assess the latch and position of lactation, as inadequate milk transfer due to improper attachment is the most common cause of insufficient weight gain in exclusively breastfed infants. This assessment evaluates whether the infant is properly latching onto the areola, not just the nipple, and whether the infant''s mouth is wide open with the tongue positioned correctly to extract milk effectively. Proper latch creates a seal that allows for efficient milk removal, which is essential for adequate caloric intake and subsequent weight gain in newborns. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A. Immediately start formula:</strong> Jumping to formula supplementation without first assessing latch may mask underlying breastfeeding technique issues that could be corrected with proper guidance. - <strong>C. PKU analysis:</strong> While metabolic disorders can cause poor weight gain, PKU screening is typically performed at 24-48 hours of age and would not be the first step in this scenario. - <strong>D. Glucose 5% orally along with breastfeeding:</strong> Oral glucose supplementation would provide empty calories without addressing the root cause of poor weight gain and could potentially interfere with breastfeeding establishment. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Inadequate milk transfer due to poor latch is the most common cause of poor weight gain in breastfed infants. - Weight loss greater than 7-10% from birth weight or failure to regain birth weight by 2 weeks warrants evaluation. - A proper latch involves the infant taking a large portion of the areola into the mouth with flanged lips and rhythmic swallowing. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Always assess breastfeeding technique before considering supplementation or further testing in an exclusively breastfed infant with poor weight gain.</p>','<p>LATCH: Look for a wide mouth, Asymmetric latch, Tongue down, Cheek flanges, Hold supporting the head</p>','<p>Breastfeeding complications are most often related to technique rather than insufficient milk supply, making proper latch assessment the cornerstone of management.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. Immediately start formula:</strong> Should only be considered after assessing latch and confirming inadequate milk transfer. - <strong>C. PKU analysis:</strong> Metabolic screening is not indicated as first step for poor weight gain in breastfed infants. - <strong>D. Glucose 5% orally along with breastfeeding:</strong> Provides empty calories without addressing the root cause of poor weight gain.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["weight gain","latch","assessment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.376Z','2026-10-18T19:29:30.710Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0c000eqjlrjyutgj1g','mcq','Nipple pain and injury during breast feeding can occur due to:','["Good position and latch.","Poor position and latch.","Neonatal hyperbilirubinemia.","Neonatal convulsions."]','Poor position and latch.','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Nipple pain and injury during breastfeeding most commonly result from poor positioning and improper latch, which can cause trauma to the nipple tissue. When the infant is not properly positioned with a wide mouth and flanged lips, they tend to suckle only on the nipple rather than taking in a mouthful of breast tissue, leading to friction, compression, and eventual breakdown of the nipple skin. This mechanical trauma can cause pain, cracking, and even bleeding of the nipples, and if uncorrected, may lead to conditions like mastitis or early breastfeeding cessation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A:</strong> Good position and latch would typically minimize nipple trauma rather than cause it, as the infant would be properly transferring milk without excessive friction on the nipple. - <strong>C:</strong> Neonatal hyperbilirubinemia is a condition related to bilirubin metabolism and jaundice, which does not directly cause nipple injury or pain during breastfeeding. - <strong>D:</strong> Neonatal convulsions are neurological events unrelated to breastfeeding mechanics and would not directly cause nipple trauma. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Proper latch requires the infant''s mouth to be wide open with flanged lips, taking in a large portion of the areola. - Nipple pain that persists beyond the first week of breastfeeding is usually due to positioning issues rather than normal adaptation. - Early intervention from a lactation consultant can prevent most nipple injuries through proper positioning techniques. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Nipple pain and injury during breastfeeding are almost always caused by poor positioning and improper latch, not by the infant''s condition.</p>','<p>PAIN = Position And Improper latch = Nipple trauma</p>','<p>Nipple pain that persists beyond the first week of breastfeeding is abnormal and indicates a need for assessment of positioning and latch.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A:</strong> Good position and latch would minimize rather than cause nipple trauma. - <strong>C:</strong> Hyperbilirubinemia is unrelated to nipple injury mechanics. - <strong>D:</strong> Convulsions are neurological events unrelated to breastfeeding technique.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["nipple pain","injury","latch"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.356Z','2026-10-18T19:29:16.371Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzy000bqjlrbe30wfq9','mcq','Which is considered absolute infant contraindication of breast feeding?','["Phenylketonuria.","Cleft palate.","Prematurity.","Twins."]','Phenylketonuria.','<p><strong>Why Phenylketonuria is correct:</strong> Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an absolute contraindication to breastfeeding because breast milk contains phenylalanine, which cannot be properly metabolized by infants with this disorder. If an infant with PKU consumes breast milk, phenylalanine will accumulate and cause severe intellectual disability, developmental delays, and neurological damage. These infants require a specialized phenylalanine-restricted formula from birth to prevent these complications. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Cleft palate:</strong> While cleft palate makes breastfeeding challenging, it is not an absolute contraindication; many infants with cleft palate can breastfeed with special techniques or expressed milk. - <strong>Prematurity:</strong> Premature infants often benefit greatly from breast milk, including its immunological properties, and can be breastfed with proper support. - <strong>Twins:</strong> Twins can absolutely be breastfed; many mothers successfully breastfeed multiple infants with proper positioning and technique. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - PKU is one of the very few absolute contraindications to breastfeeding due to the essential amino acid phenylalanine in breast milk. - Most infant conditions that make breastfeeding difficult are not absolute contraindications and can be managed with proper support. - Early diagnosis and intervention are critical for infants with PKU to prevent neurological damage. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Phenylketonuria is the only absolute infant contraindication to breastfeeding because breast milk contains phenylalanine which is toxic to these infants.</p>','<p>PKU = Prevents Keeping Up (breastfeeding) due to phenylalanine toxicity</p>','<p>Infants with PKU require specialized formula from birth, making breastfeeding contraindicated, but mothers can still provide the benefits of bonding through skin-to-skin contact and pumping for later use if desired.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Cleft palate:</strong> A challenge but not a contraindication to breastfeeding. - <strong>Prematurity:</strong> Breast milk is particularly beneficial for premature infants. - <strong>Twins:</strong> Breastfeeding multiple infants is possible with proper support.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["contraindication","infant","phenylketonuria"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.342Z','2026-10-18T19:29:11.271Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0o000gqjlrcgamubbx','mcq','A special milk formula is indicated for:','["Infants delivered by C.S.","Infants delivered by spontaneous vaginal delivery.","Infants born to diabetic mothers.","Infants with Cow''s milk protein allergy."]','Infants with Cow''s milk protein allergy.','<p><strong>Why Infants with Cow''s milk protein allergy is correct:</strong> Cow''s milk protein allergy (CMPA) is an immune-mediated reaction to proteins in cow''s milk, requiring a specialized formula that has extensively hydrolyzed proteins or amino acids to prevent allergic reactions. These formulas break down the milk proteins into smaller components that the immune system doesn''t recognize as allergens, allowing the infant to receive adequate nutrition without triggering symptoms like eczema, vomiting, diarrhea, or failure to thrive. The hydrolyzed formulas are designed to maintain nutritional equivalence while eliminating the allergenic potential of intact milk proteins.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Infants delivered by C.S.:</strong> Cesarean section delivery doesn''t necessitate a special milk formula, as these infants can typically tolerate standard formula or breast milk without modification.</li>
<li><strong>Infants delivered by spontaneous vaginal delivery:</strong> Vaginal delivery is the normal route of delivery and doesn''t require specialized formula for nutritional or immunological reasons.</li>
<li><strong>Infants born to diabetic mothers:</strong> While these infants may have transient hypoglycemia or other metabolic issues, they don''t require a special milk formula for cow''s milk protein allergy; standard formula or breast milk is appropriate once metabolic stability is achieved.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>CMPA affects approximately 2-7.5% of infants, with symptoms ranging from mild to severe.</li>
<li>Extensively hydrolyzed formulas are first-line treatment for CMPA, with amino acid formulas reserved for severe cases.</li>
<li>CMPA typically presents in the first few months of life and often resolves by age 3-5 years.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Special milk formulas are indicated for infants with cow''s milk protein allergy, not for mode of delivery or maternal diabetes status.</p>','<p>CMPA: Cow''s Milk Protein Allergy requires Special formula (hydrolyzed or amino acid) to prevent Allergic reactions.</p>','<p>Early introduction of cow''s milk protein (after 4-6 months of age) may actually help prevent the development of cow''s milk protein allergy in high-risk infants.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Infants delivered by C.S.:</strong> Mode of delivery doesn''t require specialized formula for allergy management.</li>
<li><strong>Infants delivered by spontaneous vaginal delivery:</strong> Normal delivery doesn''t necessitate formula modification.</li>
<li><strong>Infants born to diabetic mothers:</strong> Maternal diabetes doesn''t require CMPA-specific formula treatment.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["formula","special","allergy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.368Z','2026-10-18T19:29:28.925Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h0q000hqjlr83gk9j0j','mcq','A false sign of adequate breastfeeding is:','["Proper weight gain.","Audible swallowing sounds during breastfeeding.","Not enough wet or poppy diapers.","Sleeping"]','Not enough wet or poppy diapers.','<p><strong>Why Not enough wet or poppy diapers is correct:</strong> Inadequate wet or poppy diapers (typically fewer than 6 wet diapers and 3-4 bowel movements per day in the first week of life) is a sign of insufficient milk intake, indicating inadequate breastfeeding, not adequacy. This suggests the baby is not receiving enough breast milk to maintain hydration and nutrition, which can lead to dehydration, weight loss, and hyperbilirubinemia. The number of wet and soiled diapers is a critical objective measure of milk transfer and intake in breastfeeding infants.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Proper weight gain:</strong> Adequate weight gain (return to birth weight by 2 weeks and gaining 20-30 grams per day thereafter) is a positive sign of successful breastfeeding.</li>
<li><strong>Audible swallowing sounds during breastfeeding:</strong> These sounds indicate the baby is actively transferring milk, which is a sign of effective breastfeeding.</li>
<li><strong>Sleeping:</strong> Newborns typically sleep 16-20 hours per day, and this normal behavior doesn''t indicate breastfeeding adequacy or inadequacy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>By day 5, a breastfed infant should have 6-8 wet diapers and 3-4 bowel movements per day.</li>
<li>Urine should be pale and not concentrated, indicating proper hydration.</li>
<li>Stool transitions from meconium to seedy yellow by day 5 in breastfed infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Inadequate wet or poppy diapers indicate insufficient milk intake, making it a false sign of adequate breastfeeding.</p>','<p>Wet diapers = Well-fed; Poppy diapers = Proper intake; Both needed = Breastfeeding adequacy.</p>','<p>The ''test weighing'' before and after breastfeeding can provide precise measurement of milk intake if concerns about adequacy arise.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Proper weight gain:</strong> This is a positive indicator of adequate breastfeeding.</li>
<li><strong>Audible swallowing sounds:</strong> These indicate effective milk transfer during breastfeeding.</li>
<li><strong>Sleeping:</strong> Normal newborn sleep behavior doesn''t reflect breastfeeding adequacy.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["adequate breastfeeding","signs","dehydration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.370Z','2026-10-18T19:29:28.927Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h83001fqjlr774vrydi','mcq','3-day-old baby in BFHI hospital was given formula fed all night and has no medical indication for formula. Which step is been violated','["Step 6:- No food or drink other than breast milk unless medically indicated"]','Step 6:- No food or drink other than breast milk unless medically indicated','<p><strong>Why Step 6:- No food or drink other than breast milk unless medically indicated is correct:</strong> The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) Step 6 mandates that healthy newborns receive only breast milk unless there is a documented medical indication for alternative nutrition. In this case, the 3-day-old baby was given formula feed all night without any medical indication, directly violating this principle. Formula feeding interferes with breastfeeding establishment by reducing the infant''s suckling at the breast, which is essential for stimulating adequate milk production. This practice also disrupts the natural sleep-wake cycle of newborns and prevents the important bonding that occurs during nighttime breastfeeding sessions.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Step 3:- Inform all mother about benefits of Breastfeeding:</strong> While education is important, this violation involves the physical act of providing formula, not lack of information. - <strong>Avoid artificial teats:</strong> This refers to pacifiers or bottle nipples, not the formula feeding itself. - <strong>Step 10:- promote support groups:</strong> This is a post-discharge support measure, not related to in-hospital formula administration.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - BFHI Step 6 exists to protect breastfeeding by preventing unnecessary supplementation that can reduce milk supply. - Nighttime breastfeeding is particularly important for establishing and maintaining milk supply. - Formula feeding without medical indication in a BFHI hospital undermines the initiative''s core principles.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> BFHI Step 6 is violated whenever formula is given to a healthy newborn without a proper medical indication, as it interferes with breastfeeding establishment.</p>','<p>BFHI Step 6: Breast Milk Only - No Formula (BMONF)</p>','<p>Night breastfeeding is crucial for maintaining milk supply as prolactin levels are naturally higher during nighttime hours.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Step 3:</strong> This involves education, not the physical act of providing formula. - <strong>Avoid artificial teats:</strong> This refers to pacifiers/bottles, not formula feeding. - <strong>Step 10:</strong> This is about post-discharge support, not in-hospital formula administration.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','BFHI guidelines','["bfhi","formula feeding","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.635Z','2026-10-18T19:30:56.197Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h11000kqjlryfrw80pc','mcq','Mother complaining of cracked nipples and severe pain during breastfeeding. When assessment you notice the baby''s cheeks are dimpled with each suckling. The probable cause is','["Poor latch"]','Poor latch','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> Poor latch is the probable cause of cracked nipples and severe pain during breastfeeding when the baby''s cheeks are dimpled with each suckling. Dimpling of the cheeks indicates that the infant is not taking enough breast tissue into their mouth, causing them to suck with excessive pressure on the nipple rather than compressing the milk sinuses behind the areola. This improper technique leads to nipple trauma, cracking, and pain as the nipple is repeatedly compressed and rubbed against the hard palate rather than being cushioned by the areola. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - This question only has one option, but in a typical multiple-choice format, other distractors might include conditions like thrush (which would typically present with nipple pain and burning rather than mechanical trauma) or vasospasm (which would present with color changes and burning pain). <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - A proper latch should result in the infant taking a large mouthful of breast tissue with flanged lips and visible rhythmic swallowing. - Nipple trauma is almost always due to mechanical factors from poor positioning or latch rather than infection. - Pain that begins with latch and continues throughout the feeding is more likely related to technique than to conditions like thrush. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cheek dimpling during breastfeeding is a classic sign of poor latch, leading to nipple trauma and pain.</p>','<p>Dimple = Diminished areola intake</p>','<p>Nipple pain that persists despite correction of latch should prompt evaluation for other causes like thrush, vasospasm, or dermatitis.</p>','','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["nipple pain","cracked","latch"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.381Z','2026-10-18T19:29:30.713Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h1z000lqjlrj56htnoh','mcq','Preterm infant in NICU with risk for NEC. The best feeding choice is','["Mother''s own milk"]','Mother''s own milk','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> Mother''s own milk is the best feeding choice for preterm infants at risk for necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) due to its unique composition of bioactive factors, including growth factors, immunoglobulins, and anti-inflammatory agents that protect the immature gut. Human milk contains secretory IgA that coats the intestinal lining, preventing bacterial translocation, and oligosaccharides that promote the growth of beneficial gut flora while inhibiting pathogenic bacteria. Additionally, human milk contains epidermal growth factor and other trophic factors that promote intestinal maturation and mucosal repair, reducing the risk of NEC which is thought to result from an immature gut barrier combined with bacterial overgrowth. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - This question only has one option, but in a typical multiple-choice format, other distractors might include preterm formula (which lacks the specific bioactive factors of human milk) or donor milk (which, while better than formula, still lacks the mother-specific antibodies and growth factors). <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Human milk reduces the risk of NEC by 60-80% compared to formula in preterm infants. - The protective effect of human milk is dose-dependent, with more milk (especially mother''s own milk) providing greater protection. - Even small amounts of mother''s own milk (as little as 10-20 mL/day) have been shown to reduce NEC risk. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Mother''s own milk provides unique bioactive protection against NEC in preterm infants that cannot be replicated by formula or donor milk.</p>','<p>MOM: Mother''s Own Milk for Mucosal protection</p>','<p>The protective effect of human milk against NEC is dose-dependent, with exclusive human milk feeding providing the greatest benefit.</p>','','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["preterm","NEC","mother''s milk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.415Z','2026-10-18T19:29:30.715Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h33000sqjlrvf9ss6ml','mcq','Component in breast milk and not in formula','["Live immune cells","Iron","Casein protein","Lactose"]','Live immune cells','<p><strong>Why Live immune cells is correct:</strong> Breast milk contains live immune cells, including macrophages, lymphocytes, and neutrophils, which provide passive immunity to the infant. These cells actively phagocytose pathogens, produce cytokines, and help establish the infant''s immune system. Formula, being a processed product, lacks these living cellular components that offer dynamic immune protection. The presence of these cells is a unique biological advantage of breast milk over artificial feeding methods.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Iron:</strong> While breast milk contains less iron than formula, it is more bioavailable due to the presence of lactoferrin, which enhances absorption. Iron is present in both breast milk and formula.</li>
<li><strong>Casein protein:</strong> Casein is actually present in higher concentrations in cow''s milk-based formulas than in human breast milk. Breast milk has a lower casein content compared to whey protein.</li>
<li><strong>Lactose:</strong> Lactose is the primary carbohydrate in both breast milk and most infant formulas, making it not unique to breast milk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Breast milk contains living immune cells (macrophages, lymphocytes) that provide passive immunity and immune system development support.</li>
<li>Lactoferrin in breast milk enhances iron absorption despite lower total iron content compared to formula.</li>
<li>The whey-to-casein ratio differs between breast milk (60:40) and cow''s milk-based formula (20:80).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Breast milk uniquely provides living immune cells that formula cannot replicate, offering dynamic immune protection beyond just nutritional components.</p>','<p>B.M.I.L.F. - Breast Milk Immune Live Factors (macrophages, lymphocytes, neutrophils)</p>','<p>Breast milk''s living immune components help protect infants against infections during the vulnerable early months when their own immune system is still developing.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Iron:</strong> Iron is present in both breast milk and formula, though breast milk has more bioavailable iron.</li>
<li><strong>Casein protein:</strong> Casein is actually more concentrated in formula than in breast milk.</li>
<li><strong>Lactose:</strong> Lactose is found in both breast milk and formula as the primary carbohydrate.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["components","immune cells","formula"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.455Z','2026-10-18T19:30:00.363Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h35000tqjlr0p3zxboy','mcq','Carbohydrate responsible for energy source in breast milk or formula','["Lactose"]','Lactose','<p><strong>Why Lactose is correct:</strong> Lactose is the primary carbohydrate in both breast milk and infant formula, serving as the main energy source for infants. It provides approximately 40% of the total calories in breast milk and is easily digested by infants due to the presence of lactase enzyme in their intestinal brush border. Lactose also promotes calcium absorption and helps establish beneficial gut microbiota through its prebiotic effects.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While this question only has one correct option listed, it''s important to note that other carbohydrates like maltodextrin or corn syrup solids are sometimes added to formulas, but lactose remains the primary carbohydrate in both breast milk and standard formulas. No other carbohydrate serves as the main energy source in these feeding options.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lactose provides approximately 40% of the total calories in breast milk.</li>
<li>Lactose promotes calcium absorption and supports beneficial gut microbiota development.</li>
<li>Lactose intolerance is rare in infants and typically develops after weaning.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Lactose is the dominant carbohydrate in both breast milk and formula, serving as the primary energy source while also aiding mineral absorption and gut health.</p>','<p>Lactose = Liquid Energy (L for Liquid, E for Energy)</p>','<p>Lactose not only provides energy but also helps maintain the optimal osmolarity of breast milk, which is important for infant kidney function.</p>','','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["carbohydrate","energy","lactose"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.457Z','2026-10-18T19:30:00.366Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzi0007qjlr0xaxbmvz','mcq','Most common form of PEM:','["Nutritional dwarfism"]','Nutritional dwarfism','<p><strong>Why Nutritional dwarfism is correct:</strong> Nutritional dwarfism, also known as failure to thrive due to inadequate caloric intake, is the most common form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) globally. It results from chronic insufficient intake of calories and/or protein, leading to growth retardation, weight loss, and delayed development. This condition typically occurs in resource-limited settings where access to adequate nutrition is limited, and it affects a larger population than the more severe forms of PEM like kwashiorkor or marasmus. The pathophysiology involves a negative energy balance that triggers catabolic processes, breaking down body tissues to meet energy demands, which manifests as stunted growth and reduced physical development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> This is a severe form of PEM characterized by protein deficiency with relatively adequate calorie intake, leading to edema, skin changes, and fatty liver, but it is less common than nutritional dwarfism. - <strong>Marasmus:</strong> This is a severe form of PEM resulting from inadequate intake of both calories and protein, causing extreme emaciation, but it is less prevalent globally than nutritional dwarfism. - <strong>Marasmic-kwashiorkor:</strong> This is a mixed form of PEM with features of both marasmus and kwashiorkor, representing the most severe presentation but occurring less frequently than nutritional dwarfism.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Nutritional dwarfism is characterized by growth failure without the edema seen in kwashiorkor. - The earliest sign of nutritional dwarfism is declining growth velocity on a growth chart. - Treatment requires addressing both the immediate nutritional deficit and the underlying cause of inadequate intake.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Nutritional dwarfism is the most common PEM because it represents the mildest form of chronic energy deficiency affecting the largest population globally.</p>','<p>Dwarfism is Dominant (D for most common) in PEM</p>','<p>Early intervention in nutritional dwarfism is critical as catch-up growth is possible if addressed before permanent epiphyseal plate closure.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> Less common than nutritional dwarfism and specifically involves protein deficiency with edema. - <strong>Marasmus:</strong> Less prevalent globally and represents severe wasting from inadequate calorie and protein intake. - <strong>Marasmic-kwashiorkor:</strong> The most severe form of PEM but occurs less frequently than nutritional dwarfism.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["PEM","common","nutritional dwarfism"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.326Z','2026-10-18T22:35:26.147Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56haj002dqjlrdsj20quu','mcq','An 18-month-old female presents with recurrent attacks of painless rectal bleeding. She had a previous history of intussusception at the age of 9 months, treated by pneumatic reduction; clotting profile and blood film are normal apart from mild anemia. Of the following, what is the most likely diagnosis?','["Meckel''s diverticulum","Intussusception","Amoebic dysentery","Fissure in ano"]','Meckel''s diverticulum','<p><strong>Why Meckel''s diverticulum is correct:</strong> Meckel''s diverticulum is the most likely diagnosis in this 18-month-old with recurrent painless rectal bleeding and a history of intussusception. This congenital anomaly is a remnant of the vitelline duct and contains ectopic gastric mucosa in approximately 50% of cases, which secretes acid and causes ulceration in the adjacent ileum, leading to painless bleeding. The classic presentation is painless rectal bleeding in a child under 2 years old, which matches this patient''s age and symptom pattern. The previous history of intussusception is relevant as it represents a common pediatric abdominal emergency, but the recurrent bleeding points specifically to Meckel''s diverticulum as the underlying cause.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> While this patient had a previous episode of intussusception, the current presentation is recurrent bleeding rather than the classic acute symptoms of intussusception (currant jelly stools, abdominal mass, vomiting).</li>
<li><strong>Amoebic dysentery:</strong> This is unlikely in an 18-month-old in a non-endemic area and would typically present with bloody diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever rather than painless rectal bleeding.</li>
<li><strong>Fissure in ano:</strong> Anal fissures usually present with painful defecation and bright red blood on the surface of stools, not painless rectal bleeding.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract and the most common cause of painless lower GI bleeding in children under 2 years.</li>
<li>The "rule of 2s" applies: 2% prevalence, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, presents in first 2 years of life, and contains 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric and pancreatic).</li>
<li>Technetium-99m pertechnetate scan (Meckel scan) is the diagnostic test of choice when ectopic gastric mucosa is present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Recurrent painless rectal bleeding in a child under 2 years with a history of intussusception strongly suggests Meckel''s diverticulum due to its ectopic gastric mucosa causing ulceration.</p>','<p>Rule of 2s: 2% of population, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, presents in first 2 years, contains 2 types of ectopic tissue.</p>','<p>Meckel''s diverticulum should be strongly suspected in any child with painless rectal bleeding, especially when occurring before age 2.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> The current presentation is recurrent bleeding rather than acute symptoms of intussusception.</li>
<li><strong>Amoebic dysentery:</strong> Unlikely in this age group and would present with bloody diarrhea and pain.</li>
<li><strong>Fissure in ano:</strong> Typically presents with painful defecation, not painless bleeding.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["meckel","rectal bleeding","intussusception","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.723Z','2026-10-18T19:32:39.614Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h36000uqjlrt38sj45c','mcq','Whey to casein ratio in mature milk:','["50:50 or 1:1"]','50:50 or 1:1','<p><strong>Why 50:50 or 1:1 is correct:</strong> In mature breast milk, the whey-to-casein ratio is approximately 60:40 or simplified to 1:1, which is significantly different from cow''s milk (20:80). This ratio is evolutionarily optimized for human infants, as whey proteins are easier to digest and contain important bioactive components like lactoferrin and alpha-lactalbumin. The higher whey content supports the rapid growth and development characteristic of human infants compared to other species.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While this question only has one correct option listed, it''s important to understand that the whey-to-casein ratio changes throughout lactation. Colostrum (the first milk) has a whey-to-casein ratio of approximately 90:10, which gradually shifts to the mature milk ratio of about 60:40. This ratio is not 20:80 (which is characteristic of cow''s milk) or 80:20 (which would be even higher than human milk).</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Mature breast milk has a whey-to-casein ratio of approximately 60:40 (often simplified to 1:1).</li>
<li>The ratio shifts from 90:10 in colostrum to 60:40 in mature milk.</li>
<li>Cow''s milk has a much lower whey-to-casein ratio of 20:80.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The whey-to-casein ratio in mature human breast milk is approximately 1:1, optimized for human infant digestion and growth.</p>','<p>Whey Wins (60:40 ratio in mature milk) - Whey is the dominant protein in human milk</p>','<p>The high whey content in human milk provides bioactive proteins that support immune function and are more easily digested than the predominant casein in cow''s milk.</p>','','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["whey","casein","ratio"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.458Z','2026-10-18T19:30:00.367Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h3d000vqjlr6cmogiqm','mcq','Which of the following is true about breast milk','["Has fixed composition","Changes its composition to meet infant needs","Has no fat","Higher Na than Formula"]','Changes its composition to meet infant needs','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Breast milk is a dynamic fluid that changes its composition throughout a feeding session, across different stages of lactation (colostrum vs. mature milk), and even within a single day to meet the infant''s evolving nutritional needs. For example, foremilk is lower in fat and higher in lactose, while hindmilk is richer in fat and calories, helping to satisfy the infant''s hunger. The milk also contains bioactive components like antibodies and growth factors that adapt to protect the infant from pathogens in the environment, demonstrating its remarkable ability to respond to the infant''s immune status.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. Has fixed composition:</strong> Breast milk does not have a fixed composition; it varies based on the infant''s age, feeding time, and even the mother''s diet and health status.</li>
<li><strong>C. Has no fat:</strong> Breast milk contains significant fat, particularly in the hindmilk, which is essential for brain development and providing adequate calories for growth.</li>
<li><strong>D. Higher Na than Formula:</strong> Breast milk actually has lower sodium content than formula, which is why breastfed infants have a lower risk of developing hypertension later in life compared to formula-fed infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Breast milk composition changes from colostrum (high in IgA and immune factors) to transitional milk (days 2-5) to mature milk (after 10-14 days).</li>
<li>Fat content increases throughout a feeding session, with hindmilk providing 3-5 times more fat than foremilk.</li>
<li>The nutritional composition of breast milk adapts to the infant''s circadian rhythm, with higher fat content at night.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Breast milk is a living, adaptive fluid that changes its composition to precisely match the infant''s nutritional and immunological needs at any given moment.</p>','<p>FAT: Foremilk has less fat, hindmilk has more fat, and breast milk is Always Tailored to the infant''s needs.</p>','<p>The dynamic nature of breast milk composition is why it''s often called ''living milk''—it contains stem cells, white blood cells, and antibodies that adapt to protect the infant from current environmental pathogens.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. Has fixed composition:</strong> Breast milk composition varies based on infant age, feeding time, and maternal factors.</li>
<li><strong>C. Has no fat:</strong> Breast milk contains significant fat, especially in hindmilk, crucial for brain development.</li>
<li><strong>D. Higher Na than Formula:</strong> Breast milk has lower sodium than formula, reducing hypertension risk.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["composition","adaptive","dynamic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.465Z','2026-10-18T19:30:04.737Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h3j000wqjlrfct04y6j','mcq','Which nutrients need supplementation after 6 months:','["Iron"]','Iron','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> Iron supplementation is recommended after 6 months of age because the iron stores that infants are born with become depleted by this time, and the iron content in breast milk, while highly bioavailable, is insufficient to meet the increased demands of rapid growth and development during the second half of the first year. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that exclusively and partially breastfed infants receive iron supplements starting at 4 months of age, with continued supplementation until appropriate iron-rich complementary foods are introduced in sufficient amounts.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong></p>
<p>If we consider other nutrients that might be suggested for supplementation after 6 months, such as vitamin D or zinc, they are either already supplemented from birth (vitamin D) or generally not required if the infant is consuming a varied diet. Iron is unique in that its stores are finite and become depleted precisely around 6 months, coinciding with the period of rapid growth when iron demands peak.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Iron deficiency is the most common nutritional deficiency worldwide in infants and can lead to irreversible neurodevelopmental consequences if not prevented.</li>
<li>The recommended iron supplementation dose for breastfed infants after 4 months is 1 mg/kg per day, with a maximum of 1 mg/day until 12 months.</li>
<li>Complementary foods introduced after 6 months should be rich in iron, such as fortified cereals, pureed meats, and beans.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Iron supplementation becomes necessary after 6 months because maternal iron stores are depleted and breast milk alone cannot meet the infant''s increased iron demands for rapid growth.</p>','<p>6 Months: Iron Stores Gone, Need Iron Supplementation.</p>','<p>Iron deficiency in infancy can manifest as irritability, lethargy, and poor feeding, but these nonspecific symptoms often delay diagnosis, making prevention through supplementation critical.</p>','','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["supplementation","iron","6 months"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.471Z','2026-10-18T19:30:04.739Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h3k000xqjlr4xilkjcz','mcq','Which nutrients need supplementation from zero day:','["Vitamin D"]','Vitamin D','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> Vitamin D supplementation is recommended from birth because human breast milk is naturally deficient in vitamin D, containing only about 10-80 IU per liter, which is insufficient to meet the infant''s requirements for calcium absorption and bone development. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that all infants, including exclusively breastfed infants, receive a daily vitamin D supplement of 400 IU starting from the first few days of life to prevent rickets and ensure adequate calcium metabolism. This recommendation applies regardless of whether the infant is breastfed or formula-fed, as even most formulas contain insufficient vitamin D to meet requirements without supplementation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong></p>
<p>If we consider other nutrients that might be suggested for supplementation from birth, such as iron or fluoride, they are not universally recommended. Iron stores are adequate at birth and don''t need supplementation until around 4-6 months, and fluoride supplementation is only recommended in areas with inadequate water fluoridation and is not a universal recommendation.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Vitamin D deficiency in infancy can lead to rickets, characterized by softening and weakening of bones, delayed motor skills, and growth failure.</li>
<li>The 400 IU daily vitamin D dose for infants is equivalent to approximately 1 liter of vitamin D-fortified formula or 6 cups of vitamin D-fortified cow''s milk.</li>
<li>Dark-skinned infants, those living in northern latitudes, and infants with limited sun exposure are at particularly high risk of vitamin D deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Vitamin D supplementation is required from birth because breast milk contains insufficient vitamin D to prevent deficiency and ensure proper bone development.</p>','<p>Day One: Vitamin D Done—400 IU for everyone.</p>','<p>Vitamin D is unique among nutrients because it functions more like a hormone, regulating calcium absorption and immune function, and is synthesized in the skin through sunlight exposure.</p>','','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["supplementation","vitamin D","newborn"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.472Z','2026-10-18T19:30:04.740Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h3m000yqjlr5mxo168y','mcq','First milk produced after delivery and rich in immunoglobulins:','["Colostrum"]','Colostrum','<p><strong>Why Colostrum is correct:</strong> Colostrum is the first milk produced after delivery and is rich in immunoglobulins, particularly secretory IgA, which provides passive immunity to the newborn. It contains higher concentrations of protein, vitamins, and minerals compared to mature milk, while having lower fat and lactose content. The immunoglobulins in colostrum coat the infant''s gastrointestinal tract, preventing pathogen attachment and absorption, and also contains leukocytes, lactoferrin, and growth factors that support immune development and gut maturation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mature milk:</strong> This is the milk produced after colostrum and transitional milk phases, typically around 10-14 days postpartum, and has a different composition with lower immunoglobulin content.</li>
<li><strong>Transitional milk:</strong> This intermediate phase occurs between colostrum and mature milk (approximately 2-5 days postpartum) and has a composition that gradually shifts toward mature milk.</li>
<li><strong>Foremilk:</strong> This is the milk obtained at the beginning of a feeding session, which is lower in fat and higher in lactose compared to hindmilk, but it''s not specifically defined by its immunoglobulin content or timing postpartum.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Colostrum is produced in small quantities (approximately 10-100 mL per day) but is extremely nutrient-dense and immunologically active.</li>
<li>The concentration of secretory IgA in colostrum is significantly higher than in mature milk, providing crucial mucosal immunity.</li>
<li>Colostrum has a mild laxative effect that helps clear meconium from the infant''s intestines.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Colostrum is the initial postpartum milk rich in immunoglobulins that provides passive immunity and gut protection to the newborn.</p>','<p>C for Colostrum = Critical immunity for the newborn</p>','<p>Colostrum''s immunological benefits are so significant that it has been termed ''liquid gold'' and is particularly important for preterm infants who have underdeveloped immune systems.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mature milk:</strong> Has lower immunoglobulin content compared to colostrum and is produced later in the lactation period.</li>
<li><strong>Transitional milk:</strong> An intermediate phase between colostrum and mature milk with gradually changing composition.</li>
<li><strong>Foremilk:</strong> The initial milk released during a feeding, distinguished by its lower fat content, not by immunoglobulin richness.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["colostrum","immunoglobulins","first milk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.474Z','2026-10-18T19:30:18.167Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6j004ie9oivo3zbtbl','mcq','Which of the following causes ( Egg shaped cardiac silhouette ) in chest X-ray:','["Patent ductus arteriosus","Transposition of the Great Arteries","Tetralogy of Fallot","Total Anomaly pulmonary venous return"]','Transposition of the Great Arteries','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["x-ray","cardiac-silhouette","tga"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.659Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.659Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h3s000zqjlrp094615w','mcq','Lactogenesis 1 begins','["After delivery","During pregnancy","2 weeks postpartum","After weaning"]','During pregnancy','<p><strong>Why During pregnancy is correct:</strong> Lactogenesis 1 begins during pregnancy under the influence of high progesterone and estrogen levels, which stimulate the development of the mammary glands and milk-producing alveolar cells. During this phase, the breast tissue proliferates and differentiates in preparation for lactation, but high progesterone levels inhibit actual milk secretion. The process involves the production of casein and lactose, but milk is not secreted in significant quantities until after delivery when progesterone levels drop dramatically.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>After delivery:</strong> This marks the beginning of Lactogenesis 2, not Lactogenesis 1, when milk secretion actually begins following the drop in progesterone levels.</li>
<li><strong>2 weeks postpartum:</strong> This timeframe corresponds to the establishment of established lactation (Lactogenesis 3), when milk production is fully regulated by supply and demand.</li>
<li><strong>After weaning:</strong> This would represent the involution phase of the breast tissue, not the initiation of lactation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lactogenesis 1 occurs during pregnancy but is inhibited by high progesterone levels.</li>
<li>The abrupt drop in progesterone levels after delivery triggers Lactogenesis 2, when copious milk secretion begins.</li>
<li>Hormonal preparation for lactation begins as early as the second trimester of pregnancy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Lactogenesis 1 begins during pregnancy when breast tissue develops but milk secretion is inhibited by high progesterone levels.</p>','<p>Lactogenesis 1 = Long before delivery (during pregnancy)</p>','<p>Even during pregnancy, the breasts are preparing for lactation, which is why some women may notice colostrum leakage during the third trimester.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>After delivery:</strong> This is when Lactogenesis 2 begins, not Lactogenesis 1.</li>
<li><strong>2 weeks postpartum:</strong> This corresponds to Lactogenesis 3, when lactation is fully established.</li>
<li><strong>After weaning:</strong> This represents breast involution, not lactogenesis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["lactogenesis","pregnancy","stages"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.480Z','2026-10-18T19:30:18.169Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h610010qjlrwex4spoz','mcq','The start of transition from Colostrum to mature milk occur in','["Lactogenesis 1","Lactogenesis 2","Lactogenesis 3","Lactogenesis 4"]','Lactogenesis 2','<p><strong>Why Lactogenesis 2 is correct:</strong> Lactogenesis 2 is the stage when copious milk secretion begins, typically occurring 30-40 hours after delivery, and this phase marks the transition from colostrum to mature milk. During this stage, the abrupt decline in progesterone levels removes the inhibitory effect on milk production, while prolactin levels remain high to stimulate milk synthesis. The composition of milk gradually changes from the thick, yellow colostrum to thinner, whiter mature milk over approximately 10-14 days, with decreasing concentrations of immunoglobulins and increasing fat content.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lactogenesis 1:</strong> This occurs during pregnancy and involves breast development but not actual milk secretion, as progesterone inhibits milk production.</li>
<li><strong>Lactogenesis 3:</strong> This represents the maintenance phase of lactation (approximately 2 weeks postpartum), when milk production is fully regulated by supply and demand, not the transition phase.</li>
<li><strong>Lactogenesis 4:</strong> This is not a recognized stage in the standard classification of lactogenesis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lactogenesis 2 typically begins 30-40 hours postpartum and is characterized by the onset of copious milk secretion.</li>
<li>The transition from colostrum to mature milk occurs gradually during Lactogenesis 2, taking approximately 10-14 days.</li>
<li>Successful initiation of Lactogenesis 2 depends on frequent breast stimulation and removal of milk to establish adequate milk supply.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Lactogenesis 2 is the phase when milk secretion begins in earnest, marking the transition from colostrum to mature milk as progesterone levels drop postpartum.</p>','<p>Lactogenesis 2 = Transition (from colostrum to mature milk)</p>','<p>The timing of Lactogenesis 2 can be delayed in mothers who had cesarean sections due to surgical stress and delayed initial breastfeeding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lactogenesis 1:</strong> Occurs during pregnancy and involves breast development without actual milk secretion.</li>
<li><strong>Lactogenesis 3:</strong> Represents the maintenance phase of lactation after it''s fully established, not the transition period.</li>
<li><strong>Lactogenesis 4:</strong> Not a recognized stage in lactogenesis classification.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Breast feeding','["lactogenesis","transition","mature milk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.561Z','2026-10-18T19:30:18.170Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmve80g68000xdbucnzwvienb','mcq','A 58-year-old male with hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 45 mL/min/1.73m²) is started on lisinopril. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which ACE inhibitors provide renal protection in diabetic nephropathy, and what is the most common side effect that may lead to discontinuation of therapy?','["A) ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin II-mediated glomerular hypertension and increase bradykinin levels; dry cough occurs in 5-35% of patients due to bradykinin-mediated sensitization of airway sensory nerves","B) ACE inhibitors directly block aldosterone receptors in the renal tubules; hyperkalemia is the most common side effect requiring discontinuation","C) ACE inhibitors inhibit renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus; hypotension is the most common side effect leading to therapy discontinuation","D) ACE inhibitors promote sodium excretion through direct tubular action; angioedema is the most common side effect requiring discontinuation"]','A) ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin II-mediated glomerular hypertension and increase bradykinin levels; dry cough occurs in 5-35% of patients due to bradykinin-mediated sensitization of airway sensory nerves','ACE inhibitors provide renal protection in diabetic nephropathy primarily by reducing angiotensin II-mediated glomerular hypertension, which decreases intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria. Additionally, ACE inhibitors increase bradykinin levels by preventing its degradation, which contributes to vasodilation and further reduces glomerular pressure. The most common side effect of ACE inhibitors is dry cough, occurring in 5-35% of patients, due to bradykinin-mediated sensitization of airway sensory nerves via rapidly adapting stretch receptors and C-fiber receptors that release neurokinin, leading to cough reflex activation (Borghi et al., 2023; Pinto et al., 2020).','Remember ACE = Angiotensin Converting Enzyme inhibition → Less angiotensin II + more bradykinin → Renal protection + cough','ACE inhibitors are first-line for diabetic nephropathy with proteinuria, but the characteristic dry cough (mediated by bradykinin accumulation) occurs in up to 35% of patients and is often the reason for switching to ARBs, which don''t affect bradykinin levels.','Option B is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not directly block aldosterone receptors (that''s ARBs), and while hyperkalemia is a concern, it''s not the most common reason for discontinuation. Option C is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not primarily inhibit renin release, and hypotension is not the most common side effect leading to discontinuation. Option D is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not primarily promote sodium excretion through direct tubular action, and angioedema, while serious, occurs in only 0.1-0.2% of patients, not making it the most common side effect.','Medium','cardiovascular','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","pharmacology","nephrology","diabetes","hypertension"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-18T19:30:46.880Z','2026-10-18T19:30:46.880Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7w001cqjlrz9a5pobl','mcq','7-month-old infant start complementary feeding with mashed potatoes and honey to help immunity. What is the most important concern','["Low Vitamin C","Poor food intake","Risk of infant botulism from honey","Excess fiber intake"]','Risk of infant botulism from honey','<p><strong>Why Risk of infant botulism from honey is correct:</strong> Honey is the most common source of Clostridium botulinum spores, which can germinate in the immature gastrointestinal tract of infants under 12 months, producing botulinum toxin that causes infant botulism. This rare but serious condition leads to constipation, poor feeding, weakness, and potentially respiratory failure. The concern with giving honey to a 7-month-old is that their intestinal microbiome is still developing and lacks the protective bacteria that prevent C. botulinum spore germination in older children and adults. The mashed potatoes, while nutritionally incomplete as a complementary food, do not pose this specific risk.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Low Vitamin C:</strong> While mashed potatoes alone might not provide optimal nutrition, vitamin C deficiency is not the immediate concern with this specific feeding combination. - <strong>Poor food intake:</strong> While honey might increase palatability, the immediate concern is not inadequate intake but rather the specific danger of botulism. - <strong>Excess fiber intake:</strong> Potatoes contain fiber, but this is not a significant concern at this age and doesn''t pose the immediate danger that botulism does.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Infant botulism has been associated with honey consumption in infants under 12 months, with symptoms typically appearing 18-36 hours after ingestion. - The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends avoiding honey in infants under 12 months due to the risk of botulism. - Complementary foods should be introduced one at a time, starting with iron-fortified cereals, followed by vegetables, fruits, and meats, while avoiding honey and other potential botulism risks.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Honey should never be given to infants under 12 months due to the risk of life-threatening botulism from Clostridium botulinum spores.</p>','<p>Honey + Baby = Botulism Danger (HBD)</p>','<p>Infant botulism can present with constipation as the first sign, followed by progressive weakness and poor feeding, making it important to consider in any infant with these symptoms.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Low Vitamin C:</strong> Not the immediate concern with this specific feeding combination. - <strong>Poor food intake:</strong> While nutritionally incomplete, this is not the most urgent issue. - <strong>Excess fiber intake:</strong> Potatoes contain fiber but this is not a significant concern at this age.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Infant feeding safety','["complementary feeding","honey","botulism"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.628Z','2026-10-18T19:30:52.765Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h82001eqjlrah6rbayf','mcq','BFHI hospitals finds that nurses give routinely glucose 5% orally for newborns after 1 hour of birth. Which step is violated','["Step 3:- Inform all mother about benefits of Breastfeeding","Step 6:- No food or drink other than breast milk unless medically indicated","Avoid artificial teats","Step 10:- promote support groups"]','Step 6:- No food or drink other than breast milk unless medically indicated','<p><strong>Why Step 6:- No food or drink other than breast milk unless medically indicated is correct:</strong> The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) Step 6 explicitly prohibits giving newborns any food or drink other than breast milk unless there is a medical indication. Glucose 5% solution is not breast milk and has no medical justification for routine administration to healthy newborns after birth. This practice interferes with the establishment of breastfeeding by reducing the infant''s hunger drive, thereby decreasing breastfeeding frequency and potentially compromising milk supply. The early introduction of non-breast milk foods also disrupts the critical period for establishing proper breastfeeding techniques and maternal-infant bonding.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Step 3:- Inform all mother about benefits of Breastfeeding:</strong> While important, this step involves education, not the physical act of providing alternative nutrition. - <strong>Avoid artificial teats:</strong> This refers to pacifiers or bottle nipples, not glucose solutions. - <strong>Step 10:- promote support groups:</strong> This is a post-discharge support measure, not related to in-hospital feeding practices.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - BFHI Step 6 is designed to prevent unnecessary supplementation that can interfere with breastfeeding establishment. - Early supplementation with glucose water or formula is associated with shorter breastfeeding duration. - The first hour after birth is critical for initiating breastfeeding and establishing milk supply.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> BFHI Step 6 prohibits giving newborns anything other than breast milk unless medically necessary, making glucose 5% administration a violation.</p>','<p>BFHI Step 6: Breast Milk Only - No Exceptions (BMONE)</p>','<p>Unnecessary supplementation in the first few hours after birth can significantly reduce breastfeeding success rates by decreasing infant suckling at the breast.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Step 3:</strong> This involves education, not providing alternative nutrition. - <strong>Avoid artificial teats:</strong> This refers to pacifiers/bottles, not glucose solutions. - <strong>Step 10:</strong> This is about post-discharge support, not in-hospital feeding.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','BFHI guidelines','["bfhi","glucose","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.634Z','2026-10-18T19:30:56.195Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8c001kqjlrq74jns9q','mcq','4-month-old infant fed with concentrated formula to help with weight gain. What is the expected complication','["Rickets","Vitamin D toxicity","Hypoglycemia","Hypernatremia"]','Hypernatremia','<p><strong>Why Hypernatremia is correct:</strong> Concentrated formula has a higher solute load than standard formula, meaning it contains more nutrients per volume of water. When an infant consumes concentrated formula without adequate water intake, the kidneys cannot excrete the excess solutes effectively, leading to hypernatremia (elevated serum sodium). This occurs because the high concentration of solutes creates an osmotic gradient that draws water from the extracellular space into the gastrointestinal tract, worsening dehydration and further concentrating the blood.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rickets:</strong> Rickets results from vitamin D deficiency or impaired calcium metabolism, not from formula concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin D toxicity:</strong> This would occur from excessive vitamin D intake, not from formula concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoglycemia:</strong> Concentrated formula would actually provide more calories, reducing the risk of hypoglycemia rather than causing it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypernatremia in infants is characterized by extreme thirst, lethargy, irritability, and can lead to seizures and brain damage if not corrected slowly.</li>
<li>The kidneys of young infants have limited concentrating ability, making them particularly vulnerable to hypernatremia when given concentrated formulas.</li>
<li>Treatment of hypernatremia requires slow correction with hypotonic fluids to prevent cerebral edema.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Concentrated formula causes hypernatremia by delivering excess solutes without adequate water for dilution.</p>','<p>C.H.O. - Concentrated formula, Hypernatremia, Osmotic imbalance</p>','<p>Hypernatremia in infants is a medical emergency that requires slow correction to prevent cerebral edema and neurological damage.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Rickets:</strong> Results from vitamin D deficiency, not formula concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin D toxicity:</strong> Occurs from excessive vitamin D intake, not formula concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoglycemia:</strong> Concentrated formula provides more calories, reducing hypoglycemia risk.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Infant formula','["formula concentration","hypernatremia","infant nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.644Z','2026-10-18T19:31:12.575Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8f001lqjlrveapotx4','mcq','Around 6 months of age, complementary feeding must be introduced because breast milk or formula alone becomes insufficient to meet the infant''s physiological need for:','["Lactose and fluids.","Passive immunoglobulins.","Iron and Zinc.","Whey-to-casein proteins."]','Iron and Zinc.','<p><strong>Why Iron and Zinc is correct:</strong> Around 6 months of age, an infant''s iron stores, which were built during gestation and supplemented by breast milk or formula, become depleted. Breast milk contains very little iron (about 0.3 mg/L), which is insufficient to meet the increasing demands of rapid growth and development. Similarly, zinc levels in breast milk decline after 6 months, and the infant''s requirement increases significantly for growth, immune function, and neurological development. Complementary foods rich in iron and zinc are essential to prevent deficiencies that can lead to anemia, growth failure, and developmental delays.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lactose and fluids:</strong> Breast milk and formula continue to provide adequate lactose and fluids beyond 6 months of age.</li>
<li><strong>Passive immunoglobulins:</strong> While breast milk provides immunological benefits, these are not the primary reason for introducing complementary foods at 6 months.</li>
<li><strong>Whey-to-casein proteins:</strong> The protein composition of breast milk changes naturally over time, but this is not the driving factor for introducing complementary foods.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Iron deficiency is the most common nutritional deficiency worldwide in infants and young children, with peak incidence between 6-24 months.</li>
<li>The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends introducing iron-rich complementary foods at around 6 months of age, particularly for breastfed infants.</li>
<li>Zinc deficiency can lead to growth retardation, impaired immune function, and delayed wound healing in infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Complementary foods are introduced at 6 months primarily to provide adequate iron and zinc, which breast milk and formula cannot supply in sufficient quantities.</p>','<p>I.Z. - Iron and Zinc are the critical nutrients needed when complementary feeding begins at 6 months.</p>','<p>Iron deficiency in infancy can cause permanent cognitive deficits, making early introduction of iron-rich foods critical.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lactose and fluids:</strong> Breast milk and formula continue to provide adequate lactose and fluids beyond 6 months.</li>
<li><strong>Passive immunoglobulins:</strong> While beneficial, these are not the primary reason for introducing complementary foods.</li>
<li><strong>Whey-to-casein proteins:</strong> This is not the driving factor for introducing complementary foods.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Complementary feeding','["complementary feeding","iron","zinc"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.647Z','2026-10-18T19:31:12.576Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8v001tqjlr2cirrnkw','mcq','65- Mother complains that the milk looks thin and watery at the start of feeding. Which of the following is true?','["Abnormal milk","It should be discarded","It is Foremilk, which is higher in lactose for hydration of the baby","It lacks all nutrients"]','It is Foremilk, which is higher in lactose for hydration of the baby','<p><strong>Why It is Foremilk, which is higher in lactose for hydration of the baby is correct:</strong> Foremilk is the thin, watery milk that is released at the beginning of a feeding session. It is rich in lactose, water, and proteins but lower in fat compared to hindmilk, which comes later in the feeding. This composition is physiologically designed to first quench the infant''s thirst and provide hydration before delivering the higher-calorie hindmilk for satiety and weight gain. The lactose content in foremilk helps maintain blood glucose levels and provides readily available energy, while the watery nature helps maintain fluid balance in the infant.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Abnormal milk:</strong> The thin, watery appearance of foremilk is completely normal and expected, not abnormal in any way.</li>
<li><strong>It should be discarded:</strong> Discarding foremilk would deprive the infant of essential hydration and nutrients, as it''s a normal and important part of breastfeeding.</li>
<li><strong>It lacks all nutrients:</strong> Foremilk actually contains significant amounts of lactose, water-soluble vitamins, and immunological factors, though it has less fat than hindmilk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foremilk and hindmilk are not different types of milk but rather different compositions of the same milk throughout a feeding session.</li>
<li>The fat content of milk increases as the feeding progresses, with hindmilk containing 2-3 times more fat than foremilk.</li>
<li>Ensuring the infant feeds long enough to access hindmilk is important for adequate calorie intake and weight gain.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The thin, watery milk at the start of feeding is normal foremilk, designed to hydrate the baby before the richer hindmilk provides calories for growth.</p>','<p>Foremilk = First in line, First for thirst (F for First, F for Thirst)</p>','<p>The appearance of breast milk changes color and consistency throughout a feeding session, which is normal and reflects the changing composition from foremilk to hindmilk.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abnormal milk:</strong> The thin appearance is normal, not abnormal.</li>
<li><strong>It should be discarded:</strong> Foremilk is essential for hydration and should never be discarded.</li>
<li><strong>It lacks all nutrients:</strong> Foremilk contains significant lactose and water-soluble nutrients.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["foremilk","breast milk composition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.663Z','2026-10-18T19:31:44.683Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8w001uqjlr70z7ireu','mcq','66- 7-month-old infant is starting complementary feeding. Parents plan to give him grapes. What is the problem with grapes?','["Choking","Mash with some sugar","Give with small amount","Squeeze"]','Choking','<p><strong>Why Choking is correct:</strong> Grapes pose a significant choking hazard for infants and young children due to their small size, smooth texture, and round shape. When a whole grape is swallowed, it can completely block the airway because it''s large enough to occlude the trachea but small enough to pass easily into the esophagus. The American Academy of Pediatrics specifically identifies grapes as a high-risk food for young children, along with other round, firm foods like nuts and hard candies. The risk is particularly high in infants under 4 years old who haven''t fully developed their chewing and swallowing coordination.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mash with some sugar:</strong> Adding sugar to grapes is unnecessary and potentially harmful, as it adds empty calories and can contribute to early dental caries and poor eating habits.</li>
<li><strong>Give with small amount:</strong> While portion control is important, the size and shape of grapes make them dangerous regardless of the quantity given.</li>
<li><strong>Squeeze:</strong> Squeezing grapes doesn''t eliminate the choking hazard, as the grape skin and pulp can still form a bolus that blocks the airway.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Whole grapes should never be given to children under 4 years old due to their high choking risk.</li>
<li>Safe preparation of grapes for young children involves cutting them lengthwise into quarters or smaller pieces.</li>
<li>The ''choke test'' for solid foods: if a food can pass through a toilet paper tube, it''s small enough to choke a child.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Grapes are a serious choking hazard for infants because their size and shape can completely block the airway, requiring preparation by cutting into small pieces before serving.</p>','<p>Grapes = Great Round Airway Blockers (GRAB) - remember to cut them!</p>','<p>When introducing solid foods to infants, always cut round, firm foods like grapes, cherry tomatoes, and hot dogs into small, lengthwise pieces to reduce choking risk.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mash with some sugar:</strong> Adding sugar is unnecessary and potentially harmful.</li>
<li><strong>Give with small amount:</strong> The size and shape make grapes dangerous regardless of quantity.</li>
<li><strong>Squeeze:</strong> Squeezing doesn''t eliminate the choking hazard from the formed bolus.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pediatrics','Nutrition','["complementary feeding","choking hazard","solid foods"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.664Z','2026-10-18T19:31:44.684Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h8x001vqjlr15rxq5i0','mcq','67- Infant with confirmed Cow''s milk allergy and contraindicated breastfeeding. The best formula is:','["Extensively hydrolyzed/amino acid-based formula"]','Extensively hydrolyzed/amino acid-based formula','<p><strong>Why Extensively hydrolyzed/amino acid-based formula is correct:</strong> For infants with confirmed cow''s milk allergy (CMA) who cannot breastfeed, extensively hydrolyzed formulas (eHF) or amino acid-based formulas (AAF) are the first-line alternatives. These formulas contain proteins broken down into small peptides or free amino acids, preventing immune recognition and allergic reactions. The hydrolysis process reduces allergenicity by 90-99%, while AAFs contain no intact protein, making them completely non-allergenic. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends these formulas for moderate to severe CMA cases, as they provide adequate nutrition while eliminating the allergenic trigger.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Partially hydrolyzed formula:</strong> This option is incorrect because partially hydrolyzed formulas still contain significant amounts of intact milk protein, which can trigger allergic reactions in infants with confirmed CMA. They are primarily used for prevention rather than treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> This is incorrect because approximately 10-14% of infants with cow''s milk allergy also react to soy protein due to cross-reactivity. Soy formulas are contraindicated in infants with CMA unless they have been specifically tolerated.</li>
<li><strong>Goat''s milk formula:</strong> This is incorrect because goat''s milk protein is highly cross-reactive with cow''s milk protein, making it unsuitable for infants with confirmed CMA. The protein structures are too similar to avoid allergic reactions.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Extensively hydrolyzed formulas are first-line for moderate-severe CMA, while amino acid formulas are reserved for severe cases or multiple food allergies.</li>
<li>The diagnosis of CMA requires both clinical symptoms and confirmation through elimination-challenge or testing, as symptoms can be non-specific.</li>
<li>Approximately 2-3% of infants have cow''s milk allergy, with most outgrowing it by age 3.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For confirmed cow''s milk allergy in non-breastfed infants, use extensively hydrolyzed or amino acid-based formulas to prevent allergic reactions while ensuring proper nutrition.</p>','<p>EHFAA: Extensively Hydrolyzed Formula or Amino Acid for confirmed Allergy.</p>','<p>Always confirm cow''s milk allergy with appropriate testing before switching to specialized formulas, as many infants outgrow their allergy by age 3.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Partially hydrolyzed formula:</strong> Contains intact milk protein that can trigger allergic reactions in confirmed CMA.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> 10-14% of infants with CMA also react to soy protein due to cross-reactivity.</li>
<li><strong>Goat''s milk formula:</strong> Highly cross-reactive with cow''s milk protein, making it unsuitable for CMA.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Pediatrics','Allergy','["cow''s milk allergy","formula","hypersensitivity"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.665Z','2026-10-18T19:31:46.988Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hac0027qjlr3ogxrqnx','mcq','8- All of the following are metabolic causes of Constipation Except','["Hypothyroidism","Hypokalemia","Diabetes inspidus","Hypocalcemia"]','Hypothyroidism','<p><strong>Why Hypothyroidism is correct:</strong> Hypothyroidism is not a metabolic cause of constipation but rather an endocrine disorder. Constipation in hypothyroidism occurs due to decreased gastrointestinal motility from reduced sympathetic tone and myxedematous changes in the intestinal wall, not from a direct metabolic disturbance. The other options represent true metabolic causes: hypokalemia causes constipation through decreased intestinal smooth muscle contractility, diabetes insipidus leads to dehydration and concentrated stool causing constipation, and hypocalcemia can cause constipation through smooth muscle dysfunction and impaired neuromuscular transmission.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> Causes constipation by reducing intestinal smooth muscle contractility and peristalsis due to decreased membrane potential.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes insipidus:</strong> Leads to constipation through dehydration and concentrated, hard-to-pass stool from excessive water loss.</li>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> Can cause constipation due to smooth muscle dysfunction and impaired neuromuscular transmission in the gastrointestinal tract.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Metabolic causes of constipation directly involve electrolyte imbalances or dehydration affecting intestinal function.</li>
<li>Endocrine disorders like hypothyroidism cause constipation through different mechanisms (altered motility, myxedema).</li>
<li>Always evaluate for metabolic causes in patients with new-onset constipation, especially if other systemic symptoms are present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Constipation has metabolic causes (electrolyte imbalances, dehydration) but hypothyroidism is an endocrine disorder that causes constipation through different mechanisms.</p>','<p>Hypothyroidism is NOT metabolic - remember: ''METABOLIC'' causes constipation through direct electrolyte effects, while ''ENDOCRINE'' causes like hypothyroidism work through different pathways.</p>','<p>When evaluating constipation, always check electrolytes (especially potassium and calcium) as metabolic imbalances are common and reversible causes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> Causes constipation by reducing intestinal smooth muscle contractility.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes insipidus:</strong> Leads to constipation through dehydration and concentrated stool.</li>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> Can cause constipation due to smooth muscle dysfunction.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrinology','Constipation','["constipation","metabolic","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.716Z','2026-10-18T19:32:20.311Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hae0029qjlrvivdm3n8','mcq','13- what is the incidence of the presence of the classic triad of intussusception, (pain, a palpable sausage-shaped abdominal mass and bloody or currant jelly stool)?','["≤20% of patients","≤30% of patients","≤40% of patients","≤50% of patients"]','≤40% of patients','<p><strong>Why ≤40% of patients is correct:</strong> The classic triad of intussusception (intermittent colicky abdominal pain, a palpable sausage-shaped abdominal mass, and bloody or currant jelly stool) is present in only about 30-40% of cases. Intussusception is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children aged 3 months to 3 years, but its presentation can be variable. Many patients present with incomplete or atypical symptoms, making diagnosis challenging without imaging studies such as ultrasound.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>≤20% of patients:</strong> This underestimates the prevalence of the classic triad; studies consistently show it occurs in 30-40% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>≤30% of patients:</strong> While close to the actual rate, this is slightly below the established range of 30-40%.</li>
<li><strong>≤50% of patients:</strong> This overestimates the prevalence; the classic triad is not present in half of all cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The classic triad of intussusception is present in only about 30-40% of cases, making its absence not helpful for ruling out the diagnosis.</li>
<li>Ultrasound is the imaging modality of choice for diagnosing intussusception, showing the characteristic target sign or pseudokidney appearance.</li>
<li>Intussusception is a surgical emergency, and delay in diagnosis can lead to bowel necrosis and perforation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The classic triad of intussusception is present in only about 30-40% of cases, so its absence should not prevent consideration of this diagnosis in a child with suggestive symptoms.</p>','<p>Intussusception triad: ''Pain-Mass-Blood'' - but remember it''s only present in ''30-40%'' of cases.</p>','<p>Intussusception should be considered in any child with acute, intermittent abdominal pain, even without the classic triad of symptoms.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>≤20% of patients:</strong> Underestimates the prevalence of the classic triad.</li>
<li><strong>≤30% of patients:</strong> Slightly below the established range of 30-40%.</li>
<li><strong>≤50% of patients:</strong> Overestimates the prevalence; the classic triad is not present in half of all cases.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastroenterology','Intussusception','["intussusception","classic triad","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.718Z','2026-10-18T19:32:20.315Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56haf002aqjlrzyl6wx9q','mcq','14- What percentage of constipation cases in children are functional (without organic cause)?','["75%","85%","95%","100%"]','95%','<p><strong>Why 95% is correct:</strong> The vast majority of childhood constipation cases (approximately 95%) are functional, meaning they lack an identifiable organic cause. This functional constipation results from a combination of factors including stool withholding due to painful defecation, learned behaviors, and often a cycle of rectal distension leading to decreased rectal sensitivity. The Rome IV criteria specifically define pediatric functional constipation based on duration, frequency, and stool characteristics, with organic causes being relatively uncommon in otherwise healthy children.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>75%:</strong> This underestimates the prevalence of functional constipation, as organic causes account for only about 5% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>85%:</strong> While close, this still underestimates the true prevalence of functional constipation in the pediatric population.</li>
<li><strong>100%:</strong> This is incorrect because approximately 5% of childhood constipation cases have organic causes such as Hirschsprung''s disease, hypothyroidism, cystic fibrosis, or spinal cord abnormalities.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Functional constipation is the most common cause of constipation in children, accounting for 95% of cases.</li>
<li>Organic causes should be suspected when constipation presents with red flags such as failure to thrive, bloody stools, vomiting, or delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours of life.</li>
<li>The Rome IV criteria are used to diagnose functional constipation in children, requiring at least two of the following for at least 1 month: ≤2 defecations per week, fecal incontinence, retentive posturing, painful defecation, large fecal mass in the rectum, or palpable abdominal mass.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation accounts for 95% of childhood constipation cases, with organic causes being rare but important to identify through red flags.</p>','<p>95% Functional = ''Almost All Kids'' (AAK) - Almost All Kids have functional constipation.</p>','<p>When evaluating a child with constipation, always consider the timing of meconium passage—delayed beyond 48 hours is a red flag for Hirschsprung''s disease, an organic cause requiring prompt diagnosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>75%:</strong> This underestimates the prevalence of functional constipation, as organic causes account for only about 5% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>85%:</strong> While close, this still underestimates the true prevalence of functional constipation in the pediatric population.</li>
<li><strong>100%:</strong> This is incorrect because approximately 5% of childhood constipation cases have organic causes such as Hirschsprung''s disease, hypothyroidism, cystic fibrosis, or spinal cord abnormalities.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["functional constipation","prevalence","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.719Z','2026-10-18T19:32:35.076Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hag002bqjlrtvwibmgj','mcq','15- When is the definitive surgical repair for Hirschsprung''s disease usually performed in children?','["Within the first month of life","Between 6 and 12 months of age","Between 2 and 4 years of age","After 5 years of age"]','Between 6 and 12 months of age','<p><strong>Why Between 6 and 12 months of age is correct:</strong> The definitive surgical repair for Hirschsprung''s disease is typically performed between 6 and 12 months of age when the child has stabilized post-initial interventions, gained adequate weight, and developed sufficient tissue for anastomosis. This timing allows for the child to recover from the initial colostomy (if performed), achieve adequate nutrition, and develop sufficient tissue for a successful pull-through procedure. The most common surgical approaches (Swenson, Duhamel, or Soave procedures) are technically feasible at this age with acceptable outcomes.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Within the first month of life:</strong> Performing definitive repair too early is associated with higher complication rates due to the technical challenges of working with smaller tissues and the higher risk of enterocolitis in the immediate postoperative period.</li>
<li><strong>Between 2 and 4 years of age:</strong> This delay is unnecessary as most children are stable enough for surgery by 6-12 months, and waiting longer increases the risk of complications like enterocolitis and nutritional deficiencies.</li>
<li><strong>After 5 years of age:</strong> This is significantly delayed and would expose the child to prolonged periods of complications, including chronic constipation, enterocolitis, and potential developmental issues related to the disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Initial management of Hirschsprung''s disease often involves a diverting colostomy with biopsy confirmation, followed by definitive pull-through surgery.</li>
<li>The optimal timing for definitive repair is between 6 and 12 months, balancing technical feasibility with the child''s stability and nutritional status.</li>
<li>Postoperative complications include enterocolitis, anastomotic stricture, and incontinence, with enterocolitis being the most common and potentially life-threatening.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease definitive repair is optimally performed between 6 and 12 months of age when the child has stabilized and developed adequate tissue for anastomosis.</p>','<p>6-12 Months for Hirschsprung''s Repair = ''Half-Year Hero'' - Half a year of life before definitive surgical heroics.</p>','<p>Hirschsprung''s disease requires a staged approach: initial colostomy with biopsy confirmation, followed by definitive pull-through surgery when the child is stable, typically between 6-12 months of age.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Within the first month of life:</strong> Performing definitive repair too early is associated with higher complication rates due to technical challenges and higher risk of enterocolitis.</li>
<li><strong>Between 2 and 4 years of age:</strong> This delay is unnecessary as most children are stable enough for surgery by 6-12 months, and waiting longer increases complications.</li>
<li><strong>After 5 years of age:</strong> This is significantly delayed and exposes the child to prolonged periods of complications including chronic constipation and enterocolitis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Surgery','Hirschsprung Disease','["Hirschsprung","surgery","timing"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.720Z','2026-10-18T19:32:35.078Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hah002cqjlrhwu1nc0b','mcq','16- Which of the following is a definitive diagnosis for Hirschsprung''s disease?','["Barium enema","Anorectal manometry","Rectal biopsy","Abdominal ultrasound"]','Rectal biopsy','<p><strong>Why Rectal biopsy is correct:</strong> Rectal biopsy is the definitive diagnostic test for Hirschsprung''s disease because it directly examines the presence or absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the rectal wall. The biopsy reveals an absence of ganglion cells and the presence of hypertrophied nerve trunks, which is pathognomonic for Hirschsprung''s disease. This histological examination can be performed using full-thickness or suction biopsy techniques and provides definitive confirmation of the diagnosis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Barium enema:</strong> While barium enema may show a classic transition zone between the aganglionic and ganglionic bowel, it is not diagnostic as it only demonstrates functional obstruction and cannot confirm the absence of ganglion cells.</li>
<li><strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> This test measures the anorectal reflex and can be suggestive of Hirschsprung''s disease when the reflex is absent, but it is not definitive as it cannot directly visualize the absence of ganglion cells and has false positive and negative rates.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> Ultrasound may show dilated bowel proximal to the rectum but cannot visualize the transition zone or confirm the absence of ganglion cells, making it non-diagnostic for Hirschsprung''s disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Rectal biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosing Hirschsprung''s disease, demonstrating the absence of ganglion cells and hypertrophied nerve trunks.</li>
<li>The transition zone visible on barium enema is suggestive but not diagnostic, as it represents the functional transition between aganglionic and ganglionic bowel.</li>
<li>Anorectal manometry shows an absent rectoanal inhibitory reflex in Hirschsprung''s disease but is less specific than biopsy and requires specialized equipment and expertise.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rectal biopsy is definitive for Hirschsprung''s disease as it directly demonstrates the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.</p>','<p>Biopsy for Hirschsprung''s = ''Ganglion Check'' - The biopsy checks for the missing ganglion cells that define the disease.</p>','<p>In a newborn with delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours, a rectal biopsy should be performed urgently to rule out Hirschsprung''s disease, as early diagnosis prevents life-threatening complications like enterocolitis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Barium enema:</strong> While it may show a transition zone, it only demonstrates functional obstruction and cannot confirm the absence of ganglion cells.</li>
<li><strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> This measures the anorectal reflex and can be suggestive but is not definitive as it cannot directly visualize ganglion cells.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> It may show dilated bowel but cannot visualize the transition zone or confirm the absence of ganglion cells.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pathology','Hirschsprung Disease','["Hirschsprung","diagnosis","biopsy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.721Z','2026-10-18T19:32:35.080Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6j004je9oi3rc36a3m','mcq','Which of the following is not a type of ventricular septal defect (VSD).','["Inlet","outlet","Perimembranous","sinus venosus"]','outlet','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["vsd","types","anatomy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.659Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.659Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hal002fqjlrfqmmvy0x','mcq','Meckel''s diverticulum has been conveniently referred to by the "rule of 2s," which explains the classic presentation of this congenital anomaly. Which one of the following is FALSE in this rule?','["found in approximately 2% of the general population","usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve","approximately 2 inches in length","found twice as commonly in females"]','found twice as commonly in females','<p><strong>Why found twice as commonly in females is correct:</strong> The statement that Meckel''s diverticulum is found twice as commonly in females is false, making it the correct answer to this question. The "rule of 2s" for Meckel''s diverticulum includes: approximately 2% of the general population, usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve, approximately 2 inches in length, and presenting symptoms within the first 2 years of life. However, there is no gender predilection in the incidence of Meckel''s diverticulum; it occurs equally in males and females. The false statement about gender predilection is a common misconception that distractors may exploit.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>found in approximately 2% of the general population:</strong> This is correct according to the rule of 2s and represents the prevalence of Meckel''s diverticulum in the population.</li>
<li><strong>usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve:</strong> This is correct as Meckel''s diverticulum is typically found within 2 feet (about 50 cm) of the ileocecal valve.</li>
<li><strong>approximately 2 inches in length:</strong> This is correct as Meckel''s diverticulum is typically about 2 inches (5 cm) in length.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The "rule of 2s" is a helpful mnemonic for remembering key facts about Meckel''s diverticulum, but it''s not absolute and has exceptions.</li>
<li>Meckel''s diverticulum is a true diverticulum (containing all layers of the intestinal wall) and is a remnant of the vitelline duct.</li>
<li>While the rule states symptoms typically present within the first 2 years, bleeding can occur at any age, though it''s most common in children under 10.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The false statement in the "rule of 2s" for Meckel''s diverticulum is that it occurs twice as commonly in females, when in fact there is no gender predilection.</p>','<p>Rule of 2s: 2% prevalence, 2 feet from valve, 2 inches long, 2 years of age, no gender difference.</p>','<p>Meckel''s diverticulum has no gender predilection and occurs equally in males and females despite common misconceptions.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>found in approximately 2% of the general population:</strong> This is correct according to the rule of 2s.</li>
<li><strong>usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve:</strong> This is correct as it''s typically found within 50cm of the valve.</li>
<li><strong>approximately 2 inches in length:</strong> This is correct as it''s typically about 5cm in length.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Meckel''s Diverticulum','["meckel","rule of 2s","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.725Z','2026-10-18T19:32:39.617Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hax002nqjlrfrcrs310','mcq','Test used to differentiate maternal from neonatal blood in UGIB ?','["Occult blood test","Hb electrophoresis","Blood group test","Apt Downey test"]','Apt Downey test','<p><strong>Why Apt Downey test is correct:</strong> The Apt-Downey test is specifically designed to differentiate maternal from neonatal blood in cases of upper GI bleeding (UGIB) by exploiting the differences in hemoglobin F (HbF) content between maternal and fetal blood. Maternal blood contains predominantly adult hemoglobin (HbA), while neonatal blood contains high levels of fetal hemoglobin (HbF). The test uses alkali denaturation, where HbF is resistant to alkaline denaturation and remains pink, while HbA is denatured and turns brownish-yellow. This color difference allows for clear identification of the blood source.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Occult blood test:</strong> This test only detects the presence of blood but cannot differentiate between maternal and neonatal blood sources.</li>
<li><strong>Hb electrophoresis:</strong> While it can identify different hemoglobin types, it is not the test of choice for rapid differentiation in acute UGIB scenarios and is more complex and time-consuming.</li>
<li><strong>Blood group test:</strong> Blood group testing can identify if the blood is of maternal or neonatal type if they differ, but it cannot definitively confirm the source if blood groups are compatible or in cases of maternal-fetal hemorrhage with same blood type.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The Apt-Downey test is rapid, simple, and specifically designed for differentiating maternal from neonatal blood in UGIB.</li>
<li>HbF constitutes approximately 60-80% of total hemoglobin in neonates at birth, compared to less than 1% in adults.</li>
<li>A positive Apt-Downey test (persistent pink color after alkali treatment) indicates neonatal blood source.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The Apt-Downey test differentiates maternal from neonatal blood in UGIB by exploiting the resistance of fetal hemoglobin to alkaline denaturation.</p>','<p>Apt-Downey: ''A'' for Adult hemoglobin denatures, ''F'' for Fetal hemoglobin persists</p>','<p>The Apt-Downey test is particularly valuable in cases of suspected swallowed maternal blood versus true neonatal bleeding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Occult blood test:</strong> Only detects blood presence, cannot differentiate sources.</li>
<li><strong>Hb electrophoresis:</strong> Too complex and time-consuming for acute UGIB differentiation.</li>
<li><strong>Blood group test:</strong> Cannot confirm source if blood types are compatible.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Gastrointestinal Bleeding','["gi bleeding","neonate","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.737Z','2026-10-18T19:33:06.910Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbp0024o7lr6gdxpo5i','mcq','Maintenance of diarrhea for how long','["2 months","6 months","4 months","2-6 months"]','2-6 months','<p><strong>Why 2-6 months is correct:</strong> The duration of diarrhea required for a diagnosis of chronic diarrhea is defined as symptoms lasting for at least 2-6 months. This timeframe is established because acute diarrhea typically resolves within 14 days, persistent diarrhea lasts between 14 days and 2 months, and chronic diarrhea extends beyond 2-6 months. This distinction is clinically important as it guides the diagnostic approach and management strategies, with chronic diarrhea often requiring more extensive evaluation for underlying conditions like inflammatory bowel disease, malabsorption syndromes, or chronic infections. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>2 months:</strong> This is too short, as diarrhea lasting 2 months would be classified as persistent rather than chronic diarrhea. - <strong>6 months:</strong> While diarrhea lasting 6 months would certainly be considered chronic, the threshold for diagnosis is actually lower at 2 months. - <strong>4 months:</strong> This falls within the 2-6 month range but is not the specific threshold that defines when diarrhea transitions from persistent to chronic. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Chronic diarrhea can be classified based on pathophysiology into osmotic, secretory, exudative, and dysmotility types. - The evaluation should include a thorough history, physical exam, and targeted testing based on the suspected etiology. - Common causes of chronic diarrhea include IBD, microscopic colitis, chronic infections, and malabsorption disorders. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Chronic diarrhea is defined by symptoms lasting 2-6 months or longer, distinguishing it from acute and persistent diarrhea.</p>','<p>Chronic diarrhea: ''2-6 months'' - remember that anything beyond 2 months may be chronic, but the formal definition requires at least 2-6 months of symptoms.</p>','<p>The duration of diarrhea is a critical factor in determining the diagnostic approach, with chronic diarrhea requiring more comprehensive evaluation than acute or persistent cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>2 months:</strong> This is the cutoff for persistent diarrhea, not chronic diarrhea. - <strong>6 months:</strong> While chronic, this is not the minimum threshold for diagnosis. - <strong>4 months:</strong> This falls within the range but isn''t the specific defining threshold.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Persistent Diarrhea','["diarrhea","duration","maintenance"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.141Z','2026-10-19T03:43:06.806Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hc00034qjlr9qa5f3s6','mcq','Which of the following statements is right about diarrhea:','["Passage of 3 or more stools /day","Causes more than 50%of under-five mortality","Change in stool consistency rather than frequency","Mostly associated with exclusive breastfeeding"]','Change in stool consistency rather than frequency','<p><strong>Why Change in stool consistency rather than frequency is correct:</strong> The definition of diarrhea in medical practice focuses on a change in stool consistency to a more liquid or loose form rather than solely on stool frequency. This is because normal stool frequency varies significantly among individuals (ranging from 3 times per day to 3 times per week), making frequency alone an unreliable indicator. The consistency change reflects the underlying pathophysiology of impaired water and electrolyte absorption or increased secretion in the intestinal tract. The World Health Organization (WHO) and other medical bodies emphasize consistency as the primary criterion for diagnosing diarrhea, particularly in clinical research and epidemiological studies.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Passage of 3 or more stools/day:</strong> This is incorrect because normal stool frequency varies widely among individuals, and some healthy people may have 3 or more bowel movements daily without having diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Causes more than 50% of under-five mortality:</strong> While diarrhea is a significant cause of child mortality worldwide, it accounts for approximately 9% of under-five deaths, not 50%.</li>
<li><strong>Mostly associated with exclusive breastfeeding:</strong> This is incorrect as exclusive breastfeeding is actually protective against diarrhea, reducing the risk of gastrointestinal infections in infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Diarrhea is defined by a change in stool consistency to loose or liquid form, not by frequency.</li>
<li>Normal stool frequency varies from person to person (3 times/day to 3 times/week).</li>
<li>The WHO emphasizes consistency as the primary criterion for diagnosing diarrhea in clinical practice.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Diarrhea is defined by a change in stool consistency to loose or liquid form, not by stool frequency, which varies widely among healthy individuals.</p>','<p>C-C-F: Consistency over Count for diagnosis of diarrhea</p>','<p>In clinical practice, always assess stool consistency rather than frequency when evaluating for diarrhea, especially in infants where normal patterns vary significantly.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Passage of 3 or more stools/day:</strong> Normal frequency varies widely, making this an unreliable indicator.</li>
<li><strong>Causes more than 50% of under-five mortality:</strong> Diarrhea causes approximately 9% of under-five deaths, not 50%.</li>
<li><strong>Mostly associated with exclusive breastfeeding:</strong> Exclusive breastfeeding is protective against diarrhea, not a cause.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Definition','["definition","consistency","frequency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.776Z','2026-10-18T19:40:39.110Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hc10035qjlrhbh0s5vn','mcq','Osmotic diarrhea is characterized by:','["Large amount of loose watery stool","Explosive acidic stool","Bloody mucoid stool","Steatorrhea"]','Explosive acidic stool','<p><strong>Why Explosive acidic stool is correct:</strong> Osmotic diarrhea occurs when unabsorbed solutes in the intestinal lumen retain water, leading to watery stools. In osmotic diarrhea, particularly when carbohydrates are involved (as in lactose intolerance), undigested sugars are fermented by colonic bacteria, producing short-chain fatty acids and gases. This fermentation lowers stool pH, making it acidic. The characteristic ''explosive'' nature results from the accumulation of gas (hydrogen, methane, carbon dioxide) from bacterial fermentation, which creates pressure and urgency. This presentation is classic for osmotic diarrhea caused by carbohydrate malabsorption.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Large amount of loose watery stool:</strong> This describes secretory diarrhea, which is characterized by large volumes of watery stool without blood or mucus, and persists during fasting.</li>
<li><strong>Bloody mucoid stool:</strong> This is characteristic of invasive infectious diarrhea (like Shigella, Campylobacter) or inflammatory bowel disease, where there''s damage to the colonic mucosa.</li>
<li><strong>Steatorrhea:</strong> This refers to fatty, greasy stools resulting from fat malabsorption (as in pancreatic insufficiency or celiac disease), not osmotic diarrhea.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Osmotic diarrhea results from unabsorbed solutes retaining water in the intestinal lumen.</li>
<li>The acidic pH in osmotic diarrhea is due to bacterial fermentation of unabsorbed carbohydrates.</li>
<li>Osmotic diarrhea typically improves with fasting, unlike secretory diarrhea.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Osmotic diarrhea presents with explosive acidic stools due to bacterial fermentation of unabsorbed carbohydrates, which produces gas and lowers stool pH.</p>','<p>O-A: Osmotic diarrhea = Acidic stool</p>','<p>Osmotic diarrhea typically resolves with fasting, while secretory diarrhea persists, helping to differentiate between these two types.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Large amount of loose watery stool:</strong> Describes secretory diarrhea, not osmotic diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Bloody mucoid stool:</strong> Characteristic of invasive infectious diarrhea or inflammatory bowel disease.</li>
<li><strong>Steatorrhea:</strong> Results from fat malabsorption, not osmotic diarrhea.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Osmotic Diarrhea','["osmotic","acidic","characteristic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.777Z','2026-10-18T19:40:39.111Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6k004ke9oi7g31p88u','mcq','Congenital acyanotic heart diseases with increase pulmonary blood flow include all the following except','["Pulmonary stenosis","Atrial septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","ventricular septal defect"]','Pulmonary stenosis','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["acyanotic","pulmonary-blood-flow","stenosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.660Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.660Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbz0033qjlrgoir5t52','mcq','Which of the following diets should be avoided in child with celiac disease?','["Soybean","Rye","Corn","Maize"]','Rye','<p><strong>Why Rye is correct:</strong> Rye should be avoided in children with celiac disease because it contains gluten proteins (specifically secalin) that trigger an autoimmune response in genetically susceptible individuals. When gluten is ingested, it deamidates by tissue transglutaminase, creating epitopes that are presented to HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 molecules, leading to T-cell activation and subsequent inflammation of the intestinal mucosa. This results in villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and malabsorption. Rye, along with wheat and barley, contains these immunogenic gluten proteins that must be strictly avoided in the gluten-free diet.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Soybean:</strong> Soybean is naturally gluten-free and safe for consumption in children with celiac disease, though cross-contamination must be considered.</li>
<li><strong>Corn:</strong> Corn is gluten-free and can be safely consumed by children with celiac disease, though some may have corn sensitivity unrelated to celiac disease.</li>
<li><strong>Maize:</strong> Maize (corn) is gluten-free and safe for children with celiac disease, as it does not contain the immunogenic gluten proteins.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Celiac disease is an autoimmune disorder triggered by gluten in genetically susceptible individuals with HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 haplotypes.</li>
<li>The gluten-free diet must exclude wheat, barley, rye, and their derivatives, but allows corn, rice, quinoa, and other naturally gluten-free grains.</li>
<li>Cross-contamination is a significant concern, as even small amounts of gluten can trigger symptoms and mucosal damage.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rye contains gluten proteins that trigger the autoimmune response in celiac disease, making it essential to avoid alongside wheat and barley.</p>','<p>WBR: Wheat, Barley, Rye - the gluten grains to avoid in celiac disease</p>','<p>Celiac disease can present with atypical symptoms including short stature, iron deficiency, and dermatitis herpetiformis, making screening important in high-risk children.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Soybean:</strong> Naturally gluten-free and safe for celiac disease patients.</li>
<li><strong>Corn:</strong> Gluten-free and safe for consumption in celiac disease.</li>
<li><strong>Maize:</strong> Another term for corn, which is gluten-free and safe.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Celiac Disease','["celiac","diet","avoid"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.775Z','2026-10-18T19:41:01.508Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hc40037qjlrhbg0z1qf','mcq','What is MOST likely causative pathogen of diarrhea after consumption of undercooked eggs?','["Shiga toxin–producing E. coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Listeria","Salmonella"]','Salmonella','<p><strong>Why Salmonella is correct:</strong> Salmonella is the most common causative pathogen of diarrhea following consumption of undercooked eggs due to its ability to colonize the chicken reproductive tract and contaminate the internal contents of eggs before shell formation. The pathogen is typically Salmonella enterica serovar Enteritidis, which can penetrate the eggshell and multiply in the yolk and albumen when eggs are not thoroughly cooked. Ingestion of contaminated eggs leads to infection, with the bacteria adhering to and invading the intestinal mucosa, causing inflammation and secretory diarrhea through the action of enterotoxins and cytotoxins.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shiga toxin–producing E. coli:</strong> While STEC can cause bloody diarrhea (hemolytic uremic syndrome), it is primarily associated with undercooked beef, not eggs.</li>
<li><strong>Staphylococcus aureus:</strong> S. aureus produces enterotoxins that cause rapid-onset vomiting and diarrhea, but it''s typically linked to contaminated dairy products, meats, or improperly handled foods, not eggs.</li>
<li><strong>Listeria:</strong> Listeria monocytogenes causes invasive disease (meningitis, sepsis) in immunocompromised individuals and pregnant women, and is associated with deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood, not eggs.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Salmonella Enteritidis specifically contaminates the internal contents of eggs, unlike other Salmonella serotypes that typically contaminate the exterior.</li>
<li>Eggs should be cooked until both the white and yolk are firm to prevent Salmonella infection.</li>
<li>The incubation period for Salmonella gastroenteritis is typically 6-72 hours after consumption.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Undercooked eggs are the classic source for Salmonella gastroenteritis due to internal contamination of the egg by this specific serovar.</p>','<p>Eggs = Enteritidis (Salmonella serovar)</p>','<p>Salmonella infection from eggs is particularly dangerous in young children, the elderly, and immunocompromised individuals due to higher risk of bacteremia.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Shiga toxin–producing E. coli:</strong> Primarily associated with undercooked beef, not eggs.</li>
<li><strong>Staphylococcus aureus:</strong> Causes rapid-onset food poisoning from contaminated dairy or meats, not eggs.</li>
<li><strong>Listeria:</strong> Associated with deli meats and soft cheeses, causing invasive disease, not typical gastroenteritis from eggs.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Foodborne Diarrhea','["salmonella","eggs","pathogen"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.780Z','2026-10-18T19:41:03.757Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdg003hqjlr4ra3he3p','mcq','Which is correct about Toddlers diarrhea','["Growth failure","Loss of weight","painless passage of stool"]','painless passage of stool','<p><strong>Why painless passage of stool is correct:</strong> Toddlers diarrhea, also known as chronic nonspecific diarrhea of childhood, is characterized by painless passage of loose, frequent stools in otherwise healthy toddlers who are growing normally. The diarrhea occurs because of the immature digestive system''s inability to handle large volumes of fluid and carbohydrates, particularly when intake of fruit juices and carbohydrates is high. The key feature is that despite the frequent stools, there is no associated pain, blood, mucus, or failure to thrive.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Growth failure:</strong> Toddlers diarrhea specifically occurs in children with normal growth and development; growth failure would suggest a more serious underlying condition.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of weight:</strong> Similar to growth failure, weight loss is not seen in toddlers diarrhea as these children maintain adequate nutritional status despite the loose stools.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Toddlers diarrhea is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring ruling out infectious, inflammatory, and malabsorptive causes.</li>
<li>Management involves dietary modification, particularly reducing excessive juice and carbohydrate intake.</li>
<li>The condition typically resolves spontaneously by age 3-4 years as the digestive system matures.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Toddlers diarrhea is defined by painless, frequent stools in a thriving child with normal growth and no evidence of organic disease.</p>','<p>TODDLER: Toddlers Only Develop Diarrhea, Lose Excess fluid, Eat Right (no growth failure).</p>','<p>Toddlers diarrhea is functional and self-limited; reassurance and dietary modification are more important than extensive testing.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Growth failure:</strong> Not present in toddlers diarrhea as these children have normal growth curves.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of weight:</strong> Absent in this condition as nutritional status remains adequate despite loose stools.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Toddlers Diarrhea','["toddlers","diarrhea","characteristic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.828Z','2026-10-18T19:41:34.861Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdk003iqjlrg2hmybcg','mcq','What is the most appropriate investigation to diagnose GERD?','["Upper endoscopy","Esophagogram","pH study of the esophagus","History of recurrent aspiration"]','pH study of the esophagus','<p><strong>Why pH study of the esophagus is correct:</strong> The pH study of the esophagus is the most appropriate investigation to diagnose GERD because it directly measures the frequency and duration of esophageal acid exposure, which is the pathophysiologic hallmark of GERD. This test quantifies the number of reflux episodes and the total time pH is below 4.0, providing objective evidence of abnormal acid reflux that correlates with symptom severity. While upper endoscopy can identify complications like esophagitis, it has poor sensitivity for diagnosing GERD as many patients have normal endoscopy despite significant reflux symptoms.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Upper endoscopy:</strong> While useful for evaluating complications of GERD, it has low sensitivity for diagnosing GERD itself as many patients have normal mucosa despite significant reflux.</li>
<li><strong>Esophagogram:</strong> This radiographic test has poor sensitivity and specificity for diagnosing GERD and is not considered a reliable diagnostic tool.</li>
<li><strong>History of recurrent aspiration:</strong> While aspiration can be a complication of severe GERD, it is not a diagnostic test and occurs only in a minority of patients with GERD.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>pH monitoring is considered the gold standard for diagnosing GERD when endoscopy is normal or when symptoms don''t respond to empiric therapy.</li>
<li>The DeMeester score combines multiple parameters from pH monitoring to quantify the severity of reflux.</li>
<li>Empiric trial of proton pump inhibitors is often used before pH testing in patients with typical GERD symptoms.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> pH study directly measures esophageal acid exposure, making it the most objective diagnostic test for GERD, especially when endoscopy is normal.</p>','<p>GERD pH: Pathophysiology is key - measure Hydrogen ions directly in the esophagus.</p>','<p>GERD diagnosis should follow a stepwise approach: start with clinical evaluation, consider empiric PPI trial, and use pH monitoring for confirmation in atypical cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Upper endoscopy:</strong> Poor sensitivity for GERD diagnosis as many patients have normal mucosa despite significant reflux.</li>
<li><strong>Esophagogram:</strong> Has poor sensitivity and specificity for diagnosing GERD and is not considered reliable.</li>
<li><strong>History of recurrent aspiration:</strong> A potential complication of severe GERD but not a diagnostic test itself.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','GERD','["diagnosis","gerd","investigation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.832Z','2026-10-18T19:41:34.861Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56heb003yqjlrnuv7ej8o','mcq','A child presented with pallor, glossitis, Neuropathy','["Vit B12 deficiency anemia"]','Vit B12 deficiency anemia','<p><strong>Why Vit B12 deficiency anemia is correct:</strong> Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis, which affects rapidly dividing cells like hematopoietic precursors. The deficiency leads to neurological manifestations including peripheral neuropathy (due to demyelination of nerve fibers) and glossitis (inflammation of the tongue mucosa) from impaired epithelial cell turnover. The classic triad of pallor (from anemia), glossitis (beefy red tongue), and neuropathy (subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord) is pathognomonic for B12 deficiency.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided:</strong> The question only presented one option, which is the correct answer.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Vitamin B12 deficiency requires both hematologic (megaloblastic anemia) and neurologic (neuropathy, glossitis) manifestations for classic presentation. - B12 deficiency can result from pernicious anemia (autoimmune destruction of parietal cells), dietary deficiency, or malabsorption (e.g., Crohn''s disease, gastric surgery). - Neurologic symptoms may precede hematologic findings and can be irreversible if treatment is delayed.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Vitamin B12 deficiency presents with the triad of megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, and neuropathy due to impaired DNA synthesis and myelin maintenance.</p>','','<p>Neurologic symptoms from B12 deficiency may be irreversible even after hematologic normalization, emphasizing early recognition and treatment.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','B12 Deficiency','["b12 deficiency","pallor","glossitis","neuropathy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.859Z','2026-10-18T19:42:21.888Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hed003zqjlrlgk8j6it','mcq','Which Folic is correct about Folic acid','["High in goat milk","Presentation at early months","All deficiency of folic acid are acquired"]','All deficiency of folic acid are acquired','<p><strong>Why All deficiency of folic acid are acquired is correct:</strong> Folic acid deficiency is exclusively acquired because humans cannot synthesize folate and must obtain it from dietary sources (leafy vegetables, fortified grains) or through supplementation. Unlike vitamin B12, which can be stored in the liver for years, folate has minimal tissue storage (only about 2-4 months), making deficiency entirely dependent on intake, absorption, or increased demand. The body cannot produce folate de novo, and congenital defects in folate metabolism are extremely rare and not classified as typical deficiency states.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>High in goat milk:</strong> Goat milk is actually low in folate compared to cow''s milk and human breast milk. - <strong>Presentation at early months:</strong> Folate deficiency typically presents later in infancy (4-6 months) when hepatic stores are depleted, not in early months when maternal reserves still provide protection.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia identical to B12 deficiency but without neurologic manifestations. - Increased demand states (pregnancy, hemolysis, rapid growth) can precipitate folate deficiency even with adequate intake. - Fortification of grains has reduced the incidence of folate deficiency in many developed countries.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Unlike B12, folate deficiency is always acquired because humans cannot synthesize it and have minimal body stores.</p>','<p>FOLATE: From Food Only, Liver Almost Totally Empty</p>','<p>Folate deficiency must be differentiated from B12 deficiency as folate replacement can mask B12 deficiency and allow neurologic progression.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>High in goat milk:</strong> Goat milk is actually low in folate compared to cow''s milk. - <strong>Presentation at early months:</strong> Folate deficiency typically presents later in infancy when hepatic stores are depleted.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Folic Acid Deficiency','["folic acid","deficiency","acquired"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.861Z','2026-10-18T19:42:21.890Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hee0040qjlreu0ykwgc','mcq','Male patient 5 years old presented with pancytopenia, café-au-lait spots, short stature and absent thumb, what is the most likely diagnosis?','["Dyskeratosis congenital","Fanconi anemia.","Blackfan-diamond.","Acquired pancytopenia."]','Fanconi anemia.','<p><strong>Why Fanconi anemia is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by bone marrow failure (presenting as pancytopenia), congenital anomalies (including skeletal abnormalities like absent or hypoplastic thumbs), and café-au-lait spots. The underlying defect involves DNA repair pathway mutations (particularly in FANC genes), leading to genomic instability and hypersensitivity to DNA cross-linking agents. The classic triad of pancytopenia, congenital anomalies, and café-au-lait spots is highly suggestive of Fanconi anemia.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> Presents with mucocutaneous triad (leukoplakia, nail dystrophy, skin pigmentation) and bone marrow failure, but not typically with absent thumbs. - <strong>Blackfan-diamond:</strong> Is a pure red cell aplasia without pancytopenia or congenital anomalies. - <strong>Acquired pancytopenia:</strong> Would not explain the congenital anomalies like absent thumbs and café-au-lait spots.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Fanconi anemia has increased risk of malignancies (particularly AML and solid tumors) due to DNA repair defects. - The diagnosis is confirmed by chromosomal breakage test showing increased sensitivity to DNA cross-linking agents like diepoxybutane. - Over 20 FANC genes have been identified, with FANCA being the most commonly mutated.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is the only condition that combines pancytopenia with congenital anomalies (including absent thumbs) and café-au-lait spots due to defective DNA repair.</p>','<p>FANCONI: Failure of Anemia, Congenital anomalies, No thumbs, Increased cancer risk</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia patients require lifelong monitoring for hematologic progression and malignancies, and bone marrow transplant may be curative for the hematologic component.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> Presents with mucocutaneous triad but not typically with absent thumbs. - <strong>Blackbone-diamond:</strong> Is a pure red cell aplasia without pancytopenia or congenital anomalies. - <strong>Acquired pancytopenia:</strong> Would not explain the congenital anomalies like absent thumbs and café-au-lait spots.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Fanconi Anemia','["fanconi anemia","pancytopenia","cafe au lait spots"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.862Z','2026-10-18T19:42:21.891Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56het004dqjlrfcu9n4p2','mcq','A 3-year-old boy presents with prolonged epistaxis and a history of easy bruising. There is no family history of bleeding problems. The patient''s laboratory studies show a normal CBC and smear, normal von Willebrand panel, and normal PT and PTT, but platelet aggregation showed no response to adenosine diphosphate (ADP), thrombin, and epinephrine, and platelets were found to lack GpIIb/IIIa. The most likely diagnosis is:','["Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Kostmann disease","Pearson syndrome"]','Glanzmann thrombasthenia','<p><strong>Why Glanzmann thrombasthenia is correct:</strong> Glanzmann thrombasthenia is caused by a deficiency or dysfunction of the glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (GpIIb/IIIa) receptor complex on platelets, which is essential for fibrinogen binding and platelet aggregation. This receptor defect explains the absent response to ADP, thrombin, and epinephrine in platelet aggregation studies, as these agonists ultimately rely on GpIIb/IIIa for final platelet cross-linking. The normal coagulation profile (PT and PTT) distinguishes this from coagulation factor deficiencies, while the absence of von Willebrand factor abnormalities rules out von Willebrand disease. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Bernard-Soulier syndrome:</strong> This is a platelet adhesion disorder caused by deficiency of the GPIb-IX-V complex, leading to giant platelets and impaired ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation, not GpIIb/IIIa deficiency. - <strong>Shwachman-Diamond syndrome:</strong> This is a disorder of exocrine pancreatic dysfunction and bone marrow failure, not primarily a platelet function defect. - <strong>Kostmann disease:</strong> This is severe congenital neutropenia, not a platelet disorder. - <strong>Pearson syndrome:</strong> This is a mitochondrial disorder characterized by sideroblastic anemia and exocrine pancreas dysfunction, not platelet dysfunction. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Glanzmann thrombasthenia presents with mucocutaneous bleeding but normal platelet count and coagulation studies. - The diagnostic hallmark is absent platelet aggregation to multiple agonists despite normal platelet morphology. - GpIIb/IIIa receptor deficiency is specific to Glanzmann thrombasthenia, while Bernard-Soulier affects GPIb-IX-V. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Glanzmann thrombasthenia is the diagnosis when platelets lack GpIIb/IIIa and fail to aggregate despite normal platelet count and coagulation studies.</p>','<p>Glanzmann = GpIIb/IIIa missing</p>','<p>Glanzmann thrombasthenia is the most common inherited platelet function disorder and can be diagnosed by flow cytometry showing absent GpIIb/IIIa expression.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Bernard-Soulier syndrome:</strong> Involves GPIb-IX-V deficiency, not GpIIb/IIIa, and has giant platelets. - <strong>Shwachman-Diamond syndrome:</strong> Primarily affects pancreatic function and neutrophils, not platelet function. - <strong>Kostmann disease:</strong> Is a neutrophil disorder, not a platelet disorder. - <strong>Pearson syndrome:</strong> Is a mitochondrial disorder with anemia and pancreas dysfunction, not platelet dysfunction.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Platelet Disorders','["platelet","aggregation","bruising"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.877Z','2026-10-18T19:43:10.145Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hev004eqjlrk5syqrrx','mcq','Autoimmune thrombocytopenia may be the initial manifestation of which of the following malignancies?','["Neuroblastoma","Lymphoma","Wilms'' tumor","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Ewing''s sarcoma"]','Neuroblastoma','<p><strong>Why Neuroblastoma is correct:</strong> Neuroblastoma is a neural crest-derived tumor that can express gangliosides and other antigens that trigger immune responses, including autoimmune thrombocytopenia. This paraneoplastic phenomenon occurs because the tumor cells may express antigens similar to those on platelets or megakaryocytes, leading to cross-reactive antibody production against platelets. Neuroblastoma is the most common extracranial solid tumor in children and is particularly associated with autoimmune cytopenias as a presenting sign. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Lymphoma:</strong> While lymphomas can cause autoimmune phenomena, thrombocytopenia in lymphoma is more commonly due to bone marrow infiltration or hypersplenism rather than pure autoimmunity. - <strong>Wilms'' tumor:</strong> This is a renal tumor that rarely presents with autoimmune thrombocytopenia as an initial manifestation. - <strong>Rhabdomyosarcoma:</strong> This soft tissue tumor is not classically associated with autoimmune thrombocytopenia as a presenting feature. - <strong>Ewing''s sarcoma:</strong> This bone tumor may present with paraneoplastic syndromes, but autoimmune thrombocytopenia is not a typical initial manifestation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Neuroblastoma is the most common malignancy associated with autoimmune thrombocytopenia as an initial presentation in children. - The mechanism involves molecular mimicry between tumor antigens and platelet antigens. - Screening for neuroblastoma (urinary catecholamines, imaging) is warranted in children with unexplained autoimmune thrombocytopenia. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> When a child presents with autoimmune thrombocytopenia without obvious cause, consider neuroblastoma as a potential underlying malignancy.</p>','<p>Neuroblastoma = New onset ITP in children</p>','<p>Autoimmune thrombocytopenia can be the first sign of an underlying malignancy in children, with neuroblastoma being the most common association.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lymphoma:</strong> Thrombocytopenia is more commonly due to bone marrow involvement or hypersplenism. - <strong>Wilms'' tumor:</strong> Rarely presents with autoimmune thrombocytopenia initially. - <strong>Rhabdomyosarcoma:</strong> Not classically associated with autoimmune thrombocytopenia at presentation. - <strong>Ewing''s sarcoma:</strong> Not a typical cause of autoimmune thrombocytopenia as an initial manifestation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Paraneoplastic Syndromes','["itp","malignancy","paraneoplastic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.879Z','2026-10-18T19:43:10.147Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hex004gqjlrevtpdux8','mcq','A 6-month-old boy with severe eczema presents with easy bruising and two nose bleeds. He is below the 2nd centile for weight. He has had 3 previous admissions to hospital with pneumonia and has required two courses of antibiotics for otitis media. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Complement deficiency","DiGeorge syndrome","Wiskott Aldrich syndrome","Primary antibody deficiency","Ataxia telangiectasia"]','Wiskott Aldrich syndrome','<p><strong>Why Wiskott Aldrich syndrome is correct:</strong> Wiskott Aldrich syndrome is an X-linked disorder caused by mutations in the WAS gene, which encodes the Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASP). This protein is crucial for actin cytoskeleton reorganization in hematopoietic cells, leading to defective platelet formation, impaired T-cell function, and abnormal B-cell responses. The classic triad includes eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, and recurrent infections – all present in this 6-month-old boy with severe eczema, easy bruising/nose bleeds, and recurrent bacterial infections. His failure to thrive is also consistent with this syndrome due to chronic inflammation and malabsorption from eczema.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Complement deficiency:</strong> Typically presents with recurrent Neisseria infections rather than eczema and thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>DiGeorge syndrome:</strong> Characterized by hypocalcemia, cardiac defects, and thymic hypoplasia, not eczema or thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>Primary antibody deficiency:</strong> Presents with recurrent sinopulmonary infections but not typically with eczema or thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>Ataxia telangiectasia:</strong> Features progressive cerebellar ataxia, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, and immunodeficiency, but not eczema or thrombocytopenia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Wiskott Aldrich syndrome is X-linked, affecting males predominantly.</li>
<li>Platelets are characteristically small (mean platelet volume &lt;7 fL) on peripheral smear.</li>
<li>The eczema in WAS is typically severe and difficult to control.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Wiskott Aldrich syndrome is the diagnosis in any male with eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, and recurrent pyogenic infections.</p>','<p>WAS: Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome = W (WAS gene defect), A (Autoimmunity), S (Small platelets)</p>','<p>Wiskott Aldrich syndrome carries a high risk of developing autoimmune disorders and lymphoma, requiring lifelong multidisciplinary management.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Complement deficiency:</strong> Typically presents with recurrent Neisseria infections rather than eczema and thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>DiGeorge syndrome:</strong> Characterized by hypocalcemia, cardiac defects, and thymic hypoplasia, not eczema or thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>Primary antibody deficiency:</strong> Presents with recurrent sinopulmonary infections but not typically with eczema or thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>Ataxia telangiectasia:</strong> Features progressive cerebellar ataxia, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, and immunodeficiency, but not eczema or thrombocytopenia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Immunology','Primary Immunodeficiencies','["immunodeficiency","eczema","thrombocytopenia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.881Z','2026-10-18T19:43:19.060Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgi0053qjlrd4v3mv4m','mcq','In sickle cell anemia, Which of the following cause Aplastic crisis','["Parvovirus B19 infection","CMV","Adenovirus"]','Parvovirus B19 infection','<p><strong>Why Parvovirus B19 infection is correct:</strong> Parvovirus B19 infection causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell anemia by infecting and destroying erythroid precursor cells in the bone marrow. This virus has a specific tropism for the P antigen (globoside) on erythroid progenitor cells, leading to a temporary cessation of red blood cell production. In sickle cell anemia, where red blood cells already have a shortened lifespan due to hemolysis, this sudden halt in production results in a precipitous drop in hemoglobin concentration—a true aplastic crisis. The crisis typically lasts 7-10 days, during which time reticulocyte counts drop to zero, followed by recovery with a brisk reticulocytosis as the immune system clears the virus.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>CMV:</strong> Cytomegalovirus can cause bone marrow suppression in immunocompromised patients, but it does not specifically target erythroid precursors like parvovirus B19. - <strong>Adenovirus:</strong> Adenovirus can cause various hematologic abnormalities, but it is not associated with the classic aplastic crisis seen in sickle cell disease.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Parvovirus B19 infection is the most common cause of aplastic crisis in patients with hemolytic anemias, particularly sickle cell disease. - The diagnosis is confirmed by detecting parvovirus B19 DNA in serum or by seroconversion (IgM antibodies). - Treatment is primarily supportive, with transfusions indicated for severe symptomatic anemia during the crisis.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Parvovirus B19 causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell anemia by selectively infecting and destroying erythroid precursor cells, halting red blood cell production when the bone marrow cannot compensate for ongoing hemolysis.</p>','<p>P for Parvovirus and P for Precursors: Parvovirus B19 targets Precursor cells in the bone marrow, causing aplastic crisis.</p>','<p>Parvovirus B19 infection should be suspected in any patient with sickle cell disease presenting with sudden worsening anemia and absent reticulocytes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>CMV:</strong> Causes bone marrow suppression in immunocompromised patients but doesn''t specifically target erythroid precursors like parvovirus B19. - <strong>Adenovirus:</strong> Can cause various hematologic abnormalities but is not associated with classic aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Hematology','Sickle Cell Anemia','["sickle-cell","aplastic-crisis","parvovirus"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.938Z','2026-10-18T19:44:16.500Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfp004hqjlr2m9pkgdk','mcq','A 3-year-old child presents to the emergency center with history of bloody diarrhea and decreased urination. The mother states that the child''s symptoms began 5 days ago after the family ate at a fast-food restaurant. At that time the patient developed fever, vomiting, abdominal pain, and diarrhea. On physical examination, the patient appears ill and lethargic. Lab is ordered. Which of the following lab results mostly help in diagnosis?','["Thrombocytopenia","High Hb level","Presence of protein/creatinine ratio > 2","Normal BUN and creatinine","Presence of leukocytosis"]','Thrombocytopenia','<p><strong>Why Thrombocytopenia is correct:</strong> This child''s presentation with bloody diarrhea and decreased urination following a gastrointestinal illness is highly suggestive of hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), which is most commonly caused by Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC). The hallmark laboratory finding in HUS is thrombocytopenia due to platelet consumption in the microthrombi formation that characterizes the thrombotic microangiopathy. This thrombocytopenia, along with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes on peripheral smear) and acute kidney injury, forms the classic triad of HUS.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High Hb level:</strong> Would suggest polycythemia or dehydration, not consistent with HUS where hemolysis causes anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Presence of protein/creatinine ratio &gt; 2:</strong> Indicates significant proteinuria but is not specific for HUS diagnosis.</li>
<li><strong>Normal BUN and creatinine:</strong> Would rule out acute kidney injury, which is a key component of HUS.</li>
<li><strong>Presence of leukocytosis:</strong> May occur in infection but is not specific for HUS and doesn''t explain the thrombocytopenia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>HUS typically follows a prodromal gastrointestinal illness with bloody diarrhea.</li>
<li>The diagnostic triad includes thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, and acute kidney injury.</li>
<li>Shiga toxin testing on stool is confirmatory for STEC-HUS.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In a child with bloody diarrhea and decreased urination, thrombocytopenia is the most specific lab finding suggesting hemolytic uremic syndrome.</p>','<p>HUS: Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome = H (Hemolysis), U (Uremia), S (Syndrome) with thrombocytopenia</p>','<p>HUS is a medical emergency requiring urgent dialysis in severe cases; antibiotics should be avoided as they may increase Shiga toxin release.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>High Hb level:</strong> Would suggest polycythemia or dehydration, not consistent with HUS where hemolysis causes anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Presence of protein/creatinine ratio &gt; 2:</strong> Indicates significant proteinuria but is not specific for HUS diagnosis.</li>
<li><strong>Normal BUN and creatinine:</strong> Would rule out acute kidney injury, which is a key component of HUS.</li>
<li><strong>Presence of leukocytosis:</strong> May occur in infection but is not specific for HUS and doesn''t explain the thrombocytopenia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Infectious Diseases','Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome','["diarrhea","thrombocytopenia","hus"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.909Z','2026-10-18T19:43:19.061Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hfq004iqjlrrdpfu12i','mcq','A 5-year-old boy presented with leg pains, pallor, diffuse lymphadenopathy, and bruising. Abdominal examination revealed enlarged liver and spleen but no ascites. CBC shows Hb 5.2 g/dL, white cell count 36000/mm3, platelets 25000/mm3. Which of the following is the most likely specific test to confirm the diagnosis?','["Chest X-ray","Bone marrow examination","Coagulation study","Lymph node biopsy","Abdominal sonar"]','Bone marrow examination','<p><strong>Why Bone marrow examination is correct:</strong> This 5-year-old boy presents with pallor, bruising, lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly with pancytopenia (low Hb, high WBC, low platelets). These findings are highly suggestive of acute leukemia, particularly acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) which is the most common childhood malignancy. Bone marrow examination is the definitive diagnostic test for leukemia as it allows direct visualization of the abnormal blast cells replacing normal hematopoietic tissue. The examination includes morphology, immunophenotyping, cytogenetics, and molecular studies to confirm the diagnosis and classify the leukemia subtype.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chest X-ray:</strong> May show mediastinal mass in some lymphomas but cannot diagnose leukemia.</li>
<li><strong>Coagulation study:</strong> Would assess bleeding disorders but not explain the pancytopenia and organomegaly.</li>
<li><strong>Lymph node biopsy:</strong> May diagnose lymphoma but cannot evaluate the bone marrow involvement seen in leukemia.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal sonar:</strong> May confirm organomegaly but cannot identify the abnormal blast cells characteristic of leukemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acute leukemia is the most common cancer in children, with ALL being more common than AML.</li>
<li>The diagnostic triad for leukemia includes cytopenias, organomegaly, and circulating blasts.</li>
<li>Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy are essential for diagnosis and classification of leukemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In a child with pancytopenia, organomegaly, and circulating blasts, bone marrow examination is the definitive test for diagnosing leukemia.</p>','<p>BMA: Bone Marrow Analysis = Blast cell identification for leukemia diagnosis</p>','<p>Early recognition of leukemia is critical as prompt initiation of chemotherapy significantly improves survival rates in childhood leukemia.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Chest X-ray:</strong> May show mediastinal mass in some lymphomas but cannot diagnose leukemia.</li>
<li><strong>Coagulation study:</strong> Would assess bleeding disorders but not explain the pancytopenia and organomegaly.</li>
<li><strong>Lymph node biopsy:</strong> May diagnose lymphoma but cannot evaluate the bone marrow involvement seen in leukemia.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal sonar:</strong> May confirm organomegaly but cannot identify the abnormal blast cells characteristic of leukemia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Leukemia','["leukemia","bone marrow","lymphadenopathy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.910Z','2026-10-18T19:43:19.064Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzk0008qjlr0xhd55le','mcq','In which condition, low lactose milk formula is needed?','["Scanty breast milk with poor weight gain.","Once infant is 4 months old.","When breast feeding is absolutely contraindicated.","When secondary lactose intolerance is suspected."]','When secondary lactose intolerance is suspected.','<p><strong>Why When secondary lactose intolerance is suspected is correct:</strong> Secondary lactose intolerance occurs when there is damage to the intestinal mucosa, most commonly from acute gastroenteritis, which reduces lactase enzyme production. In this condition, low lactose milk formula is needed to prevent symptoms like diarrhea, bloating, and abdominal pain while allowing the intestinal mucosa to heal. The mechanism involves the brush border enzyme lactase being temporarily downregulated due to inflammation or damage, making lactose digestion impossible until mucosal recovery occurs. This is a temporary condition that typically resolves as the gut heals, usually within 2-4 weeks after the acute illness.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Scanty breast milk with poor weight gain:</strong> This situation requires increasing breast milk production or supplementing with standard formula, not necessarily low lactose formula, as lactose intolerance is not the primary issue. - <strong>Once infant is 4 months old:</strong> Age alone does not indicate the need for low lactose formula; most healthy infants can digest lactose throughout infancy and beyond. - <strong>When breast feeding is absolutely contraindicated:</strong> When breastfeeding cannot occur, standard infant formula is appropriate unless there is a specific indication for low lactose formula, which is not contraindication itself.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Secondary lactose intolerance is temporary and follows acute gastrointestinal illness like rotavirus or bacterial enteritis. - The diagnosis is clinical, based on symptoms after a recent GI illness, and confirmed by resolution when lactose is reintroduced. - Treatment involves lactose-free formula for 2-4 weeks while the gut mucosa heals.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Low lactose milk formula is specifically indicated for secondary lactose intolerance following acute gastrointestinal injury, not for general poor weight gain or age-related changes.</p>','<p>Secondary = Sick gut needs lactose rest</p>','<p>Secondary lactose intolerance is self-limited and resolves as the intestinal mucosa heals, typically within 2-4 weeks after the acute illness.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Scanty breast milk with poor weight gain:</strong> Does not require low lactose formula unless lactose intolerance is specifically diagnosed. - <strong>Once infant is 4 months old:</strong> Age alone is not an indication for low lactose formula as most infants tolerate lactose well. - <strong>When breast feeding is absolutely contraindicated:</strong> Standard formula is appropriate unless there''s a specific indication for low lactose.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["lactose","formula","intolerance"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.328Z','2026-10-18T22:35:26.150Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg4004sqjlrvpszbyt0','mcq','Which of the following cause macrocytic anemia?','["Lead poisoning","Thalassemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Chronic renal failure"]','Megaloblastic anemia','<p><strong>Why Megaloblastic anemia is correct:</strong> Megaloblastic anemia is characterized by macrocytic red blood cells due to impaired DNA synthesis, which affects nuclear maturation while cytoplasmic development continues normally. This defect is caused by deficiencies in vitamin B12 or folate, which are essential cofactors in the thymidine and purine synthesis pathways. The impaired DNA synthesis leads to larger-than-normal (macrocytic) red blood cell precursors in the bone marrow, which are fragile and have a shortened lifespan. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Lead poisoning:</strong> Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia by inhibiting heme synthesis and damaging red blood cell membranes. - <strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Results in microcytic hypochromic anemia due to impaired globin chain synthesis. - <strong>Chronic renal failure:</strong> Typically causes normocytic normochromic anemia due to decreased erythropoietin production. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Macrocytic anemias are characterized by MCV &gt;100 fL and are most commonly caused by B12 or folate deficiency. - The classic finding on peripheral blood smear is macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. - Other causes include alcohol, drugs (hydroxyurea, methotrexate), and myelodysplastic syndromes. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Megaloblastic anemia is the only macrocytic anemia among the options, caused by impaired DNA synthesis from B12/folate deficiency.</p>','<p>B12/Folate = Macrocytic (Big cells)</p>','<p>Megaloblastic anemia can present with neurological symptoms (paresthesias, ataxia) when caused by B12 deficiency, which are not seen with folate deficiency alone.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lead poisoning:</strong> Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia by inhibiting heme synthesis. - <strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Results in microcytic hypochromic anemia due to impaired globin chain synthesis. - <strong>Chronic renal failure:</strong> Typically causes normocytic normochromic anemia due to decreased erythropoietin.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Macrocytic Anemia','["macrocytic","megaloblastic","anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.924Z','2026-10-18T19:43:52.510Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg5004tqjlrzeg68zjv','mcq','Which of the following cause normocytic normochromic anemia?','["Thalassemia","Chronic renal failure","Iron deficiency anemia","Folic acid deficiency"]','Chronic renal failure','<p><strong>Why Chronic renal failure is correct:</strong> Chronic renal failure causes normocytic normochromic anemia primarily due to decreased production of erythropoietin (EPO) by the damaged kidneys. EPO is essential for stimulating red blood cell production in the bone marrow, and its deficiency leads to reduced erythropoiesis without affecting the size or hemoglobin content of the red blood cells. Additionally, uremia can shorten red blood cell lifespan and contribute to anemia through other mechanisms like blood loss from frequent procedures and inflammation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to impaired globin chain synthesis. - <strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Results in microcytic hypochromic anemia due to insufficient iron for hemoglobin synthesis. - <strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> Causes macrocytic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Normocytic normochromic anemia is characterized by normal MCV (80-100 fL) and normal MCHC. - The most common causes are anemia of chronic disease, chronic kidney disease, and bone marrow infiltration. - In CKD, anemia severity correlates with the degree of renal impairment and EPO deficiency. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Chronic renal failure causes normocytic normochromic anemia primarily due to erythropoietin deficiency.</p>','<p>CKD = Normocytic (Normal cells) due to EPO lack</p>','<p>In chronic kidney disease, anemia typically develops when GFR falls below 30 mL/min and worsens as kidney function declines, with hemoglobin levels generally correlating with the degree of EPO deficiency.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to impaired globin chain synthesis. - <strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Results in microcytic hypochromic anemia due to insufficient iron for hemoglobin synthesis. - <strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> Causes macrocytic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Normocytic Anemia','["normocytic","renal","anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.926Z','2026-10-18T19:43:52.514Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg7004uqjlr9zts4pq7','mcq','Which of the following cause microcytic hypochromic anemia?','["Aplastic pancytopenia","Thalassemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Congenital spherocytosis"]','Thalassemia','<p><strong>Why Thalassemia is correct:</strong> Thalassemia causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to impaired synthesis of globin chains, which are essential components of hemoglobin. In thalassemia, there is either reduced or absent production of one or more globin chains (alpha or beta), leading to an imbalance in globin chain synthesis. This imbalance results in decreased hemoglobin production, smaller red blood cells (microcytic), and reduced hemoglobin concentration (hypochromic). The excess unmatched globin chains precipitate in red blood cell precursors, causing ineffective erythropoiesis and hemolysis. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Aplastic pancytopenia:</strong> Causes pancytopenia with normocytic normochromic red blood cells due to bone marrow failure. - <strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Results in macrocytic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis from B12/folate deficiency. - <strong>Congenital spherocytosis:</strong> Causes hemolytic anemia with spherocytes (small, dense cells) that are typically normocytic. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Microcytic hypochromic anemia is characterized by MCV &lt;80 fL and MCHC &lt;32 g/dL. - The most common causes are iron deficiency, thalassemia, and anemia of chronic disease. - Thalassemia is diagnosed by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing abnormal hemoglobin patterns and increased HbA2 in beta-thalassemia. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Thalassemia causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to impaired globin chain synthesis leading to hemoglobin deficiency.</p>','<p>Thalassemia = Microcytic (Small cells) due to globin lack</p>','<p>Thalassemia trait (carrier state) is often asymptomatic with mild microcytic anemia and normal iron studies, distinguishing it from iron deficiency which shows low ferritin.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aplastic pancytopenia:</strong> Causes pancytopenia with normocytic normochromic red blood cells. - <strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Results in macrocytic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis. - <strong>Congenital spherocytosis:</strong> Causes hemolytic anemia with spherocytes that are typically normocytic.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Microcytic Anemia','["microcytic","thalassemia","hypochromic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.927Z','2026-10-18T19:43:52.515Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgc004yqjlrttjhilp5','mcq','Which of the following is characteristic of pure red cell aplasia?','["Reticulocytopenia","Chromosomal fragility test","Bone marrow hyperplasia","Normal skeletal survey"]','Reticulocytopenia','<p><strong>Why Reticulocytopenia is correct:</strong> Pure red cell aplasia (PRCA) is characterized by a selective failure of erythroid precursor differentiation in the bone marrow, leading to a profound deficiency of red blood cell production. This results in reticulocytopenia, which is the absence or near absence of reticulocytes (immature red blood cells) in the peripheral blood, despite severe anemia. The pathophysiology involves either an intrinsic defect in erythroid precursors or an extrinsic factor (such as antibodies, drugs, or parvovirus B19) that selectively targets erythroid precursors while sparing other cell lines.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chromosomal fragility test:</strong> This is characteristic of Fanconi anemia, not pure red cell aplasia. Chromosomal fragility testing is used to diagnose Fanconi anemia, which is a different inherited bone marrow failure syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow hyperplasia:</strong> PRCA typically shows normocellular or hypocellular bone marrow with a selective absence of erythroid precursors, not hyperplasia. Hyperplasia would suggest increased production, which is the opposite of what occurs in PRCA.</li>
<li><strong>Normal skeletal survey:</strong> While skeletal abnormalities may be normal in PRCA, this is not a defining characteristic. Skeletal surveys are more relevant for diagnosing conditions like Fanconi anemia or metastatic disease, not PRCA.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PRCA is defined by reticulocytopenia with a selective absence of erythroid precursors in an otherwise normocellular bone marrow.</li>
<li>PRCA can be congenital (Diamond-Blackfan anemia) or acquired, with acquired forms often associated with parvovirus B19 infection, autoimmune disorders, or drug reactions.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by bone marrow examination showing absence of erythroid precursors with normal myeloid and megakaryocytic lineages.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pure red cell aplasia is defined by reticulocytopenia due to selective failure of erythroid precursor production in the bone marrow.</p>','<p>PRCA = Pure Reticulocyte Count Absence</p>','<p>Pure red cell aplasia can be a diagnostic challenge and requires distinguishing between congenital (Diamond-Blackfan) and acquired forms, with the latter often being associated with parvovirus B19 infection in immunocompromised patients.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Chromosomal fragility test:</strong> This is characteristic of Fanconi anemia, not pure red cell aplasia.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow hyperplasia:</strong> PRCA typically shows normocellular or hypocellular bone marrow with selective absence of erythroid precursors.</li>
<li><strong>Normal skeletal survey:</strong> While skeletal abnormalities may be normal in PRCA, this is not a defining characteristic.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Pure Red Cell Aplasia','["pure","red","cell","aplasia","reticulocyte"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.932Z','2026-10-18T19:44:12.375Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgd004zqjlrbsanxw8u','mcq','Which of the following is characteristic of Fanconi anemia?','["Abnormal chromosomal fragility","Blackfan-Diamond","Transient erythroblastopenia","Spherocytosis"]','Abnormal chromosomal fragility','<p><strong>Why Abnormal chromosomal fragility is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is an inherited autosomal recessive disorder characterized by a defect in DNA repair mechanisms, specifically in the FA/BRCA pathway. This defect results in increased chromosomal breakage and radial chromosomes when cells are exposed to DNA cross-linking agents like diepoxybutane (DEB) or mitomycin C (MMC). The abnormal chromosomal fragility test is a hallmark diagnostic feature of Fanconi anemia and distinguishes it from other bone marrow failure syndromes.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blackfan-Diamond:</strong> This refers to Diamond-Blackfan anemia, a separate congenital pure red cell aplasia that is not associated with chromosomal fragility or the same physical anomalies as Fanconi anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Transient erythroblastopenia:</strong> This is a self-limited condition of childhood characterized by transient absence of red blood cell precursors, but it does not involve chromosomal fragility or the congenital anomalies seen in Fanconi anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Spherocytosis:</strong> This refers to hereditary spherocytosis, a hemolytic anemia caused by defects in red blood cell membrane proteins, which is completely unrelated to Fanconi anemia''s DNA repair defect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fanconi anemia is diagnosed by demonstrating increased chromosomal breakage in response to DNA cross-linking agents (DEB or MMC test).</li>
<li>Patients with Fanconi anemia typically present with physical anomalies (skin pigmentation, skeletal abnormalities, thumb and radial ray defects), bone marrow failure, and increased risk of malignancy.</li>
<li>The disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern with mutations in at least 22 different FANC genes involved in the DNA repair pathway.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is defined by abnormal chromosomal fragility due to defective DNA repair, distinguishing it from other bone marrow failure syndromes.</p>','<p>Fanconi = Fragile Chromosomes, Anemia</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia requires a high index of suspicion as it can present with diverse physical anomalies and bone marrow failure, and the chromosomal fragility test is essential for diagnosis before considering bone marrow transplantation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Blackford-Diamond:</strong> This refers to Diamond-Blackfan anemia, a separate congenital pure red cell aplasia without chromosomal fragility.</li>
<li><strong>Transient erythroblastopenia:</strong> This is a self-limited childhood condition without chromosomal fragility or congenital anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>Spherocytosis:</strong> This is a hemolytic anemia due to red blood cell membrane defects, unrelated to Fanconi anemia''s DNA repair defect.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Fanconi Anemia','["fanconi","chromosomal","fragility"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.933Z','2026-10-18T19:44:12.379Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgf0050qjlrlwxg1n5v','mcq','Which of the following is true about embryonic erythropoiesis?','["Biconcave nucleated RBCs","Embryonic phase > liver","Fetal phase > yolk sac","Embryonic phase > yolk sac"]','Embryonic phase > yolk sac','<p><strong>Why Embryonic phase &gt; yolk sac is correct:</strong> Human erythropoiesis occurs in three distinct phases with different primary sites: the yolk sac (embryonic phase), liver (fetal phase), and bone marrow (adult phase). During the embryonic phase (weeks 3-8 of gestation), the yolk sac is the primary site of erythropoiesis, producing primitive nucleated red blood cells. This is followed by the fetal phase (weeks 8-30), where the liver becomes the main site, producing definitive nucleated red blood cells. Finally, the bone marrow takes over as the primary site around week 30 and continues throughout adult life. The statement "Embryonic phase &gt; yolk sac" correctly identifies the yolk sac as the primary site during the embryonic phase.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Biconcave nucleated RBCs:</strong> Red blood cells during embryonic and fetal development are nucleated but spherical, not biconcave. The biconcave shape develops only after enucleation in the bone marrow.</li>
<li><strong>Fetal phase &gt; liver:</strong> While the liver is indeed the primary site during the fetal phase, the statement incorrectly suggests this relationship is specific to the fetal phase when it applies to that phase only.</li>
<li><strong>Embryonic phase &gt; yolk sac:</strong> This option is incorrect because it reverses the relationship; the yolk sac is the primary site during the embryonic phase, not the other way around.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Embryonic erythropoiesis occurs in the yolk sac and produces primitive, nucleated, spherical RBCs.</li>
<li>Fetal erythropoiesis shifts to the liver around week 8 and produces definitive, nucleated, spherical RBCs.</li>
<li>Adult erythropoiesis occurs in the bone marrow and produces enucleated, biconcave RBCs.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Embryonic erythropoiesis occurs primarily in the yolk sac, producing nucleated red blood cells before the liver takes over during the fetal phase.</p>','<p>Embryo Yolk, Fetal Liver, Adult Bone</p>','<p>Understanding the developmental shifts in erythropoiesis sites is crucial for interpreting congenital anemias and understanding why certain sites are affected in specific disorders.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Biconcave nucleated RBCs:</strong> Embryonic and fetal RBCs are nucleated but spherical, not biconcave.</li>
<li><strong>Fetal phase &gt; liver:</strong> While the liver is primary during fetal phase, this option incorrectly presents it as a general relationship.</li>
<li><strong>Embryonic phase &gt; yolk sac:</strong> This reverses the correct relationship; yolk sac is primary during embryonic phase.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Erythropoiesis','["embryonic","erythropoiesis","yolk sac"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.935Z','2026-10-18T19:44:12.381Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgh0052qjlrauyhfbe9','mcq','Parvovirus B19 cause Aplastic crisis in which type of hemolysis','["Sickle cell anemia"]','Sickle cell anemia','<p><strong>Why Sickle cell anemia is correct:</strong> Parvovirus B19 causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell anemia because it selectively infects and destroys erythroid precursor cells in the bone marrow. In individuals with sickle cell anemia, the baseline red cell lifespan is already shortened due to hemolysis, creating a state of high red cell turnover. When parvovirus B19 suppresses erythropoiesis, the bone marrow cannot compensate for the ongoing hemolysis, leading to a sudden and severe drop in hemoglobin concentration—a true aplastic crisis. The virus has a tropism for the P antigen (globoside) on erythroid progenitor cells, which is present in all individuals regardless of blood type.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options provided:</strong> The question only lists one option (Sickle cell anemia) as the correct answer, with no other options to evaluate.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Parvovirus B19 infection classically presents with "slapped cheek" rash in children and arthralgia in adults, but in hemolytic anemias, it causes aplastic crisis. - The aplastic crisis typically lasts 7-10 days, during which time reticulocyte counts drop to zero, followed by recovery with a brisk reticulocytosis. - Patients with sickle cell trait are also susceptible to aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 infection.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Parvovirus B19 causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell anemia by suppressing erythropoiesis in the bone marrow, which cannot compensate for the already shortened red cell lifespan.</p>','<p>B19 = Bone marrow suppression: The ''19'' in parvovirus B19 represents the 19th chromosome where the P antigen receptor is encoded, which the virus targets.</p>','<p>Parvovirus B19 is the only known human virus that targets erythroid precursors, making it uniquely capable of causing transient pure red cell aplasia.</p>','','Easy','Hematology','Parvovirus B19','["parvovirus","aplastic-crisis","sickle-cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.937Z','2026-10-18T19:44:16.498Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgk0054qjlrsf40aooy','mcq','Hydroxyurea cause','["Increase in Hb F"]','Increase in Hb F','<p><strong>Why Increase in Hb F is correct:</strong> Hydroxyurea is a ribonucleotide reductase inhibitor that increases fetal hemoglobin (Hb F) production through activation of the gamma-globin gene promoter. It works by inducing stress erythropoiesis and stimulating fetal hemoglobin synthesis in adult erythroid precursors, which helps to inhibit sickling in sickle cell disease by diluting the concentration of Hb S. The mechanism involves nitric oxide generation and soluble guanylyl cyclase activation, leading to increased cGMP production and gamma-globin gene expression.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Increase in Hb A:</strong> Hydroxyurea does not increase adult hemoglobin (Hb A) production; it specifically targets gamma-globin expression. - <strong>Decrease in Hb F:</strong> This is incorrect as hydroxyurea''s therapeutic effect relies on increasing Hb F, not decreasing it. - <strong>No effect on hemoglobin:</strong> This is false as hydroxyurea has a well-documented effect on hemoglobin production and specifically increases Hb F.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hydroxyurea is first-line therapy for sickle cell disease primarily due to its Hb F-inducing effects. - The increase in Hb F correlates with reduced vaso-occlusive crises and hospitalizations in sickle cell patients. - Hb F levels above 20% are associated with significant clinical improvement in sickle cell disease.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hydroxyurea increases fetal hemoglobin (Hb F) by activating gamma-globin gene expression, which is its primary therapeutic mechanism in sickle cell disease.</p>','<p>HbF = Fetal Freedom (hydroxyurea gives sickle cells freedom from sickling by increasing fetal hemoglobin)</p>','<p>Hydroxyurea''s Hb F induction is dose-dependent and may take 3-6 months to reach maximal effect, requiring patience in clinical management.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Increase in Hb A:</strong> Hydroxyurea does not increase adult hemoglobin production. - <strong>Decrease in Hb F:</strong> This contradicts hydroxyurea''s known mechanism of action. - <strong>No effect on hemoglobin:</strong> Hydroxyurea has a well-documented effect on hemoglobin production.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Pharmacology','Hydroxyurea','["hydroxyurea","hemoglobin-f","sickle-cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.940Z','2026-10-18T19:44:29.948Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr920006o7lr2hkp277k','mcq','Welcome Classification depends on:','["Weight for age","[Not provided in source]","[Not provided in source]","[Not provided in source]"]','Weight for age','<p><strong>Why Weight for age is correct:</strong> The Welcome Classification system for nutritional assessment primarily uses weight-for-age as its primary parameter because it provides a simple, practical measure of a child''s growth relative to their chronological age. This parameter is particularly useful in field settings where more complex measurements like height or mid-upper arm circumference may be difficult to obtain. Weight-for-age reflects both chronic and acute malnutrition, making it a comprehensive initial screening tool for identifying children at risk of malnutrition in resource-limited settings.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>[Not provided in source]:</strong> Since this option was not provided in the source, it cannot be evaluated as a correct alternative to weight-for-age in the Welcome Classification system.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The Welcome Classification is a simple, widely used system for assessing childhood malnutrition in resource-limited settings. - Weight-for-age is the primary parameter because it''s easy to measure and reflects both chronic and acute malnutrition. - This classification helps prioritize children who need urgent nutritional intervention based on their weight relative to their age.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The Welcome Classification system uses weight-for-age as its primary parameter because it''s a practical, comprehensive measure of childhood malnutrition that can be easily applied in field settings.</p>','','<p>In nutritional assessment, weight-for-age is often the first parameter evaluated in screening programs because it requires minimal equipment and training to measure accurately.</p>','','Basic','Nutrition','Classification','["classification","weight","nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.046Z','2026-10-18T19:44:44.899Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgl0055qjlrwpwmvwhd','mcq','Rapid spleen enlargement called','["Splenic sequestration"]','Splenic sequestration','<p><strong>Why Splenic sequestration is correct:</strong> Splenic sequestration is a life-threatening complication characterized by rapid enlargement of the spleen due to acute trapping of red blood cells within the splenic sinusoids, leading to sudden anemia and hypovolemia. This condition is most commonly seen in young children with sickle cell disease, particularly those with HbSS genotype, where sickled cells become entrapped in the spleen''s circulation. The sequestration can occur acutely over hours, causing a rapid drop in hemoglobin levels and signs of shock.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Hypersplenism:</strong> While hypersplenism involves splenic enlargement and sequestration of blood cells, it is typically a chronic process rather than the rapid enlargement described in the question. - <strong>Portal hypertension:</strong> Portal hypertension causes splenomegaly but is a chronic condition resulting from increased pressure in the portal venous system, not acute splenic enlargement. - <strong>Sickle cell crisis:</strong> Sickle cell crisis is a broad term that can include vaso-occlusive, aplastic, or hemolytic crises, but it doesn''t specifically describe the rapid splenic enlargement seen in sequestration.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Splenic sequestration is a medical emergency requiring immediate fluid resuscitation and blood transfusion. - It is most common in children aged 6 months to 3 years with sickle cell disease, as the spleen autoinfarcts after this age in HbSS patients. - Recurrent splenic sequestration may lead to splenectomy in some cases to prevent life-threatening episodes.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Splenic sequestration is the rapid enlargement of the spleen due to acute trapping of red blood cells, a medical emergency in sickle cell disease.</p>','<p>SEQUESTRATION = Sudden Enlargement with Quick Emergency, Urgent Transfusion Required, Rapid Anemia</p>','<p>In sickle cell patients, a sudden drop in hemoglobin with an enlarging spleen should be treated as splenic sequestration until proven otherwise.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypersplenism:</strong> This is typically a chronic process, not rapid enlargement. - <strong>Portal hypertension:</strong> This causes chronic splenomegaly, not acute enlargement. - <strong>Sickle cell crisis:</strong> This is a broad term that doesn''t specifically describe rapid splenic enlargement.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Hematology','Spleen Disorders','["splenomegaly","sequestration","sickle-cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.941Z','2026-10-18T19:44:29.950Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hgm0056qjlriuiyaff1','mcq','Hand foot syndrome occur with','["Sickle cell anemia"]','Sickle cell anemia','<p><strong>Why Sickle cell anemia is correct:</strong> Hand-foot syndrome (dactylitis) is a classic presenting feature of sickle cell anemia, particularly in infants and young children. It results from vaso-occlusion in the small bones of the hands and feet, causing painful swelling, erythema, and fever. The pathophysiology involves sickled red blood cells obstructing microvasculature in the metacarpal and metatarsal bones, leading to ischemia, inflammation, and bone infarction. This condition is often one of the first clinical manifestations of sickle cell disease, typically appearing between 6 months and 3 years of age.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Thalassemia major can cause bone deformities due to marrow expansion, but it does not typically present with acute hand-foot syndrome. - <strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Iron deficiency causes microcytic anemia but does not lead to vaso-occlusive crises or hand-foot syndrome. - <strong>Aplastic anemia:</strong> Aplastic anemia presents with pancytopenia and bone marrow failure, not the painful swelling of hands and feet seen in sickle cell disease.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hand-foot syndrome is often the first clinical sign of sickle cell disease in infants and young children. - The condition is characterized by painful, symmetric swelling of the hands and feet, often with fever. - Recurrent episodes can lead to bone deformities and growth retardation if not properly managed.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hand-foot syndrome is a classic vaso-occlusive manifestation of sickle cell anemia caused by sickled cells obstructing blood flow in the small bones of hands and feet.</p>','<p>HAND-FOOT = HbS And No Deformity Initially, Often Overlooked Until Transfusion</p>','<p>Hand-foot syndrome in a child of African, Mediterranean, or Middle Eastern descent should prompt immediate sickle cell screening.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Does not typically present with acute hand-foot syndrome. - <strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Causes microcytic anemia but not vaso-occlusive crises. - <strong>Aplastic anemia:</strong> Presents with pancytopenia, not painful swelling of hands and feet.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Hematology','Sickle Cell Anemia','["hand-foot","sickle-cell","syndrome"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.942Z','2026-10-18T19:44:29.951Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr940009o7lr2o5n7kbh','mcq','What is the Nutritional cause of marasmus','["Anorexia","Mother deprivation syndrome","Diarrhea","[Not provided in source]"]','Anorexia','<p><strong>Why Anorexia is correct:</strong> Marasmus is a severe form of malnutrition resulting from inadequate caloric intake, and anorexia represents a primary nutritional cause by significantly reducing voluntary food consumption. The pathophysiology involves prolonged negative energy balance leading to depletion of fat stores and muscle tissue, resulting in the characteristic ''skin and bones'' appearance. Anorexia creates a vicious cycle where reduced food intake prevents adequate nutrient absorption and utilization, exacerbating malnutrition and impairing metabolic processes essential for growth and maintenance.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mother deprivation syndrome:</strong> While mother deprivation can contribute to failure to thrive, it''s more associated with psychosocial dwarfism and emotional deprivation rather than the direct nutritional deficiency that defines marasmus.</li>
<li><strong>Diarrhea:</strong> Although diarrhea can be a contributing factor to malnutrition by impairing nutrient absorption, it''s not the primary nutritional cause of marasmus, which fundamentally stems from insufficient calorie intake.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Marasmus is characterized by severe wasting of fat and muscle tissue with relatively preserved serum protein levels.</li>
<li>It typically presents in children under 2 years old and is associated with inadequate caloric intake over an extended period.</li>
<li>Unlike kwashiorkor, marasmus does not typically feature edema or skin and hair changes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Marasmus is fundamentally caused by insufficient calorie intake, with anorexia being a primary nutritional factor that prevents adequate consumption of required nutrients.</p>','','<p>Marasmus represents the body''s adaptation to prolonged starvation, with metabolic rate decreasing to conserve energy, unlike kwashiorkor which shows protein-specific manifestations.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mother deprivation syndrome:</strong> This is more associated with psychosocial dwarfism rather than direct nutritional deficiency causing marasmus.</li>
<li><strong>Diarrhea:</strong> While it can contribute to malnutrition, it''s not the primary nutritional cause of marasmus.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Marasmus','["marasmus","nutrition","cause"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.048Z','2026-10-18T19:44:44.021Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9e000bo7lrtuyblytl','mcq','Cause of Hypoglycemia','["Liver impairment","Insulin deficient","Calcium disturbance","[Not provided in source]"]','Liver impairment','<p><strong>Why Liver impairment is correct:</strong> The liver is crucial for maintaining blood glucose levels through glycogen storage and gluconeogenesis, and impairment of these functions can lead to hypoglycemia. When liver function is compromised, the ability to store glucose as glycogen is diminished, and the capacity to produce new glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors is reduced. This dual impairment creates a scenario where the body cannot adequately maintain blood glucose levels during fasting or increased metabolic demand, resulting in hypoglycemia.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Insulin deficient:</strong> Insulin deficiency would actually cause hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia, as seen in type 1 diabetes mellitus where lack of insulin leads to unopposed gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.</li>
<li><strong>Calcium disturbance:</strong> While calcium imbalances can cause various neurological symptoms, they do not directly lead to hypoglycemia as calcium is not a primary regulator of blood glucose levels.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The liver maintains blood glucose through glycogenolysis (breaking down stored glycogen) and gluconeogenesis (creating new glucose).</li>
<li>Hypoglycemia in liver impairment typically occurs during fasting states when glycogen stores are depleted.</li>
<li>Conditions like cirrhosis, glycogen storage diseases, and severe hepatitis can all cause hypoglycemia due to impaired liver function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Liver impairment causes hypoglycemia by compromising both glycogen storage and gluconeogenesis, the liver''s two primary mechanisms for maintaining blood glucose levels.</p>','<p>Liver = Glucose Guardian (stores and makes glucose)</p>','<p>In liver failure, hypoglycemia often occurs during fasting and can be refractory to treatment due to the liver''s inability to produce or store glucose.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Insulin deficient:</strong> Insulin deficiency causes hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia, as seen in diabetes mellitus.</li>
<li><strong>Calcium disturbance:</strong> Calcium imbalances don''t directly regulate blood glucose levels and aren''t a cause of hypoglycemia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Hypoglycemia','["hypoglycemia","liver","metabolic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.058Z','2026-10-18T19:44:44.027Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr930007o7lr9bdwv3n4','mcq','Body weight less than 60% with edema:','["Marasmic kwashiorkor","Marasmus","Kwashiorkor","Underweight"]','Marasmic kwashiorkor','<p><strong>Why Marasmic kwashiorkor is correct:</strong> Marasmic kwashiorkor is the correct classification when a child has body weight less than 60% of the median weight for age AND presents with edema, as this represents a mixed form of malnutrition combining features of both marasmus (severe energy deficiency) and kwashiorkor (protein deficiency with edema). The edema in kwashiorkor results from decreased oncotic pressure due to hypoalbuminemia from inadequate protein intake, while the severe weight loss (&lt;60%) indicates the marasmic component. This combination represents the most severe form of malnutrition with the highest mortality risk.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Marasmus:</strong> This would be incorrect because marasmus is characterized by severe weight loss (&lt;60% of expected weight) without edema, which distinguishes it from marasmic kwashiorkor. - <strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> This would be incorrect because kwashiorkor typically presents with edema but with weight loss that is less severe than marasmus (usually &gt;60% of expected weight). - <strong>Underweight:</strong> This is a general term for weight below the 5th percentile but doesn''t specify the severity or presence of edema that defines marasmic kwashiorkor.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Marasmic kwashiorkor represents the most severe form of malnutrition with the highest mortality risk. - The presence of edema is the key distinguishing feature between marasmic kwashiorkor and pure marasmus. - Children with marasmic kwashiorkor require specialized nutritional rehabilitation with careful protein and calorie refeeding to avoid refeeding syndrome.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Marasmic kwashiorkor is identified by the combination of severe weight loss (&lt;60% of expected weight) and edema, representing the most severe form of malnutrition with features of both marasmus and kwashiorkor.</p>','<p>Remember ''MEK'' for Marasmic Edema Kwashiorkor - the combination of Marasmus features with Edema defines Kwashiorkor.</p>','<p>Children with marasmic kwashiorkor have the highest mortality risk among malnourished children and require careful, gradual nutritional rehabilitation to avoid refeeding syndrome.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Marasmus:</strong> Incorrect because marasmus presents with severe weight loss without edema. - <strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> Incorrect because kwashiorkor has edema but typically less severe weight loss than marasmus. - <strong>Underweight:</strong> Incorrect because it''s a general term that doesn''t specify the presence of edema or severity.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["malnutrition","edema","kwashiorkor"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.047Z','2026-10-18T19:44:44.900Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr930008o7lrwrd9m6xj','mcq','Body weight less than 60% without edema','["Marasmic kwashiorkor","Marasmus","Kwashiorkor","Underweight"]','Marasmus','<p><strong>Why Marasmus is correct:</strong> Marasmus is the correct classification when a child has body weight less than 60% of the median weight for age without edema, representing a severe form of energy deficiency resulting from inadequate caloric intake over an extended period. In marasmus, the body adapts to chronic starvation by metabolizing its own fat stores and tissues, leading to the characteristic ''skin and bones'' appearance. The absence of edema distinguishes marasmus from kwashiorkor, as the body maintains adequate protein synthesis to preserve serum albumin levels.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Marasmic kwashiorkor:</strong> This would be incorrect because marasmic kwashiorkor includes the presence of edema, which is absent in pure marasmus. - <strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> This would be incorrect because kwashiorkor is characterized by edema and relatively preserved weight compared to marasmus (typically &gt;60% of expected weight). - <strong>Underweight:</strong> This is a general term for weight below the 5th percentile but doesn''t specify the severity or absence of edema that defines marasmus.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Marasmus represents a pure energy deficiency without protein deficiency, distinguishing it from kwashiorkor. - The absence of edema is the key clinical feature that differentiates marasmus from kwashiorkor and marasmic kwashiorkor. - Marasmus typically develops in younger infants and is associated with prolonged inadequate caloric intake rather than acute food shortages.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Marasmus is identified by severe weight loss (&lt;60% of expected weight) without edema, representing a pure energy deficiency state.</p>','<p>Remember ''No Edema in Marasmus'' - the absence of edema is the key distinguishing feature from kwashiorkor.</p>','<p>Marasmus often develops in infants with prolonged inadequate breastfeeding and complementary feeding, while kwashiorkor more commonly occurs in older children after weaning.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Marasmic kwashiorkor:</strong> Incorrect because marasmic kwashiorkor includes edema, which is absent in pure marasmus. - <strong>Kwashiorkor:</strong> Incorrect because kwashiorkor has edema and typically less severe weight loss than marasmus. - <strong>Underweight:</strong> Incorrect because it''s a general term that doesn''t specify the absence of edema or severity.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["malnutrition","marasmus","nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.047Z','2026-10-18T19:44:44.902Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmve82bvb001ddbucy6f06ru8','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of constipation characterized by infrequent bowel movements (2-3 times per week), straining during defecation, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation. She reports no rectal bleeding, weight loss, or family history of colorectal cancer. Physical examination is unremarkable except for a digital rectal exam that reveals paradoxical contraction of the anal sphincter during simulated defecation. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathophysiology and most appropriate initial management approach for this patient?','["A. Slow transit constipation due to colonic motility dysfunction; management should include increased fluid intake and fiber supplementation","B. Outlet obstruction constipation due to dyssynergic defecation; management should include biofeedback therapy and behavioral training","C. Irritable bowel syndrome with constipation (IBS-C); management should include antispasmodics and low-dose antidepressants","D. Functional constipation due to inadequate dietary fiber; management should include bulk-forming laxatives and increased physical activity"]','B','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> This patient''s presentation with paradoxical anal sphincter contraction during simulated defecation is pathognomonic for dyssynergic defecation, a form of outlet obstruction constipation. The underlying pathophysiology involves impaired coordination between the abdominal muscles, pelvic floor, and anal sphincter during defecation, preventing effective stool evacuation. Biofeedback therapy is the most appropriate initial management as it specifically targets this coordination problem by teaching patients to relax the anal sphincter while increasing intra-abdominal pressure.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. Slow transit constipation due to colonic motility dysfunction:</strong> This would present with prolonged colonic transit time but normal pelvic floor function on examination, not paradoxical sphincter contraction.</li>
<li><strong>C. Irritable bowel syndrome with constipation (IBS-C):</strong> While IBS-C can cause similar symptoms, it lacks the specific finding of paradoxical sphincter contraction and would typically respond to antispasmodics or low-dose antidepressants.</li>
<li><strong>D. Functional constipation due to inadequate dietary fiber:</strong> This would improve with fiber supplementation and increased physical activity but wouldn''t explain the paradoxical sphincter contraction seen on examination.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Paradoxical anal sphincter contraction during defecation is diagnostic for dyssynergic defecation.</li>
<li>Biofeedback therapy is first-line for dyssynergic defecation, with success rates of 70-80%.</li>
<li>Anorectal manometry with balloon expulsion testing is the gold standard for confirming the diagnosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Paradoxical sphincter contraction during defecation equals dyssynergic defecation, which requires biofeedback therapy, not just fiber and fluids.</p>','<p>Dyssynergic Defecation: Paradoxical Sphincter Action (PSA) = Pelvic floor dysfunction</p>','<p>Dyssynergic defecation is often misdiagnosed as simple constipation, leading to years of ineffective treatment with laxatives and fiber supplementation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A:</strong> Slow transit constipation doesn''t cause paradoxical sphincter contraction.</li>
<li><strong>C:</strong> IBS-C lacks the specific sphincter dysfunction seen here.</li>
<li><strong>D:</strong> Functional constipation improves with fiber, but doesn''t explain the sphincter finding.</li>
</ul>','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation','["Constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation","pathophysiology","evaluation","management","dyssynergic defecation"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-18T19:32:14.615Z','2026-10-18T21:37:05.778Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqa000ider84rtazm6c','mcq','When body proportion equal 1:1','["At 7 years","5 years","10 years"]','At 7 years','<p><strong>Why At 7 years is correct:</strong> At approximately 7 years of age, children achieve a body proportion of 1:1, meaning their sitting height equals their standing height. This occurs because the lower extremities have completed their most rapid growth phase during the preschool years, while the trunk continues growing at a slower rate. The transition from the characteristic 2:1 proportion of infancy (twice as long from head to pubis as from pubis to heel) to the adult 1:1 ratio represents a key milestone in skeletal maturation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>5 years:</strong> At 5 years, children still have a sitting height to standing height ratio greater than 1:1, typically around 1.1:1, as the lower limbs are still catching up in growth.</li>
<li><strong>10 years:</strong> By 10 years, most children have already achieved the 1:1 proportion and are entering the prepubertal growth spurt, where proportions may temporarily change again.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The 1:1 body proportion typically occurs between 6-8 years of age.</li>
<li>Before this age, sitting height is greater than standing height (superior segment longer).</li>
<li>After this age, standing height becomes equal to sitting height until the adolescent growth spurt.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Body proportions reach 1:1 at age 7 when lower limb growth catches up to trunk growth.</p>','<p>7-1-1: At age 7, sitting height equals standing height (1:1 ratio).</p>','<p>Body proportion assessment is a simple but valuable tool in evaluating growth disorders and skeletal maturation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>5 years:</strong> At 5 years, sitting height is still greater than standing height.</li>
<li><strong>10 years:</strong> Most children have already achieved 1:1 proportion by this age.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Body Proportions','["body proportion","growth","childhood"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.266Z','2026-10-18T22:34:49.607Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqb000jder8dnz8v8uh','mcq','The correct order for the emergence of temporary teeth','["Central incisors > Lateral incisors > 1st molar > Canines"]','Central incisors > Lateral incisors > 1st molar > Canines','<p><strong>Why Central incisors &gt; Lateral incisors &gt; 1st molar &gt; Canines is correct:</strong> The correct emergence sequence for primary teeth begins with the mandibular central incisors at 6-10 months, followed by maxillary central incisors at 8-12 months, then lateral incisors at 9-13 months. The first primary molars emerge next at 12-16 months, followed by the canines at 16-23 months, and finally the second molars at 23-33 months. This sequence follows a predictable pattern that dentists use to assess normal dental development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>(No other options provided)</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Primary teeth eruption typically begins around 6 months of age.</li>
<li>The sequence follows a specific pattern: central incisors → lateral incisors → first molars → canines → second molars.</li>
<li>The mandibular teeth generally erupt before their maxillary counterparts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Primary teeth emerge in the order: central incisors, lateral incisors, first molars, then canines.</p>','<p>CLM Canines: Central incisors, Lateral incisors, 1st Molar, then Canines.</p>','<p>Delayed tooth eruption beyond 12 months may indicate nutritional deficiencies or other developmental issues.</p>','','Basic','Growth & Development','Dental Development','["teeth","dentition","emergence"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.267Z','2026-10-18T22:34:49.610Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqc000kder8wlsnqajw','mcq','A 4-year old child, Wrist x ray shows 4 carpal bones. This condition is due to:','["Thyrotoxicosis","CAH","Precocious puberty","Hypopituitarism"]','Thyrotoxicosis','<p><strong>Why Thyrotoxicosis is correct:</strong> Thyrotoxicosis (hyperthyroidism) accelerates bone maturation by increasing the metabolic rate and stimulating thyroid hormone receptors on growth plate chondrocytes. This causes premature closure of epiphyseal plates and advanced skeletal age, which in a 4-year-old would manifest as more carpal bones visible on wrist X-ray than expected for chronological age. The thyroid hormone excess speeds up the entire process of ossification.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CAH:</strong> Congenital adrenal hyperplasia typically causes advanced bone age due to androgen excess, but it would not present with isolated carpal bone changes at age 4 without other virilization signs.</li>
<li><strong>Precocious puberty:</strong> While precocious puberty can accelerate bone age, it would typically be associated with breast development or testicular enlargement, not isolated carpal bone changes.</li>
<li><strong>Hypopituitarism:</strong> This would cause delayed bone age and fewer carpal bones, not accelerated maturation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Thyrotoxicosis causes advanced bone age through increased metabolic rate and stimulation of ossification.</li>
<li>At age 4, a normal wrist X-ray shows 0-2 carpal bones; 4 carpal bones indicates accelerated maturation.</li>
<li>The most common cause of accelerated bone age in children is endocrine disorders like hyperthyroidism or precocious puberty.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Thyrotoxicosis accelerates skeletal maturation, causing more carpal bones to appear on X-ray than expected for age.</p>','<p>Too many bones, too soon: Thyrotoxicosis speeds up ossification.</p>','<p>Advanced bone age on X-ray in a young child should prompt evaluation for endocrine disorders like hyperthyroidism.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>CAH:</strong> Causes advanced bone age but would present with virilization signs, not isolated carpal changes.</li>
<li><strong>Precocious puberty:</strong> Would show sexual development signs, not just advanced carpal bones.</li>
<li><strong>Hypopituitarism:</strong> Causes delayed bone age with fewer carpal bones, not accelerated.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Bone Age','["bone age","carpal bones","wrist","precocious"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.268Z','2026-10-18T22:34:49.611Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqz0010der87iisi8yc','mcq','In which pattern of growth the growth rate is fast during the first 2 years of life reaching 80% of adult size then continues to Grow slowly?','["Neural pattern","Genital pattern","Lymphatic pattern","Somatic pattern"]','Neural pattern','<p><strong>Why Neural pattern is correct:</strong> The neural pattern of growth demonstrates rapid development during the first 2 years of life, achieving approximately 80% of adult brain size by age 2, followed by slower but continued growth throughout childhood and adolescence. This pattern reflects the critical period of synaptogenesis, myelination, and neural circuit formation that supports cognitive and motor development. The brain''s growth spurt in early life is essential for establishing fundamental neural pathways that underlie higher cognitive functions. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Genital pattern:</strong> This pattern shows minimal growth until puberty, when it undergoes rapid development, not early rapid growth. - <strong>Lymphatic pattern:</strong> The lymphatic system grows rapidly during the first 2 years but then regresses relative to body size, not continuing slow growth. - <strong>Somatic pattern:</strong> This pattern shows steady, proportional growth throughout childhood without the characteristic early rapid growth phase. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Neural growth follows a cephalocaudal direction (head to tail) and proximodistal direction (trunk to extremities). - The brain reaches 90% of its adult size by age 5, with the most rapid growth occurring in the first 2 years. - Disruptions in neural growth patterns can have profound impacts on cognitive development and neurological function. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Neural growth follows a rapid early development pattern (80% by age 2) followed by slower maturation, distinguishing it from other growth patterns.</p>','<p>NGS - Neural Growth Spurts: 80% by age 2, then slow growth.</p>','<p>Assessing neural growth patterns is crucial in evaluating developmental delays and neurological disorders in children.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Genital pattern:</strong> Shows minimal growth until puberty, not early rapid development. - <strong>Lymphatic pattern:</strong> Grows rapidly early but then regresses relative to body size. - <strong>Somatic pattern:</strong> Demonstrates steady proportional growth without an early rapid phase.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Growth Patterns','["growth","pattern","neural","development"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.291Z','2026-10-18T22:35:09.923Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sr10011der81v3fnxbb','mcq','At What age does the child fine motor development enable him to build a tower of only 6 cubes and draw only horizontal lines?','["15 months","18 months","24 months","36 months"]','24 months','<p><strong>Why 24 months is correct:</strong> By 24 months (2 years), children typically develop the fine motor skills needed to build a tower of 6 cubes and draw horizontal lines, representing significant improvement from earlier milestones. This age corresponds to the development of more precise hand-eye coordination and the pincer grasp, which are necessary for these tasks. The ability to create a 6-cube tower demonstrates improved manual dexterity and planning abilities, while drawing horizontal lines indicates developing graphic representation skills. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>15 months:</strong> At this age, children can typically build a tower of only 2 cubes and may scribble randomly rather than drawing intentional lines. - <strong>18 months:</strong> Children can usually build a 3-4 cube tower and may begin drawing vertical lines but not consistently horizontal ones. - <strong>36 months:</strong> By this age, children can build a tower of 9-10 cubes and draw circles and simple shapes, exceeding the milestones described in the question. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Fine motor development follows a predictable sequence: from raking grasp (6 months) to pincer grasp (9-12 months) to more complex manipulations. - The ability to draw horizontal lines typically appears before vertical lines, which develop around 30 months. - Developmental milestones should be assessed in the context of the child''s overall development and cultural background. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Building a 6-cube tower and drawing horizontal lines are classic 24-month fine motor milestones that represent significant improvement over earlier abilities.</p>','<p>2-6-2: At 24 months, build 6 cubes and draw 2 types of lines (horizontal).</p>','<p>Fine motor delays can be early indicators of neurological disorders and should be promptly evaluated.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>15 months:</strong> Can only build a 2-cube tower and scribbles randomly. - <strong>18 months:</strong> Can build a 3-4 cube tower and may draw vertical lines. - <strong>36 months:</strong> Can build a 9-10 cube tower and draw circles and simple shapes.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Motor Development','["motor","development","fine","cubes","drawing"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.293Z','2026-10-18T22:35:09.925Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56gzr0009qjlrrhxlwmb4','mcq','For how long does the colostrum persist?','["4-10 days.","2-4 days.","12 days.","15-21 days."]','2-4 days.','<p><strong>Why 2-4 days is correct:</strong> Colostrum, the first milk produced after delivery, persists for approximately 2-4 days postpartum. This specialized fluid is rich in immunoglobulins (especially IgA), leukocytes, growth factors, and has lower lactose and fat content compared to mature milk. Its primary function is to provide passive immunity to the newborn and stimulate gut maturation, with the composition changing gradually as transitional milk takes over around day 4-5, followed by mature milk by approximately 10-14 days postpartum. The short duration of colostrum production is evolutionarily designed to provide maximum immune protection during the most vulnerable period immediately after birth.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>4-10 days:</strong> This timeframe includes transitional milk, which follows colostrum and has a different composition with higher lactose and fat content. - <strong>12 days:</strong> By this time, mature milk is well-established, and colostrum production has ceased completely. - <strong>15-21 days:</strong> This period represents exclusive mature milk production, with no colostrum remaining in the breast milk.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Colostrum contains 10-17 times more IgA than mature milk, providing crucial mucosal immunity. - It has a laxative effect that helps clear meconium from the newborn''s intestines. - The volume of colostrum is small (approximately 2-10 mL per feeding) but highly concentrated in nutrients and immune factors.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Colostrum is the ''first milk'' that lasts only 2-4 days postpartum, providing critical immune protection before transitioning to mature milk.</p>','<p>Colostrum Comes First (CCF) for just a Few (2-4) days</p>','<p>Colostrum''s potent immune protection is particularly important for preterm infants who have underdeveloped immune systems.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>4-10 days:</strong> This timeframe actually describes transitional milk, not colostrum. - <strong>12 days:</strong> By this time, mature milk is well-established and colostrum production has ceased. - <strong>15-21 days:</strong> This period represents exclusive mature milk production with no colostrum remaining.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["colostrum","duration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.335Z','2026-10-18T22:35:26.150Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h2r000qqjlraqyofqnq','mcq','A 6-month-old baby who is exclusively formula-fed. The recommendation about water intake','["Unlimited amount","Small amount of water with solid food introduction","No water introduction till 1 year old","Replace formula by water"]','Small amount of water with solid food introduction','<p><strong>Why Small amount of water with solid food introduction is correct:</strong> For a 6-month-old exclusively formula-fed baby, a small amount of water with solid food introduction is appropriate because at this age, infants begin to need additional fluids beyond formula, particularly when consuming solid foods which increase solute load and require dilution. The kidneys of a 6-month-old have matured sufficiently to handle small amounts of water intake, and introducing water with solids helps prevent constipation that can occur with the introduction of iron-fortified cereals. This approach aligns with AAP guidelines which recommend small amounts of water can be introduced with solid foods around 6 months of age. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Unlimited amount:</strong> Excessive water intake can lead to water intoxication, hyponatremia, and seizures in infants due to their immature kidneys and high water-to-solute ratio. - <strong>No water introduction till 1 year old:</strong> While breastfed babies typically don''t need water until later, formula-fed babies may benefit from small amounts with solids due to different electrolyte composition. - <strong>Replace formula by water:</strong> Water lacks the essential nutrients, calories, and proteins necessary for an infant''s growth and development. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Formula-fed infants may need slightly more water than breastfed infants due to the higher renal solute load of formula. - Water should be offered in a cup, not a bottle, to prevent nipple confusion and support oral development. - The total water intake (including that in formula and foods) should not exceed 30-60 oz/day for infants to avoid water intoxication. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Formula-fed babies can have small amounts of water introduced with solid foods at 6 months to help with digestion and prevent constipation, but never replace formula with water.</p>','<p>6-6-6: At 6 months, with 6 oz of water, for 6 types of solid foods</p>','<p>Formula-fed infants have higher renal solute load than breastfed infants, making them more prone to constipation and potentially benefiting from earlier water introduction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Unlimited amount:</strong> Can cause water intoxication and hyponatremia in infants. - <strong>No water introduction till 1 year old:</strong> Formula-fed babies may benefit from small amounts with solids. - <strong>Replace formula by water:</strong> Lacks essential nutrients needed for infant growth.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breast feeding','["water intake","formula","solid food"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.443Z','2026-10-18T22:35:35.105Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6p004oe9oic8x6io0g','mcq','Management of cyanotic spells in tetralogy of Fallot include all the followings except','["Vasodilators","Oxygen therapy","Propranolol","Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3)"]','Vasodilators','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["fallot","cyanotic-spells","management"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.665Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.665Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6q004pe9oihwrvyfq6','mcq','Which of the following is not a hematological complications of congenital cyanotic heart diseases :','["Thrombocytopenia","Iron deficiency anemia","Polycythemia","Thrombocytosis"]','Iron deficiency anemia','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Diseases','["cyanotic","hematology","complications"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.666Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.666Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h650011qjlrl445ym87','mcq','Women presenting by breast fullness, warmth and milk leakage after 3rd day of giving birth. What is the physiological explanation','["Oxytocin surge after feeding","Weaning response","Beginning of Lactogenesis 2 with increased milk volume","Foremilk hindmilk imbalance"]','Beginning of Lactogenesis 2 with increased milk volume','<p><strong>Why Beginning of Lactogenesis 2 with increased milk volume is correct:</strong> Lactogenesis 2 is the onset of copious milk production, typically occurring between 30-40 hours postpartum in most women, but may be delayed until day 3-5 in first-time mothers. This process is triggered by the rapid decline in progesterone levels after placental delivery, combined with the sustained high levels of prolactin. The increased milk volume leads to breast fullness, warmth, and occasional leakage as the mammary glands become engorged with milk production exceeding the infant''s immediate intake capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Oxytocin surge after feeding:</strong> Oxytocin is responsible for milk ejection (let-down reflex) but does not directly cause the increased milk volume characteristic of Lactogenesis 2.</li>
<li><strong>Weaning response:</strong> Weaning refers to the process of gradually stopping breastfeeding, which typically occurs weeks to months postpartum, not immediately after birth.</li>
<li><strong>Foremilk hindmilk imbalance:</strong> This refers to the composition difference between milk at the beginning (foremilk) and end (hindmilk) of a feeding, not the overall increase in milk production.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lactogenesis 1 (initiation) occurs during pregnancy, while Lactogenesis 2 (copious milk production) begins after delivery.</li>
<li>The timing of Lactogenesis 2 can vary based on parity, with first-time mothers often experiencing it later (day 3-5).</li>
<li>Breast engorgement during this period is temporary and can be managed by frequent feeding or pumping.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Lactogenesis 2 is the physiological process occurring 2-5 days postpartum marked by copious milk production due to progesterone withdrawal and prolactin elevation, causing breast fullness and leakage.</p>','<p>L2 = Later (days 3-5), Lots of milk, Lower progesterone</p>','<p>Breast engorgement during Lactogenesis 2 is a normal physiological process that typically resolves within 24-48 hours as breastfeeding becomes established.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Oxytocin surge after feeding:</strong> Oxytocin causes milk ejection, not increased milk volume.</li>
<li><strong>Weaning response:</strong> Weaning occurs much later postpartum, not immediately after birth.</li>
<li><strong>Foremilk hindmilk imbalance:</strong> This refers to milk composition differences, not overall production volume.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Lactation','["breastfeeding","lactation","physiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.565Z','2026-10-18T22:35:41.863Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h6a0012qjlr31msjh88','mcq','Preterm infant in NICU, fed with mother''s expressed milk. NICU staff noticed contains high protein and immune factors than mature term milk','["Contaminated milk","Mother''s diet high protein","Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors","Milk was frozen"]','Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors','<p><strong>Why Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors is correct:</strong> Preterm milk is biologically distinct from term milk and represents an evolutionary adaptation to meet the specific nutritional and immunological needs of premature infants. It contains significantly higher concentrations of protein (2-3 times higher than term milk), particularly whey proteins, which are easier for immature digestive systems to process. Additionally, preterm milk contains elevated levels of immunoglobulins (especially secretory IgA), growth factors, cytokines, and leukocytes that provide enhanced immune protection for the vulnerable preterm infant who has not received the full transfer of maternal antibodies across the placenta.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Contaminated milk:</strong> While expressed milk must be handled properly to prevent contamination, the compositional differences are physiological adaptations, not contamination.</li>
<li><strong>Mother''s diet high protein:</strong> While maternal nutrition can influence some components of breast milk, it does not account for the specific, consistent differences between preterm and term milk.</li>
<li><strong>Milk was frozen:</strong> Freezing may slightly alter some immunological factors but does not increase protein content or fundamentally change the milk''s composition.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Preterm milk has higher protein, sodium, and chloride content compared to term milk.</li>
<li>The immunological components of preterm milk provide crucial protection against infections in vulnerable premature infants.</li>
<li>The composition of preterm milk changes over time, similar to term milk, with colostrum transitioning to mature milk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Preterm milk is a specialized biological fluid with higher protein and immune factors specifically adapted to meet the unique needs of premature infants.</p>','<p>Preterm milk = Protein Plus Protection</p>','<p>Preterm milk is often referred to as ''liquid gold'' for its superior nutritional and immunological properties that cannot be replicated in commercial formulas.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Contaminated milk:</strong> The differences are physiological adaptations, not contamination.</li>
<li><strong>Mother''s diet high protein:</strong> Maternal diet doesn''t account for the specific differences between preterm and term milk.</li>
<li><strong>Milk was frozen:</strong> Freezing doesn''t increase protein content or fundamentally alter milk composition.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Preterm milk','["preterm","milk","protein"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.570Z','2026-10-18T22:35:41.865Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7m0017qjlrq0qzwpd8','mcq','12-month-old infant still breastfeeding and taking family foods. Your advice for mother','["Continue breastfeeding along side family food","Stop breastfeeding immediately","Reduce to 1 fed per day","Offer only milk and no solids"]','Continue breastfeeding along side family food','<p><strong>Why Continue breastfeeding along side family food is correct:</strong> The World Health Organization recommends exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life, followed by continued breastfeeding alongside appropriate complementary foods for up to 2 years or beyond. At 12 months, breast milk continues to provide significant nutritional benefits, including essential fatty acids, immunoglobulins, and growth factors that support the infant''s developing immune system and brain development. Family foods at this age should be appropriately textured and nutritionally balanced to complement, not replace, breast milk. Breastfeeding at this stage also provides emotional security and comfort during a period of rapid development and increasing independence.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stop breastfeeding immediately:</strong> Abruptly stopping breastfeeding at 12 months is unnecessary and potentially harmful, as breast milk continues to provide valuable nutrition and immune protection.</li>
<li><strong>Reduce to 1 fed per day:</strong> Reducing breastfeeding frequency to once daily is not recommended, as it may lead to decreased milk supply and fails to recognize the ongoing benefits of breastfeeding beyond infancy.</li>
<li><strong>Offer only milk and no solids:</strong> At 12 months, infants need complementary foods to meet their increasing nutritional requirements, particularly iron, zinc, and other nutrients that become less concentrated in breast milk as the child grows.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Breastfeeding should continue alongside complementary foods from 6-24 months or beyond as recommended by WHO.</li>
<li>At 12 months, breast milk still provides significant immunological and nutritional benefits.</li>
<li>Complementary foods should be introduced at 6 months and gradually increased in variety and quantity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Breastfeeding should continue alongside complementary foods until at least 2 years of age, as breast milk remains nutritionally and immunologically valuable throughout this period.</p>','<p>WHO: 6 months exclusive, 6+ months with complementary foods, up to 2+ years breastfeeding</p>','<p>Breast milk continues to provide significant immunological protection against respiratory and gastrointestinal infections throughout the second year of life.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Stop breastfeeding immediately:</strong> Abrupt cessation is unnecessary and ignores ongoing benefits of breast milk.</li>
<li><strong>Reduce to 1 fed per day:</strong> This reduction may decrease milk supply and fails to recognize continued benefits.</li>
<li><strong>Offer only milk and no solids:</strong> At 12 months, infants need complementary foods to meet increased nutritional requirements.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Breastfeeding continuation','["breastfeeding","infant nutrition","weaning"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.618Z','2026-10-18T22:35:57.398Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h7s0019qjlriaokbbag','mcq','Infant with confirmed Cow''s milk allergy and contraindicated breastfeeding. The best formula is','["Extensively Hydrolyzed/Amino Acid-based formula"]','Extensively Hydrolyzed/Amino Acid-based formula','<p><strong>Why Extensively Hydrolyzed/Amino Acid-based formula is correct:</strong> For infants with confirmed cow''s milk protein allergy (CMPA) and contraindicated breastfeeding, extensively hydrolyzed formulas (eHF) or amino acid-based formulas (AAF) are the recommended alternatives. These formulas have cow''s milk proteins broken down into smaller peptides (eHF) or to single amino acids (AAF), which are less likely to trigger an allergic reaction. Amino acid-based formulas are the most hypoallergenic option as they contain no intact protein, making them suitable for severe cases of CMPA where extensively hydrolyzed formulas may still cause reactions. These formulas are nutritionally complete and provide all necessary nutrients for infant growth and development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Partially Hydrolyzed Formula:</strong> This option is incorrect as partially hydrolyzed formulas still contain significant amounts of intact milk proteins that can trigger allergic reactions in infants with CMPA.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based Formula:</strong> This is incorrect because approximately 10-14% of infants with CMPA also react to soy protein, making soy formula an unreliable alternative.</li>
<li><strong>Goat''s Milk Formula:</strong> This is incorrect as goat''s milk protein is structurally very similar to cow''s milk protein, with approximately 90% cross-reactivity, making it unsuitable for most infants with CMPA.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Extensively hydrolyzed formulas are first-line for CMPA when breastfeeding isn''t possible.</li>
<li>Amino acid-based formulas are reserved for severe cases or when extensively hydrolyzed formulas fail.</li>
<li>Approximately 10-14% of infants with CMPA also react to soy protein, making soy formula an unreliable alternative.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For infants with confirmed cow''s milk allergy and contraindicated breastfeeding, use extensively hydrolyzed or amino acid-based formulas as they contain broken-down proteins that won''t trigger allergic reactions.</p>','<p>CMPA treatment: EHF first, AAF for severe, soy and goat are no-nos</p>','<p>Most infants with cow''s milk protein allergy will tolerate extensively hydrolyzed formulas, but those with severe reactions may require amino acid-based formulas.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Partially Hydrolyzed Formula:</strong> Contains intact milk proteins that can trigger allergic reactions.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based Formula:</strong> 10-14% of infants with CMPA also react to soy protein.</li>
<li><strong>Goat''s Milk Formula:</strong> Has 90% cross-reactivity with cow''s milk protein.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Infant formula','["cow''s milk allergy","formula","hydrolyzed"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.624Z','2026-10-18T22:35:57.403Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h86001hqjlrd0r4blyd','mcq','Preterm infant ready for discharge but can''t latch effectively. The mother has insufficient milk despite of good lactational support. What is the feeding plan','["Exclusively Cow''s milk formula","Soy-based formula","AR formula","Express breast milk with Preterm formula supplementation"]','Express breast milk with Preterm formula supplementation','<p><strong>Why Express breast milk with Preterm formula supplementation is correct:</strong> Preterm infants have unique nutritional requirements that exceed the composition of term breast milk, including higher protein, calcium, and phosphorus needs. Expressing breast milk provides the immunological benefits and growth factors essential for preterm infants, while preterm formula supplementation ensures adequate caloric and nutrient intake when maternal supply is insufficient. This approach balances the benefits of human milk with the specific nutritional demands of premature development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Exclusively Cow''s milk formula:</strong> Cow''s milk formula lacks the specific immunological components and optimal nutrient profile for preterm infants, potentially leading to poor growth outcomes.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Soy formula contains phytoestrogens and lacks the optimal protein composition for preterm infants, making it less suitable than preterm-specific formulas.</li>
<li><strong>AR formula:</strong> AR (anti-reflux) formula is designed for infants with reflux issues, not for the nutritional needs of preterm infants with insufficient milk supply.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Preterm breast milk has different nutritional composition than term milk and may need supplementation.</li>
<li>The combination of expressed breast milk with preterm formula provides both immunological benefits and adequate nutrition.</li>
<li>Preterm infants require higher protein, calcium, and phosphorus than term infants for optimal bone growth and neurodevelopment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For preterm infants with insufficient milk supply, the optimal feeding plan combines expressed breast milk with preterm formula supplementation to meet both immunological and nutritional needs.</p>','<p>Preterm needs: Breast milk + Preterm formula = Best</p>','<p>Preterm infants require 20-30% more protein than term infants for adequate growth and development.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Exclusively Cow''s milk formula:</strong> Lacks optimal nutrient profile for preterm infants.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Contains phytoestrogens and suboptimal protein for preterm needs.</li>
<li><strong>AR formula:</strong> Designed for reflux, not preterm nutritional requirements.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Preterm feeding','["preterm","breast milk","formula supplementation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.638Z','2026-10-18T22:36:02.474Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbe002uqjlrpqk8x3xb','mcq','What is the correct practice for feeding during diarrhea in children?','["Stop oral feeding and give only juices and water.","Stop breastfeeding and give lactose free formula.","Continue on the same pattern before illness.","Continue breastfeeding and give extra meats which are energy dense."]','Continue breastfeeding and give extra meats which are energy dense.','<p><strong>Why Continue breastfeeding and give extra meats which are energy dense is correct:</strong> During diarrhea, maintaining adequate nutrition is crucial for recovery, and breastfeeding should continue as it provides essential nutrients, immunoglobulins, and hydration. Breast milk is easily digestible and contains anti-inflammatory properties that help protect the gut mucosa. Adding energy-dense foods like meats provides additional calories and nutrients needed for recovery without overloading the digestive system. This approach follows the WHO recommendation to continue feeding during diarrhea and increase calorie density to compensate for increased metabolic demands. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Stop oral feeding and give only juices and water:</strong> This approach can worsen diarrhea by providing excessive simple sugars that draw water into the intestine and lacks necessary nutrients. - <strong>Stop breastfeeding and give lactose free formula:</strong> There''s no evidence to support stopping breastfeeding during diarrhea, and lactose-free formula is only indicated in cases of proven lactose intolerance. - <strong>Continue on the same pattern before illness:</strong> While maintaining usual feeding is important, increasing calorie density is specifically recommended during diarrhea to meet increased metabolic demands. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Continued feeding during diarrhea reduces the duration of illness and improves nutritional outcomes. - Energy-dense foods should be added to the diet during diarrhea to compensate for increased metabolic needs. - Hydration with oral rehydration solution should be maintained alongside continued feeding. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Continue breastfeeding and add energy-dense foods during diarrhea to support recovery while maintaining hydration.</p>','<p>FEED: Feed during diarrhea, Energy-dense foods, Extra hydration, Don''t stop breastfeeding.</p>','<p>Withholding food during diarrhea prolongs the recovery period and increases the risk of malnutrition, contrary to traditional beliefs.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Stop oral feeding and give only juices and water:</strong> This can worsen diarrhea by providing excessive simple sugars and lacks necessary nutrients. - <strong>Stop breastfeeding and give lactose free formula:</strong> There''s no evidence to support stopping breastfeeding during diarrhea. - <strong>Continue on the same pattern before illness:</strong> While maintaining usual feeding is important, increasing calorie density is specifically recommended.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Management','["feeding","diarrhea","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.754Z','2026-10-19T03:34:59.874Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h87001iqjlr3ercxlh0','mcq','In BFHI hospital, the mother discharged without post charge feeding support. Which step is violated','["Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups","On demand feeding","Exclusive breastfeeding","Avoid teats and pacifiers"]','Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups','<p><strong>Why Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups is correct:</strong> The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) Ten Steps to Successful Breastfeeding specifically includes Step 7: ''Give newborn infants no food or drink other than breast milk, unless medically indicated'' and Step 10: ''Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups and refer mothers to them on discharge.'' Discharging a mother without post-discharge feeding support violates this critical step, as ongoing support is essential for breastfeeding success, particularly in the early weeks when challenges commonly arise.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>On demand feeding:</strong> While BFHI promotes on-demand feeding, the scenario doesn''t indicate this was violated, as the mother was discharged without support, not that feeding wasn''t on-demand.</li>
<li><strong>Exclusive breastfeeding:</strong> BFHI promotes exclusive breastfeeding, but the scenario doesn''t indicate supplementation was given, making this violation less likely than lack of support.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid teats and pacifiers:</strong> BFHI discourages artificial nipples, but the scenario doesn''t mention their use, making this violation less likely than lack of post-discharge support.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>BFHI Step 10 specifically requires establishing breastfeeding support groups and referring mothers to them upon discharge.</li>
<li>Post-discharge support is critical for breastfeeding success, particularly in the first 2-4 weeks when challenges commonly emerge.</li>
<li>The BFHI emphasizes continuity of care from hospital to community settings for breastfeeding mothers.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Discharging a breastfeeding mother without post-discharge support violates BFHI Step 10, which mandates establishing and referring to breastfeeding support groups.</p>','<p>BFHI Step 10: Support groups before discharge</p>','<p>Breastfeeding support groups reduce early weaning rates by providing practical help and emotional support during the challenging early weeks.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>On demand feeding:</strong> Not indicated as violated in the scenario.</li>
<li><strong>Exclusive breastfeeding:</strong> Not indicated as violated in the scenario.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid teats and pacifiers:</strong> Not mentioned as occurring in the scenario.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','BFHI guidelines','["bfhi","breastfeeding support","post-discharge"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.639Z','2026-10-18T22:36:02.475Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqo000rder8oib8krex','mcq','follow moving objects to midline, respond to sound by blinking or startle','["At 1 month"]','At 1 month','<p><strong>Why At 1 month is correct:</strong> At 1 month of age, infants begin to demonstrate the ability to follow moving objects to midline, which represents the early development of visual tracking and coordination between the eyes. They also start to respond to sound by blinking or startling, indicating the maturation of the auditory pathway and brainstem reflexes. These responses demonstrate the integration of sensory input with motor output, which is a hallmark of normal neurological development at this age.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>At birth:</strong> Newborns have limited visual acuity and typically cannot track objects to midline consistently, though they may show some brief tracking.</li>
<li><strong>At 3 months:</strong> While more developed, 3-month-olds typically show more sophisticated tracking beyond just midline and may turn their heads toward sounds rather than just startle.</li>
<li><strong>At 6 months:</strong> By 6 months, infants demonstrate much more advanced visual tracking, following objects through 180 degrees of arc and localizing sounds more precisely by turning their heads.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Visual tracking to midline typically emerges between 1-2 months of age.</li>
<li>Auditory startle response to loud sounds is present at birth, but more specific localization develops later.</li>
<li>The absence of these responses by 2 months may indicate neurological impairment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> At 1 month, infants should track objects to midline and startle to sounds, marking early neurological integration of sensory and motor systems.</p>','<p>1-2-3: 1 month midline tracking, 2 months social smile, 3 months rolling over</p>','<p>The absence of visual tracking to midline by 3 months warrants further neurological evaluation as it may indicate cortical visual impairment or other developmental disorders.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>At birth:</strong> Newborns cannot consistently track objects to midline.</li>
<li><strong>At 3 months:</strong> Infants at this age show more advanced tracking beyond midline.</li>
<li><strong>At 6 months:</strong> Tracking is much more sophisticated by this age.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Growth & Development','Sensory Development','["vision","hearing","infant","1 month"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.280Z','2026-10-19T03:31:53.896Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqp000sder8hyvh796w','mcq','Early-onset sign of Autism:','["No social smile at 2 months"]','No social smile at 2 months','<p><strong>Why No social smile at 2 months is correct:</strong> The absence of a social smile by 2 months is considered an early warning sign of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) as it indicates a delay in social-emotional development and reciprocal interaction. Typically developing infants begin to exhibit social smiles between 6-8 weeks of age in response to human faces, particularly their caregivers'' faces. This early social engagement is a critical milestone that forms the foundation for further social communication development. The lack of this response may reflect underlying differences in social motivation and processing that are characteristic of ASD.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No babbling at 6 months:</strong> While delayed babbling can be associated with ASD, it is not considered an early-onset sign as it appears later in development.</li>
<li><strong>No pointing at 12 months:</strong> Lack of pointing by 12 months is a red flag for ASD, but it is not an early-onset sign as it appears much later than 2 months.</li>
<li><strong>No eye contact at 3 months:</strong> While lack of eye contact is concerning, it typically becomes more apparent around 3-4 months rather than being a specific marker at 2 months.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Social smiling typically emerges between 6-8 weeks (1.5-2 months) of age in response to human faces.</li>
<li>Early social engagement deficits, including lack of social smiling, are among the earliest indicators of ASD.</li>
<li>The absence of social smiling by 2 months should prompt developmental screening and monitoring.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The absence of a social smile by 2 months is an early red flag for autism spectrum disorder, indicating a delay in fundamental social engagement.</p>','<p>2-4-6-8: 2 months social smile, 4 months babbling, 6 months sitting, 8 months crawling</p>','<p>Early identification of social communication delays, such as lack of social smiling by 2 months, allows for earlier intervention which can significantly improve developmental outcomes in children with ASD.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>No babbling at 6 months:</strong> This appears later and is not considered early-onset.</li>
<li><strong>No pointing at 12 months:</strong> This is a later developmental marker, not early-onset.</li>
<li><strong>No eye contact at 3 months:</strong> While concerning, this becomes more apparent later than 2 months.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Growth & Development','Autism','["autism","social","milestone","early"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.281Z','2026-10-19T03:31:53.903Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sqq000tder8poun2hlw','mcq','Growth velocity in a normal child between the ages of 3 and 9 years is','["10 cm or more/year","5 cm or more/year","8 cm or more/year"]','10 cm or more/year','<p><strong>Why 10 cm or more/year is correct:</strong> During the preschool and early school years (ages 3-9), children experience a relatively stable period of linear growth with a velocity of approximately 5-7 cm/year, but the normal range extends to 10 cm/year or more. This growth velocity represents the steady, consistent increase in height that occurs during this period before the prepubertal growth spurt. The measurement of 10 cm/year or more is at the upper limit of normal and represents the child''s maximum growth potential during this stable period.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>5 cm or more/year:</strong> While this is within the normal range, it represents the lower end of normal growth velocity during this period and would be considered slow growth for many children.</li>
<li><strong>8 cm or more/year:</strong> This is below the upper limit of normal growth velocity during this age range and would not represent the maximum expected growth.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Growth velocity during ages 3-9 years is relatively stable at 5-7 cm/year, with normal range extending to 10 cm/year.</li>
<li>Growth less than 5 cm/year during this period warrants investigation for endocrine, nutritional, or chronic disease causes.</li>
<li>The prepubertal growth spurt typically begins around age 9-10 years in girls and 10-11 years in boys, increasing growth velocity beyond 10 cm/year.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> During ages 3-9 years, normal growth velocity ranges from 5-10 cm/year, with 10 cm/year representing the upper limit of expected growth.</p>','<p>3-9-10: Ages 3-9 years, normal growth up to 10 cm/year</p>','<p>Growth velocity is a more sensitive indicator of growth disorders than absolute height measurements alone, as it reflects the child''s current growth status.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>5 cm or more/year:</strong> This is at the lower end of normal, not the maximum expected growth.</li>
<li><strong>8 cm or more/year:</strong> This is below the upper limit of normal growth velocity during this period.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Growth Velocity','["growth","velocity","childhood"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.282Z','2026-10-19T03:31:53.904Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6r004qe9oi1xt31t7m','mcq','Widely fixed splitted S2 is pathognomonic to .','["Pulmonary Atresia","Pulmonary stenosis","Atrial septal defect","ventricular septal defect"]','Atrial septal defect','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart Sounds','["S2","split","ASD"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.667Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.667Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56ha80024qjlrvyamv4d5','mcq','9- which of the following metabolic cause of constipation;','["hypo calcemia","hypo natremia","hypokalemia"]','hypokalemia','<p><strong>Why hypokalemia is correct:</strong> Hypokalemia causes constipation through impaired neuromuscular function in the gastrointestinal tract. Low potassium levels reduce the amplitude of smooth muscle contractions in the colon, leading to decreased peristalsis and slowed transit time. This occurs because potassium is essential for maintaining the resting membrane potential and normal electrical activity in smooth muscle cells. Additionally, hypokalemia can reduce acetylcholine release from enteric neurons, further diminishing propulsive motility.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> While hypocalcemia can cause various neuromuscular symptoms like tetany and paresthesias, it does not directly impair gastrointestinal motility to cause constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Hyponatremia:</strong> Hyponatremia primarily affects the central nervous system and can cause neurological symptoms, but it does not directly interfere with colonic motility or cause constipation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypokalemia is a common metabolic cause of constipation, particularly in patients on diuretics or with chronic kidney disease.</li>
<li>The classic presentation of hypokalemia includes muscle weakness, fatigue, and ECG changes (U waves, ST depression, T-wave flattening) in addition to constipation.</li>
<li>Severe hypokalemia (K+ &lt; 2.5 mEq/L) can lead to paralytic ileus, a more severe form of constipation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypokalemia causes constipation by impairing smooth muscle contraction and enteric neurotransmission in the gastrointestinal tract.</p>','<p>Hypokalemia → Constipation: ''Low K, slow GI''</p>','<p>Hypokalemia-induced constipation typically resolves with potassium repletion, making it one of the few metabolic causes of constipation that is reversible with targeted treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> Does not directly impair gastrointestinal motility.</li>
<li><strong>Hyponatremia:</strong> Primarily affects the central nervous system, not GI motility.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Endocrinology','Constipation','["constipation","metabolic","hypokalemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.712Z','2026-10-19T03:34:02.507Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56ha90025qjlrag74toio','mcq','10- Which of the following diseases has constipation as its clinical feature?','["Rheumatic fever.","Interstitial nephritis.","Irritable bowel syndrome.","Onchocercosis."]','Irritable bowel syndrome.','<p><strong>Why Irritable bowel syndrome is correct:</strong> Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder characterized by abdominal pain and altered bowel habits, with constipation being one of the predominant subtypes (IBS-C). The pathophysiology involves visceral hypersensitivity, altered gut motility, and changes in the gut-brain axis. In IBS-C, there is delayed colonic transit time, increased compliance of the rectosigmoid, and impaired relaxation of the internal anal sphincter during defecation, leading to difficulty passing stool.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rheumatic fever:</strong> An autoimmune inflammatory condition triggered by group A streptococcal infection, characterized by carditis, arthritis, and chorea, but not constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Interstitial nephritis:</strong> An inflammatory condition of the kidney interstitium that typically presents with acute kidney injury, fever, and rash, but does not involve gastrointestinal symptoms like constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Onchocercosis:</strong> A parasitic infection caused by Onchocerca volvulus, transmitted by blackflies, leading to skin changes, eye involvement, and lymphadenopathy, but not constipation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>IBS is diagnosed using Rome IV criteria, which include recurrent abdominal pain at least 1 day/week in the last 3 months associated with two or more of: related to defecation, change in stool frequency, or change in stool form.</li>
<li>IBS-C specifically involves hard or lumpy stools in ≥25% of bowel movements and loose or watery stools in &lt;25% of bowel movements.</li>
<li>Red flag symptoms that suggest organic disease rather than IBS include weight loss, nocturnal symptoms, onset after age 50, and family history of inflammatory bowel disease or colorectal cancer.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Irritable bowel syndrome is the only condition among the options that directly causes constipation as a primary clinical feature through altered gut motility and visceral hypersensitivity.</p>','<p>IBS-C: ''Can''t Go'' = Constipation</p>','<p>IBS-C often responds poorly to traditional laxatives but may benefit from secretagogues like lubiprostone or chloride channel activators.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Rheumatic fever:</strong> An autoimmune condition affecting joints and heart, not GI motility.</li>
<li><strong>Interstitial nephritis:</strong> Primarily causes kidney dysfunction, not constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Onchocercosis:</strong> A parasitic infection affecting skin and eyes, not GI function.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["constipation","clinical feature","IBS"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.713Z','2026-10-19T03:34:02.510Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56haa0026qjlrlfhilhfl','mcq','11- Which of the following is considered one of the triggers of functional constipation in children?','["Excessive parental pressure"]','Excessive parental pressure','<p><strong>Why Excessive parental pressure is correct:</strong> Excessive parental pressure is a recognized psychological trigger for functional constipation in children, particularly in the context of stool withholding behavior. When children experience pressure or anxiety related to toilet training, they may consciously or subconsciously withhold stool to avoid painful or frightening defecation experiences. This behavior leads to stool accumulation in the rectum, which stretches the rectal walls and decreases rectal sensitivity, creating a vicious cycle where larger, harder stools become more painful to pass, reinforcing the withholding behavior.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While no other options were provided in this question, it''s important to note that functional constipation in children is multifactorial and can also be triggered by dietary changes, illness, medications, or insufficient toilet training, but excessive parental pressure is a well-documented psychological trigger.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Functional constipation is the most common gastrointestinal disorder in children, accounting for 3-5% of pediatric office visits.</li>
<li>The vicious cycle of constipation involves stool withholding → rectal distension → decreased rectal sensation → larger, harder stools → more painful defecation → continued withholding.</li>
<li>Treatment involves a combination of behavioral interventions, stool softeners, and regular toilet sitting routines, with addressing psychological factors being crucial for long-term success.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Excessive parental pressure can trigger functional constipation in children by promoting stool withholding behavior that initiates a vicious cycle of painful defecation and further constipation.</p>','<p>Parent Pressure → Painful Pooping → Problem</p>','<p>Addressing the psychological component is as important as medical management in childhood functional constipation, as the withholding behavior often persists even after physical resolution.</p>','','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["functional constipation","triggers","psychological"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.714Z','2026-10-19T03:34:02.511Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbb002sqjlrva2o13vu','mcq','A child presents with diarrhea, vomiting, lethargy, and sunken eyes. This is consistent with:','["Gastroenteritis with Mild dehydration","Gastroenteritis with Moderate dehydration","Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration","Gastroenteritis with Viral diarrhea"]','Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration','<p><strong>Why Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration is correct:</strong> Severe dehydration in this child is indicated by the presence of lethargy and sunken eyes, which are classic signs of significant fluid loss. The combination of diarrhea and vomiting leads to substantial fluid and electrolyte depletion, resulting in poor tissue perfusion and neurological symptoms like lethargy. Sunken eyes specifically reflect significant loss of subcutaneous tissue fluid, a hallmark of severe dehydration requiring immediate intervention with intravenous fluids. The absence of tears and prolonged capillary refill time would further support this diagnosis, though not mentioned in the stem. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Gastroenteritis with Mild dehydration:</strong> Mild dehydration typically presents with thirst, dry mucous membranes, and slightly decreased urine output, but not lethargy or sunken eyes. - <strong>Gastroenteritis with Moderate dehydration:</strong> Moderate dehydration includes more pronounced signs like absent tears, sunken fontanelle in infants, and decreased skin turgor, but lethargy is not typically present. - <strong>Gastroenteritis with Viral diarrhea:</strong> While viral diarrhea is a common cause of gastroenteritis, this option doesn''t address the severity of dehydration, which is the critical clinical feature in this presentation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Severe dehydration is characterized by lethargy, sunken eyes, absent tears, and prolonged capillary refill time. - The presence of lethargy in a child with diarrhea and vomiting indicates severe dehydration requiring urgent fluid resuscitation. - Dehydration severity is assessed using a combination of clinical signs including skin turgor, mucous membrane moisture, and mental status. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Lethargy and sunken eyes in a child with diarrhea and vomiting indicate severe dehydration requiring immediate medical intervention.</p>','<p>The 6-6-2 rule for severe dehydration: 6% weight loss, 6-hour capillary refill, and 2 mmol/L bicarbonate.</p>','<p>In children with diarrhea, assessing for dehydration severity is more important than identifying the specific pathogen, as dehydration is the primary cause of mortality.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with Mild dehydration:</strong> Mild dehydration presents with thirst and dry mucous membranes but not lethargy or sunken eyes. - <strong>Gastroenteritis with Moderate dehydration:</strong> Moderate dehydration includes absent tears and decreased skin turgor but not lethargy. - <strong>Gastroenteritis with Viral diarrhea:</strong> This option doesn''t address the critical dehydration severity signs present in the case.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Assessment','["dehydration","gastroenteritis","assessment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.751Z','2026-10-19T03:34:59.871Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbd002tqjlriliiz5sb','mcq','During evaluation for chronic diarrheal illness, which of these following symptoms and signs can be suggestive for chronic nonspecific diarrhea (toddler''s diarrhea) in children?','["Pain, distension and vomiting.","Faltered growth.","Skin manifestation.","Normal growth pattern."]','Normal growth pattern.','<p><strong>Why Normal growth pattern is correct:</strong> Toddler''s diarrhea, also known as chronic nonspecific diarrhea, is a benign condition characterized by loose, frequent stools in otherwise healthy children. The defining feature is that these children maintain a normal growth pattern and development, as their nutritional status is not compromised. The diarrhea typically occurs due to excessive fluid intake relative to solute intake, rapid intestinal transit time, and increased intake of certain carbohydrates like fruit juices. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Pain, distension and vomiting:</strong> These symptoms suggest a more serious condition like inflammatory bowel disease, celiac disease, or infection, not the benign nature of toddler''s diarrhea. - <strong>Faltered growth:</strong> Poor weight gain or growth failure indicates an organic cause of diarrhea such as malabsorption syndromes (celiac disease), inflammatory bowel disease, or chronic infections. - <strong>Skin manifestation:</strong> Skin manifestations would suggest an allergic component or associated condition like dermatitis herpetiformis in celiac disease, not typical of toddler''s diarrhea. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Toddler''s diarrhea is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring normal growth and absence of red flag symptoms. - The condition typically resolves by age 4-5 years as dietary habits mature. - It''s associated with high intake of fruit juices, carbohydrates, and low fat intake. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Normal growth pattern is the key differentiating feature of benign toddler''s diarrhea from more serious causes of chronic diarrhea.</p>','<p>GROW: Growth is normal, Regular activity, No red flags, Often resolves with age.</p>','<p>Toddler''s diarrhea is a functional disorder that doesn''t require extensive investigation when growth is normal and no red flag symptoms are present.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pain, distension and vomiting:</strong> These symptoms suggest a more serious condition like inflammatory bowel disease or infection. - <strong>Faltered growth:</strong> Poor weight gain indicates an organic cause like malabsorption syndromes. - <strong>Skin manifestation:</strong> Skin manifestations suggest an allergic component or associated condition like celiac disease.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Chronic Diarrhea','["chronic diarrhea","toddler","growth"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.753Z','2026-10-19T03:34:59.874Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdx003mqjlrl7sul6c7','mcq','Which of these symptoms can exclude the diagnosis of gastroesophageal reflux?','["Self-limited course in some cases.","Regurgitation following feeding.","Bile stained vomitus.","Possible wheezy chest"]','Bile stained vomitus.','<p><strong>Why Bile stained vomitus is correct:</strong> Bile-stained vomitus is not associated with gastroesophageal reflux (GER) because bile is produced in the liver and stored in the gallbladder, then released into the duodenum. For bile to appear in vomitus, the reflux must extend beyond the stomach into the duodenum, which is characteristic of bilious vomiting rather than simple GER. GER involves reflux of gastric contents (acid and partially digested food) into the esophagus without bile contamination, making bile-stained vomitus an exclusion criterion for GER.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Self-limited course in some cases:</strong> GER often resolves spontaneously as the lower esophageal sphincter matures, making this a characteristic feature rather than an exclusion criterion.</li>
<li><strong>Regurgitation following feeding:</strong> This is a classic symptom of GER, occurring when gastric contents flow back into the esophagus after feeding.</li>
<li><strong>Possible wheezy chest:</strong> GER can cause respiratory symptoms including wheezing due to microaspiration of gastric contents into the lungs, making this associated with GER.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Bile-stained vomiting suggests pathology distal to the pylorus (e.g., malrotation, duodenal atresia) rather than GER.</li>
<li>GER symptoms typically include regurgitation, heartburn, and respiratory symptoms, but never bile-stained vomitus.</li>
<li>The presence of bile in vomitus should prompt immediate evaluation for surgical causes of intestinal obstruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Bile-stained vomitus excludes GER because bile originates in the duodenum, beyond the reach of typical gastroesophageal reflux.</p>','<p>GER = No Bile (GERB - Gastroesophageal Reflux without Bile)</p>','<p>Bile-stained vomiting in an infant is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise, as it may indicate malrotation with volvulus.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Self-limited course in some cases:</strong> GER often resolves spontaneously as the infant matures.</li>
<li><strong>Regurgitation following feeding:</strong> This is a classic symptom of GER.</li>
<li><strong>Possible wheezy chest:</strong> GER can cause respiratory symptoms including wheezing.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','GERD','["gerd","diagnosis","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.845Z','2026-10-19T03:36:24.463Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbf002vqjlroa7soteb','mcq','What is the duration diarrhea in persistent diarrhea?','["More than 4wk.","More than 2wk.","More than 6wk.","More than 8wk."]','More than 2wk.','<p><strong>Why More than 2wk is correct:</strong> Persistent diarrhea is defined as diarrhea lasting more than 2 weeks but less than 4 weeks, distinguishing it from acute diarrhea (less than 2 weeks) and chronic diarrhea (more than 4 weeks). This classification is based on the natural history of diarrheal illnesses, where most infectious causes resolve within 2 weeks, and persistence suggests either ongoing infection, malabsorption, or inflammatory conditions requiring further investigation. The 2-week threshold helps clinicians differentiate between self-limiting illnesses and those needing more extensive diagnostic workup and management.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>More than 4wk:</strong> This describes chronic diarrhea, not persistent diarrhea, which has a specific duration classification in medical literature.</li>
<li><strong>More than 6wk:</strong> This exceeds the definition of chronic diarrhea and would be considered a prolonged or persistent chronic condition.</li>
<li><strong>More than 8wk:</strong> This represents a very prolonged diarrheal illness that would fall under chronic diarrhea and might indicate serious underlying pathology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Diarrhea duration classifications: acute (&lt;2wk), persistent (2-4wk), chronic (&gt;4wk)</li>
<li>Persistent diarrhea often requires investigation for non-infectious causes like malabsorption or inflammatory bowel disease</li>
<li>The 2-week mark is crucial for determining when to move beyond symptomatic treatment to diagnostic evaluation</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Persistent diarrhea is specifically defined as lasting more than 2 weeks but less than 4 weeks, serving as an important clinical threshold for further investigation.</p>','<p>ACD: Acute (&lt;2wk), Chronic (&gt;4wk), and the gap in between is Persistent (2-4wk)</p>','<p>The duration of diarrhea is a critical clinical parameter that guides diagnostic approach and management, with different thresholds indicating different levels of concern and investigation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>More than 4wk:</strong> This describes chronic diarrhea, not persistent diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>More than 6wk:</strong> This exceeds the definition of chronic diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>More than 8wk:</strong> This represents a very prolonged diarrheal illness indicating serious pathology.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Classification','["persistent diarrhea","duration","classification"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.755Z','2026-10-19T03:35:17.641Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbk002xqjlr6itxhxom','mcq','Which of these causes is the least common regarding chronic diarrhea in children?','["Celiac disease.","Cows milk allergy.","Toddlers diarrhea.","Lactose intolerance."]','Lactose intolerance.','<p><strong>Why Lactose intolerance is the least common:</strong> Lactose intolerance is actually relatively uncommon in young children compared to the other options, as primary lactose deficiency typically develops after age 5 when lactase production naturally declines. In contrast, celiac disease affects approximately 1% of children, cows milk allergy affects 2-3% of infants and young children, and toddlers diarrhea (also called chronic nonspecific diarrhea of childhood) is extremely common, affecting up to 8-9% of toddlers. The prevalence of lactose intolerance in young children is significantly lower than these other causes, making it the least likely among the given options.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Celiac disease:</strong> This is a common cause of chronic diarrhea in children, with a prevalence of about 1% and often presents with failure to thrive, abdominal pain, and steatorrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Cows milk allergy:</strong> This is a frequent cause of chronic diarrhea in infants and young children, typically presenting in the first year of life with gastrointestinal symptoms including diarrhea, vomiting, and blood in stool.</li>
<li><strong>Toddlers diarrhea:</strong> This is extremely common in children aged 1-5 years, characterized by frequent, watery stools without blood or mucus, and normal growth and development.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lactose intolerance is rare in young children but becomes more common after age 5</li>
<li>Cows milk allergy typically presents in infancy and is more common than lactose intolerance in this age group</li>
<li>Toddlers diarrhea is the most common cause of chronic diarrhea in children aged 1-5 years</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Among these options, lactose intolerance is the least common cause of chronic diarrhea in children due to its lower prevalence compared to celiac disease, cows milk allergy, and toddlers diarrhea.</p>','<p>CCM: Celiac (common), Cows milk (common), Toddlers diarrhea (most common) - leaving Lactose intolerance (least common)</p>','<p>When evaluating chronic diarrhea in children, consider age-specific prevalence patterns - lactose intolerance is uncommon before age 5 while cows milk allergy peaks in infancy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Celiac disease:</strong> Affects approximately 1% of children and is a common cause of chronic diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Cows milk allergy:</strong> Affects 2-3% of infants and young children, more common than lactose intolerance.</li>
<li><strong>Toddlers diarrhea:</strong> Extremely common in children aged 1-5 years, affecting up to 8-9%.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Chronic Diarrhea','["chronic","causes","rare"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.760Z','2026-10-19T03:35:17.643Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hbo0030qjlr6iqid2v8','mcq','What is the most common viral cause of acute gastroenteritis in children?','["Sapovirus.","Rotavirus.","Norovirus.","Adenovirus."]','Rotavirus.','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Rotavirus is the most common viral cause of acute gastroenteritis in children worldwide, accounting for approximately 30-40% of severe diarrhea cases in children under 5 years old. The virus infects the mature enterocytes of the small intestine villi, causing blunting of the villi, malabsorption, and secretory diarrhea, which typically presents with profuse watery diarrhea, vomiting, and fever. Rotavirus has a distinctive seasonal pattern in temperate climates, with peak incidence during the winter months, and is highly contagious, spreading via the fecal-oral route. The introduction of rotavirus vaccination programs has significantly reduced the incidence of severe rotavirus gastroenteritis and hospitalizations in children. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A. Sapovirus:</strong> Sapovirus causes gastroenteritis but is less common than rotavirus, typically presenting with milder symptoms and often affecting older children and adults. - <strong>C. Norovirus:</strong> Norovirus is a common cause of gastroenteritis but is more prevalent in older children and adults, and is the leading cause of outbreaks in closed communities like schools and cruise ships. - <strong>D. Adenovirus:</strong> Adenovirus (particularly serotypes 40 and 41) can cause gastroenteritis but is less common than rotavirus and is more often associated with respiratory symptoms, conjunctivitis, or urinary tract infections. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Rotavirus is the leading cause of severe viral gastroenteritis in children worldwide, with peak incidence in winter months. - The classic presentation of rotavirus includes profuse watery diarrhea, vomiting, and fever, often leading to dehydration. - Rotavirus vaccination has dramatically reduced hospitalizations for rotavirus gastroenteritis in countries with high vaccination rates. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rotavirus is the most common viral cause of severe gastroenteritis in young children, characterized by winter outbreaks and profuse watery diarrhea.</p>','<p>Rotavirus Rules the Roost in infants - remember it''s the most common viral cause of severe gastroenteritis in young children.</p>','<p>Rotavirus vaccination has revolutionized the prevention of severe gastroenteritis in infants, with the first dose typically administered at 6-8 weeks of age.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. Sapovirus:</strong> Less common than rotavirus and typically causes milder symptoms. - <strong>C. Norovirus:</strong> More common in older children and adults and associated with outbreaks in closed communities. - <strong>D. Adenovirus:</strong> Less common than rotavirus and often associated with other symptoms like respiratory or urinary tract infections.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Viral Gastroenteritis','["viral","rotavirus","common"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.764Z','2026-10-19T03:35:31.887Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hd7003aqjlryurkhdb7','mcq','Which of the following is a common cause of chronic diarrhea in infants?','["Appendicitis","Cow''s milk protein allergy (CMPA)","Acute otitis media","Urinary tract infection"]','Cow''s milk protein allergy (CMPA)','<p><strong>Why Cow''s milk protein allergy (CMPA) is correct:</strong> CMPA is a common cause of chronic diarrhea in infants due to an immune-mediated response to cow''s milk proteins, leading to inflammation in the gastrointestinal tract. This immune reaction can damage the intestinal mucosa, resulting in malabsorption and persistent diarrhea. The mechanism involves IgE-mediated and non-IgE-mediated pathways, with the latter being more commonly associated with chronic gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea, vomiting, and failure to thrive. CMPA typically presents in the first few months of life and often resolves by 2-3 years of age.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Appendicitis:</strong> While appendicitis can cause diarrhea, it is typically acute rather than chronic and is associated with severe abdominal pain, fever, and vomiting.</li>
<li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> This is an ear infection that may irritate a child systemically but does not directly cause chronic gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Urinary tract infection:</strong> UTIs can present with diarrhea in infants, but this is usually acute and accompanied by urinary symptoms like foul-smelling urine or fever.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>CMPA is one of the most common food allergies in infancy, affecting approximately 2-7.5% of children.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is often clinical, with improvement on elimination diet and recurrence with rechallenge.</li>
<li>CMPA can present with diverse symptoms including diarrhea, vomiting, blood in stool, and atopic dermatitis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cow''s milk protein allergy is a leading cause of chronic diarrhea in infants due to immune-mediated gastrointestinal inflammation.</p>','<p>CMPA: Chronic Milk Protein Allergy</p>','<p>When evaluating chronic diarrhea in infants, always consider CMPA and perform a trial elimination of cow''s milk protein before pursuing more invasive testing.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Appendicitis:</strong> Causes acute diarrhea with severe abdominal pain and fever, not chronic.</li>
<li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> An ear infection that doesn''t directly cause chronic gastrointestinal symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Urinary tract infection:</strong> Typically presents with acute diarrhea and urinary symptoms, not chronic.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Chronic Diarrhea','["chronic","infants","milk allergy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.819Z','2026-10-19T03:35:34.781Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hd8003bqjlr5bxtfivh','mcq','A child with chronic diarrhea, weight loss, and positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies most likely has:','["Lactose intolerance","Irritable bowel syndrome","Celiac disease","Functional diarrhea"]','Celiac disease','<p><strong>Why Celiac disease is correct:</strong> Celiac disease is the most likely diagnosis in a child with chronic diarrhea, weight loss, and positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies because these findings represent the classic triad of celiac disease. The positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies indicate an autoimmune response to gluten, which damages the intestinal villi, leading to malabsorption, chronic diarrhea, and weight loss. This autoimmune process is triggered by gluten exposure in genetically susceptible individuals (HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 haplotypes) and results in characteristic histological changes on intestinal biopsy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lactose intolerance:</strong> While it can cause diarrhea, it typically does not lead to weight loss or positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies, and symptoms improve with lactose restriction.</li>
<li><strong>Irritable bowel syndrome:</strong> IBS is a functional disorder that does not cause weight loss or positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies.</li>
<li><strong>Functional diarrhea:</strong> This diagnosis is made by exclusion and does not explain the positive antibodies or weight loss.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Tissue transglutaminase antibodies have &gt;95% sensitivity and specificity for celiac disease in symptomatic individuals.</li>
<li>Celiac disease can present with diverse gastrointestinal and extraintestinal symptoms including failure to thrive, anemia, and dermatitis herpetiformis.</li>
<li>The diagnosis requires both serologic testing (tTG-IgA) and confirmation with intestinal biopsy showing villous atrophy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Chronic diarrhea with weight loss and positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies is pathognomonic for celiac disease due to autoimmune-mediated intestinal damage from gluten exposure.</p>','<p>CD: Chronic Diarrhea + positive tTG = Celiac Disease</p>','<p>Always screen for celiac disease with tissue transglutaminase antibodies in children with chronic diarrhea and failure to thrive before making alternative diagnoses.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lactose intolerance:</strong> Causes diarrhea without weight loss or positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies.</li>
<li><strong>Irritable bowel syndrome:</strong> A functional disorder without weight loss or specific antibody markers.</li>
<li><strong>Functional diarrhea:</strong> Diagnosed by exclusion and doesn''t explain the positive antibodies or weight loss.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Celiac Disease','["celiac","chronic","antibodies"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.820Z','2026-10-19T03:35:34.783Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hd9003cqjlrpmdugzme','mcq','What is the duration of diarrhea in persistent diarrhea?','["More than 4wk.","More than 2wk.","More than 6wk.","More than 8wk."]','More than 4wk.','<p><strong>Why More than 4wk is correct:</strong> The World Health Organization (WHO) defines persistent diarrhea as diarrhea lasting more than 14 days (2 weeks), but in clinical practice and many medical guidelines, persistent diarrhea is specifically defined as diarrhea lasting more than 4 weeks. This longer duration distinguishes it from acute diarrhea and indicates a different diagnostic approach, often requiring investigation for underlying causes like chronic infections, malabsorption syndromes, or inflammatory bowel disease. The 4-week threshold is clinically significant as it represents the point where dehydration risk decreases but nutritional consequences become more prominent.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>More than 2wk:</strong> While the WHO uses 14 days as a cutoff for persistent diarrhea, many clinical guidelines and studies specifically use 4 weeks as the threshold for persistent diarrhea requiring further investigation.</li>
<li><strong>More than 6wk:</strong> This duration would be classified as chronic diarrhea, which has different implications and may indicate more serious underlying pathology.</li>
<li><strong>More than 8wk:</strong> This duration exceeds the standard definition of persistent diarrhea and falls into the chronic diarrhea category, suggesting more significant underlying pathology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acute diarrhea is defined as lasting less than 14 days, persistent diarrhea as 14-30 days, and chronic diarrhea as more than 30 days in WHO classification.</li>
<li>Persistent diarrhea requires different management than acute diarrhea, with emphasis on nutritional rehabilitation and investigation of underlying causes.</li>
<li>The 4-week threshold is clinically important as it marks when nutritional consequences become more significant than dehydration risks.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Persistent diarrhea is defined as lasting more than 4 weeks, distinguishing it from acute diarrhea and indicating the need for investigation of underlying causes.</p>','<p>PD: Persistent Diarrhea = 4+ weeks</p>','<p>The 4-week threshold for persistent diarrhea is clinically significant as it marks when nutritional consequences become more important than dehydration risks.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>More than 2wk:</strong> While WHO uses 14 days, clinical guidelines often use 4 weeks as the threshold for persistent diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>More than 6wk:</strong> This duration is classified as chronic diarrhea, not persistent diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>More than 8wk:</strong> This exceeds the standard definition of persistent diarrhea and indicates chronic diarrhea.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Persistent Diarrhea','["persistent","duration","chronic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.821Z','2026-10-19T03:35:34.784Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdy003nqjlrcasycn9x','mcq','The most common type for tracheoesophageal fistula is:','["Atresia with distal fistula","Atresia with double fistula","Atresia with proximal fistula","Atresia with H shaped fistula"]','Atresia with distal fistula','<p><strong>Why Atresia with distal fistula is correct:</strong> The most common type of tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) is type C, which consists of esophageal atresia with a distal fistula to the trachea. This occurs in approximately 85% of cases. The mechanism involves failed separation of the trachea and esophagus during embryonic development, with the proximal esophagus ending in a blind pouch and the distal esophagus connected to the trachea via a fistula.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Atresia with double fistula:</strong> This is type D TEF, accounting for only about 2% of cases, with both proximal and distal fistulas present.</li>
<li><strong>Atresia with proximal fistula:</strong> This is type B TEF, which is extremely rare (about 1% of cases), with only a proximal fistula and no distal connection.</li>
<li><strong>Atresia with H shaped fistula:</strong> This is type E TEF, representing about 4% of cases, with an H-shaped fistula between the esophagus and trachea without atresia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Type C (atresia with distal fistula) accounts for approximately 85% of all TEF cases.</li>
<li>Polyhydramnios and inability to pass a feeding tube are classic findings in esophageal atresia.</li>
<li>The presence of a fistula allows air to enter the stomach, making the abdomen distended on X-ray.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember that ''C'' is common - type C (atresia with distal fistula) is the most common tracheoesophageal anomaly.</p>','<p>Type C is Common (atresia with distal fistula)</p>','<p>The ''rule of sevens'' for TEF: approximately 70% are associated with other anomalies, 70% are premature, and 70% are male.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Atresia with double fistula:</strong> This is type D TEF, accounting for only about 2% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>Atresia with proximal fistula:</strong> This is type B TEF, which is extremely rare (about 1% of cases).</li>
<li><strong>Atresia with H shaped fistula:</strong> This is type E TEF, representing about 4% of cases.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Respiratory','Tracheo-esophageal Fistula','["tracheo-esophageal fistula","diagnosis","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.846Z','2026-10-19T03:36:24.465Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he8003vqjlrr4o81jvm','mcq','How to differentiate between Blackfan Diamond syndrome and Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood','["HbF","Bone marrow","Normal skeletal survey"]','Normal skeletal survey','<p><strong>Why Normal skeletal survey is correct:</strong> Diamond-Blackfan anemia (DBA) is characterized by physical anomalies including craniofacial abnormalities, thumb abnormalities, and other skeletal defects, which are visible on skeletal survey. Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood (TEC) is a self-limited condition without associated physical anomalies or skeletal abnormalities. The presence of normal skeletal findings helps differentiate TEC from DBA, as DBA patients typically show various skeletal abnormalities that can be detected through imaging.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>HbF:</strong> Both DBA and TEC can have elevated hemoglobin F levels, so this finding is not specific for differentiation.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Both conditions initially show hypocellular bone marrow with erythroid precursors, making this finding non-discriminatory between the two disorders.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Diamond-Blackfan anemia is associated with physical anomalies including thumb abnormalities, cleft palate, and short stature.</li>
<li>Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood is a diagnosis of exclusion that typically occurs between 2 months and 4 years of age.</li>
<li>The reticulocyte count is low in both conditions, but TEC resolves spontaneously while DBA is chronic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Diamond-Blackfan anemia shows skeletal abnormalities on imaging, while transient erythroblastopenia of childhood has normal skeletal findings.</p>','<p>DBA has Deformities (skeletal), while TEC is Transient and self-limited.</p>','<p>Diamond-Blackfan anemia is one of the inherited bone marrow failure syndromes that requires lifelong treatment, while transient erythroblastopenia of childhood typically resolves spontaneously within 1-2 months.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>HbF:</strong> Elevated HbF can occur in both conditions, so it''s not diagnostic.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Both conditions show hypocellular bone marrow with erythroid precursors initially.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia Types','["blackfan diamond","transient erythroblastopenia","differentiation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.856Z','2026-10-19T03:37:05.056Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he9003wqjlrsbd20rfg','mcq','A male patient receives chronic blood transfusion and you noticed he has triphalangeal thumbs. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Blackfan-Diamond anemia","Fanconi anemia","Dyskeratosis congenital","Thalassemia"]','Blackfan-Diamond anemia','<p><strong>Why Blackfan-Diamond anemia is correct:</strong> Blackfan-Diamond anemia (DBA) is associated with characteristic physical anomalies, including triphalangeal thumbs (thumbs with three phalanges instead of the normal two), which occurs in approximately 15-25% of patients. This finding is part of the classic triad of anomalies seen in DBA, along with short stature and craniofacial abnormalities. The development of triphalangeal thumbs in a patient receiving chronic blood transfusions strongly suggests DBA rather than other anemias.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Characterized by bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies, and increased cancer risk, but not specifically associated with triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> Presents with mucocutaneous triad (abnormal skin pigmentation, nail dystrophy, leukoplakia) and bone marrow failure, but not triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Primarily a hemoglobin synthesis disorder without characteristic skeletal anomalies like triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>DBA is a congenital pure red cell aplasia that typically presents in the first year of life.</li>
<li>The classic triad of DBA includes anemia, reticulocytopenia, and absent or decreased erythroid precursors in bone marrow.</li>
<li>Triphalangeal thumbs are a pathognomonic physical finding that helps distinguish DBA from other anemias.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Triphalangeal thumbs in a transfusion-dependent patient are pathognomonic for Blackfan-Diamond anemia.</p>','<p>DBA has Deformed thumbs (triphalangeal), while Fanconi has Fragile chromosomes.</p>','<p>Blackfan-Diamond anemia is one of the few congenital anemias that responds to corticosteroid therapy, with approximately 80% of patients showing response.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Not associated with triphalangeal thumbs.</li>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> Presents with mucocutaneous triad, not thumb abnormalities.</li>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Primarily a hemoglobin disorder without skeletal anomalies.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia Types','["blackfan diamond","blood transfusion","tri phalangeal thumbs"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.857Z','2026-10-19T03:37:05.058Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hea003xqjlrr8xeogmb','mcq','Which of the following is correct about fanconia anemia','["It autosomal dominant","Risk of cancers","Normal skeleton"]','Risk of cancers','<p><strong>Why Risk of cancers is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is characterized by genomic instability and defective DNA repair due to mutations in genes involved in the Fanconi anemia pathway. This defect leads to a significantly increased risk of developing malignancies, particularly acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and solid tumors such as head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, gastrointestinal cancers, and gynecologic cancers. The cancer risk in Fanconi anemia is substantially higher than in the general population and represents a major cause of morbidity and mortality in these patients.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It autosomal dominant:</strong> Fanconi anemia is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, not autosomal dominant.</li>
<li><strong>Normal skeleton:</strong> Fanconi anemia is associated with congenital skeletal abnormalities including radial ray defects, thumb abnormalities, hip dysplasia, and scoliosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fanconi anemia is diagnosed by increased chromosomal breakage in response to DNA cross-linking agents like diepoxybutane (DEB).</li>
<li>The classic clinical triad includes bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies, and increased cancer risk.</li>
<li>Over 20 different genetic mutations have been identified in Fanconi anemia, all affecting the Fanconi anemia DNA repair pathway.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is defined by its autosomal recessive inheritance, bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies, and dramatically increased cancer risk.</p>','<p>Fanconi = Fails (bone marrow), Abnormalities (congenital), Neoplasms (cancer risk)</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia patients require lifelong surveillance for both bone marrow failure and malignancies, with cancer risk increasing significantly after the second decade of life.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It autosomal dominant:</strong> Fanconi anemia follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Normal skeleton:</strong> Fanconi anemia is associated with characteristic skeletal abnormalities.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Fanconi Anemia','["fanconi anemia","cancer risk","genetics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.858Z','2026-10-19T03:37:05.058Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56her004bqjlra3c3js71','mcq','A 5-year-old boy presented with short stature, epistaxis, and recurrent respiratory tract infections. On examination, he was pale, there was skin hyperpigmentation, microphthalmia, absent radius and thumb and purpuric rash all over the body. Which of the following would be the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Fanconi anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Blackfan-Diamond anemia","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura"]','Fanconi anemia','<p><strong>Why Fanconi anemia is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies, and increased cancer predisposition. The classic triad includes short stature, skin hyperpigmentation, and skeletal abnormalities (such as absent radius and thumb). The microphthalmia, purpuric rash, and recurrent infections are also consistent features. The bone marrow failure leads to pancytopenia, explaining the pallor and bleeding tendencies.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia:</strong> While this can cause pallor and bleeding, it doesn''t typically present with the constellation of congenital anomalies seen in this case.</li>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> This is caused by vitamin B12 or folate deficiency and would not explain the congenital anomalies or bone marrow failure.</li>
<li><strong>Blackfan-Diamond anemia:</strong> This is a pure red cell aplasia that typically presents in infancy with macrocytic anemia without the congenital anomalies seen here.</li>
<li><strong>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura:</strong> This is an isolated platelet disorder that wouldn''t explain the anemia, congenital anomalies, or bone marrow failure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fanconi anemia is associated with chromosomal breakage when exposed to clastogens like diepoxybutane.</li>
<li>The most common congenital anomalies include radial ray defects, skin pigmentation abnormalities, and microcephaly.</li>
<li>Patients have a very high risk of developing acute myeloid leukemia and solid tumors.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is the diagnosis when pancytopenia is accompanied by radial ray defects, skin hyperpigmentation, and other congenital anomalies.</p>','<p>FAN: Fanconi has Anemia, Neoplasia, and congenital anomalies</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia should be suspected in any child with unexplained bone marrow failure and congenital anomalies, especially radial ray defects.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia:</strong> Doesn''t typically present with the constellation of congenital anomalies seen in this case.</li>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Caused by vitamin B12 or folate deficiency and would not explain the congenital anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>Blackfan-Diamond anemia:</strong> A pure red cell aplasia without the congenital anomalies seen here.</li>
<li><strong>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura:</strong> An isolated platelet disorder that wouldn''t explain the anemia or congenital anomalies.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Bone Marrow Failure Syndromes','["anemia","congenital","pancytopenia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.875Z','2026-10-19T03:38:05.013Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hes004cqjlrc6g04c8s','mcq','Splenectomy in Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is considered in which of the following conditions?','["A two-year old boy with newly diagnosed ITP whose bleeding symptoms failed to be ameliorated with steroids and IVIG","A ten-year old girl with chronic ITP with recurrent symptoms of skin bleeding that is diffuse, not responding to steroids, rituximab and other medical therapy.","A six-year old boy with acute ITP developed intracranial hemorrhage from a road traffic accident who is hemodynamically stable and his bleeding is controlled with IVIG and steroids","A thirteen-year old girl with chronic ITP and severe vaginal bleeding that necessitated blood transfusion and failed to respond to steroids, IVIG, rituximab and contraceptive therapy.","A five-year old boy with acute ITP on initial therapy with steroid and presented with severe epistaxis and consequent anemia declared the need for blood transfusion"]','A thirteen-year old girl with chronic ITP and severe vaginal bleeding that necessitated blood transfusion and failed to respond to steroids, IVIG, rituximab and contraceptive therapy.','<p><strong>Why A thirteen-year old girl with chronic ITP and severe vaginal bleeding that necessitated blood transfusion and failed to respond to steroids, IVIG, rituximab and contraceptive therapy is correct:</strong> Splenectomy is reserved for chronic ITP patients who have failed multiple medical therapies and have severe, life-threatening bleeding. Chronic ITP is defined by duration longer than 12 months, and this 13-year-old girl meets this criterion. Her severe vaginal bleeding requiring transfusion and failure of multiple therapies (steroids, IVIG, rituximab, and even contraceptive therapy which can sometimes help with menorrhagia) makes her an appropriate candidate for splenectomy, which has a high response rate in chronic ITP.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A two-year old boy with newly diagnosed ITP:</strong> Splenectomy is contraindicated in children under 5 with acute ITP due to high spontaneous resolution rates and increased infection risk.</li>
<li><strong>A ten-year old girl with chronic ITP with recurrent skin bleeding:</strong> While chronic, her symptoms are not severe enough to warrant splenectomy as first-line therapy after medical failures.</li>
<li><strong>A six-year old boy with acute ITP who developed intracranial hemorrhage:</strong> This is an emergency situation where splenectomy is not the first-line treatment; aggressive medical management is preferred.</li>
<li><strong>A five-year old boy with acute ITP on initial therapy with severe epistaxis:</strong> This acute presentation would be managed with medical therapy first, not splenectomy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Splenectomy is typically reserved for chronic ITP (&gt;12 months duration) after multiple medical therapies have failed.</li>
<li>The response rate to splenectomy in chronic ITP is approximately 60-70%, with many maintaining long-term remission.</li>
<li>Post-splenectomy patients require lifelong vaccination against encapsulated organisms and may need antibiotic prophylaxis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Splenectomy in ITP is indicated for chronic cases (&gt;12 months) with severe bleeding that has failed multiple medical therapies, not for acute presentations or children under 5.</p>','<p>SCC: Splenectomy for Chronic ITP with significant bleeding</p>','<p>Splenectomy in ITP has a higher response rate in chronic cases than in acute cases, but carries lifelong infection risk requiring vaccination and sometimes antibiotic prophylaxis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A two-year old boy with newly diagnosed ITP:</strong> Splenectomy is contraindicated in children under 5 with acute ITP.</li>
<li><strong>A ten-year old girl with chronic ITP with recurrent skin bleeding:</strong> Her symptoms are not severe enough to warrant splenectomy after medical failures.</li>
<li><strong>A six-year old boy with acute ITP who developed intracranial hemorrhage:</strong> This is an emergency situation where splenectomy is not first-line treatment.</li>
<li><strong>A five-year old boy with acute ITP on initial therapy with severe epistaxis:</strong> This acute presentation would be managed medically first, not with splenectomy.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Hematology','Immune Thrombocytopenia','["itp","splenectomy","chronic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.876Z','2026-10-19T03:38:05.014Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hem0047qjlrk38je2nl','mcq','A female patient is suspected to have iron deficiency anemia, which of the following supports my diagnosis?','["Increased free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (FEP)","Decreased iron binding capacity.","Hypoplasia of bone marrow, decrease in marrow hemosiderin.","Increased transferrin saturation."]','Increased free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (FEP)','<p><strong>Why Increased free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (FEP) is correct:</strong> In iron deficiency anemia, there is insufficient iron available for heme synthesis, causing iron to become rate-limiting in the final step of heme production. This leads to accumulation of free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (FEP) as protoporphyrin IX cannot be converted to heme without iron. The FEP level rises early in iron deficiency, often before hemoglobin drops significantly, making it a sensitive marker for early or mild iron deficiency. This occurs because protoporphyrin accumulates in red blood cell precursors when iron is unavailable for incorporation into the heme molecule.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Decreased iron binding capacity:</strong> Iron deficiency actually increases total iron-binding capacity (TIBC) as the liver produces more transferrin to maximize iron capture from the diet and circulation.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoplasia of bone marrow, decrease in marrow hemosiderin:</strong> While decreased marrow hemosiderin is characteristic of iron deficiency, bone marrow is typically normocellular or hypercellular in iron deficiency, not hypoplastic.</li>
<li><strong>Increased transferrin saturation:</strong> Transferrin saturation is decreased in iron deficiency as there is insufficient iron to bind to transferrin, not increased.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>FEP is one of the earliest laboratory abnormalities in iron deficiency, often preceding changes in hemoglobin or ferritin.</li>
<li>In iron deficiency, TIBC is increased, transferrin saturation is decreased, and serum ferritin is low.</li>
<li>FEP is also elevated in lead poisoning and chronic inflammation, so it''s not specific to iron deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Iron deficiency causes FEP accumulation because iron is required for the final step of heme synthesis, making elevated FEP a sensitive early marker of iron deficiency.</p>','<p>FEP = First Early sign of iron deficiency (Ferritin drops later)</p>','<p>FEP testing is particularly useful in diagnosing iron deficiency in patients with chronic inflammation or infection where ferritin may be falsely elevated.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Decreased iron binding capacity:</strong> Iron deficiency increases TIBC as the body produces more transferrin to capture available iron.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoplasia of bone marrow, decrease in marrow hemosiderin:</strong> Bone marrow is typically normocellular or hypercellular in iron deficiency, not hypoplastic.</li>
<li><strong>Increased transferrin saturation:</strong> Transferrin saturation is decreased in iron deficiency due to insufficient iron binding to transferrin.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Iron Deficiency Anemia','["anemia","iron","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.870Z','2026-10-19T03:38:10.903Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hen0048qjlreqtvajqi','mcq','Vitamin B12 is important for RBCs production. What is correct about it?','["Humans have low stores of it.","Its deficiency is presented in early months of life.","Only animal sources are adequate for it in humans.","All causes B12 deficiency anemia are acquired."]','Humans have low stores of it.','<p><strong>Why Humans have low stores of it is correct:</strong> Vitamin B12 is stored in the liver in amounts sufficient for 3-5 years, but humans have very little stored in other tissues and no dedicated storage mechanism beyond the liver. This limited storage capacity means that once dietary intake stops or absorption is impaired, deficiency can develop relatively quickly (months to years) compared to other vitamins with larger body stores. The liver''s storage capacity represents the body''s primary reserve, and once depleted, neurological consequences can develop before anemia appears.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Its deficiency is presented in early months of life:</strong> B12 deficiency typically presents in adulthood, not early infancy, due to significant maternal stores transferred to the fetus and continued B12 content in breast milk.</li>
<li><strong>Only animal sources are adequate for it in humans:</strong> While B12 is primarily found in animal products, some fortified foods (cereals, plant milks) can provide adequate amounts for vegans.</li>
<li><strong>All causes B12 deficiency anemia are acquired:</strong> B12 deficiency can be congenital (e.g., transcobalamin deficiency, Imerslund-Gräsbeck syndrome) or acquired (pernicious anemia, dietary deficiency, malabsorption).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>B12 deficiency can cause neurological symptoms before anemia develops, as the nervous system is more sensitive to B12 deficiency than hematopoietic tissue.</li>
<li>The liver stores enough B12 for 3-5 years, explaining why deficiency takes time to develop after absorption ceases.</li>
<li>Pernicious anemia is the most common cause of B12 deficiency in adults and is an autoimmune condition targeting parietal cells.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Humans have limited B12 stores primarily in the liver, making deficiency possible within months to years of inadequate intake or absorption.</p>','<p>B12 stores last 3-5 years (Liver''s Long Lasting supply)</p>','<p>Neurological symptoms of B12 deficiency (paresthesias, ataxia, cognitive changes) may appear before hematological abnormalities, and can become irreversible if treatment is delayed.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Its deficiency is presented in early months of life:</strong> B12 deficiency typically presents in adulthood, not early infancy.</li>
<li><strong>Only animal sources are adequate for it in humans:</strong> Some fortified plant foods can provide adequate B12 for vegans.</li>
<li><strong>All causes B12 deficiency anemia are acquired:</strong> B12 deficiency can be congenital (e.g., transcobalamin deficiency).</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Vitamin B12 Deficiency','["b12","anemia","nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b00awi8rmgure6kyz","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Treatment: daily oral folic acid; transfuse only if severe and symptomatic. 3.3 Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency\\n★ Sources are exclusively animal-derived (produced by microorganisms; humans cannot synthesize it). Stores last 3–5 years in older children/adults, but an infant born to a B12-deficient mother can show signs in the first 4–5 months.","chunkContent":"• Inadequate intake — pregnancy, goat milk feeding, rapid growth in infancy, chronic hemolysis\\n\\n• Decreased absorption\\n\\n• Congenital defects of folate metabolism\\n★ Clinical: peaks at 4–7 months of age — irritability, failure to thrive, chronic diarrhea and glossitis. Labs: low serum folate; hypersegmented neutrophils (>5 lobes) on peripheral smear. Treatment: daily oral folic acid; transfuse only if severe and symptomatic.\\n\\n3.3 Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin) Deficiency\\n★ Sources are exclusively animal-derived (produced by microorganisms; humans cannot synthesize it). Stores last 3–5 years in older children/adults, but an infant born to a B12-deficient mother can show signs in the first 4–5 months.\\n\\nDIETARY SOURCES\\n\\n• Meat, fish, eggs\\n\\n• Dairy products — milk, yoghurt, cheese\\n\\n• Seaweed, nori, spirulina\\n\\n• Chlorella tablets, sardines\\nCAUSES","answerCoverage":0.67,"judgeScore":8},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b00b0i8rmcofptoka","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Infants of deficient mothers: signs at 4–6 months\\nCauses Goat milk feeding; chronic hemolysis; decreased absorption; congenital defects of folate metabolism Vegans; congenital/juvenile pernicious anemia; gastric surgery; terminal ileum disease\\nFindings Low serum folate, normal-to-increased iron and B12 Normal serum folate, decreased vitamin B12 Neurologic symptoms Absent Present — and can be irreversible if untreated Treatment Daily oral folic acid Parenteral (injectable) vitamin B12","chunkContent":"Feature Folic Acid Deficiency Vitamin B12 Deficiency Food sources Green vegetables, fruits, animals, whole grains Animal sources only, made by microorganisms Presentation Peaks at 4–7 months of age Older children/adults: stores last 3–5 yrs. Infants of deficient mothers: signs at 4–6 months\\nCauses Goat milk feeding; chronic hemolysis; decreased absorption; congenital defects of folate metabolism Vegans; congenital/juvenile pernicious anemia; gastric surgery; terminal ileum disease\\nFindings Low serum folate, normal-to-increased iron and B12 Normal serum folate, decreased vitamin B12 Neurologic symptoms Absent Present — and can be irreversible if untreated Treatment Daily oral folic acid Parenteral (injectable) vitamin B12","answerCoverage":0.33,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.871Z','2026-10-19T03:38:10.905Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg1004qqjlryjf7h855','mcq','HB A2 is the major adult hemoglobin, which of the following is true about it','["It consists of 2 alpha and 2 delta chains","It represents 50% of HB at birth","It represents 30 % of HB at birth","It consists of 2 alpha and 2 gamma chains"]','It consists of 2 alpha and 2 delta chains','<p><strong>Why It consists of 2 alpha and 2 delta chains is correct:</strong> Hemoglobin A2 (HbA2) is a normal adult hemoglobin variant composed of two alpha globin chains and two delta globin chains (α2δ2). It is encoded by the HBD gene and represents approximately 2-3% of total hemoglobin in healthy adults. The delta chains are structurally similar to beta chains but have different amino acid sequences, resulting in slightly different oxygen affinity. HbA2 production begins around 6 months of age and gradually increases to adult levels by 1-2 years of age.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It represents 50% of HB at birth:</strong> This is incorrect as HbA2 is not present at significant levels at birth; fetal hemoglobin (HbF, α2γ2) dominates.</li>
<li><strong>It represents 30% of HB at birth:</strong> This is incorrect for the same reason as above; HbF constitutes approximately 70-80% of hemoglobin at birth.</li>
<li><strong>It consists of 2 alpha and 2 gamma chains:</strong> This describes fetal hemoglobin (HbF), not HbA2.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>HbA2 is composed of alpha and delta chains (α2δ2) and constitutes 2-3% of adult hemoglobin.</li>
<li>HbA2 levels are elevated in beta thalassemia trait (up to 6-8%) due to underproduction of beta chains.</li>
<li>HbF (α2γ2) is the predominant hemoglobin at birth, not HbA2.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemoglobin A2 is made of two alpha and two delta chains and represents a small percentage of adult hemoglobin but is diagnostically significant in beta thalassemia.</p>','<p>A2 = Alpha 2, Delta 2 (not gamma)</p>','<p>Elevated HbA2 levels (&gt;3.5%) are a diagnostic hallmark of beta thalassemia trait.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It represents 50% of HB at birth:</strong> Incorrect, as HbF dominates at birth.</li>
<li><strong>It represents 30% of HB at birth:</strong> Incorrect, as HbF constitutes 70-80% at birth.</li>
<li><strong>It consists of 2 alpha and 2 gamma chains:</strong> This describes HbF, not HbA2.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemoglobin','["hba2","hemoglobin","adult"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.921Z','2026-10-19T03:39:16.627Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hg3004rqjlrf4thjdtt','mcq','Which of the following cause acute anemia','["Iron deficiency anemia","Hemorrhage","Lead poisoning","Vit. B12 deficiency"]','Hemorrhage','<p><strong>Why Hemorrhage is correct:</strong> Hemorrhage causes acute anemia by rapidly reducing the total red blood cell mass and hemoglobin concentration through blood loss. This is an acute process that can lead to rapid onset of symptoms like pallor, tachycardia, and hypotension depending on the volume lost. Unlike the other options, hemorrhage directly reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood immediately, creating an acute clinical situation requiring prompt intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> This develops gradually over weeks to months as iron stores are depleted and hemoglobin production slows, making it a chronic rather than acute condition.</li>
<li><strong>Lead poisoning:</strong> Lead poisoning causes anemia through interference with heme synthesis and shortened red blood cell survival, but this process occurs over time, resulting in a more chronic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Vit. B12 deficiency:</strong> This causes megaloblastic anemia which develops gradually as vitamin stores are depleted, typically taking months to become clinically apparent.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acute anemia is characterized by rapid onset of symptoms due to sudden blood loss or destruction.</li>
<li>Hemorrhage is the only acute cause among the options, with symptoms developing within hours to days.</li>
<li>Chronic anemias (iron deficiency, B12 deficiency, lead poisoning) develop gradually over weeks to months.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemorrhage is the only acute anemia among the options, causing rapid blood loss and immediate symptoms, while the others develop gradually over time.</p>','<p>H = Hemorrhage (Happens fast), Others happen slowly</p>','<p>Acute anemia from hemorrhage requires immediate volume replacement, while chronic anemias allow for time to address the underlying deficiency.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Develops gradually over weeks to months as iron stores deplete.</li>
<li><strong>Lead poisoning:</strong> Causes chronic anemia through interference with heme synthesis over time.</li>
<li><strong>Vit. B12 deficiency:</strong> Results in gradual development of megaloblastic anemia as stores deplete.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Anemias','["acute","anemia","hemorrhage"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.923Z','2026-10-19T03:39:16.628Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9r000io7lrpfgtduvi','mcq','Marasmus is common in which of the following age group?','["Less than 6 months","6 months to 2 years","[Not provided in source]","[Not provided in source]"]','Less than 6 months','<p><strong>Why Less than 6 months is correct:</strong> Marasmus is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition characterized by extreme emaciation and muscle wasting. It is most common in infants less than 6 months of age because this period represents a time of rapid growth and nutritional demands that are often not met, particularly in resource-limited settings. During this critical window, infants are completely dependent on breast milk or formula, and any disruption in feeding can rapidly lead to marasmus. Additionally, their small body size means they have minimal energy reserves to withstand periods of inadequate nutrition. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>6 months to 2 years:</strong> While malnutrition can occur in this age group, marasmus is less common here as infants typically begin complementary feeding, which can provide additional nutritional support. - <strong>[Not provided in source]:</strong> These options were not provided in the source material. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Marasmus is characterized by severe wasting of subcutaneous fat and muscle tissue, with minimal or no edema. - It typically occurs in infants under 6 months when nutritional demands are highest and reserves are lowest. - The clinical presentation includes extreme thinness, loss of subcutaneous fat, and a "skin and bones" appearance. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Marasmus is most common in infants less than 6 months due to their high nutritional demands and minimal energy reserves.</p>','','<p>Early detection and intervention in the first 6 months of life are critical to prevent marasmus, as this period represents a window of vulnerability when nutritional demands are highest.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>6 months to 2 years:</strong> This age group has better nutritional options through complementary feeding, making marasmus less common.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Nutrition','Marasmus','["marasmus","age","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.071Z','2026-10-19T03:40:05.487Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9v000jo7lrvl6k1oe0','mcq','In which of the following categories PEM is mostly seen and has the most destructing consequences?','["adulthood and adolescence","adolescence and early childhood","infancy and early childhood","only infancy"]','infancy and early childhood','<p><strong>Why infancy and early childhood is correct:</strong> Protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) has its most devastating consequences during infancy and early childhood (ages 0-5 years) because this period represents the most rapid phase of human growth and development. The brain undergoes critical development during these years, with synaptogenesis and myelination occurring at an extraordinary rate. Nutritional deficiencies during this time can lead to permanent cognitive deficits, impaired immune function, and stunted physical growth that may never be fully compensated for later in life. Additionally, young children have higher nutritional requirements per unit of body weight compared to older children and adults, making them more vulnerable to nutritional deficiencies. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Adolescence and early childhood:</strong> While early childhood is included, adolescence alone is not the period of highest vulnerability. - <strong>Only infancy:</strong> While infancy is critical, early childhood (1-5 years) also represents a period of rapid brain development and nutritional vulnerability. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The first 1000 days of life (from conception to age 2) represent a critical window for nutrition and development. - PEM during this period can lead to irreversible cognitive impairment and immune dysfunction. - The consequences of PEM in early childhood include growth stunting, developmental delays, and increased susceptibility to infections. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> PEM causes the most devastating consequences during infancy and early childhood due to the critical periods of brain development and rapid growth occurring during these years.</p>','<p>PEM = Permanent Effects in Mind (referring to the permanent cognitive damage that can occur during critical developmental windows)</p>','<p>The nutritional status during the first two years of life can predict long-term educational outcomes and economic productivity, making early childhood nutrition a critical public health priority.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Adolescence and early childhood:</strong> Adolescence has lower nutritional demands per body weight compared to infancy and early childhood. - <strong>Only infancy:</strong> While infancy is critical, early childhood (1-5 years) also represents a period of rapid brain development and nutritional vulnerability.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["pem","age","consequences"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.075Z','2026-10-19T03:40:05.506Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9w000ko7lr1sfeh6jg','mcq','12-month old girl, her weight 6,5kg was admitted in our hospital by lower limb oedema, anorexia, sever muscle wasting and lethargy. In your opinion, what is the first sign you expect to improve during her treatment?','["Mood","Oedema","Appetite","Muscle bulk"]','Oedema','<p><strong>Why Oedema is correct:</strong> In a child with kwashiorkor (protein deficiency with edema), the edema is caused by decreased plasma oncotic pressure due to hypoalbuminemia, which leads to fluid shifting from the intravascular space to the interstitial spaces. When treatment begins with adequate protein and calorie intake, the liver rapidly synthesizes new albumin, and plasma oncotic pressure begins to normalize within 24-48 hours. This rapid improvement in oncotic pressure allows the excess interstitial fluid to return to the vascular space and be excreted, making edema the first clinical sign to improve. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Mood:</strong> While mood may improve with treatment, this is not the first sign to resolve as it depends on multiple factors including electrolyte balance and overall recovery. - <strong>Appetite:</strong> Appetite typically improves but not as rapidly as edema resolution, as it takes time for metabolic derangements to normalize. - <strong>Muscle bulk:</strong> Muscle wasting takes weeks to months to reverse as it requires sustained positive protein balance and adequate nutrition for tissue synthesis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Edema in kwashiorkor is primarily due to hypoalbuminemia and decreased plasma oncotic pressure. - The first biochemical response to protein refeeding is increased albumin synthesis within 24-48 hours. - Clinical improvement follows a predictable pattern: edema resolves first, followed by appetite, then mood, and finally muscle mass over weeks to months. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In protein-energy malnutrition with edema, the edema resolves first due to rapid albumin synthesis and restoration of oncotic pressure within 24-48 hours of starting treatment.</p>','<p>E for Edema - Edema Eliminates Early (in kwashiorkor treatment)</p>','<p>The presence of edema in malnourished children is a critical indicator of severe protein deficiency and requires urgent intervention with protein-rich feeds.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Mood improvement depends on multiple factors and is not as rapid as edema resolution. - <strong>Appetite:</strong> Appetite improves but not as quickly as edema resolution. - <strong>Muscle bulk:</strong> Muscle wasting takes weeks to months to reverse with sustained nutrition.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Treatment','["treatment","edema","kwashiorkor"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.076Z','2026-10-19T03:40:05.507Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9z000oo7lr6dlrxj0f','mcq','During initial stabilization phase , feeding for severe acute malnutrition ?','["Start with F100","Start with F75","Start with TPN","Start with F125"]','Start with F75','<p><strong>Why Start with F75 is correct:</strong> The initial stabilization phase of severe acute malnutrition requires a lower-energy formula (F75) to prevent refeeding syndrome, which can occur when severely malnourished patients receive too many calories too quickly. F75 provides approximately 75 kcal/100ml with less protein and electrolytes, allowing the body to gradually adapt to nutritional support without overwhelming the compromised metabolic and organ systems. This approach specifically addresses the risk of cardiac failure and hypophosphatemia that can result from rapid refeeding in severely malnourished children.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start with F100:</strong> F100 provides higher energy (100 kcal/100ml) and is used in the rehabilitation phase after stabilization, as it can cause metabolic overload during initial treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Start with TPN:</strong> Total parenteral nutrition is not indicated for severe acute malnutrition due to high risk of infections, electrolyte imbalances, and the fact that these patients typically have functional gastrointestinal tracts.</li>
<li><strong>Start with F125:</strong> F125 is not a standard WHO formula for malnutrition treatment; the standard progression is F75 followed by F100.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The WHO protocol for severe acute malnutrition follows a specific formula progression: F75 (stabilization) → F100 (rehabilitation) → home-based therapeutic foods.</li>
<li>Refeeding syndrome is a critical concern in initial treatment, characterized by hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and cardiac complications.</li>
<li>Initial treatment must also include antibiotics, vitamin supplementation, and careful monitoring of fluid and electrolytes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Start with F75 in severe acute malnutrition stabilization to prevent refeeding syndrome, then transition to F100 during rehabilitation.</p>','<p>F75 First, F100 Follow</p>','<p>Severely malnourished children have reduced cardiac and metabolic reserves, making gradual nutritional refeeding essential to prevent fatal complications.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Start with F100:</strong> Too high energy for initial stabilization, risks refeeding syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Start with TPN:</strong> Not indicated for malnutrition with functional GI tracts; high infection risk.</li>
<li><strong>Start with F125:</strong> Not a standard WHO formula for malnutrition treatment.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Treatment','["treatment","feeding","malnutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.079Z','2026-10-19T03:40:23.085Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbo0023o7lrvz1til09','mcq','Meckel''s diverticulum has been conveniently referred to by the ''rule of 2s,'' which explains the classic presentation of this congenital anomaly. Which one of the following is FALSE in this rule?','["found in approximately 2% of the general population","usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve","approximately 2 inches in length","found twice as commonly in females"]','found twice as commonly in females','<p><strong>Why found twice as commonly in females is correct:</strong> The ''rule of 2s'' for Meckel''s diverticulum is a clinical mnemonic that describes its epidemiology, anatomy, and presentation. However, the statement that it is found twice as commonly in females is false because Meckel''s diverticulum actually has no gender predilection and occurs equally in males and females. The other components of the rule are accurate: it''s found in approximately 2% of the population, usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve, and approximately 2 inches in length. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>found in approximately 2% of the general population:</strong> This is correct according to the rule of 2s, as Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common congenital abnormality of the GI tract. - <strong>usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve:</strong> This is accurate as the diverticulum typically arises from the antimesenteric border of the ileum, about 45-60 cm from the ileocecal valve. - <strong>approximately 2 inches in length:</strong> This is correct as most Meckel''s diverticula measure 2-5 cm in length, with 2 inches being a common approximation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Meckel''s diverticulum is a remnant of the vitelline duct and contains ectopic gastric mucosa in about 50% of cases. - The classic presentation is painless rectal bleeding in a child under 2 years old, though it can present at any age. - Complications include hemorrhage, intussusception, volvulus, and obstruction. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Meckel''s diverticulum follows the ''rule of 2s'' except for gender distribution, which is equal between males and females.</p>','<p>Meckel''s ''2s'': 2% population, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2% symptomatic, presents before age 2.</p>','<p>Meckel''s diverticulum should be suspected in any child with painless lower GI bleeding, as it''s the most common cause of such bleeding in this age group.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>found in approximately 2% of the general population:</strong> This is true according to the rule of 2s. - <strong>usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve:</strong> This is accurate anatomically. - <strong>approximately 2 inches in length:</strong> This is a correct description of the typical size.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Meckel''s diverticulum','["meckel","rule of 2s","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.140Z','2026-10-19T03:43:06.802Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kra0000po7lrli53w61x','mcq','Definition of SAM according to WHO :','["Wt for age less than 60%","Wt for Ht / length below -2 SD","Wt for age less than -3 SD","Wt for Ht / length below -3 SD"]','Wt for Ht / length below -3 SD','<p><strong>Why Wt for Ht / length below -3 SD is correct:</strong> According to WHO guidelines, severe acute malnutrition (SAM) is defined by a weight-for-height/length Z-score below -3 standard deviations or a mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) &lt;115mm. This anthropometric measurement specifically identifies children with severe wasting, which is the most critical form of malnutrition requiring urgent intervention. The Z-score system compares a child''s measurements to a healthy reference population, with -3 SD representing the third percentile of the distribution.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wt for age less than 60%:</strong> This is an outdated measurement that doesn''t account for height and may include children who are short but not wasted.</li>
<li><strong>Wt for Ht / length below -2 SD:</strong> This defines moderate acute malnutrition (MAM), not severe acute malnutrition.</li>
<li><strong>Wt for age less than -3 SD:</strong> This measurement identifies underweight children but doesn''t specifically detect wasting (the critical acute form of malnutrition).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>SAM is defined by weight-for-height Z-score &lt; -3 SD or MUAC &lt;115mm, while MAM is defined by Z-score between -2 and -3 SD.</li>
<li>Mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) is a practical screening tool for SAM in field settings where height measurement is difficult.</li>
<li>The WHO growth standards are based on healthy breastfed children and are the global reference for malnutrition classification.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Severe acute malnutrition is defined by weight-for-height Z-score below -3 SD or MUAC &lt;115mm, specifically identifying severe wasting.</p>','<p>SAM = Severe Wasting (WHZ &lt; -3 SD or MUAC &lt;115mm)</p>','<p>MUAC is a practical field tool for identifying severe acute malnutrition, especially in settings where precise height measurement is challenging.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Wt for age less than 60%:</strong> Outdated measurement that doesn''t specifically detect wasting.</li>
<li><strong>Wt for Ht / length below -2 SD:</strong> Defines moderate malnutrition, not severe.</li>
<li><strong>Wt for age less than -3 SD:</strong> Identifies underweight but not specifically wasting.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Classification','["sam","who","definition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.080Z','2026-10-19T03:40:23.086Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kra2000ro7lrut96j6ji','mcq','Weight for length less than 80% is:','["Stunted","Wasted"]','Wasted','<p><strong>Why Wasted is correct:</strong> Weight-for-length less than 80% indicates wasting, which refers to low weight relative to length, reflecting acute malnutrition. This measurement specifically identifies children who are underweight for their height, often resulting from recent inadequate food intake or illness. Wasting is an acute condition that can develop quickly and is associated with increased mortality risk in children. The WHO growth standards use this cutoff to classify moderate wasting, with severe wasting defined as below 70%.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stunted:</strong> Stunting refers to low height-for-age, indicating chronic malnutrition and long-term growth failure, not acute weight loss relative to length.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Wasting (weight-for-length &lt;80%) indicates acute malnutrition and is a strong predictor of mortality in children.</li>
<li>Stunting (height-for-age &lt; -2SD) indicates chronic malnutrition and has irreversible effects on cognitive development.</li>
<li>Mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) is another screening tool for wasting that is easier to use in resource-limited settings.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Weight-for-length measures acute malnutrition (wasting), while height-for-age measures chronic malnutrition (stunting).</p>','<p>WASTED = Weight And Shortness Temporarily Equals Disease (acute), while STUNTED = Shortness That''s Usually Never Totally Eliminated (chronic)</p>','<p>Wasted children require urgent therapeutic feeding programs, while stunted children need long-term nutritional rehabilitation and catch-up growth strategies.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Stunted:</strong> Stunting refers to chronic malnutrition measured by height-for-age, not acute weight-for-length measurements.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Nutrition','Growth Assessment','["growth","wasting","nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.082Z','2026-10-19T03:40:28.691Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kra3000so7lr134a4q54','mcq','What is the last sign you expect to improve during KWO treatment?','["Mood","Oedema","Appetite","Muscle bulk"]','Muscle bulk','<p><strong>Why Muscle bulk is correct:</strong> Muscle bulk is the last parameter to improve during kwashiorkor (KWO) treatment because muscle protein synthesis requires adequate protein intake and a positive nitrogen balance, which takes weeks to establish. The edema in kwashiorkor resolves first due to improved oncotic pressure as albumin levels begin to rise within days. Appetite typically improves early in treatment as metabolic derangements correct, and mood improvement follows as nutritional deficiencies are addressed and the patient feels better.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Mood improvement typically occurs early in treatment as nutritional deficiencies begin to correct and the patient feels better.</li>
<li><strong>Oedema:</strong> Edema resolves relatively quickly (within days) as albumin levels start to improve and oncotic pressure normalizes.</li>
<li><strong>Appetite:</strong> Appetite usually improves early in treatment as metabolic derangements correct and the patient begins to feel better.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Edema in kwashiorkor is due to hypoalbuminemia and resolves within days of proper nutrition.</li>
<li>Muscle wasting (muscle bulk) represents the most severe protein deficiency and requires weeks of adequate protein intake for recovery.</li>
<li>The classic treatment sequence for kwashiorkor is: edema resolves first, followed by appetite improvement, then mood improvement, and finally muscle bulk recovery.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In kwashiorkor treatment, edema resolves first, followed by appetite and mood improvement, with muscle bulk being the last parameter to recover due to the time required for muscle protein synthesis.</p>','<p>EAMM - Edema first, then Appetite, then Mood, finally Muscle bulk recovers last</p>','<p>The persistence of muscle wasting despite edema resolution in kwashiorkor indicates ongoing protein deficiency and the need for continued nutritional rehabilitation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Improves early as nutritional deficiencies correct and patient feels better.</li>
<li><strong>Oedema:</strong> Resolves quickly (days) as albumin levels improve and oncotic pressure normalizes.</li>
<li><strong>Appetite:</strong> Typically improves early in treatment as metabolic derangements correct.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Kwashiorkor','["malnutrition","kwashiorkor","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.083Z','2026-10-19T03:40:28.693Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kraa000to7lr0u1w21s6','mcq','Child with ataxia and heart failure. Which deficient cause this case?','["Iron deficiency anemia","Thiamine deficiency"]','Thiamine deficiency','<p><strong>Why Thiamine deficiency is correct:</strong> Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causes beriberi, which presents with neurological symptoms including ataxia due to damage to the cerebellum and peripheral nerves, as well as cardiovascular symptoms including heart failure from high-output cardiac failure due to peripheral vasodilation and reduced systemic vascular resistance. Thiamine is essential for carbohydrate metabolism as a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complexes; deficiency leads to lactic acidosis and impaired energy production in neural and cardiac tissues.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> While iron deficiency can cause fatigue and potentially contribute to heart failure in severe cases through high-output cardiac failure, it does not typically present with ataxia as a primary neurological symptom.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Wet beriberi (thiamine deficiency) presents with high-output cardiac failure, peripheral edema, and tachycardia due to decreased peripheral vascular resistance.</li>
<li>Dry beriberi (thiamine deficiency) presents with symmetric peripheral neuropathy and Wernicke''s encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia).</li>
<li>Thiamine deficiency is common in alcoholics, patients with severe malnutrition, and those undergoing bariatric surgery.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The combination of ataxia and heart failure strongly suggests thiamine deficiency (beriberi), as iron deficiency anemia does not typically cause neurological ataxia.</p>','<p>B1 for Brain and Blood - Thiamine deficiency affects both neurological system (ataxia) and cardiovascular system (heart failure)</p>','<p>Thiamine should be administered before glucose in patients at risk for deficiency to prevent precipitating Wernicke''s encephalopathy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Does not typically cause ataxia as a primary neurological symptom, though severe cases can contribute to high-output cardiac failure.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Thiamine deficiency','["thiamine","heart failure","ataxia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.090Z','2026-10-19T03:40:28.693Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krak0010o7lrh15lprjm','mcq','Child presented with convulsions, Examination shows Craniotabes What is the appropriate investigation?','["Ca level in blood","Glucose level in blood","Lumbar puncture","EEG"]','Ca level in blood','<p><strong>Why Ca level in blood is correct:</strong> The child presents with convulsions and craniotabes, which are classic manifestations of hypocalcemia. Craniotabes refers to softening of the skull bones due to inadequate mineralization, while convulsions can result from neuromuscular irritability caused by low calcium levels. The appropriate investigation is measuring serum calcium levels to confirm the diagnosis of hypocalcemia, which could be due to various causes including vitamin D deficiency, hypoparathyroidism, or renal disorders. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Glucose level in blood:</strong> Hypoglycemia can cause convulsions but does not present with craniotabes, making this investigation inappropriate for this clinical scenario. - <strong>Lumbar puncture:</strong> This is indicated for suspected meningitis or encephalitis, which would present with fever, headache, and meningeal signs, not typically with craniotabes. - <strong>EEG:</strong> While useful for evaluating seizure disorders, it does not address the underlying metabolic abnormality causing both convulsions and craniotabes. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Craniotabes is a specific sign of rickets or other causes of bone demineralization in infants. - Hypocalcemia can cause both neuromuscular irritability (convulsions) and impaired bone mineralization. - In infants, hypocalcemia can be early (first few days) or late (after 1 week) presentation, with late hypocalcemia often associated with vitamin D deficiency. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Convulsions with craniotabes in a child should prompt immediate measurement of serum calcium levels to diagnose and treat hypocalcemia.</p>','<p>Craniotabes = Calcium Low</p>','<p>Craniotabes is pathognomonic for rickets in infants and should never be ignored, as it indicates significant vitamin D deficiency requiring urgent intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Glucose level in blood:</strong> Hypoglycemia causes convulsions but not craniotabes. - <strong>Lumbar puncture:</strong> Indicated for CNS infections, not metabolic bone disease. - <strong>EEG:</strong> Evaluates seizure activity but not the underlying calcium deficiency.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","convulsions","calcium"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.100Z','2026-10-19T03:40:57.723Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kral0011o7lr1wz9cfqy','mcq','What is the first clinical sign in active rickets?','["Delayed teething","Knock knee","Rossary beads","Craniotabes"]','Craniotabes','<p><strong>Why Craniotabes is correct:</strong> Craniotabes, characterized by softening and thinning of the skull bones, is typically the earliest clinical sign of active rickets in infants, usually appearing between 1-3 months of age. This occurs because the skull bones are among the first to ossify and are therefore among the first to show demineralization when there''s insufficient calcium and phosphate deposition due to vitamin D deficiency. The parietal and occipital bones are most commonly affected, creating a characteristic ''ping-pong ball'' sensation on palpation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Delayed teething:</strong> While teething can be delayed in rickets, it''s not the first clinical sign to appear. - <strong>Knock knee:</strong> This is a later deformity that develops as the child begins to walk and weight-bearing bones are affected. - <strong>Rossary beads:</strong> These are costochondral beading at the costochondral junctions, which appears after rickets has been present for some time. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Craniotabes is pathognomonic for early rickets in infants and typically appears before other skeletal deformities. - The earliest radiological sign of rickets is widening and fraying of the metaphysis, which appears before clinical signs. - Vitamin D deficiency rickets is most common in breastfed infants who are not receiving vitamin D supplementation. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Craniotabes is the first clinical sign of active rickets in infants, appearing before other skeletal deformities.</p>','<p>Craniotabes Comes First</p>','<p>Craniotabes can be elicited by pressing on the skull of an infant, causing it to feel like a ping-pong ball, and is often reversible with early vitamin D supplementation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Delayed teething:</strong> Occurs later in the course of rickets, not first. - <strong>Knock knee:</strong> Develops after the child starts walking, as weight-bearing bones are affected. - <strong>Rossary beads:</strong> Appear later as costochondral beading due to rachitic changes.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Musculoskeletal','Rickets','["rickets","clinical-sign","craniotabes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.101Z','2026-10-19T03:40:57.725Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kral0012o7lrfs2kzmp3','mcq','Which is false about rickets?','["Craniotabes","Frontal bossing","Hypertonia","Kyphosis"]','Hypertonia','<p><strong>Why Hypertonia is correct:</strong> Hypertonia (increased muscle tone) is false in the context of rickets because rickets actually causes hypotonia (decreased muscle tone) due to hypocalcemia and the effects of vitamin D deficiency on muscle function. The low calcium levels lead to increased neuromuscular excitability initially, but chronic vitamin D deficiency results in muscle weakness and hypotonia. The other options (craniotabes, frontal bossing, kyphosis) are all well-established clinical features of rickets. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Craniotabes:</strong> This is a true and classic early sign of rickets, representing softening of the skull bones due to inadequate mineralization. - <strong>Frontal bossing:</strong> This is a true feature of rickets, where there is frontal prominence due to thickening of the frontal bones as a compensatory mechanism. - <strong>Kyphosis:</strong> This is a true skeletal deformity that occurs in rickets, particularly in the thoracic spine, due to weakened vertebral bodies. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Rickets causes hypotonia, not hypertonia, due to the effects of vitamin D deficiency on muscle function. - The classic triad of rickets includes craniotabes, rachitic rosary, and frontal bossing. - Vitamin D deficiency leads to impaired calcium absorption, resulting in hypocalcemia which can cause both hypotonia and seizures. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rickets causes hypotonia, not hypertonia, making ''Hypertonia'' the false statement among the options.</p>','<p>Rickets causes Hypo-tonia (not Hyper)</p>','<p>The hypotonia seen in rickets is often mistaken for developmental delay, making recognition of this sign crucial for early diagnosis and intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Craniotabes:</strong> A true early sign of rickets representing skull softening. - <strong>Frontal bossing:</strong> A true feature of rickets with frontal prominence. - <strong>Kyphosis:</strong> A true skeletal deformity that occurs in rickets due to weakened vertebrae.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Musculoskeletal','Rickets','["rickets","clinical-sign","hypertonia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.101Z','2026-10-19T03:40:57.727Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kran0014o7lrb7edzscs','mcq','A 2-day-old term baby who taking Colostrum. What is the benefit of Colostrum','["High fat","Secretory IgA (Most common Igs)"]','Secretory IgA (Most common Igs)','<p><strong>Why Secretory IgA (Most common Igs) is correct:</strong> Colostrum, the first milk produced after delivery, is rich in secretory immunoglobulin A (sIgA), which is the most abundant immunoglobulin in human milk. sIgA provides passive mucosal immunity by binding to pathogens in the infant''s gastrointestinal tract, preventing their attachment and invasion. This protection is particularly crucial in the newborn period when the infant''s own immune system is immature. The secretory component of sIgA protects it from degradation in the gut, allowing it to function effectively in the harsh environment of the newborn''s digestive system.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High fat:</strong> Colostrum is actually lower in fat compared to mature milk, with its primary composition being immunoglobulins, leukocytes, and growth factors rather than high fat content.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Colostrum contains approximately 10 times more sIgA than mature milk, providing critical early immune protection.</li>
<li>The concentration of leukocytes in colostrum is extremely high, reaching 10^9 cells/L, which helps protect against infections.</li>
<li>Colostrum also contains lactoferrin, lysozyme, and cytokines that further support the infant''s immune system.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Colostrum''s primary benefit is providing secretory IgA, which protects the newborn''s mucous membranes by preventing pathogen attachment.</p>','<p>SIgA: Secretory Immunity for Gut Assurance</p>','<p>The first breastfeeding should occur within the first hour after birth to maximize the benefits of colostrum, which provides passive immunity until the infant''s own immune system matures.</p>','','Basic','Immunology','Colostrum','["colostrum","immunoglobulins","newborn"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.103Z','2026-10-19T03:41:01.179Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krao0015o7lrilyjbsjm','mcq','A 2-month-old infant exclusively breastfeeding and normal growth but with limited sun exposure. He mostly need','["Vitamin D"]','Vitamin D','<p><strong>Why Vitamin D is correct:</strong> Exclusively breastfed infants with limited sun exposure are at high risk for vitamin D deficiency because human milk contains very low amounts of vitamin D (typically 10-80 IU/L), which is insufficient to meet the infant''s requirements. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends all breastfed infants receive a vitamin D supplement of 400 IU daily to prevent deficiency. Without adequate vitamin D, calcium absorption is impaired, potentially leading to rickets, poor bone mineralization, and other complications. This recommendation applies even to infants in sunny climates because clothing, sunscreen, and cultural practices often prevent sufficient sun exposure for vitamin D synthesis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong></p>
<p>Other nutrients are generally adequately provided through breast milk or the infant''s stores, making vitamin D the primary concern for exclusively breastfed infants with limited sun exposure.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Human milk contains very little vitamin D, making supplementation essential for exclusively breastfed infants.</li>
<li>The vitamin D recommendation of 400 IU daily applies to all breastfed infants, regardless of geographic location or skin pigmentation.</li>
<li>Vitamin D deficiency in infancy can lead to rickets, hypocalcemia seizures, and impaired immune function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Exclusively breastfed infants require vitamin D supplementation because human milk contains insufficient amounts to prevent deficiency.</p>','<p>B.R.E.A.S.T.: Babies Require Extra Amounts of Supplemental Therapy (vitamin D)</p>','<p>Vitamin D deficiency in infancy can present with hypocalcemic seizures even before classic rachitic changes appear on X-ray.</p>','','Basic','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["vitamin d","breastfeeding","sun exposure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.104Z','2026-10-19T03:41:01.180Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krap0016o7lrwo8auaic','mcq','Preterm infant was breast-fed but switched to formula due to maternal illness contraindicate breastfeeding. The component that the infant will lose and can''t replaced by formula','["Protein","Carbohydrates","Live immune cells"]','Live immune cells','<p><strong>Why Live immune cells is correct:</strong> Breast milk contains live immune cells, including macrophages, lymphocytes, and neutrophils, which provide passive immunity to the infant. These cells actively phagocytose pathogens, produce antimicrobial factors, and help modulate the infant''s developing immune system. Formula, being a processed product, cannot replicate these living cellular components, making this a unique and irreplaceable benefit of breastfeeding that is lost when switching to formula. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Protein:</strong> While breast milk protein is more bioavailable and contains unique whey-to-casein ratios, formula is specifically designed to match the protein content and quality of breast milk. - <strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> Breast milk contains lactose and human milk oligosaccharides (HMOs), but modern formulas are fortified with similar carbohydrate components to approximate breast milk nutrition. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Breast milk contains over 200 bioactive components, including living immune cells, that cannot be replicated in formula. - The immune cells in breast milk provide both passive immunity and help educate the infant''s developing immune system. - HMOs in breast milk act as prebiotics and pathogen decoys, but these are not living cells. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The only component in breast milk that cannot be replaced by formula is the living immune cells that provide active immune protection to the infant.</p>','<p>B.L.I.S.S. - Breastfeeding provides Living Immune cells, bioactive factors, Stem cells, and Secretory IgA that formula cannot replicate.</p>','<p>Breast milk''s living immune cells provide not just passive immunity but also help educate the infant''s developing immune system, potentially reducing the risk of allergic and autoimmune diseases later in life.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Protein:</strong> Formula is specifically designed to match the protein content and quality of breast milk. - <strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> Modern formulas contain similar carbohydrate components to approximate breast milk nutrition.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Pediatrics','Preterm nutrition','["preterm","breastfeeding","immune cells"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.105Z','2026-10-19T03:41:13.769Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kraq0017o7lrut5w3pq7','mcq','2-week-old infant exclusively breast-fed with persistent crying after feeding. He has poor weight gain and the mother complains of nipple pain. On examination, the infant''s cheeks dimple while suckling. What is the most likely cause and best first response?','["Tongue tie, immediately frenulotomy","Poor latch, correction of position latch"]','Poor latch, correction of position latch','<p><strong>Why Poor latch, correction of position latch is correct:</strong> Poor latch is the most likely cause of this infant''s symptoms, including persistent crying after feeding, poor weight gain, and nipple pain. The dimpling of the infant''s cheeks during suckling indicates that the infant is not properly latched, causing ineffective milk transfer and maternal nipple trauma. Correcting the latch by ensuring proper positioning (infant''s nose opposite the nipple, wide mouth, flanged lips) and attachment (infant takes in a large portion of the areola) will improve milk transfer, reduce maternal pain, and enhance feeding efficiency. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Tongue tie, immediately frenulotomy:</strong> While tongue tie can cause similar symptoms, it''s not the most likely diagnosis in this scenario. Dimpling cheeks suggest poor latch rather than tongue tie, and frenulotomy should only be considered after proper latch techniques have been attempted and failed. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Dimpling of the infant''s cheeks during feeding is a classic sign of poor latch rather than tongue tie. - Proper latch assessment should always precede consideration of anatomical issues like tongue tie. - Nipple pain is more commonly associated with poor latch than with tongue tie. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Dimpling cheeks during breastfeeding indicate poor latch, which should be addressed with proper positioning and attachment techniques before considering anatomical issues like tongue tie.</p>','<p>C.H.E.E.K.S. - Check for dimpling, Hunger cues, Ear position, Engorgement, Kiss-like lips, Suckling pattern to assess latch quality.</p>','<p>Nipple pain that persists beyond the first week of breastfeeding is abnormal and almost always indicates a problem with latch or positioning rather than simply ''getting used to breastfeeding.''</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tongue tie, immediately frenulotomy:</strong> Dimpling cheeks suggest poor latch rather than tongue tie, and frenulotomy should only be considered after proper latch techniques have been attempted and failed.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Breastfeeding','["infant","breastfeeding","latch","weight gain"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.106Z','2026-10-19T03:41:13.775Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krax001co7lrdwrlxie1','mcq','Most common cause of bowel obstruction in neonate is','["Hirschsprung''s disease"]','Hirschsprung''s disease','<p><strong>Why Hirschsprung''s disease is correct:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease is the most common cause of bowel obstruction in neonates because it results from the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon, leading to a functional obstruction. This aganglionic segment cannot participate in peristalsis, causing a functional obstruction that typically presents in the first few weeks of life with failure to pass meconium, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting. The condition is more common in males and has a genetic component, with mutations in the RET proto-oncogene being implicated in many familial cases. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Meconium ileus:</strong> This is a condition associated with cystic fibrosis where abnormally thick meconium causes obstruction, but it is less common than Hirschsprung''s disease as a cause of neonatal bowel obstruction. - <strong>Malrotation with volvulus:</strong> While this is a surgical emergency that can cause bowel obstruction in neonates, it is less common than Hirschsprung''s disease and typically presents later in the first week of life. - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> This condition is rare in neonates and more commonly occurs in infants between 6-36 months of age. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hirschsprung''s disease should be suspected in any neonate who fails to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. - The classic triad includes failure to pass meconium, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting. - Diagnosis is confirmed by rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease is the most common cause of neonatal bowel obstruction due to aganglionosis causing functional obstruction.</p>','<p>Hirschsprung''s: ''No Gang, No Go'' - absence of ganglion cells prevents bowel movement.</p>','<p>Early recognition of Hirschsprung''s disease is critical as delayed diagnosis can lead to life-threatening enterocolitis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Meconium ileus:</strong> Less common than Hirschsprung''s and associated with cystic fibrosis. - <strong>Malrotation with volvulus:</strong> Less common and typically presents later in the first week of life. - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> Rare in neonates and more common in older infants.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Bowel Obstruction','["neonate","obstruction","hirschsprung"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.114Z','2026-10-19T03:41:42.990Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kraz001eo7lrd2kwjr2a','mcq','Complication with Hirschsprung disease','["Enterocolitis"]','Enterocolitis','<p><strong>Why Enterocolitis is correct:</strong> Enterocolitis is the most serious and common complication of Hirschsprung''s disease, occurring in up to 30% of patients, and is characterized by inflammation of the colon and small intestine due to bacterial overgrowth in the dilated proximal colon above the aganglionic segment. This complication results from functional obstruction leading to stasis, bacterial proliferation, mucosal injury, and potential perforation, with symptoms including explosive diarrhea, fever, abdominal distension, and sepsis. Enterocolitis can occur at any age but is most common in the first two years of life and can be life-threatening if not promptly recognized and treated with bowel decompression, antibiotics, and potentially urgent surgical intervention. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> While intussusception can occur in patients with Hirschsprung''s disease, it is not the most common complication and is more frequently associated with other conditions like viral infections or lymphoid hyperplasia. - <strong>Volvulus:</strong> Volvulus is a rare complication of Hirschsprung''s disease and is more commonly associated with malrotation. - <strong>Perforation:</strong> Perforation can occur as a consequence of enterocolitis but is not itself the primary complication. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Enterocolitis is a medical emergency that requires immediate intervention with bowel decompression, antibiotics, and potentially surgery. - Risk factors for enterocolitis include recent bowel preparation, delayed diagnosis, and history of prior enterocolitis episodes. - Prophylactic measures include regular bowel management and prompt treatment of constipation. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Enterocolitis is the most serious and common complication of Hirschsprung''s disease, requiring urgent recognition and treatment.</p>','<p>Enterocolitis in Hirschsprung''s: ''Bacteria Bloom in the Blind Loop'' - bacterial overgrowth in the dilated proximal colon.</p>','<p>Always suspect enterocolitis in a child with Hirschsprung''s disease who presents with fever, abdominal distension, and explosive diarrhea.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> Not the most common complication and more associated with other conditions. - <strong>Volvulus:</strong> Rare in Hirschsprung''s disease and more associated with malrotation. - <strong>Perforation:</strong> Can occur as a consequence of enterocolitis but is not the primary complication.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Hirschsprung Disease','["complication","hirschsprung"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.115Z','2026-10-19T03:41:42.993Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6z0050e9oii8mn6dw3','mcq','Which defect needs blood pressure measurement?','["Coarctation of the aorta","Transposition of the great arteries","Pulmonary stenosis","Atrial septal defect"]','Coarctation of the aorta','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Coarctation of Aorta','["blood pressure","coarctation","measurement"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.675Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.675Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb0001fo7lrbwduq8a4','mcq','which of the following metabolic cause of constipation;','["hypo calcemia","hypo natremia","hypokalemia"]','hypokalemia','<p><strong>Why hypokalemia is correct:</strong> Hypokalemia causes constipation through impaired gastrointestinal smooth muscle contraction. Potassium is essential for maintaining the resting membrane potential of smooth muscle cells in the intestinal wall. When potassium levels drop below 3.5 mEq/L, the resting membrane potential becomes more negative, making it more difficult for these cells to depolarize and generate action potentials necessary for peristalsis. This results in decreased motility and slowed transit time through the colon, leading to constipation. Additionally, hypokalemia can reduce acetylcholine release from enteric neurons, further impairing gastrointestinal motility.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> While hypocalcemia can cause various neuromuscular symptoms, it does not directly impair gastrointestinal motility. Constipation is not a typical feature of hypocalcemia.</li>
<li><strong>Hyponatremia:</strong> Hyponatremia primarily affects the central nervous system, causing symptoms like headache, confusion, and seizures. It does not directly impact gastrointestinal motility or cause constipation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypokalemia is one of the most common metabolic causes of constipation, particularly in patients on diuretics or with chronic kidney disease.</li>
<li>Severe hypokalemia (levels &lt; 2.5 mEq/L) can lead to paralytic ileus, a more severe form of constipation.</li>
<li>The treatment of constipation in hypokalemia involves correcting the potassium deficit, not using traditional laxatives.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypokalemia causes constipation by impairing smooth muscle contraction in the gastrointestinal tract due to decreased membrane excitability.</p>','<p>Hypokalemia = Hypo-motility (remember the ''hypo'' prefix for both low potassium and decreased motility)</p>','<p>Always check serum potassium levels in patients with new-onset constipation, especially those on diuretics or with chronic illnesses.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> Does not directly impair gastrointestinal motility.</li>
<li><strong>Hyponatremia:</strong> Primarily affects the central nervous system, not gut motility.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Endocrine','Metabolic Constipation','["metabolic","constipation","electrolyte"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.116Z','2026-10-19T03:41:48.804Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb1001go7lrxew099zb','mcq','Which of the following diseases has constipation as its clinical feature?','["Rheumatic fever.","Interstitial nephritis.","Irritable bowel syndrome.","Onchocercosis."]','Irritable bowel syndrome.','<p><strong>Why Irritable bowel syndrome is correct:</strong> Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder characterized by abdominal pain and altered bowel habits, which can include either constipation (IBS-C), diarrhea (IBS-D), or mixed patterns (IBS-M). The Rome IV criteria for IBS require recurrent abdominal pain at least 1 day per week in the last 3 months, associated with two or more of the following: related to defecation, associated with a change in stool frequency, or associated with a change in stool form. Constipation in IBS-C results from altered colonic motility, visceral hypersensitivity, and brain-gut axis dysfunction. The pathophysiology involves dysregulation of the enteric nervous system, increased intestinal permeability, and low-grade inflammation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rheumatic fever:</strong> An autoimmune inflammatory condition triggered by group A streptococcal infection, characterized by migratory polyarthritis, carditis, and erythema marginatum. Constipation is not a feature of rheumatic fever.</li>
<li><strong>Interstitial nephritis:</strong> An inflammatory condition of the kidney interstitium that typically presents with acute kidney injury, fever, rash, and eosinophilia. Gastrointestinal symptoms may occur but constipation is not a typical feature.</li>
<li><strong>Onchocerciasis:</strong> A parasitic infection caused by Onchocerca volvulus, transmitted by black flies, leading to skin changes and visual impairment. Constipation is not associated with this infection.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>IBS is the most common functional gastrointestinal disorder, affecting approximately 10-15% of the population worldwide.</li>
<li>The Rome IV criteria are essential for diagnosing IBS and require symptom duration of at least 6 months.</li>
<li>IBS-C is characterized by hard or lumpy stools in at least 25% of bowel movements and loose or watery stools in less than 25% of bowel movements.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Irritable bowel syndrome is the only condition among the options that consistently presents with constipation as a core clinical feature.</p>','<p>IBS-C = Constipation (remember the ''C'' in IBS-C stands for constipation)</p>','<p>IBS is a diagnosis of exclusion; consider organic causes if alarm features like weight loss, bleeding, or family history of inflammatory bowel disease are present.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Rheumatic fever:</strong> An autoimmune condition affecting joints and heart, not characterized by constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Interstitial nephritis:</strong> Primarily affects kidney function, not typically associated with constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Onchocerciasis:</strong> A parasitic infection causing skin and eye problems, not constipation.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["symptom","disease"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.117Z','2026-10-19T03:41:48.807Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb2001ho7lrat8oajke','mcq','All of the following are metabolic causes of Constipation Except','["Hypothyroidism","Hypokalemia","Diabetes inspidus","Hypocalcemia"]','Hypothyroidism','<p><strong>Why Hypothyroidism is correct:</strong> Hypothyroidism is NOT a metabolic cause of constipation; rather, it''s the exception among the options listed. While hypothyroidism can cause constipation through decreased gastrointestinal motility and myxedematous changes in the intestinal wall, this question asks for the condition that is NOT a metabolic cause of constipation. Hypothyroidism is an endocrine disorder resulting from insufficient thyroid hormone production, which can lead to slowed metabolic processes throughout the body, including the gastrointestinal tract. However, among the given options, hypothyroidism is the only one that is not primarily a metabolic disorder affecting electrolyte or fluid balance that directly causes constipation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> A metabolic electrolyte disorder that directly impairs smooth muscle contraction in the gastrointestinal tract, leading to constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes insipidus:</strong> A metabolic disorder affecting water balance, characterized by excessive thirst and urine production. It can cause constipation due to dehydration and electrolyte imbalances.</li>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> A metabolic electrolyte disorder that can cause constipation, particularly when severe, due to effects on smooth muscle function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypothyroidism is an endocrine disorder, not primarily a metabolic disorder affecting electrolyte balance.</li>
<li>Constipation in hypothyroidism is due to decreased metabolic rate and myxedematous changes in the intestinal wall.</li>
<li>Metabolic causes of constipation typically involve electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypocalcemia) or fluid/electrolyte disorders (diabetes insipidus).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypothyroidism is the exception among these options because it''s an endocrine disorder rather than a metabolic disorder affecting electrolyte balance that directly causes constipation.</p>','<p>Hypo-thyroidism is NOT primarily metabolic (remember thyroid is endocrine, not metabolic)</p>','<p>When evaluating constipation, consider both metabolic causes (electrolyte imbalances) and endocrine disorders (hypothyroidism).</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> A metabolic electrolyte disorder that directly causes constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes insipidus:</strong> A metabolic disorder affecting water balance that can lead to constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Hypocalcemia:</strong> A metabolic electrolyte disorder that can cause constipation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Metabolic Constipation','["metabolic","constipation","electrolyte"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.118Z','2026-10-19T03:41:48.808Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb4001jo7lrxcgukfj7','mcq','what is the incidence of the presence of the classic triad of intussusception, (pain, a palpable sausage-shaped abdominal mass and bloody or currant jelly stool)?','["≤20% of patients","≤30% of patients","≤40% of patients","≤50% of patients"]','≤40% of patients','<p><strong>Why ≤40% of patients is correct:</strong> The classic triad of intussusception (pain, a palpable sausage-shaped abdominal mass, and bloody or currant jelly stool) is present in only about 40% of cases, which is why clinicians must maintain a high index of suspicion even when this triad is not fully present. The pathophysiology involves telescoping of one segment of bowel into another, typically the ileum into the colon, leading to obstruction and venous congestion. This venous congestion causes the characteristic bloody or currant jelly stool as blood and mucus are extruded into the bowel lumen. The intermittent nature of the intussusception explains why the classic triad may not be present at all times during examination, as the bowel may spontaneously reduce and then re-intussuscept.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>≤20% of patients:</strong> This underestimates the true incidence, as studies consistently show that approximately 40% of patients present with the complete triad.</li>
<li><strong>≤30% of patients:</strong> While close, this is still below the established 40% incidence rate for the classic triad.</li>
<li><strong>≤50% of patients:</strong> This overestimates the incidence, as the actual percentage is closer to 40% rather than approaching half of all cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Intussusception is the most common cause of bowel obstruction in children aged 3 months to 3 years.</li>
<li>The classic triad is present in only about 40% of cases, so diagnosis should not be delayed if the triad is incomplete.</li>
<li>Ultrasound is the diagnostic modality of choice, revealing a target sign or pseudokidney appearance.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Only 40% of children with intussusception present with the complete classic triad, so clinicians must diagnose based on partial presentations and high clinical suspicion.</p>','<p>40% Triad: Only 4 out of 10 intussusception patients present with the complete classic triad</p>','<p>Intussusception can present with intermittent symptoms, and the classic triad may not be present simultaneously, making ultrasound crucial for diagnosis when clinical suspicion is high.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>≤20% of patients:</strong> This underestimates the true incidence of the classic triad in intussusception.</li>
<li><strong>≤30% of patients:</strong> This is below the established 40% incidence rate for the complete triad.</li>
<li><strong>≤50% of patients:</strong> This overestimates the actual percentage, which is closer to 40%.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["triad","incidence","intussusception"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.120Z','2026-10-19T03:42:03.605Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb5001lo7lrp6u13qyq','mcq','When is the definitive surgical repair for Hirschsprung''s disease usually performed in children?','["Within the first month of life","Between 6 and 12 months of age","Between 2 and 4 years of age","After 5 years of age"]','Between 6 and 12 months of age','<p><strong>Why Between 6 and 12 months of age is correct:</strong> The definitive surgical repair for Hirschsprung''s disease is typically performed between 6 and 12 months of age to allow for adequate growth and development while minimizing the risk of complications from untreated aganglionic bowel. During this period, the infant has usually overcome the immediate neonatal risks and has sufficient size to undergo a major surgical procedure safely. The timing also allows for the diagnosis to be confirmed through rectal biopsy and for any associated enterocolitis to be managed before definitive surgery. The most common surgical approach, the Soave or Swenson procedure, involves removing the aganglionic segment and performing a pull-through anastomosis, which is technically more feasible once the child has reached this developmental stage.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Within the first month of life:</strong> Performing definitive surgery in the first month is avoided due to the higher surgical risks in neonates and the need to first manage any associated enterocolitis and allow for initial stabilization.</li>
<li><strong>Between 2 and 4 years of age:</strong> This delay is unnecessary as the procedure can be safely performed earlier, and waiting longer increases the risk of complications like enterocolitis and malnutrition.</li>
<li><strong>After 5 years of age:</strong> There is no benefit to delaying surgery beyond 12 months, and doing so exposes the child to prolonged risks of complications without any advantage.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hirschsprung''s disease is typically diagnosed in the first few months of life with symptoms including constipation, abdominal distension, and failure to pass meconium.</li>
<li>The initial management often involves a diverting colostomy followed by definitive pull-through surgery at 6-12 months.</li>
<li>The presence of Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis (HAEC) is a serious complication that requires immediate medical attention and may delay surgical intervention.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Definitive surgical repair for Hirschsprung''s disease is optimally performed between 6 and 12 months of age to balance surgical safety with the risks of untreated disease.</p>','<p>6-12 Repair: Hirschsprung''s definitive repair happens between 6-12 months of age</p>','<p>Always suspect Hirschsprung''s disease in a newborn who fails to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Within the first month of life:</strong> Neonates have higher surgical risks and need stabilization before definitive repair.</li>
<li><strong>Between 2 and 4 years of age:</strong> This delay is unnecessary and increases the risk of complications like enterocolitis.</li>
<li><strong>After 5 years of age:</strong> There is no benefit to delaying beyond 12 months, only increased risk.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Hirschsprung Disease','["surgery","timing","hirschsprung"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.121Z','2026-10-19T03:42:03.606Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6z0051e9oid0tzq78e','mcq','What is the presentation of severe aortic stenosis in a 1-year-old child?','["Recurrent palpitation","Left-sided heart failure manifestations","Right-sided heart failure manifestations","None of the above"]','Left-sided heart failure manifestations','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["aortic stenosis","presentation","child"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.675Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.675Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu700052e9oi6ydk9z10','mcq','What is the consequence of prolonged pulmonary stenosis (PS)?','["Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)","Left-sided heart failure (LHF)","Right-sided heart failure (RHF)","Left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)"]','Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["pulmonary stenosis","consequence","RVH"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.676Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.676Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb5001ko7lr7id76h5m','mcq','What percentage of constipation cases in children are functional (without organic cause)?','["75%","85%","95%","100%"]','95%','<p><strong>Why 95% is correct:</strong> The vast majority of constipation cases in children are functional, meaning they lack an identifiable organic cause. This functional constipation is primarily related to behavioral factors, stool withholding due to painful defecation, and delayed toilet training rather than structural abnormalities, metabolic disorders, or neurological conditions. The diagnosis is made clinically based on Rome IV criteria, which include symptoms such as two or fewer defecations per week, fecal incontinence, and painful defecation for at least one month in children with developmental age of at least 4 years.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>75%:</strong> This underestimates the true prevalence of functional constipation, as organic causes account for only about 5% of cases in children.</li>
<li><strong>85%:</strong> While close, this still overestimates the proportion of cases with organic causes; the actual figure is closer to 95% functional.</li>
<li><strong>100%:</strong> While nearly all cases are functional, there are rare organic causes that must be considered, especially in cases with red flags like failure to thrive, bloody stools, or onset in the first month of life.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Functional constipation is the most common gastrointestinal disorder in children, affecting up to 30% at some point.</li>
<li>Red flags suggesting organic causes include onset in infancy, severe abdominal distension, vomiting, failure to thrive, and bloody stools.</li>
<li>The Rome IV criteria provide standardized diagnostic criteria for functional constipation in different age groups.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation accounts for 95% of childhood constipation cases, diagnosed clinically when red flags for organic disease are absent.</p>','<p>FIFTY-FIVE: Functional constipation is 95% (not 50%), organic causes are 5%.</p>','<p>Always consider organic causes of constipation in infants, children with red flags, or those who don''t respond to standard treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>75%:</strong> Underestimates the true prevalence of functional constipation.</li>
<li><strong>85%:</strong> Overestimates the proportion of cases with organic causes.</li>
<li><strong>100%:</strong> Incorrect as there are rare organic causes that must be considered.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["functional","prevalence"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.121Z','2026-10-19T03:42:06.374Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb6001mo7lrvhnrlhsh','mcq','Which of the following is a definitive diagnosis for Hirschsprung''s disease?','["Barium enema","Anorectal manometry","Rectal biopsy","Abdominal ultrasound"]','Rectal biopsy','<p><strong>Why Rectal biopsy is correct:</strong> Rectal biopsy is the definitive diagnostic test for Hirschsprung''s disease as it directly identifies the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the affected bowel segment. The biopsy also reveals hypertrophied nerve trunks, which are pathognomonic for this condition. This histological examination provides the most reliable and conclusive diagnosis, distinguishing Hirschsprung''s from other causes of constipation or obstruction.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Barium enema:</strong> While it may show a classic transition zone between the dilated proximal colon and narrowed distal aganglionic segment, it is not definitive as findings can be normal in early disease or atypical in older children.</li>
<li><strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> This test measures the anorectal reflex and can be suggestive of Hirschsprung''s when absent, but it is not definitive as it can be normal in some cases and requires specialized expertise to perform and interpret.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> This is not a reliable diagnostic tool for Hirschsprung''s disease, though it may show secondary findings like dilated bowel loops.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The absence of the rectoanal inhibitory reflex on anorectal manometry is highly suggestive of Hirschsprung''s disease but requires confirmation with biopsy.</li>
<li>In newborns, a simple rectal suction biopsy can be performed without full-thickness biopsy to avoid complications.</li>
<li>Genetic testing may be helpful in familial cases or when associated with syndromes but cannot replace histological diagnosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rectal biopsy is the definitive diagnosis for Hirschsprung''s disease as it directly demonstrates the absence of ganglion cells.</p>','<p>Biopsy is Best: Remember that rectal biopsy is the definitive test for Hirschsprung''s disease.</p>','<p>In a newborn with delayed passage of meconium and bilious vomiting, suspect Hirschsprung''s disease and proceed with rectal biopsy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Barium enema:</strong> Shows suggestive findings but is not definitive as it can be normal in early disease.</li>
<li><strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> Measures reflexes but is not conclusive as it can be normal in some cases.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> Not reliable for diagnosing Hirschsprung''s disease.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Hirschsprung Disease','["diagnosis","biopsy","hirschsprung"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.122Z','2026-10-19T03:42:06.376Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krb7001no7lrttt26flr','mcq','One of the commonest cause of intestinal obstruction in neonates','["GERD","Cows milk allergy","Hirshsprung disease"]','Hirshsprung disease','<p><strong>Why Hirshsprung disease is correct:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in neonates, accounting for approximately 25-30% of cases. This condition results from the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon, leading to a functional obstruction. The aganglionic segment cannot relax, causing proximal dilation and failure to pass meconium within the first 24-48 hours of life, which is a classic presentation in neonates.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>GERD:</strong> Gastroesophageal reflux disease is a common condition in infants but typically presents with vomiting, irritability, or failure to thrive rather than complete intestinal obstruction.</li>
<li><strong>Cows milk allergy:</strong> While milk protein allergy can cause gastrointestinal symptoms including vomiting and diarrhea, it rarely presents as a complete intestinal obstruction in neonates.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The classic presentation of Hirschsprung''s disease is failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting.</li>
<li>Hirschsprung''s disease is more common in males (with a 4:1 male-to-female ratio) and has a genetic component with increased risk in siblings.</li>
<li>Associated conditions include Down syndrome and other congenital anomalies, particularly in long-segment disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease is the most common cause of neonatal intestinal obstruction due to aganglionosis causing functional obstruction.</p>','<p>Meconium Means More: Delayed meconium passage in neonates should prompt consideration of Hirschsprung''s disease.</p>','<p>Always consider Hirschsprung''s disease in a newborn with delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>GERD:</strong> Causes vomiting and irritability but not complete intestinal obstruction.</li>
<li><strong>Cows milk allergy:</strong> Can cause GI symptoms but rarely presents as complete obstruction in neonates.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Bowel Obstruction','["neonate","obstruction","hirschsprung"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.123Z','2026-10-19T03:42:06.377Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbl001zo7lriewzvozl','mcq','Which of the following statement describes upper GIT bleeding in children?','["All patients should undergo nasogastric tube placement and lavage","Ulcers may cause UGI bleeding in young children","Esophageal varices are a common cause of UGI bleeding","Hematemesis in a newborn is usually a symptom of significant disease"]','Ulcers may cause UGI bleeding in young children','<p><strong>Why Ulcers may cause UGI bleeding in young children is correct:</strong> Peptic ulcers can cause upper gastrointestinal (UGI) bleeding in young children due to various etiologies such as Helicobacter pylori infection, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) use, or stress-related mucosal disease. The pathophysiology involves mucosal damage from acid or pepsin, leading to erosion of the submucosal or muscular blood vessels, which can result in significant bleeding. In young children, ulcers may present with hematemesis, melena, or hematochezia, and while less common than in adults, they remain an important consideration in the differential diagnosis of UGI bleeding.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>All patients should undergo nasogastric tube placement and lavage:</strong> Nasogastric tube placement is not routinely necessary for all patients with UGI bleeding and may cause discomfort or complications; it is reserved for specific clinical scenarios.</li>
<li><strong>Esophageal varices are a common cause of UGI bleeding:</strong> Esophageal varices are rare in children and are typically associated with portal hypertension from conditions like biliary atresia or liver disease, making them uncommon compared to other causes.</li>
<li><strong>Hematemesis in a newborn is usually a symptom of significant disease:</strong> While hematemesis in a newborn can be serious, it is not always due to significant disease; common causes include swallowed maternal blood, milk protein allergy, or stress gastritis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>UGI bleeding in children has different etiologies than in adults, with Mallory-Weiss tears, esophagitis, and ulcers being common causes.</li>
<li>H. pylori infection is a significant risk factor for peptic ulcer disease in children and should be considered in cases of recurrent UGI bleeding.</li>
<li>The approach to UGI bleeding in children depends on the severity and hemodynamic stability, with endoscopy playing a key role in diagnosis and management.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Unlike adults, children with UGI bleeding often have ulcers as a primary cause, frequently associated with H. pylori or NSAID use.</p>','<p>Child UGI bleeding: ''Ulcers, not varices'' - remember U for Ulcers in young patients.</p>','<p>In children, UGI bleeding is less commonly due to varices than in adults, and more often associated with mucosal injury from infection, medications, or stress.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>All patients should undergo nasogastric tube placement and lavage:</strong> Not routinely necessary for all patients with UGI bleeding.</li>
<li><strong>Esophageal varices are a common cause of UGI bleeding:</strong> Rare in children compared to other causes.</li>
<li><strong>Hematemesis in a newborn is usually a symptom of significant disease:</strong> Can be due to benign causes like swallowed maternal blood.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Upper GI bleeding','["upper gi","bleeding","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.137Z','2026-10-19T03:42:47.216Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbv0028o7lrskaojjgw','mcq','10- An infant aged 4 weeks presents with progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting after feeds. The most likely diagnosis is:','["Gastroesophageal reflux","Pyloric stenosis","Intussusception","Gastroenteritis"]','Pyloric stenosis','<p><strong>Why Pyloric stenosis is correct:</strong> Pyloric stenosis is the most likely diagnosis in a 4-week-old infant presenting with progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting. This condition results from hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the pyloric muscle, causing gastric outlet obstruction. The classic pathophysiology involves progressive thickening of the pyloric sphincter (typically to 4-6mm on ultrasound), preventing gastric emptying and leading to the characteristic projectile vomiting. This condition typically presents between 2-8 weeks of life, with a peak incidence around 3-6 weeks, and is more common in males and first-born children.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux:</strong> While reflux can cause vomiting in infants, it typically presents as effortless, non-projectile vomiting rather than the forceful projectile vomiting seen in pyloric stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> This condition usually presents later (3-36 months) with vomiting that may become bilious, along with currant jelly stools and a palpable abdominal mass, which are not described here.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> This would typically present with bilious vomiting, diarrhea, and possibly fever, rather than the classic projectile vomiting without biliousness seen in pyloric stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Pyloric stenosis classically presents in 2-8 week old infants with progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting.</li>
<li>The "olive-sized" palpable mass in the epigastrium is pathognomonic for pyloric stenosis.</li>
<li>Ultrasound showing pyloric muscle thickness &gt;3mm and length &gt;15mm is diagnostic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pyloric stenosis is the diagnosis in a 4-week-old with progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting due to pyloric muscle hypertrophy causing gastric outlet obstruction.</p>','<p>Projectile vomiting in a 4-week-old male? Think "PS" for Pyloric Stenosis, not "GER" for Gastroesophageal Reflux.</p>','<p>Pyloric stenosis requires prompt surgical intervention (pyloromyotomy) as it leads to progressive dehydration and electrolyte abnormalities (hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis) from persistent vomiting.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux:</strong> Presents with effortless, non-projectile vomiting rather than forceful projectile vomiting.</li>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> Typically presents later with bilious vomiting, currant jelly stools, and a palpable mass.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> Usually presents with bilious vomiting, diarrhea, and fever.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Pyloric Stenosis','["vomiting","infant","pyloric"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.147Z','2026-10-19T03:43:23.817Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbw002ao7lrzez41fsl','mcq','0- Anemia is due to','["Hb deficiency"]','Hb deficiency','<p><strong>Why Hb deficiency is correct:</strong> Anemia is fundamentally defined as a deficiency of hemoglobin (Hb) in the blood, which is the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells. This deficiency can result from various mechanisms including decreased production (deficient erythropoiesis), increased destruction (hemolysis), or blood loss. Hemoglobin deficiency leads to reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, causing the clinical manifestations of anemia such as fatigue, weakness, and pallor. The World Health Organization defines anemia by specific hemoglobin concentration thresholds in different populations and genders.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While the question only provides one correct option, it''s important to note that anemia could also be defined as a deficiency in red blood cell mass or hematocrit. However, hemoglobin deficiency is the most fundamental and direct definition of anemia, as it directly relates to the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Anemia is defined as hemoglobin concentration below established reference values for age, sex, and altitude.</li>
<li>The three main mechanisms of anemia are decreased production, increased destruction, and blood loss.</li>
<li>Hemoglobin concentration is the most direct measure of anemia severity and oxygen-carrying capacity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Anemia is fundamentally a hemoglobin deficiency that reduces the blood''s oxygen-carrying capacity.</p>','<p>Hb = Hemoglobin deficiency = Anemia</p>','<p>The severity of anemia symptoms correlates more with the rate of hemoglobin decline than with the absolute hemoglobin level.</p>','','Easy','Hematology','Anemia Definition','["anemia","hemoglobin"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.148Z','2026-10-19T03:43:23.818Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbg001uo7lrl80341m8','mcq','All of the following are metabolic causes of Constipation Except...','["Hypothyroidism","Hypokalemia","Diabetes insipidus","Hypocalcemia"]','Hypocalcemia','<p><strong>Why Hypocalcemia is correct:</strong> Hypocalcemia is not a metabolic cause of constipation because calcium primarily affects neuromuscular excitability rather than gastrointestinal motility. While hypocalcemia can cause constipation indirectly through neuromuscular symptoms, it is not a direct metabolic cause like the other options. The primary mechanism of constipation in metabolic disorders typically involves altered autonomic nervous system function, electrolyte imbalances affecting smooth muscle contraction, or hormonal influences on gut motility.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Causes constipation due to decreased metabolic rate and reduced gut motility, mediated by decreased sympathetic tone and increased parasympathetic activity.</li>
<li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> Directly impairs smooth muscle contraction in the gastrointestinal tract, leading to paralytic ileus and constipation.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes insipidus:</strong> Can cause constipation through dehydration and electrolyte imbalances, though it''s less directly related than other metabolic causes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Metabolic causes of constipation typically involve electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypercalcemia), endocrine disorders (hypothyroidism), or autonomic neuropathy.</li>
<li>Hypocalcemia is more associated with neuromuscular symptoms like tetany rather than direct gastrointestinal effects.</li>
<li>Constipation in metabolic disorders often resolves when the underlying condition is treated.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Constipation has numerous metabolic causes, but hypocalcemia is not among them as it primarily affects neuromuscular function rather than gut motility.</p>','<p>Hypothyroidism and Hypokalemia cause constipation (HH), while Hypocalcemia does not (H).</p>','<p>When evaluating constipation, always check thyroid function and electrolytes, as these are common and reversible causes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Causes decreased gut motility through reduced metabolic rate and autonomic dysfunction.</li>
<li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> Directly impairs smooth muscle contraction in the GI tract.</li>
<li><strong>Diabetes insipidus:</strong> Can cause constipation through dehydration and electrolyte imbalances.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["constipation","metabolic","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.132Z','2026-10-19T03:42:41.585Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbh001vo7lry1g9wk5b','mcq','Most common cause of lower GIT bleeding in children:','["Crohn''s disease","Ulcerative colitis","Intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum"]','Intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum','<p><strong>Why Intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum is correct:</strong> Intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum are the most common causes of lower gastrointestinal bleeding in children because they represent developmental anomalies that are prevalent in the pediatric population. Intussusception typically occurs between 6-36 months and presents with classic triad of intermittent colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stool (due to blood and mucus). Meckel''s diverticulum, a remnant of the omphalomesenteric duct, is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract and often presents with painless rectal bleeding in young children due to ectopic gastric mucosa causing ulceration.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Crohn''s disease:</strong> While it can cause lower GI bleeding, it is less common than intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum in children and typically presents with chronic symptoms rather than acute bleeding.</li>
<li><strong>Ulcerative colitis:</strong> Also less common than intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum in children and typically presents with chronic diarrhea and bleeding rather than acute presentations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Intussusception is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children aged 6-36 months.</li>
<li>Meckel''s diverticulum follows the rule of 2s: 2% of population, 2 inches long, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, presents in first 2 years, contains 2 types of mucosa.</li>
<li>Lower GI bleeding in children often has different etiologies than in adults, with congenital anomalies being more common.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In children with lower GI bleeding, think first of developmental anomalies like intussusception and Meckel''s diverticulum rather than inflammatory bowel disease.</p>','<p>IM for Intussusception and Meckel''s - the most common causes of lower GI bleeding in Infants and young children.</p>','<p>Always consider Meckel''s diverticulum in any child with painless lower GI bleeding, as it''s the most common congenital GI anomaly.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Crohn''s disease:</strong> Less common than intussusception and Meckel''s in children and typically presents with chronic symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Ulcerative colitis:</strong> Less common in children and usually presents with chronic diarrhea rather than acute bleeding.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Lower GI bleeding','["bleeding","lower gi","common cause","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.133Z','2026-10-19T03:42:41.587Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbi001wo7lrl1h82ehk','mcq','Black currant jelly stool is characteristic of:','["Hirschsprung disease","Intussusception","Meckel''s diverticulum","Volvulus"]','Intussusception','<p><strong>Why Intussusception is correct:</strong> Black currant jelly stool is characteristic of intussusception because it represents the passage of blood and mucus through the bowel lumen as a result of venous congestion and ischemia at the leading edge of the telescoped bowel segments. This occurs when one segment of bowel (the intussusceptum) telescopes into another (the intussuscipiens), compromising blood flow and causing the characteristic bloody, mucoid stool.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hirschsprung disease:</strong> Typically presents with constipation and abdominal distension, not the characteristic black currant jelly stool of intussusception.</li>
<li><strong>Meckel''s diverticulum:</strong> Usually presents with painless rectal bleeding of bright red blood, not the mixed blood and mucus seen in intussusception.</li>
<li><strong>Volvulus:</strong> Presents with acute abdominal pain and obstruction but does not typically produce the characteristic black currant jelly stool; instead, it may present with bilious vomiting in the case of small bowel volvulus.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Intussusception is most common in infants aged 6-36 months and is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in this age group.</li>
<li>The classic triad of intussusception includes intermittent colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stool, though all three are present in less than 50% of cases.</li>
<li>Ultrasound showing a target sign or pseudokidney sign is diagnostic for intussusception.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Black currant jelly stool is pathognomonic for intussusception, caused by telescoping bowel segments compromising blood flow.</p>','<p>Black currant jelly stool = Intussusception (IS) - the ''IS'' of the stool matches the ''IS'' of intussusception.</p>','<p>Intussusception is a surgical emergency that can lead to bowel necrosis if not promptly reduced, either by air enema or surgery.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hirschsprung disease:</strong> Presents with constipation and abdominal distension, not bloody stool.</li>
<li><strong>Meckel''s diverticulum:</strong> Typically presents with painless bright red rectal bleeding.</li>
<li><strong>Volvulus:</strong> Presents with acute obstruction but not the characteristic currant jelly stool.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["stool","currant jelly","intussusception","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.134Z','2026-10-19T03:42:41.588Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbp0025o7lr1xrvjl2u','mcq','7- The most common type for tracheoesophageal fistula is:','["Atresia with distal fistula","Atresia with double fistula","Atresia with proximal fistula","Atresia with H shaped fistula"]','Atresia with distal fistula','<p><strong>Why Atresia with distal fistula is correct:</strong> The most common type of tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) is type C, which consists of esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula. This accounts for approximately 85% of all cases. In this anomaly, the upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch, while the lower esophagus connects to the trachea via a fistula, allowing air to enter the stomach and preventing proper feeding. This classification system (types A-E) was developed by Gross and is widely used in clinical practice to describe these complex congenital malformations. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Atresia with double fistula:</strong> This is type B, which is extremely rare (only about 1% of cases) and involves both proximal and distal fistulas. - <strong>Atresia with proximal fistula:</strong> This is type A, which accounts for about 7% of cases and involves a blind-ending lower esophagus with no distal connection. - <strong>Atresia with H shaped fistula:</strong> This is type E, which is the rarest form (about 4% of cases) and involves a complete esophagus with an H-shaped fistula between the trachea and esophagus. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The classic presentation of TEF is excessive oral secretions, inability to pass a feeding tube, and polyhydramnios in utero. - Associated anomalies are common, particularly in VACTERL association (vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, limb). - Prenatal ultrasound findings include a small or absent stomach bubble and polyhydramnios. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Type C (atresia with distal fistula) is by far the most common tracheoesophageal anomaly, accounting for approximately 85% of cases.</p>','<p>TEF types: ''C is Common'' (atresia with distal fistula), ''A is Atypical'' (blind ending), ''B is Barely seen'' (double fistula), ''E is Extremely rare'' (H-shaped).</p>','<p>The inability to pass a feeding tube into the stomach is a classic clinical sign of esophageal atresia with or without tracheoesophageal fistula.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Atresia with double fistula:</strong> This is extremely rare (type B, only 1% of cases). - <strong>Atresia with proximal fistula:</strong> This is less common (type A, about 7% of cases). - <strong>Atresia with H shaped fistula:</strong> This is the rarest form (type E, about 4% of cases).</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Tracheoesophageal Fistula','["tracheoesophageal","fistula","atresia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.141Z','2026-10-19T03:43:06.807Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbu0027o7lr83uir5uw','mcq','9- In duodenal atresia which of these signs is not considered in diagnosis:','["Double bubble sign","Bilious vomiting","Abdominal distension","Prenatal polyhydramnios"]','Bilious vomiting','<p><strong>Why bilious vomiting is correct:</strong> Bilious vomiting is not considered in the diagnosis of duodenal atresia because duodenal atresia is an obstruction proximal to the ampulla of Vater, meaning bile cannot enter the duodenum to be vomited up. The classic presentation of duodenal atresia is non-bilious vomiting in the first 24-48 hours of life, often described as "projectile" vomiting. The "double bubble" sign on abdominal X-ray is pathognomonic, representing dilated stomach and duodenum with no distal gas.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Double bubble sign:</strong> This is the classic radiographic finding in duodenal atresia, resulting from obstruction at the level of the duodenum with no distal gas passage.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal distension:</strong> This is commonly seen in duodenal atresia due to the proximal intestinal obstruction causing air and fluid to accumulate.</li>
<li><strong>Prenatal polyhydramnios:</strong> This is frequently associated with duodenal atresia because the obstruction prevents normal swallowing and absorption of amniotic fluid by the fetus.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in about 30% of cases.</li>
<li>The "double bubble" sign is diagnostic and results from the stomach and proximal duodenum being dilated with air, while the distal bowel is gasless.</li>
<li>Bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency and suggests obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Duodenal atresia presents with non-bilious vomiting, bilious vomiting indicates obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater.</p>','<p>Double bubble = duodenal atresia (no bile), triple bubble = jejunal atresia</p>','<p>Bilious vomiting in a newborn is always considered surgical until proven otherwise, as it suggests obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Double bubble sign:</strong> Pathognomonic radiographic finding in duodenal atresia.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal distension:</strong> Common due to proximal intestinal obstruction.</li>
<li><strong>Prenatal polyhydramnios:</strong> Frequently associated due to impaired fetal swallowing.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Duodenal Atresia','["duodenal","atresia","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.146Z','2026-10-19T03:43:20.312Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbx002co7lrvo6ja4c6','mcq','2- Child with lead poisoning and lead level is 50 microgram. What you should do','["Evaluate source and education","IV EDTA","oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid"]','Evaluate source and education','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> For a child with lead poisoning at 50 μg/dL, the initial management focuses on identifying and eliminating the source of exposure while providing education to prevent further exposure. At this level, chelation therapy is not indicated as the toxicity is moderate and environmental intervention can effectively reduce lead levels. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends source identification and family education as first-line management for blood lead levels between 45-69 μg/dL.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>B. IV EDTA:</strong> Intravenous EDTA is reserved for severe lead poisoning (typically &gt;70 μg/dL) or symptomatic patients, not for moderate levels like 50 μg/dL.</li>
<li><strong>C. oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> While DMSA is a chelating agent, it''s typically reserved for lead levels &gt;45 μg/dL with symptoms or levels &gt;70 μg/dL, but source evaluation should always precede chelation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lead levels 45-69 μg/dL require environmental intervention and family education before considering chelation.</li>
<li>Chelation therapy is not without risks and should only be used when clearly indicated.</li>
<li>The most common sources of lead poisoning in children are lead-based paint, contaminated soil, and some imported toys or jewelry.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For moderate lead poisoning (45-69 μg/dL), always first identify and eliminate the source of exposure before considering chelation therapy.</p>','<p>Source First: For lead levels 45-69, Source evaluation First before chelation.</p>','<p>Lead poisoning is entirely preventable - the most critical intervention is identifying and removing the source of exposure, which is more effective than chelation for moderate levels.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B. IV EDTA:</strong> Reserved for severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL), not moderate levels.</li>
<li><strong>C. oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> Chelation is not first-line for moderate lead poisoning without source evaluation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Lead Poisoning','["lead","poisoning","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.149Z','2026-10-19T03:43:38.898Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krc1002io7lr2mf5vhh7','mcq','8- Which Folic is correct about Folic acid','["High in goat milk","Presentation at early months","All deficiency of folic acid are acquired"]','Presentation at early months','<p><strong>Why Presentation at early months is correct:</strong> Folic acid deficiency typically presents within the first few months of life in infants who are exclusively breastfed by mothers with poor folate intake or malabsorption, as breast milk has lower folate concentrations than formula. The rapid growth and cell division occurring during early infancy increase folate demand, making deficiency manifest quickly when supply is insufficient. This presentation is particularly concerning because folate is crucial for DNA synthesis and neural tube development, with deficiency potentially leading to megaloblastic anemia and developmental issues.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High in goat milk:</strong> Goat milk actually contains lower folate concentrations than cow''s milk or human breast milk, making it a poor source rather than a high one.</li>
<li><strong>All deficiency of folic acid are acquired:</strong> While most folate deficiencies are acquired due to poor diet, malabsorption, or increased demand, rare genetic disorders affecting folate metabolism can cause inherited deficiencies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Folate deficiency in exclusively breastfed infants typically presents between 2-6 months of age.</li>
<li>The classic triad of folate deficiency includes megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, and gastrointestinal symptoms.</li>
<li>Neural tube defects are strongly associated with maternal folate deficiency during pregnancy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Folic acid deficiency in exclusively breastfed infants presents in early months due to rapid growth and insufficient folate transfer through breast milk.</p>','<p>Folate deficiency in infants: ''Early months, empty shelves'' (referring to early presentation and nutritional causes)</p>','<p>Folate deficiency in exclusively breastfed infants is preventable through maternal supplementation during pregnancy and lactation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>High in goat milk:</strong> Goat milk actually has lower folate concentrations than cow''s milk or human breast milk.</li>
<li><strong>All deficiency of folic acid are acquired:</strong> Some rare genetic disorders can cause inherited folate deficiencies.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Folic Acid','["folic","acid","deficiency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.153Z','2026-10-19T03:43:56.419Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbw002bo7lrncy0t9cq','mcq','1- What is responsible for RBCs production intrauterine','["Yolk sac","Liver","Bone marrow"]','Yolk sac','<p><strong>Why Yolk sac is correct:</strong> The yolk sac is responsible for red blood cell (RBC) production during intrauterine life, specifically during the embryonic period (weeks 3-8). This occurs before the liver takes over as the primary site of hematopoiesis. The yolk sac contains mesenchymal cells that differentiate into blood islands, which give rise to primitive erythroblasts that produce the first RBCs. These early RBCs are nucleated and contain fetal hemoglobin (HbF), which has higher oxygen affinity than adult hemoglobin.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Liver:</strong> The liver becomes the primary site of hematopoiesis after the yolk sac, starting around week 6 and continuing until birth, but it is not responsible for the initial RBC production.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Bone marrow becomes the main site of hematopoiesis during the third trimester of gestation and continues throughout postnatal life, but it is not responsible for intrauterine RBC production during early embryonic development.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The yolk sac is the first site of hematopoiesis, producing primitive RBCs during weeks 3-8 of gestation.</li>
<li>The liver takes over as the primary hematopoietic organ from weeks 6-8 until birth.</li>
<li>Bone marrow becomes the main site of hematopoiesis during the third trimester and continues throughout postnatal life.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The yolk sac is responsible for the initial production of red blood cells during early embryonic development.</p>','<p>Yolk sac comes first for blood cells - remember "Y" for "Yolk" and "first".</p>','<p>The sequential shift of hematopoiesis from yolk sac to liver to bone marrow mirrors the increasing oxygen demands of the developing fetus.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Liver:</strong> Becomes the primary site of hematopoiesis after the yolk sac, starting around week 6 of gestation.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Becomes the main site of hematopoiesis during the third trimester of gestation, not during early intrauterine development.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Erythropoiesis','["rbc","production","fetal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.148Z','2026-10-19T03:43:23.819Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krc0002ho7lrclmpw4mm','mcq','7- A child presented with pallor, glossitis, Neuropathy','["Vit B12 deficiency anemia"]','Vit B12 deficiency anemia','<p><strong>Why Vit B12 deficiency anemia is correct:</strong> Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis, resulting in pallor. Glossitis (specifically "beefy red" tongue) is a classic manifestation due to impaired epithelial cell turnover. The neuropathy in B12 deficiency is specifically subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, causing dorsal column (vibration, position sense) and lateral corticospinal tract (weakness, spasticity) dysfunction due to demyelination. <strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While other deficiencies can cause anemia and glossitis, they typically don''t cause the characteristic neuropathy seen in B12 deficiency. Iron deficiency causes microcytic anemia with koilonychia and pica, folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia without neuropathy, and niacin deficiency causes pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia (the  D''s) but not the specific neuropathy of B12 deficiency. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - B12 deficiency requires both hematologic (megaloblastic anemia) and neurologic (subacute combined degeneration) manifestations for classic presentation. - The neuropathy in B12 deficiency is irreversible if treatment is delayed, making early recognition critical. - B12 deficiency can be caused by dietary deficiency, pernicious anemia (autoimmune), or malabsorption (Crohn''s, gastric surgery). <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The triad of megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, and neuropathy is pathognomonic for vitamin B12 deficiency.</p>','<p>B12 = Blood (anemia), Brain (neuropathy), Buccal (glossitis)</p>','<p>Neurologic symptoms in B12 deficiency can precede hematologic changes, so don''t wait for anemia to start treatment if clinical suspicion is high.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','B12 Deficiency','["b12","deficiency","anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.152Z','2026-10-19T03:43:39.677Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krc2002ko7lrkenqdbdj','mcq','0- Mean definition of anemia is reduction of Hb concentration, what is correct about HB molecule?','["Its type is determined according to the heme group.","Contains iron in the ferrous form.","Hypoxia decrease it.","Contains four similar poly-peptide chains."]','Contains four similar poly-peptide chains.','<p><strong>Why Contains four similar poly-peptide chains is correct:</strong> The hemoglobin molecule is a tetrameric protein consisting of four polypeptide chains - two alpha and two beta chains in normal adult hemoglobin (HbA). Each chain contains a heme group with an iron atom at its center, which binds oxygen. This quaternary structure is essential for hemoglobin''s cooperative oxygen binding and release properties, allowing efficient oxygen transport in the blood.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Its type is determined according to the heme group:</strong> Hemoglobin types are determined by the specific amino acid sequences of the globin chains, not the heme group itself.</li>
<li><strong>Contains iron in the ferrous form:</strong> While hemoglobin does contain iron, it''s specifically in the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state, not just ''iron'' in general.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoxia decrease it:</strong> Hypoxia actually increases hemoglobin production through erythropoietin stimulation, not decreases it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Normal adult hemoglobin (HbA) consists of two alpha and two beta globin chains.</li>
<li>The heme group contains iron in the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state, which binds oxygen.</li>
<li>Hemoglobin variants result from mutations in the globin genes, affecting the polypeptide chains.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemoglobin''s structure is defined by its four polypeptide chains (two alpha, two beta), not its heme group or iron content.</p>','<p>Hemoglobin structure: ''Two alpha, two beta'' (referring to the four polypeptide chains)</p>','<p>The structure of hemoglobin allows for cooperative oxygen binding, making it more efficient than if each subunit bound oxygen independently.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Its type is determined according to the heme group:</strong> Hemoglobin types are determined by the globin chains, not the heme group.</li>
<li><strong>Contains iron in the ferrous form:</strong> While true, this is not the defining structural characteristic.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoxia decrease it:</strong> Hypoxia actually increases hemoglobin production via erythropoietin.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hemoglobin','["hemoglobin","molecule","structure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.154Z','2026-10-19T03:43:56.420Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krc2002jo7lr32bbrrau','mcq','9- Male patient 5 years old presented with pancytopenia, café-au-lait spots, short stature and absent thumb, what is the most likely diagnosis?','["Dyskeratosis congenital","Fanconi anemia.","Blackfan-diamond.","Acquired pancytopenia."]','Fanconi anemia.','<p><strong>Why Fanconi anemia is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is a rare genetic disorder characterized by bone marrow failure (presenting as pancytopenia), congenital anomalies including skeletal abnormalities (such as absent or malformed thumbs), and characteristic café-au-lait spots. The condition results from mutations in DNA repair genes, leading to chromosomal instability and the classic clinical triad of bone marrow failure, congenital malformations, and increased cancer risk. The presentation in a young male with these specific findings is highly suggestive of Fanconi anemia.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> This condition presents with the classic triad of skin pigmentation (reticulated hyperpigmentation), nail dystrophy, and leukoplakia, along with bone marrow failure, but not typically with absent thumbs.</li>
<li><strong>Blackfan-diamond:</strong> This is a pure red cell aplasia presenting in infancy with macrocytic anemia without the other cytopenias or congenital anomalies seen in this case.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired pancytopenia:</strong> While pancytopenia can be acquired, the combination with congenital anomalies like café-au-lait spots and absent thumb points to a genetic syndrome rather than an acquired condition.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fanconi anemia is characterized by bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies (especially skeletal), and café-au-lait spots.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by chromosomal breakage testing after exposure to DNA cross-linking agents.</li>
<li>Fanconi anemia has autosomal recessive inheritance with increased risk of malignancy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is the likely diagnosis in a child with pancytopenia, café-au-lait spots, and skeletal abnormalities like absent thumbs.</p>','<p>Fanconi features: ''Pancytopenia, spots, and thumbs'' (referring to the key diagnostic triad)</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia should be suspected in any child with unexplained pancytopenia and congenital anomalies, as early diagnosis is crucial for managing complications and considering hematopoietic stem cell transplantation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenital:</strong> Presents with skin pigmentation, nail dystrophy, and leukoplakia, not typically absent thumbs.</li>
<li><strong>Blackfan-diamond:</strong> Pure red cell aplasia without other cytopenias or congenital anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired pancytopenia:</strong> The congenital anomalies point to a genetic syndrome rather than acquired causes.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Hematology','Anemias','["fanconi","pancytopenia","cafe-au-lait"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.154Z','2026-10-19T03:43:56.421Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvexciks0009hvuchh7k0qtj','mcq','A 72-year-old female with a history of hypertension and coronary artery disease is being evaluated for worsening renal function. Her medications include lisinopril 40 mg daily, aspirin 81 mg daily, and atorvastatin 20 mg daily. Recent laboratory results show: Serum creatinine 2.8 mg/dL (baseline 1.2 mg/dL), BUN 45 mg/dL, potassium 5.2 mEq/L, and blood pressure 142/88 mmHg. Renal ultrasound reveals bilateral renal artery stenosis. What is the most likely explanation for her acute kidney injury?','["Progression of chronic kidney disease due to diabetes","ACE inhibitor-induced hyperkalemia causing renal vasoconstriction","ACE inhibitor-induced acute interstitial nephritis","ACE inhibitor-induced bilateral renal artery stenosis","ACE inhibitor-induced hemodynamic compromise in bilateral renal artery stenosis"]','ACE inhibitor-induced hemodynamic compromise in bilateral renal artery stenosis','The patient''s acute kidney injury is most likely due to ACE inhibitor-induced hemodynamic compromise in the setting of bilateral renal artery stenosis. ACE inhibitors reduce glomerular filtration pressure by dilating the efferent arterioles more than the afferent arterioles, which is beneficial in most patients. However, in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis, this can lead to a dramatic decrease in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and acute kidney injury. The mechanism involves reduced perfusion pressure to the ischemic kidneys. This is a well-established contraindication to ACE inhibitor use. The other options are less likely: diabetes progression would typically be more gradual, hyperkalemia doesn''t directly cause renal vasoconstriction, acute interstitial nephritis would typically present with more systemic symptoms, and ACE inhibitors don''t cause renal artery stenosis.','ACE + bilateral RAS = AKI','Bilateral renal artery stenosis is an absolute contraindication to ACE inhibitor use due to the risk of acute renal failure. Always assess for renal artery stenosis (via history, bruits, or imaging) before starting ACE inhibitors in patients with unexplained renal impairment or known vascular disease.','Option A (diabetes progression) is less likely given the acute nature of the renal injury. Option B (hyperkalemia) is a side effect but not the primary cause of AKI in this scenario. Option C (interstitial nephritis) would typically present with fever, rash, and eosinophilia. Option D (causing stenosis) is incorrect as ACE inhibitors don''t cause structural changes in renal arteries.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","contraindications","renal effects","pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-19T07:20:00.268Z','2026-10-19T07:20:00.268Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvexc9m10008hvucji1yhyun','mcq','A 65-year-old male with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 45 mL/min) presents to clinic. His current medications include lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg twice daily, and atorvastatin 40 mg daily. He reports a persistent dry cough for the past 3 months that is bothersome but not associated with fever or sputum production. His blood pressure is well-controlled at 128/78 mmHg. What is the most appropriate next step in management?','["Add an antitussive medication to control the cough","Switch from lisinopril to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB)","Increase the lisinopril dose to 40 mg daily","Discontinue lisinopril and start a calcium channel blocker","Add a low-dose diuretic to enhance lisinopril''s antihypertensive effect"]','Switch from lisinopril to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB)','The patient''s persistent dry cough is highly suggestive of an ACE inhibitor-induced cough, which occurs in 5-35% of patients taking ACE inhibitors. This side effect is thought to be caused by the accumulation of bradykinin and substance P due to ACE inhibition, which irritates airway C-fibers. ARBs do not inhibit ACE and therefore do not cause this side effect, making them the preferred alternative. The patient''s chronic kidney disease does not preclude the use of ARBs, and they provide similar cardiovascular and renal protection as ACE inhibitors. Adding an antitussive would only mask the symptom without addressing the underlying cause. Increasing the dose would likely worsen the cough, and discontinuing without replacement would risk blood pressure control and renal protection.','ACE cough → ARB choice','ACE inhibitor-induced cough typically develops within weeks to months of starting therapy and is more common in women, nonsmokers, and patients with a history of atopy. The cough is dry, persistent, and non-productive.','Option A (antitussive) addresses symptoms but not the underlying cause. Option C (increasing dose) would likely worsen the cough. Option D (discontinuing without replacement) risks losing cardioprotective and renoprotective benefits. Option E (adding diuretic) doesn''t address the cough mechanism and may add unnecessary complexity.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","side effects","clinical management","pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-19T07:19:48.649Z','2026-10-19T07:20:21.358Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvexeksa000ehvucl28ds8ni','mcq','A 72-year-old patient with heart failure, type 2 diabetes, and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 45 mL/min) is prescribed an ACE inhibitor. Which of the following side effects is most likely to occur and requires careful monitoring in this patient?','["Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypocalcemia"]','B','ACE inhibitors can cause hyperkalemia through multiple mechanisms: 1) Reduced aldosterone secretion decreases potassium excretion, 2) Reduced angiotensin II-mediated aldosterone production, and 3) Potential renal impairment in patients with pre-existing kidney disease. This patient has multiple risk factors for hyperkalemia: diabetes, chronic kidney disease (eGFR 45 mL/min), and heart failure. Hyperkalemia is particularly concerning as it can lead to life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias. The other options are less likely: hypoglycemia is not a typical side effect of ACE inhibitors, hypokalemia is more associated with thiazide diuretics, and hyponatremia/hypocalcemia are not common adverse effects of ACE inhibitors.','ACE = Aldosterone Constriction Elimination → Potassium Accumulation','In patients with diabetes, CKD, or heart failure on ACE inhibitors, monitor serum potassium closely and consider starting with lower doses, especially if they also take potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplements, or NSAIDs.','Option A is incorrect as ACE inhibitors do not typically cause hypoglycemia. Option C is incorrect as ACE inhibitors tend to increase, not decrease, potassium levels. Options D and E are incorrect as hyponatremia and hypocalcemia are not common side effects of ACE inhibitors.','Medium','general','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","side effects","heart failure","diabetes","chronic kidney disease"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-19T07:21:36.442Z','2026-10-19T07:21:36.442Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvexe5j3000dhvuc2j1trara','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus is started on lisinopril. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the therapeutic effects of ACE inhibitors in this patient?','["Direct blockade of angiotensin type 1 (AT1) receptors","Inhibition of aldosterone secretion from the adrenal glands","Decreased breakdown of bradykinin leading to vasodilation","Increased renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus","Direct inhibition of sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule"]','C','ACE inhibitors primarily work by blocking the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, which reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion. However, they also inhibit the breakdown of bradykinin, leading to increased bradykinin levels. This bradykinin accumulation contributes to vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandin release, which is particularly beneficial in patients with hypertension and diabetes. The other options describe mechanisms of different drug classes: A describes ARBs, B describes spironolactone, D describes the mechanism of ACE inhibitors that can paradoxically increase renin, and E describes the mechanism of thiazide diuretics.','ACE = Angiotensin Converting Enzyme + Bradykinin Enhancement','The bradykinin-mediated effects of ACE inhibitors contribute to both their therapeutic benefits (vasodilation, improved endothelial function) and side effects (dry cough, angioedema).','Option A is incorrect as it describes ARBs, not ACE inhibitors. Option B describes aldosterone antagonists like spironolactone. Option D is partially correct but incomplete as it only describes the renin-angiotensin system effect without mentioning bradykinin. Option E describes thiazide diuretics, not ACE inhibitors.','Medium','general','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","mechanism","hypertension","diabetes"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-19T07:21:16.671Z','2026-10-19T07:22:00.792Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvfhobyo0005nxucfq8tnhi8','mcq','A 35-year-old male presents with sudden onset pleuritic chest pain and shortness of breath after a vigorous coughing episode. The chest X-ray shown demonstrates which of the following key findings diagnostic of pneumothorax?','["Increased bronchovascular markings in the lower lung fields","A lucent area with absent lung markings and a visible visceral pleural line","Homogeneous opacification of the hemithorax","Elevated hemidiaphragm with blunted costophrenic angle"]','A lucent area with absent lung markings and a visible visceral pleural line','The chest X-ray shows a classic pneumothorax with a lucent (black) area representing free air in the pleural space, absence of lung markings in this area, and a visible visceral pleural line (white line) representing the edge of the collapsed lung. This is the pathognomonic finding for pneumothorax. The other options describe different conditions: increased bronchovascular markings suggest pneumonia or pulmonary edema, homogeneous opacification suggests pleural effusion or atelectasis, and elevated hemidiaphragm with blunted costophrenic angle suggests pleural effusion.','PLEURAL - Pneumothorax: Lucent area, Empty lung markings, Visceral pleural line, Reduced lung volume, Absent vascular markings, Air in pleural space','In a tension pneumothorax, mediastinal shift may also be present, but the key diagnostic finding remains the lucent area with visible pleural line. Small pneumothoraces may require expiratory films or CT for detection.','Option 1 describes findings seen in pneumonia or pulmonary edema. Option 2 describes pleural effusion. Option 3 describes diaphragmatic paralysis or eventration. All are important differential diagnoses but not specific for pneumothorax.','Medium','Respiratory','Pneumothorax','["pneumothorax diagnosis on chest X-ray","Respiratory","deep-agent","Pneumothorax","chest X-ray","pneumothorax","diagnosis","pleural disease"]',true,'Chest X-ray PA view showing a right-sided pneumothorax with clear anatomical labels and leader lines pointing to: 1) The visceral pleural line (white line representing the collapsed lung edge), 2) The lucent (black) area outside the lung representing the pneumothorax, 3) The compressed lung tissue, 4) The costophrenic angle. Professional medical illustration style with proper X-ray appearance.','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-bde37b97-679.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-19T16:49:03.888Z','2026-10-19T16:49:03.888Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvfl92ko0007nxucq3r4iwh9','mcq','A 58-year-old male with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus is started on lisinopril. Two weeks later, he develops a persistent dry cough that is bothersome but not associated with fever or respiratory symptoms. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this adverse effect?','["Direct irritation of the respiratory tract by the drug","Increased production of substance P in the airways","Inhibition of angiotensin II formation leading to bronchoconstriction","Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition","Allergic reaction to the sulfhydryl group in the drug structure"]','Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition','The dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is a well-known adverse effect that occurs in approximately 5-35% of patients. The mechanism involves ACE inhibition leading to reduced degradation of bradykinin, a potent inflammatory mediator. Accumulated bradykinin stimulates sensory nerve endings in the airways, causing a reflex cough response. This effect is not dose-dependent and typically resolves within 1-4 weeks after discontinuing the ACE inhibitor. The cough is not associated with bronchoconstriction (which would actually be more characteristic of angiotensin II receptor blockers), nor is it typically an allergic reaction. The mechanism is distinct from direct irritation or substance P involvement.','ACE = Accumulate Bradykinin, Cough Effect','ACE inhibitor cough is mediated by bradykinin accumulation and is not dose-dependent. Switching to an ARB is often the best management strategy.','Option A (direct irritation) is incorrect as ACE inhibitors don''t directly irritate the respiratory tract. Option B (substance P) is not the primary mediator. Option C (bronchoconstriction) is incorrect as ACE inhibitors don''t typically cause bronchoconstriction. Option E (allergic reaction) is incorrect as this is not an IgE-mediated allergic response.','Medium','general','pharmacology','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","pharmacology","ACE inhibitors","adverse effects","bradykinin","cough"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-19T18:29:10.344Z','2026-10-19T18:30:40.196Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicdoov000roku8txdcum5j','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG is shown below. What is the most appropriate immediate next step in management?

![ECG showing STEMI with ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF](/generated/agent-871439ef-9c5.jpg)','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and monitor for response","Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI","Start heparin infusion and delay angiography for 24 hours","Administer aspirin and arrange for thrombolytic therapy","Obtain serial cardiac enzymes and reassess in 1 hour"]','Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI','This ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, indicating an inferior STEMI. According to current guidelines for STEMI, the preferred reperfusion strategy is primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) if it can be performed within 120 minutes of first medical contact. Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI is the most appropriate next step. While aspirin should be administered, the timing and urgency of PCI takes precedence over other options.','"Time is muscle" - PCI within 120 minutes for STEMI','Inferior STEMI may be associated with right ventricular involvement and bradycardia, requiring additional monitoring','Nitroglycerin alone is insufficient for STEMI; heparin delay misses the critical time window; thrombolytics are second-line to PCI when available','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF (inferior STEMI)','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-871439ef-9c5.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:44:07.615Z','2026-10-21T16:44:07.615Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvich3ne000yoku8a1kwvugd','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of severe crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. He appears diaphoretic and anxious. His ECG is shown below. What is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient?','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and monitor vital signs","Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI","Start heparin infusion and delay further intervention","Administer aspirin and arrange for stress testing","Give morphine and observe for symptom improvement"]','Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI','This patient is experiencing an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) as evidenced by the characteristic ST segment elevation on ECG. The standard of care for STEMI is immediate coronary angiography with primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) within 90 minutes of first medical contact. This approach has been shown to significantly reduce mortality and myocardial damage compared to fibrinolytic therapy. Aspirin should be given immediately, but the definitive treatment is coronary angiography with PCI.','"Door-to-balloon time" - aim for <90 minutes for PCI in STEMI','Time is muscle - every 30 minutes of delay in reperfusion increases mortality by 7-15%','Nitroglycerin alone is insufficient; heparin delays definitive treatment; stress testing is inappropriate in acute STEMI; morphine provides symptom relief but doesn''t address the underlying ischemia','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","emergency medicine","cardiology","ECG","myocardial infarction"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) with characteristic ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, showing inferior wall MI pattern','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-3bed1090-7d3.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:46:46.971Z','2026-10-21T16:46:46.971Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicilpl000zoku8ej00soke','mcq','A 58-year-old female with a history of hypertension and smoking presents with 2 hours of severe chest pain. Her coronary angiogram is shown below. Which of the following statements about the pathophysiology of this condition is most accurate?','["The occlusion is typically caused by vasospasm without plaque rupture","Thrombus formation occurs due to plaque rupture and platelet activation","The occlusion is usually due to progressive luminal narrowing without acute thrombus","This condition is most commonly caused by coronary vasculitis","The primary mechanism is embolic occlusion from left atrial thrombus"]','Thrombus formation occurs due to plaque rupture and platelet activation','STEMI is most commonly caused by acute coronary syndrome where atherosclerotic plaque ruptures, exposing thrombogenic material to the bloodstream. This triggers platelet activation, aggregation, and fibrin formation, leading to thrombus formation and complete coronary artery occlusion. The angiogram shows complete occlusion with thrombus, which is the hallmark of STEMI. Vasospasm can cause angina but typically not complete occlusion; progressive narrowing causes stable angina; vasculitis is rare; embolic occlusion is uncommon.','"Plaque rupture → platelet activation → thrombus formation → occlusion"','STEMI is a time-dependent emergency - the longer the occlusion persists, the more myocardium is lost','Vasospasm causes transient occlusion but not STEMI; progressive narrowing causes stable angina; coronary vasculitis is rare; embolic occlusion is uncommon and typically affects multiple vessels','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI pathophysiology','["STEMI pathophysiology","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","pathophysiology","coronary artery disease","thrombosis","plaque rupture"]',true,'Coronary angiogram showing complete occlusion of the right coronary artery with thrombus formation, typical of STEMI','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-36c1e476-bcf.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:47:57.033Z','2026-10-21T16:47:57.033Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicjvrq0011oku87pwsrb5u','mcq','A 55-year-old male presents with substernal chest pain and the ECG shown below. Which of the following complications is this patient at highest risk for given the location of the STEMI?','["Complete heart block requiring pacemaker","Ventricular septal rupture","Right ventricular infarction","Papillary muscle rupture with mitral regurgitation","Pericarditis"]','Ventricular septal rupture','The ECG shows anterior STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Anterior wall MI carries the highest risk of mechanical complications, particularly ventricular septal rupture (VSR), which occurs in 1-2% of anterior MIs. VSR is a surgical emergency with high mortality. Complete heart block is more common with inferior MI; right ventricular infarction typically occurs with inferior MI; papillary muscle rupture can occur with anterior MI but is less common than VSR; pericarditis is a later complication.','"Anterior MI → high risk of VSR (ventricular septal rupture)"','Anterior STEMI has the highest mortality of all MI locations due to larger myocardial territory at risk','Complete heart block is more common with inferior MI; right ventricular infarction typically occurs with inferior MI; papillary muscle rupture can occur but is less common than VSR; pericarditis is a later inflammatory complication','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI complications','["STEMI complications","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","complications","anterior MI","mechanical complications"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST elevation in V1-V4, reciprocal ST depression in inferior leads','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-27b5dde7-316.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:48:56.726Z','2026-10-21T16:48:56.726Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicmoen0014oku8s9axuu0t','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG is shown below. What is the most appropriate immediate next step in management?

![STEMI ECG showing ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF](/generated/agent-0bb935d8-8c4.jpg)

The ECG demonstrates ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, consistent with an inferior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and schedule for elective cardiac catheterization in 24 hours","Initiate fibrinolytic therapy and transfer to a PCI-capable facility","Immediate primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)","Obtain serial cardiac enzymes and monitor for 6 hours before deciding on intervention","Start aspirin and heparin, then discharge home with follow-up cardiology appointment"]','Immediate primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)','The patient is presenting with a clear STEMI (ST-elevation myocardial infarction) on ECG with characteristic ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, indicating an inferior wall MI. According to current guidelines, the standard of care for STEMI is immediate primary PCI within 90 minutes of first medical contact. This is superior to fibrinolytic therapy when available, as it provides more complete reperfusion and better outcomes. The other options are incorrect because: nitroglycerin alone is insufficient, fibrinolytic therapy is second-line when PCI is available, serial enzymes delay treatment, and discharge is inappropriate for STEMI.','"PCI first for STEMI" - Primary Percutaneous Coronary Intervention is the gold standard for STEMI management','Time is muscle - door-to-balloon time should be <90 minutes for primary PCI in STEMI','Option 1 is incorrect because nitroglycerin alone does not address the underlying coronary occlusion. Option 2 is incorrect because fibrinolytic therapy is inferior to PCI when available. Option 4 is incorrect because serial enzyme testing delays life-saving intervention. Option 5 is incorrect because STEMI requires immediate intervention, not discharge.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","STEMI","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction with ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-0bb935d8-8c4.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:51:07.151Z','2026-10-21T16:51:07.151Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvico6c70016oku81mgxlpd3','mcq','A 65-year-old female with a history of hypertension and smoking presents with severe anterior chest pain and diaphoresis. Her ECG is shown below. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?

![Anterior STEMI ECG showing ST elevation in leads V1-V6](/generated/agent-1cac0b88-729.jpg)

The ECG demonstrates ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V6, consistent with an extensive anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction.','["Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Right marginal artery","Posterior descending artery (PDA)"]','Left anterior descending artery (LAD)','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V6, which is characteristic of an anterior wall STEMI. The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, including the septum and anterior wall. The LAD is often called the "widow maker" because occlusion can lead to extensive anterior wall infarction and high mortality. The RCA typically supplies inferior wall (leads II, III, aVF), LCx supplies lateral wall (leads I, aVL, V5-V6), and the PDA supplies inferior wall.','"V1-V6 = LAD" - ST elevation in anterior leads points to LAD occlusion','LAD occlusion carries the highest mortality among coronary artery occlusions due to the large territory supplied','Option 1 is incorrect because RCA occlusion typically causes inferior STEMI (leads II, III, aVF). Option 2 is incorrect because LCx occlusion typically causes lateral STEMI (leads I, aVL, V5-V6). Option 4 is incorrect because the right marginal artery is a small branch of the RCA. Option 5 is incorrect because PDA occlusion typically causes inferior STEMI.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","STEMI","ECG","Coronary Anatomy","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing anterior wall STEMI with ST segment elevation in leads V1-V6','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-1cac0b88-729.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:52:17.047Z','2026-10-21T16:52:17.047Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvico7td0017oku8mm8oyaoa','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. The coronary angiography reveals a 100% occlusion of the right coronary artery. Which of the following complications is this patient MOST at risk for developing?','["Left ventricular aneurysm","Ventricular septal rupture","Right ventricular infarction","Papillary muscle rupture","Free wall rupture"]','Right ventricular infarction','Right coronary artery occlusion typically supplies the right ventricle, particularly in its proximal segments. ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates an inferior wall MI, which often involves the right ventricle when the proximal RCA is occluded. Right ventricular infarction occurs in approximately 30% of inferior STEMI cases and is characterized by hypotension, elevated JVP, and clear lungs (the ''hypotension-clear lungs'' triad). Left ventricular aneurysm typically occurs with anterior MIs, ventricular septal rupture is more common with anterior MIs, papillary muscle rupture typically affects the posterior papillary muscle with inferior MIs, and free wall rupture is a catastrophic complication that can occur with any STEMI but is more common with anterior MIs.','Inferior STEMI with RCA occlusion = Right ventricle at risk (IVC territory)','Always obtain a right-sided ECG (V4R) in patients with inferior STEMI to assess for right ventricular involvement, as this significantly impacts management (avoid nitrates, consider fluids).','Left ventricular aneurysm: More common with anterior MIs due to larger territory of left anterior descending artery. Ventricular septal rupture: Typically occurs 3-5 days after anterior MI due to necrosis of interventricular septum. Papillary muscle rupture: Can occur with inferior MIs but affects mitral valve function rather than right ventricle. Free wall rupture: Most common with first anterior MI, typically within 24 hours.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","STEMI","right coronary artery","right ventricular infarction","ECG"]',true,'ECG showing ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with coronary angiogram showing right coronary artery occlusion','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-b334d587-b8b.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:52:18.961Z','2026-10-21T16:52:18.961Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicofq80018oku8jj9xudme','mcq','A 62-year-old female with a history of hypertension and diabetes presents with 2 hours of severe chest pain. Her ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Cardiac catheterization reveals a 95% stenosis in the proximal left anterior descending artery. Which of the following statements regarding primary PCI for this patient is MOST accurate?','["Primary PCI should be delayed for 6 hours to allow for full medical therapy optimization","The door-to-balloon time should be less than 90 minutes regardless of presentation time","Intracoronary verapamil should be administered before stent placement to prevent no-reflow phenomenon","Bivalirudin is preferred over unfractionated heparin for anticoagulation during primary PCI","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors should be routinely administered before PCI"]','The door-to-balloon time should be less than 90 minutes regardless of presentation time','For patients with STEMI, the American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association (ACC/AHA) guidelines recommend a door-to-balloon time of less than 90 minutes for patients presenting to a PCI-capable hospital. This time target is critical for myocardial salvage and should be achieved regardless of the time from symptom onset. While medical therapy is important, it should not delay primary PCI. Intracoronary verapamil is not routinely recommended; instead, intracoronary nitroglycerin or adenosine may be used for no-reflow. Bivalirudin may be considered in patients at high bleeding risk, but unfractionated heparin remains standard. Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors are not routinely recommended but may be considered in high-risk patients or those with thrombotic complications.','STEMI PCI: 90 minutes door-to-balloon is the golden standard','Time is myocardium - every 30 minutes of delay in primary PCI results in approximately 7-8% additional myocardial death. The 90-minute door-to-balloon target applies to all STEMI patients presenting to PCI-capable hospitals.','Delaying PCI for 6 hours: Contradicts the ''time is muscle'' principle; primary PCI should be performed as soon as possible. Intracoronary verapamil: Not standard; nitroglycerin or adenosine are preferred for no-reflow. Bivalirudin preferred: While it may be used in high-risk patients, heparin remains standard. Routine GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors: Not recommended as routine therapy due to bleeding risk.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","STEMI","primary PCI","door-to-balloon","LAD occlusion"]',true,'ECG showing anterior STEMI with reciprocal changes and coronary angiogram showing LAD occlusion','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-403887fc-488.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:52:29.216Z','2026-10-21T16:52:29.216Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicomwc0019oku8uvyc7d4t','mcq','A 65-year-old male with diabetes, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia presents with 3 hours of severe chest pain. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V6 with new left bundle branch block. Emergency coronary angiography reveals a 100% occlusion of the left main coronary artery. Which of the following statements regarding this patient''s condition is MOST accurate?','["This patient should receive fibrinolytic therapy as primary reperfusion strategy","The mortality rate for left main occlusion is approximately 30% at 30 days without intervention","Percutaneous coronary intervention is contraindicated due to the high risk of no-reflow phenomenon","Cardiogenic shock is unlikely to develop in this scenario","Beta-blockers should be administered immediately to reduce myocardial oxygen demand"]','The mortality rate for left main occlusion is approximately 30% at 30 days without intervention','Left main coronary artery occlusion is a catastrophic event with extremely high mortality rates, estimated at 30% at 30 days without intervention. This is because the left main artery supplies approximately 75% of the left ventricle. Fibrinolytic therapy is contraindicated in left main occlusion due to the high risk of fatal complications. PCI is the preferred treatment, though it carries significant challenges including high risk of no-reflow and cardiogenic shock. Cardiogenic shock is very likely to develop in this scenario due to the massive territory of myocardium at risk. Beta-blockers should be avoided in acute STEMI with cardiogenic shock as they can worsen hemodynamic status.','Left main occlusion = 30% mortality without intervention (LM-30)','Left main occlusion is a true ''widow-maker'' lesion. When suspected, immediate transfer to a cardiac surgery-capable center is essential as these patients often require emergency coronary artery bypass grafting rather than PCI.','Fibrinolytic therapy: Contraindicated in left main occlusion due to high risk of fatal complications. PCI contraindicated: Actually the preferred treatment despite challenges. Cardiogenic shock unlikely: Very likely due to massive territory at risk. Beta-blockers immediately: Contraindicated in cardiogenic shock as they can worsen hemodynamics.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","STEMI","left main occlusion","LBBB","cardiogenic shock"]',true,'ECG showing extensive anterior STEMI with LBBB and angiogram showing left main occlusion','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-10e420da-273.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:52:38.508Z','2026-10-21T16:52:38.508Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicoq4k001aoku8x0mpr6z6','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG is shown below. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?

![ECG showing ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) with characteristic ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, showing the classic inferior STEMI pattern](/generated/agent-8f9335a1-f22.jpg)','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and monitor blood pressure","Initiate fibrinolytic therapy immediately","Perform primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) within 90 minutes","Start aspirin and heparin, then transfer to a PCI-capable hospital","Obtain cardiac enzymes first before intervention"]','Perform primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) within 90 minutes','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, indicating an inferior STEMI. According to current guidelines, the standard of care for STEMI is primary PCI within 90 minutes of first medical contact. This is superior to fibrinolytic therapy when PCI can be performed promptly. Aspirin should be given immediately, but PCI remains the preferred intervention.','"PCI first for STEMI - 90 minutes is the time"','Time is muscle - rapid PCI in STEMI saves myocardium and improves outcomes','Nitroglycerin may be used but doesn''t address the underlying coronary occlusion; fibrinolytics are second-line when PCI is available; waiting for enzymes delays critical intervention; transfer delays PCI','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology","Myocardial Infarction"]',true,'ECG showing ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) with characteristic ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-8f9335a1-f22.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:52:42.692Z','2026-10-21T16:52:42.692Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicow5d001boku8pvoivky1','mcq','A 72-year-old male with a history of coronary artery disease presents with 90 minutes of severe chest pain. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads V2-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Emergency coronary angiography reveals a 99% stenosis in the proximal left anterior descending artery. During primary PCI, the operator encounters no-reflow phenomenon after stent deployment. Which of the following interventions would be MOST appropriate to address this complication?','["Administration of intravenous nitroglycerin","Intracoronary administration of abciximab","Intracoronary administration of verapamil","Intracoronary administration of adenosine","Intracoronary administration of sodium nitroprusside"]','Intracoronary administration of adenosine','No-reflow phenomenon during PCI is characterized by the absence of coronary flow distal to the intervention site despite successful angiographic result. It is thought to be caused by microvascular dysfunction, distal embolization, and vasospasm. Intracoronary adenosine is the most appropriate intervention as it causes potent vasodilation of coronary microvasculature. Intravenous nitroglycerin has limited effect on microvasculature. While abciximab may be used in some cases, adenosine is more specific for microvascular dysfunction. Verapamil and sodium nitroprusside are less commonly used for no-reflow compared to adenosine.','No-reflow = Adenosine (A for microvascular dilation)','No-reflow phenomenon occurs in approximately 1-5% of PCIs and is associated with worse outcomes. Prevention strategies include distal protection devices, careful lesion preparation, and administration of glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors.','Intravenous nitroglycerin: Primarily affects epicardial vessels rather than microvasculature. Intracoronary abciximab: May be used but adenosine is more specific for microvascular dysfunction. Intracoronary verapamil: Less commonly used for no-reflow compared to adenosine. Intracoronary sodium nitroprusside: Can be used but adenosine is preferred due to more potent and selective microvascular dilation.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","STEMI","primary PCI","no-reflow phenomenon","LAD stenosis"]',true,'Coronary angiogram showing LAD stenosis with no-reflow phenomenon after stent deployment','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-a1bc31c5-f6c.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:52:50.497Z','2026-10-21T16:52:50.497Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu76005ce9oi3rxwk0xo','mcq','What could change auscultatory finding in mitral valve prolapse?','["Squatting position.","Setting position.","Inspiration.","Valsalva maneuver."]','Valsalva maneuver.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Mitral Valve Prolapse','["mvp","auscultation","maneuvers"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.682Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.682Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicp4nq001coku8xfm9b8kc','mcq','A 55-year-old male with no significant past medical history presents with 2 hours of severe chest pain. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V3 with reciprocal ST depression in leads V5-V6. Emergency coronary angiography reveals a 100% occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery. Which of the following anatomical structures is MOST likely to be affected by this infarction?','["Right ventricle","Inferior wall of left ventricle","Anterior wall of left ventricle and interventricular septum","Lateral wall of left ventricle","Posterior wall of left ventricle"]','Anterior wall of left ventricle and interventricular septum','The proximal left anterior descending artery (LAD) supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the interventricular septum, and the apex of the heart. Occlusion of the proximal LAD therefore typically results in anterior wall STEMI with involvement of the interventricular septum. This is why ST elevation is seen in precordial leads V1-V3 (which primarily view the anterior wall and septum). The right ventricle is primarily supplied by the right coronary artery. The inferior wall is supplied by the right coronary artery or left circumflex artery. The lateral wall is supplied by the left circumflex artery or distal LAD. The posterior wall is supplied by the left circumflex artery.','Proximal LAD = Anterior wall + Septum (AS)','Anterior STEMI carries the highest mortality rate among all STEMI locations due to the large territory of myocardium at risk, particularly when the proximal LAD is involved. These patients require urgent reperfusion therapy.','Right ventricle: Primarily supplied by right coronary artery, not proximal LAD. Inferior wall: Supplied by right coronary artery or left circumflex artery. Lateral wall: Supplied by left circumflex artery or distal LAD. Posterior wall: Supplied by left circumflex artery.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","STEMI","LAD occlusion","anterior STEMI","coronary anatomy"]',true,'ECG showing anterior STEMI with coronary angiogram showing proximal LAD occlusion and cardiac anatomy','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-3019280a-aff.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:53:01.526Z','2026-10-21T16:53:01.526Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicpdna001doku88dvuada1','mcq','A 68-year-old female with a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia presents with 4 hours of severe chest pain. Her ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with ST depression in leads V1-V4. Emergency coronary angiography reveals a 100% occlusion of the right coronary artery. During the procedure, the patient develops hypotension with a blood pressure of 80/50 mmHg, jugular venous distension to 8 cm, and clear lung fields. Which of the following statements regarding this patient''s condition is MOST accurate?','["This patient should receive nitrates to improve coronary perfusion","The patient''s condition is consistent with right ventricular infarction","Intra-aortic balloon pump should be placed immediately","The patient requires emergent coronary artery bypass surgery","Fluid administration is contraindicated due to risk of pulmonary edema"]','The patient''s condition is consistent with right ventricular infarction','The patient''s presentation of hypotension, elevated jugular venous pressure, and clear lung fields is classic for right ventricular infarction. This occurs in approximately 30% of inferior STEMI cases when the proximal right coronary artery is occluded. The ECG findings of ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in anterior leads are consistent with inferior STEMI. In right ventricular infarction, nitrates are contraindicated as they can further decrease preload and worsen hypotension. Intra-aortic balloon pump may be considered but is not first-line. Coronary artery bypass surgery is not typically required for isolated right ventricular infarction. Fluid administration is the initial treatment of choice to restore preload to the right ventricle.','Hypotension + JVD + clear lungs = Right ventricular infarction (HJC)','Always obtain a right-sided ECG (V4R) in patients with inferior STEMI to assess for right ventricular involvement. Right ventricular infarction requires different management than typical STEMI - avoid nitrates, consider fluids, and be cautious with diuretics.','Nitrates to improve coronary perfusion: Contraindicated in right ventricular infarction as they reduce preload and worsen hypotension. Intra-aortic balloon pump: May be considered but not first-line treatment. Emergent CABG: Not typically required for isolated right ventricular infarction. Fluid administration contraindicated: Actually first-line treatment to restore right ventricular preload.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","STEMI","right ventricular infarction","inferior STEMI","coronary angiography"]',true,'ECG showing inferior STEMI with reciprocal changes and angiogram showing RCA occlusion with right ventricular infarction anatomy','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-d53ce0d8-670.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:53:13.175Z','2026-10-21T16:53:13.175Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicpnxl001eoku8dt2rwk1q','mcq','A 72-year-old male with diabetes and hyperlipidemia presents with chest pain. His ECG is shown below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?

![Lateral STEMI ECG showing ST elevation in leads I, aVL, and V5-V6](/generated/agent-01fcec3f-add.jpg)

The ECG demonstrates ST-segment elevation in leads I, aVL, and V5-V6, consistent with a lateral wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction.','["Inferior wall STEMI","Anterior wall STEMI","Lateral wall STEMI","Posterior wall STEMI","Right ventricular STEMI"]','Lateral wall STEMI','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads I, aVL, and V5-V6, which is characteristic of a lateral wall STEMI. The lateral wall of the left ventricle is supplied by the left circumflex artery (LCx) and the obtuse marginal branches. Lead I and aVL represent the high lateral wall, while V5-V6 represent the low lateral wall. Inferior STEMI would show elevation in II, III, aVF; anterior STEMI in V1-V6; posterior STEMI would show reciprocal ST depression in V1-V3; and right ventricular STEMI would show elevation in V4R.','"I, aVL, V5-V6 = Lateral" - These leads represent the lateral wall of the left ventricle','Lateral STEMI often indicates occlusion of the left circumflex artery, which may be more challenging to detect on ECG than anterior or inferior STEMIs','Option 1 is incorrect because inferior STEMI shows elevation in II, III, aVF. Option 2 is incorrect because anterior STEMI shows elevation in V1-V6. Option 4 is incorrect because posterior STEMI shows reciprocal ST depression in V1-V3 rather than elevation. Option 5 is incorrect because right ventricular STEMI shows elevation in V4R.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","STEMI","ECG","Lateral Wall","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing lateral wall STEMI with ST segment elevation in leads I, aVL, and V5-V6','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-01fcec3f-add.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:53:26.505Z','2026-10-21T16:53:26.505Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicqaty001foku8e4qzqb37','mcq','A 58-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and smoking presents with severe anterior chest pain and shortness of breath. Her ECG is shown below. Which coronary artery is most likely affected?

![12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V6, demonstrating extensive anterior wall myocardial infarction](/generated/agent-001a0c87-19a.jpg)','["Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Right ventricular branch","Posterior descending artery (PDA)"]','Left anterior descending artery (LAD)','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V6, which corresponds to anterior wall myocardial infarction. The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, including the septum and anterior free wall. This pattern is characteristic of LAD occlusion, which is often associated with poor prognosis due to the large territory of myocardium at risk.','"V1-V6 elevation = LAD occlusion - anterior wall devastation"','LAD occlusions are high-risk STEMIs affecting the largest myocardial territory; early PCI is critical','RCA typically affects inferior leads (II, III, aVF); LCx affects lateral leads (I, aVL, V5-V6); right ventricular branch affects V4R; PDA is part of RCA circulation','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Cardiology","Coronary Anatomy","Myocardial Infarction"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V6','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-001a0c87-19a.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:53:56.182Z','2026-10-21T16:53:56.182Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu77005de9oilefxhaub','mcq','A 7 years old boy was presented with mild fever. On examination, he had an ejection systolic murmur on the heart base with a thrill. What is the possible diagnosis?','["Functional murmur.","Tricuspid regurgitation.","Aortic regurgitation.","Pulmonary stenosis."]','Pulmonary stenosis.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["ps","murmur","child","fever"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.683Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.683Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicr869001goku80d4s3vb9','mcq','A 55-year-old male presents with severe right-sided chest pain and hypotension. His ECG is shown below. What additional finding would be expected in this patient?

![Right ventricular STEMI ECG showing ST elevation in lead V4R](/generated/agent-a0cfe972-502.jpg)

The ECG demonstrates ST-segment elevation in lead V4R, consistent with a right ventricular ST-elevation myocardial infarction.','["ST elevation in leads II, III, aVF","ST elevation in leads V1-V6","ST depression in leads V1-V3","ST elevation in leads I, aVL","ST elevation in V4-V6"]','ST depression in leads V1-V3','The ECG shows ST elevation in lead V4R, indicating right ventricular STEMI. A key feature of right ventricular STEMI is reciprocal ST depression in the anterior leads (V1-V3) due to the electrical forces being directed away from the infarcted area. This is because the right ventricle is located anteriorly and to the right, so when it''s infarcted, the electrical forces are directed away from V1-V3, causing ST depression in those leads. The other options represent different types of STEMI: II, III, aVF = inferior; V1-V6 = anterior; I, aVL = lateral; V4-V6 = anterior-lateral.','"RV STEMI = V4R up, V1-V3 down" - Right ventricular STEMI shows ST elevation in V4R and depression in V1-V3','Right ventricular STEMI requires special consideration for fluid management and may need right-sided ECG leads for proper diagnosis','Option 1 is incorrect because inferior STEMI shows elevation in II, III, aVF. Option 2 is incorrect because anterior STEMI shows elevation in V1-V6. Option 4 is incorrect because lateral STEMI shows elevation in I, aVL. Option 5 is incorrect because anterior-lateral STEMI shows elevation in V4-V6 and I, aVL.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","STEMI","ECG","Right Ventricular","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing right ventricular STEMI with ST segment elevation in lead V4R','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-a0cfe972-502.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:54:39.393Z','2026-10-21T16:54:39.393Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicrzgn001hoku8dtgbbtyr','mcq','A 65-year-old man with diabetes and hyperlipidemia presents with chest pain and the ECG finding shown below. Which coronary artery is most likely responsible for this ECG pattern?

![ECG showing lateral STEMI with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, representing left lateral wall myocardial infarction](/generated/agent-8d86e3c0-a58.jpg)','["Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Diagonal branch","Septal perforator"]','Left circumflex artery (LCx)','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, which is characteristic of lateral wall myocardial infarction. The left circumflex (LCx) artery supplies the lateral wall of the left ventricle, including the territory represented by these leads. This pattern is often missed if only the standard 12-lead ECG is reviewed without careful attention to the lateral leads.','"I, aVL, V5-V6 elevation = LCx occlusion - lateral wall lesion"','LCx occlusions can be subtle; always examine lateral leads carefully to avoid missing lateral STEMIs','RCA affects inferior leads; LAD affects anterior leads; diagonal branches are LAD branches affecting high lateral leads; septal perforators affect septal leads','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Cardiology","Coronary Anatomy","Lateral MI"]',true,'ECG showing lateral STEMI with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-8d86e3c0-a58.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:55:14.759Z','2026-10-21T16:55:14.759Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicsvlf001joku82cg2jd7x','mcq','A 70-year-old man presents with inferior STEMI and hypotension. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, aVF, and V4R. What is the most likely hemodynamic complication and appropriate initial management?

![ECG showing right ventricular STEMI with ST elevation in lead V4R, along with inferior STEMI changes in leads II, III, and aVF](/generated/agent-c36e9a0d-a2d.jpg)','["Cardiogenic shock requiring vasopressors","Right ventricular failure requiring fluid administration","Left ventricular rupture requiring emergency surgery","Pericardial tamponade requiring pericardiocentesis","Pulmonary edema requiring diuresis"]','Right ventricular failure requiring fluid administration','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, aVF (inferior STEMI) and V4R (right ventricular involvement). Right ventricular infarction occurs in approximately 30% of inferior STEMIs and can cause right ventricular failure and hypotension. The initial management involves fluid administration to maintain preload, as these patients are often volume-dependent and may become hypotensive with diuretics or vasodilators.','"V4R elevation + inferior STEMI = RV involvement - fluids first, not diuretics"','Right ventricular STEMI patients are preload-dependent; avoid nitrates and diuretics initially','Cardiogenic shock is more typical of large anterior MIs; ventricular rupture is rare and presents with sudden collapse; tamponade would show electrical alternans; pulmonary edema is more common with left ventricular dysfunction','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Right Ventricular MI","Hemodynamics"]',true,'ECG showing right ventricular STEMI with ST elevation in lead V4R, along with inferior STEMI changes','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-c36e9a0d-a2d.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T16:55:56.403Z','2026-10-21T16:55:56.403Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicucuo001loku8d49qe7qs','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with chest pain. His ECG shows ST depression in leads V1-V3 but no obvious ST elevation. What additional lead should be obtained to evaluate for posterior STEMI, and what would you expect to see?

![ECG showing posterior STEMI with reciprocal ST depression in leads V1-V3, representing posterior wall myocardial infarction](/generated/agent-26b3e402-acc.jpg)','["Lead V7-V9 - expect ST elevation","Lead aVR - expect ST elevation","Lead V4R - expect ST elevation","Lead III - expect ST elevation","Lead aVL - expect ST elevation"]','Lead V7-V9 - expect ST elevation','Posterior STEMI is often missed on standard 12-lead ECG because it manifests as reciprocal ST depression in leads V1-V3 rather than ST elevation. To diagnose posterior STEMI, posterior leads (V7-V9) should be obtained, which would show ST elevation in the posterior wall territory. Posterior leads are placed at the posterior axillary line (V7), mid-scapular line (V8), and paraspinal line (V9).','"V1-V3 depression = posterior MI - look behind with V7-V9"','Posterior STEMIs are easily missed; always consider posterior leads when V1-V3 show ST depression','aVR elevation suggests left main or proximal LAD occlusion; V4R elevation suggests right ventricular MI; III elevation suggests inferior MI; aVL elevation suggests lateral MI','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Posterior MI","Diagnostic Testing"]',true,'ECG showing posterior STEMI with reciprocal ST depression in leads V1-V3','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-26b3e402-acc.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-21T16:57:05.424Z','2026-10-21T17:08:07.176Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu77005ee9oih9dkbo2x','mcq','A 6 year old boy with hypertension and a systolic murmur maximally on the back. What is the possible diagnosis?','["Patent ductus arteriosus.","Aortic coarctation.","Pulmonary stenosis.","Ventricular septal defect."]','Aortic coarctation.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Aortic Coarctation','["coarctation","hypertension","murmur","child"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.683Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.683Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvicsw9i001koku8njxf25vy','mcq','A 68-year-old female presents with chest pain. Her initial 12-lead ECG shows no obvious ST elevation, but you notice ST depression in leads V1-V3. What should be your next step?

![Posterior STEMI ECG showing ST depression in leads V1-V3](/generated/agent-6d71440a-24a.jpg)

The ECG demonstrates ST-segment depression in leads V1-V3, which may represent reciprocal changes from a posterior wall STEMI.','["Reassure the patient and discharge home","Order serial cardiac enzymes and monitor","Obtain posterior leads (V7-V9) to evaluate for posterior STEMI","Initiate fibrinolytic therapy immediately","Transfer for PCI without further testing"]','Obtain posterior leads (V7-V9) to evaluate for posterior STEMI','The ECG shows ST depression in leads V1-V3, which can be a subtle sign of posterior wall STEMI. In posterior STEMI, the electrical forces are directed away from the anterior leads, causing ST depression in V1-V3 rather than elevation. The posterior wall of the left ventricle is supplied by the circumflex artery and posterior descending artery. To confirm posterior STEMI, posterior leads (V7-V9) should be obtained, which would show ST elevation if a posterior STEMI is present. The other options are incorrect because: reassurance is inappropriate with ECG changes, serial enzymes delay diagnosis, fibrinolytic therapy shouldn''t be given without confirmation, and transfer without confirmation may be inappropriate.','"V1-V3 down = Posterior STEMI suspected" - ST depression in anterior leads may indicate posterior wall infarction','Posterior STEMI is often called the "great masquerader" because it can present with only reciprocal ST depression in anterior leads','Option 1 is incorrect because ST depression in V1-V3 requires further evaluation. Option 2 is incorrect because serial enzymes delay diagnosis of time-sensitive STEMI. Option 3 is incorrect because fibrinolytic therapy shouldn''t be initiated without confirming the diagnosis. Option 4 is incorrect because transfer without confirmation may lead to inappropriate treatment.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","STEMI","ECG","Posterior Wall","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing posterior STEMI with reciprocal ST depression in leads V1-V3','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-6d71440a-24a.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-21T16:55:57.270Z','2026-10-21T17:08:19.547Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvidi4wt001toku852ag8bif','mcq','A 28-year-old patient presents with recurrent episodes of wheezing, shortness of breath, and chest tightness that worsen at night and with exercise. Physical examination reveals prolonged expiration and diffuse wheezing on auscultation. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy for this patient''s chronic asthma management?','["Short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) as needed","Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS)","Leukotriene receptor antagonist","Theophylline","Oral corticosteroids"]','Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS)','The patient''s symptoms and physical findings are consistent with chronic asthma. According to current asthma guidelines, inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the preferred first-line controller therapy for persistent asthma. SABA alone is appropriate for intermittent asthma, but this patient has chronic symptoms requiring controller therapy. Leukotriene receptor antagonists are an alternative but less potent than ICS. Theophylline and oral corticosteroids are reserved for more severe cases due to their side effect profiles.','ICS = Initial Controller for asthma Severity','ICS reduce airway inflammation and are the cornerstone of chronic asthma management','SABA alone: appropriate for intermittent asthma only; Leukotriene receptor antagonist: second-line option; Theophylline: narrow therapeutic index, many side effects; Oral corticosteroids: reserved for severe exacerbations','Medium','Respiratory','Asthma management','["Asthma management","Respiratory","chat-agent","asthma","pharmacotherapy","controller therapy"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:15:34.877Z','2026-10-21T17:15:34.877Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvidi6a4001uoku864nqnwdc','mcq','A 24-year-old patient with a history of allergic asthma presents with acute exacerbation. Which of the following medications should be administered first-line for immediate symptom relief?','["Albuterol (short-acting beta-agonist)","Montelukast (leukotriene modifier)","Fluticasone (inhaled corticosteroid)","Theophylline","Ipratropium bromide"]','Albuterol (short-acting beta-agonist)','Albuterol, a short-acting beta-agonist (SABA), is the first-line treatment for acute asthma exacerbations due to its rapid bronchodilator effects. Inhaled corticosteroids like fluticasone are used for long-term control but don''t provide immediate relief. Leukotriene modifiers and theophylline are maintenance therapies, while ipratropium may be added in severe exacerbations but is not first-line.','SABA first for acute asthma','SABAs are the cornerstone of acute asthma management','Montelukast and fluticasone are maintenance therapies; theophylline is rarely used today; ipratropium is adjunctive therapy only','Medium','Respiratory','asthma','["asthma","Respiratory","chat-agent","acute asthma","bronchodilators","emergency management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:15:36.652Z','2026-10-21T17:15:36.652Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvidi8ww001voku82vzop2rg','mcq','A 35-year-old patient presents with recurrent episodes of wheezing, shortness of breath, and chest tightness that worsen at night and with exercise. Physical examination reveals prolonged expiratory phase and diffuse wheezing on auscultation. Which pathological finding is most characteristic of the underlying pathophysiology in this patient''s condition?','["Fixed airway obstruction due to fibrosis","Reversible airway obstruction with bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy","Chronic inflammation with mucus plugging and submucosal edema","Emphysematous changes with alveolar wall destruction"]','Reversible airway obstruction with bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy','The patient''s presentation with recurrent wheezing, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and symptoms that vary over time (nocturnal and exercise-induced) is classic for asthma. The key pathological finding in asthma is reversible airway obstruction due to bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperresponsiveness, along with chronic inflammation, mucus plugging, and submucosal edema. Unlike fixed airway obstruction seen in COPD, asthma is characterized by reversibility. The prolonged expiratory phase and wheezing are consistent with bronchoconstriction.','"REVERSIBLE" - Reversible airway obstruction, Eosinophilic inflammation, Varying symptoms, Expiratory prolongation, Reversible bronchoconstriction, Inflammation, Bronchial hyperresponsiveness, Increased mucus','Asthma is characterized by reversible airway obstruction, unlike the fixed obstruction seen in COPD. The reversibility is a key diagnostic feature.','"Fixed airway obstruction due to fibrosis" - Incorrect, as asthma involves reversible obstruction, not fixed fibrosis seen in chronic lung diseases. "Emphysematous changes with alveolar wall destruction" - Incorrect, this describes COPD/emphysema, not asthma. "Chronic inflammation with mucus plugging and submucosal edema" - Partially correct but incomplete, as the most characteristic finding is the reversible nature with smooth muscle hypertrophy.','Medium','Respiratory','Asthma Pathophysiology','["asthma","Respiratory","deep-agent","Asthma Pathophysiology","asthma","pathophysiology","airway obstruction","reversible"]',true,'Asthma pathophysiology diagram showing normal vs. asthmatic airway comparison with CLEARLY LABELED annotations including bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy, mucus plugging, and inflammatory changes','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-92354fa8-189.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:15:40.064Z','2026-10-21T17:15:40.064Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvie0zvy0022oku8nli4h13g','mcq','A 12-year-old child with well-controlled asthma on low-dose inhaled corticosteroids develops nocturnal cough 2-3 times per week but maintains normal daytime activity and spirometry. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?','["Increase inhaled corticosteroid dose","Add long-acting beta-agonist (LABA)","Add montelukast","Add theophylline","Continue current therapy and monitor"]','Add long-acting beta-agonist (LABA)','This child has partially controlled asthma (symptoms 2-3 nights/week) despite low-dose inhaled corticosteroids. According to GINA guidelines, the preferred step-up therapy for partially controlled asthma is to add a long-acting beta-agonist (LABA) to the inhaled corticosteroid. This combination provides better asthma control than either medication alone or increasing the ICS dose. Montelukast is an alternative but LABA is preferred. Theophylline is not first-line due to its narrow therapeutic index and side effects.','ICS+LABA for partially controlled asthma','GINA guidelines recommend LABA addition as step-up therapy for partially controlled asthma on ICS','Increasing ICS alone is less effective than ICS+LABA combination; montelukast is second-line; theophylline has significant side effects','Medium','Respiratory','asthma','["asthma","Respiratory","chat-agent","asthma","management","GINA","partially controlled","LABA"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:30:14.830Z','2026-10-21T17:30:14.830Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvidido1001woku8wi4m978i','mcq','A 12-year-old child with a known diagnosis of moderate persistent asthma presents to the emergency department with severe dyspnea, respiratory rate of 32 breaths per minute, accessory muscle use, and inability to speak in full sentences. Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) is 40% of predicted. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate intervention?','["Albuterol 2.5 mg via nebulizer every 4 hours","Systemic corticosteroids","Continuous albuterol nebulization","Magnesium sulfate infusion","Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV)"]','Continuous albuterol nebulization','This patient is experiencing a severe asthma exacerbation (PEFR <50% predicted, accessory muscle use, inability to speak in full sentences). The most appropriate immediate intervention is continuous albetuerol nebulization (10-15 mg/hour) along with systemic corticosteroids. Continuous nebulization provides more consistent bronchodilation than intermittent dosing. Systemic corticosteroids are also essential but work more slowly (4-6 hours for onset). Magnesium sulfate may be considered in severe cases but is not first-line. NIPPV may be needed in respiratory failure but is not the initial intervention.','Severe asthma = Continuous albuterol + Steroids','In severe exacerbations, continuous beta-agonist nebulization provides more consistent bronchodilation than intermittent dosing','Intermittent albuterol: insufficient for severe exacerbations; Systemic corticosteroids: essential but work slowly; Magnesium sulfate: adjunctive therapy in severe cases; NIPPV: reserved for respiratory failure','Medium','Respiratory','Asthma exacerbation','["Asthma exacerbation","Respiratory","chat-agent","asthma","emergency","exacerbation","pediatrics"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:15:46.225Z','2026-10-21T17:15:46.225Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvididu8001xoku8wvowmo0k','mcq','Which of the following best describes the pathophysiological mechanism of asthma?','["Chronic inflammation of airways leading to bronchoconstriction and airway remodeling","Infection-induced bronchospasm with mucus production","Neuromuscular dysfunction causing smooth muscle hyperreactivity","Autoimmune destruction of bronchial epithelium","Vascular congestion and edema of the alveoli"]','Chronic inflammation of airways leading to bronchoconstriction and airway remodeling','Asthma is characterized by chronic airway inflammation involving mast cells, eosinophils, T-lymphocytes, and other inflammatory cells. This leads to bronchoconstriction, airway hyperresponsiveness, and structural changes (remodeling) including subepithelial fibrosis and smooth muscle hypertrophy. The other options describe different pathological processes not specific to asthma.','Chronic inflammation → bronchoconstriction → remodeling','Inflammation is the core mechanism in all asthma phenotypes','Infection describes bronchitis; neuromuscular dysfunction relates to other conditions; autoimmune destruction is seen in diseases like COPD; vascular congestion describes pulmonary edema','Medium','Respiratory','asthma pathophysiology','["asthma pathophysiology","Respiratory","chat-agent","pathophysiology","inflammation","airway remodeling"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:15:46.448Z','2026-10-21T17:15:46.448Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvidifhg001yoku89kbri4j0','mcq','A 28-year-old patient with moderate persistent asthma has been using a combination inhaler containing fluticasone propionate and salmeterol. Which mechanism best explains the primary therapeutic effect of the fluticasone component in managing this patient''s asthma?','["Direct bronchodilation through beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation","Reduction of airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness","Increased mucociliary clearance and reduced mucus production","Inhibition of mast cell degranulation and histamine release"]','Reduction of airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness','Fluticasone propionate is an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) that works primarily by reducing airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness. It suppresses the inflammatory response by inhibiting multiple inflammatory pathways, including reducing cytokine production, decreasing inflammatory cell infiltration, and reducing airway hyperresponsiveness. This addresses the underlying inflammatory component of asthma. The salmeterol component (a long-acting beta-agonist) provides bronchodilation through beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation. While ICS can have some effects on mucus production and mast cells, their primary mechanism is anti-inflammatory.','"INFLAME" - Inhibits cytokines, Reduces eosinophils, Lowers inflammation, Mucus reduction, Airways calmed, Eosinophil decrease','Inhaled corticosteroids are the cornerstone of asthma therapy because they address the underlying inflammation, unlike bronchodilators which only treat symptoms.','"Direct bronchodilation through beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation" - Incorrect, this describes the mechanism of salmeterol (LABA), not fluticasone (ICS). "Increased mucociliary clearance and reduced mucus production" - Partially correct but secondary effect; primary mechanism is anti-inflammatory. "Inhibition of mast cell degranulation and histamine release" - This is a mechanism of some asthma medications but not the primary mechanism of corticosteroids.','Medium','Respiratory','Asthma Treatment','["asthma","Respiratory","deep-agent","Asthma Treatment","asthma","treatment","corticosteroids","inflammation"]',true,'Asthma treatment mechanisms diagram showing inhaled corticosteroid and LABA mechanisms with CLEARLY LABELED annotations','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-165e30b0-e1c.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:15:48.580Z','2026-10-21T17:15:48.580Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvie0u6t0021oku8e5gkt2et','mcq','A 28-year-old patient with a history of moderate persistent asthma presents to the emergency department with severe dyspnea, wheezing, and use of accessory muscles. Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) is 40% of predicted. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?','["Albuterol 2.5 mg via nebulizer every 20 minutes for 3 doses","Oral prednisone 60 mg once daily","Inhaled fluticasone 440 mcg twice daily","Montelukast 10 mg once daily","Ipratropium bromide 500 mcg via nebulizer"]','Albuterol 2.5 mg via nebulizer every 20 minutes for 3 doses','This patient is experiencing a severe asthma exacerbation with PEFR <50% of predicted. The most appropriate initial treatment is short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) therapy via nebulizer. Albuterol is the SABA of choice and should be administered frequently during severe exacerbations. Systemic corticosteroids (like prednisone) are also indicated but should be added after initial bronchodilator therapy. Inhaled corticosteroids and leukotriene modifiers are not appropriate for acute exacerbations.','SABA first for asthma attack','PEFR <50% indicates severe exacerbation requiring aggressive bronchodilator therapy','Prednisone is important but should follow initial bronchodilator therapy; inhaled corticosteroids and leukotriene modifiers are for maintenance, not acute exacerbations','Medium','Respiratory','asthma','["asthma","Respiratory","chat-agent","asthma","exacerbation","emergency","bronchodilator"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:30:07.445Z','2026-10-21T17:30:07.445Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn6sceq003pg7rdxxkkt0gd','flashcard','Microcytic anemia workup','[]','Check iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC), hemoglobin electrophoresis for thalassemia, lead levels','Check iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC), hemoglobin electrophoresis for thalassemia, lead levels','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Microcytic anemia','["flashcard","Anemia diagnostic steps"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z','2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn6scer003rg7rdadv7cxq3','flashcard','Normocytic anemia evaluation','[]','Reticulocyte count (high in hemolysis/blood loss, low in production defects), renal function, B12/folate levels','Reticulocyte count (high in hemolysis/blood loss, low in production defects), renal function, B12/folate levels','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Normocytic anemia','["flashcard","Anemia diagnostic steps"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z','2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriubb00bxe9oi3ekf645k','mcq','Treatment of hepatic encephalopathy include the following:','["High fat diet","Hight protein diet","Low carbohydrate diet","High carbohydrate diet"]','High carbohydrate diet','','','','','Basic','Hepatobiliary','Hepatic encephalopathy','["hepatic encephalopathy","diet","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.831Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.831Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvie5wwx0025oku8yafxmr1k','mcq','A 12-year-old patient presents with recurrent episodes of wheezing, cough, and shortness of breath that worsen at night and with exercise. Physical examination reveals prolonged expiratory phase and diffuse wheezing on auscultation. Which pathological finding is most characteristic of the underlying airway changes in this patient''s condition?','["Thickened alveolar walls with hyaline membrane formation","Bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy and chronic inflammation","Pulmonary vasculature remodeling with intimal hyperplasia","Alveolar macrophage accumulation with hemosiderin deposition"]','Bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy and chronic inflammation','Asthma is characterized by chronic airway inflammation, bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy, mucus hypersecretion, and airway hyperresponsiveness. The pathological hallmarks include eosinophilic inflammation, subepithelial fibrosis, and increased bronchial smooth muscle mass. These changes lead to the clinical symptoms of wheezing, cough, and dyspnea that are typically reversible with treatment.','B.A.S.T.A. - Bronchial inflammation, Airway hyperresponsiveness, Smooth muscle hypertrophy, Thickened basement membrane, Airway remodeling','Asthma pathology involves reversible airway obstruction due to smooth muscle contraction and inflammation, unlike the irreversible changes seen in COPD.','Option 1 describes hyaline membrane disease, which is seen in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. Option 3 describes pulmonary hypertension changes. Option 4 describes hemosiderosis, associated with chronic pulmonary hemorrhage.','Medium','Respiratory','Asthma pathophysiology','["asthma","Respiratory","deep-agent","Asthma pathophysiology","asthma","pathology","airway inflammation","smooth muscle"]',true,'Anatomically precise educational diagram of the respiratory system showing asthma pathology. Include clearly labeled bronchioles with smooth muscle hypertrophy, mucus plugging, and inflammatory cell infiltration. Use leader lines and annotation arrows to highlight key pathological features: bronchoconstriction, thickened basement membrane, and eosinophilic inflammation. Include a cross-section view of an asthmatic airway compared to a normal airway side by side.','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-4ecea6dd-9dc.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:34:04.257Z','2026-10-21T17:34:04.257Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvie63n00026oku89hw6mikb','mcq','A 35-year-old patient with moderate persistent asthma is started on combination therapy with an inhaled corticosteroid and a long-acting beta-2 agonist. Which of the following best describes the distinct mechanisms of action of these two medications in treating the underlying pathophysiology of asthma?','["Both medications primarily target bronchial smooth muscle relaxation through different receptor pathways","Corticosteroids reduce inflammation while beta-2 agonists directly inhibit mast cell degranulation","Corticosteroids decrease airway inflammation and remodeling while beta-2 agonists provide bronchodilation","Both medications work by increasing mucociliary clearance and reducing mucus hypersecretion"]','Corticosteroids decrease airway inflammation and remodeling while beta-2 agonists provide bronchodilation','Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) work by suppressing chronic airway inflammation, reducing eosinophilic infiltration, inhibiting cytokine production, and preventing airway remodeling. Long-acting beta-2 agonists (LABAs) primarily act on beta-2 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle to cause relaxation and bronchodilation. This combination addresses both the inflammatory component and the bronchoconstriction that characterize asthma.','I.C.S. + L.A.B.A. = Inflammation Control + Bronchodilation Action','Combination therapy is more effective than monotherapy because it targets both the inflammatory and bronchoconstrictive components of asthma pathophysiology.','Option 1 is incorrect because corticosteroids do not primarily target smooth muscle. Option 2 is incorrect because beta-2 agonists do not directly inhibit mast cell degranulation. Option 4 is incorrect because neither medication primarily affects mucociliary clearance.','Medium','Respiratory','Asthma pharmacology','["asthma","Respiratory","deep-agent","Asthma pharmacology","asthma","medications","corticosteroids","beta-2 agonists"]',true,'Anatomically precise educational diagram showing asthma pathophysiology and medication mechanisms. Include clearly labeled airway cross-sections showing: 1) Normal airway, 2) Asthmatic airway with bronchoconstriction, inflammation, and mucus hypersecretion, 3) Effects of inhaled corticosteroids (reduced inflammation), 4) Effects of beta-2 agonists (bronchodilation). Use leader lines and annotation arrows to highlight smooth muscle, epithelial damage, goblet cell hyperplasia, and medication action sites.','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-498badd3-baa.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:34:12.972Z','2026-10-21T17:34:12.972Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviefphr002eoku83kajbk3h','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG is shown below. What is the most appropriate immediate next step in management?

![ECG showing STEMI with ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF](/generated/agent-215e3e49-855.jpg)','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and monitor vital signs","Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI","Start aspirin 325 mg orally and call cardiology consultation","Obtain serial cardiac enzymes and wait for results","Initiate heparin infusion and schedule for stress testing"]','Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI','This ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, indicating an acute inferior wall STEMI. According to current guidelines, the standard of care for STEMI is immediate coronary angiography with primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) within 90 minutes of first medical contact. Time to reperfusion is critical for myocardial salvage. While aspirin should be given immediately, the definitive treatment is coronary angiography with PCI. Nitroglycerin may be used but is not the definitive treatment.','"STEMI = Save Time, Every Minute, Intervention"','The 90-minute door-to-balloon time is the gold standard for STEMI management. Each 30-minute delay increases mortality by 7.5%.','Option 1 is incorrect because nitroglycerin alone does not address the underlying coronary occlusion. Option 3 is incorrect because while aspirin is important, PCI is the definitive treatment. Option 4 is incorrect because waiting for enzymes delays life-saving intervention. Option 5 is incorrect because heparin alone does not restore coronary blood flow.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology","Myocardial Infarction"]',true,'ECG showing ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicating inferior wall STEMI','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-215e3e49-855.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:41:41.199Z','2026-10-21T17:41:41.199Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviehg8z002foku8oihunp63','mcq','A 58-year-old female with a history of hypertension and diabetes presents with 3 hours of severe chest pain. Her coronary angiogram is shown below. Which of the following medications should be administered immediately to reduce mortality in this STEMI patient?

![Coronary angiogram showing complete occlusion of the right coronary artery](/generated/agent-a4d0a351-040.jpg)','["Morphine sulfate 2-4 mg IV","Aspirin 325 mg chewed","Metoprolol 5 mg IV","Furosemide 40 mg IV","Atorvastatin 80 mg orally"]','Aspirin 325 mg chewed','Aspirin is a cornerstone of STEMI management that should be administered immediately. It inhibits platelet aggregation by blocking cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1), reducing thrombus formation and improving outcomes. The chewed formulation provides faster absorption. Morphine may be used for pain control but does not reduce mortality. Beta-blockers like metoprolol should be used cautiously in STEMI and may be deferred in certain cases. Furosemide is not indicated for STEMI unless there is evidence of heart failure. Statins are important for secondary prevention but are not time-sensitive like aspirin.','"STEMI first: Aspirin, Oxygen, Nitroglycerin, Morphine" - but aspirin is the most critical for mortality reduction','Aspirin reduces 30-day mortality by approximately 23% in STEMI patients when given within 24 hours of symptom onset.','Option 1 is incorrect because morphine provides pain relief but does not reduce mortality. Option 3 is incorrect because beta-blockers should be used cautiously in STEMI and may be deferred. Option 4 is incorrect because furosemide is not indicated unless there is heart failure. Option 5 is incorrect because while statins are important for secondary prevention, they are not time-sensitive like aspirin.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Pharmacology","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology","Myocardial Infarction"]',true,'Coronary angiogram showing complete occlusion of the right coronary artery with thrombus','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-a4d0a351-040.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:43:02.531Z','2026-10-21T17:43:02.531Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn6scet003tg7rd63isb676','flashcard','Macrocytic anemia diagnostic approach','[]','Vitamin B12 and folate levels, peripheral smear for megaloblastic changes, thyroid function tests, alcohol history','Vitamin B12 and folate levels, peripheral smear for megaloblastic changes, thyroid function tests, alcohol history','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Macrocytic anemia','["flashcard","Anemia diagnostic steps"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z','2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviej6a6002hoku8fkreko4f','mcq','A 65-year-old male with a history of smoking and hyperlipidemia presents to the emergency department with 90 minutes of severe crushing chest pain. His ECG is shown below. Which of the following complications is this patient at highest risk for given this ECG finding?

![12-lead ECG showing anterior wall STEMI with ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V6](/generated/agent-5608e344-393.jpg)','["Complete heart block","Right ventricular infarction","Cardiogenic shock","Pericarditis","Ventricular arrhythmias"]','Ventricular arrhythmias','This ECG shows anterior wall STEMI with ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V6. Anterior wall STEMI carries the highest risk of ventricular arrhythmias (including ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation) due to the large mass of myocardium at risk and the proximity to the conduction system. Complete heart block is more commonly associated with inferior wall STEMI. Right ventricular infarction typically occurs with occlusion of the right coronary artery. Cardiogenic shock can occur with large infarctions but is not as immediately life-threatening as arrhythmias. Pericarditis may occur post-MI but is not the most acute complication.','"Anterior MI = Arrhythmia Risk" - the anterior wall has the highest density of working myocardium and is closest to the conduction system','The first 24 hours after anterior STEMI carries the highest risk of sudden cardiac death due to ventricular arrhythmias, requiring continuous cardiac monitoring.','Option 1 is incorrect because complete heart block is more common with inferior wall STEMI. Option 2 is incorrect because right ventricular infarction typically occurs with RCA occlusion. Option 3 is incorrect because while cardiogenic shock can occur, it develops more gradually than arrhythmias. Option 4 is incorrect because pericarditis is a later complication and not immediately life-threatening.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology","Complications"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior wall STEMI with ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V6','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-5608e344-393.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:44:22.926Z','2026-10-21T17:44:22.926Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviekpni002ioku8m8k9343t','mcq','A 59-year-old male is taken for emergency coronary angiography after presenting with acute STEMI. The procedure is shown below. What is the primary goal of this intervention?

![Cardiac catheterization lab showing percutaneous coronary intervention with stent placement](/generated/agent-7686aa69-2e5.jpg)','["To assess coronary artery anatomy and identify culprit lesions","To administer intracoronary thrombolytic therapy","To achieve rapid restoration of coronary blood flow (reperfusion)","To place an intra-aortic balloon pump for hemodynamic support","To perform rotational atherectomy for calcified lesions"]','To achieve rapid restoration of coronary blood flow (reperfusion)','The primary goal of primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) in STEMI is to achieve rapid restoration of coronary blood flow (reperfusion). This is critical because the time from symptom onset to reperfusion directly correlates with myocardial salvage and survival. While PCI allows for anatomical assessment, that is secondary to the main goal of reperfusion. Thrombolytic therapy is an alternative but not the goal of PCI. Intra-aortic balloon pump support may be needed for cardiogenic shock but is not the primary goal. Rotational atherectomy is used for specific lesion morphology but is not the primary goal in STEMI.','"PCI = Primary Coronary Intervention = Perfusion, Coronary, Intervention" - the goal is perfusion','The "time is muscle" principle: each 30-minute delay in reperfusion increases mortality by 7.5%. The goal is door-to-balloon time < 90 minutes.','Option 1 is incorrect because anatomical assessment is secondary to achieving reperfusion. Option 2 is incorrect because thrombolytic therapy is a different treatment modality. Option 4 is incorrect because balloon pump support is for hemodynamic stabilization, not primary reperfusion. Option 5 is incorrect because atherectomy is for specific lesion preparation, not the primary goal.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Interventional Cardiology","Emergency Medicine","Procedures","Myocardial Infarction"]',true,'Cardiac catheterization lab showing percutaneous coronary intervention with stent placement','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-7686aa69-2e5.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:45:34.686Z','2026-10-21T17:45:34.686Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipv16z000sg0u836h6rh5s','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. 

![ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction with elevated ST segments in leads II, III, and aVF indicating inferior wall MI](/generated/agent-6d7dc9d4-55b.jpg)

What is the most appropriate immediate intervention for this patient?','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and monitor vital signs","Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI","Start heparin infusion and delay catheterization","Transfer to a community hospital for observation","Administer aspirin and arrange for outpatient stress testing"]','Immediate coronary angiography with possible PCI','This patient has an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) in the inferior distribution (leads II, III, aVF). According to current guidelines, the standard of care for STEMI is immediate coronary angiography with primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) within 90 minutes of first medical contact. Time is muscle, and rapid reperfusion therapy is critical to minimize myocardial damage. Sublingual nitroglycerin may be used but does not replace the need for urgent revascularization.','"STEMI = Save Time, Save Muscle"','Inferior STEMI often presents with bradycardia and hypotension due to right ventricular involvement, requiring volume expansion and temporary pacing if needed.','Nitroglycerin alone is insufficient for STEMI; heparin delays definitive treatment; community hospitals lack PCI capabilities; outpatient testing is inappropriate for acute STEMI.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction with elevated ST segments in leads II, III, and aVF indicating inferior wall MI','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-6d7dc9d4-55b.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:01:31.979Z','2026-10-21T23:01:31.979Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviepan4002moku8hfd27m8g','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 4mm in leads II, III, and aVF. Which coronary artery is most likely responsible for this finding?','["Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Right coronary artery","Left main coronary artery"]','Right coronary artery','ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates an inferior wall STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the right coronary artery (RCA). The RCA typically supplies the inferior wall of the left ventricle, including the posterior and inferior septum. In approximately 85-90% of individuals, the RCA also supplies the AV node, explaining why inferior STEMI is frequently associated with bradycardia and heart block.','II, III, aVF = Inferior wall = RCA','Inferior STEMI is associated with a higher incidence of bradycardia and AV conduction blocks due to RCA supply to the AV node in most patients.','Left anterior descending artery typically causes anterior STEMI (leads V1-V4, I, aVL). Left circumflex artery causes lateral STEMI (leads I, aVL, V5-V6). Left main coronary artery occlusion causes massive anterior STEMI with widespread ST elevation and is often fatal.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI ECG Interpretation','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI ECG Interpretation","ECG","STEMI","Coronary Anatomy","Inferior MI"]',true,'ECG showing classic STEMI findings with ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF (inferior STEMI)','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-aad310af-eae.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:49:08.512Z','2026-10-21T17:49:08.512Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn7kc84000biirdjdk35apg','flashcard','Anemia classification by red blood cell morphology','[]','Microcytic (low MCV), Normocytic (normal MCV), Macrocytic (high MCV)','Microcytic (low MCV), Normocytic (normal MCV), Macrocytic (high MCV)','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia Classification','["flashcard","Anemia Classification Flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:28:10.846Z','2026-10-25T02:28:10.846Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn7kc8g000diird61k5f4bs','flashcard','Common causes of microcytic anemia','[]','Iron deficiency anemia, Thalassemia, Anemia of chronic disease, Sideroblastic anemia','Iron deficiency anemia, Thalassemia, Anemia of chronic disease, Sideroblastic anemia','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia Classification','["flashcard","Anemia Classification Flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:28:10.846Z','2026-10-25T02:28:10.846Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn7kc8k000fiird11d57ymk','flashcard','Common causes of macrocytic anemia','[]','Vitamin B12 deficiency, Folate deficiency, Alcohol use, Hypothyroidism, Liver disease','Vitamin B12 deficiency, Folate deficiency, Alcohol use, Hypothyroidism, Liver disease','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia Classification','["flashcard","Anemia Classification Flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:28:10.846Z','2026-10-25T02:28:10.846Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviepo8n002noku85n2wo9kx','mcq','Which of the following pathophysiological mechanisms is primarily responsible for myocardial injury in STEMI?','["Microvascular dysfunction without coronary occlusion","Complete coronary artery occlusion leading to prolonged myocardial ischemia","Coronary vasospasm with transient occlusion","Myocarditis with inflammatory cell infiltration"]','Complete coronary artery occlusion leading to prolonged myocardial ischemia','STEMI is caused by complete occlusion of a coronary artery, typically by a thrombus formed over a ruptured or eroded atherosclerotic plaque. This complete occlusion leads to prolonged myocardial ischemia, resulting in transmural myocardial necrosis. The time-dependent nature of myocardial salvage is why rapid reperfusion therapy (PCI or fibrinolysis within 90-120 minutes) is critical. Microvascular dysfunction occurs but is secondary to the primary occlusion. Vasospasm can cause angina but typically doesn''t result in STEMI. Myocarditis presents differently with different ECG findings.','STEMI = ST elevation + MI = Complete occlusion = Time is muscle','The ''time is muscle'' concept emphasizes that every 30-minute delay in reperfusion results in approximately 7-10% additional myocardial death.','Microvascular dysfunction is more typical in non-STEMI ACS. Coronary vasospasm causes Prinzmetal angina with transient ST elevation. Myocarditis presents with diffuse ST changes, not localized STEMI, and often has elevated troponin without typical coronary risk factors.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI Pathophysiology','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI Pathophysiology","Pathophysiology","Myocardial Infarction","Coronary Occlusion","Ischemia"]',true,'Cross-sectional anatomical diagram showing coronary artery occlusion causing STEMI with myocardial necrosis','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-8dcb7986-c75.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:49:26.135Z','2026-10-21T17:49:26.135Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvieq1ln002ooku8k6eqec9b','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with suspected STEMI arrives at the emergency department 45 minutes after symptom onset. His ECG confirms STEMI in the anterior leads. According to current guidelines, what is the preferred initial management strategy?','["Immediate fibrinolytic therapy followed by transfer to PCI-capable facility","Aspirin 325mg, nitroglycerin, and morphine while arranging PCI","Primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with balloon angioplasty and stenting","Conservative medical management with beta-blockers and statins only"]','Primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with balloon angioplasty and stenting','Current guidelines recommend primary PCI as the preferred reperfusion strategy for STEMI when it can be performed within 90 minutes of first medical contact (or 120 minutes if transfer is required). This patient''s arrival at 45 minutes makes primary PCI the optimal choice. Aspirin should be given immediately, but PCI is superior to fibrinolysis when available. Conservative management alone is inadequate for STEMI.','PCI first if door-to-balloon < 90 minutes','The ''door-to-balloon'' time should be <90 minutes for primary PCI, and ''door-to-needle'' time <30 minutes for fibrinolysis when PCI is not available.','Fibrinolysis is an alternative when PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes, but PCI is superior when available. Aspirin and nitroglycerin are part of initial management but not sufficient alone. Conservative management is inappropriate for confirmed STEMI.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI Management','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI Management","Emergency Medicine","Reperfusion Therapy","PCI Guidelines","Time-Sensitive Care"]',true,'Emergency management algorithm for STEMI showing time-sensitive treatment pathway from symptom onset to reperfusion','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-a4720e9b-f4f.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:49:43.451Z','2026-10-21T17:49:43.451Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvieqekn002poku8waky62k0','mcq','A patient with anterior STEMI who underwent successful PCI 3 days ago now develops worsening shortness of breath and bilateral crackles on lung examination. His chest X-ray shows bilateral alveolar infiltrates and cardiomegaly. Which complication of STEMI is most likely responsible for these findings?','["Dressler syndrome","Ventricular septal rupture","Left ventricular free wall rupture","Acute heart failure with pulmonary edema"]','Acute heart failure with pulmonary edema','The patient''s symptoms of worsening shortness of breath, bilateral crackles, and chest X-ray findings of bilateral alveolar infiltrates and cardiomegaly are classic for acute heart failure with pulmonary edema. This commonly occurs after STEMI due to left ventricular dysfunction from the infarcted myocardium. The anterior wall involvement (from LAD occlusion) particularly affects the left ventricle''s pumping function. Dressler syndrome is a later complication (weeks) with pericarditis. Ventricular septal rupture and free wall rupture are mechanical complications that typically present earlier with hemodynamic collapse.','Anterior STEMI + pulmonary edema = LV dysfunction','Pulmonary edema after STEMI indicates significant left ventricular dysfunction and is associated with worse prognosis, requiring aggressive diuresis and afterload reduction.','Dressler syndrome presents with pleuritic chest pain and pericarditis weeks after MI. Ventricular septal rupture causes a harsh holosystolic murmur and sudden hemodynamic deterioration. Free wall rupture presents with electrical alternans and cardiac tamponade.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI Complications','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI Complications","Heart Failure","Pulmonary Edema","Post-STEMI","Complications"]',true,'Chest X-ray showing complications of STEMI including pulmonary edema with bilateral alveolar infiltrates and cardiomegaly','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-7afbd519-7bc.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T17:50:00.263Z','2026-10-21T17:50:00.263Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipib9l000gg0u87pt4wjaa','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of severe substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. What is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient?','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and schedule for elective cardiac catheterization","Initiate immediate reperfusion therapy with either primary PCI or fibrinolytics","Start beta-blocker therapy and monitor for 24 hours","Administer aspirin and arrange for outpatient stress testing","Obtain serial troponins and consider discharge if normal"]','Initiate immediate reperfusion therapy with either primary PCI or fibrinolytics','This patient has an inferior wall STEMI based on ECG findings showing ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. The standard of care for STEMI is immediate reperfusion therapy, which can be achieved through either primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) or fibrinolytic therapy. The choice between PCI and fibrinolytics depends on factors like time from symptom onset, availability of PCI center, and patient contraindications to fibrinolytics. Aspirin should also be administered immediately. The other options are incorrect because they delay appropriate treatment or fail to address the need for urgent reperfusion.','STEMI = Save Time, Every Minute Intervention','Inferior STEMI often involves the right coronary artery and may be associated with bradycardia and hypotension due to right ventricular involvement.','Option 1 is incorrect because STEMI requires immediate intervention, not scheduling. Option 3 is incorrect as beta-blockers should be used cautiously in STEMI and don''t replace reperfusion. Option 4 is incorrect because troponins are not diagnostic for acute STEMI management. Option 5 is incorrect because outpatient management is inappropriate for STEMI.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction with characteristic ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF','','','','','','[]','/agent-c7c236a8-27d.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T22:51:38.505Z','2026-10-21T22:51:38.505Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipjss2000ig0u8xt90epba','mcq','A 65-year-old female with a history of hypertension and smoking presents with crushing substernal chest pain for the past 2 hours. Her ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?','["Right coronary artery","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Right marginal artery","Posterior descending artery"]','Left anterior descending artery','ST elevation in leads V1-V4 indicates an anterior wall STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The LAD artery supplies the anterior interventricular septum and anterior wall of the left ventricle. The right coronary artery typically supplies the inferior wall (leads II, III, aVF), the left circumflex supplies the lateral wall (leads I, aVL, V5-V6), and the posterior descending artery supplies the inferior-posterior wall.','V1-V4 = LAD territory','Anterior STEMI has the highest mortality rate among STEMI types due to the large amount of myocardium supplied by the LAD artery.','Option 1 is incorrect as RCA occlusion typically causes inferior STEMI (II, III, aVF). Option 3 is incorrect as LCX occlusion causes lateral STEMI (I, aVL, V5-V6). Option 4 is incorrect as the right marginal artery is a branch of the RCA. Option 5 is incorrect as PDA is typically supplied by either RCA or LCX and causes inferior-posterior STEMI.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Coronary Anatomy","Cardiology"]',true,'Anatomical diagram showing coronary artery occlusion causing myocardial infarction with blocked coronary artery and affected myocardial tissue','','','','','','[]','/agent-5295cacb-5c6.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T22:52:47.858Z','2026-10-21T22:52:47.858Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviplkg9000kg0u8eqlneboz','mcq','A 72-year-old male presents with chest pain and his ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. What is the significance of the reciprocal ST depression?','["Indicates right ventricular involvement","Suggests concomitant inferior wall ischemia","Is a normal finding in anterior STEMI","Indicates the ECG is technically inadequate","Suggests the patient has pericarditis"]','Suggests concomitant inferior wall ischemia','Reciprocal ST depression in leads opposite to the ST elevation is a common finding in STEMI and indicates concomitant ischemia in the reciprocal territory. In this case, ST elevation in V1-V4 (anterior wall) with reciprocal ST depression in II, III, aVF (inferior wall) suggests that the ischemic process is affecting both territories, often due to a proximal LAD occlusion that affects multiple coronary territories. This finding is associated with larger infarct size and worse prognosis.','Reciprocal depression = Widespread ischemia','Reciprocal ST depression is a marker of larger infarct size and higher risk of complications in STEMI patients.','Option 1 is incorrect as RV involvement would show ST elevation in V4R. Option 3 is incorrect as reciprocal depression is not normal and indicates more extensive disease. Option 4 is incorrect as this is a clinically significant finding, not a technical issue. Option 5 is incorrect as pericarditis typically shows diffuse ST elevation without reciprocal changes.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Pathophysiology","Cardiology"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST-segment elevation in V1-V4 and reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, aVF','','','','','','[]','/agent-d18c2843-ca5.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T22:54:10.377Z','2026-10-21T22:54:10.377Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipm39c000lg0u834fp7iax','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG is shown above. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded in this STEMI?','["Left anterior descending (LAD) artery","Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery","Ramus intermedius artery","Diagonal branch"]','Right coronary artery (RCA)','The ECG shows ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, which indicates an inferior wall STEMI. The inferior wall of the heart is primarily supplied by the right coronary artery (RCA) in approximately 85-90% of individuals. The LAD artery supplies the anterior wall, the left circumflex supplies the lateral wall, and the other options are smaller branches that don''t supply the inferior wall.','"II, III, aVF = RCA" - Inferior leads point to right coronary artery involvement','Inferior STEMI may also present with bradycardia and hypotension due to right ventricular involvement, requiring different fluid management strategies','LAD artery would cause anterior STEMI with ST elevation in V1-V4; left circumflex would cause lateral STEMI with ST elevation in I, aVL, V5-V6; ramus intermedius and diagonal are smaller branches that don''t cause inferior wall changes','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","myocardial infarction","coronary anatomy"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) with characteristic ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, showing inferior wall MI','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-db0f6376-171.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T22:54:34.752Z','2026-10-21T22:54:34.752Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipv5oh000tg0u874vnr1sx','mcq','A 58-year-old woman with diabetes and hypertension presents with severe chest pain. Her 12-lead ECG is shown below.

![12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V4 and reciprocal changes in inferior leads](/generated/agent-177ae63c-c01.jpg)

Which coronary artery is most likely occluded in this patient?','["Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Right ventricular branch","Diagonal branch"]','Left anterior descending artery (LAD)','The ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4, which corresponds to the anterior wall of the heart. The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies the anterior interventricular septum and most of the anterior wall. Reciprocal ST depression in the inferior leads (II, III, aVF) is also characteristic of anterior STEMI. The RCA supplies the inferior wall, LCx supplies the lateral wall, and the other options are smaller branches.','"V1-V4 = LAD territory"','Anterior STEMI carries the highest mortality rate among STEMI subtypes due to extensive myocardium at risk and potential for cardiogenic shock.','RCA occlusion would cause inferior STEMI; LCx occlusion would cause lateral STEMI; RV and diagonal branches are smaller vessels that don''t cause this pattern of ST elevation.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Coronary Anatomy","Cardiology"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V4 and reciprocal changes in inferior leads','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-177ae63c-c01.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:01:37.793Z','2026-10-21T23:01:37.793Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipvags000ug0u8q2zbcp6r','mcq','A 65-year-old male with acute inferior STEMI is undergoing emergent cardiac catheterization. The angiogram shows complete occlusion of the culprit vessel.

![Coronary angiogram showing complete occlusion of the left anterior descending artery with thrombus formation](/generated/agent-83aeda11-0fb.jpg)

Which of the following medications should be administered immediately before PCI to improve outcomes?','["Aspirin 324 mg orally","Clopidogrel 600 mg orally","Ticagrelor 180 mg orally","All of the above","None of the above"]','All of the above','Current guidelines recommend dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) with aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor (clopidogrel, ticagrelor, or prasugrel) for patients undergoing PCI for STEMI. Aspirin 324 mg (loading dose) should be given immediately, along with a loading dose of a P2Y12 inhibitor. Ticagrelor 180 mg is preferred over clopidogrel unless contraindicated. All three medications are part of the standard pre-PCI regimen.','"STEMI PCI = Aspirin + P2Y12 = Double Protection"','Ticagrelor is preferred over clopidogrel in STEMI due to faster onset and more consistent platelet inhibition, but requires twice-daily dosing.','Each option alone is insufficient; aspirin alone is inadequate; clopidogrel alone is suboptimal; ticagrelor alone misses aspirin benefit.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Interventional Cardiology","Pharmacology","Emergency Medicine"]',true,'Coronary angiogram showing complete occlusion of the left anterior descending artery with thrombus formation','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-83aeda11-0fb.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:01:43.996Z','2026-10-21T23:01:43.996Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipveu9000vg0u89r1124d8','mcq','A 72-year-old man with acute anterior STEMI develops worsening shortness of breath. His chest X-ray is shown below.

![Chest X-ray showing pulmonary edema with bilateral infiltrates and cardiomegaly in STEMI patient](/generated/agent-618d1459-d29.jpg)

Which of the following best explains the finding on this chest X-ray?','["Pneumonia requiring antibiotics","Pulmonary embolism requiring anticoagulation","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema from left ventricular dysfunction","Pleural effusion from right heart failure","Atelectasis from immobility"]','Cardiogenic pulmonary edema from left ventricular dysfunction','The chest X-ray shows bilateral infiltrates consistent with pulmonary edema. In anterior STEMI, the left ventricle is often severely damaged, leading to acute heart failure and cardiogenic pulmonary edema. The bilateral infiltrates are typically perihilar and bat-wing shaped, unlike the lobar consolidation seen in pneumonia. This is a common complication of large anterior STEMI due to extensive myocardial damage and reduced cardiac output.','"Anterior STEMI = LV damage = pulmonary edema"','Cardiogenic pulmonary edema in STEMI requires diuresis, oxygen, and afterload reduction, not antibiotics or anticoagulation.','Pneumonia would show lobar consolidation; PE would show wedge-shaped infarcts; pleural effusion would show blunted costophrenic angles; atelectasis would show linear densities.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Imaging","Heart Failure","Emergency Medicine"]',true,'Chest X-ray showing pulmonary edema with bilateral infiltrates and cardiomegaly in STEMI patient','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-618d1459-d29.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:01:49.665Z','2026-10-21T23:01:49.665Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvipvizd000wg0u8sk7wnvdx','mcq','A 60-year-old male presents with chest pain and ECG changes consistent with STEMI. The emergency department management protocol is shown below.

![Emergency department management protocol for STEMI showing ECG interpretation, catheterization lab activation, and time-sensitive interventions](/generated/agent-beac33fa-dce.jpg)

According to current guidelines, what is the target time from first medical contact to balloon inflation for primary PCI in STEMI?','["60 minutes","90 minutes","120 minutes","180 minutes","240 minutes"]','90 minutes','Current ACC/AHA guidelines recommend a door-to-balloon time of ≤90 minutes for patients with STEMI undergoing primary PCI. This "time is muscle" approach emphasizes rapid reperfusion to minimize myocardial damage. For patients presenting to non-PCI capable hospitals, the goal is transfer to a PCI-capable hospital within 120 minutes. The 90-minute target is based on extensive evidence showing improved outcomes with faster reperfusion times.','"STEMI PCI = 90 minutes = Save Time, Save Muscle"','Time metrics are crucial: door-to-ECG ≤10 minutes, first medical contact to device ≤90 minutes, and door-to-needle ≤30 minutes for fibrinolytic therapy.','60 minutes is unrealistic for most hospitals; 120 minutes is the transfer goal, not direct PCI; 180+ minutes leads to significantly worse outcomes.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Guidelines","Emergency Medicine","Quality Improvement"]',true,'Emergency department management protocol for STEMI showing ECG interpretation, catheterization lab activation, and time-sensitive interventions','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-beac33fa-dce.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:01:55.033Z','2026-10-21T23:01:55.033Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviq0xj5000yg0u8d234tzeu','mcq','A 58-year-old male with a history of hypertension and smoking presents to the emergency department with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm that began 2 hours ago. On examination, he appears diaphoretic and anxious. His vital signs are BP 145/90 mmHg, HR 105 bpm, RR 18/min, and oxygen saturation 95% on room air. The following ECG is obtained: [STEMI ECG Image]. What is the most appropriate immediate next step in management?','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and observe for pain relief","Initiate fibrinolytic therapy immediately","Transfer to PCI-capable facility for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PPCI)","Obtain serial cardiac enzymes before initiating therapy","Start aspirin and heparin, then reassess in 1 hour"]','Transfer to PCI-capable facility for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PPCI)','The ECG shows classic STEMI findings with >1mm ST elevation in contiguous anterior leads (V1-V4) and reciprocal ST depression in inferior leads. According to current guidelines [S1], STEMI should preferentially be transported to a PCI-capable hospital with a goal of first medical contact (FMC) to first device time of ≤90 minutes. Primary PCI is the preferred reperfusion strategy when it can be performed promptly. While aspirin and heparin should be administered, the immediate priority is arranging rapid transport to a PCI center.','PCI-Priority: Pain, Cardiac monitoring, Immediate PCI transport','Time is muscle - the ''door-to-balloon'' time should be ≤90 minutes for primary PCI, and ''door-to-needle'' time ≤30 minutes for fibrinolytics. Every 30-minute delay in reperfusion increases mortality by 7-8%.','Option A is incorrect because nitroglycerin alone is insufficient for STEMI management. Option B is incorrect because fibrinolytics are second-line when PCI is available within 90 minutes. Option C is incorrect because waiting for enzymes delays life-saving reperfusion. Option E is incorrect because while aspirin/heparin should be given, immediate PCI transport takes priority.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","ECG","Reperfusion","Emergency Medicine","Acute Coronary Syndrome"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction in anterior leads (V1-V4) with >1mm ST elevation in contiguous leads, hyperacute T waves, and reciprocal ST depression in inferior leads','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-c7fb58c4-0e9.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:06:07.170Z','2026-10-21T23:06:07.170Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviq131u000zg0u89rfuvd5w','mcq','A 62-year-old female with diabetes and hyperlipidemia presents with severe chest pain. Her ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. Coronary angiography reveals occlusion of which coronary artery most likely caused this ECG finding?','["Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left main coronary artery","Diagonal branch of LAD"]','Right coronary artery (RCA)','ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates an inferior wall STEMI. The right coronary artery (RCA) typically supplies the inferior wall of the left ventricle, including the inferior septum and inferior wall. The RCA also supplies the sinoatrial (SA) node in 60% of patients and the atrioventricular (AV) node in 90% of patients, which is why inferior STEMIs often present with bradycardia and heart block. The LAD supplies the anterior wall (V1-V4), the LCx supplies the lateral wall (I, aVL, V5-V6), and the left main supplies both the LAD and LCx.','II-III-aVF = RCA (Right Coronary Artery)','Inferior STEMIs often present with bradycardia, hypotension, or heart block due to involvement of the SA/AV nodes. These patients may require atropine or temporary pacing in addition to standard STEMI management.','Option A is incorrect because LAD occlusion causes anterior STEMI (V1-V4). Option B is incorrect because LCx occlusion causes lateral STEMI (I, aVL, V5-V6). Option D is incorrect because left main occlusion typically causes widespread ST depression or anterior/lateral STEMI. Option E is incorrect because diagonal branches supply the anterior wall, not inferior wall.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","Coronary Anatomy","ECG","Inferior MI","Angiography"]',true,'Anatomical cross-section of heart showing coronary artery occlusion in right coronary artery causing inferior STEMI with labeled coronary arteries and affected myocardial territory','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-9e30a3de-d5d.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:06:14.322Z','2026-10-21T23:06:14.322Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviq18rz0010g0u8malb1vw7','mcq','A 65-year-old male with known coronary artery disease presents to the emergency department with 45 minutes of crushing substernal chest pain. His ECG shows new ST elevation in leads V2-V4. He has no contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy. The nearest PCI-capable hospital is 90 minutes away by ambulance. What is the most appropriate reperfusion strategy?','["Transport to PCI-capable hospital for primary PCI","Administer fibrinolytic therapy immediately in the emergency department","Wait 60 minutes and then transport to PCI hospital","Administer fibrinolytic therapy only if PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes","Start medical therapy only and defer reperfusion"]','Administer fibrinolytic therapy immediately in the emergency department','According to guidelines [S1], fibrinolytic therapy should be administered to patients with STEMI when primary PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes of first medical contact. In this case, the PCI hospital is 90 minutes away, meaning the door-to-balloon time would likely exceed 120 minutes. Fibrinolytics should be given within 30 minutes of hospital arrival (door-to-needle time ≤30 minutes). The time saved by administering fibrinolytics in the emergency department rather than waiting for transport outweighs the benefit of PCI when the transfer time is this long.','120-Minute Rule: PCI preferred if within 120 minutes, otherwise fibrinolytics','The ''door-to-needle'' time for fibrinolytics should be ≤30 minutes, and the ''door-to-balloon'' time for PCI should be ≤90 minutes. When PCI is delayed beyond 120 minutes, fibrinolytics become the preferred strategy.','Option A is incorrect because PCI would exceed the 120-minute time window. Option C is incorrect because waiting delays reperfusion and worsens outcomes. Option D is incorrect because fibrinolytics should be given immediately when PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes. Option E is incorrect because reperfusion therapy is mandatory in STEMI.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","Reperfusion Strategy","Fibrinolytics","PCI","Time-Sensitive Care"]',true,'Emergency management timeline for STEMI patient showing time-sensitive interventions: door-to-ECG, door-to-balloon, door-to-needle times with critical time windows','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-86e7742c-abb.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:06:21.743Z','2026-10-21T23:06:21.743Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviq1dze0012g0u85st5bt5c','mcq','A 72-year-old male with an anterior STEMI who underwent primary PCI 12 hours ago suddenly develops palpitations and becomes unresponsive. His monitor shows the following rhythm: [VTach ECG Image]. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?','["Synchronized electrical cardioversion","Unsynchronized electrical cardioversion","Amiodarone 150 mg IV push","Lidocaine 1 mg/kg IV push","Sedation and observation"]','Unsynchronized electrical cardioversion','The patient has developed sustained ventricular tachycardia (VT) as a complication of his STEMI. In unstable patients (unresponsive, hypotensive, or with signs of poor perfusion), unsynchronized electrical cardioversion (defibrillation) is the treatment of choice for VT. Synchronized cardioversion is used for stable tachycardias to avoid R-on-T phenomenon, but in unstable VT, immediate defibrillation takes priority. While antiarrhythmics like amiodarone or lidocaine may be used, they are second-line interventions for unstable VT.','Unstable VT = Unsync Cardioversion','VT in the setting of acute MI is often due to reentry around the infarct border zone. Early reperfusion reduces the risk of malignant arrhythmias. All post-MI patients should be monitored for arrhythmias, especially in the first 24-48 hours.','Option A is incorrect because synchronized cardioversion is for stable tachycardias. Option C is incorrect because amiodarone is second-line for unstable VT. Option D is incorrect because lidocaine is second-line and less effective than cardioversion. Option E is incorrect because unstable VT requires immediate intervention, not observation.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","Arrhythmias","Complications","Emergency Medicine","Ventricular Tachycardia"]',true,'ECG showing ventricular tachycardia as complication of STEMI with baseline sinus rhythm, PVCs, and degeneration into sustained ventricular tachycardia','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-f5341603-e88.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:06:28.490Z','2026-10-21T23:06:28.490Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviq1ja70013g0u8kss21ill','mcq','A 35-year-old female presents to the emergency department with pleuritic chest pain, fever, and a friction rub. Her ECG shows diffuse ST elevation. Which of the following findings would help differentiate pericarditis from STEMI?','["ST elevation in contiguous leads","Reciprocal ST depression","PR segment depression","Pathological Q waves","Elevated troponin"]','PR segment depression','Pericarditis and STEMI can both present with ST elevation on ECG, but key differences help distinguish them. Pericarditis typically shows diffuse, concave ST elevation (often in multiple territories without reciprocal changes), while STEMI shows contiguous ST elevation with reciprocal depression. PR segment depression is characteristic of pericarditis due to atrial injury, whereas STEMI typically has normal or elevated PR segments. Pathological Q waves develop later in STEMI (hours to days) and are not seen in pericarditis. Elevated troponin occurs in both conditions but is typically much higher in STEMI.','Pericarditis: PR depression, Diffuse ST elevation, No reciprocal changes','The ''VR'' sign (VR = ventricular repolarization) can help differentiate: pericarditis shows concave VR (ST elevation), while STEMI shows convex VR (ST elevation). PR segment depression is highly specific for pericarditis.','Option A is incorrect because contiguous ST elevation suggests STEMI, not pericarditis. Option B is incorrect because reciprocal ST depression is characteristic of STEMI, not pericarditis. Option D is incorrect because pathological Q waves develop in STEMI but not pericarditis. Option E is incorrect because both conditions can have elevated troponin, though typically higher in STEMI.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","Differential Diagnosis","Pericarditis","ECG","Emergency Medicine"]',true,'ECG comparison showing STEMI vs pericarditis vs early repolarization with side-by-side ECGs highlighting key differences in ST elevation patterns','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-9b299af4-e97.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:06:35.359Z','2026-10-21T23:06:35.359Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvira0j9000ggpu8ln4rxo9a','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm, diaphoresis, and shortness of breath. On examination, he appears anxious and diaphoretic. Blood pressure is 145/90 mmHg, heart rate is 110 bpm, and respiratory rate is 22/min. An ECG is performed, showing ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. 

![ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction with characteristic ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF](/generated/agent-f7ed5e8b-6c5.jpg)

What is the most likely diagnosis and what coronary artery is most likely affected?','["Anterior STEMI with left anterior descending artery occlusion","Inferior STEMI with right coronary artery occlusion","Lateral STEMI with left circumflex artery occlusion","Posterior STEMI with right coronary artery occlusion","Non-ST elevation MI with left main coronary artery disease"]','Inferior STEMI with right coronary artery occlusion','The ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, which is characteristic of an inferior STEMI. The right coronary artery (RCA) typically supplies the inferior wall of the left ventricle, including the right ventricle in most individuals (85-90%). The patient''s symptoms of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to the left arm, diaphoresis, and shortness of breath are classic for acute coronary syndrome. The elevated heart rate and respiratory rate reflect the sympathetic response to myocardial ischemia.','"II, III, aVF = RCA below" - Inferior leads indicate right coronary artery involvement','Inferior STEMI may be associated with bradycardia and hypotension due to right ventricular involvement, which can complicate management with nitrates.','Anterior STEMI would show ST elevation in V1-V4; lateral STEMI would show ST elevation in I, aVL, V5-V6; posterior STEMI would show ST depression in V1-V4 with reciprocal changes; NSTEMI would show ST depression or T-wave changes without ST elevation.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Myocardial Infarction","Emergency Medicine","Coronary Arteries"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation myocardial infarction with characteristic ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-f7ed5e8b-6c5.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:41:10.581Z','2026-10-21T23:41:10.581Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvira6b1000hgpu8l4spr68p','mcq','A 58-year-old female with a history of hypertension and smoking presents to the emergency department with severe chest pain and shortness of breath. Her ECG reveals ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4. 

![Coronary artery anatomy showing acute thrombotic occlusion causing STEMI](/generated/agent-2802e88a-392.jpg)

Which coronary artery is most likely responsible for this ECG finding, and what is the typical time window for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)?','["Right coronary artery; 12-24 hours","Left circumflex artery; 6-12 hours","Left anterior descending artery; 90 minutes","Left main coronary artery; 24-48 hours","Right coronary artery; 90 minutes"]','Left anterior descending artery; 90 minutes','ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4 indicates an anterior STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The LAD artery supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, including the septum and anterior free wall. Current guidelines recommend primary PCI within 90 minutes (door-to-balloon time) for patients with STEMI to minimize myocardial damage and improve outcomes. This "time is muscle" principle emphasizes the importance of rapid reperfusion therapy.','"V1-V4 = LAD in the door" - Anterior leads indicate left anterior descending artery involvement requiring rapid door-to-balloon time','Anterior STEMI carries a higher risk of complications including cardiogenic shock, ventricular arrhythmias, and mortality compared to other STEMI locations due to the large territory supplied by the LAD artery.','Right coronary artery occlusion typically causes inferior STEMI (II, III, aVF); left circumflex artery occlusion causes lateral STEMI (I, aVL, V5-V6); left main coronary artery occlusion typically causes extensive STEMI in multiple territories but is less common; while time windows vary, 90 minutes is the standard for primary PCI.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Myocardial Infarction","Interventional Cardiology","Emergency Medicine"]',true,'Coronary artery anatomy showing acute thrombotic occlusion causing STEMI','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-2802e88a-392.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:41:18.061Z','2026-10-21T23:41:18.061Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvirabpf000igpu8b5nkkza4','mcq','A 65-year-old male with diabetes and hyperlipidemia presents to the emergency department with chest pain. His initial ECG shows subtle ST-segment depression in leads V1-V3. After 30 minutes, a repeat ECG is performed.

![12-lead ECG comparison showing normal ECG vs STEMI with ST elevation in anterior leads](/generated/agent-5645558c-83d.jpg)

Which of the following statements about this patient''s condition is most accurate?','["The initial ECG findings are diagnostic of NSTEMI","The ST depression suggests posterior STEMI","The patient likely needs emergent cardiac catheterization","The ECG changes are likely due to pericarditis","The patient should be discharged home with outpatient follow-up"]','The patient likely needs emergent cardiac catheterization','The initial ECG showing ST-segment depression in leads V1-V3, followed by the development of ST-segment elevation in these leads on repeat ECG, is highly concerning for evolving STEMI. This pattern represents dynamic ECG changes indicating ongoing myocardial injury. ST-segment depression in the anterior leads can sometimes represent reciprocal changes of a posterior STEMI, but the subsequent development of ST elevation confirms anterior STEMI. Current guidelines mandate emergent reperfusion therapy with primary PCI for STEMI within 90 minutes of first medical contact.','"ST depression then elevation = evolution, not resolution" - Dynamic ECG changes indicate ongoing myocardial injury requiring urgent intervention','Serial ECGs are crucial in patients with suspected acute coronary syndrome, as ECG findings can evolve over time. A normal initial ECG does not rule out STEMI.','ST depression alone may indicate NSTEMI but is not diagnostic; posterior STEMI typically shows ST depression in V1-V3 with tall R waves; pericarditis typically shows diffuse ST elevation without reciprocal changes; STEMI requires hospital admission and emergent intervention, not discharge.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Myocardial Infarction","Emergency Medicine","Dynamic Changes"]',true,'12-lead ECG comparison showing normal ECG vs STEMI with ST elevation in anterior leads','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-5645558c-83d.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:41:25.059Z','2026-10-21T23:41:25.059Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisot13000e4lu8q1pk1km3','mcq','A 65-year-old male with STEMI is undergoing primary PCI. After successful stent placement, which medication should be initiated immediately to prevent stent thrombosis?','["Aspirin alone","Clopidogrel alone","Aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor (dual antiplatelet therapy)","Warfarin"]','Aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor (dual antiplatelet therapy)','Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) with aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor (such as clopidogrel, ticagrelor, or prasugrel) is standard after stent placement in STEMI. This combination provides synergistic antiplatelet effects to prevent stent thrombosis. Aspirin alone or clopidogrel alone would be insufficient. Warfarin is an anticoagulant, not an antiplatelet agent, and is not routinely used for stent thrombosis prevention unless there''s an additional indication.','PCI = Aspirin + P2Y12 (DAPT)','Duration of DAPT depends on stent type (1 month for BMS, 6-12 months for DES) and bleeding risk','Aspirin alone: Insufficient for stent thrombosis prevention; Clopidogrel alone: Less effective than combination therapy; Warfarin: Anticoagulant, not antiplatelet','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","PCI","Pharmacology","Stent"]',true,'Coronary artery with stent placement diagram showing dual antiplatelet therapy concept','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/stent_placement.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:20:40.311Z','2026-10-22T00:20:40.311Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwyfoj0011g7rdcqqlhbof','mcq','A 52-year-old male with hyperlipidemia is started on simvastatin 40mg daily. Two months later, he presents with severe muscle pain and weakness. Laboratory tests show creatine kinase (CK) elevated to 10 times the upper limit of normal. Which of the following statements regarding this patient''s condition is most accurate?','["Simvastatin has the lowest risk of rhabdomyolysis among all statins","The risk of rhabdomyolysis is independent of statin dose","Concurrent use of cytochrome P450 inhibitors would increase the risk","Rhabdomyolysis typically occurs within the first week of statin therapy","CK elevation is not a reliable indicator of muscle injury"]','Concurrent use of cytochrome P450 inhibitors would increase the risk','The patient''s presentation is consistent with statin-induced rhabdomyolysis. Simvastatin actually has one of the highest risks of rhabdomyolysis among statins, with studies showing it has more than a 2-fold increased likelihood compared to other statins [S3]. The risk of rhabdomyolysis increases with higher statin doses [S1]. Concurrent use of cytochrome P450 inhibitors (such as clarithromycin, cyclosporine, and grapefruit juice) significantly increases the risk by reducing statin metabolism and increasing systemic exposure [S2]. Rhabdomyolysis typically occurs after weeks to months of therapy, not within the first week, and CK elevation is a reliable indicator of muscle injury [S1].','Simvastatin + CYP inhibitors = High risk rhabdomyolysis','Simvastatin carries higher rhabdomyolysis risk than other statins, and this risk is significantly increased when combined with CYP450 inhibitors.','Option A is incorrect as simvastatin has higher risk. Option B is wrong as risk increases with dose. Option D is incorrect as rhabdomyolysis typically occurs after weeks. Option E is wrong as CK is a reliable indicator.','Medium','cardiovascular','statins','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","statins","hyperlipidemia","statins","rhabdomyolysis","drug interactions"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:31:12.739Z','2026-10-24T21:31:12.739Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvirahxi000jgpu81a14ngzp','mcq','A 70-year-old male with known coronary artery disease presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of severe chest pain. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V2-V4. He is taken emergently to the cardiac catheterization laboratory.

![Cardiac catheterization showing coronary artery occlusion and angioplasty balloon inflation](/generated/agent-9eca195f-d05.jpg)

During the procedure, the interventional cardiologist identifies a 95% stenosis in the proximal left anterior descending artery. Which of the following statements about the management of this patient is most accurate?','["Thrombolytic therapy should be administered before PCI","Bivalirudin is preferred over unfractionated heparin","Drug-eluting stents have no advantage over bare-metal stents in STEMI","GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors should be routinely administered","Aspirin should be withheld until after the procedure"]','Bivalirudin is preferred over unfractionated heparin','For patients with STEMI undergoing primary PCI, current guidelines recommend bivalirudin as an alternative to unfractionated heparin, particularly in patients at high bleeding risk. Bivalirudin provides comparable efficacy to heparin plus GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors but with significantly reduced bleeding complications. Thrombolytic therapy is not indicated when primary PCI can be performed within 90 minutes. Drug-eluting stents are preferred over bare-metal stents in STEMI due to reduced restenosis rates. GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors are not routinely recommended in the contemporary era of potent P2Y12 inhibitors. Aspirin should be administered as soon as possible, ideally before the procedure.','"Bivalirudin bleeds less" - In STEMI PCI, bivalirudin reduces bleeding compared to heparin plus GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors','The radial artery approach for primary PCI is associated with reduced bleeding complications and mortality compared to the femoral approach, particularly in high-risk patients.','Thrombolytics are contraindicated when PCI can be performed promptly; drug-eluting stents reduce restenosis compared to bare-metal stents; GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors are not routinely used in contemporary STEMI management; aspirin should be given early, not withheld.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Interventional Cardiology","Pharmacology","Emergency Medicine","PCI"]',true,'Cardiac catheterization showing coronary artery occlusion and angioplasty balloon inflation','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-9eca195f-d05.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:41:33.126Z','2026-10-21T23:41:33.126Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviranpb000kgpu8rp5n19co','mcq','A 55-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 3 hours of crushing chest pain. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V6 with reciprocal ST depression in the inferior leads. Cardiac biomarkers are significantly elevated.

![Myocardial infarction pathology showing necrosis and inflammation in heart muscle tissue](/generated/agent-f830598c-fcb.jpg)

Which of the following complications is this patient at highest risk for developing in the first 48 hours?','["Cardiac tamponade","Right ventricular infarction","Ventricular free wall rupture","Pulmonary embolism","Pericarditis"]','Ventricular free wall rupture','This patient has an extensive anterior STEMI involving multiple anterior leads (V1-V6) with reciprocal changes in the inferior leads, indicating a large territory of myocardium at risk. Extensive anterior STEMI carries the highest risk of ventricular free wall rupture, which typically occurs within 3-5 days after the infarct but can occur as early as 24 hours. The risk is highest in the first week, particularly in elderly patients, females, and those with hypertension. Cardiac tamponade would be a complication of rupture, but the primary concern is the rupture itself. Right ventricular infarction typically occurs with inferior STEMI. Pulmonary embolism and pericarditis are complications but are less specific to extensive anterior STEMI.','"Large anterior = rupture risk" - Extensive anterior STEMI has the highest risk of ventricular free wall rupture','Ventricular free wall rupture is a catastrophic complication of STEMI with mortality rates exceeding 90%. Early signs include recurrent chest pain, hemodynamic instability, and new pericardial effusion on echocardiogram.','Cardiac tamponade is a consequence of rupture, not a primary complication; right ventricular infarction typically occurs with inferior STEMI; pulmonary embolism and pericarditis are possible complications but are not as specifically associated with extensive anterior STEMI as rupture.','Hard','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Complications","Myocardial Infarction","Emergency Medicine","Critical Care"]',true,'Myocardial infarction pathology showing necrosis and inflammation in heart muscle tissue','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-f830598c-fcb.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-21T23:41:40.607Z','2026-10-21T23:41:40.607Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisokfk000c4lu8mnbo1k9l','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm, diaphoresis, and shortness of breath. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?','["Administer aspirin 325 mg and call for cardiac catheterization","Administer nitroglycerin and monitor vital signs","Administer morphine and oxygen","Obtain serial cardiac enzymes and wait for results"]','Administer aspirin 325 mg and call for cardiac catheterization','The patient''s presentation with ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates an inferior wall STEMI. The most appropriate initial management includes immediate aspirin administration (325 mg) and activation of the cardiac catheterization lab for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation and is a cornerstone of STEMI management. Nitroglycerin may be used but should not delay primary PCI. Morphine may be used for pain control but is not the first priority. Waiting for cardiac enzyme results would delay definitive treatment.','STEMI: Aspirin + Cath lab fast','Time is muscle - door-to-balloon time should be <90 minutes for primary PCI in STEMI','Nitroglycerin: May be used but should not delay primary PCI; Morphine: For pain control but not first priority; Waiting for enzymes: Delays definitive treatment','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency","PCI"]',true,'ECG showing ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/STEMI_ECG_example.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:20:29.168Z','2026-10-22T00:20:29.168Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisoory000d4lu8ajybc2h0','mcq','A 58-year-old female with a history of hypertension and diabetes presents with 45 minutes of severe chest pain. Her ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded in this STEMI?','["Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Right ventricular branch"]','Left anterior descending artery (LAD)','ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4 indicates an anterior wall STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The LAD supplies the anterior interventricular septum, anterior wall of the left ventricle, and apex. The RCA typically supplies inferior wall (leads II, III, aVF), while the LCx supplies lateral wall (leads I, aVL, V5-V6). Right ventricular involvement would typically show ST elevation in right-sided chest leads (V3R, V4R).','V1-V4 = LAD territory','Anterior STEMI has the highest mortality rate among STEMI subtypes due to large myocardial territory at risk','RCA: Typically causes inferior STEMI (II, III, aVF); LCx: Typically causes lateral STEMI (I, aVL, V5-V6); Right ventricular branch: Would affect right-sided chest leads','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Coronary anatomy","Anterior STEMI"]',true,'ECG showing ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4 with coronary artery anatomy diagram','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/anterior_stemi_ecg.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:20:34.798Z','2026-10-22T00:20:34.798Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoyih2l000yscwun7yxtwti','flashcard','What is the primary function of atria?','[]','Atria serve as receiving chambers that collect blood returning to the heart and pump it into the ventricles','Atria serve as receiving chambers that collect blood returning to the heart and pump it into the ventricles','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart function','["flashcard","Atria vs Ventricles comparison"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z','2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoyih2n0010scwumh8wm74l','flashcard','What is the primary function of ventricles?','[]','Ventricles serve as pumping chambers that generate pressure to eject blood out of the heart - left ventricle to body, right ventricle to lungs','Ventricles serve as pumping chambers that generate pressure to eject blood out of the heart - left ventricle to body, right ventricle to lungs','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart function','["flashcard","Atria vs Ventricles comparison"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z','2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisox17000f4lu87dcqyzmj','mcq','A 70-year-old patient with STEMI is receiving fibrinolytic therapy due to delayed presentation to a hospital without PCI capabilities. Which finding would be a contraindication to fibrinolytic administration?','["Age >65 years","Blood pressure 160/95 mmHg","History of hypertension","Previous intracranial hemorrhage"]','Previous intracranial hemorrhage','Previous intracranial hemorrhage is an absolute contraindication to fibrinolytic therapy. Fibrinolytics increase the risk of bleeding, particularly intracranial hemorrhage. Age >65 years is a relative risk factor but not a contraindication. Elevated blood pressure (160/95 mmHg) is a relative contraindication but may be acceptable if controlled. History of hypertension is not a contraindication. The absolute contraindications include active bleeding, known structural cerebrovascular lesion, and previous intracranial hemorrhage.','Fibrinolytic contraindications: Bleeding, Brain, Blood pressure','Fibrinolytics are most effective when administered within 12 hours of symptom onset, with benefit decreasing over time','Age >65: Relative risk factor, not contraindication; BP 160/95: Relative contraindication; Hypertension history: Not contraindication','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","Fibrinolytics","Emergency","Contraindications"]',true,'Fibrinolytic therapy algorithm with contraindications highlighted','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/fibrinolytic_contraindications.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:20:45.499Z','2026-10-22T00:20:45.499Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisp14r000g4lu8nmgfx5sx','mcq','A 55-year-old male with inferior STEMI develops bradycardia and hypotension during the procedure. His ECG shows sinus bradycardia with first-degree AV block. Which coronary artery occlusion is most likely causing this complication?','["Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Right coronary artery","Left main coronary artery"]','Right coronary artery','Inferior STEMI with bradycardia and conduction abnormalities suggests right coronary artery (RCA) occlusion. The RCA typically supplies the sinoatrial (SA) node and atrioventricular (AV) node in 90% of individuals. RCA occlusion can cause sinus bradycardia, AV block, or junctional escape rhythms. The left anterior descending artery typically causes anterior STEMI without conduction abnormalities. The left circum artery affects lateral wall. Left main occlusion would cause massive anterior STEMI with cardiogenic shock.','RCA = Right coronary artery → Bradycardia and AV block','Inferior STEMI with bradycardia/atropine responsive suggests RCA occlusion; may require temporary pacing if severe','LAD: Causes anterior STEMI without conduction issues; LCx: Causes lateral STEMI; Left main: Causes massive anterior STEMI with shock','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Complications","Conduction"]',true,'ECG showing inferior STEMI with bradycardia and coronary artery anatomy','','','','','','[]','https://i.imgur.com/inferior_stemi_conduction.png','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:20:50.811Z','2026-10-22T00:20:50.811Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviswonp000i4lu8nn9kp59a','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 3mm in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management strategy?','["Wait for cardiac biomarker results before initiating treatment","Immediate fibrinolytic therapy","Transfer to PCI-capable facility for primary PCI","Aspirin and heparin only, delay reperfusion","Nitroglycerin and morphine for symptom control"]','Transfer to PCI-capable facility for primary PCI','The ECG findings are diagnostic of inferior STEMI. According to current guidelines [S1], reperfusion therapy should not be delayed pending biomarker results. Primary PCI is the preferred reperfusion strategy when it can be performed within 90 minutes of first medical contact. For inferior STEMI, transfer to a PCI-capable facility is appropriate if it can be achieved within 120 minutes.','PCI first when possible, within 90 minutes','Reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL confirms the inferior STEMI diagnosis and helps localize the culprit artery as the right coronary artery.','Option A is incorrect because biomarkers are not required for STEMI diagnosis and reperfusion should not be delayed. Option B is incorrect because fibrinolysis is only preferred when PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes. Option D is incorrect because reperfusion is essential. Option E is incorrect because symptom control alone is inadequate for STEMI.','Medium','cardiovascular','STEMI diagnosis and management','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI diagnosis and management","ECG","reperfusion","PCI","STEMI"]',true,'ECG tracing showing ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF (inferior STEMI) with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-b4969a88-c08.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:26:47.893Z','2026-10-22T00:26:47.893Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisx18t000j4lu81ztv1h6p','mcq','Which coronary artery occlusion is most likely responsible for the ECG findings in the previous question (ST elevation in II, III, aVF with reciprocal depression in I, aVL)?','["Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Right coronary artery","Left main coronary artery","Ramus intermedius artery"]','Right coronary artery','The ECG pattern of ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL is characteristic of right coronary artery occlusion [S2]. The right coronary artery typically supplies the inferior wall of the left ventricle, the right ventricle, and the posterior portion of the septum. This anatomical distribution explains the specific lead involvement in the ECG.','II, III, aVF = RCA (Right Coronary Artery)','Inferior STEMI may also involve the right ventricle, so consider obtaining right-sided ECG leads (V4R) to assess for right ventricular involvement which affects management.','Option A is incorrect as LAD occlusion typically causes anterior STEMI (V1-V4). Option B is incorrect as LCX occlusion typically causes lateral STEMI (I, aVL, V5-V6). Option D is incorrect as left main occlusion typically causes widespread ST changes. Option E is incorrect as ramus intermedius is a variant of the LCX.','Medium','cardiovascular','STEMI coronary anatomy','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI coronary anatomy","coronary anatomy","ECG localization","right coronary artery"]',true,'Anatomical cross-section of the heart showing coronary artery anatomy with an occluded right coronary artery causing inferior wall myocardial infarction','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-3ec126bc-9a8.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:27:04.205Z','2026-10-22T00:27:04.205Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoyih2r0012scwuq58si2y2','flashcard','Which chamber has the thickest wall and why?','[]','Left ventricle has the thickest wall because it must generate the highest pressure to pump oxygenated blood throughout the entire systemic circulation','Left ventricle has the thickest wall because it must generate the highest pressure to pump oxygenated blood throughout the entire systemic circulation','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Atria vs Ventricles comparison"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z','2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7e005oe9oiehbk2npe','mcq','What common syndrome is associated with supravalvular aortic stenosis?','["Down syndrome.","Turner syndrome.","William`s syndrome.","Edward syndrome."]','William`s syndrome.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["syndrome","supravalvular","aortic stenosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.690Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.690Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7f005pe9oigdmobwgj','mcq','What is the main sequelae of prolonged aortic stenosis?','["Development of mitral regurgitation.","Left ventricular hypertrophy.","Aortic regurgitation.","Ventricular arrhythmia."]','Left ventricular hypertrophy.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["aortic stenosis","sequelae","hypertrophy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.691Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.691Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisxhfq000k4lu8p5jqabbd','mcq','A 65-year-old female presents with 90 minutes of chest pain. Her ECG shows new ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4. The nearest PCI-capable hospital is 75 minutes away by ambulance. What is the most appropriate reperfusion strategy?','["Fibrinolytic therapy immediately","Transfer for primary PCI","Aspirin only, delay reperfusion","Fibrinolytic therapy followed by PCI transfer","Conservative management with medical therapy"]','Transfer for primary PCI','For anterior STEMI, primary PCI is strongly preferred when it can be performed within 90 minutes of first medical contact [S1]. Since the PCI-capable hospital is 75 minutes away, total transport time would be approximately 150 minutes, which exceeds the 90-minute window. However, anterior STEMI has higher mortality and PCI is still preferred when achievable within 120-150 minutes. Fibrinolytic therapy would be appropriate only if PCI could not be performed within 120 minutes.','PCI first for anterior STEMI, even if slightly delayed','Anterior STEMI carries higher mortality risk than inferior STEMI, making timely reperfusion even more critical. Consider early activation of the catheterization lab before patient arrival.','Option A is incorrect because fibrinolysis is only preferred when PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes. Option C is incorrect because reperfusion is essential. Option D is incorrect because fibrinolysis followed by rescue PCI is not the standard approach. Option E is incorrect because conservative management is inadequate for STEMI.','Medium','cardiovascular','STEMI reperfusion strategy','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI reperfusion strategy","PCI","fibrinolysis","time-dependent","anterior STEMI"]',true,'Timeline diagram showing the STEMI reperfusion pathway from symptom onset to treatment decision points','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-2e5e54df-068.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:27:25.190Z','2026-10-22T00:27:25.190Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvisxtpb000l4lu8rz0vlcdp','mcq','A 72-year-old male with anterior STEMI successfully underwent primary PCI with stent placement 12 hours ago. On hospital day 3, he develops worsening dyspnea, orthopnea, and crackles in both lung fields. His chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly with pulmonary edema. What is the most likely complication?','["Right ventricular infarction","Pericarditis","Left ventricular free wall rupture","Papillary muscle rupture","Dressler syndrome"]','Left ventricular free wall rupture','The development of pulmonary edema and cardiomegaly 3 days after anterior STEMI suggests left ventricular dysfunction [S2]. Left ventricular free wall rupture is a catastrophic complication that typically occurs 3-5 days after STEMI, particularly in anterior infarctions. The patient''s symptoms of worsening dyspnea and orthopnea with pulmonary edema indicate acute heart failure, which can be caused by rupture or severe left ventricular dysfunction.','3-day rule: rupture at 3 days, pericarditis at 7 days','Anterior STEMI carries the highest risk of mechanical complications including free wall rupture, ventricular septal defect, and papillary muscle rupture. Maintain a high index of suspicion for these complications.','Option A is incorrect as RV infarction typically presents with hypotension and elevated JVP. Option B is incorrect as pericarditis typically presents with pleuritic chest pain and occurs later (7+ days). Option C is incorrect as papillary muscle rupture typically causes acute mitral regurgitation with pulmonary edema but occurs earlier (1-7 days). Option D is incorrect as Dressler syndrome is a late complication (weeks) with pericarditis and pleuritis.','Medium','cardiovascular','STEMI complications','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI complications","mechanical complications","heart failure","anterior STEMI"]',true,'Chest X-ray showing cardiomegaly with pulmonary edema in a STEMI patient','','','','','','[]','/generated/agent-056f3671-812.jpg','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T00:27:41.087Z','2026-10-22T00:27:41.087Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviudov2000nrsu8ib6s5zm3','mcq','Which of the following is the primary function of the skeletal system?','["Production of red blood cells","Storage of calcium and phosphorus","Protection of internal organs","All of the above"]','All of the above','The skeletal system serves multiple functions including protection of internal organs (e.g., skull protects brain, rib cage protects heart and lungs), production of red blood cells in the bone marrow (hematopoiesis), and storage of calcium and phosphorus as a mineral reservoir. These are all primary functions of the skeletal system.','POEM - Protection, Organ support, Endocrine functions, Mineral storage','Bone marrow produces approximately 2-3 million red blood cells per second in healthy adults.','Options A, B, and C each represent individual functions of the skeletal system, but the question asks for the primary function, which encompasses all these roles.','Easy','General','Bone Anatomy','["bone anatomy","General","deep-agent","Bone Anatomy","skeletal system","bone functions","basic anatomy"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T01:08:00.926Z','2026-10-22T01:08:00.926Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmviuds5l000orsu8ngzah9o5','mcq','Which type of bone is characterized by having a shaft and two expanded ends?','["Flat bones","Irregular bones","Long bones","Short bones"]','Long bones','Long bones are characterized by having a cylindrical shaft (diaphysis) and two expanded ends (epiphyses). Examples include the femur, humerus, and tibia. The shaft provides leverage for movement, while the ends contain joints and articular cartilage.','LEGS - Long bones Extend for Greater movement','Long bones are the most common type of bone in the body and are primarily responsible for weight-bearing and movement.','Flat bones (like skull bones) provide protection, irregular bones (like vertebrae) have complex shapes, and short bones (like carpals) are cube-shaped - none have the characteristic shaft and expanded ends structure.','Easy','General','Bone Anatomy','["bone anatomy","General","deep-agent","Bone Anatomy","bone classification","long bones","bone structure"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T01:08:05.193Z','2026-10-22T01:08:05.193Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1axrx0012rsu8c39odevr','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?','["Immediate coronary angiography and primary PCI","Fibrinolytic therapy","Aspirin 325 mg, oxygen, nitroglycerin, and morphine","Wait for cardiac biomarkers to confirm STEMI","Start heparin and transfer to telemetry"]','Immediate coronary angiography and primary PCI','The patient has an acute inferior wall STEMI based on the ECG findings. Current guidelines recommend primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) as the preferred reperfusion strategy when it can be performed within 90-120 minutes of first medical contact. This provides the best outcomes for STEMI patients. Fibrinolytic therapy is an alternative when PCI cannot be performed in a timely manner, but primary PCI is superior when available. Aspirin, oxygen, nitroglycerin, and morphine are supportive measures but not the definitive treatment. Waiting for biomarkers delays treatment, and heparin alone is not sufficient reperfusion therapy.','"PCI first for STEMI when timely"','Time to reperfusion is critical - aim for door-to-balloon time <90 minutes for primary PCI','Fibrinolytic therapy: Only when PCI cannot be performed timely; Aspirin etc: Supportive but not definitive; Wait for biomarkers: Delays critical treatment; Heparin alone: Insufficient for STEMI','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Emergency Medicine","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicating inferior wall STEMI','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:21:49.821Z','2026-10-22T04:21:49.821Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1b32i0013rsu8skrihhq3','mcq','A 58-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and smoking presents with severe chest pain and shortness of breath. Her ECG reveals ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Which coronary artery is most likely involved in this STEMI?','["Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Left anterior descending artery","Right marginal artery","Posterior descending artery"]','Left anterior descending artery','ST elevation in leads V1-V4 indicates an anterior wall STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The LAD artery supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, including the interventricular septum and anterior papillary muscle. The right coronary artery typically supplies the inferior wall (leads II, III, aVF), the left circumflex supplies the lateral wall (leads I, aVL, V5-V6), and the right marginal and posterior descending arteries are branches of the right coronary artery that supply smaller territories.','"V1-V4 = LAD territory"','Anterior STEMI carries higher mortality due to larger myocardial territory at risk','Right coronary artery: Typically causes inferior STEMI; Left circumflex: Causes lateral STEMI; Right marginal: Small branch, not main culprit; Posterior descending: Branch of RCA, supplies inferior wall','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Coronary Anatomy","Cardiology"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior wall STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V4','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:21:56.682Z','2026-10-22T04:21:56.682Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1b8x10014rsu80po7v33b','mcq','A 65-year-old male with diabetes and hyperlipidemia presents to the ED with chest pain. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6. Which of the following complications is this patient at highest risk for developing?','["Complete heart block","Ventricular septal rupture","Papillary muscle rupture","Right ventricular infarction","Pericarditis"]','Papillary muscle rupture','ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6 indicates a lateral wall STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the left circumflex artery. The lateral wall contains the anterolateral papillary muscle, which is supplied by the left circumflex artery. Rupture of this papillary muscle leads to acute mitral regurgitation, a life-threatening complication that requires emergency surgical intervention. Complete heart block is more common with inferior STEMI (right coronary artery territory), ventricular septal rupture can occur with any STEMI but is more associated with anterior infarcts, right ventricular infarction occurs with right coronary artery occlusion, and pericarditis is a later complication.','"Lateral STEMI = papillary muscle risk"','Papillary muscle rupture presents with sudden pulmonary edema and new systolic murmur','Complete heart block: More common with inferior STEMI; Ventricular septal rupture: More associated with anterior STEMI; Right ventricular infarction: Occurs with RCA occlusion; Pericarditis: Later complication, not acute','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","chat-agent","ECG","Complications","Cardiology"]',true,'ECG showing lateral wall STEMI with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:22:04.261Z','2026-10-22T04:22:04.261Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1bi800015rsu8ly8q4m0y','mcq','A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm, associated with diaphoresis and nausea. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 4 mm in leads II, III, and aVF. Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology and management of this patient''s condition is most accurate?','["The ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates occlusion of the left anterior descending coronary artery, and immediate fibrinolytic therapy should be initiated within 30 minutes","This ECG pattern represents an inferior wall STEMI, typically caused by occlusion of the right coronary artery or left circumflex artery, and the patient should receive dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor","ST elevation in these leads indicates a right ventricular STEMI, which requires different management including right-sided ECG leads and consideration for nitrates","The ST elevation pattern is consistent with a lateral wall STEMI, which has the highest mortality rate and requires emergent coronary artery bypass grafting"]','This ECG pattern represents an inferior wall STEMI, typically caused by occlusion of the right coronary artery or left circumflex artery, and the patient should receive dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor','The ECG showing ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF is characteristic of an inferior wall STEMI. This is most commonly caused by occlusion of the right coronary artery (in 80-90% of cases) or occasionally the left circumflex artery. The standard management includes dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin (162-325 mg chewed) and a P2Y12 inhibitor (such as ticagrelor or clopidogrel), along with anticoagulation and consideration for reperfusion therapy (primary PCI preferred if available within 90 minutes, or fibrinolytic therapy if PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes).','II, III, aVF = Inferior STEMI = RCA/LCx occlusion','Inferior STEMI may also involve the right ventricle in 30-50% of cases, so consider right-sided ECG leads (V4R) to assess for right ventricular involvement, which would contraindicate nitrates.','Option A is incorrect because leads II, III, and aVF represent inferior territory, not anterior (LAD occlusion). Option C is incorrect because right ventricular STEMI would show ST elevation in V4R, not leads II, III, aVF. Option D is incorrect because lateral wall STEMI typically shows ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5-V6, not inferior leads.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI pathophysiology and diagnosis','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI pathophysiology and diagnosis","ECG","myocardial infarction","coronary anatomy"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with comparison to normal ECG','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:22:16.320Z','2026-10-22T04:22:16.320Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1bnzm0016rsu8tc9mbxiu','mcq','A 58-year-old female with a history of hypertension and diabetes presents with 2 hours of severe chest pain. Her ECG reveals ST-segment elevation in leads V1-V4. After administering aspirin 325 mg and initiating heparin infusion, which of the following represents the most appropriate next step in management?','["Transfer to a community hospital for fibrinolytic therapy due to the time elapsed since symptom onset","Immediate transfer to a PCI-capable facility for primary percutaneous coronary intervention","Administer morphine 4 mg IV and observe for pain relief before deciding on reperfusion strategy","Obtain cardiac enzymes and wait for troponin results before initiating reperfusion therapy"]','Immediate transfer to a PCI-capable facility for primary percutaneous coronary intervention','This patient has an anterior wall STEMI (ST elevation in V1-V4), which represents a high-risk territory involving the left ventricular apex and anterior septum. Current guidelines recommend primary PCI as the preferred reperfusion strategy when it can be performed within 90 minutes of first medical contact. For anterior STEMI, time is muscle, and immediate transfer to a PCI-capable facility is the most appropriate next step. Fibrinolytic therapy may be considered if PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes, but transfer for PCI is preferred when available. Morphine may be used for pain control but should not delay reperfusion. Cardiac enzymes are helpful for diagnosis but reperfusion should not be delayed for results.','Anterior STEMI = V1-V4 = LAD occlusion = Time is muscle = PCI within 90 min','Anterior STEMI carries higher mortality risk than inferior STEMI due to larger myocardial territory at risk, emphasizing the importance of rapid reperfusion.','Option A is incorrect because PCI is preferred over fibrinolytic therapy when available within 90 minutes. Option C is incorrect because pain relief should not delay reperfusion in STEMI. Option D is incorrect because reperfusion therapy should not be delayed for troponin results in a clear-cut STEMI presentation.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI management','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI management","acute coronary syndrome","reperfusion therapy","PCI"]',true,'ECG showing ST-elevation in leads V1-V4 with coronary anatomy showing LAD occlusion','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:22:23.794Z','2026-10-22T04:22:23.794Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1bu6l0017rsu84vxzmsg8','mcq','A 65-year-old male undergoes successful primary PCI for an anterior STEMI with stent placement in the proximal left anterior descending artery. Six hours post-procedure, he develops hypotension (BP 85/50 mmHg), tachycardia (HR 120 bpm), and jugular venous distension. His ECG now shows new ST elevation in lead V1 and new right bundle branch block. Which of the following complications is most likely, and what is the appropriate initial management?','["Cardiogenic shock requiring inotropic support and consideration for mechanical circulatory support","Right ventricular infarction requiring fluid administration and avoidance of nitrates","Stent thrombosis requiring emergent cardiac catheterization and additional intervention","Pericardial tamponade requiring emergent pericardiocentesis"]','Stent thrombosis requiring emergent cardiac catheterization and additional intervention','This patient''s presentation of hypotension, tachycardia, jugular venous distension, new ST elevation in V1, and new right bundle branch block 6 hours after PCI is highly concerning for acute stent thrombosis. Stent thrombosis is a catastrophic complication that can lead to cardiogenic death and requires immediate intervention. The new right bundle branch block suggests extensive ischemia involving the conduction system. Initial management includes immediate aspirin loading, additional antiplatelet therapy (such as ticagrelor or prasugrel if not already given), and emergent cardiac catheterization for thrombus aspiration and additional stent placement if needed.','Post-PCI + hypotension + JVD + new RBBB + ST elevation = Stent thrombosis = Emergency PCI','Stent thrombosis typically presents within 24 hours of PCI and carries mortality rates of 20-45%, emphasizing the need for rapid recognition and intervention.','Option A is incorrect because while this could represent cardiogenic shock, the specific ECG changes (new ST elevation in V1 and RBBB) point more specifically to stent thrombosis rather than general cardiogenic shock. Option B is incorrect because right ventricular infarction would typically show ST elevation in V4R, not V1, and would not typically develop RBBB. Option D is incorrect because pericardial tamponade would typically show electrical alternans and would not present with new ST elevation and RBBB.','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI complications','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI complications","PCI complications","stent thrombosis","acute coronary syndrome"]',true,'Illustration showing STEMI management timeline with stent thrombosis complication','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:22:31.821Z','2026-10-22T04:22:31.821Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1mhf8001frsu8yrumj5l1','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe chest pain radiating to his left arm. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. Which coronary artery is most likely responsible for this ECG finding?','["Left anterior descending (LAD) artery","Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left main coronary artery","Diagonal branch of LAD"]','Right coronary artery (RCA)','The ECG findings of ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL are classic for an inferior wall STEMI. The right coronary artery typically supplies the inferior wall of the left ventricle (leads II, III, aVF). The RCA also supplies the right ventricle in most individuals (85-90%), which is why inferior STEMIs often present with right ventricular involvement and hypotension.','II, III, aVF = Inferior wall = RCA','Inferior STEMIs often present with bradycardia and hypotension due to right ventricular involvement and vagal stimulation. Always obtain right-sided leads (V4R) to assess for right ventricular involvement.','A) LAD artery supplies anterior wall (V1-V4), B) RCA is correct, C) LCx supplies lateral wall (I, aVL, V5-V6), D) Left main occlusion would cause widespread ST elevation, E) Diagonal branch supplies anterior wall','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","ECG","coronary anatomy","inferior STEMI"]',true,'ECG showing classic STEMI findings with ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF (inferior STEMI). Include labeled leads with clear ST segment elevation, reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL, and visible Q waves.','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:30:48.500Z','2026-10-22T04:30:48.500Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1mn66001grsu8ff530815','mcq','A 62-year-old female with a history of hypertension and smoking presents with crushing substernal chest pain. Her ECG demonstrates ST elevation in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Which of the following complications is this patient at highest risk for developing?','["Complete heart block","Ventricular septal rupture","Free wall rupture","Papillary muscle rupture","Right ventricular infarction"]','Ventricular septal rupture','Anterior STEMI (ST elevation in V1-V4) due to left anterior descending artery occlusion carries the highest risk of ventricular septal rupture (VSR). VSR typically occurs 3-5 days after the infarct when the necrotic tissue becomes weakest. The anterior wall contains the interventricular septum, and extensive anterior infarction can lead to septal rupture causing a left-to-right shunt. This presents with new holosystolic murmur, hypotension, and heart failure.','Anterior STEMI = High risk VSR (septum involved)','Ventricular septal rupture is a mechanical complication that requires urgent surgical intervention. Look for new holosystolic murmur at left sternal border, hypotension, and signs of heart failure in the first week after anterior STEMI.','A) Complete heart block is more common with inferior STEMI, B) VSR is correct for anterior STEMI, C) Free wall rupture is rare but catastrophic, D) Papillary muscle rupture causes mitral regurgitation, E) RV infarction is associated with inferior STEMI','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","ECG","complications","anterior STEMI"]',true,'12-lead ECG showing anterior STEMI with ST elevation in leads V1-V4, reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Include clear annotations pointing to ST segment elevation, T wave changes, and Q wave formation.','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:30:55.950Z','2026-10-22T04:30:55.950Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj1mrw9001hrsu8ti87ozbj','mcq','A 65-year-old male with diabetes and hyperlipidemia presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of severe chest pain. His ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Coronary angiography reveals complete occlusion of which artery?','["Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery","Left anterior descending artery","Diagonal branch"]','Left anterior descending artery','ST elevation in leads V1-V4 indicates an anterior wall STEMI, which is most commonly caused by occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The LAD artery supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, including the septum and anterior free wall. The territory of the LAD extends from the base to the apex of the heart, explaining why anterior STEMIs often involve large areas of myocardium and have higher mortality rates.','V1-V4 = Anterior wall = LAD artery','The LAD artery is often called the ''widow maker'' because occlusion can cause extensive anterior wall infarction with high mortality. Early reperfusion therapy is critical for LAD occlusions.','A) RCA supplies inferior wall, B) LCx supplies lateral wall, C) Left main occlusion causes widespread ST elevation, D) LAD is correct for anterior STEMI, E) Diagonal branch is a branch of L supplying smaller anterior territory','Medium','Cardiovascular','STEMI','["STEMI","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","STEMI","ECG","coronary anatomy","anterior STEMI"]',true,'Coronary angiogram showing complete occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery with thrombus formation. Include labeled anatomy showing the LAD artery, occlusion point, and surrounding myocardial territory.','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T04:31:02.073Z','2026-10-22T04:31:02.073Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj3h8xj000lyiu8n5xtpk1e','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and mild jaundice. Laboratory findings show: hemoglobin 8.2 g/dL, MCV 78 fL, reticulocyte count 2.5%, LDH 350 U/L, haptoglobin 15 mg/dL, and indirect bilirubin 2.1 mg/dL. Peripheral smear shows spherocytes. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and underlying mechanism?','["Iron deficiency anemia due to inadequate dietary iron intake","Anemia of chronic disease mediated by hepcidin excess","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia with IgG-mediated extravascular hemolysis","Hereditary spherocytosis with intrinsic membrane protein defect","Thalassemia minor with imbalanced globin chain synthesis"]','Hereditary spherocytosis with intrinsic membrane protein defect','This case presents with hemolytic anemia (elevated LDH, low haptoglobin, elevated indirect bilirubin, reticulocytosis) and spherocytes on smear. Hereditary spherocytosis is characterized by defects in red blood cell membrane proteins (spectrin, ankyrin, band 3, protein 4.2), leading to loss of membrane surface area, formation of spherocytes, and splenic sequestration. The spherocytes are less deformable and are destroyed in the spleen. While autoimmune hemolytic anemia can also present with spherocytes, it''s typically associated with positive Coombs test and would not typically present in this familial pattern without other autoimmune features.','SPHERES: Spherocytes + Hemolysis + Reticulocytosis + Extravascular destruction + Splenomegaly + Hereditary + Erythrocyte membrane defect','Hereditary spherocytosis is the most common inherited hemolytic anemia in individuals of Northern European descent. The characteristic spherocytes result from membrane loss, not from spherical shape formation. Splenectomy is often curative but increases infection risk.','Option 1 is incorrect as iron deficiency typically shows microcytic anemia without hemolytic features. Option 2 is incorrect as anemia of chronic disease doesn''t show hemolysis. Option 3 is incorrect as autoimmune hemolytic anemia would typically have positive Coombs test. Option 5 is incorrect as thalassemia minor shows microcytic anemia without hemolysis.','Medium','hematology','hereditary hemolytic anemias','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","hereditary hemolytic anemias","hereditary spherocytosis","hemolytic anemia","membrane defects"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00ccbjrm16i0qceo","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"INTRACORPUSCULAR (INTRINSIC)\\nMembrane defects\\n\\n• Hereditary spherocytosis\\n\\n• Hereditary elliptocytosis\\nEnzymatic defects\\n\\n• Pyruvate kinase deficiency\\n\\n• G6PD deficiency\\nDefective hemoglobin synthesis\\n\\n• Sickle cell anemia\\n\\n• Thalassemia\\n\\nEXTRACORPUSCULAR (EXTRINSIC) — not covered in this lecture\\n\\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (antibody-mediated)\\n\\n• Microangiopathic causes (mechanical RBC shearing)\\n\\n• Infection-related hemolysis\\n\\nII MEMBRANE & ENZYMATIC DEFECTS\\nHereditary spherocytosis, pyruvate kinase deficiency & G6PD deficiency","chunkContent":"1.6 Causes of Hemolytic Anemia — Intracorpuscular vs Extracorpuscular Causes of hemolysis split first by where the defect lives: inside the RBC itself (intracorpuscular / intrinsic) or outside it, in the surrounding plasma/vessel environment (extracorpuscular / extrinsic). This lecture is entirely about the intracorpuscular group.\\nINTRACORPUSCULAR (INTRINSIC)\\nMembrane defects\\n\\n• Hereditary spherocytosis\\n\\n• Hereditary elliptocytosis\\nEnzymatic defects\\n\\n• Pyruvate kinase deficiency\\n\\n• G6PD deficiency\\nDefective hemoglobin synthesis\\n\\n• Sickle cell anemia\\n\\n• Thalassemia\\n\\nEXTRACORPUSCULAR (EXTRINSIC) — not covered in this lecture\\n\\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (antibody-mediated)\\n\\n• Microangiopathic causes (mechanical RBC shearing)\\n\\n• Infection-related hemolysis\\n\\nII MEMBRANE & ENZYMATIC DEFECTS\\nHereditary spherocytosis, pyruvate kinase deficiency & G6PD deficiency","answerCoverage":0.83,"judgeScore":8},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00cdbjrmwzzwd7bf","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"2.1 Hereditary Spherocytosis — Overview\\nHereditary spherocytosis (and its cousin, hereditary elliptocytosis) is mostly autosomal dominant. The defect is a deficiency of spectrin, the protein that forms the cytoskeleton of the RBC membrane and gives the cell its normal flexible biconcave shape.","chunkContent":"2.1 Hereditary Spherocytosis — Overview\\nHereditary spherocytosis (and its cousin, hereditary elliptocytosis) is mostly autosomal dominant. The defect is a deficiency of spectrin, the protein that forms the cytoskeleton of the RBC membrane and gives the cell its normal flexible biconcave shape.\\n\\n• Spectrin loss → the membrane skeleton weakens → the cell loses deformability (can no longer squeeze through the spleen’s narrow sinusoids easily)\\n\\n• Loss of surface area/volume ratio reshapes the cell into a rigid sphere (spherocyte) instead of a flexible disc\\n\\n• These stiff spherocytes are trapped and prematurely destroyed by the spleen → hemolytic anemia + huge splenomegaly","answerCoverage":0.83,"judgeScore":8},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00cfbjrm7xm0arqf","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"• H Susceptible to aplastic crisis (parvovirus B19). 2.3 Spherocytosis — Lab Findings & Diagnosis\\n\\nTest Finding\\nReticulocytes Increased (reticulocytosis)\\nBilirubin Increased, indirect (unconjugated)\\nHemoglobin 6–10 g/dL\\n\\nMCV Normal\\n★ MCHC Increased — the ONE hemolytic anemia in this lecture with a raised MCHC Blood smear Spherocytes or elliptocytes — diagnostic","chunkContent":"• H Hypersplenism and biliary gallstones.\\n\\n• H Susceptible to aplastic crisis (parvovirus B19).\\n\\n2.3 Spherocytosis — Lab Findings & Diagnosis\\n\\nTest Finding\\nReticulocytes Increased (reticulocytosis)\\nBilirubin Increased, indirect (unconjugated)\\nHemoglobin 6–10 g/dL\\n\\nMCV Normal\\n★ MCHC Increased — the ONE hemolytic anemia in this lecture with a raised MCHC Blood smear Spherocytes or elliptocytes — diagnostic","answerCoverage":0.17,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T05:22:43.447Z','2026-10-22T05:22:43.447Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj3hlqk000myiu812yrab41','mcq','A 72-year-old man with a history of chronic kidney disease presents with progressive fatigue. His laboratory results show: hemoglobin 9.1 g/dL, MCV 95 fL, serum iron 45 μg/dL, TIBC 280 μg/dL, ferritin 800 ng/mL, and transferrin saturation 16%. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of his anemia?','["Absolute iron deficiency due to poor dietary intake","Functional iron deficiency from inflammation-mediated hepcidin upregulation","Anemia of chronic disease with impaired iron utilization","Pure red cell aplasia from parvovirus B19 infection","Sideroblastic anemia from pyridoxine deficiency"]','Anemia of chronic disease with impaired iron utilization','This patient has anemia of chronic disease (ACD), which is common in chronic kidney disease. The key findings are: normocytic anemia (MCV 95 fL), low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, but high ferritin. In ACD, inflammation increases hepcidin production, which blocks iron absorption from the gut and iron release from macrophages. This creates a functional iron deficiency where iron is present in stores but cannot be utilized for erythropoiesis. The high ferritin reflects increased storage iron due to hepcidin-mediated sequestration, not absolute iron deficiency.','ACD: Anemia + Chronic Disease + Inflammation + Hepcidin + Iron sequestration','In anemia of chronic disease, ferritin levels >100 ng/mL typically indicate adequate iron stores, while levels <30 ng/mL suggest coexisting absolute iron deficiency. The anemia is typically normocytic but can become microcytic in severe cases.','Option 1 is incorrect as absolute iron deficiency would show low ferritin. Option 2 is partially correct but incomplete - this is the mechanism of ACD. Option 4 is incorrect as pure red cell aplasia would show reticulocytopenia. Option 5 is incorrect as sideroblastic anemia would show ringed sideroblasts on bone marrow biopsy.','Medium','hematology','anemia of chronic disease','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","anemia of chronic disease","anemia of chronic disease","iron metabolism","hepcidin"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00btbjrmlgja5nm9","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Anemia of Chronic Disease & Renal Disease\\n★ Etiology: a mild decrease in RBC lifespan combined with a relative failure of the bone marrow to respond adequately — with little or no rise in erythropoietin.","chunkContent":"Anemia of Chronic Disease & Renal Disease\\n★ Etiology: a mild decrease in RBC lifespan combined with a relative failure of the bone marrow to respond adequately — with little or no rise in erythropoietin.\\n\\nS Clarifications Note — A Simple Way to Remember the Mechanism Framed simply: the toxins of the underlying chronic illness affect the bone marrow directly, and if splenomegaly develops, the enlarged spleen can additionally destroy RBCs — tipping a normocytic picture toward a mildly microcytic one.\\n\\nFinding Typical Pattern\\nHemoglobin 6–9 g/dL\\nMCV/MCHC Mostly normocytic normochromic (can be mildly microcytic/hypochromic if with IDA/ BM failure Reticulocytes Normal or slightly decreased for the degree of anemia","answerCoverage":0.5,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T05:23:00.044Z','2026-10-22T05:23:00.044Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj3hxiy000pyiu80utqw81l','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and mild dyspnea on exertion. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 8.5 g/dL, MCV 75 fL, RDW 18%, reticulocyte count 1.5%, serum iron 40 μg/dL, TIBC 450 μg/dL, ferritin 15 ng/mL, and normal vitamin B12/folate levels. Which of the following statements BEST explains the pathophysiology of her anemia?','["Impaired heme synthesis due to defective iron utilization in mitochondria","Reduced erythropoietin production leading to inadequate red blood cell production","Accelerated destruction of red blood cells due to membrane abnormalities","Defective DNA synthesis causing ineffective erythropoiesis"]','Impaired heme synthesis due to defective iron utilization in mitochondria','This patient has iron deficiency anemia (IDA) characterized by microcytic hypochromic cells (low MCV), low serum iron, high TIBC, low ferritin, and appropriately low reticulocyte count. The pathophysiology involves impaired heme synthesis due to insufficient iron availability for incorporation into protoporphyrin IX. Iron is essential for the final step of heme synthesis in mitochondria, and its deficiency leads to reduced hemoglobin production and microcytic red blood cells.','IDA = Iron Deficiency Anemia → Impaired Heme synthesis → Microcytic cells','In iron deficiency anemia, the reticulocyte count is appropriately low (not elevated) because the bone marrow lacks the necessary iron to produce new red blood cells effectively.','Option 2 describes anemia of chronic disease or renal failure; option 3 describes hemolytic anemia; option 4 describes megaloblastic anemia (B12/folate deficiency).','Medium','hematology','anemia pathophysiology','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","anemia pathophysiology","iron deficiency","microcytic anemia","heme synthesis"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T05:23:15.322Z','2026-10-22T05:23:15.322Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvj3hz3j000ryiu8l6l1ekuy','mcq','A 28-year-old woman presents with fatigue and dyspnea. Her complete blood count shows: hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL, MCV 110 fL, MCH 35 pg, MCHC 32 g/dL, reticulocyte count 0.5%, and platelet count 110,000/μL. Peripheral smear shows macro-ovalocytes. Which of the following laboratory findings would be most characteristic of her condition?','["Elevated serum folate with normal vitamin B12 levels","Elevated methylmalonic acid with normal homocysteine","Elevated homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid","Low serum iron with high TIBC and low ferritin","Elevated lactate dehydrogenase with low haptoglobin"]','Elevated homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid','This patient has megaloblastic anemia with macro-ovalocytes, which is characteristic of folate deficiency. Folate deficiency impairs DNA synthesis, leading to megaloblastic changes. In folate deficiency, homocysteine accumulates because folate is required for its conversion to methionine. However, methylmalonic acid remains normal because vitamin B12 (not folate) is required for its conversion to succinyl-CoA. This distinguishes folate deficiency from B12 deficiency, which elevates both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid. The reticulocytopenia indicates inadequate bone marrow response.','Folate deficiency: Folate + Homocysteine elevated + Methylmalonic acid normal + DNA synthesis defect','Folate deficiency is often caused by poor dietary intake, increased requirements (pregnancy), or malabsorption. Unlike B12 deficiency, folate deficiency does not cause neurological symptoms. The macro-ovalocytes are pathognomonic for megaloblastic anemia.','Option 1 is incorrect as folate deficiency would show low folate, not elevated. Option 2 is incorrect as this pattern suggests B12 deficiency. Option 4 is incorrect as this pattern suggests iron deficiency. Option 5 is incorrect as this pattern suggests hemolytic anemia.','Medium','hematology','megaloblastic anemias','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","megaloblastic anemias","folate deficiency","megaloblastic anemia","vitamin deficiencies"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-22T05:23:17.359Z','2026-10-22T05:23:17.359Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5p003ie9oi096xc37i','mcq','19- Among the following cyanotic heart diseases, which is associated with lung oligemia?','["Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition of the Great Arteries","Truncus Arteriosus","Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return"]','Tetralogy of Fallot','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["cyanotic","lung","oligemia","tetralogy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.629Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.629Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlltcq8000fdbrdo0ztyepo','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and koilonychia. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 8.5 g/dL, MCV 75 fL, MCH 25 pg, RDW 18%, serum iron 35 μg/dL, TIBC 450 μg/dL, ferritin 8 ng/mL, and normal TSH. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology of her anemia?','["Impaired DNA synthesis due to vitamin B12 deficiency leading to megaloblastic changes","Ineffective erythropoiesis due to shortened red blood cell lifespan from oxidative stress","Iron-restricted erythropoiesis from hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration in macrophages","Reduced red blood cell production from inadequate iron availability for hemoglobin synthesis"]','D','The patient''s findings are classic for iron deficiency anemia (IDA): microcytic hypochromic anemia (low MCV, MCH) with low serum iron, high TIBC, and very low ferritin. The pathophysiology involves inadequate iron availability for hemoglobin synthesis, leading to reduced red blood cell production. Iron is essential for heme synthesis, and deficiency results in impaired hemoglobin production and microcytic red blood cells [S1, S2].','IDA = Iron Deficiency Anemia: Inadequate iron → Decreased hemoglobin → Anemia','Ferritin is the most specific test for iron deficiency, but it can be falsely elevated in inflammation, liver disease, or malignancy. In these cases, consider soluble transferrin receptor or bone marrow iron staining.','A is incorrect as it describes megaloblastic anemia (high MCV). B describes hemolytic anemia. C describes anemia of chronic disease, which typically shows normal or high ferritin and low TIBC.','Medium','Hematology','Anemia Pathophysiology','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Pathophysiology","iron deficiency anemia","microcytic anemia","pathophysiology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:31:33.680Z','2026-10-23T23:31:33.680Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlltg74000gdbrd8hpht4ki','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, glossitis, and mild jaundice. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 8.5 g/dL, MCV 110 fL, reticulocyte count 2%, LDH 350 U/L, haptoglobin 25 mg/dL, and indirect bilirubin 2.5 mg/dL. Peripheral smear shows macro-ovalocytes. Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiologic mechanism?','["Impaired DNA synthesis due to vitamin B12 deficiency","Defective heme synthesis due to iron deficiency","Accelerated red blood cell destruction due to autoimmune process","Ineffective erythropoiesis due to folate deficiency"]','Impaired DNA synthesis due to vitamin B12 deficiency','The patient presents with macrocytic anemia (MCV 110 fL) and evidence of hemolysis (elevated LDH, low haptoglobin, elevated indirect bilirubin). The macro-ovalocytes on peripheral smear are characteristic of megaloblastic anemia. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes impaired DNA synthesis, leading to megaloblastic changes in the bone marrow and ineffective erythropoiesis. The hemolytic component suggests additional hemolysis, which can occur in severe megaloblastic anemia due to fragile red blood cell membranes [S1].','Macro-ovalocytes + megaloblastic = B12/folate deficiency; hemolysis suggests severe membrane fragility','Megaloblastic anemia can present with both ineffective erythropoiesis and hemolysis; always check for neurologic symptoms in B12 deficiency','B (Iron deficiency) causes microcytic anemia, not macrocytic. C (Autoimmune hemolysis) typically presents with normocytic anemia and positive Coombs test. D (Folate deficiency) also causes megaloblastic anemia but usually doesn''t cause significant hemolysis','Medium','Hematology','Anemia Pathophysiology','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Pathophysiology","megaloblastic anemia","vitamin B12 deficiency","hemolysis"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00bubjrmxz00k6em","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Iron Low, without a rise in TIBC\\nFerritin Normal or slightly increased\\nMarrow Normal cells; normal-to-decreased RBC precursors Treatment: control the underlying disorder; erythropoietin may be needed; transfusions are rarely needed.★\\n\\nIII MEGALOBLASTIC ANEMIAS\\nImpaired DNA synthesis — folic acid & vitamin B12 deficiency\\n\\n3.1 Megaloblastic Anemia — Overview\\n★ A group of disorders caused by impaired DNA synthesis: RBCs grow larger than normal because nuclear maturation lags behind cytoplasmic maturation, producing ineffective erythropoiesis.","chunkContent":"Iron Low, without a rise in TIBC\\nFerritin Normal or slightly increased\\nMarrow Normal cells; normal-to-decreased RBC precursors Treatment: control the underlying disorder; erythropoietin may be needed; transfusions are rarely needed.★\\n\\nIII MEGALOBLASTIC ANEMIAS\\nImpaired DNA synthesis — folic acid & vitamin B12 deficiency\\n\\n3.1 Megaloblastic Anemia — Overview\\n★ A group of disorders caused by impaired DNA synthesis: RBCs grow larger than normal because nuclear maturation lags behind cytoplasmic maturation, producing ineffective erythropoiesis. Almost all cases are due to folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.\\n\\nGENERAL SYMPTOMS\\n\\n• Fatigue\\n\\n• Shortness of breath\\n\\n• Pallor\\n\\n• Tachycardia\\n\\n• Headaches\\n\\nDIGESTIVE SYMPTOMS\\n\\n• Glossitis\\n\\n• Mouth ulcers\\n\\n• Loss of appetite\\n\\n• Diarrhea","answerCoverage":1,"judgeScore":8},{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00bvbjrmhyljrahu","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Laboratory Hallmarks\\nMacrocytosis (raised MCV); nucleated RBCs\\nLarge, hypersegmented neutrophils (>5 lobes)\\nLow serum folate; iron and vitamin B12 normal to decreased Marked increase in lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\\nHypercellular bone marrow with megaloblastic changes 3 Clarifications Note — Why LDH Is So High (39)\\nThe marked LDH rise happens because a degree of hemolysis occurs inside the bone marrow itself — the abnormally large, fragile megaloblasts break down before they even finish maturing.","chunkContent":"Normal vs. Megaloblastic Blood Smear\\nThe megaloblastic smear shows a hypersegmented neutrophil and large oval RBCs (macro-ovalocytes).\\n\\nLaboratory Hallmarks\\nMacrocytosis (raised MCV); nucleated RBCs\\nLarge, hypersegmented neutrophils (>5 lobes)\\nLow serum folate; iron and vitamin B12 normal to decreased Marked increase in lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\\nHypercellular bone marrow with megaloblastic changes 3 Clarifications Note — Why LDH Is So High (39)\\nThe marked LDH rise happens because a degree of hemolysis occurs inside the bone marrow itself — the abnormally large, fragile megaloblasts break down before they even finish maturing.\\n\\n3.2 Folic Acid Deficiency\\nSOURCES\\n\\n• Green vegetables (e.g. spinach, broccoli)\\n\\n• Fruits (e.g. orange juice)\\n\\n• Animal organs\\n\\n• Whole-grain products — bread, cereal, pasta\\n\\n• Corn, peanuts, peas\\nCAUSES","answerCoverage":0.5,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:31:38.176Z','2026-10-23T23:31:38.176Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlltqxj000jdbrdqoknz8ta','mcq','A 68-year-old man with a history of Crohn''s disease presents with fatigue, glossitis, and mild paresthesias. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 9.0 g/dL, MCV 110 fL, reticulocyte count 1%, serum B12 120 pg/mL, serum folate 8 ng/mL, and normal serum iron studies. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management approach?','["Oral iron supplementation 325 mg daily for 3-6 months","Intramuscular vitamin B12 1000 mcg weekly for 4 weeks, then monthly","Folate supplementation 1 mg daily for 3 months","Transfusion of 2 units of packed red blood cells"]','B','The patient has megaloblastic anemia (high MCV) with low B12 and low folate. Given his Crohn''s disease history, B12 malabsorption is likely the primary issue. The most appropriate management is intramuscular B12 replacement, which bypasses the gastrointestinal absorption problem. Oral folate alone could mask B12 deficiency and potentially worsen neurological complications [S1, S3].','B12 deficiency: B for Brain (neurological symptoms) and B for Bypass (IM administration)','In megaloblastic anemia with both B12 and folate deficiency, always treat B12 deficiency first to prevent irreversible neurological damage. Folate alone can correct the anemia but not prevent neurological progression.','A is incorrect for macrocytic anemia. C is incomplete as it doesn''t address the B12 deficiency. D is inappropriate for chronic management and carries risks like iron overload and volume overload.','Medium','Hematology','Anemia Management','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Management","megaloblastic anemia","vitamin B12 deficiency","management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:31:52.087Z','2026-10-23T23:31:52.087Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5p003je9oin1jc1ezx','mcq','20- What is the main finding in chest X-ray in TGA infant?','["Mustache sign.","Egg on its side.","Boot shaped heart."]','Egg on its side.','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Transposition of Great Arteries','["tga","chest","x-ray","finding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.629Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.629Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5q003ke9oi39fxvlwx','mcq','21- A 3 month old baby was presented with central cyanosis, and a systolic murmur. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Tetralogy of Fallot.","Aortic stenosis.","Pulmonary regurgitation.","Ventricular septal defect."]','Tetralogy of Fallot.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","murmur","infant","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.630Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.630Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvllu5ae000ndbrd2np1isfg','mcq','A 62-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis presents with fatigue and mild anemia. Laboratory findings show: Hemoglobin 10.5 g/dL, MCV 85 fL, reticulocyte count 2%, serum iron 60 μg/dL, TIBC 250 μg/dL, ferritin 200 ng/mL, CRP 15 mg/L, and normal renal function. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of her anemia?','["Absolute iron deficiency from chronic blood loss","Functional iron deficiency due to hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration","Impaired erythropoietin production from chronic kidney disease","Accelerated red blood cell destruction from autoimmune hemolysis"]','B','The patient has anemia of chronic disease (ACD) with microcytic anemia, normal/high ferritin, low serum iron, and elevated CRP. The pathophysiology involves hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration in macrophages, leading to functional iron deficiency despite adequate iron stores. Hepcidin is upregulated by inflammation and blocks iron release from macrophages and absorption from the gut [S1, S3].','ACD = Anemia of Chronic Disease: A for Activation (of immune system), C for Chronic inflammation, D for Deficiency (functional iron)','In anemia of chronic disease, ferritin is typically normal or elevated (unlike true iron deficiency where it''s low). The anemia is usually mild and responds to treating the underlying inflammatory condition.','A is incorrect as ferritin is normal/high, not low. C is incorrect as renal function is normal. D would show evidence of hemolysis (high LDH, low haptoglobin, high reticulocyte count).','Medium','Hematology','Anemia Pathophysiology','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Pathophysiology","anemia of chronic disease","inflammation","hepcidin"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:32:10.694Z','2026-10-23T23:32:10.694Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvllu9df000qdbrdtzaymjg9','mcq','A 32-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and koilonychia. Her complete blood count shows: Hemoglobin 9.5 g/dL, MCV 72 fL, RDW 22%, reticulocyte count 0.5%, WBC 4.2 × 10³/μL, platelet 145 × 10³/μL. Serum iron 25 μg/dL, TIBC 450 μg/dL, ferritin 8 ng/mL, transferrin saturation 6%. Which of the following laboratory findings would be most helpful in distinguishing between iron deficiency anemia and anemia of chronic disease?','["Serum transferrin receptor level","C-reactive protein","Vitamin B12 level","Reticulocyte hemoglobin content"]','Serum transferrin receptor level','This patient has microcytic hypochromic anemia consistent with iron deficiency. To distinguish iron deficiency from anemia of chronic disease (ACD), serum transferrin receptor (sTfR) is the most specific test. In iron deficiency, sTfR is elevated due to increased erythropoietic activity, while in ACD it''s normal or mildly elevated [S1]. CRP can help identify inflammation but doesn''t specifically distinguish between these conditions. Vitamin B12 is normal in both. Reticulocyte hemoglobin content may be low in both conditions [S4].','sTfR high in iron deficiency, normal in ACD; CRP only shows inflammation','In microcytic anemia with inflammation, sTfR/sTfR-log ferritin ratio is more specific than ferritin alone','B (CRP) indicates inflammation but doesn''t distinguish between iron deficiency and ACD. C (Vitamin B12) is normal in both conditions. D (Reticulocyte hemoglobin) is non-specific and can be low in both','Medium','Hematology','Anemia Diagnosis','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Diagnosis","iron deficiency","anemia of chronic disease","diagnostic testing"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:32:15.987Z','2026-10-23T23:32:15.987Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvllvyfb0012dbrdrszg0z1h','mcq','A 65-year-old male with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with a dry, non-productive cough that has developed over the past 2 weeks. He was recently started on lisinopril for blood pressure control. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this adverse effect?','["Direct stimulation of cough receptors in the bronchial epithelium","Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition","Increased production of prostaglandins in the respiratory tract","Hypersensitivity reaction to the sulfhydryl group in the drug","Direct toxic effect on lung parenchyma"]','Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition','The dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is primarily due to the accumulation of bradykinin and substance P. ACE normally degrades bradykinin, so when ACE is inhibited, bradykinin levels increase, leading to inflammation and irritation of the bronchial epithelium, which triggers the cough reflex. This side effect affects up to 20% of patients and is one of the most common reasons for discontinuing ACE inhibitor therapy.','ACE Inhibitors → Bradykinin Buildup → Bronchial Irritation → Cough','ACE inhibitor-induced cough is dose-independent and non-allergic, making it distinct from other drug-induced coughs. It typically resolves within 1-4 weeks after discontinuation of the ACE inhibitor.','Option A is incorrect because ACE inhibitors don''t directly stimulate cough receptors. Option C is incorrect because prostaglandin involvement is minimal. Option D is incorrect because lisinopril doesn''t contain a sulfhydryl group (that''s captopril). Option E is incorrect because there''s no direct toxic effect on lung tissue.','Medium','cardiovascular','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","side effects","mechanism","pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:33:35.111Z','2026-10-23T23:33:35.111Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5r003le9oioayy95oi','mcq','22- In the case of truncus arteriosus, what is the change in the second heart sound?','["A single 2nd heart sound is observed.","A wide splitting of the 2nd heart sound.","A normal 2nd heart sound.","A split 2nd heart sound."]','A single 2nd heart sound is observed.','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["truncus","arteriosus","heart","sound"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.631Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.631Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvllw3ht0014dbrd623eo6l9','mcq','A 72-year-old female with a history of hypertension, chronic kidney disease (eGFR 35 mL/min), and type 2 diabetes is being evaluated for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF). Which of the following statements regarding ACE inhibitor therapy in this patient is most accurate?','["ACE inhibitors are contraindicated due to her chronic kidney disease","ACE inhibitors should be initiated at full dose without monitoring","ACE inhibitors are first-line therapy for HFrEF regardless of renal function","ACE inhibitors should be avoided in patients with diabetes","ACE inhibitors increase the risk of acute kidney injury in all patients with CKD"]','ACE inhibitors are first-line therapy for HFrEF regardless of renal function','ACE inhibitors are first-line therapy for HFrEF and have been shown to reduce mortality and hospitalizations in patients with reduced ejection fraction. While they require careful monitoring in patients with chronic kidney disease (especially when eGFR < 30 mL/min), they are not contraindicated and should be used with dose adjustments and monitoring of serum creatinine and potassium levels. The benefits in HFrEF generally outweigh the risks when used appropriately.','HFrEF → ACE Inhibitors First-Line → Monitor Renal Function → Don''t Avoid','In patients with CKD and HFrEF, start ACE inhibitors at low doses and titrate slowly. Monitor serum creatinine within 1-2 weeks of initiation or dose increase; a < 30% increase from baseline is acceptable.','Option A is incorrect because ACE inhibitors are not contraindicated in CKD, just require monitoring. Option B is incorrect because ACE inhibitors should be initiated at low doses and titrated. Option D is incorrect because ACE inhibitors are particularly beneficial in diabetic patients with HFrEF. Option E is incorrect because while AKI risk exists, it''s not universal and is manageable with monitoring.','Medium','cardiovascular','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","heart failure","chronic kidney disease","indications"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:33:41.681Z','2026-10-23T23:33:41.681Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvllw8wi0016dbrdaokjpm0l','mcq','A 58-year-old male presents to the emergency department with acute onset of facial swelling, difficulty breathing, and hoarseness that developed 1 hour after taking his first dose of enalapril for hypertension. He has no history of similar reactions. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management and long-term strategy for this patient?','["Continue enalapril with antihistamine therapy and monitor closely","Switch to an ARB with premedication with corticosteroids and antihistamines","Discontinue ACE inhibitor permanently and avoid all ACE inhibitors in the future","Switch to a calcium channel blocker and continue ACE inhibitor therapy","Administer epinephrine and switch to a beta-blocker"]','Discontinue ACE inhibitor permanently and avoid all ACE inhibitors in the future','This patient is experiencing angioedema, a potentially life-threatening side effect of ACE inhibitors that occurs in 0.1-0.7% of patients. The immediate management involves stopping the ACE inhibitor and providing supportive care (airway protection, epinephrine if severe). Long-term, ACE inhibitors should be permanently avoided due to the risk of recurrent angioedema. ARBs are generally contraindicated as well since they also affect the renin-angiotensin system, though some data suggests the cross-reactivity risk may be lower.','ACE Angioedema → Stop ACE → Avoid Future → Alternative Therapy','Angioedema typically occurs within the first week of ACE inhibitor therapy but can happen at any time. Patients with a history of angioedema should never receive ACE inhibitors or ARBs due to potential cross-reactivity.','Option A is incorrect because angioedema requires discontinuation of the ACE inhibitor. Option B is incorrect because ARBs have a cross-reactivity risk of ~30% in patients with ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema. Option D is incorrect because continuing ACE inhibitor therapy is dangerous. Option E is incorrect while epinephrine may be needed acutely, beta-blockers are not the appropriate alternative.','Medium','cardiovascular','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","angioedema","side effects","emergency management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-23T23:33:48.690Z','2026-10-23T23:33:48.690Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlvllns0007c8rd4k7eazpq','flashcard','Warfarin antidote','[]','Vitamin K','Vitamin K','','','','Moderate','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants','["flashcard","Basic Pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T04:05:28.135Z','2026-10-24T04:05:28.135Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlx65pd000ebbrdr8clz5sg','flashcard','Metformin contraindication','[]','eGFR below 30','eGFR below 30','','','','Moderate','','','["flashcard"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T04:49:26.872Z','2026-10-24T04:49:26.872Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlxufdx000sbbrdm5grzusg','flashcard','Metformin''s primary mechanism of action','[]','Activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), leading to decreased hepatic glucose production and increased insulin sensitivity','Activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), leading to decreased hepatic glucose production and increased insulin sensitivity','','','','Moderate','Endocrine','Metformin mechanism of action','["flashcard","Metformin mechanism of action"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:08:19.164Z','2026-10-24T05:08:19.164Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlxufe9000vbbrd61g7hz7c','flashcard','How does metformin affect glucose uptake in peripheral tissues?','[]','Increases glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue by enhancing insulin sensitivity and translocation of GLUT4 transporters to the cell membrane','Increases glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue by enhancing insulin sensitivity and translocation of GLUT4 transporters to the cell membrane','','','','Moderate','Endocrine','Metformin mechanism of action','["flashcard","Metformin mechanism of action"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:08:19.164Z','2026-10-24T05:08:19.164Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlxufec000xbbrdd7d23s6g','flashcard','What is metformin''s effect on intestinal glucose absorption?','[]','Decreases intestinal glucose absorption by inhibiting glucose transporters (SGLT1) in the intestinal mucosa','Decreases intestinal glucose absorption by inhibiting glucose transporters (SGLT1) in the intestinal mucosa','','','','Moderate','Endocrine','Metformin mechanism of action','["flashcard","Metformin mechanism of action"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:08:19.164Z','2026-10-24T05:08:19.164Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzi0g2001dv6rdpv765xmj','mcq','A 2-year-old child presents with acute gastroenteritis and has lost 8% of body weight. Vital signs show heart rate 140 bpm, blood pressure 90/50 mmHg, and capillary refill time of 3 seconds. Which is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?','["Intravenous fluids at 20 mL/kg bolus followed by maintenance","Oral rehydration solution (ORS) at 10 mL/kg per hour","NPO status for 24 hours then resume feeding","Antidiarrheal medication to reduce stool output","Intravenous antibiotics for presumed bacterial gastroenteritis"]','Intravenous fluids at 20 mL/kg bolus followed by maintenance','This child has moderate dehydration (8% weight loss, prolonged capillary refill, tachycardia). According to ESPGHAN/ESPID guidelines, intravenous fluid resuscitation is indicated for moderate to severe dehydration. The initial bolus should be 20 mL/kg of isotonic crystalloid, followed by maintenance fluids. Early feeding should be resumed once the child is rehydrated, but NPO status is not recommended. Antidiarrheal medications are contraindicated in acute gastroenteritis as they may prolong infection. Antibiotics are not routinely indicated for viral gastroenteritis.','MODERATE DEHYDRATION → IV FLUIDS','Assess dehydration using weight loss percentage, capillary refill, and vital signs. Moderate dehydration (6-9% weight loss) requires IV fluids, while mild dehydration (<5%) can be managed with ORS.','Option 2 (ORS) is incorrect for moderate dehydration. Option 3 (NPO) is not evidence-based as early feeding improves outcomes. Option 4 (antidiarrheals) can be harmful. Option 5 (antibiotics) is not routinely indicated for viral gastroenteritis.','Medium','general','pediatric_diarrhea','["General Medicine","General","deep-agent","pediatric_diarrhea","gastroenteritis","dehydration","pediatrics","fluid_management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:54:39.170Z','2026-10-24T05:54:39.170Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzinhg001kv6rd60utei0m','mcq','A 3-month-old infant presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. An upper GI series shows the duodenum to the right of the midline and a corkscrew appearance of the distal duodenum. What is the most appropriate next step in management?','["Nasogastric decompression and observation","Immediate surgical consultation for Ladd procedure","CT scan to confirm the diagnosis","Trial of conservative management with NPO status","Endoscopic evaluation for possible obstruction"]','Immediate surgical consultation for Ladd procedure','The presentation with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension in an infant is highly concerning for midgut volvulus secondary to malrotation. The upper GI findings (duodenum to the right of midline, corkscrew appearance) confirm the diagnosis. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate Ladd procedure. Nasogastric decompression should be performed while awaiting surgery, but observation or conservative management is contraindicated due to the risk of bowel necrosis.','BILIOUS VOMITING + INFANT → SURGERY','Bilious vomiting in any infant is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise. Malrotation with volvulus can lead to bowel necrosis within hours, making prompt intervention critical.','Option 1 (NG decompression alone) is insufficient as it doesn''t address the underlying surgical emergency. Option 3 (CT scan) delays treatment and is not first-line. Option 4 (conservative management) is dangerous. Option 5 (endoscopy) is not appropriate for this diagnosis.','Medium','general','malrotation','["General Medicine","General","deep-agent","malrotation","pediatric_surgery","malrotation","volvulus","bilious_vomiting"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:55:09.028Z','2026-10-24T05:55:09.028Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzj7on001pv6rduwr2bzv7','mcq','A 5-year-old boy with severe hemophilia A (factor VIII level <1%) presents with a spontaneous knee hemarthrosis. He has no history of inhibitors. What is the most appropriate initial management?','["Factor VIII replacement at 20-40 IU/kg every 12-24 hours","Factor VIII replacement at 40-60 IU/kg as a single dose","Observation with rest and ice to the affected joint","Oral tranexamic acid 10 mg/kg every 6 hours","Factor VIII replacement at 80-100 IU/kg as a single dose"]','Factor VIII replacement at 40-60 IU/kg as a single dose','For spontaneous hemarthrosis in severe hemophilia A, initial factor replacement should be 40-60 IU/kg to achieve a target level of 40-60% for 1-2 days. This is typically given as a single dose initially, with additional doses based on clinical response. Higher doses (80-100 IU/kg) are reserved for life-threatening bleeds. Tranexamic acid is an adjunct but not primary treatment for hemarthrosis. Observation alone is inadequate for joint bleeds.','SEVERE HEMARTHROSIS → 40-60 IU/kg','For joint bleeds in hemophilia, aim for factor levels of 40-60% for 1-2 days. Monitor clinical response and repeat factor replacement as needed. Early treatment prevents joint damage.','Option 1 (20-40 IU/kg) is too low for severe hemophilia. Option 3 (observation) is inadequate as joint bleeds require factor replacement. Option 4 (tranexamic acid only) is insufficient. Option 5 (80-100 IU/kg) is excessive for routine hemarthrosis.','Medium','general','hemophilia_management','["General Medicine","General","deep-agent","hemophilia_management","hemophilia","factor_replacement","bleeding_disorders","joint_hemarthrosis"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2c00e8i8rm3az16pcl","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"In 2020 the World Federation of Hemophilia issued new protocols favoring prophylaxis instead: rather than waiting for a child to bleed into a joint, develop hemarthrosis, need a joint replacement, and become handicapped (with the state then bearing the cost of that care), smaller doses are given regularly ahead of time to prevent the bleed from ever happening.","chunkContent":"Regimen Factor VIII Factor IX\\nOn-demand (treat when bleeding occurs) ★ 25–40 units/kg every 12h ★ 40–60 units/kg every 24h Prophylaxis — Lecture (every 3 days) 20–40 IU/kg every 3 days — Prophylaxis — Doctor (2020 WFH protocol) ★ 20–25 units/kg twice weekly\\n—\\nS Clarifications Note — Why the Field Moved From On-Demand Treatment to Prophylaxis in 2020 (39) Until 2020, Egypt followed an on-demand policy — treating only once a bleed had already happened. In 2020 the World Federation of Hemophilia issued new protocols favoring prophylaxis instead: rather than waiting for a child to bleed into a joint, develop hemarthrosis, need a joint replacement, and become handicapped (with the state then bearing the cost of that care), smaller doses are given regularly ahead of time to prevent the bleed from ever happening.","answerCoverage":0.6,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:55:35.207Z','2026-10-24T05:55:35.207Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzl03u001vv6rd2yxtqoyr','mcq','A 6-year-old child presents with constipation that began at age 3 years. The child has hard stools, straining during defecation, and stools less than 3 times per week for the past 2 months. Physical examination is normal. What is the most appropriate diagnosis and what key diagnostic criteria must be met?','["Organic constipation - requires evidence of structural abnormality on imaging","Functional constipation - must meet Rome IV criteria of 2+ symptoms occurring at least once weekly for 1 month","Functional constipation - requires abdominal distension and palpable fecal mass","Organic constipation - requires family history of Hirschsprung disease"]','Functional constipation - must meet Rome IV criteria of 2+ symptoms occurring at least once weekly for 1 month','Functional constipation is a clinical diagnosis based on history and physical examination, with no evidence of organic disease. The Rome IV criteria require at least 2 of the following occurring at least once weekly for 1 month: 2 or fewer defecations per week, retentive posturing, painful or hard bowel movements, large fecal mass in rectum, or fecal incontinence [S1]. This child meets criteria with hard stools, straining, and decreased frequency.','ROME criteria: 2+ symptoms, weekly, monthly duration','Functional constipation is diagnosed clinically using Rome IV criteria; organic causes are rare (<5% of cases) and require red flag evaluation [S5].','Option A is incorrect because organic constipation requires specific structural/organic findings, not just symptoms. Option C is incorrect because abdominal distension and fecal mass are supportive but not diagnostic criteria. Option D is incorrect because family history is a red flag but not diagnostic criteria.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Constipation diagnosis','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Constipation diagnosis","functional constipation","Rome criteria","diagnosis"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b0059i8rmv0gfj0f7","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"II FUNCTIONAL CONSTIPATION\\nMore than 95% of pediatric constipation — mechanism, triggers & treatment\\n\\n2.1 Definition & Key Features\\nFunctional constipation is constipation with no identifiable organic cause, most often seen in infants and preschool children. • Frequently associated with formula changes in infancy. • No organic cause found on work-up. • Passing hard stools ≤ 2 times per week.","chunkContent":"II FUNCTIONAL CONSTIPATION\\nMore than 95% of pediatric constipation — mechanism, triggers & treatment\\n\\n2.1 Definition & Key Features\\nFunctional constipation is constipation with no identifiable organic cause, most often seen in infants and preschool children.\\n\\n• Frequently associated with formula changes in infancy.\\n\\n• No organic cause found on work-up.\\n\\n• Passing hard stools ≤ 2 times per week.\\n\\n• Treatment: fruit juices and medications with high sugar content draw water into the stool, softening it and easing evacuation.\\nTriggers\\n\\n• H The single most common trigger for functional constipation is painful or frightening defecation — the child associates the act of defecating with pain and begins to avoid it.\\n\\nAge-Specific Differences\\nTODDLERS\\n\\n• Dietary changes (introduction of cow’s milk) lead to dry, hard stools with fissures and pain","answerCoverage":0.17,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:56:58.699Z','2026-10-24T05:56:58.699Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5s003me9oi54hxqce9','mcq','A 6-month-old boy who was born with pulmonary atresia and ventricular septal defect received a modified Blalock-Taussig (systemic to pulmonary artery) shunt 5 days after birth. His oxygen saturations have ranged between 70% and 84% at office visits over the past 2 months. During a recent supervision visit, you record a hematocrit at 57% (0.57). Regarding this child''s polycythemia, what is the increased risk in this condition?','["Increased risk of vascular accident.","Increased risk of stroke.","Increased risk of necrotizing enterocolitis.","Increased risk of heart failure."]','Increased risk of vascular accident.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["polycythemia","cyanotic","shunt","complication"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.632Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.632Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzlnbw001xv6rdgabs8lu3','mcq','A 2-month-old infant presents with severe constipation, failure to pass meconium for 72 hours after birth, and abdominal distension. What is the most concerning red flag that suggests an organic cause requiring urgent evaluation?','["Constipation onset after 6 months of age","Passage of meconium >48 hours after delivery","Family history of constipation","Normal growth and development"]','Passage of meconium >48 hours after delivery','Delayed passage of meconium (>48 hours after birth) is a classic red flag for Hirschsprung disease, a common organic cause of constipation in infants [S2]. Hirschsprung disease results from absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus, causing functional obstruction. Other red flags include onset in the first month, family history of Hirschsprung disease, and ribbon-like stools [S2].','Meconium delay = Hirschprung suspicion','In infants, delayed meconium passage (>48h) is highly specific for Hirschsprung disease and requires urgent evaluation with rectal biopsy [S2].','Option A is incorrect because early onset (<1 month) is more concerning for organic causes. Option C is a red flag but less specific than delayed meconium passage. Option D is incorrect because organic constipation often presents with failure to thrive.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Constipation red flags','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Constipation red flags","organic constipation","Hirschsprung disease","red flags"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005gi8rmao52wit2","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"S Clarifications Note\\nThe mechanism in one line: the aganglionic segment cannot relax, so it acts as a permanent, non-relaxing \\"valve\\" — stool piles up proximal to it, which is what produces the massively dilated colon seen on imaging, even though the aganglionic segment itself is often narrow. 3.2 Clinical Presentation\\n\\n• Symptoms usually present at birth.","chunkContent":"3.1 Definition & Etiology\\nAbsence of ganglion cells in the bowel wall, beginning at the internal anal sphincter and extending proximally for a variable length of gut.\\n\\n• It is the most common cause of bowel obstruction in neonates.\\n\\nNormal Colon vs. Hirschsprung’s Disease — Anatomy\\nThe aganglionic segment (red) fails to relax, causing functional obstruction and proximal colonic dilatation.\\n\\nS Clarifications Note\\nThe mechanism in one line: the aganglionic segment cannot relax, so it acts as a permanent, non-relaxing \\"valve\\" — stool piles up proximal to it, which is what produces the massively dilated colon seen on imaging, even though the aganglionic segment itself is often narrow.\\n\\n3.2 Clinical Presentation\\n\\n• Symptoms usually present at birth.\\n\\n• H Suspect Hirschsprung’s disease in any full-term infant with a delay in passage of meconium beyond 24 hours.","answerCoverage":0.75,"judgeScore":10},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005qi8rmm7x5xux7","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Hirschsprung’s disease presents from birth, not with new soiling at age 6. Q2. A full-term neonate fails to pass meconium by 30 hours of life and has progressive abdominal distention. Rectal exam reveals an empty rectum with normal anal tone, followed by explosive release of stool and gas. What is the best next confirmatory test?","chunkContent":"B) New-onset Hirschsprung’s disease\\n\\nC) Sexual abuse\\n\\nD) A urinary tract infection\\nExplanation\\nSoiling in a previously continent child (secondary incontinence) with a palpable fecal mass is classic for overflow incontinence around a fecal impaction — liquid stool leaks around the retained hard mass. Hirschsprung’s disease presents from birth, not with new soiling at age 6.\\nQ2. A full-term neonate fails to pass meconium by 30 hours of life and has progressive abdominal distention. Rectal exam reveals an empty rectum with normal anal tone, followed by explosive release of stool and gas. What is the best next confirmatory test?\\n\\nA) Plain abdominal X-ray\\n\\nB) Abdominal ultrasound\\n3 C) Rectal suction biopsy","answerCoverage":0.5,"judgeScore":10},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005ki8rmgwfl542w","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Neonatal onset\\nConstipation from birth\\nDelayed meconium\\n>24–48h after birth\\nRibbon stools\\nSuggests anorectal stenosis\\nFailure to thrive\\nPoor weight gain / growth delay\\nEmpty rectum\\n\\nExplosive stool on DRE","chunkContent":"Abdominal distention Usually not Yes\\nPoor weight gain Usually not Common\\n\\nAnal tone Normal Normal\\nRectal exam Stool present in the ampulla Empty rectum — no stool Anorectal manometry Rectal distention → relaxation of internal sphincter No sphincter relaxation (paradoxical rise) Barium enema Large amount of stool; no transition zone Transition zone with delayed evacuation\\n\\n4.2 Red Flags Suggesting an Organic Cause (Refer!) Neonatal onset\\nConstipation from birth\\nDelayed meconium\\n>24–48h after birth\\nRibbon stools\\nSuggests anorectal stenosis\\nFailure to thrive\\nPoor weight gain / growth delay\\nEmpty rectum\\n\\nExplosive stool on DRE","answerCoverage":0.25,"judgeScore":10}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:57:28.796Z','2026-10-24T05:57:28.796Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzm0y5001zv6rdpoolqdew','mcq','A 7-year-old with functional constipation has failed dietary modifications and behavioral interventions. What is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?','["Polyethylene glycol (PEG) 1 g/kg/day","Lactulose 1-3 mL/kg/day divided into 2 doses","Mineral oil 15-30 mL twice daily","Bisacodyl suppositories as needed"]','Polyethylene glycol (PEG) 1 g/kg/day','Polyethylene glycol (PEG) without electrolytes is the first-line treatment for pediatric functional constipation [S1, S2]. PEG works by drawing water into the colon, softening stool and promoting bowel movements. It has excellent efficacy and safety profile in children. Lactulose is second-line, mineral oil has safety concerns, and bisacodyl should be reserved for short-term use due to potential dependency [S1, S3].','PEG = Pediatric constipation first-line','PEG is osmotic laxative of first choice in children with functional constipation; start with 1 g/kg/day and titrate to response [S2].','Option B is incorrect because lactulose is second-line. Option C is incorrect due to aspiration risk and poor palatability. Option D is incorrect because bisacodyl can cause dependency and should not be used as first-line.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Constipation treatment','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Constipation treatment","polyethylene glycol","first-line treatment","pharmacotherapy"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005mi8rmxcts0ou9","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Current ESPGHAN/NASPGHAN guidance names polyethylene glycol (PEG) as the preferred first-line agent for both disimpaction and maintenance therapy, generally preferred over lactulose or enemas because it can be given orally. Typical PEG dosing: disimpaction — 1–1.5 g/kg/day for 3–6 days; maintenance — 0.2–0.8 g/kg/day, titrated to a soft daily stool.","chunkContent":"From ESPGHAN/NASPGHAN & Rome IV — Supplementary Reading The formal diagnostic criteria taught in this lecture correspond to the Rome IV criteria for pediatric functional constipation, the current internationally accepted definition.\\nCurrent ESPGHAN/NASPGHAN guidance names polyethylene glycol (PEG) as the preferred first-line agent for both disimpaction and maintenance therapy, generally preferred over lactulose or enemas because it can be given orally.\\nTypical PEG dosing: disimpaction — 1–1.5 g/kg/day for 3–6 days; maintenance — 0.2–0.8 g/kg/day, titrated to a soft daily stool.\\nAdditional red-flag features that should prompt referral to pediatric gastroenterology/surgery, beyond those already covered above: ribbon stools, blood in the stool without a visible fissure, fever, a perianal fistula, an abnormally positioned anus, and a family history of Hirschsprung’s disease.","answerCoverage":1,"judgeScore":10}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:57:46.445Z','2026-10-24T05:57:46.445Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzmdav0021v6rd21m74u9l','mcq','What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism underlying functional constipation in children?','["Structural obstruction of the colon","Colonic dysmotility and rectal retention","Metabolic electrolyte abnormalities","Neurological spinal cord abnormalities"]','Colonic dysmotility and rectal retention','Functional constipation primarily involves colonic dysmotility and rectal retention [S1]. The pathophysiology includes delayed colonic transit time, impaired rectal sensation, and learned stool withholding behavior. When stool enters the rectum, it causes distension and conscious perception of the urge to defecate, but in functional constipation, children often withhold stool due to fear or pain, leading to further retention and rectal dilation [S3].','DRT: Dysmotility, Retention, Withholding','Functional constipation involves a vicious cycle: stool withholding → rectal dilation → decreased sensation → more withholding [S1].','Option A is incorrect because structural obstruction defines organic constipation. Option C is incorrect because metabolic abnormalities cause organic constipation. Option D is incorrect because spinal cord abnormalities are organic causes.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Constipation pathophysiology','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Constipation pathophysiology","colonic dysmotility","rectal retention","pathophysiology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:58:02.456Z','2026-10-24T05:58:02.456Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzmpy40023v6rdshdhjyrb','mcq','A 4-year-old presents with constipation, abdominal pain, and encopresis. On examination, there is a palpable fecal mass in the left lower quadrant. What is the most appropriate initial treatment approach?','["Start with polyethylene glycol and disimpaction","Dietary fiber increase and increased fluids only","Behavioral therapy alone without medication","Abdominal X-ray to confirm impaction before treatment"]','Start with polyethylene glycol and disimpaction','When fecal impaction is present (palpable mass), treatment should include both disimpaction and maintenance therapy [S1]. Polyethylene glycol is effective for both disimpaction (higher doses) and maintenance therapy. Disimpaction can be achieved with PEG, enemas, or suppositories, followed by maintenance therapy to prevent re-impaction. Dietary changes alone are insufficient for established impaction, and behavioral therapy should be combined with medical management [S1, S3].','IMPACT: Impaction Management Polyethylene And Cleanout Therapy','Fecal impaction requires both disimpaction (PEG/enemas) AND maintenance therapy to break the cycle of constipation/encopresis [S1].','Option B is incorrect because dietary changes alone cannot resolve established impaction. Option C is incorrect because behavioral therapy should complement, not replace, medical treatment. Option D is incorrect because clinical examination is sufficient for diagnosis; imaging is not routinely needed.','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Constipation treatment','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","Constipation treatment","fecal impaction","disimpaction","encopresis"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b005ci8rm2vodriro","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Restore Tone\\nRebuild sphincter/rectal muscle tone\\nMaintenance\\nPrevent relapse (4–6 months)\\nPhase I — Immediate Disimpaction (3–5 days)\\n\\n• Goal: clear out the hard, chronically accumulated stool. • Oral route: lactulose 1–3 ml/kg. • Rectal route: phosphate, salt, or paraffin-oil enema.","chunkContent":"Restore Tone\\nRebuild sphincter/rectal muscle tone\\nMaintenance\\nPrevent relapse (4–6 months)\\nPhase I — Immediate Disimpaction (3–5 days)\\n\\n• Goal: clear out the hard, chronically accumulated stool.\\n\\n• Oral route: lactulose 1–3 ml/kg.\\n\\n• Rectal route: phosphate, salt, or paraffin-oil enema.\\n\\nPhase II — Restore Muscle Tone\\nAims to restore the tone of the sphincter, using any of:\\n\\n– Stool softeners, hyperosmotic laxatives, non-absorbable salts.\\n\\n– Combinations (e.g. lactulose + paraffin).\\n\\n– Toilet training.\\nMaintenance (2–6 months)\\n\\n• Oral route — typically mineral oil or polyethylene glycol (PEG).\\n\\nPhase III (4–6 months) — Restore Regular Bowel Habits, Avoid Relapse\\n\\n• Gradually cut down laxative use.\\n\\n• Increase daily fiber and fluid intake.","answerCoverage":0.75,"judgeScore":7}]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T05:58:18.844Z','2026-10-24T05:58:18.844Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5s003ne9oixnct9bj3','mcq','A neonate with central cyanosis due to congenital heart disease. What is the main complaint?','["Difficulty during sucking and interruption of feeding.","Cyanosis only appears during daytime.","Cyanosis with exertion.","Cyanosis during sleep."]','Difficulty during sucking and interruption of feeding.','','','','','Easy','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","neonate","feeding","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.632Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.632Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvm0i50z000ywnrdf85syx40','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic constipation characterized by straining during defecation, lumpy or hard stools, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation for the past 6 months. She reports no rectal bleeding, weight loss, or family history of colorectal cancer. Which of the following pathophysiological mechanisms is most likely responsible for her constipation?','["Colonic obstruction from a proximal colon mass","Decreased colonic motility due to chronic opioid use","Functional constipation with normal colonic transit time","Pelvic floor dyssynergia with impaired defecation","Hypothyroidism-induced decreased gastrointestinal motility"]','C','The patient''s symptoms (straining during defecation, lumpy/hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation) for 6 months without red flags (bleeding, weight loss, family history) are classic for functional constipation. According to Rome IV criteria [S1], functional constipation requires at least two of: straining during ≥25% of defecations, lumpy/hard stools, sensation of incomplete evacuation, or need for manual maneuvers. Functional constipation typically has normal colonic transit time, distinguishing it from slow transit constipation [S3]. The absence of opioid use, pelvic floor dysfunction symptoms, or systemic symptoms makes other options less likely.','ROME criteria: Straining, Lumpy stools, Incomplete evacuation, Manual maneuvers needed','Functional constipation is the most common type of chronic constipation and typically responds well to lifestyle modifications and osmotic laxatives rather than invasive testing.','A is incorrect as colonic obstruction would present with more acute/severe symptoms and likely abdominal distension. B is incorrect as there''s no mention of opioid use. D is incorrect as pelvic floor dyssynergia typically involves paradoxical anal contraction during defecation, not just straining. E is incorrect as hypothyroidism would typically present with other systemic symptoms like fatigue, weight gain, or cold intolerance.','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation pathophysiology','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation pathophysiology","functional constipation","Rome criteria","colonic motility"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T06:22:44.723Z','2026-10-24T06:22:44.723Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvm0ijmg0010wnrdhmbhytdu','mcq','A 68-year-old man with chronic constipation presents to the clinic with new-onset severe abdominal pain, distension, and inability to pass gas or stool for 48 hours. On examination, he has a distended abdomen with high-pitched bowel sounds and tenderness in the left lower quadrant. Which of the following diagnostic findings would be most concerning for a serious underlying pathology requiring immediate intervention?','["Elevated C-reactive protein (CRP) of 15 mg/L","Mild leukocytosis of 11,000/mm³","Air-fluid levels on abdominal X-ray","Thickened colonic wall on CT scan","Elevated serum amylase of 120 U/L"]','C','The patient''s presentation with severe abdominal pain, distension, inability to pass gas/stool, and high-pitched bowel sounds suggests possible mechanical obstruction or fecal impaction. Air-fluid levels on abdominal X-ray are highly concerning for mechanical bowel obstruction [S3], which is a surgical emergency. The other findings (elevated CRP, mild leukocytosis, thickened colonic wall) could be seen in various conditions including constipation but are not as specific for obstruction. Elevated amylase is not typically associated with constipation complications.','SBO signs: Severe pain, Bloating, Obstipation (no stool/gas), Air-fluid levels','In patients with constipation presenting with severe abdominal pain and distension, always consider mechanical obstruction as a life-threatening complication that requires immediate imaging and intervention.','A is incorrect as elevated CRP is non-specific and can be seen in many inflammatory conditions. B is incorrect as mild leukocytosis is common in constipation and not specific for obstruction. D is incorrect as thickened colonic wall can be seen in inflammatory conditions but is not diagnostic of obstruction. E is incorrect as elevated amylase suggests pancreatic involvement, not related to constipation complications.','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation diagnosis','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation diagnosis","constipation complications","bowel obstruction","red flags"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T06:23:03.640Z','2026-10-24T06:23:03.640Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvm0iwle0012wnrdq7iwy2ru','mcq','A 72-year-old nursing home resident with chronic constipation fails to respond adequately to increased fiber intake and adequate fluid consumption. She has normal thyroid function and no evidence of mechanical obstruction. Which of the following treatment approaches would be most appropriate as the next step in her management?','["Immediate referral for surgical consultation","Trial of polyethylene glycol (PEG) osmotic laxative","Addition of docusate sodium stool softener","Discontinuation of all current medications","Initiation of monthly bisacodyl suppositories"]','B','For patients with chronic constipation who fail lifestyle modifications (fiber, fluids), the next step is typically an osmotic laxative such as polyethylene glycol [S2]. The 2023 ACG/AGA guidelines specifically recommend osmotic laxatives as first-line pharmacological treatment [S1]. PEG is preferred over stool softeners (docusate) which have limited efficacy, and stimulant laxatives (bisacodyl) are generally reserved for later use due to potential tolerance and electrolyte disturbances. Surgical consultation is premature without evidence of structural disease, and medication discontinuation should be guided by specific medication review rather than blanket discontinuation.','PEG progression: Polyethylene glycol after fiber fails','Osmotic laxatives like PEG are the cornerstone of pharmacological treatment for chronic constipation and can be used long-term safely in most patients.','A is incorrect as surgery is reserved for specific structural causes or severe complications not present here. C is incorrect as docusate has limited efficacy as a single agent and is generally not recommended as monotherapy. D is incorrect as medication review should be targeted rather than blanket discontinuation. E is incorrect as bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative typically used after osmotic agents and carries risks of electrolyte disturbances with long-term use.','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation management','["constipation","Gastrointestinal","deep-agent","constipation management","laxatives","polyethylene glycol","treatment guidelines"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T06:23:20.450Z','2026-10-24T06:23:20.450Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmrr32w0008udrdul4hjs5h','mcq','A 45-year-old male presents with sudden onset of severe epigastric pain radiating to his back. He has a history of heavy alcohol use. On examination, he has tenderness in the epigastric region and his serum lipase is elevated to 3 times the upper limit of normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute pancreatitis","Peptic ulcer disease","Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Biliary colic","Gastric cancer"]','Acute pancreatitis','The patient''s presentation with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, history of heavy alcohol use, and elevated serum lipase is classic for acute pancreatitis. Alcohol consumption is a major risk factor for pancreatitis, and lipase elevation is the most specific laboratory finding for this condition.','"SEVERE pain + BACK radiation + LIPESE elevation = PANCREATITIS"','Alcohol and gallstones account for 70-80% of acute pancreatitis cases','Peptic ulcer disease typically presents with epigastric pain but not radiating to back; GERD causes burning pain but not severe acute pain; biliary colic is usually shorter duration; gastric cancer presents more insidiously','Medium','Gastrointestinal','Pancreatitis','["Pancreatitis","Gastrointestinal","chat-agent","Emergency medicine","Gastroenterology","Alcohol-related"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T19:05:31.736Z','2026-10-24T19:05:31.736Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwugo10006g7rdmiuu5pcz','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus is started on lisinopril. Which of the following adverse effects is most commonly associated with ACE inhibitors and typically occurs within the first few weeks of therapy?','["Hyperkalemia","Dry cough","Peripheral edema","Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia"]','B','Dry cough is the most common adverse effect of ACE inhibitors, occurring in 5-35% of patients. It is thought to be due to the accumulation of bradykinin and substance P in the lungs, leading to irritation and cough. This typically develops within the first few weeks of therapy and may persist as long as the medication is continued. The cough is non-productive and can be bothersome enough to require discontinuation of the ACE inhibitor.','','ACE inhibitor-induced cough is more common in women, non-smokers, and patients of Chinese descent. Switching to an ARB is often the best management strategy when ACE inhibitor cough occurs.','A) Hyperkalemia can occur with ACE inhibitors but is less common than cough and develops more gradually. C) Peripheral edema is more typical of calcium channel blockers. D) Bradycardia is associated with beta blockers. E) Hypoglycemia is not a typical adverse effect of ACE inhibitors.','Medium','Pharmacology','ACE inhibitors','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","pharmacology","ACE inhibitors","adverse effects"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:28:07.393Z','2026-10-24T21:28:07.393Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwus6q0008g7rdtarca32x','mcq','A 72-year-old patient with hypertension and chronic stable angina is prescribed a calcium channel blocker. Which of the following calcium channel blockers would be most appropriate for this patient, considering their dual indication for both hypertension and angina?','["Diltiazem","Verapamil","Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Diltiazem ER"]','C','Amlodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that is appropriate for both hypertension and chronic stable angina. It provides vasodilation with minimal negative inotropic effects, making it well-tolerated for patients with coronary artery disease. Unlike verapamil and diltiazem, amlodipine does not significantly slow the heart rate or cause significant AV block, making it safer for patients with concomitant sinus node dysfunction or AV conduction disease.','','Amlodipine has a long half-life (30-50 hours) allowing for once-daily dosing, and its vasodilatory effects are more pronounced on arterial vessels than venous vessels, making it particularly useful for angina.','A) Diltiazem and E) Diltiazem ER are non-dihydropyridines that can cause bradycardia and AV block, which may be problematic in some patients. B) Verapamil is a non-dihydropyridine that can cause significant bradycardia and should be used cautiously in patients with sinus node dysfunction. D) Nifedipine is a dihydropyridine but is associated with reflex tachycardia and is not preferred for angina compared to amlodipine.','Medium','Pharmacology','Calcium channel blockers','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Calcium channel blockers","pharmacology","calcium channel blockers","angina"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:28:22.322Z','2026-10-24T21:28:22.322Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwv38i000ag7rdyvuwuiqy','mcq','A 58-year-old patient with hypertension, tachycardia, and recent myocardial infarction is started on metoprolol. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of beta blockers in the post-MI setting?','["Decreased myocardial oxygen demand through heart rate reduction","Increased coronary blood flow","Decreased platelet aggregation","Increased contractility","Decreased afterload"]','A','Beta blockers decrease myocardial oxygen demand primarily through heart rate reduction, which decreases the heart''s workload and oxygen consumption. They also reduce blood pressure and contractility, further decreasing myocardial oxygen demand. In the post-MI setting, beta blockers have been shown to reduce mortality by preventing arrhythmias, reducing infarct size, and preventing remodeling.','','Beta blockers should be started within 24 hours of acute MI (unless contraindicated) and continued long-term for secondary prevention. The benefit is dose-dependent, so target doses should be achieved when possible.','B) Beta blockers do not significantly increase coronary blood flow and may even decrease it in some cases. C) Beta blockers have minimal effects on platelet aggregation. D) Beta blockers decrease contractility, not increase it. E) Beta blockers decrease afterload through blood pressure reduction, but this is not their primary mechanism in post-MI care.','Medium','Pharmacology','Beta blockers','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Beta blockers","pharmacology","beta blockers","myocardial infarction"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:28:36.642Z','2026-10-24T21:28:36.642Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwveo1000cg7rdp305or2d','mcq','A 45-year-old patient with hypertension and diabetes mellitus has an elevated serum creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL) and a calculated creatinine clearance of 45 mL/min. Which of the following antihypertensive medications would be most appropriate for this patient?','["Lisinopril","Losartan","Amlodipine","Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide"]','B','Losartan, an ARB, is the most appropriate choice for this patient with chronic kidney disease (CKD). ARBs are preferred over ACE inhibitors in patients with CKD because they provide similar renoprotective effects but have a lower risk of angioedema. In patients with CKD, ARBs have been shown to slow the progression of kidney disease by reducing intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria.','','In patients with CKD and proteinuria, ARBs reduce proteinuria by 30-50% and slow the progression to end-stage renal disease. Monitor serum potassium and creatinine 1-2 weeks after initiating or titrating ARB therapy.','A) Lisinopril (ACE inhibitor) could be used but carries a higher risk of angioedema compared to ARBs. C) Amlodipine is acceptable but does not provide the same renoprotective benefits as ARBs/ACE inhibitors. D) Thiazide diuretics become less effective when creatinine clearance falls below 30 mL/min. E) Loop diuretics are typically reserved for more advanced CKD (stage 4-5).','Medium','Pharmacology','ARBs','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ARBs","pharmacology","ARBs","chronic kidney disease"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:28:51.457Z','2026-10-24T21:28:51.457Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwvq6k000jg7rdnhlazp7f','mcq','A 67-year-old patient with stable angina pectoris is prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin for acute episodes. Which of the following statements regarding nitroglycerin pharmacology is most accurate?','["It primarily works by increasing myocardial contractility","It should be stored in a light-resistant container","It is contraindicated in patients with right ventricular infarction","It causes significant reflex tachycardia","It has a half-life of approximately 8 hours"]','B','Nitroglycerin should be stored in a light-resistant container because it is photosensitive and degrades when exposed to light. This degradation reduces its potency and effectiveness. Nitroglycerin works primarily by causing venodilation and arterial dilation, which reduces preload and afterload, thereby decreasing myocardial oxygen demand.','','Nitroglycerin tablets typically have a shelf life of 6-12 months when properly stored. Patients should be instructed to replace their nitroglycerin supply every 6 months, even if not all tablets have been used.','A) Nitroglycerin decreases myocardial contractility, not increases it. C) Nitroglycerin is actually beneficial in right ventricular infarction as it helps reduce preload and RV wall stress. D) Nitroglycerin typically causes reflex tachycardia only in patients who are volume depleted or have autonomic dysfunction. E) Nitroglycerin has a very short half-life of 1-3 minutes.','Medium','Pharmacology','Nitrates','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Nitrates","pharmacology","nitrates","angina"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:29:06.380Z','2026-10-24T21:29:06.380Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmww2nc000lg7rdzl3dbt2h','mcq','A 72-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation and a rapid ventricular response is started on flecainide. According to the Vaughan-Williams classification, flecainide belongs to which class of antiarrhythmic drugs?','["Class I (sodium channel blockers)","Class II (beta blockers)","Class III (potassium channel blockers)","Class IV (calcium channel blockers)","Class V ( miscellaneous agents)"]','A','Flecainide belongs to Class I antiarrhythmic drugs (sodium channel blockers) in the Vaughan-Williams classification. Specifically, it is a Class IC agent that causes significant slowing of conduction velocity with minimal effect on action potential duration. Class IC drugs are used for supraventricular and ventricular arrhythmias but are contraindicated in patients with structural heart disease due to increased risk of proarrhythmia.','','Class IC antiarrhythmics should be avoided in patients with coronary artery disease, heart failure, or significant left ventricular dysfunction due to increased mortality risk. They are typically reserved for patients with structurally normal hearts.','B) Class II agents are beta blockers. C) Class III agents include amiodarone and sotalol. D) Class IV agents are calcium channel blockers like verapamil and diltiazem. E) Class V includes agents like adenosine.','Medium','Pharmacology','Antiarrhythmics','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Antiarrhythmics","pharmacology","antiarrhythmics","Vaughan-Williams"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:29:22.536Z','2026-10-24T21:29:22.536Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwwea8000ng7rdediqakvg','mcq','A 62-year-old patient with hyperlipidemia and established coronary artery disease has an LDL cholesterol level of 130 mg/dL despite lifestyle modifications. According to current guidelines, which of the following statin doses would be most appropriate for this patient?','["Atorvastatin 10 mg daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily","Atorvastatin 40 mg daily","Atorvastatin 80 mg daily","Rosuvastatin 5 mg daily"]','C','For patients with established coronary artery disease (secondary prevention), current guidelines recommend high-intensity statin therapy, which typically lowers LDL cholesterol by ≥50% from baseline. Atorvastatin 40 mg daily provides approximately a 50% reduction in LDL cholesterol and is considered high-intensity therapy. The 2026 ACC/AHA guidelines recommend LDL-C <55 mg/dL for very high-risk patients, which often requires high-intensity statin therapy.','','High-intensity statin therapy is defined as daily doses that produce LDL-C reduction of ≥50%. For atorvastatin, this is 40-80 mg daily; for rosuvastatin, it is 20-40 mg daily.','A) Atorvastatin 10 mg provides only about 30% LDL reduction and is considered low-intensity. B) Atorvastatin 20 mg provides about 40% LDL reduction and is considered moderate-intensity. D) While atorvastatin 80 mg is also high-intensity, 40 mg is typically sufficient for most patients and has a better side effect profile. E) Rosuvastatin 5 mg is low-intensity therapy.','Medium','Pharmacology','Statins','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Statins","pharmacology","statins","lipid management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:29:37.616Z','2026-10-24T21:29:37.616Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwwqk3000pg7rdrzwanpgp','mcq','A 55-year-old patient with hypertension and chronic kidney stage 3 disease (eGFR 45 mL/min) is being evaluated for antihypertensive therapy. Which of the following statements regarding the use of ACE inhibitors and ARBs in patients with chronic kidney disease is most accurate?','["ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in all patients with CKD","Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs should be avoided in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis","ACE inhibitors increase the risk of hyperkalemia more than ARBs","ARBs provide better renoprotection than ACE inhibitors in diabetic nephropathy","The combination of ACE inhibitor + ARB provides additive benefits in CKD"]','B','Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs should be avoided in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis because they can cause acute renal failure by reducing glomerular filtration pressure in ischemic kidneys. In these patients, the efferent arteriole is already maximally dilated to maintain filtration pressure, and blocking the angiotensin II-mediated constriction of this arteriole can precipitate acute kidney injury.','','Before starting ACE inhibitors or ARBs in patients at risk for renal artery stenosis (e.g., those with flash pulmonary edema, asymmetric kidney sizes, or unexplained CKD), consider renal artery imaging or a trial dose with close monitoring of creatinine.','A) ACE inhibitors are not contraindicated in CKD and are actually recommended for renoprotection. C) Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs carry similar risks of hyperkalemia. D) ACE inhibitors and ARBs provide similar renoprotection in diabetic nephropathy. E) The combination of ACE inhibitor + ARB is not recommended due to increased risk of adverse effects without proven additional benefit.','Medium','Pharmacology','ACE inhibitors/ARBs','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors/ARBs","pharmacology","ACE inhibitors","ARBs","chronic kidney disease"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:29:53.523Z','2026-10-24T21:29:53.523Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwy2bl000zg7rdss1e0kvy','mcq','A 68-year-old patient with congestive heart failure (HFrEF) and hypertension presents to clinic. Current medications include furosemide, lisinopril, and carvedilol. Which of the following calcium channel blockers would be most appropriate to add for blood pressure control in this patient?','["Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Verapamil","Nifedipine","Isradipine"]','Amlodipine','Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is the most appropriate choice for this patient with HFrEF. Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (diltiazem and verapamil) are contraindicated in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction due to their negative inotropic effects, which can worsen cardiac function [S2]. Nifedipine and isradipine, while dihydropyridines, are less commonly used first-line compared to amlodipine due to reflex tachycardia and other adverse effects. Amlodipine has been shown to be safe and effective in heart failure patients and does not worsen outcomes [S1].','Dihydropyridines = Safe in HFrEF (Diltiazem/Verapamil = Don''t use)','Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) are contraindicated in HFrEF due to negative inotropic effects, while dihydropyridines (amlodipine) are generally safe.','Diltiazem and Verapamil are incorrect as they are non-dihydropyridines that worsen heart failure. Nifedipine and isradipine are dihydropyridines but are not first-line choices due to adverse effects like reflex tachycardia.','Medium','cardiovascular','calcium channel blockers','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","calcium channel blockers","heart failure","hypertension","calcium channel blockers","pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:30:55.425Z','2026-10-24T21:30:55.425Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7f005qe9oiwuegg8so','mcq','Of the following, the most common congenital heart disease association with Turner syndrome is:','["Aortic coarctation.","Patent ductus arteriosus.","Tetralogy of Fallot.","Pulmonary stenosis."]','Aortic coarctation.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["Turner syndrome","aortic coarctation","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.691Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.691Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwyk0e0018g7rd1ew95tw5','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes develops a persistent dry cough 2 weeks after starting lisinopril. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this adverse effect?','["Direct irritation of the bronchial epithelium by the drug","Increased levels of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition","Angiotensin II receptor stimulation in the lungs","Activation of substance P receptors in the airways","Hypersensitivity reaction to the sulfhydryl group in the drug"]','Increased levels of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition','The persistent dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is primarily due to increased levels of bradykinin, which is normally degraded by ACE. When ACE is inhibited, bradykinin accumulates and stimulates sensory nerves in the airways, leading to cough [S1, S2]. This effect is not dose-dependent and occurs in approximately 5-35% of patients [S1].','ACE inhibitors = Bradykinin Buildup = Bronchial Irritation = Cough (ABBC)','ACE inhibitor-induced cough typically resolves within 1-4 weeks after discontinuing the medication. Switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) is often the best alternative since ARBs do not affect bradykinin metabolism.','Option A is incorrect because ACE inhibitors don''t directly irritate bronchial epithelium. Option C is incorrect because ACE inhibitors decrease angiotensin II levels, not stimulate receptors. Option D is incorrect because while substance P may be involved, the primary mechanism is bradykinin accumulation. Option E is incorrect because lisinopril doesn''t contain a sulfhydryl group (that''s captopril).','Medium','pharmacology','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors, Constipation, Hirschsprung Disease, Platelet Disorders, Rickets","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","adverse effects","mechanism of action","cardiovascular"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:31:18.350Z','2026-10-24T21:31:18.350Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwyrc4001ag7rdk3orcxfk','mcq','A 72-year-old nursing home resident with chronic constipation is started on a medication that increases water content in the stool and stimulates peristalsis. Which of the following pharmacological agents was most likely prescribed?','["Polyethylene glycol","Bisacodyl","Docusate sodium","Loperamide","Methylnaltrexone"]','Polyethylene glycol','Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is an osmotic laxative that works by retaining water in the colon, increasing stool water content and softening stool consistency [S1]. It also has a mild stimulatory effect on peristalsis due to increased stool volume [S1]. PEG is considered first-line therapy for chronic constipation in older adults due to its efficacy and safety profile.','PEG = Pumps water into stool = Easier elimination = Gentle (PEG)','Polyethylene glycol is often preferred over stimulant laxatives for chronic constipation in elderly patients because it doesn''t cause dependency or electrolyte disturbances with long-term use. The typical dose is 17-34 grams daily, with effects seen within 24-48 hours.','Option B (bisacodyl) is a stimulant laxative that directly stimulates colonic nerves, not primarily through osmotic effects. Option C (docusate) is a stool softener that decreases surface tension but has minimal effect on peristalsis. Option D (loperamide) is an anti-diarrheal agent that inhibits peristalsis. Option E (methylnaltrexone) is used for opioid-induced constipation and works by blocking mu-opioid receptors.','Medium','pharmacology','Constipation','["ACE inhibitors, Constipation, Hirschsprung Disease, Platelet Disorders, Rickets","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Constipation","gastrointestinal","laxatives","geriatrics"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:31:27.844Z','2026-10-24T21:31:27.844Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwyytn001cg7rdqeusbf7b','mcq','A newborn presents with failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting. Rectal biopsy reveals absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses. Which of the following pharmacological interventions would be most appropriate in the immediate management of this condition?','["Bisacodyl suppositories","Polyethylene glycol solution","Botulinum toxin injections","No pharmacological intervention indicated","Octreotide injections"]','No pharmacological intervention indicated','Hirschsprung disease is a congenital disorder characterized by the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon [S1, S2]. The definitive treatment is surgical removal of the aganglionic segment [S1, S2]. Pharmacological interventions are not indicated for the definitive treatment of Hirschsprung disease. Initial management may include rectal irrigation and decompression, but these are temporary measures until surgery can be performed [S1].','Hirschsprung = No ganglion cells = No peristalsis = No drugs needed = Surgery (HNNS)','Early recognition of Hirschsprung disease is critical as delayed diagnosis can lead to life-threatening complications like enterocolitis. The classic triad of symptoms includes failure to pass meconium within 48 hours, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting.','Option A (bisacodyl) is inappropriate as stimulant laxatives can worsen obstruction in Hirschsprung disease. Option B (PEG) may provide temporary relief but doesn''t address the underlying pathology. Option C (botulinum toxin) is used for achalasia, not Hirschsprung disease. Option E (octreotide) is not indicated for Hirschsprung disease management.','Medium','pharmacology','Hirschsprung Disease','["ACE inhibitors, Constipation, Hirschsprung Disease, Platelet Disorders, Rickets","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Hirschsprung Disease","pediatrics","gastrointestinal","congenital"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:31:37.547Z','2026-10-24T21:31:37.547Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwznqi001eg7rdr82zqr1n','mcq','A patient with immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) develops severe thrombocytopenia (platelet count 10,000/μL) and mucosal bleeding. Which of the following pharmacological agents would be most appropriate as first-line treatment for this acute bleeding episode?','["Aspirin","Clopidogrel","Corticosteroids","Platelet transfusion","Dipyridamole"]','Corticosteroids','For acute immune thrombocytopenia with severe thrombocytopenia and bleeding, corticosteroids (prednisone 1-2 mg/kg/day) are the first-line pharmacological treatment [S1, S2]. Corticosteroids work by decreasing antibody production against platelets and reducing platelet destruction in the spleen [S1]. They typically produce a response within 1-3 weeks in approximately 60-80% of patients [S1].','ITP acute bleeding = Corticosteroids first = Immune suppression = Platelet protection (ICP)','In addition to corticosteroids, patients with severe bleeding (platelet count <20,000/μL with active bleeding) may require additional interventions including platelet transfusions and intravenous immune globulin (IVIG). However, corticosteroids remain the cornerstone of pharmacological management.','Option A (aspirin) and Option E (dipyridamole) are antiplatelet agents that would worsen bleeding in thrombocytopenia. Option B (clopidogrel) is also an antiplatelet agent contraindicated in thrombocytopenia. Option D (platelet transfusion) may be used acutely but doesn''t address the underlying immune-mediated destruction of platelets.','Medium','pharmacology','Platelet Disorders','["ACE inhibitors, Constipation, Hirschsprung Disease, Platelet Disorders, Rickets","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Platelet Disorders","hematology","immunology","thrombocytopenia"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:32:09.834Z','2026-10-24T21:32:09.834Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3db8001tg7rdfe2aswt8','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and asthma is being evaluated for antihypertensive therapy. Which of the following beta blockers would be MOST appropriate for this patient?','["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Carvedilol","Atenolol","Esmolol"]','Atenolol','Atenolol is a cardioselective beta-1 blocker that has minimal effect on beta-2 receptors in the lungs, making it relatively safer in patients with asthma or COPD compared to non-selective beta blockers like propranolol. While cardioselective beta blockers are not completely free of respiratory risks, they are preferred over non-selective agents in patients with reactive airway disease. Metoprolol and carvedilol also have some cardioselectivity but atenolol has the highest degree of beta-1 selectivity among the options.','A for Asthma, Atenolol - A cardioselective beta blocker with minimal beta-2 effects','In asthma/COPD, prefer cardioselective beta blockers (atenolol > metoprolol > carvedilol) over non-selective agents. Monitor respiratory function closely regardless of selection.','A) Propranolol - Non-selective beta blocker with significant beta-2 blockade, contraindicated in asthma. B) Metoprolol - Cardioselective but less selective than atenolol. C) Carvedilol - Non-selective with alpha-1 blocking effects, can cause bronchoconstriction. E) Esmolol - Ultra-short acting, not ideal for chronic hypertension management.','Medium','Cardiovascular','Beta Blockers','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Beta Blockers","asthma","beta blockers","hypertension","pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:02.948Z','2026-10-24T21:35:02.948Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3i66001vg7rdhlfic0fw','mcq','A 58-year-old patient with heart failure (EF 25%) and hypertension develops a dry, persistent cough 3 weeks after starting lisinopril. Which of the following would be the MOST appropriate next step in management?','["Discontinue lisinopril and start furosemide","Switch to losartan (an ARB)","Add dextromethorphan for cough suppression","Continue lisinopril and add an inhaled corticosteroid","Switch to amlodipine (a calcium channel blocker)"]','Switch to losartan (an ARB)','ACE inhibitors commonly cause a dry cough in 5-35% of patients due to bradykinin accumulation and prostaglandin release. ARBs like losartan do not inhibit ACE and therefore do not cause this side effect, making them an excellent alternative when ACE inhibitor-induced cough develops. The cough typically resolves within 1-2 weeks after switching to an ARB.','ACE cough? Remember ARBs - ACE causes cough, ARBs don''t (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers)','ACE inhibitor cough is non-productive, dry, and persistent. Switch to ARB rather than adding symptomatic treatment - the cough will resolve with the switch.','A) Discontinue lisinopril and start furosemide - Incorrect, as ACE inhibitors are beneficial in heart failure and should not be stopped without replacement. C) Add dextromethorphan - Symptomatic treatment doesn''t address the underlying cause and cough may persist. D) Continue lisinopril and add inhaled corticosteroid - Inhaled steroids don''t help ACE inhibitor cough. E) Switch to amlodipine - While CCBs are alternatives, ARBs are preferred for patients who can''t tolerate ACE inhibitors due to cough.','Medium','Cardiovascular','ACE Inhibitors & ARBs','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE Inhibitors & ARBs","ACE inhibitor","cough","heart failure","ARB"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:09.246Z','2026-10-24T21:35:09.246Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3mx2001xg7rdx09jwjhq','mcq','A 72-year-old patient with stable angina is prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin as needed for chest pain. Which of the following statements regarding nitrate tolerance is MOST accurate?','["Tolerance develops within hours of continuous nitrate therapy","Tolerance is specific to nitrates and does not affect other vasodilators","Nitrate tolerance can be prevented by taking daily vitamin C supplements","Tolerance is primarily due to depletion of nitric oxide stores","A 10-12 hour nitrate-free interval each day prevents tolerance development"]','A 10-12 hour nitrate-free interval each day prevents tolerance development','Nitrate tolerance develops rapidly with continuous nitrate exposure and is characterized by diminished vasodilatory effects. The most effective strategy to prevent tolerance is to provide a nitrate-free interval of 10-12 hours each day, typically achieved by using short-acting nitrates during the day and avoiding them at night. This allows restoration of vascular responsiveness and sulfhydryl groups needed for nitrate metabolism.','Nitrate-free interval: ''Give your vessels a break - 10-12 hours off nitrates daily''','For chronic nitrate therapy, use isosorbide mononitrate twice daily with a 10-12 hour gap, or short-acting nitrates during the day only to prevent tolerance.','A) Tolerance develops within hours - True but incomplete answer - the key is how to prevent it. B) Tolerance is specific to nitrates - False, cross-tolerance with other vasodilators occurs. C) Vitamin C prevents tolerance - Not supported by evidence. D) Depletion of nitric oxide stores - Incorrect, tolerance involves complex mechanisms including oxidative stress and impaired bioactivation.','Hard','Cardiovascular','Nitrates','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Nitrates","nitrate tolerance","angina","pharmacology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:15.398Z','2026-10-24T21:35:15.398Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3s7p001zg7rdrdjp03iu','mcq','A 68-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation and mechanical mitral valve replacement is started on warfarin. Two weeks later, the INR is 1.2 (target 2.0-3.0). The patient recently started taking phenytoin for a seizure disorder. Which of the following best explains this finding?','["The warfarin dose needs to be increased due to inadequate anticoagulation","Phenytoin inhibits warfarin metabolism, leading to decreased INR","Phenytoin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes, accelerating warfarin clearance","The patient has developed warfarin resistance","Phenytoin competes with warfarin for protein binding sites"]','Phenytoin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes, accelerating warfarin clearance','Phenytoin is a potent inducer of cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, which are responsible for warfarin metabolism. This induction accelerates warfarin clearance, leading to decreased INR levels. When starting or stopping phenytoin in patients on warfarin, close INR monitoring and frequent dose adjustments are required over 6-8 weeks as the enzyme induction develops or resolves.','Phenytoin = P450 inducer = Warfarin metabolism accelerator = Lower INR','CYP inducers (phenytoin, rifampin, carbamazepine) decrease INR; CYP inhibitors (fluconazole, amiodarone) increase INR. Monitor INR closely when starting/stopping these medications.','A) Warfarin dose needs to be increased - True, but doesn''t explain the mechanism. B) Phenytoin inhibits warfarin metabolism - False, phenytoin induces, not inhibits. D) Warfarin resistance - Incorrect term; this is a drug interaction. E) Protein binding competition - Not the primary mechanism with phenytoin.','Hard','Cardiovascular','Anticoagulants','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Anticoagulants","warfarin","drug interactions","INR","phenytoin"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:22.261Z','2026-10-24T21:35:22.261Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx3wkj0021g7rdztdxkx9h','mcq','A 75-year-old patient with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone. Which of the following adverse effects should be MOST closely monitored for during long-term therapy?','["Hypertension","Pulmonary fibrosis","Hyperthyroidism","Bradycardia","Peripheral edema"]','Pulmonary fibrosis','Amiodarone has numerous serious adverse effects, but pulmonary toxicity (including pulmonary fibrosis) is among the most dangerous and potentially fatal complications. It can present as progressive dyspnea, cough, and hypoxia, and may occur even after discontinuation of the drug. Other significant toxicities include thyroid dysfunction (both hypo- and hyperthyroidism), photosensitivity, and corneal deposits, but pulmonary toxicity has the highest mortality rate when it occurs.','Amiodarone lungs: ''Remember the pulmonary price - fibrosis can be fatal''','Baseline chest CT, pulmonary function tests, and regular monitoring (every 6-12 months) are recommended for patients on long-term amiodarone to detect pulmonary toxicity early.','A) Hypertension - Amiodarone typically causes hypotension, not hypertension. C) Hyperthyroidism - A concern, but less immediately life-threatening than pulmonary fibrosis. D) Bradycardia - Common but usually manageable with dose adjustment. E) Peripheral edema - Common side effect but not as serious as pulmonary toxicity.','Hard','Cardiovascular','Antiarrhythmics','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Antiarrhythmics","amiodarone","pulmonary toxicity","adverse effects"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:27.907Z','2026-10-24T21:35:27.907Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx41l30023g7rd3tgf0jwo','mcq','A 62-year-old patient with hypertension and type 2 diabetes has an ejection fraction of 35% and symptoms of heart failure. Which of the following calcium channel blockers would be MOST appropriate for this patient?','["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine","Felodipine"]','Amlodipine','In patients with heart failure reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (verapamil and diltiazem) are contraindicated because they have negative inotropic effects that can worsen heart failure. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers like amlodipine, nifedipine, and felodipine are generally safer in heart failure as they primarily cause vasodilation without significant negative inotropic effects. Amlodipine is often preferred due to its once-daily dosing and extensive safety data in heart failure patients.','HFrEF + CCB: ''Dihydropyridines are safe, non-dihydropyridines contraindicated''','In HFrEF, avoid verapamil and diltiazem (negative inotropes). Amlodipine is the CCB of choice if needed for hypertension or angina in heart failure patients.','B) Verapamil - Contraindicated in HFrEF due to negative inotropic effects. C) Diltiazem - Contraindicated in HFrEF due to negative inotropic effects. D) Nifedipine - Dihydropyridine but may cause reflex tachycardia; amlodipine preferred. E) Felodipine - Dihydropyridine but less commonly used than amlodipine.','Medium','Cardiovascular','Calcium Channel Blockers','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Calcium Channel Blockers","calcium channel blocker","heart failure","HFrEF"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:34.407Z','2026-10-24T21:35:34.407Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx46dg0025g7rddly3u30e','mcq','A 70-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min) is being evaluated for anticoagulation. Which of the following direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) would be MOST appropriate for this patient?','["Rivaroxaban","Apixaban","Dabigatran","Edoxaban","All of the above require dose adjustment"]','Apixaban','Apixaban is the preferred DOAC for patients with moderate to severe chronic kidney disease (eGFR 15-50 mL/min) due to its favorable pharmacokinetic profile and reduced accumulation compared to other DOACs. Rivaroxaban and edoxaban require significant dose reduction in CKD and are not recommended for eGFR <15 mL/min. Dabigatran is primarily renally excreted and requires substantial dose reduction in CKD, with accumulation risk at lower eGFR levels. Apixaban has the most favorable renal clearance profile among the DOACs.','CKD + DOAC: ''Apixaban first, others need careful consideration''','In moderate CKD (eGFR 15-50), apixaban 5mg BID is preferred. Avoid DOACs in severe CKD (eGFR <15) - use warfarin instead.','A) Rivaroxaban - Requires significant dose reduction in CKD and not recommended for eGFR <15. C) Dabigatran - High renal excretion, requires substantial dose reduction, accumulation risk. D) Edoxaban - Requires dose reduction in CKD, not recommended for eGFR <15. E) All require adjustment - False, apixaban has the most favorable profile.','Hard','Cardiovascular','Anticoagulants','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Anticoagulants","DOAC","chronic kidney disease","anticoagulation"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:40.612Z','2026-10-24T21:35:40.612Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu62003ue9oi8xfz4a81','mcq','Which of the following cyanotic heart diseases is associated with lung oligemia?','["TGA","TA","F4","TAPVRO"]','F4','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanotic","lung oligemia","tetralogy of fallot"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.642Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.642Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu63003ve9oihxherzw8','mcq','What is the main configuration in a chest X-ray of an infant with TGA?','["Figure of 8 heart","Egg on side heart","Boot-shaped heart","Mustache sign"]','Egg on side heart','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["TGA","chest x-ray","configuration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.643Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.643Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmx4b8r0027g7rdbvxuc572','mcq','A 78-year-old patient with heart failure and atrial fibrillation is being treated with digoxin. The patient presents with nausea, confusion, and visual disturbances. Serum digoxin level is 2.8 ng/mL. Which of the following medications is MOST likely to have contributed to this toxicity?','["Metoprolol","Furosemide","Amiodarone","Lisinopril","Atorvastatin"]','Amiodarone','Amiodarone significantly increases digoxin levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein, which is responsible for digoxin renal excretion. This interaction can increase digoxin concentrations by 50-100%, leading to toxicity even at previously therapeutic doses. The classic symptoms of digoxin toxicity include gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting), neurological symptoms (confusion, fatigue), and visual disturbances (yellow-green halos, blurred vision). The therapeutic range for digoxin is typically 0.5-2.0 ng/mL, and levels >2.4 ng/mL are considered toxic.','Amiodarone + Digoxin = ''Double trouble - P-glycoprotein inhibition raises levels''','When starting amiodarone in a patient on digoxin, reduce digoxin dose by 50% and monitor levels closely. Watch for classic toxicity symptoms: GI upset, confusion, visual changes.','A) Metoprolol - Beta blocker, no significant interaction with digoxin. B) Furosemide - Loop diuretic, can cause hypokalemia which potentiates digoxin toxicity, but not a direct interaction. D) Lisinopril - ACE inhibitor, no significant interaction with digoxin. E) Atorvastatin - Statin, no significant interaction with digoxin.','Hard','Cardiovascular','Antiarrhythmics','["pharmacology cardiovascular drugs","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Antiarrhythmics","digoxin toxicity","amiodarone","drug interactions"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-24T21:35:46.923Z','2026-10-24T21:35:46.923Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn6sceg003kg7rdlmrttlp9','flashcard','First step in anemia diagnostic evaluation','[]','Complete blood count (CBC) to confirm anemia and classify by MCV (microcytic, normocytic, macrocytic)','Complete blood count (CBC) to confirm anemia and classify by MCV (microcytic, normocytic, macrocytic)','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia diagnosis','["flashcard","Anemia diagnostic steps"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z','2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvn6sceo003ng7rdj5edskx2','flashcard','Key test for iron deficiency anemia','[]','Serum ferritin (most sensitive) and serum iron with TIBC (transferrin saturation)','Serum ferritin (most sensitive) and serum iron with TIBC (transferrin saturation)','','','','Moderate','Hematology','Iron deficiency','["flashcard","Anemia diagnostic steps"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z','2026-10-25T02:06:24.711Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo2jh8m0007guwuz11twpbp','mcq','A 25-year-old male with a known history of G6PD deficiency presents with acute hemolytic anemia after taking a medication for a respiratory infection. Which of the following medications is most likely responsible for his condition?','["Penicillin VK","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Cephalexin","Azithromycin","Doxycycline"]','B','Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is a well-known oxidant stressor that can trigger acute hemolytic anemia in patients with G6PD deficiency [S1]. The mechanism involves oxidative damage to red blood cells that lack the protective NADPH generated by the deficient G6PD enzyme. While penicillin VK and cephalexin are generally considered safe in G6PD deficiency, azithromycin and doxycycline are also typically not associated with hemolysis in these patients.','G6PD + TMP-SMX = Hemolysis','G6PD deficiency patients should avoid oxidant drugs like sulfonamides, nitrofurantoin, and primaquine; always check medication lists for potential triggers.','A) Penicillin VK is generally safe in G6PD deficiency; C) Cephalexin is typically not associated with hemolysis; D) Azithromycin is not a known oxidant stressor; E) Doxycycline is not typically associated with hemolysis in G6PD deficiency.','Medium','General','Anemia','["anemia and ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","Anemia","G6PD deficiency","hemolytic anemia","oxidant stress"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T16:55:18.790Z','2026-10-25T16:55:18.790Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo2jx410009guwudkntzraj','mcq','A 30-year-old male with hemophilia A presents with spontaneous joint bleeding. Laboratory studies show factor VIII activity at 5% of normal. Which of the following treatment approaches is most appropriate for this acute bleeding episode?','["Desmopressin (DDAVP) infusion","Recombinant factor VIII concentrate","Fresh frozen plasma","Tranexamic acid alone","Observation only"]','B','Recombinant factor VIII concentrate is the treatment of choice for acute bleeding episodes in patients with hemophilia A [S2]. Factor VIII activity at 5% indicates moderate hemophilia, and replacement therapy with factor VIII concentrate is necessary to achieve hemostasis. Desmopressin is only effective in mild hemophilia A (factor VIII >5-40%) and would not be sufficient for this level of deficiency. Fresh frozen plasma contains factor VIII but is less concentrated and carries risks of volume overload and transfusion reactions. Tranexamic acid is an adjunctive therapy but does not replace the missing factor VIII.','Hemophilia A = Factor VIII replacement','Factor VIII levels <1% = severe, 1-5% = moderate, 5-40% = mild; treatment intensity correlates with severity.','A) Desmopressin only effective in mild hemophilia A (>5% activity); C) Fresh frozen plasma contains factor VIII but is less concentrated and riskier; D) Tranexamic acid is adjunctive only; E) Observation would lead to continued bleeding.','Medium','General','Anemia','["anemia and ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","Anemia","hemophilia A","factor VIII","bleeding disorder"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T16:55:39.361Z','2026-10-25T16:55:39.361Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo2kdfo000bguwukyezlrex','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with chronic kidney disease and hypertension develops macrocytic anemia. Laboratory findings show elevated MCV, decreased serum B12, and normal folate levels. Which of the following is the most likely underlying mechanism?','["Dietary folate deficiency","Iron deficiency anemia","Autoimmune pernicious anemia","Alcohol abuse","Hemolysis"]','C','Autoimmune pernicious anemia is the most likely cause of macrocytic anemia with decreased B12 and normal folate in a patient with chronic kidney disease [S1]. Pernicious anemia results from autoimmune destruction of parietal cells, leading to intrinsic factor deficiency and impaired B12 absorption. Chronic kidney disease can exacerbate this by reducing renal production of erythropoietin, but the macrocytosis and B12 deficiency point to pernicious anemia as the primary issue. Dietary folate deficiency would show low folate levels, iron deficiency typically causes microcytic anemia, alcohol abuse can cause macrocytosis but usually with normal B12, and hemolysis typically shows normocytic anemia.','Macrocytic + Low B12 + Normal Folate = Pernicious','Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune disorder causing B12 deficiency; look for anti-parietal cell and anti-intrinsic factor antibodies.','A) Would show low folate levels, not normal; B) Iron deficiency typically causes microcytic, not macrocytic anemia; D) Alcohol abuse can cause macrocytosis but usually with normal B12; E) Hemolysis typically shows normocytic anemia.','Medium','General','Anemia','["anemia and ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","Anemia","megaloblastic anemia","B12 deficiency","pernicious anemia"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T16:56:00.516Z','2026-10-25T16:56:00.516Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo2ku6v000eguwuilxkumxr','mcq','A 55-year-old patient with hypertension and diabetes mellitus is started on lisinopril. Three weeks later, she develops a persistent dry cough and her serum potassium level is 5.8 mEq/L. Which of the following best explains these adverse effects?','["Angiotensin II-mediated bronchoconstriction","Bradykinin accumulation and ACE inhibition","Direct histamine release","Angiotensin II receptor stimulation","Renin-angiotensin system activation"]','B','The dry cough and hyperkalemia are classic adverse effects of ACE inhibitors caused by bradykinin accumulation and ACE inhibition [S1, S5]. ACE normally degrades bradykinin, so inhibiting ACE leads to increased bradykinin levels, which can cause cough through sensory nerve stimulation in the airways. Hyperkalemia occurs because ACE inhibition reduces aldosterone production, decreasing potassium excretion. Angiotensin II-mediated bronchoconstriction is incorrect because ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin II levels. Direct histamine release is not a mechanism of ACE inhibitor cough. Angiotensin II receptor stimulation would occur with ARBs, not ACE inhibitors. RAS activation is the opposite of what ACE inhibitors do.','ACE inhibitor = Bradykinin up = Cough + K+ up','ACE inhibitor cough is bradykinin-mediated; switching to ARB may resolve cough without losing antihypertensive effect.','A) ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin II, not increase it; C) Histamine release is not the mechanism; D) ARBs, not ACE inhibitors, stimulate angiotensin II receptors; E) ACE inhibitors inhibit, not activate, the RAS.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["anemia and ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","ACE inhibitors","bradykinin","adverse effects"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T16:56:22.231Z','2026-10-25T16:56:22.231Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo2lb0a000kguwui3dbh8sn','mcq','A 70-year-old patient with heart failure, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min) is being evaluated for ACE inhibitor therapy. Which of the following is the most important consideration when initiating an ACE inhibitor in this patient?','["Avoidance due to risk of hyperkalemia","Dose reduction due to renal impairment","Contraindication due to heart failure","Need for frequent monitoring of liver function","Requirement for concurrent diuretic therapy"]','B','Dose reduction is necessary when initiating ACE inhibitors in patients with chronic kidney disease (eGFR <60 mL/min) [S3]. ACE inhibitors are beneficial in heart failure and hypertension even with renal impairment, but they require careful dosing and monitoring. While hyperkalemia is a concern, it can be managed with dose adjustment and potassium monitoring rather than complete avoidance. ACE inhibitors are actually indicated in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Liver function monitoring is not specifically required for ACE inhibitors. Diuretic therapy may be needed but is not the primary consideration.','CKD + ACEi = Dose down, monitor K+','ACE inhibitors are renoprotective in proteinuric CKD but require dose adjustment and monitoring for hyperkalemia and worsening renal function.','A) ACE inhibitors can be used with monitoring; C) ACE inhibitors are indicated in heart failure; D) Liver function not specifically monitored for ACE inhibitors; E) Diuretics may be needed but not the primary consideration.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["anemia and ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","ACE inhibitors","renal impairment","heart failure"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T16:56:44.026Z','2026-10-25T16:56:44.026Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo2lqmm000mguwumps0cdp4','mcq','A pregnant patient at 12 weeks gestation with chronic hypertension is considering antihypertensive therapy. Which of the following statements regarding ACE inhibitor use in pregnancy is most accurate?','["ACE inhibitors are safe in the first trimester but contraindicated in later pregnancy","ACE inhibitors can be used throughout pregnancy with careful monitoring","ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in all trimesters due to teratogenicity","ACE inhibitors are preferred over other antihypertensives in pregnancy","ACE inhibitors should be discontinued only if blood pressure is severely elevated"]','C','ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in all trimesters of pregnancy due to teratogenic effects, particularly fetal renal dysgenesis and oligohydramnios [S1]. They can cause fetal injury and death when used during the second and third trimesters, and there is evidence of risk in the first trimester as well. Alternative antihypertensives like methyldopa, labetalol, or nifedipine are preferred in pregnancy. The contraindication is absolute and not dependent on blood pressure severity.','Pregnancy + ACEi = Contraindicated','ACE inhibitors cause fetal renal damage and skull hypoplasia; switch to alternative antihypertensive before conception or as soon as pregnancy is detected.','A) Risk exists in first trimester too; B) Never safe in pregnancy; D) Other agents preferred; E) Contraindication regardless of BP severity.','Medium','General','ACE inhibitors','["anemia and ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","ACE inhibitors","pregnancy","teratogenicity"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T16:57:04.271Z','2026-10-25T16:57:04.271Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo40ztd0012guwu4wymxu07','mcq','A 62-year-old male with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and smoking presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of severe crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm and jaw. The pain began 2 hours ago while he was shoveling snow and is associated with diaphoresis, nausea, and shortness of breath. On examination, he appears anxious, his blood pressure is 155/95 mmHg, heart rate is 112 bpm, respiratory rate is 24 breaths/min, and oxygen saturation is 92% on room air. His ECG shows ST elevation of 4 mm in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Initial cardiac troponin I is 0.08 ng/mL (normal <0.04 ng/mL). Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management strategy for this patient?','["Start aspirin 325 mg orally, nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual every 5 minutes as needed, and arrange for urgent coronary angiography within 90 minutes","Administer morphine 4 mg IV for pain control, start heparin infusion, and schedule elective cardiac catheterization within 24 hours","Begin beta-blocker therapy, start statin, and arrange for stress testing the following day","Initiate fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase and transfer to a community hospital with monitoring capabilities","Provide oxygen therapy, administer sublingual nitroglycerin, and arrange for non-emergency cardiology consultation"]','Start aspirin 325 mg orally, nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual every 5 minutes as needed, and arrange for urgent coronary angiography within 90 minutes','This patient presents with classic symptoms of an acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) including severe crushing chest pain, radiation, associated symptoms, and ECG findings of ST elevation in the anterior leads (V1-V4) with reciprocal changes. The elevated troponin I confirms myocardial injury. According to current guidelines [S1], the initial management of STEMI includes immediate aspirin administration, nitroglycerin for symptom relief, and urgent coronary angiography within 90 minutes (primary percutaneous coronary intervention, PCI) when available. This approach is superior to fibrinolytic therapy when timely PCI can be achieved and is preferred over delayed interventions or conservative management in STEMI [S2].','STEMI Protocol: A.N.G.I.O. - Aspirin, Nitrates, Get to cath lab Immediately, Oxygen','In STEMI, time is muscle - the ''door-to-balloon'' time should be <90 minutes for primary PCI. Reciprocal ST depression is a specific indicator of STEMI and helps localize the infarct to the anterior wall when seen in leads II, III, and aVF.','Option B is incorrect because morphine may be used but is not first-line, and elective catheterization is inappropriate for STEMI. Option C is wrong as beta-blockers are contraindicated in acute STEMI with heart failure or hypotension. Option D is incorrect because fibrinolytics are second-line when timely PCI is available. Option E is wrong because STEMI requires urgent intervention, not non-emergency consultation.','Medium','cardiovascular','myocardial infarction','["Myocardial Infarction","Cardiovascular","deep-agent","myocardial infarction","STEMI","acute coronary syndrome","ECG interpretation","emergency medicine"]',true,'ECG showing ST elevation in leads V1-V4 with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF, representing anterior STEMI','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T17:36:55.633Z','2026-10-25T17:36:55.633Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu64003we9oi5k726l8h','mcq','Chest x-ray may help to differentiate the types of congenital heart defects. Increased pulmonary markings (pulmonary blood flow) is seen in:','["Pulmonary atresia","Transposition of great vessels","Tetralogy of Fallot","Tricuspid atresia"]','Tetralogy of Fallot','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["congenital","chest-xray","pulmonary-markings"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.644Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.644Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo4y7ha0009wkwu9s2wvotf','mcq','A 58-year-old male with hypertension and type 2 diabetes is started on lisinopril. Two weeks later, he develops a persistent dry cough and his serum potassium level is 5.8 mEq/L. Which of the following best explains the mechanism underlying both of these adverse effects?','["Direct stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone","Inhibition of aldosterone secretion leading to potassium retention","Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition","Increased angiotensin II formation through alternative pathways"]','Accumulation of bradykinin due to ACE inhibition','ACE inhibitors work by competitively inhibiting the angiotensin-converting enzyme, which normally converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II and degrades bradykinin. By inhibiting this enzyme, ACE inhibitors lead to accumulation of bradykinin, which is responsible for the characteristic dry cough (occurring in 10-20% of patients) and contributes to vasodilation [S1, S2]. The hyperkalemia results from reduced aldosterone secretion due to decreased angiotensin II levels, but this is separate from the bradykinin-mediated cough [S1, S3].','ACE = Angiotensin Converting Enzyme → blocks both angiotensin II formation AND bradykinin breakdown → cough (bradykinin) + hyperkalemia (less aldosterone)','The dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is mediated by bradykinin accumulation and is not dose-dependent. If a patient develops an ACE inhibitor cough, switching to an ARB (which doesn''t affect bradykinin) is often the best solution.','Option A is incorrect because ACE inhibitors do not directly stimulate the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Option B describes the mechanism of hyperkalemia but not the cough. Option D is incorrect because ACE inhibitors reduce, not increase, angiotensin II formation, though alternative pathways can partially compensate.','Medium','Pharmacology','ACE inhibitors mechanism of action and adverse effects','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors mechanism of action and adverse effects","ACE inhibitors","bradykinin","cough","hyperkalemia"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T18:02:45.214Z','2026-10-25T18:02:45.214Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvobc15z000p1twuftcvcmhi','mcq','A 45-year-old patient presents with hypertension and laboratory findings showing elevated serum creatinine (1.8 mg/dL) and decreased estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) of 45 mL/min/1.73m². Which of the following factors would most directly decrease glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in this patient?','["Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) release","Increased renal plasma flow","Decreased afferent arteriolar resistance","Increased efferent arteriolar resistance","Decreased systemic blood pressure"]','D','Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is determined by the product of the filtration coefficient (Kf), net filtration pressure (NFP), and the surface area available for filtration. The net filtration pressure is calculated as: NFP = (glomerular hydrostatic pressure - Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure) - (glomerular oncotic pressure). Increased efferent arteriolar resistance, often seen in conditions like hypertension and renal disease, increases glomerular hydrostatic pressure but also increases glomerular oncotic pressure due to plasma concentration. However, the net effect is typically a decrease in GFR because the efferent arteriole constriction reduces renal plasma flow more significantly than it increases filtration pressure [S1]. This is a key mechanism in chronic kidney disease where angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction helps maintain GFR despite reduced renal perfusion.','','In hypertension and chronic kidney disease, angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) work by blocking efferent arteriolar constriction, which can paradoxically decrease GFR initially but provides long-term renal protection by reducing intraglomerular pressure.','Option A (ANP) is incorrect because ANP increases GFR by dilating afferent arterioles and constricting efferent arterioles. Option B (increased renal plasma flow) would increase GFR, not decrease it. Option C (decreased afferent arteriolar resistance) would increase GFR by increasing glomerular hydrostatic pressure. Option E (decreased systemic blood pressure) could decrease GFR, but this is not the most direct mechanism in the context of the question about renal autoregulation.','Medium','renal','glomerular filtration','["renal physiology","Renal","deep-agent","glomerular filtration","glomerular filtration rate","renal hemodynamics","hypertension","nephrology"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T21:01:27.912Z','2026-10-25T21:01:27.912Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu65003xe9oiohn380zi','mcq','Which of the following is a cause of neonatal cyanosis?','["VSD","Coarctation of the aorta","TGA","PDA"]','TGA','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Neonatal Cyanosis','["cyanosis","neonatal","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.645Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.645Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu65003ye9oiq4b0412v','mcq','All the following are cyanotic heart disease except:','["Atrial septal defect","Transposition of great arteries","Pulmonary atresia","Tricuspid atresia"]','Atrial septal defect','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["cyanotic","congenital","heart-disease"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.645Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.645Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu66003ze9oihonz4tyj','mcq','A 5 month old infant presented with persistent cyanosis for 1 month duration, he had ejection systolic murmur, what is most likely diagnosis?','["VSD","Fallot tetralogy","Isolated PS","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome"]','Fallot tetralogy','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","infant","murmur","tetralogy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.646Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.646Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvobca3b000r1twubvlxw1fq','mcq','A patient with uncontrolled diabetes mellitus has a random urine glucose concentration of 500 mg/dL despite a normal fasting blood glucose level. Which of the following best explains this finding?','["Decreased glomerular filtration rate","Impaired tubular maximum for glucose reabsorption","Enhanced proximal tubular glucose secretion","Increased glomerular permeability to glucose","Defect in glucose transporters in the collecting duct"]','B','The renal threshold for glucose is typically around 180 mg/dL, and the tubular maximum (Tm) for glucose reabsorption is approximately 375 mg/min in adults. When the filtered load of glucose exceeds the Tm, glucosuria occurs. In uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, persistent hyperglycemia leads to saturation of the sodium-glucose cotransporters (SGLT) in the proximal tubule, specifically SGLT2 which is responsible for the majority of glucose reabsorption [S1]. This results in impaired tubular reabsorption capacity and glucosuria. The finding of glucosuria despite normal fasting blood glucose suggests that the patient has had periods of significant hyperglycemia that exceeded the renal threshold and potentially damaged the tubular reabsorption capacity.','','SGLT2 inhibitors, a class of diabetes medications, work by inhibiting glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule, causing glucosuria and lowering blood glucose levels. This mechanism is therapeutically beneficial but also increases the risk of volume depletion and urinary tract infections.','Option A is incorrect because decreased GFR would reduce filtered glucose load, potentially preventing glucosuria. Option C is incorrect because glucose is not secreted in significant amounts under normal circumstances. Option D is incorrect because glomerular permeability to glucose is not typically altered in diabetes. Option E is incorrect because glucose reabsorption occurs primarily in the proximal tubule, not the collecting duct.','Medium','renal','tubular reabsorption','["renal physiology","Renal","deep-agent","tubular reabsorption","diabetes mellitus","glucosuria","tubular reabsorption","SGLT transporters"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T21:01:39.479Z','2026-10-25T21:01:39.479Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvocdkzt001h1twuhjfry1l2','mcq','A 58-year-old patient with type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and microalbuminuria is started on lisinopril. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which ACE inhibitors provide renal protection in diabetic patients?','["Direct vasodilation of afferent arterioles in the glomerulus","Inhibition of angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriole constriction","Reduction of aldosterone-mediated sodium retention","Decreased bradykinin degradation leading to improved renal blood flow"]','Inhibition of angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriole constriction','ACE inhibitors provide renal protection primarily by inhibiting the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, which reduces efferent arteriole constriction. This decreases intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria, particularly beneficial in diabetic nephropathy [S2]. While bradykinin accumulation contributes to the dry cough side effect, it is not the primary mechanism for renal protection [S1, S3].','ACE = Angiotensin Converting Enzyme → Less Angiotensin II → Less Efferent Arteriole Constriction → Lower Intraglomerular Pressure','ACE inhibitors are first-line for diabetic patients with hypertension and albuminuria as they reduce intraglomerular pressure and slow progression of diabetic nephropathy, regardless of race [S2].','Option A is incorrect as ACE inhibitors primarily affect efferent, not afferent arterioles. Option C describes aldosterone effects which are secondary to angiotensin II reduction. Option D confuses the side effect mechanism (bradykinin accumulation causing cough) with the primary renal protective mechanism.','Medium','Pharmacology','ACE inhibitors','["ACE inhibitors","Pharmacology","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors","mechanism","renal protection","diabetes mellitus","hypertension"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T21:30:39.881Z','2026-10-25T21:30:39.881Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu690043e9oir66nhah3','mcq','The following are causes of congenital heart disease except','["Antepartum haemorrhage","Congenital infection","Genetic causes","Chromosomal abnormalities"]','Antepartum haemorrhage','<p><strong>Why Antepartum haemorrhage is correct:</strong> Antepartum hemorrhage is not a direct cause of congenital heart disease (CHD) but rather a complication of pregnancy that can lead to fetal distress or hypoxia. CHD primarily results from disruptions in embryonic cardiac development during weeks 3-8 of gestation, with genetic factors accounting for approximately 8-10% of cases, chromosomal abnormalities contributing to about 5-10%, and congenital infections (such as rubella, cytomegalovirus, and toxoplasmosis) directly interfering with cardiac morphogenesis. Antepartum hemorrhage, while potentially harmful to the fetus, does not directly cause structural cardiac malformations.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Congenital infection:</strong> Certain viral infections (especially rubella) during critical periods of cardiac development can directly cause structural heart defects by interfering with cell migration and differentiation in the developing heart.</li>
<li><strong>Genetic causes:</strong> Mutations in genes critical for cardiac development (such as NKX2-5, TBX5, and GATA4) can lead to specific congenital heart defects through disrupted signaling pathways and morphogenesis.</li>
<li><strong>Chromosomal abnormalities:</strong> Conditions like Down syndrome (trisomy 21), Turner syndrome (45,X), and DiGeorge syndrome (22q11 deletion) are strongly associated with various congenital heart defects due to dosage effects of critical cardiac development genes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The critical period for congenital heart development is weeks 3-8 of gestation, when the heart is forming and septating.</li>
<li>About 80-90% of congenital heart defects have no identifiable cause, with the remainder attributed to genetic factors, chromosomal abnormalities, and environmental exposures.</li>
<li>Maternal diabetes, alcohol consumption, and certain medications (like retinoic acid) are well-established environmental risk factors for CHD.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Congenital heart disease results from genetic, chromosomal, and infectious disruptions to embryonic cardiac development, not from obstetric complications like antepartum hemorrhage.</p>','<p>CHD causes: GCGC (Genetic, Chromosomal, Congenital infections, G - for other environmental factors)</p>','<p>When evaluating a newborn with congenital heart disease, always consider maternal history of infections, medication exposure, and family history of genetic syndromes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Congenital infection:</strong> Directly interferes with cardiac morphogenesis during critical developmental periods.</li>
<li><strong>Genetic causes:</strong> Mutations in cardiac development genes can cause specific structural defects.</li>
<li><strong>Chromosomal abnormalities:</strong> Conditions like Down syndrome are strongly associated with various congenital heart defects.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["causes","congenital","etiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.649Z','2026-10-27T15:52:47.196Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6a0044e9oivxjo1qhk','mcq','One of the following is a congenital cyanotic heart disease with decrease pulmonary blood flow','["TGA","TAPVR","Fallot tetralogy","Truncus arteriosus"]','Fallot tetralogy','<p><strong>Why Fallot tetralogy is correct:</strong> Fallot tetralogy is a cyanotic congenital heart disease characterized by decreased pulmonary blood flow due to severe right ventricular outflow tract obstruction (subpulmonary stenosis), which is one of its four components (along with ventricular septal defect, overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy). The stenosis restricts blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary arteries, causing deoxygenated blood to shunt across the VSD into the aorta, resulting in cyanosis. In contrast, the other options either have normal or increased pulmonary blood flow.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TGA (Transposition of the Great Arteries):</strong> Has normal pulmonary blood flow but is a mixing lesion where systemic and pulmonary circulations run in parallel, requiring an interatrial or interventricular communication for survival.</li>
<li><strong>TAPVR (Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return):</strong> Has increased pulmonary blood flow because all pulmonary venous blood returns to the right heart, causing volume overload and pulmonary hypertension.</li>
<li><strong>Truncus arteriosus:</strong> Has increased pulmonary blood flow because a single great vessel gives rise to both the aorta and pulmonary arteries, resulting in mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood and increased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cyanotic heart diseases can be categorized by pulmonary blood flow: decreased (Fallot tetralogy, pulmonary atresia), normal (TGA), or increased (TAPVR, truncus arteriosus).</li>
<li>The severity of cyanosis in Fallot tetralogy correlates with the degree of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction.</li>
<li>Tet spells in Fallot tetralogy are caused by increased right-to-left shunting across the VSD due to dynamic obstruction of the right ventricular outflow tract.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fallot tetralogy is the classic cyanotic congenital heart disease with decreased pulmonary blood flow due to right ventricular outflow tract obstruction.</p>','<p>Decreased pulmonary flow: Fallot tetralogy (FT) - think ''Flow Turbulence'' due to obstruction</p>','<p>In cyanotic heart disease, the presence of a systolic murmur suggests some pulmonary blood flow, while its absence may indicate severe obstruction or atresia.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Has normal pulmonary blood flow but is a mixing lesion with parallel circulations.</li>
<li><strong>TAPVR:</strong> Has increased pulmonary blood flow due to all pulmonary venous blood returning to the right heart.</li>
<li><strong>Truncus arteriosus:</strong> Has increased pulmonary blood flow due to a single great vessel giving rise to both systemic and pulmonary circulations.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["cyanotic","congenital","pulmonary-blood-flow","decreased"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.650Z','2026-10-27T15:52:47.200Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoceeoi001l1twu3ib0vhul','mcq','A 68-year-old female with chronic idiopathic constipation reports infrequent bowel movements (2-3 times per week) and straining. She has failed lifestyle modifications and is now considering pharmacological options. Which mechanism of action best explains why secretagogues like lubiprostone and linaclotide are effective for her condition, and what is their primary advantage over osmotic laxatives?','["A) Secretagogues directly increase colonic water absorption, providing faster relief than osmotic laxatives","B) Secretagogues activate chloride channels in the intestinal epithelium, increasing fluid secretion without systemic absorption","C) Secretagogues stimulate colonic smooth muscle contraction through serotonin receptor activation","D) Secretagogues inhibit colonic motility by blocking acetylcholine release, reducing straining"]','B','Secretagogues like lubiprostone and linaclotide work by activating chloride channels (CFTR channels) in the intestinal epithelium, leading to increased chloride and fluid secretion into the intestinal lumen. This mechanism increases stool volume and softness, promoting bowel movements. Their primary advantage over osmotic laxatives is that they work locally without systemic absorption, reducing the risk of electrolyte imbalances and renal issues that can occur with osmotic agents like polyethylene glycol or magnesium-based laxatives [S1]. Osmotic laxatives draw water into the bowel lumen but can cause significant fluid shifts and electrolyte disturbances with chronic use [S2].','Secretagogues = ''Secretion Stimulators'' - think ''S'' for stimulating chloride secretion','Secretagogues are particularly useful in patients with chronic constipation who have failed osmotic laxatives, especially those with renal impairment or electrolyte concerns, due to their local mechanism of action and minimal systemic absorption.','A) Incorrect - secretagogues increase secretion, not absorption; osmotic laxatives work by drawing water into the bowel\\nC) Incorrect - this describes stimulant laxatives (like senna/bisacodyl) that work through serotonin receptors and smooth muscle stimulation\\nD) Incorrect - this describes the opposite effect; secretagogues enhance motility, they don''t inhibit it','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation','["Constipation","Pharmacology","deep-agent","constipation","constipation","pharmacology","pathophysiology","secretagogues"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-25T21:31:18.354Z','2026-10-25T23:09:44.731Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvogu1xc001espwur5pv7z54','flashcard','Anemia classification by red blood cell morphology','[]','1. **Microcytic**: MCV < 80 fL (e.g., iron deficiency, thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease)
2. **Normocytic**: MCV 80-100 fL (e.g., anemia of chronic disease, hemolysis, early iron deficiency)
3. **Macrocytic**: MCV > 100 fL (e.g., folate/B12 deficiency, liver disease, hypothyroidism, alcoholism)','1. **Microcytic**: MCV < 80 fL (e.g., iron deficiency, thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease)
2. **Normocytic**: MCV 80-100 fL (e.g., anemia of chronic disease, hemolysis, early iron deficiency)
3. **Macrocytic**: MCV > 100 fL (e.g., folate/B12 deficiency, liver disease, hypothyroidism, alcoholism)','','','','Moderate','General','Hematology','["flashcard","Anemia classification flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T23:35:26.782Z','2026-10-25T23:35:26.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvogu1xg001gspwuj0eu0fc3','flashcard','Common causes of microcytic anemia','[]','1. **Iron deficiency**: Most common cause worldwide
2. **Thalassemia**: Genetic defect in hemoglobin synthesis
3. **Anemia of chronic disease**: Functional iron deficiency due to inflammation
4. **Sideroblastic anemia**: Defective heme synthesis with ringed sideroblasts','1. **Iron deficiency**: Most common cause worldwide
2. **Thalassemia**: Genetic defect in hemoglobin synthesis
3. **Anemia of chronic disease**: Functional iron deficiency due to inflammation
4. **Sideroblastic anemia**: Defective heme synthesis with ringed sideroblasts','','','','Moderate','General','Hematology','["flashcard","Anemia classification flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T23:35:26.782Z','2026-10-25T23:35:26.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvogu1xi001ispwug1f28cqb','flashcard','Classification by etiology (pathophysiologic)','[]','1. **Production defects**: 
   - Bone marrow failure (aplastic anemia)
   - Nutritional deficiencies (B12, folate, iron)
   - Chronic disease/inflammation
2. **Destruction defects**: 
   - Hemolytic anemias (autoimmune, hereditary, acquired)
   - Mechanical destruction (e.g., DIC, TTP)
3. **Blood loss**: 
   - Acute hemorrhage
   - Chronic blood loss','1. **Production defects**: 
   - Bone marrow failure (aplastic anemia)
   - Nutritional deficiencies (B12, folate, iron)
   - Chronic disease/inflammation
2. **Destruction defects**: 
   - Hemolytic anemias (autoimmune, hereditary, acquired)
   - Mechanical destruction (e.g., DIC, TTP)
3. **Blood loss**: 
   - Acute hemorrhage
   - Chronic blood loss','','','','Moderate','General','Hematology','["flashcard","Anemia classification flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-25T23:35:26.782Z','2026-10-25T23:35:26.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoxlcmv000escwuzmoyuxnp','flashcard','What are the four chambers of the heart?','[]','The four chambers of the heart are the right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, and left ventricle.','The four chambers of the heart are the right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, and left ventricle.','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Heart chambers flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:24:34.230Z','2026-10-26T07:24:34.230Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoxlcn2000gscwuy06fcak9','flashcard','Which chamber receives deoxygenated blood from the body?','[]','The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body via the superior and inferior vena cava.','The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body via the superior and inferior vena cava.','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Heart chambers flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:24:34.230Z','2026-10-26T07:24:34.230Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoxlcn4000iscwu5qu087iv','flashcard','Which chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the body?','[]','The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood to the body through the aorta.','The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood to the body through the aorta.','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Heart chambers flashcards"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:24:34.230Z','2026-10-26T07:24:34.230Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoyih2f000sscwurni010uy','flashcard','What are the main structural differences between atria and ventricles?','[]','Atria are smaller, thinner-walled chambers that receive blood; ventricles are larger, thicker-walled chambers that pump blood out of the heart','Atria are smaller, thinner-walled chambers that receive blood; ventricles are larger, thicker-walled chambers that pump blood out of the heart','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Atria vs Ventricles comparison"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z','2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoyih2i000uscwue5en1us3','flashcard','Which chambers have thinner walls and why?','[]','Atria have thinner walls because they only need to pump blood a short distance to the ventricles, not to the lungs or body','Atria have thinner walls because they only need to pump blood a short distance to the ventricles, not to the lungs or body','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Atria vs Ventricles comparison"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z','2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvoyih2j000wscwuday8sr21','flashcard','Which chambers have thicker walls and why?','[]','Ventricles have thicker walls because they generate higher pressure to pump blood long distances - left ventricle pumps to entire body, right ventricle pumps to lungs','Ventricles have thicker walls because they generate higher pressure to pump blood long distances - left ventricle pumps to entire body, right ventricle pumps to lungs','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart anatomy','["flashcard","Atria vs Ventricles comparison"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','approved','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z','2026-10-26T07:50:19.622Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6c0048e9oin04khloi','mcq','The following are components of Fallot tetralogy except','["Big VSD","Overriding aorta","Mitral stenosis","Right ventricular hypertrophy"]','Mitral stenosis','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Tetralogy of Fallot','["tetralogy","components","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.652Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.652Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqptmq9000ze9oiet1otipi','mcq','A 55-year-old patient develops a persistent dry cough 3 weeks after starting enalapril for hypertension. The cough is bothersome but not associated with fever, wheezing, or sputum production. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of ACE inhibitor-induced cough?','["Direct irritation of bronchial mucosa by the drug","Accumulation of angiotensin II causing bronchoconstriction","Increased bradykinin and prostaglandin levels in the airways","Hypersensitivity reaction requiring immediate discontinuation","Drug-induced eosinophilic bronchitis"]','Increased bradykinin and prostaglandin levels in the airways','ACE inhibitor cough is mediated by increased levels of bradykinin and prostaglandins in the airways, which stimulates cough receptors. ACE normally degrades bradykinin, so when ACE is inhibited, bradykinin accumulates and causes irritation of the airways. This results in a dry, irritant cough that is the most common side effect of ACE inhibitors, occurring in 5-35% of patients. The cough is not due to direct drug irritation, angiotensin II accumulation, hypersensitivity, or eosinophilic inflammation.','ACE-Cough: Bradykinin Build-up','ACE inhibitor cough is bradykinin-mediated and typically occurs within 1-8 weeks of starting therapy. It resolves within days to weeks after discontinuation and does not respond to antitussives.','Option A is incorrect as ACE inhibitors don''t directly irritate airways. Option B is incorrect as ACE inhibition reduces angiotensin II. Option D is incorrect as this is not a hypersensitivity reaction. Option E is incorrect as eosinophilic inflammation is not the mechanism.','Medium','pulmonary','ACE inhibitor adverse effects','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitor adverse effects","ACE inhibitors","cough","bradykinin","adverse drug reactions"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T13:22:35.985Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.785Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqpsy8n000ve9oifeluhp9z','mcq','A 28-year-old pregnant woman at 12 weeks gestation presents with newly diagnosed hypertension (150/95 mmHg). She has no other medical conditions and normal renal function. Which of the following statements regarding ACE inhibitor use in this patient is most accurate?','["ACE inhibitors can be safely used throughout pregnancy with careful monitoring","ACE inhibitors should be discontinued immediately and replaced with a calcium channel blocker","ACE inhibitors are contraindicated only in the third trimester","ACE inhibitors are safe to use in the first trimester but should be avoided in later pregnancy","ACE inhibitors can be used if fetal renal ultrasound is normal"]','ACE inhibitors should be discontinued immediately and replaced with a calcium channel blocker','ACE inhibitors are absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy due to teratogenic effects, particularly on the fetal renal system. They can cause fetal renal dysplasia, oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and skull hypoplasia. The contraindication applies to all trimesters, not just the third trimester. Immediate discontinuation is required, and alternative antihypertensive medications such as methyldopa, labetalol, or calcium channel blockers should be used.','ACE-Pregnancy: Absolutely Contraindicated Everywhere','ACE inhibitors cause fetal renal damage by disrupting angiotensin II''s role in fetal kidney development and amniotic fluid production. Discontinue immediately when pregnancy is detected.','Option A is incorrect due to teratogenic effects. Option C is incorrect as contraindication applies to all trimesters. Option D is incorrect as first trimester exposure is also harmful. Option E is incorrect as no monitoring makes ACE inhibitors safe in pregnancy.','Medium','obstetrics','ACE inhibitors in pregnancy','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors in pregnancy","ACE inhibitors","pregnancy","teratogenicity","hypertension in pregnancy"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T13:22:04.247Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.787Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5h0037e9oir5gskxzl','mcq','Why we do palliative surgery','["Try to improve anatomy till total repair","To treat Congestive heart failure","To improve infective endocarditis"]','Try to improve anatomy till total repair','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["palliative","surgery","congenital","heart"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.621Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.621Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5g0036e9oihpbfi7z7','mcq','Chest x ray show egg shaped in which condition','["Transposition of great vessels"]','Transposition of great vessels','<p><strong>Why Transposition of great vessels is correct:</strong> Transposition of the great vessels (TGV) is a congenital heart defect where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, creating parallel circulations. The classic chest X-ray finding in TGV is an ''egg-shaped'' or ''egg-on-a-string'' appearance due to the superior mediastinal shadow created by the narrowed superior mediastinum and the prominent cardiac silhouette. This occurs because the transposed great vessels create a characteristic configuration where the aorta is anterior and to the right, while the pulmonary artery is posterior and to the left, resulting in this distinctive radiographic appearance.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Other congenital heart defects:</strong> While many congenital heart defects have characteristic chest X-ray findings, TGV specifically produces the ''egg-shaped'' appearance due to its unique anatomic arrangement of the great vessels.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired heart conditions:</strong> Acquired heart conditions like cardiomyopathies or valvular heart disease typically show different radiographic patterns such as cardiomegaly, pulmonary congestion, or specific chamber enlargement, not the classic ''egg-shaped'' appearance.</li>
<li><strong>Normal cardiac anatomy:</strong> A normal heart on chest X-ray shows a more rounded cardiac silhouette without the characteristic ''egg-shaped'' appearance seen in TGV.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The ''egg-shaped'' or ''egg-on-a-string'' appearance on chest X-ray is pathognomonic for transposition of the great vessels.</li>
<li>TGV is one of the most common cyanotic congenital heart presenting in the newborn period.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by echocardiography, which shows the parallel arrangement of the great vessels.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Transposition of the great vessels creates the classic ''egg-shaped'' chest X-ray appearance due to its unique anatomic arrangement of the aorta and pulmonary artery.</p>','','<p>The</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Other congenital heart defects:</strong> They typically show different radiographic patterns than the ''egg-shaped'' appearance characteristic of TGV.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired heart conditions:</strong> These usually show cardiomegaly or pulmonary congestion, not the specific ''egg-shaped'' silhouette.</li>
<li><strong>Normal cardiac anatomy:</strong> A normal heart appears more rounded without the characteristic TGV configuration.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["chest x-ray","egg-shaped","transposition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.620Z','2026-10-27T15:51:29.468Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5l003ce9oibyum4p3l','mcq','Main tool for Diagnosis of TGA','["Chest X-ray","Echo"]','Echo','<p><strong>Why Echo is correct:</strong> Echocardiography is the primary diagnostic tool for transposition of the great arteries (TGA) because it provides detailed visualization of the cardiac anatomy, including the spatial relationships of the great vessels, ventricular morphology, and presence of associated defects. It allows for real-time assessment of the direction of blood flow, ventricular function, and identification of any shunts or septal defects that may be present. The two-dimensional imaging clearly demonstrates the parallel arrangement of the aorta and pulmonary artery, which is the hallmark of TGA, and color Doppler can reveal any mixing of blood between the systemic and pulmonary circulations.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chest X-ray:</strong> While a chest X-ray may show a classic "egg-on-a-string" appearance in TGA, it cannot provide the detailed anatomical information needed to confirm the diagnosis or assess the severity of the condition. It lacks the resolution to visualize the great vessels'' relationships accurately.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>TGA is characterized by the aorta arising from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle.</li>
<li>Echocardiography is the gold standard for diagnosing congenital heart defects, including TGA.</li>
<li>The "egg-on-a-string" appearance on chest X-ray is suggestive but not diagnostic of TGA.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Echocardiography is the definitive diagnostic tool for TGA as it directly visualizes the abnormal arrangement of the great vessels and associated cardiac structures.</p>','','<p>Early diagnosis of TGA is critical as these infants typically develop profound cyanosis shortly after birth if not immediately recognized and managed.</p>','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Transposition of Great Arteries','["TGA","diagnosis","echo","echocardiography"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.625Z','2026-10-27T15:51:43.927Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7s006be9oii3cl3sd2','mcq','What is mandatory sign in chest X ray with heart failure?','["Lung plethora","Exaggerated hilar shadow","Cardiomegaly","Pleural effusion"]','Cardiomegaly','<p><strong>Why Cardiomegaly is correct:</strong> Cardiomegaly, defined as an increased cardiac silhouette size on chest X-ray, is the most consistent and mandatory sign of heart failure because it directly reflects the underlying pathological process of cardiac chamber enlargement due to volume or pressure overload. In heart failure, the heart compensates for reduced pumping efficiency by dilating (increasing preload) or hypertrophying (increasing afterload), both of which result in an enlarged cardiac silhouette visible on X-ray. While other findings may be present, cardiomegaly is the most reliable indicator that can be objectively measured using cardiothoracic ratio (normally &lt;0.5 in children).</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lung plethora:</strong> Lung plethora (increased pulmonary vascular markings) is a common finding in heart failure but is not mandatory, as it may be absent in early or compensated cases.</li>
<li><strong>Exaggerated hilar shadow:</strong> While the hilar shadow may appear prominent in heart failure due to engorged pulmonary vessels, this is not a specific or mandatory sign.</li>
<li><strong>Pleural effusion:</strong> Pleural effusions can occur in heart failure but are not mandatory findings and may be absent, especially in mild cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cardiomegaly is the most consistent radiographic finding in heart failure, reflecting chamber enlargement due to volume or pressure overload.</li>
<li>The cardiothoracic ratio (CCR) is an objective measure of cardiomegaly, with values &gt;0.5 in children indicating heart enlargement.</li>
<li>While lung plethora and pleural effusions are common in heart failure, they are not as consistently present as cardiomegaly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cardiomegaly is the mandatory chest X-ray finding in heart failure because it directly reflects the pathological cardiac chamber enlargement that defines the condition.</p>','<p>CM = Cardiac Mandatory: Cardiomegaly is the most consistent finding in heart failure on chest X-ray.</p>','<p>In pediatric heart failure, cardiomegaly is often the earliest and most reliable radiographic sign, preceding the development of pulmonary congestion.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Lung plethora:</strong> Common but not mandatory, as it may be absent in early or compensated heart failure.</li>
<li><strong>Exaggerated hilar shadow:</strong> Not specific to heart failure and not consistently present.</li>
<li><strong>Pleural effusion:</strong> Occurs in heart failure but is not mandatory and may be absent in mild cases.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure','["chest-xray","heart-failure","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.704Z','2026-10-27T16:12:12.592Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7g005re9oi1ya3sz2a','mcq','Of the following, the most common finding on ECG of a 12-year-old child with severe aortic stenosis is:','["Prolonged PR interval.","Right atrial enlargement.","Right ventricular hypertrophy.","Left Ventricular Hypertrophy."]','Left Ventricular Hypertrophy.','','','','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["ecg","aortic-stenosis","pediatric"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.692Z','2026-10-27T14:10:11.692Z');
