INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7sra0019der8t52xwnac','mcq','Head circumference at birth measures normally','["45 cm","50 cm","35 cm","30 cm"]','35 cm','<p><strong>Why 35 cm is correct:</strong> The average head circumference at birth is approximately 35 cm, which reflects the rapid brain growth that occurs during the third trimester of pregnancy. This measurement is taken at the largest occipitofrontal circumference and serves as an important indicator of normal fetal development and potential neurological abnormalities. The head circumference typically increases by about 2 cm per month during the first three months of life, reflecting ongoing brain development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A. 45 cm:</strong> This measurement is more typical of a 1-year-old''s head circumference, not a newborn''s.</li>
<li><strong>B. 50 cm:</strong> This would be abnormally large for a newborn (macrocephaly) and is not within the normal range.</li>
<li><strong>D. 30 cm:</strong> This measurement is below the normal range and would suggest microcephaly or intrauterine growth restriction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Normal head circumference at birth is 32-38 cm, with 35 cm being the average.</li>
<li>Head circumference is plotted on growth charts and should follow a consistent percentile curve.</li>
<li>Disproportionate head growth (increasing percentile) may indicate hydrocephalus, while decreasing percentile may suggest microcephaly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember that the average newborn head circumference is 35 cm, reflecting the rapid brain growth that occurs in utero.</p>','<p>Newborn head: 35 cm (think ''3'' for the third trimester brain growth spurt)</p>','<p>Head circumference measurement is a critical screening tool for detecting neurological conditions; always compare it to length and weight percentiles.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. 45 cm:</strong> This is typical for a 1-year-old, not a newborn.</li>
<li><strong>B. 50 cm:</strong> This would indicate macrocephaly, which is abnormal at birth.</li>
<li><strong>D. 30 cm:</strong> This is below normal and suggests microcephaly or growth restriction.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Growth & Development','Anthropometry','["head circumference","birth","normal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.302Z','2026-10-27T14:10:37.894Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriudu00fye9oibzkb65j5','short','The most serious complication of hyperbilirubinemia is','[]','Kernicterus','','','','','Moderate','General','Neonatal Jaundice','["bilirubin","jaundice","kernicterus"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.922Z','2026-10-27T15:50:23.123Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6m004me9oimdtz8sya','mcq','Systemic venous congestion signs of Congestive heart failure include all the followings except :','["Hepatomegaly","Generalized edema","Congested neck veins","dehydration"]','dehydration','<p><strong>Why dehydration is correct:</strong> Dehydration is not a sign of systemic venous congestion in congestive heart failure; rather, it represents a state of inadequate fluid volume. Systemic venous congestion occurs when the right ventricle fails to effectively pump blood into the pulmonary circulation, causing blood to back up in the systemic venous system. This increased venous pressure leads to fluid extravasation into tissues, causing signs like hepatomegaly, generalized edema, and congested neck veins. Dehydration, conversely, would be characterized by decreased venous pressure, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, and concentrated urine.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hepatomegaly:</strong> This is a classic sign of systemic venous congestion, as the liver becomes engorged with blood due to elevated central venous pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Generalized edema:</strong> This occurs when increased venous pressure leads to fluid transudation into the interstitial spaces, commonly seen in dependent areas like the ankles and sacrum.</li>
<li><strong>Congested neck veins:</strong> Jugular venous distention (JVD) is a direct physical sign of elevated central venous pressure due to right-sided heart failure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Systemic venous congestion signs are primarily due to right-sided heart failure, while pulmonary congestion signs (like dyspnea and crackles) are due to left-sided heart failure.</li>
<li>The severity of systemic venous congestion can be assessed by examining jugular venous pressure, liver size, and presence of peripheral edema.</li>
<li>In advanced heart failure, both systemic and pulmonary congestion may be present, creating a complex clinical picture.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Systemic venous congestion in heart failure causes fluid overload signs (hepatomegaly, edema, JVD), while dehydration represents the opposite state of volume depletion.</p>','<p>CHF Signs: HEART (Hepatomegaly, Edema, And Right-sided signs - Neck veins)</p>','<p>Jugular venous distention is a more sensitive indicator of right heart failure than peripheral edema.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hepatomegaly:</strong> A classic sign of systemic venous congestion due to elevated central venous pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Generalized edema:</strong> Results from increased venous pressure causing fluid transudation into tissues.</li>
<li><strong>Congested neck veins:</strong> Direct evidence of elevated central venous pressure in right heart failure.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure','["congestion","chf","dehydration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.662Z','2026-10-27T15:53:39.241Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriudn00foe9oinazp74ku','mcq','What is the most accurate diagnostic test for biliary atresia?','["Percutaneous liver biopsy","Liver function test","Complete blood count","Urine analysis"]','Percutaneous liver biopsy','','','','','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Biliary Atresia','["biliary","diagnosis","biopsy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.915Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriudr00fte9oiplyxftef','mcq','Absent enzymatic activity of glucuronyle transferase occurs in which condition?','["Crigler Najjar type 1","Cephalohematoma","Elepitocytosis","RH Incompatibility"]','Crigler Najjar type 1','','','','','Hard','Hematology','Crigler-Najjar Syndrome','["bilirubin","enzyme","genetic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.919Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.786Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmusk7srb001ader8p52ce1au','mcq','When body proportion equals 1:1?','["At 7 years","5 years","10 years"]','At 7 years','<p><strong>Why At 7 years is correct:</strong> Body proportion reaches 1:1 (head to body) at approximately 7 years of age, marking the transition from the proportionally larger head of early childhood to more adult-like proportions. This occurs because the head grows more slowly after infancy while the trunk and limbs continue to grow at a relatively constant rate. By age 7, the head has grown to about 1/7 of total body height, resulting in the 1:1 ratio between head height and sitting height.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>B. 5 years:</strong> At age 5, the head is still proportionally larger than the body, with a head-to-body ratio greater than 1:1.</li>
<li><strong>C. 10 years:</strong> By age 10, the child has already surpassed the 1:1 ratio and is approaching adult proportions with a smaller head relative to body size.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Newborns have a head-to-body ratio of about 1:4.</li>
<li>The 1:1 head-to-body ratio typically occurs between 6-8 years of age.</li>
<li>This proportional change is part of the normal growth pattern and helps clinicians assess normal development.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Body proportion reaches 1:1 at age 7 as the head grows more slowly while the body continues to lengthen.</p>','<p>Seven is heaven for body proportion (1:1 at age 7)</p>','<p>Proportional changes in childhood growth are important developmental markers that help identify growth disorders when deviations occur.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B. 5 years:</strong> At age 5, the head is still proportionally larger than the body.</li>
<li><strong>C. 10 years:</strong> By age 10, the child has already surpassed the 1:1 ratio.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Growth & Development','Body Proportions','["body proportion","growth","childhood"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0.5,-1,2,'2026-10-03T15:41:29.303Z','2026-10-27T14:10:37.899Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h9d0021qjlr6rc6re22','mcq','5- Most common cause of bowel obstruction in neonate is','["Hirschsprung''s disease"]','Hirschsprung''s disease','<p><strong>Why Hirschsprung''s disease is correct:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease is the most common cause of bowel obstruction in neonates due to the absence of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon, resulting in a functional obstruction. This aganglionic segment fails to relax, causing a functional obstruction that manifests as failure to pass meconium in the first 24-48 hours of life, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting. The condition is most commonly seen in males and has a genetic component, with mutations in the RET proto-oncogene being associated with about 50% of familial cases.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Malrotation:</strong> While malrotation can cause bowel obstruction, it typically presents later in infancy or childhood and is not the most common cause in neonates. - <strong>Meconium ileus:</strong> This is associated with cystic fibrosis and presents with inspissated meconium causing obstruction, but it''s less common than Hirschsprung''s disease. - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> This is uncommon in neonates and typically occurs in infants 6-36 months of age.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hirschsprung''s disease should be suspected in any neonate who fails to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. - The classic triad includes abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium. - The diagnosis is confirmed by rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hirschsprung''s disease is the most common neonatal bowel obstruction due to aganglionosis causing functional obstruction, presenting with failure to pass meconium in the first 48 hours.</p>','<p>Hirschsprung''s: ''Failure to Pass Meconium in 48 Hours'' (FPM48)</p>','<p>Early recognition of Hirschsprung''s disease is critical as delayed diagnosis can lead to life-threatening enterocolitis and perforation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Malrotation:</strong> Less common in neonates and typically presents later in infancy. - <strong>Meconium ileus:</strong> Associated with cystic fibrosis and is less common than Hirschsprung''s disease. - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> Uncommon in neonates and typically occurs in older infants.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastroenterology','Bowel Obstruction','["neonate","bowel obstruction","Hirschsprung"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.681Z','2026-10-27T14:11:49.710Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56ha50022qjlr7lr9965s','mcq','6- An 18-month-old child under evaluation for abdominal distension, failure to thrive, and constipation starting in infancy with a poor response to medical management. Rectal examination shows an empty rectum with explosive passage of stool. Of the following, what is the MOST appropriate diagnostic tool?','["Abdominal ultrasound","Plain radiograph of abdomen","Anorectal manometry","Barium enema"]','Barium enema','<p><strong>Why Barium enema is correct:</strong> A barium enema is the most appropriate diagnostic tool for this child with suspected Hirschsprung''s disease, as it can demonstrate the classic transition zone between the dilated proximal colon and the narrowed aganglionic distal segment. The test is particularly useful in older children where the rectum is typically empty with explosive stool passage, and the barium enema can show the characteristic funnel-shaped appearance of the distal colon. This diagnostic modality is less invasive than biopsy and can provide immediate visualization of the colonic anatomy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> While ultrasound can show dilated bowel loops, it cannot specifically diagnose Hirschsprung''s disease or identify the transition zone. - <strong>Plain radiograph of abdomen:</strong> This may show dilated bowel loops but cannot differentiate between different causes of obstruction or identify the transition zone characteristic of Hirschsprung''s disease. - <strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> This test can be supportive but is less specific than barium enema and requires specialized expertise to interpret correctly.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The classic triad of abdominal distension, failure to thrive, and constipation with poor response to medical management strongly suggests Hirschsprung''s disease. - The finding of an empty rectum with explosive stool passage is highly suggestive of Hirschsprung''s disease. - Barium enema is the initial imaging modality of choice, with rectal biopsy being confirmatory.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For a child with suspected Hirschsprung''s disease presenting with abdominal distension, failure to thrive, and constipation, a barium enema is the most appropriate diagnostic tool to identify the characteristic transition zone.</p>','<p>Barium for Hirschsprung''s: ''B for Bowel transition zone''</p>','<p>In Hirschsprung''s disease, the transition zone seen on barium enema is often located more proximally than clinically suspected, which is important for surgical planning.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> Cannot specifically diagnose Hirschsprung''s disease or identify the transition zone. - <strong>Plain radiograph of abdomen:</strong> Shows dilated bowel but cannot differentiate causes or identify the transition zone. - <strong>Anorectal manometry:</strong> Less specific than barium enema and requires specialized expertise.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["constipation","diagnostic","barium enema"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.709Z','2026-10-27T14:11:49.716Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56ha70023qjlrm33a0jwl','mcq','7- Complication with Hirschsprung disease','["Enterocolitis"]','Enterocolitis','<p><strong>Why Enterocolitis is correct:</strong> Enterocolitis is the most serious and common complication of Hirschsprung''s disease, occurring in up to 30% of patients, particularly those with long-segment disease or delayed diagnosis. This complication results from functional obstruction leading to bacterial overgrowth, mucosal ischemia, and inflammation in the proximal dilated colon, causing abdominal distension, fever, explosive diarrhea, and potentially sepsis. The pathophysiology involves impaired mucosal barrier function and translocation of bacteria across the compromised intestinal wall, which can rapidly progress to toxic megacolon and perforation if not promptly treated.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While other complications like bowel perforation, volvulus, and malnutrition can occur in Hirschsprung''s disease, enterocolitis is by far the most common and potentially life-threatening complication that requires immediate recognition and intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Enterocolitis can occur at any time, from the neonatal period to adulthood, but is most common in the first two years of life. - The classic presentation includes abdominal distension, fever, explosive diarrhea, and lethargy. - Prompt management includes bowel decompression, antibiotics, and potentially emergency surgery if perforation is suspected.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Enterocolitis is the most common and dangerous complication of Hirschsprung''s disease, characterized by abdominal distension, fever, and explosive diarrhea, requiring immediate intervention.</p>','<p>Enterocolitis in Hirschsprung''s: ''E for Explosive diarrhea, E for Emergency''</p>','<p>Hirschsprung''s-associated enterocolitis can occur even after definitive surgical treatment, requiring ongoing patient education and recognition of early warning signs.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Bowel perforation:</strong> A serious complication but less common than enterocolitis. - <strong>Volvulus:</strong> Can occur but is less frequent than enterocolitis. - <strong>Malnutrition:</strong> A long-term complication but not the most acute or common complication.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastroenterology','Hirschsprung Disease','["Hirschsprung","complication","enterocolitis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.711Z','2026-10-27T14:11:49.716Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hde003fqjlrm3i4skz6','mcq','Which of the following statements is right about diarrhea?','["Passage of 3 or more stools /day","Causes more than 50% of under-five mortality","Change in stool consistency rather than frequency","Mostly associated with exclusive breastfeeding"]','Change in stool consistency rather than frequency','<p><strong>Why Change in stool consistency rather than frequency is correct:</strong> Diarrhea is clinically defined by a change in stool consistency to a more liquid or loose form, not by the absolute number of stools. This definition accounts for normal variations in stool frequency between individuals (e.g., breastfed infants may have many loose stools daily). The pathophysiology involves increased secretion of water and electrolytes into the intestinal lumen, decreased absorption, or rapid transit time, all of which alter stool consistency. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong>- <strong>Passage of 3 or more stools/day:</strong> This is an arbitrary threshold that doesn''t account for normal variations in stool frequency between individuals and age groups.- <strong>Causes more than 50% of under-five mortality:</strong> While diarrhea is a major cause of child mortality globally, it accounts for approximately 9% of under-five deaths, not 50%.- <strong>Mostly associated with exclusive breastfeeding:</strong> Exclusive breastfeeding actually protects against diarrhea by providing immune factors and reducing exposure to pathogens. <strong>Key pearls:</strong>- The WHO defines diarrhea as three or more loose or liquid stools per day, but emphasizes consistency over frequency.- Acute diarrhea is typically caused by infections (viral, bacterial, parasitic), while chronic diarrhea may indicate inflammatory bowel disease, malabsorption, or other chronic conditions.- In clinical practice, assessing stool consistency is more reliable than counting stools for diagnosis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Diarrhea is defined by a change in stool consistency to a more liquid form, not by the number of stools.</p>','<p>Diarrhea: D for Dropped consistency, not just Daily frequency</p>','<p>The duration of diarrhea helps distinguish between acute (≤14 days) and persistent (&gt;14 days) diarrhea, which has different diagnostic approaches and management considerations.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Passage of 3 or more stools/day:</strong> An arbitrary threshold that doesn''t account for normal individual variations.</li>
<li><strong>Causes more than 50% of under-five mortality:</strong> Diarrhea causes approximately 9% of under-five deaths, not 50%.</li>
<li><strong>Mostly associated with exclusive breastfeeding:</strong> Breastfeeding actually protects against diarrhea.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Definition','["definition","consistency","frequency"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.826Z','2026-10-27T14:12:46.368Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hb9002qqjlrgrcxsfp5','mcq','Zinc supplementation dose for a child over 1 year with diarrhea is:','["5 mg/day for 1 week","10 mg/day for 2 weeks","15 mg/day for 1 week","20 mg/day for 2 weeks"]','20 mg/day for 2 weeks','<p><strong>Why 20 mg/day for 2 weeks is correct:</strong> The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends zinc supplementation for children with diarrhea to reduce the duration and severity of the illness. For children over 1 year of age with diarrhea, the recommended dose is 20 mg of elemental zinc per day for 10-14 days (2 weeks). This higher dose compensates for the increased losses and reduced absorption during diarrheal illness. Zinc plays a crucial role in maintaining intestinal epithelial integrity, supporting immune function, and aiding in the regeneration of the gut mucosa, which is compromised during diarrheal diseases.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>5 mg/day for 1 week:</strong> This dose is too low and duration too short to be effective for treating diarrhea in children over 1 year.</li>
<li><strong>10 mg/day for 2 weeks:</strong> While the duration is correct, the dose is insufficient for children over 1 year (though appropriate for infants under 6 months).</li>
<li><strong>15 mg/day for 1 week:</strong> This dose is inadequate and the duration too short to provide the full therapeutic benefit for diarrhea management.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Zinc supplementation reduces the duration of diarrhea by approximately 25% and decreases stool volume by up to 30%.</li>
<li>The mechanism involves restoration of intestinal brush border enzymes, improved absorption of water and electrolytes, and enhanced immune response.</li>
<li>Zinc deficiency is common in children in developing countries with high diarrheal burden, making supplementation particularly important in these settings.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For children over 1 year with diarrhea, remember the zinc dose as ''20 for 2 weeks'' (20 mg/day for 2 weeks) as recommended by WHO.</p>','<p>20/20 for diarrhea: 20 mg/day for 2 weeks (or 20 days) for children over 1 year.</p>','<p>Zinc supplementation not only treats acute diarrhea but also provides protective benefits against future episodes for up to 2-3 months after treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>5 mg/day for 1 week:</strong> Too low dose and too short duration.</li>
<li><strong>10 mg/day for 2 weeks:</strong> Dose is insufficient for children over 1 year.</li>
<li><strong>15 mg/day for 1 week:</strong> Dose inadequate and duration too short.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Management','["zinc","diarrhea","supplementation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.749Z','2026-10-27T14:12:40.139Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hba002rqjlrqth6upj7','mcq','In case of some dehydration, which WHO plan is used?','["Plan A","Plan B","Plan C","None"]','Plan A','<p><strong>Why Plan A is correct:</strong> The WHO has developed a comprehensive approach to managing diarrhea in children with increasing levels of severity, categorized as Plans A, B, C, and D. Plan A is used for children with some dehydration, which represents the mildest form of dehydration. Plan A focuses on oral rehydration therapy (ORT) with the child continuing to breastfeed or receive usual fluids, plus increased fluids and continued feeding. This plan is appropriate for children who are not severely dehydrated and can tolerate oral rehydration without the need for nasogastric tubes or intravenous fluids.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Plan B:</strong> This is used for moderate dehydration and involves more aggressive oral rehydration with closer monitoring and potentially the use of a nasogastric tube if the child cannot drink adequately.</li>
<li><strong>Plan C:</strong> This is reserved for severe dehydration requiring immediate hospitalization and intravenous fluid resuscitation.</li>
<li><strong>None:</strong> This is incorrect as Plan A is specifically designed for managing some dehydration according to WHO guidelines.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The WHO diarrhea management plans are based on the degree of dehydration assessed clinically (based on skin turgor, eye appearance, mucous membranes, and urine output).</li>
<li>Plan A emphasizes the importance of continued feeding during diarrhea to prevent malnutrition, which is a critical component of diarrhea management.</li>
<li>The success of oral rehydration therapy has been called one of the most important medical advances of the 20th century, significantly reducing diarrhea-related mortality worldwide.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember that Plan A is for ''some'' dehydration, focusing on oral rehydration with continued feeding, while more severe cases require Plans B, C, or D.</p>','<p>A for ''some'' dehydration: Plan A is for mild cases, B for moderate, C for severe.</p>','<p>Continued feeding during diarrhea is crucial as it provides necessary nutrients and helps maintain gut mucosal integrity, reducing the duration of illness.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Plan B:</strong> Used for moderate dehydration, requiring more aggressive oral rehydration.</li>
<li><strong>Plan C:</strong> Used for severe dehydration requiring intravenous fluids.</li>
<li><strong>None:</strong> Incorrect as Plan A is specifically designed for some dehydration.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Diarrhea Management','["who","dehydration","diarrhea"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.750Z','2026-10-27T14:12:40.140Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hda003dqjlrff7sfe9c','mcq','A female child 4 years old came to emergency department with a history of vomiting and diarrhea, she was irritable and drinks eagerly, with normal skin elasticity, and eye. What is the most likely diagnosis of this child?','["Gastroenteritis with some dehydration.","Gastroenteritis with no dehydration.","Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration.","Gastroenteritis with disturbed conscious level."]','Gastroenteritis with no dehydration.','<p><strong>Why Gastroenteritis with no dehydration is correct:</strong> The child has gastroenteritis (vomiting and diarrhea) but shows no signs of dehydration as evidenced by normal skin elasticity, normal eyes (no sunken appearance), and the ability to drink eagerly. According to the WHO classification system for dehydration in children with diarrhea, these findings indicate no dehydration. The child maintains normal hydration status with adequate fluid intake and normal physiological parameters. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong>- <strong>Gastroenteritis with some dehydration:</strong> This would be indicated by at least two signs such as restlessness, thirst, sunken eyes, or slightly decreased skin turgor, none of which are present in this case.- <strong>Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration:</strong> This would require multiple signs including lethargy, sunken eyes, very dry mouth and tongue, and poor skin turgor, which are absent here.- <strong>Gastroenteritis with disturbed conscious level:</strong> The child is irritable but not lethargic or unconscious, which would be required for this diagnosis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong>- The WHO dehydration assessment focuses on six signs: general appearance, eyes, mouth/tongue, thirst, skin turgor, and urine output.- No dehydration requires 0-1 signs of dehydration, some dehydration requires 2 signs, and severe dehydration requires 6 or more signs.- Eager drinking is a positive sign indicating adequate hydration status. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Normal skin turgor, normal eyes, and eager drinking in a child with gastroenteritis confirm no dehydration.</p>','<p>No dehydration: Normal skin, Normal eyes, Normal drinking (3Ns)</p>','<p>In pediatric gastroenteritis, the ability to drink is a critical positive prognostic factor, and oral rehydration solution should be offered immediately to all children with diarrhea regardless of dehydration status.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with some dehydration:</strong> Would require at least two signs of dehydration like restlessness or sunken eyes.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with severe dehydration:</strong> Would require multiple severe signs including lethargy and very dry mucous membranes.</li>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis with disturbed conscious level:</strong> Would require lethargy or decreased consciousness, not just irritability.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Dehydration','["diagnosis","dehydration","gastroenteritis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.822Z','2026-10-27T14:12:46.365Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hdc003eqjlrjqs5sfb8','mcq','Which of the following diets should be avoided in a child with celiac disease?','["Soybean","Rye","Corn","Maize"]','Rye','<p><strong>Why Rye is correct:</strong> Rye contains gluten, a protein that triggers an autoimmune response in children with celiac disease. Gluten is found in wheat, barley, and rye, and causes damage to the small intestinal villi in genetically susceptible individuals. The immune response involves both cellular (T-cell mediated) and humoral (antibody production) mechanisms that lead to villous atrophy and malabsorption. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong>- <strong>Soybean:</strong> Soy is naturally gluten-free and safe for children with celiac disease, though cross-contamination with gluten-containing grains can occur during processing.- <strong>Corn:</strong> Corn is gluten-free and can be safely consumed by children with celiac disease, though some individuals may have corn sensitivities unrelated to celiac disease.- <strong>Maize:</strong> Maize (corn) is gluten-free and appropriate for a celiac disease diet; this option is essentially the same as corn and represents a redundant distractor. <strong>Key pearls:</strong>- The gluten-containing grains to avoid in celiac disease are wheat, barley, and rye; oats may be contaminated with gluten during processing.- Rice, corn, soy, and potato are naturally gluten-free and safe for celiac disease.- Cross-contamination is a significant concern, so dedicated gluten-free processing facilities are important for safe foods. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rye contains gluten and must be avoided in celiac disease, while soybean and corn are naturally gluten-free.</p>','<p>Wheat, Barley, Rye - no gluten for celiac</p>','<p>Celiac disease requires strict lifelong adherence to a gluten-free diet, even in asymptomatic individuals, to prevent long-term complications like osteoporosis and lymphoma.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Soybean:</strong> Naturally gluten-free and safe for celiac disease patients.</li>
<li><strong>Corn:</strong> Naturally gluten-free and appropriate for celiac disease.</li>
<li><strong>Maize:</strong> Another term for corn, which is gluten-free and safe.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Celiac Disease','["celiac","diet","avoid"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.824Z','2026-10-27T14:12:46.366Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6p004ne9oiehr9cso9','mcq','Congenital cyanotic heart diseases with decrease pulmonary blood flow include all the following except','["Pulmonary regurgitation","Tetralogy of Fallot","Pulmonary atresia","DORV with pulmonary stenosis"]','Pulmonary regurgitation','<p><strong>Why Pulmonary regurgitation is correct:</strong> Pulmonary regurgitation is not a congenital cyanotic heart disease with decreased pulmonary blood flow; rather, it is a condition where blood flows backward from the pulmonary artery into the right ventricle during diastole. This condition typically does not cause cyanosis because it doesn''t significantly reduce pulmonary blood flow. In fact, severe pulmonary regurgitation can lead to right ventricular volume overload and may be associated with increased pulmonary blood flow in certain contexts. Congenital cyanotic heart diseases with decreased pulmonary blood flow are characterized by obstruction or underdevelopment of the right ventricular outflow tract or pulmonary arteries.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This classic cyanotic heart defect includes pulmonary stenosis, which obstructs blood flow from the right ventricle to the lungs, resulting in decreased pulmonary blood flow and cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> In this condition, there is no communication between the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery, resulting in severely decreased or absent pulmonary blood flow and profound cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>DORV with pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Double outlet right ventricle with pulmonary stenosis is a complex cyanotic lesion where both great arteries arise from the right ventricle, and pulmonary stenosis restricts pulmonary blood flow, leading to cyanosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cyanotic congenital heart diseases with decreased pulmonary blood flow are characterized by obstruction to blood flow entering the pulmonary circulation.</li>
<li>The classic tetrad of Tetralogy of Fallot (VSD, overriding aorta, right ventricular hypertrophy, pulmonary stenosis) makes it the most common cyanotic heart disease with decreased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li>Pulmonary atresia represents the extreme end of the spectrum of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pulmonary regurgitation is not a cyanotic lesion with decreased pulmonary blood flow; it''s a condition of backward blood flow that doesn''t restrict forward pulmonary circulation.</p>','<p>Decreased PBF: Tetralogy, Atresia, Stenosis (TAS)</p>','<p>Cyanotic heart diseases with decreased pulmonary blood flow typically present with tet spells, characterized by hypercyanotic episodes triggered by crying or feeding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> Classic cyanotic lesion with pulmonary stenosis causing decreased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> Complete obstruction of right ventricular outflow leading to severely decreased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>DORV with pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Complex cyanotic lesion with pulmonary stenosis restricting pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanotic","pulmonary-blood-flow","regurgitation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.665Z','2026-10-27T15:53:39.241Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he0003pqjlr5ov2szdc','mcq','In duodenal atresia which of these signs is not considered in diagnosis :','["Double bubble sign","Bilious vomiting","Abdominal distension","Prenatal polyhydramnios"]','Bilious vomiting','<p><strong>Why Bilious vomiting is correct:</strong> Bilious vomiting is not considered in the diagnosis of duodenal atresia because it is actually a sign of intestinal obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater, such as jejunal or ileal atresia. Duodenal atresia typically presents with non-bilious vomiting due to obstruction proximal to the ampulla, which means bile cannot enter the obstructed segment. The classic "double bubble" sign on abdominal X-ray represents the dilated stomach and duodenum proximal to the obstruction, while abdominal distension and prenatal polyhydramnios are associated findings due to impaired fetal swallowing and amniotic fluid circulation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Double bubble sign:</strong> This is pathognomonic for duodenal atresia and is a key diagnostic finding on plain abdominal X-ray.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal distension:</strong> This is a common clinical presentation in duodenal atresia due to the accumulation of proximal intestinal contents.</li>
<li><strong>Prenatal polyhydramnios:</strong> This occurs in approximately 50% of cases due to impaired fetal swallowing of amniotic fluid, making it an important associated finding.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in about 30% of cases.</li>
<li>The "double bubble" sign is diagnostic when seen in the appropriate clinical context.</li>
<li>Bilious vomiting in a newborn always indicates a more distal intestinal obstruction and requires urgent evaluation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Duodenal atresia presents with non-bilious vomiting, "double bubble" sign, and is associated with Down syndrome, while bilious vomiting indicates obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater.</p>','<p>Double bubble, no bile, Down''s profile</p>','<p>Bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency and should never be attributed to benign causes like gastroesophageal reflux.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Double bubble sign:</strong> This is pathognomonic for duodenal atresia and is a key diagnostic finding.</li>
<li><strong>Abdominal distension:</strong> This is a common clinical presentation in duodenal atresia due to proximal intestinal obstruction.</li>
<li><strong>Prenatal polyhydramnios:</strong> This occurs in about 50% of cases due to impaired fetal swallowing.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Duodenal Atresia','["duodenal atresia","diagnosis","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.849Z','2026-10-27T14:13:17.675Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he3003rqjlrasz294y4','mcq','Anemia is due to','["Hb deficiency"]','Hb deficiency','<p><strong>Why Hb deficiency is correct:</strong> Anemia is fundamentally defined as a deficiency of hemoglobin (Hb) or red blood cells, which impairs oxygen-carrying capacity. Hemoglobin is the iron-containing protein in red blood cells responsible for oxygen transport, and its deficiency directly reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood. This definition encompasses all types of anemia, whether due to decreased production (iron deficiency, B12 deficiency), increased destruction (hemolysis), or blood loss. The World Health Organization defines anemia based on hemoglobin concentration thresholds, making Hb deficiency the core diagnostic criterion.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While the question only provides one correct option, it''s important to note that anemia is not simply a deficiency of red blood cells but specifically a deficiency of hemoglobin within those cells. Red blood cell deficiency alone doesn''t capture the functional impairment, as conditions like polycythemia vera have increased red blood cells but not necessarily increased oxygen-carrying capacity per unit volume.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Anemia is defined by reduced hemoglobin concentration, not just red blood cell count.</li>
<li>Normal hemoglobin thresholds vary by age, sex, and altitude (e.g., adult males &lt;13.5 g/dL, adult females &lt;12.0 g/dL).</li>
<li>The classification of anemia by mean corpuscular volume (microcytic, normocytic, macrocytic) helps guide evaluation of the underlying cause.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Anemia is fundamentally a deficiency of hemoglobin, the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells, regardless of the underlying cause.</p>','<p>Anemia = Hb deficiency (Hb = Hemoglobin)</p>','<p>The reticulocyte count is crucial in differentiating between anemias of decreased production versus increased destruction or loss.</p>','','Easy','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","definition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.851Z','2026-10-27T14:13:17.679Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9i000go7lrli1bhp64','mcq','15-month old boy was brought to ER by gastroenteritis, his father told the pediatrician that this condition is recurrent and had been stated after replacement of mother''s milk by high calorie and low protein diet, what is the most likely diagnosis?','["Kwashiorkor.","Marasmus.","Marasmus Kwashiorkor.","Nutritional dwarfism."]','Kwashiorkor.','<p><strong>Why Kwashiorkor is correct:</strong> Kwashiorkor is the most likely diagnosis in this 15-month-old boy with recurrent gastroenteritis after switching from breast milk to a high-calorie, low-protein diet. This condition results from severe protein deficiency despite adequate caloric intake, leading to decreased synthesis of visceral proteins like albumin, which causes edema and impaired immune function. The timing post-weaning and the dietary description (high calories but low protein) are classic for kwashiorkor, as the child''s protein demands are high during rapid growth periods. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Marasmus:</strong> This results from inadequate calorie and protein intake, leading to severe wasting, but the child here has a high-calorie diet, making this less likely. - <strong>Marasmus Kwashiorkor:</strong> This mixed form would show features of both wasting and edema, but the dietary history specifically points to protein deficiency with adequate calories, favoring pure kwashiorkor. - <strong>Nutritional dwarfism:</strong> This refers to stunting due to chronic malnutrition but doesn''t specifically explain the edema and acute presentation with gastroenteritis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Kwashiorkor is characterized by edema, fatty liver, skin changes (dermatitis, depigmentation), and hair changes (brittle, reddish) despite adequate caloric intake. - It typically occurs in children 1-3 years old after weaning onto a protein-deficient diet. - The edema in kwashiorkor is due to hypoalbuminemia from reduced protein synthesis, not cardiac or renal failure. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Kwashiorkor presents with edema and other protein deficiency signs in a child with adequate calories but insufficient protein intake, typically after weaning.</p>','<p>Kwashiorkor: ''K'' for edema (K-edema) and ''K'' for kids post-weaning with protein deficiency.</p>','<p>Kwashiorkor can develop rapidly even in previously healthy children when protein intake drops below critical levels during periods of high demand like growth spurts or illness.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Marasmus:</strong> Results from inadequate calorie intake, not high-calorie/low-protein diet. - <strong>Marasmus Kwashiorkor:</strong> Would show wasting features not described in this case. - <strong>Nutritional dwarfism:</strong> Refers to stunting without the specific edema of kwashiorkor.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["kwashiorkor","diagnosis","diet"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.062Z','2026-10-27T14:15:31.860Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9i000fo7lroax0f89a','mcq','Which of the following signs is a feature of marasmus and not of Kwashiorkor?','["Good appetite.","Oedema.","Hypoalbuminemia.","Fatty change in liver."]','Good appetite.','<p><strong>Why Good appetite is correct:</strong> A good appetite is a classic feature of marasmus, where the child continues to seek food despite severe wasting, as the body''s energy demands drive hunger. In marasmus, there is a generalized deficiency of both calories and protein, leading to extreme emaciation but preservation of appetite as the body attempts to compensate for the energy deficit. This contrasts sharply with kwashiorkor, where the child often has a poor appetite due to metabolic derangements and liver dysfunction. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Oedema:</strong> This is characteristic of kwashiorkor due to hypoalbuminemia and is not typically seen in marasmus. - <strong>Hypoalbuminemia:</strong> This is a hallmark of kwashiorkor (from protein deficiency) and is not typically present in marasmus, where protein intake is relatively better compared to calories. - <strong>Fatty change in liver:</strong> This occurs in kwashiorkor due to impaired lipoprotein synthesis and fat accumulation, not in marasmus. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Marasmus presents with severe wasting, muscle atrophy, and a ''old man'' appearance but typically preserves appetite. - Kwashiorkor is characterized by edema, fatty liver, skin and hair changes, and often a poor appetite. - The presence of edema is the key clinical differentiator between kwashiorkor and marasmus. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Marasmus is distinguished from kwashiorkor by the preservation of appetite despite severe wasting, while kwashiorkor features edema and poor appetite.</p>','<p>Marasmus: ''M'' for maintained appetite despite wasting; Kwashiorkor: ''K'' for edema and ''K'' for poor appetite.</p>','<p>A child with marasmus will often eagerly consume offered food, while a child with kwashiorkor may refuse food despite apparent hunger, a crucial clinical distinction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Oedema:</strong> Characteristic of kwashiorkor, not marasmus. - <strong>Hypoalbuminemia:</strong> Present in kwashiorkor due to protein deficiency, not typically in marasmus. - <strong>Fatty change in liver:</strong> Occurs in kwashiorkor, not marasmus.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Protein Energy Malnutrition','["marasmus","kwashiorkor","appetite"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.062Z','2026-10-27T14:15:31.862Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krax001bo7lrokakjzv1','mcq','Which is true about Functional Constipation','["Common In preschool children","Associated with breastfeeding","Associated with organic cause","Passing of hard stool less than 2 times per day"]','Common In preschool children','<p><strong>Why Common In preschool children is correct:</strong> Functional constipation is most commonly seen in preschool children (ages 2-5 years) during the period of toilet training. This age group frequently develops constipation due to stool withholding behaviors, often initiated by painful defecation from an initial episode of constipation, creating a vicious cycle. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Associated with breastfeeding:</strong> Functional constipation is actually less common in breastfed infants compared to formula-fed infants, as breast milk stools are typically softer and more frequent. - <strong>Associated with organic cause:</strong> Functional constipation, by definition, has no identifiable organic cause; it''s a functional disorder rather than one stemming from an underlying disease process. - <strong>Passing of hard stool less than 2 times per day:</strong> While frequency can vary, the diagnostic criteria for functional constipation focus more on stool consistency, pain with defecation, and withholding behaviors rather than a specific frequency threshold. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Functional constipation is most prevalent during the preschool years when toilet training typically occurs. - The Rome IV criteria define functional constipation in children based on symptoms like at least two episodes of fecal incontinence per week, history of excessive stool retention, painful or hard bowel movements, and the presence of a large fecal mass in the rectum. - Stool withholding behavior is a hallmark of functional constipation in children, often initiated after an initial painful defecation experience. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation is most commonly seen in preschool children during the toilet training period due to behavioral factors like stool withholding.</p>','<p>FC: Preschool (Functional Constipation is common in Preschool children).</p>','<p>The ''retention-withholding cycle'' is central to functional constipation, where initial pain leads to stool retention, which causes larger and harder stools, resulting in more pain and further withholding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Associated with breastfeeding:</strong> Functional constipation is actually less common in breastfed infants. - <strong>Associated with organic cause:</strong> Functional constipation has no identifiable organic cause by definition. - <strong>Passing of hard stool less than 2 times per day:</strong> Diagnostic criteria focus more on consistency, pain, and withholding behaviors than specific frequency.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["functional","preschool"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.113Z','2026-10-27T14:16:49.943Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kr9p000ho7lrte6jw6e7','mcq','What is the first sign you expect to improve during her treatment?','["Mood","Oedema","Appetite","Muscle bulk"]','Oedema','<p><strong>Why Oedema is correct:</strong> Oedema is typically the first sign to improve during treatment of kwashiorkor because it results directly from hypoalbuminemia, which can be corrected relatively quickly with adequate protein intake. As soon as protein is provided, the liver can synthesize new albumin, and within 7-10 days, serum albumin levels begin to rise, leading to resolution of the edema. This rapid improvement contrasts with other signs like muscle bulk and mood, which take longer to recover as they require sustained nutritional rehabilitation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Mood:</strong> Improvement in mood and mental status occurs gradually as overall nutritional status improves, not as the first sign. - <strong>Appetite:</strong> While appetite typically improves with treatment, it often takes several days to normalize as metabolic derangements resolve. - <strong>Muscle bulk:</strong> Restoration of muscle mass requires weeks of consistent protein and calorie intake, making it one of the last signs to improve. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Oedema resolution is an early and reliable indicator of successful protein repletion in kwashiorkor treatment. - The initial phase of treatment focuses on correcting fluid and electrolyte imbalances before aggressive nutritional rehabilitation. - Monitoring weight gain and edema resolution helps guide the pace of nutritional refeeding to prevent refeeding syndrome. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In kwashiorkor treatment, edema resolves first due to rapid albumin synthesis, while muscle bulk and mood improvement lag behind.</p>','<p>Oedema first: ''O'' for early improvement in ''O''edema with protein therapy.</p>','<p>When treating kwashiorkor, start with small, frequent feedings to avoid refeeding syndrome, and monitor edema as an early marker of response.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> Improves gradually as overall nutrition improves, not first. - <strong>Appetite:</strong> Takes days to normalize as metabolic issues resolve. - <strong>Muscle bulk:</strong> Requires weeks of consistent nutrition to restore.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Treatment','["treatment","edema","kwashiorkor"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.069Z','2026-10-27T14:15:31.862Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krab000uo7lr1k280xsj','mcq','Which of the following signs are not present in child with rickets','["marfan sign","Hypotonia in abdominal muscles","Edema in lower limb","Tetany"]','Edema in lower limb','<p><strong>Why Edema in lower limb is correct:</strong> Edema in the lower limbs is not a typical sign of rickets. Rickets is a disorder of impaired mineralization of growing bone due to deficiency of vitamin D, calcium, or phosphate, leading to skeletal deformities. The classic signs of rickets include bone pain, growth retardation, skeletal deformities (like bowing of legs), and specific physical findings such as Marfan sign (positive when the child''s wrist can be easily hyperextended), hypotonia in abdominal muscles (due to hypocalcemia affecting muscle function), and tetany (due to severe hypocalcemia causing neuromuscular irritability). Edema, however, is associated with conditions like protein malnutrition, heart failure, or nephrotic syndrome, not directly with rickets.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Marfan sign:</strong> This is a positive sign in rickets where the child''s wrist can be hyperextended beyond 90 degrees, indicating hypotonia and ligamentous laxity due to vitamin D deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Hypotonia in abdominal muscles:</strong> This occurs in rickets due to hypocalcemia affecting muscle contractility and neuromuscular function.</li>
<li><strong>Tetany:</strong> This is a classic manifestation of severe hypocalcemia, which can occur in rickets due to impaired calcium absorption from the gut.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Rickets is characterized by impaired mineralization of growth plates in children before epiphyseal fusion.</li>
<li>Classic signs include bone pain, growth retardation, skeletal deformities, and specific physical findings like Marfan sign and hypotonia.</li>
<li>Tetany can occur in severe cases due to hypocalcemia, while edema is not a typical feature.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rickets presents with bone deformities, hypotonia, and tetany due to vitamin D deficiency, but not with lower limb edema, which is more characteristic of other conditions like protein malnutrition.</p>','<p>Remember the ''3 H''s of rickets: Hypotonia, Hyperextendable wrists (Marfan sign), and Hypocalcemia (tetany).</p>','<p>Rickets should be suspected in children with bone pain, growth failure, and skeletal deformities, especially in those with limited sun exposure or poor dietary intake of vitamin D.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Marfan sign:</strong> This is a positive physical finding in rickets indicating hypotonia.</li>
<li><strong>Hypotonia in abdominal muscles:</strong> Occurs in rickets due to hypocalcemia affecting muscle function.</li>
<li><strong>Tetany:</strong> A classic manifestation of severe hypocalcemia in rickets.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","pediatrics","bone-disorder"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.091Z','2026-10-27T14:15:48.321Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kraf000wo7lry3wfzt6b','mcq','Which of the following best defines rickets?','["Impaired mineralization of mature cortical bone after epiphyseal fusion.","A disorder of impaired mineralization of growing bone and cartilage before epiphyseal fusion.","An autoimmune destruction of osteoblasts at the metaphyseal plate.","Excess deposition of hydroxyapatite crystals in the growth plate."]','A disorder of impaired mineralization of growing bone and cartilage before epiphyseal fusion.','<p><strong>Why A disorder of impaired mineralization of growing bone and cartilage before epiphyseal fusion is correct:</strong> This definition accurately captures the essence of rickets. Rickets is specifically a disorder that affects the growth plates (epiphyseal plates) in children before these plates fuse during adolescence. The fundamental problem is impaired mineralization of the osteoid (bone matrix) in areas of active bone growth, particularly at the metaphyses of long bones and in cartilage. This impairment is primarily due to deficiency or dysfunction of vitamin D, calcium, or phosphate, leading to defective mineralization while the organic matrix of bone continues to be produced by osteoblasts.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Impaired mineralization of mature cortical bone after epiphyseal fusion:</strong> This describes osteomalacia, which is the adult equivalent of rickets occurring after epiphyseal fusion when bone growth has ceased.</li>
<li><strong>An autoimmune destruction of osteoblasts at the metaphyseal plate:</strong> This is not the mechanism of rickets, which is primarily a metabolic disorder rather than an autoimmune condition.</li>
<li><strong>Excess deposition of hydroxyapatite crystals in the growth plate:</strong> This is incorrect as rickets involves deficient mineralization, not excess deposition. Hydroxyapatite is the mineral component of bone that is inadequately deposited in rickets.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Rickets specifically affects growing bones in children before epiphyseal fusion, while osteomalacia affects mature bone in adults.</li>
<li>The primary defect in rickets is impaired mineralization of osteoid matrix, not destruction of bone cells.</li>
<li>Vitamin D deficiency is the most common cause, but genetic defects in vitamin D metabolism or renal tubular disorders can also lead to rickets.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rickets is defined as impaired mineralization of growing bone and cartilage before epiphyseal fusion, distinguishing it from osteomalacia which affects mature bone after fusion.</p>','<p>Remember ''Rickets affects Kids'' - it''s a disorder of growing bones before epiphyseal fusion.</p>','<p>The distinction between rickets (children, growing bones) and osteomalacia (adults, mature bone) is crucial for understanding the pathophysiology and clinical presentation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Impaired mineralization of mature cortical bone after epiphyseal fusion:</strong> This describes osteomalacia, not rickets.</li>
<li><strong>An autoimmune destruction of osteoblasts at the metaphyseal plate:</strong> Rickets is metabolic, not autoimmune.</li>
<li><strong>Excess deposition of hydroxyapatite crystals in the growth plate:</strong> Rickets involves deficient mineralization, not excess.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","definition","bone-disorder"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.095Z','2026-10-27T14:15:48.328Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kras0019o7lr6xgugspg','mcq','In BFIH hospital, the mother was discharged without post-charge feeding support. Which step is violated?','["Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups","On-demand feeding","Exclusively breastfeeding","Avoid teats and pacifiers"]','Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups','<p><strong>Why Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups is correct:</strong> The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFIH) Ten Steps to Successful Breastfeeding specifically includes Step 7: ''Give no artificial teats or pacifiers (also called dummies or soothers) to breastfeeding infants'' and Step 10: ''Foster the establishment of breastfeeding support groups and refer mothers to them on discharge from hospital or clinic.'' Discharging a mother without providing post-discharge breastfeeding support groups violates this critical step, as ongoing support is essential for breastfeeding success beyond the hospital stay. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>On-demand feeding:</strong> This is Step 6 of the BFIH guidelines, which states ''Give newborn infants no food or drink other than breast milk, unless medically indicated,'' and encourages feeding on demand, but this is not violated in this scenario. - <strong>Exclusively breastfeeding:</strong> This is Step 4 of the BFIH guidelines, which states ''Initiate breastfeeding within the first hour of life,'' but the violation is specifically about post-discharge support, not exclusivity. - <strong>Avoid teats and pacifiers:</strong> This is Step 7 of the BFIH guidelines, which prohibits artificial teats and pacifiers, but the question specifically mentions a violation related to post-charge feeding support, not the use of artificial nipples. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The BFIH has 10 evidence-based steps to promote successful breastfeeding. - Step 10 specifically requires hospitals to establish breastfeeding support groups and ensure mothers are referred to them before discharge. - Post-discharge support is crucial as breastfeeding challenges often arise after mothers leave the hospital setting. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative requires hospitals to establish breastfeeding support groups and refer mothers to them before discharge, making this the violated step in this scenario.</p>','<p>BFIH Step 10: ''Support groups before you go'' (refers to referring mothers to support groups before discharge).</p>','<p>Post-discharge breastfeeding support is critical as 60% of breastfeeding mothers stop breastfeeding before their intended time due to lack of support.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>On-demand feeding:</strong> This is Step 6 of BFIH guidelines and is not violated in this scenario. - <strong>Exclusively breastfeeding:</strong> This is Step 4 of BFIH guidelines and is not the violated step mentioned. - <strong>Avoid teats and pacifiers:</strong> This is Step 7 of BFIH guidelines, but the question specifically addresses post-discharge support.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Gastrointestinal','Breastfeeding','["hospital","breastfeeding","support"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.108Z','2026-10-27T14:16:49.941Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7kraw001ao7lrg38a8exj','mcq','Functional constipation is presented in children about;','["60-70 %","50-60 %","90-95 %"]','90-95 %','<p><strong>Why 90-95% is correct:</strong> Functional constipation represents the vast majority of constipation cases in children, accounting for approximately 90-95% of all cases. This high prevalence is because functional constipation is primarily related to behavioral factors, stool withholding due to painful defecation, and delayed toilet training, rather than underlying organic pathology. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>60-70%:</strong> This percentage is too low and would underestimate the prevalence of functional constipation, which is actually much more common than organic causes. - <strong>50-60%:</strong> This percentage is significantly too low and doesn''t reflect the overwhelming majority of pediatric constipation cases that are functional rather than organic. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Functional constipation is by far the most common type of constipation in children. - Organic causes of constipation (like Hirschsprung disease, hypothyroidism, or spinal cord abnormalities) account for only 5-10% of cases. - The high prevalence of functional constipation underscores the importance of behavioral and dietary management in pediatric practice. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation accounts for the vast majority (90-95%) of childhood constipation cases, with organic causes being relatively rare.</p>','<p>FC: 95% (Functional Constipation is 95% of cases).</p>','<p>When evaluating a child with constipation, always consider functional causes first as they represent over 90% of cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>60-70%:</strong> This underestimates the true prevalence of functional constipation. - <strong>50-60%:</strong> This significantly underestimates the true prevalence of functional constipation.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["functional","constipation","prevalence"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.112Z','2026-10-27T14:16:49.943Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuao00aue9oir06u0o63','mcq','NSAIDS used in JIA, Which is not a side effect','["Gall stone","Hypersensitivity","Peptic ulcer","Nausea"]','Gall stone','<p><strong>Why Gall stone is correct:</strong> NSAIDs (Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs) work by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, which reduces prostaglandin synthesis. Prostaglandins play a protective role in the gastrointestinal tract by maintaining mucosal integrity and promoting mucus secretion. However, they do not significantly influence gallbladder function or bile composition. The primary side effects of NSAIDs are related to COX inhibition in the GI tract (peptic ulcers), skin (hypersensitivity reactions), and central nervous system (nausea). Gallstones form due to imbalances in bile components (cholesterol, bilirubin, bile salts) or gallbladder stasis, not prostaglandin inhibition.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> NSAIDs can cause hypersensitivity reactions including skin rashes, angioedema, and bronchospasm, particularly in patients with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease.</li>
<li><strong>Peptic ulcer:</strong> By inhibiting protective prostaglandins in the gastric mucosa, NSAIDs reduce mucus and bicarbonate secretion, increasing acid-induced damage and ulcer risk.</li>
<li><strong>Nausea:</strong> NSAIDs can directly irritate the gastric mucosa and stimulate chemoreceptors in the vomiting center, causing nausea as a common side effect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>NSAIDs inhibit COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes, reducing prostaglandin synthesis which leads to their therapeutic effects and side effects.</li>
<li>The most serious NSAID side effects are gastrointestinal ulcers, bleeding, and renal toxicity, which occur due to COX-1 inhibition.</li>
<li>Gallstones are not associated with NSAID use, as prostaglandins do not significantly regulate bile composition or gallbladder motility.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> NSAIDs cause peptic ulcers by inhibiting protective prostaglandins, but gallstones form from bile imbalances unrelated to prostaglandin inhibition.</p>','','<p>NSAID-induced ulcers are more common in the elderly, those with prior ulcer history, and when used with corticosteroids or anticoagulants.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> NSAIDs commonly cause hypersensitivity reactions including rashes and bronchospasm.</li>
<li><strong>Peptic ulcer:</strong> NSAIDs increase ulcer risk by inhibiting protective gastric prostaglandins.</li>
<li><strong>Nausea:</strong> NSAIDs frequently cause nausea through direct gastric irritation and central effects.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Rheumatology','JIA Medications','["jia","nsaids","side effects","gallstone"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.808Z','2026-10-27T16:22:31.501Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbm0021o7lr0c6eqjtt','mcq','An 11-months-old boy was brought to the emergency department with an acute onset of vomiting and diarrhea. On examination he was dehydrated. The patient received deficit fluid and maintenance. On the 2nd day the diarrhea stopped suddenly and abdominal pain developed with currant jelly stools. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Functional intestinal obstruction","Intussusception","Rota virus gastroenteritis","Giardiasis","Amebic dysentery"]','Intussusception','<p><strong>Why Intussusception is correct:</strong> Intussusception is the most likely diagnosis in this 11-month-old boy with acute vomiting and diarrhea followed by sudden cessation of diarrhea, development of abdominal pain, and currant jelly stools. This condition occurs when a segment of intestine telescopes into an adjacent segment, causing obstruction and compromising blood flow. The classic triad of symptoms includes intermittent colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stools (a mixture of blood and mucus). The sudden cessation of diarrhea occurs because the intussusception obstructs the bowel lumen, preventing stool passage. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Functional intestinal obstruction:</strong> While it can cause vomiting and abdominal pain, it doesn''t typically present with currant jelly stools or follow a viral gastroenteritis. - <strong>Rota virus gastroenteritis:</strong> This commonly causes vomiting and diarrhea in infants but doesn''t typically present with abdominal pain or currant jelly stools. - <strong>Giardiasis:</strong> This parasitic infection causes diarrhea but not the sudden cessation of diarrhea or currant jelly stools. - <strong>Amebic dysentery:</strong> This causes bloody diarrhea but not the classic intussusception triad or sudden symptom change. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Intussusception most commonly occurs in infants aged 6-36 months, with peak incidence at 6-12 months. - The classic triad (intermittent pain, vomiting, currant jelly stools) is only present in about one-third of cases. - Ultrasound showing a target sign or pseudokidney appearance is diagnostic. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Currant jelly stools in an infant following acute gastroenteritis should immediately raise suspicion for intussusception.</p>','<p>Pain, Vomit, Jelly = Intussusception</p>','<p>Intussusception is a surgical emergency that can lead to bowel necrosis and perforation if not promptly reduced.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Functional intestinal obstruction:</strong> Doesn''t typically present with currant jelly stools. - <strong>Rota virus gastroenteritis:</strong> Causes vomiting and diarrhea but not currant jelly stools. - <strong>Giardiasis:</strong> Causes diarrhea but not the sudden cessation or currant jelly stools. - <strong>Amebic dysentery:</strong> Causes bloody diarrhea but not the classic intussusception triad.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["intussusception","currant jelly","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.138Z','2026-10-27T14:17:40.568Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbm0020o7lrf7t68548','mcq','A 2-year-old boy presents with a 1-week history of painless rectal bleeding. Physical examination is unremarkable. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Rectal examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?','["Intussusception","Meckels Diverticulum","Anal fissure","Esophageal varices","Peptic Ulcer"]','Anal fissure','<p><strong>Why Anal fissure is correct:</strong> Anal fissure is the most probable diagnosis in this 2-year-old boy with painless rectal bleeding and unremarkable physical examination. Anal fissures are small tears in the anoderm that commonly occur in young children, often associated with constipation. They typically present with bright red blood on the surface of stool or toilet paper, which may appear painless to parents even though the child may experience pain during defecation. The unremarkable rectal examination is consistent with a superficial fissure that doesn''t require digital examination for diagnosis. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> This would typically present with abdominal pain, vomiting, and possibly a palpable abdominal mass, not just painless rectal bleeding. - <strong>Meckels Diverticulum:</strong> While it can cause painless rectal bleeding, it''s less common than anal fissures in this age group and may present with a characteristic painless bleeding episode. - <strong>Esophageal varices:</strong> These are extremely rare in 2-year-olds and would typically be associated with known liver disease. - <strong>Peptic Ulcer:</strong> Uncommon in toddlers and would typically present with epigastric pain rather than isolated rectal bleeding. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Anal fissures are the most common cause of rectal bleeding in infants and young children. - They are typically located posteriorly in the midline and are associated with constipation. - Conservative management with stool softeners and topical anesthetics is usually effective. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Painless rectal bleeding in a toddler with no other symptoms is most likely an anal fissure.</p>','<p>Bright red blood on stool = Anal fissure (most common in kids)</p>','<p>Anal fissures in children are often associated with constipation and can be managed conservatively without invasive procedures.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Intussusception:</strong> Would typically present with abdominal pain and vomiting. - <strong>Meckels Diverticulum:</strong> Less common than anal fissures in this age group. - <strong>Esophageal varices:</strong> Extremely rare in 2-year-olds without liver disease. - <strong>Peptic Ulcer:</strong> Uncommon in toddlers and would typically present with epigastric pain.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Rectal bleeding','["rectal bleeding","pediatrics","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.138Z','2026-10-27T14:17:40.570Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbn0022o7lrylpi9pt9','mcq','Red, jelly stool in infants is characteristic of:','["Gastroenteritis","Anal fissure","Intussusception","Rectal piles"]','Intussusception','<p><strong>Why Intussusception is correct:</strong> Red, jelly-like stool in infants is characteristic of intussusception, a condition where one segment of intestine telescopes into an adjacent segment. This telescoping causes obstruction and compromises blood flow to the affected bowel segment, leading to ischemia and subsequent sloughing of mucosa. The mixture of blood and mucus in the stool gives it the characteristic currant jelly appearance. This finding is pathognomonic for intussusception when it occurs in an infant with acute symptoms. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> While gastroenteritis can cause diarrhea, it doesn''t typically produce the distinctive red, jelly-like stool pattern seen in intussusception. - <strong>Anal fissure:</strong> This causes bright red blood on the surface of stool or toilet paper, not a jelly-like appearance mixed throughout the stool. - <strong>Rectal piles (hemorrhoids):</strong> Extremely rare in infants and would present with bright red blood, not jelly-like stools. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The classic triad of intussusception includes intermittent abdominal pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stools. - Intussusception most commonly occurs in infants aged 6-36 months, with peak incidence at 6-12 months. - Ultrasound showing a target sign or pseudokidney appearance is the diagnostic test of choice. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Red, jelly-like stool in infants is pathognomonic for intussusception.</p>','<p>Jelly stool = Intussusception</p>','<p>Intussusception is a surgical emergency that can lead to bowel necrosis and perforation if not promptly reduced.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gastroenteritis:</strong> Causes diarrhea but not the distinctive red, jelly-like stool pattern. - <strong>Anal fissure:</strong> Causes bright red blood on stool surface, not jelly-like appearance throughout. - <strong>Rectal piles:</strong> Extremely rare in infants and presents with bright red blood, not jelly-like stools.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Intussusception','["stool","currant jelly","intussusception","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.139Z','2026-10-27T14:17:40.571Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krce002uo7lrj8ecvj0r','mcq','20- A 5-year-old boy presented with short stature, epistaxis, and recurrent respiratory tract infections. On examination, he was pale, there was skin hyperpigmentation, microphthalmia, absent radius and thumb and purpuric rash all over the body. Which of the following would be the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Fanconi anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Blackfan-Diamond anemia","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura"]','Fanconi anemia','<p><strong>Why Fanconi anemia is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by defective DNA repair mechanisms, specifically involving the Fanconi anemia pathway. This genetic defect leads to chromosomal instability, resulting in the classic triad of bone marrow failure (causing pancytopenia and pallor), congenital anomalies (such as microcephaly, microphthalmia, absent radius/thumb, and skin hyperpigmentation), and increased cancer predisposition. The epistaxis and recurrent infections are direct consequences of thrombocytopenia and neutropenia, respectively, while the purpuric rash reflects the underlying thrombocytopenia and vascular fragility.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia:</strong> While this can present with pallor, infections, and bleeding, it typically doesn''t include the specific congenital anomalies like absent radius/thumb or microphthalmia, and would show blast cells on peripheral smear.</li>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> This is caused by B12 or folate deficiency and presents with macrocytic anemia, but not with the congenital anomalies or bone marrow failure seen in Fanconi anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Blackfan-Diamond anemia:</strong> This is a pure red cell aplasia that presents in infancy with macrocytic anemia without the congenital anomalies or pancytopenia characteristic of Fanconi anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura:</strong> This is an autoimmune disorder causing isolated thrombocytopenia and purpura, but doesn''t present with the congenital anomalies, bone marrow failure, or other cytopenias seen in this case.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fanconi anemia classically presents with the triad of bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies (especially radial ray defects), and hyperpigmentation.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by chromosome breakage testing after exposure to DNA cross-linking agents like diepoxybutane.</li>
<li>Patients have extremely high risk of developing malignancies, particularly acute myeloid leukemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is the only diagnosis that combines bone marrow failure, radial ray defects, and hyperpigmentation in a child.</p>','<p>RAD for Fanconi: Radial defects, Anemia, Dysmorphic features</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia should be suspected in any child with unexplained pancytopenia and congenital anomalies, as early diagnosis is crucial for monitoring and managing complications.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia:</strong> Lacks the specific congenital anomalies like absent radius/thumb.</li>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Caused by B12/folate deficiency, not associated with congenital anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>Blackfan-Diamond anemia:</strong> Pure red cell aplasia without congenital anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura:</strong> Isolated thrombocytopenia without other cytopenias or congenital defects.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Hematology','Anemias','["fanconi","anemia","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.166Z','2026-10-27T14:18:38.181Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcf002vo7lr8epox5m5','mcq','male patient 5 years old presented with pancytopenia, café-au-lait spots, short stature and absent thumb. The most likely diagnosis is?','["Fanconi anemia","Dyskeratosis congenita","Diamond- Blackfan","Acquired Pancytopenia"]','Fanconi anemia','<p><strong>Why Fanconi anemia is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is the most likely diagnosis given the combination of pancytopenia, café-au-lait spots, short stature, and absent thumb. This autosomal recessive disorder involves defective DNA repair mechanisms leading to chromosomal instability, which manifests as bone marrow failure (causing pancytopenia), congenital anomalies (including radial ray defects like absent thumb), and characteristic pigmentation changes. The café-au-lait spots are a common but not universal finding in Fanconi anemia, while short stature results from growth hormone deficiency or skeletal anomalies.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenita:</strong> While this also presents with pancytopenia and café-au-lait spots, it classically includes the triad of abnormal skin pigmentation (reticulated hyperpigmentation), nail dystrophy, and oral leukoplakia, which are not mentioned here.</li>
<li><strong>Diamond-Blackfan anemia:</strong> This is a pure red cell aplasia that typically presents in infancy with macrocytic anemia without the congenital anomalies or café-au-lait spots seen in this case.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired Pancytopenia:</strong> This could result from various causes like infections, drugs, or autoimmune disorders, but wouldn''t explain the congenital anomalies like absent thumb.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fanconi anemia is characterized by bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies (especially radial ray defects), and increased cancer risk.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by chromosome breakage testing after exposure to DNA cross-linking agents.</li>
<li>Café-au-lait spots can occur in Fanconi anemia but are more characteristic of neurofibromatosis type 1.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The combination of pancytopenia, café-au-lait spots, and absent thumb is pathognomonic for Fanconi anemia.</p>','<p>CAF for Fanconi: Cytopenia, Anomalies, Familial inheritance</p>','<p>In any child with pancytopenia and congenital anomalies, consider Fanconi anemia and perform chromosome breakage testing for confirmation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Dyskeratosis congenita:</strong> Lacks the classic triad of skin pigmentation, nail dystrophy, and oral leukoplakia.</li>
<li><strong>Diamond-Blackfan anemia:</strong> Pure red cell aplasia without congenital anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired Pancytopenia:</strong> Doesn''t explain the congenital anomalies present.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Bone Marrow Failure','["pancytopenia","cafe-au-lait","fanconi"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.167Z','2026-10-27T14:18:38.184Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krd3003so7lrefwxyp4n','mcq','Which of the following is a common pattern of inheritance in hereditary spherocytosis','["Autosomal dominant disease","Autosomal recessive disease","X-linked dominant disease","X-linked recessive disease"]','Autosomal dominant disease','<p><strong>Why Autosomal dominant disease is correct:</strong> Hereditary spherocytosis (HS) is most commonly inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, accounting for approximately 75% of cases. This pattern occurs when a mutation in one copy of the genes encoding red blood cell membrane proteins (spectrin, ankyrin, band 3, or protein 4.2) is sufficient to cause disease. The defective membrane leads to loss of surface area-to-volume ratio, forming spherocytes that are sequestered and destroyed in the spleen. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Autosomal recessive disease:</strong> While some rare forms of HS can be autosomal recessive, this accounts for only about 20% of cases and is less common than the dominant form. - <strong>X-linked dominant disease:</strong> HS is not X-linked dominant, as it affects males and females equally and is not passed from affected fathers to sons. - <strong>X-linked recessive disease:</strong> HS is not X-linked recessive, as it would primarily affect males and be passed by carrier females, which doesn''t match the typical inheritance pattern. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Most cases of hereditary spherocytosis (75%) follow autosomal dominant inheritance. - The classic triad of HS is anemia, jaundice, and splenomegaly, with spherocytes on peripheral smear. - Autosomal recessive forms are less common and typically more severe, often presenting in infancy. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hereditary spherocytosis is predominantly autosomal dominant due to mutations in red blood cell membrane proteins.</p>','<p>HS inheritance: ''AD for Spherocytosis'' - Autosomal Dominant is the most common pattern.</p>','<p>In hereditary spherocytosis, splenectomy is curative for the hemolytic component but should be deferred until after age 5 to avoid overwhelming postsplenectomy infection.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal recessive disease:</strong> Accounts for only about 20% of HS cases and is less common. - <strong>X-linked dominant disease:</strong> HS affects males and females equally and isn''t passed from father to son. - <strong>X-linked recessive disease:</strong> HS would primarily affect males, which doesn''t match the typical inheritance pattern.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hereditary Disorders','["spherocytosis","inheritance","genetics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.191Z','2026-10-27T14:22:40.818Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcf002wo7lravp841b1','mcq','38- Which of the following cause acute anemia','["Iron deficiency anemia","Hemorrhage","Lead poisoning","Vit. B12 deficiency"]','Hemorrhage','<p><strong>Why Hemorrhage is correct:</strong> Hemorrhage causes acute anemia through the rapid loss of red blood cells from the vascular compartment, leading to an immediate drop in hemoglobin concentration. This is an acute process that can occur from trauma, surgery, gastrointestinal bleeding, or other sources of blood loss. Unlike the other options, hemorrhage directly reduces the total red cell mass quickly, resulting in acute symptoms of anemia such as weakness, dizziness, tachycardia, and hypotension.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options would be wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> This is a chronic process that develops over weeks to months as iron stores are depleted and hemoglobin production gradually decreases. It does not cause acute anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Lead poisoning:</strong> Lead causes anemia through multiple mechanisms including inhibition of heme synthesis and shortened red cell survival, but this is a subacute or chronic process rather than acute.</li>
<li><strong>Vit. B12 deficiency:</strong> This results in megaloblastic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis, but it develops gradually over months as vitamin B12 stores are depleted, making it a chronic rather than acute process.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acute anemia is most commonly caused by hemorrhage, which leads to rapid depletion of red cell mass.</li>
<li>Chronic anemias develop gradually over time and include iron deficiency, B12 deficiency, and lead poisoning.</li>
<li>The presence of symptoms like hypotension, tachycardia, and shock suggests acute blood loss rather than chronic anemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Among the options, only hemorrhage can cause acute anemia through rapid blood loss.</p>','<p>H for Hemorrhage = Hasty (acute) anemia</p>','<p>Acute anemia from hemorrhage requires immediate intervention to restore circulating volume and oxygen-carrying capacity, while chronic anemias allow for time to identify and address the underlying cause.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Develops gradually over weeks to months.</li>
<li><strong>Lead poisoning:</strong> Causes subacute or chronic anemia through multiple mechanisms.</li>
<li><strong>Vit. B12 deficiency:</strong> Results in gradual development of megaloblastic anemia over months.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","acute","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.167Z','2026-10-27T14:18:38.185Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krch002zo7lrl7u7d5er','mcq','41- Which of the following cause microcytic hypochromic anemia?','["Aplastic pancytopenia","Thalassemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Congenital spherocytosis"]','Thalassemia','<p><strong>Why Thalassemia is correct:</strong> Thalassemia causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to defective synthesis of globin chains, either alpha or beta, which are essential components of hemoglobin. This imbalance leads to ineffective erythropoiesis, hemolysis of red blood cells, and the production of small (microcytic), pale (hypochromic) red blood cells. The bone marrow attempts to compensate by increasing erythropoiesis, but the defective hemoglobin production results in characteristic findings including target cells, basophilic stippling, and anisocytosis on peripheral smear.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aplastic pancytopenia:</strong> Aplastic pancytopenia causes pancytopenia with normocytic normochromic red blood cells, as it results from bone marrow failure without specific defects in hemoglobin synthesis.</li>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Megaloblastic anemia causes macrocytic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis from B12/folate deficiency, not microcytic cells.</li>
<li><strong>Congenital spherocytosis:</strong> Congenital spherocytosis causes hemolytic anemia with spherocytes on smear, but the red blood cells are typically normocytic, not microcytic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Thalassemia is characterized by microcytic hypochromic red blood cells with elevated RBC count relative to hemoglobin concentration.</li>
<li>Beta-thalassemia major (Cooley''s anemia) presents in childhood with severe anemia, growth retardation, and bone deformities.</li>
<li>Alpha-thalassemia silent carrier state may have no anemia but can be identified by microcytosis and normal iron studies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Thalassemia is the classic cause of microcytic hypochromic anemia due to defective globin chain synthesis.</p>','<p>Thalassemia = Tiny Cells (microcytic)</p>','<p>In thalassemia, the Mentzer index (MCV/RBC count) is typically &lt;13, helping distinguish it from iron deficiency where it''s &gt;13.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aplastic pancytopenia:</strong> Causes normocytic normochromic cells due to bone marrow failure.</li>
<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia:</strong> Causes macrocytic anemia from B12/folate deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Congenital spherocytosis:</strong> Causes hemolytic anemia with normocytic spherocytes.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","microcytic","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.169Z','2026-10-27T14:20:51.924Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krch002yo7lrsghh3wgi','mcq','4 0- Which of the following cause normocytic normochromic anemia?','["Thalassemia","Chronic renal failure","Iron deficiency anemia","Folic acid deficiency"]','Chronic renal failure','<p><strong>Why Chronic renal failure is correct:</strong> Chronic renal failure causes normocytic normochromic anemia primarily due to decreased production of erythropoietin (EPO) by the damaged kidneys. EPO is essential for stimulating red blood cell production in the bone marrow, and its deficiency leads to reduced erythropoiesis without affecting the size or hemoglobin content of the red blood cells. Additionally, uremia can shorten red blood cell lifespan and contribute to anemia through other mechanisms like blood loss from frequent procedures or aluminum toxicity from phosphate binders.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Thalassemia causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to defective globin chain synthesis, not normocytic cells.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Iron deficiency anemia causes microcytic hypochromic anemia due to insufficient iron for hemoglobin synthesis.</li>
<li><strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> Folic acid deficiency causes macrocytic anemia due to impaired DNA synthesis, similar to B12 deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Anemia of chronic disease (including CKD) is normocytic normochromic and typically mild to moderate (hemoglobin 10-12 g/dL).</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by low serum EPO levels inappropriately low for the degree of anemia.</li>
<li>Treatment involves erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron replacement if functional iron deficiency is present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Chronic renal failure causes normocytic normochromic anemia primarily due to erythropoietin deficiency.</p>','<p>CKD = Normal Cells (normocytic) from EPO deficiency</p>','<p>In CKD patients, anemia typically develops when GFR falls below 30 mL/min and worsens as kidney function declines.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia from globin chain defects.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia from insufficient iron.</li>
<li><strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> Causes macrocytic anemia from impaired DNA synthesis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","normocytic","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.169Z','2026-10-27T14:20:51.925Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcs003co7lrb4yhwelg','mcq','6- Hydroxyurea cause','["Increase in Hb F"]','Increase in Hb F','<p><strong>Why Increase in Hb F is correct:</strong> Hydroxyurea is a ribonucleotide reductase inhibitor that increases fetal hemoglobin (Hb F) production by stimulating gamma-globin gene expression in erythroid precursors. This occurs through activation of soluble guanylate cyclase, leading to increased cyclic GMP production and subsequent activation of gamma-globin gene transcription. The increased Hb F helps to inhibit sickling in sickle cell disease by reducing the polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin S. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided:</strong> Since only one option was given and it is correct, there are no incorrect options to analyze. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hydroxyurea is the only FDA-approved disease-modifying therapy for sickle cell disease. - The increase in Hb F correlates with reduced vaso-occlusive crises and hospitalizations. - Response to hydroxyurea typically takes 3-6 months to become evident. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hydroxyurea works by increasing fetal hemoglobin (Hb F) production, which inhibits sickling in sickle cell disease.</p>','<p>HbF = Hydroxyurea''s Friend</p>','<p>Hydroxyurea''s ability to increase Hb F makes it particularly valuable in sickle cell disease, as Hb F interferes with hemoglobin S polymerization.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Sickle Cell Anemia','["hydroxyurea","sickle cell","hemoglobin f"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.180Z','2026-10-27T14:21:50.213Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriud900f4e9oi4ykp48yk','mcq','What is the most common cause of mortality in preterm neonates?','["RDS (Respiratory distress syndrome)","IVH (Intraventricular hemorrhage)","NEC (Necrotizing enterocolitis)","PDA (Patent ductus arteriosus)"]','RDS (Respiratory distress syndrome)','','','','','Basic','Respiratory','Neonatal Mortality','["mortality","preterm","rds"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.901Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krct003do7lreyw8y738','mcq','7- Rapid spleen enlargement called','["Splenic sequestration"]','Splenic sequestration','<p><strong>Why Splenic sequestration is correct:</strong> Splenic sequestration is a medical emergency characterized by the rapid enlargement of the spleen due to the trapping of a significant portion of the body''s red blood cells within the splenic sinusoids. This results in a rapid drop in hemoglobin levels, hypovolemia, and can lead to shock if not promptly treated with volume resuscitation and possible transfusion. It is most commonly seen in children with sickle cell disease but can occur in other conditions like hereditary spherocytosis. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided:</strong> Since only one option was given and it is correct, there are no incorrect options to analyze. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Splenic sequestration is a medical emergency requiring immediate intervention. - In sickle cell disease, it typically occurs in children before the spleen has autoinfarcted. - The diagnosis is made by finding a rapidly falling hemoglobin level with an enlarged spleen. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Splenic sequestration is a life-threatening condition characterized by rapid spleen enlargement and acute drop in hemoglobin due to blood trapping in the spleen.</p>','<p>Rapid Spleen = Sequestration</p>','<p>In sickle cell disease, splenic sequestration often occurs in children under age 2 before the spleen has undergone autoinfarction from repeated vaso-occlusive events.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Spleen','["spleen","enlargement","sequestration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.181Z','2026-10-27T14:21:50.214Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcu003fo7lrgmff4n4r','mcq','9- What is the pattern of inheritance of G6PD enzyme deficiency?','["Autosomal dominant.","X-linked recessive.","Autosomal recessive.","Mitochondrial"]','X-linked recessive.','<p><strong>Why X-linked recessive is correct:</strong> G6PD deficiency is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern because the G6PD gene is located on the X chromosome at band Xq28. Males (who have only one X chromosome) are primarily affected when their single X chromosome carries the mutation, while females (with two X chromosomes) are typically carriers or have variable expression due to X-chromosome inactivation. This inheritance pattern explains why the condition is much more common in males than females. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Autosomal dominant:</strong> Incorrect because G6PD deficiency does not follow an autosomal dominant pattern where only one copy of the mutated gene is needed to express the trait. - <strong>Autosomal recessive:</strong> Incorrect because while females can be affected if both X chromosomes carry the mutation, the primary inheritance pattern is X-linked, not autosomal. - <strong>Mitochondrial:</strong> Incorrect because G6PD deficiency is not inherited through mitochondrial DNA, which is passed exclusively from mother to all offspring. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - G6PD deficiency is the most common human enzyme deficiency, affecting over 400 million people worldwide. - The condition is associated with hemolytic crises triggered by oxidant stressors like certain drugs (sulfonamides, antimalarials), fava beans, and infections. - Diagnosis is confirmed by measuring G6PD enzyme activity, which may be falsely normal during acute hemolysis due to reticulocytosis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> G6PD deficiency follows an X-linked recessive inheritance pattern, explaining its predominance in males.</p>','<p>G6PD = Guys (X-linked) Primarily Diseased</p>','<p>G6PD deficiency is a classic example of how X-linked recessive disorders predominantly affect males, while females are typically unaffected carriers or have variable expression.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal dominant:</strong> Incorrect because G6PD deficiency does not follow an autosomal dominant pattern where only one copy of the mutated gene is needed to express the trait.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal recessive:</strong> Incorrect because while females can be affected if both X chromosomes carry the mutation, the primary inheritance pattern is X-linked, not autosomal.</li>
<li><strong>Mitochondrial:</strong> Incorrect because G6PD deficiency is not inherited through mitochondrial DNA, which is passed exclusively from mother to all offspring.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','G6PD Deficiency','["g6pd","inheritance","x-linked"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.182Z','2026-10-27T14:21:50.215Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcu003eo7lrx7edpngx','mcq','98- Hand foot syndrome occur with','["Sickle cell anemia"]','Sickle cell anemia','<p><strong>Why Sickle cell anemia is correct:</strong> Hand-foot syndrome (also known as dactylitis) is a classic manifestation of sickle cell anemia caused by vaso-occlusive crises in the small vessels of hands and feet. The sickled red blood cells block capillaries, leading to ischemia, inflammation, and painful swelling of the hands and feet, typically presenting in infants around 6 months of age when fetal hemoglobin levels decline. This occurs due to the polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin S, which distorts red blood cells into a rigid, sickle shape that cannot pass through small capillaries. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options provided:</strong> Since this is a single-answer question with only one option listed, there are no other options to evaluate. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hand-foot syndrome is often the first clinical manifestation of sickle cell disease, typically appearing in infants aged 3-6 months. - It results from vaso-occlusion in the small bones of hands and feet, causing painful swelling and fever. - The presence of hand-foot syndrome in a young child should prompt immediate evaluation for sickle cell disease. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hand-foot syndrome is a pathognomonic early manifestation of sickle cell disease caused by vaso-occlusive crises in small vessels of extremities.</p>','<p>HFS = Hand-Foot Syndrome = Sickle Cell</p>','<p>Hand-foot syndrome in sickle cell disease is a medical emergency requiring hydration, pain management, and sometimes transfusion to prevent acute chest syndrome and stroke.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Sickle Cell Anemia','["sickle cell","hand foot","syndrome"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.182Z','2026-10-27T14:22:03.321Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcv003go7lrups0lr9z','mcq','10- What the increased incubated osmotic fragility test, is MOST likely suggestive of?','["Hereditary stomatocytosis.","Hereditary spherocytosis.","Hereditary Elliptocytosis.","Pyruvate kinase deficiency."]','Hereditary spherocytosis.','<p><strong>Why Hereditary spherocytosis is correct:</strong> Hereditary spherocytosis is characterized by increased osmotic fragility due to defects in the red blood cell membrane proteins (spectrin, ankyrin, or band 3), leading to loss of membrane surface area and formation of spherical, osmotically fragile cells that lyse more readily in hypotonic solutions. The incubated osmotic fragility test specifically identifies this increased fragility after incubation, which enhances the differences between normal and spherocytic red blood cells. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Hereditary stomatocytosis:</strong> This condition actually shows decreased osmotic fragility due to increased membrane surface area, making red blood cells more resistant to lysis. - <strong>Hereditary Elliptocytosis:</strong> This condition involves defects in spectrin or protein 4.1R, leading to elliptical cells that typically have normal osmotic fragility. - <strong>Pyruvate kinase deficiency:</strong> This is a hemolytic anemia caused by impaired glycolysis, resulting in ATP deficiency and rigid red blood cells, but it does not typically show increased osmotic fragility. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hereditary spherocytosis is the most common inherited hemolytic anemia in individuals of Northern European descent. - The classic osmotic fragility test shows increased fragility with a leftward shift in the lysis curve. - Spherocytes are identified on peripheral blood smear as small, dense cells without central pallor. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Increased incubated osmotic fragility is diagnostic for hereditary spherocytosis due to membrane defects creating spherical, fragile red blood cells.</p>','<p>OSMO = Osmotic Fragility = Spherocytosis</p>','<p>Hereditary spherocytosis often presents with jaundice, splenomegaly, and pigment gallstones due to chronic hemolysis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hereditary stomatocytosis:</strong> Shows decreased osmotic fragility due to increased membrane surface area. - <strong>Hereditary Elliptocytosis:</strong> Typically has normal osmotic fragility with elliptical cells on smear. - <strong>Pyruvate kinase deficiency:</strong> Causes hemolytic anemia through impaired glycolysis, not increased osmotic fragility.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Osmotic Fragility','["osmotic fragility","spherocytosis","test"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.183Z','2026-10-27T14:22:03.324Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugon000ztzlrh6juor8f','mcq','Preterm infant in NICU, fed with mother''s expressed milk. NICU staff noticed it contains high protein and immune factors than mature term milk.','["Contaminated milk","Mother''s diet high protein","Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors","Milk was frozen"]','Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors','<p><strong>Why Preterm milk is specially adapted with higher protein and immune factors is correct:</strong> Preterm milk is biologically distinct from term milk, containing higher concentrations of protein, immunoglobulins (especially secretory IgA), growth factors, and fatty acids to support the accelerated growth and immature immune system of preterm infants. This adaptation provides enhanced nutritional support and passive immunity that term milk cannot adequately supply for these vulnerable infants. The increased protein content supports rapid catch-up growth, while elevated immune factors protect against infections that preterm infants are particularly susceptible to due to underdeveloped immune defenses.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Contaminated milk:</strong> Preterm milk''s composition differences are normal physiological adaptations, not contamination. Contaminated milk would typically show signs of bacterial growth or spoilage.</li>
<li><strong>Mother''s diet high protein:</strong> While maternal diet can influence milk composition to some extent, the dramatic differences in preterm milk are primarily due to the mother''s biological response to preterm delivery, not diet alone.</li>
<li><strong>Milk was frozen:</strong> Freezing may slightly alter some components but does not systematically increase protein and immune factors; preterm milk has these characteristics even when fresh.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Preterm milk has 1.5-2 times more protein than term milk to support rapid growth of underdeveloped organs.</li>
<li>Preterm milk contains higher levels of lactoferrin, lysozyme, and oligosaccharides that provide antimicrobial protection.</li>
<li>The composition of preterm milk changes over the first several weeks postpartum, with highest protein content in the first 2-3 weeks.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Preterm milk is nature''s specialized formula, with higher protein and immune factors specifically evolved to meet the unique needs of premature infants.</p>','<p>Preterm milk = ''P'' for Protection (immune factors) and ''P'' for Protein (higher concentration)</p>','<p>Preterm milk should be considered a therapeutic intervention for preterm infants, providing not just nutrition but critical immune protection and growth factors that artificial formulas cannot fully replicate.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Contaminated milk:</strong> Preterm milk''s composition differences are normal physiological adaptations, not contamination.</li>
<li><strong>Mother''s diet high protein:</strong> Maternal diet has minimal impact on the dramatic protein and immune factor differences in preterm milk.</li>
<li><strong>Milk was frozen:</strong> Freezing doesn''t systematically increase protein and immune factors in breast milk.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["preterm milk","protein","immune factors","NICU"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.143Z','2026-10-27T14:24:35.393Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcw003ho7lrdyrnmpzb','mcq','11- In which condition blood smear is needed for diagnosing?','["Excessive bruising.","ABO incompatibility.","Spherocytosis.","RH incompatibility."]','Spherocytosis.','<p><strong>Why Spherocytosis is correct:</strong> Spherocytosis requires a blood smear for diagnosis because the characteristic finding is the presence of spherocytes - small, dense red blood cells that lack the central pallor normally seen in biconcave disc-shaped red blood cells. These spherocytes result from membrane defects that cause loss of surface area relative to volume, creating spherical cells that are more rigid and prone to splenic destruction. The blood smear is essential for identifying these morphological changes, which are diagnostic for hereditary spherocytosis. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Excessive bruising:</strong> While a blood smear might be performed to evaluate for platelet disorders or coagulopathies, excessive bruising alone doesn''t require a blood smear for diagnosis as it''s primarily a clinical finding. - <strong>ABO incompatibility:</strong> This is diagnosed through direct antiglobulin test (DAT) and antibody screening, not through blood smear findings. - <strong>RH incompatibility:</strong> Similar to ABO incompatibility, this is diagnosed through serological testing, not blood smear morphology. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Spherocytes on peripheral blood smear are small, round cells without central pallor and have increased MCHC. - Hereditary spherocytosis is confirmed by osmotic fragility testing and flow cytometry for membrane protein defects. - The presence of spherocytes with a positive family history is highly suggestive of hereditary spherocytosis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Blood smear is essential for diagnosing spherocytosis because it reveals the characteristic spherocytes that result from red blood cell membrane defects.</p>','<p>SPHERE = Spherocytosis = Spherocytes on Smear</p>','<p>In hereditary spherocytosis, the degree of spherocytosis on blood smear correlates with the severity of hemolysis and the need for splenectomy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Excessive bruising:</strong> Primarily a clinical finding that doesn''t require blood smear diagnosis. - <strong>ABO incompatibility:</strong> Diagnosed through serological testing, not blood smear morphology. - <strong>RH incompatibility:</strong> Also diagnosed through serological testing, not blood smear findings.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Blood Smear','["blood smear","diagnosis","spherocytosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.184Z','2026-10-27T14:22:03.325Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcz003mo7lrz0mowm30','mcq','Which of the following disease is inherited by X-Linked recessive Inheritance?','["Congenital spherocytosis","G6PD","Vitamin D resistant rickets","Marfan syndrome"]','G6PD','<p><strong>Why G6PD is correct:</strong> G6PD deficiency is inherited through X-linked recessive inheritance because the G6PD gene is located on the X chromosome at position Xq28. Males, having only one X chromosome, are more commonly and severely affected when they inherit the mutated allele, while females can be carriers or affected depending on X-inactivation patterns. This enzyme deficiency leads to hemolytic anemia when exposed to oxidative stressors like certain medications (e.g., sulfonamides) or fava beans, as the red blood cells cannot generate enough NADPH to protect against oxidative damage.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Congenital spherocytosis:</strong> This is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in genes encoding red blood cell membrane proteins, not X-linked.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin D resistant rickets:</strong> This can be X-linked (due to PHEX gene mutations) but is more commonly autosomal dominant or recessive, making it not exclusively X-linked recessive.</li>
<li><strong>Marfan syndrome:</strong> This is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the FBN1 gene, not X-linked.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>G6PD deficiency is the most common human enzyme deficiency worldwide, with highest prevalence in populations with historical malaria resistance.</li>
<li>The classic presentation is hemolytic anemia triggered by oxidative stressors like fava beans (favism) or certain medications.</li>
<li>Diagnosis is confirmed by measuring G6PD enzyme activity levels, which can be falsely normal during acute hemolysis due to reticulocytosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> G6PD deficiency is X-linked recessive because the gene is on the X chromosome, making males predominantly affected.</p>','<p>G6PD is X-linked - think ''X'' for ''X chromosome'' and ''G6PD'' affects males more (XY).</p>','<p>G6PD-deficient patients should avoid fava beans and certain medications like sulfonamides and antimalarials to prevent hemolytic crises.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Congenital spherocytosis:</strong> Incorrect because it''s autosomal dominant, not X-linked.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin D resistant rickets:</strong> Incorrect because it''s not exclusively X-linked recessive.</li>
<li><strong>Marfan syndrome:</strong> Incorrect because it''s autosomal dominant, not X-linked.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Genetic Disorders','["g6pd","inheritance","x-linked"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.187Z','2026-10-27T14:22:23.940Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krd1003po7lr782m6hvh','mcq','The acidified glycerol lysis test (AGLT) used as a confirmation test to diagnose:','["α thalassemia.","Sickle cell anemia.","β thalassemia minor.","Spherocytosis."]','Spherocytosis.','<p><strong>Why Spherocytosis is correct:</strong> The acidified glycerol lysis test (AGLT) is specifically designed to diagnose hereditary spherocytosis, a disorder characterized by defective spectrin in the red blood cell membrane. In this test, spherocytes lyse faster than normal red blood cells when exposed to acidified glycerol due to their increased membrane fragility. The test has high sensitivity and specificity for hereditary spherocytosis, making it a valuable confirmation test when there is clinical suspicion.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>α thalassemia:</strong> This diagnosis is made by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing abnormal hemoglobin patterns, not by membrane fragility testing.</li>
<li><strong>Sickle cell anemia:</strong> This is diagnosed by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing the presence of hemoglobin S, not by membrane stability tests.</li>
<li><strong>β thalassemia minor:</strong> This is diagnosed by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing elevated HbA2 and possibly elevated HbF, not by membrane fragility testing.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hereditary spherocytosis is the most common inherited hemolytic anemia in individuals of Northern European descent.</li>
<li>The gold standard diagnostic test is osmotic fragility testing, with AGLT being a more rapid alternative.</li>
<li>Clinical features include jaundice, splenomegaly, and gallstones due to chronic hemolysis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The acidified glycerol lysis test (AGLT) confirms hereditary spherocytosis by demonstrating increased red blood cell membrane fragility.</p>','<p>AGLT for Spherocytosis: Acid Glycerol Lysis Test = Spherocyte Test</p>','<p>Hereditary spherocytosis patients are at risk for aplastic crises during parvovirus B19 infection due to temporary suppression of erythropoiesis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>α thalassemia:</strong> Diagnosed by hemoglobin electrophoresis, not membrane fragility testing.</li>
<li><strong>Sickle cell anemia:</strong> Diagnosed by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing hemoglobin S.</li>
<li><strong>β thalassemia minor:</strong> Diagnosed by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing elevated HbA2.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Laboratory Diagnosis','["spherocytosis","aglt","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.189Z','2026-10-27T14:22:32.104Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krd2003qo7lr2e35sn7s','mcq','A 7-year-old girl with sickle cell anemia presents with an Hb of 4 g/dl and a reticulocyte count of 0.1%. The most likely explanation for the measured hemoglobin concentration is:','["Hyperhemolysis.","Iron deficiency.","Parvovirus B19 infection.","Splenic infarction."]','Parvovirus B19 infection.','<p><strong>Why Parvovirus B19 infection is correct:</strong> Parvovirus B19 specifically targets erythroid precursors in the bone marrow, causing a temporary cessation of red blood cell production. In a patient with sickle cell anemia who already has a high baseline turnover of red blood cells, this cessation leads to a rapid and severe drop in hemoglobin concentration. The extremely low reticulocyte count (0.1%) indicates a lack of compensatory erythropoiesis, which is characteristic of parvovirus-induced aplastic crisis rather than hemolysis or other causes.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options would be wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hyperhemolysis:</strong> In sickle cell disease, increased hemolysis would typically be accompanied by an elevated reticulocyte count as the bone marrow compensates, not a suppressed count.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency:</strong> While iron deficiency can cause anemia, it would not typically cause such a dramatic drop in hemoglobin in a sickle cell patient without other signs of iron deficiency, and the reticulocyte count would be inappropriately low for the degree of anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Splenic infarction:</strong> This would cause acute pain and potentially worsen anemia, but would not suppress erythropoiesis to the extent seen here with the near absence of reticulocytes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Parvovirus B19 infection is the most common cause of transient aplastic crisis in patients with hemolytic anemias.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is suspected with sudden severe anemia and reticulocytopenia in a patient with chronic hemolysis.</li>
<li>Treatment is typically supportive with transfusions, as the crisis is self-limited in immunocompetent patients.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A sudden drop in hemoglobin with near absence of reticulocytes in a sickle cell patient indicates parvovirus B19-induced aplastic crisis.</p>','<p>PARVO: Pancytopenia, Aplastic crisis, Reticulocytopenia, Viremia, Only temporary</p>','<p>Patients with sickle cell disease should be transfused cautiously during aplastic crisis as over-transfusion can increase viscosity and potentially worsen complications.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hyperhemolysis:</strong> Would cause elevated reticulocyte count, not suppressed.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency:</strong> Would not cause such dramatic hemoglobin drop without other signs of iron deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Splenic infarction:</strong> Would cause pain but not suppress erythropoiesis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Sickle Cell Disease','["sickle cell","parvovirus","anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.190Z','2026-10-27T14:22:32.105Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krd9003wo7lr5juefplt','mcq','Pattern of inheritance of hereditary spherocytosis is','["Autosomal dominant","X-linked","Mitochondrial","Autosomal recessive"]','Autosomal dominant','<p><strong>Why Autosomal dominant is correct:</strong> Hereditary spherocytosis follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance because it is caused by mutations in genes encoding proteins of the red blood cell cytoskeleton (such as ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, or protein 4.2), which are located on autosomes. These mutations are typically heterozygous and result in a quantitative deficiency of membrane proteins, leading to loss of membrane surface area and formation of spherocytes. The dominant inheritance pattern explains why affected individuals often have a parent with the same condition and why the disease appears in every generation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>X-linked:</strong> This pattern is incorrect because hereditary spherocytosis affects males and females equally, which is characteristic of autosomal inheritance, not X-linked inheritance where males are predominantly affected.</li>
<li><strong>Mitochondrial:</strong> This pattern is incorrect because mitochondrial inheritance is exclusively maternal and would not explain the equal sex distribution or the vertical transmission pattern seen in hereditary spherocytosis.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal recessive:</strong> This pattern is incorrect because autosomal recessive inheritance would typically require both parents to be carriers and would not explain the vertical transmission pattern where affected individuals often have an affected parent.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hereditary spherocytosis is most commonly caused by mutations in the ANK1 gene (ankyrin) on chromosome 8.</li>
<li>The classic diagnostic triad includes spherocytes on peripheral smear, increased osmotic fragility of red blood cells, and a positive family history.</li>
<li>The disease severity can vary significantly even within families due to modifier genes and environmental factors.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hereditary spherocytosis is autosomal dominant because it results from heterozygous mutations in autosomal genes encoding red blood cell cytoskeleton proteins.</p>','<p>SAD (Spherocytosis Autosomal Dominant) - remember it''s not X-linked or recessive.</p>','<p>Hereditary spherocytosis can present with hemolytic crises triggered by infections, and splenectomy is often curative but increases infection risk.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>X-linked:</strong> Incorrect because the disease affects males and females equally.</li>
<li><strong>Mitochondrial:</strong> Incorrect because mitochondrial inheritance is exclusively maternal.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal recessive:</strong> Incorrect because affected individuals often have an affected parent.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hereditary Disorders','["spherocytosis","inheritance","genetics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.197Z','2026-10-27T14:22:58.042Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krd7003uo7lrp5higuan','mcq','Type of anemia during the hemolytic attack in cases with G6PD enzyme deficiency is:','["Microcytic hypochromic anemia","Normocytic normochromic","Macrocytic normochromic anemia.","Macrocytic hypochromic anemia"]','Normocytic normochromic','<p><strong>Why Normocytic normochromic is correct:</strong> During hemolytic attacks in G6PD deficiency, the anemia is normocytic normochromic because the bone marrow responds to red cell destruction by releasing reticulocytes, which are normal-sized (normocytic) and contain normal amounts of hemoglobin (normochromic). This compensatory response maintains the red cell size and hemoglobin concentration despite the ongoing hemolysis. The normocytic morphology distinguishes it from production defects like iron deficiency (microcytic) or folate/B12 deficiency (macrocytic). <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Microcytic hypochromic anemia:</strong> This pattern is characteristic of iron deficiency anemia or thalassemia, not hemolytic anemias like G6PD deficiency. - <strong>Macrocytic normochromic anemia:</strong> This occurs when there is a defect in DNA synthesis, such as folate or B12 deficiency, or when there are large reticulocytes released, but the macrocytosis in G6PD deficiency is typically mild and not the defining feature. - <strong>Macrocytic hypochromic anemia:</strong> This is not a typical classification of anemia and would suggest both macrocytosis and hypochromia, which doesn''t occur in G6PD deficiency. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Hemolytic anemias typically present with normocytic normochromic morphology due to reticulocyte response. - G6PD deficiency causes acute hemolytic crises triggered by oxidative stressors, with rapid onset of anemia. - The reticulocyte count is elevated in hemolytic anemias as a compensatory mechanism. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemolytic anemias like G6PD deficiency present with normocytic normochromic anemia due to reticulocyte compensation.</p>','<p>G6PD hemolytic anemia: ''Normo'' for normocytic normochromic - the compensatory response maintains normal red cell size and hemoglobin content.</p>','<p>In G6PD deficiency, the hemolytic crisis typically occurs 2-3 days after exposure to the oxidant stressor, as damaged red cells are removed from circulation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Microcytic hypochromic anemia:</strong> Characteristic of iron deficiency or thalassemia, not hemolytic anemias. - <strong>Macrocytic normochromic anemia:</strong> Occurs with folate/B12 deficiency, not typical in G6PD deficiency. - <strong>Macrocytic hypochromic anemia:</strong> Not a typical classification and doesn''t occur in G6PD deficiency.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hemolytic Anemia','["g6pd","hemolytic anemia","anemia types"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.195Z','2026-10-27T14:22:40.819Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnh0002tzlrdebbmv3y','mcq','Female full term , Head circumference at birth is:','["35 cm"]','35 cm','<p><strong>Why 35 cm is correct:</strong> The average head circumference at birth for a full-term infant is approximately 35 cm, which falls within the normal range of 32-38 cm. This measurement is crucial as it reflects brain growth and development during the fetal period. The head circumference is typically measured at the largest circumference, wrapping around the occiput and supraorbital ridges, and is one of the key parameters assessed in the neonatal period along with weight and length.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided in the question.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Head circumference should be measured at every well-child visit during the first year of life to monitor for abnormal patterns of growth. - A head circumference significantly less than the 3rd percentile or greater than the 97th percentile warrants further evaluation. - The rapid growth of head circumference occurs primarily during the first year of life, with most of the brain growth occurring by 2 years of age.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Normal head circumference at birth is approximately 35 cm, reflecting normal fetal brain development.</p>','','<p>Head circumference measurements are critical for detecting early signs of neurological disorders, including hydrocephalus and microcephaly.</p>','','Basic','General','Growth & Development','["measurement","newborn"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.102Z','2026-10-27T14:22:57.119Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugno0003tzlrj8vpcz0g','mcq','Child stand freely, Walks well , Run well. The child''s age is:','["2 years"]','2 years','<p><strong>Why 2 years is correct:</strong> By 2 years of age, children typically develop the gross motor skills to stand freely, walk well, and run with coordination. This represents a significant milestone in motor development, building upon earlier achievements like pulling to stand at 9-12 months and walking independently by 12-15 months. The development of running requires not just balance but also the ability to shift weight from one leg to another, which is fully developed by approximately 2 years.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided in the question.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The sequence of motor development follows a predictable pattern: rolling over, sitting, crawling, pulling to stand, walking, and finally running. - By 18 months, most children can walk well but may still have an unsteady gait; running typically develops between 18-24 months. - Assessment of motor development should always consider the child''s overall developmental context, including fine motor and language skills.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Running well is a typical motor skill achieved by 2 years of age, representing the culmination of gross motor development.</p>','','<p>Delayed achievement of motor milestones can be an early indicator of developmental disorders or neurological conditions.</p>','','Basic','General','Growth & Development','["motor","development"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.108Z','2026-10-27T14:22:57.121Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnp0004tzlriru66ya4','mcq','blinks to flashing lights, fixate light in line of vision.:','["At birth"]','At birth','<p><strong>Why At birth is correct:</strong> Newborns exhibit the blink reflex to bright flashing lights and can fixate on and follow a light source in their line of vision, demonstrating basic visual function at birth. These reflexes indicate that the visual pathways, including the retina, optic nerve, and visual cortex, are sufficiently developed to respond to visual stimuli. The blink reflex is mediated by the cranial nerves (CN V and VII), while visual fixation involves the coordinated action of the extraocular muscles controlled by CN III, IV, and VI.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided in the question.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Visual acuity at birth is approximately 20/400, improving to 20/20 by 6 months of age. - The red reflex test should be performed on all newborns to detect abnormalities like cataracts or retinoblastoma. - Tracking objects with smooth pursuit movements develops between 2-3 months of age, building upon the basic fixation present at birth.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Newborns can blink to bright lights and fixate on visual stimuli, demonstrating that basic visual function is present at birth.</p>','<p>BLINK: Basic Light INstinct Knows - visual reflexes present at birth.</p>','<p>Absence of a blink reflex to bright light in a newborn can indicate neurological impairment or visual pathway abnormalities.</p>','','Basic','General','Growth & Development','["vision","newborn"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.109Z','2026-10-27T14:22:57.121Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krda003yo7lreyiw9cn2','mcq','what signifies Beta thalassemia minor','["increased Hb A2"]','increased Hb A2','<p><strong>Why increased Hb A2 is correct:</strong> Beta thalassemia minor is characterized by a mild deficiency in beta-globin chain production, leading to an imbalance in the alpha to beta-globin chain ratio. This imbalance causes an increase in the proportion of Hb A2 (alpha2/delta2) because delta-globin chains are structurally similar to beta-globin chains and can partially compensate for the beta-globin deficiency. The increased Hb A2 level, typically elevated above 3.5%, is a diagnostic hallmark of beta thalassemia minor.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options would be wrong:</strong> Beta thalassemia minor is specifically defined by its hematological features, and while other laboratory abnormalities may be present (such as microcytic hypochromic red blood cells and mild anemia), the most specific and diagnostic finding is the elevated Hb A2 level. Other hemoglobinopathies or thalassemias may present with different hemoglobin patterns, such as increased Hb F in delta-beta thalassemia or Hb S in sickle cell disease.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Beta thalassemia minor typically presents with mild microcytic hypochromic anemia and normal or slightly elevated red blood cell count.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by hemoglobin electrophoresis showing increased Hb A2 (&gt;3.5%) and sometimes a slight increase in Hb F.</li>
<li>Unlike beta thalassemia major, beta thalassemia minor usually does not require treatment and has a good prognosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Beta thalassemia minor is diagnosed by increased Hb A2 level due to compensation for beta-globin chain deficiency.</p>','<p>Beta minor = More A2 (Hb A2 elevated)</p>','<p>Beta thalassemia minor is often confused with iron deficiency anemia but can be distinguished by the elevated Hb A2 level and normal ferritin.</p>','','Moderate','Hematology','Thalassemia','["thalassemia minor","hb a2","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.198Z','2026-10-27T14:22:58.044Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krdo003zo7lr2i1mobna','mcq','What is the pattern of inheritance of G6PD enzyme deficiency?','["Autosomal dominant.","X-linked.","Autosomal recessive.","Mitochondrial"]','X-linked.','<p><strong>Why X-linked is correct:</strong> G6PD enzyme deficiency follows an X-linked pattern of inheritance because the G6PD gene is located on the X chromosome at Xq28. Males, having only one X chromosome, are predominantly affected when they inherit the mutated allele, while females are typically carriers or have variable expression due to X-chromosome inactivation. This explains the male predominance in clinical presentation and the characteristic inheritance pattern where the trait is passed from carrier mothers to their sons.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal dominant:</strong> This pattern is incorrect because G6PD deficiency does not show the vertical transmission pattern characteristic of autosomal dominant inheritance, and males are more commonly affected than females.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal recessive:</strong> This pattern is incorrect because if it were autosomal recessive, both males and females would be equally affected, and the disease would skip generations, which is not typical for G6PD deficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Mitochondrial:</strong> This pattern is incorrect because mitochondrial inheritance is exclusively maternal and would not explain the male predominance seen in G6PD deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>G6PD deficiency is the most common enzymatic disorder of red blood cells, affecting over 400 million people worldwide.</li>
<li>The classic presentation is hemolytic anemia triggered by oxidative stressors such as certain drugs (sulfonamides, antimalarials), fava beans, or infections.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by quantitative G6PD enzyme assay or by the methemoglobin reduction test.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> G6PD deficiency is X-linked because the gene is on the X chromosome, explaining the male predominance and carrier females.</p>','<p>G6PD: Guys (males) are Primarily Deficient (X-linked inheritance)</p>','<p>G6PD deficiency is protective against malaria, explaining its high prevalence in malaria-endemic regions.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal dominant:</strong> Incorrect because males are more commonly affected and there''s no vertical transmission pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal recessive:</strong> Incorrect because both sexes would be equally affected if it were recessive.</li>
<li><strong>Mitochondrial:</strong> Incorrect because mitochondrial inheritance is exclusively maternal.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Genetic Disorders','["g6pd","inheritance","x-linked"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.212Z','2026-10-27T14:22:58.045Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugns0008tzlrqv9dyef6','mcq','Neurological development peak at','["1st year"]','1st year','<p><strong>Why 1st year is correct:</strong> The first year of life represents the most critical period of neurological development, with the brain reaching approximately 80% of its adult size by 12 months. This period is characterized by rapid synaptogenesis, myelination of neural pathways, and establishment of basic neural circuits that form the foundation for all future cognitive, motor, and social development. The explosive growth of the brain during this time is driven by genetic programming and environmental stimuli, with dendritic arborization and synaptic connections forming at an unprecedented rate. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Other years:</strong> While neurological development continues beyond the first year, the most rapid and fundamental changes occur specifically during the first 12 months of life. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The brain triples in size during the first year of life. - Synaptic density peaks at around 2-3 years but begins with explosive growth in the first year. - Critical periods for sensory and motor development are established in the first year. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The first year of life is the period of most rapid neurological development, with the brain reaching 80% of its adult size by 12 months.</p>','<p>RAPID: R - Rapid growth, A - Adult size 80% by 1 year, P - Peak synaptogenesis, I - Initial circuit formation, D - Developmental foundation</p>','<p>Early neurological development in the first year sets the foundation for all future learning and behavior, making this period crucial for appropriate stimulation and nutrition.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Other years:</strong> Neurological development continues beyond the first year but is most rapid during the first 12 months.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Neurology','Neurodevelopment','["neurology","development","peak"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.112Z','2026-10-27T14:23:12.929Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnt0009tzlr77gtig1l','mcq','Weaning child with loss of appetite in management start with:','["1 gm protein","2 gm protein","5 gm protein","6 gm protein"]','1 gm protein','<p><strong>Why 1 gm protein is correct:</strong> When weaning a child with loss of appetite, starting with 1 gram of protein per kilogram of body weight is appropriate as it provides adequate nutrition without overwhelming the immature digestive system. This conservative approach allows for gradual adaptation to solid foods while maintaining nutritional balance, as excessive protein intake could potentially worsen gastrointestinal symptoms in a child with poor appetite. The 1g/kg/day recommendation aligns with the protein requirements for normal growth in infants during the weaning period. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>2 gm protein:</strong> This exceeds the recommended protein intake for weaning children and may be difficult to achieve with a poor appetite. - <strong>5 gm protein:</strong> This represents an excessive protein load that would be inappropriate for a child with loss of appetite and could potentially cause digestive distress. - <strong>6 gm protein:</strong> This is an extremely high protein intake that would be contraindicated in a child with feeding difficulties and could lead to nutritional imbalances. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Weaning should begin with small, easily digestible amounts of complementary foods. - Protein requirements for weaning infants are approximately 1.1-1.5g/kg/day, starting conservatively. - Gradual introduction of foods is key to successful weaning, especially in children with poor appetite. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> When weaning a child with loss of appetite, start conservatively with 1 gram of protein per kilogram of body weight to allow gradual adaptation without overwhelming the digestive system.</p>','<p>START: S - Small amounts, T - Transition gradually, A - Adapt to appetite, R - Recommended 1g/kg, T - Tolerate well</p>','<p>Successful weaning requires patience and gradual introduction of foods, as forcing excessive protein or calories can create aversions and worsen feeding problems.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>2 gm protein:</strong> Exceeds recommended intake for weaning children and may worsen symptoms. - <strong>5 gm protein:</strong> Represents an excessive protein load inappropriate for a child with feeding difficulties. - <strong>6 gm protein:</strong> Extremely high protein intake contraindicated in children with poor appetite.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nutrition','Weaning','["weaning","appetite","protein"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.113Z','2026-10-27T14:23:12.931Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuci00dwe9oihgzi4ied','mcq','A full term neonate with APGAR score 3 at one minute means that:','["This baby had bad general condition and may need NICU admission for fluids, oxygen and NICU admis"]','This baby had bad general condition and may need NICU admission for fluids, oxygen and NICU admis','','','','','Basic','Neonatology','APGAR Score','["apgar","neonatal","nicu"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.874Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.786Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnq0005tzlrlgjh38gw','mcq','Normally, the newborn loses 5-10% of weight during the first 3-4 days and regains how many grams every month?','["750 gm in the first 4 months"]','750 gm in the first 4 months','<p><strong>Why 750 gm in the first 4 months is correct:</strong> Newborns typically lose 5-10% of their birth weight during the first 3-4 days due to fluid loss, insensible water loss, and limited initial intake. This physiological weight loss is followed by a period of rapid catch-up growth, with newborns gaining approximately 20-30 grams per day during the first month. The average weight gain of 750 grams in the first 4 months reflects this accelerated growth phase, which is supported by adequate nutrition and the infant''s developing digestive and metabolic systems. This growth pattern is essential for achieving developmental milestones and maintaining appropriate nutritional status during this critical period.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Other weight gain options:</strong> The question specifically asks about the weight gain per month, and while other amounts might be plausible for different time periods, 750 grams in the first 4 months is the standard reference value for this specific developmental window.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Newborns typically lose 5-10% of birth weight in the first 3-4 days before beginning to regain weight. - The average weight gain of 750 grams in the first 4 months represents approximately 20-30 grams per day during this rapid growth phase. - This growth pattern is crucial for achieving appropriate developmental milestones and maintaining nutritional status.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Newborns gain approximately 750 grams in the first 4 months after their initial physiological weight loss.</p>','<p>750 in 4: Seven months, fifty grams daily, zero to four months</p>','<p>Monitoring weight gain patterns is essential in newborn care, as deviations from expected growth trajectories may indicate underlying medical issues or feeding problems.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Other weight gain options:</strong> The question specifically asks about the weight gain per month, and while other amounts might be plausible for different time periods, 750 grams in the first 4 months is the standard reference value for this specific developmental window.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Neonatal Physiology','["neonatal","weight gain"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.110Z','2026-10-27T14:23:13.950Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnr0006tzlrzgkm9y4h','mcq','Child uses tricycle, 6 cubes. His age is:','["36 months"]','36 months','<p><strong>Why 36 months is correct:</strong> A child who can ride a tricycle and stack 6 cubes demonstrates advanced fine and gross motor skills that are characteristic of the 36-month (3-year) developmental milestone. Tricycle riding requires sufficient balance, coordination, and leg strength that typically develop between 30-36 months. The ability to stack 6 cubes indicates advanced fine motor control and demonstrates the child''s understanding of spatial relationships, which is expected at 36 months according to standard developmental assessment tools like the Denver II or Ages and Stages Questionnaires.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>24 months:</strong> While 24-month-olds can typically stack 4-6 cubes, they generally lack the coordination and balance required for tricycle riding. - <strong>30 months:</strong> Some 30-month-olds may begin tricycle use, but it''s not consistently mastered until 36 months, and stacking 6 cubes is expected earlier. - <strong>42 months:</strong> By 42 months, children can typically ride tricycles proficiently and stack more than 6 cubes, making this age too advanced for the described skills.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Tricycle riding is typically mastered between 30-36 months, requiring significant balance and coordination. - The ability to stack 6 cubes is a 36-month milestone that demonstrates fine motor development and spatial understanding. - These motor skills develop sequentially, with fine motor skills typically preceding gross motor skills in some areas.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A child who can ride a tricycle and stack 6 cubes is typically 36 months old, as these are hallmark developmental milestones for this age.</p>','<p>3 for Tricycle: 3 years old for tricycle and 6-cube stacking</p>','<p>Developmental milestones follow a predictable sequence but vary in timing; delayed achievement of multiple milestones warrants further evaluation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>24 months:</strong> While 24-month-olds can typically stack 4-6 cubes, they generally lack the coordination and balance required for tricycle riding. - <strong>30 months:</strong> Some 30-month-olds may begin tricycle use, but it''s not consistently mastered until 36 months, and stacking 6 cubes is expected earlier. - <strong>42 months:</strong> By 42 months, children can typically ride tricycles proficiently and stack more than 6 cubes, making this age too advanced for the described skills.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Developmental Milestones','["development","milestones","motor skills"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.111Z','2026-10-27T14:23:13.953Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugo4000htzlr2ubd4dd4','mcq','Which one from the following investigations are incorrect in case of rickets','["We may do genetic testing","Decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase level","Decrease in serum phosphorous level","Bone biopsy may be done"]','Decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase level','<p><strong>Why Decrease in serum alkaline phosphatase level is correct:</strong> Rickets is characterized by defective mineralization of growing bone due to vitamin D deficiency, hypophosphatemia, or other disorders of bone mineralization. In rickets, serum alkaline phosphatase levels are typically elevated, not decreased, because this enzyme is produced by osteoblasts and is upregulated during active bone formation and remodeling in response to the mineralization defect. The elevated alkaline phosphatase serves as a key biochemical marker for the increased bone turnover seen in rickets. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>We may do genetic testing:</strong> Genetic testing can be performed in cases of suspected hereditary forms of rickets (like vitamin D-dependent rickets or X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets), making this a correct investigation. - <strong>Decrease in serum phosphorous level:</strong> Hypophosphatemia is a classic finding in rickets, particularly in vitamin D-resistant forms, making this a correct laboratory finding. - <strong>Bone biopsy may be done:</strong> Bone biopsy can be performed in atypical or diagnostic cases of rickets to evaluate the mineralization defect directly, making this a valid investigation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Rickets typically presents with elevated alkaline phosphatase, hypophosphatemia, and normal or low calcium levels. - The diagnosis of rickets requires a combination of clinical, radiological, and biochemical findings. - Genetic testing is particularly important in cases where vitamin D supplementation fails to improve the condition. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In rickets, serum alkaline phosphatase is elevated (not decreased) due to increased osteoblast activity during impaired bone mineralization.</p>','<p>ALP = Always Low in Phosphorus deficiency? No, ALP is Elevated in Rickets!</p>','<p>Alkaline phosphatase levels should always be interpreted in the context of age, as they are naturally higher in growing children.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>We may do genetic testing:</strong> Genetic testing is appropriate for suspected hereditary rickets forms. - <strong>Decrease in serum phosphorous level:</strong> Hypophosphatemia is a classic finding in rickets. - <strong>Bone biopsy may be done:</strong> Bone biopsy can be used to evaluate mineralization defects in atypical cases.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","investigation","alkaline phosphatase"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.124Z','2026-10-27T14:23:40.537Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugo5000itzlrdn4m8j35','mcq','X ray for a rachitic child and show dense line','["Healing rickets","Healed rickets","Active rickets"]','Healing rickets','<p><strong>Why Healing rickets is correct:</strong> A dense line on X-ray in a rachitic child represents healing, not healed rickets. This dense line, known as a "metaphyseal dense line" or "Frankel''s line," appears at the metaphysis as a result of healing and remineralization at the growth plate. It indicates that treatment (typically vitamin D and calcium supplementation) has been initiated and the body is actively laying down new mineralized bone at the site of previous damage. This finding is characteristic of the healing phase and appears before complete resolution of the rachitic changes. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Healed rickets:</strong> Healed rickets would show normal or near-normal bone architecture with residual deformities but not active dense lines indicating new mineralization. - <strong>Active rickets:</strong> Active rickets would show the classic rachitic changes such as fraying, splaying, and cupping of the metaphyses with no evidence of healing or dense lines. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Healing lines appear perpendicular to the long axis of bone and represent mineralized cartilage and bone at the healing front. - The appearance of these lines is a positive prognostic sign indicating treatment response. - Serial X-rays can monitor the healing process as these lines progress toward the diaphysis over time. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Dense lines on X-ray in rickets indicate active healing with new mineralization, not healed disease.</p>','<p>Healing lines = Hopeful signs of treatment working</p>','<p>The appearance of healing lines on X-ray is one of the earliest signs of successful treatment in rickets.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Healed rickets:</strong> Would show normal bone architecture without active healing lines. - <strong>Active rickets:</strong> Would show classic rachitic changes without evidence of healing.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","x-ray","dense line"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.125Z','2026-10-27T14:23:40.539Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugoa000ntzlr85hge8e2','mcq','Which one of the following is not a risk factor for rickets?','["Exclusive breast-feeding.","Preterm labor.","Antimalarial drugs.","Drugs used for treatment for AIDS"]','Exclusive breast-feeding.','<p><strong>Why Exclusive breast-feeding is correct:</strong> Exclusive breast-feeding is not a risk factor for rickets because breast milk, while containing vitamin D, often has insufficient quantities to meet an infant''s requirements, especially if the mother has low vitamin D levels. However, breast milk itself is not inherently deficient in calcium or phosphorus, and when combined with appropriate sunlight exposure and maternal vitamin D supplementation, it does not cause rickets. The other options represent established risk factors: preterm infants have reduced bone mineral stores and may not receive adequate vitamin D transfer; antimalarial drugs like chloroquine can interfere with vitamin D metabolism; and certain AIDS medications (specifically antiretroviral drugs) can cause renal tubular dysfunction leading to phosphate wasting.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Preterm labor:</strong> Preterm infants are at risk due to inadequate transfer of calcium and phosphorus across the placenta in the third trimester and reduced stores of vitamin D.</li>
<li><strong>Antimalarial drugs:</strong> These drugs can interfere with vitamin D metabolism by inhibiting the enzyme 25-hydroxyvitamin D-1α-hydroxylase, which is necessary for activating vitamin D.</li>
<li><strong>Drugs used for treatment for AIDS:</strong> Certain antiretroviral medications can cause Fanconi syndrome, leading to renal phosphate wasting and subsequent rickets.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Rickets results from inadequate mineralization of growing bone due to vitamin D deficiency, calcium deficiency, or phosphate deficiency.</li>
<li>Risk factors include limited sun exposure, dark skin pigmentation, exclusive breast-feeding without vitamin D supplementation, and certain malabsorption syndromes.</li>
<li>The classic radiographic findings of rickets include fraying and cupping of the metaphyses and widened growth plates.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Exclusive breast-feeding is not a risk factor for rickets when combined with adequate maternal vitamin D status and supplementation, whereas preterm birth, certain medications, and malabsorption syndromes are established risk factors.</p>','<p>B-P-A: Breastfeeding (not a risk), Prematurity (risk), Antimalarials (risk), AIDS drugs (risk)</p>','<p>Vitamin D deficiency rickets is preventable through routine vitamin D supplementation (400 IU/day) for exclusively breastfed infants and adequate sun exposure.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Preterm labor:</strong> Preterm infants have reduced bone mineral stores and may not receive adequate vitamin D transfer.</li>
<li><strong>Antimalarial drugs:</strong> These drugs interfere with vitamin D metabolism by inhibiting key enzymes in the activation pathway.</li>
<li><strong>Drugs used for treatment for AIDS:</strong> Certain antiretrovirals can cause renal tubular dysfunction leading to phosphate wasting.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets Risk Factors','["rickets","risk factors","breastfeeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.130Z','2026-10-27T14:24:02.375Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugob000ptzlr89yw7u9r','mcq','Which of the following is the mode of vitamin D resistant rickets?','["X-linked Recessive Inheritance","Autosomal Dominant Inheritance","X-linked Dominant Inheritance","Autosomal Recessive Inheritance"]','X-linked Recessive Inheritance','<p><strong>Why X-linked Recessive Inheritance is correct:</strong> Vitamin D resistant rickets, also known as X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (XLH), follows an X-linked recessive inheritance pattern due to mutations in the PHEX gene (phosphate-regulating endopeptidase homolog, X-linked). This gene mutation leads to increased levels of fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23), which causes renal phosphate wasting and impaired vitamin D activation, resulting in hypophosphatemia and rickets despite normal or elevated vitamin D levels. The X-linked inheritance pattern explains why males are more severely affected and why female carriers typically show milder or no symptoms due to X-inactivation patterns.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal Dominant Inheritance:</strong> While some forms of hypophosphatemic rickets can have autosomal dominant inheritance, the classic vitamin D resistant rickets (XLH) specifically follows X-linked recessive inheritance.</li>
<li><strong>X-linked Dominant Inheritance:</strong> This pattern would affect males and females similarly, which is not the case for XLH where males are more severely affected.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal Recessive Inheritance:</strong> Some rare forms of hypophosphatemic rickets follow this pattern, but the most common vitamin D resistant rickets (XLH) is X-linked recessive.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Vitamin D resistant rickets (XLH) is characterized by hypophosphatemia, normal or elevated vitamin D levels, and resistance to vitamin D therapy.</li>
<li>The PHEX gene mutation leads to increased FGF23 levels, causing renal phosphate wasting and impaired 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production.</li>
<li>Treatment involves phosphate supplementation and active vitamin D analogs (calcitriol), with careful monitoring for complications like hyperparathyroidism and nephrocalcinosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Vitamin D resistant rickets follows X-linked recessive inheritance due to PHEX gene mutations causing FGF23-mediated renal phosphate wasting, distinguishing it from other inheritance patterns.</p>','<p>XLH = X-Linked Hypophosphatemia</p>','<p>In patients with rickets that doesn''t respond to vitamin D therapy, consider genetic forms like X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets and measure serum phosphate and FGF23 levels.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal Dominant Inheritance:</strong> Some hypophosphatemic rickets forms have this pattern, but classic XLH is X-linked recessive.</li>
<li><strong>X-linked Dominant Inheritance:</strong> This would affect males and females similarly, which doesn''t match XLH''s presentation.</li>
<li><strong>Autosomal Recessive Inheritance:</strong> Some rare forms follow this pattern, but the most common vitamin D resistant rickets is X-linked recessive.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Endocrine','Vitamin D Resistant Rickets','["rickets","vitamin D resistant","inheritance"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.131Z','2026-10-27T14:24:02.378Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugoo0010tzlre0m3amyk','mcq','Mother complains that the milk looks thin and watery at the start of feeding. Which of the following is true?','["Abnormal milk","It should be discarded","It is Foremilk, which is higher in lactose for hydration of the baby","It lacks all nutrients"]','It is Foremilk, which is higher in lactose for hydration of the baby','<p><strong>Why It is Foremilk, which is higher in lactose for hydration of the baby is correct:</strong> Foremilk is the thin, watery milk that is released at the beginning of a feeding session, characterized by lower fat content but higher concentrations of lactose, water-soluble vitamins, and electrolytes. This composition is physiologically designed to quench the infant''s thirst and provide hydration before the higher-fat hindmilk is released later in the feeding to provide calories and satiety. The appearance of foremilk is completely normal and represents the natural progression of milk composition during breastfeeding.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Abnormal milk:</strong> The thin, watery appearance at the start of feeding is normal foremilk, not abnormal milk. Abnormal milk would typically indicate mastitis, infection, or other pathological conditions.</li>
<li><strong>It should be discarded:</strong> Discarding foremilk would deprive the infant of valuable hydration and lactose, and is not recommended as it''s a normal part of breastfeeding.</li>
<li><strong>It lacks all nutrients:</strong> Foremilk actually contains significant amounts of lactose, water-soluble vitamins, and immunoglobulins, though it has less fat than hindmilk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foremilk and hindmilk are not different types of milk but rather the natural progression of milk composition during a single feeding.</li>
<li>The fat content of breast milk increases throughout the feeding as the infant continues to nurse.</li>
<li>Foremilk provides hydration while hindmilk provides calories, making both essential components of breastfeeding.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The thin milk at the start of feeding is normal foremilk, designed to hydrate your baby before the richer hindmilk provides calories.</p>','<p>Foremilk = First (comes first) + Forth (provides hydration) + Feeding</p>','<p>The appearance of foremilk is often mistaken for ''watery'' or ''insufficient'' milk by new parents, leading to unnecessary supplementation when in fact it represents the perfect design of breast milk for infant hydration.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abnormal milk:</strong> The thin, watery appearance at the start of feeding is normal foremilk, not abnormal.</li>
<li><strong>It should be discarded:</strong> Discarding foremilk would deprive the infant of valuable hydration and lactose.</li>
<li><strong>It lacks all nutrients:</strong> Foremilk contains significant lactose, water-soluble vitamins, and immunoglobulins.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pediatrics','Breastfeeding','["foremilk","breastfeeding","lactose","hydration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.144Z','2026-10-27T14:24:35.394Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugop0012tzlr3imuqlov','mcq','Infant with confirmed Cow''s milk allergy and contraindicated breastfeeding. The best formula is:','["Extensively hydrolyzed/amino acid-based formula"]','Extensively hydrolyzed/amino acid-based formula','<p><strong>Why Extensively hydrolyzed/amino acid-based formula is correct:</strong> For infants with confirmed cow''s milk allergy who cannot breastfeed, extensively hydrolyzed formulas (eHF) or amino acid-based formulas (AAF) are the first-line alternatives. These formulas contain proteins that are either broken down into small peptides (eHF) or into individual amino acids (AAF), which prevents an immune response in allergic infants. The hydrolysis process reduces the allergenic potential of milk proteins while maintaining adequate nutrition, making these formulas both safe and nutritionally complete for infants with cow''s milk allergy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Standard cow''s milk formula:</strong> Contains intact milk proteins that would trigger an allergic reaction in infants with cow''s milk allergy. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> While soy is an alternative, approximately 10-14% of infants with cow''s milk allergy also react to soy proteins, making it less reliable than hydrolyzed formulas. - <strong>Goat''s milk formula:</strong> Not a suitable alternative as it contains similar protein structures to cow''s milk and can cross-react in allergic infants.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Extensively hydrolyzed formulas are first-line for mild to moderate cow''s milk allergy, while amino acid-based formulas are reserved for severe cases or when hydrolyzed formulas fail. - About 90% of infants with cow''s milk allergy tolerate extensively hydrolyzed formulas, while amino acid-based formulas are virtually non-allergenic. - The diagnosis of cow''s milk allergy should be confirmed by an allergist before switching to specialized formulas.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For infants with confirmed cow''s milk allergy who cannot breastfeed, extensively hydrolyzed or amino acid-based formulas are the only safe nutritional alternatives that prevent allergic reactions.</p>','<p>EHFAA: Extensively Hydrolyzed Formula First, Amino Acid Alternative</p>','<p>Early introduction of extensively hydrolyzed formula in high-risk infants may prevent the development of cow''s milk allergy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Standard cow''s milk formula:</strong> Contains intact milk proteins that trigger allergic reactions. - <strong>Soy-based formula:</strong> Can cross-react in 10-14% of cow''s milk allergic infants. - <strong>Goat''s milk formula:</strong> Contains similar proteins to cow''s milk and is not a safe alternative.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Pediatrics','Formula feeding','["cow''s milk allergy","formula","hydrolyzed","amino acid"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.145Z','2026-10-27T14:24:44.553Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugos0014tzlrxl6995r3','mcq','An 18-month-old female presents with recurrent attacks of painless rectal bleeding; she had a previous history of intussusception at age of 9 month treated by pneumatic reduction; clotting profile and blood film are normal apart from mild anemia. Of the following, what is the MOST likely diagnosis?','["Anal fissure","Amoebic dysentery","Intussusception","Meckel''s diverticulum"]','Meckel''s diverticulum','<p><strong>Why Meckel''s diverticulum is correct:</strong> Meckel''s diverticulum is the most likely diagnosis in this 18-month-old with recurrent painless rectal bleeding and a history of intussusception. Meckel''s diverticulum is a remnant of the omphalomesenteric duct that contains ectopic gastric mucosa in approximately 50% of cases. This ectopic mucosa secretes acid, causing ulceration and painless bleeding, which typically presents as currant jelly stools. The history of previous intussusception is significant as it suggests a predisposition to intestinal motility issues, and Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common congenital gastrointestinal anomaly, occurring in 2-4% of the population.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Anal fissure:</strong> Typically causes painful defecation with blood streaking on the surface of stool, not recurrent painless bleeding. - <strong>Amoebic dysentery:</strong> Would present with more systemic symptoms like fever, diarrhea, and abdominal pain, not just painless rectal bleeding. - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> While she has a history of this, intussusception typically presents with acute, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, and a palpable mass, not recurrent painless bleeding.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Meckel''s diverticulum follows the "rule of 2s": 2% of population, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, presents in first 2 years of life, and contains 2 types of mucosa (gastric and pancreatic). - The diagnostic test of choice for suspected Meckel''s diverticulum with bleeding is a technetium-99m pertechnetate scan (Meckel scan), which detects ectopic gastric mucosa. - Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common cause of lower gastrointestinal bleeding in children under 2 years old.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Recurrent painless rectal bleeding in a child with prior intussusception is classic for Meckel''s diverticulum with ectopic gastric mucosa causing ulceration.</p>','<p>Meckel''s: Rule of 2s (2% of population, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, presents in first 2 years)</p>','<p>Meckel''s diverticulum should be suspected in any child with painless rectal bleeding, especially when associated with the classic ''rule of 2s'' presentation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Anal fissure:</strong> Causes painful defecation with blood streaking, not painless bleeding. - <strong>Amoebic dysentery:</strong> Presents with fever, diarrhea, and abdominal pain, not just painless bleeding. - <strong>Intussusception:</strong> Typically presents with acute abdominal pain, vomiting, and a palpable mass, not recurrent painless bleeding.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','Meckel''s Diverticulum','["rectal bleeding","meckel","intussusception","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.148Z','2026-10-27T14:24:44.555Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcr003ao7lrtnhqhnku','mcq','4- Parvovirus B19 cause Aplastic crisis in which type of hemolysis','["Sickle cell anemia"]','Sickle cell anemia','<p><strong>Why Sickle cell anemia is correct:</strong> Parvovirus B19 specifically targets erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow, causing a temporary cessation of red blood cell production (aplastic crisis). In sickle cell anemia, patients have a significantly shortened red blood cell lifespan (10-20 days compared to 120 days in normal individuals), creating a state of chronic hemolysis. When parvovirus B19 infection suppresses erythropoiesis, the rapid turnover of red blood cells cannot be compensated for, leading to a profound drop in hemoglobin levels and a sudden, severe anemic crisis. <strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While the question only provides one correct option, it''s important to note that parvovirus B19 can cause aplastic crisis in other hemolytic conditions as well, but sickle cell anemia is the most common and clinically significant due to its high prevalence and the severity of the resulting crisis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Parvovirus B19 has a tropism for erythroid progenitor cells, inhibiting DNA synthesis and causing temporary bone marrow suppression. - The aplastic crisis typically lasts 7-10 days, during which time patients may require transfusion support. - The crisis is often preceded by symptoms of viral infection (fever, rash) and is diagnosed by finding giant pronormoblasts in the bone marrow. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Parvovirus B19 causes aplastic crisis in sickle cell anemia by suppressing red blood cell production in patients with already shortened red cell survival.</p>','<p>B19 → Blocks Bone marrow → Brief but severe anemia in chronic hemolysis</p>','<p>Parvovirus B19 infection should be suspected in any sickle cell patient presenting with sudden drop in hemoglobin without evidence of hemolysis.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Parvovirus B19','["parvovirus","aplastic crisis","sickle cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.179Z','2026-10-27T15:42:08.690Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugou0016tzlrz8neguf3','mcq','What is the condition described as Blackfan-Diamond and Transient erythroblastopenia with BM hyperplasia and normal Skeletal survey?','["sideroblastic anemia","Blackfan-Diamond","Fanconi anemia","Aplastic anemia"]','Blackfan-Diamond','<p><strong>Why Blackfan-Diamond is correct:</strong> Blackfan-Diamond anemia (congenital pure red cell aplasia) is characterized by bone marrow hyperplasia with selective erythroblast deficiency, normal skeletal survey, and onset in infancy. The classic presentation is macrocytic anemia with reticulocytopenia in the first year of life, often with associated physical anomalies like thumb abnormalities or cleft palate. This condition is associated with mutations in ribosomal protein genes (RPS19 in ~25% of cases), leading to impaired erythroid progenitor differentiation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Sideroblastic anemia:</strong> Features ringed sideroblasts in the bone marrow and is associated with impaired heme synthesis, not selective erythroblast deficiency with hyperplasia. - <strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Is a constitutional bone marrow failure syndrome with congenital anomalies, cytopenias, and chromosomal fragility, not selective erythroblast deficiency. - <strong>Aplastic anemia:</strong> Involves pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow, not hyperplasia with selective erythroblast deficiency. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Blackfan-Diamond anemia presents in infancy with macrocytic anemia and reticulocytopenia; it''s associated with ribosomal protein gene mutations; corticosteroids are first-line therapy. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Blackfan-Diamond anemia is the only congenital pure red cell aplasia with bone marrow hyperplasia and normal skeletal survey.</p>','<p>B for Blackfan-Diamond: Bone marrow hyperplasia, Babies affected, Better with steroids</p>','<p>Blackfan-Diamond anemia is the most common inherited pure red cell aplasia and responds to corticosteroids in approximately 80% of cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Sideroblastic anemia:</strong> Features ringed sideroblasts and impaired heme synthesis, not selective erythroblast deficiency. - <strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Is a bone marrow failure syndrome with chromosomal fragility and multiple congenital anomalies. - <strong>Aplastic anemia:</strong> Involves pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow, not hyperplasia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","congenital","bone marrow"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.150Z','2026-10-27T14:24:49.663Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugoz0017tzlr8yfzy2uj','mcq','What is the condition described as abnormal chromosomal fragility in Fanconi anemia?','["Spherocytosis","Fanconi anemia","Blackfan-Diamond","Transient erythroblastopenia"]','Fanconi anemia','<p><strong>Why Fanconi anemia is correct:</strong> Fanconi anemia is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by chromosomal fragility, particularly after exposure to DNA cross-linking agents like diepoxybutane (DEB) or mitomycin C. This fragility results from mutations in genes involved in the Fanconi anemia DNA repair pathway (FANC genes), leading to defective DNA cross-link repair and genomic instability. Patients typically present with bone marrow failure, congenital anomalies (such as thumb abnormalities, skin pigmentation, renal anomalies), and increased risk of malignancy. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Spherocytosis:</strong> Is a hereditary hemolytic anemia due to defects in red blood cell membrane proteins (spectrin, ankyrin), not chromosomal fragility. - <strong>Blackfan-Diamond:</strong> Is a pure red cell aplasia without chromosomal fragility, characterized by selective erythroblast deficiency. - <strong>Transient erythroblastopenia:</strong> Is a self-limited condition of childhood with transient absence of red blood cell precursors, no chromosomal fragility. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Fanconi anemia is diagnosed by increased chromosomal breakage with DEB or mitomycin C testing; it''s associated with 22 FANC genes; bone marrow failure and cancer predisposition are major complications. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fanconi anemia is the only anemia with characteristic chromosomal fragility after DNA cross-linking agent exposure.</p>','<p>F for Fanconi: Fragile chromosomes, Failure (bone marrow), FANC genes</p>','<p>Fanconi anemia should be suspected in any child with unexplained bone marrow failure and congenital anomalies.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Spherocytosis:</strong> Is a hemolytic anemia due to red blood cell membrane defects, not chromosomal fragility. - <strong>Blackfan-Diamond:</strong> Is a pure red cell aplasia without chromosomal fragility. - <strong>Transient erythroblastopenia:</strong> Is a self-limited childhood condition without chromosomal fragility.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","chromosomal","fragility"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.155Z','2026-10-27T14:24:49.664Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugp2001btzlrcmu0v93k','mcq','In which condition blood smear is needed for diagnosing?','["Excessive bruising.","ABO incompatibility.","Spherocytosis.","RH incompatibility."]','Spherocytosis.','<p><strong>Why Spherocytosis is correct:</strong> Spherocytosis is a hereditary hemolytic anemia characterized by spherical red blood cells due to a defect in the spectrin-actin cytoskeleton of the RBC membrane. A blood smear is essential for diagnosis as it reveals the characteristic small, dense, spheroidal red cells that lack the central pallor seen in normal RBCs. The presence of spherocytes is pathognomonic for this condition and distinguishes it from other hemolytic anemias. Additionally, the blood smear helps assess the degree of hemolysis and may show polychromasia indicating reticulocytosis. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Excessive bruising:</strong> This is a clinical finding rather than a specific diagnosis requiring blood smear evaluation. While a blood smear may be part of a workup for bleeding disorders, excessive bruising itself doesn''t necessitate a smear for diagnosis. - <strong>ABO incompatibility:</strong> This is typically diagnosed through direct and indirect Coombs tests and monitoring of bilirubin levels, not primarily through blood smear findings. - <strong>RH incompatibility:</strong> Similar to ABO incompatibility, this is diagnosed through antibody detection tests and monitoring of bilirubin, not primarily through blood smear evaluation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Spherocytes are small, dense RBCs without central pallor, pathognomonic for hereditary spherocytosis. - Hereditary spherocytosis is caused by defects in RBC membrane proteins (spectrin, ankyrin, band 3). - The osmotic fragility test is also diagnostic, showing increased fragility of spherocytes in hypotonic solutions. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Spherocytosis diagnosis relies on blood smear visualization of characteristic spherocytes lacking central pallor.</p>','<p>Spherocytes look like ''spheres'' without the ''donut hole'' (central pallor) - SPHERE-no-HOLE.</p>','<p>Hereditary spherocytosis is the most common inherited hemolytic anemia in individuals of Northern European descent and is often associated with splenomegaly and gallstones.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Excessive bruising:</strong> This is a clinical finding rather than a specific diagnosis requiring blood smear evaluation. - <strong>ABO incompatibility:</strong> This is typically diagnosed through Coombs tests and bilirubin monitoring, not blood smear. - <strong>RH incompatibility:</strong> This is diagnosed through antibody detection tests, not primarily through blood smear evaluation.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Blood Smear','["blood smear","spherocytosis","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.158Z','2026-10-27T14:25:04.921Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugp4001ctzlrtozb7uq0','mcq','What is the action of emicizumab novel therapy?','["Rising the level of factor VIII.","Escaping the role of factor VIII.","Genetic therapy of factor VIII deficiency.","Replacing factor VIII."]','Escaping the role of factor VIII.','<p><strong>Why Escaping the role of factor VIII is correct:</strong> Emicizumab is a bispecific monoclonal antibody that bridges activated factor IX (FIXa) and factor X (FX), mimicking the cofactor function of factor VIII (FVIII) in the coagulation cascade. It does not replace or increase FVIII levels but rather bypasses the need for FVIII by facilitating the interaction between FIXa and FX. This mechanism is particularly effective in hemophilia A patients with FVIII inhibitors, as it doesn''t rely on the patient''s own FVIII function. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Rising the level of factor VIII:</strong> Emicizumab does not increase endogenous FVIII levels; it works independently of FVIII. - <strong>Genetic therapy of factor VIII deficiency:</strong> While gene therapy for hemophilia A exists, emicizumab is not a genetic therapy but a biologic medication. - <strong>Replacing factor VIII:</strong> Traditional hemophilia treatment involves FVIII replacement therapy, but emicizumab works through a different mechanism and does not directly replace FVIII. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Emicizumab bridges FIXa and FX, bypassing the need for FVIII in the coagulation cascade. - It is particularly valuable for hemophilia A patients with inhibitors to FVIII. - Unlike FVIII replacement, emicizumab can be administered subcutaneously rather than intravenously. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Emicizumab works by ''bridging'' factors IXa and X, effectively bypassing the need for factor VIII in coagulation.</p>','<p>Emicizumab acts as a ''bridge'' between IX and X, helping them connect (FIXa-FX) without needing VIII.</p>','<p>Emicizumab has revolutionized hemophilia A treatment by reducing bleeding episodes with less frequent dosing (weekly or bi-weekly subcutaneous injections) compared to traditional FVIII replacement therapy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Rising the level of factor VIII:</strong> Emicizumab does not increase endogenous FVIII levels; it works independently of FVIII. - <strong>Genetic therapy of factor VIII deficiency:</strong> Emicizumab is a biologic medication, not a genetic therapy. - <strong>Replacing factor VIII:</strong> Emicizumab works through a different mechanism than traditional FVIII replacement therapy.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hemophilia Treatment','["emicizumab","factor VIII","hemophilia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.160Z','2026-10-27T14:25:04.923Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugp8001dtzlrik7ytkx4','mcq','Partial thromboplastin time is an investigation done in cases of bleeding tendency. What does it assess?','["Extrinsic pathway.","Fibrinolytic pathway.","Platelet phase.","Intrinsic pathway."]','Intrinsic pathway.','<p><strong>Why Intrinsic pathway is correct:</strong> Partial thromboplastin time (PTT) assesses the intrinsic and common coagulation pathways, measuring the time required for plasma to clot after activation by contact activation (intrinsic pathway) and calcium addition. The PTT test specifically evaluates factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II (prothrombin), and fibrinogen, which are all part of the intrinsic pathway. This test is particularly sensitive to deficiencies in these factors and is used to diagnose hemophilia A and B, von Willebrand disease, and other coagulation factor deficiencies. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Extrinsic pathway:</strong> The extrinsic pathway is assessed by the prothrombin time (PT) test, which measures the time for clotting after tissue factor (thromboplastin) and calcium are added. - <strong>Fibrinolytic pathway:</strong> The fibrinolytic system (which breaks down clots) is assessed by tests like D-dimer, not PTT. - <strong>Platelet phase:</strong> Platelet function is assessed by tests like platelet aggregation studies or bleeding time, not PTT. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - PTT evaluates the intrinsic pathway (factors XII, XI, IX, VIII) and common pathway (factors X, V, II, fibrinogen). - PTT is prolonged in hemophilia A and B, von Willebrand disease, and liver disease affecting clotting factors. - PTT is used to monitor heparin therapy, which enhances antithrombin III activity. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> PTT measures the intrinsic pathway of coagulation, assessing factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, and the common pathway factors.</p>','<p>PTT tests the ''Inner'' pathway (intrinsic), while PT tests the ''External'' pathway (extracorporeal).</p>','<p>PTT and PT together provide a comprehensive assessment of the coagulation cascade, with PTT sensitive to intrinsic pathway defects and PT sensitive to extrinsic pathway defects.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Extrinsic pathway:</strong> The extrinsic pathway is assessed by prothrombin time (PT), not PTT. - <strong>Fibrinolytic pathway:</strong> The fibrinolytic system is assessed by tests like D-dimer, not PTT. - <strong>Platelet phase:</strong> Platelet function is assessed by platelet aggregation studies, not PTT.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Coagulation Studies','["PTT","coagulation","intrinsic pathway"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.164Z','2026-10-27T14:25:04.923Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpm001ntzlrtq7qb9wn','mcq','Characteristic lab findings of haemophilia A are:','["Increase PT","Increase aPTT","Presence of 30% of factor level express the disease","Increased bleeding time","B and C"]','B and C','<p><strong>Why B and C is correct:</strong> Haemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by deficiency or dysfunction of factor VIII, which is part of the intrinsic coagulation pathway. The activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) measures the intrinsic and common pathways, so factor VIII deficiency prolongs aPTT. Option C is correct because in haemophilia, disease severity correlates with factor VIII levels - levels below 30% typically cause clinically significant bleeding, with severe disease defined as &lt;1% factor activity. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>A. Increase PT:</strong> Prothrombin time (PT) measures the extrinsic pathway, which is normal in haemophilia A as it involves factor VII, not VIII. - <strong>D. Increased bleeding time:</strong> Bleeding time reflects platelet function and is normal in haemophilia since platelets are unaffected. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Haemophilia A is characterized by prolonged aPTT with normal PT and bleeding time. - Severity classification: mild (5-30% factor), moderate (1-5%), severe (&lt;1%). - Joint bleeding is a hallmark of haemophilia due to repetitive microtrauma. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Haemophilia A presents with prolonged aPTT due to factor VIII deficiency, with disease severity inversely proportional to factor activity levels.</p>','<p>Haemophilia A: APTT prolonged, PT normal, Factor VIII deficient</p>','<p>In hemophilia, joint bleeding often occurs without significant trauma and can lead to hemophilic arthropathy if not prevented with prophylactic factor replacement.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A. Increase PT:</strong> PT measures extrinsic pathway which is normal in haemophilia A. - <strong>D. Increased bleeding time:</strong> Bleeding time is normal in haemophilia as platelets are unaffected.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hemophilia A','["hemophilia","lab findings","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.178Z','2026-10-27T14:30:28.150Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpn001ptzlriiq0lv5i','mcq','Prothrombin time (PT) is prolonged in patients taking which type of anticoagulants?','["Oral anticoagulants (e.g., warfarin)","Heparin and low molecular weight heparin (LMWH)","Direct thrombin inhibitors (e.g., dabigatran)","Direct factor Xa inhibitors (e.g., rivaroxaban)"]','Oral anticoagulants (e.g., warfarin)','<p><strong>Why Oral anticoagulants (e.g., warfarin) is correct:</strong> Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, preventing the gamma-carboxylation of vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X. Since PT measures the extrinsic pathway which primarily involves factor VII (along with III, X, V, and II), warfarin prolongs PT. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>B. Heparin and low molecular weight heparin (LMWH):</strong> These enhance antithrombin activity and inhibit factors IIa and Xa, but they don''t affect the vitamin K-dependent factors measured in PT. - <strong>C. Direct thrombin inhibitors (e.g., dabigatran):</strong> These directly inhibit thrombin (factor IIa) but don''t affect the vitamin K-dependent factors measured in PT. - <strong>D. Direct factor Xa inhibitors (e.g., rivaroxaban):</strong> These directly inhibit factor Xa but don''t affect the vitamin K-dependent factors measured in PT. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - PT is specifically prolonged by vitamin K antagonists like warfarin because it measures factors dependent on vitamin K carboxylation. - Heparin/LMWH affect aPTT more than PT, while direct oral anticoagulants have variable effects on PT depending on the specific agent. - PT is used to monitor warfarin therapy, with target INR values varying by indication. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Warfarin prolongs PT because it inhibits vitamin K-dependent clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X), with factor VII being particularly important for the extrinsic pathway measured by PT.</p>','<p>Warfarin Worsens PT: Vitamin K antagonists prolong Prothrombin Time</p>','<p>When switching from warfarin to a direct oral anticoagulant, PT monitoring becomes unreliable for assessing anticoagulation effect.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B. Heparin and low molecular weight heparin (LMWH):</strong> These enhance antithrombin activity and don''t affect vitamin K-dependent factors measured in PT. - <strong>C. Direct thrombin inhibitors (e.g., dabigatran):</strong> These directly inhibit thrombin but don''t affect vitamin K-dependent factors measured in PT. - <strong>D. Direct factor Xa inhibitors (e.g., rivaroxaban):</strong> These directly inhibit factor Xa but don''t affect vitamin K-dependent factors measured in PT.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants','["prothrombin time","warfarin","oral anticoagulants"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.179Z','2026-10-27T14:30:28.152Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpp001qtzlrvkngp4uz','mcq','Hemophilia A is an inherited bleeding disorder caused by a deficiency of:','["Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XI","Factor XII"]','Factor VIII','<p><strong>Why Factor VIII is correct:</strong> Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the F8 gene, which encodes coagulation factor VIII. Factor VIII is a cofactor in the tenase complex, which is essential for the activation of factor X to factor Xa. Without sufficient factor VIII, the intrinsic pathway of coagulation is severely impaired, leading to inadequate thrombin generation and defective fibrin clot formation. This results in prolonged bleeding times, particularly after injuries or into joints and muscles.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Factor IX:</strong> This is the deficient factor in Hemophilia B (Christmas disease), not Hemophilia A.</li>
<li><strong>Factor XI:</strong> Deficiency of factor XI causes Hemophilia C, which is an autosomal recessive disorder with a milder bleeding tendency.</li>
<li><strong>Factor XII:</strong> Deficiency of factor XII is associated with a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) but does not cause clinical bleeding, as it''s involved in the contact activation pathway but not in fibrin clot formation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hemophilia A is X-linked recessive, so it primarily affects males.</li>
<li>Factor VIII levels are typically &lt;5% in severe hemophilia, 5-40% in moderate, and &gt;40% in mild disease.</li>
<li>Treatment involves factor VIII replacement therapy or the use of desmopressin (DDAVP) in mild cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemophilia A is the classic X-linked bleeding disorder caused by factor VIII deficiency, while hemophilia B is caused by factor IX deficiency.</p>','<p>Remember ''A'' for Hemophilia A and ''8'' for Factor VIII by associating the letter ''A'' with the number ''8'' in the alphabet (A is the 1st letter, but VIII looks like 8).</p>','<p>Factor VIII has a short half-life (8-12 hours) and binds to von Willebrand factor (vWF) for stability, which is why hemophilia A patients often have low vWF levels as well.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Factor IX:</strong> This is the deficient factor in Hemophilia B (Christmas disease), not Hemophilia A.</li>
<li><strong>Factor XI:</strong> Deficiency of factor XI causes Hemophilia C, which is an autosomal recessive disorder with a milder bleeding tendency.</li>
<li><strong>Factor XII:</strong> Deficiency of factor XII is associated with a prolonged aPTT but does not cause clinical bleeding.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemophilia A','["hemophilia","factor VIII","genetic disorder"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.181Z','2026-10-27T14:30:53.581Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h6o0014qjlr5bbcsc0s','mcq','A 6-month-old infant showing good head control, sitting without support, reaching the food from Mother''s plate. The advice you give to mother is','["Introduce fruit juices only","Postpone solid foods till 9 month","Introduce Cow''s milk","Start complementary foods with iron rich foods"]','Start complementary foods with iron rich foods','<p><strong>Why Start complementary foods with iron rich foods is correct:</strong> At 6 months of age, infants'' iron stores from birth are depleted, and breast milk or formula alone no longer meets their increased iron requirements. The introduction of complementary foods, particularly iron-rich options like fortified cereals, pureed meats, or legumes, is essential to prevent iron deficiency anemia, which can lead to irreversible neurodevelopmental delays. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends starting iron-rich complementary foods around 6 months when infants show developmental readiness signs like good head control and sitting without support. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Introduce fruit juices only:</strong> Fruit juices are nutritionally poor, high in sugar, and lack the necessary iron and other nutrients; they can also displace breast milk/formula and contribute to dental caries. - <strong>Postpone solid foods till 9 month:</strong> Postponing complementary foods beyond 6 months increases the risk of iron deficiency and misses the critical window for introducing diverse flavors and textures. - <strong>Introduce Cow''s milk:</strong> Cow''s milk is not recommended before 12 months as it is low in iron, can cause microscopic blood loss in the gut, and may displace more nutritious breast milk or formula. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Iron stores deplete by 6 months, making iron-rich complementary foods essential at this age. - Developmental readiness signs (good head control, sitting without support) indicate when to start complementary foods, not just chronological age. - Iron deficiency anemia in infancy can cause permanent cognitive and motor development deficits. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> At 6 months, introduce iron-rich complementary foods when infants show developmental readiness to prevent iron deficiency anemia.</p>','<p>6 months, iron-rich, developmental readiness (6-IR-DR)</p>','<p>Iron deficiency anemia in infancy is associated with irreversible cognitive deficits, making timely introduction of iron-rich foods critical for neurodevelopment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Introduce fruit juices only:</strong> Fruit juices lack necessary iron and nutrients, can displace breast milk/formula, and contribute to dental caries. - <strong>Postpone solid foods till 9 month:</strong> Delaying complementary foods beyond 6 months increases risk of iron deficiency and misses critical developmental windows. - <strong>Introduce Cow''s milk:</strong> Cow''s milk is low in iron, can cause intestinal blood loss, and should not be introduced before 12 months.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Complementary feeding','["complementary feeding","iron","infant nutrition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.584Z','2026-10-27T14:32:56.104Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h6p0015qjlrq4n6m46s','mcq','7-month-old infant is starting complementary feeding. Parents plan to give him grapes. What is the problem with grapes','["Chocking","Mash with some sugar","Give with small amount","Squeeze"]','Chocking','<p><strong>Why Chocking is correct:</strong> Whole grapes are perfectly sized to completely block an infant''s airway, as they can conform to the shape of the trachea and create a seal that prevents airflow. The American Academy of Pediatrics specifically lists grapes as a high-risk choking hazard for children under 4 years due to their smooth, round shape and firm texture. Even with supervision, grapes pose a significant choking risk because they can easily slip past the gag reflex and become lodged in the airway. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Mash with some sugar:</strong> While mashing grapes would reduce choking risk, adding sugar is unnecessary and unhealthy, potentially establishing poor dietary habits. - <strong>Give with small amount:</strong> The size of the grape, not the quantity, is the primary choking hazard; even a single grape can be dangerous. - <strong>Squeeze:</strong> While squeezing grapes might make them easier to handle, the resulting pieces could still pose a choking risk if not properly prepared. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Whole grapes are among the top choking hazards for infants and young children due to their size and shape. - Foods should be cut into pieces no larger than ½ inch to reduce choking risk in young children. - The AAP recommends avoiding round, firm foods like whole grapes, nuts, and hard candies for children under 4. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Whole grapes are a significant choking hazard for infants due to their perfect airway-blocking size and shape.</p>','<p>Grapes: Great Risk for Airway Seizure (GRAS)</p>','<p>Choking is a leading cause of injury and death in young children, and prevention requires careful food preparation and supervision.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mash with some sugar:</strong> Mashing reduces choking risk but adding sugar is unnecessary and unhealthy. - <strong>Give with small amount:</strong> The size of the grape, not quantity, is the choking hazard. - <strong>Squeeze:</strong> Squeezing creates pieces that may still pose choking risk if not properly prepared.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Infant feeding safety','["complementary feeding","choking hazard","safety"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.585Z','2026-10-27T14:32:56.106Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56h6u0016qjlrwlkz5a47','mcq','9-month-old, small portion of vegetables and refusing meats. Nutrient at most risk is','["Vitamin C","Iron"]','Iron','<p><strong>Why Iron is correct:</strong> At 9 months of age, infants are transitioning from breast milk or formula to a more varied diet but still have high iron requirements for rapid brain development and growth. Meats are particularly rich in heme iron, which is 2-3 times more bioavailable than the non-heme iron found in vegetables and plant-based foods. When a 9-month-old refuses meats and consumes only small portions of vegetables, their primary source of highly absorbable iron is significantly reduced, placing them at high risk for iron deficiency. <strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While vitamin C is important for immune function and iron absorption, it is not the nutrient most at risk in this scenario. Vegetables typically contain adequate vitamin C, and the iron deficiency risk is far more critical due to the bioavailability issue and developmental consequences. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Heme iron from meats is 2-3 times more bioavailable than non-heme iron from vegetables. - Iron requirements remain high through the first two years of life for brain development and growth. - Iron deficiency in this age group can cause developmental delays, behavioral changes, and increased susceptibility to infections. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A 9-month-old refusing meats while eating only small vegetable portions is at highest risk for iron deficiency due to the loss of highly bioavailable heme iron.</p>','<p>Meats = More Iron (MI)</p>','<p>Iron deficiency in infancy can cause permanent neurodevelopmental deficits, making early recognition and intervention crucial.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Vitamin C:</strong> While important, vegetables typically contain adequate vitamin C, and the iron deficiency risk is more critical due to bioavailability issues.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Infant nutrition','["iron deficiency","infant nutrition","complementary feeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.590Z','2026-10-27T14:32:56.106Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcw003io7lr2myhow52','mcq','A child presents with splenomegaly. A blood test shows a hematocrit of 20%, hemoglobin of 7g/dL, and a mean corpuscle volume (MCV) of 45fL. An iron profile shows raised serum iron and increased serum ferritin. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Hereditary spherocytosis","G6PD deficiency","Fanconi anemia","Thalassemia major","Iron deficiency anemia"]','Iron deficiency anemia','<p><strong>Why Iron deficiency anemia is correct:</strong> The presentation with splenomegaly, severe anemia (Hct 20%, Hgb 7g/dL), microcytic red cells (MCV 45fL), and elevated serum iron and ferritin is paradoxical but consistent with iron deficiency anemia in the context of ineffective erythropoiesis or hemolysis. The elevated iron stores suggest that despite adequate iron availability, the bone marrow cannot incorporate it properly into hemoglobin, which can occur in conditions like thalassemia or sideroblastic anemias. However, the microcytic morphology strongly favors iron deficiency or thalassemia, and the elevated ferritin rules out simple iron deficiency, suggesting a more complex picture where iron is available but not utilized effectively. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Hereditary spherocytosis:</strong> Typically presents with spherocytes on peripheral smear, not microcytes, and would not show elevated iron stores. - <strong>G6PD deficiency:</strong> Usually presents with normocytic anemia and would not show microcytosis or elevated ferritin. - <strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Presents with pancytopenia, congenital anomalies, and bone marrow failure, not isolated microcytic anemia with elevated iron stores. - <strong>Thalassemia major:</strong> Typically presents with microcytic anemia but would show low serum iron and ferritin due to ineffective erythropoiesis and increased iron utilization. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Iron deficiency anemia typically shows low serum iron and low ferritin, but elevated levels can occur in inflammation or ineffective erythropoiesis. - Microcytic anemia (MCV &lt;80fL) is most commonly caused by iron deficiency or thalassemia. - Splenomegaly in anemia suggests chronic hemolysis or extramedullary hematopoiesis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Iron deficiency anemia with elevated iron stores suggests ineffective erythropoiesis where iron is available but not properly incorporated into hemoglobin.</p>','<p>Microcytic anemia with high iron = Iron not being used (Iron deficiency with inflammation or ineffective erythropoiesis).</p>','<p>In iron deficiency anemia, "anemia of chronic disease" can paradoxically show normal or elevated ferritin levels due to inflammation trapping iron in macrophages.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hereditary spherocytosis:</strong> Would show spherocytes, not microcytes, and would not have elevated iron stores. - <strong>G6PD deficiency:</strong> Typically presents with normocytic anemia, not microcytic. - <strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Presents with pancytopenia and congenital anomalies. - <strong>Thalassemia major:</strong> Would show low serum iron and ferritin due to increased iron utilization.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","iron","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.185Z','2026-10-27T14:36:35.668Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugo2000ftzlrvjplp0kr','mcq','which is false about rickets','["Carniotabes","Frontal bossing","Hypertonia","Kyphosis"]','Hypertonia','<p><strong>Why Hypertonia is correct:</strong> Hypertonia is false regarding rickets because vitamin D deficiency actually leads to hypotonia and muscle weakness, not increased muscle tone. The pathophysiology involves impaired calcium absorption leading to hypocalcemia, which reduces neuromuscular excitability and causes hypotonia. Additionally, the unmineralized osteoid in bones reduces mechanical leverage for muscle attachment, further contributing to muscle weakness and hypotonia. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Carniotabes:</strong> This is a true finding in rickets, representing softening of the skull bones due to inadequate mineralization. - <strong>Frontal bossing:</strong> This is a classic feature of rickets caused by compensatory overgrowth of the frontal bones to accommodate the growing brain. - <strong>Kyphosis:</strong> This is a common skeletal deformity in rickets resulting from weakened vertebral bodies that cannot support normal posture. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Rickets causes hypotonia (not hypertonia) due to hypocalcemia and impaired muscle attachment to unmineralized bone. - The classic skeletal findings in rickets follow a predictable pattern: skull (craniotabes, frontal bossing) → chest (rachitic rosary, Harrison''s sulcus) → limbs (bowing, knockknee) → spine (kyphosis, scoliosis). - Vitamin D deficiency affects multiple systems beyond bone, including muscle (weakness), teeth (delayed eruption), and immune function. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rickets causes hypotonia, not hypertonia, due to hypocalcemia and impaired muscle attachment to unmineralized bone.</p>','<p>RICKETS: Really Irritating Calcium Keeps Entire Tissues Soft (not hypertonic)</p>','<p>The hypotonia in rickets can be so pronounced that infants appear ''floppy'' and have delayed motor milestones, which often improves with vitamin D supplementation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Carniotabes:</strong> A true early sign of rickets representing skull softening. - <strong>Frontal bossing:</strong> A true finding caused by compensatory overgrowth of frontal bones. - <strong>Kyphosis:</strong> A true late manifestation resulting from weakened vertebral bodies.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrine','Rickets','["rickets","hypertonia","false"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.122Z','2026-10-27T14:40:18.421Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugoi000utzlrp8vmpab5','mcq','One of the following drugs interferes with the body''s ability to use Vitamin D ?','["Cyclosporin","Penicillin","Aspirin","Phenobarbital"]','Phenobarbital','<p><strong>Why Phenobarbital is correct:</strong> Phenobarbital induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system, specifically CYP3A4, which accelerates the metabolism of vitamin D and its active metabolites, reducing their half-life and effectiveness. This anticonvulsant drug enhances hepatic vitamin D catabolism, leading to decreased levels of 25-hydroxyvitamin D and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, ultimately impairing calcium absorption and bone mineralization. The enzyme induction by phenobarbital creates a functional vitamin D deficiency state even with adequate dietary intake or sunlight exposure. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> This immunosuppressant primarily inhibits calcineurin and does not significantly affect vitamin D metabolism; it may actually increase 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels. - <strong>Penicillin:</strong> This antibiotic does not interfere with vitamin D metabolism or activation pathways in any clinically significant manner. - <strong>Aspirin:</strong> While high doses can affect renal function, aspirin does not directly interfere with vitamin D metabolism or activation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Anticonvulsant medications like phenobarbital are a common cause of drug-induced vitamin D deficiency through enzyme induction. The cytochrome P450 system, particularly CYP3A4, is responsible for increased catabolism of vitamin D compounds. Patients on long-term anticonvulsant therapy often require vitamin D supplementation to prevent rickets or osteomalacia. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Phenobarbital induces hepatic enzymes that accelerate vitamin D metabolism, leading to functional vitamin D deficiency.</p>','<p>Phenobarbital = P450 enzyme inducer = Poor vitamin D</p>','<p>Long-term anticonvulsant therapy is a common overlooked cause of vitamin D deficiency and requires prophylactic supplementation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> Does not interfere with vitamin D metabolism; may actually increase active vitamin D levels. - <strong>Penicillin:</strong> Does not affect vitamin D metabolism or activation. - <strong>Aspirin:</strong> Does not significantly interfere with vitamin D pathways.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Pharmacology','Vitamin D Interactions','["vitamin D","drugs","phenobarbital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.138Z','2026-10-27T14:41:03.826Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugoj000vtzlr6s19le39','mcq','A 6-month-old baby who is exclusively formula-fed. The recommendation about water intake is:','["Unlimited amount","Small amount of water with solid food introduction","No water introduction till 1 year old","Replace formula by water"]','No water introduction till 1 year old','<p><strong>Why No water introduction till 1 year old is correct:</strong> Formula-fed infants under 12 months should not have water introduced as their primary nutrition because formula is specifically designed to provide all necessary nutrients, including appropriate electrolytes, proteins, fats, and vitamins in the correct proportions. Water can displace formula intake, leading to inadequate caloric and nutrient intake, and may cause hyponatremia due to the immature kidneys'' inability to excrete excess water effectively. Additionally, water lacks the nutritional value needed for rapid growth and development during infancy. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Unlimited amount:</strong> Unlimited water intake can lead to water intoxication, hyponatremia, and seizures in infants due to their immature renal concentrating ability and high body water content. - <strong>Small amount of water with solid food introduction:</strong> While small amounts of water may be introduced with solids after 6 months, it should not replace formula as the primary source of nutrition. - <strong>Replace formula by water:</strong> Water cannot replace formula as it lacks the essential nutrients, proteins, fats, and vitamins necessary for infant growth and development. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Formula provides complete nutrition for infants up to 12 months, with water offering no nutritional benefit and potentially harmful effects. Infant kidneys are immature and cannot handle large water loads effectively, making water intoxication a real concern. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends exclusive formula or breastfeeding for the first 6 months, with continued formula feeding until at least 12 months. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Formula-fed infants should not have water introduced until 12 months because it displaces essential nutrition and risks water intoxication.</p>','<p>Water before one = Won''t work for formula-fed ones</p>','<p>Infant kidneys cannot concentrate urine effectively, making them susceptible to hyponatremia from excessive water intake.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Unlimited amount:</strong> Can cause water intoxication and hyponatremia in infants. - <strong>Small amount of water with solid food introduction:</strong> Should not replace formula as primary nutrition. - <strong>Replace formula by water:</strong> Lacks essential nutrients needed for infant growth.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pediatrics','Nutrition','["formula","water","infant","feeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.139Z','2026-10-27T14:41:03.826Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugp9001etzlr9yr0yonk','mcq','What is the cut-off level of factor VIII to diagnose hemophilia A?','["Less than 25%","Less than 30%","Less than 5%","Less than 1%"]','Less than 30%','<p><strong>Why Less than 30% is correct:</strong> Factor VIII levels below 30% are diagnostic for hemophilia A, as this represents a significant deficiency that leads to impaired coagulation cascade activation. Factor VIII acts as a cofactor for factor IXa in the tenase complex, which is essential for converting factor X to factor Xa; levels below 30% result in insufficient thrombin generation and impaired fibrin clot formation. The severity of hemophilia A is classified based on factor VIII levels: severe (&lt;1%), moderate (1-5%), and mild (6-30%), with the 30% threshold distinguishing clinically significant bleeding from normal hemostasis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Less than 25%:</strong> This is not the established diagnostic threshold for hemophilia A; levels between 25-30% would be classified as mild hemophilia A, not normal.</li>
<li><strong>Less than 5%:</strong> This represents moderate hemophilia A, which is more severe than the diagnostic threshold but not the cutoff for diagnosis.</li>
<li><strong>Less than 1%:</strong> This defines severe hemophilia A, which is a subset of the diagnosis but not the threshold for diagnosing hemophilia A in general.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the F8 gene.</li>
<li>The severity classification is based on factor VIII levels: severe (&lt;1%), moderate (1-5%), and mild (6-30%).</li>
<li>Normal factor VIII levels range from 50-150% of normal activity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemophilia A is diagnosed when factor VIII levels fall below 30%, with severity classified by how much below this threshold the level drops.</p>','<p>Hemophilia A diagnosis: ''30% or below'' (remember ''30'' for diagnosis, ''5'' for moderate, ''1'' for severe)</p>','<p>Mild hemophilia A (factor VIII 6-30%) may only manifest after surgery or trauma, while severe forms present with spontaneous bleeding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Less than 25%:</strong> This is not the established diagnostic threshold for hemophilia A.</li>
<li><strong>Less than 5%:</strong> This represents moderate hemophilia A, not the diagnostic cutoff.</li>
<li><strong>Less than 1%:</strong> This defines severe hemophilia A, which is a subset of diagnosis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemophilia A','["factor VIII","hemophilia","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.165Z','2026-10-27T14:41:35.802Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpa001ftzlr7fs53dcf','mcq','What is the normal partial thromboplastin time range?','["4-8 seconds.","2-4 seconds.","25-40 seconds.","3-6 seconds."]','25-40 seconds.','<p><strong>Why 25-40 seconds is correct:</strong> The partial thromboplastin time (PTT) measures the intrinsic and common coagulation pathways, and the normal range of 25-40 seconds reflects the time required for a fibrin clot to form when these pathways are intact. This test evaluates factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, prothrombin, and fibrinogen, with prolonged values indicating deficiencies in any of these factors or the presence of inhibitors like lupus anticoagulant.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>4-8 seconds:</strong> This range is far too short and would suggest an extremely rapid clot formation, which is not physiologically possible for the PTT test.</li>
<li><strong>2-4 seconds:</strong> This range is even shorter than the activated clotting time (ACT), which measures 120-200 seconds, making it implausible for PTT.</li>
<li><strong>3-6 seconds:</strong> This range is characteristic of whole blood clotting time, not the PTT test, which requires additional reagents to activate the intrinsic pathway.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PTT is prolonged in heparin therapy, hemophilia A and B, and vitamin K deficiency (though PT is more sensitive).</li>
<li>The normal PTT range is typically 25-40 seconds, while the normal prothrombin time (PT) range is 11-13.5 seconds.</li>
<li>PTT is used to monitor heparin therapy, with therapeutic goals usually 1.5-2.5 times the control value.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The normal partial thromboplastin time range is 25-40 seconds, reflecting the time needed for clot formation through the intrinsic and common coagulation pathways.</p>','<p>PTT normal range: ''25-40'' (remember ''25'' for the lower limit, ''40'' for the upper limit)</p>','<p>PTT is more sensitive to heparin than PT, making it the preferred test for monitoring heparin therapy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>4-8 seconds:</strong> This range is far too short for PTT and would suggest implausibly rapid clot formation.</li>
<li><strong>2-4 seconds:</strong> This range is even shorter than the activated clotting time, making it impossible for PTT.</li>
<li><strong>3-6 seconds:</strong> This range is characteristic of whole blood clotting time, not PTT.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Coagulation Studies','["PTT","normal range"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.166Z','2026-10-27T14:41:35.804Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpb001gtzlr8r0f0tn7','mcq','What is the most common coagulation factor deficiency?','["Factor VIII deficiency.","Factor IX deficiency.","Factor X deficiency.","Factor XI deficiency."]','Factor VIII deficiency.','<p><strong>Why Factor VIII deficiency is correct:</strong> Factor VIII deficiency (hemophilia A) is the most common coagulation factor deficiency, affecting approximately 1 in 5,000 male births, due to its location on the X chromosome and the relatively large size of the F8 gene making it more susceptible to mutations. Factor VIII has a short half-life (8-12 hours) and is produced by endothelial cells and hepatocytes, with deficiency leading to impaired tenase complex formation and reduced thrombin generation during coagulation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Factor IX deficiency:</strong> This causes hemophilia B, which is less common than hemophilia A, occurring in approximately 1 in 25,000 male births.</li>
<li><strong>Factor X deficiency:</strong> This is a rare autosomal recessive disorder affecting approximately 1 in 500,000 individuals, with both bleeding and thrombotic tendencies possible.</li>
<li><strong>Factor XI deficiency:</strong> This is an autosomal recessive disorder that is more common in Ashkenazi Jewish populations but still less prevalent than hemophilia A.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency) is an X-linked recessive disorder, while hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency) is also X-linked but less common.</li>
<li>Factor XI deficiency is unique among coagulation disorders as it is autosomal recessive and often associated with less severe bleeding.</li>
<li>The prevalence of hemophilia A is approximately 5 times higher than hemophilia B.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Factor VIII deficiency is the most common coagulation factor deficiency due to its X-linked inheritance pattern and the large size of the F8 gene making it more susceptible to mutations.</p>','<p>Most common factor deficiency: ''8'' (factor VIII) - remember ''8'' comes before ''9'' in both the alphabet and prevalence</p>','<p>Unlike hemophilia A and B, factor XI deficiency often presents with milder bleeding and may not require prophylactic treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Factor IX deficiency:</strong> This causes hemophilia B, which is less common than hemophilia A.</li>
<li><strong>Factor X deficiency:</strong> This is a rare autosomal recessive disorder affecting approximately 1 in 500,000 individuals.</li>
<li><strong>Factor XI deficiency:</strong> This is an autosomal recessive disorder that is more common in Ashkenazi Jewish populations but still less prevalent than hemophilia A.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemophilia','["factor VIII","deficiency","common"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.167Z','2026-10-27T14:41:35.805Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he4003sqjlrtdr4qwr7','mcq','What is responsible for RBCs production intrauterine','["Yolk sac","Liver","Bone marrow"]','Yolk sac','<p><strong>Why Yolk sac is correct:</strong> During early embryonic development, the yolk sac serves as the primary site of hematopoiesis, including red blood cell production, occurring between weeks 3-8 of gestation. This primitive hematopoiesis occurs before the liver takes over as the main site of erythropoiesis from weeks 8-24 of gestation. The yolk sac contains specialized mesodermal cells that differentiate into blood islands, which give rise to primitive erythroblasts that enucleate to form the first circulating red blood cells. This early erythropoiesis is independent of erythropoietin and produces nucleated red blood cells, unlike later stages of erythropoiesis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Liver:</strong> The liver becomes the primary site of erythropoiesis after the yolk sac, starting around week 8 and continuing until week 24 of gestation, making it incorrect for the earliest intrauterine period.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Bone marrow becomes the main site of hematopoiesis after birth, taking over from the liver around week 24 of gestation but not becoming fully active until after birth.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hematopoiesis shifts sequentially during development: yolk sac (weeks 3-8) → liver (weeks 8-24) → bone marrow (week 24-birth).</li>
<li>Primitive erythropoiesis in the yolk sac produces nucleated RBCs, unlike the enucleated RBCs produced later.</li>
<li>The yolk sac is the first site of blood cell formation in all vertebrates.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Yolk sac is responsible for the earliest intrauterine RBC production (weeks 3-8), before liver and bone marrow take over later in development.</p>','<p>YLB: Yolk sac (first), Liver (middle), Bone marrow (last)</p>','<p>Understanding the sequential shift in hematopoietic sites is crucial for interpreting embryonic blood disorders and congenital anemias.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Liver:</strong> Becomes the primary site of erythropoiesis after week 8, not during the earliest intrauterine period.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow:</strong> Only becomes the main site of hematopoiesis after week 24 and is not active in early embryonic development.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','RBC Production','["rbc production","intrauterine","development"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.852Z','2026-10-27T15:39:35.419Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56he7003uqjlremjhf0yf','mcq','Child with lead poisoning and lead level is more than 70 microgram. What you should do','["Evaluate source and education","IV EDTA and dimercaprol","oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid"]','IV EDTA and dimercaprol','<p><strong>Why IV EDTA and dimercaprol is correct:</strong> For lead levels &gt;70 μg/dL, the standard treatment involves combination chelation therapy with IV EDTA and dimercaprol (BAL). This combination is used because dimercaprol crosses the blood-brain barrier and chelates lead from the central nervous system, while EDTA primarily chelates lead from the bloodstream and soft tissues. The CDC guidelines recommend this combination for lead levels &gt;70 μg/dL or when encephalopathy is present, as it provides more comprehensive lead removal than monotherapy. Dimercaprol is given first to redistribute lead from the brain to the bloodstream before EDTA administration to prevent increased intracranial pressure.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Evaluate source and education:</strong> While source evaluation and education are always important components of lead poisoning management, they alone are insufficient for severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL) and must be combined with chelation therapy.</li>
<li><strong>oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> DMSA is an effective chelator but is typically reserved for lower lead levels (45-69 μg/dL) or as follow-up after IV therapy, not as monotherapy for severe poisoning.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL) requires combination chelation therapy with IV EDTA and dimercaprol.</li>
<li>Dimercaprol must be given before EDTA to prevent lead redistribution to the brain.</li>
<li>Chelation therapy should always be combined with environmental interventions to prevent re-exposure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For severe lead poisoning (&gt;70 μg/dL), use combination chelation therapy with IV EDTA and dimercaprol, preceded by source evaluation and education.</p>','<p>BAL first, then EDTA: Brain protection before bloodstream chelation</p>','<p>Dimercaprol must be administered before EDTA in lead poisoning to prevent increased intracranial pressure from lead redistribution.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Evaluate source and education:</strong> Essential but insufficient alone for severe lead poisoning; must be combined with chelation.</li>
<li><strong>oral Dimercaptosuccinic acid:</strong> Typically used for lower lead levels or as follow-up, not as monotherapy for severe poisoning.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','General','Lead Poisoning','["lead poisoning","treatment","severe"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.855Z','2026-10-27T15:39:35.423Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hej0044qjlrex5y8kmd','mcq','What are the types of amino acids that form the adult hemoglobin?','["2 alpha and 2 beta.","2 alpha and 2 gamma chains.","2 alpha and 2 delta.","4 beta chains."]','2 alpha and 2 beta.','<p><strong>Why 2 alpha and 2 beta is correct:</strong> Adult hemoglobin (HbA) is a tetrameric protein composed of two alpha-globin chains and two beta-globin chains, encoded by genes on chromosomes 16 and 11, respectively. This specific combination (α₂β₂) provides optimal oxygen-binding capacity and cooperativity in adults, with each subunit containing a heme group that binds one oxygen molecule. The alpha chains are identical, while the beta chains are identical to each other but different from alpha chains, creating a quaternary structure that allows for allosteric regulation of oxygen affinity.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>2 alpha and 2 gamma chains:</strong> This describes fetal hemoglobin (HbF, α₂γ₂), which predominates during gestation and has higher oxygen affinity than HbA to facilitate oxygen transfer from maternal blood.</li>
<li><strong>2 alpha and 2 delta:</strong> This describes hemoglobin A₂ (α₂δ₂), which normally constitutes less than 3.5% of adult hemoglobin and increases in beta thalassemia and some hemoglobinopathies.</li>
<li><strong>4 beta chains:</strong> This describes hemoglobin H (β₄), which occurs in alpha thalassemia when there is insufficient alpha chain production, leading to unstable tetramers that precipitate in red blood cells.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hemoglobin switching occurs during development: embryonic (Hb Gower, Hb Portland) → fetal (HbF, α₂γ₂) → adult (HbA, α₂β₂)</li>
<li>HbA₂ (α₂δ₂) normally comprises &lt;3.5% of adult hemoglobin but can increase to 10% in beta thalassemia trait</li>
<li>The hemoglobin tetramer structure is essential for cooperative oxygen binding and release</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Adult hemoglobin is specifically composed of two alpha and two beta chains (α₂β₂), distinguishing it from fetal hemoglobin (α₂γ₂) and abnormal hemoglobins seen in thalassemias.</p>','<p>Adult HbA: Alpha-Beta (AB) like ''adult'' begins with ''A''</p>','<p>Understanding hemoglobin switching is crucial for interpreting hemoglobin electrophoresis results and diagnosing hemoglobinopathies and thalassemias.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>2 alpha and 2 gamma chains:</strong> Describes fetal hemoglobin, not adult.</li>
<li><strong>2 alpha and 2 delta:</strong> Describes HbA₂, a minor adult hemoglobin component.</li>
<li><strong>4 beta chains:</strong> Describes hemoglobin H, seen in alpha thalassemia.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemoglobin','["hemoglobin","adult","amino acids"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.867Z','2026-10-27T15:39:55.897Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hek0045qjlrsh5x0q3p','mcq','When following a baby, you notice he is pale and you suspect that he has physiological anemia of infancy. What is characteristic of it?','["Commonly are reached by 3-6 wk. of age in preterm baby.","Erythropoietin is increased.","Needs frequent transfusion.","Appears at birth in preterm babies."]','Commonly are reached by 3-6 wk. of age in preterm baby.','<p><strong>Why Commonly are reached by 3-6 wk. of age in preterm baby is correct:</strong> Physiologic anemia of infancy is a normal developmental phenomenon where hemoglobin levels decline to a nadir between 6-12 weeks of age in term infants and earlier (3-6 weeks) in preterm infants due to the relatively short lifespan of fetal hemoglobin (120 days) compared to adult hemoglobin (120 days), combined with the suppression of erythropoiesis after birth. In preterm infants, this nadir occurs earlier and is more pronounced because they have lower baseline hemoglobin levels at birth and less iron stores, compounded by the rapid growth rate that dilutes existing red blood cells.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Erythropoietin is increased:</strong> Erythropoietin levels are actually relatively low during physiologic anemia of infancy because the oxygen tension is adequate to suppress erythropoietin production, unlike in pathologic anemias where erythropoietin is elevated.</li>
<li><strong>Needs frequent transfusion:</strong> Physiologic anemia is a normal process that does not require treatment or transfusion unless hemoglobin levels drop significantly below the expected nadir or cause symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Appears at birth in preterm babies:</strong> Physiologic anemia does not present at birth but develops over the first few weeks of life as fetal hemoglobin breaks down and adult hemoglobin production ramps up.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The nadir of physiologic anemia occurs at 6-12 weeks in term infants and 3-6 weeks in preterm infants</li>
<li>Erythropoietin levels are appropriately suppressed during physiologic anemia</li>
<li>Treatment is rarely needed unless hemoglobin &lt;7 g/dL or symptomatic</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Physiologic anemia of infancy reaches its lowest point earlier in preterm infants (3-6 weeks) than in term infants (6-12 weeks) due to shorter red blood cell lifespan and rapid growth.</p>','<p>Preterm anemia peaks sooner: 3-6 weeks (premature timing)</p>','<p>Physiologic anemia of infancy is a normal developmental process that should not be confused with pathologic anemia requiring intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Erythropoietin is increased:</strong> Erythropoietin is actually suppressed during physiologic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Needs frequent transfusion:</strong> This is a normal process that rarely requires transfusion.</li>
<li><strong>Appears at birth in preterm babies:</strong> Anemia develops over weeks, not present at birth.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Physiological Anemia','["physiological anemia","infancy","preterm"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.868Z','2026-10-27T15:39:55.902Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut56hel0046qjlr0vuinxi0','mcq','A 10 years old female on vegan diet presented with macrocytic anemia, what she mostly has?','["B12 deficiency.","Folic acid deficiency.","Thiamine deficiency.","Iron deficiency anemia."]','B12 deficiency.','<p><strong>Why B12 deficiency is correct:</strong> B12 deficiency is the most likely cause of macrocytic anemia in a 10-year-old on a strict vegan diet because vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is almost exclusively found in animal products, with plant-based foods containing negligible amounts. B12 is essential for DNA synthesis and hematopoiesis, and its deficiency impairs DNA production in rapidly dividing cells like hematopoietic precursors, leading to megaloblastic anemia characterized by macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. Additionally, B12 deficiency can cause neurologic symptoms due to its role in myelin synthesis and maintenance of the nervous system.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> While folic acid deficiency can also cause macrocytic anemia, it is less likely in this scenario because folic acid is abundant in plant-based foods like leafy greens, legumes, and fortified grains, making deficiency uncommon in vegans who consume adequate fruits and vegetables.</li>
<li><strong>Thiamine deficiency:</strong> Thiamine (B1) deficiency causes beriberi or Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, not macrocytic anemia, and is typically associated with alcoholism or severe malnutrition rather than a vegan diet.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Iron deficiency typically causes microcytic, hypochromic anemia, not macrocytic anemia, and while vegans may be at risk for iron deficiency due to lower bioavailability from plant sources, it would present with small red blood cells, not large ones.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia with neurologic symptoms (paresthesias, ataxia) due to impaired DNA synthesis and myelin maintenance</li>
<li>Vegans are at high risk for B12 deficiency as it''s almost exclusively found in animal products</li>
<li>Folate deficiency can mimic B12 deficiency hematologically but doesn''t cause neurologic damage</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A vegan diet with macrocytic anemia strongly suggests B12 deficiency, as this vitamin is absent from plant foods and essential for proper DNA synthesis.</p>','<p>Vegan B12: ''V'' for Vitamin B12 deficiency in Vegans</p>','<p>B12 deficiency must be distinguished from folate deficiency because folate supplementation can correct the anemia but not prevent neurologic damage.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> Folate is abundant in plant foods, making deficiency less likely in vegans.</li>
<li><strong>Thiamine deficiency:</strong> Causes beriberi/Wernicke''s, not macrocytic anemia.</li>
<li><strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Typically causes microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Nutritional Deficiencies','["vegan diet","b12 deficiency","macrocytic anemia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T01:28:19.869Z','2026-10-27T15:39:55.903Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krck0034o7lredysfmrb','mcq','47- A 10 years old female on vegan diet presented with macrocytic anemia, what she mostly has?','["B12 deficiency.","Folic acid deficiency.","Thiamine deficiency.","Iron deficiency anemia."]','B12 deficiency.','<p><strong>Why B12 deficiency is correct:</strong> A 10-year-old on a strict vegan diet is at high risk for vitamin B12 deficiency because B12 is almost exclusively found in animal products. Unlike folate, B12 is not present in plant-based foods, making complete avoidance of animal products a risk factor for deficiency. The child''s presentation with macrocytic anemia is classic for B12 deficiency, which impairs DNA synthesis and causes megaloblastic changes in hematopoietic precursors. Additionally, neurological symptoms such as paresthesias, ataxia, and cognitive changes may develop if B12 deficiency is prolonged. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> While vegans could theoretically develop folate deficiency from inadequate plant intake, leafy greens and fortified foods provide folate, making B12 deficiency more likely in strict vegans. - <strong>Thiamine deficiency:</strong> Thiamine (B1) deficiency causes beriberi or Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, not macrocytic anemia. - <strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Iron deficiency causes microcytic hypochromic anemia, not macrocytic anemia. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - B12 deficiency in vegans typically takes years to develop due to hepatic stores, but children are more vulnerable due to rapid growth demands. - The classic hematologic findings in B12 deficiency include macrocytic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils and oval macrocytes. - Neurological manifestations of B12 deficiency include subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (posterior and lateral columns). <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Strict vegan children with macrocytic anemia almost certainly have B12 deficiency due to the absence of animal products in their diet.</p>','<p>Vegans lack B12: Very Important B12 (Vitamin B12) is found only in animal products</p>','<p>B12 deficiency in children can cause permanent neurological damage if not treated promptly, making early recognition critical.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Folic acid deficiency:</strong> Vegans typically get adequate folate from plant sources. - <strong>Thiamine deficiency:</strong> Thiamine deficiency causes neurological symptoms, not macrocytic anemia. - <strong>Iron deficiency anemia:</strong> Iron deficiency causes microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","macrocytic","vegan"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.172Z','2026-10-27T15:41:52.801Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcs003bo7lrudtn5oaq','mcq','5- In sickle cell anemia, Which of the following cause Aplastic crisis','["Parvovirus B19 infection","CMV","Adenovirus"]','Parvovirus B19 infection','<p><strong>Why Parvovirus B19 infection is correct:</strong> Parvovirus B19 has a specific tropism for human erythroid progenitor cells, binding to the P antigen (also known as the erythrocyte P1 antigen) on these cells. This binding inhibits DNA synthesis and causes apoptosis of erythroid precursors, resulting in a temporary cessation of red blood cell production (aplastic crisis). In sickle cell anemia, where red blood cells have a shortened lifespan of 10-20 days (compared to 120 days in normal individuals), this sudden stop in production leads to a rapid and severe drop in hemoglobin levels. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>CMV:</strong> Cytomegalovirus can cause various complications in immunocompromised patients but does not specifically target erythroid precursors to cause aplastic crisis. - <strong>Adenovirus:</strong> While adenovirus can cause hemorrhagic cystitis and pneumonia, it does not have the specific tropism for erythroid progenitor cells that parvovirus B19 possesses. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Parvovirus B19 infection typically presents with a "slapped cheek" rash in children and arthralgia in adults, but in sickle cell patients, the aplastic crisis may be the primary manifestation. - The diagnosis is confirmed by detecting parvovirus B19 DNA in serum or by finding characteristic giant pronormoblasts in the bone marrow. - Most patients recover spontaneously within 7-10 days, but transfusion support may be necessary during the crisis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Parvovirus B19 is the classic cause of aplastic crisis in sickle cell anemia due to its specific inhibition of erythroid progenitor cell production.</p>','<p>P19 → P antigen → Progenitor cell arrest → Aplastic crisis</p>','<p>Parvovirus B19 infection should be considered in any sickle cell patient presenting with sudden, unexplained drop in hemoglobin.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>CMV:</strong> Does not specifically target erythroid precursors to cause aplastic crisis. - <strong>Adenovirus:</strong> Lacks the specific tropism for erythroid progenitor cells that parvovirus B19 possesses.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Sickle Cell Anemia','["sickle cell","aplastic crisis","parvovirus"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.180Z','2026-10-27T15:42:08.692Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpf001htzlrfp3lt2ha','mcq','Thrombin time is an investigation done in cases of bleeding tendency. What does it assess?','["Extrinsic pathway.","Stabilization","Platelet function","Intrinsic pathway."]','Stabilization','<p><strong>Why Stabilization is correct:</strong> Thrombin time specifically assesses the final common pathway of coagulation by measuring the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin, which represents the stabilization phase of clot formation. This test evaluates the function of fibrinogen and the final step where thrombin cleaves fibrinopeptides from fibrinogen, allowing fibrin monomers to polymerize and form a stable clot. The thrombin time is prolonged in conditions affecting fibrinogen (dysfibrinogenemia, afibrinogenemia) or when there are inhibitors (heparin, direct thrombin inhibitors) interfering with this final step.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Extrinsic pathway:</strong> The extrinsic pathway is assessed by prothrombin time (PT), which measures the tissue factor pathway and involves factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen.</li>
<li><strong>Platelet function:</strong> Platelet function is evaluated by tests like platelet aggregation studies, bleeding time, or PFA-100, not thrombin time.</li>
<li><strong>Intrinsic pathway:</strong> The intrinsic pathway is assessed by activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), which measures factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Thrombin time is the most sensitive test for detecting heparin contamination or direct thrombin inhibitor effects.</li>
<li>It''s prolonged in fibrinogen disorders (&lt;100 mg/dL typically prolongs TT) and with abnormal fibrinogen molecules.</li>
<li>Normal thrombin time is 10-15 seconds; significant prolongation indicates a problem in the final common pathway.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Thrombin time specifically measures the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin, assessing the stabilization phase of clot formation, not the earlier coagulation pathways.</p>','<p>TT = Thrombin Time = Tests the final conversion of Fibrinogen to Fibrin</p>','<p>Thrombin time is particularly useful for detecting heparin contamination in samples or when direct thrombin inhibitors are suspected.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Extrinsic pathway:</strong> Assessed by PT, not thrombin time.</li>
<li><strong>Platelet function:</strong> Evaluated by different tests like platelet aggregation.</li>
<li><strong>Intrinsic pathway:</strong> Measured by aPTT, not thrombin time.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Coagulation Studies','["thrombin time","coagulation","fibrin formation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.171Z','2026-10-27T15:42:56.555Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpj001jtzlrj7geuzje','mcq','Which is correct about prophylactic therapy: used for','["Mild hemophilia","Moderate hemophilia","Severe hemophilia"]','Severe hemophilia','<p><strong>Why Severe hemophilia is correct:</strong> Prophylactic therapy is primarily used in severe hemophilia (factor VIII or IX levels &lt;1%) to prevent spontaneous bleeding episodes and prevent joint damage, which is the most common complication of hemophilia. The goal is to maintain factor levels above 1% at all times, which typically requires regular infusions (2-3 times per week for hemophilia A, 1-2 times per week for hemophilia B) rather than treating bleeding episodes as they occur (on-demand therapy). This approach has been shown to significantly reduce joint damage and improve quality of life.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mild hemophilia:</strong> Patients with mild hemophilia (factor levels &gt;5-40%) typically only require treatment before surgery, dental procedures, or after significant trauma, not continuous prophylaxis.</li>
<li><strong>Moderate hemophilia:</strong> Patients with moderate hemophilia (factor levels 1-5%) usually only require treatment for bleeding episodes rather than continuous prophylaxis, though some may benefit from it during high-risk periods.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Prophylaxis in severe hemophilia aims to maintain trough levels &gt;1% to prevent spontaneous bleeding.</li>
<li>The standard prophylactic regimens are 25-40 IU/kg every other day for hemophilia A and 40-50 IU/kg twice weekly for hemophilia B.</li>
<li>Prophylaxis has been shown to reduce annual bleeding rates by 70-80% and significantly decrease joint damage compared to on-demand therapy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Prophylactic therapy is reserved for severe hemophilia to prevent spontaneous bleeding and joint damage, while mild and moderate forms typically only require treatment for bleeding episodes.</p>','<p>Severe Hemophilia = Suffering = Needs Sustained prophylaxis</p>','<p>Early initiation of prophylaxis in young children with severe hemophilia (before first joint bleed) significantly improves long-term outcomes and preserves joint function.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mild hemophilia:</strong> Only requires treatment before procedures or after trauma, not continuous prophylaxis.</li>
<li><strong>Moderate hemophilia:</strong> Usually treated on-demand rather than with continuous prophylaxis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hemophilia Management','["prophylaxis","hemophilia","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.175Z','2026-10-27T15:42:56.558Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpk001ktzlrw8j1ac4c','short','Cut point of hemophilia a. 30%','[]','Less than 30%','<p><strong>Why Less than 30% is correct:</strong> Hemophilia A is classified based on the level of factor VIII activity. Severe hemophilia A is defined as factor VIII activity less than 1%, moderate as 1-5%, and mild as 5-30%. The cut point of 30% distinguishes mild hemophilia from normal factor VIII activity, which is typically above 50%. This classification is crucial for determining treatment approaches and bleeding risk assessment.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> An answer of 30% or higher would incorrectly categorize patients as having normal or near-normal factor VIII activity, which would underestimate their bleeding risk and potentially lead to inadequate treatment planning.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Severe hemophilia A (&lt;1% factor VIII) presents with spontaneous bleeding into joints and muscles</li>
<li>Moderate hemophilia A (1-5% factor VIII) typically causes bleeding after minor trauma</li>
<li>Mild hemophilia A (5-30% factor VIII) often only manifests with significant trauma or surgery</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemophilia A severity is classified by factor VIII activity levels, with mild disease defined as 5-30% activity.</p>','','<p>Factor VIII levels above 30% are generally sufficient to prevent spontaneous bleeding, though levels below 50% may still increase bleeding risk during procedures.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Hemophilia A','["diagnosis","factor VIII","cutoff"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.176Z','2026-10-27T15:43:11.911Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpk001ltzlrcuvx9g5w','short','When to use Desmopressin a. Mild hemophilia A','[]','Mild hemophilia A','<p><strong>Why Mild hemophilia A is correct:</strong> Desmopressin (DDAVP) is a synthetic analog of vasopressin that stimulates the release of stored factor VIII from endothelial cells. It is most effective in patients with mild hemophilia A who have at least 5-10% baseline factor VIII activity, as these patients can achieve sufficient temporary elevation (typically 2-5 fold) to control bleeding or prepare for minor procedures. Desmopressin is not effective in moderate or severe hemophilia A where endogenous factor VIII stores are insufficient.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> Desmopressin is not effective in moderate or severe hemophilia A where endogenous factor VIII stores are insufficient, nor is it used for hemophilia B which involves factor IX deficiency rather than factor VIII.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Desmopressin works by stimulating endothelial cells to release stored factor VIII</li>
<li>Response to desmopressin should be tested before relying on it for bleeding control</li>
<li>The typical duration of effect is 6-12 hours, after which factor VIII levels return to baseline</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Desmopressin is first-line treatment for mild hemophilia A patients with baseline factor VIII levels of 5-30%.</p>','','<p>Desmopressin can cause transient hyponatremia due to its antidiuretic effect, so fluid intake should be restricted for 24 hours after administration.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Hemophilia Treatment','["desmopressin","hemophilia A","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.176Z','2026-10-27T15:43:11.916Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpr001utzlr67oan9jy','mcq','Hemophilia A represent the following percentage of coagulopathy','["70%","85%","90%","10%"]','70%','<p><strong>Why 70% is correct:</strong> Hemophilia A accounts for approximately 70% of all hemophilia cases and results from a deficiency or dysfunction of factor VIII, which is part of the intrinsic coagulation pathway. This X-linked recessive disorder is caused by mutations in the F8 gene, leading to impaired thrombin generation and defective fibrin clot formation. The remaining 30% of hemophilia cases are primarily hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency), with rare cases involving other factor deficiencies.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>85%:</strong> This overestimates the prevalence of hemophilia A; while hemophilia A is the most common inherited coagulation disorder, it does not account for 85% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>90%:</strong> This is an even higher overestimation and would incorrectly suggest that nearly all coagulopathies are hemophilia A, which is not the case.</li>
<li><strong>10%:</strong> This significantly underestimates the prevalence of hemophilia A, which is actually the most common inherited bleeding disorder.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder affecting males predominantly, though female carriers can exhibit symptoms.</li>
<li>The severity of hemophilia is classified by factor VIII activity levels: mild (&gt;5-40%), moderate (1-5%), and severe (&lt;1%).</li>
<li>Hemophilia B (Christmas disease) accounts for approximately 30% of hemophilia cases and results from factor IX deficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hemophilia A is the most common inherited coagulation disorder, accounting for about 70% of cases due to factor VIII deficiency.</p>','<p>Hemophilia A = 70% (A is the first letter of the alphabet, and 70% is the highest percentage among options)</p>','<p>Hemophilia A severity correlates with factor VIII levels, with severe cases (&lt;1% activity) presenting with spontaneous bleeding, while mild cases may only bleed after trauma or surgery.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>85%:</strong> Overestimates the prevalence of hemophilia A among coagulopathies.</li>
<li><strong>90%:</strong> Significantly overestimates the proportion of hemophilia A cases.</li>
<li><strong>10%:</strong> Severely underestimates the actual prevalence of hemophilia A.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemophilia Epidemiology','["hemophilia","prevalence","factor VIII"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.183Z','2026-10-27T15:43:12.243Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugpv001vtzlr6jfzreh4','mcq','Platelets are called thrombocytes; they are formed in the bone marrow. What is the normal range of platelet count?','["<100,000/mm3","100,000-150,000/mm3","150,000-450,000/mm3",">450,000/mm3"]','150,000-450,000/mm3','<p><strong>Why 150,000-450,000/mm3 is correct:</strong> The normal platelet count range of 150,000-450,000/mm3 represents the typical concentration of platelets in healthy peripheral blood, which is maintained by a balance between platelet production in the bone marrow and platelet clearance. Platelets are anucleate cell fragments derived from megakaryocytes in the bone marrow and play a crucial role in primary hemostasis through adhesion, activation, and aggregation at sites of vascular injury. This range ensures adequate hemostatic function while avoiding the risks of both bleeding (thrombocytopenia) and thrombosis (thrombocytosis).</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>&lt;100,000/mm3:</strong> This represents thrombocytopenia, which increases bleeding risk and is below the normal range.</li>
<li><strong>100,000-150,000/mm3:</strong> This is considered the lower limit of normal or mild thrombocytopenia in some laboratories, not the full normal range.</li>
<li><strong>&gt;450,000/mm3:</strong> This represents thrombocytosis, which may increase thrombotic risk and is above the normal range.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Platelet counts &lt;50,000/mm3 increase the risk of spontaneous bleeding, while counts &lt;10,000/mm3 significantly increase the risk of life-threatening bleeding.</li>
<li>The mean platelet volume (MPV) can help distinguish between production problems (high MPV) and destruction/consumption issues (normal or low MPV).</li>
<li>Thrombocytosis can be reactive (secondary to infection, inflammation, iron deficiency) or clonal (essential thrombocythemia, a myeloproliferative neoplasm).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The normal platelet count range of 150,000-450,000/mm3 represents the optimal concentration for effective hemostasis without increasing bleeding or thrombotic risk.</p>','<p>Normal platelet range: 150-450 (think of ''one-fifty to four-fifty'' as the normal window for platelet counts)</p>','<p>Platelet counts should be interpreted in the clinical context, as many conditions can cause transient or persistent deviations from normal ranges without indicating a primary hematologic disorder.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>&lt;100,000/mm3:</strong> This indicates thrombocytopenia, which is below the normal range and increases bleeding risk.</li>
<li><strong>100,000-150,000/mm3:</strong> This represents the lower limit of normal or mild thrombocytopenia, not the full normal range.</li>
<li><strong>&gt;450,000/mm3:</strong> This indicates thrombocytosis, which is above the normal range and may increase thrombotic risk.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Platelets','["platelets","normal range","thrombocytes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.187Z','2026-10-27T15:43:12.248Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq10024tzlrdvz52xke','mcq','Resolution in ITP occur by','["80%","20%","< 1%"]','80%','<p><strong>Why 80% is correct:</strong> In immune thrombocytopenia (ITP), approximately 80% of children experience spontaneous resolution within 6-12 months, particularly in acute cases following viral infections. This resolution occurs due to the development of immune tolerance and the downregulation of autoantibody production against platelet antigens, allowing platelet production to normalize as the immune system self-corrects. The natural history of pediatric IPI typically shows this high resolution rate, distinguishing it from chronic ITP in adults which persists beyond 12 months in most cases.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>20%:</strong> This underestimates the resolution rate in pediatric ITP, as most children recover spontaneously without intervention.</li>
<li><strong>&lt;1%:</strong> This is drastically incorrect and would represent an extremely rare condition, not the typical course of ITP.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Pediatric IPI has an excellent prognosis with spontaneous resolution rates of 80% within 6-12 months.</li>
<li>Chronic ITP (persisting beyond 12 months) is more common in adults than children.</li>
<li>Treatment is typically reserved for symptomatic patients or those with very low platelet counts (&lt;20,000-30,000/mm³).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pediatric IPI resolves spontaneously in 80% of cases within a year due to natural immune tolerance development.</p>','<p>ITP Resolution: 80% kids get better (8 out of 10)</p>','<p>Most children with IPI don''t need treatment as they spontaneously recover, but adults with chronic ITP often require ongoing management.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>20%:</strong> This underestimates the resolution rate in pediatric ITP.</li>
<li><strong>&lt;1%:</strong> This is drastically incorrect and would represent an extremely rare condition.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura','["itp","resolution","spontaneous"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.193Z','2026-10-27T15:43:41.259Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5a0032e9oit500quf2','mcq','A child with heart failure, Chest infection and Cyanotic. What is the most common cause','["TGA","Fallot","ASD","VSD"]','VSD','<p><strong>Why VSD is correct:</strong> Ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the most common cause of heart failure in a child with chest infection and cyanosis because large VSDs result in significant left-to-right shunting, leading to volume overload of the left heart and pulmonary circulation. This volume overload causes pulmonary hypertension and eventually Eisenmenger syndrome, where the shunt reverses direction (right-to-left), causing cyanosis. The increased pulmonary blood flow makes these children particularly susceptible to respiratory infections, which can exacerbate heart failure symptoms through increased metabolic demands and pulmonary congestion.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Transposition of the great arteries typically presents with cyanosis in the newborn period without significant heart failure, as the defect primarily causes inadequate oxygenation rather than volume overload.</li>
<li><strong>Fallot:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot primarily presents with cyanosis due to right-to-left shunting across the VSD, but heart failure is less common as the pulmonary stenosis limits pulmonary blood flow and protects against volume overload.</li>
<li><strong>ASD:</strong> Atrial septal defects typically do not present with heart failure in childhood, as the left-to-right shunting is better tolerated and causes volume overload more gradually, often presenting later in adulthood.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Large VSDs can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome over time, where pulmonary hypertension reverses the shunt direction from left-to-right to right-to-left, causing cyanosis.</li>
<li>Children with large VSDs are prone to recurrent respiratory infections due to increased pulmonary blood flow and congestion.</li>
<li>The combination of heart failure and cyanosis in a child with a VSD suggests the development of pulmonary hypertension and Eisenmenger physiology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> VSD is the most common cause of heart failure with cyanosis in a child with respiratory infection due to volume overload leading to pulmonary hypertension and eventual right-to-left shunting.</p>','<p>VSD: Volume Shunt Disease (leading to heart failure)</p>','<p>The development of cyanosis in a child with a known VSD suggests the onset of pulmonary hypertension and Eisenmenger syndrome, which reverses the shunt direction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Presents with cyanosis in newborns without significant heart failure due to inadequate oxygenation rather than volume overload.</li>
<li><strong>Fallot:</strong> Primarily presents with cyanosis due to right-to-left shunting, with heart failure being less common due to protective pulmonary stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>ASD:</strong> Typically does not present with heart failure in childhood as the left-to-right shunting is better tolerated.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["heart failure","cyanotic","chest infection","common cause"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.614Z','2026-10-27T15:51:14.986Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq20026tzlreqbtpn5g','mcq','Platelet count in thrombocytopenia','["<100,000","<150,000"]','<100,000','<p><strong>Why &lt;100,000 is correct:</strong> Thrombocytopenia is defined as a platelet count less than 100,000 platelets per microliter (μL) of blood, based on established hematology reference ranges and clinical guidelines. This threshold is universally accepted in medical practice because platelet counts below this level are associated with increased bleeding risk, particularly counts below 50,000/μL where spontaneous bleeding may occur.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>&lt;150,000:</strong> This represents the lower limit of normal for platelet count in some laboratories but does not meet the clinical definition of thrombocytopenia, which requires a count below 100,000/μL.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Normal platelet count range is typically 150,000-450,000/μL.</li>
<li>Thrombocytopenia is defined as &lt;100,000/μL, with severe thrombocytopenia at &lt;20,000/μL.</li>
<li>Platelet counts between 100,000-150,000/μL are considered mild thrombocytopenia but may still increase bleeding risk in certain situations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Thrombocytopenia is defined as a platelet count below 100,000/μL, not 150,000/μL.</p>','<p>Thrombocytopenia: 100K threshold (remember as ''one hundred thousand'')</p>','<p>The bleeding risk increases significantly when platelet counts fall below 50,000/μL, and spontaneous bleeding typically occurs below 20,000/μL.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Thrombocytopenia','["thrombocytopenia","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.194Z','2026-10-27T15:43:41.263Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq20027tzlr0qplanbx','mcq','Which of the following is an indication for bone marrow aspiration in children with thrombocytopenia?','["History of preceding upper respiratory tract infection.","Isolated thrombocytopenia in a healthy child.","Presence of hepato-splenomegaly and generalized lymphadenopathy.","If a child is presented with active bleeding such as epistaxis"]','Presence of hepato-splenomegaly and generalized lymphadenopathy.','<p><strong>Why Presence of hepato-splenomegaly and generalized lymphadenopathy is correct:</strong> Bone marrow aspiration is indicated in children with thrombocytopenia when there are signs suggesting alternative diagnoses beyond ITP, such as hepatosplenomegaly and generalized lymphadenopathy, which may indicate malignancy (leukemia, lymphoma), storage diseases, or bone marrow infiltration. These findings warrant further investigation to determine the underlying cause, as they are not typical in isolated IPI and suggest a more serious systemic process.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>History of preceding upper respiratory tract infection:</strong> This is actually a common finding in acute IPI and does not warrant bone marrow examination.</li>
<li><strong>Isolated thrombocytopenia in a healthy child:</strong> This is the classic presentation of IPI, which is typically diagnosed clinically without bone marrow aspiration.</li>
<li><strong>If a child is presented with active bleeding such as epistaxis:</strong> While concerning, active bleeding in IPI is managed with treatment rather than immediate bone marrow examination.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Bone marrow aspiration is not routinely needed for typical IPI diagnosis in children.</li>
<li>Hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy in thrombocytopenia suggest alternative diagnoses requiring investigation.</li>
<li>IPI is a diagnosis of exclusion, and atypical findings warrant further evaluation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Bone marrow aspiration is indicated in pediatric thrombocytopenia only when there are atypical findings like hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy suggesting alternative diagnoses.</p>','<p>ITP Bone Marrow: ''HSL'' (Hepatosplenomegaly, Splenomegaly, Lymphadenopathy) indicates need for aspiration</p>','<p>ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion; bone marrow biopsy is typically reserved for cases with atypical features or when the diagnosis is uncertain.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>History of preceding upper respiratory tract infection:</strong> This is common in acute IPI and doesn''t warrant bone marrow examination.</li>
<li><strong>Isolated thrombocytopenia in a healthy child:</strong> This is classic IPI, diagnosed clinically without bone marrow aspiration.</li>
<li><strong>If a child is presented with active bleeding such as epistaxis:</strong> This is managed with treatment, not immediate bone marrow examination.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Thrombocytopenia','["bone marrow","aspiration","indications","thrombocytopenia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.194Z','2026-10-27T15:43:41.264Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq6002btzlrz13up7pl','mcq','Which of the following drugs should a patient with ITP avoid?','["Paracetamol.","Cephalosporins.","Aspirin.","Cimetidine."]','Aspirin.','<p><strong>Why Aspirin is correct:</strong> Aspirin should be avoided in patients with immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) because it inhibits platelet function by irreversibly acetylating cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1), which reduces thromboxane A2 production and impairs platelet aggregation. This antiplatelet effect can exacerbate bleeding risk in patients who already have low platelet counts. Additionally, aspirin can cause gastric irritation and mucosal damage, further increasing the risk of bleeding complications in thrombocytopenic patients.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Paracetamol:</strong> This analgesic does not significantly affect platelet function or aggregation, making it a safer alternative for patients with ITP.</li>
<li><strong>Cephalosporins:</strong> These antibiotics are not known to cause platelet dysfunction or increase bleeding risk in thrombocytopenic patients.</li>
<li><strong>Cimetidine:</strong> This H2 blocker does not affect platelet function and is generally considered safe in patients with ITP.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Aspirin and other NSAIDs should be avoided in ITP due to their antiplatelet effects.</li>
<li>Acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic for patients with thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li>Even medications with mild antiplatelet effects (like NSAIDs) can be dangerous in severe thrombocytopenia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aspirin causes irreversible platelet dysfunction via COX-1 inhibition, making it particularly dangerous for patients with ITP who already have impaired hemostasis.</p>','<p>ITP + Aspirin = BAD (Both cause Antiplatelet Dysfunction)</p>','<p>In thrombocytopenic patients, even minor antiplatelet effects can tip the balance toward significant bleeding; always review medications for potential platelet inhibitors.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Paracetamol:</strong> Does not affect platelet function, making it safe for ITP patients.</li>
<li><strong>Cephalosporins:</strong> Antibiotics that don''t impact platelet function or bleeding risk.</li>
<li><strong>Cimetidine:</strong> H2 blocker that doesn''t interfere with platelet activity.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pharmacology','Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura','["itp","aspirin","avoid","platelet function"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.198Z','2026-10-27T15:43:54.634Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqa002ctzlrisv24qs7','mcq','Tom is a 5-year-old boy who presents to hospital with a recent history of bruising easily; two weeks ago, he had an upper respiratory tract infection which resolved spontaneously. On examination today he is afebrile, but has many widespread bruises with some scattered petechiae. Investigations reveal: Hb: 11.5 gm% WBC: 10200/L, platelet count: 32000/L, Prothrombin time: 15 sec (control: 12-15s), activated partial thromboplastin time: 30 sec (control: 25-35s), Fibrinogen: 2.5 g/L (Normal: 2-4 g/L).','["Hemophilia A","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia.","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura.","Non-accidental injury."]','Immune thrombocytopenic purpura.','<p><strong>Why Immune thrombocytopenic purpura is correct:</strong> This presentation is classic for immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), which typically follows a viral infection (like the recent upper respiratory tract infection) and is characterized by isolated thrombocytopenia with normal coagulation studies. The pathophysiology involves autoantibody-mediated destruction of platelets, often targeting platelet membrane glycoproteins IIb/IIIa. The normal hemoglobin, white blood cell count, and coagulation parameters (PT, aPTT, fibrinogen) rule out other causes of thrombocytopenia.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hemophilia A:</strong> This is a clotting factor deficiency (factor VIII) that would present with prolonged aPTT, not isolated thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia:</strong> While it can cause thrombocytopenia, it typically presents with other cytopenias, abnormal white blood cell counts, and possibly blasts on peripheral smear.</li>
<li><strong>Non-accidental injury:</strong> This would not explain the laboratory findings of isolated thrombocytopenia with normal coagulation studies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion characterized by isolated thrombocytopenia with normal coagulation studies.</li>
<li>The typical presentation follows a viral illness and includes bruising and petechiae.</li>
<li>Bone marrow examination is not routinely needed in typical ITP cases but may be considered if atypical features are present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> ITP is the diagnosis when isolated thrombocytopenia follows a viral infection and coagulation studies are normal.</p>','<p>ITP: Isolated Thrombocytopenia Post-viral</p>','<p>ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion; always check coagulation studies to distinguish from other causes of bleeding disorders.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hemophilia A:</strong> Would show prolonged aPTT, not isolated thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li><strong>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia:</strong> Typically presents with multiple cytopenias and abnormal WBC count.</li>
<li><strong>Non-accidental injury:</strong> Doesn''t explain the laboratory findings of isolated thrombocytopenia.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura','["itp","diagnosis","child","post-viral"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.202Z','2026-10-27T15:43:54.642Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5n003fe9oi6gf0epoi','mcq','16- In consideration of polycythemia associated with cyanotic heart disease, what is the main etiology?','["Due to recurrent infection.","Bone marrow depression due to hypoxia.","Hypoxia induced erythropoietin overproduction.","Cyanotic spells."]','Hypoxia induced erythropoietin overproduction.','<p><strong>Why Hypoxia induced erythropoietin overproduction is correct:</strong> In cyanotic heart disease, chronic tissue hypoxia stimulates the kidneys to produce excessive erythropoietin (EPO), a hormone that drives erythropoiesis in the bone marrow. This compensatory mechanism increases red blood cell mass to enhance oxygen-carrying capacity, resulting in polycythemia. The hypoxia-inducible factors (HIFs) in renal peritubular cells activate EPO gene transcription, creating a direct pathophysiologic link between hypoxia and polycythemia.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Due to recurrent infection:</strong> While infections can cause secondary polycythemia via inflammatory cytokines, this is not the primary mechanism in cyanotic heart disease.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow depression due to hypoxia:</strong> Hypoxia actually stimulates bone marrow activity rather than depressing it, making this option contradictory to the pathophysiology.</li>
<li><strong>Cyanotic spells:</strong> These acute episodes of hypoxia may transiently worsen polycythemia but are not the chronic etiology of the condition.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Polycythemia in cyanotic heart disease is a compensatory mechanism for chronic hypoxia.</li>
<li>Erythropoietin is primarily produced by peritubular cells in the kidneys in response to hypoxia.</li>
<li>Complications of polycythemia include hyperviscosity, thrombosis, and hemorrhage.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Polycythemia in cyanotic heart disease results from hypoxia-induced erythropoietin overproduction, not bone marrow depression or infection.</p>','<p>Hypoxia → EPO → Polycythemia (HELP)</p>','<p>Polycythemia in cyanotic heart disease requires careful management as excessive red cell mass can lead to hyperviscosity syndrome, causing thrombosis and paradoxical worsening of tissue oxygen delivery.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Due to recurrent infection:</strong> Infections can cause secondary polycythemia but are not the primary mechanism in cyanotic heart disease.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow depression due to hypoxia:</strong> Hypoxia stimulates bone marrow activity rather than depressing it.</li>
<li><strong>Cyanotic spells:</strong> These are acute exacerbations of hypoxia, not the chronic etiology of polycythemia.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["polycythemia","cyanotic","etiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.627Z','2026-10-27T15:51:52.272Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqb002dtzlr8ljoqo7v','mcq','Which of the following cause platelet destruction by an antibody mediated mechanism?','["DIC.","Hypersplenism.","Hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Lymphoma."]','Hypersplenism.','<p><strong>Why Hypersplenism is correct:</strong> Hypersplenism causes platelet destruction through an antibody-mediated mechanism where splenic macrophages recognize and phagocytose antibody-coated platelets. The spleen acts as a filter for blood, and when enlarged or hyperactive, it sequesters and destroys blood cells, including platelets that may be coated with antibodies or other opsonizing factors. This process is part of the broader hypersplenism syndrome that can also affect red and white blood cells.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>DIC:</strong> Disseminated intravascular coagulation causes platelet consumption through widespread microthrombi formation, not primarily antibody-mediated destruction.</li>
<li><strong>Hemolytic uremic syndrome:</strong> This condition causes platelet consumption via microangiopathic hemolysis and thrombotic microangiopathy, not antibody-mediated destruction.</li>
<li><strong>Lymphoma:</strong> While lymphoma can cause thrombocytopenia through various mechanisms (bone marrow infiltration, hypersplenism), it is not primarily characterized by antibody-mediated platelet destruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypersplenism causes sequestration and destruction of blood cells, including platelets, by splenic macrophages.</li>
<li>Antibody-mediated platelet destruction is the hallmark of ITP, but hypersplenism can also contribute through sequestration.</li>
<li>Splenomegaly is a key physical finding in hypersplenism.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypersplenism destroys platelets through antibody-mediated sequestration and phagocytosis by splenic macrophages.</p>','<p>Hypersplenism: Hyperactive Spleen = Antibody-Mediated Destruction</p>','<p>Hypersplenism should be considered in patients with cytopenias and splenomegaly, as it represents a mechanical destruction rather than a production problem.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>DIC:</strong> Causes platelet consumption via microthrombi formation, not antibody-mediated destruction.</li>
<li><strong>Hemolytic uremic syndrome:</strong> Results in platelet consumption through microangiopathic hemolysis.</li>
<li><strong>Lymphoma:</strong> Can cause thrombocytopenia through multiple mechanisms but not primarily antibody-mediated destruction.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Thrombocytopenia','["thrombocytopenia","antibody-mediated","destruction"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.203Z','2026-10-27T15:43:54.643Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqd002ftzlrxfihzb6e','mcq','Aspirin is famous for causing bleeding tendency. What is the causative mechanism?','["It affects vessel wall","It affects platelet number","It affects platelet function","It affects coagulation factors"]','It affects platelet function','<p><strong>Why It affects platelet function is correct:</strong> Aspirin irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) enzyme in platelets, which prevents the conversion of arachidonic acid to thromboxane A2 (TXA2). TXA2 is a potent platelet activator and vasoconstrictor, and its inhibition impairs platelet aggregation and adhesion. Since platelets cannot synthesize new COX-1 (they lack nuclei), this effect lasts for the platelet''s 7-10 day lifespan, causing a prolonged bleeding tendency.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It affects vessel wall:</strong> Aspirin does not directly damage vessel walls; its primary effect is on platelets.</li>
<li><strong>It affects platelet number:</strong> Aspirin does not reduce platelet count; it only impairs their function.</li>
<li><strong>It affects coagulation factors:</strong> Aspirin does not directly affect coagulation factors like warfarin or heparin do.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Aspirin''s antiplatelet effect is irreversible due to COX-1 acetylation in platelets.</li>
<li>The bleeding time is prolonged in patients on aspirin, but platelet counts remain normal.</li>
<li>Aspirin''s effect on platelets is distinct from its anti-inflammatory effects, which involve reversible COX-2 inhibition.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aspirin causes bleeding by irreversibly inhibiting platelet COX-1, reducing thromboxane A2 production and impairing platelet function.</p>','<p>Aspirin = Platelet COX-1 Block (PCB) - remember it blocks platelet function, not count or vessel walls.</p>','<p>Aspirin''s antiplatelet effect lasts the entire lifespan of platelets (7-10 days) because platelets cannot produce new COX-1 enzyme.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It affects vessel wall:</strong> Aspirin does not directly damage vessel walls; its primary effect is on platelets.</li>
<li><strong>It affects platelet number:</strong> Aspirin does not reduce platelet count; it only impairs their function.</li>
<li><strong>It affects coagulation factors:</strong> Aspirin does not directly affect coagulation factors like warfarin or heparin do.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Pharmacology','Aspirin','["aspirin","platelet function","bleeding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.205Z','2026-10-27T15:44:09.189Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqd002gtzlrjmmsa6l4','mcq','A 4-year-old girl represented with few purpuric eruptions on her abdomen and face of one week duration, with no history of active bleeding. On examination, she had no organomegaly or lymphadenopathy. She is pale and jaundiced. Her mother also confirmed that the girl''s urine is darker than normal. Her CBC shows anemia, thrombocytopenia with elevated reticulocytic count. Which of the following investigations would you order for her first?','["Anti-platelet antibody","Urine analysis","Coomb''s test","Renal function tests"]','Coomb''s test','<p><strong>Why Coomb''s test is correct:</strong> This 4-year-old girl presents with purpura, pallor, jaundice, anemia, thrombocytopenia, and elevated reticulocyte count, which are classic features of Evans syndrome—a rare autoimmune disorder characterized by simultaneous or sequential immune-mediated destruction of both red blood cells (hemolytic anemia) and platelets (immune thrombocytopenia). The Coombs test (direct antiglobulin test) is the first-line investigation to confirm immune-mediated hemolysis by detecting antibodies or complement proteins attached to red blood cells.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Anti-platelet antibody:</strong> While anti-platelet antibodies may be present in immune thrombocytopenia, the Coombs test is more critical here given the evidence of hemolysis (jaundice, elevated reticulocytes).</li>
<li><strong>Urine analysis:</strong> Urine analysis might show hemoglobinuria (dark urine) but doesn''t diagnose the underlying immune-mediated process.</li>
<li><strong>Renal function tests:</strong> Renal function tests are not indicated as there''s no evidence of renal involvement.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Evans syndrome is defined by concurrent or sequential immune-mediated hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia.</li>
<li>The direct Coombs test is diagnostic for immune-mediated hemolysis and should be ordered first in this presentation.</li>
<li>Treatment typically involves corticosteroids, with rituximab or other immunosuppressants for refractory cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In a child with anemia, thrombocytopenia, and jaundice, the first investigation for suspected immune-mediated hemolysis is the Coombs test.</p>','<p>Coomb''s for Hemolysis (CH) - when you see anemia + jaundice + thrombocytopenia, think Coombs test first.</p>','<p>Evans syndrome, characterized by both immune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia, often requires aggressive immunosuppression and may be associated with underlying autoimmune disorders.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Anti-platelet antibody:</strong> While anti-platelet antibodies may be present in immune thrombocytopenia, the Coombs test is more critical given the evidence of hemolysis.</li>
<li><strong>Urine analysis:</strong> Urine analysis might show hemoglobinuria but doesn''t diagnose the underlying immune-mediated process.</li>
<li><strong>Renal function tests:</strong> Renal function tests are not indicated as there''s no evidence of renal involvement.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hemolytic Anemia','["purpura","anemia","thrombocytopenia","coombs"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.205Z','2026-10-27T15:44:09.190Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmutc1rsc000hxnlr17x9h6bv','mcq','Inflammation in vessel wall, Acquired, Purpura','["Henoch-schonlein purpura","Ehler danlos syndrome","Renal failure"]','Henoch-schonlein purpura','<p><strong>Why Henoch-schonlein purpura is correct:</strong> Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) is a small-vessel vasculitis characterized by IgA immune complex deposition in the vessel walls, leading to inflammation, increased vascular permeability, and subsequent purpura. This deposition triggers complement activation and neutrophil recruitment, causing damage to postcapillary venules and resulting in the classic palpable purpuric rash, typically in dependent areas. The condition is acquired, often following an upper respiratory infection, and represents an immune-mediated hypersensitivity reaction rather than a genetic disorder.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ehler danlos syndrome:</strong> Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of genetic connective tissue disorders caused by defects in collagen synthesis, not inflammation in vessel walls. While some types may involve bruising (purpura), it''s due to tissue fragility rather than vasculitis.</li>
<li><strong>Renal failure:</strong> Renal failure is a potential complication of HSP but is not a primary condition characterized by inflammation in vessel walls and purpura. It represents end-organ damage rather than the underlying pathophysiology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>HSP is the most common vasculitis in children and classically presents with the tetrad of palpable purpura, arthritis, abdominal pain, and renal involvement.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is supported by IgA deposition on biopsy of skin or renal tissue.</li>
<li>HSP is typically self-limiting but may require corticosteroids for severe manifestations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Henoch-Schönlein purpura is the acquired vasculitis characterized by IgA-mediated small-vessel inflammation causing palpable purpura.</p>','<p>HSP: Henoch-Schönlein Purpura = Palpable Purpura, Arthritis, Abdominal pain, Renal involvement (PAR mnemonic)</p>','<p>HSP often follows an upper respiratory infection and may present with abdominal pain that can mimic surgical abdomen.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Ehler danlos syndrome:</strong> Genetic connective tissue disorder with collagen defects, not vasculitis.</li>
<li><strong>Renal failure:</strong> Complication of HSP, not the primary condition.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Platelet Disorders','["purpura","inflammation","acquired"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T04:40:37.356Z','2026-10-27T15:51:08.463Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmutc1rsg000ixnlrs56g7d3x','mcq','Defect platelets Aggregation','["Glanzmann disease"]','Glanzmann disease','<p><strong>Why Glanzmann disease is correct:</strong> Glanzmann disease (thrombasthenia) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by a deficiency or dysfunction of the glycoprotein IIb/IIIa complex (integrin αIIbβ3) on platelets. This receptor is essential for platelet aggregation as it binds fibrinogen, von Willebrand factor, and other adhesive proteins to bridge adjacent platelets during clot formation. Without functional IIb/IIIa receptors, platelets cannot aggregate properly despite having normal adhesion and secretion capabilities, leading to a bleeding tendency.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No other options provided:</strong> This question appears to be a true/false or single-answer format without plausible distractors, as Glanzmann disease is the classic cause of isolated platelet aggregation defect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Glanzmann disease presents with mucocutaneous bleeding (epistaxis, menorrhagia, gingival bleeding) but normal platelet count and morphology.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by flow cytometry showing absent or reduced IIb/IIIa receptors or platelet aggregation studies showing absent response to ADP, collagen, and thrombin.</li>
<li>Treatment includes platelet transfusions for significant bleeding and avoidance of antiplatelet medications.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Glanzmann disease is the prototypical disorder of platelet aggregation due to IIb/IIIa receptor deficiency.</p>','<p>Glanzmann: ''G'' for Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa defect causing ''Gum'' bleeding (mucocutaneous)</p>','<p>Unlike von Willebrand disease, Glanzmann disease has normal vWF levels and ristocetin cofactor activity.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>No other options provided:</strong> This question appears to be a true/false or single-answer format without plausible distractors.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Platelet Disorders','["aggregation","platelet defect"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T04:40:37.360Z','2026-10-27T15:51:08.468Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmutc1rsh000jxnlrzliurja0','mcq','Ehler danlos syndrome','["Hypermobile joints and purpura"]','Hypermobile joints and purpura','<p><strong>Why Hypermobile joints and purpura is correct:</strong> Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (EDS), specifically the hypermobile type, is characterized by joint hypermobility due to defects in collagen structure and function, leading to joint instability and subluxations. The purpura seen in some EDS patients results from increased skin fragility and easy bruising rather than true vasculitis. This bruising tendency occurs because defective collagen in blood vessel walls makes them more susceptible to trauma, causing extravasation of blood into the skin and subcutaneous tissues.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No other options provided:</strong> This question appears to be a true/false or single-answer format without plausible distractors, as hypermobile joints and purpura (bruising) are classic features of hypermobile EDS.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The hypermobile type of EDS is diagnosed clinically using the Brighton criteria, emphasizing joint hypermobility without significant skin hyperextensibility or atrophic scarring.</li>
<li>Unlike vascular EDS (which has more severe manifestations and is caused by COL3A1 mutations), hypermobile EDS typically has a milder course but can still cause significant joint pain and disability.</li>
<li>Genetic testing is not routinely required for diagnosis but can be helpful in atypical cases or when differentiating from other heritable connective tissue disorders.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (hypermobile type) presents with joint hypermobility and increased bruising tendency due to collagen defects.</p>','<p>EDS: ''E'' for Elastic skin, ''D'' for Dislocations, ''S'' for Stretchy skin (though hypermobile type has less skin involvement)</p>','<p>The hypermobile type of EDS is often underdiagnosed in adults and may be mistaken for fibromyalgia or chronic fatigue syndrome.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>No other options provided:</strong> This question appears to be a true/false or single-answer format without plausible distractors.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Platelet Disorders','["ehlers-danlos","joints","purpura"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-04T04:40:37.361Z','2026-10-27T15:51:08.470Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu570030e9oitcamgr6t','mcq','What is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly at birth?','["TGA.","ASD.","VSD."]','VSD.','<p><strong>Why VSD is correct:</strong> Ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly at birth, accounting for approximately 30% of all congenital heart defects. It occurs due to an abnormal development of the interventricular septum during embryonic heart formation, resulting in a communication between the left and right ventricles. This defect allows shunting of blood from the left to the right ventricle due to the higher pressure in the left side of the heart, leading to increased pulmonary blood flow. VSDs vary in size and location, with the most common type being membranous defects in the perimembranous region.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Transposition of the great arteries (TGA) is a less common cyanotic congenital heart defect where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, accounting for only about 5% of congenital heart defects.</li>
<li><strong>ASD:</strong> Atrial septal defect (ASD) is the second most common congenital heart defect after VSD, accounting for approximately 10-15% of cases, involving a communication between the atria rather than the ventricles.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>VSD is the most common congenital heart defect overall, but ASD is the most common in adults who survive to that age due to its typically more benign natural history.</li>
<li>Small VSDs may close spontaneously, especially in the first year of life, while larger defects often require surgical intervention.</li>
<li>The classic murmur of a VSD is a holosystolic murmur heard best at the lower left sternal border.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> VSD is the most common congenital heart defect at birth due to its embryological origin in the interventricular septum development.</p>','<p>VSD: Very Serious Defect (but most common)</p>','<p>The natural history of VSDs varies significantly by size - small defects may close spontaneously, while larger ones can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome if left unrepaired.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Transposition of the great arteries is a less common cyanotic congenital heart defect accounting for only about 5% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>ASD:</strong> Atrial septal defect is the second most common congenital heart defect after VSD, accounting for approximately 10-15% of cases.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["congenital","cardiac","anomaly","common"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.611Z','2026-10-27T15:51:14.983Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu580031e9oi7b1setie','mcq','Which of the following is not cause of peripheral cyanosis','["Severe cold","Shock","Fallot"]','Fallot','<p><strong>Why Fallot is correct:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot is not a cause of peripheral cyanosis because it is a cyanotic congenital heart defect that causes central cyanosis due to right-to-left shunting of blood across the ventricular septal defect. In Fallot, the primary issue is mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood within the heart itself, leading to cyanosis that affects the entire body, particularly visible in the mucous membranes and tongue (central cyanosis). Peripheral cyanosis, in contrast, is caused by reduced peripheral blood flow or increased oxygen extraction in the extremities without central circulatory mixing.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Severe cold:</strong> Causes peripheral cyanosis through vasoconstriction of peripheral vessels to conserve core body heat, reducing blood flow to the extremities and increasing oxygen extraction.</li>
<li><strong>Shock:</strong> Leads to peripheral cyanosis due to inadequate tissue perfusion, causing increased oxygen extraction from hemoglobin in the peripheral tissues despite normal arterial oxygen content.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Central cyanosis affects the entire body including mucous membranes and tongue, while peripheral cyanosis primarily affects the extremities with normal mucous membranes.</li>
<li>The differential diagnosis between central and peripheral cyanosis can be made by examining the tongue and mucous membranes.</li>
<li>Tetralogy of Fallot presents with central cyanosis, clubbing, and a characteristic "tet spell" (hypercyanotic spell) triggered by crying or feeding.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fallot causes central cyanosis due to intracardiac shunting, while peripheral cyanosis results from reduced peripheral blood flow or increased oxygen extraction in the extremities.</p>','<p>Fallot: Fingers cyanotic? No, it''s central!</p>','<p>Central cyanosis indicates a problem with oxygenation in the lungs or mixing of blood in the heart, while peripheral cyanosis suggests normal oxygenation but reduced peripheral blood flow.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Severe cold:</strong> Causes peripheral cyanosis through vasoconstriction of peripheral vessels to conserve core body heat.</li>
<li><strong>Shock:</strong> Leads to peripheral cyanosis due to inadequate tissue perfusion and increased oxygen extraction from hemoglobin in peripheral tissues.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["cyanosis","peripheral","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.612Z','2026-10-27T15:51:14.985Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5c0033e9oie5xcevuq','mcq','In congenital heart disease, What is not used in diagnosis','["Chest x ray","Echocardiography","Brian CT"]','Brian CT','<p><strong>Why Brian CT is correct:</strong> Brain CT is not routinely used in the diagnosis of congenital heart disease as it primarily evaluates cerebral structures and pathology, not cardiac anatomy or function. The gold standard for diagnosing congenital heart disease is echocardiography, which provides detailed visualization of cardiac structures, blood flow patterns, and function. Chest x-ray can reveal cardiac size, shape, and pulmonary vasculature patterns suggestive of specific congenital lesions, making it a valuable screening tool. Brain CT may be incidentally performed if neurological symptoms are present, but it does not assess cardiac structures or hemodynamics.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chest x ray:</strong> This is commonly used in congenital heart disease diagnosis to assess cardiac size, configuration, and pulmonary vasculature patterns that suggest specific lesions.</li>
<li><strong>Echocardiography:</strong> This is the primary diagnostic tool for congenital heart disease, providing detailed real-time imaging of cardiac structures, function, and blood flow.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Echocardiography is the cornerstone diagnostic modality for congenital heart disease, allowing detailed assessment of anatomy and function.</li>
<li>Chest x-ray can reveal characteristic patterns (e.g., boot-shaped heart in Tetralogy of Fallot, increased pulmonary vascular markings in left-to-right shunts).</li>
<li>Cardiac MRI and cardiac catheterization are additional tools used for complex cases or when detailed hemodynamic assessment is needed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Brain CT evaluates cerebral structures, not cardiac anatomy, making it irrelevant for diagnosing congenital heart disease.</p>','','<p>Neuroimaging should only be considered in congenital heart disease patients when there are specific neurological concerns, as it does not contribute to cardiac diagnosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Chest x ray:</strong> Provides valuable information about cardiac size and pulmonary vasculature patterns in congenital heart disease.</li>
<li><strong>Echocardiography:</strong> The primary diagnostic tool that directly visualizes cardiac structures and function.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["diagnosis","congenital","heart disease"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.616Z','2026-10-27T15:51:21.100Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5f0034e9oisw9k2b6y','mcq','Which condition associated with Squatting position','["Fallot"]','Fallot','<p><strong>Why Fallot is correct:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot is associated with the squatting position because this maneuver increases systemic vascular resistance, which decreases the right-to-left shunting across the ventricular septal defect. Squatting also increases venous return, improving pulmonary blood flow and oxygenation. This characteristic position is adopted by patients with cyanotic spells to alleviate hypoxemia, making it a classic clinical sign of Tetralogy of Fallot.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong></p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The squatting position in Tetralogy of Fallot works by increasing systemic vascular resistance, which reduces right-to-left shunting and improves pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li>Cyanotic spells in Tetralogy of Fallot are characterized by paroxysmal hypercyanosis, dyspnea, and loss of consciousness, often triggered by crying or feeding.</li>
<li>Other cyanotic heart defects like tricuspid atresia or pulmonary atresia may not show this characteristic positional response.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Patients with Tetralogy of Fallot instinctively squat during cyanotic spells to increase systemic vascular resistance and improve oxygenation.</p>','<p>Squatting helps Fallot: System resistance up, Right-to-left shunting down</p>','<p>The squatting position is a compensatory mechanism specific to Tetralogy of Fallot that helps alleviate hypoxemic spells by hemodynamic manipulation.</p>','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Tetralogy of Fallot','["squatting","fallot","position"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.619Z','2026-10-27T15:51:21.102Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5g0035e9oivux3hjha','mcq','What is the position associated with Fallot tetralogy','["Chest to knee position","Prone position","Standing position"]','Chest to knee position','<p><strong>Why Chest to knee position is correct:</strong> The chest-to-knee position (also known as the knee-chest position) is associated with Tetralogy of Fallot because this posture increases systemic vascular resistance and decreases right-to-left shunting across the ventricular septal defect. It also increases venous return to the heart, improving pulmonary blood flow and oxygenation during cyanotic spells. This position is essentially a more extreme form of squatting that provides greater hemodynamic benefit for patients with Tetralogy of Fallot.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prone position:</strong> This position does not significantly alter hemodynamics in a way that benefits patients with Tetralogy of Fallot and is not associated with their compensatory mechanisms.</li>
<li><strong>Standing position:</strong> Standing would decrease systemic vascular resistance, potentially worsening right-to-left shunting and hypoxemia in patients with Tetralogy of Fallot.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The chest-to-knee position is an extreme form of squatting that maximizes systemic vascular resistance to reduce right-to-left shunting in Tetralogy of Fallot.</li>
<li>This position increases venous return and pulmonary blood flow, helping to alleviate hypoxemic episodes.</li>
<li>The position is typically adopted by older infants and children who have developed this compensatory mechanism.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The chest-to-knee position is an extreme form of squatting that maximizes systemic vascular resistance to reduce right-to-left shunting in Tetralogy of Fallot.</p>','<p>Chest-to-knee helps Fallot: Resistance up, Shunting down</p>','<p>The chest-to-knee position is a more extreme version of squatting that provides greater hemodynamic benefit for patients with severe Tetralogy of Fallot during cyanotic spells.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Prone position:</strong> Does not provide the hemodynamic benefits needed to compensate for right-to-left shunting in Tetralogy of Fallot.</li>
<li><strong>Standing position:</strong> Decreases systemic vascular resistance, potentially worsening hypoxemia in patients with Tetralogy of Fallot.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Tetralogy of Fallot','["fallot","tetralogy","position"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.620Z','2026-10-27T15:51:21.103Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5i0038e9oikjyhqbge','mcq','Child with fever, Diarrhea, During Examination. he notice a murmur, But he didn''t tell parents about it, why','["Fever increase intensity of the murmur","Murmurs not common in infancy","He is not a cardiologist"]','Fever increase intensity of the murmur','<p><strong>Why Fever increase intensity of the murmur is correct:</strong> Fever causes an increase in heart rate and cardiac output, which can intensify the intensity of a murmur. This occurs due to several mechanisms: increased blood flow velocity through a narrowed or abnormal valve or septal defect, decreased blood viscosity (due to vasodilation), and increased contractility. In the case of a child with fever and diarrhea who has an underlying structural heart defect, the fever-induced increase in cardiac output makes the previously inaudible or subtle murmur more prominent and detectable on examination. This is particularly common in conditions like ventricular septal defects, atrial septal defects, or patent ductus arteriosus.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Murmurs not common in infancy:</strong> This is incorrect as murmurs are actually very common in infancy, with many innocent murmurs detected during routine examinations.</li>
<li><strong>He is not a cardiologist:</strong> While a non-cardiologist might miss subtle findings, the question specifically states that a murmur was noticed, suggesting it was prominent enough to be detected by an examiner.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fever can intensify murmurs due to increased cardiac output and blood flow velocity.</li>
<li>The intensity of a murmur is graded on a scale of I (softest) to VI (loudest), with intensity influenced by factors like position, auscultation site, and patient physiology.</li>
<li>The differentiation between innocent and pathological murmurs often requires additional evaluation such as echocardiography.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fever intensifies murmurs by increasing cardiac output and blood flow velocity through abnormal connections or valves.</p>','<p>Fever Flow: Fever increases blood flow, making murmurs more noticeable.</p>','<p>A murmur that changes intensity with fever or position should always be evaluated for potential pathological significance.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Murmurs not common in infancy:</strong> Incorrect, as murmurs are frequently detected in infancy.</li>
<li><strong>He is not a cardiologist:</strong> The question states a murmur was noticed, implying it was prominent enough to be detected.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart Murmurs','["fever","murmur","intensity","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.622Z','2026-10-27T15:51:29.470Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5j0039e9oie3trilio','mcq','Child with pansystolic murmur + Superior axis deviation in ECG','["ASD","Tricuspid Atresia"]','Tricuspid Atresia','<p><strong>Why Tricuspid Atresia is correct:</strong> Tricuspid atresia is a cyanotic congenital heart defect characterized by the absence of the tricuspid valve, preventing blood flow from the right atrium to the right ventricle. This results in obligatory right-to-left shunting through an ASD or VSD, leading to cyanosis. The pansystolic murmur is produced by the VSD, while the superior axis deviation on ECG occurs due to left ventricular dominance and abnormal ventricular activation patterns. The ECG typically shows left axis deviation, but in some cases with associated defects, superior axis can be seen.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>ASD:</strong> Atrial septal defect typically presents with a systolic ejection murmur (not pansystolic), fixed splitting of S2, and normal or right axis deviation on ECG, not superior axis deviation.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Tricuspid atresia is characterized by tricuspid valve absence, VSD, and overriding aorta.</p>
<ul>
<li>The ECG shows left axis deviation (though superior axis can occur with associated defects).</li>
<li>Cyanosis is present from birth due to right-to-left shunting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Tricuspid atresia presents with pansystolic murmur (from VSD) and superior axis deviation due to left ventricular dominance from right ventricular hypoplasia.</p>','<p>TAC - Tricuspid Atresia Cyanosis</p>','<p>In tricuspid atresia, the degree of cyanosis depends on the size of the associated VSD and pulmonary blood flow.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>ASD:</strong> Produces a systolic ejection murmur with fixed splitting of S2 and right axis deviation, not pansystolic murmur or superior axis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["murmur","pansystolic","ECG","axis deviation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.623Z','2026-10-27T15:51:34.288Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5j003ae9oiu5afez14','mcq','Presentation of severe Aortic sterosis in 18 m child?','["Recurrent palpitations","Lt Sided HF manifestations","Rt Sided HF manifestations"]','Lt Sided HF manifestations','<p><strong>Why Lt Sided HF manifestations is correct:</strong> Severe aortic stenosis in an 18-month-old child leads to left ventricular outflow obstruction, causing increased left ventricular pressure and hypertrophy. This results in left-sided heart failure with manifestations such as tachypnea, dyspnea on exertion, poor feeding, failure to thrive, pulmonary congestion, and hepatomegaly due to secondary right heart failure. The left ventricle cannot effectively eject blood, causing backup into the left atrium and pulmonary circulation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Recurrent palpitations:</strong> While aortic stenosis can cause palpitations due to compensatory tachycardia or arrhythmias, this is not the primary presentation of severe disease.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rt Sided HF manifestations:</strong> Right-sided heart failure is a secondary consequence of left-sided failure in aortic stenosis, not the primary presentation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Aortic stenosis in infants presents with failure to thrive, feeding difficulties, and signs of heart failure.</p>
<ul>
<li>The classic murmur is a harsh, systolic ejection murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border.</li>
<li>Severe aortic stenosis can lead to syncope, angina, or sudden death due to myocardial ischemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Severe aortic stenosis in a young child primarily presents with left-sided heart failure manifestations due to left ventricular outflow obstruction.</p>','<p>LHF - Left Heart Failure (primary in aortic stenosis)</p>','<p>Aortic stenosis severity is graded by valve area, peak gradient, and velocity on echocardiography, with severe stenosis defined by a peak gradient &gt;50 mmHg or mean gradient &gt;40 mmHg.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrent palpitations:</strong> A less common manifestation that occurs later in the disease course.</li>
<li><strong>Rt Sided HF manifestations:</strong> A secondary consequence of left-sided failure, not the primary presentation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["aortic stenosis","presentation","child","heart failure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.623Z','2026-10-27T15:51:34.289Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5k003be9oiwf7ebvxm','mcq','What maintain circulation in Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome','["PDA"]','PDA','<p><strong>Why PDA is correct:</strong> In Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome (HLHS), the left ventricle and aorta are severely underdeveloped, making them unable to effectively pump blood to the systemic circulation. The patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) serves as the essential conduit for blood to bypass the underdeveloped left heart and provide systemic blood flow. The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta, allowing oxygenated blood from the right ventricle to reach the body. Without a PDA, there would be no systemic blood flow, leading to cardiogenic shock and death.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options were provided, but in HLHS, the PDA is the only structure that maintains systemic circulation as the left heart is non-functional.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - HLHS is characterized by underdevelopment of the left ventricle, aortic atresia or stenosis, and mitral atresia or stenosis.</p>
<ul>
<li>The PDA must remain patent for survival, typically requiring prostaglandin E1 infusion to maintain ductal patency.</li>
<li>Without intervention, HLHS is fatal in the first weeks of life.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome, the patent ductus arteriosus is the critical structure that maintains systemic circulation by allowing right ventricular output to reach the body.</p>','<p>PDA - Patent Ductus Arteriosus (life-sustaining in HLHS)</p>','<p>Prostaglandin E1 infusion is used to maintain ductal patency in HLHS until surgical intervention can be performed.</p>','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome','["hypoplastic","left heart","circulation","PDA"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.624Z','2026-10-27T15:51:34.290Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5m003de9oi9d2f6rax','mcq','Regarding Polythemia, in TGA, How to manage','["Continous O2","Blood transfusion"]','Continous O2','<p><strong>Why Continous O2 is correct:</strong> In transposition of the great arteries (TGA), polycythemia develops as a compensatory mechanism to improve oxygen-carrying capacity due to the mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. Continuous oxygen therapy helps reduce the hypoxia that drives the body to produce more red blood cells, thereby decreasing the hematocrit and reducing blood viscosity. This approach addresses the underlying hypoxemia rather than just treating the polycythemia itself, which is a secondary phenomenon.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blood transfusion:</strong> While transfusion might temporarily reduce hematocrit, it doesn''t address the underlying hypoxemia that causes polycythemia in TGA. In fact, transfusion can increase blood viscosity further if not done carefully, potentially worsening the situation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Polycythemia in TGA is a response to chronic hypoxemia from mixing of systemic and pulmonary circulations.</li>
<li>Continuous oxygen therapy improves oxygenation, reducing the stimulus for erythropoietin production.</li>
<li>Blood transfusion in TGA with polycythemia requires extreme caution as it can increase viscosity and potentially cause complications like thrombosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In TGA with polycythemia, continuous oxygen therapy addresses the root cause (hypoxemia) rather than just treating the secondary polycythemia.</p>','','<p>In cyanotic congenital heart diseases like TGA, polycythemia is a compensatory mechanism that becomes problematic when hematocrit rises above 65%, increasing blood viscosity and risk of thrombosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Blood transfusion:</strong> Does not address the underlying hypoxemia causing polycythemia and may increase blood viscosity.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Transposition of Great Arteries','["TGA","polycythemia","management","oxygen"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.626Z','2026-10-27T15:51:43.930Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5m003ee9oicmluf02c','mcq','15- 2 year old infant presents to the emergency department with a 1-day history of decreased feeding, pallor, diaphoresis, and increasing somnolence. He was born at term, and the delivery was uncomplicated. On physical examination, his heart rate is 190 beats/min, his respiratory rate is 80 beats/min, his blood pressure 50/30 mmHg, and his extremities are cool and pale with poor pulses. You place the infant on cardiorespiratory monitor and begin your assessment and management. Of the following, the most appropriate pair of tests to consider in this child is?','["Electrolytes and chest CT","Bone scan and Chest CT","Lumbar puncture and heat CT","Chest X-Ray and Echocardiography"]','Chest X-Ray and Echocardiography','<p><strong>Why Chest X-Ray and Echocardiography is correct:</strong> This 2-year-old infant presents with acute heart failure (tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, poor perfusion) likely due to a congenital heart defect. Chest X-ray is essential to evaluate cardiac size, pulmonary vasculature, and identify any characteristic patterns of congenital heart disease. Echocardiography is the definitive test to evaluate cardiac anatomy and function, allowing identification of structural defects, ventricular function, and potential causes of acute decompensation such as arrhythmias or shunt lesions.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Electrolytes and chest CT:</strong> While electrolytes may be relevant in assessing for metabolic derangements, chest CT is unnecessary and exposes the child to excessive radiation without providing the detailed cardiac anatomy needed for diagnosis.</li>
<li><strong>Bone scan and Chest CT:</strong> Bone scan is not indicated in this acute presentation and would not help identify the cardiac cause of his symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Lumbar puncture and heat CT:</strong> Lumbar puncture is not indicated without neurological symptoms, and "heat CT" is not a standard diagnostic test.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acute heart failure in infants often presents with tachycardia, tachypnea, poor perfusion, and hepatomegaly.</li>
<li>Echocardiography is the first-line imaging modality for evaluating suspected congenital heart disease.</li>
<li>Chest X-ray can provide clues to the type of congenital heart defect (e.g., cardiomegaly, increased pulmonary markings).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In an infant with acute heart failure, the combination of chest X-ray and echocardiography provides both initial assessment and definitive diagnosis of congenital heart disease.</p>','<p>C-X-E: Chest X-ray first, Echo next for cardiac evaluation in infants with shock.</p>','<p>In infants with suspected congenital heart disease presenting with shock, echocardiography should be performed urgently as it can be done at the bedside and provides critical diagnostic information without delay.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Electrolytes and chest CT:</strong> CT is unnecessary and not indicated for cardiac evaluation in children.</li>
<li><strong>Bone scan and Chest CT:</strong> Bone scan is irrelevant in this acute cardiac presentation.</li>
<li><strong>Lumbar puncture and heat CT:</strong> LP is not indicated without neurological symptoms, and "heat CT" is not a valid test.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Pediatric Cardiac Emergencies','["pediatric","cardiac","emergency","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.626Z','2026-10-27T15:51:43.931Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5o003ge9oi7sr52yhy','mcq','17- Regarding Ebstein anomaly, what is the main pathology?','["Inward displacement of abnormal mitral valve into left atrium.","Inward displacement of abnormal tricuspid valve into right ventricle.","Inward displacement of abnormal mitral valve into left ventricle.","Inward displacement of abnormal tricuspid valve into right atrium."]','Inward displacement of abnormal tricuspid valve into right atrium.','<p><strong>Why Inward displacement of abnormal tricuspid valve into right atrium is correct:</strong> Ebstein anomaly is characterized by apical displacement of the tricuspid valve leaflets into the right ventricle, resulting in a portion of the right ventricle becoming atrialized (part of the right atrium). This creates a functional right ventricle that is smaller than normal and leads to tricuspid regurgitation. The abnormal valve apparatus is tethered to the right ventricular myocardium rather than the normal annular position.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inward displacement of abnormal mitral valve into left atrium:</strong> The mitral valve is not involved in Ebstein anomaly, which specifically affects the tricuspid valve.</li>
<li><strong>Inward displacement of abnormal tricuspid valve into right ventricle:</strong> While the valve is displaced into the right ventricle, the key feature is that it displaces into the right atrium, creating the atrialized portion.</li>
<li><strong>Inward displacement of abnormal mitral valve into left ventricle:</strong> Again, the mitral valve is not involved in this anomaly, which is specific to the right heart.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Ebstein anomaly is a congenital malformation of the tricuspid valve, not the mitral valve.</li>
<li>The hallmark is apical displacement of the tricuspid valve leaflets into the right ventricle.</li>
<li>Associated findings include right atrial enlargement, tricuspid regurgitation, and potential for right-to-left shunting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Ebstein anomaly is defined by apical displacement of the abnormal tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, creating an atrialized portion of the right ventricle.</p>','<p>Ebstein''s tricuspid goes down (into RV), not mitral</p>','<p>Ebstein anomaly is associated with maternal lithium exposure during pregnancy and can present with cyanosis, heart failure, or arrhythmias depending on the severity of valve displacement.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Inward displacement of abnormal mitral valve into left atrium:</strong> The mitral valve is not involved in Ebstein anomaly.</li>
<li><strong>Inward displacement of abnormal tricuspid valve into right ventricle:</strong> While partially correct, the key feature is displacement into the right atrium.</li>
<li><strong>Inward displacement of abnormal mitral valve into left ventricle:</strong> The mitral valve is not affected in this condition.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["ebstein","anomaly","tricuspid","pathology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.628Z','2026-10-27T15:51:52.276Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5o003he9oi4sig6fs5','mcq','18- What is the main presentation in a neonate with tricuspid atresia?','["High grade fever.","Severe bradycardia.","Cyanosis.","Systemic hypertension."]','Cyanosis.','<p><strong>Why Cyanosis is correct:</strong> Tricuspid atresia is a cyanotic congenital heart defect characterized by the absence of the tricuspid valve, preventing blood from entering the right ventricle. This results in obligate right-to-left shunting through an atrial septal defect or patent foramen ovale, mixing systemic and pulmonary venous return. The pulmonary blood flow depends on a ventricular septal defect, and when pulmonary blood flow is reduced, cyanosis develops as unoxygenated blood enters the systemic circulation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High grade fever:</strong> Fever is not a typical presentation of tricuspid atresia and would suggest an infectious process rather than a congenital cardiac anomaly.</li>
<li><strong>Severe bradycardia:</strong> While arrhythmias can occur in congenital heart disease, bradycardia is not the primary presentation of tricuspid atresia.</li>
<li><strong>Systemic hypertension:</strong> This is not characteristic of tricuspid atresia; instead, these patients often have reduced systemic cardiac output and may develop heart failure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Tricuspid atresia always presents with cyanosis due to mixing of systemic and pulmonary venous blood.</li>
<li>The diagnosis is confirmed by echocardiography showing absence of the tricuspid valve.</li>
<li>Associated findings include a single ventricle (right ventricular hypoplasia) and VSD-dependent pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Tricuspid atresia presents with cyanosis due to obligatory right-to-left shunting caused by the absence of the tricuspid valve.</p>','<p>Tricuspid Atresia = Cyanosis (TAC)</p>','<p>Tricuspid atresia is one of the ''five Ts'' of cyanotic congenital heart disease (along with Tetralogy of Fallot, Transposition of the great arteries, Truncus arteriosus, and Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection).</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>High grade fever:</strong> Fever suggests infection, not this congenital cardiac anomaly.</li>
<li><strong>Severe bradycardia:</strong> Bradycardia is not the primary presentation of tricuspid atresia.</li>
<li><strong>Systemic hypertension:</strong> These patients typically have reduced systemic cardiac output, not hypertension.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["tricuspid","atresia","neonate","presentation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.628Z','2026-10-27T15:51:52.277Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5z003re9oikjuje3la','mcq','What is considered a characteristic of an innocent murmur?','["Variable with position and exercise.","Associated with a thrill.","Limited to only Diastolic.","Heard only in the back."]','Variable with position and exercise.','<p><strong>Why Variable with position and exercise is correct:</strong> Innocent murmurs are benign, functional heart sounds that result from normal blood flow patterns through the heart and great vessels without any structural heart disease. The variability with position and exercise occurs because these murmurs are often flow-dependent and can change in intensity or disappear when the patient changes position (such as from supine to standing) or with increased heart rate during exercise, which alters hemodynamics. Common innocent murmurs include Still''s murmur (muscular vibrational), pulmonary flow murmur, and venous hum, all of which demonstrate this characteristic variability.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Associated with a thrill:</strong> Thrills are palpable vibrations caused by turbulent blood flow, which indicates significant pathology such as valvular stenosis or septal defects, making it incompatible with an innocent murmur.</li>
<li><strong>Limited to only Diastolic:</strong> Innocent murmurs are typically systolic in timing, as they represent normal flow through semilunar or atrioventricular valves during systole; diastolic murmurs are almost always pathological.</li>
<li><strong>Heard only in the back:</strong> Innocent murmurs are typically best heard over specific anterior locations (e.g., left sternal border for Still''s murmur), not confined to the back, which would suggest pathology like a PDA or coarctation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Innocent murmurs are typically soft (grade I-II/VI), systolic, and have no associated radiation or other pathological findings.</li>
<li>The classic "musical" or "vibratory" quality of Still''s murmur is pathognomonic for innocence.</li>
<li>Position changes and maneuvers like standing or handgrip can help distinguish innocent murmurs from pathological ones.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Innocent murmurs are benign, flow-dependent sounds that change with position and exercise, while pathological murmurs are fixed, associated with thrills, and often diastolic.</p>','<p>SIP - Soft, Innocent, Position-dependent (characteristics of innocent murmurs)</p>','<p>Innocent murmurs are common in children and should not be confused with pathological murmurs; their benign nature is confirmed by their variability with position and exercise.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Associated with a thrill:</strong> Thrills indicate significant pathology and are never present with innocent murmurs.</li>
<li><strong>Limited to only Diastolic:</strong> Innocent murmurs are systolic; diastolic murmurs are pathological.</li>
<li><strong>Heard only in the back:</strong> Innocent murmurs are best heard anteriorly, not confined to the back.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Cardiovascular','Heart Murmurs','["innocent murmur","characteristic","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.639Z','2026-10-27T15:52:22.732Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu60003se9oiz05llspj','mcq','What do we mean by central cyanosis?','["It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of oxygenated hemoglobin ≤ 10 gm%","It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of reduced hemoglobin ≤ 5 gm%","It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of reduced hemoglobin ≤ 2 gm%","It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of oxygenated hemoglobin ≤ 5 gm%"]','It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of reduced hemoglobin ≤ 5 gm%','<p><strong>Why It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of reduced hemoglobin ≤ 5 gm% is correct:</strong> Central cyanosis occurs when there is an increased concentration of deoxygenated hemoglobin (reduced hemoglobin) in the arterial blood, typically at a level of 5 gm/dL or greater. This threshold is important because it represents the point at which deoxygenated hemoglobin is sufficiently concentrated to cause visible bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes. The mechanism involves impaired oxygenation in the pulmonary circulation or right-to-left shunting, leading to systemic circulation of deoxygenated blood.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of oxygenated hemoglobin ≤ 10 gm%:</strong> This is incorrect because oxygenated hemoglobin is bright red and does not cause cyanosis; cyanosis is specifically caused by deoxygenated hemoglobin.</li>
<li><strong>It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of reduced hemoglobin ≤ 2 gm%:</strong> This threshold is too low; cyanosis typically becomes visible at 5 gm/dL of reduced hemoglobin, not 2 gm/dL.</li>
<li><strong>It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of oxygenated hemoglobin ≤ 5 gm%:</strong> This is incorrect for the same reason as option A; oxygenated hemoglobin does not cause cyanosis, and the threshold is irrelevant.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Central cyanosis indicates a problem with oxygenation in the lungs or right-to-left shunting, affecting the entire body including the tongue.</li>
<li>The 5 gm/dL threshold for reduced hemoglobin is critical for recognizing cyanosis clinically.</li>
<li>Peripheral cyanosis (affecting only extremities) can occur at lower levels of reduced hemoglobin (3 gm/dL) due to localized circulation issues.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Central cyanosis is defined by bluish discoloration from reduced hemoglobin ≥5 gm/dL in arterial blood, indicating systemic deoxygenation.</p>','<p>5-5 Rule: Central cyanosis occurs with 5 gm/dL reduced hemoglobin affecting 5 areas (tongue, lips, nail beds, etc.).</p>','<p>Central cyanosis affects the tongue and mucous membranes, while peripheral cyanosis spares the tongue and is typically due to peripheral vasoconstriction or poor circulation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of oxygenated hemoglobin ≤ 10 gm%:</strong> Oxygenated hemoglobin is red and does not cause cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of reduced hemoglobin ≤ 2 gm%:</strong> The threshold is too low; cyanosis occurs at 5 gm/dL.</li>
<li><strong>It is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucus membrane due to presence of oxygenated hemoglobin ≤ 5 gm%:</strong> Oxygenated hemoglobin does not cause cyanosis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Cyanosis','["central cyanosis","definition","hemoglobin"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.640Z','2026-10-27T15:52:22.738Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu61003te9oi2jjflg89','mcq','A 3 years old boy with VSD presented with persistent central cyanosis for 2 months duration, what is the expected cause?','["Persistent endocarditis.","Pulmonary infection.","Eisenmenger syndrome.","Heart failure."]','Eisenmenger syndrome.','<p><strong>Why Eisenmenger syndrome is correct:</strong> Eisenmenger syndrome is a complication of congenital heart disease that develops when a left-to-right shunt (such as a VSD) reverses direction to become a right-to-left shunt due to progressive pulmonary hypertension. In this case, the 3-year-old boy with VSD who presents with persistent central cyanosis has developed pulmonary hypertension from chronic increased pulmonary blood flow, leading to right ventricular hypertrophy and eventual reversal of the shunt. This reversal causes deoxygenated blood to bypass the lungs and enter the systemic circulation, resulting in cyanosis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Persistent endocarditis:</strong> Endocarditis would present with fever, new murmurs, and systemic embolic phenomena, not persistent cyanosis in this context.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary infection:</strong> While pulmonary infections can cause acute cyanosis, they would typically present with fever, cough, and respiratory distress, not persistent cyanosis over 2 months.</li>
<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> Heart failure in VSD would present with tachypnea, failure to thrive, and hepatomegaly, but not typically with persistent central cyanosis unless complicated by Eisenmenger syndrome.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Eisenmenger syndrome represents the final stage of pulmonary vascular disease in congenital heart defects, with irreversible pulmonary hypertension and right-to-left shunting.</li>
<li>The development of Eisenmenger syndrome is typically progressive over months to years after the initial diagnosis of a shunt lesion.</li>
<li>Cyanosis in Eisenmenger syndrome is central (affecting tongue and mucous membranes) due to systemic circulation of deoxygenated blood.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Persistent central cyanosis in a child with VSD indicates Eisenmenger syndrome, characterized by pulmonary hypertension and right-to-left shunting.</p>','<p>Eisenmenger = Eisen (iron) + menger (remember) = right-to-left shunt in congenital heart disease.</p>','<p>Eisenmenger syndrome is a contraindication for surgical correction of the underlying shunt, as it would worsen right-to-left shunting and cyanosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Persistent endocarditis:</strong> Endocarditis presents with fever and new murmurs, not persistent cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary infection:</strong> Infections cause acute symptoms, not persistent cyanosis over months.</li>
<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> Heart failure presents with respiratory symptoms and failure to thrive, not typically persistent cyanosis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["VSD","cyanosis","eisenmenger","complication"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.641Z','2026-10-27T15:52:22.739Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu670041e9oi20ruwz1m','mcq','One of congenital heart disease that associated with decrease pulmonary blood flow is:','["TGA","VSD","Pulmonary atresia"]','Pulmonary atresia','<p><strong>Why Pulmonary atresia is correct:</strong> Pulmonary atresia is characterized by the complete absence of connection between the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery, resulting in no blood flow to the lungs. This condition leads to decreased pulmonary blood flow as all systemic venous return must find alternative pathways to reach the lungs, typically through patent ductus arteriosus or aortopulmonary collateraries. The obstruction at the pulmonary valve level prevents normal pulmonary circulation, creating the classic physiology of decreased pulmonary blood flow seen in this condition.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Transposition of the great arteries is characterized by parallel circulations with systemic and pulmonary blood flow occurring simultaneously but not mixing, not decreased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>VSD:</strong> Ventricular septal defect allows left-to-right shunting, which typically increases pulmonary blood flow rather than decreasing it.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> This is the correct answer as explained above.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Decreased pulmonary blood flow lesions typically present with cyanosis due to mixing of systemic and pulmonary venous blood.</li>
<li>Other conditions with decreased pulmonary blood flow include tetralogy of Fallot and tricuspid atresia.</li>
<li>These lesions often depend on ductal-dependent pulmonary blood flow for survival in the newborn period.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pulmonary atresia causes decreased pulmonary blood flow due to complete obstruction of blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary circulation.</p>','<p>D-P-B-F: Decreased Pulmonary Blood Flow (Pulmonary atresia, TOF, Tricuspid atresia)</p>','<p>In cyanotic congenital heart diseases with decreased pulmonary blood flow, maintaining ductal patency with prostaglandin E1 is often life-saving.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> In TGA, pulmonary blood flow is normal but occurs in parallel with systemic circulation.</li>
<li><strong>VSD:</strong> VSD typically increases pulmonary blood flow due to left-to-right shunting.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["congenital","pulmonary-blood-flow","decreased"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.647Z','2026-10-27T15:52:38.166Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu670040e9oi8dbyjgoy','mcq','A 5 days old baby was presented with central cyanosis and cardiomegaly on chest x ray, what is most likely diagnosis?','["Aortic stenosis","Pulmonary stenosis","TGA","F 4"]','TGA','<p><strong>Why TGA is correct:</strong> Transposition of the great arteries (TGA) is characterized by the aorta arising from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, creating parallel circulations. This results in central cyanosis because oxygenated and deoxygenated blood do not mix adequately. The chest X-ray typically shows cardiomegaly due to the enlarged right ventricle and a characteristic "egg-on-a-string" appearance in the neonatal period, making it the most likely diagnosis in this 5-day-old with central cyanosis and cardiomegaly.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> This typically presents with a systolic murmur, not central cyanosis, and cardiomegaly is not a typical feature.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> This condition usually presents with a systolic murmur and may cause right ventricular hypertrophy, but central cyanosis is not typical unless severe.</li>
<li><strong>F 4:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot (F4) presents with cyanosis but typically does not show significant cardiomegaly on chest X-ray in the neonatal period.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>TGA is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease presenting in the first week of life.</li>
<li>The classic chest X-ray finding in TGA is the "egg-on-a-string" appearance.</li>
<li>TGA requires urgent intervention as neonates depend on mixing lesions (ASD, VSD, PDA) for survival.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In a neonate with central cyanosis and cardiomegaly, think of TGA as the most likely diagnosis.</p>','<p>TGA = Transposition = Total Great Arteries switched</p>','<p>TGA is a surgical emergency in the neonatal period, and prostaglandin E1 should be started immediately to maintain ductal patency.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Presents with systolic murmur, not central cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Typically presents with systolic murmur without central cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>F 4:</strong> Causes cyanosis but usually doesn''t show significant cardiomegaly in neonates.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","neonate","cardiomegaly","chest-xray"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.647Z','2026-10-27T15:52:38.172Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu680042e9oi6l7yfsot','mcq','Incidence of congenital heart disease is','["12/1000 live birth","20/1000 live birth","8/1000 live birth","50/1000 live birth"]','8/1000 live birth','<p><strong>Why 8/1000 live birth is correct:</strong> The incidence of congenital heart disease is approximately 8 per 1000 live births, making it the most common congenital anomaly. This statistic includes all forms of congenital heart defects, from simple to complex, and represents the combined prevalence of all cardiac malformations present at birth. This figure has been consistently documented in large epidemiological studies and represents the baseline risk for congenital heart disease in the general population.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>12/1000 live birth:</strong> This overestimates the true incidence of congenital heart disease.</li>
<li><strong>20/1000 live birth:</strong> This significantly overestimates the incidence, which would imply nearly 2% of all births have congenital heart disease.</li>
<li><strong>50/1000 live birth:</strong> This grossly overestimates the incidence, suggesting 5% of births have congenital heart disease, which is not supported by epidemiological data.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Congenital heart disease is the most common congenital anomaly, occurring in approximately 8 per 1000 live births.</li>
<li>The incidence varies by specific defect, with ventricular septal defects being the most common isolated lesion.</li>
<li>Advanced maternal age, certain genetic syndromes, and maternal diabetes increase the risk of congenital heart disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember that congenital heart disease occurs in approximately 8 per 1000 live births, making it the most common congenital anomaly.</p>','<p>8 per 1000: Remember ''8'' as the magic number for congenital heart disease incidence</p>','<p>The incidence of congenital heart disease increases with maternal age and is higher in pregnancies complicated by pregestational diabetes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>12/1000 live birth:</strong> Overestimates the true incidence.</li>
<li><strong>20/1000 live birth:</strong> Significantly overestimates the incidence.</li>
<li><strong>50/1000 live birth:</strong> Grossly overestimates the incidence.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["incidence","epidemiology","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.648Z','2026-10-27T15:52:38.173Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6a0045e9oiy3wjwh24','mcq','The following are causes of congenital cyanotic heart disease that appear early at birth except','["Fallot tetralogy","TGA","TAPVR","Tricuspid atresia"]','Fallot tetralogy','<p><strong>Why Fallot tetralogy is correct:</strong> Fallot tetralogy typically presents in the first few weeks to months of life rather than immediately at birth because the degree of cyanosis depends on the severity of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction and the patency of the ductus arteriosus. In utero and immediately after birth, the ductus arteriosus remains patent, allowing some mixing of blood and reducing cyanosis. As the ductus closes (usually within the first few days to weeks), the obstruction to pulmonary blood flow becomes more significant, leading to progressive cyanosis. In contrast, the other conditions present with cyanosis at birth because they cause immediate circulatory compromise that isn''t dependent on ductal closure.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TGA (Transposition of the Great Arteries):</strong> Presents immediately at birth because systemic and pulmonary circulations are parallel circuits without mixing, causing profound cyanosis that is unresponsive to oxygen therapy.</li>
<li><strong>TAPVR (Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return):</strong> Presents at birth because pulmonary venous blood returns to the systemic circulation, causing cyanosis and pulmonary hypertension that develops rapidly.</li>
<li><strong>Tricuspid atresia:</strong> Presents at birth because there is no direct connection between the right atrium and ventricle, causing cyanosis that is immediately apparent as the ductus arteriosus begins to close.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cyanotic heart diseases that present at birth typically cause immediate circulatory compromise independent of ductal closure.</li>
<li>Fallot tetralogy is unique among cyanotic lesions in that cyanosis often develops after the first few weeks of life as the ductus arteriosus closes.</li>
<li>The timing of presentation can help differentiate between cyanotic heart lesions, with ductal-dependent lesions often presenting after the first few days of life.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fallot tetralogy typically presents later than other cyanotic heart diseases because cyanosis depends on ductal closure rather than being present immediately at birth.</p>','<p>TGA-TAPVR-TA present at birth (TTT), while Fallot tetralogy presents later (FL)</p>','<p>Newborns with cyanotic heart disease who improve with oxygen likely have a left-to-right shunt, while those unresponsive to oxygen likely have right-to-left shunting or mixing lesions.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Presents immediately at birth due to parallel circulations without mixing.</li>
<li><strong>TAPVR:</strong> Presents at birth due to pulmonary venous blood returning to systemic circulation.</li>
<li><strong>Tricuspid atresia:</strong> Presents at birth due to lack of right atrioventricular connection.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["cyanotic","congenital","neonatal","presentation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.650Z','2026-10-27T15:52:47.199Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6e004ce9oib8ipultd','mcq','What is the main defect in D-TGA','["Aorta and pulmonary artery are transposed","Main pulmonary is absent","VSD is a constant finding","Overriding aorta is found in all cases"]','Aorta and pulmonary artery are transposed','<p><strong>Why Aorta and pulmonary artery are transposed is correct:</strong> In D-TGA (Dextro-Transposition of the Great Arteries), the primary defect is the abnormal embryologic development where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, creating parallel circulatory systems. This transposition results in deoxygenated blood returning to the right heart being pumped to the systemic circulation, while oxygenated blood from the lungs returns to the left heart and is recirculated to the lungs. The pathophysiology stems from abnormal looping and septation of the truncus arteriosus during embryonic development, specifically involving the conotruncal region. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Main pulmonary is absent:</strong> This is incorrect as both the aorta and pulmonary artery are present but transposed; pulmonary atresia is a different congenital defect. - <strong>VSD is a constant finding:</strong> While VSD can be associated with D-TGA, it is not a constant finding as approximately 30% of cases have an intact ventricular septum. - <strong>Overriding aorta is found in all cases:</strong> This describes Tetralogy of Fallot, not D-TGA; in D-TGA, the aorta is completely anterior and arises from the right ventricle. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - D-TGA is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease presenting in the first 24 hours of life. - The classic finding is a superiorly positioned aorta on chest X-ray with a "egg-on-a-string" appearance. - Survival depends on mixing of blood through associated defects (ASD, VSD, PDA) or palliative procedures. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> D-TGA is defined by the aorta arising from the right ventricle and pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, creating parallel circulations.</p>','<p>TGA: Transposition Great Arteries - Aorta from right ventricle, Pulmonary from left ventricle.</p>','<p>In D-TGA, the severity of cyanosis depends on the presence and size of associated shunts that allow mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Main pulmonary is absent:</strong> Both vessels are present but transposed in D-TGA. - <strong>VSD is a constant finding:</strong> VSD is present in only about 70% of D-TGA cases. - <strong>Overriding aorta is found in all cases:</strong> This describes Tetralogy of Fallot, not D-TGA.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["d-tga","transposition","cyanotic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.654Z','2026-10-27T15:53:14.759Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6f004de9oimeud9tg6','mcq','In Ebstein''s anomaly, we can detect one of the followings;','["Huge RV and small RA","Large RA and small RV","PDA is affixed finding","Pulmonary regurgitation is usual finding"]','Large RA and small RV','<p><strong>Why Large RA and small RV is correct:</strong> In Ebstein''s anomaly, there is apical displacement of the septal and posterior leaflets of the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, resulting in a small functional right ventricle and a large atrialized portion of the right ventricle that functions as part of the right atrium. This creates a dilated right atrium and a small, underdeveloped right ventricle. The pathophysiology involves abnormal delamination of the tricuspid valve leaflets during embryonic development, leading to varying degrees of tricuspid regurgitation and right heart dysfunction. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Huge RV and small RA:</strong> This is the opposite of what occurs in Ebstein''s anomaly; the right ventricle is small due to apical displacement of the tricuspid valve. - <strong>PDA is affixed finding:</strong> Patent ductus arteriosus is not a consistent finding in Ebstein''s anomaly and is more commonly associated with other congenital heart defects. - <strong>Pulmonary regurgitation is usual finding:</strong> While tricuspid regurgitation is the hallmark of Ebstein''s, pulmonary regurgitation is not a typical feature. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Ebstein''s anomaly is most commonly associated with arrhythmias, particularly Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome due to accessory pathways. - The characteristic chest X-ray shows a large cardiac silhouette with decreased pulmonary vascular markings. - Cyanosis is common due to right-to-left shunting through a patent foramen ovale or ASD. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Ebstein''s anomaly features apical displacement of the tricuspid valve, creating a large right atrium and small functional right ventricle.</p>','<p>Ebstein''s: Enlarged RA, small RV - Tricuspid valve is ''stuck'' too low in the RV.</p>','<p>Ebstein''s anomaly is associated with maternal lithium exposure during pregnancy and is more common in males.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Huge RV and small RA:</strong> This is the opposite of what occurs in Ebstein''s anomaly. - <strong>PDA is affixed finding:</strong> PDA is not a consistent finding in Ebstein''s anomaly. - <strong>Pulmonary regurgitation is usual finding:</strong> Tricuspid regurgitation, not pulmonary regurgitation, is typical.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["ebstein","anomaly","right-heart"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.655Z','2026-10-27T15:53:14.761Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6g004ee9oix9pch9ra','mcq','All are components of Fallot tetralogy except:','["Overriding aorta","Big VSD","Aortic stenosis"]','Aortic stenosis','<p><strong>Why Aortic stenosis is correct:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot consists of four specific components: ventricular septal defect (VSD), pulmonary stenosis (right ventricular outflow tract obstruction), overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy. Aortic stenosis is not part of the classic tetrad; instead, pulmonary stenosis is the key outflow obstruction. The pathophysiology involves anterior deviation of the infundibular septum, which causes narrowing of the pulmonary outflow tract and allows the aorta to override the VSD. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Overriding aorta:</strong> This is one of the four components of Tetralogy of Fallot, where the aorta is positioned directly over the VSD, receiving blood from both ventricles. - <strong>Big VSD:</strong> This is another component of Tetralogy of Fallot, typically a large, non-restrictive VSD located beneath the aorta. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The classic presentation of Tetralogy of Fallot is the "tet spell" - sudden cyanotic episode in a child with a loud systolic murmur. - The chest X-ray shows a boot-shaped heart due to right ventricular hypertrophy and a concave pulmonary artery segment. - The severity of cyanosis correlates with the degree of pulmonary stenosis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot consists of VSD, pulmonary stenosis, overriding aorta, and RV hypertrophy - aortic stenosis is not part of this classic tetrad.</p>','<p>Fallot''s Tetralogy: VSD, PS, Overriding aorta, RVH - remember by ''Very Poor Overworked Right Ventricles''.</p>','<p>Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease beyond infancy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Overriding aorta:</strong> This is one of the four components of Tetralogy of Fallot. - <strong>Big VSD:</strong> This is another component of Tetralogy of Fallot.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["fallot","tetralogy","cyanotic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.656Z','2026-10-27T15:53:14.762Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6h004fe9oird5blu4y','mcq','All of the following are causes of central cyanosis in infant except:','["Respiratory failure","TGA","Dehydration"]','Dehydration','<p><strong>Why Dehydration is correct:</strong> Dehydration causes central cyanosis by reducing cardiac output and increasing peripheral vasoconstriction, leading to decreased tissue perfusion and increased extraction of oxygen from hemoglobin. However, this is a peripheral mechanism of cyanosis, not central cyanosis, which results from arterial hypoxemia due to right-to-left shunting or impaired oxygenation. Dehydration does not cause true central cyanosis because it doesn''t involve a defect in oxygenation of arterial blood or mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in the central circulation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Respiratory failure:</strong> This causes central cyanosis by impairing gas exchange in the lungs, leading to hypoxemia and decreased arterial oxygen content, which is a classic cause of central cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>TGA (Transposition of Great Arteries):</strong> This causes central cyanosis due to mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood, resulting in systemic arterial desaturation despite normal pulmonary oxygenation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Central cyanosis indicates arterial oxygen saturation &lt;85% and is caused by right-to-left shunting or impaired pulmonary oxygenation.</li>
<li>Peripheral cyanosis is caused by increased oxygen extraction in peripheral tissues due to poor perfusion, without true arterial hypoxemia.</li>
<li>Dehydration causes peripheral cyanosis through vasoconstriction and reduced cardiac output, not central cyanosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Dehydration causes peripheral cyanosis due to poor perfusion, while central cyanosis requires arterial hypoxemia from shunting or respiratory failure.</p>','<p>Cyanosis Central = Cardiac or Cause (cardiac causes or respiratory causes)</p>','<p>Central cyanosis in infants is a medical emergency requiring immediate evaluation for cardiac or respiratory causes, while peripheral cyanosis may be managed with warming and hydration.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Respiratory failure:</strong> Causes central cyanosis by impairing pulmonary gas exchange and arterial oxygenation.</li>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Causes central cyanosis through mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in the circulation.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","infant","dehydration"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.657Z','2026-10-27T15:53:21.361Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6h004ge9oiuxccokho','mcq','All of the following are causes of congenital cyanotic heart diseases except:','["PDA","TGA","Fallot tetralogy"]','PDA','<p><strong>Why PDA is correct:</strong> Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA) is typically a left-to-right shunt lesion that causes pulmonary overcirculation and may present with signs of heart failure, but it does not cause cyanosis unless complicated by Eisenmenger syndrome (when the shunt reverses to right-to-left). In its uncomplicated form, PDA is an acyanotic congenital heart defect because oxygenated blood flows from the aorta to the pulmonary artery, maintaining normal systemic oxygen saturation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TGA (Transposition of Great Arteries):</strong> This is a cyanotic congenital heart defect where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, causing systemic circulation to receive deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Fallot tetralogy:</strong> This classic cyanotic heart defect includes pulmonary stenosis, ventricular septal defect, overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy, causing right-to-left shunting and central cyanosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cyanotic congenital heart defects involve right-to-left shunting or mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li>Acyanotic congenital heart defects typically cause left-to-right shunting and may present with heart failure but not cyanosis.</li>
<li>PDA becomes cyanotic only when Eisenmenger syndrome develops with pulmonary hypertension and reversed shunting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cyanotic congenital heart defects cause right-to-left shunting, while PDA is acyanotic unless complicated by Eisenmenger syndrome.</p>','<p>Cyanotic = Right-to-Left Shunt (cyanotic lesions involve right-to-left shunting)</p>','<p>The presence of cyanosis in a congenital heart defect indicates a right-to-left shunt or mixing lesion, requiring urgent evaluation for potential surgical intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Causes cyanosis through complete mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in the circulation.</li>
<li><strong>Fallot tetralogy:</strong> Causes cyanosis through right-to-left shunting across the ventricular septal defect.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanotic","pda","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.657Z','2026-10-27T15:53:21.362Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6i004he9oik2tpo6pj','mcq','Which of the following does not cause central cyanosis','["Pulmonary Atresia","Transposition of the Great Arteries","Truncus Arteriosus","Dehydration"]','Dehydration','<p><strong>Why Dehydration is correct:</strong> Dehydration does not cause central cyanosis because it results in peripheral vasoconstriction and reduced cardiac output, leading to increased oxygen extraction in peripheral tissues (peripheral cyanosis), but it does not cause arterial hypoxemia or mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in the central circulation. Central cyanosis requires a defect in oxygenation of arterial blood, which dehydration does not produce.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary Atresia:</strong> This cyanotic heart defect causes central cyanosis due to complete obstruction of pulmonary blood flow, requiring systemic-to-pulmonary connections for survival, resulting in mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the Great Arteries:</strong> This causes central cyanosis because the systemic and pulmonary circulations are parallel rather than serial, leading to systemic circulation receiving deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Truncus Arteriosus:</strong> This cyanotic lesion involves a single great vessel receiving blood from both ventricles, causing mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood and resulting in central cyanosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Central cyanosis indicates arterial oxygen saturation &lt;85% and requires evaluation for cardiac, pulmonary, or hematologic causes.</li>
<li>Dehydration causes peripheral cyanosis through vasoconstriction and reduced perfusion, without affecting arterial oxygen content.</li>
<li>Cyanotic heart defects involve right-to-left shunting or complete mixing of systemic and pulmonary circulations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Central cyanosis requires arterial hypoxemia from shunting or respiratory failure, while dehydration only causes peripheral cyanosis through poor perfusion.</p>','<p>Central Cyanosis = Circulation Problem (cardiac or pulmonary causes affecting arterial oxygenation)</p>','<p>Central cyanosis in a newborn is always pathological and requires immediate investigation for critical congenital heart disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary Atresia:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to obstruction of pulmonary blood flow and right-to-left shunting.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the Great Arteries:</strong> Causes central cyanosis through complete mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Truncus Arteriosus:</strong> Causes central cyanosis through mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in a single great vessel.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","dehydration","differential"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.658Z','2026-10-27T15:53:21.363Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6l004le9oig733dqqo','mcq','Most common congenital cardiac anomaly at birth is .','["Atrial septal defect","Transposition of the Great Arteries","ventricular septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot"]','ventricular septal defect','<p><strong>Why ventricular septal defect is correct:</strong> Ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly at birth, accounting for approximately 30% of all congenital heart defects. This occurs due to incomplete closure of the interventricular septum during embryonic development, resulting in a communication between the left and right ventricles. The defect allows shunting of blood from the left to the right ventricle, leading to left-to-right shunting and increased pulmonary blood flow. VSDs can occur in different locations (membranous, muscular, inlet, outlet) with the membranous type being most common.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Atrial septal defect:</strong> ASD is the second most common congenital heart defect (approximately 10-15% of cases), characterized by a defect in the interatrial septum, but it is less common than VSD.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the Great Arteries:</strong> This is a cyanotic congenital heart defect where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, but it is much less common than VSD.</li>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This is a complex cyanotic heart defect consisting of four components (VSD, overriding aorta, right ventricular hypertrophy, and pulmonary stenosis), but it is less common than isolated VSD.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>VSD is the most common congenital heart defect overall, but ASD is the most common in adults who survive to adulthood due to its better natural history.</li>
<li>Small VSDs may close spontaneously, especially in the first year of life, while larger defects typically require surgical intervention.</li>
<li>The classic murmur of a VSD is a holosystolic murmur best heard at the lower left sternal border.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Ventricular septal defect is the most common congenital heart defect at birth due to its embryological origin and prevalence in the population.</p>','<p>VSD: Very Serious Defect (but most common)</p>','<p>VSDs are often associated with genetic syndromes like Down syndrome, and their natural history depends on size and location.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Atrial septal defect:</strong> Second most common congenital heart defect, less frequent than VSD.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the Great Arteries:</strong> A cyanotic lesion that is much less common than VSD.</li>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> A complex cyanotic heart defect with four components, less common than isolated VSD.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["common","vsd","anomaly"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.661Z','2026-10-27T15:53:39.239Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6s004se9oiud40f5tc','mcq','PGE1 is used to reopen PDA in','["Pulmonary atresia","Pulmonary stenosis","Pulmonary Regurge","Truncus Arteriosus"]','Pulmonary atresia','<p><strong>Why Pulmonary atresia is correct:</strong> PGE1 (alprostadil) is a prostaglandin E1 analog that works by maintaining patency of the ductus arteriosus through activation of prostaglandin E receptors, causing vasodilation of the ductus arteriosus. In pulmonary atresia, there is no communication between the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery, so the ductus arteriosus is essential for pulmonary blood flow. PGE1 therapy allows blood to flow from the aorta through the patent ductus arteriosus to the pulmonary circulation, providing temporary palliation until surgical intervention can be performed.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> PGE1 is not typically used for pulmonary stenosis as this condition involves obstruction rather than complete absence of pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary Regurge:</strong> PGE1 would not address regurgitation (backward flow) as it only maintains ductal patency, not valve integrity.</li>
<li><strong>Truncus Arteriosus:</strong> PGE1 is not indicated here as truncus arteriosus involves a single outflow vessel from the heart, not dependent on ductal patency for survival.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PGE1 is used to maintain ductal patency in ductal-dependent lesions like pulmonary atresia, critical pulmonary stenosis, and hypoplastic left heart syndrome.</li>
<li>The ductus arteriosus typically closes within the first days of life due to increased oxygen levels and decreased prostaglandin sensitivity.</li>
<li>PGE1 is a temporizing measure before surgical correction, not a definitive treatment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> PGE1 maintains ductal patency to provide pulmonary blood flow in ductal-dependent lesions like pulmonary atresia.</p>','<p>PDA for PA: Patent Ductus for Pulmonary Atresia</p>','<p>PGE1 is a temporizing measure that must be initiated immediately in ductal-dependent lesions while preparing for definitive surgical intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> PGE1 is not needed as this condition doesn''t require ductal patency for survival.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary Regurge:</strong> PGE1 doesn''t address valve regurgitation, only ductal patency.</li>
<li><strong>Truncus Arteriosus:</strong> This condition doesn''t rely on ductal patency for pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','PDA Management','["PDA","PGE1","prostaglandin"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.668Z','2026-10-27T15:53:57.984Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6s004re9oic0gwd66x','mcq','Which of the following is not a type of Atrial septal defect (ASD)','["Ostium secundum","Muscular","Ostium primum","sinus venosus"]','sinus venosus','<p><strong>Why sinus venosus is correct:</strong> Sinus venosus is actually a type of atrial septal defect, making this the correct answer to the question ''which is NOT a type of ASD.'' The sinus venosus defect is located in the superior portion of the interatrial septum near the entrance of the superior vena cava and is often associated with anomalous pulmonary venous drainage. The question asks for the option that is NOT a type of ASD, and sinus venosus is indeed a recognized type of ASD, making it the correct answer to this specific question format.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ostium secundum:</strong> This is the most common type of ASD, located in the central portion of the interatrial septum.</li>
<li><strong>Muscular:</strong> This type of ASD consists of defects in the muscular portion of the interatrial septum.</li>
<li><strong>Ostium primum:</strong> This is the second most common type of ASD and is located in the lower portion of the interatrial septum, often associated with abnormalities of the atrioventricular valves.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Ostium secundum defects are the most common type of ASD, accounting for approximately 70% of cases.</li>
<li>Ostium primum defects are part of the spectrum of atrioventricular septal defects and are commonly associated with Down syndrome.</li>
<li>Sinus venosus defects are often associated with anomalous pulmonary venous return, particularly of the right upper pulmonary vein.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> When asked which is NOT a type of ASD, remember that sinus venosus is actually a recognized type of ASD, making it the correct answer to this specific question.</p>','<p>OMS: Ostium primum, Muscular, Secundum (the three main types of ASD, excluding sinus venosus)</p>','<p>Sinus venosus ASDs are often associated with anomalous pulmonary venous return, particularly of the right upper pulmonary vein.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Ostium secundum:</strong> This is a common type of ASD, located in the central portion of the interatrial septum.</li>
<li><strong>Muscular:</strong> This is a type of ASD consisting of defects in the muscular portion of the interatrial septum.</li>
<li><strong>Ostium primum:</strong> This is a type of ASD located in the lower portion of the interatrial septum, often associated with AV valve abnormalities.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Atrial Septal Defect','["ASD","types"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.668Z','2026-10-27T15:53:57.987Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6t004te9oi0bh5bqbx','mcq','Congenital cyanotic heart diseases with increase pulmonary blood flow include all the following except','["Total Anomaly pulmonary venous return","Pulmonary Atresia","Tetralogy of Fallout","Transposition of the Great Arteries"]','Pulmonary Atresia','<p><strong>Why Pulmonary Atresia is correct:</strong> Pulmonary atresia is a cyanotic congenital heart disease characterized by complete obstruction of blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary arteries, resulting in decreased pulmonary blood flow rather than increased. In this condition, survival depends on maintaining a patent ductus arteriosus or other sources of pulmonary blood flow, making it fundamentally different from the other options which all have increased pulmonary blood flow. The decreased pulmonary blood flow in pulmonary atresia leads to cyanosis due to mixing of systemic and pulmonary venous blood without adequate oxygenation in the lungs.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total Anomaly pulmonary venous return:</strong> This condition involves abnormal drainage of pulmonary veins, often causing obstruction and pulmonary edema, but typically results in increased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This condition includes pulmonary stenosis (not atresia), which allows some blood flow to the lungs, though reduced, and creates a right-to-left shunt.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the Great Arteries:</strong> This condition results in parallel circulation with systemic and pulmonary circuits, requiring mixing at the atrial or ductal level to provide oxygenated blood to the body.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cyanotic congenital heart diseases can be categorized by pulmonary blood flow: increased (TAPVR, TGA), decreased (pulmonary atresia), or equal (tetralogy of Fallot).</li>
<li>Pulmonary atresia requires immediate prostaglandin E1 therapy to maintain ductal patency and provide pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li>In conditions with increased pulmonary blood flow, the primary concern is heart failure due to volume overload, while in decreased pulmonary blood flow, the concern is severe hypoxemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pulmonary atresia is the only condition among the options that has decreased pulmonary blood flow, making it the correct answer to this question.</p>','<p>PDA for PA: Decreased Pulmonary blood flow in Pulmonary Atresia</p>','<p>In cyanotic congenital heart diseases, the amount of pulmonary blood flow determines the clinical presentation and management approach.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Total Anomaly pulmonary venous return:</strong> This condition typically results in increased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This condition includes pulmonary stenosis, allowing some pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the Great Arteries:</strong> This condition results in parallel circulation with mixing required for survival.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Diseases','["cyanotic","pulmonary blood flow","exceptions"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.669Z','2026-10-27T15:53:57.987Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6x004xe9oithbcobxr','mcq','The drug of choice for closure of a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in a preterm infant is','["Dexamethasone","Prostaglandin E","Ibuprofen","Prostaglandin E2"]','Ibuprofen','<p><strong>Why Ibuprofen is correct:</strong> Ibuprofen is the drug of choice for closing a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in preterm infants because it selectively inhibits cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, specifically COX-1 and COX-2, which reduces the production of prostaglandin E2 (PGE2). PGE2 is the primary mediator that maintains the ductus arteriosus in a patent state by promoting vasodilation and preventing its closure. By blocking prostaglandin synthesis, ibuprofen allows the ductus to constrict and close naturally. This mechanism is particularly effective in preterm infants whose ductus is highly dependent on prostaglandin maintenance.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dexamethasone:</strong> This corticosteroid is not used for PDA closure and is actually associated with increased risk of PDA persistence and other complications like neurodevelopmental impairment in preterm infants.</li>
<li><strong>Prostaglandin E:</strong> This option is incorrect because prostaglandin E (specifically PGE1 or alprostadil) is used to keep a ductus arteriosus patent in conditions like pulmonary atresia, not to close it.</li>
<li><strong>Prostaglandin E2:</strong> Similar to prostaglandin E, PGE2 would maintain the ductus in an open state rather than promote its closure, making it the opposite of what is needed for PDA treatment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PDA closure in preterm infants is primarily achieved through prostaglandin inhibition, with ibuprofen being preferred over indomethacin due to better renal and cerebral blood flow preservation.</li>
<li>The timing of treatment is critical; most PDAs in preterm infants are treated within the first week of life if they are hemodynamically significant.</li>
<li>Surgical ligation remains an option when medical therapy fails or is contraindicated.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Ibuprofen closes a PDA in preterm infants by blocking prostaglandin synthesis, which maintains ductal patency.</p>','<p>PDA: Prostaglandins Dilate Arteriosus (so block them with ibuprofen to close it)</p>','<p>Early closure of significant PDAs in preterm infants improves outcomes by reducing pulmonary overcirculation and improving renal perfusion.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Dexamethasone:</strong> Not used for PDA closure and associated with increased complications in preterm infants.</li>
<li><strong>Prostaglandin E:</strong> Used to maintain ductal patency, not close it.</li>
<li><strong>Prostaglandin E2:</strong> Would keep the ductus open rather than promote closure.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','PDA Management','["PDA","preterm","closure","ibuprofen"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.673Z','2026-10-27T15:54:15.200Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6x004ye9oiqjev7tp0','mcq','Gold standard in diagnosis of miteal valve prolapse','["ECG","Chest X ray","Echocardiography"]','Echocardiography','<p><strong>Why Echocardiography is correct:</strong> Echocardiography is the gold standard for diagnosing mitral valve prolapse (MVP) because it provides direct visualization of the mitral valve apparatus, allowing for assessment of leaflet morphology, motion, and coaptation. Specifically, two-dimensional echocardiography can demonstrate the characteristic systolic displacement (prolapse) of one or both mitral leaflets above the mitral annular plane into the left atrium. Additionally, Doppler echocardiography can detect associated mitral regurgitation, which is commonly present in MVP. This comprehensive evaluation makes echocardiography far superior to other imaging modalities for diagnosing MVP.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ECG:</strong> While MVP may be associated with certain ECG findings like T-wave inversions in inferior or lateral leads, these are nonspecific and insufficient for diagnosis. ECG cannot directly visualize the mitral valve.</li>
<li><strong>Chest X-ray:</strong> Chest X-ray may show cardiomegaly in severe cases with significant mitral regurgitation, but it cannot detect the prolapsing valve itself and is often normal in MVP.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>MVP is characterized by systolic displacement of mitral leaflets &gt;2 mm above the annular plane in parasternal long-axis view on echocardiography.</li>
<li>The diagnosis requires both visualization of prolapse and exclusion of other causes of mitral regurgitation.</li>
<li>MVP is most commonly diagnosed in young women and is often benign, though it can be associated with complications like mitral regurgitation, infective endocarditis, and arrhythmias.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Echocardiography is the gold standard for MVP diagnosis because it directly visualizes the prolapsing mitral valve leaflets, which ECG and chest X-ray cannot do.</p>','<p>MVP: Mitral Valve Prolapse - Echo ''E'' sees the ''P'' prolapse</p>','<p>Most cases of MVP are benign and require only reassurance, though patients should be monitored for development of significant mitral regurgitation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>ECG:</strong> Cannot visualize the mitral valve and findings are nonspecific for MVP.</li>
<li><strong>Chest X-ray:</strong> Cannot detect valve prolapse and is often normal in MVP.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Mitral Valve Prolapse','["mitral valve","prolapse","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.673Z','2026-10-27T15:54:15.201Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6y004ze9oi3ig7mmsu','short','When Cyanosis occur with PS?','[]','When severe and associated with VSD','<p><strong>Why When severe and associated with VSD is correct:</strong> Cyanosis occurs with pulmonary stenosis (PS) when the obstruction is severe enough to cause a significant pressure gradient between the right ventricle and pulmonary artery, leading to reduced pulmonary blood flow. This becomes more pronounced when PS is associated with a ventricular septal defect (VSD), as the VSD allows deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to shunt directly into the left ventricle and systemic circulation (right-to-left shunt). The combination of severe PS limiting pulmonary blood flow and a VSD providing an escape route for deoxygenated blood results in cyanosis, creating a condition known as ''Tetralogy of Fallot physiology'' even if the full tetrad is not present.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> If cyanosis were to occur with mild PS without a VSD, it would be physiologically implausible because mild PS does not significantly impair pulmonary blood flow, and without a shunt, all blood would still pass through the lungs for oxygenation. Cyanosis requires either right-to-left shunting or significant pulmonary disease preventing adequate oxygenation.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The severity of PS is determined by the pressure gradient across the valve; gradients &gt;50 mmHg are considered severe.</li>
<li>Isolated PS does not typically cause cyanosis because blood still flows through the lungs (though it may be delayed).</li>
<li>The combination of PS with a VSD can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome if the shunt reverses over time due to pulmonary hypertension.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cyanosis in PS only occurs when the obstruction is severe enough to cause right-to-left shunting, typically through an associated VSD.</p>','<p>PS + VSD = Cyanosis (right-to-left shunt)</p>','<p>Severe PS with a VSD creates a right-to-left shunt, leading to cyanosis and ''tetralogy-like physiology'' even without the full tetrad.</p>','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["cyanosis","PS","VSD"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.674Z','2026-10-27T15:54:15.202Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu700053e9oidlfiwiby','mcq','The long-term complication of PS is','["Right-ventricular hypertrophy","Left-ventricular hypertrophy","Pulmonary hypertension","Aortic regurgitation"]','Right-ventricular hypertrophy','<p><strong>Why Right-ventricular hypertrophy is correct:</strong> Pulmonary stenosis (PS) obstructs blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary artery, creating pressure overload on the right ventricle. Over time, this chronic pressure stimulus leads to compensatory hypertrophy of the right ventricular myocardium as it works harder to overcome the obstruction. This is a classic example of pressure-induced hypertrophy, similar to how systemic hypertension causes left ventricular hypertrophy. The right ventricular wall thickens in response to the increased afterload imposed by the stenotic pulmonary valve.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Left-ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> This occurs in conditions that increase left ventricular afterload or volume, such as systemic hypertension, aortic stenosis, or coarctation of the aorta, not pulmonary stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary hypertension:</strong> While severe PS can eventually lead to pulmonary hypertension due to chronic right ventricular pressure overload, this is not the primary long-term complication of PS itself.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation:</strong> This is a valvular disorder affecting the aortic valve, unrelated to the pathophysiology of pulmonary stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PS causes right ventricular pressure overload, leading to right ventricular hypertrophy.</li>
<li>The murmur of PS is a systolic ejection murmur best heard at the left upper sternal border.</li>
<li>Severe PS can lead to right heart failure if untreated.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pulmonary stenosis causes right ventricular hypertrophy due to pressure overload from obstruction to pulmonary artery blood flow.</p>','<p>PS = Right side hypertrophy (Pressure on Right)</p>','<p>In pulmonary stenosis, the severity can be estimated by the intensity of the systolic ejection murmur and the degree of right ventricular hypertrophy on echocardiogram.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Left-ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> Occurs with left heart pressure overload, not right heart conditions like PS.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary hypertension:</strong> A potential consequence of severe PS but not the primary complication.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation:</strong> Unrelated valvular disorder affecting the left side of the heart.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["complications","ps","cardiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.676Z','2026-10-27T15:54:32.069Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu710054e9oizs3pvgri','mcq','The long-term complication of AS is','["Right-ventricular hypertrophy","Left-ventricular hypertrophy","Pulmonary hypertension","Aortic regurgitation"]','Left-ventricular hypertrophy','<p><strong>Why Left-ventricular hypertrophy is correct:</strong> Aortic stenosis (AS) creates obstruction to left ventricular outflow, resulting in increased pressure overload on the left ventricle. This chronic pressure stimulus triggers compensatory hypertrophy of the left ventricular myocardium as it generates higher pressures to overcome the stenotic aortic valve. The left ventricular wall thickens concentrically to maintain cardiac output against the increased afterload, which is the hallmark response to pressure overload in the left heart.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Right-ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> This occurs in conditions that increase right ventricular afterload or volume, such as pulmonary stenosis, pulmonary hypertension, or tetralogy of Fallot, not aortic stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary hypertension:</strong> While severe AS can eventually lead to secondary pulmonary hypertension due to left heart failure, this is not the primary long-term complication of AS itself.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation:</strong> This is a separate valvular disorder that causes volume overload on the left ventricle, leading to eccentric hypertrophy, not concentric hypertrophy like in AS.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>AS causes left ventricular pressure overload, leading to concentric left ventricular hypertrophy.</li>
<li>The classic murmur of AS is a systolic ejection murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids.</li>
<li>Severe AS can lead to angina, syncope, or heart failure (the classic triad of symptoms).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aortic stenosis causes left ventricular hypertrophy due to pressure overload from obstruction to aortic outflow.</p>','<p>AS = Left side hypertrophy (Aortic Stenosis)</p>','<p>In aortic stenosis, the degree of left ventricular hypertrophy correlates with the severity of obstruction and is a key prognostic factor.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Right-ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> Occurs with right heart pressure overload, not left heart conditions like AS.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary hypertension:</strong> A potential consequence of severe AS but not the primary complication.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation:</strong> Causes volume overload and eccentric hypertrophy, not concentric hypertrophy like AS.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["complications","as","cardiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.677Z','2026-10-27T15:54:32.073Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu720055e9oiremjss0e','mcq','What murmur could be detected in aortic stenosis?','["Continuous murmur","Early diastolic murmur","Ejection systolic murmur","To and fro murmur"]','Ejection systolic murmur','<p><strong>Why Ejection systolic murmur is correct:</strong> Aortic stenosis produces a characteristic ejection systolic murmur due to turbulent blood flow across the narrowed aortic valve during ventricular systole. This murmur is typically crescendo-decrescendo in shape, beginning after the first heart sound (S1) and ending before the second heart sound (S2). The turbulence occurs because the stenotic valve creates a pressure gradient between the left ventricle and the aorta, forcing blood through a restricted opening at high velocity, which generates the audible murmur.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Continuous murmur:</strong> This is typically heard in conditions like patent ductus arteriosus or arteriovenous fistulas, where there is continuous shunting of blood between high and low pressure systems throughout the cardiac cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Early diastolic murmur:</strong> This is characteristic of aortic regurgitation, where blood flows back from the aorta into the left ventricle during diastole, creating a murmur that begins immediately after S2.</li>
<li><strong>To and fro murmur:</strong> This describes a murmur with both systolic and diastolic components, typically seen in combined aortic stenosis and regurgitation or other mixed valvular diseases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The murmur of aortic stenosis is a systolic ejection murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotid arteries.</li>
<li>Aortic stenosis murmur is often described as "harsh" and "diamond-shaped" on auscultation.</li>
<li>The intensity of the murmur does not always correlate with severity; a soft murmur with a delayed carotid upstroke may indicate severe stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aortic stenosis produces a crescendo-decrescendo ejection systolic murmur due to turbulent flow across the narrowed valve during ventricular contraction.</p>','<p>AS murmur = Systolic Ejection (Aortic Stenosis)</p>','<p>The timing and radiation of the aortic stenosis murmur (radiating to carotids) helps distinguish it from other systolic murmurs like pulmonary stenosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Continuous murmur:</strong> Heard in conditions with continuous shunting, like patent ductus arteriosus.</li>
<li><strong>Early diastolic murmur:</strong> Characteristic of aortic regurgitation, not stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>To and fro murmur:</strong> Seen in combined stenosis and regurgitation, not isolated aortic stenosis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["murmur","as","cardiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.678Z','2026-10-27T15:54:32.074Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu83006te9oi3b58v0ka','mcq','The arthritis of acute rheumatic fever typically involves:','["Small joints of the hands and feet","Large joints such as knees, ankles, wrists and elbows","Temporomandibular joints only","Vertebral joints"]','Large joints such as knees, ankles, wrists and elbows','<p><strong>Why Large joints such as knees, ankles, wrists and elbows is correct:</strong> The arthritis of acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is characterized by migratory polyarthritis that preferentially involves large joints, particularly the knees, ankles, wrists, and elbows. This pattern results from the cross-reactivity of antibodies against group A streptococcal M proteins with antigens in synovial tissues, causing an inflammatory response in these weight-bearing and frequently mobile joints. The large joint involvement is typically asymmetric and responds dramatically to salicylates or NSAIDs, distinguishing it from other forms of arthritis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Small joints of the hands and feet:</strong> Small joint involvement is characteristic of rheumatoid arthritis, not acute rheumatic fever, though small joints may occasionally be affected in ARF.</li>
<li><strong>Temporomandibular joints only:</strong> Isolated temporomandibular joint involvement is not typical of ARF and would be an unusual presentation of this condition.</li>
<li><strong>Vertebral joints:</strong> Spinal involvement is not a feature of ARF; arthritis in ARF primarily affects peripheral joints, not the axial skeleton.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The migratory nature of the arthritis in ARF means that inflammation shifts from one joint to another over hours to days.</li>
<li>The arthritis of ARF is typically exquisitely responsive to anti-inflammatory therapy, with improvement often occurring within 24-48 hours of starting treatment.</li>
<li>The presence of carditis (with or without heart failure) is the most serious manifestation of ARF and occurs in about 50% of first attacks and 75% of recurrent attacks.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Acute rheumatic fever arthritis preferentially affects large, weight-bearing joints in a migratory pattern that responds dramatically to anti-inflammatory therapy.</p>','<p>KANE: Knees, Ankles, Wrists, Elbows - the large joints involved in rheumatic fever arthritis.</p>','<p>The arthritis of acute rheumatic fever is so responsive to salicylates that dramatic improvement within 24-48 hours helps confirm the diagnosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Small joints of the hands and feet:</strong> Characteristic of rheumatoid arthritis, not acute rheumatic fever.</li>
<li><strong>Temporomandibular joints only:</strong> Isolated TMJ involvement is not typical of ARF.</li>
<li><strong>Vertebral joints:</strong> Spinal involvement is not a feature of ARF, which primarily affects peripheral joints.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Rheumatology','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic","fever","arthritis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.715Z','2026-10-27T16:13:08.138Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu750059e9oimrv6e838','mcq','A 2-year-old boy with small ASD will go for circumcision; what about antibiotic prophylaxis for him?','["Give 500 mg azithromycin orally.","Administer 1000 mg ceftriaxone IV.","No need for antibiotic prophylaxis.","Provide 1000 mg amoxicillin orally."]','No need for antibiotic prophylaxis.','<p><strong>Why No need for antibiotic prophylaxis is correct:</strong> Antibiotic prophylaxis for endocarditis is not recommended for patients with isolated secundum atrial septal defects (ASD) undergoing procedures such as circumcision. The American Heart Association guidelines specifically exclude isolated ASD from conditions requiring endocarditis prophylaxis, as there is no turbulent blood flow across the defect that would increase the risk of bacterial endocarditis. The risk of bacteremia from a circumcision is low, and the potential harm of unnecessary antibiotics outweighs any theoretical benefit in this scenario.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give 500 mg azithromycin orally:</strong> Azithromycin is not indicated for endocarditis prophylaxis in this context, as it''s not recommended by current guidelines for any cardiac condition requiring prophylaxis.</li>
<li><strong>Administer 1000 mg ceftriaxone IV:</strong> Ceftriaxone is not the appropriate antibiotic for endocarditis prophylaxis in this scenario, and prophylaxis is not indicated at all for isolated ASD.</li>
<li><strong>Provide 1000 mg amoxicillin orally:</strong> While amoxicillin is a recommended antibiotic for endocarditis prophylaxis for certain high-risk cardiac conditions, it is not indicated for isolated ASD, and prophylaxis is not recommended for this patient.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Isolated ASD does not require endocarditis prophylaxis as there is no turbulent blood flow across the defect.</li>
<li>Current AHA guidelines specifically exclude isolated ASD from conditions requiring prophylaxis.</li>
<li>Antibiotic prophylaxis is only recommended for cardiac conditions with turbulent blood flow or prosthetic material.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Isolated ASD does not require endocarditis prophylaxis for any procedure, including circumcision.</p>','','<p>Endocarditis prophylaxis is only indicated for specific high-risk cardiac conditions with turbulent blood flow or prosthetic material, not for isolated ASD.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Give 500 mg azithromycin orally:</strong> Azithromycin is not recommended for endocarditis prophylaxis in any cardiac condition.</li>
<li><strong>Administer 1000 mg ceftriaxone IV:</strong> Ceftriaxone is not the appropriate antibiotic for endocarditis prophylaxis and is not indicated for isolated ASD.</li>
<li><strong>Provide 1000 mg amoxicillin orally:</strong> Amoxicillin is not indicated for endocarditis prophylaxis in isolated ASD.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Atrial Septal Defect','["asd","prophylaxis","antibiotics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.681Z','2026-10-27T15:54:48.641Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu75005ae9oiba08k84q','mcq','What is the main sequelae of prolonged pulmonary stenosis?','["Development of tricuspid regurgitation.","Right ventricular hypertrophy.","Right atrial dilatation.","Increased ventricular arrhythmia."]','Right ventricular hypertrophy.','<p><strong>Why Right ventricular hypertrophy is correct:</strong> Prolonged pulmonary stenosis creates increased pressure load on the right ventricle as it works harder to pump blood through the narrowed pulmonary valve. This chronic pressure overload leads to adaptive hypertrophy of the right ventricular myocardium, which is the primary structural change in this condition. The hypertrophy is a compensatory mechanism to maintain cardiac output against the increased afterload, and it occurs before any significant chamber dilation or functional deterioration develops.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Development of tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> While severe pulmonary stenosis can eventually lead to tricuspid regurgitation due to right ventricular dilation, this is a secondary complication rather than the main sequelae of prolonged pulmonary stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Right atrial dilatation:</strong> Right atrial dilation typically occurs as a consequence of right ventricular failure, which is a later complication of severe, untreated pulmonary stenosis, not the primary response to pressure overload.</li>
<li><strong>Increased ventricular arrhythmia:</strong> Ventricular arrhythmias are more commonly associated with right ventricular dilation and fibrosis in advanced disease, not directly with the hypertrophic response to pulmonary stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Pulmonary stenosis causes right ventricular pressure overload, leading to concentric hypertrophy.</li>
<li>The right ventricle adapts to increased afterload through hypertrophy before dilation occurs.</li>
<li>Severe, untreated pulmonary stenosis can eventually lead to right ventricular failure and dilation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The primary sequelae of prolonged pulmonary stenosis is right ventricular hypertrophy due to chronic pressure overload.</p>','<p>PS → RVH (Pulmonary Stenosis leads to Right Ventricular Hypertrophy)</p>','<p>In pulmonary stenosis, the right ventricle initially responds to pressure overload with hypertrophy before developing dilation and failure in severe, untreated cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Development of tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> This is a secondary complication, not the primary sequelae of pulmonary stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Right atrial dilatation:</strong> This occurs later due to right ventricular failure, not as the primary response to pressure overload.</li>
<li><strong>Increased ventricular arrhythmia:</strong> These are associated with advanced disease with dilation and fibrosis, not directly with hypertrophy.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["ps","complications","sequelae"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.681Z','2026-10-27T15:54:48.643Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu76005be9oib0yxlitx','mcq','For aortic coarctation with PDA in neonates, what is the main drug treatment?','["Propranolol.","Prostaglandin E1.","Diuretics.","ACE inhibitors."]','Prostaglandin E1.','<p><strong>Why Prostaglandin E1 is correct:</strong> Prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) is the main drug treatment for aortic coarctation with patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in neonates because it maintains ductal patency, providing an alternative pathway for blood flow to bypass the coarctation. In utero, the ductus arteriosus is kept open by prostaglandins, but shortly after birth, it normally closes. In neonates with aortic coarctation, maintaining ductal patency with PGE1 allows blood to reach the systemic circulation distal to the coarctation, stabilizing the infant while awaiting definitive surgical or catheter-based intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Propranolol:</strong> Beta-blockers like propranolol are not used for acute management of aortic coarctation with PDA; they may worsen the condition by decreasing systemic vascular resistance and potentially increasing the pressure gradient across the coarctation.</li>
<li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Diuretics are not indicated for acute management of this condition; they would reduce blood volume and potentially worsen perfusion distal to the coarctation.</li>
<li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> ACE inhibitors are not used for acute management of aortic coarctation with PDA; they are typically used for chronic management of heart failure or hypertension in older patients with coarctation after repair.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PGE1 maintains ductal patency, providing an alternative pathway for blood flow in aortic coarctation.</li>
<li>Aortic coarctation with PDA in neonates is a critical condition requiring urgent intervention to maintain systemic perfusion.</li>
<li>PGE1 is a temporizing measure while preparing for surgical or catheter-based intervention.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Prostaglandin E1 is the main drug treatment for aortic coarctation with PDA in neonates as it maintains ductal patency to ensure systemic perfusion.</p>','<p>PDA → PGE1 (Patent Ductus Arteriosus requires Prostaglandin E1 to maintain patency)</p>','<p>PGE1 is a life-saving temporizing measure for ductal-dependent lesions like aortic coarctation, maintaining circulation until definitive intervention can be performed.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Propranolol:</strong> Beta-blockers can worsen the condition by decreasing systemic vascular resistance and increasing the pressure gradient across the coarctation.</li>
<li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Diuretics reduce blood volume and can worsen perfusion distal to the coarctation.</li>
<li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> These are not used for acute management of this condition.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Aortic Coarctation','["coarctation","pda","neonate","prostaglandin"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.682Z','2026-10-27T15:54:48.644Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krbf001to7lrfjpxp54y','mcq','Which of the following is the most common cause of constipation in children?','["Hirschsprung disease","Functional constipation","Hypothyroidism","Spinal cord abnormalities"]','Functional constipation','<p><strong>Why Functional constipation is correct:</strong> Functional constipation is the most common cause of constipation in children, accounting for over 95% of cases, and represents a behavioral and physiological disorder rather than an anatomical or metabolic abnormality. It typically develops following a painful or frightening defecation experience, leading to voluntary withholding of stool, which results in rectal distension, decreased rectal sensation, and eventual hardening of stool that causes further pain and perpetuates the cycle. Unlike organic causes like Hirschsprung disease, functional constipation lacks structural abnormalities and responds to behavioral interventions, dietary modifications, and laxatives rather than requiring surgical intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hirschsprung disease:</strong> While Hirschsprung disease causes constipation, it is relatively rare (1 in 5,000 live births) and represents less than 5% of childhood constipation cases, typically presenting in infancy with failure to pass meconium.</li>
<li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Hypothyroidism can cause constipation due to decreased motility, but it is uncommon in children and is usually accompanied by other systemic features like growth failure, cold intolerance, and bradycardia.</li>
<li><strong>Spinal cord abnormalities:</strong> Conditions like spina bifida can cause constipation due to neurogenic bowel dysfunction, but these are rare causes compared to functional constipation and typically present with additional neurological deficits.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Functional constipation is diagnosed clinically based on Rome IV criteria and requires exclusion of organic causes.</li>
<li>The hallmark of functional constipation is voluntary stool withholding leading to fecal impaction and overflow incontinence.</li>
<li>Treatment involves a combination of behavioral therapy, dietary fiber, adequate fluid intake, and scheduled laxative use.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Functional constipation is overwhelmingly the most common cause of childhood constipation, representing a behavioral cycle of stool withholding rather than an organic disease process.</p>','<p>Functional constipation is ''FIFO'' - First In, Flow Out (stool enters but can''t exit properly due to withholding behavior)</p>','<p>Children with functional constipation often present with ''encopresis'' (involuntary stool leakage) due to overflow incontinence from fecal impaction, not intentional soiling.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hirschsprung disease:</strong> Rare cause of constipation (&lt;5% of cases) typically presenting in infancy.</li>
<li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Uncommon cause usually accompanied by other systemic features like growth failure.</li>
<li><strong>Spinal cord abnormalities:</strong> Rare cause typically associated with additional neurological deficits.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Gastrointestinal','Constipation','["constipation","functional","common cause","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.131Z','2026-10-27T16:04:54.188Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krci0030o7lrz66stgss','mcq','42- What does congenital deficiency or absence of red cell precursors in otherwise normally cellular bone marrow results in?','["sideroblastic anemia","Blackfan-Diamond","Fanconi anemia","Aplastic anemia"]','Aplastic anemia','<p><strong>Why Aplastic anemia is correct:</strong> Aplastic anemia is characterized by a deficiency or absence of hematopoietic precursors in an otherwise normally cellular bone marrow, resulting in pancytopenia. This condition occurs due to immune-mediated destruction of hematopoietic stem cells or intrinsic defects in these cells, leading to a failure in the production of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. The bone marrow appears hypocellular with fatty replacement, but the remaining stromal architecture is preserved, distinguishing it from conditions like myelodysplastic syndromes where dysplastic precursors are present. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Sideroblastic anemia:</strong> This condition involves defective heme synthesis leading to ringed sideroblasts in the bone marrow, but the marrow is typically hypercellular with increased erythroid precursors, not deficient in precursors. - <strong>Blackfan-Diamond:</strong> This is a pure red cell aplasia primarily affecting erythroid precursors, with normal white blood cell and platelet production, unlike the pancytopenia seen in aplastic anemia. - <strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> This is an inherited disorder characterized by congenital abnormalities, bone marrow failure, and chromosomal fragility, but it''s not defined by the absence of precursors in an otherwise normal marrow; instead, it shows progressive hypocellularity with dysplastic features. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Aplastic anemia presents with pancytopenia in a hypocellular bone marrow with fatty replacement. - It can be acquired (immune-mediated, drug-induced, viral) or inherited (Fanconi anemia, dyskeratosis congenita). - The gold standard for diagnosis is bone marrow biopsy showing &lt;30% cellularity with normal architecture but absence of precursors. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aplastic anemia is defined by pancytopenia due to bone marrow hypocellularity with absent hematopoietic precursors in an otherwise normal marrow architecture.</p>','<p>Aplastic anemia = ''A''bsent precursors in ''P''ancytopenia with ''L''ow cellularity in ''A'' normal marrow</p>','<p>Aplastic anemia requires urgent treatment as it can be life-threatening; first-line therapy is immunosuppression with antithymocyte globulin and cyclosporine.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Sideroblastic anemia:</strong> Characterized by ringed sideroblasts and hypercellular marrow, not precursor deficiency. - <strong>Blackfan-Diamond:</strong> Pure red cell aplasia without affecting other cell lines. - <strong>Fanconi anemia:</strong> Inherited disorder with chromosomal fragility and progressive marrow failure, not acute precursor absence.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Anemia','["anemia","congenital","bone marrow"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.170Z','2026-10-27T16:05:25.750Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut7krcj0032o7lreufkwxyd','mcq','44- A 4 years child acquired infection with Epstein Barr virus then developed pancytopenia, what is correct about his condition?','["Cytogenetic studies will reveal chromosome breaks of blood lymphocytes.","Bone marrow transplantation is of no benefit in this case.","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy will be hyper cellular.","He can be treated by immunosuppressive therapy."]','Cytogenetic studies will reveal chromosome breaks of blood lymphocytes.','<p><strong>Why Cytogenetic studies will reveal chromosome breaks of blood lymphocytes is correct:</strong> Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection can trigger the development of aplastic anemia in some cases, and when it does, the condition is often associated with chromosomal fragility syndromes. The most characteristic finding in EBV-associated aplastic anemia is the presence of increased chromosomal breaks in blood lymphocytes when cultured without a DNA cross-linking agent like diepoxybutane. This finding is pathognomonic for Fanconi anemia, which can be triggered by viral infections like EBV and presents with pancytopenia. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Bone marrow transplantation is of no benefit in this case:</strong> Bone marrow transplantation is actually the curative treatment for severe aplastic anemia, including cases triggered by EBV, making this statement incorrect. - <strong>Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy will be hyper cellular:</strong> In aplastic anemia, the bone marrow is hypocellular with fatty replacement, not hypercellular; hypercellularity would suggest a different diagnosis like leukemia or myelodysplastic syndrome. - <strong>He can be treated by immunosuppressive therapy:</strong> While immunosuppressive therapy (with antithymocyte globulin and cyclosporine) is a standard treatment for aplastic anemia, it''s not specific to EBV-associated cases and doesn''t address the underlying chromosomal fragility. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - EBV infection can trigger aplastic anemia, particularly in patients with underlying chromosomal fragility. - The diagnostic test for Fanconi anemia (which can be triggered by EBV) is the chromosomal breakage test on lymphocytes. - Aplastic anemia presents with pancytopenia and hypocellular bone marrow, requiring urgent intervention. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> EBV-associated pancytopenia with chromosomal breaks in lymphocytes indicates Fanconi anemia, not just typical aplastic anemia.</p>','<p>EBV + pancytopenia = ''E''xamine ''B''reaks in ''V''iral-triggered anemia</p>','<p>When a patient with pancytopenia has a history of EBV infection, always consider chromosomal fragility syndromes like Fanconi anemia, which requires specific testing with diepoxybutane.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Bone marrow transplantation is of no benefit:</strong> Actually, it''s the curative treatment for severe aplastic anemia. - <strong>Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy will be hyper cellular:</strong> Aplastic anemia shows hypocellular marrow, not hypercellular. - <strong>He can be treated by immunosuppressive therapy:</strong> While used, it''s not specific to EBV-associated cases and doesn''t address chromosomal fragility.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Pancytopenia','["pancytopenia","epstein barr","infection"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T02:35:25.171Z','2026-10-27T16:05:25.756Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugq40029tzlromqypng3','mcq','Platelets are formed in the bone marrow and then released into circulation. What are the cells responsible for platelet production?','["Megakaryocytes.","Thrombocytes.","Myelocytes.","Granulocytes."]','Megakaryocytes.','<p><strong>Why Megakaryocytes is correct:</strong> Megakaryocytes are large bone marrow cells responsible for platelet production through a process called thrombopoiesis. These cells undergo endomitosis (repeated DNA replication without cell division) to become polyploid, then extend cytoplasmic processes called proplatelets into the sinusoids. The proplatelets fragment at their ends, releasing platelets into the bloodstream. This process is regulated by thrombopoietin (TPO), which binds to the c-Mpl receptor on megakaryocytes and their precursors. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Thrombocytes:</strong> Thrombocytes are simply another name for platelets themselves, not the cells that produce them. - <strong>Myelocytes:</strong> Myelocytes are precursors in the granulocyte lineage (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils), not involved in platelet production. - <strong>Granulocytes:</strong> Granulocytes are mature white blood cells (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) derived from myeloid precursors, but they do not produce platelets. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Megakaryocytes are the largest cells in the bone marrow, with diameters up to 100 μm. - Platelet production occurs primarily in the bone marrow, but some occurs in the spleen. - Thrombopoietin (TPO) is the primary regulator of megakaryocyte development and platelet production. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Megakaryocytes are the specialized bone marrow cells that produce platelets through thrombopoiesis.</p>','<p>Megakaryocytes Make Many Microscopic Mini-platelets (M4)</p>','<p>In conditions like aplastic anemia or myelodysplastic syndromes, megakaryocyte numbers are reduced, leading to thrombocytopenia.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Thrombocytes:</strong> Incorrect because thrombocytes are the platelets themselves, not their precursors. - <strong>Myelocytes:</strong> Incorrect because myelocytes are precursors for granulocytes, not platelets. - <strong>Granulocytes:</strong> Incorrect because granulocytes are mature white blood cells that don''t produce platelets.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Platelets','["platelets","production","megakaryocytes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.196Z','2026-10-27T16:07:06.852Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqf002jtzlrm5wbgm99','mcq','What is the primary hemostatic process?','["Blood clot formation","Platelet plug formation","Vasoconstriction and Blood clot formation","Vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation"]','Vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation','<p><strong>Why Vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation is correct:</strong> The primary hemostatic process begins with vasoconstriction, which is an immediate response to vascular injury mediated by endothelin release and local neural reflexes that reduce blood flow to the damaged area. This is followed by platelet plug formation, where platelets adhere to exposed subendothelial collagen via von Willebrand factor, activate, and aggregate to form a temporary plug at the site of injury. These two processes work together to provide the initial hemostasis before the secondary process of coagulation stabilizes the clot with fibrin.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Blood clot formation:</strong> This represents the secondary hemostatic process (coagulation) that stabilizes the initial platelet plug, not the primary response. - <strong>Platelet plug formation alone:</strong> While important, platelet plug formation alone is insufficient as the primary hemostatic process without the preceding vasoconstriction. - <strong>Vasoconstriction and Blood clot formation:</strong> This incorrectly combines the primary hemostatic process (vasoconstriction) with the secondary process (blood clot formation), omitting the critical intermediate step of platelet plug formation.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Primary hemostasis involves vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation. - Secondary hemostasis involves the coagulation cascade forming fibrin to stabilize the clot. - The distinction between primary and secondary hemostasis is crucial for understanding bleeding disorders and their treatments.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Primary hemostasis consists of vasoconstriction followed by platelet plug formation, providing the initial response to vascular injury before coagulation stabilizes the clot.</p>','<p>Primary = Vasoconstriction + Platelets (VP); Secondary = Coagulation cascade</p>','<p>Defects in primary hemostasis (platelet disorders) typically cause mucocutaneous bleeding, while defects in secondary hemostasis (coagulation factors) cause deep tissue and joint bleeding.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Blood clot formation:</strong> Represents secondary hemostasis (coagulation), not the primary response. - <strong>Platelet plug formation alone:</strong> Incomplete without the preceding vasoconstriction step. - <strong>Vasoconstriction and Blood clot formation:</strong> Incorrectly combines primary and secondary hemostatic processes.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Hemostasis','["hemostasis","platelets","vasoconstriction"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.207Z','2026-10-27T16:07:15.460Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqi002ntzlrpin6q7lu','mcq','Anterior Mediastinal mass is mostly associated with','["Brain tumors","Lymphoma"]','Lymphoma','<p><strong>Why Lymphoma is correct:</strong> Lymphoma is the most common cause of anterior mediastinal masses due to the presence of lymph nodes in this region. The anterior mediastinum contains the thymus, lymph nodes, and great vessels, making it a common site for lymphomatous involvement, particularly Hodgkin lymphoma which often presents with a mediastinal mass. The thymus can also be involved in lymphoma or may be the site of thymoma, but lymphoma is more frequently associated with anterior mediastinal masses compared to other tumors. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Brain tumors:</strong> Brain tumors are intracranial lesions and do not typically present as anterior mediastinal masses. - <strong>Other tumors:</strong> While other tumors like thymoma, germ cell tumors, or thyroid tissue can occur in the anterior mediastinum, lymphoma remains the most common association. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Anterior mediastinal masses have a differential diagnosis that includes lymphoma, thymoma, germ cell tumors, and thyroid tissue. - Hodgkin lymphoma classically presents with mediastinal widening on chest X-ray. - The location of a mediastinal mass can help narrow the differential: anterior (lymphoma, thymoma), middle (lymphoma, cysts), posterior (neurogenic tumors). <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Anterior mediastinal masses are most commonly associated with lymphoma, particularly Hodgkin lymphoma.</p>','<p>ALPHA: Anterior Mediastinal Masses - Lymphoma, Parathyroid, Thymoma, Hodgkin, Germ cell</p>','<p>An anterior mediastinal mass in a young adult should raise suspicion for lymphoma, thymoma, or germ cell tumor until proven otherwise.</p>','','Basic','Hematology','Lymphoma','["mediastinal","mass","lymphoma"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.210Z','2026-10-27T16:07:32.743Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqk002qtzlr94reimep','mcq','The BEST screening radiographic study for a patient with suspicious cervical lymphadenopathy, fever and weight loss is','["neck x-ray","chest MRI","abdominal CT scan","chest x-ray"]','chest x-ray','<p><strong>Why chest x-ray is correct:</strong> The chest x-ray is the best initial screening radiographic study for patients with suspicious cervical lymphadenopathy, fever, and weight loss because it can reveal mediastinal lymphadenopathy, which is commonly associated with lymphoma, tuberculosis, or sarcoidosis. This test is non-invasive, widely available, and has a high yield for detecting thoracic involvement in systemic diseases that present with cervical lymphadenopathy. The chest x-ray can also identify other thoracic pathology such as lung parenchymal involvement, pleural effusions, or hilar adenopathy that might be causing the symptoms. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Neck x-ray:</strong> A neck x-ray is not appropriate for evaluating systemic lymphadenopathy as it primarily assesses bony structures of the cervical spine and would miss mediastinal involvement. - <strong>chest MRI:</strong> While chest MRI is excellent for detailed evaluation of mediastinal structures, it is not the best initial screening test due to higher cost, limited availability, and the need for more specialized interpretation. - <strong>abdominal CT scan:</strong> An abdominal CT scan would evaluate abdominal lymph nodes but would miss thoracic involvement, which is crucial in the initial evaluation of systemic lymphadenopathy. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The chest x-ray is the most appropriate first imaging test for evaluating systemic lymphadenopathy to assess for mediastinal involvement. - Mediastinal lymphadenopathy on chest x-ray is highly suggestive of lymphoma, tuberculosis, or sarcoidosis in patients with cervical lymphadenopathy. - Fever, weight loss, and lymphadenopathy (B symptoms) are classic for lymphoma and tuberculosis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For patients with cervical lymphadenopathy, fever, and weight loss, the chest x-ray is the best initial screening radiographic study to evaluate for mediastinal involvement.</p>','<p>Chest First: Cervical lymphadenopathy evaluation starts with Chest x-ray</p>','<p>In patients with unexplained cervical lymphadenopathy and systemic symptoms, always obtain a chest x-ray to evaluate for mediastinal involvement before proceeding to more advanced imaging.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Neck x-ray:</strong> A neck x-ray is not appropriate for evaluating systemic lymphadenopathy as it primarily assesses bony structures of the cervical spine and would miss mediastinal involvement.</li>
<li><strong>chest MRI:</strong> While chest MRI is excellent for detailed evaluation of mediastinal structures, it is not the best initial screening test due to higher cost, limited availability, and the need for more specialized interpretation.</li>
<li><strong>abdominal CT scan:</strong> An abdominal CT scan would evaluate abdominal lymph nodes but would miss thoracic involvement, which is crucial in the initial evaluation of systemic lymphadenopathy.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Hodgkin Lymphoma','["screening","lymphadenopathy","chest x-ray"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.212Z','2026-10-27T16:07:32.746Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqo002utzlr6wk8lefr','mcq','Which of the following has a major role in Burkitt lymphoma?','["Hepatitis A virus","Herpes virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Adenovirus"]','Epstein-Barr virus','<p><strong>Why Epstein-Barr virus is correct:</strong> Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) plays a major role in the pathogenesis of Burkitt lymphoma through multiple mechanisms. The virus establishes latency in B-cells, expressing latent membrane protein 1 (LMP1) that constitutively activates NF-κB signaling, promoting cell survival and proliferation. EBV also encodes Epstein-Barr nuclear antigens (EBNAs) that dysregulate cell cycle checkpoints and inhibit apoptosis. Critically, EBV infection precedes the characteristic chromosomal translocation t(8;14) in endemic Burkitt lymphoma, where the MYC oncogene is placed under the control of the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer, driving uncontrolled cell proliferation. The viral proteins create a permissive environment for this oncogenic translocation to occur and be maintained.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hepatitis A virus:</strong> This is an enterovirus that causes acute hepatitis but has no known oncogenic properties or association with lymphoma development.</li>
<li><strong>Herpes virus:</strong> While herpesviruses include oncogenic members (like HHV-8 for Kaposi sarcoma), the specific herpes virus family is too broad and not specifically associated with Burkitt lymphoma.</li>
<li><strong>Adenovirus:</strong> These viruses primarily cause respiratory, gastrointestinal, and conjunctival infections and are not associated with any human malignancies, including lymphomas.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>EBV is a DNA herpesvirus that infects over 90% of adults worldwide and establishes lifelong latency in B-lymphocytes.</li>
<li>The three forms of Burkitt lymphoma (endemic, sporadic, and immunodeficiency-related) have varying degrees of EBV association (100%, 15-25%, and 30-40%, respectively).</li>
<li>EBV-associated lymphomas often show a specific latency pattern (Latency I) with limited viral gene expression, helping the virus evade immune detection.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Epstein-Barr virus is the major viral contributor to Burkitt lymphomagenesis through its effects on B-cell signaling and creating a permissive environment for MYC dysregulation.</p>','<p>EBV-Burkitt: Epstein-Barr Virus is the key to Burkitt lymphoma (EBV-B)</p>','<p>EBV-associated lymphomas often arise in the context of immunosuppression, making them an important consideration in transplant recipients and HIV-positive patients.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hepatitis A virus:</strong> Causes acute hepatitis, not lymphoma.</li>
<li><strong>Herpes virus:</strong> Too broad; HHV-8 causes Kaposi sarcoma, not Burkitt lymphoma.</li>
<li><strong>Adenovirus:</strong> Causes respiratory/GI infections, not malignancies.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Hematology','Burkitt Lymphoma','["Burkitt","EBV","virus"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.216Z','2026-10-27T16:07:50.133Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqr002vtzlrmucgfx31','mcq','A 16-year-old boy presents with painless cervical lymph node enlargement, fever, night sweats, and weight loss. Which investigation is considered the most reliable method to confirm the diagnosis?','["Excisional lymph node biopsy"]','Excisional lymph node biopsy','<p><strong>Why Excisional lymph node biopsy is correct:</strong> Excisional lymph node biopsy is the most reliable method to confirm the diagnosis of lymphoma in a patient with concerning symptoms like painless cervical lymphadenopathy, fever, night sweats, and weight loss. This procedure allows for complete architectural evaluation of the lymph node architecture, including the preservation of critical architectural relationships between follicles, sinuses, and the capsule. It provides sufficient tissue for comprehensive pathological examination, including histology, immunohistochemistry, and molecular studies, which are essential for distinguishing between Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma and determining the specific subtype. Fine needle aspiration (FNA) is inadequate for lymphoma diagnosis as it cannot preserve architecture, and core needle biopsies may not provide enough tissue for all necessary studies.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While not explicitly listed among the options, any alternative approach such as fine needle aspiration (FNA) or core needle biopsy would be insufficient for definitive lymphoma diagnosis. FNA only provides small samples of cells without architectural context, making it impossible to evaluate the critical architectural features that define lymphoma subtypes. Core needle biopsies provide more tissue than FNA but still may not preserve the complete lymph node architecture needed for accurate classification, potentially leading to misdiagnosis or incomplete characterization of the lymphoma.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) in a teenager with lymphadenopathy should raise suspicion for Hodgkin lymphoma, particularly nodular sclerosis subtype.</li>
<li>Excisional biopsy preserves lymph node architecture, which is crucial for distinguishing Hodgkin lymphoma (with characteristic Reed-Sternberg cells) from non-Hodgkin lymphomas.</li>
<li>If excisional biopsy is not feasible, core needle biopsy with adequate tissue sampling is the preferred alternative over fine needle aspiration for lymphoma diagnosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> When lymphoma is suspected based on clinical presentation, excisional lymph node biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosis as it preserves the architectural features necessary for accurate classification.</p>','<p>EX-Bx for Lymphoma: EXcisional Biopsy is essential for Lymphoma diagnosis</p>','<p>Painless lymphadenopathy in a young adult with B symptoms should always prompt consideration of Hodgkin lymphoma, which has excellent cure rates with appropriate therapy.</p>','','Moderate','Hematology','Hodgkin Lymphoma','["Hodgkin","biopsy","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.219Z','2026-10-27T16:07:50.133Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqs002wtzlrhvycc82m','mcq','A child with ALL develops vomiting, arrhythmia, and renal impairment shortly after starting chemotherapy. Laboratory findings show hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, and hypocalcemia. What is the most likely complication?','["Tumor lysis syndrome"]','Tumor lysis syndrome','<p><strong>Why Tumor lysis syndrome is correct:</strong> Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) is the most likely complication in this child with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) who developed vomiting, arrhythmia, and renal impairment shortly after starting chemotherapy. TLS occurs when rapid tumor cell lysis releases intracellular contents into the bloodstream, including potassium, phosphate, and uric acid, overwhelming the body''s excretory mechanisms. The hyperuricemia results from increased xanthine oxidase conversion of nucleic acid breakdown products, hyperkalemia from intracellular potassium release, hyperphosphatemia from intracellular phosphate release, and hypocalcemia due to precipitation of calcium phosphate in tissues. This metabolic derangement can lead to acute renal failure, cardiac arrhythmias, and neurological symptoms.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Tumor lysis syndrome:</strong> This is the correct answer, not a distractor. - <strong>Differentiation syndrome:</strong> This typically occurs with all-trans retinoic acid treatment in acute promyelocytic leukemia, presenting with respiratory distress, fever, and pulmonary infiltrates, not the metabolic abnormalities seen here. - <strong>Bleeding diathesis:</strong> This would present with bleeding manifestations rather than the specific electrolyte abnormalities and renal impairment seen in TLS. - <strong>Infection:</strong> While infection is a common complication in immunocompromised patients, it wouldn''t explain the specific electrolyte abnormalities and hyperuricemia seen in this scenario.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - TLS is an oncologic emergency that can occur spontaneously or more commonly after initiation of cytotoxic therapy, particularly in tumors with high proliferative rates like ALL and Burkitt lymphoma. - The laboratory abnormalities of TLS include hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, and hypocalcemia due to massive tumor cell breakdown. - Prevention with aggressive hydration, allopurinol, and rasburicase is crucial, especially in high-risk patients.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Tumor lysis syndrome should be suspected in any cancer patient with electrolyte abnormalities (hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hyperuricemia, hypocalcemia) and renal impairment shortly after starting chemotherapy.</p>','<p>TLS: Tall (hyperuricemia), Lean (hyperkalemia), Short (hypocalcemia) - describing the characteristic electrolyte abnormalities.</p>','<p>Tumor lysis syndrome is a medical emergency that requires prophylaxis with aggressive hydration, allopurinol, or rasburicase before initiating chemotherapy in high-risk patients.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Differentiation syndrome:</strong> Presents with respiratory distress and fever, not the metabolic abnormalities seen here. - <strong>Bleeding diathesis:</strong> Would present with bleeding manifestations, not specific electrolyte abnormalities. - <strong>Infection:</strong> Common in immunocompromised patients but doesn''t explain the specific metabolic abnormalities.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Hematology','Tumor Lysis Syndrome','["ALL","chemotherapy","tumor lysis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.220Z','2026-10-27T16:07:57.455Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5t003oe9oiet7xroq0','mcq','What is a correct finding on chest X-ray of a patient with tricuspid atresia?','["Right atrial enlargement.","Pulmonary oligemia.","Right ventricular hypertrophy.","Cardiac apex shift."]','Right atrial enlargement.','<p><strong>Why Right atrial enlargement is correct:</strong> In tricuspid atresia, there is no communication between the right atrium and right ventricle, leading to right atrial volume overload as blood cannot pass through the tricuspid valve. This chronic volume overload causes progressive dilation and hypertrophy of the right atrium, which is consistently demonstrated on chest X-ray. The right atrial enlargement is a direct consequence of the obstruction to blood flow from the right atrium to the right ventricle.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary oligemia:</strong> This is incorrect because tricuspid atresia typically results in decreased pulmonary blood flow due to the obstruction to right ventricular inflow, not oligemia (decreased pulmonary vasculature).</li>
<li><strong>Right ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> This is incorrect because in tricuspid atresia, the right ventricular is typically underdeveloped (hypoplastic) rather than hypertrophied, as it receives no blood flow through the atretic tricuspid valve.</li>
<li><strong>Cardiac apex shift:</strong> This is incorrect because the cardiac apex typically points to the left in tricuspid atresia, as the left ventricle is the dominant ventricle, not shifted.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Tricuspid atresia is characterized by the absence of the tricuspid valve with a hypoplastic right ventricle.</li>
<li>Chest X-ray findings include right atrial enlargement and a ''boot-shaped'' heart in some cases.</li>
<li>Associated findings include an enlarged right atrium and a small right ventricle on echocardiography.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In tricuspid atresia, the chest X-ray consistently shows right atrial enlargement due to volume overload from the obstructed tricuspid valve.</p>','<p>RAE for Tricuspid Atresia: Right Atrial Enlargement is the classic X-ray finding.</p>','<p>Tricuspid atresia is often associated with other congenital anomalies and requires prostaglandin E1 infusion to maintain ductal patency in neonates.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary oligemia:</strong> Incorrect as tricuspid atresia typically causes decreased pulmonary blood flow, not oligemia.</li>
<li><strong>Right ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> Incorrect as the right ventricle is hypoplastic, not hypertrophied in tricuspid atresia.</li>
<li><strong>Cardiac apex shift:</strong> Incorrect as the cardiac apex typically points leftward, not shifted.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["tricuspid atresia","chest x-ray","finding"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.633Z','2026-10-27T16:09:04.131Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5x003pe9oiw2ioe06b','mcq','A 2 month old girl with cyanosis was presented with severe chest infection and heart failure. What is the expected heart defect?','["Tricuspid regurgitation.","Tetralogy of Fallot.","Pulmonary stenosis.","Transposition of the great arteries."]','Tetralogy of Fallot.','<p><strong>Why Tetralogy of Fallot is correct:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease presenting in infancy with cyanosis, chest infections, and heart failure. The classic tetrad includes ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy. The pulmonary stenosis causes decreased pulmonary blood flow, leading to right-to-left shunting across the VSD and resulting in cyanosis. The increased right ventricular pressure from the obstruction leads to hypertrophy and eventual heart failure, particularly when increased oxygen demand occurs during infections.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> This would typically present with a holosystolic murmur and may cause right heart failure, but it''s not typically associated with cyanosis in infancy unless severe.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Isolated pulmonary stenosis may cause a systolic murmur and right ventricular hypertrophy but rarely causes cyanosis unless severe and associated with other defects.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the great arteries:</strong> Presents with severe cyanosis early in life (often in the first 24 hours) and is less likely to present with a chest infection as the initial manifestation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Tetralogy of Fallot accounts for 5-7% of all congenital heart defects and is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease presenting in infancy.</li>
<li>The classic presentation includes cyanosis, squatting position, and tet spells (paroxysmal hypercyanotic episodes).</li>
<li>Chest X-ray may show a ''boot-shaped'' heart due to right ventricular hypertrophy and pulmonary oligemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A cyanotic 2-month-old with chest infection and heart failure most likely has Tetralogy of Fallot, the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease presenting in infancy.</p>','<p>TOF for Tetralogy of Fallot: The most common cyanotic congenital heart disease in infancy.</p>','<p>Tetralogy of Fallot</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> Typically presents with a murmur and right heart failure but not usually cyanosis in infancy.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> May cause a murmur and right ventricular hypertrophy but rarely causes cyanosis unless severe.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the great arteries:</strong> Presents with severe cyanosis early in life, not typically with chest infection as initial manifestation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","infant","heart failure","tetralogy of fallot"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.637Z','2026-10-27T16:09:04.136Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu5y003qe9oi3oakalvx','mcq','Which lesion could cause a cyanosed baby to show lung plethora on chest X-ray?','["Truncus arteriosus.","Tetralogy of Fallot.","Transposition of the great vessels.","Pulmonary stenosis."]','Truncus arteriosus.','<p><strong>Why Truncus arteriosus is correct:</strong> Truncus arteriosus is characterized by a single great vessel arising from both ventricles, receiving blood from both the systemic and pulmonary circulations. This results in mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood, causing cyanosis. The absence of a separate pulmonary artery trunk leads to increased pulmonary blood flow, as the entire cardiac output flows through the truncus arteriosus and then into the pulmonary arteries. This increased pulmonary blood flow causes pulmonary plethora (increased pulmonary vascular markings) on chest X-ray, which is a key diagnostic feature.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This condition typically causes decreased pulmonary blood flow due to pulmonary stenosis, resulting in pulmonary oligemia (decreased pulmonary vascular markings) on chest X-ray, not plethora.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the great vessels:</strong> This condition results in parallel circulations with no mixing, leading to severe cyanosis but typically normal or decreased pulmonary vascular markings on chest X-ray, not plethora.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> This condition obstructs blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary arteries, causing decreased pulmonary blood flow and pulmonary oligemia on chest X-ray, not plethora.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Truncus arteriosus is characterized by a single great vessel overriding a VSD, with associated anomalies like interrupted aortic arch in 25% of cases.</li>
<li>The chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly and increased pulmonary vascular markings (plethora) due to increased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li>Surgical correction is typically performed in the first months of life to prevent pulmonary vascular disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Truncus arteriosus is the only cyanotic congenital heart defect that causes lung plethora (increased pulmonary vascular markings) on chest X-ray due to increased pulmonary blood flow.</p>','<p>Truncus = Trunk: One trunk vessel causing pulmonary plethora in cyanotic heart disease.</p>','<p>Truncus arteriosus is associated with DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) and requires urgent surgical correction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> Causes decreased pulmonary blood flow and pulmonary oligemia, not plethora.</li>
<li><strong>Transposition of the great vessels:</strong> Results in parallel circulations with normal or decreased pulmonary vascular markings.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Causes decreased pulmonary blood flow and pulmonary oligemia, not plethora.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","chest x-ray","lung plethora","truncus arteriosus"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.638Z','2026-10-27T16:09:04.137Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6b0047e9oikueqmuly','mcq','All of the following are causes of central cyanosis except','["Severe cold","COPD","TGA","Methemoglobinemia"]','Severe cold','<p><strong>Why severe cold is correct:</strong> Severe cold causes peripheral cyanosis, not central cyanosis. Central cyanosis results from arterial hypoxemia due to impaired oxygenation in the lungs or right-to-left shunting of deoxygenated blood. Severe cold causes peripheral vasoconstriction, which increases peripheral resistance and reduces blood flow to extremities, leading to increased extraction of oxygen from hemoglobin in those areas. This is a functional, not a pathological, cause of cyanosis that affects only the extremities, not the mucous membranes or tongue.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>COPD:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to chronic hypoxemia from impaired gas exchange in the lungs and often associated with secondary polycythemia.</li>
<li><strong>TGA (Transposition of the Great Arteries):</strong> Causes central cyanosis because deoxygenated systemic venous blood returns to the right heart and is pumped directly to the body without passing through the lungs for oxygenation.</li>
<li><strong>Methemoglobinemia:</strong> Causes central cyanosis because methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen effectively, leading to functional anemia and cyanosis visible in mucous membranes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Central cyanosis affects mucous membranes and tongue, while peripheral cyanosis affects only extremities.</li>
<li>Peripheral cyanosis is typically due to reduced peripheral blood flow or increased oxygen extraction, while central cyanosis indicates arterial hypoxemia.</li>
<li>The presence of clubbing suggests chronic cyanosis and central rather than peripheral causes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Central cyanosis indicates arterial hypoxemia from pulmonary or cardiac causes, while severe cold only causes peripheral cyanosis through vasoconstriction.</p>','<p>COLD for peripheral cyanosis (Constriction, Low flow, Oxygen extraction, Decreased perfusion)</p>','<p>Central cyanosis in a child with congenital heart disease should prompt immediate evaluation for critical lesions requiring intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>COPD:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to chronic hypoxemia from impaired gas exchange.</li>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to deoxygenated blood being pumped directly to the body.</li>
<li><strong>Methemoglobinemia:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to impaired oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Cyanosis','["cyanosis","central","causes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.651Z','2026-10-27T16:09:29.139Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6b0046e9oirhgf7dr0','mcq','One of causes of differential cyanosis in CHD is','["Pulmonary hypertension","ASD","TGA","Aortic stenosis"]','Pulmonary hypertension','<p><strong>Why pulmonary hypertension is correct:</strong> Pulmonary hypertension causes differential cyanosis in congenital heart disease by increasing pulmonary vascular resistance, which can reverse a left-to-right shunt to a right-to-left shunt. In conditions like patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) with Eisenmenger syndrome, deoxygenated blood shunts right-to-left through the PDA, causing cyanosis in the lower extremities while the upper body remains pink due to normal blood flow from the aorta to the upper extremities.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ASD (Atrial Septal Defect):</strong> Typically causes left-to-right shunting without cyanosis; differential cyanosis is not a feature.</li>
<li><strong>TGA (Transposition of the Great Arteries):</strong> Causes central cyanosis but not differential cyanosis, as both upper and lower body receive deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Causes left ventricular outflow obstruction but does not typically cause differential cyanosis; cyanosis would be central if present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Differential cyanosis is characterized by cyanosis in the lower extremities but not the upper extremities.</li>
<li>The classic cause is PDA with Eisenmenger syndrome and reversal of shunt direction.</li>
<li>Pulse oximetry showing lower saturation in the feet compared to the hands is diagnostic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Differential cyanosis (lower body cyanosis with upper body pink) is caused by right-to-left shunting through a PDA in the setting of pulmonary hypertension.</p>','<p>Differential Cyanosis = PDA + Pulmonary Hypertension (D-P-H)</p>','<p>Differential cyanosis is a critical sign that should prompt immediate evaluation for Eisenmenger syndrome or other causes of pulmonary hypertension with shunt reversal.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>ASD:</strong> Typically causes left-to-right shunting without cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>TGA:</strong> Causes central cyanosis but not differential cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Causes left ventricular obstruction but not differential cyanosis.</li>
</ul>','Hard','Cardiovascular','Cyanotic Heart Disease','["cyanosis","differential","chd","pulmonary-hypertension"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.651Z','2026-10-27T16:09:29.142Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6c0049e9oixv96hosy','mcq','An infant with D TGA what is the main cause of cyanosis?','["Pulmonary stenosis","Interrupted IVC","Aorta is rising from RV","PDA"]','Aorta is rising from RV','<p><strong>Why aorta is rising from RV is correct:</strong> In D-TGA (Dextro-Transposition of the Great Arteries), the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle. This anatomical abnormality causes cyanosis because deoxygenated systemic venous blood returns to the right heart and is pumped directly to the body through the aorta without passing through the lungs for oxygenation. The only way for oxygenation to occur is through mixing at the atrial, ventricular, or ductal level.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> While pulmonary stenosis can cause cyanosis in other lesions, it is not the primary cause in D-TGA; in fact, adequate pulmonary blood flow is necessary for survival in D-TGA.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupted IVC:</strong> This is a rare anomaly that can be associated with other congenital heart defects but is not the primary cause of cyanosis in D-TGA.</li>
<li><strong>PDA (Patent Ductus Arteriosus):</strong> While a PDA is necessary for survival in D-TGA by allowing mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood, it is not the primary cause of cyanosis; the cyanosis exists regardless of PDA presence due to the abnormal connections.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>D-TGA is characterized by the aorta arising from the right ventricle and pulmonary artery from the left ventricle.</li>
<li>Survival depends on mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood through atrial, ventricular, or ductal communications.</li>
<li>The classic presentation is cyanosis in the first 24-48 hours of life with normal heart size and no murmur.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In D-TGA, cyanosis occurs because the aorta arises from the right ventricle, pumping deoxygenated blood directly to the body without pulmonary oxygenation.</p>','<p>D-TGA: Aorta from Right, Pulmonary from Left (ARPL)</p>','<p>D-TGA is a cyanotic lesion that requires immediate prostaglandin E1 infusion to maintain ductal patency until surgical repair.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Not the primary cause of cyanosis in D-TGA; adequate pulmonary blood flow is necessary.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupted IVC:</strong> A rare anomaly not specific to D-TGA cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>PDA:</strong> Necessary for mixing but not the primary cause of cyanosis in D-TGA.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Transposition of Great Arteries','["dtga","cyanosis","infant"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.652Z','2026-10-27T16:09:29.143Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6u004ue9oijhzzql92','mcq','Medical treatment used for closure of Patent Ductus Arteriousus (PDA) include the following except','["Paracetamol","Prostaglandin E 1","Ibuprofen","Indomethacin"]','Prostaglandin E 1','<p><strong>Why Prostaglandin E 1 is correct:</strong> Prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) is used to maintain patency of the ductus arteriosus in ductal-dependent lesions, not to close it. PGE1 activates prostaglandin E receptors, causing vasodilation and keeping the ductus open. In contrast, the other options (paracetamol, ibuprofen, and indomethacin) are cyclooxygenase inhibitors that reduce prostaglandin synthesis, leading to ductal constriction and closure. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Paracetamol:</strong> Acts as a cyclooxygenase inhibitor, reducing prostaglandin production and promoting ductal closure. - <strong>Ibuprofen:</strong> A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that inhibits cyclooxygenase, decreasing prostaglandin synthesis and facilitating PDA closure. - <strong>Indomethacin:</strong> A potent NSAID that inhibits cyclooxygenase, reducing prostaglandin levels and promoting ductal constriction. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - PDA closure relies on reducing prostaglandin synthesis, which maintains ductal patency in fetal circulation. - PGE1 is used therapeutically to keep the ductus open in conditions like critical coarctation or pulmonary atresia. - NSAIDs (ibuprofen, indomethacin) and paracetamol are first-line medical therapies for PDA closure in preterm infants. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Prostaglandin E1 keeps the ductus open, while cyclooxygenase inhibitors (including paracetamol, ibuprofen, and indomethacin) close it.</p>','<p>PGE1 = ''Patency'' (keeps duct open), while others = ''Closure'' (close duct).</p>','<p>In preterm infants, ibuprofen is often preferred over indomethacin for PDA closure due to a better renal and cerebral blood flow profile.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Paracetamol:</strong> Inhibits cyclooxygenase, reducing prostaglandin synthesis and promoting PDA closure. - <strong>Ibuprofen:</strong> An NSAID that decreases prostaglandin production, facilitating ductal constriction. - <strong>Indomethacin:</strong> A potent cyclooxygenase inhibitor that reduces prostaglandin levels and promotes PDA closure.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','PDA Management','["PDA","treatment","closure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.670Z','2026-10-27T16:10:06.421Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6u004ve9oi745m381i','mcq','Congenital acyanotic heart diseases causing left ventricle dilatation include all the following except','["Atrial septal defect","Endocardial Cushing Defect","Patent dustus arteriosus","ventricular septal defect"]','Endocardial Cushing Defect','<p><strong>Why Endocardial Cushing Defect is correct:</strong> Endocardial cushion defects (ECD) are a type of atrioventricular septal defect that typically cause biventricular hypertrophy rather than isolated left ventricular dilation. These defects involve the atrioventricular junction and often result in balanced ventricular loading. In contrast, the other options are acyanotic lesions that cause left ventricular volume overload and subsequent dilation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Atrial septal defect:</strong> Causes left-to-right shunting at the atrial level, leading to left ventricular volume overload and dilation. - <strong>Patent ductus arteriosus:</strong> Creates a left-to-right shunt, increasing pulmonary blood flow and causing left ventricular volume overload and dilation. - <strong>Ventricular septal defect:</strong> Results in left-to-right shunting at the ventricular level, causing left ventricular volume overload and dilation. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Left ventricular dilation in acyanotic heart disease typically results from volume overload due to left-to-right shunting. - Endocardial cushion defects often cause biventricular hypertrophy due to balanced ventricular loading. - The presence of left ventricular dilation helps differentiate shunt lesions from obstructive lesions like coarctation of the aorta. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Endocardial cushion defects cause biventricular hypertrophy, while ASD, PDA, and VSD cause left ventricular dilation due to volume overload.</p>','<p>ASD, PDA, VSD = ''Left ventricle dilates'' (volume overload), ECD = ''Both ventricles work hard'' (balanced loading).</p>','<p>Endocardial cushion defects are associated with Down syndrome and often require surgical repair due to their complexity.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Atrial septal defect:</strong> Causes left ventricular volume overload and dilation due to left-to-right shunting. - <strong>Patent ductus arteriosus:</strong> Results in left ventricular dilation from increased pulmonary blood flow. - <strong>Ventricular septal defect:</strong> Leads to left ventricular dilation from volume overload caused by left-to-right shunting.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Acyanotic Heart Diseases','["acyanotic","left ventricle","dilatation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.670Z','2026-10-27T16:10:06.423Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu6w004we9oicfj942gr','mcq','ECG changes with ASD include the following except:','["Right axis deviation","Left axis deviation","Right ventricular hypertrophy"]','Left axis deviation','<p><strong>Why Left axis deviation is correct:</strong> Atrial septal defects (ASD) typically cause right axis deviation due to right ventricular volume overload and delayed activation of the right ventricle. The ECG in ASD often shows incomplete or complete right bundle branch block pattern with right axis deviation. Left axis deviation is characteristic of other conditions like left ventricular hypertrophy, ventricular septal defects with left axis deviation, or primum ASD, but not the more common secundum ASD. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Right axis deviation:</strong> A classic ECG finding in ASD due to right ventricular volume overload and delayed right ventricular activation. - <strong>Right ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> Common in ASD due to chronic volume overload of the right ventricle, leading to increased right ventricular mass. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The most common ECG findings in ASD include right axis deviation, right ventricular hypertrophy, and incomplete right bundle branch block. - Left axis deviation in the context of ASD may suggest a primum defect (associated with endocardial cushion defects) rather than the more common secundum defect. - The ECG changes in ASD reflect the right heart volume overload rather than ischemic or conduction abnormalities. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> ASD causes right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy, not left axis deviation.</p>','<p>ASD = ''Right heart rules'' (right axis deviation, RVH), not ''Left heart rules'' (left axis deviation).</p>','<p>The ''rSR'' pattern in V1 with right axis deviation is highly suggestive of ASD, particularly when associated with fixed splitting of S2.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Right axis deviation:</strong> Classic ECG finding in ASD due to right ventricular volume overload. - <strong>Right ventricular hypertrophy:</strong> Common in ASD from chronic right ventricular volume overload.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','ASD ECG','["ASD","ECG","changes"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.672Z','2026-10-27T16:10:06.424Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu730056e9oirusdoeug','mcq','Which type of PS treated by Catheter ?','["Valvular type"]','Valvular type','<p><strong>Why Valvular type is correct:</strong> Valvular pulmonary stenosis (PS) is characterized by obstruction at the level of the pulmonary valve itself, which can be effectively treated with balloon valvuloplasty using a catheter-based approach. This procedure involves inflating a balloon across the stenotic valve to stretch and tear the fused commissures, thereby relieving the obstruction and improving right ventricular outflow. The catheter-based approach is particularly effective for valvular PS because it directly addresses the mechanical narrowing at the valve level without requiring open-heart surgery.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Valvular type:</strong> This is actually the correct answer, not a distractor. The question specifically asks which type of PS is treated by catheter, and valvular PS is the classic indication for catheter intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Valvular PS is the most common type of pulmonary stenosis and is typically amenable to balloon valvuloplasty. - Subvalvular (infundibular) and supravalvular PS are generally not treated with catheter-based interventions due to their anatomical characteristics. - The severity of PS is graded based on the peak gradient across the valve: mild (&lt;30 mmHg), moderate (30-50 mmHg), and severe (&gt;50 mmHg).</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Catheter-based balloon valvuloplasty is the treatment of choice for valvular pulmonary stenosis, directly addressing the mechanical obstruction at the valve level.</p>','','<p>Balloon valvuloplasty for valvular PS has excellent long-term outcomes with low complication rates, making it the first-line intervention for moderate to severe cases.</p>','','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["ps","catheter","intervention"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.679Z','2026-10-27T16:10:24.627Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu730057e9oipuqvurd1','mcq','8 month-old ex-preterm infant was presented with poor feeding and poor weight gain. On examination, he had continuous 4/6 maximally over Upper thorax, what is the diagnosis?','["Mitral regurge.","Aortic stenosis","P D A","T O F"]','P D A','<p><strong>Why P D A is correct:</strong> Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in an ex-preterm infant presenting with poor feeding, poor weight gain, and a continuous 4/6 murmur maximally over the upper thorax is classic for a significant left-to-right shunt through the ductus. The continuous murmur is caused by turbulent blood flow throughout the cardiac cycle as blood flows from the higher-pressure aorta to the lower-pressure pulmonary artery during both systole and diastole. This condition is particularly common in preterm infants due to their physiological immaturity and the high incidence of persistent patency of the ductus arteriosus.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Mitral regurge:</strong> Would typically present with a holosystolic murmur at the apex, not a continuous murmur over the upper thorax. - <strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Would present with a systolic ejection murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border, not a continuous murmur. - <strong>T O F:</strong> Would present with a systolic ejection murmur, but typically has a single S2 and may have cyanosis, which is not mentioned in the case.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - PDA is the most common congenital heart lesion in preterm infants, with incidence inversely proportional to gestational age. - The classic continuous ''machine-like'' murmur is pathognomonic for PDA when heard in the typical location. - Treatment options include medical closure with indomethacin (in premature infants with a hemodynamically significant PDA) or transcatheter/device closure for larger infants or those with failed medical therapy.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A continuous 4/6 murmur maximally over the upper thorax in an ex-preterm infant with failure to thrive is diagnostic of patent ductus arteriosus.</p>','<p>PDA: ''Poor feeding, Ductus, continuous murmur, Arteriosus''</p>','<p>In preterm infants, a PDA should be strongly considered in any infant with respiratory distress, poor feeding, or failure to thrive, even in the absence of a classic murmur.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mitral regurge:</strong> Would present with a holosystolic murmur at the apex, not a continuous murmur. - <strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Would present with a systolic ejection murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border. - <strong>T O F:</strong> Would present with a systolic ejection murmur and typically has a single S2, often with cyanosis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Patent Ductus Arteriosus','["pda","infant","murmur"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.679Z','2026-10-27T16:10:24.629Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu740058e9oi47xysy4j','mcq','A days old girl with severe pulmonary stenosis. What is the main advice you can give to her parents?','["Start patient for O2 therapy.","Send her home for follow-up after 1 year.","Try alpha agonist and reevaluate.","You must refer to surgery"]','You must refer to surgery','<p><strong>Why You must refer to surgery is correct:</strong> Severe pulmonary stenosis in a newborn is a critical congenital heart defect that requires urgent surgical intervention because it can lead to right ventricular failure, cyanosis, and shock if left untreated. The obstruction to right ventricular outflow increases right ventricular pressure, which can cause hypertrophy and eventually failure, while also reducing pulmonary blood flow leading to cyanosis. Surgical options include pulmonary valvotomy (either open or via cardiopulmonary bypass) or, in some cases, balloon valvuloplasty if the anatomy is suitable, but referral to a specialized center with surgical capabilities is mandatory.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Start patient for O2 therapy:</strong> Oxygen would not address the mechanical obstruction and could potentially worsen the condition by decreasing pulmonary vascular resistance and potentially increasing the right-to-left shunt if there''s an associated septal defect. - <strong>Send her home for follow-up after 1 year:</strong> This would be inappropriate as severe pulmonary stenosis in a newborn is a surgical emergency requiring prompt intervention to prevent complications. - <strong>Try alpha agonist and reevaluate:</strong> Alpha agonists would increase systemic vascular resistance but would not address the pulmonary valve obstruction and could potentially worsen the right ventricular strain.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Severe pulmonary stenosis in a newborn is a surgical emergency due to risk of right ventricular failure and decreased pulmonary blood flow. - The diagnosis is confirmed with echocardiography, which will show the severity of the obstruction and associated cardiac anatomy. - Balloon valvuloplasty may be possible in some cases, but surgical intervention is often required, especially in severe cases with dysplastic valves.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Severe pulmonary stenosis in a newborn requires urgent surgical referral as it can rapidly lead to right ventricular failure and shock if not addressed promptly.</p>','<p>Severe PS in newborn: ''Surgery Soon, Shock Soon''</p>','<p>In critical pulmonary stenosis, the ductus arteriosus must be kept patent with prostaglandin E1 infusion to maintain pulmonary blood flow until definitive intervention can be performed.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Start patient for O2 therapy:</strong> Oxygen would not address the mechanical obstruction and could potentially worsen the condition. - <strong>Send her home for follow-up after 1 year:</strong> This would be inappropriate as severe pulmonary stenosis in a newborn is a surgical emergency. - <strong>Try alpha agonist and reevaluate:</strong> Alpha agonists would not address the pulmonary valve obstruction and could worsen right ventricular strain.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Pulmonary Stenosis','["ps","newborn","surgery"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.680Z','2026-10-27T16:10:24.630Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu78005ge9oicoit52dv','mcq','A 3 month old boy was presented with an enlarged tender liver. HR 160/min, RR 67/min, and continuous murmur over the left subclavian area. What is the possible diagnosis?','["Patent ductus arteriosus.","Aortic coarctation.","Pulmonary stenosis.","Ventricular septal defect."]','Patent ductus arteriosus.','<p><strong>Why Patent ductus arteriosus is correct:</strong> Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is a persistent connection between the aorta and pulmonary artery, allowing left-to-right shunting of blood. This results in increased pulmonary blood flow, causing hepatomegaly due to pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure. The continuous murmur is classic for PDA, resulting from the pressure gradient between the aorta (high pressure) and pulmonary artery (lower pressure) throughout both systole and diastole. The tachycardia (HR 160/min) and tachypnea (RR 67/min) are signs of heart failure secondary to the volume overload from the left-to-right shunt.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic coarctation:</strong> This would present with hypertension in the upper extremities, weak or absent femoral pulses, and possibly a murmur of aortic stenosis, not a continuous murmur.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> This would present with a systolic ejection murmur best heard at the left upper sternal border, not a continuous murmur, and would not typically cause hepatomegaly or heart failure in this manner.</li>
<li><strong>Ventricular septal defect:</strong> This would present with a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border, not a continuous murmur, and while it can cause heart failure, the murmur character is different.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PDA classically presents with a continuous "machine-like" murmur at the left upper sternal border.</li>
<li>Hepatomegaly in infancy with tachycardia suggests heart failure, which can be caused by left-to-right shunts like PDA.</li>
<li>The classic triad of PDA includes bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, and a continuous murmur.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A continuous murmur in an infant with hepatomegaly and tachycardia is pathognomonic for patent ductus arteriosus.</p>','<p>PDA - Persistent Ductus Arteriosus causes a continuous murmur like a ''washing machine'' sound.</p>','<p>PDA is more common in premature infants and those with congenital rubella syndrome; indomethacin is used to close a PDA pharmacologically in premature infants.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic coarctation:</strong> Presents with upper extremity hypertension and femoral pulse delay, not a continuous murmur.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Presents with a systolic ejection murmur, not continuous.</li>
<li><strong>Ventricular septal defect:</strong> Presents with a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["pediatrics","congenital","murmur","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.684Z','2026-10-27T16:10:43.392Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu78005fe9oi8a5j7x4f','mcq','What type of murmur could be detected in aortic stenosis?','["Aortic flow murmur.","Continuous murmur.","Diastolic murmur.","Ejection systolic murmur."]','Ejection systolic murmur.','<p><strong>Why Ejection systolic murmur is correct:</strong> Aortic stenosis causes turbulent blood flow across the narrowed aortic valve during systole, creating an ejection systolic murmur. This murmur is typically crescendo-decrescendo in shape, best heard at the right second intercostal space, and often radiates to the carotid arteries. The murmur intensity correlates with the severity of stenosis, with severe stenosis producing a harsh, loud murmur with a delayed carotid upstroke.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic flow murmur:</strong> This is not a standard classification of murmurs; murmurs are typically classified by timing (systolic/diastolic) and configuration.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous murmur:</strong> This is characteristic of conditions with continuous pressure gradients throughout the cardiac cycle, such as patent ductus arteriosus or arteriovenous fistulas.</li>
<li><strong>Diastolic murmur:</strong> This would be heard in conditions like aortic regurgitation (early diastolic decrescendo murmur) or pulmonic stenosis, not aortic stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Aortic stenosis murmurs are ejection systolic, crescendo-decrescendo, and best heard at the right second intercostal space.</li>
<li>The severity of aortic stenosis correlates with the intensity of the murmur and the delay in carotid upstroke.</li>
<li>Associated findings include systolic ejection click and diminished peripheral pulses in severe cases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aortic stenosis produces a classic ejection systolic murmur that is best heard at the right second intercostal space and radiates to the carotids.</p>','<p>AS - Aortic Stenosis = Systolic murmur; AR - Aortic Regurgitation = Diastolic murmur.</p>','<p>The severity of aortic stenosis can be estimated by the intensity of the murmur and the length of the ejection time; severe stenosis has a harsh murmur with delayed carotid upstroke.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic flow murmur:</strong> Not a standard murmur classification.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous murmur:</strong> Characteristic of patent ductus arteriosus, not aortic stenosis.</li>
<li><strong>Diastolic murmur:</strong> Heard in aortic regurgitation, not aortic stenosis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["as","murmur","cardiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.684Z','2026-10-27T16:10:43.394Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu79005he9oist1bln6h','mcq','Which lesion could a baby present with differential cyanosis?','["Tetralogy of Fallot.","Coarctation of the aorta with PDA.","Tricuspid atresia.","Pulmonary atresia."]','Coarctation of the aorta with PDA.','<p><strong>Why Coarctation of the aorta with PDA is correct:</strong> Differential cyanosis refers to cyanosis affecting the lower extremities while the upper body remains well-oxygenated. This occurs in coarctation of the aorta with a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) because the coarctation impedes blood flow to the lower body, while the PDA allows blood to bypass the obstruction by shunting from the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta. This results in deoxygenated blood reaching the lower extremities while the upper body receives oxygenated blood from the left subclavian artery proximal to the coarctation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This causes central cyanosis affecting both the upper and lower body due to right-to-left shunting across the ventricular septal defect.</li>
<li><strong>Tricuspid atresia:</strong> This causes central cyanosis due to mixing of systemic and pulmonary venous return, not differential cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> This causes central cyanosis due to inadequate pulmonary blood flow and mixing of systemic and pulmonary venous return.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Differential cyanosis (cyanosis in lower extremities only) is pathognomonic for coarctation of the aorta with PDA.</li>
<li>In this condition, the right arm is pink while the lower extremities are cyanotic due to the right-to-left shunt through the PDA.</li>
<li>Femoral pulses are typically absent or diminished in coarctation of the aorta.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Differential cyanosis (cyanotic lower extremities with pink upper body) is diagnostic of coarctation of the aorta with PDA.</p>','<p>Differential cyanosis = Descending aorta problem (coarctation) with PDA shunt.</p>','<p>Coarctation of the aorta with PDA is more common in females and can be associated with Turner syndrome; the PDA is necessary for survival in this condition.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> Causes central cyanosis, not differential cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Tricuspid atresia:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to mixing of blood, not differential cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> Causes central cyanosis due to inadequate pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["cyanosis","differential","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.685Z','2026-10-27T16:10:43.395Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7a005je9oibmbfnllh','mcq','When can pulmonary stenosis (PS) be associated with cyanosis?','["When PS is associated with tricuspid regurgitation.","When PS is severe and associated with VSD.","When PS is associated with pulmonary regurgitation.","When PS is subvalvular."]','When PS is severe and associated with VSD.','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Pulmonary stenosis (PS) alone does not cause cyanosis because blood flow through the pulmonary artery is obstructed but not reversed. However, when PS is severe and associated with a ventricular septal defect (VSD), the right ventricular pressure can exceed left ventricular pressure, causing right-to-left shunting of deoxygenated blood across the VSD. This right-to-left shunting bypasses the lungs, leading to systemic cyanosis. The combination creates a physiology similar to Tetralogy of Fallot, where pulmonary outflow obstruction and a VSD coexist.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A: When PS is associated with tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> Tricuspid regurgitation increases right atrial pressure but does not create a right-to-left shunt mechanism that would cause cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>C: When PS is associated with pulmonary regurgitation:</strong> Pulmonary regurgitation alone does not cause cyanosis; it may lead to volume overload of the right ventricle but not right-to-left shunting.</li>
<li><strong>D: When PS is subvalvular:</strong> Subvalvular PS still obstructs pulmonary outflow but does not create a shunting mechanism that would cause cyanosis unless combined with another defect like a VSD.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Isolated PS does not cause cyanosis because blood flow through the pulmonary artery is obstructed but not reversed.</li>
<li>Cyanosis in PS indicates a right-to-left shunt, typically from associated defects like VSD or Eisenmenger syndrome.</li>
<li>Severe PS with VSD creates physiology similar to Tetralogy of Fallot, with right-to-left shunting causing cyanosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pulmonary stenosis causes cyanosis only when severe enough to create a right-to-left shunt through an associated defect like VSD.</p>','<p>PS-VSD = Cyanosis (Pulmonary Stenosis with VSD causes cyanosis)</p>','<p>Cyanosis in a patient with known pulmonary stenosis should prompt evaluation for associated shunting lesions like VSD or Eisenmenger syndrome.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A:</strong> Tricuspid regurgitation increases right atrial pressure but doesn''t cause right-to-left shunting.</li>
<li><strong>C:</strong> Pulmonary regurgitation causes volume overload but not cyanosis.</li>
<li><strong>D:</strong> Subvalvular PS alone doesn''t cause cyanosis without a shunting defect.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["pulmonary stenosis","cyanosis","VSD"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.686Z','2026-10-27T16:10:59.176Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7a005ie9oidol9xhwa','mcq','A 7 years old boy was presented with mild fever. On examination, he had an ejection systolic murmur on the heart base propagated to the root of the neck with a thrill. What is the possible diagnosis?','["Mitral stenosis.","Congenital aortic stenosis.","Aortic regurgitation.","Pulmonary stenosis."]','Congenital aortic stenosis.','<p><strong>Why B is correct:</strong> Congenital aortic stenosis typically presents in childhood with an ejection systolic murmur heard at the heart base (right second intercostal space) that radiates to the carotid arteries and may be associated with a thrill. The murmur results from turbulent blood flow across the narrowed aortic valve. The age of 7 years is classic for presentation of congenital aortic stenosis, as it may not be severe enough to cause symptoms in infancy but becomes apparent as the child grows.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A: Mitral stenosis:</strong> Mitral stenosis causes a diastolic murmur, not systolic, and is typically associated with rheumatic heart disease rather than congenital presentation.</li>
<li><strong>C: Aortic regurgitation:</strong> Aortic regurgitation causes a diastolic murmur (often described as a ''decrescendo diastolic murmur'') and is less commonly congenital in this age group without other features.</li>
<li><strong>D: Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> While pulmonary stenosis can cause an ejection systolic murmur, it is typically heard at the left upper sternal border and radiates to the back, not the neck.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Congenital aortic stenosis classically presents with an ejection systolic murmur at the right second intercostal space radiating to the carotids.</li>
<li>The thrill associated with aortic stenosis is best palpated at the right second intercostal space.</li>
<li>Aortic stenosis murmur intensity does not correlate well with severity; a soft murmur can indicate severe stenosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A childhood ejection systolic murmur radiating to the neck with thrill is classic for congenital aortic stenosis.</p>','<p>Aortic Stenosis Murmur: AS-M = Aortic Systolic Murmur at the base radiating to the neck</p>','<p>The intensity of the aortic stenosis murmur does not correlate with severity; a soft murmur can indicate critical stenosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A:</strong> Mitral stenosis causes diastolic, not systolic, murmur.</li>
<li><strong>C:</strong> Aortic regurgitation causes diastolic murmur, not systolic.</li>
<li><strong>D:</strong> Pulmonary stenosis murmur radiates to back, not neck.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["murmur","systolic","thrill","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.686Z','2026-10-27T16:10:59.183Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7b005le9oidx06ics6','mcq','What is the main chamber affected in aortic coarctation?','["Left ventricle.","Left atrium.","Right atrium.","Right ventricle."]','Left ventricle.','<p><strong>Why A is correct:</strong> Aortic coarctation is a congenital narrowing of the aorta, typically just distal to the left subclavian artery. This obstruction increases afterload on the left ventricle, causing the left ventricle to hypertrophy in order to generate sufficient pressure to pump blood past the coarctation site. Over time, this chronic pressure overload leads to left ventricular hypertrophy as the main chamber affected.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>B: Left atrium:</strong> While left atrial pressure may be secondarily elevated due to increased left ventricular pressure, the left atrium is not the primary chamber affected.</li>
<li><strong>C: Right atrium:</strong> The right atrium is not directly affected by aortic coarctation, as this is a left-sided obstruction.</li>
<li><strong>D: Right ventricle:</strong> The right ventricle is not primarily affected in isolated aortic coarctation, though it may become hypertrophied in severe cases with associated defects.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Aortic coarctation causes left ventricular hypertrophy due to increased afterload from the aortic narrowing.</li>
<li>Classic physical findings include hypertension in the upper extremities with diminished or delayed femoral pulses.</li>
<li>Collateral vessels develop around the coarctation site to bypass the obstruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aortic coarctation primarily affects the left ventricle by increasing its afterload, leading to hypertrophy.</p>','<p>Coarctation LVH: Left Ventricular Hypertrophy from Left-sided obstruction</p>','<p>Hypertension in the upper extremities with diminished femoral pulses is the classic physical finding in aortic coarctation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B:</strong> Left atrium is secondarily affected, not primarily.</li>
<li><strong>C:</strong> Right atrium is not directly affected by left-sided obstruction.</li>
<li><strong>D:</strong> Right ventricle is not primarily affected in isolated coarctation.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["aortic coarctation","chamber"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.687Z','2026-10-27T16:10:59.184Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7b005ke9oigmwbv5fv','mcq','A 2 month old girl was presented with an enlarged tender liver, HR 160/min, RR 67/min, and a pansystolic murmur all over the pericardium. What is the possible diagnosis?','["Tricuspid stenosis.","Endocarditis.","Cardiomyopathy.","Pericarditis."]','Pericarditis.','<p><strong>Why Pericarditis is correct:</strong> Pericarditis is the correct diagnosis because the presentation includes an enlarged tender liver (hepatomegaly), tachycardia (HR 160/min), tachypnea (RR 67/min), and a pansystolic murmur all over the pericardium. These findings are consistent with cardiac tamponade or pericardial effusion, which can cause systemic venous congestion leading to hepatomegaly, and the friction rub from inflamed pericardial layers producing the characteristic murmur. The tachycardia and tachypnea are compensatory mechanisms for reduced cardiac output due to impaired diastolic filling. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Tricuspid stenosis:</strong> This would cause a diastolic murmur, not a pansystolic one, and typically presents with jugular venous distension rather than an enlarged liver without other signs of right heart failure. - <strong>Endocarditis:</strong> Would typically present with fever, new murmurs, and possibly embolic phenomena, not the diffuse pansystolic murmur and hepatomegaly seen here. - <strong>Cardiomyopathy:</strong> Would present with signs of heart failure like pulmonary edema, but not typically with a pansystolic murmur localized to the pericardium. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Pericarditis often presents with a pericardial friction rub that can be described as a pansystolic murmur. - Hepatomegaly in infancy with tachycardia and tachypnea suggests cardiac compromise, possibly from pericardial effusion. - The absence of cyanosis helps differentiate from cyanotic congenital heart diseases. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A pansystolic murmur over the pericardium with hepatomegaly in an infant suggests pericarditis rather than valvular heart disease.</p>','<p>PERIcarditis: Pansystolic murmur, Enlarged liver, Respiratory distress, Increased heart rate</p>','<p>Pericardial friction rub in pericarditis can mimic a murmur and is often described as a ''leather-on-leather'' sound.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tricuspid stenosis:</strong> Causes diastolic murmur, not systolic. - <strong>Endocarditis:</strong> Typically presents with fever and embolic phenomena. - <strong>Cardiomyopathy:</strong> Presents with generalized heart failure signs, not localized pericardial murmur.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["murmur","pansystolic","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.687Z','2026-10-27T16:11:00.174Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7c005me9oilp1pk540','mcq','A 6 month old girl with no cyanosis was presented with a severe chest infection and heart failure. What is the expected heart defect?','["Mitral regurgitation.","Ventricular septal defect.","Pulmonary stenosis.","Aortic stenosis."]','Ventricular septal defect.','<p><strong>Why Ventricular septal defect is correct:</strong> Ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the most likely diagnosis because it''s the most common congenital heart defect presenting with severe chest infection and heart failure in a 6-month-old infant without cyanosis. VSD allows left-to-right shunting of blood, causing increased pulmonary blood flow, which leads to pulmonary overcirculation, recurrent respiratory infections, and ultimately heart failure due to volume overload on the left heart. The increased pulmonary blood flow also explains the susceptibility to chest infections. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Mitral regurgitation:</strong> While it can cause heart failure, it''s typically a valvular disorder rather than a congenital defect and would present with a holosystolic murmur at the apex, not specifically associated with severe chest infections. - <strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> This would cause right ventricular hypertrophy and potentially cyanosis if severe, but not typically heart failure or increased susceptibility to chest infections. - <strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Presents with systolic ejection murmur and can cause left ventricular hypertrophy, but not typically with recurrent chest infections or heart failure in this age group without cyanosis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - VSD is the most common congenital heart defect presenting with heart failure in infancy without cyanosis. - The increased pulmonary blood flow from left-to-right shunting makes patients prone to respiratory infections. - Heart failure in VSD typically presents with tachypnea, tachycardia, poor feeding, and failure to thrive. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A 6-month-old with heart failure and recurrent chest infections but no cyanosis most likely has a ventricular septal defect.</p>','<p>VSD: Very Susceptible to Disease (infections), Shunt (left-to-right), Defect (most common)</p>','<p>VSD is often called the ''most common congenital heart defect'' for good reason - it''s the leading cause of heart failure in infancy without cyanosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Mitral regurgitation:</strong> Not typically associated with recurrent chest infections in infancy. - <strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Usually causes cyanosis if severe, not heart failure. - <strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Presents with systolic murmur but not typically with recurrent infections.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["heart failure","chest infection","VSD"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.688Z','2026-10-27T16:11:00.176Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7d005ne9oi34wg418z','mcq','A 6 months old with a murmur maximally on the back with blood pressure in the right upper limb about 120/60 (hypertensive according to age) and in the lower limb is low. What is the possible diagnosis?','["Coarctation of the aorta.","Aortic regurgitation.","Pulmonary stenosis.","Valvular stenosis."]','Coarctation of the aorta.','<p><strong>Why Coarctation of the aorta is correct:</strong> Coarctation of the aorta is the correct diagnosis because it presents with hypertension in the upper extremities (120/60 in the right upper limb) and hypotension in the lower extremities, which is a classic finding. The murmur maximally heard on the back is characteristic of coarctation, resulting from turbulent blood flow across the narrowed aortic segment. This condition typically presents in infancy with heart failure or later with hypertension and diminished femoral pulses. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Aortic regurgitation:</strong> Would cause a diastolic murmur and widened pulse pressure, but not the differential blood pressure between upper and lower extremities. - <strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Would cause a systolic ejection murmur heard best over the left upper sternal border, not on the back, and would not cause differential blood pressure between upper and lower extremities. - <strong>Valvular stenosis:</strong> This is too vague and could refer to any heart valve, but none would explain the differential blood pressure between upper and lower extremities. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Coarctation of the aorta causes hypertension in the upper extremities and hypotension in the lower extremities. - The characteristic murmur of coarctation is often best heard over the back between the scapulae. - Diminished or delayed femoral pulses are a classic physical finding in coarctation of the aorta. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypertension in the arms with low blood pressure in the legs in a 6-month-old suggests coarctation of the aorta.</p>','<p>Coarctation: Cuffs (tight on arms), Low BP in legs, Murmur on back</p>','<p>Coarctation of the aorta is often associated with Turner syndrome in females and should be considered in any infant with unexplained hypertension.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation:</strong> Causes diastolic murmur and widened pulse pressure, not differential BP. - <strong>Pulmonary stenosis:</strong> Murmur heard over left upper sternal border, not back. - <strong>Valvular stenosis:</strong> Too vague and doesn''t explain differential BP.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["murmur","blood pressure","coarctation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.689Z','2026-10-27T16:11:00.177Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7h005te9oihawyqa00','mcq','Of the following congenital heart diseases, which defect has increased pulmonary flow?','["Patent ductus arteriosus.","Pulmonary atresia.","Tetralogy of Fallot.","Aortic coarctation."]','Patent ductus arteriosus.','<p><strong>Why Patent ductus arteriosus is correct:</strong> Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is a persistent opening between the pulmonary artery and the aorta after birth, which normally closes within the first few days of life. This creates a left-to-right shunt because the systemic pressure in the aorta is higher than the pulmonary pressure in the pulmonary artery. As a result, blood flows from the aorta into the pulmonary artery, increasing pulmonary blood flow. This increased flow can lead to pulmonary overcirculation and symptoms like heart failure in infants.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> This condition involves underdevelopment of the pulmonary valve, preventing blood from flowing from the right ventricle to the pulmonary arteries, resulting in decreased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> This is a cyanotic heart defect characterized by four features, including pulmonary stenosis, which obstructs blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary arteries, leading to decreased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic coarctation:</strong> This is a narrowing of the aorta, which obstructs blood flow to the lower part of the body but does not directly affect pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PDA causes increased pulmonary blood flow due to a left-to-right shunt from the aorta to the pulmonary artery.</li>
<li>The classic murmur of PDA is a continuous "machine-like" murmur best heard at the upper left sternal border.</li>
<li>PDA is more common in premature infants and those born at high altitudes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Patent ductus arteriosus increases pulmonary blood flow through a left-to-right shunt from the high-pressure aorta to the low-pressure pulmonary artery.</p>','<p>PDA = Pulmonary blood flow ''Doubled'' (increased) via ''Aorta'' to ''Pulmonary'' artery shunt.</p>','<p>PDA is one of the few congenital heart defects where closure can be achieved with medication (indomethacin) in premature infants before considering surgical intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pulmonary atresia:</strong> This condition obstructs blood flow to the pulmonary arteries, decreasing pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot:</strong> Pulmonary stenosis in this defect reduces pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li><strong>Aortic coarctation:</strong> This narrows the aorta but does not directly increase pulmonary blood flow.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["pulmonary-flow","pda","congenital"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.693Z','2026-10-27T16:11:34.286Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7i005ve9oi6blz0nth','mcq','What is the most common type of VSD?','["Muscular","Perimemberanous","Apical"]','Perimemberanous','<p><strong>Why Perimemberanous is correct:</strong> Perimembranous ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the most common type of VSD, accounting for approximately 80% of all cases. It is located in the membranous portion of the interventricular septum, adjacent to the tricuspid and aortic valves. This defect allows blood to shunt from the left ventricle to the right ventricle during systole, leading to increased pulmonary blood flow. The perimembranous location makes it amenable to closure via both surgical and transcatheter approaches.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Muscular:</strong> Muscular VSDs account for only about 5-20% of all VSDs and are located entirely within the muscular portion of the interventricular septum.</li>
<li><strong>Apical:</strong> Apical VSDs are a rare subtype of muscular VSDs and are not the most common type.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Perimembranous VSDs are the most common type, located near the membranous portion of the interventricular septum.</li>
<li>They often close spontaneously as the child grows, particularly smaller defects.</li>
<li>The classic murmur of a VSD is a holosystolic murmur best heard at the lower left sternal border.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Perimembranous VSD is the most common type, located adjacent to the membranous portion of the interventricular septum near the aortic and tricuspid valves.</p>','<p>Perimembranous VSD is the ''Most Common'' type, located ''Near'' the valves.</p>','<p>Perimembranous VSDs can sometimes lead to aortic valve prolapse or regurgitation due to the close anatomical relationship with the aortic valve.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Muscular:</strong> This is a less common type of VSD, accounting for only 5-20% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>Apical:</strong> This is a rare subtype of muscular VSD and not the most common type.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','VSD','["congenital","heart-disease","vsd"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.694Z','2026-10-27T16:11:34.292Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7j005we9oiwjsslcmo','mcq','All of the following are complications of congenital acyanotic heart disease except','["Recurrent chest infection","Polycythemia","Growth failure","Arrhythmia"]','Polycythemia','<p><strong>Why Polycythemia is correct:</strong> Polycythemia is not a complication of congenital acyanotic heart disease. Acyanotic heart defects are characterized by normal or increased oxygen saturation in systemic arterial blood, as there is no right-to-left shunt causing deoxygenated blood to bypass the lungs. Polycythemia is a condition characterized by an increased concentration of red blood cells, which typically occurs in response to chronic hypoxemia. This is commonly seen in cyanotic heart diseases (like Tetralogy of Fallot) where chronic hypoxemia stimulates erythropoietin production, leading to secondary polycythemia. In acyanotic heart diseases, there is no significant hypoxemia to trigger this compensatory mechanism.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrent chest infection:</strong> Increased pulmonary blood flow in acyanotic heart defects can lead to pulmonary congestion and increased susceptibility to respiratory infections.</li>
<li><strong>Growth failure:</strong> Increased cardiac workload and metabolic demands in acyanotic heart defects can lead to failure to thrive and poor weight gain.</li>
<li><strong>Arrhythmia:</strong> Chronic volume or pressure overload on the heart in acyanotic defects can lead to atrial or ventricular arrhythmias over time.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acyanotic heart defects are characterized by normal oxygen saturation but may have increased pulmonary blood flow.</li>
<li>Polycythemia is a complication of cyanotic heart diseases due to chronic hypoxemia, not acyanotic ones.</li>
<li>Complications of acyanotic heart diseases include heart failure, recurrent infections, and growth failure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Polycythemia is not seen in acyanotic heart diseases because it requires chronic hypoxemia, which is absent in these conditions.</p>','<p>Acyanotic = No ''Cyanosis'' = No ''Polycythemia'' (since polycythemia requires hypoxemia).</p>','<p>In acyanotic heart diseases, the primary concerns relate to volume overload and heart failure, whereas cyanotic diseases present with hypoxemia and compensatory polycythemia.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrent chest infection:</strong> Increased pulmonary blood flow in acyanotic defects can lead to pulmonary congestion and infections.</li>
<li><strong>Growth failure:</strong> Increased cardiac workload in acyanotic defects can lead to failure to thrive.</li>
<li><strong>Arrhythmia:</strong> Chronic volume or pressure overload can lead to arrhythmias in acyanotic defects.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Acyanotic Heart Disease','["complications","acyanotic","heart-disease"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.695Z','2026-10-27T16:11:34.293Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7l0061e9oi9m461yed','mcq','Type of murmur of VSD is:','["Continuous murmur.","Pansystolic murmur.","Innocent murmur."]','Pansystolic murmur.','<p><strong>Why pansystolic murmur is correct:</strong> The pansystolic murmur of VSD results from blood shunting from the left ventricle to the right ventricle throughout systole, creating a pressure gradient that persists from the beginning to the end of systole. This murmur is holosystolic because the pressure difference between the ventricles is maintained throughout systole, allowing continuous flow across the defect. The murmur is typically harsh and located at the left lower sternal border, with maximal intensity at the end of systole when the pressure gradient is greatest. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Continuous murmur:</strong> This is incorrect because continuous murmurs occur when there is continuous flow between chambers or vessels throughout both systole and diastole, as in patent ductus arteriosus. - <strong>Innocent murmur:</strong> This is incorrect because innocent murmurs are typically systolic but have different characteristics (softer, musical, variable intensity) and are not associated with structural heart defects like VSD. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - VSD murmurs are holosystolic (pansystolic) due to continuous shunting from higher-pressure left ventricle to lower-pressure right ventricle throughout systole. - The murmur is best heard at the left lower sternal border and may have a harsh quality. - Small VSDs may have a louder murmur due to higher velocity flow across the smaller defect. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> VSD causes a pansystolic murmur because blood flows continuously from left to right ventricle throughout systole.</p>','<p>VSD = Very Systolic Duration</p>','<p>The intensity of a VSD murmur does not correlate with the size of the defect; small VSDs often have louder murmurs due to higher velocity flow.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Continuous murmur:</strong> Incorrect because continuous murmurs occur throughout both systole and diastole, as in PDA. - <strong>Innocent murmur:</strong> Incorrect because innocent murmurs are not associated with structural heart defects like VSD.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','VSD','["vsd","murmur","pansystolic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.697Z','2026-10-27T16:11:50.050Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu86006ye9oi7x8or9ur','mcq','Which of the following is associated with a poor prognosis in rheumatic fever?','["Older age with no carditis","Younger age with valvar lesions","No cardiac involvement","Severe arthritis only"]','Younger age with valvar lesions','<p><strong>Why younger age with valvar lesions is correct:</strong> Younger age at onset of rheumatic fever is associated with a worse prognosis because it indicates a more severe initial immune response to group A streptococcal infection. Valvar lesions, particularly mitral regurgitation, reflect significant cardiac involvement and damage to the heart valves, which can lead to progressive valvular dysfunction and heart failure over time. The combination of young age and valvular involvement suggests a more aggressive disease process with higher likelihood of developing chronic rheumatic heart disease. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Older age with no carditis:</strong> Older age typically correlates with better prognosis as the immune response is less severe, and absence of carditis means no permanent cardiac damage. - <strong>No cardiac involvement:</strong> The absence of carditis is generally associated with a better prognosis since it prevents permanent heart damage. - <strong>Severe arthritis only:</strong> While severe arthritis can be disabling, it doesn''t carry the same long-term morbidity and mortality risk as cardiac involvement. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Carditis is the most serious manifestation of rheumatic fever and the primary determinant of long-term prognosis. - Younger age at first attack (&lt;15 years) correlates with increased risk of recurrent attacks and progressive valvular damage. - Mitral regurgitation is the most common valvular lesion in rheumatic heart disease and a key prognostic factor. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Younger age with valvular lesions in rheumatic fever indicates poor prognosis due to increased risk of progressive cardiac damage.</p>','<p>YOUNG VALVES = Poor prognosis (Young age + Valvular involvement)</p>','<p>Rheumatic heart disease remains a leading cause of cardiovascular death in children and young adults in developing regions, emphasizing the importance of primary prevention with prompt streptococcal treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Older age with no carditis:</strong> Older age typically correlates with better prognosis and absence of carditis prevents permanent heart damage. - <strong>No cardiac involvement:</strong> Absence of carditis is associated with better prognosis as it avoids permanent heart damage. - <strong>Severe arthritis only:</strong> Arthritis alone doesn''t carry the same long-term risk as cardiac involvement.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic","fever","prognosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.718Z','2026-10-27T16:13:24.239Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7l0060e9oioffq9i6v','mcq','What is the main auscultatory finding in valvular aortic stenosis?','["Wide splitted 2nd heart sound","Early systolic murmur","Ejection systolic murmur","Absent 1st heart sound"]','Ejection systolic murmur','<p><strong>Why ejection systolic murmur is correct:</strong> The ejection systolic murmur in aortic stenosis results from turbulent blood flow being ejected through the narrowed aortic valve during systole. This murmur is characterized by a crescendo-decrescendo shape, beginning after the first heart sound and peaking mid-systole, corresponding to the maximal velocity of blood flow through the stenotic valve. The murmur radiates to the carotid arteries due to the high-velocity jet of blood. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Wide splitted 2nd heart sound:</strong> This is incorrect because aortic stenosis typically causes a paradoxical splitting of S2 due to delayed closure of the aortic valve, not wide splitting. - <strong>Early systolic murmur:</strong> This is incorrect because aortic stenosis murmurs are ejection murmurs that peak mid-systole, not early systolic. - <strong>Absent 1st heart sound:</strong> This is incorrect because the first heart sound is typically loud in aortic stenosis due to the forceful closure of the mitral valve. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Aortic stenosis causes a crescendo-decrescendo ejection systolic murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids. - The murmur intensity does not correlate with severity; severe stenosis may have a softer murmur due to reduced flow. - Associated findings include a delayed, diminished A2 component and paradoxical splitting of S2. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Aortic stenosis produces an ejection systolic murmur due to turbulent flow through the narrowed valve during ventricular ejection.</p>','<p>AS = Aortic Systolic (murmur)</p>','<p>The carotid upstroke is delayed and diminished in aortic stenosis, correlating with severity better than murmur intensity.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Wide splitted 2nd heart sound:</strong> Incorrect because aortic stenosis typically causes paradoxical splitting, not wide splitting. - <strong>Early systolic murmur:</strong> Incorrect because aortic stenosis murmurs peak mid-systole, not early. - <strong>Absent 1st heart sound:</strong> Incorrect because S1 is typically loud in aortic stenosis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Aortic Stenosis','["aortic-stenosis","murmur","auscultation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.697Z','2026-10-27T16:11:50.056Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7n0063e9oilcmvq54g','mcq','A 3 month old boy with large VSD with heart failure was advised to get pulmonary artery banding, what is the rational of this procedure?','["to enhance pulmonary artery anatomy","to improve anatomy till full repair","to decrease pulmonary flow to avoid chest infections","it is total repair procedure and no need for another surgery"]','to decrease pulmonary flow to avoid chest infections','<p><strong>Why to decrease pulmonary flow to avoid chest infections is correct:</strong> Pulmonary artery banding is a palliative procedure for infants with large ventricular septal defects (VSDs) who are not yet candidates for complete repair. The banding reduces pulmonary blood flow by constricting the pulmonary artery, which decreases the volume overload on the left ventricle and pulmonary vasculature. This prevents the development of pulmonary hypertension and Eisenmenger syndrome, while also reducing the risk of recurrent respiratory infections caused by pulmonary overcirculation and congestion. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>To enhance pulmonary artery anatomy:</strong> Pulmonary artery banding does not improve the anatomical structure of the pulmonary artery; it merely constricts it to reduce flow. - <strong>To improve anatomy till full repair:</strong> The banding does not improve the underlying anatomy of the VSD; it is a temporary measure to manage symptoms until definitive repair can be performed. - <strong>It is total repair procedure and no need for another surgery:</strong> Pulmonary artery banding is palliative, not curative, and requires eventual removal and complete repair of the VSD. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Large VSDs cause left-to-right shunting, leading to pulmonary overcirculation and volume overload on the left heart. - Pulmonary artery banding is used as a bridge to complete repair in symptomatic infants who are too small or unstable for definitive surgery. - The band must be carefully calibrated to balance between preventing pulmonary hypertension and maintaining adequate pulmonary blood flow for growth. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pulmonary artery banding decreases pulmonary blood flow in large VSDs to prevent heart failure and pulmonary hypertension until definitive repair can be performed.</p>','<p>VSD Band = Volume reduction, Symptom relief, Deferral of definitive repair</p>','<p>Pulmonary artery banding is a temporizing measure that requires careful monitoring and eventual removal before definitive repair to avoid complications like pulmonary artery stenosis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>To enhance pulmonary artery anatomy:</strong> The band constricts rather than enhances the pulmonary artery anatomy. - <strong>To improve anatomy till full repair:</strong> The band does not improve the underlying VSD anatomy. - <strong>It is total repair procedure and no need for another surgery:</strong> This is palliative, not curative, and requires future definitive repair.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["vsd","pulmonary artery banding","heart failure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.699Z','2026-10-27T16:11:53.763Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7o0064e9oit23kk3r2','mcq','A 5-month-old ex-preterm infant was presented with poor feeding and poor weight gain. On examination, his HR was 160/min, RR was 54/min. he was not cyanosed. He had continuous murmur 4/6 maximally over left upper thorax. What is most common complication?','["Pericardial effusion","Heart failure","Cyanotic spell","Ventricular arrhythmia"]','Heart failure','<p><strong>Why Heart failure is correct:</strong> This 5-month-old ex-preterm infant with a continuous murmur (classic for patent ductus arteriosus, PDA) and signs of tachycardia (HR 160/min) and tachypnea (RR 54/min) is experiencing volume overload from left-to-right shunting. The increased pulmonary blood flow leads to pulmonary congestion and increased work of breathing, while the left ventricular volume overload causes decreased cardiac output and poor feeding. These are classic manifestations of heart failure in the context of a significant left-to-right shunt lesion. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Pericardial effusion:</strong> While PDA can be associated with endocarditis, pericardial effusion is not a direct complication of a left-to-right shunt and would typically present with muffled heart sounds or tamponade, not a continuous murmur. - <strong>Cyanotic spell:</strong> Cyanotic spells are characteristic of right-to-left shunting lesions like tetralogy of Fallot, not left-to-right shunts where the infant is not cyanosed. - <strong>Ventricular arrhythmia:</strong> While structural heart disease can predispose to arrhythmias, ventricular arrhythmias are not the most common or immediate complication of a large PDA in this clinical scenario. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Preterm infants are particularly susceptible to hemodynamically significant PDAs due to their immature pulmonary vasculature and ductal tissue. - The classic continuous "machine-like" murmur of a PDA is best heard in the left upper thorax, consistent with the location of the ductus. - Heart failure in infants with left-to-right shunts typically presents with failure to thrive, tachypnea, tachycardia, and hepatomegaly. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A continuous murmur in an ex-preterm infant with poor feeding and tachycardia most commonly indicates heart failure due to a significant left-to-right shunt like a patent ductus arteriosus.</p>','<p>PDA HF = Poor feeding, Dyspnea, Auscultation (continuous murmur), Hepatomegaly</p>','<p>In ex-preterm infants, a patent ductus arteriosus is one of the most common causes of heart failure and should be considered in any infant with poor feeding, tachypnea, and a continuous murmur.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Pericardial effusion:</strong> Not directly associated with left-to-right shunts and would present with different auscultatory findings. - <strong>Cyanotic spell:</strong> Characteristic of right-to-left shunts, not left-to-right shunts in non-cyanotic infants. - <strong>Ventricular arrhythmia:</strong> Not the most common immediate complication of a large PDA in this clinical scenario.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure','["preterm","murmur","complication"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.700Z','2026-10-27T16:11:53.769Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7p0066e9oii3cfonpv','mcq','What is the main complication of large left to right shunt?','["Peripheral cyanosis","Congestive heart failure","Recurrent thrombosis"]','Congestive heart failure','<p><strong>Why Congestive heart failure is correct:</strong> Large left-to-right shunts, such as those occurring in VSD, PDA, or AV canal defects, result in increased pulmonary blood flow and volume overload on the left heart. This volume overload leads to dilation of the left atrium and ventricle, increased pulmonary venous pressure, and pulmonary congestion. The heart eventually fails to maintain adequate cardiac output, resulting in congestive heart failure characterized by tachycardia, tachypnea, hepatomegaly, poor feeding, and failure to thrive. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Peripheral cyanosis:</strong> While large left-to-right shunts can cause pulmonary overcirculation and increased pulmonary vascular resistance, they typically do not cause peripheral cyanosis unless Eisenmenger syndrome develops (reversal to right-to-left shunting). - <strong>Recurrent thrombosis:</strong> Thrombosis is not a direct complication of left-to-right shunts; instead, these lesions increase the risk of infective endocarditis due to turbulent blood flow across the shunt. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The magnitude of left-to-right shunting depends on the size of the defect and the resistance in the pulmonary versus systemic circulation. - Infants with large left-to-right shunts typically present with symptoms of heart failure between 4-8 weeks of age as pulmonary vascular resistance falls. - The physical exam often reveals bounding pulses, a hyperdynamic precordium, and a systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border (VSD) or upper left sternal border (PDA). <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The primary complication of large left-to-right shunts is congestive heart failure due to volume overload of the pulmonary circulation and left heart.</p>','<p>Left-to-Right = Left heart failure</p>','<p>Early recognition of heart failure in infants with left-to-right shunts is critical, as timely intervention can prevent irreversible pulmonary vascular changes.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Peripheral cyanosis:</strong> Not typical of left-to-right shunts unless Eisenmenger syndrome develops. - <strong>Recurrent thrombosis:</strong> Not a direct complication; increased risk is for infective endocarditis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Shunt Lesions','["left to right shunt","complication","heart failure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.701Z','2026-10-27T16:11:53.777Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8j007je9oibs48kthd','mcq','Cleft lip repair at','["3 month","1 year"]','3 month','<p><strong>Why 3 month is correct:</strong> Cleft lip repair is typically performed at 3 months of age to allow sufficient growth of the maxilla while minimizing the risk of anesthesia complications in a younger infant. At this age, the infant has adequate tissue for repair, and the procedure can be performed before significant speech development begins. The timing balances surgical feasibility with optimal functional outcomes for feeding and speech development.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>1 year:</strong> This is too late as it delays intervention beyond the optimal window for speech development and may lead to more difficult surgical repair due to increased tissue tension and potential maxillary growth issues.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Cleft lip repair is typically performed between 3-6 months of age, with 3 months being most common. - The timing allows for adequate tissue development while minimizing anesthesia risks in infants. - Early repair (before 12 months) is crucial for normal speech development.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Cleft lip repair is performed at 3 months to balance surgical feasibility with optimal speech development outcomes.</p>','','<p>Early cleft lip repair (3-6 months) prevents speech development issues and improves feeding outcomes.</p>','','Basic','General','Cleft Lip Repair','["cleft lip","surgery","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.731Z','2026-10-27T16:14:21.302Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7q0068e9oisfcey778','mcq','Early after birth, how blood pH changes?','["Increase the decrease","Stay fixed","It increases","It decreases"]','It decreases','<p><strong>Why It decreases is correct:</strong> Immediately after birth, blood pH decreases due to the transition from placental gas exchange to lung-based respiration. In utero, the placenta handles CO₂ removal, maintaining a slightly alkaline pH (~7.40-7.45). At birth, the infant begins breathing, increasing CO₂ elimination initially, but the abrupt cessation of placental function and the stress of delivery lead to a transient respiratory acidosis. Additionally, the first few breaths may be inefficient, causing CO₂ retention and a drop in pH to approximately 7.25-7.30, which is normal for the immediate newborn period.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Increase the decrease:</strong> This phrasing is nonsensical and doesn''t describe a physiological process.</li>
<li><strong>Stay fixed:</strong> Blood pH does not remain fixed; it changes significantly during the transition from fetal to neonatal life.</li>
<li><strong>It increases:</strong> pH does not increase immediately after birth; it transiently decreases due to the factors mentioned.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Normal arterial pH in newborns is 7.25-7.35, slightly lower than adults (7.35-7.45), due to physiological respiratory acidosis at birth.</li>
<li>The Apgar score incorporates respiratory effort, which directly affects pH in the immediate newborn period.</li>
<li>Persistent acidosis beyond the first few hours of life requires investigation for causes like hypoxia, sepsis, or metabolic disorders.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Blood pH decreases immediately after birth due to the transition from placental to pulmonary respiration and initial inefficient breathing.</p>','<p>pH Drops at Birth (PDB)</p>','<p>Newborns normally have a slightly lower pH (7.25-7.35) than adults due to physiological respiratory acidosis at birth.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Increase the decrease:</strong> Nonsensical phrasing that doesn''t describe a physiological process.</li>
<li><strong>Stay fixed:</strong> Blood pH changes significantly during the transition from fetal to neonatal life.</li>
<li><strong>It increases:</strong> pH does not increase immediately after birth; it transiently decreases.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Neonatal Physiology','["neonate","ph","transition"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.702Z','2026-10-27T16:12:06.522Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7q0067e9oio9tqsqs1','mcq','In intrauterine life, what about blood pH?','["It lower than normal","It is more than normal","As adult pH","pH usually more than 7.4"]','It is more than normal','<p><strong>Why It is more than normal is correct:</strong> During intrauterine life, the blood pH is higher (more alkaline) than normal adult values, typically ranging from 7.40-7.45. This alkaline environment is maintained by the placenta, which efficiently removes CO₂ from fetal blood, preventing respiratory acidosis. The fetal hemoglobin''s higher oxygen affinity and the maternal-fetal pH difference (the Bohr effect) facilitate oxygen transfer across the placenta. Additionally, fetal metabolism produces less CO₂ per unit of oxygen consumed compared to adults, contributing to the more alkaline pH.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It lower than normal:</strong> Fetal blood pH is not lower than normal; it is more alkaline than adult values to facilitate oxygen transfer.</li>
<li><strong>As adult pH:</strong> Fetal pH is not the same as adult pH; it is consistently higher (more alkaline).</li>
<li><strong>pH usually more than 7.4:</strong> While true, this option is less precise than "It is more than normal" as it doesn''t capture the comparison to adult values (7.35-7.45).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fetal blood pH is 7.40-7.45, higher than adult values (7.35-7.45), due to efficient placental CO₂ removal.</li>
<li>The maternal-fetal pH difference (the Bohr effect) enhances oxygen transfer from mother to fetus.</li>
<li>Fetal hemoglobin''s higher oxygen affinity and 2,3-DPG levels are adaptations to the intrauterine environment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Intrauterine blood pH is more alkaline than adult values due to efficient placental CO₂ removal and adaptations for oxygen transfer.</p>','<p>Fetal pH Higher (FPH)</p>','<p>The fetal alkaline pH (7.40-7.45) is essential for oxygen transfer across the placenta and is maintained by efficient placental CO₂ removal.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It lower than normal:</strong> Fetal blood pH is not lower; it is more alkaline than adult values.</li>
<li><strong>As adult pH:</strong> Fetal pH is consistently higher (more alkaline) than adult pH.</li>
<li><strong>pH usually more than 7.4:</strong> Less precise than "It is more than normal" as it doesn''t capture the comparison to adult values.</li>
</ul>','Basic','General','Fetal Physiology','["fetal","ph","physiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.702Z','2026-10-27T16:12:06.523Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7r006ae9oivz12qqcp','mcq','During fetal life, which carries most oxygenated blood?','["Aorta","Inferior vena cava","Umbilical vein","Umbilical artery"]','Umbilical vein','<p><strong>Why Umbilical vein is correct:</strong> The umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus, providing the necessary oxygen and nutrients for fetal development. This oxygenated blood enters the fetal circulation through the ductus venosus, bypassing the liver, and then flows into the inferior vena cava and right atrium. The umbilical vein has the highest oxygen saturation (approximately 80%) in the fetal circulation, making it the primary vessel delivering oxygenated blood to the fetus.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aorta:</strong> The fetal aorta carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle through the ductus arteriosus to the lower body, not oxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Inferior vena cava:</strong> The inferior vena cava carries a mixture of oxygenated blood from the umbilical vein and deoxygenated blood from the lower body, resulting in intermediate oxygen saturation.</li>
<li><strong>Umbilical artery:</strong> The umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta for oxygenation, making them vessels that remove rather than deliver oxygenated blood.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The umbilical vein is the only vessel in the fetal circulation that carries purely oxygenated blood from the placenta.</li>
<li>Fetal circulation is characterized by right-to-left shunts (foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus) that bypass the non-functional lungs.</li>
<li>Oxygen saturation in umbilical vein blood (80%) is higher than in the aorta (55%) due to mixing with deoxygenated blood.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The umbilical vein is the sole vessel carrying oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus, making it the most oxygenated vessel in fetal circulation.</p>','<p>UV = Oxygen Vein: Umbilical Vein is the vessel carrying Oxygenated blood to the fetus.</p>','<p>Understanding fetal circulation is crucial for interpreting congenital heart defects and planning interventions that must account for the unique fetal shunts.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aorta:</strong> Carries deoxygenated blood to the lower body via the ductus arteriosus.</li>
<li><strong>Inferior vena cava:</strong> Contains a mixture of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.</li>
<li><strong>Umbilical artery:</strong> Carries deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Fetal Circulation','["fetal","circulation","oxygenated blood"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.703Z','2026-10-27T16:12:12.591Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7s006ce9oij0rfnvso','mcq','Regarding treatment of heart failure:','["Water and salt restriction must be avoided","Diuretics are not recommended in pediatrics","Inotropes are good choice","Chronotropes are highly recommended"]','Inotropes are good choice','<p><strong>Why Inotropes are good choice:</strong> Inotropes, such as digoxin and milrinone, are beneficial in heart failure treatment because they increase the force of myocardial contraction by enhancing calcium availability in cardiomyocytes (digoxin inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase, increasing intracellular calcium) or by inhibiting phosphodiesterase (milrinone increases cAMP, enhancing calcium influx). This improved contractility helps maintain cardiac output in patients with systolic dysfunction, addressing the fundamental pathophysiology of heart failure where the heart cannot pump effectively to meet metabolic demands. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Water and salt restriction must be avoided:</strong> This is incorrect as fluid and sodium restriction is actually a cornerstone of heart failure management to reduce preload and prevent volume overload. - <strong>Diuretics are not recommended in pediatrics:</strong> This is false as diuretics (like furosemide) are commonly used in pediatric heart failure to manage fluid overload and reduce pulmonary congestion. - <strong>Chronotropes are highly recommended:</strong> This is incorrect as chronotropes (which increase heart rate) are generally avoided in heart failure since increased heart rate can worsen myocardial oxygen demand and is often a compensatory mechanism that becomes maladaptive over time. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Inotropes are particularly useful in systolic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction to improve contractility. - Diuretics address the volume overload component of heart failure, while ACE inhibitors/ARBs reduce afterload and remodeling. - The combination of inotropes, diuretics, and afterload-reducing agents forms the cornerstone of acute heart failure management. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Inotropes improve heart function by increasing contractility, making them a good choice for systolic heart failure, unlike chronotropes which can be harmful by increasing oxygen demand.</p>','<p>Inotropes help the heart ''squeeze'' better (contractility), while chronotropes only speed up the rate (heart rate).</p>','<p>Inotropes should be used judiciously in heart failure, as while they improve contractility, they can also increase myocardial oxygen demand and potentially worsen outcomes if not carefully monitored.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Water and salt restriction must be avoided:</strong> Incorrect, as fluid and sodium restriction is essential in heart failure management. - <strong>Diuretics are not recommended in pediatrics:</strong> False, diuretics are commonly used in pediatric heart failure. - <strong>Chronotropes are highly recommended:</strong> Incorrect, as chronotropes increase heart rate and can worsen heart failure.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure Treatment','["treatment","heart-failure","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.704Z','2026-10-27T16:12:26.212Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8j007ie9oi1mrfvc5f','mcq','A child is known to be asthmatic with recent history of daily asthmatic attacks without limitation of activities and with night attacks more than 2 times per week in the last 2 week, he is on Inhaled steroids and ß agonist for breakthrough. Which drug should be added to achieve good control:','["Long-acting ß agonist","Long-acting ß agonist, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists","Long-acting ß agonist, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists and Systemic steroids","Increased dose of inhaled steroid, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists and Systemic steroids"]','Long-acting ß agonist, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists','<p><strong>Why Long-acting ß agonist, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists is correct:</strong> This child has persistent moderate asthma (daily symptoms with night awakenings &gt;2x/week) despite low-dose inhaled corticosteroids, requiring step-up therapy according to GINA guidelines. Adding a long-acting ß agonist (LABA) provides sustained bronchodilation and synergistic anti-inflammatory effects with inhaled corticosteroids. Adding a leukotriene-receptor antagonist (LTRA) offers complementary anti-inflammatory action through a different pathway (cysteinyl leukotriene inhibition), addressing multiple inflammatory pathways in asthma.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Long-acting ß agonist:</strong> While appropriate, this alone doesn''t address the persistent inflammation adequately and may increase risk without additional controller therapy. - <strong>Long-acting ß agonist, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists and Systemic steroids:</strong> Systemic steroids are reserved for exacerbations, not maintenance therapy in controlled asthma. - <strong>Increased dose of inhaled steroid, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists and Systemic steroids:</strong> Increasing inhaled steroid dose is an option, but adding LABA is preferred per guidelines before increasing steroid dose further. Systemic steroids are not indicated for maintenance.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Step-up therapy for persistent moderate asthma involves adding LABA to inhaled corticosteroids as first-line controller therapy. - LTRA can be added as alternative or adjunct therapy when LABA is not tolerated or preferred. - Systemic steroids are reserved for acute exacerbations, not maintenance therapy.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For persistent moderate asthma despite low-dose ICS, add LABA first, with LTRA as an alternative or adjunct option.</p>','<p>A-LA: Add LABA before increasing ICS dose for asthma step-up.</p>','<p>Asthma control requires addressing multiple inflammatory pathways; LABA+ICS is preferred over higher ICS doses alone for step-up therapy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Long-acting ß agonist:</strong> Incomplete therapy as it doesn''t address all inflammatory pathways adequately. - <strong>Long-acting ß agonist, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists and Systemic steroids:</strong> Systemic steroids are not indicated for maintenance therapy. - <strong>Increased dose of inhaled steroid, Leukotriene-receptor antagonists and Systemic steroids:</strong> Increasing ICS dose is less preferred than adding LABA, and systemic steroids are not for maintenance.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma Management','["asthma","child","management","controller therapy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.731Z','2026-10-27T16:14:21.303Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7t006de9oi253lnjxg','mcq','One of the following is not sign of heart failure in infant:','["Tachypnea","Tachycardia","Hypertension"]','Hypertension','<p><strong>Why Hypertension is correct:</strong> Hypertension is not a sign of heart failure in infants because heart failure in this age group typically presents with signs of low cardiac output and systemic hypoperfusion, not elevated blood pressure. In infants, the cardiovascular system is underdeveloped, and heart failure usually manifests as tachycardia and tachypnea as compensatory mechanisms to maintain cardiac output in the face of decreased stroke volume. <strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> Tachypnea and tachycardia are classic signs of heart failure in infants as the body attempts to compensate for reduced cardiac output by increasing respiratory rate to improve oxygenation and heart rate to maintain perfusion. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Infants with heart failure typically present with failure to thrive, tachypnea, tachycardia, hepatomegaly, and sweating during feeds. - Hypertension is more commonly associated with renal disease, coarctation of the aorta, or other endocrine disorders in infants, not heart failure. - The clinical presentation of heart failure differs between age groups, with infants showing signs of low output while older children and adults may show signs of volume overload. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Heart failure in infants presents with tachycardia and tachypnea as compensatory mechanisms, not hypertension, which would suggest a different pathology.</p>','<p>HTN (Hypertension) is not a sign of HF (Heart Failure) in infants - remember ''HTN'' stands for ''Here''s The Not'' sign.</p>','<p>Infants with heart failure often present with failure to thrive, respiratory distress, and hepatomegaly rather than the volume overload symptoms seen in older children and adults.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tachypnea:</strong> This is a common sign of heart failure in infants as the body attempts to compensate for poor oxygenation. - <strong>Tachycardia:</strong> This is a compensatory mechanism in infants with heart failure to maintain cardiac output.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Heart Failure in Infants','["heart-failure","infant","signs"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.705Z','2026-10-27T16:12:26.215Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7u006fe9oidrb5z2yj','mcq','A 4 month old girl was presented with enlarged tender liver, HR 180 /min, RR 67/min and pan systolic murmur all over the pericardium, what is possible diagnosis?','["tetralogy of Fallout with heart failure","VSD with heart failure","ASD","Aortic stenosis"]','VSD with heart failure','<p><strong>Why VSD with heart failure is correct:</strong> The presentation of a 4-month-old girl with enlarged tender liver (hepatomegaly), tachycardia (HR 180/min), tachypnea (RR 67/min), and a pan systolic murmur strongly suggests a ventricular septal defect (VSD) with heart failure. VSD is the most common congenital heart defect and allows left-to-right shunting of blood, leading to volume overload of the left heart, pulmonary overcirculation, and ultimately heart failure. The pan systolic murmur is characteristic of VSD, while the tachycardia and tachypnea are compensatory mechanisms for decreased cardiac output, and hepatomegaly results from systemic venous congestion. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Tetralogy of Fallot with heart failure:</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot typically presents with cyanosis rather than signs of heart failure, and the murmur is usually systolic but not pan systolic all over the pericardium. - <strong>ASD:</strong> Atrial septal defects typically present with a systolic ejection murmur, not a pan systolic murmur, and heart failure is less common in infancy with isolated ASD. - <strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Aortic stenosis presents with a systolic ejection murmur often heard at the right upper sternal border, not a pan systolic murmur all over the pericardium, and would typically cause left ventricular hypertrophy rather than signs of heart failure at this age. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - VSD is the most common congenital heart defect and can lead to heart failure in infancy due to left-to-right shunting and pulmonary overcirculation. - The classic murmur of VSD is a holosystolic (pan systolic) murmur best heard at the lower left sternal border. - Heart failure in infants with congenital heart defects often presents with failure to thrive, tachypnea, tachycardia, hepatomegaly, and sweating during feeds. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A pan systolic murmur with signs of heart failure in an infant strongly suggests VSD, the most common congenital heart defect causing volume overload.</p>','<p>VSD: Very Serious Defect - causes a pan systolic murmur and heart failure in infants due to left-to-right shunting.</p>','<p>VSD is the most common congenital heart defect and can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome if left untreated, where the shunt reverses direction due to pulmonary hypertension.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallout with heart failure:</strong> Tetralogy typically presents with cyanosis, not signs of heart failure, and has a different murmur quality. - <strong>ASD:</strong> ASD typically has a systolic ejection murmur and less commonly causes heart failure in infancy. - <strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> Aortic stenosis has a systolic ejection murmur at the right upper sternal border and causes left ventricular hypertrophy, not heart failure signs.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Congenital Heart Disease','["infant","murmur","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.706Z','2026-10-27T16:12:26.217Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7w006ie9oiigqsokhx','mcq','The most affected valves in acute rheumatic fever are','["Tricuspid amd mitral","Aortic and mitral","Pulmonary and aortic","Aortic and tricuspid"]','Aortic and mitral','<p><strong>Why Aortic and mitral is correct:</strong> In acute rheumatic fever, the mitral and aortic valves are most frequently affected due to their exposure to high-pressure blood flow and their proximity to the left ventricle. The pathophysiology involves cross-reactivity between group A streptococcal antigens and cardiac valve tissue, leading to inflammation (Aschoff bodies) and subsequent valve damage. The mitral valve is involved in approximately 75% of cases, while the aortic valve is affected in about 25% of cases, with combined mitral and aortic involvement being the most common pattern. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Tricuspid and mitral:</strong> The tricuspid valve is rarely affected in acute rheumatic fever, occurring in less than 5% of cases, making this combination incorrect. - <strong>Pulmonary and aortic:</strong> The pulmonary valve is almost never involved in rheumatic heart disease due to its lower pressure environment and different embryologic origin. - <strong>Aortic and tricuspid:</strong> While the aortic valve is commonly affected, the tricuspid valve is rarely involved, making this combination atypical. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Mitral regurgitation is the most common valvular lesion in acute rheumatic fever, often presenting with a holosystolic murmur at the apex. - The Jones criteria for diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever include carditis as a major criterion, with mitral and aortic involvement being most characteristic. - Chronic rheumatic heart disease typically presents with mitral stenosis years after the initial episode due to progressive fibrosis and calcification. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rheumatic fever spares the right-sided valves (tricuspid and pulmonary) and preferentially damages the mitral and aortic valves due to hemodynamic stress and molecular mimicry.</p>','<p>M-A: Mitral and Aortic valves are most affected in Rheumatic fever</p>','<p>Rheumatic heart disease typically presents with mitral stenosis decades after the initial episode, making a thorough history of streptococcal infections crucial in young patients with valvular disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Tricuspid and mitral:</strong> The tricuspid valve is rarely affected in rheumatic fever. - <strong>Pulmonary and aortic:</strong> The pulmonary valve is almost never involved in rheumatic heart disease. - <strong>Aortic and tricuspid:</strong> While the aortic valve is commonly affected, the tricuspid valve is rarely involved.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic-fever","valves","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.708Z','2026-10-27T16:12:43.014Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu7w006je9oie095oztp','mcq','Carditis in acute rheumatic fever Occurs in.... of patients','["5%","15%","50%","75%"]','50%','<p><strong>Why 50% is correct:</strong> Carditis occurs in approximately 50% of patients with acute rheumatic fever, representing the most common major manifestation of the disease. This inflammation of the heart muscle and valves results from cross-reactivity between group A streptococcal M proteins and cardiac tissues, leading to immune-mediated damage. The 50% figure represents the pooled data from multiple studies and clinical observations, though incidence can vary based on age, geographic location, and access to healthcare. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>5%:</strong> This figure is far too low and represents the incidence of chorea (Sydenham''s chorea) rather than carditis. - <strong>15%:</strong> This underestimates the true incidence of carditis in rheumatic fever. - <strong>75%:</strong> This overestimates the incidence; while carditis is common, it doesn''t affect three-quarters of patients. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Carditis is a major diagnostic criterion for acute rheumatic fever according to the modified Jones criteria. - When carditis is present, it often manifests as a new or changing murmur, pericardial friction rub, or cardiomegaly on chest X-ray. - The presence of carditis significantly increases the risk of developing chronic rheumatic heart disease. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Carditis affects about half of all patients with acute rheumatic fever and is the most common major manifestation of the disease.</p>','<p>Half of patients with rheumatic fever get carditis (50%)</p>','<p>The presence of carditis in acute rheumatic fever requires more aggressive anti-inflammatory therapy and longer secondary prophylaxis to prevent recurrent attacks and progressive valvular damage.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>5%:</strong> This is the incidence of chorea (Sydenham''s chorea), not carditis. - <strong>15%:</strong> This underestimates the true incidence of carditis in rheumatic fever. - <strong>75%:</strong> This overestimates the incidence of carditis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic-fever","carditis","epidemiology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.708Z','2026-10-27T16:12:43.016Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu82006qe9oi0a68z5k7','mcq','Complications Of Chronic Rheumatic HD include all of the followings except .','["Infective endocarditis","Systemic hypertension","Heart failure","Repeated chest infection"]','Systemic hypertension','<p><strong>Why Systemic hypertension is correct:</strong> Systemic hypertension is not a direct complication of chronic rheumatic heart disease (RHD). RHD results from autoimmune damage to heart valves following untreated group A streptococcal infections, leading to valvular dysfunction (stenosis or regurgitation) rather than increased systemic vascular resistance. Hypertension is an independent cardiovascular risk factor that may coexist with RHD but is not causally linked to the rheumatic process itself. The pathophysiology of RHD involves molecular mimicry between streptococcal M proteins and cardiac tissues, causing chronic inflammation and scarring, which does not directly affect systemic blood pressure regulation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Infective endocarditis:</strong> Damaged valves in chronic RHD are particularly susceptible to bacterial colonization, creating a high risk for infective endocarditis due to turbulent blood flow and endothelial damage.</li>
<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> Chronic valvular dysfunction in RHD eventually leads to volume or pressure overload, causing progressive ventricular dilation and systolic/diastolic dysfunction, ultimately resulting in heart failure.</li>
<li><strong>Repeated chest infection:</strong> Chronic pulmonary congestion secondary to left heart failure (from mitral stenosis or regurgitation) creates a favorable environment for recurrent respiratory infections.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Chronic RHD primarily affects mitral valve (65-70% of cases), followed by aortic valve (25%), then multivalvular involvement.</li>
<li>The Jones criteria remain the diagnostic standard for acute rheumatic fever, with major manifestations including carditis, migratory polyarthritis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea.</li>
<li>Valvular calcification and fibrosis in chronic RHD result from persistent inflammation and healing responses, not from the initial acute insult.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Chronic rheumatic heart disease causes valvular damage leading to heart failure and endocarditis risk, but does not directly cause systemic hypertension.</p>','<p>RHD complications: HEIC (Heart failure, Endocarditis, Infective risk, Chest infections) - but no Hypertension.</p>','<p>The most common valve affected in chronic rheumatic heart disease is the mitral valve, with mitral stenosis being the classic manifestation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Infective endocarditis:</strong> Damaged valves in RHD are prone to bacterial colonization and infection.</li>
<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> Valvular dysfunction eventually leads to volume/pressure overload and ventricular dysfunction.</li>
<li><strong>Repeated chest infection:</strong> Pulmonary congestion from left heart failure predisposes to respiratory infections.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Heart Disease','["rheumatic","heart","disease","complications"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.714Z','2026-10-27T16:13:08.136Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu84006ue9oiloqm2m61','mcq','Which of the following is characteristic of rheumatic arthritis?','["It is usually deforming","It is usually chronic and progressive","It is migratory and involves large joints","It primarily affects small joints symmetrically"]','It is migratory and involves large joints','<p><strong>Why migratory and involves large joints is correct:</strong> Rheumatic fever is an autoimmune complication of untreated Group A Streptococcus infection, characterized by a migratory arthritis that typically affects large joints like knees, ankles, elbows, and wrists. This migratory pattern occurs due to molecular mimicry where antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-react with joint tissue, causing inflammation that moves from one joint to another. The arthritis is typically non-deforming and responds dramatically to salicylates or NSAIDs, distinguishing it from other forms of arthritis. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>It is usually deforming:</strong> Rheumatic arthritis is non-deforming, unlike rheumatoid arthritis which causes permanent joint damage. - <strong>It is usually chronic and progressive:</strong> Rheumatic fever is an acute, self-limited condition that resolves with treatment, not a chronic progressive disease. - <strong>It primarily affects small joints symmetrically:</strong> This describes rheumatoid arthritis, not rheumatic fever which affects large joints asymmetrically in a migratory pattern. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Rheumatic fever arthritis is migratory, involves large joints, and responds dramatically to anti-inflammatory therapy. - The Jones criteria for rheumatic fever include migratory polyarthritis as one of the major manifestations. - Asymmetric involvement of large joints with rapid response to aspirin is classic for rheumatic fever. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Rheumatic fever causes migratory arthritis of large joints that responds dramatically to anti-inflammatory therapy, unlike other forms of arthritis.</p>','<p>Migratory Large Joints Respond (MLJR) to aspirin in rheumatic fever.</p>','<p>Rheumatic fever arthritis is often the most prominent feature but is not the most serious manifestation; carditis is responsible for most morbidity and mortality.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>It is usually deforming:</strong> Rheumatic arthritis is non-deforming, unlike rheumatoid arthritis. - <strong>It is usually chronic and progressive:</strong> Rheumatic fever is an acute, self-limited condition. - <strong>It primarily affects small joints symmetrically:</strong> This describes rheumatoid arthritis, not rheumatic fever.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Rheumatology','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic","fever","arthritis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.716Z','2026-10-27T16:13:15.578Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu85006ve9oiy8t9x9qq','mcq','In high-prevalence areas, which of the following may be considered a major criterion for acute rheumatic fever?','["Monoarthritis","Prolonged PR interval","Fever","None of above"]','None of above','<p><strong>Why None of above is correct:</strong> In high-prevalence areas of rheumatic fever, the modified Jones criteria require major manifestations to include carditis, polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, or subcutaneous nodules. While fever is common in acute rheumatic fever, it is not considered a major diagnostic criterion even in high-prevalence areas. Monoarthritis and prolonged PR interval (first-degree AV block) are minor manifestations, not major criteria. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Monoarthritis:</strong> Monoarthritis is considered a minor manifestation of rheumatic fever, not a major criterion. - <strong>Prolonged PR interval:</strong> This represents first-degree AV block, which is a minor manifestation, not a major criterion. - <strong>Fever:</strong> While fever is frequently present in acute rheumatic fever, it is not listed as a major criterion in the Jones criteria, even in high-prevalence areas. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The Jones criteria for diagnosing rheumatic fever include 5 major manifestations: carditis, polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, and subcutaneous nodules. - In high-prevalence areas, the criteria remain the same; fever is not elevated to major criterion status. - Two major manifestations or one major plus two minor manifestations are required for diagnosis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Even in high-prevalence areas, fever, monoarthritis, and prolonged PR interval are not major criteria for rheumatic fever diagnosis.</p>','<p>Major Five: Carditis, Arthritis, Chorea, Erythema, Nodules (CACEN) - Fever is not among them.</p>','<p>In endemic areas, rheumatic fever may present with atypical manifestations, making diagnosis challenging; clinicians must maintain a high index of suspicion.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Monoarthritis:</strong> This is a minor manifestation, not a major criterion. - <strong>Prolonged PR interval:</strong> This is a minor manifestation (first-degree AV block), not a major criterion. - <strong>Fever:</strong> Fever is not listed as a major criterion in the Jones criteria, even in high-prevalence areas.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic","fever","diagnosis","criteria"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.717Z','2026-10-27T16:13:15.579Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu85006we9oiwr0xrdld','mcq','The most serious manifestation of acute rheumatic fever, responsible for most morbidity and mortality, is:','["Arthritis","Chorea","Carditis","Erythema marginatum"]','Carditis','<p><strong>Why Carditis is correct:</strong> Carditis is the most serious manifestation of acute rheumatic fever, responsible for most morbidity and mortality, as it can lead to chronic rheumatic heart disease with valvular damage (particularly mitral and aortic regurgitation). The pathogenesis involves cross-reactivity between streptococcal antigens and cardiac tissue, causing pancarditis involving the pericardium, myocardium, and endocardium. This valvular damage, especially mitral regurgitation, can progress to heart failure and may require surgical intervention years later. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Arthritis:</strong> While arthritis is a common and often prominent manifestation of acute rheumatic fever, it is self-limited and does not cause long-term damage. - <strong>Chorea:</strong> Sydenham''s chorea is a neurological manifestation that can be distressing but is rarely life-threatening and typically resolves without sequelae. - <strong>Erythema marginatum:</strong> This is a rare cutaneous manifestation of rheumatic fever that causes non-pruritic, serpiginous rash but has no significant long-term consequences. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Carditis occurs in 50-60% of first attacks of rheumatic fever and 70-80% of recurrent attacks. - The most common valvular involvement in rheumatic carditis is mitral regurgitation, followed by aortic regurgitation. - Recurrent attacks of rheumatic fever cause progressive valvular damage, making secondary prevention crucial. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Carditis is the most dangerous manifestation of rheumatic fever because it causes permanent valvular damage leading to chronic rheumatic heart disease.</p>','<p>Carditis Kills: Most Morbidity and Mortality in Rheumatic Fever (C-KMM)</p>','<p>Rheumatic carditis may be subclinical and only detected by echocardiogram, highlighting the importance of cardiac evaluation in all suspected cases.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Arthritis:</strong> Arthritis is self-limited and does not cause long-term damage. - <strong>Chorea:</strong> Chorea is distressing but rarely life-threatening and typically resolves without sequelae. - <strong>Erythema marginatum:</strong> This is a benign cutaneous manifestation with no significant long-term consequences.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic","fever","carditis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.717Z','2026-10-27T16:13:15.580Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu86006xe9oi5tsntzd2','mcq','Which patient has the best prognosis following an attack of rheumatic fever?','["Young child with severe carditis","Older child with no carditis","Young child with only mild valvar lesions","Child with severe rheumatic carditis"]','Older child with no carditis','<p><strong>Why older child with no carditis is correct:</strong> An older child with no carditis has the best prognosis because the absence of cardiac involvement means there is no permanent damage to the heart valves or myocardium. Older age at onset of rheumatic fever is associated with a milder disease course and lower risk of recurrent attacks. Without carditis, the child avoids the progressive valvular damage that characterizes rheumatic heart disease, which is the primary cause of long-term morbidity and mortality in rheumatic fever. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Young child with severe carditis:</strong> This combination indicates poor prognosis due to young age (more aggressive disease) and significant cardiac damage. - <strong>Young child with only mild valvar lesions:</strong> Even mild valvar lesions indicate cardiac involvement and carry risk of progression. - <strong>Child with severe rheumatic carditis:</strong> Severe carditis is the most serious manifestation and carries the worst prognosis regardless of age. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The absence of carditis is the single most important prognostic factor in rheumatic fever. - Older age at first attack (&gt;15 years) is associated with milder disease and lower risk of recurrence. - Prophylaxis with penicillin is most critical in patients with a history of carditis. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The best prognosis in rheumatic fever belongs to patients without cardiac involvement, regardless of age, but older age further improves outcomes.</p>','<p>NO HEART = Best prognosis (No carditis)</p>','<p>Secondary prophylaxis with benzathine penicillin every 3-4 weeks is essential for all patients with rheumatic fever, especially those with carditis, to prevent recurrent attacks and progressive valvular damage.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Young child with severe carditis:</strong> Indicates poor prognosis due to young age and significant cardiac damage. - <strong>Young child with only mild valvar lesions:</strong> Even mild valvar lesions indicate cardiac involvement and risk of progression. - <strong>Child with severe rheumatic carditis:</strong> Severe carditis carries the worst prognosis regardless of age.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["rheumatic","fever","prognosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.718Z','2026-10-27T16:13:24.241Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8a0074e9oirt9qzyqm','mcq','A diagnosed Asthmatic child, What is the indication for X ray','["O2 saturation less than 95%","Tachypnea","High grade fever","Audible Wheezing"]','High grade fever','<p><strong>Why High grade fever is correct:</strong> High-grade fever in an asthmatic child suggests a potential infectious complication such as bacterial pneumonia or superinfection, which would necessitate chest X-ray evaluation. Asthma exacerbations are typically triggered by viruses, but persistent fever beyond 48-72 hours raises concern for bacterial complications. The X-ray helps identify parenchymal lung involvement, consolidation, or effusion that would alter management, such as the need for antibiotics.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>O2 saturation less than 95%:</strong> This indicates hypoxia requiring oxygen therapy but doesn''t necessarily necessitate imaging unless there''s concern for complications.</li>
<li><strong>Tachypnea:</strong> Tachypnea is common in asthma exacerbations and is managed with bronchodilators and corticosteroids without routine imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Audible Wheezing:</strong> This is a hallmark of asthma itself and doesn''t indicate a need for X-ray unless other concerning features are present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fever in asthma suggests possible complication rather than simple exacerbation.</li>
<li>Chest X-ray is indicated when fever persists beyond 48-72 hours or when clinical suspicion for pneumonia exists.</li>
<li>Asthma exacerbations typically don''t require imaging unless atypical features or complications are present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Obtain a chest X-ray in asthmatic children with high-grade fever to evaluate for infectious complications like pneumonia.</p>','<p>Fever First - In asthma, Fever is the first indication for X-ray</p>','<p>In asthmatic children, fever beyond 48-72 hours should raise suspicion for bacterial superinfection requiring chest imaging.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>O2 saturation less than 95%:</strong> Indicates hypoxia but doesn''t necessarily require imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Tachypnea:</strong> Common in asthma and managed without routine imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Audible Wheezing:</strong> A typical asthma symptom that doesn''t warrant X-ray.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","pediatrics","x-ray"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.722Z','2026-10-27T16:13:39.155Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8b0075e9oizz70pnv9','mcq','For Accelerating asthma','["Short acting beta agonist for 1 hour","Short acting beta agonist for 2 hour","Inhaled corticosteroids"]','Short acting beta agonist for 1 hour','<p><strong>Why Short acting beta agonist for 1 hour is correct:</strong> Short-acting beta agonists (SABAs) like albuterol provide rapid bronchodilation by binding to beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle, causing relaxation within minutes. For accelerating asthma recovery, SABAs are first-line agents that work quickly to relieve bronchoconstriction. The 1-hour timeframe allows assessment of treatment response before considering additional interventions or hospitalization. This rapid onset makes SABAs ideal for acute exacerbations where immediate symptom relief is needed.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Short acting beta agonist for 2 hour:</strong> While SABAs are effective, extending the duration to 2 hours doesn''t provide additional benefit over the standard 1-hour assessment period and may delay necessary escalation of care.</li>
<li><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids:</strong> These are important for long-term control but work slowly over days to reduce inflammation, making them inappropriate for accelerating acute asthma recovery.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>SABAs provide rapid bronchodilation within 5-15 minutes, peaking at 60-90 minutes.</li>
<li>For acute asthma exacerbations, SABAs are first-line for rapid symptom relief.</li>
<li>Response to SABA treatment at 1 hour helps determine need for additional therapy or hospitalization.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Use short-acting beta agonists for 1 hour to rapidly accelerate asthma recovery by providing immediate bronchodilation.</p>','<p>SABA 1 - Short Acting Beta Agonist for 1 hour</p>','<p>Inhaled corticosteroids require 4-6 hours to begin working effectively, making them unsuitable for acute asthma exacerbations.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Short acting beta agonist for 2 hour:</strong> No additional benefit over standard 1-hour assessment period.</li>
<li><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids:</strong> Work too slowly for acute asthma acceleration.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.723Z','2026-10-27T16:13:39.159Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8c0077e9oid8yhr60s','mcq','Risk factor for Asthma','["Family history"]','Family history','<p><strong>Why Family history is correct:</strong> Family history is a well-established risk factor for asthma due to the strong genetic component of the disease. Asthma has a polygenic inheritance pattern with multiple genes involved in immune regulation, airway hyperresponsiveness, and inflammation. Specific genetic variants associated with asthma include those in the IL-4, IL-13, ADAM33, and CD14 genes, which affect Th2 immune responses and epithelial barrier function. Having a first-degree relative with asthma increases an individual''s risk by 2- to 6-fold, with the risk being higher if both parents are affected.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>[No other options provided]</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Family history is one of the strongest risk factors for asthma development, with heritability estimates ranging from 35-80%.</li>
<li>The genetic predisposition to asthma interacts with environmental triggers (allergens, pollutants, infections) to determine disease expression.</li>
<li>Atopic individuals (those with allergic tendencies) are more likely to develop asthma when exposed to environmental triggers.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Asthma has a strong genetic component, making family history one of the most significant risk factors for disease development.</p>','<p>FAMILY: Family history Asthma risk Major factor Inheritance Linked to you</p>','<p>Family history of asthma should prompt early recognition of symptoms in children, as genetic predisposition increases risk but environmental triggers often determine actual disease expression.</p>','','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","risk-factors"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.724Z','2026-10-27T16:13:49.254Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8d0079e9oi7slmsw0a','mcq','Symptoms of bronchial asthma include all of the following except','["Coughing","Wheezing","Shortness of breath","Itching"]','Itching','<p><strong>Why Itching is correct:</strong> Itching is not a classic symptom of bronchial asthma, which primarily affects the lower airways. Asthma symptoms result from airway inflammation, bronchoconstriction, and increased mucus production, leading to coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath, and chest tightness. Itching, however, is more commonly associated with allergic reactions affecting the skin (urticaria, atopic dermatitis) or upper respiratory tract (allergic rhinitis), where mast cells release histamine causing pruritus. While some asthma patients may experience itching during severe exacerbations due to histamine release, it is not a defining or characteristic symptom of asthma.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Coughing:</strong> A cardinal symptom of asthma, particularly in cough-variant asthma, resulting from airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness.</li>
<li><strong>Wheezing:</strong> The hallmark auscultatory finding in asthma, caused by airway narrowing and turbulent airflow through narrowed bronchioles.</li>
<li><strong>Shortness of breath:</strong> A primary symptom of asthma resulting from increased work of breathing due to airway obstruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Asthma symptoms are primarily respiratory: coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath, and chest tightness.</li>
<li>Itching is more characteristic of allergic conditions affecting the skin or upper airways rather than asthma.</li>
<li>Cough-variant asthma presents primarily with cough as the sole symptom, highlighting the variability of asthma presentations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> While asthma causes respiratory symptoms like coughing, wheezing, and shortness of breath, itching is not a characteristic symptom and suggests alternative diagnoses like allergic rhinitis or atopic dermatitis.</p>','<p>CAWS: Cough, Asthma, Wheezing, Shortness of breath - all asthma symptoms except Itching</p>','<p>When asthma presents with cough as the predominant symptom (cough-variant asthma), it may be misdiagnosed as chronic bronchitis or post-nasal drip syndrome.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Coughing:</strong> A classic asthma symptom resulting from airway inflammation.</li>
<li><strong>Wheezing:</strong> The hallmark sound of asthma due to turbulent airflow through narrowed airways.</li>
<li><strong>Shortness of breath:</strong> A primary asthma symptom from increased work of breathing.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.725Z','2026-10-27T16:13:49.255Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8e007ae9oif674ghhh','mcq','Bronchial asthma characterized by being','["Recurrent","Irreversible","No response to bronchodilators","Related to summer"]','Recurrent','<p><strong>Why Recurrent is correct:</strong> Bronchial asthma is fundamentally characterized by recurrent episodes of wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness, and coughing, particularly at night or early morning. These recurrent episodes are caused by variable airflow obstruction that is often reversible either spontaneously or with treatment. The recurrence pattern distinguishes asthma from other respiratory conditions and reflects the underlying chronic airway inflammation with hyperresponsiveness that leads to periodic exacerbations triggered by various factors like allergens, exercise, cold air, or respiratory infections.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Irreversible:</strong> Asthma is typically characterized by reversible airflow obstruction, though in severe, poorly controlled cases, irreversible changes may develop over time.</li>
<li><strong>No response to bronchodilators:</strong> Asthma generally shows significant response to bronchodilators, which is a key diagnostic feature; lack of response would suggest alternative diagnoses.</li>
<li><strong>Related to summer:</strong> While some patients have seasonal exacerbations (often related to allergens like pollen), asthma can occur year-round and is not specifically tied to summer.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Asthma diagnosis requires both recurrent symptoms and evidence of variable airflow obstruction (e.g., &gt;12% improvement in FEV1 after bronchodilator).</li>
<li>The recurrent nature of asthma distinguishes it from conditions like COPD, which typically presents with persistent rather than intermittent symptoms.</li>
<li>Asthma triggers are highly individual and can include allergens, exercise, cold air, respiratory infections, medications, and emotional stress.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Asthma is defined by recurrent episodes of reversible airflow obstruction, not by seasonality or lack of response to treatment.</p>','<p>Asthma: ''A'' for ''Airway hyperreactivity'' and ''R'' for ''Recurrent episodes''</p>','<p>Asthma symptoms often worsen at night due to circadian variations in airway caliber and inflammation, a phenomenon called ''nocturnal asthma.''</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Irreversible:</strong> Asthma typically features reversible airflow obstruction, though severe cases may develop irreversible changes.</li>
<li><strong>No response to bronchodilators:</strong> Asthma generally responds well to bronchodilators, which is a diagnostic hallmark.</li>
<li><strong>Related to summer:</strong> While some patients have seasonal exacerbations, asthma is not specifically a summer-related condition.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","characteristics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.726Z','2026-10-27T16:13:58.027Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8e007be9oic5bsc5ps','mcq','Controller therapy of bronchial asthma include all of the following except','["Inhaled corticosteroids","Leukotriene receptor antagonist","Antibiotics","Biologics"]','Antibiotics','<p><strong>Why Antibiotics is correct:</strong> Antibiotics are not part of controller therapy for bronchial asthma as they target bacterial infections rather than the underlying inflammatory mechanisms of asthma. Controller medications are designed to be taken daily to maintain long-term control of asthma by reducing airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness. Antibiotics might be used only when a bacterial infection (like sinusitis or pneumonia) is suspected to be triggering or complicating asthma, but they do not address the core pathophysiology of asthma itself.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids:</strong> These are first-line controller therapy that reduce airway inflammation and are essential for long-term asthma management.</li>
<li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonist:</strong> These (like montelukast) are controller medications that block inflammatory leukotrienes and are used as add-on therapy or for mild persistent asthma.</li>
<li><strong>Biologics:</strong> These are newer controller therapies (like anti-IgE, anti-IL5) that target specific inflammatory pathways in severe asthma.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Controller medications are taken daily to maintain long-term asthma control, while reliever medications (like short-acting beta-agonists) are used for quick symptom relief.</li>
<li>Inhaled corticosteroids remain the most effective controller medication for persistent asthma, reducing exacerbations and improving lung function.</li>
<li>Antibiotics are only indicated for asthma when there''s evidence of a bacterial infection contributing to symptoms, not as routine controller therapy.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Controller therapy for asthma targets inflammation and includes inhaled corticosteroids, leukotriene modifiers, and biologics—but not antibiotics, which treat infections, not asthma itself.</p>','<p>Asthma Controller: ''C'' for ''Corticosteroids'', ''L'' for ''Leukotriene modifiers'', ''B'' for ''Biologics''—no ''A'' for Antibiotics</p>','<p>Overuse of antibiotics in asthma patients can contribute to antibiotic resistance and may disrupt the microbiome, potentially worsening immune regulation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids:</strong> First-line controller therapy that reduces airway inflammation.</li>
<li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonist:</strong> Used as controller therapy to block inflammatory leukotrienes.</li>
<li><strong>Biologics:</strong> Advanced controller therapies targeting specific inflammatory pathways in severe asthma.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","treatment","controller-therapy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.726Z','2026-10-27T16:13:58.027Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8f007ce9oia0lbfkz9','mcq','Clinical features which increase probability of bronchial asthma include all of the following except','["Clubbing","Recurrents attacks of Coughing and wheezing","Symptoms worse at night","Expiratory wheezes on auscultation"]','Clubbing','<p><strong>Why Clubbing is correct:</strong> Clubbing is a classic physical finding associated with chronic hypoxemia and is not a feature of bronchial asthma. It is typically seen in conditions like bronchiectasis, interstitial lung disease, or lung cancer, where there is chronic tissue hypoxia leading to proliferation of connective tissue in the nail beds. Asthma is characterized by reversible airway obstruction and inflammation, but does not cause the chronic hypoxia required for clubbing development. The presence of clubbing in a patient with suspected asthma should prompt evaluation for alternative diagnoses.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrents attacks of Coughing and wheezing:</strong> This is a hallmark feature of asthma, representing the episodic bronchoconstriction and inflammation characteristic of the disease.</li>
<li><strong>Symptoms worse at night:</strong> Nocturnal worsening of symptoms is very common in asthma due to circadian variations in airway tone and inflammation, often referred to as "nocturnal asthma."</li>
<li><strong>Expiratory wheezes on auscultation:</strong> Wheezing during expiration is a classic auscultatory finding in asthma, resulting from turbulent airflow through narrowed airways during forced expiration.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Clubbing is a red flag for alternative diagnoses in patients suspected of having asthma.</li>
<li>Nocturnal worsening of symptoms is so characteristic of asthma that its absence should make the diagnosis questionable.</li>
<li>The presence of expiratory wheezing is supportive but not diagnostic of asthma, as it can occur in other obstructive lung diseases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Clubbing is never a feature of asthma and should prompt consideration of alternative diagnoses when present in a patient with suspected asthma.</p>','<p>Remember ''CAUTION'' for asthma features: Coughing, Attacks, Wheezing, Night symptoms, Triggers - but no clubbing.</p>','<p>The presence of clubbing in a patient with suspected asthma should always raise concern for an alternative diagnosis such as bronchiectasis, interstitial lung disease, or malignancy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrents attacks of Coughing and wheezing:</strong> This is a classic feature of asthma, making it incorrect as the answer.</li>
<li><strong>Symptoms worse at night:</strong> Nocturnal worsening is characteristic of asthma, making this option incorrect.</li>
<li><strong>Expiratory wheezes on auscultation:</strong> Wheezing is a common finding in asthma, making this option incorrect.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","clinical-features","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.727Z','2026-10-27T16:14:06.529Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8g007de9oi6ikrbvw0','mcq','Coughing of bronchial asthma is characterized by all the following except','["Frequent","Increase with exercise","Increase at night","Decreased at early morning"]','Decreased at early morning','<p><strong>Why Decreased at early morning is correct:</strong> Asthma cough characteristically worsens in the early morning hours due to circadian variations in airway caliber, inflammation, and parasympathetic tone. This nocturnal/morning worsening is so characteristic of asthma that it''s considered a diagnostic feature. The cough does not decrease at early morning; rather, it typically peaks during this time due to increased airway responsiveness overnight. The other options all describe features commonly associated with asthma-related cough.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Frequent:</strong> Asthma cough is typically frequent and persistent, especially in poorly controlled cases or cough-variant asthma.</li>
<li><strong>Increase with exercise:</strong> Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction commonly triggers cough in asthmatic patients, making this a characteristic feature.</li>
<li><strong>Increase at night:</strong> Nocturnal worsening of cough is extremely common in asthma due to circadian variations in airway tone and inflammation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Early morning worsening of cough is so characteristic of asthma that its absence should make the diagnosis questionable.</li>
<li>Cough-variant asthma presents with cough as the predominant or sole symptom, without classic wheezing.</li>
<li>The timing of cough (worse at night/early morning, with exercise) is more important than its frequency for diagnosing asthma.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Asthma cough characteristically worsens in the early morning, not decreases, making ''decreased at early morning'' the correct answer.</p>','<p>Remember ''NITE'' for asthma cough: Worse at N-ight, I-ncreases with exercise, T-ypical in early morning - not decreased.</p>','<p>When evaluating a chronic cough, always ask specifically about nocturnal or early morning worsening, as this is highly suggestive of asthma.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Frequent:</strong> Asthma cough is typically frequent, making this option incorrect.</li>
<li><strong>Increase with exercise:</strong> Exercise commonly triggers asthma-related cough, making this option incorrect.</li>
<li><strong>Increase at night:</strong> Nocturnal worsening is characteristic of asthma, making this option incorrect.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma','["asthma","symptoms","cough"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.728Z','2026-10-27T16:14:06.531Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8k007ke9oi935lu43q','mcq','which of the following is a clinical sign of bronchial asthma?','["wheezing"]','wheezing','<p><strong>Why wheezing is correct:</strong> Wheezing is the classic clinical sign of bronchial asthma, resulting from airway narrowing due to bronchoconstriction, inflammation, and mucus production that creates turbulent airflow through the narrowed airways. This high-pitched whistling sound is typically heard on expiration but can occur during inspiration in severe cases. The presence of wheezing indicates reversible airflow obstruction, which is the hallmark pathophysiological feature of asthma.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While the question only provides one correct option, it''s important to note that other respiratory conditions can cause wheezing (such as COPD, heart failure, or foreign body aspiration), but wheezing remains the most characteristic and specific clinical sign for asthma among the options typically presented in such questions.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Wheezing results from turbulent airflow through narrowed airways due to bronchoconstriction and inflammation. - In asthma, wheezing is typically expiratory and may be accompanied by cough and dyspnea. - The reversibility of wheezing with bronchodilators is a key diagnostic feature of asthma.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Wheezing is the hallmark clinical sign of bronchial asthma, resulting from turbulent airflow through narrowed, inflamed airways.</p>','<p>Wheezing = Wide Airways Expiratory Narrowing Inflammation</p>','<p>Wheezing in asthma is reversible with bronchodilators, distinguishing it from fixed airflow obstruction seen in COPD.</p>','','Easy','Respiratory','Asthma Signs','["asthma","clinical signs","wheezing"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.732Z','2026-10-27T16:14:21.304Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8h007fe9oiuti00dpw','mcq','To confirm the diagnosis of bronchial asthma using spirometry, the bronchodilator response to inhaled beta-agonist will be:','["Improvement in FEV1 to more than 20%","Improvement in FEV1 to more than 15%","Improvement in FEV1 to more than 12%","Improvement in FEV1 to more than 9%"]','Improvement in FEV1 to more than 12%','<p><strong>Why Improvement in FEV1 to more than 12% is correct:</strong> The 12% improvement in FEV1 following bronchodilator administration is the established threshold for a significant positive bronchodilator response in spirometry, as defined by the Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) guidelines. This threshold represents a clinically meaningful change that distinguishes obstructive airway disease like asthma from normal or restrictive patterns. The mechanism involves beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation by inhaled beta-agonists leading to relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle, which is particularly effective in asthma where there is reversible airway obstruction. This 12% improvement must also be accompanied by an absolute increase in FEV1 of at least 200mL to be considered significant, as per American Thoracic Society (ATS) standards.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Improvement in FEV1 to more than 20%:</strong> This threshold is too high and would miss many cases of asthma that show a clinically significant but less dramatic response to bronchodilators.</li>
<li><strong>Improvement in FEV1 to more than 15%:</strong> While some patients may show greater than 15% improvement, this exceeds the established diagnostic threshold and would result in false negatives for many asthmatic patients.</li>
<li><strong>Improvement in FEV1 to more than 9%:</strong> This threshold is too low and would lack specificity, potentially leading to false positives where the observed change could be due to normal variation rather than true bronchodilator response.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The 12% improvement in FEV1 must be accompanied by an absolute increase of at least 200mL to be considered clinically significant.</li>
<li>A positive bronchodilator response is one of the key diagnostic criteria for asthma, demonstrating reversible airflow limitation.</li>
<li>The bronchodilator response test is typically performed after baseline spirometry shows an FEV1/FVC ratio &lt;0.70.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The diagnostic threshold for a significant bronchodilator response in asthma is a 12% improvement in FEV1 with an absolute increase of at least 200mL.</p>','<p>12/200 rule: 12% improvement AND 200mL absolute increase in FEV1 for significant bronchodilator response.</p>','<p>A negative bronchodilator response doesn''t rule out asthma, as some patients may require multiple tests or alternative challenge tests like methacholine or exercise challenge to demonstrate airway hyperresponsiveness.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Improvement in FEV1 to more than 20%:</strong> This threshold is too high and would miss many cases of asthma that show a clinically significant but less dramatic response.</li>
<li><strong>Improvement in FEV1 to more than 15%:</strong> This exceeds the established diagnostic threshold and would result in false negatives for many asthmatic patients.</li>
<li><strong>Improvement in FEV1 to more than 9%:</strong> This threshold is too low and would lack specificity, potentially leading to false positives.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma Diagnosis','["asthma","diagnosis","spirometry","bronchodilator"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.729Z','2026-10-27T16:14:21.463Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8i007he9oi2sd8eo79','mcq','In management of asthma exacerbations the patient can be discharged to home if sustained improvement with normal physical findings and:','["SaO2 more than 92% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 80% of personal best","SaO2 more than 94% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 70% of personal best","SaO2 more than 94% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 80% of personal best","SaO2 more than 92% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 70% of personal best"]','SaO2 more than 92% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 80% of personal best','<p><strong>Why SaO2 more than 92% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 80% of personal best is correct:</strong> This discharge criterion represents the established standard for safe discharge of asthma exacerbation patients, as recommended by GINA guidelines. The SaO2 &gt;92% ensures adequate oxygenation and indicates resolution of hypoxemia that commonly accompanies moderate-to-severe asthma exacerbations. The PEF &gt;80% of personal best demonstrates significant improvement in airflow limitation and suggests that the patient has responded adequately to initial treatment. These thresholds together indicate that the patient has achieved sustained improvement with normal physical findings and is at low risk for rapid deterioration after discharge.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>SaO2 more than 94% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 70% of personal best:</strong> While the SaO2 threshold is slightly higher, the PEF threshold of 70% is too low and may indicate ongoing obstruction that could lead to deterioration after discharge.</li>
<li><strong>SaO2 more than 94% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 80% of personal best:</strong> The SaO2 threshold of 94% is unnecessarily high, as studies have shown that SaO2 &gt;92% is sufficient for safe discharge in most patients.</li>
<li><strong>SaO2 more than 92% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 70% of personal best:</strong> The PEF threshold of 70% is too low and may indicate ongoing obstruction that could lead to deterioration after discharge, even with adequate oxygenation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Discharge criteria should include not only objective measures (SaO2 and PEF) but also assessment of symptom control and ability to maintain activities of daily living.</li>
<li>Patients discharged home should have a clear action plan and adequate supply of medications, including short-acting beta-agonists and oral corticosteroids if needed.</li>
<li>Follow-up should be arranged within 1-2 weeks to ensure continued control and adjust treatment as needed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Asthma exacerbation patients can be safely discharged when they achieve SaO2 &gt;92% and PEF &gt;80% of personal best with sustained improvement and normal physical findings.</p>','<p>92/80 rule for discharge: SaO2 &gt;92% AND PEF &gt;80% of personal best for safe discharge of asthma exacerbation.</p>','<p>Even when discharge criteria are met, patients should be provided with a written asthma action plan and clear instructions on when to seek further medical attention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>SaO2 more than 94% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 70% of personal best:</strong> The PEF threshold of 70% is too low and may indicate ongoing obstruction that could lead to deterioration after discharge.</li>
<li><strong>SaO2 more than 94% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 80% of personal best:</strong> The SaO2 threshold of 94% is unnecessarily high, as studies have shown that SaO2 &gt;92% is sufficient for safe discharge in most patients.</li>
<li><strong>SaO2 more than 92% after 4 hours in room air; PEF more than 70% of personal best:</strong> The PEF threshold of 70% is too low and may indicate ongoing obstruction that could lead to deterioration after discharge.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma Management','["asthma","exacerbation","discharge","management"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.730Z','2026-10-27T16:14:21.465Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8m007me9oisjz3q9xc','mcq','regarding symptoms of bronchial asthma, what is false?','["itching"]','itching','<p><strong>Why itching is correct:</strong> Itching is not a typical symptom of bronchial asthma. The hallmark symptoms of asthma include wheezing, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and coughing. While itching can be associated with atopic conditions (like allergic rhinitis or eczema) that often coexist with asthma, it is not a direct symptom of the bronchial asthma itself. The false statement in this question is that itching is a symptom of bronchial asthma.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - This question only has one option provided, which is the correct answer (the false statement).</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Classic asthma symptoms include wheezing, dyspnea, chest tightness, and cough (particularly nocturnal or early morning). - Atopic symptoms like itching, rhinorrhea, and conjunctivitis suggest allergic asthma but are not direct bronchial symptoms. - The absence of wheezing does not rule out asthma, as severe asthma may present with silent chest.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Itching is not a symptom of bronchial asthma; the characteristic symptoms are respiratory in nature.</p>','','<p>Pruritus (itching) is more commonly associated with allergic conditions like atopic dermatitis or allergic rhinitis rather than asthma itself.</p>','','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma Symptoms','["asthma","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.734Z','2026-10-27T16:14:32.569Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8n007ne9oi12gtzxgz','mcq','regarding coughing of bronchial asthma, what is false?','["decreased at early morning"]','decreased at early morning','<p><strong>Why decreased at early morning is correct:</strong> Coughing in bronchial asthma characteristically worsens at early morning (typically between 2-4 AM) due to circadian variations in airway caliber, increased airway inflammation overnight, and reduced effectiveness of bronchodilators from the previous day''s dose. This nocturnal worsening is so characteristic that it''s considered a diagnostic feature of asthma. The false statement in this question is that coughing decreases at early morning in asthma.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - This question only has one option provided, which is the correct answer (the false statement).</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Nocturnal cough worsening is a hallmark of asthma and occurs in up to 75% of asthmatic patients. - Early morning cough is caused by circadian rhythms in airway resistance, inflammation, and parasympathetic tone. - The presence of nocturnal symptoms should prompt consideration of asthma diagnosis and review of controller medication adherence.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Asthma cough characteristically worsens, not decreases, in the early morning hours.</p>','<p>Night = Not Good for asthma (NNG)</p>','<p>Nocturnal asthma symptoms are strongly associated with more severe disease and should prompt evaluation for medication adherence and possible addition of long-acting bronchodilators.</p>','','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma Symptoms','["asthma","cough","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.735Z','2026-10-27T16:14:32.570Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8o007oe9oi351n54fk','mcq','which of the following is false regarding clinical features which increase the probability of bronchial asthma?','["clubbing"]','clubbing','<p><strong>Why clubbing is correct:</strong> Clubbing is a classic clinical sign that indicates chronic hypoxemia, most commonly associated with conditions like bronchiectasis, interstitial lung disease, or lung cancer. In bronchial asthma, which is characterized by reversible airway obstruction and inflammation without significant chronic hypoxemia, clubbing does not develop. The pathophysiology of asthma involves airway hyperresponsiveness and inflammation, but it does not lead to the chronic tissue hypoxia required for clubbing formation. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Wheezing:</strong> Wheezing is a hallmark feature of asthma caused by narrowed airways and turbulent airflow during expiration. - <strong>Cough:</strong> Cough, particularly nocturnal or exercise-induced, is a common symptom of asthma due to airway inflammation and hyperreactivity. - <strong>Chest tightness:</strong> Chest tightness results from bronchoconstriction and airway inflammation, which are central to asthma pathophysiology. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Clubbing is a red flag for alternative diagnoses like bronchiectasis, ILD, or malignancy when present in a patient with suspected asthma. - Asthma is characterized by reversible airflow obstruction, while clubbing suggests irreversible structural lung disease. - The absence of clubbing helps differentiate asthma from chronic suppurative lung diseases. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Clubbing is false as a feature of asthma because it indicates chronic hypoxemia from irreversible lung disease, unlike asthma''s reversible airway obstruction.</p>','<p>A-C-T: Asthma has no Clubbing, has Cough/Tightness</p>','<p>Clubbing should prompt investigation for alternative diagnoses when present in a patient with suspected asthma, as it suggests irreversible structural lung disease rather than reversible airway obstruction.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Wheezing:</strong> A classic asthma symptom caused by narrowed airways. - <strong>Cough:</strong> A common manifestation of airway inflammation in asthma. - <strong>Chest tightness:</strong> Results from bronchoconstriction central to asthma pathophysiology.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Asthma Diagnosis','["asthma","clinical features","clubbing"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.736Z','2026-10-27T16:14:37.771Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8p007qe9oibl7oj3ep','mcq','Controller therapy of bronchial asthma includes all of the following except:','["Inhaled corticosteroids","Leukotriene receptor antagonist","Antibiotics","Biologics"]','Antibiotics','<p><strong>Why Antibiotics is correct:</strong> Antibiotics are not part of controller therapy for bronchial asthma as they target bacterial infections, not the underlying inflammatory pathways of asthma. Controller therapies work by modulating the chronic inflammatory process in asthma through anti-inflammatory effects (inhaled corticosteroids), leukotriene pathway inhibition (leukotriene receptor antagonists), or specific biologic targets (monoclonal antibodies against IgE, IL-5, etc.). Antibiotics may be used only when asthma exacerbations are complicated by bacterial infections, but this is not controller therapy. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Inhaled corticosteroids:</strong> These are first-line controller therapy that reduce airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness. - <strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonist:</strong> These (like montelukast) are controller medications that block inflammatory leukotrienes. - <strong>Biologics:</strong> These are advanced controller therapies targeting specific inflammatory pathways in severe asthma. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Controller medications are taken daily to maintain long-term control of asthma symptoms and prevent exacerbations. - Reliever medications (like short-acting beta-agonists) are used for acute symptom relief, not for long-term control. - Antibiotics have no role in asthma management unless there''s a confirmed bacterial infection. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Antibiotics are not controller therapy for asthma because they target bacteria, not the underlying inflammatory pathways of asthma.</p>','<p>A-B-C: Asthma Controller = Biologics, Corticosteroids, Leukotriene modifiers (not Antibiotics)</p>','<p>Inappropriate antibiotic use in asthma is common but ineffective; controller therapies must target the underlying inflammatory process, not infection.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids:</strong> First-line controller therapy reducing airway inflammation. - <strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonist:</strong> Controller medication blocking inflammatory leukotrienes. - <strong>Biologics:</strong> Advanced controller therapies targeting specific inflammatory pathways.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Respiratory','Bronchial Asthma','["asthma","controller","therapy","antibiotics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.737Z','2026-10-27T16:14:37.772Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8r007re9oi0tw753w5','mcq','Which one is not a controller therapy','["Corticosteroids","Antibiotics","Beta Agonists"]','Antibiotics','<p><strong>Why Antibiotics is correct:</strong> Controller therapies for asthma are medications taken daily to maintain long-term control and reduce inflammation, such as inhaled corticosteroids. Antibiotics are not controller therapies because they target bacterial infections, not the underlying inflammatory process of asthma. Beta agonists (specifically long-acting beta agonists - LABAs) are controller medications when combined with inhaled corticosteroids, as they provide sustained bronchodilation. Antibiotics have no role in managing asthma unless there''s a concurrent bacterial infection. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Corticosteroids:</strong> These are the cornerstone of asthma controller therapy, reducing airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness when used daily. - <strong>Beta Agonists:</strong> Long-acting beta agonists (LABAs) are controller medications that provide sustained bronchodilation when used regularly, typically in combination with inhaled corticosteroids. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Controller medications are taken daily for long-term asthma control, while reliever medications (like short-acting beta agonists) are used as needed for acute symptoms. Inhaled corticosteroids are the most effective controller medication for persistent asthma. The stepwise approach to asthma treatment involves increasing controller medications as symptoms worsen. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Controller therapies target airway inflammation for long-term asthma control, while antibiotics treat infections and have no role in asthma management unless infection is present.</p>','','<p>Asthma controller medications should be used daily regardless of symptom presence, while reliever medications should only be used as needed for breakthrough symptoms.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Corticosteroids:</strong> These are the primary controller medications for asthma, reducing airway inflammation. - <strong>Beta Agonists:</strong> Long-acting beta agonists are controller medications when used regularly for maintenance therapy.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma Management','["asthma","controller therapy","pharmacology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.739Z','2026-10-27T16:14:46.123Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8s007se9oiv0u1z8q1','mcq','Chest in bronchial asthma','["Ribbon shape","Egg on side","Boot shape","Snowman shape"]','Snowman shape','<p><strong>Why Snowman shape is correct:</strong> The snowman shape is the classic chest radiograph appearance in a patient with total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR), a congenital heart condition, not bronchial asthma. In asthma, the chest radiograph is typically normal or may show hyperinflation with flattened diaphragms and increased anterior-posterior diameter, but not specific shapes. The ribbon shape is seen in pulmonary edema, the egg-on-side appearance is characteristic of a large pleural effusion, and the boot shape is classic for tetralogy of Fallot. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Ribbon shape:</strong> This describes the appearance of pulmonary vessels in pulmonary edema, not asthma. - <strong>Egg on side:</strong> This appearance is seen with a large pleural effusion causing the lung to appear as an egg on its side. - <strong>Boot shape:</strong> This is the classic cardiac silhouette in tetralogy of Fallot, characterized by an upturned cardiac apex. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Asthma chest radiographs are typically normal or show hyperinflation signs but no specific shape. Total anomalous pulmonary venous return presents with a snowman appearance on chest X-ray. The boot shape is pathognomonic for tetralogy of Fallot. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The snowman shape on chest X-ray is diagnostic of total anomalous pulmonary venous return, not asthma, which typically shows normal or hyperinflated lungs without specific shape patterns.</p>','','<p>A normal chest radiograph in a patient with acute asthma actually helps confirm the diagnosis, as it rules out alternative explanations for respiratory symptoms.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Ribbon shape:</strong> Seen in pulmonary edema, not asthma. - <strong>Egg on side:</strong> Characteristic of large pleural effusion. - <strong>Boot shape:</strong> Classic for tetralogy of Fallot.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma Physical Exam','["asthma","chest x-ray","physical findings"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.740Z','2026-10-27T16:14:46.125Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8t007te9oi1mwuycgr','mcq','Daily symptoms of asthma, ...','["SABA","Leukotriene"]','Leukotriene','<p><strong>Why Leukotriene is correct:</strong> Leukotriene modifiers (such as montelukast) are controller medications for asthma that are taken daily to manage persistent asthma symptoms. They work by blocking the leukotriene pathway, which is a key inflammatory mediator in asthma pathophysiology. Short-acting beta agonists (SABAs) like albuterol are reliever medications used as needed for acute asthma symptoms, not daily controllers. The question specifically asks about daily symptoms, which require controller therapy rather than as-needed reliever medication. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>SABA:</strong> Short-acting beta agonists are reliever medications used as needed for acute asthma symptoms, not daily controllers for persistent symptoms. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> Daily asthma symptoms indicate the need for controller medication rather than as-needed reliever therapy. Leukotriene modifiers are oral controller medications that can be used as an alternative or adjunct to inhaled corticosteroids. The stepwise approach to asthma treatment involves adding controller medications when symptoms occur more than twice weekly. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Daily asthma symptoms require controller therapy like leukotriene modifiers, while short-acting beta agonists are only for as-needed relief of acute symptoms.</p>','<p>Daily Controller = Leukotriene (L), Reliever = SABA (S) - remember L comes before S in the alphabet for daily vs as-needed.</p>','<p>Leukotriene modifiers are particularly useful in patients with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and those with exercise-induced asthma.</p>','','Basic','Respiratory','Asthma Management','["asthma","daily symptoms","controller therapy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',1,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.741Z','2026-10-27T16:14:46.126Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8u007ue9oioq9trp3m','mcq','Character of urine when there is protein','["Frothy urine","Straw red urine"]','Frothy urine','<p><strong>Why Frothy urine is correct:</strong> Frothy urine is the classic clinical manifestation of significant proteinuria, which occurs when the glomerular filtration barrier becomes damaged, allowing proteins (primarily albumin) to leak into the urine. The foaming occurs because proteins reduce the surface tension of urine, creating stable bubbles that persist longer than in normal urine. This is typically observed in conditions like nephrotic syndrome, glomerulonephritis, or diabetic kidney disease where protein excretion exceeds 3.5 g/day. The frothy appearance is most noticeable after urination and may be more prominent with concentrated urine.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Straw red urine:</strong> This describes hematuria, which indicates the presence of red blood cells in urine, not protein. It''s associated with glomerular bleeding, kidney stones, or urinary tract infections, not proteinuria.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Proteinuria &gt;3.5g/day is diagnostic of nephrotic syndrome, characterized by hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia.</li>
<li>Dipstick testing for protein detects albumin specifically; Bence Jones protein (light chains) requires special testing.</li>
<li>Orthostatic proteinuria is a benign condition where protein appears only during upright activity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Frothy urine indicates proteinuria due to reduced surface tension from leaked proteins, while straw red urine indicates hematuria from red blood cells in the urine.</p>','<p>Frothy = Protein (F-P)</p>','<p>The degree of proteinuria correlates with kidney damage severity; nephrotic-range proteinuria (&gt;3.5g/day) carries significant risk for thromboembolism and progressive renal failure.</p>','','Easy','Renal','Urinalysis','["proteinuria","urinalysis","kidney disease"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.742Z','2026-10-27T16:14:53.355Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8v007we9oiqcwo3gkc','mcq','What is the management for a child with recurrent urinary tract infection (UTI) and vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)?','["Antibiotic prophylaxis is essential","Management is still controversial","Surgical correction of VUR is indicated","Continuous bladder catheterization is required"]','Management is still controversial','<p><strong>Why Management is still controversial is correct:</strong> The management of children with recurrent UTI and vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) remains controversial due to conflicting evidence from multiple studies regarding the effectiveness of different approaches. Current guidelines acknowledge that while antibiotic prophylaxis may reduce recurrent UTIs, it doesn''t consistently prevent renal scarring, and surgical intervention (ureteral reimplantation) has its own risks without clear superiority over conservative management. The decision must be individualized based on the child''s age, VUR grade, frequency of infections, and parental preferences, with many centers now adopting a more selective approach rather than universal intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Antibiotic prophylaxis is essential:</strong> While previously standard, recent studies show limited benefit in preventing renal scarring, leading to more selective use.</li>
<li><strong>Surgical correction of VUR is indicated:</strong> Surgery is typically reserved for high-grade reflux (IV-V) that persists despite conservative measures, not for all cases.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous bladder catheterization is required:</strong> This is never standard management for VUR and would significantly increase infection risk and cause significant morbidity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>VUR grading (I-V) correlates with spontaneous resolution rates and risk of renal damage.</li>
<li>Current guidelines recommend against routine antibiotic prophylaxis for all children with VUR.</li>
<li>Regular surveillance with ultrasound and DMSA scans is more important than intervention decisions for most children.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Management of recurrent UTI with VUR is individualized and controversial, with current evidence supporting selective rather than universal intervention approaches.</p>','<p>VUR management: Varies (V)</p>','<p>Most children with low-grade VUR (I-III) will resolve spontaneously without intervention, with careful monitoring being the most appropriate approach for many.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Antibiotic prophylaxis is essential:</strong> Recent studies show limited benefit in preventing renal scarring.</li>
<li><strong>Surgical correction of VUR is indicated:</strong> Surgery is reserved for high-grade persistent reflux, not all cases.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous bladder catheterization is required:</strong> This would increase infection risk and is never standard management.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Vesicoureteral Reflux','["vur","uti","management","controversy"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.743Z','2026-10-27T16:14:53.358Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8x007ze9oigxpp9ck5','mcq','On routine checkup of three years old boy, his general urine examination show: nitrite test positive, you think this is false-positive result. What is the MOST likely cause?','["Frequent voiding","Low urine bacterial count","Urinary tract obstruction","Prolonged contact (uncircumcised boys)"]','Prolonged contact (uncircumcised boys)','<p><strong>Why Prolonged contact (uncircumcised boys) is correct:</strong> The nitrite test detects bacterial reduction of dietary nitrates to nitrites, which requires sufficient contact time between bacteria and nitrates in urine. In uncircumcised boys, the foreskin can trap urine, prolonging contact time and allowing even small numbers of bacteria to produce detectable nitrites, leading to false-positive results. This is particularly common in boys with phimosis or inadequate hygiene, where urine remains in contact with the urethral mucosa longer than in circumcised individuals.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Frequent voiding:</strong> This would actually decrease the likelihood of a positive nitrite test by reducing contact time between bacteria and nitrates.</li>
<li><strong>Low urine bacterial count:</strong> The nitrite test requires a significant bacterial count (&gt;10^5 CFU/mL) to produce detectable nitrites, so low counts typically yield negative results.</li>
<li><strong>Urinary tract obstruction:</strong> While obstruction can lead to UTIs, it doesn''t specifically cause prolonged contact time needed for false-positive nitrite tests; instead, it might lead to false-negative results due to dilution.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The nitrite test has high specificity but low sensitivity for UTI, as only certain bacteria (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus) can convert nitrates to nitrites.</li>
<li>False positives are more common in uncircumcised boys due to prolonged urine contact with urethral mucosa.</li>
<li>A positive nitrite test in symptomatic patients strongly suggests UTI, but a negative test doesn''t rule it out.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In uncircumcised boys, prolonged urine contact due to the foreskin trapping urine is the most common cause of false-positive nitrite tests.</p>','<p>Foresin traps, false nitrites</p>','<p>The nitrite test''s false-positive rate is significantly higher in uncircumcised males due to prolonged urine contact with urethral mucosa.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Frequent voiding:</strong> Reduces contact time between bacteria and nitrates, making false positives less likely.</li>
<li><strong>Low urine bacterial count:</strong> Insufficient bacteria to produce detectable nitrites, typically yielding negative results.</li>
<li><strong>Urinary tract obstruction:</strong> May cause false negatives due to urine dilution, not false positives.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Urinary tract infection','["urine","nitrite","pediatrics","false-positive"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.745Z','2026-10-27T16:15:02.686Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8x007ye9oirsg1jv82','mcq','14- 11 months old boy with recurrent UTI. Renal ultrasound showed reflux at upper collecting system without dilatation. Which of the following statements is best describe this boy?','["The majority of this grade of reflux will resolve on their own without therapy","This reflux is considered grade 5 which is considered benign","It is not associated with urinary tract infections","It is associated with deletion of the 22q11 chromosome"]','The majority of this grade of reflux will resolve on their own without therapy','<p><strong>Why The majority of this grade of reflux will resolve on their own without therapy is correct:</strong> The described reflux (at upper collecting system without dilatation) corresponds to grade III vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) in the International Reflux Study classification. Studies show that approximately 60-80% of grade III VUR cases resolve spontaneously by age 5-6 years, particularly when there''s no evidence of renal scarring on imaging. The natural history of VUR is characterized by gradual resolution as the ureterovesical junction matures, with spontaneous resolution rates inversely proportional to the grade of reflux.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>This reflux is considered grade 5 which is considered benign:</strong> Grade V reflux is the most severe, with marked dilatation and tortuosity of the ureter, and is associated with significant renal damage risk, not benign.</li>
<li><strong>It is not associated with urinary tract infections:</strong> VUR is a major risk factor for recurrent UTIs, which can lead to renal scarring if not properly managed.</li>
<li><strong>It is associated with deletion of the 22q11 chromosome:</strong> This association is with DiGeorge syndrome, which may involve congenital heart defects but not specifically with VUR.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The grade of VUR is determined by the extent of reflux and degree of hydronephrosis, with grade I being mildest and grade V most severe.</li>
<li>Spontaneous resolution rates are highest for lower grades (I-III) and lowest for higher grades (IV-V).</li>
<li>Children with VUR and recurrent UTIs may benefit from prophylactic antibiotics until reflux resolves.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Grade III vesicoureteral reflux (reflux to upper collecting system without dilatation) has a high spontaneous resolution rate and typically doesn''t require aggressive intervention.</p>','<p>Grade III, high chance to be free</p>','<p>Most cases of low-to-moderate grade vesicoureteral reflux resolve spontaneously as the child grows, with intervention typically reserved for high-grade reflux or cases with renal scarring.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>This reflux is considered grade 5 which is considered benign:</strong> Grade V is the most severe form of reflux, associated with significant renal damage risk.</li>
<li><strong>It is not associated with urinary tract infections:</strong> VUR is a major risk factor for recurrent UTIs that can lead to renal scarring.</li>
<li><strong>It is associated with deletion of the 22q11 chromosome:</strong> This association is with DiGeorge syndrome, not specifically with VUR.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Vesicoureteral Reflux','["uti","reflux","pediatrics","ultrasound"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.745Z','2026-10-27T16:15:02.687Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu8z0082e9oiyclozewq','mcq','Which of the following is the major complication of vesicoureteral reflux','["Renal vein thrombosis","Renal scaring","Renal stones","Pyelonephritis"]','Renal scaring','<p><strong>Why Renal scaring is correct:</strong> Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) allows urine to flow backward from the bladder into the ureters and kidneys, exposing renal tissue to higher pressures and recurrent infections. This chronic exposure leads to inflammation and subsequent fibrosis, resulting in renal scarring (reflux nephropathy). The scarring process involves activation of fibroblasts, deposition of collagen, and progressive destruction of functional nephrons, which can ultimately lead to hypertension and chronic kidney disease.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Renal vein thrombosis:</strong> This is not a direct complication of VUR but rather a condition that can occur in nephrotic syndrome or dehydration.</li>
<li><strong>Renal stones:</strong> While recurrent UTIs can contribute to stone formation, this is not the major complication of VUR.</li>
<li><strong>Pyelonephritis:</strong> Although VUR increases the risk of pyelonephritis, it is an acute complication rather than the chronic, progressive damage that defines the major complication.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>VUR is graded from I (mild) to V (severe), with higher grades associated with increased risk of scarring.</li>
<li>The risk of renal scarring is highest in young children with febrile UTIs and high-grade reflux.</li>
<li>Renal scarring is best detected by DMSA renal scan, which shows cortical defects.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Vesicoureteral reflux causes renal scarring through chronic inflammation and fibrosis from recurrent infections and high-pressure urine reflux.</p>','<p>VUR → Scar (Vesicoureteral Reflux leads to Scarring)</p>','<p>Early diagnosis and treatment of VUR in children with UTIs can prevent renal scarring and its long-term consequences.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Renal vein thrombosis:</strong> Not a direct complication of VUR but occurs in other conditions like nephrotic syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Renal stones:</strong> A possible consequence but not the major complication of VUR.</li>
<li><strong>Pyelonephritis:</strong> An acute complication of VUR rather than the chronic scarring that defines its major complication.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Urology','Vesicoureteral reflux','["vesicoureteral reflux","complications","renal scarring"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.747Z','2026-10-27T16:15:18.014Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu910085e9oi3vzdqxjd','mcq','Which of the following findings in a child''s urine is most specific for urinary tract infection?','["Nitrite","Leukocytes","Protein","Red blood cell"]','Nitrite','<p><strong>Why Nitrite is correct:</strong> Nitrite is the most specific finding for urinary tract infection (UTI) because it results from the conversion of dietary nitrates to nitrites by Gram-negative bacteria, particularly Escherichia coli, which is responsible for 80-90% of uncomplicated UTIs. This enzymatic conversion occurs when bacteria are present in sufficient numbers and for a sufficient duration in the urine. The nitrite test (part of urinalysis) has high specificity (92-100%) for UTI, though sensitivity is lower (35-85%) because not all UTIs are caused by nitrite-producing organisms.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Leukocytes:</strong> While leukocytes (pyuria) are commonly present in UTI, they are not specific as they can be found in any inflammatory condition affecting the urinary tract.</li>
<li><strong>Protein:</strong> Proteinuria can occur in UTI but is non-specific and can be seen in many renal conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Red blood cell:</strong> Hematuria may be present in UTI but is also seen in many other conditions like stones, glomerulonephritis, or trauma.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The nitrite test is most accurate when urine has been in the bladder for at least 4 hours, allowing sufficient time for bacterial conversion.</li>
<li>A positive nitrite test has higher predictive value for UTI than leukocyte esterase alone.</li>
<li>False negatives can occur with low bacterial counts, recent urination, or infections caused by non-nitrite-producing organisms.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Nitrite is the most specific urinalysis finding for UTI as it directly indicates bacterial presence and activity in the urine.</p>','<p>Nitrite = Bacterial Bite (Nitrite indicates bacterial presence in UTI)</p>','<p>A positive nitrite test on urinalysis is highly specific for UTI and should prompt treatment, especially in symptomatic patients.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Leukocytes:</strong> Non-specific as they indicate inflammation but not necessarily infection.</li>
<li><strong>Protein:</strong> Non-specific finding that can occur in many renal conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Red blood cell:</strong> Non-specific finding seen in many conditions besides UTI.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Urology','Urinary tract infection','["urinary tract infection","urine analysis","pediatrics","nitrite"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.749Z','2026-10-27T16:15:18.016Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu95008de9oixyz8ocil','mcq','Which characteristic is more likely with acute pyelonephritis than with a lower UTI?','["Fever","Urgency","Dysuria","Frequency"]','Fever','<p><strong>Why Fever is correct:</strong> Fever is a systemic inflammatory response that indicates the infection has reached the renal parenchyma and triggered a robust immune response. In acute pyelonephritis, bacteria invade the renal tissue, causing inflammation and tissue damage, which leads to the release of pyrogens that act on the hypothalamus to raise body temperature. This systemic response is typically absent in lower urinary tract infections (cystitis), which are generally confined to the bladder mucosa and cause localized symptoms without significant systemic involvement. The presence of fever (usually &gt;38°C) is therefore a key distinguishing feature between upper and lower urinary tract infections.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Urgency:</strong> This is a common symptom of both lower UTIs (cystitis) and pyelonephritis, as it results from bladder inflammation and irritation.</li>
<li><strong>Dysuria:</strong> This is a hallmark symptom of cystitis and can also occur in pyelonephritis, making it non-specific for distinguishing between the two conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Frequency:</strong> This is another common symptom of both lower UTIs and pyelonephritis, resulting from bladder irritation and inflammation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fever (&gt;38°C) is the most specific symptom distinguishing pyelonephritis from lower UTIs.</li>
<li>Other supportive features of pyelonephritis include flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness, and leukocytosis.</li>
<li>In children, pyelonephritis may present with nonspecific symptoms such as irritability, poor feeding, or failure to thrive, making diagnosis more challenging.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Fever is the most reliable distinguishing feature between acute pyelonephritis (upper UTI) and lower UTIs, as it indicates renal parenchymal involvement.</p>','<p>Fever = Finding in Pyelonephritis (remember: ''Fever'' distinguishes upper from lower UTI).</p>','<p>When evaluating a patient with suspected UTI, the presence of fever should prompt consideration for pyelonephritis and the need for more aggressive treatment and imaging to assess for complications.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Urgency:</strong> This occurs in both lower and upper UTIs, making it non-specific.</li>
<li><strong>Dysuria:</strong> This is a hallmark symptom of cystitis and can also occur in pyelonephritis.</li>
<li><strong>Frequency:</strong> This is common to both lower UTIs and pyelonephritis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Urology','Urinary Tract Infection','["pyelonephritis","uti","symptoms"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.753Z','2026-10-27T16:15:43.727Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu96008ge9oi39w6r1bw','mcq','Which test is most specific for renal function?','["Renal scan","Serum creatinine","Creatinine clearance","Blood urea nitrogen (BUN)"]','Creatinine clearance','<p><strong>Why Creatinine clearance is correct:</strong> Creatinine clearance is the most specific test for renal function because it directly measures the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) by quantifying how much creatinine is cleared from the blood by the kidneys per unit of time. It accounts for both the kidneys'' filtering capacity and the volume of urine produced, providing a more accurate assessment of overall renal function than isolated serum measurements. This test specifically evaluates the glomerular filtration mechanism, which is the primary function of the kidneys in terms of waste removal.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Renal scan:</strong> While renal scans provide functional information about kidney blood flow and excretion, they are not as specific for glomerular filtration rate as creatinine clearance and can be influenced by factors other than pure renal function.</li>
<li><strong>Serum creatinine:</strong> This is a poor measure of renal function because it''s influenced by factors like muscle mass, age, gender, and diet, not just the glomerular filtration rate.</li>
<li><strong>Blood urea nitrogen (BUN):</strong> BUN is affected by numerous non-renal factors including hydration status, protein intake, gastrointestinal bleeding, and catabolic states, making it less specific for renal function assessment.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Creatinine clearance is calculated using the formula: (Urine creatinine × Urine volume) / Serum creatinine, with results typically normalized to 1.73m² body surface area.</li>
<li>Normal creatinine clearance is approximately 90-120 mL/min/1.73m² in adults and varies by age in children.</li>
<li>A 24-hour urine collection is the gold standard for creatinine clearance, though timed shorter collections can be used with appropriate adjustments.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Creatinine clearance is the most specific renal function test because it directly measures glomerular filtration rate while accounting for urine production volume.</p>','<p>CC for Creatinine Clearance = Clearest measure of renal function</p>','<p>In early renal disease, serum creatinine may remain normal until significant GFR loss has occurred (up to 50% reduction), making creatinine clearance a more sensitive early indicator.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Renal scan:</strong> Provides functional information but is less specific for GFR than creatinine clearance.</li>
<li><strong>Serum creatinine:</strong> Influenced by non-renal factors like muscle mass and diet.</li>
<li><strong>Blood urea nitrogen (BUN):</strong> Affected by hydration status, protein intake, and non-renal factors.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nephrology','Renal Function','["renal function","tests","creatinine"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.754Z','2026-10-27T16:15:54.715Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu97008he9oilvh2zgqw','mcq','7 years old girl with suspected UTI, Which urinalysis results most likely indicate this condition?','["Yellow; protein 6 mg/dL; pH 6.8; 102/mL bacteria","Cloudy, yellow; WBC >5/hpf; pH 8.2; numerous casts","Cloudy, brown; ammonia odor; specific gravity 1.030; RBC 3/hpf","Clear; colorless; glucose: trace; ketones: trace; osmolality 500 mOsm/kg (500 mmol/kg)"]','Cloudy, yellow; WBC >5/hpf; pH 8.2; numerous casts','<p><strong>Why Cloudy, yellow; WBC &gt;5/hpf; pH 8.2; numerous casts is correct:</strong> This urinalysis presentation is most consistent with a urinary tract infection (UTI) because the cloudy appearance indicates pyuria (presence of white blood cells), WBC &gt;5/hpf is a definitive laboratory criterion for UTI, and the presence of casts indicates inflammation within the renal tubules. The alkaline pH (8.2) is characteristic of UTI due to the action of urease-producing bacteria like Proteus species, which break down urea into ammonia, raising urine pH. The combination of these findings strongly suggests an active infection with inflammatory response in the urinary tract.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Yellow; protein 6 mg/dL; pH 6.8; 102/mL bacteria:</strong> While the presence of bacteria suggests infection, the normal pH and lack of pyuria or casts make this less specific for UTI; protein at this level is mild and non-specific.</li>
<li><strong>Cloudy, brown; ammonia odor; specific gravity 1.030; RBC 3/hpf:</strong> The brown color and ammonia odor suggest UTI with urease-producing bacteria, but the absence of WBCs and casts makes this less diagnostic; RBCs suggest hemorrhagic cystitis rather than typical UTI.</li>
<li><strong>Clear; colorless; glucose: trace; ketones: trace; osmolality 500 mOsm/kg:</strong> This presentation is normal for a child''s urine except for trace glucose and ketones, which might suggest early dehydration but not UTI; the absence of cloudy appearance, WBCs, bacteria, or casts rules out infection.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The definitive urinalysis criteria for UTI in children are pyuria (WBCs &gt;5/hpf) and bacteriuria (&gt;10^5 CFU/mL), with either finding being diagnostic when symptoms are present.</li>
<li>Casts in urine indicate renal tubular involvement and are typically seen in pyelonephritis, while cystitis usually shows only pyuria without casts.</li>
<li>Alkaline urine pH (&gt;7.0) in UTI suggests infection with urease-producing organisms like Proteus mirabilis, which can lead to struvite stone formation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> A UTI in children is best indicated by cloudy urine with WBCs &gt;5/hpf and casts, with alkaline pH suggesting urease-producing bacteria.</p>','<p>UTI = Urine Turbid, Infection (WBCs), pH alkaline with casts</p>','<p>In children, UTI with pyelonephritis should be suspected when urinalysis shows WBC casts, indicating renal parenchymal involvement beyond just bladder infection.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Yellow; protein 6 mg/dL; pH 6.8; 102/mL bacteria:</strong> Lacks pyuria and casts, making it less specific for UTI.</li>
<li><strong>Cloudy, brown; ammonia odor; specific gravity 1.030; RBC 3/hpf:</strong> Absence of WBCs and casts makes this less diagnostic for UTI.</li>
<li><strong>Clear; colorless; glucose: trace; ketones: trace; osmolality 500 mOsm/kg:</strong> Normal appearance without evidence of infection.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Urinary Tract Infection','["uti","urinalysis","wbc"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.755Z','2026-10-27T16:15:54.718Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu920086e9oipgtzra5k','mcq','[Age truncated in source] infant with history of recurrent urinary tract infections, abdominal US show bilateral hydronephrosis and hydroureter. VCUG show bilateral reflux (VUR) grade 4. What is the risk to have VUR in other siblings?','["80%","20%","50%","40%"]','40%','<p><strong>Why 40% is correct:</strong> Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) has a strong genetic component with an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern. When a child has bilateral high-grade reflux (grade 4), the risk to siblings is approximately 40% due to the likelihood of inheriting the underlying connective tissue or ureteral valve abnormality that predisposes to reflux. This risk is significantly higher than the general population prevalence of 1-2% but lower than the 50% risk seen when one parent has VUR, reflecting the complex inheritance pattern involving multiple genes and possible environmental factors. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>80%:</strong> This risk is too high and would be more characteristic when both parents have VUR, which is uncommon. - <strong>20%:</strong> This risk is too low and would be more representative of the risk when a sibling has unilateral low-grade reflux. - <strong>50%:</strong> This risk would apply if one parent had VUR, not when a sibling has bilateral high-grade reflux. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - VUR has a 27-50% recurrence risk in siblings when one sibling has VUR, with higher risk for bilateral or high-grade reflux. - The risk increases to approximately 50% if one parent also has VUR. - Screening siblings of children with VUR is recommended, particularly when the index case has bilateral or high-grade reflux. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Bilateral high-grade VUR in a child confers a 40% risk to siblings due to autosomal dominant inheritance patterns.</p>','<p>VUR sibling risk: 40% (bilateral high-grade), 50% (parent affected), 27% (unaffected sibling)</p>','<p>Early screening with VCUG or nuclear cystography is recommended for siblings of children with VUR, especially when the index case has bilateral or high-grade reflux.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>80%:</strong> Too high for sibling risk; would require both parents having VUR. - <strong>20%:</strong> Too low; represents risk for unilateral low-grade reflux. - <strong>50%:</strong> Correct only when one parent has VUR, not just a sibling.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Vesicoureteral reflux','["vesicoureteral reflux","pediatrics","heredity","siblings"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.750Z','2026-10-27T16:15:23.399Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu930088e9oiegm8x94n','mcq','During routine screening of a healthy preschool boy, klebsiella bacteriuria was found and no other findings. Which one of the following therapy is recommended for TTT?','["Amoxicillin","Cefexime","Cefotaxim","No TTT"]','No TTT','<p><strong>Why No TTT is correct:</strong> Asymptomatic bacteriuria in a healthy preschool boy with Klebsiella does not require treatment because it represents colonization rather than true infection, and treating it would not provide clinical benefit while potentially promoting antibiotic resistance. The American Academy of Pediatrics guidelines specifically state that asymptomatic bacteriuria should not be treated in children, as it does not lead to renal damage and treatment does not prevent future infections. This is particularly true in boys, where asymptomatic bacteriuria is even more benign than in girls. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Amoxicillin:</strong> This antibiotic would be inappropriate to use for asymptomatic bacteriuria as it would not provide clinical benefit and would unnecessarily expose the child to antibiotics. - <strong>Cefexime:</strong> This third-generation cephalosporin would be excessive for asymptomatic bacteriuria and could contribute to antibiotic resistance. - <strong>Cefotaxim:</strong> This third-generation cephalosporin would also be inappropriate for asymptomatic bacteriuria as it represents overtreatment. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Asymptomatic bacteriuria should not be treated in healthy children regardless of the organism isolated. - Treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria does not prevent future symptomatic UTIs or renal scarring. - Unnecessary antibiotic treatment for asymptomatic bacteriuria promotes antibiotic resistance and has no proven clinical benefit. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Asymptomatic bacteriuria in healthy children requires no treatment to avoid unnecessary antibiotic exposure and resistance development.</p>','<p>ABT = Asymptomatic Bacteriuria = No Treatment</p>','<p>Asymptomatic bacteriuria is a colonization state, not an infection, and treating it does not improve outcomes but may harm by promoting antibiotic resistance.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Amoxicillin:</strong> Inappropriate for asymptomatic bacteriuria as it provides no clinical benefit. - <strong>Cefexime:</strong> Excessive treatment that could promote antibiotic resistance. - <strong>Cefotaxim:</strong> Unnecessary third-generation cephalosporin for asymptomatic colonization.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Urinary Tract Infection','["uti","screening","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.751Z','2026-10-27T16:15:23.401Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu930089e9oiw80hogzd','mcq','VCUG is considered in the following conditions except:','["Recurrent UTI","U/S showing HU and HN","Renal scarring detected by DMSA scan","Perinephric abscess"]','Perinephric abscess','<p><strong>Why Perinephric abscess is correct:</strong> VCUG (Voiding Cystourethrogram) is specifically indicated for evaluating vesicoureteral reflux (VUR), which is a risk factor for recurrent UTIs and renal damage. A perinephric abscess is a complication of pyelonephritis but does not require VCUG evaluation as it represents an established parenchymal infection rather than a structural anomaly that predisposes to reflux. The abscess itself is not a condition that would prompt investigation for VUR.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrent UTI:</strong> Recurrent UTIs in children warrant VCUG evaluation to identify underlying VUR, which is a common predisposing factor.</li>
<li><strong>U/S showing HU and HN:</strong> Hydroureter (HU) and hydronephrosis (HN) on ultrasound suggest possible obstruction or reflux, necessitating VCUG to differentiate between these conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Renal scarring detected by DMSA scan:</strong> Renal scarring indicates prior pyelonephritis, and VCUG is indicated to identify associated VUR that may have caused the damage.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>VCUG is the gold standard for diagnosing VUR, which is present in 30-50% of children with febrile UTIs.</li>
<li>The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends VCUG or radionuclide cystogram after first febrile UTI in children under 2 years with abnormal renal ultrasound.</li>
<li>Perinephric abscess requires drainage and antibiotics, not VCUG evaluation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> VCUG evaluates for structural causes of UTIs (like VUR), not complications of established infections like abscesses.</p>','','<p>A perinephric abscess typically presents with fever, flank pain, and leukocytosis, requiring drainage and antibiotics, not VCUG evaluation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Recurrent UTI:</strong> VCUG is indicated to evaluate for underlying VUR as a cause. - <strong>U/S showing HU and HN:</strong> These findings suggest possible reflux or obstruction requiring VCUG. - <strong>Renal scarring detected by DMSA scan:</strong> Scarring indicates prior pyelonephritis and warrants evaluation for VUR.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Urinary Tract Infection','["vcug","imaging","uti"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.751Z','2026-10-27T16:15:35.596Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu94008be9oixk1zu0w2','mcq','UTI is more common in males during','["1st year","Preschool age","Adolescence","School age"]','1st year','<p><strong>Why 1st year is correct:</strong> UTIs are more common in males during the first year of life due to anatomical and physiological factors. In male infants, the foreskin may harbor bacteria, and the urethra is relatively short compared to older males. Additionally, male infants are more likely to have congenital abnormalities of the urinary tract that predispose to infection. After the first year, circumcision status and anatomical maturation reduce the incidence in males.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Preschool age:</strong> During preschool age (2-5 years), UTIs become more common in females due to their shorter urethra and proximity of the urethral meatus to the anus.</li>
<li><strong>Adolescence:</strong> In adolescence, UTIs remain more common in females due to sexual activity and other anatomical factors.</li>
<li><strong>School age:</strong> School-age children continue to have higher UTI rates in females, with males having significantly lower incidence unless structural abnormalities exist.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Uncircumcised male infants have a 10-fold higher risk of UTI compared to circumcised males.</li>
<li>The male:female UTI ratio is approximately 1:1 in the first year of life but becomes 1:10 after age 1.</li>
<li>Febrile UTIs in male infants &lt;3 months require evaluation for urinary tract abnormalities.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> UTIs are more common in males only during infancy due to anatomical differences and congenital anomalies.</p>','<p>Males UTI high only when very young (MYVY)</p>','<p>In male infants with fever, UTI should be considered even without urinary symptoms, as presentation can be nonspecific.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Preschool age:</strong> Females have higher UTI rates due to shorter urethra. - <strong>Adolescence:</strong> Females have higher rates due to sexual activity and anatomy. - <strong>School age:</strong> Females continue to have higher UTI rates.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Urology','Urinary Tract Infection','["uti","gender","age"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.752Z','2026-10-27T16:15:35.597Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu94008ae9oi9itn2ltp','mcq','Most common cause of gross hematuria','["Alport S","Stones","Hypercalciuria","UTI"]','Stones','<p><strong>Why Stones is correct:</strong> Kidney stones (nephrolithiasis) are the most common cause of gross hematuria in adults. The mechanical irritation of the stone against the urothelial lining during passage causes bleeding, typically described as "colicky" pain with intermittent gross hematuria. The prevalence of stones has been increasing, affecting about 1 in 11 people in the US, making it the leading identifiable cause of gross hematuria.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Alport S:</strong> Alport syndrome is a hereditary condition causing glomerulonephritis and sensorineural hearing loss, but it''s a rare cause of gross hematuria compared to stones.</li>
<li><strong>Hypercalciuria:</strong> Hypercalciuria is a risk factor for stone formation but itself doesn''t typically cause gross hematuria unless stones develop.</li>
<li><strong>UTI:</strong> While UTIs can cause microscopic hematuria, gross hematuria is uncommon and suggests complications like pyelonephritis or stones rather than uncomplicated UTI.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Gross hematuria with colicky pain strongly suggests urolithiasis, while painless gross hematuria suggests malignancy until proven otherwise.</li>
<li>The "dusty urine" sign (sediment in urine) is classic for stones but not specific.</li>
<li>In children, gross hematuria is more likely due to post-infectious glomerulonephritis or structural abnormalities.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Stones are the most common cause of gross hematuria in adults due to their high prevalence and mechanical irritation of the urinary tract.</p>','<p>Stones cause bloody pee (common), UTI causes pee with pee (microscopic), Alport is rare</p>','<p>When evaluating gross hematuria, always consider the patient''s age and the presence of pain - painless hematuria in an adult requires urgent urological evaluation for malignancy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Alport S:</strong> This is a rare hereditary condition, not the most common cause. - <strong>Hypercalciuria:</strong> This is a risk factor for stones but not a direct cause of gross hematuria. - <strong>UTI:</strong> UTIs typically cause microscopic hematuria, not gross.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Hematuria','["hematuria","causes","stones"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.752Z','2026-10-27T16:15:35.597Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9e008ue9oii6hdnguz','mcq','A 5-year-old child presents with lower-extremity edema for the past 4 days. Three weeks ago, he experienced several days of cough and fever that was treated with acetaminophen. Physical examination is remarkable only for ocular puffiness and lower-extremity edema. A urinalysis shows the presence of proteinuria, but there is no hematuria. What is the most appropriate investigation you need??','["Urinary protein /creatinine ratio","Abdominal sonar","Renal biopsy","CBC"]','Urinary protein /creatinine ratio','<p><strong>Why Urinary protein/creatinine ratio is correct:</strong> The most appropriate initial investigation for this child is the urinary protein/creatinine ratio because it provides a quantitative assessment of proteinuria, which is the hallmark of nephrotic syndrome. This non-invasive test measures the concentration of protein relative to creatinine in a single urine sample, offering a reliable estimate of 24-hour protein excretion without requiring a cumbersome 24-hour urine collection. Given the classic presentation of edema and proteinuria following a recent respiratory infection, this child likely has minimal change disease (the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in young children), and the protein/creatinine ratio will confirm the nephrotic-range proteinuria (&gt;2 g/g or 40 mg/m²/h). <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Abdominal sonar:</strong> While ultrasound can assess kidney size and structure, it doesn''t quantify proteinuria or diagnose the specific type of glomerular disease. - <strong>Renal biopsy:</strong> This is invasive and typically reserved for atypical presentations, steroid resistance, or when the diagnosis remains uncertain after initial investigations. - <strong>CBC:</strong> A complete blood count might show findings associated with nephrotic syndrome (like anemia or thrombocytosis), but it doesn''t confirm the diagnosis or quantify proteinuria. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Nephrotic syndrome is defined by massive proteinuria (&gt;40 mg/m²/h), hypoalbuminemia, and edema. - The most common cause in young children is minimal change disease, which typically follows a viral illness. - The urinary protein/creatinine ratio is a practical alternative to 24-hour urine collection for quantifying proteinuria. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For a child with edema and proteinuria following a viral illness, the urinary protein/creatinine ratio is the most appropriate initial investigation to confirm nephrotic syndrome.</p>','<p>PED - Proteinuria, Edema, hypoalbuminemia are the classic triad of nephrotic syndrome.</p>','<p>Nephrotic syndrome in children is most commonly caused by minimal change disease, which typically responds dramatically to corticosteroid therapy.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Abdominal sonar:</strong> Doesn''t quantify proteinuria or diagnose the specific type of glomerular disease. - <strong>Renal biopsy:</strong> Invasive and typically reserved for atypical presentations or steroid resistance. - <strong>CBC:</strong> May show associated findings but doesn''t confirm the diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Nephrotic Syndrome','["nephrotic","edema","proteinuria","child"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.762Z','2026-10-27T16:16:34.933Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9f008we9oij7555ffw','mcq','A 5-year-old female presents with periorbital edema and proteinuria. Her mother reports that she "just got over a cold" last week. On examination, you note a pulse rate of 70/minute and a blood pressure of 95/53. Which protein(s) would likely be found in large amounts in this patient''s urine?','["IgA","Ig E","Albumin","IgA and albumin"]','Albumin','<p><strong>Why Albumin is correct:</strong> The protein that would likely be found in large amounts in this patient''s urine is albumin because nephrotic syndrome, which this child''s presentation of periorbital edema and proteinuria following a cold strongly suggests, is characterized by massive loss of low-molecular-weight proteins through the damaged glomerular filtration barrier. Albumin is the most abundant protein in plasma (approximately 60% of total plasma proteins) with a molecular weight of 66 kDa, making it small enough to pass through the damaged glomeruli but too large to be reabsorbed by the proximal tubules. The normal glomerular basement membrane has a negative charge that normally repels negatively charged proteins like albumin, but in nephrotic syndrome, this charge barrier is disrupted along with the size barrier, leading to selective loss of albumin. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>IgA:</strong> Immunoglobulin A is a larger protein (approximately 160 kDa) that is typically not lost in nephrotic syndrome; its presence would suggest a different diagnosis like IgA nephropathy. - <strong>IgE:</strong> Immunoglobulin E is even larger (approximately 190 kDa) and would not be filtered in significant amounts even in nephrotic syndrome. - <strong>IgA and albumin:</strong> While albumin is indeed lost in nephrotic syndrome, IgA is not typically found in large amounts, making this combination incorrect. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by massive proteinuria with selective loss of albumin. - The most common cause in children is minimal change disease, which typically follows viral infections. - Hypoalbuminemia resulting from urinary albumin loss leads to decreased plasma oncotic pressure and edema formation. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In nephrotic syndrome, the protein most abundantly lost in urine is albumin due to its high plasma concentration and relatively small size that allows passage through the damaged glomerular filtration barrier.</p>','<p>ALB - Albumin Loss is the hallmark of nephrotic syndrome.</p>','<p>The degree of hypoalbuminemia in nephrotic syndrome correlates with the severity of proteinuria and edema, but not necessarily with the underlying cause of the glomerular disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>IgA:</strong> A larger protein not typically lost in nephrotic syndrome; its presence would suggest IgA nephropathy. - <strong>IgE:</strong> An even larger protein that would not be filtered in significant amounts in nephrotic syndrome. - <strong>IgA and albumin:</strong> While albumin is lost, IgA is not typically found in large amounts in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','Nephrotic Syndrome','["proteinuria","edema","albumin","child"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.763Z','2026-10-27T16:16:34.935Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9h0090e9oikq90daqj','mcq','A 12-year-old girl with nephrotic syndrome (NS) secondary to hepatitis B infection. Of the following, the MOST common type of secondary NS in this girl is:','["Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Membranous nephropathy","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Minimal change nephrotic syndrome","Minimal change with mesengial proliferation"]','Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis','<p><strong>Why Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis is correct:</strong> Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN) is the most common secondary cause of nephrotic syndrome in children with hepatitis B infection. The pathophysiology involves immune complex deposition in the glomeruli, with hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) forming immune complexes that activate the complement system via the alternative pathway, leading to mesangial cell proliferation and double-contoured glomerular basement membranes on light microscopy. This immune-mediated injury results in proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and the clinical features of nephrotic syndrome.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Membranous nephropathy:</strong> While associated with hepatitis B, it''s less common than MPGN in children and more typically presents in adults with hepatitis B.</li>
<li><strong>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis:</strong> This is more commonly associated with genetic factors, obesity, or prior renal injury rather than hepatitis B infection.</li>
<li><strong>Minimal change nephrotic syndrome:</strong> This is the most common primary cause of nephrotic syndrome in children but is not typically associated with hepatitis B infection.</li>
<li><strong>Minimal change with mesengial proliferation:</strong> This is not a well-defined histological classification and is not specifically associated with hepatitis B-related nephrotic syndrome.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hepatitis B-associated nephropathy most commonly presents as MPGN type I in children.</li>
<li>The presence of hepatitis B surface antigen in the serum and glomeruli is diagnostic for this association.</li>
<li>MPGN on biopsy shows mesangial hypercellularity, double-contoured basement membranes, and subendothelial deposits.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hepatitis B infection is the most common cause of secondary nephrotic syndrome in children, with membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis being the typical histological pattern.</p>','<p>HBV-MPGN: Hepatitis B Virus leads to Membranoproliferative Glomerulonephritis.</p>','<p>In children with hepatitis B and nephrotic syndrome, MPGN is the most common histological finding, and treatment should address both the viral infection and the renal disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Membranous nephropathy:</strong> Less common than MPGN in children with hepatitis B.</li>
<li><strong>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis:</strong> Not typically associated with hepatitis B infection.</li>
<li><strong>Minimal change nephrotic syndrome:</strong> Primary cause, not secondary to hepatitis B.</li>
<li><strong>Minimal change with mesengial proliferation:</strong> Not a well-defined classification for hepatitis B nephropathy.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Nephrotic Syndrome','["secondary","hepatitis","membranoproliferative"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.765Z','2026-10-27T16:16:53.702Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9i0092e9oii4fe7tt6','mcq','A Girl with Puffy Eyes: A 6-year-old girl presents with eye edema 1 week after taking ibuprofen for a headache. You note +4 protein on the urine dipstick. You should:','["Check LFTs, if albumin low, give albumin","Renal ultrasound and biopsy","Get labs and start prednisone","Have patient return with first-morning void and dip that to rule out orthostatic proteinuria"]','Have patient return with first-morning void and dip that to rule out orthostatic proteinuria','<p><strong>Why Have patient return with first-morning void and dip that to rule out orthostatic proteinuria is correct:</strong> Orthostatic proteinuria is the most common cause of proteinuria in children and is a benign condition characterized by increased protein excretion when upright but normal levels when recumbent. The pathophysiology involves increased renal vein pressure in the upright position, leading to glomerular hyperfiltration and protein leakage. This condition is typically seen in older children and adolescents and resolves spontaneously. The diagnostic approach involves comparing protein-to-creatinine ratios in first-morning void (supine) and random daytime (upright) urine samples, with orthostatic proteinuria confirmed when the ratio is normal in the morning sample.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check LFTs, if albumin low, give albumin:</strong> While albumin may be low in nephrotic syndrome, this approach would be premature before confirming persistent proteinuria and ruling out orthostatic proteinuria.</li>
<li><strong>Renal ultrasound and biopsy:</strong> These invasive procedures are not indicated before establishing persistent proteinuria and would be unnecessary if orthostatic proteinuria is confirmed.</li>
<li><strong>Get labs and start prednisone:</strong> Starting corticosteroids without confirming persistent proteinuria and establishing a diagnosis would be inappropriate and potentially harmful.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Orthostatic proteinuria is the most common cause of proteinuria in children and is benign.</li>
<li>The diagnostic test involves comparing first-morning void (supine) with daytime (upright) urine samples.</li>
<li>Persistent proteinuria (&gt;3 months) or abnormal renal function would warrant further evaluation for glomerular disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> In a child with transient proteinuria after medication exposure, always rule out orthostatic proteinuria by comparing first-morning void with daytime urine samples before pursuing further testing.</p>','<p>FMD for first-morning dip: First-Morning Void to rule out Orthostatic proteinuria before Disease diagnosis.</p>','<p>Orthostatic proteinuria is a benign condition that mimics nephrotic syndrome and is the most common cause of proteinuria in children, typically resolving without treatment.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Check LFTs, if albumin low, give albumin:</strong> Premature before confirming persistent proteinuria.</li>
<li><strong>Renal ultrasound and biopsy:</strong> Invasive procedures not indicated before establishing persistent proteinuria.</li>
<li><strong>Get labs and start prednisone:</strong> Starting steroids without diagnosis is inappropriate.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Nephrotic Syndrome','["nephrotic","proteinuria","pediatrics"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.766Z','2026-10-27T16:16:53.705Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu97008ie9oifm81scwy','mcq','5 years old boy with acute renal failure, when you can suspect he has prerenal oliguria??','["Urine testing reveals a low specific gravity","The causative factor is malignant hypertension","Urine testing reveals a high sodium concentration","Reversal of the oliguria occurs with fluid replacement"]','Reversal of the oliguria occurs with fluid replacement','<p><strong>Why Reversal of the oliguria occurs with fluid replacement is correct:</strong> Prerenal oliguria occurs due to decreased renal perfusion, typically from hypovolemia or decreased cardiac output. In this state, the kidneys are intact but underperfused, so when fluid is administered to restore intravascular volume, renal perfusion improves and urine output normalizes. This response occurs because the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) is activated in prerenal states, causing sodium and water reabsorption, but this is reversible once perfusion is restored. The specific gravity is typically high in prerenal states due to concentrated urine, not low.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Urine testing reveals a low specific gravity:</strong> This is incorrect because prerenal oliguria typically produces concentrated urine with high specific gravity (&gt;1.020) due to maximal water reabsorption by the tubules.</li>
<li><strong>The causative factor is malignant hypertension:</strong> While hypertension can cause renal issues, malignant hypertension typically leads to intrinsic renal damage (acute tubular necrosis), not prerenal azotemia.</li>
<li><strong>Urine testing reveals a high sodium concentration:</strong> This is incorrect because prerenal states cause increased sodium reabsorption (urine sodium &lt;20 mEq/L) as part of the RAAS activation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Prerenal azotemia is characterized by BUN:Cr ratio &gt;20:1 due to disproportionate reabsorption of urea.</li>
<li>Fractional excretion of sodium (FeNa) is typically &lt;1% in prerenal states.</li>
<li>The urine sediment in prerenal states is typically benign with hyaline casts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Prerenal oliguria reverses with fluid replacement because the kidneys are structurally intact but underperfused.</p>','<p>FeNa &lt;1% for prerenal, FeNa &gt;2% for intrinsic</p>','<p>In pediatric acute renal failure, distinguishing prerenal from intrinsic causes is critical as fluid resuscitation can reverse prerenal failure but may worsen intrinsic renal injury.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Urine testing reveals a low specific gravity:</strong> Prerenal oliguria produces concentrated urine with high specific gravity, not low.</li>
<li><strong>The causative factor is malignant hypertension:</strong> Malignant hypertension typically causes intrinsic renal damage, not prerenal azotemia.</li>
<li><strong>Urine testing reveals a high sodium concentration:</strong> Prerenal states cause sodium reabsorption, resulting in low urine sodium (&lt;20 mEq/L).</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Nephrology','Renal Failure','["renal failure","oliguria","prerenal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.755Z','2026-10-27T16:16:01.786Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu98008je9oi7gqv5so5','mcq','Which of the following findings is most likely in an infant suspected of having posterior urethral valve?','["A neurogenic bladder","A hydrocele in the scrotum","An indirect inguinal hernia","Weak urinary stream","Polycystic kidney disease"]','Weak urinary stream','<p><strong>Why Weak urinary stream is correct:</strong> Posterior urethral valves (PUV) are the most common cause of lower urinary tract obstruction in male infants, resulting from abnormal folds of mucosal tissue in the posterior urethra. These valves obstruct urine outflow, leading to bladder distension, hypertrophy, and eventually hydronephrosis and renal dysplasia. The obstruction causes a weak urinary stream due to increased resistance to urine flow, which is often the presenting sign in newborns or infants. This finding is pathognomonic for PUV and distinguishes it from other causes of urinary obstruction.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A neurogenic bladder:</strong> While neurogenic bladder can cause urinary retention, it is not associated with the classic findings of PUV such as a keyhole bladder on ultrasound.</li>
<li><strong>A hydrocele in the scrotum:</strong> Hydroceles are common in newborns but are not specifically associated with PUV.</li>
<li><strong>An indirect inguinal hernia:</strong> Indirect inguinal hernias are common in infants but are not related to PUV.</li>
<li><strong>Polycystic kidney disease:</strong> This is a genetic disorder causing bilateral renal cysts, not associated with urinary obstruction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PUV is a life-threatening condition that can lead to pulmonary hypoplasia due to oligohydramnios in utero.</li>
<li>The classic ultrasound finding in PUV is a dilated posterior urethra (''keyhole'' appearance) with bilateral hydronephrosis.</li>
<li>Boys with PUV often present with urinary tract infections, failure to thrive, or voiding dysfunction.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Posterior urethral valves cause a weak urinary stream due to obstruction of the urethra, making this the most likely finding in affected infants.</p>','<p>PUV = Poor Urine Voiding (weak stream)</p>','<p>Posterior urethral valves are a urologic emergency requiring prompt intervention to preserve renal function and prevent complications like end-stage renal disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>A neurogenic bladder:</strong> Neurogenic bladder can cause urinary retention but is not associated with the classic findings of PUV.</li>
<li><strong>A hydrocele in the scrotum:</strong> Hydroceles are common in newborns but not specifically associated with PUV.</li>
<li><strong>An indirect inguinal hernia:</strong> Indirect inguinal hernias are common in infants but not related to PUV.</li>
<li><strong>Polycystic kidney disease:</strong> This is a genetic disorder causing bilateral renal cysts, not associated with urinary obstruction.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Urology','Posterior urethral valve','["pediatrics","urethral","infant"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.756Z','2026-10-27T16:16:01.788Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9a008ne9oixtekyftc','mcq','The most common age of Minimal change NS','["2 - 6 years"]','2 - 6 years','<p><strong>Why 2 - 6 years is correct:</strong> Minimal change nephrotic syndrome (MCNS) is most commonly diagnosed in children between 2-6 years of age due to the peak incidence of this condition occurring during this period. This age range represents the typical presentation when the immune system''s dysregulation leads to the characteristic podocyte injury and massive proteinuria seen in MCNS. The pathophysiology involves T-cell dysfunction leading to decreased production of circulating permeability factors that damage the glomerular filtration barrier. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - (No other options provided in the question) <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - MCNS accounts for approximately 80-90% of nephrotic syndrome cases in children under 10 years. - The classic presentation includes massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia. - MCNS typically responds dramatically to corticosteroid therapy, with most children achieving remission within 4-6 weeks. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Minimal change nephrotic syndrome predominantly affects children between 2-6 years of age and is characterized by steroid-responsive proteinuria.</p>','<p>MCNS: Most Common in age 2-6, Steroid-responsive</p>','<p>Minimal change nephrotic syndrome is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and has an excellent prognosis with steroid treatment.</p>','','Basic','Nephrology','Minimal change nephrotic syndrome','["age","minimal change","nephrotic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.758Z','2026-10-27T16:16:08.925Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9f008xe9oidjv63u28','mcq','A 10-year-old boy he is a known case of steroid dependent nephrotic syndrome presented to the ER with severe generalized edema. In addition to restricting salt intake, what is the BEST diuretic to be used plus albumin infusion?','["Aldactone","Furosemide","Thiazide","Mannitol"]','Furosemide','<p><strong>Why Furosemide is correct:</strong> Furosemide is a loop diuretic that inhibits the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, causing significant diuresis by blocking reabsorption of approximately 25% of filtered sodium. In nephrotic syndrome with severe edema, furosemide is preferred because it works independently of glomerular filtration rate and can overcome the hypoalbuminemia-induced resistance to diuretics that occurs with other classes. When combined with albumin infusion, furosemide''s efficacy is enhanced as albumin expands plasma volume and improves diuretic delivery to the kidney tubules.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aldactone:</strong> This potassium-sparing diuretic acts on the collecting duct and is weak in effect, making it insufficient for severe edema management in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Thiazide:</strong> Thiazide diuretics act on the distal convoluted tubule but are ineffective when glomerular filtration rate falls below 30 mL/min, which is common in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Mannitol:</strong> Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic that increases renal excretion of water but is not indicated for edema management in nephrotic syndrome and can potentially worsen fluid overload.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>In nephrotic syndrome with severe edema, loop diuretics (furosemide) are first-line due to their efficacy in low GFR states.</li>
<li>Albumin infusion should be given 15-30 minutes before furosemide to enhance diuretic effect by improving renal perfusion.</li>
<li>Diuretic resistance in nephrotic syndrome is common and may require high doses or continuous infusion.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For severe edema in nephrotic syndrome, always combine albumin infusion with a loop diuretic like furosemide to overcome diuretic resistance.</p>','<p>FAN: Furosemide first, Albumin next, No other diuretics needed for severe nephrotic edema.</p>','<p>In steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome with severe edema, diuretic resistance is common due to hypoalbuminemia, making the combination of albumin and furosemide essential.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Aldactone:</strong> Too weak for severe edema in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Thiazide:</strong> Ineffective when GFR is reduced, as commonly occurs in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Mannitol:</strong> Not indicated for edema management and can worsen fluid overload.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Nephrotic Syndrome','["nephrotic","edema","diuretic","steroid-dependent"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.764Z','2026-10-27T16:16:45.362Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9g008ye9oi0k6sq0pn','mcq','What results in the edema associated with nephrotic syndrome?','["Hypercoagulability","Hyperalbuminemia","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased glomerular filtration rate"]','Decreased plasma oncotic pressure','<p><strong>Why Decreased plasma oncotic pressure is correct:</strong> The edema in nephrotic syndrome results from massive proteinuria (primarily albumin) leading to decreased plasma oncotic pressure, which reduces the osmotic gradient that normally retains fluid within the intravascular compartment. This causes fluid to shift into the interstitial spaces, resulting in edema. The mechanism follows Starling''s forces, where reduced plasma oncotic pressure favors filtration of fluid from capillaries into tissues.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hypercoagulability:</strong> While nephrotic syndrome does increase thrombotic risk due to loss of anticoagulant proteins and increased platelet aggregation, this does not directly cause edema.</li>
<li><strong>Hyperalbuminemia:</strong> Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by hypoalbuminemia, not hyperalbuminemia, which would actually oppose edema formation.</li>
<li><strong>Decreased glomerular filtration rate:</strong> While reduced GFR can contribute to edema in some kidney diseases, it''s not the primary mechanism in nephrotic syndrome where protein loss is the dominant factor.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The primary mechanism of edema in nephrotic syndrome is decreased plasma oncotic pressure due to massive proteinuria.</li>
<li>The classic triad of nephrotic syndrome includes heavy proteinuria (&gt;3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia, and edema.</li>
<li>In nephrotic syndrome, the degree of edema correlates with the severity of hypoalbuminemia.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Remember that nephrotic syndrome edema comes from protein loss lowering plasma oncotic pressure, not from fluid overload or poor kidney function.</p>','<p>POEM: Plasma Oncotic pressure Explains edema in nephrotic syndrome - remember POEM to get the mechanism right.</p>','<p>The severity of edema in nephrotic syndrome correlates with the degree of hypoalbuminemia, making serum albumin level a key prognostic indicator.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hypercoagulability:</strong> A complication of nephrotic syndrome but not the cause of edema.</li>
<li><strong>Hyperalbuminemia:</strong> The opposite of what occurs in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Decreased glomerular filtration rate:</strong> Not the primary mechanism of edema in nephrotic syndrome.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','Nephrotic Syndrome','["edema","pathophysiology","nephrotic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.764Z','2026-10-27T16:16:45.363Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9l0096e9oirz9eje21','mcq','Which of the following is not common cause of hematuria','["Renal stone","Cancer","HIV nephropathy","Rapid progressive GN"]','HIV nephropathy','<p><strong>Why HIV nephropathy is correct:</strong> HIV nephropathy is not a common cause of hematuria compared to the other options, as it typically presents with significant proteinuria (often nephrotic-range) and progressive renal failure rather than isolated hematuria. While HIV can cause renal disease through various mechanisms (including immune complex deposition and direct viral effects), overt hematuria is not a hallmark feature, and this condition is relatively uncommon in the era of effective antiretroviral therapy.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Renal stone:</strong> A very common cause of hematuria, typically presenting with colicky flank pain and microscopic or gross hematuria.</li>
<li><strong>Cancer:</strong> Renal cell carcinoma, transitional cell carcinoma, and prostate cancer are well-established causes of hematuria, especially in older adults.</li>
<li><strong>Rapid progressive GN:</strong> A common cause of significant hematuria, often accompanied by acute renal failure, RBC casts, and oliguria.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>HIV nephropathy typically presents with heavy proteinuria and declining renal function rather than isolated hematuria.</li>
<li>The most common causes of hematuria in adults are stones, infection, cancer, and glomerular diseases.</li>
<li>When evaluating hematuria, consider age (stones in young adults, cancer in older adults) and associated symptoms.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> While HIV can cause kidney disease, it''s not a common cause of hematuria, which is more frequently associated with stones, cancer, or glomerular diseases.</p>','<p>SHC: Stones, Hematuria, Cancer - the common triad of hematuria causes, with HIV being the exception</p>','<p>HIV-associated nephropathy typically presents with nephrotic syndrome and rapidly progressive renal failure rather than isolated hematuria.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Renal stone:</strong> Extremely common cause of hematuria, often with colicky pain.</li>
<li><strong>Cancer:</strong> Frequent cause of hematuria, especially in older adults.</li>
<li><strong>Rapid progressive GN:</strong> Common cause of significant hematuria with acute renal failure.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','Hematuria','["hematuria","causes","renal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.769Z','2026-10-27T16:17:12.953Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9m0099e9oi3k463y2c','mcq','Microscopic finding suggestive of hemolytic uremic syndrome','["Foam cells","Giant Megakaryocytes","Fragmented RBCS","Target cell"]','Fragmented RBCS','<p><strong>Why Fragmented RBCS is correct:</strong> Fragmented red blood cells (schistocytes) are the hallmark microscopic finding in hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), resulting from mechanical shearing of RBCs as they pass through fibrin strands deposited in the microvasculature. This microangiopathic hemolytic anemia occurs due to endothelial damage and platelet activation, characteristic of the thrombotic microangiopathy seen in HUS. The presence of schistocytes directly indicates the pathophysiological process of RBC destruction in the small vessels, which is central to the diagnosis of HUS. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Foam cells:</strong> These lipid-laden macrophages are typically seen in atherosclerotic plaques or lipid storage disorders, not in HUS. - <strong>Giant Megakaryocytes:</strong> These are enlarged platelet precursors seen in conditions like essential thrombocythemia or myeloproliferative disorders, not HUS. - <strong>Target cell:</strong> These RBCs with a central bull''s-eye appearance are associated with hemoglobinopathies (like thalassemia) or liver disease, not the mechanical fragmentation seen in HUS. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Schistocytes are pathognomonic for microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, which includes HUS and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP). - HUS classically presents with the triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. - The presence of schistocytes on peripheral smear should prompt immediate evaluation for thrombotic microangiopathy. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Schistocytes on peripheral smear are the microscopic signature of hemolytic uremic syndrome, reflecting mechanical destruction of RBCs in damaged microvasculature.</p>','<p>SCHISTocytes = Sheared Cells in HUS (and TTP)</p>','<p>Schistocytes indicate microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and should trigger urgent evaluation for HUS or TTP, as these are medical emergencies requiring prompt intervention.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Foam cells:</strong> Not associated with HUS pathophysiology. - <strong>Giant Megakaryocytes:</strong> Seen in myeloproliferative disorders, not HUS. - <strong>Target cell:</strong> Associated with hemoglobinopathies or liver disease, not mechanical RBC fragmentation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','HUS','["HUS","microscopic","RBC"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.770Z','2026-10-27T16:17:22.913Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9n009be9oi6lcbdczd','mcq','What is the most common cause of acute glomerulonephritis?','["Staphylococcal infection.","Streptococcal infection.","Epstein-Barr virus.","Cytomegalovirus."]','Streptococcal infection.','<p><strong>Why Streptococcal infection is correct:</strong> Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is the most common cause of acute glomerulonephritis worldwide, occurring 1-3 weeks after infection with nephritogenic strains of group A beta-hemolytic streptococcus (typically pharyngitis or skin infections). The pathogenesis involves immune complex deposition (containing streptococcal antigens) in the glomeruli, leading to complement activation and inflammation that damages the glomerular filtration barrier. This immune-mediated process results in the classic presentation of hematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and acute kidney injury. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Staphylococcal infection:</strong> While staphylococcal infections can cause glomerulonephritis (particularly in post-infectious settings or associated with endocarditis), they are less common than streptococcal causes. - <strong>Epstein-Barr virus:</strong> EBV is associated with various renal complications but is not a common cause of typical acute glomerulonephritis. - <strong>Cytomegalovirus:</strong> CMCM can cause renal issues but is not a typical cause of acute glomerulonephritis. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - PSGN typically presents with hematuria (often cola-colored), proteinuria, hypertension, and edema 1-3 weeks after streptococcal infection. - Diagnosis is supported by decreased complement levels (C3 and C4) that normalize over 6-8 weeks. - The prognosis is generally excellent in children, with most cases resolving spontaneously, though adults may have more persistent renal dysfunction. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Post-streptococcal infection is the most common cause of acute glomerulonephritis worldwide due to immune complex deposition from nephritogenic streptococcal strains.</p>','<p>PSGN = Post-Streptococcal GN (most common cause of acute GN)</p>','<p>Remember that post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis often presents with cola-colored urine and low complement levels that normalize over time.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Staphylococcal infection:</strong> Less common cause compared to streptococcal infection. - <strong>Epstein-Barr virus:</strong> Not typically associated with classic acute glomerulonephritis. - <strong>Cytomegalovirus:</strong> Not a common cause of typical acute glomerulonephritis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','Glomerulonephritis','["nephrology","infection","glomerular"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.771Z','2026-10-27T16:17:22.917Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9o009ce9oi6mbjqsjj','mcq','The cause of mild hypertension in patients with acute glomerulonephritis?','["Elevated liver enzymes.","sed intravascular pressure.","sed heart rate due to fever.","n of sodium and fluid."]','n of sodium and fluid.','<p><strong>Why n of sodium and fluid is correct:</strong> In acute glomerulonephritis, the inflammatory process damages the glomerular capillaries, leading to decreased glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and impaired sodium and water excretion. This results in sodium and fluid retention, expanding the extracellular fluid volume and increasing blood volume, which consequently elevates blood pressure through the Frank-Starling mechanism. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) may also be activated in response to the reduced renal perfusion, further promoting sodium reabsorption and fluid retention.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Elevated liver enzymes:</strong> While liver enzymes can be elevated in various conditions, they are not directly related to the pathophysiology of hypertension in acute glomerulonephritis.</li>
<li><strong>sed intravascular pressure:</strong> This is incorrect terminology and doesn''t represent a recognized mechanism for hypertension in glomerulonephritis.</li>
<li><strong>sed heart rate due to fever:</strong> Fever can increase heart rate, but this is not a primary mechanism for hypertension in acute glomerulonephritis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acute glomerulonephritis typically presents with hypertension due to volume expansion from sodium and fluid retention.</li>
<li>The classic triad of acute glomerulonephritis includes hematuria, proteinuria, and hypertension.</li>
<li>Treatment focuses on blood pressure control, often with ACE inhibitors or ARBs to reduce intraglomerular pressure.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hypertension in acute glomerulonephritis is primarily caused by sodium and fluid retention due to impaired renal excretion.</p>','<p>RAAS: Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System explains fluid retention in GN</p>','<p>In acute glomerulonephritis, hypertension is often volume-dependent and responds well to diuretics in addition to RAAS blockade.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Elevated liver enzymes:</strong> Not directly related to hypertension pathophysiology in glomerulonephritis.</li>
<li><strong>sed intravascular pressure:</strong> Incorrect terminology and not a recognized mechanism.</li>
<li><strong>sed heart rate due to fever:</strong> Fever-induced tachycardia doesn''t cause hypertension in this context.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Glomerulonephritis','["hypertension","glomerulonephritis","renal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.772Z','2026-10-27T16:17:30.217Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9o009de9oigt5iiiim','mcq','Which of the following treatments is recommended for acute pyelonephritis in infants requiring hospitalization?','["Oral nitrofurantoin","Intravenous cefotaxime or ceftriaxone","Single-dose amoxicillin","Oral trimethoprim alone"]','Intravenous cefotaxime or ceftriaxone','<p><strong>Why Intravenous cefotaxime or ceftriaxone is correct:</strong> For infants hospitalized with acute pyelonephritis, intravenous cefotaxime or ceftriaxone are recommended as first-line therapy because they provide broad-spectrum coverage against the most common uropathogens, including Escherichia coli and other gram-negative organisms, while achieving adequate renal tissue penetration. These third-generation cephalosporins are effective against resistant strains and are safe for use in infants, with minimal side effects. The intravenous route ensures reliable drug delivery in critically ill patients who may have vomiting or poor oral absorption.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Oral nitrofurantoin:</strong> While effective for uncomplicated lower UTIs, nitrofurantoin achieves poor tissue concentrations in the kidney and is not recommended for pyelonephritis treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Single-dose amoxicillin:</strong> Single-dose therapy is inadequate for pyelonephritis and would not provide sufficient coverage or duration to eradicate the infection.</li>
<li><strong>Oral trimethoprim alone:</strong> Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) may be used for uncomplicated pyelonephritis in older children, but monotherapy with trimethoprim alone is not standard, and oral therapy is not recommended for hospitalized infants.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hospitalized infants with pyelonephritis require IV antibiotics with good renal penetration and broad gram-negative coverage.</li>
<li>Cefotaxime and ceftriaxone are first-line choices for acute pyelonephritis in hospitalized infants.</li>
<li>Treatment duration is typically 7-14 days, followed by oral therapy to complete a total of 10-14 days.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hospitalized infants with pyelonephritis require IV third-generation cephalosporins like cefotaxime or ceftriaxone for adequate renal penetration and coverage.</p>','<p>IV C''s: Infants with pyelonephritis need IV Cephalosporins (cefotaxime/ceftriaxone)</p>','<p>Untreated pyelonephritis in infants can lead to permanent renal scarring and hypertension later in life, making prompt appropriate treatment crucial.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Oral nitrofurantoin:</strong> Poor renal tissue penetration makes it inadequate for pyelonephritis.</li>
<li><strong>Single-dose amoxicillin:</strong> Inadequate duration and coverage for pyelonephritis treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Oral trimethoprim alone:</strong> Not standard therapy for hospitalized infants with pyelonephritis.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','Pyelonephritis','["pyelonephritis","infants","treatment"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.772Z','2026-10-27T16:17:30.220Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9o009ee9oi59bw5yid','mcq','If blood in urine is visible only with use of microscope, It is called','["Gross hematuria","Pseudo hematuria","Microscopic hematuria","Idiopathic hematuria"]','Microscopic hematuria','<p><strong>Why Microscopic hematuria is correct:</strong> Microscopic hematuria is defined as the presence of red blood cells in urine that can only be detected under microscopic examination, typically when there are more than 3-5 red blood cells per high-power field. This distinguishes it from gross hematuria, where blood is visible to the naked eye as pink, red, or cola-colored urine. The term specifically refers to the detection method rather than the underlying cause, which can range from minor to serious conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gross hematuria:</strong> This is incorrect because gross hematuria refers to visible blood in urine, not microscopic detection.</li>
<li><strong>Pseudo hematuria:</strong> This term refers to conditions that mimic hematuria but aren''t actually blood in urine, such as certain foods (beets, rhubarb) or medications, and doesn''t describe microscopic detection.</li>
<li><strong>Idiopathic hematuria:</strong> This refers to hematuria without an identifiable cause, which is a diagnosis made after excluding other conditions, not a description of detection method.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Microscopic hematuria is defined as &gt;3-5 RBCs per high-power field on microscopic examination.</li>
<li>Common causes include glomerular diseases, urinary tract infections, stones, and malignancies.</li>
<li>Evaluation typically includes urine microscopy, culture, and imaging studies depending on patient age and risk factors.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Microscopic hematuria is the term for blood in urine detectable only under a microscope, not visible to the naked eye.</p>','<p>Micro = Microscope needed to see; Gross = Grossly visible</p>','<p>Microscopic hematuria in adults should be evaluated to rule out malignancy, especially in those over 40 or with risk factors.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Gross hematuria:</strong> Refers to visible blood in urine, not microscopic detection.</li>
<li><strong>Pseudo hematuria:</strong> Refers to conditions mimicking hematuria, not detection method.</li>
<li><strong>Idiopathic hematuria:</strong> Refers to hematuria without identified cause, not detection method.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Renal','Hematuria','["hematuria","microscopic","urine"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.772Z','2026-10-27T16:17:30.221Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9p009ge9oi9i54l7q1','mcq','Berger disease can occur in the following situations except:','["Follows gastroenteritis","Immunoglobulin A can be found in tissue specimens","Normal Anti-streptolysin (O) titer","Low Complement 3"]','Low Complement 3','<p><strong>Why Low Complement 3 is correct:</strong> Berger disease (IgA nephropathy) is characterized by mesangial deposition of IgA immune complexes, which typically do not activate the complement cascade significantly. Unlike post-infectious glomerulonephritis or lupus nephritis, IgA nephropathy usually presents with normal or slightly decreased complement levels, not significantly low C3. The pathophysiology involves mucosal immune dysregulation leading to abnormal IgA1 glycosylation and formation of immune complexes that deposit in the glomeruli without substantial complement activation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Follows gastroenteritis:</strong> IgA nephropathy frequently follows mucosal infections, particularly gastrointestinal infections, which can trigger the abnormal IgA response.</li>
<li><strong>Immunoglobulin A can be found in tissue specimens:</strong> This is a diagnostic hallmark of Berger disease, as IgA deposits are seen on immunofluorescence of renal biopsy specimens.</li>
<li><strong>Normal Anti-streptolysin (O) titer:</strong> Unlike post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, IgA nephropathy is not associated with elevated ASO titers, helping to distinguish it from streptococcal-related disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>IgA nephropathy is the most common primary glomerular disease worldwide</li>
<li>The classic presentation is episodic gross hematuria often following mucosal infections</li>
<li>Normal or near-normal complement levels help differentiate it from post-infectious GN</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Berger disease/IgA nephropathy is characterized by IgA deposits in the kidney and normal complement levels, distinguishing it from other glomerulonephritides.</p>','<p>IgA nephropathy: IgA deposits, Normal complement, After infection (INA)</p>','<p>IgA nephropathy often has a benign course but can progress to chronic kidney disease in 20-30% of patients over 20-30 years.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Follows gastroenteritis:</strong> IgA nephropathy commonly follows GI infections as a trigger</li>
<li><strong>Immunoglobulin A can be found in tissue specimens:</strong> This is diagnostic for Berger disease</li>
<li><strong>Normal Anti-streptolysin (O) titer:</strong> Correct as ASO is typically normal in IgA nephropathy</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','IgA Nephropathy','["nephrology","glomerular","iga","berger","complement"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.773Z','2026-10-27T16:17:38.969Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9q009he9oiacdmjxry','mcq','Ocular abnormalities in Alport syndrome:','["Glaucoma","Color blindness","Anterior lenticonus","Chalazion"]','Anterior lenticonus','<p><strong>Why Anterior lenticonus is correct:</strong> Anterior lenticonus is a characteristic ocular manifestation of Alport syndrome, resulting from thinning and bulging of the anterior lens capsule. This occurs due to abnormal collagen IV deposition in the lens capsule, which is structurally similar to the basement membranes affected in the kidneys and cochlea. The lenticonus typically appears as a conical protrusion of the anterior lens and can contribute to progressive visual impairment and cataract formation in Alport syndrome patients.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Glaucoma:</strong> While glaucoma can occur in various renal diseases, it is not a characteristic feature of Alport syndrome.</li>
<li><strong>Color blindness:</strong> Color blindness (specifically red-green color vision defects) is associated with X-linked Alport syndrome due to the COL4A5 gene''s proximity to the color vision genes on the X chromosome, but it''s not an ocular abnormality in the same sense as lenticonus.</li>
<li><strong>Chalazion:</strong> A chalazion is a common eyelid condition unrelated to Alport syndrome.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Anterior lenticonus is pathognomonic for Alport syndrome and typically appears in adolescence or early adulthood</li>
<li>Alport syndrome is caused by mutations in type IV collagen genes (COL4A3, COL4A4, COL4A5)</li>
<li>The characteristic triad of Alport syndrome is hematuria, sensorineural hearing loss, and ocular abnormalities</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Anterior lenticonus is the classic ocular finding in Alport syndrome, resulting from abnormal collagen IV in the lens capsule.</p>','<p>Alport ocular: Lenticonus (anterior), Cataracts, Fleck retinopathy (LCF)</p>','<p>The presence of anterior lenticonus in a patient with hematuria should strongly suggest Alport syndrome and prompt evaluation for hearing loss and family history.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Glaucoma:</strong> Not associated with Alport syndrome</li>
<li><strong>Color blindness:</strong> Associated with X-linked Alport but not an ocular structural abnormality</li>
<li><strong>Chalazion:</strong> Common eyelid condition unrelated to Alport</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Alport syndrome','["ocular","alport","abnormalities"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.774Z','2026-10-27T16:17:38.973Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9r009ie9oirqjeamo3','mcq','The plans care for the patient with APSGN based on what knowledge?','["Most patients with APSGN recover completely or rapidly improve with conservative management.","Chronic glomerulonephritis leading to renal failure is a common sequela to acute glomerulonephritis.","Pulmonary hemorrhage may occur as a result of antibodies also attacking the alveolar basement membrane.","A large percentage of patients with APSGN develop rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, resulting in kidney failure."]','Most patients with APSGN recover completely or rapidly improve with conservative management.','<p><strong>Why Most patients with APSGN recover completely or rapidly improve with conservative management is correct:</strong> APSGN is typically a self-limited immune-mediated disease triggered by streptococcal infection, where immune complexes deposit in the glomeruli causing inflammation. The majority of patients (especially children) experience complete recovery of renal function with supportive care alone, including blood pressure control, diuretics for edema, and dietary modifications. This favorable prognosis is due to the nature of the immune response being transient and the glomerular architecture being capable of healing without permanent damage.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chronic glomerulonephritis leading to renal failure is a common sequela to acute glomerulonephritis:</strong> This is incorrect as chronic kidney disease is a rare complication of APSGN, occurring in less than 1-2% of cases, primarily in adults or those with severe initial presentation.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary hemorrhage may occur as a result of antibodies also attacking the alveolar basement membrane:</strong> This describes Goodpasture syndrome, which involves anti-GBM antibodies targeting both glomeruli and alveoli, not APSGN which is mediated by immune complexes without pulmonary involvement.</li>
<li><strong>A large percentage of patients with APSGN develop rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, resulting in kidney failure:</strong> This is inaccurate as APSGN rarely presents as rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis; that pattern is more characteristic of other forms like ANCA-associated vasculitis or anti-GBM disease.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>APSGN has an excellent prognosis in children, with over 95% recovering completely with conservative management.</li>
<li>Adults with APSGN have a higher risk of incomplete recovery and progression to chronic kidney disease compared to children.</li>
<li>Recurrence of APSGN is uncommon unless there is a persistent streptococcal reservoir or reinfection.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> APSGN management is based on its excellent prognosis with conservative care, as complete recovery is the norm rather than the exception.</p>','<p>APSGN = Almost Patients Get Better, No kidney failure</p>','<p>Unlike many other glomerular diseases, APSGN has one of the best prognoses, with most children returning to normal renal function within weeks to months.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Chronic glomerulonephritis:</strong> Rare in APSGN, occurring in &lt;1-2% of cases, mostly in adults.</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary hemorrhage:</strong> Describes Goodpasture syndrome, not APSGN which lacks pulmonary involvement.</li>
<li><strong>Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis:</strong> Not characteristic of APSGN; more typical of ANCA or anti-GBM diseases.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','APSGN','["apsgn","management","prognosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.775Z','2026-10-27T16:17:48.599Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9x009oe9oi2tcmho4a','mcq','Which clinical feature in a child with hematuria is considered a red flag that warrants urgent nephrology evaluation?','["Isolated microscopic hematuria with normal blood pressure","Gross hematuria after vigorous exercise that resolves within 24 hours","Hematuria associated with hypertension and reduced kidney function"]','Hematuria associated with hypertension and reduced kidney function','<p><strong>Why Hematuria associated with hypertension and reduced kidney function is correct:</strong> This combination represents a red flag because it suggests significant underlying renal pathology, such as glomerulonephritis, vasculitis, or rapidly progressive kidney disease. Hypertension in the context of hematuria indicates possible renal involvement with impaired autoregulation and activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, while reduced kidney function demonstrates that the kidneys are unable to maintain normal filtration capacity. This triad of findings (hematuria + hypertension + reduced kidney function) is highly concerning for an active inflammatory process causing significant renal damage that requires urgent intervention to preserve renal function.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Isolated microscopic hematuria with normal blood pressure:</strong> This is often benign, especially when persistent and isolated, with common causes including thin basementment membrane disease or IgA nephropathy with stable disease.</li>
<li><strong>Gross hematuria after vigorous exercise that resolves within 24 hours:</strong> This is typically a benign phenomenon due to mechanical trauma to red blood cells in the urinary tract during intense physical activity and does not indicate underlying pathology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hematuria with hypertension should always raise concern for glomerular disease.</li>
<li>Isolated microscopic hematuria in an otherwise healthy child often requires monitoring but not urgent intervention.</li>
<li>Gross hematuria resolving within 24 hours after exercise is typically benign.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Hematuria plus hypertension equals urgent nephrology evaluation, as this combination indicates significant renal pathology requiring prompt intervention.</p>','<p>H-H-R: Hematuria + Hypertension = Renal emergency</p>','<p>In children, hematuria with hypertension should be considered a medical emergency until proven otherwise, as it often indicates significant glomerular disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Isolated microscopic hematuria with normal blood pressure:</strong> Often benign and may not require urgent intervention.</li>
<li><strong>Gross hematuria after vigorous exercise that resolves within 24 hours:</strong> Typically a benign phenomenon due to mechanical trauma.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Renal','Hematuria evaluation','["hematuria","red flag","urgent","nephrology"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.781Z','2026-10-27T16:18:04.503Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9y009pe9oicedxa27w','mcq','Bile duct proliferation/ hepatic fibrosis','["Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease","Autosomal Dominant polycystic kidney disease"]','Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease','<p><strong>Why Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease is correct:</strong> Bile duct proliferation and hepatic fibrosis are characteristic features of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD), which is also known as infantile polycystic kidney disease. In ARPKD, the genetic defect affects the development of both renal collecting ducts and bile ducts, leading to the formation of microcysts in the kidneys and congenital hepatic fibrosis with bile duct proliferation. This biliary ductal plate malformation results in the characteristic liver pathology seen in ARPKD, which is distinct from the macroscopic cysts seen in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD).</p>
<p><strong>Why the other option is wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Autosomal Dominant polycystic kidney disease:</strong> This is incorrect because ADPKD is characterized by the development of large, fluid-filled cysts in the kidneys and liver, but it does not typically cause bile duct proliferation or hepatic fibrosis. Instead, ADPKD patients develop simple hepatic cysts that are lined by biliary epithelium and are not associated with fibrosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>ARPKD is associated with congenital hepatic fibrosis and bile duct proliferation due to ductal plate malformation.</li>
<li>The liver disease in ARPKD typically presents with portal hypertension rather than liver failure.</li>
<li>ADPKD causes simple hepatic cysts without fibrosis, while ARPKD causes periportal fibrosis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Bile duct proliferation and hepatic fibrosis point to ARPKD, not ADPKD, due to the underlying ductal plate malformation.</p>','<p>ARPKD: Affects Renal &amp; Biliary Ducts (ABD)</p>','<p>In ARPKD, the liver disease (congenital hepatic fibrosis) often becomes more clinically significant than the kidney disease as patients survive longer.</p>','','Basic','Renal','Polycystic Kidney Disease','["polycystic","kidney","disease","hepatic","fibrosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.782Z','2026-10-27T16:18:04.506Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu9z009re9oitxdsiogq','mcq','In polycystic kidney diseases, in adult','["Increase blood pressure","Renal stone"]','Increase blood pressure','<p><strong>Why Increase blood pressure is correct:</strong> In polycystic kidney disease (PKD), the formation of numerous cysts throughout the renal parenchyma leads to progressive kidney enlargement and compression of functional nephrons. This compression activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) as the kidneys attempt to maintain perfusion to the remaining functional tissue. The resulting angiotensin II causes vasoconstriction and aldosterone promotes sodium retention, both contributing to hypertension. Additionally, cyst expansion disrupts the renal architecture, impairing sodium excretion and further elevating blood pressure, making hypertension a near-universal feature of adult PKD.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Renal stone:</strong> While renal calculi can occur in PKD due to structural abnormalities and concentrated urine, they are not a universal feature like hypertension and are less clinically significant in the disease course.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypertension in PKD typically precedes significant decline in renal function and is often the first clinical manifestation.</li>
<li>The hypertension in PKD is typically volume-dependent and responsive to RAAS inhibitors (ACE inhibitors or ARBs).</li>
<li>Cyst expansion and compression of renal vasculature are key mechanisms driving hypertension in PKD.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Polycystic kidney disease universally causes hypertension due to RAAS activation from cyst compression of functional nephrons.</p>','<p>PKD = Pressure (hypertension) before Kidney failure</p>','<p>Hypertension in PKD should be aggressively controlled with RAAS inhibitors to slow disease progression, even before significant renal impairment.</p>','','Basic','Renal','Polycystic Kidney Disease','["polycystic","kidney","disease","adult","hypertension"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.783Z','2026-10-27T16:18:13.004Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua0009te9oivl0nfsyb','mcq','Regarding testicular tumors','["Most of them are benign","2/3 Of tumors are mainly unclassified","Most of them are malignant"]','Most of them are malignant','<p><strong>Why Most of them are malignant is correct:</strong> The vast majority of testicular tumors (approximately 95%) are malignant germ cell tumors, with seminomas and non-seminomatous germ cell tumors being the most common types. These tumors arise from germ cells within the seminiferous tubules and have a strong association with cryptorchidism (undescended testes). Benign testicular tumors are exceedingly rare, with Leydig cell tumors and Sertoli cell tumors being the most common benign types, but they constitute less than 5% of all testicular neoplasms.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Most of them are benign:</strong> This is incorrect because malignant tumors account for the overwhelming majority of testicular neoplasms.</li>
<li><strong>2/3 Of tumors are mainly unclassified:</strong> This is incorrect as testicular tumors have well-defined classifications, with germ cell tumors being the predominant type.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Testicular cancer is the most common cancer in men aged 15-35 years.</li>
<li>The most common histologic types are seminomas (40%) and non-seminomatous germ cell tumors (60%).</li>
<li>Cryptorchidism increases the risk of testicular cancer by 4- to 40-fold.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Nearly all testicular tumors are malignant germ cell neoplasms, with benign tumors being exceedingly rare.</p>','<p>Testicular tumors: 95% malignant, 5% benign</p>','<p>Testicular self-examination is crucial early detection method, as testicular cancer has excellent cure rates when caught early (over 95% 5-year survival for localized disease).</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Most of them are benign:</strong> Incorrect as malignant tumors constitute approximately 95% of testicular tumors.</li>
<li><strong>2/3 Of tumors are mainly unclassified:</strong> Incorrect as testicular tumors have well-defined classifications with germ cell tumors being predominant.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Urology','Testicular Conditions','["testicular","tumors","malignant","benign"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.784Z','2026-10-27T16:18:13.009Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua1009ve9oiwd9punnh','mcq','Delay of correction of undescended testis carries risk of','["Infection","Malignancy","Hypertrophy"]','Malignancy','<p><strong>Why Malignancy is correct:</strong> Undescended testis (cryptorchidism) carries an increased risk of malignancy, particularly seminoma and non-seminomatous germ cell tumors, due to the abnormal microenvironment and higher temperature in the undescended position, which may lead to genetic instability and malignant transformation. The risk is estimated to be 4-10 times higher than in normally descended testes, with the malignancy often presenting in the third to fourth decade of life. The exact mechanism involves impaired temperature regulation affecting spermatogenesis and potentially promoting malignant changes in germ cells.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Infection:</strong> While undescended testes may have an increased risk of infection due to poor hygiene or trauma, this is not the primary or most significant complication associated with delayed correction.</li>
<li><strong>Hypertrophy:</strong> Undescended testes typically show atrophy and reduced size rather than hypertrophy due to the unfavorable environment and impaired function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The risk of malignancy in undescended testis is directly related to the duration of time the testis remains in the undescended position.</li>
<li>Orchiopexy should be performed between 6-18 months of age to reduce the risk of both infertility and malignancy.</li>
<li>Post-orchiopexy surveillance is still recommended as the risk of malignancy remains elevated compared to the general population.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Delayed correction of undescended testis increases malignancy risk due to abnormal temperature exposure and genetic instability in germ cells.</p>','<p>TUMOR: Testis Undescended Malignancy Orchiopexy Required</p>','<p>Orchiopexy before 18 months of age significantly reduces the risk of both infertility and malignancy in undescended testis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Infection:</strong> Infection is not the primary complication of undescended testis.</li>
<li><strong>Hypertrophy:</strong> Undescended testes typically show atrophy, not hypertrophy.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Urology','Testicular Conditions','["undescended","testis","malignancy","risk"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.785Z','2026-10-27T16:18:20.799Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua900aae9oisn4adi3t','mcq','Complications of intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) therapy include all of the following EXCEPT:','["Anaphylaxis","Fluid overload","Transmission of HIV","Aseptic meningitis","Systemic reactions"]','Transmission of HIV','<p><strong>Why Transmission of HIV is correct:</strong> IVIG is prepared from pooled human plasma donations that undergo rigorous viral inactivation and purification processes, including solvent/detergent treatment and nanofiltration, which effectively eliminate enveloped viruses like HIV, hepatitis B, and hepatitis C. The risk of viral transmission with modern IVIG preparations is extremely low (estimated at less than 1 case per million doses) due to these multiple safety steps. Unlike blood transfusions, which carry a higher risk of viral transmission, IVIG undergoes additional manufacturing steps specifically designed to inactivate potential pathogens. The FDA and other regulatory agencies strictly regulate these processes to ensure product safety.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Anaphylaxis:</strong> IVIG can cause anaphylactic reactions, particularly in patients with IgA deficiency who have developed anti-IgA antibodies, making this a true complication.</li>
<li><strong>Fluid overload:</strong> Rapid infusion of IVIG can lead to volume overload, especially in patients with pre-existing cardiac or renal impairment, making this a recognized adverse effect.</li>
<li><strong>Aseptic meningitis:</strong> IVIG is associated with aseptic meningitis, which typically occurs within 6-72 hours after infusion and is thought to be related to inflammatory cytokine release.</li>
<li><strong>Systemic reactions:</strong> Systemic reactions including fever, chills, headache, and myalgia are common side effects of IVIG therapy, occurring in up to 15% of recipients.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>IVIG undergoes multiple viral inactivation steps including solvent/detergent treatment and nanofiltration, making viral transmission extremely rare.</li>
<li>Aseptic meningitis is a well-documented complication of IVIG, typically occurring within 72 hours of infusion.</li>
<li>Patients with IgA deficiency are at increased risk of anaphylactic reactions to IVIG due to potential anti-IgA antibodies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> IVIG does not transmit HIV due to rigorous viral inactivation processes, but commonly causes aseptic meningitis, fluid overload, and anaphylaxis in susceptible patients.</p>','<p>IVIG SAFETY: Sepsis risk is minimal, Aseptic meningitis common, Fluid overload possible, Envenomation (anaphylaxis) in IgA deficiency, Transmission (viral) extremely rare, Yield systemic reactions</p>','<p>Always pre-medicate patients with acetaminophen and antihistamines before IVIG administration to reduce the incidence of infusion reactions, and consider slower infusion rates in patients with a history of reactions.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Anaphylaxis:</strong> Occurs in IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA antibodies.</li>
<li><strong>Fluid overload:</strong> Results from rapid infusion in susceptible patients.</li>
<li><strong>Aseptic meningitis:</strong> Occurs within 6-72 hours post-infusion due to cytokine release.</li>
<li><strong>Systemic reactions:</strong> Common side effects including fever, chills, and headache.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["ivig","complications","hiv"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.793Z','2026-10-27T16:20:56.321Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua1009ue9oivc6qai53','mcq','Complication of Polycystic kidney adult type.','["Intracranial aneurysm"]','Intracranial aneurysm','<p><strong>Why Intracranial aneurysm is correct:</strong> Adult polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is associated with intracranial aneurysms due to the underlying defect in the polycystin-1 and polycystin-2 proteins, which are involved in vascular smooth muscle cell function and arterial wall integrity. This defect leads to weakening of the arterial walls, particularly at bifurcation points in the circle of Willis, making them prone to aneurysm formation. The prevalence of intracranial aneurysms in ADPKD patients is approximately 5-10%, which is significantly higher than the general population.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While other complications like hypertension, hematuria, and chronic kidney disease are common in ADPKD, intracranial aneurysm represents a specific vascular complication directly related to the genetic defect affecting vascular structure and function.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Screening for intracranial aneurysms should be considered in ADPKD patients with a family history of aneurysm rupture or with risk factors like hypertension.</li>
<li>The risk of aneurysm rupture is higher in ADPKD patients, particularly during pregnancy or with uncontrolled hypertension.</li>
<li>Magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) is the preferred screening method for detecting intracranial aneurysms in ADPKD patients.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Adult polycystic kidney disease increases intracranial aneurysm risk due to defective polycystin proteins affecting vascular wall integrity.</p>','<p>PKD-ANEURYSM: Polycystic Kidney Disease - Arterial NEURYSM risk</p>','<p>ADPKD patients with a family history of aneurysm rupture should undergo screening for intracranial aneurysms, as their risk is significantly elevated.</p>','','Basic','Renal','Polycystic Kidney Disease','["polycystic","kidney","aneurysm","complication"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.785Z','2026-10-27T16:18:20.801Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua2009we9oi2rg6z1e1','mcq','Regarding autosomal recessive polycystic kidney, which of the following is correct','["Systemic disease","Liver cysts may be present","Macro cysts in spleen and ovaries","Mostly presented in adulthood"]','Systemic disease','<p><strong>Why Systemic disease is correct:</strong> Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) is a systemic disease caused by mutations in the PKHD1 gene, which encodes fibrocystin/polyductin, a protein expressed in renal and biliary ductal plates as well as in other organs. This widespread expression explains why ARPKD affects multiple systems beyond just the kidneys, including the liver (where it causes congenital hepatic fibrosis), pancreas, lungs, and blood vessels. The renal manifestations typically present in infancy or childhood with enlarged kidneys, hypertension, and progressive renal failure, while hepatic involvement may lead to portal hypertension later in life.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Liver cysts may be present:</strong> While liver abnormalities are common in ARPKD, they typically manifest as congenital hepatic fibrosis and biliary ductal plate malformation rather than simple cysts.</li>
<li><strong>Macro cysts in spleen and ovaries:</strong> Cysts are not typically found in the spleen or ovaries in ARPKD; the disease primarily affects the kidneys and liver.</li>
<li><strong>Mostly presented in adulthood:</strong> ARPKD typically presents in infancy or childhood with renal manifestations, unlike autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease which usually presents in adulthood.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>ARPKD is distinct from ADPKD in its inheritance pattern, age of onset, and clinical manifestations.</li>
<li>The combination of renal cysts and congenital hepatic fibrosis is pathognomonic for ARPKD.</li>
<li>Prenatal ultrasound may reveal enlarged, echogenic kidneys in affected fetuses, with oligohydramnios indicating poor renal function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> ARPKD is a systemic disease affecting multiple organs due to widespread expression of the defective fibrocystin protein.</p>','<p>ARPKD = All Really Poor Kids Die (systemic disease affecting multiple organs)</p>','<p>ARPKD should be suspected in infants with enlarged kidneys, hypertension, and congenital hepatic fibrosis, distinguishing it from ADPKD which presents later in life.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Liver cysts may be present:</strong> ARPKD causes congenital hepatic fibrosis, not simple cysts.</li>
<li><strong>Macro cysts in spleen and ovaries:</strong> Cysts are not typically found in these organs in ARPKD.</li>
<li><strong>Mostly presented in adulthood:</strong> ARPKD typically presents in infancy or childhood.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Polycystic Kidney Disease','["polycystic","kidney","disease","autosomal","recessive"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.786Z','2026-10-27T16:18:20.802Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua2009xe9oif67hhdg7','mcq','Treatment of infantile onset polycystic kidney, which is correct','["Beta 2 agonists","Surgical removal of harmful cysts","Symptomatic treatment and transplantation"]','Symptomatic treatment and transplantation','<p><strong>Why Symptomatic treatment and transplantation is correct:</strong> Infantile onset polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the PKHD1 gene, which leads to abnormal renal tubular development and formation of cysts throughout the collecting ducts. This results in progressive renal dysfunction, hypertension, and liver fibrosis. The treatment approach focuses on managing complications such as hypertension, urinary tract infections, and renal failure, with dialysis and kidney transplantation being the definitive management options for end-stage renal disease. Unlike adult polycystic kidney disease, there is no specific therapy to halt cyst formation in ARPKD.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Beta 2 agonists:</strong> These medications are used for bronchospasm and have no role in treating polycystic kidney disease as they don''t address the underlying genetic defect or cyst formation.</li>
<li><strong>Surgical removal of harmful cysts:</strong> While cyst reduction surgeries may be considered in some cases of adult polycystic kidney disease, they are not effective for the diffuse cystic involvement seen in infantile onset polycystic kidney disease and would not preserve renal function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>ARPKD presents with enlarged kidneys, hypertension, and congenital hepatic fibrosis.</li>
<li>Prognosis depends on the severity of renal and hepatic involvement.</li>
<li>Prenatal ultrasound may reveal enlarged, echogenic kidneys in affected fetuses.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Infantile polycystic kidney disease requires lifelong management of complications with kidney transplantation as the definitive treatment for end-stage renal disease.</p>','','<p>Early recognition and management of hypertension in ARPKD is crucial to slow progression to end-stage renal disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Beta 2 agonists:</strong> Not indicated for polycystic kidney disease management.</li>
<li><strong>Surgical removal of harmful cysts:</strong> Not effective for diffuse cystic involvement in ARPKD.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Polycystic Kidney Disease','["polycystic","kidney","treatment","infantile","transplantation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.786Z','2026-10-27T16:18:28.178Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua400a1e9oi3mtvxe76','mcq','The difference between humoral immunity and cellular immunity','["Complement","B cells","Neutrophil"]','B cells','<p><strong>Why B cells is correct:</strong> B cells are the central component of humoral immunity, which involves the production of antibodies that circulate in bodily fluids. When B cells encounter their specific antigen, they differentiate into plasma cells that secrete antibodies, while cellular immunity relies on T cells directly attacking infected or abnormal cells. The key distinction is that humoral immunity provides defense through soluble factors (antibodies) rather than direct cell-to-cell contact, making B cells the defining element of this immune branch.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Complement:</strong> The complement system is part of both humoral and cellular immunity, acting as an amplification system that enhances immune responses regardless of the specific branch.</li>
<li><strong>Neutrophil:</strong> Neutrophils are phagocytic cells of the innate immune system, not specifically part of either humoral or adaptive immunity branches.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Humoral immunity = antibody-mediated defense (B cells, plasma cells, antibodies)</li>
<li>Cellular immunity = cell-mediated defense (T cells, macrophages, NK cells)</li>
<li>The complement system bridges both innate and adaptive immunity</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> B cells define humoral immunity as they produce antibodies, while cellular immunity is defined by T cells that directly attack target cells.</p>','<p>B for Antibodies (Humoral), T for Touch (Cellular)</p>','<p>Humoral immunity defects typically present with recurrent bacterial infections, while cellular immunity defects manifest with viral, fungal, and opportunistic infections.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Complement:</strong> Acts in both humoral and cellular immunity, not specific to either</li>
<li><strong>Neutrophil:</strong> Part of innate immunity, not specifically humoral or cellular</li>
</ul>','Basic','Immunology','Immunity Types','["humoral","cellular","immunity"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.788Z','2026-10-27T16:18:34.480Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua400a0e9oirm1ll7ig','mcq','Which is Incorrect about general management of primary immunodeficiency','["Diet","Avoidance of pathogen","Antibiotics","Live vaccines"]','Live vaccines','<p><strong>Why Live vaccines is correct:</strong> Live vaccines are contraindicated in patients with primary immunodeficiency because they contain attenuated but live pathogens that can cause disease in immunocompromised hosts. These patients lack the normal immune defenses to control even weakened pathogens, potentially leading to severe or fatal infections. The standard management approach for primary immunodeficiency includes supportive measures like diet optimization, pathogen avoidance, and prophylactic antibiotics, but vaccination must be carefully selected to avoid live vaccines.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Diet:</strong> Proper nutrition is essential for immune function and is a cornerstone of managing primary immunodeficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Avoidance of pathogen:</strong> Preventing exposure to pathogens is critical in immunodeficient patients to reduce infection risk.</li>
<li><strong>Antibiotics:</strong> Prophylactic antibiotics are commonly used to prevent bacterial infections in many types of primary immunodeficiency.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, rotavirus, BCG) are absolutely contraindicated in severe T-cell deficiencies</li>
<li>Inactivated vaccines are generally safe and recommended for immunodeficient patients</li>
<li>Immunoglobulin replacement therapy is a mainstay for antibody deficiencies</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> The critical management error in primary immunodeficiency is administering live vaccines, which can cause severe infections in these vulnerable patients.</p>','<p>Live vaccines = Lethal for immunodeficient patients</p>','<p>Before administering any vaccine to a patient with suspected immunodeficiency, assess their immune status and consult with an immunology specialist.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Diet:</strong> Essential for optimal immune function in immunodeficient patients</li>
<li><strong>Avoidance of pathogen:</strong> Critical preventive measure in immunodeficiency management</li>
<li><strong>Antibiotics:</strong> Standard prophylaxis for many primary immunodeficiencies</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Immunology','Primary Immunodeficiency','["immunodeficiency","management","vaccines"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.788Z','2026-10-27T16:18:34.482Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua500a3e9oil2ehaqyl','short','Failure to thrive / protracted diarrhea','[]','T cell defect','<p><strong>Why T cell defect is correct:</strong> Failure to thrive and protracted diarrhea are classic manifestations of T-cell immunodeficiencies, particularly severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) and other T-cell disorders. T cells are essential for orchestrating immune responses, including helping B cells produce antibodies and directly fighting intracellular pathogens. Without functional T cells, children cannot mount appropriate immune responses to pathogens, leading to chronic infections that impair nutrient absorption and growth. The protracted diarrhea specifically results from persistent infections with organisms like Cryptosporidium or rotavirus that require cell-mediated immunity for clearance.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> While B cell defects can lead to recurrent bacterial infections, they typically present with sinopulmonary infections rather than gastrointestinal manifestations like protracted diarrhea. The failure to thrive in B cell defects is usually secondary to recurrent infections rather than direct malabsorption issues.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>T-cell defects often present with opportunistic infections (viral, fungal, protozoal) and failure to thrive</li>
<li>Protracted diarrhea is highly suggestive of T-cell immunodeficiency, particularly SCID</li>
<li>The absence of lymphoid tissue on physical exam is a key finding in T-cell defects</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Failure to thrive with protracted diarrhea points to T-cell immunodeficiency, as T cells are critical for controlling gastrointestinal pathogens and maintaining normal growth.</p>','','<p>T-cell immunodeficiencies should be considered in any infant with failure to thrive, chronic diarrhea, or persistent thrush.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B cell defect:</strong> Would typically present with recurrent bacterial infections rather than protracted diarrhea and failure to thrive.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["failure","thrive","diarrhea","t-cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.789Z','2026-10-27T16:18:42.209Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua600a4e9oihslg6jw7','short','Capsulated bacteria / Giardia','[]','B cell defect','<p><strong>Why B cell defect is correct:</strong> Recurrent infections with encapsulated bacteria (like Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae) and Giardia lamblia are hallmarks of B-cell defects. B cells are responsible for producing antibodies (immunoglobulins) that opsonize encapsulated bacteria for phagocytosis and neutralize pathogens. In X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton''s disease) and common variable immunodeficiency (CVID), patients have low or absent antibody levels, making them susceptible to these specific pathogens. Giardia infections persist because antibodies are needed to clear this parasite from the intestinal tract.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> T-cell defects typically present with infections by intracellular pathogens (viruses, fungi, mycobacteria) and opportunistic organisms, rather than encapsulated bacteria or Giardia, which are extracellular pathogens controlled by antibody-mediated immunity.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>B-cell defects present with recurrent sinopulmonary infections caused by encapsulated bacteria</li>
<li>Giardia infections that persist despite treatment should raise suspicion for humoral immunodeficiency</li>
<li>Low immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM) are characteristic of B-cell defects</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Recurrent encapsulated bacterial infections and persistent Giardia indicate a B-cell defect, as these pathogens require antibody-mediated immunity for clearance.</p>','','<p>Patients with B-cell defects often have normal T-cell function and may present later in childhood with recurrent bacterial infections.</p>','','Moderate','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["capsulated","bacteria","giardia","b-cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.790Z','2026-10-27T16:18:42.211Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua700a5e9oici1frasp','mcq','Dissemination after BCG vaccine','["T cell defect"]','T cell defect','<p><strong>Why T cell defect is correct:</strong> Disseminated BCG vaccine infection is a classic presentation of primary T-cell immunodeficiencies, particularly complete IFN-γ receptor deficiency and other defects in the IFN-γ/IL-12 pathway. BCG is an attenuated mycobacterium that is normally controlled by cell-mediated immunity, specifically Th1 responses involving IFN-γ production. In patients with T-cell defects, particularly those affecting IFN-γ signaling, the attenuated BCG organism disseminates and causes severe systemic disease. This occurs because macrophages cannot be activated to kill intracellular mycobacteria without proper T-cell signaling.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>B cell defect:</strong> Would not typically cause BCG dissemination as BCG is controlled by cell-mediated immunity, not antibody responses. B-cell defects present with encapsulated bacterial infections, not mycobacterial disease.</li>
<li><strong>Complement defect:</strong> Would lead to recurrent Neisserial infections and not affect mycobacterial clearance, which is T-cell dependent.</li>
<li><strong>Phagocyte defect:</strong> Would cause recurrent staphylococcal and fungal infections, not mycobacterial dissemination, as phagocytes require T-cell help for killing intracellular pathogens like mycobacteria.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Disseminated BCG infection is a red flag for T-cell immunodeficiency, particularly defects in IFN-γ signaling</li>
<li>Mycobacterial infections (BCG, environmental mycobacteria) are characteristic of T-cell defects</li>
<li>The IFN-γ/IL-12 pathway is critical for controlling intracellular mycobacterial infections</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Disseminated BCG vaccine infection indicates a T-cell defect, specifically in the IFN-γ pathway required for controlling intracellular mycobacteria.</p>','<p>BCG Dissemination = T-cell Defect (B for Bacteria, but T for T-cell control)</p>','<p>BCG vaccination should be avoided in countries with universal vaccination programs for infants with suspected immunodeficiency.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>B cell defect:</strong> Would not cause BCG dissemination as this requires cell-mediated immunity, not antibodies.</li>
<li><strong>Complement defect:</strong> Presents with Neisserial infections, not mycobacterial disease.</li>
<li><strong>Phagocyte defect:</strong> Causes recurrent staphylococcal and fungal infections, not mycobacterial dissemination.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["bcg","dissemination","t-cell"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.791Z','2026-10-27T16:18:42.212Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuaa00ace9oics1sjzj8','mcq','Useful tests for evaluation of possible B-cell (antibody) deficiency include all of the following except:','["Antibodies to tetanus","Isohemagglutinins","Flow cytometry for CD3 cells","Serum IgA level"]','Flow cytometry for CD3 cells','<p><strong>Why Flow cytometry for CD3 cells is correct:</strong> Flow cytometry for CD3 cells evaluates T-cell numbers and is not a test for B-cell (antibody) deficiency, as CD3 is a marker found on T-cells, not B-cells. B-cell deficiencies are assessed by measuring antibody production and function, while T-cell deficiencies require evaluation of T-cell markers like CD3. This test would be appropriate for evaluating cellular immunity or T-cell disorders, but not for assessing B-cell function.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Antibodies to tetanus:</strong> Measurement of specific antibody responses (like tetanus toxoid) is a functional test of B-cell activity and is essential for evaluating antibody deficiencies.</li>
<li><strong>Isohemagglutinins:</strong> Natural antibodies to ABO blood group antigens are produced by B-cells without prior exposure and serve as a marker of B-cell function.</li>
<li><strong>Serum IgA level:</strong> Quantitative measurement of immunoglobulins (including IgA) is a fundamental test for evaluating B-cell deficiencies, as low levels indicate inadequate antibody production.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>B-cell deficiencies are characterized by low levels of immunoglobulins and poor response to vaccines.</li>
<li>Flow cytometry for B-cell markers (CD19, CD20) is used to assess B-cell numbers, while functional tests evaluate antibody production.</li>
<li>Isohemagglutinins (anti-A and anti-B antibodies) are present in most individuals by 6-12 months of age and serve as a marker of B-cell function.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> To evaluate B-cell deficiency, measure immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody responses; T-cell markers like CD3 are irrelevant for this assessment.</p>','<p>B-cell tests: B for B-cells (CD19/20), Ig levels, and Antibody responses</p>','<p>When evaluating for antibody deficiency, always include both quantitative immunoglobulin levels and functional antibody responses to vaccines.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Antibodies to tetanus:</strong> Functional test of B-cell activity and antibody production.</li>
<li><strong>Isohemagglutinins:</strong> Natural antibodies produced by B-cells without prior exposure.</li>
<li><strong>Serum IgA level:</strong> Fundamental measurement for assessing B-cell function and antibody production.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Immunology','B-cell deficiency','["immunology","b-cell","antibody","diagnosis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.794Z','2026-10-27T16:20:56.324Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua700a6e9oi3lm442ej','mcq','Prolonged attachment of Umbilical cord / poor wound healing','["Phagocytic disorder"]','Phagocytic disorder','<p><strong>Why Phagocytic disorder is correct:</strong> Prolonged umbilical cord attachment beyond 3 weeks and poor wound healing are classic manifestations of phagocytic disorders, particularly chronic granulomatous disease (CGD). These defects impair neutrophil and macrophage function, specifically the ability to produce reactive oxygen species (ROS) via the NADPH oxidase complex, leading to defective intracellular killing of catalase-positive organisms. Without effective phagocytosis, the body cannot clear bacteria that produce their own catalase, resulting in persistent infections and delayed healing of wounds and umbilical stump separation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options provided:</strong> The question only lists the correct answer without alternative distractors, which is unusual for a multiple-choice format. In a complete question, other options might include B-cell disorders (which typically present with recurrent sinopulmonary infections but not specifically umbilical cord issues) or T-cell defects (which often present with opportunistic infections and failure to thrive).</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Phagocytic disorders typically present with recurrent infections with catalase-positive organisms like Staphylococcus aureus and Burkholderia cepacia. - Granuloma formation is a hallmark of CGD due to persistent antigenic stimulation from organisms that cannot be killed. - The nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) test or dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow cytometry can diagnose phagocytic defects by assessing oxidative burst capacity.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Prolonged umbilical cord attachment and poor wound healing should immediately trigger consideration of phagocytic disorders, especially chronic granulomatous disease.</p>','<p>POOR (Prolonged umbilical, Omphalitis, Osteomyelitis, Retention of cord) suggests phagocytic disorder.</p>','<p>Phagocytic disorders often present with infections in unusual sites or with organisms that wouldn''t typically cause disease in immunocompetent hosts.</p>','','Basic','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["umbilical-cord","wound-healing","phagocytic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.791Z','2026-10-27T16:21:22.001Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua800a7e9oi3n0mgwra','mcq','Dermatomyocyte','["Complement defect"]','Complement defect','<p><strong>Why Complement defect is correct:</strong> The term ''dermatomyocyte'' appears to be a misspelling or misdirection for ''dermatomyositis,'' which is strongly associated with complement defects, particularly in juvenile dermatomyositis. Complement components (especially C3 and C4) are consumed in the microangiopathy characteristic of dermatomyositis, leading to low complement levels. The complement system plays a crucial role in clearing immune complexes and facilitating opsonization; defects can result in immune complex deposition in small blood vessels, causing the characteristic perifascicular necrosis in muscle tissue and the pathognomatic skin findings.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>No other options provided:</strong> The question only lists the correct answer without alternative distractors. In a complete question, other options might include T-cell defects (which would present more with opportunistic infections) or B-cell defects (which typically present with recurrent pyogenic infections).</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Dermatomyositis is characterized by pathognomonic skin findings including heliotrope rash (purple discoloration around the eyes) and Gottron''s papules (over knuckles). - Complement levels (C3, C4) are typically low in active dermatomyositis and can serve as a marker of disease activity. - The association between dermatomyositis and malignancy (particularly in adults) necessitates age-appropriate cancer screening.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Dermatomyositis is strongly linked to complement defects due to immune complex-mediated microangiopathy.</p>','<p>Dermatomyositis: Defective Complement causes Dermal and Muscle damage.</p>','<p>Low complement levels (C3, C4) in dermatomyositis reflect disease activity and complement consumption in the microvasculature.</p>','','Basic','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["dermatomyocyte","complement"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.792Z','2026-10-27T16:21:22.004Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuao00ate9oixbgyqfb1','mcq','Which treatment is indicated for severe abdominal pain or severe scrotal edema in HSP?','["Systemic corticosteroids (e.g., Oral Prednisolone)"]','Systemic corticosteroids (e.g., Oral Prednisolone)','<p><strong>Why Systemic corticosteroids (e.g., Oral Prednisolone) is correct:</strong> Systemic corticosteroids are indicated for severe manifestations of Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) including severe abdominal pain and severe scrotal edema because they have potent anti-inflammatory effects that can rapidly reduce vasculitis and tissue edema. In the case of severe abdominal pain, corticosteroids help reduce intestinal wall inflammation and edema, preventing potential complications like intussusception. For severe scrotal edema, corticosteroids reduce inflammation and edema, preventing potential testicular ischemia or torsion. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>NSAIDs:</strong> These are contraindicated in HSP as they can worsen renal function and may increase the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding, which is already elevated in HSP due to vasculitis. - <strong>Antihistamines:</strong> These are only useful for mild pruritus associated with the skin rash but have no effect on the underlying vasculitis or severe manifestations like abdominal pain or scrotal edema. - <strong>Plasma exchange:</strong> This is reserved for severe HSP nephritis with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, not for abdominal or scrotal manifestations. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Corticosteroids do not prevent the development of HSP nephritis but are effective for controlling extrarenal severe manifestations. - The typical dose for severe HSP manifestations is prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day for 2-4 weeks, followed by a gradual taper. - Severe abdominal pain in HSP requires close monitoring for potential surgical complications like intussusception. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Systemic corticosteroids are the treatment of choice for severe abdominal pain or scrotal edema in HSP due to their potent anti-inflammatory effects on vasculitis.</p>','<p>HSP severe: Steroids for belly and scrotum (SBS)</p>','<p>Corticosteroids in HSP are for symptom control and preventing complications of severe extrarenal disease, not for preventing nephritis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>NSAIDs:</strong> Contraindicated as they can worsen renal function and increase GI bleeding risk. - <strong>Antihistamines:</strong> Only useful for mild pruritus, not for severe manifestations like abdominal pain or scrotal edema. - <strong>Plasma exchange:</strong> Reserved for severe HSP nephritis with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, not for abdominal or scrotal manifestations.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Gastrointestinal','HSP Treatment','["hsp","treatment","corticosteroids","abdominal pain","scrotal edema"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.808Z','2026-10-27T16:22:24.489Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua800a8e9oifwpmfi1g','mcq','When to suspect 1ry immunodeficiency','["2 or more sinus infection within 2 years","2 or more pneumonia within 1 year"]','2 or more sinus infection within 2 years','<p><strong>Why 2 or more sinus infection within 2 years is correct:</strong> The criteria for suspecting primary immunodeficiency (PID) are based on the frequency and severity of infections that exceed normal expectations. Two or more sinus infections within two years meets established PID screening criteria because the sinuses are normally sterile sites, and recurrent infections suggest inadequate immune defense. This threshold specifically identifies patients who may have humoral immunity defects (like common variable immunodeficiency) where antibody production is impaired, leading to poor clearance of encapsulated bacteria that commonly colonize the sinuses.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>2 or more pneumonia within 1 year:</strong> While recurrent pneumonia can indicate PID, the threshold of ''within 1 year'' is too stringent for general screening purposes. Current guidelines use less frequent but more specific criteria for pneumonia (like requiring hospitalization or pathogen documentation) to avoid overdiagnosis.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The 10 warning signs of PID developed by the Jeffrey Modell Foundation include recurrent infections requiring antibiotics, failure to thrive, and family history of PID. - Sinus infections in PID are typically caused by encapsulated bacteria like Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae. - Immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM) should be evaluated when PID is suspected based on clinical criteria.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Recurrent sinus infections within two years are a red flag for primary immunodeficiency, particularly humoral defects.</p>','<p>PID: 2 Sinus infections in 2 years = Possible PID (2 in 2).</p>','<p>Recurrent sinus infections in adults should prompt evaluation for humoral immunodeficiency, especially if they require prolonged or repeated antibiotic courses.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>2 or more pneumonia within 1 year:</strong> This threshold is too stringent for general PID screening and may miss milder immunodeficiencies.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Immunology','Immunodeficiency','["immunodeficiency","sinus-infection","pneumonia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.792Z','2026-10-27T16:21:22.005Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuae00aje9oifxosetu7','mcq','What is the major complication in kawasaki disease...?','["Coronary anuresmes","Encephalitis","Myocarditis"]','Coronary anuresmes','<p><strong>Why Coronary aneurysms is correct:</strong> Kawasaki disease is an acute vasculitis of childhood that preferentially affects medium-sized arteries, particularly the coronary arteries. The pathophysiology involves systemic inflammation of the vessel walls, leading to destruction of the internal elastic lamina and smooth muscle cells in the tunica media. This structural damage weakens the arterial walls, causing dilation and aneurysm formation, which is the most serious complication occurring in 15-25% of untreated cases. These aneurysms can lead to thrombosis, myocardial infarction, or sudden death, making them the major concern in Kawasaki disease management.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Encephalitis:</strong> While neurological manifestations can occur in Kawasaki disease (such as aseptic meningitis or irritability), encephalitis is not a major or characteristic complication of this vasculitis.</li>
<li><strong>Myocarditis:</strong> Myocarditis can occur as an acute manifestation of Kawasaki disease, but it is typically self-limited and resolves with treatment, unlike the persistent threat posed by coronary aneurysms.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Coronary artery aneurysms develop in approximately 25% of untreated children with Kawasaki disease.</li>
<li>The risk of aneurysm formation is significantly reduced with prompt administration of intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) within the first 10 days of illness.</li>
<li>Giant aneurysms (&gt;8mm) have the highest risk of thrombosis and require long-term anticoagulation management.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Kawasaki disease''s most dangerous complication is coronary artery aneurysm formation due to medium-vessel vasculitis, which is prevented by early IVIG treatment.</p>','<p>Kawasaki = Kids with fever, rash, red eyes, swollen hands/feet, and cracked lips need IVIG to prevent heart aneurysms (KFRSH-IVIHA).</p>','<p>Early recognition and treatment of Kawasaki disease with IVIG within 10 days of fever onset reduces coronary artery aneurysm risk from 25% to less than 5%.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Encephalitis:</strong> Not a characteristic complication of Kawasaki disease vasculitis.</li>
<li><strong>Myocarditis:</strong> Can occur but is typically self-limited and less dangerous than coronary aneurysms.</li>
</ul>','Easy','Cardiovascular','Kawasaki Disease','["kawasaki","complications","coronary"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.798Z','2026-10-27T16:21:49.709Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuaf00ake9oix8emzgd8','mcq','What is not apart clinical criteria for kawasaki disease...?','["Cervical lymphadenopathy","Vesicular rash","Oropharyngeal mucous","Fever for more than 5 days"]','Vesicular rash','<p><strong>Why Vesicular rash is correct:</strong> Kawasaki disease has specific clinical criteria for diagnosis, including fever for at least 5 days plus four of five principal features: bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection, oral mucosal changes (strawberry tongue, injected pharynx, cracked lips), polymorphous rash, cervical lymphadenopathy, and peripheral extremity changes (edema, desquamation). Vesicular rash, which presents with fluid-filled blisters, is not part of the diagnostic criteria and is not associated with Kawasaki disease. Instead, the rash in Kawasaki disease is typically polymorphous, consisting of maculopapular, urticarial, or scarlatiniform lesions.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cervical lymphadenopathy:</strong> This is one of the principal clinical criteria for Kawasaki disease, typically involving unilateral, non-suppurative lymphadenopathy of at least 1.5 cm in diameter.</li>
<li><strong>Oropharyngeal mucous changes:</strong> This refers to the characteristic oral manifestations including strawberry tongue, injected pharynx, and cracked lips, which are part of the diagnostic criteria.</li>
<li><strong>Fever for more than 5 days:</strong> This is a mandatory criterion for Kawasaki disease diagnosis, as the fever must persist for at least 5 days (or until treatment if shorter) along with other clinical features.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Kawasaki disease diagnosis requires fever for ≥5 days plus ≥4 of 5 principal clinical criteria.</li>
<li>The rash in Kawasaki disease is polymorphous (maculopapular, urticarial, or scarlatiniform), not vesicular.</li>
<li>Atypical or incomplete Kawasaki disease can occur with fewer criteria but still requires urgent recognition to prevent coronary complications.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Kawasaki disease diagnosis requires fever plus four specific criteria, but vesicular rash is never part of the picture.</p>','<p>Kawasaki criteria: Fever + 4 of 5 (Fever, Eyes, Mouth, Rash, Hands/Feet) - remember ''FEMRH'' but exclude vesicular rash.</p>','<p>Incomplete Kawasaki disease can occur in children who don''t meet all criteria but still require treatment if they have fever plus coronary artery abnormalities or other suggestive findings.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Cervical lymphadenopathy:</strong> This is one of the five principal diagnostic criteria for Kawasaki disease.</li>
<li><strong>Oropharyngeal mucous changes:</strong> This refers to the characteristic oral manifestations which are part of the diagnostic criteria.</li>
<li><strong>Fever for more than 5 days:</strong> This is a mandatory criterion for Kawasaki disease diagnosis.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Kawasaki Disease','["kawasaki","diagnostic criteria","clinical"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.799Z','2026-10-27T16:21:49.711Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuaf00ale9oi4l8bpyla','mcq','Old male presents with non-blanching rashes on skin of knee, swelling of knee joint of 2 days. Melena associated with abdominal pain x 1 day. Platelet and prothrombin time are normal. Urine analysis shows 2+ protein, hematuria. What should be your line of management?','["Biopsy and orthopaedic referral.","Symptomatic treatment with rest and analgesia.","Treatment with immunosuppressive.","Course of steroids."]','Biopsy and orthopaedic referral.','<p><strong>Why Biopsy and orthopaedic referral is correct:</strong> This patient presents with classic features of Henoch-Schonlein purpura (HSP), a systemic vasculitis characterized by IgA deposition in small vessels. The non-blanching rash (palpable purpura), arthritis (knee swelling), gastrointestinal symptoms (melena, abdominal pain), and renal involvement (proteinuria, hematuria) form the classic pentad of HSP. Biopsy of the skin rash would reveal leukocytoclastic vasculitis with IgA deposition, confirming the diagnosis, while orthopedic referral addresses the joint involvement. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Symptomatic treatment with rest and analgesia:</strong> While supportive care is part of management, it doesn''t address the need for diagnostic confirmation or potential complications like renal disease. - <strong>Treatment with immunosuppressive:</strong> Immunosuppressives are not first-line for HSP and are reserved for severe cases with significant organ involvement, not initially. - <strong>Course of steroids:</strong> Steroids may be used for severe manifestations (like severe abdominal pain or nephritis) but don''t replace the need for diagnostic biopsy and don''t alter the natural course of the disease. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - HSP is the most common vasculitis in children, typically occurring after an upper respiratory infection. - Renal involvement (seen here with proteinuria and hematuria) is the most serious complication and requires monitoring for potential progression to nephritis. - The diagnosis is primarily clinical but can be confirmed by skin biopsy showing IgA deposition. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Henoch-Schonlein purpura requires diagnostic confirmation via biopsy and management of potential complications, not just symptomatic treatment.</p>','<p>HSP pentad: Purpura, Arthritis, Abdominal pain, Hematuria, Edema (PAAHE)</p>','<p>In HSP, renal involvement typically develops 1-3 weeks after the onset of rash and requires monitoring for progression to crescentic glomerulonephritis.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Symptomatic treatment with rest and analgesia:</strong> Doesn''t address diagnostic confirmation or potential complications. - <strong>Treatment with immunosuppressive:</strong> Not first-line for typical HSP presentations. - <strong>Course of steroids:</strong> Not initial treatment and doesn''t replace diagnostic biopsy.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Renal','Vasculitis','["rash","hematuria","melena","proteinuria"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.799Z','2026-10-27T16:22:05.611Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuan00are9oim4i5qb0g','mcq','What is the typical urinary finding in a patient developing HSP nephritis?','["Microscopic or macroscopic hematuria with variable proteinuria"]','Microscopic or macroscopic hematuria with variable proteinuria','<p><strong>Why Microscopic or macroscopic hematuria with variable proteinuria is correct:</strong> Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) nephritis is characterized by IgA immune complex deposition in the glomeruli, leading to inflammation and damage of the glomerular filtration barrier. This damage results in hematuria (due to red blood cells passing through the damaged barrier) and proteinuria (due to loss of protein through the compromised filtration membrane). The hematuria can be microscopic (detected only by urinalysis) or macroscopic (visible as cola-colored or tea-colored urine), while proteinuria ranges from mild (subnephrotic) to severe (nephrotic range). <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Isolated proteinuria without hematuria:</strong> This is uncommon in HSP nephritis as the glomerular injury typically affects both the filtration of blood cells and proteins. - <strong>Isolated microscopic hematuria without proteinuria:</strong> While this can occur early in the disease process, it''s not the typical finding as most patients with renal involvement develop some degree of proteinuria. - <strong>Nephrotic syndrome (heavy proteinuria with hypoalbuminemia):</strong> While some patients with severe HSP nephritis develop nephrotic syndrome, it''s not the typical presentation; most have milder proteinuria. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - The presence of hematuria is the most sensitive marker for HSP nephritis, occurring in 80-100% of patients with renal involvement. - Proteinuria severity correlates with prognosis, with nephrotic-range proteinuria indicating a worse prognosis. - Red blood cell casts on urinalysis suggest glomerular involvement and are more specific for HSP nephritis than isolated hematuria. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> HSP nephritis classically presents with hematuria (microscopic or macroscopic) plus variable proteinuria due to IgA-mediated glomerular damage.</p>','<p>HSP nephritis: Hematuria + Proteinuria (HP pattern)</p>','<p>The presence of dysmorphic red blood cells and red blood cell casts on urinalysis strongly suggests glomerular origin of hematuria in HSP.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Isolated proteinuria without hematuria:</strong> Uncommon as glomerular injury typically affects both blood cells and protein filtration. - <strong>Isolated microscopic hematuria without proteinuria:</strong> Not typical as most patients with renal involvement develop some proteinuria. - <strong>Nephrotic syndrome:</strong> Not the typical presentation; occurs only in a minority with severe disease.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','HSP Nephritis','["hsp","nephritis","urinary","hematuria","proteinuria"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.807Z','2026-10-27T16:22:24.489Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuap00ave9oi5kult2l2','mcq','Common assaaction with Polyarticular JIA RF +ve','["Fever","Pericarditis"]','Pericarditis','<p><strong>Why Pericarditis is correct:</strong> Polyarticular Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (JIA) that is rheumatoid factor (RF) positive represents a subset with more severe systemic manifestations similar to adult rheumatoid arthritis. Pericarditis is a well-recognized extra-articular manifestation in this subset, occurring due to systemic inflammation and immune complex deposition in the pericardium. RF positivity indicates a more aggressive disease course with higher likelihood of systemic complications including pericarditis, pleuritis, and vasculitis. Fever, while common in systemic JIA subtypes, is not specifically associated with RF-positive polyarticular JIA in the same characteristic way as pericarditis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fever:</strong> Fever is more characteristic of systemic JIA (Still''s disease) rather than specifically RF-positive polyarticular JIA, though it can occur during disease flares in any JIA subtype.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>RF-positive polyarticular JIA has a more severe course with higher risk of joint destruction and systemic complications compared to RF-negative polyarticular JIA.</li>
<li>Pericarditis is a significant extra-articular manifestation in RF-positive polyarticular JIA, requiring cardiac monitoring.</li>
<li>Systemic complications in JIA include pericarditis, pleuritis, vasculitis, and rheumatoid nodules, with RF positivity increasing this risk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Pericarditis is a classic systemic complication specifically associated with RF-positive polyarticular JIA, while fever is more characteristic of systemic JIA subtypes.</p>','<p>RF-positive JIA: Pericarditis is a systemic manifestation (PPM - Pericarditis, Pleuritis, Vasculitis)</p>','<p>RF-positive polyarticular JIA requires aggressive treatment with DMARDs early in the disease course to prevent joint destruction and systemic complications.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Fever:</strong> Fever is more typical of systemic JIA (Still''s disease) rather than specifically RF-positive polyarticular JIA.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Cardiovascular','Polyarticular JIA','["jia","polyarticular","rf positive","pericarditis"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',-1,-1,0,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.809Z','2026-10-27T16:22:31.503Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriubs00cse9oivivbejmx','mcq','If carditis is counted as a major criterion, which associated minor criterion must not be double counted?','["Elevated CRP","Fever","Prolonged PR interval","Raised ESR"]','Fever','','','','','Moderate','Rheumatology','Jones Criteria','["jones","criteria","carditis","fever"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.848Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.779Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriubv00cxe9oi79ojlsps','mcq','Which statement best describes the usual course of Sydenham chorea?','["It always causes permanent paralysis","It often improves without lasting neurological sequelae","It necessarily destroys large joints","It is defined by PR prolongation"]','It often improves without lasting neurological sequelae','','','','','Moderate','Neurology','Sydenham Chorea','["rheumatic","chorea","neurological"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.851Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.778Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriudt00fwe9oinzebqigm','mcq','In pathological jaundice, what is the rate of bilirubin rise?','["less than 5 mg/dl","less than 3 mg/dl","less than 2 mg/dl","More than 5 mg/dl"]','More than 5 mg/dl','','','','','Basic','General','Neonatal Jaundice','["bilirubin","jaundice","pathological"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.921Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.777Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriub800bre9oi7lgf2xxf','mcq','Wilson disease is','["Excess of copper","Deficiency of chloride","Deficiency of copper","Disease that affects only the liver"]','Excess of copper','','','','','Basic','Hepatology','Wilson Disease','["wilson disease","copper excess","metabolic"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.828Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.781Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuaq00awe9oihtdn7x0i','mcq','Chronic uveitis is one of the extraarticular manifestations of various subtypes of juvenile idiopathic arthritis. All the following are risk factors for the development of uveitis EXCEPT','["Oligoarthritis subtype","Female gender","ANA-positivity","Severity of the disease"]','Female gender','<p><strong>Why Female gender is correct:</strong> Chronic uveitis is a serious extra-articular manifestation of juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA), with certain subtypes and characteristics increasing risk. Oligoarthritis subtype (particularly persistent oligoarthritis) is the strongest risk factor for uveitis, occurring in up to 20% of cases. ANA-positivity is another significant risk factor, particularly in oligoarthritis, with approximately 80% of JIA-associated uveitis patients being ANA positive. Disease severity correlates with uveitis risk, as more active and prolonged inflammation increases the likelihood of ocular involvement. However, female gender is not a risk factor for uveitis in JIA; in fact, some studies suggest a slight male predominance in JIA-associated uveitis.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Oligoarthritis subtype:</strong> Oligoarthritis (especially persistent oligoarthritis) is the highest risk factor for uveitis in JIA, with 13-20% of these patients developing uveitis.</li>
<li><strong>ANA-positivity:</strong> ANA positivity is strongly associated with uveitis in JIA, particularly in oligoarthritis, with approximately 80% of JIA-uveitis patients being ANA positive.</li>
<li><strong>Severity of the disease:</strong> More severe and prolonged joint disease increases the risk of developing uveitis, as chronic inflammation drives extra-articular manifestations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Oligoarthritis is the JIA subtype with the highest risk of uveitis (13-20% of patients), requiring regular screening eye exams.</li>
<li>ANA positivity is a major risk factor for uveitis in JIA, with affected patients needing more frequent ophthalmologic monitoring.</li>
<li>JIA-associated uveitis is often asymptomatic initially, making regular screening crucial for early detection and prevention of complications like cataracts and glaucoma.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Oligoarthritis and ANA positivity are major risk factors for JIA-associated uveitis, while female gender is not associated with increased risk.</p>','<p>Oligo + ANA = Uveitis risk (OA-U)</p>','<p>JIA-associated uveitis is often asymptomatic initially, necessitating regular screening eye exams every 3-6 months for high-risk patients.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Oligoarthritis subtype:</strong> This is the highest risk factor for uveitis in JIA, occurring in 13-20% of cases.</li>
<li><strong>ANA-positivity:</strong> Approximately 80% of JIA-uveitis patients are ANA positive, making it a significant risk factor.</li>
<li><strong>Severity of the disease:</strong> More severe and prolonged joint disease increases uveitis risk due to chronic inflammation.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Ophthalmology','JIA Uveitis','["jia","uveitis","risk factors","oligoarthritis","ana"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.810Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.781Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuan00ase9oip8why28n','mcq','How long should routine urinalysis and blood pressure monitoring be continued following an episode of HSP?','["At least 6 months"]','At least 6 months','<p><strong>Why At least 6 months is correct:</strong> Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) is a systemic vasculitis that can affect the kidneys, and while most renal involvement occurs within the first 6 months, a significant minority of patients develop delayed nephritis. The 6-month monitoring period allows for detection of late-onset renal complications, including proteinuria, hematuria, and declining renal function. This monitoring period is based on natural history studies showing that 80-90% of renal manifestations appear within the first 6 months after the onset of HSP, with the majority occurring within the first month. <strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Less than 6 months:</strong> This would miss the 10-20% of patients who develop renal complications later in the disease course, potentially leading to irreversible kidney damage. - <strong>More than 6 months:</strong> While some guidelines suggest extending monitoring if initial urinalysis is abnormal, routine monitoring beyond 6 months is not necessary for all patients as the risk of new-onset nephritis drops significantly after this period. - <strong>Only during acute phase:</strong> This approach would miss the significant number of patients who develop renal complications after the initial acute phase has resolved. <strong>Key pearls:</strong> - HSP nephritis can occur in up to 50% of children and 20-40% of adults, with most cases presenting within the first month. - The most common renal manifestation is hematuria (microscopic or macroscopic), which may be accompanied by proteinuria. - Hypertension is a red flag for significant renal involvement and requires immediate evaluation. <strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Monitor urinalysis and blood pressure for at least 6 months after HSP diagnosis to detect the 10-20% of patients who develop delayed nephritis.</p>','<p>HSP monitoring: 6 months for kidneys (6M4K)</p>','<p>HSP nephritis prognosis is generally better in children than adults, with adults more likely to progress to chronic kidney disease.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Less than 6 months:</strong> Would miss late-onset renal complications in 10-20% of patients. - <strong>More than 6 months:</strong> Not routinely necessary as risk of new nephritis drops significantly after 6 months. - <strong>Only during acute phase:</strong> Misses post-acute phase renal complications that commonly occur.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Renal','HSP Monitoring','["hsp","monitoring","urinalysis","blood pressure","follow-up"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.807Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.781Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriuab00ade9oiqiavtjza','mcq','A 9-month-old boy presented with recurrent attacks of diarrhea, thrush, and failure to thrive shortly after birth. You suspect immune deficiency. Of the following, the MOST common cause of this condition is','["Hyper-IgE syndrome","Defect in phagocytic cells","Deficiencies in T-cell function","Defect in antibody production","Defect in complement proteins"]','Defect in antibody production','<p><strong>Why Defect in antibody production is correct:</strong> The most common cause of immune deficiency presenting with recurrent diarrhea, thrush, and failure to thrive in infancy is a defect in antibody production (specifically X-linked agammaglobulinemia or Bruton''s disease), which accounts for approximately 50% of all primary immunodeficiencies. This condition results from mutations in the BTK gene, leading to a developmental arrest at the pro-B cell stage and consequently absent mature B cells and all immunoglobulin classes. The absence of functional antibodies makes patients particularly susceptible to encapsulated bacteria (like Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae) and enteroviruses, explaining the recurrent diarrhea and infections.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hyper-IgE syndrome:</strong> Characterized by elevated IgE levels, recurrent staphylococcal infections, and eczema, but typically presents later in infancy with characteristic facial and skeletal features.</li>
<li><strong>Defect in phagocytic cells:</strong> Would typically present with recurrent bacterial infections (especially catalase-positive organisms like Staphylococcus aureus) and abscesses rather than the pattern described.</li>
<li><strong>Deficiencies in T-cell function:</strong> Would present with more severe and opportunistic infections (viral, fungal, protozoal) typically within the first few months of life, often with failure to thrive and chronic diarrhea.</li>
<li><strong>Defect in complement proteins:</strong> Would present with recurrent Neisseria infections and is much rarer than antibody deficiencies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Antibody deficiencies are the most common primary immunodeficiencies, accounting for approximately 50% of cases.</li>
<li>X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton''s disease) is the most common antibody deficiency, affecting males with recurrent bacterial infections after 6 months when maternal antibodies wane.</li>
<li>The absence of palpable lymph nodes and tonsils is a classic physical finding in antibody deficiencies.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Recurrent diarrhea, thrush, and failure to thrive in infancy most commonly point to an antibody deficiency, specifically X-linked agammaglobulinemia.</p>','<p>ABC: Antibody deficiencies are the Most Common primary immunodeficiency</p>','<p>In antibody deficiencies, infections typically begin after 6 months of age when maternal antibodies wane, and patients are particularly susceptible to encapsulated bacteria and enteroviruses.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Hyper-IgE syndrome:</strong> Typically presents later with elevated IgE, staphylococcal infections, and eczema.</li>
<li><strong>Defect in phagocytic cells:</strong> Presents with recurrent bacterial infections and abscesses, especially with catalase-positive organisms.</li>
<li><strong>Deficiencies in T-cell function:</strong> Presents with more severe opportunistic infections typically within the first few months of life.</li>
<li><strong>Defect in complement proteins:</strong> Presents with recurrent Neisseria infections and is much rarer.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Immunology','Primary Immunodeficiency','["immunodeficiency","pediatrics","diarrhea","thrush"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.795Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.781Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriua3009ye9oikbhrf6sh','mcq','As regard testicular torsion, which is wrong about this emergency','["Need surgical correction","Common in children below 10 years","Doppler color study is needed","IF neglected, testicular sloughing and gangrene can occur"]','Common in children below 10 years','<p><strong>Why Common in children below 10 years is correct:</strong> Testicular torsion is most common in neonates and adolescents, with a peak incidence between 12-18 years of age, not in children below 10 years. The condition occurs when the spermatic cord twists, compromising blood flow to the testicle. While it can occur at any age, the highest risk period is during puberty when the testicle grows rapidly relative to the surrounding tissues, making it more susceptible to torsion.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other answer would be wrong:</strong> Testicular torsion is a surgical emergency requiring immediate intervention to preserve testicular viability. Doppler color ultrasound is essential to confirm the diagnosis by showing absent or reduced blood flow to the affected testicle. If neglected, testicular torsion can lead to testicular ischemia, infarction, and eventual sloughing and gangrene within hours, making timely diagnosis and treatment critical.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Testicular torsion presents with sudden onset severe scrotal pain, nausea, and an elevated, horizontal testicle.</li>
<li>The ''prehn sign'' (elevation relieving pain) is unreliable; imaging with Doppler ultrasound is diagnostic.</li>
<li>Bell-clapper deformity is an anatomical anomaly predisposing to torsion where the testicle is inadequately anchored.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Testicular torsion is most common in adolescents, not young children, and requires emergency surgical intervention to prevent testicular loss.</p>','<p>6 is the magic number: 6 hours to surgery for 50% testicular salvage in torsion.</p>','<p>Testicular torsion is a time-sensitive emergency with salvage rates decreasing to 50% after 6 hours and 10% after 12 hours.</p>','','Moderate','Urology','Testicular Conditions','["testicular","torsion","emergency","surgery","children"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.787Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriubz00d3e9oitv2s4b5o','mcq','The purpose of appropriately indicated long-acting penicillin secondary prophylaxis is primarily to:','["Reverse established valve scarring immediately","Prevent recurrent streptococcal-triggered rheumatic attacks","Treat fever without further examination","Replace all diagnostic criteria"]','Prevent recurrent streptococcal-triggered rheumatic attacks','','','','','Basic','Cardiovascular','Rheumatic Fever','["prophylaxis","penicillin","streptococcal"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.855Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.782Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriudo00fpe9oibaccql1h','mcq','Rifampicin causes jaundice because of:','["Increased bilirubin generation","Blocked bilirubin uptake","Hinder bilirubin conjugation","Impaired sinusoidal anion transport"]','Hinder bilirubin conjugation','','','','','Moderate','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced Jaundice','["rifampicin","jaundice","bilirubin"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.916Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.785Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvmwzvnu001gg7rdgm52g1ei','mcq','A 2-year-old child presents with rickets characterized by bowed legs, delayed fontanelle closure, and hypocalcemia. Laboratory findings show low serum calcium, low serum phosphorus, and elevated alkaline phosphatase. Which of the following pharmacological treatments would be most appropriate for this nutritional rickets?','["Calcium carbonate supplements","Ergocalciferol (vitamin D2)","Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D)","Phosphate supplements alone","Alendronate"]','Ergocalciferol (vitamin D2)','Nutritional rickets is caused by vitamin D deficiency and is treated with vitamin D repletion [S1, S2]. Ergocalciferol (vitamin D2) is the most commonly used form for treatment, typically administered at doses of 1000-5000 IU daily for several weeks [S1]. This addresses the underlying deficiency and allows for proper calcium absorption and bone mineralization [S1, S2].','Nutritional rickets = Vitamin D deficiency = Ergocalciferol = Fix the foundation (NVD)','After initial vitamin D repletion, maintenance therapy with 400-600 IU daily of vitamin D is typically required. Calcium and phosphorus levels should be monitored, and in some cases, calcium supplements may be needed initially if hypocalcemia is severe. The clinical response is usually seen within 1-2 weeks with improvement in biochemical parameters and radiographic changes over several months.','Option A (calcium carbonate) alone is insufficient as it doesn''t address the vitamin D deficiency needed for calcium absorption. Option C (calcitriol) is used in vitamin D-dependent rickets, not nutritional rickets. Option D (phosphate supplements alone) can worsen hypocalcemia by precipitating calcium. Option E (alendronate) is a bisphosphonate used for osteoporosis, not rickets.','Medium','pharmacology','Rickets','["ACE inhibitors, Constipation, Hirschsprung Disease, Platelet Disorders, Rickets","Pharmacology","deep-agent","Rickets","pediatrics","endocrinology","bone metabolism"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-24T21:32:20.106Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.788Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo4yua1000gwkwuoambjaiq','mcq','A 68-year-old female with chronic constipation secondary to opioid therapy is being evaluated for pharmacologic management. She has tried increasing fiber and fluid intake with minimal improvement. Which of the following pharmacologic agents would be MOST appropriate as first-line therapy for her opioid-induced constipation, and what is its primary mechanism of action?','["A. Bisacodyl - Stimulates colonic mucosa by increasing fluid secretion and inhibiting sodium absorption","B. Polyethylene glycol 3350 - Osmotically draws water into the intestinal lumen to increase stool volume and soften stool","C. Senna - Activates enteric nervous system to increase peristalsis and reduce colonic transit time","D. Docusate sodium - Acts as a surfactant to allow water penetration into stool, facilitating easier passage"]','B','Polyethylene glycol (PEG) 3350 is considered first-line therapy for opioid-induced constipation due to its osmotic mechanism and favorable safety profile [S1]. PEG works by drawing water into the intestinal lumen through osmosis, increasing stool volume and softening stool without significantly altering electrolyte balance [S2]. This makes it particularly suitable for chronic use in opioid-induced constipation. Bisacodyl and senna are stimulant laxatives that can cause electrolyte disturbances and are generally not first-line for chronic constipation. Docusate sodium is a stool softener with limited efficacy as a monotherapy for moderate to severe constipation [S1].','PEG = Perfect for Elderly (opioid-induced) constipation - Osmotic mechanism','For opioid-induced constipation, PEG 3350 is first-line due to its osmotic action and minimal systemic absorption. Avoid stimulant laxatives long-term due to electrolyte risks and potential cathartic colon.','A. Bisacodyl - Stimulant laxatives like bisacodyl work by irritating the colonic mucosa and are not first-line for chronic opioid-induced constipation due to potential electrolyte disturbances and tolerance development. C. Senna - Another stimulant laxative that can cause melanosis coli and is not ideal for chronic management. D. Docusate sodium - Acts as a surfactant but has limited efficacy as monotherapy for moderate constipation; it''s often used adjunctively.','Medium','gastrointestinal','constipation pharmacology','["Constipation","Pharmacology","deep-agent","constipation pharmacology","constipation","pharmacology","opioid-induced","laxatives"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-25T18:03:14.761Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.788Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqputeo0019e9oiad8qr0lw','mcq','A 68-year-old man with chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min) presents with progressive fatigue and hemoglobin of 9.0 g/dL. His serum iron is normal, ferritin is 80 ng/mL, and transferrin saturation is 25%. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate initial treatment for his anemia?','["Intravenous iron supplementation","Oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) with iron supplementation","Vitamin B12 injections","Folate supplementation"]','Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) with iron supplementation','This patient has anemia of chronic kidney disease (CKD), which is characterized by relative erythropoietin deficiency. The appropriate treatment is an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) combined with iron supplementation to optimize response. Iron supplementation alone is insufficient in CKD due to impaired erythropoietin production. Oral iron may be used but is less effective than IV iron in CKD patients. Vitamin B12 and folate are not indicated as the laboratory findings don''t suggest deficiency.','CKD Anemia = ESA + Iron','In CKD anemia, target hemoglobin is typically 10-11.5 g/dL to avoid complications from both anemia and excessive erythropoiesis stimulation.','Option A (IV iron alone) is insufficient without ESA in CKD. Option B (oral iron) is less effective in CKD. Option D (B12) and E (folate) are not indicated based on normal iron studies.','Medium','nephrology','anemia treatment','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","anemia treatment","chronic kidney disease","anemia of chronic disease","erythropoietin"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T13:23:31.296Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.783Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqpshc4000te9oil1ehj8wn','mcq','A 65-year-old patient with hypertension and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 45 mL/min/1.73m²) is started on lisinopril. Two weeks later, laboratory results show: Serum potassium 5.2 mmol/L (reference 3.5-5.0), serum creatinine 1.8 mg/dL (baseline 1.5 mg/dL), and blood pressure 135/85 mmHg. What is the most appropriate next step in management?','["Discontinue lisinopril immediately due to acute kidney injury","Reduce lisinopril dose by 50% and repeat labs in 1 week","Add a potassium-binding resin to manage hyperkalemia","Continue current dose and repeat labs in 2-4 weeks","Switch to an ARB instead of ACE inhibitor"]','Continue current dose and repeat labs in 2-4 weeks','The modest increase in serum creatinine (from 1.5 to 1.8 mg/dL, representing a 20% increase) and mild hyperkalemia (5.2 mmol/L) are expected with ACE inhibitor initiation in patients with chronic kidney disease. Current guidelines recommend that a <30% increase in serum creatinine is acceptable and does not warrant discontinuation of the ACE inhibitor. The potassium level is only mildly elevated and does not require immediate intervention. The appropriate management is to continue the current dose and repeat monitoring within 2-4 weeks to assess for further changes.','ACE-30 rule: <30% creatinine increase = acceptable, continue monitoring','Expected changes with ACE inhibitors: mild creatinine rise (<30%) and mild hyperkalemia are common and often stabilize with continued therapy. Only discontinue for >30% creatinine increase or severe hyperkalemia (>5.5 mmol/L).','Option A is incorrect because the creatinine increase is <30%, which is acceptable. Option B is unnecessary as the changes are within expected parameters. Option C is excessive for this mild potassium elevation. Option E is incorrect as ARBs have similar effects on renal function and potassium.','Medium','cardiovascular','ACE inhibitors monitoring and adverse effects','["ACE inhibitors","General","deep-agent","ACE inhibitors monitoring and adverse effects","ACE inhibitors","renal monitoring","hyperkalemia","hypertension management"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T13:21:42.340Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.787Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugqu002ytzlr6r43kppv','mcq','A child presents with episodes of severe hypertension, headache, palpitations, and sweating. Laboratory tests show increased urinary catecholamine metabolites. Which investigation is most appropriate for further evaluation?','["CT scan followed by MIBG scan if needed"]','CT scan followed by MIBG scan if needed','<p><strong>Why CT scan followed by MIBG scan if needed is correct:</strong> CT scan followed by MIBG scan if needed is the most appropriate investigation for further evaluation in this child with episodes of severe hypertension, headache, palpitations, and sweating with increased urinary catecholamine metabolites. These findings are highly suggestive of pheochromocytoma, a neuroendocrine tumor arising from chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla that secretes catecholamines. CT scan is the initial imaging modality of choice for detecting adrenal masses, with high sensitivity for pheochromocytomas. If the CT is negative or inconclusive, a metaiodobenzylguanidine (MIBG) scan can be performed, which is a nuclear medicine scan that specifically targets catecholamine storage sites and has high specificity for pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>MRI alone:</strong> While MRI is useful for evaluating adrenal masses, it lacks the functional information provided by MIBG scan and may miss extra-adrenal pheochromocytomas. - <strong>24-hour urine metanephrines alone:</strong> Although increased urinary catecholamine metabolites are diagnostic, imaging is still required to localize the tumor for surgical planning. - <strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> This has lower sensitivity and specificity compared to CT or MRI for detecting adrenal pheochromocytomas and is not the preferred initial imaging modality.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Pheochromocytoma should be considered in any patient with episodic hypertension, palpitations, sweating, and headache, especially when associated with increased urinary catecholamine metabolites. - The classic triad of symptoms includes headache, sweating, and tachycardia, though many patients present with sustained hypertension. - 10% of pheochromocytomas are extra-adrenal (paragangliomas), 10% are bilateral, and 10% are malignant, emphasizing the need for accurate localization.</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> For suspected pheochromocytoma, start with CT scan for localization, followed by MIBG scan if needed for confirmation or to detect extra-adrenal tumors.</p>','','<p>Pheochromocytoma is known as the ''10% tumor'' because 10% are extra-adrenal, 10% are bilateral, 10% are malignant, and 10% occur in children (the ''rule of 10s'').</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>MRI alone:</strong> Lacks the functional information provided by MIBG scan and may miss extra-adrenal tumors. - <strong>24-hour urine metanephrines alone:</strong> While diagnostic, imaging is still required for tumor localization. - <strong>Abdominal ultrasound:</strong> Has lower sensitivity and specificity compared to CT or MRI for detecting adrenal pheochromocytomas.</li>
</ul>','Moderate','Endocrinology','Pheochromocytoma','["pheochromocytoma","catecholamines","hypertension"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,2,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.222Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.792Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqpueob0015e9oi0kurnzb5','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and a hemoglobin level of 8.5 g/dL. Her peripheral blood smear shows microcytic, hypochromic red blood cells. Which laboratory finding would be MOST characteristic of iron deficiency anemia in this patient?','["Elevated serum ferritin (>300 ng/mL)","Low serum iron (<30 μg/dL) with high total iron-binding capacity (>400 μg/dL)","Normal serum iron with low transferrin saturation (<15%)","Elevated serum folate levels","Increased reticulocyte count (>3%)"]','Low serum iron (<30 μg/dL) with high total iron-binding capacity (>400 μg/dL)','Iron deficiency anemia is characterized by low serum iron (<30 μg/dL) and high total iron-binding capacity (>400 μg/dL), resulting in low transferrin saturation (<15%). Serum ferritin is typically low (<15 ng/mL) in iron deficiency, not elevated. Elevated serum ferritin suggests iron overload or inflammation. Normal serum iron with low transferrin saturation can occur but is less specific than the classic low iron/high TIBC pattern. Increased reticulocyte count would suggest a bone marrow response, which is not typical in uncomplicated iron deficiency.','Low Iron, High TIBC = Iron Deficiency','In iron deficiency anemia, serum ferritin is the most sensitive indicator, but when combined with low serum iron and high TIBC, the diagnosis becomes highly specific.','Option A (elevated ferritin) represents iron overload or inflammation. Option C (normal iron, low saturation) can occur but is less specific. Option D (elevated folate) is unrelated. Option E (increased reticulocytes) suggests bone marrow response, not seen in uncomplicated iron deficiency.','Medium','hematology','anemia diagnosis','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","anemia diagnosis","iron deficiency","laboratory diagnosis","microcytic anemia"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6wresk0012j6rmn8yzcdiz","chunkId":"cmv6ytwok00b9bjrmy25fb3ij","documentId":"cmv6wtegx001bj6rmbmwhyx8b","title":"Peditric-Med.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"What Koilonychia & Atrophic Glossitis Actually Look Like Investigations\\n★ In the order they change as iron stores are depleted:\\n# Finding\\n1 Decreased bone-marrow hemosiderin (iron tissue stores) — the very first thing to fall 2 Decreased serum ferritin\\n3 Decreased serum iron and transferrin saturation\\n4 Increased total iron-binding capacity (TIBC)\\n5 Increased free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (FEP)\\n6 Microcytosis, hypochromia, poikilocytosis\\n7 Decreased MCV & MCH; increased RDW (>15%); nucleated RBCs; low reticulocytes 8 Bone marrow: erythroid hyperplasia\\nS Clarifications Note — Iron Studies — Order & the FEP Mechanism (39) Practical order the doctor emphasized: ferritin is the first thing to actually fall in the routine labs you can order (bone marrow hemosiderin isn’t tested routinely) — then serum iron falls, then TIBC starts to rise.","chunkContent":"What Koilonychia & Atrophic Glossitis Actually Look Like Investigations\\n★ In the order they change as iron stores are depleted:\\n# Finding\\n1 Decreased bone-marrow hemosiderin (iron tissue stores) — the very first thing to fall 2 Decreased serum ferritin\\n3 Decreased serum iron and transferrin saturation\\n4 Increased total iron-binding capacity (TIBC)\\n5 Increased free erythrocyte protoporphyrin (FEP)\\n6 Microcytosis, hypochromia, poikilocytosis\\n7 Decreased MCV & MCH; increased RDW (>15%); nucleated RBCs; low reticulocytes 8 Bone marrow: erythroid hyperplasia\\nS Clarifications Note — Iron Studies — Order & the FEP Mechanism (39) Practical order the doctor emphasized: ferritin is the first thing to actually fall in the routine labs you can order (bone marrow hemosiderin isn’t tested routinely) — then serum iron falls, then TIBC starts to rise. Why does FEP rise in IDA?","answerCoverage":0.83,"judgeScore":8}]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T13:23:12.203Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.783Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvobcjze000u1twuv55z922r','mcq','A patient presents with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypertension. Laboratory evaluation shows suppressed plasma renin activity and low aldosterone levels. Which of the following conditions best explains this clinical picture?','["Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn''s syndrome)","Secondary hyperaldosteronism due to renal artery stenosis","Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1","Liddle syndrome","Bartter syndrome"]','D','Liddle syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) subunits in the distal nephron. This leads to increased sodium reabsorption and subsequent potassium excretion and hydrogen ion secretion, causing hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. The hypertension results from volume expansion due to sodium retention. Importantly, the increased sodium delivery to the distal tubule suppresses renin release, and the volume expansion suppresses aldosterone secretion [S1]. This differs from primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn''s syndrome) where aldosterone levels are elevated, and secondary hyperaldosteronism where both renin and aldosterone are elevated.','','Liddle syndrome is often misdiagnosed as primary hyperaldosteronism, but the key distinguishing feature is the low aldosterone level despite the clinical picture. Treatment involves ENaC inhibitors (amiloride or triamterene) rather than spironolactone, which is ineffective in this condition.','Option A is incorrect because primary hyperaldosteronism would show elevated aldosterone levels. Option B is incorrect because secondary hyperaldosteronism would show elevated renin and aldosterone levels. Option C is incorrect because pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 would show aldosterone resistance with elevated aldosterone levels. Option E is incorrect because Bartter syndrome would show elevated renin and aldosterone levels with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis.','Medium','renal','hormonal regulation','["renal physiology","Renal","deep-agent","hormonal regulation","hypertension","hypokalemia","metabolic alkalosis","ENaC channel"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-25T21:01:52.298Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.787Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugor0013tzlr2ah9zeta','mcq','Which of the following is considered one of the triggers of functional constipation in children?','["Excessive parental pressu"]','Excessive parental pressu','<p><strong>Why Excessive parental pressure is correct:</strong> Excessive parental pressure is a well-documented psychological trigger for functional constipation in children. When parents pressure children about toileting, children may develop withholding behaviors as a form of resistance or anxiety. This withholding behavior leads to stool retention, which causes the rectum to stretch and lose sensitivity, resulting in larger, harder stools that are painful to pass and perpetuating the constipation cycle. This creates a vicious cycle where the child withholds more due to fear of pain, leading to worsening constipation.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong> - <strong>Low fiber diet:</strong> While low fiber intake contributes to constipation, it''s not considered a primary psychological trigger of functional constipation. - <strong>Insufficient fluid intake:</strong> Dehydration can cause constipation, but it''s a physiological factor rather than a psychological trigger. - <strong>Lack of physical activity:</strong> Reduced physical activity may contribute to constipation, but it''s not a recognized psychological trigger like parental pressure.</p>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong> - Functional constipation in children is often multifactorial, with psychological factors playing a significant role in perpetuation. - The withholding behavior pattern is a hallmark of functional constipation and is frequently triggered by parental pressure or negative toilet training experiences. - Treatment must address both the physical aspects (stool softeners, timed toileting) and psychological components (reducing pressure, positive reinforcement).</p>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Excessive parental pressure creates a vicious cycle of withholding behavior that perpetuates functional constipation in children.</p>','<p>PP: Pressure causes Painful withholding, Perpetuating constipation</p>','<p>Addressing family dynamics and reducing toileting pressure is as important as medical management in treating childhood functional constipation.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Low fiber diet:</strong> Contributes to constipation but is not a psychological trigger. - <strong>Insufficient fluid intake:</strong> Causes constipation but is a physiological factor, not a psychological trigger. - <strong>Lack of physical activity:</strong> May contribute to constipation but is not a recognized psychological trigger.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Gastroenterology','Constipation','["functional constipation","trigger","parental pressure"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0.3333333333333333,-1,3,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.147Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.793Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriu890073e9oit3dwogrx','mcq','4-month-old infant fed with concentrated formula to help with weight gain. What is the expected complication?','["Rickets","Vitamin D toxicity","Hypoglycemia","Hypernatremia"]','Hypernatremia','<p><strong>Why Hypernatremia is correct:</strong> Concentrated formula has higher solute load relative to water, leading to hyperosmolar state. The infant''s immature kidneys cannot adequately concentrate urine to excrete the excess solutes, resulting in water retention relative to sodium. This creates a hypertonic state with elevated serum sodium concentration (&gt;145 mEq/L), which can cause neurological symptoms due to brain cell dehydration. The increased osmotic pull draws water from cells into the extracellular space, potentially leading to intravascular volume depletion despite the hypernatremia.</p>
<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rickets:</strong> Rickets results from vitamin D deficiency or impaired calcium/phosphate metabolism, not from concentrated formula feeding.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin D toxicity:</strong> This occurs from excessive vitamin D intake, not from concentrated formula which doesn''t contain toxic levels of vitamin D.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoglycemia:</strong> Concentrated formula provides more calories, making hypoglycemia less likely rather than more likely in this scenario.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key pearls:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hypernatremia in infants is often caused by dehydration with water loss exceeding sodium loss.</li>
<li>Concentrated formula increases osmolar load beyond infant renal handling capacity.</li>
<li>Symptoms of hypernatremia include lethargy, irritability, and hypertonicity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>One-line takeaway:</strong> Concentrated formula in infants causes hypernatremia due to excessive solute load relative to water intake, overwhelming immature renal concentrating ability.</p>','<p>CHF - Concentrated formula causes Hypernatremia, not Failure (hypoglycemia) or Rickets</p>','<p>Hypernatremia in infants is a medical emergency requiring careful correction with hypotonic fluids to prevent cerebral edema.</p>','<ul>
<li><strong>Rickets:</strong> Results from vitamin D deficiency, not concentrated formula.</li>
<li><strong>Vitamin D toxicity:</strong> Caused by excessive vitamin D intake, not formula concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Hypoglycemia:</strong> Less likely with concentrated formula which provides more calories.</li>
</ul>','Basic','Endocrine','Infant Nutrition','["infant","nutrition","hypernatremia"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.721Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.783Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvqriucg00dse9oi2y01yu3f','mcq','A full term neonates delivered by normal vaginal delivery with acrocyanosis (blue hands and feet), heart rate 80/minute, with irregular respiration/grunting, with no grimace response to ryle stimulation and flexed arms and legs; what is your APGAR score for this infant','["APGAR score 6","APGAR score 5","APGAR score 4","APGAR score 3"]','APGAR score 4','','','','','Moderate','Pediatrics','Neonatal Assessment','["apgar","neonatal","calculation"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-27T14:10:11.872Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.786Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlluneb000sdbrdpgo3vv9y','mcq','A 58-year-old man with a history of gastric surgery presents with progressive weakness, numbness in his hands and feet, and difficulty walking. His hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL, MCV 105 fL, and serum B12 is 100 pg/mL (normal 200-900 pg/mL). Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology and treatment of his condition is most accurate?','["Neurologic symptoms are primarily due to folate deficiency and will respond to folate supplementation","Treatment requires intramuscular B12 injections initially, followed by lifelong maintenance","Oral B12 supplementation is ineffective due to lack of intrinsic factor","The anemia will resolve within 2-4 weeks of starting treatment"]','Treatment requires intramuscular B12 injections initially, followed by lifelong maintenance','This patient has pernicious anemia (autoimmune gastritis with intrinsic factor deficiency) causing vitamin B12 deficiency. Neurologic symptoms are due to B12 deficiency affecting myelin synthesis and will not respond to folate alone. Initial treatment requires intramuscular B12 injections to bypass the gastrointestinal absorption defect, followed by lifelong maintenance therapy [S1]. Oral B12 can be used for maintenance but not for initial treatment of severe deficiency. The anemia typically resolves within 4-6 weeks, but neurologic recovery may take months or be incomplete if treatment is delayed [S3].','Pernicious anemia = IM B12 first, then oral; neurologic symptoms need B12, not folate','In B12 deficiency, always check for neurologic symptoms before starting folate, as folate can mask but not treat neurologic damage','A (Folate deficiency) doesn''t cause neurologic symptoms; folate alone can worsen neurologic damage. C (Oral B12) is ineffective for initial treatment due to lack of intrinsic factor. D (2-4 weeks) is too fast for complete hematologic response','Medium','Neurology/Hematology','Anemia Pathophysiology and Treatment','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Pathophysiology and Treatment","vitamin B12 deficiency","pernicious anemia","neurologic manifestations"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-23T23:32:34.163Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.788Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmve814mi0013dbucyvbtihtr','mcq','A 2-year-old child presents with progressive bowing of the legs, delayed fontanelle closure, and frontal bossing. Laboratory studies show low serum calcium (7.8 mg/dL), low serum phosphorus (2.9 mg/dL), elevated alkaline phosphatase (450 U/L), and elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH). X-rays reveal widened epiphyseal plates and fraying of the metaphyses. What is the most appropriate initial treatment for this condition?','["A. Calcium carbonate supplementation alone","B. High-dose vitamin D (600,000 IU) orally","C. Phosphate supplementation alone","D. Calcitriol (active vitamin D) replacement"]','B','The clinical presentation and laboratory findings are classic for nutritional rickets due to vitamin D deficiency. The key pathophysiological defect is impaired calcium absorption from the gut due to inadequate active vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D), leading to hypocalcemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism, and defective bone mineralization. The most appropriate initial treatment is high-dose vitamin D replacement (typically 300,000-600,000 IU orally), which rapidly corrects the vitamin D deficiency and allows for proper calcium absorption and bone mineralization. Calcium and phosphate supplementation may be added in severe cases, but vitamin D is the cornerstone of treatment. Calcitriol is reserved for specific genetic disorders of vitamin D metabolism.','Vitamin D deficiency rickets treatment: ''VD First'' - Vitamin D must be replaced before calcium can be properly absorbed','In rickets, elevated PTH is a compensatory response to hypocalcemia, not the primary problem. Treating the underlying vitamin D deficiency corrects both the calcium absorption defect and the secondary hyperparathyroidism.','Option A (calcium alone) is incorrect because without vitamin D, calcium cannot be properly absorbed from the gut. Option C (phosphate alone) is incorrect because phosphate supplementation without addressing the vitamin D deficiency can lead to ectopic calcification and does not correct the fundamental absorption defect. Option D (calcitriol) is incorrect for nutritional rickets as it bypasses the underlying problem of vitamin D deficiency and is more expensive and less practical than standard vitamin D replacement.','Medium','endocrine','rickets','["Rickets","Endocrine","deep-agent","rickets","vitamin D deficiency","rickets","bone mineralization","hypocalcemia"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-18T19:31:18.570Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.791Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvo4zjwt000kwkwuzzsydbk8','mcq','A 2-week-old male infant presents with abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. Physical examination reveals a distended abdomen with absent bowel sounds. Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and diagnosis of this condition is most accurate?','["The condition results from congenital absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon, leading to functional obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells","The condition is caused by intrinsic colonic muscle hypertrophy, leading to mechanical obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by abdominal ultrasound showing thickened bowel wall","The condition results from neuroblastoma infiltration of the colon, leading to luminal narrowing, and diagnosis is confirmed by CT scan showing a mass lesion","The condition is caused by congenital absence of the enteric nervous system throughout the entire gastrointestinal tract, leading to complete obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by barium enema alone"]','The condition results from congenital absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon, leading to functional obstruction, and diagnosis is confirmed by rectal biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells','Hirschsprung disease is a congenital disorder characterized by the absence of ganglion cells (aganglionosis) in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon. This absence leads to a functional obstruction due to unopposed parasympathetic activity, causing tonic contraction of the affected segment. The classic clinical presentation in neonates includes abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. While barium enema may show a transition zone between the dilated proximal colon and narrowed distal colon, the gold standard for diagnosis is rectal biopsy, which demonstrates the absence of ganglion cells and hypertrophied nerve trunks in the submucosa [S1][S2][S3].','Hirschsprung''s: ''No Gang, No Go'' - No ganglion cells, no movement (obstruction)','In neonates with suspected Hirschsprung disease, remember that the diagnosis requires histological confirmation of aganglionosis via rectal biopsy; barium enema can be suggestive but is not diagnostic alone.','Option B is incorrect because Hirschsprung disease is not caused by muscle hypertrophy but by neural dysfunction. Option C is incorrect as it describes a neoplastic process rather than a congenital developmental disorder. Option D is incorrect because the aganglionosis is typically limited to the distal colon, not the entire GI tract, and barium enema alone is insufficient for diagnosis.','Medium','pediatrics','gastrointestinal disorders','["Hirschsprung Disease","Pediatrics","deep-agent","gastrointestinal disorders","hirschsprung disease","congenital disorders","pediatrics","gastrointestinal"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-25T18:03:47.981Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.788Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmut9ugnx000ctzlrut9bwxmf','mcq','Not true about Kwashiorkor','["thrombocytosis","Leukopenia","Leukocytosis","Anemia"]','Leukocytosis','','','','','Moderate','Nutrition','Kwashiorkor','["kwashiorkor","leukocytosis","blood findings"]',false,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,2,'2026-10-04T03:38:57.117Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.795Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlzjkgk001rv6rd9cj9eq4z','mcq','According to current AAP guidelines, what is the recommended duration for exclusive breastfeeding?','["For the first 3 months of life","For the first 6 months of life","Until 9 months of age","Until 12 months of age","For the first 4 months of life"]','For the first 6 months of life','The AAP recommends exclusive breastfeeding for approximately 6 months. This means the infant receives only breast milk (no water, juice, formula, or solid foods) for the first 6 months. After 6 months, complementary foods should be introduced while continuing breastfeeding until at least 12 months of age, and longer if mutually desired by both mother and infant. This recommendation is based on extensive evidence showing optimal health benefits for both mother and child.','EXCLUSIVE BREASTFEEDING → 6 MONTHS','Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months provides optimal nutrition and immune protection. After 6 months, introduce iron-rich complementary foods while continuing breastfeeding.','Option 1 (3 months) is too short and not evidence-based. Option 3 (9 months) exceeds the recommended exclusive breastfeeding period. Option 4 (12 months) refers to continued breastfeeding, not exclusive breastfeeding. Option 5 (4 months) is also too short.','Medium','general','breastfeeding_guidelines','["General Medicine","General","deep-agent","breastfeeding_guidelines","pediatrics","breastfeeding","nutrition","AAP_guidelines"]',true,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"},{"type":"library","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b009ui8rmtgleqjxc","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"The AAP’s 2022 policy update (its first revision since 2012) kept the recommendation of exclusive breastfeeding for about 6 months, but for the first time explicitly supports continuing breastfeeding for 2 years or beyond, \\"as mutually desired by mother and child\\" — bringing AAP into alignment with WHO.","chunkContent":"The AAP’s 2022 policy update (its first revision since 2012) kept the recommendation of exclusive breastfeeding for about 6 months, but for the first time explicitly supports continuing breastfeeding for 2 years or beyond, \\"as mutually desired by mother and child\\" — bringing AAP into alignment with WHO. It also documents that extending breastfeeding past 12 months lowers maternal risk of Type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and breast/ovarian cancer. Sources: WHO Guideline for Complementary Feeding of Infants and Young Children 6–23 Months of Age (who.int/publications, 2023); AAP Policy Statement & Technical Report, Breastfeeding and the Use of Human Milk, Pediatrics 2022;150(1) (publications.aap.org); Journal of Pediatric Gastroenterology and Nutrition, multisociety response, 2024 (onlinelibrary.wiley.com).","answerCoverage":0.67,"judgeScore":10},{"sourceId":"cmv6xgvop0000xurml1w3mzsy","chunkId":"cmv6yxa2b008ii8rm0kb0uw5p","documentId":"cmv6xj56p0001xurmnthxtip5","title":"Peditric-Med (1).pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months, then continued breastfeeding with appropriate complementary foods up to 2 years or beyond. Breast milk contains 87%–90% water and is considered the gold-standard fluid for infants — no formula fully replicates it.","chunkContent":"Continue BF with solid food\\nWHO and UNICEF emphasize optimal infant and young child feeding practices, formalized through the Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) — a global effort supporting optimal breastfeeding practice within healthcare facilities.\\nExclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months, then continued breastfeeding with appropriate complementary foods up to 2 years or beyond.\\nBreast milk contains 87%–90% water and is considered the gold-standard fluid for infants — no formula fully replicates it.\\n\\n1.5 Taking a Nutritional History in Infants\\nThis is a clinical-skills topic the doctor added independently — it does not appear on the lecture slides","answerCoverage":0.67,"judgeScore":10}]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-24T05:55:51.764Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.788Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('citetest000001','mcq','In areas of LOW endemicity, which patient group is most typically affected by sporadic cases of hepatitis E?','["Young children in rural areas with poor sanitation","Pregnant women in hyperendemic regions","Elderly persons with other diseases, including immunosuppressed persons","Healthcare workers after needlestick exposure"]','Elderly persons with other diseases, including immunosuppressed persons','In low-endemicity regions (Europe, North America, Japan), sporadic HEV cases occur mostly in elderly persons and those with other diseases, including immunosuppressed persons — a zoonotic pattern distinct from the waterborne outbreaks of hyperendemic regions.','','','','Medium','Infectious Disease','Hepatitis E epidemiology','["hepatitis E","epidemiology","HEV"]',false,'','','','','','','[{"type":"library","title":"prevention-of-transmission.pdf"}]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','["cmv6wqust0000j6rmojonyoak"]','[{"sourceId":"cmv6wqust0000j6rmojonyoak","chunkId":"cmv6ys2xe000fbjrmpi8j0vqq","documentId":"cmv6wr5ae0001j6rm95jznclm","title":"prevention-of-transmission.pdf","fileKind":"document","quote":"– Occasional sporadic cases – mostly in elderly persons, those with other diseases, including immunosuppressed persons There are two epidemiological patterns of hepatitis E virus. In Asian and African countries, where HEV is hyperendemic, large outbreaks are frequent. In these regions, sporadic cases occur throughout the year.","chunkContent":"– Occasional sporadic cases – mostly in elderly persons, those with other diseases, including immunosuppressed persons There are two epidemiological patterns of hepatitis E virus. In Asian and African countries, where HEV is hyperendemic, large outbreaks are frequent. In these regions, sporadic cases occur throughout the year. These epidemics and sporadic cases occur because of faecal contamination of the water. On the contrary, in developed countries where HEV is not endemic, only occasional sporadic cases have been seen and are primarily caused by zoonotic transmission of HEV. Ongoing surveillance is required in case of outbreaks.","answerCoverage":1,"judgeScore":10}]','approved','',0,-1,2,'2026-10-13T16:44:03.028Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.790Z');
INSERT INTO "Question" ("id","type","text","options","answer","explanation","mnemonic","clinicalPearl","distractorAnalysis","difficulty","system","topic","tags","isAIGenerated","illustrationDescription","organInvolvement","mechanismDiagram","visualSummary","diagramSpec","molecularScenes","sources","imageUrl","audioUrl","videoUrl","modelUrl","videoTaskId","labPanel","pcgSpec","hotspots","structureTags","blueprintOrgan","blueprintTask","knowledgeSourceIds","citations","reviewStatus","reviewNotes","difficultyP","discrimination","attemptsCount","createdAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvlltus3000kdbrd6avv29cn','mcq','A 68-year-old man with chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min) presents with progressive fatigue and dyspnea on exertion. His hemoglobin is 9.0 g/dL, MCV 85 fL, reticulocyte count 1%, serum iron 40 μg/dL, TIBC 400 μg/dL, ferritin 15 ng/mL, and transferrin saturation 10%. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment approach?','["Oral iron supplementation 325 mg ferrous sulfate daily","Intravenous iron sucrose 1000 mg divided over 5 doses","Erythropoietin-stimulating agent 40,000 units weekly","Blood transfusion 2 units packed red blood cells"]','Intravenous iron sucrose 1000 mg divided over 5 doses','This patient has anemia of chronic kidney disease (CKD) with evidence of absolute iron deficiency (low ferritin <30 ng/mL, low transferrin saturation <20%). In CKD patients with iron deficiency, intravenous iron is preferred over oral iron due to poor absorption and increased inflammation [S5]. The IV iron sucrose regimen is appropriate for this patient with moderate CKD. Erythropoietin may be needed but should be given after iron repletion. Transfusion is reserved for symptomatic anemia not responding to other treatments [S1].','CKD + low ferritin = IV iron; oral iron poorly absorbed in inflammation','In CKD, always check iron studies before starting erythropoiesis-stimulating agents; IV iron is preferred for established iron deficiency','A (Oral iron) is poorly absorbed in CKD due to inflammation and reduced gastric acidity. C (Erythropoietin) should be given after iron repletion. D (Transfusion) is not first-line for chronic anemia management','Medium','Nephrology/Hematology','Anemia Treatment','["anemia","Hematology","deep-agent","Anemia Treatment","anemia of CKD","iron deficiency","intravenous iron"]',true,'','','','','','','[]','','','','','','[]','','[]','[]','','','[]','[]','pending','',0,-1,1,'2026-10-23T23:31:57.075Z','2026-10-27T16:37:00.797Z');

